Do you know who this person is?
Do you? Maybe yes, maybe no?
He's Leonard Nimoy.
Who's Leonard Nimoy? Speak up!
He's Spock, great!
That man is Mr. Spock,
probably no one recognized him
at first, maybe only this girl, - but when I told you he was Mr. Spock ... Who knows Mr. Spock?
A lot of people.
He's an actor, an artist; a person with no real job; being an artist doesn't really pay your home mortgage.
A real job is when you got a degree, you're an engineer, a doctor, a lawyer.
Do you know who's this lady?
She certainly has got a real job and is a former army pilot and an astronaut.
She's Samantha Cristoforetti, who speaks five languages and has two degrees.
At the moment, she's on orbit, and the problem with Leonard, the previous fellow, is that he died last year. So Samantha, while in space, gave us the Star Trek Vulcan salute and wore a Star Trek t-shirt.
If you think about this, she's in outer space because she has been inspired by Star Trek movies;
maybe you've been inspired by another book, by something else, any other thing written by a dreamer. A crew worked like hell to build her spaceship. That's a real space station, she's really on orbit and what you see is the Earth's curvature.
She's maybe an astronaut because she wanted to do the things they did in Star Trek: go in outer space, explore it much like you made your choice after that special movie or book, or after something that inspired you.
So yes, engineers and this type of fellows are important and have a real job; but dreamers are also important. They invent things even if they don't physically build them. They're important, those guys are inspiring the future
and as importantly, lots of people are really creating the future.
I studied Computer Graphics: if I wanted to become a doctor, my main problem would have been all those years I should have studied, tons and tons of books, so much stuff.
Architecture is another real job, but it's not that better: because I got so many stuff to study there too!
Engineering? Wow, that's even worse.
Photography? Slightly better, just got 200-300 years. So it's pretty good.
What if I would like to do CG? Yesterday. Yes, because CG was born yesterday so there are very few things to study.
So I chose CG! This is how a CG worker was perceived. So when you tell someone you're doing CG, the second question they ask you is: I got a problem with Word, can you fix it?

Sorry, I don't know how to use Word.
That's weird, you're a genius!
Just because people think those things, people think about that guy behind the computer doing incredible stuffs in his basement, not talking to anyone, no one knows what he does. Then suddenly a movie hit the theaters, probably just seven people saw it..
Did anybody see it in this room?
They're all here, unbelievable!
So that's a moment in history.
(Applause) This movie represents the first time artists used CG to make stuffs that used to be made by engineers or other pointy-head folks.
But people didn't get it completely, so Pixar came and did this.
Who watched Toy Story?
From there, it has become clear that CG could make money but could also tell a lot of stories; that technology wasn't so complicated anymore and artists could finally use CG like a pen.
Let's start a short CG course:
what a CG artist does is opening his computer, opening Maya - a software among others, 3D Studio Max or no matter what, pushing the Create button, then pushing the Polygone, Cube buttons and after that Move Vertex. Those are real commands of the software; he will do it again and again, a million times, all day long; and then he finally gets this. That's cool, isn't it?
He will do it again,
so many times, all day long, maybe for a couple of weeks too and then at the end you'll see this, because he made the Star Trek spaceship.
Artists make them in movies by moving vertexes.
But what's most interesting is that so many people are studying gravitational waves, black holes, warp engines, teleport or are willing to reach Mars because they've been inspired by this, because a dreamer has made this movie.
You've already been on Mars, you've seen The Martian, or all those movies that depict Mars in detail.
You already went there,
For you guys, all future missions would probably be like, no big deal, we're going to Mars..
Being able to do this means that you're continuously inspired by movies, every day.
So many dreamers, so many people are working on things that probably other people will realize.
I'm so lucky I'm working in a school where we grow tons of those dreamers.
Beyond explaining them CG, we tell that everything is possible and we bring them in the middle of nowhere, to the desert between California and Utah,
we take these guys more or less 20-25 years old, and after the CG course, we leave them in the desert with ten jeeps, saying,
"Just do what you feel,
everything is possible."
Want to go somewhere? Go!
Do you really want to go with seven cars
in the middle of nowhere? Just go there!
Ready, set, go,
because you can, it's possible.
If I had a magic wand now and brought you back in time - maybe someone is around 40 here like me - if they could give me a magic wand and put me back to 15, if I wanted to become an astronaut, I would probably be into orbit now,
100% granted.
Who would undoubtedly change
their life, if I put them back to 15?
Just me? Two people? Only four of us?
So everyone else had a perfect life?
Well, sounds great.
That's what we have to explain to our kids, we need to convey now - not when they'll be 40 - that you can change your life but you have to do it now,
because everything is possible.
For example, have a look at this incredible view.
What strikes you as weird?
That star?
That's not a star!
This picture has been taken by Curiosity on Mars.
That's Mars and the star; the pixel you see in the middle is the Earth.
We are on that pixel.
All the troubles we make, - I do this because I can't see you - all the troubles we make all wars and problems, all that stuff about crude oil, a total mess; that pixel.
Everything is downsized from Mars, which is our neighbor, is truly around the corner,
it's not hard to get there. Going to Alpha Centauri is, or to other galaxies; but there's so much stuff in the middle.
We can discover incredible things;
CG practicioners have already been there. You all saw Star Trek and those movies, normal people should see it also. Because when you realize that you are on that pixel, you have to choose: either you're a dreamer and design the future; or you can be inspired by a dreamer and truly create it.
If you're in the middle, you're wasting your time.
(Applause)
Good evening,
I am not sure whether it is meta-advertising but it’s a nice way to do advertising these days, it’s a nice way to generate ideas, to tackle creativity in advertising to talk about brands and products that we deal with on a daily basis.
While preparing this speech I found myself writing down this term as I was thinking about the borders and boundaries of this job
and I found 'borderlive', which in fact does not exist: it unites, contaminates the border with life.
This is what we do nowadays surrounded by the many borders of this job, the many perimeters and daily limits of creativity in advertising. Don’t let the word creativity mislead you, it’s a trap...
Creativity in advertising has plenty of borders. As professionals, we are involved every day in something very important, the collective imagination, and we're somehow asked to tackle this job with briefs.
I apologise for the English of this slide unluckily, advertisement professional jargon is filled with many English words that sound like: brief, insight, target, budget, competitor, research, media, format, guideline.
These are the many, too many constraints that creative advertisers face every day.
Perhaps this is a new way of facing creativity in advertising that many of us are already using especially in our agency - along with many colleagues, mainly at international level, and we are learning to take some of these borders and limits and to move them further away and to take a bolder step towards people.
I will show you how some of these limits, those belonging to the creative talent, to who has to “think” creativity,
insights, targets, media, time, space, truth, if managed in a different way, completely change the planning approach.
Thinking about these borders and limits, and trying to consider them in a completely new and different way compared to the past, forces all of us to advertise in a different way.
If we keep talking about insight we will always deal with needs to be met, indeed, often needs to be invented, induced.
No one needs a shiny smile.
That shiny smile of ads is a marketing invention.
People need a healthy and clean mouth.
But at some stage, a multinational company, at the sight of packed shelves, decided that in order to be more convincing it would be enough to say that your smile had to be shiny like the movie stars, otherwise you’d be a loser.
We are learning to use cultural tensions as a subject of our communications.
It means raising the bar of communication, dealing with people’s lives, it means looking outside our power-points and our meeting rooms, it means tackling topics that are relevant to our communication.
If you produce a snack, nutrition, child obesity, are your problem, and you have to deal with that somehow.
If you deal with beauty and beauty products you need to demolish some paradigms, to break clichés,
you cannot keep talking to your audience using stereotypes.
Women in ad, tipically, either is a 90-60-90 hottie or the feminine audience will despise her.
Women’s self-esteem must become a topic.
This brand, through the campaign on authentic beauty, finally features attainable models of beauty.
Everyone can somehow listen to her own truth, through the brand communication.
Of course some people also say - and I deem it a crime - that cellulitis is a pathology.
But I prefer staying on this side, namely I’d rather deal with cultural tension, get into it, and face this topic with responsibility.
Another limit of this job is the target.
The target forces to adopt a very precise planning approach.
Target means that you: aim at; hit; kill; aim at; hit; conquer.
Addressing a target forces us to move the communication in a certain way.
Dealing with an audience, instead, changes completely the planning approach.
Because I look for consent, I look for an applause, I try to tune in with the audience in order to gain consent.
And consent equals reputation, reputation equals money,
and there is nothing philanthropic in this all.
It is simply about doing this job in a deeply different way.
I ask my student to develop projects without the word target.
Finding synonyms, audience, interlocutors, people, citizens, you name it; but not 'addressing target clusters to destroy'.
Because this is the only way to do ads that people will chose.
Amongst the 10 most visualised videos in 2014, 4 were ads.
Do you realise that?
Millions of people <i>decided</i> to watch an ad?
It means that it is not true what we do is bad.
Not true.
If we manage to spread important communication topics, and the brand is plausible in making that kind of statement, and is able to entertain its audience rather than pushing it into supermarkets at any cost, and people choose that brand.
If the brand is able to make people choose it, it somehow enters their lives in a slightly more relevant way and is doomed to stay for a long time because people end up believing in that brand, for what the brand itself represents.
The fate of the brand and the fate of people walk hand in hand.
The narrative matrix of the brand and its narrative ends up resembling the tale of those who recognises him/herself in that brand.
Another extremely important border within our job has always been the media.
Our ads end up on the media: tv, press, bill boarding, digital culture, the Internet, social networks, - yes, social networks.
They are called social networks, meaning people have accessed facebook, twitter, instagram in order to be in touch.
<i>We</i> called them social media, because that definition meant that they were somehow used as media.
Talking about narrative habitats means to fully respect such spaces.
And look, social network users put likes, follow brands, link brand communication in their feed.
When you put a like on a brand page, the content of that brand appears on your, on our existential feed like the partner, the wife, the son, the colleague, the schoolmate who found us and organizes a reunion dinner.
Narrative habitat means being very aware of the existential nature of that space.
The brands that are learning to perform this job better, are subsequently renewing their communication. are brands followed by people for the quality of the contents that they are able to feature.
Which is not the apology of products, functions and what advertising logics have always been promoting.
Narrative becomes something else, it is shared, participated, it becomes entertainment, it becomes many things, in order to speak the same language of people.
In Italy, there are over 4 million people connected to the Nutella page, over 30 million all over the world.
I do work with these people, and I can assure you that: creating a little content every day for this brand, forces you to think with a different approach about the development of the idea.
Look at Dolce &amp; Gabbana, and the number of social networks they have in their ecosystem, as the smart ones call it.
And for each social network there is a different content, for each social network there is a different grammar, the need to communicate constantly and keep in touch, to have a dialogue with those people who have somehow chosen that brand and brought it in their lives.
And so, millions of people ask brands for a relationship,
because eventually, within such narrative habitats, if we think about our daily behaviour, 70 -80% of conversations is about what we eat, we drive, about what we wear, our consumption choices that we constantly display once they represent us in some way.
But they're no longer a status symbol. They represent us in a deeper way they represent us because the brands we choose, at least some of them, also personify the matrix of our values.
We identify ourselves with their story, their fate is somehow similar to ours.
The concept of time and space… borders of advertising up until some time ago.
This has all been made possible thanks to the maturation of the Internet, to an increasingly more pervasive digital culture.
Advertisement has always been confined within very precise times and spaces. The ad spot would last 30 seconds; the pre-roll on YouTube lasts 15, billboards are 6 by 3, 70-100, ads on papers are one page, which has a very exact perimeter.
Therefore, time and space have a certain influence on our job; to move within this new scenario and to try to cross that border in a new direction, makes us say: let’s start dealing with the audience attention not with time and space.
Let’s try to grasp attention but not like the ads used to do.
Do you know that the etymology of reclame (ad) is “to call loudly”?
To call for someone’s attention you had to shout.
Of course you had to, given the crowding of communication, the incitement overdose is so high that ads have to shout.
There is always someone who has to speak louder.
Whereas attention has to do with something memorable, not with memorising.
Advertising has always asked to memorise messages. Do you remember the ear worms of advertisement…?
If you try to remember, over the last 2 or 3 years you will not find such an ear worm.
You can’t find those:
“Have a break, have a Kit Kat”, nor “ah Bisto”.
You won’t find it simply because, at some point, it got harder to call attention in that way and we learnt how to become memorable.
Gaining attention through memorability means, for instance, to transform the poster of an online real estate agency claiming “searching for a home is easy, search for a home in one click”. And these people around airports and stations, thinking about the context of display of the advertisement - it is not <i>planned</i>, but <i>displayed</i>.
Wars are planned, advertisement can also be displayed -
so they transformed this structure in a tiny flat for homeless people because it is easy to say, just search for a home online, when outside stations, big city airports there are so many homeless people.
You can see that advertising can change attitude towards the social context hosting it.
This became a tiny flat that these local NGOs manage every week, or 15 days in order to host homeless people.
Hence, that promise made by the ad acquires a new meaning.
As a TED speaker would say, it gets <i>meaningful</i>.
It acquires new meanings, the paradigm falls apart.
Ultimately there's the issue of truth. Truth, in advertising, has always been a real border, almost a taboo.
Let’s admit that: advertising and truth have never got on very well some bullshit were said, and there will be more to come for a while, let’s get over it.
But I don’t want to give up this word, and I’m realising that turning truth from a taboo to a need, clearly and definitely changes our projects, gets us closer to people, creating more confidence.
Brands are doing this very well in some of their projects.
Think how controversial the subject McDonald's is.
In the US it opened a digital platform that remains online for days, where people ask questions over and over again… and answers arrive in real time from McDonald’s staff, the nutritionist… to address even very controversial topics, as the McJob for example, and a whole series of themes.
Then Domino's did the same in an important campaign...
In Italy we have the Barilla campaign: when the problem of illegal hiring, toxic waste disposal in the south caused several problems, they opened a completely transparent platform showing people, using technology, where their tomatoes come from, they accessed the factory.
Wind has been trying for years to impose the value of transparency, decided to start a digital magazine, Wind Transparency.
Every year there is a prize for the most transparent companies, for start-ups working in this field, And there is no testimonial, there's no data plan; just the desire to talk with people in a different way.
Whether this is meta-advertising or invertising, I do not really care, how this is or will be named.
I am very interested in the attitude that we have towards creativity towards borders that are slightly shifted towards people.
In all fairness, there is no philanthropism here.
There is the desire to renew the field, maybe even to redeem it, reinvent it,
make profit, because this job is about selling something to someone.
But profit can also be made in a healthier way.
I'd avoid the word 'ethics', because I respect that word very much also. But I am convinced that if we work in a new way, we will definitely renew the language of advertising, and maybe also improve the collective imagination we mistreated for years.
And in this logic, we are all winners: the advertising creative wins, the company that invests wins, and above all, consumers win.
And this, in my opinion, is the most important thing.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Some 10.000 years ago, humans invented agriculture.
Plant and animal species were domesticated in specific areas of the world. The Fertile Crescent was one of the most important domestication centres of plants - we heard about wheat - and livestock species.
Domestic animal and plants have thereafter colonised the planet, along with the expansion of agriculture, human migrations and conquests.
And they have settled in agro-climatic areas much unlike the domestication centre.
Several millennia of natural selection have adapted domestic species to a range of diversified conditions: extreme climate, different pathogens, different soils, different feed sources.
This process has fostered animal and plant adaptation to produce under diversified conditions.
For example this Moroccan goat, a goat domesticated in Taurus mountains, in Anatolia, has adapted to live in the much hotter Morocco and feed, when there's no vegetation, on the Argan tree fruits.
By the way, it also helps spreading the seeds of this tree.
These “genetic solutions” discovered throughout millennia by natural selection, are a heritage of local breeds.
Unfortunately, local breeds are at risk of extinction, we are loosing them.
In 2015 FAO estimated one third of all the planet's genetic and animal resources, of all livestock breeds, is under threat of extinction.
And we know nothing for another third.
And these resources are a valuable source of adaptation for agriculture in general.
Natural selection has been integrated, from domestication on, with the man-made one,
At first in order to tame behaviour, and later for the sake of efficiency.
After the Industrial Revolution, human ability to select superior genotypes increased significantly, thanks to the adoption of sophisticated scientific approaches.
This has progressively led to the development of what we define intensive agriculture: intensive livestocks, and industrial cultivation of plants.
On one hand, we all know, these systems have a serious environmental impact. They use energy, deep ploughing, pesticides, fertilizers, drugs in the livestock, and a high livestock density that, when not properly managed, may negatively impact animals' health. On the other hand, they increased yield a lot: milk production quadrupled, and corn yield increased a staggering ten-fold, in just one century.
They responded to some social dynamics: after the Industrial Revolution, human population increased exponentially, moved from the countryside to cities and people started asking for more abundant, safer and cheaper food.
The result, the industry's response, was industrial agriculture.
A second step forward, on productivity is very recent, fostered by the DNA revolution.
In the last 10-12 years, 20 years, our ability in reading the DNA, that is the genetic code of plants, animals and humans, has speed up exponentially: consider that if the speed in 1980 could be compared to a snail moving at one meter per hour - so a rather slow speed even for a snail - today we move as fast as a supersonic jet flying at several thousand kilometres per hour.
Likewise, DNA analysis has gotten a lot cheaper: from one billion dollars in 2001, to sequence the first human genome, to less than 1.000 dollars today.
This allowed us to greatly increase the level of resolution of DNA analysis and consequently, the understanding of its biology.
As comparison, we may say that we have switched from low resolution, a few pixels, as you see in this portrait that seemingly hides Mona Lisa, to a much higher resolution.
This also made us realize when we were making mistakes, of course.
(Laughter). [Level of resolution in DNA analysis] Indeed, DNA analysis has allowed to increase the selection efficiency.
Increasing the selection efficiency - dairy bulls are a good example for that:
dairy bulls do not produce milk, so their genetic assessment, up until five years ago, was related to the production of their daughters.
But their daughters only make milk when a calf is born.
So, long story short, a bull was evaluated only when grand-sire [grand-father],
Then and only then, it could be chosen as an improved bull, worth to spread gametes across the population, or discarded.
Genomics, that is DNA analysis, allows now to evaluate a calf at birth.
We no longer need to wait for daughters' production: we immediately know, looking at his DNA, if the bull's genetic value will be high enough or it is to be discarded.
This has accelerated by three times the selection speed in the dairy cattle field.
The same technologies are fostering the same process in plants. As a result, the selection speed has increased.
Why speed is now more and more important?
It is important because some ongoing processes are progressing very rapidly.
First process: human population's exponential growth, there will be 10 billions of us in 2050.
FAO estimated a need of 70% more food in 2050, to feed the world.
This is a problem, as agriculture can count on 1,5 billion hectares on the planet.
And this number can’t significantly increased. We could exploit some areas now considered suboptimal, but certainly we can’t cut forests.
What should we do?
Firstly, we certainly have to reduce waste, from field to fork.
However, that is not enough. We must increase the production efficiency on these available hectares.
Second problem: global warming.
Carbon dioxide is steadily increasing, it has been exponentially growing since the Industrial Revolution, as a result of human activities.
Agriculture contributes to this trend, and while estimates of this contribution vary a lot - they're marginal according to farmers, or very relevant according to industries,
it's anyway somewhere between nine and 25 percent, with at least half the impact due to animal farming alone.
And in particular, to the methane emitted by ruminants.
Methane is a powerful greenhouse gas.
Anyway, whatever the contribution of agriculture to carbon dioxide production is, this is to be surely reduced. We need to reduce this impact
while increase productivity.
That's a challenge!
Why should we reduce CO2 emissions?
Because the planet's average temperature is increasing.
It is increasing: NASA scientists estimate it increased about one degree Celsius in the last century.
A very rapid increase, and this is why I previously stressed the need to act quickly.
For those who do not trust NASA data, here is clear, spot-on evidence of global warming.
We're all waiting for the next step.
(Laughter). So, will there be a food crisis?
Will we be quick enough to increase yield and decrease pollution?
Both issues are urgent.
Well, research is exploring new technologies.
One of these is called genome editing.
It is a new technology that, much like in a word processor, can cut a DNA letter and paste a different one.
Sometimes, replacing or erasing a single letter can profoundly alter a plant or animal trait. In this case, for example, erasing a single letter in rice makes it resistant to a disease known as rice-blast, which is usually treated with pesticides with a high environmental impact.
This mutation is naturally present in some varieties. What editing does, essentially, is copying those genetic solutions that were found by natural selection and conserved in local breeds, varieties.
It simply copy-paste them into more productive varieties.
The same goes for bovine polledness.
Some breeds are polled [hornless]. Polledness is a favourable trait for both humans, farmers who work in close contact with cattle, and the cattle themself, as they can hurt one another
while trying to establish a hierarchy in the herd.
Also in this case, the mutation has been copied from local breeds and pasted in highly productive ones.
Another piece of news is that last year mouse stem cells have been differentiated into in-vitro sperm cells.
For us males, it's a punch in the gut,
Why?
Because if we take a female embryo, and differentiate its sperm cells it will only generate females, and males will no longer be needed.
So I put myself in the shoes of poor male mice.
In any case, let’s say that if we connect these technologies - genomics, reproductive biotechnologies and editing, that is copying from local breeds - while genomics alone can speed up selection by three-folds, with all these systems together we can take the whole process in the lab, moving the selection from the field into the lab, and we could speed up selection from 3X to 30X.
This means we could make in five years as much progress as in the last 150 years.
This, I’d like to say, on the one side could also allow to select very difficult traits:
those related to environmental adaptation;
Those decreasing environmental impact.
Think about it: plants that resist diseases; that can grow on very salty soils; that tolerate water scarcity.
They might even have a higher efficiency, especially in photosynthesis.
Therefore they would capture more CO2 from the atmosphere mitigating the environmental impacts.
Likewise, animals could also get more resistant to diseases and therefore would reduce drug consumption; that have a lower impact, meaning cattle producing less methane, which is useless for them and harmful for the environment.
As a scientist, I must say that before integrating and applying all these technologies together we still have to break a number of technical barriers.
However, looking back, many such barriers have been quickly broken, over the last five - ten years.
We may therefore be fast enough to reach this difficult goal of increasing food production, while lowering its footprint on the environment.
However, as these technologies are hi-tech and very powerful, a debate is needed between science and society.
Our duty, as scientists, is to inform, look for solutions and provide information to open a debate as thoughtful as possible, without polarization.
In order to understand how and when to use and regulate these technologies.
In the meantime, we must preserve livestock and crop biodiversity, because those solutions we might be able to copy in the near future will be critical for us and even more so for future generations.
Thank you.
(Applause)
My maps aim to be a word-colored bridge.
Why words?
Because to me words are evocative.
When I start drawing, my sheet of paper is white.
I draw with a vector system called Illustrator, but I sketch by hand using a vector pen or a mouse.
I build all my work step by step, starting from geographical borders.
As I said before, to me, words are a source of inspiration. Because I often work with many authors who write about and describe the world’s political situation;
therefore, I read what they write in their articles and step by step, in my mind, I start creating images that I try to reproduce in my maps.
In this case, I brought this drawing because it's a geographical representation we are not used to seeing since America is in the middle.
As a rule, maps in atlases usually have Europe in the middle,
but now we need to change our perspective.
Then, the other peculiarity of this map is its color; actually, let’s say, after words, color is the second most important characteristic of my maps because colors have a significant evocative power.
They evoke feelings, much like a film soundtrack: When we watch a film with a romantic soundtrack, we predispose ourselves for that feeling or sensation.
And the same happens when we look at a colorful picture.
If I always painted a country using the same color, for example Russia or the United States, two very important players on the world stage, I would give it a specific connotation.
For example, if I always used purple, which is a color, let’s say, that somehow anticipates sorrow, rather an ill-fated one, all in all, I would connote immediately, it would always give that country a rather sinister look.
So basically, I try to alternate colors according to the idea I want to convey of that area in the world.
In this case, there was Trump’s world.
So Russia, the moment he won the elections, given his electoral campaign, Russia understood he wasn't going to be a true foe, but rather almost an ally, above all when he went to war in Syria.
Therefore, in this map, I drew Russia in light blue and the United States, in the middle, the main player, in the colours of the American flag and then, gradually, all the connections that this particular world of Trump had with the rest of the world.
In this map - this is the war in Syria.
Since the war began, I have been following the events day by day; I have created a lot of maps monitoring the war, from its beginning up to now.
And I chose specific colors for every country involved in the war: red represents the area, let’s say, controlled by Assad; what you can see in yellow in the North is the Kurdish area, while purple is the Islamic State.
Then there are other areas. For instance, the green one at the top, where there are, let’s say, Turkish soldiers, and then the other areas that are somehow more mixed, occupied by both rebels and international powers involved in this theatre of war.
Here, I used red.
But I don’t usually use red; I use it really sparingly because it’s an alarming color, one that spells emergency.
So in an image, it’s really strong, you have to use it carefully.
In this instance, in the years from 2005 onwards, since the outbreak of the war, I have always been using more or less the same colors, to be able to follow, also from a visual point of view, the factions involved.
Now, imagine that all my life long, I’ve been drawing for 25 years, I drew up to a certain point, more or less up to 2012; then I counted the maps, I had created almost 5,000 of them, and I decided to stop because that growing number had started to distress me.
And, just think, every day I deal with articles about different dictators: I don’t know, for instance, the fights for gas and oil.
In short, as I am used to say, I look the world right in the eye.
There aren’t many screens between the world and me, I look it right in the eye through the things I read.
And so, at a certain point, I felt the desire to draw some maps with a broader approach, maps I then called "geopoetic", since a set of forces led me in this direction.
I wanted to create a more imaginative map, free from any political schemes and working constraints.
Just then, a friend of mine who teaches at the "Sapienza" University in Rome came to my rescue asking me to try and draw a map based on a poem by Paul Celan, who possibly not everyone will know. He’s a very important early 20th-century poet who composed this beautiful poem, though in a hermetic style, you’ll understand it from its title, "And with the book from Tarussa - all poets are Jews (Marina Cvetaeva)".
But this is just the title.
Then for the rest, he continues with a series of metaphors and very geographical, poetical concepts.
But these are all metaphors.
So, my work consisted in unwrapping words, in looking beyond them to unveil their true meaning.
This poet is a Jewish poet who was born in 1920 in a European region called Bukovina.
Bukovina is a really small region: here is the geopoetic map that I have made based on his poetry.
This poet was born in a territory, it’s the green area above the Black Sea, that was always contended by major powers.
Actually, there is no real reason for this: it’s not wealthy; it’s a very small territory.
Currently, it is divided between Romania and Ukraine, but it first was under the Ottoman Empire, then it passed under the rule of the Austro-Hungarian Empire, later on, it became Romanian, then Ukrainian-Soviet and finally, it was shared between Romania and Ukraine, as it is now.
So, it is per se a restless land.
And this is where Paul Celan comes from,
which is in itself very meaningful.
But in his poetry, Paul describes this territory as if it were - you know in harbors, when somebody throws a rope to secure a boat and pulls it?
That’s it, that’s how he uses his birthplace: to move away and move back.
But, he does so always metaphorically speaking because he’s an exile: he has to leave his birthplace because he's Jewish: his family and him, they are persecuted.
And so, unfortunately, his parents die in a concentration camp, but he himself survives and is eventually freed.
Which is why, when he writes this poem, he is in Paris alone living as an exile.
But he imagines, he's drawing, he is writing on this table, all alone, and he imagines going on a trip to the area where he was born by crossing through his life as if, let's say, he is using rivers.
As you can see, here I drew several rivers that flow from the Black Sea turning the Black Sea into the throbbing heart of this area of the world, a throbbing heart from which rivers are born: these rivers are important, in Paul Celan’s life.
The first is the Oka river, which is located in the East, and it is the one that recalls his happy moments when he was studying in Russia. In Tarussa, to be precise, as in the poem's title. And it reminds him of that mindless period of his life.
After that, from the Rhine, he observes the "zone of the mute nations".
The zone of the mute nations is a metaphor for Germany,
as he cannot say the word "Germany", because it’s the homeland of his parents' murderers.
So that’s what he calls it.
After this, we pass to the River Seine that flows through Paris - the country that welcomed him, his second chance of life, but he calls it "Pont Mirabeau".
Pont Mirabeau is an important bridge: just to start, it already appeared in another poem by Apollinaire.
It is not in the city center, it is on the outskirts,
and unfortunately, it is the same bridge he would jump off at the age of 50, in 1970.
He quotes it in this poem, relating to the city as if it were Paris.
This to clarify, let's say, what this poem is like, so how toponyms, geographical places, turn into places of the heart. And suddenly, places we are accustomed to calling by a given name when seen on the maps start to produce sounds and different rhythms while still representing geographical locations.
So, this inner journey through these rivers is also an inner journey inside his own veins, a network of veins:
forced as he was to sever all ties with his homeland Bukovina, he couldn't even go back as it had become Soviet in the meantime, so there was the wall, the iron curtain, he couldn't even go back to visit his homeland.
And it isn't easy to live without being able to set foot where you spent your childhood; it is an extremely painful form of uprooting.
But in the meantime, he dreams of going back.
Another fundamental element of his poetry and also of his vision of life are constellations. Because someone who has lost his bearings on Earth just like him, with no friends, no relatives, and whose whole world is elsewhere, nowhere near him, studies the sky to find his bearings on Earth, the way ancient sailors did.
So, there are Orion's Belt and Canes Venatici, which are located more or less to the East - and represent Bukovina and Russia because, after all, that's his country of adoption, where he studied, while Canes Venatici is in the South to represent Israel, the land of his Jewish origins.
This journey takes place in Europe, as you can see: I drew it jagged, a bit strained, torn.
Why?
Because Europe has just emerged from World War II, and therefore it is no longer young: it is troubling, it has suffered greatly and is badly scarred.
It is also old at this point.
So, I wanted to represent it as the skin of an old person who has wrinkles, a weathered skin.
The poem ends with the word "Colchis", which you can see to the East of the Black Sea.
So, I placed Colchis right there. Let's say it's another almost hidden toponym because it represents Georgia, which is a country to the south of the Caucasus, exactly where I put it.
It also represents the reign of Colchis, which really existed but also belongs to Greek mythology.
It was in the kingdom of Colchis that the Golden Fleece was held.
The Golden Fleece was a hiding tool: fleece means hide. And it was the hide of a mythological animal, which was a gold-haired winged ram.
It was created by Zeus, and it had gone to the rescue of two demigods, two brothers that had to escape from a mean stepmother who wanted to kill them. And they were carried to the reign of Colchis, where the king saved them and gave them a harbor.
And to thank him for his mercy, they killed and sacrificed the animal, kept the fleece and donated it to the king.
Why is it important?
Because the Golden Fleece is magical and has the power to heal all wounds,
not only physical ones but also those of the soul.
And so the poet who wants to describe, who places the word "Colchis" as a close to the poem, is actually saying, I will never recover from my wounds because, sadly, history took the course it did and, to some extent, so did my life.
Like Primo Levi, he feels guilty, in a sense, he expresses the struggle to survive major events, like World War II.
And so he says, I will never recover. Healing would only be possible through the intervention of a magical element, impossible to find.
Going through the lines of these poems was like embarking on a journey for me, another journey beside geopolitics that is my job and my passion.
Poetry allowed me to discover a parallel world, a sort of Constellation of Poets, as I like to call it, because by collecting and reading poems, from ancient Greek to modern ones, I realized they all thread on feelings. Poetic lines are feelings and describe feelings, but they are also about history, about what we have lived through,
and so it’s a sort of reframing historical and political facts through our gut, our feelings.
So I thought, this could be, I like to think, the cluster of feelings we all have, we were born with, is indeed strongly rooted inside us all.
It's something we all receive as a gift, and feelings are what brings us together. Basically, human beings are bonded together by feelings, among other things.
I like to think that they came from the Big Bang, from that huge explosion.
Each of us carries a splinter of the big explosion, and this gives us the strength to change the course of our lives when, for instance, love erupts inside of us.
Thank you.
(Applause)
This is a true story.
I was invited some time ago to join a literary café in the Dolomites.
Each year a surgeon invites. a figure from a specific sector. That year they talked space, and I was invited.
That had been a thriving year, because our Mars Express satellite had found liquid, saline water on Mars, with the aid of an Italian radar.
Saline liquid water on Mars, from a scientific point of view is a landmark discovery, because it leads to think, life on Mars is likely to exist.
At the end of the event, I was still surrounded by tourists who wanted to know more about our new missions, about our new technologies, and I've been faced by a man who asked me to look at this picture.
I want to read it with you: I think there's no need for me to focus on the tragic nature of the picture.
Let me just read with you the sentence attached to it:
"Humanity can't afford to extract water in drylands.
But they can afford to look for water on Mars.
The question is: is there intelligent life on Earth?"
This is a situation space experts are often exposed to. So I decided to answer, with that calm, sobering approach that science taught me, and I want to do this with you right now.
I will begin with probably the most iconic mission of all space exploration missions: the man on the Moon.
That mission screamed to the world: "We can do it."
And even more, "We can conceive it."
We are the only species of this planet who actually did it, that actually left its planet, went on its satellite, and then came back.
The only species.
Moreover, this mission left us with something more:
135,000 patents.
135,000 patents means new products, new companies, new jobs.
It means economic progress as well as scientific, Shortly, it means wealth.
But one could still argue, we didn't answer
the question of the kid yet.
So let's see them: first, the CT scan.
The CT scan was used to seek flaws in the structures and materials of the Apollo ships.
Nowadays we find CT scans in hospitals all over the world to make diagnoses and help surgery.
The water filters we used to distillate water for astronauts, are now used worldwide wherever there's lack of water, to allow those children to drink polluted pond water, contaminated with viruses and bacteria, because now we even made them portable.
The microprocessor:
processors were already in place.
But who does my job knows that whatever is shipped to space must be extremely light, and extremely small. Thus, the processor had to be forcibly squeezed into a microprocessor.
Now tell me, is there a car on Earth today without a microprocessor on board?
I mean, everything - from the car to your phone, to medical devices, water detecting devices - everything.
Plus, an endless breed of technical products: clothing for extreme cold and extreme heat, even for the flames: fireproof suits for firemen without asbestos, portable oxygen masks, thermal blankets, now sadly used by migrants.
Freeze-dried food:
we took water out of food to ship it into space. Today it can be easily carried all over the world, thanks to this technology.
But this happened 50 years ago:
what are we doing today?
Nowadays, the Earth is our priority.
We are living a time of great changes from a global point of view.
But we have the opportunity to observe them, understand them and intervene to modify them, by watching them from space.
So we ran a series of missions, European Space Agency's Sentinels, which allow us to do that:
first, we can detect in the air all the noxious substances,
and also the amount of carbon dioxide. Carbon dioxide has a secondary effect, it raises the temperature of this planet: so we measure ice melting.
The North Pole has lost ten "Italys" of ice.
As a consequence, we measure rising water levels.
60% of the global population lives within 100 km from the coast, then this information is extremely important.
And we also do another thing.
Not only do we find water for that kid, we also find humidity, so we find potential areas where water can be found, even where it's extremely difficult.
Moreover, as we find humidity, we can provide a better agriculture: so not only we supply water to that kid, we also feed him.
The interesting part is, the same radar that allows us to find water today is an evolution of the same category - a synthetic-aperture radar - it's the evolution of the very radar that allowed us to find water on Mars.
With its interferometric function, this radar allows us to measure millimetric shifts of all human structures on Earth. For example, structural solidity of our children's schools, dams, bridges.
Who has a phone?
(Laughter) Who has the Internet on the phone?
Who has GPS on the phone?
All these services that you take for granted, are provided from space, by the European Space Agency.
But observing Earth from space allowed us to do something more: we understood how fragile Earth is, how exposed it is to threats like this:
Five mass extinctions - all five, according to the scientific consensus, were caused by the collision with an asteroid larger than one kilometer.
Today we could do nothing about it, not one space agency in the world could.
I don't want to ruin your Sunday, don't worry.
The ESA and the NASA are working on this mission: DART and HERA.
This is Didymos, a one kilometer wide asteroid,
Don't worry, it won't smash on Earth: but it's a fine object for tests.
We want to hit its moon with a missile, and by changing the speed of the moon, we will deflect Didymos' trajectory of about 6,000 km.
Earth is 12,000 km far,
Didymos will never collide with us.
Through our engeneering, our competences we build ourselves this chance of survival: dinosaurs could not, and so they got extinct.
But we care about the man in space, If during the Moon mission in '69 man stayed in space just a few days, by now we have flied 90 crews on this object: the International Space Station.
They were heterogeneous groups, males and females, and we left there for a longer period, at least six months.
This allowed us to discover many aspects: first, man is not meant to fly into space.
We have many problems: osteoporosis, muscle loss, cardiovascular system thinning, problems with radiations.
Not only did these missions allowed us to know all of this, they also allowed us to develop drugs against these symptoms, and now we find the same drugs back here on Earth.
But the station was something more, it was a political experiment.
Five countries teamed up to build it: Europe, USA, Russia, Canada and Japan.
And it showed that space is such a honorable mission, that even those countries that were quarreling on Earth back then, found an agreement, suspended hostilities to achieve this mission.
Therefore, the problem of food and water is technical in the first place: we need to find it, we need to extract it.
But then there is a distribution problem.
There's no such thing as space missions to help international diplomacy, and consequently a fairer distribution of resources.
In addition to this, that mission allowed us to discover many technologies: we repaired DNA for the first time in the history of the mankind, damaged by radiation exposure.
The orbital station is a close system, water on board is limited; therefore - and this might disgust you a little bit - we have closed-loop systems for urine purification.
Astronauts' urine and sweat are endlessly redrank.
All these technologies can be used on Earth where water is lacking.
We developed algae that are highly nutritional, and supply nourishment where it's most lacking on Earth: for example, in Central Africa and South America.
Watch what it's like to do a cardiac massage on the Station.
As you can see, it's a rather complex sequence: that's the reason why we tweaked the defibrillator and made it portable.
This allows you to find it everywhere, Not only in sports facilities, but also in public streets and squares.
And it saves lives.
But we want to do more, we want to achieve this:
we want to put on a drone the defibrillator.
You will have an app on your phone, you tap the app, and the drone gets right to you.
It gets there thanks to a GPS signal, that's also provided by us, so, it's very precise.
(Applause) That drone lands before you, talks to people passing by: "This person doesn't feel well: put me on his chest, I'll do the rest", and saves a life.
Meet Simon, now.
It's the first robot with AI.
It has a face recognition software so, not only it does recognize the face of commanders, Alexander Gerst in this case, but he's able to recognize his feelings, then it addresses him with tones and manners, "tuned" to his emotional status in that moment.
For long term missions, like going on Mars, this technology is fundamental, as it allows to ease the workload and understand the dynamics within the crew.
Brought back to Earth, it can be used in many ways. For example, in polar exploration missions, where people are isolated.
Ships that sail the sea for more than six months,
or elderly people.
It's never going to be human, that's for sure, but it's still far better than a speechless machine.
The station allowed us to change another paradigm.
This is Skylon, the future of aviation.
Skylon has a hybrid engine: a part of the engine is commercial, it's the same used on contemporary planes, and the other part is a rocket engine.
Skylon gain a certain altitude with the commercial engine, then rockets are turned on, and it flies at 28,000 km/h, it's like New York - Sydney in just three hours.
So less pollution is spitted, because the flight lasts less; and it's outside the atmosphere, hence we don't pollute Earth.
But we want to do more, we want to do this.
This is a gateway to deep space.
It is a new spacecraft, a new orbital station - around the Moon this time, not the Earth.
It will be ten times cheaper than the Space Station - we also learned to optimize our processes -
and it will allows us to do a few things:
first, it will support Moon surface missions; second, it will support Mars missions.
Mind you: astronauts on board in 2024, first woman on the moon in 2024.
And it will also allow us to fulfill another dream: to build a station on the moon. Because that's our aim:
we want to go back to the moon, and not just to pay a visit this time: we will get there to stay.
The moon is rich in platinum, titanium, and there's Helium-3 too, which allows the production of nuclear power without toxic wastes.
It's also a scientific opportunity, as we could observe space without the aberration of the atmosphere. So we could have radio telescopes not filtered by atmosphere, and it's an opportunity to field-test all the technologies that we will use later on in the much more complex flight to Mars.
But the moon is, let's say, this new challenge allows us to develop technologies.
First, building the Moon station. We're not going to build it as if we were on Earth. Tools, material: think about what it means to build a house on Earth.
We won't do that:
we won't launch all the stuff to the Moon.
It can't work like this.
We'll do it totally differently.
A 3D printer will be launched, that will be fed with the Moon powder, using the Sun as the only energy source,
and we will build the station through a fully automatic system.
Now bring this technology back to Earth: what does that entail?
It implies the possibility of building houses after earthquakes or tsunamis in difficult environments.
But astronauts won't only need a station,
they will also need tools and equipment:
some of these tools will be launched, others will be assembled on-site. So we're going to need plastic and metals.
What's obviously the source of plastic and metal on the Moon?
The stuff we already brought there: the Landers.
After the landing, Landers get useless.
Hence, we will cannibalize all plastic and metal and recycle them with 3D printers, printing useful tools for this mission stage.
Recycling will be fundamental, if we want to preserve Earth.
This is Shackleton crater, in the south of the Moon.
The red marks you see are water.
Water means life, it means water to drink it means oxygen and hydrogen.
Oxygen to breath, oxygen and hydrogen as fuels to support the station.
But not only this water is freezed, it's also mixed with mould.
That means we need technologies to extract water,
and those same technologies will be very useful for that kid.
We're already testing all this.
We are testing it here, in the Zermatt glacier.
Eleven European universities, including the Politecnico di Milano, are all testing these technologies in a Moon-ish environment.
And then we have robotics.
Our commander Luca Parmitano will control this robot from the station, as if he was on the moon.
Telerobotics becomes telemedecin on Earth.
It means that a surgeon in New York can perform surgery on a patient in Milan.
Morover, this robotic arm lays the foundation of microsurgery on Earth.
But we want to do more, we want to go to Mars.
There'll be astronauts on Mars within the end of 2030, and to do that we will need this:
Mars is two years away from us.
For a number of astrodynamic reasons we cannot interrupt the mission, so we have to go all the way around.
Which means, we have to fix astronauts:
skin, human bones, stem cells powered 3D printers,
human organs. It means transplanting without waiting for donors, testing drugs directly on the organ you want to treat, painless for both humans and animals.
But why going to Mars?
The reason is this: Earth doesn't grow, but we keep growing in numbers, hence we have a problem of space and resources, we have a problem of pollution. So, we go to Mars to acquire new spaces and resources, but don't worry, we are not going to Mars because we think Earth is doomed, and so we leave.
Actually,
this is the reason why we go to Mars:
Mars was like Earth.
We don't know why, but it turned into the ice desert it still is today, with greenhouse effect that is totally out of control.
We go to Mars to understand what happened there, and to avoid that it'll happen to us.
And we go to Mars in peace and reverence.
We have an office called Planetary Protection, it will prevent us from making the same mistake we did on Earth.
We are becoming a multi-planetary species. It means we have the opportunity, for the first time, to bring our lives, our ideals, our culture, to another planet.
But in order to do that, we need all of you, not just engineers like me.
We will need biologists, doctors, psychologists, philosophers.
It's all happening now.
Space is inspirational, thus we attract the best minds, confront them with challenges like these and produce science and technology for the protection of our Earth, and ultimately our lives.
I was just missing -
this costs you the price of one movie ticket per year.
Now, I have my answer to this question, and it's the same answer that inspires my whole life, and gives meaning to my experience: because I think we can do something practical for that kid.
Naturally, I give you the freedom to answer it yourselves.
Thank you.
(Applause)
[L.A. Confidential -
Behind Hollywood's scenes] Good evening to all! I’m smiling because in my career, I’m usually the one who puts other people on stage. I place other people in front of the camera. And now that I'm standing here, I have to admit, it isn’t an easy task to do.
Let’s pretend that we are in a movie and this is a story about a boy.
This is me, when I was four years old - and yes, I had hair.
(Laughter) As a child, I had three very different dreams: becoming an NBA player; becoming a professional soccer player; becoming a Hollywood actor.
Three dreams with, at the very least, a common denominator: a great ambition.
But also three dreams, if you think about it, that were pretty common among children my age in the '80s.
Children fans of Hellas Verona during the Bagnoli era; grown up watching the first NBA footage, watching movies like E.T., or "The Never Ending Story" that captivated a generation.
As a child, I had a great imagination, a great creativity, and at night I dreamed a lot.
And I would patiently recall my mom, every morning, what I had dreamed.
I often dreamed of fantastic castles, endless landscapes, and wild animals.
Then one evening, during a dream:

“Zeno? Wake up. Wake up!
I am Danilo Gallinari.
You don't know me now, but I assure you that in the future you will have the chance to meet me.
I have to tell you, you will never be an NBA player. You will never be as gifted as me: but you will certainly have a bright future. One day you will understand, so many things are waiting for you.
In that dream, Gallinari killed all of my NBA ambitions.
Not a problem, I would go on to become a great soccer player and win the FIFA World Cup with the Italian national team.
Some time passed, and I had another dream. “Hi Zeno! I'm Alessandro Del Piero.
You don't know me now, but I'm here to tell you something very important - even a bit hard to swallow. You'll never become a soccer player. You'll never become a World Champion.
Believe me, it's impossible. I know it's hard to accept, but you better learn it now, so you can concentrate on other things.
But I also have cheerful news for you: we will become great friends, and just maybe you will become a world champion in another industry.
So, see you soon.
Ciao!"
Now, both of my idols had killed all of my sport dreams.
Two out of my three aspirations failed and I thought the third was impossible to reach.
I then veered onto something more secure, something I could already attain. I decided to follow in the footsteps of my family and work in the building company started by my Dad, a person I had always admired.
It was a secure path.
For this reason, I graduated in Architecture in :
but more than the design part, the business part always attracted me more.
So once I completed my training in Italy, I went to Los Angeles, America, to learn how Americans taught marketing and their sales techniques. Little did I know, this all would shortly come in handy.
In the meantime, I fell in love with Los Angeles and Hollywood wasn’t too far away afterall.
While working for my family, my life seemed to be on the right track -
but you have to remember, we are in a movie.
And like in every movie, there’s a “plot twist”, as Americans say. Italy's economic crisis worsened, and wiped all ambitions out. The safety that once was there suddenly disappeared.
During that time, I thought of a sentence my father always told me: “Zeno, remember my advice, look for a niche in the market, and try to be the best at it”
In a moment of extreme hardship, I said to myself, “What do I do?
Do I give up or do I fight?”
I had to start from zero. Actually, making a play on words, I had to start from Zeno.
“Do what you are”, I told myself.
I had passion and ambition. And above all, a great idea, a big goal: aiming for Hollywood.
And with a sort of an "all-in mentality", I nosedived into this world, where I had already made my first steps.
Today, after 15 years, I am the owner and founder of a Beverly Hills-based company called Cangrande Inc., whose name is a tribute to my city, Verona.
My job consists in managing and negotiating all the aspects of the contract to secure the biggest names in sports, fashion, cinema, and entertainment industry to be placed in tv shows, brand &amp; ad campaigns, film productions.
I went to America to learn how to manage and sell buildings. Today I am a full-fledged seller: I get talent, I sell dreams, I sell people.
Said this way, it sounds very bad, I can see your shocked faces - (Laughter) But we are in a movie, don’t forget. It is up to you to distinguish between fiction and reality.
Now, let's leave the set: I'll take you behind the scenes of my job to explain you how to negotiate in Hollywood.
Which is a world apart, with its own set of rules.
[NEGOTIATIONS] In a negotiation, the most important part is within the first three minutes - where anything but the sale is talked about.
This is something Americans taught me. They showed me how to flip concepts and see things from another perspective.
Another fundamental thing is the handshake, which is your bodily business card.
So you should stare into the person’s eyes, smile, and repeat the name of the person before you. It is essential.
Now, talking about movies, how many of you, by a show of hands, remember the movie, Jerry Maguire?
Enough of you, OK.
There is a beautiful scene in the movie where Cuba Gooding Jr., who plays the part of a football player shouts to Tom Cruise on the phone, "Show me the money, show me the money!!"
You might remember it.
This is something that we can keep as a staple for all things in Hollywood: while money in Hollywood actually moves everything, they also have more than enough of it to decline any offer presented to them, if there's a side detail they don't see fit.
So I typically have to leverage something else. I always have to walk in their shoes, with a deep understanding of their personal interests as details sometimes make the biggest difference.
What is considered a side dish is sometimes more important than the "main serving".
In Hollywood - I have a rule that I always keep in mind.
I call it “five for one”
and it can be split into two phases: research and closure.
I give myself five attempts to find the right talent, then a month to close the deal and sign the contract.
If I break these boundaries, I know that the situation is not safe, because one of the two parts does not have the urgency to close.
I have to be good at pushing the right buttons.
It's like a poker game where you have to be surgical, you have zero margins of error.
If you make any mistake, you're out.
You have to be good at anticipating the moves of those before you.
It's like a sword duel: you have to know when to attack and when to pull back.
An essential feat of negotiation is to keep the knife by your side. It is a tough thing to do here, as you are the one chasing them and you hope that they will listen to you.
Everything must always be done with the utmost specialization and precision.
It is a job where the concentration must be extreme and professionalism has to be high.
If you don't know the rules of this game, you don't get to the finish line.
It's a career you can do on your own,
being available 24 hours a day. I have clients all over the world and I have to honor their time zone. Your strength lies in your relationships: with your clients, on one hand; and with artists and agents on the other hand, who expect you to move things forward and they want to hear your voice. As a result,
the only thing that matters here, more than any marketing tactic, is a referral; your reputation should precede you.
Now, I’ve given you five key points that in theory could be ported to every negotiation.
But they can’t. because in the end, only experience matters.
When you are working with the subjectivity of others - and here the others are fickle - there is no set of rules, no precanned formula.
Artists and actors have incredible talent and a unique and sensitivity, that is expressed in quixotic forms.
The requests they ask you, at times, are particularly peculiar.
Americans use to say, "The devil is in the details". [Translates into Italian]
Now, I will give you some examples of details that made all the diference in closing the contracts.
This dog was essential to closing a $2 million contract.
The dog!
This is a famous dog because it’s owner is very famous, and wherever the owner goes, the dog follows along.
The treatment for the dog must be that of a five-star extra luxury service, because they demand it from you.
They’ll ask you for 20 toys for the dog. They want you to put the dog on a comfortable bed: well, three beds,
because one has to be put in the hotel, one in the dressing room and one on the set.
And they will ask you a three star Michelin-grade food.
This cocktail is called "Penicillin." How many of you, party animals, know this drink?
Let’s see, no one?
No, one actually does.
Folks, there's a hero with us.
This is the favorite cocktail of a famous actor.
As soon as I signed this contract, one-minute later, I received an e-mail
titled: “Penicillin Cocktail”
with the recipe.
I had to double check,
this e-mail was meant for me. So I call them and said: "Look, I got this email, Penicillin Cocktail.
I think it's for your bartender that is organizing the evening."
Their answer, "No, it's the actor's favorite cocktail.
You have to tell your client to prepare a 24-hour barista who's always ready to make him”.
In three days, he took it once.
(Laughter) The bartender was there all the time.
Six suitcase full of shoes, a request made to me by a female artist.
I see men shaking their heads and saying, "Yes, my wife also makes the same request and she is not an artist."
(Laughter) There were 100 pairs of shoes for only two days of an advertising campaign.
I reckoned, had the artist been awake 24 hours, she could have changed one pair of shoes every 33 minutes.
Try to imagine my client’s face when I came and told him he had to pay tens of thousands of Euros to carry these suitcases.
But no suitcases, no artist.
And these are just small examples of a particular world where the strength is in creativity.
In Los Angeles, there is a unique light. The temperature year round is 20 to 25 Celsius degrees and you can only tell summer from winter by the sunset time.
In LA, and Hollywood, you get the feeling you can achieve anything you want. Hollywood is a microcosm where the best of the best comes in, where the intellectual level of people is very high,
where they judge you for what's in your head, not how you look.
And where there is an endless stream of ideas, and you are sure that the idea coming out right now is better than the previous one.
But the strength of this system, based on an extreme competitiveness, is also its weakness.
It is a vortex that takes you and overwhelms you.
In Hollywood, every day is a battle
and nobody is safe, not even the actors because all the jobs are unstable.
One thing you immediately get is, from an outside perspective, it looks huge. It is actually a microcosm and the elite is at the tip of a pyramid, where everyone knows each other but one mistake and you're out.
In the end, what matters is what you contribute.
Today, I live in Beverly Hills and work with Hollywood everyday.
If I had to write a letter, talk to the child me, I'd tell him not to worry, because dreams will eventually become true - though in a different way.
I would tell him to believe in himself because passion is what matters.
To love the things you do, even at the cost of sacrifices.
Luckily, I’m not acting on stage - otherwise the anxiety would overcome me.
I'm not in movies, I didn't become an actor, and I'm not in any ad campaign either.
But knowing that an artist is there because I helped secure that deal and there was a child who dreamed big, makes me so proud.
As Americans say, "Shoot for the moon: even if you miss, you’ll land among the stars." [Translates into Italian]
Today - (Applause) Today, I still dream at night, as much as I did as a child. But instead of telling my dreams to my mother, I tell them to my wife.
There's been an evolution.
