I was suggested to begin with a brief presentation of myself, and of the issues I'm going to treat, so I'll start with this image showing all my bold athletic thew since my childhood.
Don't worry, I'll not show all the photos in the album of my childhood.
But I'll start from here because I realized that these were important years among the most formative of my life during which I learned a few basic things.
I learned the love of nature, I learned the value of friendship, I learned the pleasure of playing, and the displeasure of grazing my knees as I think it happened to many of us when children.
Not less important were the following years during which I choose to commit myself as a volunteer with the association "Mani Tese".
I was 20 times in Africa to follow projects of solidarity.
We realized many projects such as wells, schools, hospitals.
We wiped out from whole areas, especially in Burkina Faso, but not only, hunger, thirst, illiteracy.
We made plans for promoting the rights of children and women.
It was a joy, a wonderful feeling.
I learned so much, I changed my way of seeing the world, I understood that things can change.
In Africa I understood the core values of solidarity, justice, peace, cross-culture, and I developed this empathy toward our brothers, both near and far that is, the values I try to promote with my activity both educational and cultural, especially with the kids, but not only.
It is a commitment to education I try to carry on, a bit like a game.
I believe that George Bernard Shaw was right when he said that we don't stop playing because we grow older, but we age because we stop playing.
If this is true we have the elixir of eternal youth.
And I think it's true, because it happened to me in my lifetime to meet young people in junior high school, already old and sad whereas, on the other hand, old men still young.
I remember that one time I was about to leave for Africa and, a few days before, I was at the International Vaccinations Office when I came across a 96 year old woman along the hallway going out just while I was entering, it was a beautiful scene that I want to share with you the nurse rushed out the door, and ran after her to say something she forgot to tell.
According to the procedure,
the nurse, visibly embarrassed, told her: "Dear madam, I forgot to tell you that in 10 years there would be a recall." (Laughter)
And the lady, she turned and said: "And if I'll forget?" (Laughter) And the nurse replied:
"Do not worry, we will remember you."
I was there witnessing, I didn't know her, I said to myself: "I want to grow old well, exactly like her!"
So if it is playing that can keep us young, I will ask you now to play with me.
Just as children do.
I try to live this way.
But which are the issues?
I forgot, the issues I want to debate with you today.
They are basically three:
