How was that?
Did all of you succeed in this?
Three simple considerations after this little exercise.
First remark: change is possible!
(Laughter) Second remark: it needs a little effort because it goes against our habits which make us get rusty and stiffen.
Third remark: it can also be fun!
You can sit back down.
(Applause) The saying goes: "He who seeks, he finds," but which of us, at least once in his life, did not loose anything in his house. Now tell me the truth.
This is an unpleasant sensation.
We look everywhere for it, even if we have it under our nose, but we can't find it.
The more we insist, the worse it is.
If on the contrary we leave for a moment, we do something else, and come back; it was there on the table.
The same thing happens when we can't succeed recollecting a word, do you remember the feeling of having it on the tip of your tongue?
Or for a musician when it happens to jam, it doesn't matter if it's a piano or guitar or any other instrument, we jam on a wrong sequence of notes, and we insist trying and trying to recall what we lost and we go worse.
There are scientific studies that absolutely demonstrate that repeated attempts to recall something are counterproductive.
It is as if, paradoxically, the mistakes we make in our research when they are repeated so many times, even if corrected, paradoxically, lead us to learn the mistake.
So I reflect on the famous phrase I heard 100 times when I was a child, "You learn from your mistakes."
I rather think a bit like Charlie Brown about this.
[It seems to me there is someone else who is learning from my mistakes instead of me.] (Applause) If it were true that wrong is learned, we should all be perfect, both as individuals and as a society.
Instead we continue, think a little while, paradoxically repeating always the same mistakes.
For example, we continue doing the same quarrel as the day before, and as a society too, we repeat the same mistakes.
So I believe that the saying is not right.
That is, basically we learn in another way: we learn from our successes.
I wish to give you just one example which is the starting intuition for a path of my research on this topic, that came to light from the observation, of 20 years of educational experience with the boys as well as from my experience in social commitment.
Well, I noticed that learning basically originates from our successes.
Let me give an example, because we all experienced it when children.
The time we learned to ride a bike without training wheels.
Do you remember it?
I remember the day when my father said: "Michael, now you are becoming a big boy you can remove the wheels, let's try."
"Dad, it doesn't stand up."
I tried, I tried.
By the way, it's a dramatic moment because one looks around and sees that everybody rides.
For self-esteem is a terrible blow.
Am I the only idiot in the universe who can not learn to ride a bike?
But when exactly we can say we really learned to ride a bike?
Of course we fell down 50 times, and we had to get up.
But the time we really can say we learned to ride was when for the first time, maybe by chance and a bit wobbly, we were able to stay balanced.
Because that was the movement we stored and then learned to replicate.
Sure, it was necessary to fall and rise.
There is no other way leading to success than this.
A Japanese Zen proverb says: "Success in life is to fall 9 times and rise 10 times".
So we should not be afraid if doing mistakes, they told us this morning that this must not curb or paralyze, in the knowledge that the time for successes will arrive and they will lead us to a reinforced learning, but on this I will no more bore you.
There are dozens of researches both ethological, and animal, and physiological and neurophysiological showing that the memory of learning due to a success is 7 times more durable in our brain than the memory of a failure.
The idea that we must necessarily burn one of our hand to learn is unfounded.
Here's how this is fundamental. The other aspect I wish to underline is that we just do not learn only from our successes, but also from the successes of others.
And this is a decisive evolutionary transition.
If we understand that we can learn from the successes of others, This greatly accelerates our learning process.
If we reflect a moment on this point, we realize that it always went this way.
What I propose is nothing new it always happened this way throughout history.
We actually are the result of a cultural heritage of peoples which for hundreds of years, for centuries, millennia, exchanged everything they could trade:
ideas, discoveries, inventions, foods, art, music, dance, entertainment, science wives, all what you could trade.
(Laughter) Thank you. From all points of view, joking with the guys I make a bet and say: I bet you speak better Arabic than English.
Obviously, we speak Arabic very well.
in Italian language, we use hundreds of words borrowed from other languages, Arabic, Gothic, Lombardic, French, Aramaic.
The same things goes for games: as children we played with the Egyptian spinners, the Philippine yoyo, the Chinese kite, the Egyptian checkers, the Persian chess.
All our food, clothing, everything.
We are the fruit of this intercultural exchange that has enriched us for centuries and millennia bringing us where we are now.
It means a lot to me to say this thing:
since antiquity, from the wheel of the Sumerians, the helm of the Phoenicians, the compass of the Chinese, till the beer that the Sumerians produced 4,000 years before the Germans, or hot chocolate, that Aztecs produced 1,200 years before the Swiss.
All this is us.
I summarized the concept by this expression: 1 + 1 = 3 And it's magic, you know well that it's not right with money, or with objects, even if it would be nice.
Culture is the only thing that really grows globally when we trade it.
