I must make a small distinction, because it's too important, from the lexical point of view.
I used so far in a fairly interchangeable way the terms "mistake" and "error".
I didn't do a right thing.
Because in fact they have two very different etymologies.
Wrong comes from the Latin varius, shimmering, dazzling that distracts us, we make mistakes when we are distracted, when we are absent-minded.
Instead err, as said us this morning the friend in the front row, err literally means going without a goal, wandering, and this on the contrary, can be very fruitful and take us to discover many things we not even expected, things we were not looking for.
This is what is normally called serendipity, also recalled in the beautiful intervention opening this TED.
What does it mean?
Being able to grasp what randomly lights upon, along the way being present indeed, and from here some of the most amazing things were born throughout history.
This morning it was reminded us the famous apple fallen on Newton's head which inspired the law of universal gravity.
But think of Archimedes, when he went out of the bath tub shouting Eureka!
From penicillin to the Pap test, insulin anesthesia, passing through the universal gravitation or the electric current, many discoveries come to my mind. Materials too such as nylon, the non-stick pan, Velcro, post-it, all these things are the result of serendipity, that is, discoveries made by people who were not looking for these or they were not trying at all, but who were present at the time.
The mistake would have been being distracted, then not being able to grasp what along the way offered us a chance to intuition as has been said before.
Another clarification I would do: many of these discoveries were made by people who were concerned with quite anything in life.
We used to think that there are professionals and that the specialists are the ones who seek and find.
But we discover that, for example, Kodachrome film was invented by a musician, the parking meter by a journalist, the automatic phone by a funeral director, the ballpoint pen by a sculptor and I could go on. So you understand there isn't someone to whom we can delegate.
I liked it before, when De Angelis said: "I already made two companies, now make one you too."
We are all indispensable, I think this is important.
Then we have to start focusing on the relationships, the miracle of the reports, poetry, the wonder that is in human relations.
So, in my opinion, we must free ourselves from the chains of consumerism which steals us, first of all, time for affections, for hobbies, for dreams, and instead we must reclaim the pleasure of wonder that is in relationships.
