(Applause) A black hole is a compact mass, so crushed on itself that nothing can escape its tremendous force of attraction. Not even light.
A ray of light, on its surface, stays there.
Without moving, without being able to get out.
Frozen.
Nothing comes out of a black hole, and everything can enter it.
Don Giovanni is like a black hole: he escapes our understanding.
If we settle with the erotic side, we trivialize it;
if we assign him a philosophical status, we overstate it.
And yet, he remains there.
And much like the surface of a black hole, matter gravitates around it.
We'll never get who Don Giovanni is without the characters spinning around him, as for the black holes.
John Weeler says that "A black hole is like a man dressed in black who dances the waltz in a softly lit room, with a lady dressed in white.
We only know it's there because we see a clear star circling around it".
And so it is for our hero: he would not exist without Donna Anna, Donna Elvira, Don Ottavio, Masetto, Zerlina - and last but not least, without the Commendatore.
Don Giovanni is the symbol of what is most forbidden and unspeakable.
He gathers around him, like a black hole, the matter of evil, of arrogance, of excess.
And men have always tended to be attracted by evil.
The Commendatore, therefore, does not need to return to the scene to plunge the libertine into hell.
Don Giovanni's punishment is too human a matter to bother the sky.
It is enough for men, with their fury, to spectacularly punish their concealed attraction to evil.
They get to think, it's right to punish a guilty person, so he can set an example.
Mozart and Da Ponte speak us today of two opposing tendencies of man: the fatal attraction to evil, and the urge to exorcise it, repel it with shame and indignation.
Because the sky is full of black holes.
