So I'm here to tell you a little bit about quantum connections.
Why "Connections to the cube"?
Well, what are we doing now is to use quantum technology, even if it may not seem so,
because only by knowing the laws of quantum mechanics we can have a laser that allows me to point above, we can have a computer, transistors, processors and so on.
But the story I want to tell is that of the second quantum revolution.
Because today what we have in our bits, in our digital society, the bits are zeroes and ones that turn on, turn off the circuits and so many electrons come and go. There are many photons that are in an optical fiber, which move.
What is the second quantum revolution?
To tell you, I would like to start with something that happened to me 20 years ago, during my first week at the end of my doctorate, my first job at the University of Innsbruck, I take the bike, I leave the cellar, a black limousine comes.
The Dalai Lama comes out of it.
And he had come to the cellar. There was not only the bike, but there was also the lab of my colleague Rainer Blatt
and there His Holiness could do something that to him, at school, to all of us at school, and also to my son, recently, it has been taught that it is not possible. We were told: it is not possible to see a single atom.
Then they say to you: "Yes, the electronic microscope you can perhaps get an indirect sight, etc.", even the black hole of the other day is not really a picture but a reconstructed thing, but you can't take the picture, you don't see it with the eyes.
Then there is a small trap in my colleague's laboratory:
there are metal electrodes. Shortly, a small empty box with a glass above
and between the box and my eye there is a microscope, there are only lenses.
I wait a few minutes, and so did his holiness.
After three or four minutes the eye becomes accustomed to the dark,
I rest my eye on the eyepiece and inside I see it:
a small, small greenish atomin, all alone in the middle of the dark. And yet I see it directly.
What the founding fathers of quantum mechanics deemed impossible - they said, "it's easier is to raise a dinosaur in a zoo than doing an experiment with a single atom", as if to say "it will never happen", it happens today, every day and happens in many laboratories, around the world, around Europe and even around Italy.
Italy is also far ahead on this field of research.
And for me that's a starting point because, somehow, building this little atom is something that is fundamentally useless, it's just for wonder, only to be fascinated, if you want. But this is the basis, it is the foundation on which we develop a whole series of things that go very far, and this is what I'd like to tell you about a little bit.
Step back: I talked about today's digital technology.
This is the first transistor, which was built in '47. It was that big, then it was more and more miniaturized. Today we have millions and billions of transistors on each of the chips we carry in our pockets, in our phones and so on.
Moore's law, formulated more than 50 years ago, says: "Every couple of years we can stash twice as little elements for digital circuits on the same space,
and at the same cost,
so we have computers more and more powerful".
And we see this - here, for example, the number of transistors there, depending on the time, has grown enormously from a thousand to 20 billion and this is what is carried by everyone in the pocket.
And yet, now, this thing is coming to an end.
This is actually the basis for all the technologies we use for all our social media.
Your social media, Facebook and Twitter, without quantum mechanics would not be possible.
Now we can discuss whether it would be a positive or negative thing , but anyway this clearly tells us that it has a very important impact on what we do.
But now, going very far is not possible because if I make it smaller and smaller, at the end I arrive to the atom, and atoms cannot be cut in half. Smaller than an atom it is impossible,
it is a quantum, there's nothing smaller.
So what does it mean?
That when we do smaller and smaller things, at some point, we reach a limit. This enormous growth, at some point, will break and stop.
And we can either despair or, as if we were a big computer industry, or even some big computer companies like Microsoft, IBM, Intel, Google, which are investing hundreds of millions each year, and in a few weeks there will be a very important announcement from Google in this sense, they say: "Oh well, it has broken, there is no longer this huge growth. Let's try to invent something new."
This is a stimulus for future innovation,
and this is the idea of quantum technologies.
So here we go from the connections to the first power, the normal, daily ones - Facebook, the Internet - to the connections to the two,
and "spooky connections" is something that Einstein had said.
Einstein did not believe that quantum mechanics was right. He was convinced that it was wrong. He said: "Now I am going to show you that it can only be wrong."
Why? Because it provides these absurd connections, spooky connections, in his own words. And here I have a sketch:
of course I don't do the formulas, but the difference is not much.
Here I have a little sketch of a small quantum system, like the one of the atom before.
Somehow inside, as in this drawing of a cube, there is both the possibility of the cube being face up, and the possibility of the cube being face down.
They both are there.
Try looking at it: somehow, you are not able to see it at the same time from one side or the other.
Somehow it does "click!", back and forth or it settles on one side and you have to think, it could also be from the other.
When you look at it, it's either one or the other.
If I have more than one of these, and this is a figure that I borrowed from a fellow Nobel Prize winner in 1997, Bill Phillips, what happens is: I have two, in these are also two possibilities and when I look at them there too, they collapse, either face down or face up.
The trick here is that I don't know anyone, try doing this exercise, to see them one down and one up at the same time. Either you see them both down, or both up.
Einstein said: "This is absurd, because in quantum mechanics I can prepare states so that, if I then I separate these two cubes, these two electrons, they are always connected".
And even if I have an electron here and the other is on Alpha Centauri, I look at one and I immediately know what will happen up there, when my colleague goes to check it,
instantly.
And this is something you need to do teleportation.
This may sound like a joke. I can tell you that, because you have heard
that there is teleportation on Star Trek, no one will be taken or teleported to Star Trek, because to be teleported you need to be cooled a lot, like towards absolute zero, so it's not easy to remember after it happened, but anyway (Laughing) I can tell you that the Star Trek one is much more boring than the real quantum one, because in Star Trek what happens is: "Beam me up, Scotty!", which means "transmit me", I know what is and where it is transmitted. But in quantum teleportation it has no importance where this other electron or photon went. It could be on Alpha Centauri, it could be in Milan Lambrate, it could be where you want it.
and in any case this thing works, and a secret message is transported, an unknown state - because I don't know what is before I measure it, and it is transported to a place we don't know.
And this, again, seems a useless thing, maybe a fanciful one, but not so realistic and maybe not even so practical. And instead, what we can do - and here we get to the connections to the cube, the third level - it's the one where we can use these things as technology.
They start by asking themselves, as Einstein did, in a completely speculative way, if reality does exist or doesn't. For example, there are experiments done by colleagues who have realized this Einstein thing wondering if reality exists locally or not.
The answer is, reality doesn't exist locally.
Now we have no time, it would take another talk to explain this, but what matters here is that we can use this.
Why?
Because if I use my status to send a message, and if this message, which can be zero or one, can be a bit of a long string of a message I want to send, if this message, this data is not there before I measure it, then it cannot be intercepted by anyone simply because there isn't.
The state, the letter, zero or one, only appears after I have taken that aforementioned measure.
And this sounds like science fiction, and instead there are companies selling you devices that do these things.
There is a company in Geneva that is the world leader in the field, it is called AD Quantic,
and I am not advertising that company, I am just saying, here in Europe we managed, starting from these fundamental, basic ideas, in developing technologies which now around the world truly dominate the market.
And in Vienna, the colleague Zeilinger had managed to do the first teleportation,
and the state we are now is that it is possible to buy these devices that banks and governments are buying to make secure communications: I send you a photon: no one can intercept it because if it does, it is ruined, destroyed. If someone tried, we realized that he tried to measure it. And therefore, if we verify that no one has actually intercepted, we have a secure communication, which protects your data, which protects your credit card on the Internet, which protects sensitive data, medical data and so on.
And this is something that now happens over a distance of several hundred kilometers. And the colleagues who, for the first time did this, this is a fantasy card from a decade ago they did this for the first time, realized that maybe they could send it on satellite and they tried to propose it to the European Space Agency, who in the following ten years slept on it
and, in the meantime, the first author who did this, who proposed this idea - his name is Jianwei Pan; he is not exactly Tyrolean, as you can imagine- he returned to Shanghai, and from President Xi received two billion dollars which allowed him to accomplish this thing here, which is a Chinese satellite that is now in orbit and with which safe quantum video calls have already been made at thousands of kilometers between China and Austria.
And this is a consequence of taking these individual, strange, unexpected particles having these behaviors that are not normal, and using them to do something technological with them.
That would be an example.
And now another example involving Milan, here near to us,
we do not see it yet, but if I use a photon, I send it to you as on the optical fiber, we can communicate information. If instead I use atoms, I can use them to process information.
Instead of a transistor I take an atom so, as I said before, I make it smaller and smaller. At the end I get to the single atom.
The atom as well behaves in this strange, quantum way.
If I have many of these atoms, they are, at the same time, in all possible states and all possible combinations.
So, if I have a quantum computer that can manipulate them, keeping them well cold, as we said before, what I can do is: I have a problem. I ask a question to my computer, but instead of asking one question at a time and getting the answer, I can simoultaneously write all the possible questions in this quantum computer and get all the answers at the same time.
It's like searching in a labyrinth:
instead of going, I try here, then there, after there, I try to explore simultaneously all the possible paths and you understand that, in a single round, I get to the solution.
And this is a huge computing power, which can be used for optimization.
Volkswagen used a quantum simulator to optimize taxi traffic in Beijing. And this, as I often repeat, is something that a physicist, such as me, would not even come to mind not even under the influence of a very impressive amount of vodka. Because, it's like - the physicist does not think of these things, we care about our toys, our atoms and so on. But then people come along who say, wait, we can do something useful!
Two weeks ago, Adidas called me to find out what could be done with a quantum simulator: I get to that in a moment.
So here is an Intel chip with 7, 17 and 49 quantum bits that has been presented at the Las Vegas electronics show last year.
This year IBM introduced the first commercial quantum computer. You can buy it.
It was made in Milan so it was pretty elegant. No, really: the external case was made in Milan so that it had a sufficient visual impact and so I think that even here in Busto we can be proud of this. But what is relevant is that these things are really coming into reality.
Ten years ago, when I started teaching these things, I said students yes, maybe, maybe one day, but really I don't know. But today they are here.
This is for me the most incredible thing of all right now.
I have two other slides to show you on two other possibilities.
One is quantum simulation: I use these single atoms, I put them in a regular structure similar to an egg carton, one by one, and I can reproduce the behavior of new materials, maybe new chemical compounds. I can use them to develop new chemical systems, new substances, maybe high-temperature super-conductive substances with which I could make power lines that don't dissipate electricity and, therefore, I would immediately have 50% more free electricity. And another example is: if I use single electrons, I can use them as sensors. I can put a single electron near a neuron, and it notices if the single neuron is turned on or off in real time.
Bosch, in Germany, is creating a pilot project to put sensors of this type on a helmet that should help us read brain activity to drive a computer, for example. Or, people who can't communicate due to neurological problems, could be able to communicate with the outside world for medical diagnosis. There is a mountain of possible applications.
And these are the reasons why the European Commission has decided to set up this very big program that we were talking about earlier,
and another one is being added that's being discussed these days, to create a Europe-wide secure communication network, therefore another billion euros is invested there, for the protection of all our data.
There is a large number of researchers involved. The details are not important: what is important is, not only is there a connection between these four fields that I showed you before - communication, simulation, computation and sensors - but that everything is rooted on basic science,
because these things are possible, and on this I want to return in a moment, only if we continue to ask ourselves the fundamental questions, those that seem completely useless
and the idea, the goal, the hope that we have is to somehow be able, like in a mission to bring man to the moon, but here you see a flag, as we saw a moment ago, of other continents that are far ahead on this issue
and maybe our hope is to be able to replace these flags or accompany them with our own flag.
I know that the European Union is not always perceived as the closest thing to citizens: but this is certainly an example where the European Union can really do something strong for us all, for the future of the new generations because the foundations of this thing go precisely in this direction.
The future, the professional one also, not only of services and technological possibilities, but also of jobs for the new generations -
and this allows me to get back to the foundation of things.
This is a slide that might sound cloying, but I put it on - I finished my presentation in front of a dozen EU ministers, a few months ago,
I rented the Vienna Hofburg, the imperial palace in Vienna. I'm not telling you how much it cost me for the initial day of these projects that we have just begun.
And my final slide was this, about a "flagship" to rebuild.
And the main point, the only thing I would like each of you to take home today, is this.
And this connects us exactly with the new generations. It is something we can all do, we can learn from them in this way: no matter how complicated, mysterious, esoteric something seems. It doesn't even matter how useful something seems.
What matters, if we want to have not only the innovation of tomorrow but also that of the next day, and also that of the generations after, is to never stop looking at reality with curiosity, with wonder,
getting ourselves surprised, getting ourselves fascinated.
Thank you.
(Applause)
It's beautiful to be here, although according to most scientists we shouldn't be here.
Actually, in the Ice Age, we should have gone extinct, but judging by the number of people I see here, that didn't happen.
I'm here to tell you what I think is a good story.
The story of a magnificent species which was very afraid.
So I'm going to talk to you about their fear - which, actually, is our fear too - and I'm going to talk to you about the mind.
The mind, which in trying endlessly to help us survive, doesn't hesitate to trick us.
Lastly, I'm going to talk about courage.
Courage is something we invented to protect us from ourselves.
I am afraid; as Homo sapiens, I am very afraid.
Someone has asked me, "What is with this fixation on fear?
Why do you care so much about it?" I am a trainer, a mentor, I've worked for several years with groups and with individuals.
I found out how much fear can hold us back from all of our aspirations.
Fear tastes like metal on your tongue.
Tastes a bit like blood, I don't know if you can tell.
It really can be bothering.
The worst of all is the fear of fear.
It works like this with prejudice, preconceptions, and preoccupations.
"pre", meaning "before they happen", it brings out those voices we hear, - that I'm hearing right now - "Give up, walk away, you'd better not!
Are you sure you have
all the information?
You sure don't like it! It's not for you!
Come on, everyone's looking at you!" This is a very simple, and yet very complex, mechanism.
This is how it works, more or less: our mind receives data, impulses from the external world.
Immediately, we get a warning and our blood is flooded with substances that make us react.
There are three kinds of reactions.
The first one is run. The second one is fight, and the third one is stop.
I have to say, our mind does that to protect us, she has great interest in taking us to the end of the race.
But she is also rather selfish, because she thinks about herself.
She thinks about protecting herself, saving energy, has this instinct of self-preservation, and to pursue it, she lies to us.
She sends us pictures which are not real, they are not the truth.
For example, I'm walking through the woods; I see a sinusoidal-shaped branch on the ground, and I immediately get the image of a snake!
I protect myself, but that is not true, it is a lie.
Our mind can turn into a stopping device in this way and somewhat, she becomes a terrible master.
I ask myself and you, did you ever happen to want to make the right choice, do the right thing?
"I'll do the right thing. I'll think it through.
Think more than once, think for ten seconds before acting!"
But what is ultimately the right thing?
Every time we choose, we leave something behind.
How many of us, because of fear, carry some regret or remorse?
To understand how all of this works I dared go back in time and see how evolution has somehow affected this story.
Remember, we invented the courage, and we invented it to protect ourselves from fear.
Let's talk about our ancestor, the Homo sapiens, 100,000 years ago.
We used to live in a rather comfortable natural environment: quite a bit of resources available, some redundant predators, but we had a decent life anyway.
Then, suddenly, some abrupt climatic changes - abrupt is a figure of speech, we are talking ages - transformed our natural environment completely: the savannah.
In the savannah, we weren't much at ease anymore: there were many predators; homo sapiens didn't want to be fed to lions and hyenas.
So for some inexplicable reason, 70,000 years ago, he decided to head north, and he left.
An incredible journey, a journey towards the unknown, towards uncertainty: a journey of 70,000 years, consider!
Headed north, to new discoveries, facing the unknown.
The unknown becomes an opportunity.
They had a lot of fear, and some tools made out of flint.
Nothing more.
Over 70.000, however, you get to learn a lot of things.
Homo sapiens always wanted to explore and innovate, until eventually, he found the European continent and there, he found a crib where he could stop in a way, but always keep exploring and innovating.
Then, 40.000 years ago, probably while hunting, he jumps into his more charming and sophisticated cousin: Homo neanderthalensis
Homo neanderthalensis is strong, fast, he's perfectly adapted to cold temperatures, is an incredibly efficient predator.
They share many experiences for a while, and probably, some DNA too.
So what happened?
They start behaving in completely different ways.
These are hard times for everyone, but Homo sapiens decides to be a nomad: he never stops, he doesn't settle for what he has, wants to see beyond the mountains, he looks for the Great Water, he explores.
Homo neanderthalensis lives in a small area, exploits it to the fullest, and when the resources are finished, he leaves it.
Sapiens dares join other groups, different from the familiar group, so he takes a little risk.
He does that to exchange knowledge, and he creates the first proto-tribes.
Neanderthalensis is the opposite: familiar group, a few people, a few individuals, and of course, his genetic code is impoverished and female mortality increases.
Sapiens is always innovating: it seems endlessly interested in improvement.
His tools change, they become always more sophisticated, along with his hunting techniques.
Neanderthalensis does the exact same things for thousands of years.
Finally, Sapiens invents language: a sophisticated code useful to exchange knowledge.
Neanderthalensis doesn't have much to share, so: Sapiens lives on, Neanderthalensis goes extinct.
Darwin, our beloved Darwin, was right: the species most capable to adapt to change lives on, and of course, he does that to the expenses of the strongest one.
That's how it went, biologically and not only, behaviour-wise too;
our ancestors had the courage to adopt new and effective social behaviors in a context of perpetual change, like the Ice Age.
In every Homo sapiens, in every one of them, there is a natural impulse to dare and face fear.
We are a species with incredible capacity for wishing and this expose us to the uncertain.
After me, on this stage, you will hear incredible stories: people who got lost in storms in the ocean, people who are even thinking of going to Mars, people who challenged serious and debilitative illnesses, and they did so without looking for the absolute certainty, for the absolute certainty prevents us from satisfying our aspirations.
So I am here to tell you that in my professional experience, I found out that fear has a biological root, and will forever be with us, it can't be eliminated.
We can't not be afraid.
Courage, however, is different: courage is a competence.
Courage can be learned, can be practiced, courage is the quantity of uncertainty with which we are willing to live.
If we catch this opportunity, we will be able to grow and make change our new chance.
This way, I dare say, that my mind will go from being a terrible master to being a great servant.
So, in these uncertain times, when this world seems to go so fast we can't keep up with it, everything changes, and we are a little overwhelmed.
In these times, I think it's useful to have a new man and a new woman: Neo sapiens, if you will, no more Homo sapiens.
We don't need other walls, barbed wire, laws regulating all that surrounds us, we don't need to join other tribes fighting different tribes under ancient seals.
Sapiens survives any symbol and any ideology.
We don't need many skills and technologies, but we need empathy and understanding.
We need humanity.
It's a lot of stuff, but don't worry, we don't have to do this alone.
Look around you: who are these people next to you?
Who is in this room?
Move your head, look at them!
Who are these people?
Why are they here today?
Why did you choose to dedicate today to TED?
Who did you choose to come to TED with?
This is our tribe.
This is the tribe we need to learn to join again, but really, like our ancestors did.
It is crucial to learn, re-learn to communicate deeply, and to share our needs.
It is important to be generous and share our knowledge, lay our competences to serve the great tribe's needs.
And above all, it is important to have the courage to change everything.
If we can understand this, if we can, we will have an opportunity.
And so I tell you that the great opportunity to grow and progress, for the human race, goes through the need to accept the uncertain.
And in this way, we will succeed, like 70,000 years ago, like always.
So I ask you: how much uncertainty are you willing to live with?
What's the challenge you want to accept today, in this room?
Darwin was right: if I don't change, I go extinct.
This is the key to our survival.
Thank you for listening.
(Applause)
We are at the dawn of the digital civilization,
we have probably already figured that out because our mail has become digital, music and movies have become liquid, and all those things that used to sit on our shelves, like encyclopedia, have gone, replaced now by Wikipedia.
Nowadays we expect, correctly or not, that an entire banking and financial system will fit in our smartphones.
The Nobel Prize in Economics, Milton Friedman, had already predicted it. In 1999 he said: "What we are missing now, and will be certainly created in a short while, is a digital currency - mind you, not a digital coin linked to an individual; but rather personal cash that can be used anonimously, by anyone, online.
This prophecy has been fulfilled by the creation of Bitcoin, which absolutely is pole position in the race.
Why?
Not so much for being, quite obviously, digital: our euros and dollars have been digital for quite a long time too.
Rather, it's because it is decentralized: there is no organization, no government behind it that holds the power to manage it and supervise its functionality.
And this, albeit a bit scary, it’s a very strong guarantee that Bitcoin cannot be manipulated.
It's that type of innovation known in the English-speaking world as "permissionless innovation".
What does that mean?
It’s a type of innovation that has no centralized safety mechanisms, no entry checks, not even an editorial content control system.
If you're getting scared by this seemingly anarchic plan, there's no need to worry.
We've already witnessed this type of innovation in action, and it proved to be both effective and gentle.
The e-mail, for example: it was not invented by a consortium of post-offices,
the Internet was not invented by a telcos consortium,
So why should a transactional network of value be built by a consortium of banks? I can't really wrap my mind around it.
I came across Bitcoin in 2014 -
I studied physics, and I worked for more than 20 years in the field of investment banks, finance and so on.
But I had never asked myself, not even once, what money is.
The first time I thought about it was when I stumbled upon Bitcoin:
up to now, that's the best gift that Bitcoin has ever given me.
So I'd like to play a game with you, let's try and go over the history of currency in only three minutes.
Here we go!
The earlies form of currency we find in history is gold.
Why?
It might seems weird, but gold had two peculiar characteristics: there wasn't much of it, and it shone.
Turns out, human beings love shining things.
Gold was also quite malleable, and it is fairly easy to determine its purity.
Take a pot, put it on the fire, let the gold melt and then wait for it to cool down.
If it's still shining after this thermal shock, it's definitely gold, trust me: any other material would stop shining,
But if for every business transaction the process needed to be repeated, it would have been difficult.
So we asked Julius - yes, I mean Julius Caesar - "Look, Julius, how about putting your face on the gold coin as a guarantee there's that much gold in it?"
The first deflationay crisis came about with his heir, Augustus Caesar.
Even the peasants realized, sestertia did not contain the figure amount of gold.
But for the medioeval merchant, gold had even another problem: it was really heavy. Gold has a very high density, so it was uncomfortable to carry it around.
And also risky, for you can be robbed by bandits.
Almost at the same time in history, London-based goldsmiths and those who would later become the Italian bankers, they came up with the same idea: they suggested the merchant to leave the gold in their vaults in exchange for a certificate that first was nominal, and would later become a bearer certificate. It was a bank-note with which one goes to her pawn counter and redeem her gold back.
That's how the banknote was born: a currency that represents an amount of value stored elsewhere.
But both the Britons and the Italians couldn't resist a temptation: after noting that most of the time people did not ask for the gold back. So they had what we might call a spark of genius.
They thought: "If we print more banknotes than there's gold in our vaults, who will notice it?"
Do you know what the answer is?
No, no one!
So the fractional coin appeared:
there are more banknotes that the redeemable gold.
Jumping forward in history: in 1972, even in a regime of fractional currency, the redeemibility of money, US dollars in this case, in gold, was still a limiting factor for those in charge of monetary policy.
But Richard Nixon had enough, and he decided that the US dollar couldn't be converted in gold anymore.
From that point on, we have "Fiat money", and I do mean exactly "Fiat Lux et Lux Fuit": a currency whose value is purely conventional, its value it’s based on a social contract that we all agree on.
And if someone does not agree, we can force that person by using something know as “legal tender”, which means that fiat money must be accepted to pay back a debt.
Let me say it, this is a bad coin, actually, we could even say that it’s a terrible coin: we don’t need to recall the most egregious examples in the history of money. Our dear old friend US dollar, already proves it: its performance it’s not exactly impeccable.
Starting from 1913, the year in which the Federal Reserve was funded, the USA dollar has lost more than 96% of its purchase power. That means, it’s a bad currency. It's the typical result of a monopoly situation.
Friedrich von Hayek, Nobel Prize in Economics and founder of the Austrian school of economic thought, wrote an entire book bemoaning the fact that: "We deem essential the government's monopoly of the currency.
We would accept the monopoly over no kind of good, in a market economy. Yet we do accept the monopoly of that syntethic product that has half of the power in any business transaction: the currency.
Like any type of monopoly, not only it delivered us a bad product, but it forbid us from looking for a better one.”
Therefore, Hayek did conclude: "If we ever want to have a good currency again, we have to take it away from the control of the States.
And since we cannot do that using force - he was a good man, and I comply with his invitation - we should do it with a clever gimmick, something they wouldn’t be able to stop.”
Bitcoin is exactly that clever gimmick.
Now let’s try to dive deeply into what a currency is.
Money is, in it's essence, a tool for social relationships.
I know that this might not be the definition you were expecting; but if you think about your own experience, we were all born in a gift economy.
I hope that none of you had to pay for all the parental care you received: otherwise I would feel very sorry for you all, and quite happy for your therapist, who will certainly have a lot to work on with you.
But this gift economy doesn’t only encompass our family, our friends, our neighbours, what we could call “our tribe”.
It’s an economy that doesn’t scale, because sooner or later we will run into people we don’t know, and that is why we don’t trust them - or maybe, sometimes, we don’t trust them precisely because we know them, which is more or less the same.
But we are inherently social animals, that’s what we are anthropologically, so we want to cooperate even with those we do not trust.
So, at first, we invented the practice of bartering: I give you my eggs, but it just so happens you’re going to give me your milk today.
Alternatively, in order to barter anywhere and anytime, we invented the currency: a syntethic good that allows us to cooperate with people we do not trust.
I'm not sure about you, but I hope for a moment of intellectual upheaval, because I reckon this remark as hugely important: a currency is a tool we use to cooperate with people we have no trust in.
So it's quite clear that, today, in what it’s for the first time a digital and global information economy, the need arises for a supranational currency,
a digital coin, not controlled by states, a global currency, a currency of the Internet.
The problem is, it is insanely hard to create an Internet currency.
The biggest problem is known as “double expense problem”.
Every time that we have a digital artifact that represents a value, we've always needed a centralized authority in order to prevent its duplication.
Digital as it is, it’s easily duplicable. It’s easy to grasp: consider your current account balance.
If I transfer my current balance account to your account - don’t get your hopes up, I don’t have that much money - and then I try to transfer it again to the gentleman here, my bank, which controls the updates of the ledger, is going to tell me: "No Ferdinando, you cannot do that.”
How are we going to create a valuable digital asset, that is to say an non-duplicable asset? Because – think about the painting "Gioconda".
Gorgeous, no doubts about that.
Its market price is incalculable.
But if the painting could be duplicated without control, for an unlimited number of perfect copies, it would still be gorgeous, but its market price would drop down to zero.
That’s what is happening with Bitcoin, and it's limited to 21 milion Bitcoins.
Bitcoin, in a way, is as scarce as physical gold: gold on the physical level, Bitcoin on the digital level.
Bitcoin is, or at least is aspires to be, the digital counterpart of gold.
And now I'm going to hope for a second moment of upheaval. I’m pretty sure, you all today came here in this theatre believing every digital thing can be duplicated.
This is the very intuition that Bitcoin proves wrong: It’s a digital gold and attached to it, inside of the Bitcoin itself, there is a transnational network, which is both safe and not-censurable, that can be used to transfer this gold globally.
I know you are skeptical, I can see it in your faces.
Let’s play another game, then: imagine that an alien was going to land here, okay?
You have to explain the traditional currencies to him, and I’m going to explain Bitcoin.
I will start right away by telling the alien: "Look, their currecies have no inherent value."
At this point you might respond, slightly irritated: "The same goes for Bitcoins, Ferdinando!"
And I will say, you serious? I’ve just explained to you the shortage in the digital world!.
But your game is easy: What about the social contract, centuries of history of the currency, the legal tender - Alright, 1-1, time for the kickoff, let’s start this over.
This time I’m going to start by pulling your leg, saying that the cash you carry around is just a bunch of colored paper.
Yeah, right, special paper, special ink: but, let’s be honest, it's just the same as the cash in the game of Monopoly. My coin, on the other hand,
is pure math and encryption.
Not only that, I will also tell the alien that while I am a kind person, I’m not forcing my Bitcoin on you, you are instead rather coercive.
I cannot refuse your euro.
The legal tender tells me, if a debtor wants to settle his debt with me in euro, I can’t deny him that.
I’m goint to let you figure out how the alien is going to see all of this.
Oh, I almost forgot, maybe I should also tell the alien that a gentleman, in Frankurt - a very composed gentlemen, the most serious of us all - he can print as much of that colored paper as he likes, whenever he likes, and give it to anyone he likes. He was not elected, and he won’t need to take responsability for how he carries out his duty.
Meanwhile I’ve already explained to the alien that Bitcoin’s monetary policy is perfectly deterministic, it can’t be influenced.
You might say: "Right, but no one is using Bitcoin.
there are no business transactions carried out using Bitcoin."
That’s for sure: those who bought, in 2010, two pizzas, paying them 10.000 Bitcoin - which at the current rate woul be 40 milion dollars - Well, let’s just hope that those pizzas were really, really good, because I wouldn’t have been able to digest them in any way.
Bitcoin is a safe-haven asset, so it's stored. Therefore it makes much more sense not to compare it to a currency, but to actual gold.
Gold has been accepted by all civilizations as the earliest form of currency, without any type of centralized planning.
It was for centuries the most successful currency, it set off the development of all the monetary systems we know, it was overtaken by forms of currencies much more sophisticated without it becoming outdated.
What happened over the centuries to physical gold, is happening in these ten years, and will probably happen in the upcoming decades, to Bitcoin.
The Bitcoin's most scary trait is the value volatlity: it goes up and down.
There isn’t much to fear, to be honest.
I mean, every time someone talks about Bitcoin you should fasten your seatbelts, because the ride tends to be quite bumpy. But Bitcoin's volatility is physiological.
When market mechanisms of supply and demand try to assess the value of a certain good, if the product is controversial, like digital gold, the process is going to be just as mush controversial.
We’ve seen it already happen, in history, with e-commerce: take a look at the history of Amazon’s ratings, its volatility.
The worrying part about Bitcoin is the so-called "drowdown": highest to lowest point, it managed to lose more that 93 percent of its value.
So my bottom line is, if you ever plan to invest in Bitcoin, do it with a small percentage of your savings, small enough that you would be able to reasonably sustain its complete loss.
The good news is that Bitcoin is not correlated to other investment asset classes, to other investment opportunities: It doesn’t move with them, it diversifies the risks. So, if put it in an investment portfolio, even if it sounds counterintuitive, but it draws dawn the risks of that portfolio, expected rate of return being equal, or, given the same risk, it increases greatly the expected return.
So it is a good idea to invest a small amount in Bitcoin, and this gives us a cue for some final considerations.
Let’s imagine that 2 percent of the assets under management - I’m not referring to 2 percent of the global wealth, but 2 percent of those assets that are professionally managed - let’s imagine that they invest in Bitcoin over the next few years.
Well, if we do the math, the value of a Bitcoin is going to be a hundred thousand dollars.
But if Bitcoin is, or proves to be, the digital gold - I say if because it’s a bold experiment and it might fail, let’s not forget that, but it’s an experiment substantiated both culturally and technologically - again, if it proved to be the digital gold, well, ladies and gentlemen, it would be better than actual gold: extremely light, instantly transferable, low transactional costs, no logistic problems, non-censurable and unstoppable.
Therefore, if Bitcoin was to capitalize the same amount as gold today, its value would raise to 400.000 dollars.
100.000 dollars, 400.000 dollars - I’m not here to sell you Bitcoin,
I’m just saying, if Bitcoin is digital gold, it's currently greatly underestimated.
Someone might object saying that: "I've read that it’s not Bitcoin, It’s the blockchain, the technology underlying it, the real deal".
When it comes to this, I love to paraphrase Confucio, he said: “When a wise man points at the moon, the fool looks at the finger”.
The moon is Bitcoin, the finger is the blockchain.
You can’t overlook the relevance of the Bitcoin phenomenon.
If you have the slightest understanding of the role that gold played in the history of civilization, finance and currency, the rise of gold's digital counterpart in the digital civilization and in the future of finance and currency is going to be explosive.
I would like to end with a thought: 25-27 year ago, I used the e-mail and surfed the web, but I'd never imagine that on top of the TCP/IP protocol, the technology underlying the web and the e-mail, would be used to organize our weekends in digital clubs – Facebook, or buy liquid, digital books and CDs from online shops, or we would ask questions in natural language to a computer, a digital assistant, expecting a meaningful answer.
I can’t tell you, 20 years from now, what we will be able to make on the value's TCP/IP protocol, which type of incredible apps we will have managed to create: that’s exactly the adventure that is waiting for us over the horizon.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Bap meogeosseoyo.
Bap meogeosseoyo.
(Audience) Bap meogeosseoyo. Bap meogeosseoyo is a Korean greeting, very common until a few years ago.
Literally it means, "Have you eaten today?"
In fact, it shows the concern of the Koreans for their own health.
We know that South Korea was going through a deep crisis, until a few years ago, but today it has become a global power within the industry and technology fields.
"Bap meogeosseoyo," in my Korean experience, is a symbol that crises not only can be overcome, but can make us much stronger.
But how is it possible?
I have been studying crisis for years, in societies, organizations and on a private level.
And one of the reasons why I am passionate about this is, as they say in Rome, I had it coming.
I moved to eight different countries in the past 12 years. I changed three jobs, so I had to reinvent myself and start over from scratch many times.
And some of these changes have led to crises.
Other changes have occurred, instead, thanks to crises.
Today I would like to talk to you about personal crises, how to go through them and use them to start over.
What is a personal crisis?
It is a period of our life where we feel a deep negative energy within ourselves, be it relatable to a particular event, a person or even a few words.
In the workplace, could be "your performance is poor" or "you are fired."
In a love relationship, "I don't love you anymore, I want to break up."
Within family or friends, "I have a health problem, I need surgery."
So I would like to ask you about your last crisis.
What was the event, the phrase that generated it? [What was your last crisis?]
Done?
If you haven't found one yet, please come next time to talk about how not to have crises.
This is one of the most significant crises of my life.
What you see, the geyser of Lake Geneva,
is the symbol of the city of Geneva.
But it's also the metaphor of how I was feeling in Geneva.
In fact, I was at the top of my career, while freefalling in my personal life.
Two years earlier I had managed to enter, immediately after graduating, in one of the largest multinational consumer goods companies.
It was my dream.
After two years I managed to be transferred to the headquarters, to the European headquarters of that company.
I was thrilled.
But after a few weeks, things weren't going as I thought.
I was having many misunderstandings with my boss, whom I couldn't get along with, he was very demanding.
The brand I was working for was in a deep crisis, so there was a lot of pressure inside the company and I couldn't settle in Geneva.
So I reacted in the only way I knew, working longer hours.
But things did not improve that much.
In fact, working longer hours I started having less free time and less time for friends, I started eating badly, sleeping badly, working badly. I became less productive, so I had to work even longer hours.
I was in a vicious circle and I didn't know how to get out.
I weighed up to 99 kilos, and at times I cried before going to work because I was too anxious to go.
Then a dramatic coincidence forced me to stay in bed for 15 days.
And for the first time ever, I didn't check my work emails.
I dedicated those 15 days to myself, to understand my situation, why I felt that way,
understanding myself better.
And when I got back to work, I started talking to other colleagues who had worked with my boss, who worked for other brands that were similar to mine, who felt a lot of pressure, colleagues who had just moved to Geneva too.
I started asking them,
But how do you do it? What do you feel?
And to my amazement, their answers were different from what I had expected.
In fact, they had the same anxiety, crises and struggles, but they reacted differently.
They saw in those struggles a chance to learn something because they liked their job.
So maybe, it crossed my mind, the problem wasn't the context, it wasn't my boss, nor the company, nor Geneva: maybe I was the problem.
Because I was reacting in this way; because others reacted differently.
And then I started to look for the real problem, what the real cause was.
And I did this exercise, the exercise of values.
I took a chart with all the most important values and things in life, and I began to identify which were the closest to me and put them in order,
and this exercise made me understand a lot of things about my situation.
In fact, I had chosen this career because it gave me financial security and social security.
The famous permanent contract that many Italians look for.
In addition, it was also what my family wanted for me, what would give me the stability to create my family.
But my true value, my main value, is passion.
And to the question "Am I passionate about what I'm doing?"
the answer was simply "No."
A second question helped me: do I want to do my boss's boss job?
You know, in a company, one of the reasons people work so much to get a promotion
and do the work of their boss. Then work even more and become the boss of their boss.
And this process, if you're lucky, takes years.
There's an important question you need to ask yourself: after so many years, will I be happy to get the job of my boss's boss?
My answer was simply "No."
So these two exercises, among others, made me realize what the real problem was: I no longer liked that career.
So I explored new alternatives and decided to get a PhD.
I graduated in Paris, to my parents' satisfaction and mine.
Later this led me to teach in Helsinki and Seoul, where I now teach management, and I'm specialized in crisis management.
This is one of my last crises.
March 25, 2019, two months ago.
I remember this date very well.
I was in bed, looking at my room's ceiling with a negative energy fueled by my feelings, work and family.
So I did the exercise I told you earlier to understand what the real problem was.
Normally, to understand what the real problem is, it takes a few days, a few weeks to make decisions.
Meanwhile, you have this negative energy.
Have you ever experienced lying in your bed, having no energy to go to work or see your friends?
So how can we handle this negative energy?
One of the reasons we feel it is because our mind sometimes is our best friend, but sometimes it becomes our worst enemy, as psychology studies tell us.
In fact, the mind recreates that person, that sentence, millions of times, perhaps looking for a solution that now is useless anyway.
And every time this negative thought crosses our mind, we get another wave of negative energy within us.
Therefore, in order to handle this energy, we have to handle our thoughts first.
The silent talk inside our mind, our mental script.
And I applied this technique: I created the "help button."
You select a small circle of friends and family whom you know can help you with this specific crisis.
The help button is thus a metaphor. We can press a button, we can make a call when we are particularly sad.
Talking to another person helps give us a new perspective and change our mental path.
This time I added something different, I used a path - more of a social, virtual help button.
In fact, I had been following two personal growth groups for a few months.
And I've seen that in the last few months, people have started to post requests for help and advice on books or general stuff, suggestions about time management or how to manage stress, how to lose weight, how to manage a colleague they don't like or how to move on after a break up.
And so I decided to write my question too.
I got more than 120 comments.
The fact that some strangers, people whom I don't know, had made time to give me suggestions, made me feel less lonely.
Later I got in touch with some of these people through PM, and we helped each other to overcome that crisis.
And I also had a series of books, audio and video that I started reading, listening to and watching that helped me understand what the real problem was and finally overcome it.
This is the second technique, the technique of the wish table.
The wish table is that image you see in front of my bed.
It's a simple PowerPoint slide image,
I split it into four areas, the most important to me in life. They might be different for you. Love, finance, career and health.
Then I opened Google, Pinterest and Instagram to look for images of how I wanted to be feeling in that particular area of my life.
So about love: I want to get married, have three children and a relationship filled with intimacy, connection, joyfulness. About finances, I want to reach a balance, the so-called financial freedom. About my career, I put all the academic journals I set my mind on to publish, the prizes I want to win as a professor, the books I want to publish and also how many social followers I want.
And about my health, I put all the images of how I'd like to see myself and how I'd like to feel when I eat.
Once I created this wishes table, I printed it in different formats and put it in the places where I spent most of my time: in my bedroom, in my living room, at the office.
I also set it as a cover, my phone cover.
And every time my mind wanted to cheat me or send me back to the past, I tried to trick it and send it to the future instead, to my desires,
how I would want to feel, not how I actually did.
And I focused on this whole image and above all on the number 79.
What is 79?
79 kilos is my year-long target weight.
As I told you, during the Geneva crisis I weighed 99 kilos, almost 100.
But I had lost so much weight, I managed to reach 82.
But I never managed to weigh less than 82 kilos.
On March 25, 2019, two months ago, I weighed over 90 kilos.
So I said to myself, I want to use all this negative energy and turn it into my goal, the goal I've had for so many years:
reaching 79 kilos by May 25th.
That is today.
And so as a good engineer, what did I do?
I created a chart, I asked myself, how much do I have to lose on average every week to reach this goal.
And I managed to lose - around April 20th I weighted 83-84 kilos.
I had lost so much by then. But we know, the last kilos are the hardest to lose.
And then I stopped weighing myself for a while,
and today I would like to share this moment with you.
Have I managed to reach my goal or not?
(Applause) So we have a scale that is calibrated with my weight, the weight of my clothes.
Can we ask someone to check?
(Applause) These two months have been a beautiful journey.
And these are some tools of mine to transform a crisis.
The first is to ask what the real problem is because, as in my case, I could have changed bosses, I could have changed brand, company or even city.
But then the problem would come back because the real problem was, I didn't like that career.
This exercise of values can help overcome and understand the real problem of many crises.
And then change the mental path, and then create a real or virtual help button.
And this wish table that helped me so much to focus, to turn negative energy into positive.
Because you know, I know I will have other crises, I can't avoid them, probably, I can't even keep you from having them.
But I know that you can go through them, and you can transform them.
So I would like to ask you how you will transform your next crisis.
What will be your 79?
Bap meogeosseoyo.
(Applause)
When I joined my company, many years ago, the first thing I saw was a sort of mysterious box where something came in and something came out from.
And above all, where something happened.
That was a few generations of PCs ago - that used to be my computer, I'm a bit old - but mostly, a few generations of hair ago - this used to be my hair.
And then I realized
what the rules of the game actually were.
Especially, the unwritten ones.
Because I realized that apart from the cash flows, the production patterns, the industrial logic, and the budgets, every company is based, consciously or not, on certain standpoints: its own values.
And values are precisely what we'll talk about today.
Because we ask ourselves: how critical are these values, today, to compete?
And what's their impact on business?
Looking at Fortune's Most Admired Companies 2015 ranking, at the very top we find very well-known brands, such as Apple, Google, Amazon, and so on.
If then we look at the Forbes' The World’s Most Valuable Brands list, we still find Apple, Microsoft, Google, Coca-Cola.
What do these companies have in common, besides being among the world's most popular ones?
Simple: a sound system of shared values, which all employees at all levels refer to.
These are the pillars that enable people to manage the company with a management style that really makes people feel "aboard".
And also, allows them to compete more efficiently.
We don't have to look too far away from here to find interesting examples.
Sometimes, it's just a matter of deciding what to focus on and right messages, turns out, appear a lot closer than you think.
Today, I'd like to share with you a short document written 80 years ago.
On October 28, 1935, in Campodarsego, a small town north of Padua, an entrepreneur, in a machine's delivery note, wrote by hand, "The seed drill I sold you is guaranteed for two years against defects in construction and materials, as well as against any operating failure.
In witness whereto, Giovanni."
Who would do that today?
He put himself on the line.
He himself guaranteed both the product and the service.
That lets him convey a strong message, both to his customers and to his employees.
Of course, we live in a completely different era,
we are in a small town.
We are in a world where relationships between people, both inside and outside the company, count more than anything else.
A world where shared values, not just because they're written or coded but shared and experienced in proximity, spread through all levels a sort of "value osmosis”.
It was 1935.
Companies, by their very nature, grow and change.
Languages, people, structures change.
And above all, their logic and dynamics change.
And it is during those changes that values become a critical factor.
The risk is to create gaps that are hard to bridge.
When I was looking for an image that could shortly resume the meaning and nature of these values, an unusual material came to my mind, and the advisers know how unpopular it is: mortar.
Mortar, whose main function is not to "bind" the bricks or stones together but rather distribute the weight and smooth edges.
Fantastic.
Doesn't this make us think about our organizations?
Our needs?
Let's see which "mortar" is adopted by the most successful companies namely, what their reference values are.
People talk all the time about value creation, innovation, or increasingly, in a changing world, where responsibility gets critical, about responsibility at all levels, both inside and outside the company, with the extended value chain.
But above all – in all cases – it's about people.
So let's talk about people,
and let's do so with a simple diagram.
In order to be productive, companies typically buy a plant.
Plants have a relevant start-up cost, and a value that is poised to decrease over time.
It depreciates, it's normal.
Human resources, ideally, a parallel, different, but above all an opposite path should be followed.
People join their companies with no practical skills, and over time, they acquire value, increasing their knowledge.
And if, for a plant, I do all I can to replace the parts, to invest in maintenance - in short, to endure machines' life cycle and their productivity - it is essential to think about some kind of investment, support, also for people.
But are we talking about euros?
About money?
About a raise?
About paychecks?
If we think that, as time goes by, money are the only thing to care about, we'll necessarily be surrounded by mercenaries, that look for the bonus rather than results.
Is that what we really want?
Rather, and I wrap up with it, let's focus on two key words.
First, listening.
Machines routinely undergo diagnostics to understand what is happening over time; for people, conscious listening is mandatory.
They have to feel cared about, assessed, and supported, and acknowledged along the way.
Plus, maintenance.
When buying a machine, a production system, a maintenance program is included, of course.
But for people?
Even with people, it is important to define serious training and a support plan, which go far beyond technical notions, which don't solve our deepest problems.
People who are "listened to" and "maintained" will be happy to belong to the company, and will return value to it in the long term.
So let's focus on people. This is the great value of values.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Tonight I'd like to talk to you about an experiment I did a few years ago, that improved my life in so many ways.
However, before getting into the details, I wanted to take you on a trip, and photography can help us, to Africa, to try to understand a bit the context of this whole experiment.
Just imagine these four hunters moving through a typical Savannah scenario.
They are on the hunt.
They are following a herd of enormous herbivores, called kudu, and specifically a much larger one that left the group.
They are putting him under pressure.
The human being evolved for resistance race; a race that is not too fast, but that can go on for a very long time.
The hunters are putting him under pressure, and the kudu - unlike human beings - cannot regulate his body temperature; and because he is a large herbivore, his intestine does not allow him to breathe correctly on the run.
The fate of the kudu is unfortunately marked, because at some point it will have to stop, otherwise its heart would burst due to the rise in temperature.
So the hunter, with just a simple pointed wooden shaft, can end the life of this large herbivore.
Even if this is a cruel scene, it does not happen often.
It is difficult for the hunters to carry it out.
Today our guys will take home about 38,000 calories to share with the rest of the village, women, children and less fortunate hunters, for that day.
I spent ten years in Africa, in Capetown to be precise, where I worked in a center for population genetics, and together with other researchers, I became passionate about the lifestyle of these hunter-gatherers, who are still present in different parts of Africa, so also in South Africa, the state of Lesotho; and Botswana, Namibia and Tanzania.
And so we often spent time with them to try to understand their lifestyle, their eating habits, their movement patterns throughout the day.
We had incredible experiences: I remember we used to follow them in the hunt, in their gatherings, all day long, maybe a 38-degree hot day, with our super technological shoes and Camel Bags full of water, mineral salts and maybe the latest generation of supplements, but it was practically impossible to keep up with them. Sometimes we had to sit in the shade of an acacia, and while I watched them disappear on the horizon, I wondered if they weren't really superior beings, I mean really incredibly strong people from all points of view, also considering how stressful their daily lifestyle is.
I then returned to Italy and decided to start this experiment, because something didn't add up.
From this slide you can see that some agencies, such as FAO, ENSA or the American FDA, suggest the correct intake of calories to maintain body homeostasis and good health.
If we look at it, everyone will find their range, according to age and sex, somewhere around 2,600-2,800 calories.
As far as I was concerned, something didn't add up.
While living with them, they didn't have those daily calories.
Yes, if the hunt was successful, surely there were even more than that, but it wasn't always the case.
So I decided to start an experiment that lasted for months.
I greatly reduced the amount of suggested calories.
I made a food plan that got as close as possible to what I had seen and I followed it for a while.
From a hematological perspective, I monitored all the parameters that came out, taking samples every three - four weeks, in a laboratory, and trying to see the changes in blood glucose, azotemia, cholesterol, creatinine and all the other factors that are influenced by diet, one way or another.
Plus, I also tried to monitor how my body changed in terms of lean mass, fat mass, visceral fat and body hydration, and obviously I kept a daily journal to see day by day how I was reacting mentally,
how my moods and energy levels were.
I carried out this experiment.
It was not easy at first - my wife knows something about that - because moods change very very often, energy falls, and so it also has an influence on your work.
But I wanted to look beyond the horizon; I tried to move forward, despite a lot of people said to me, "What are you doing?
You're decreasing your calories and increasing your physical activity:
that's not good, it's wrong."
I went further, tried to continue, and after a while something changed.
I remember it was friday, because I was keeping a journal.
I woke up, and felt an energy I hadn't felt in a long time.
My sense of smell and taste were completely altered for the better. I could feel everything around me in a 360 degrees fashion, as though I were experiencing the real "here and now".
I was not happy, so I tried to involve, at the beginning, a bunch of crazy friends, who said, OK, come on, we're right behind you. I also gave them a plan with the same concept: lower by 1000 calories their daily nutritional intake.
And with them I monitored, using specific questionnaires, a whole series of parameters.
Then I went from friends to acquaintances, and we really increased in number. Some people wanted to try it just for the sake of challenge, at least for a while.
We eventually became so many.
I collected all this data, and although obviously with different timing, eventually everything converged into the same feelings I had experienced:
a general sense of well-being and health.
These are parameters that perhaps may not make sense to you, but make it clear that when the body undergoes caloric stress, something happens; it is like an awakening, inside our cells, of genes that had been hidden and dormant for hundreds of thousands of years.
So I tried to go a little more deeply into the topic, and through PubMed , which is a database where you can find all the research done by laboratories around the world, I looked for ones that had somehow studied deeper and for a much longer time, the difference between our society's lifestyle and that of hunter-gatherer populations still in place around the world.
And there were - This is one of the graphs that basically shows caloric diversity from plants and animals across the different populations.
Essentially, I found three main differences.
One is caloric regime; another is caloric expenditure, the amount of calories burned in a day; The third, perhaps most important thing - that struck me so much - is the very presence in our society of a whole series of degenerative diseases that are statistically increasing in the population, even in the youngsters - take obesity, for example. These diseases are completely unknown
in these hunter-gatherer populations.
So I wondered, what if these calories are the real culprit?
If we look at this graph, 1990 to 2015, the caloric intake worldwide has increased exponentially.
I am one of those who did not raise his hand before, when the math teacher asked who was good at it.
So if we want to, we can do an experiment: take out our cell phone, and go to the calculator application.
Ok? Let's put in 1000 calories. We multiply that by seven, one for every day of the week, and then by four, one for every week in a month. And then we go on, by 12, and we get to 336,000 - Then let's put in 20 years.
That gets us to six million calories.
I want to leave you with this thought: what if these six million extra calories were the unexpected cause for these diseases and do not allow us to age in a healthy manner, and prevent us to become like those hunters, with all that great energy we have?
Thank you for your attention.
(Applause)
Nobody kills me anymore, I will live in a hundred years.
I don't know why I say so and why I think so, perhaps to declare war on fate after it went to war with me.
Or maybe I just talk to myself, my children and Bobo: now that you're gone, I'll be there forever.
I read these sentences in a book, and I suddenly realize that "Nobody kills me anymore" is what I'm saying.
It's my book, and it's my life.
What's going on?
That everything changed in a second.
March 24, 2012: you go out, finally a night out one can never do, you go to dance with your friends, you greet at lunch your husband who goes to play, after a career made of two Olympics and many other championships.
I'll see you tonight!
You leave four children at home: Alessandro, seven; Arianna, three; and two twins aged one, Angelica and Aurora.
And while you're on this quiet, peaceful evening, you get a call.
And somebody told me, Bobo felt bad.
So you take the car, you make this absurd journey to Macerata, because he was playing there.
And you hear, it was like a movie.
You see the team outside, the long ER aisle, and two doctors are only waiting for me.
Because then at the end of the day, the whole world knew, but me.
I was always inside this glass bell - because he had always put me inside the bell, because he was the head of the family.
So that's okay, and you accept.
Because then in life one accepts roles as well.
And from there they tell you, he's gone.
And I got up, something came to me I can hardly explain, these are all inner emotions.
There was a fire inside me: it was like, Federica had gone, and the next moment Federica was reborn.
Because it's fair to admit that Federica was reborn.
So I get up, go home, and take my four little angels - that we had through many hardships, that we managed to get through hardships, because, when we were young, were told that we couldn't have children.
And the very phrase "you can't" does not belong to me at all.
If you say "I can't", I do everything because that is my goal, I have to go there, one way or the other.
So we raised our hands and we asked for help, and thanks to assisted fertilization Bobo and I had our four little angels.
So you take them, you put them next to you, and I felt like transmitting "upliftingly" that negative thing that occurred us "Uplifting" may be too much, but in a different way than what had actually happened.
Like, you have a pain, but you process it, you put it in the right part, maybe.
Where it should be, because one must look forward, he was gone.
One can't do anything about the past.
No way, all you have to do is deal with it.
And I got everyone, and I told them, it was so cool!
Dad went to play for the best team ever, it's the team from heaven.
And so, that's where my game starts.
I started playing when I was nine.
And I played volleyball, then.
A sport where you are in such a small space, but you are many, because you are six.
So start along this path that you have to trust the people next to you.
You have to go and give your best, because at the end of the game you can either win or lose, but you must leave the field [feeling that] with the sweaty jersey you can finally make it.
And from there that book, those book sentences I started with: "I'll never get killed again" chapter was over, and I opened another one.
And it was my book, with my children, and with all the people I would then meet
and surely I would take my hand. Because then, even the help is not - I don't mind asking for help, I don't see it as a sign of weakness, but perhaps it was my greatest strength.
Raising my hand and say, I can't do this right now alone.
And then something happens at the right time inside right person, because on March 24th Bobo leaves, and I go to make a checkup at the gynecologist - remember, we could not have children. And in that ecography, the doctor says there's a life in me.
You look at the sky and say, "Maybe there's something I should do?
Yeah, maybe I have to do something.
Because there was a link - too many rings had chained themselves.
Bobo was a famous player; you can't have kids, you have four.
He's gone while playing,
and I don't feel like the only one who lost her husband and got floored by pain.
But my voice, perhaps, is a choir of voices.
But I'm here and I can talk because those rings brought me here.
And the ring was Andrea, my fifth son.
So there was an afterward, and a realization that I had to do something.
And you start organizing.
You start organizing events, you start organizing parties, you start organizing everything that is life and is not death.
Because, in the end, life and death intertwine and life and death unite us.
There's no one famous or famous - yes, you can discover anything, you can do anything, but in the end we're all in the same boat.
In the end, we're all here looking at each other, having a heart and having so many emotions.
I began to follow these emotions, I began to listen to my heart.
And during all these events, all this great going around, you start to share your story with the people in front of you.
And many times, when I was driving - I went to schools, I shared my experience with a lot with people, you realize you're in the car and you say: yes, it happened to me, it happened to him too early - because at 37 it was a bit too early. With five kids at home, really anti-nature.
But instead, then you realize that there are so many
dreadful stories around you.
How did they come? I don't know, really.
But one thing depends on us.
It's how we do the path life to death.

Because we all have our own path to take.
And so many times things happen that we raise our hands to and we can't help ourselves.
But how we lead out lives, that does depend on us.
So you hold an event, and you meet Red Ronnie, that you maybe saw on television, with all his music broadcasts - I'm an absolute fan, of music, and a fan of Italian music.
Because when I hear the songs I have to understand what they say, I can't bear it otherwise.
So you immerse yourself in the notes, you immerse yourself in the words.
There were some singer-songwriters who had really gotten into me, and it wasn't air but it was just oxygen.
Including a singer-songwriter named Pia Tuccitto.
So, when Red Ronnie invites me to her show and tells me that Pia is there, then I said: great, I'm going.
And this meeting was made of looks, it was made of listening because she presented a single of her own, I was talking about my story, and about my event, and from there - here's the ring perhaps these two women were meant - with a totally different life, because then Federica sportswoman who decided to stop very soon to play, at 26 years maybe I was at the height of my career, and I decided to throw myself into this creation of the family, and therefore this maternal instinct, to a person who always and anyway followed her passion: the music, the notes.
Everything that came from there, and made her write poems and songs that got their way into people.
And when this meeting took place, something clicked perfectly, because all she wrote in her songs at the end was her life.
It was all the emotion she felt and keeps feeling.
And all I wrote about in my life, was my life.
But not past life, also present and future life.
So, on a road trip, we looked at each other and it came to me spontaneously - it turned on that light I said before?
Since that evening of March 24th lights have been on and I really feel the strength of a person who takes me and says to me: you have to go.
And it puts me right in front of the path I've got to take.
I just have to be willing to walk.
And I looked at her and I said: Pia, can we do something together?
And from there she wrote those songs that I heard but that I didn't feel like I had a singer next to me writing those songs that I heard on the radio.
And as I listened to them carefully, more and more carefully, I looked at her and said: Pia, we're identical because your life intertwines with mine.
And from there we have - in half a day we found ourselves at her house, and we knocked down ten songs.
And when I heard the notes of her songs passing by, I would take them like a demonic - I took my book and started to write the pieces.
That's "Io e Lei" genesis.
Io e Lei [Me and Here] are two people.
It's about the strength of being together.
But "Io e Lei" may also be a lonely person.
I mean, when one wakes up in the evening or in the morning put herself in front of the mirror and she look at herself - because then, in the end, being together is fundamental because sharing, meeting people in my opinion is the best of life:.
But in the end, no one give the right answers but us.
Only by really looking us in the eye - or stopping us for a second.
Now we go, now you have to run, you have to work, you have to do, you never stop.
You're all the time on the phone, or online or - I have to work, I have to go shopping, buy this or that.
But one second, if you stop, you can appreciate what's important.
Because there's nothing taken for granted in life.
"It never happens to me"; "Look at her, now she's here... " And instead you realize that overnight, in a moment, you are here and you're the main character.
And you just have to go on, and bravely try to make this route as nicely as possible.
And the show is this: the show is a set of emotions where you talk about - what?
You talk about death, about pain, but you mainly talk about rebirth, awakening, love, friendship, something that you have - because we all have it, in us - and with Pia we took our hand and we are taking it around Italy to share it with people.
We share social projects, because then we also go and take hands with associations that carry messages: ours is that, "Let's never give up".
Anything that happens to you, anything, a tragedy or an obstacle, anything.
Look ahead and go straight on, because someone will always be there who supports you to be winning.
I feel like a very lucky and very successful woman.
No children for me, and I have five. Same thing for Pia: she worked 15 years with Vasco Rossi.
After 15 years, the music icon said hello because she did accept no compromise.
And she is making it, and she is following her heart, her passion which is writing songs, writing music and sharing them with people passing even difficult times.
We are carrying messages that are the ones that get to your heart and really bring you to light - we light sparks.
Then people go home, and it's up to us to feed the spark.
We give messages that can also be those of medical attention, right?
Bobo went to play [and] he was fine. He never came home.
Medical examination? Well, he did many.
I found out, however,
he felt bad in the field.
Being brave in organ donation - Pia, unfortunately, lost her a sister suffering from Down's syndrome, a while ago.
And Mom, the family decided to donate the liver.
That no one could imagine that a little girl with Down's syndrome could do such a thing.
Everything can be done: it's just having the courage.
The courage to look at each other, the courage to go ahead and the courage to say: I really can do it.
And if you manage to get in and make the most out of what you have - I don't want to be the one who says: I look at the sun, what a wonder. the lawn, the moon.
And all this is beautiful, but it really is beautiful because so many situations and so many times we go - this trip is like going by train.
Let's slow down, start looking at the landscape.
Let's start to share also, with the people next to us, a hug, a kiss, a I love you
It can make the day to you and mainly to the person next to you.
So it's something that, in here, silence speaks.
And listen to each other.
Thank you so much, good life to everyone.
(Applause)
Utopia or reality?
An achievable, concrete, feasible objective or pure madness in a world increasingly determined by consumerist logics of waste, of materials, of disposables?
Well I think the definition is possible.
Not only it's possible, but rather it is necessary and fundamental for building a sustainable future.
Indeed to build a possible future.
Because there is no possible future without sustainability.
Let's begin this short journey by two images, two charts.
The first chart tells us a very simple and worrying data at the same time: the consumption of materials per year on Earth.
Over the last 100 years and for the next thirty.
If today we are around 85 giga-tons per year, within the next 30 years, in this trend, we will reach 170 giga-tons per year.
Too bad that, scientists tell us, we will not have them on our planet
and we do not have a spare planet.
So we have to understand how we can reconcile the physical limits of our planet with a different model of development.
The other chart tells us another equally important thing: it is the trend of climate-changing emissions, CO2 in the atmosphere in recent years.
A growing trend that has led to climate change, scientists tell us, for about a degree on our planet.
So it's a bit like we have a fever around 37.
The Paris agreement states that we must stay within two degrees, the maximum of climate change,
otherwise for humanity it will be very very dangerous and difficult to continue living on this planet.
Better to keep us within the half grade, so this is the trend we have to give to our climate-altering emissions in the atmosphere if we want to stay within two degrees, or better, because it is less risky, within the degree and a half.
If we do not want to get to scenarios that would be really disturbing.
Let's continue this journey in a beautiful Italian city.
Which of you has ever been to Venice?
Beautiful city.
This is a shame because Venice, like all the seaside cities, will be the first to pay the hardest and most devastating consequences of climate change, with the rise of the seas that will make it increasingly difficult to live in these cities.
But let's go to the other
sideof the world. On 11 March 2017, a landslide in Addis Ababa landfill generated 65 deaths, mostly women and children living around to collect materials, even our waste, and continue to give life to that material by extracting some more that value they still have.
Here this image can not but arouse two reflections:
one is a shock of responsibility that each of us has for every choice he makes.
And the other is the alternative.
The alternatives we need to grow.
The more we increase the responsibility of each of us the more we grow alternatives,
the more the alternatives grow and take strength, the more we can increase the collective responsibility.
And the alternatives exist.
And it is precisely Europe that is pushing us, it is pushing all European countries to move towards models of circular economy.
Because our economy, for the most part, is traditionally linear: To extract - To produce - To consume - To discard.
On the contrary, that waste must return as raw material in the substitution of the continuous extraction of raw materials, otherwise we will certainly have neither future nor sustainability.
But the Zero Waste strategy aims at even more ambitious objectives such as reducing waste production on our planet.
And, if until a few years ago, in fact, talking about Zero Waste seemed a utopia, a few months ago I had the honor of being the first international speaker of the first world congress of all the cities Zero Waste.
We found ourselves in Brasilia, the capital of Brazil, and there were cities from all over the world from Australia to Japan to Asia to Africa to the United States.
And there we were aware that we are a movement that is growing in the world and that increasingly builds results and advantages.
One of the largest cities in the world that has made this choice is San Francisco.
Over one million inhabitants have started recycling policies for over 80% of their waste and this has avoided the construction of a new landfill.
It has created tens of thousands of jobs through recycling policies reusing recycled basic materials.
And in 2007, seeing precisely the example of San Francisco, we also wanted to bring it to Italy, because no community had made this choice yet, this path
And we started from Capannori, a town of about 50 thousand inhabitants, in the province of Lucca, to want to build this new challenge
and many told us that it was a utopia that it was impossible to get zero waste.
But we have acted on these two levers: responsibility and alternatives in a process of citizen involvement and in building alternatives. Alternatives that feed responsibility.
So we started from the first fundamental step that is to eliminate the collection systems that feed irresponsibility, such as the container collection systems.
it seemed to break a religion to remove the bins from the street. 10/12 years ago Today, fortunately, it is a policy that more and more cities applied in Italy and in the world - instead to organize home collection systems that feed a responsibility. So that everyone is forced to take their own scraps, ask themselves and increase their responsibilities.
it is precisely through these policies that we have built this incredible result that seemed impossible.
You see in this very simple graph, red is everything that was for disposal eleven years ago.
And it is gradually tapering more and more towards zero thanks to two fundamental factors: the increase in separate waste collection and the reduction of waste production.
Where, the central element, was the responsibility of the citizens we have nurtured through, precisely, this simple and banal mechanism that is the need to have to ask where every scrap goes.
And then we start to ask why we make so many discards.
We begin to see them and then understand the problem.
But, we said, it is also important to reduce waste
and the first things we can do, for those who have the possibility, is to act on the organic.
The workforce is about 40% of urban waste, it is the one that creates major problems when it ends up in landfills, which creates leachate, creates many environmental problems.
So who has the opportunity to do home composting, and therefore to treat waste at home, in the garden, it's the first step to take.
So we do not travel that material by truck and give back to earth the important nutritious elements that we have removed to make crops.
So it's the first basic thing to do to build environmental sustainability.
But those who live in a city do not have the chance to do it, so there are more and more industrial facilities that allow us to transform our potato peels, not only into quality compost that is used in agriculture, but also to replace biomethane fuel, as this facility that is in Pinerolo in Piedmont and where biomethane is produced, which feeds those vehicles that make the collection of waste in that area.
So a perfect model of circular economy that is increasingly replicating in many Italian cities.
But, after the organic, we move to recyclable, dried material: paper, plastic, metals.
On each of these industrial chains we could make a very long report and discover marvelous journeys through the recycling policies they generate, through much simpler systems than disposal, they generate much more employment.
It is estimated that the economic and occupational performance of recycling plants is about 4/5 times more compared to disposal plants.
But we have said that Zero Waste does not only aim to make this first step, that is to start recycling all the materials, but has the ambition of wanting to reduce the production of waste too.
And it is not true that reducing the production of waste is synonymous with deprivation because, instead, in many projects, to reduce waste has been combined with the reduction of waste to economic development.
For example this trivial project of a company in Piedmont. It has created two brands of eco-catering and eco-hotels. We have recognized them as restaurants and hotels that have agreed to implement seven out of ten possible actions,at least seven, to reduce environmental impacts and waste production in their commercial activities.
This gave them publicity, so he made them known and benefited from them in economic terms.
The environment has benefited because they have produced less waste. the local economy has also benefited because the disposal started: less waste
and therefore citizens have also had a benefit, so everyone has won.
Like these, there are tens of hundreds of projects that can be implemented in the field of waste reduction to combine all types of social, environmental, economic and employment benefits.
The other fundamental step, again to reduce waste, is to lengthen their life cycle and for example through reuse centers.
A few years ago I visited this spectacular place which is the largest European center of reuse.
The city of Gothenburg built it by investing 4 million euros
and 30 people work in this space.
Every day 200 citizens go, buy anything from a door, a window, a sanitary, or an electrical appliance.
And in this way, after having given work to more than 30 people every year, the profits of the company are paid to the Municipality.
On average, between 700 and 800 thousand euros a year, so a rather profitable investment for the municipality.
Here, the same thing we did in Capannori, right within the Zero Waste strategy, with a center called Daccapo.
It returns to give life to all the materials that are conferred and gives work to 7 people.
And this is, in fact, a model that is increasingly replicating in many many cities around Italy.
But let's move on to a further step of the Zero Waste strategy, the punctual pricing.
What does it mean?
It means applying that trivial concept that we have always used to think about when we consume water, and therefore we pay it according to how much we consume or light according to how much we consume.
Why should it not be like that for waste too?
Precisely to increase responsibility against the waste that the bill of water or light encourages us to do.
And so, starting from this concept, we made an innovation a few years ago in Capannori. For the first time in Italy a feed has been inserted, a microscopic microchip, which, when applied in the bag or even in the sling, inserts the data of the user that is registered for each transfer of undifferentiated material that a person makes.
And this allows to understand how many times in each year the citizen has given his own discards.
This project was nominated for a competition called Innovation In Politics and, thanks also to all my collaborators, we won the competition as the first and only experiment in Italy of this project.
I want to show you the video they did just to summarize our project.
Here,
if with responsibly sorted house
collection routes
we get to 70 to 80% of the start
of recycling materials,
with the punctual pricing we can even reach 90/95%.
We did it in Capannori and now I'm doing it in many other communities, following it in this simple, trivial implementation process that proves a very simple thing: that there are no distances that can not allow these processes to be carried out because there are processessimilar to the South, in Central and Northern Italy, in small, large and medium-sized cities.
So the will to want to achieve these goals is enough.
But it is not that that 10%, that 5%, that 15% that is, that still goes to disposal must continue to go to waste disposal indefinitely. because as well as the raw materials market is constantly growing in cost, because raw materials are increasingly scarce, the same for the industries, even the big multinationals, are investing more and more to go to collect the value of our waste.
It is no coincidence that our discards are defined as "urban mines".
And it is no coincidence that precisely in Italy, a large multinational, has created this facility in the province of Treviso.
This facility that recycles for the first time in the world
the diapers, diapers of our children who, after an initial sterilization phase, are recycled through the extraction of cellulose and plastic materials that go into the paper supply chains and the recovery of plastic materials.
And the same happens to this material.
It's called palper, it's the waste from the paper reciclyng process.
They are the covers, perhaps, of the notebooks of our children that end up by mistake in the separate collection or are those transparent labels in the envelopes we receive at home, which are therefore a foreign matter in the collection and end up disposal.
But, if until a few months ago they only ended up with disposal, for a few weeks thanks to a very important innovationof a local company and an important European funding, this material goes to recycling
and therefore a large part of this material is intercepted and both is launching an industrial chain to turn it into a pallet that is then used by other companies for internal logistics.
Even though we saw that until a few years ago they told us that it was utopia, it was impossible, they were unattainable goals.
Today we have seen that instead there are more and more advantages that are built from the feasibility of these projects.
We are also getting some satisfaction, because when we receive these confirmations, these institutional acknowledgments - this is the European Secretary for Environment, Vella, that a couple of years ago in a conference in which we illustrated the best Italian experiences in waste management, said that these are the inspiring principles of the circular economy package on which Europe is also working to push all European countries towards sustainability, recycling and waste reduction.
I want to leave you with a beautiful image that is the Sacra di San Michele in Piedmont.
What does this image tell us?
That climbing up there is a terrifying, very tiring climb,
but this is what it tells us to get up there, that you see a wonderful landscape
and every time you go there, you understand that it was worth it.
But we also understand another thing, that from that beautiful landscape you can see eve nhigher goals and objectives to reach.
It's beautiful. And the beautiful thing is not so much to reach them, as the path we can take to reach them. As we understand, we know and discover the path we take to try to reach it.
I have children.
And when I look at them I want to be sure I have done everything possible and the impossible avoiding to leave them a disaster as instead unfortunately we often see and discover that we are doing to our planet.
I wish everyone a good job.
Thank you.
(Applausi)
Hi everyone.
My name is Ezio, and today I will talk to you about connections: connections between new subjects and new learning objects; connections that come from listening
to the new generations, that I am lucky enough to live every day at work and in the family.
What is my job?
I work for a leading company in the field of automation and robotics.
And what is my duty?
I am the director of the Academy, I am responsible for the training of 9,000 people in the world and, at the same time, I am responsible for opening the doors of the company to the youngsters, to the outside.
You see me in a white shirt in the closing ceremony of an executive master, but it is a listening experience that I also have the lucky chance to live every day in the family.
I have four children, alas, all males. I would have liked a female so much and I'm willing to trade one of my sons, if someone had a daughter to give me.
I have three children: Francesco, Giacomo, Filippo and little Tesfa, adopted from Ethiopia; you see him in my arms.
My kids, like all young people today, attend an educational system that struggles to face the challenge of knowledge related to technological evolution
and related to how the fourth industrial revolution is totally changing our personal and professional life.
Yes, the new technologies of artificial intelligence, additive manufacturing, the Internet of Things and collaborative robotics are really changing our experience
and above all the way companies design, produce and distribute their products and services.
To work with these technologies, you need new skills, new mentalities, new jobs;
then, as I wrote in my book, a new training perimeter is needed, which connects new subjects - young people, schools, universities and companies - and also knows how to connect new learning objects: technology, school subjects and work culture.
The story I will tell you today is a story about how business Academies are promoting these connections and how, thanks to innovative artifacts, they are succeeding in creating the skills of the future in young people.
So let's start with the connections between new learning subjects.
Some initiatives, which are expressions of these connections, are those destined for young talents and, why not, also for professionals.
Some examples:
the robotics driving license. In addition to language certification and computer certification, a wonderful initiative carried out by the business academies together with the teachers, is this: a project to offer young people the skills to use and program an industrial robot.
There already are 5,000 young people in Italy who have tried this certification;
when they are 18 they will be able to work in a factory with 1,000 robots and guide and program them.
Another initiative resulting from these connections is a Summer School, "Project and People Management".
Yes, in this technological evolution we have to manage projects and to be very good at managing people.
Ten days in Shanghai, Detroit or Belo Horizonte to acquire these methods and tools.
Still, is it all made by whom?
By a Business Academy together with the university.
But the initiative we are mostly proud of is a degree course, called "Innovation and Technology Management".
What’s new about it?
Seated in the same counter, you can find engineers, physics graduates, psychologists, sociologists, anthropologists; and thy get a cross-disciplinary degree about connections.
Because this fourth industrial revolution must not only be guided by computer scientists and engineers, who I really love, but also by psychologists, sociologists, anthropologists, human experts,
so that they can take all the opportunities of the technological evolution, trying to mitigate risks.
Here are some initiatives for talents. But also initiatives for youngsters who already work in companies.
"4.0 Manufacturing" Master program is now seven years old, run by the Institute of Technology and by a Business Academy:
kids go to the company, their master is paid from day one, every month, three weeks of work and one of the master, not only provided by the teachers but from the company's Subject Matter Experts.
And one last initiative, another expression of these connections, is an executive master where more than 40 managers from all over Europe acquire the technological and managerial skills to lead this transformation.
Here's the new training perimeter, where kids are not passive users of other people's services, but are protagonists of innovation.
Where school, university do not settle themselves with providing theories and models but are part of the true reality of the companies, and give their important and precious contribution;
it's a new training perimeter where the company does not give up its ambition to create and spread culture and transfer it to the new generations, with that typical DNA of the corporate identity: learning by doing and by connecting to others.
Now I would like to tell you about some other connections between learning objects, school subjects, technology and business culture.
It all started when we began to develop this robot: we were closed in a laboratory together with many young people and many experts,
and, while we were there, we said Well, let's follow these three principles: we would like this robot to be modular, open - it was an open innovation - and easy to use.
While we were so busy working in this laboratory, in this garage, we went to visit a business Academy that had a clever idea. It started a project called "Physics on a motorcycle":
they said, let's use motorcycles to attract young people and teach them how much physics is in a motorcycle.
And then we thought about our generation, the one of the past millennium, because motorcycles were a symbol of our generation; many of my friends and I grew up with three basic milestones: fourteen, sixteen and eighteen years old.
These are the steps that have marked our lives.
At 14, we were waiting
for the monocilinder scooter: having the Ciao or the Sì was a fundamental goal.
After that goal, at sixteen, we wanted the motorcycle license.
I had a Cagiva Elephant.
Prehistoric, now.
And finally, at 18, the driving license, the car.
On this generational aspect, I want to tell you an anecdote about my family.
My wife and I were ready to face the demands of our children related to these three steps; so when Francesco was 14, we were prepared to oppose the reasons why we would never buy him a moped.
We made a list, like Lorenzo's, and got ready: "Francesco, we will never buy it because of this and that",
we were ready.
At 14, Francesco didn't ask us anything.
We said: "Francesco is smart, he wants a motorcycle. Let's wait two years.
We keep the list, we have saved it in a special folder". And when he was 16, we were ready.
Francesco did not ask us anything, not even at the age of 16.
We said, man, he wants a car as big as this stage.
When he was 18, nothing happened.
We had to force him to the driving school to avoid dealing on Saturday nights with logistics services.
What does this story tell us?
That new generations are attracted to values and objects that are obviously different from ours.
They are attracted to tablets and phones. In the evening, at my home, everyone stares at his own tablet or phone, scrolling the feed of Facebook or Instagram.
This is the present, and who knows about the future.
And so while we were developing that robot, we said: "Aren't robots even more appealing than a motorcycle, to attract the new generations? "
And so we went on and said, Let's use it, not only to code or to do robotic stuff but to do Math, Physics, and the STEM subjects that are fundamental for the future".
We created what we call a Robo-Experience: a series of experiences, centered around the robot that connect young people to the robot,
such as workshops, centres, events and programs, This short video shows you one of these initiatives.
[e.Do LEARNING CENTER - An innovative Educational Environment] [TO LEARN: School subjects, Soft skills and Industrial Culture with Robotics] [4 FOCUSES: STEM, Robotics, Soft Skills, Working Culture] [INSPIRE] [PLAY] [LEARN] [LESSON PLAN] [WELCOME / INTRODUCTION TO ROBOTICS/ SCHOOL SUBJECTS THROUGH e.Do / EVALUATION] [WELCOME] [INTRODUCTION TO ROBOTICS] [INTRODUCTION TO ROBOTICS - PLAY WITH e.DO] [SCHOOL SUBJECTS THROUGH e.DO - APPLY SCHOOL SUBJECTS] [SCHOOL SUBJECTS THROUGH e.DO - USE e.DO] [EVALUATION] Here, this is an example of one of these center attended by more than 10,000 people. But our dream is different.
Our dream is to place a robot in every school, to help innovate teaching and help new generations to be protagonists of the future.
The magic is not a robot but our children;
it is a teacher, a facilitator, a robot, simple wooden objects that the robot moves, and it moves them in the reality, not in a virtual screen, before which our children already spend too much time.
And then there's a tablet.
Yes, this robot is easy to use.
Robotics is experiencing today what PCs went through in the 1980s.
In the 80s, you may recall, only computer experts could use the Z Spectrum or the Commodore 64.
Computers, today, are in everyone's house.
Well, the same thing is happening to the robots:
up to now, guiding them was a programmer's job; today even our children, Andrea, Alessia, Federico, can use it without taking any lessons.
Yes, there is this interaction, which is the same we have in our mobile phones, this interface that allows you to control and move it with just one tap. But there is also a further evolution: it can be programmed with Blockly,
that thought and that logical-computational language that helps put things in order.
Then, of course, if computer scientists want to play, they have many chances to.
What kind of activities are offered?
As you saw in the video, the kids first assemble it
using their own manual skills.
And then? And then, they play:
learning by doing something is so pedagogically important -
but "andragogically", also,
Then we face and solve problems that involve the use of mathematics and physics.
Math?
Some examples: the sum, the subtraction, the equivalences, the geometric figures that children in elementary school, like Alessia, study every day at school; well, it's even more fun with e.Do, and even more effective. But there are something even for the older ones,
like Federico: analytical geometry, first and second degree equations that's what they are for!
Not only to solve boring exercises, but to make children learn how to move a robot in the room.
Therefore, we have lived many experiences, we have understood some things. First, this robot promotes participation and inclusion:
even children with dyslexia, or ADHD, could use it.
Even my little Tesfa, with an extra chromosome and disabled, is very good at using this robot, and that's important.
It then promotes the inclusion, the application of subjects to reality, and, above all, spreads the culture of work, which is so precious: working in team, keeping tidy, solving problems, being creative, having critical thinking. And also, it connects the cognitive, manual and emotional dimensions.
I would like to wrap up with a quote of Walt Disney: [If you can dream it, you can do it].
Here is our dream: to connect new subjects and new learning objects. And above all, listen to young people.
I decided to close my speech with a photo of my son Tesfa while listening to J-Ax:
we must not forget, they are the protagonists of the future.
(Applause)
I'm here to share with you an experiment I started five years ago.
I sat down in front of my computer and I sent a mail to all my contacts in which I essentially asked them to do three things. First: stop doing what they were doing. Second: look around. Third: choose an object.
The object had to be chosen based on a principle of equality; the chosen object should trigger feelings of neither superiority nor inferiority. On the contrary, it should be perceived as something close, gentle and friendly - in short, an object on a par with the relevant person.
To explain more clearly: I, personally, could not consider a Latin dictionary as on a par with me, because I do not speak in Latin, I do not use it despite having learned it at school. On the other hand, I can spontaneously choose and feel comfortable with, that is, be on a par with, an espresso maker. Actually, I believe I can have
a dialogue with an espresso maker.
The mail also included an invitation to come to my studio with the object of their choice, and share with me the rationale of their choice.
I also explained that while they did so, I would be taking photos.
Well, 24 people accepted my invitation.
They came, some more committed, others more hesitant, yet others more curious, each with their chosen object.
Take for instance, Alberto.
He chose a clothes peg, and here he is talking about self-respect.
Gabriella, who is fond of sports cars, chose a model car in order to challenge it, and here she's speaking about the joy of slowness.
Andrea, for example, is wondering what he is doing with an aubergine in his hands.
But he knows he's chosen the right object!
Loris, on the other hand, had chosen a hand-made megaphone. He looks at it, examines it, and here is listening to his deep voice.
And there's Chiara, who loves life, and, with her shoe, talks about love and family.
By observing all these pictures I started getting evidence that I was right. An object, just because it has been chosen, offers something in return to the person who has chosen it.
Therefore, I concluded, choice is an act of communication.
But then I thought it was not conclusive because I already knew those people.
So, I decided to make a project out of this experiment. I called it 'On a Par Conversation'. I introduced it in different social contexts, such as companies, universities and theatres.
Here's the result I got in some companies.
I met over a hundred people doing different jobs: office boys, white-collar workers, blue-collar workers, CEO's.
So, I have become sure of the value of this concept.
In order to let you better understand what dynamics are in place when we choose an object on a par, I'll tell you about this young man.
I was at a printing house and he was one of the print workers. - Actually he came with the tools he uses for his job, spatulas for spreading ink.
In printing, there are four colours, cyan, magenta, yellow and black, and the spatulas have the same colours -
While I was inside the factory waiting for the next person with an object, this young man came in with a detached attitude. He said to me, 'You know, I have not read many books in my life, but I know something you do not know.
I know what the colour of dawn is - because I see it every morning as I get up very early to come to work.
No sunrise is ever the same.
There may just be a small difference in shade but it's never the same colour.
I too have different shades.'
He starting using his spatulas.
'I have the red colour, which represents a part of me I hold back, the anger I do not express. At the same time I also have my yellow-orange part, the most friendly part of mine, the one that likes others' company. Then I have the blue colour, my most meditative, but also most melancholic part, the thinking one however. And then I have my black part. It is the part of me which scares me, that which resides in shadow.
You will realize, putting together all these shades is not easy for me.' This is how he made a speech about character on a par with his spatulas.
So you can understand that in the moment it's made, choice is important - from the moment my feelings are drawn towards an object up until the moment I choose and decide that object is the right one for me and then act accordingly, talk about it, justify it, take pictures of it. All this together feeds back an amazing self-portrait.
In other words, to the extent that we make a choice according to the deepest part of us with respect to our character, we are able to let our most beautiful and effective part emerge.
Why did I do all this?
Because five years ago I had to address what was for me a very delicate decision: Whether to abandon my profession as an artist, for financial reasons, - I'd lost my most important contracts and had no work - or to persist despite the darkest outlook.
While immersed in these thoughts, directing my gaze to a horizon I could not see, I was holding a flower in my hands.
And the flower started talking to me.
You can just imagine my state of mind: I was talking to flowers, I was feeling lost.
But guess what? The flower replied to me! Literally.
The flower told me something interesting: 'I'm a gerbera, I'm red and that's fine with me, I'm happy with myself.
Should I become a tulip?'
I listened to the flower, I decided to follow my own inclinations, I decided to persist. And this gave me the strength to climb back up the hill, and, as you might have guessed, inspired the project I'm talking about, which has become part of my work.
Therefore I confirm my initial intuition. Choosing in full alignment with our deepest nature makes our most active and successful part emerge. I strongly invite you to do the same.
Try now, later, tomorrow.
Think about an object, choose it, listen to it, ask yourself why you chose it.
The most important thing is though, is that you choose it on a par level.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good evening, thank you for coming.
I have to admit, I am a bit afraid: after the empathetic dolphin, 18 minutes of philosophy aren’t a piece of cake.
Resist the impulse to leave, I promise that it will be interesting.
Before coming here, I was discussing about the talk with a friend and I told her the title.
“Artificial intelligence and intelligent design”. And she said to me, where is empathy in there?
Two days later, we were still arguing about the relevance of empathy.
I promise that in 18 minutes we should make it; but if I’m good enough, empathy will be one of the last words that I will say in this talk, because it will be the answer to the question: if machines are intelligent, what does it mean to be human?
A bit trivial, deceptively simple question.
Actually, the interesting thing about this question, that is also in the event's description, is that it’s biased, it’s assuming that machines are indeed intelligent, and if so, they are as intelligent as human beings.
Actually, the “if” in this question has an essential role: we have to ask ourselves if machines are really intelligent, and if yes, what kind of intelligence do they have.
I will start this talk by telling you what AI is; understanding what promises and perils this technology brings about, what should we take or avoid.
And understand how these risks and opportunities impact human beings today.
When we talk about artificial intelligence people have very different opinions, we have a really broad range.
On one side, those who say: "Oh no, AI is great, it will allow us to overcome biological limitations, we will be able to live forever, be immortal, do something called “mind uploading”: transferring all the information we have in our brains upload them onto a machine, that will be so powerful that it will become an avatar: us, without our bodies.
We will be really powerful, extremely intelligent, eternal.'
On the other side of the spectrum are the ones who say "Say no to artificial intelligence: machines will always be more powerful, finely crafted, and will be able to define the rules of their design and will then become so powerful, so finely crafted that human beings will become redundant, they will exterminate, imprison and enslave us’.
So, the singularity, the “transhumanism”, and the science fiction. Nothing to do with research, nothing to do with science.
If you happen to hear someone talk about one of these two things, it's a nice book, a nice discussion at the pub on a Friday night, but nothing to do with the reality in front of us.
Artificial intelligence – this is an academic definition, but it’s the only academic thing in this talk, so bear with me -- is "A resource of autonomous agents able to interact and learn and that can be used to perform tasks that would otherwise need human intelligence to be successfully performed".
We wrote this in a paper that we published last year in "Science robotics".
Two things are important in this definition: first, what type of intelligence machines do have.
Machines aren’t intelligent: they don’t have ideas, intuitions, empathy, awareness, conscience, no intuitions like the ones we have when we do even the simplest things.
Machines just behave as if they were intelligent.
Regulating the temperature here based on outside temperature, to how the people inside the theatre react; if I did it, you would think, "She's intelligent, she knew we were cold and turned up the thermostat."
Machines do the same thing, achieves the same goal, but it's not intelligent.
It just behaves in a way we would deem intelligent if a human did the same.
The other important aspect is that AI allows us to have autonomous agents, that are capable to interact and learn while interacting with the environment.
In the history of humanity we had automatic machines: the clock we carry on our wrist. or some types of car.
But they never were autonomous:
and it’s this autonomy, not the one of human beings, that carries problems, risks, but also opportunities of AI.
I'll also talk about the risks, but what I really want to talk about positive things, opportunities.
The first risk has to do with profiling: you've heard about it today from the speakers before me. It’s what allows Google to give us information similar to the one we are looking for when we open the page.
I'm from Naples, if I look for a restaurant in London the first thing I see are pizzerias. Don't show me a sushi restaurant, I could have a breakdown.
Google knows it, and acts accordingly.
Profiling is a good thing, as long as it doesn’t become an engine for discrimination, when it perpetuates prejudices that are already in society and artificial intelligence facilitates.
Another example linked to Google: some years ago a case became famous, where Google’s algorithm would show ads for management positions more often to men than to women.
This is a case of discrimination, this is a risk we have to avoid, because in a society where artificial intelligence is more and more distributed, we can’t afford to facilitate and distribute and multiply these types of discriminations.
The second problem has to do with the nature of this technology.
Very often, AI is defined as a black box: we have the inputs, we design the technology, but we can’t predict the outputs.
We could do so just by having the same calculation ability, but it’s hard.
And why is that important?
It’s important because we trust AI, we delegate to AI very important tasks: cancer diagnosis, deciding who we should hire from our next interview, where to have dinner on a Friday night.
But in reality, we trust these machines that we don’t understand and which behavior we can’t predict – this is also an important aspect, a lot of research focuses on this topic.
And the other aspect is about the use of AI and the effects on human beings.
We heard about the fourth revolution earlier.
A fourth industrial revolution, a sentence often repeated, and it’s used to indicate that we've become a digital society.
Actually, the fourth industrial revolution isn’t really about finding our friends on Facebook or that we buy books on Amazon, it’s more about our fridge doing groceries on Amazon by itself, based on what our tastes are, inferred by our comments on Facebook. It's this correlation.
AI facilitates this correlation, AI is an invisible facilitator that mediates between the interactions, our interactions with the environment; and in mediating them, it is actually guiding us.
To what extent is this guidance at odds with our self-determination, with our ability to choose?
My job is partially making sure that we use AI in support of our determination, not at the expenses of it.
I already told you: these are risks, but also opportunities.
The great advantage of AI is that it allows us to delegate critical tasks to machines that are tedious, boring, sometimes dangerous, and free up our time.
In the UK we all have gardens, in the summer we'd like to throw parties but we never get to Saturday wanting to throw one, because we spent all day mowing the lawn.
And some time ago I moved house, I spent a weekend assembling Ikea furniture, fights I can’t even describe, there is no stable chair in my house.
When I found out that there were robots guided by AI that could assemble Ikea furniture by themselves, I had a moment of unspeakable emotion.
Just as driving in traffic areas, being able to read or interact, instead of spending time driving, and so on.
In a society where AI will be distributed "horizontally" - it will be everywhere, in our pocket, in our phone, in our toaster, in our thermostat, in the hospital, at school, in the office - the task is making sure that more AI means more “human flourishing”, that is, the capacity of developing and realizing our existential projects, our human projects, both on an individual and on a social level.
In 2015 the UN defined what we call "Sustainable development goals" 17 challenges to be reached successfully within 2030, and that will allow humankind to live better, to do better, to be better.
And they span from research in the biomedical field to healthcare, environment protection, gender equality, diversity.
We have found out that artificial intelligence can help facilitate the development of all these objectives.
There are AI applications that allow farmers in India to understand when sow to maximize the harvest based on climate conditions.
We have AI applications that ease plastic collection from the oceans, one of the environmental tragedies that are most felt nowadays.
We know that by 2050 there will be 131 million people with Alzheimer’s, a disease we can’t cure, that’s hard to diagnose, and we know that AI allows us to diagnose it six years earlier, therefore limiting its impact because we can treat it earlier.
So what about all these risks and opportunities, how do they affect us, and afflict us as human beings?
The answer I usually give is this one. It’s a Leonardo da Vinci quote that says: "I have been impressed by the urgency of doing.
Knowing isn’t enough, we need to act.
Having great intentions isn’t enough, we need to do".
And in front of the potentials of AI one really wants to say, let’s take advantage of it, let’s take this chance and change the world.
In a superficial reading, it can be hotught of as one of those Silicon Valley mottos.
A few years ago, in San Francisco, they would have said “fail often, fail fast”: don’t worry if you fail, repeat the mistake, the important is that you bounce back, try again and again. Sooner or later, you will succeed.
In the process we had Cambridge Analytica, we had all the scandals that we heard, so it’s actually not really a good idea.
Leonardo isn’t telling us "fail often, fail fast".
Leonardo is a man of the Renaissance, a poetic man, his actions follow his thoughts, using the tool of today in order to change tomorrow.
Acting means designing and actually he’s telling us so, he’s telling us, knowledge is not enough:
it’s necessary but it’s not sufficient.
Good intentions are useful, but not enough; we need to do more, in addition.
So what good intentions, what knowledge are needed today in order to do what Leonardo would mean us to do, designing the future?
And here there is a tiny bit more of philosophy.
AI is one of the technologies maybe the most advanced technology of the digital, and the digital one is a revolutionary technology, because it allows us to do new things, and in so doing, it also changes the reality around us, It's a transformative technology.
With digital we do new things, we could read a book while we drive.
But it changes the things we understand too.
Think of the concept of war, that is one of the fields I work in.
In the history of humankind, war meant violence, destruction, death.
Nowadays we talk about "cyber conflicts" An expression of strength, of aggressiveness; but are completely detached from the concept of violence.
We can fight a cyber war without killing anybody, or destroying anything, while still reaching the same goal.
A concept has changed: the concept of war, tied as it was to strength exhibition and violence shifted to a concept where violence is detached from strength.
This transformation of the environment, of our interaction, of our comprehension brought about by digital and AI are a leverage, the element over which we need to push in order to trigger the “action” suggested by Leonardo.
What’s the nature, or the value, of the human being in the digital society, in a society where machines behave as if they were intelligent?
And we can delegate to AI the computational power: artificial intelligence can gather information, process information, make correlations in a quicker, more accurate way than any other intelligence we know.
Think of cancer diagnosis: AI can recognize a cancer from a CT scan without ever getting tired, without ever forgetting any of the CT scans it has seen, without a moment of stress, of fear.
It’s super-efficient - but this is the delegation.
AI is a tool, a tool we need to use to change the reality in which we live.
We need to use AI to design society: our society, but also the ones we call “post-AI” societies, those societies where AI won’t be a transformative, revolutionary technology anymore, but will be taken for granted, a bit like electric power today.
We need to use AI to make sure we can develop societies that are tolerant, fair, democratic, pluralist.
The order of the words isn’t accidental: tolerance is a fundamental value in this context.
So if we look at intelligent design, why is that important?
Because intelligent design, Leonardo-wise intelligent the way the future should be designed is what makes us human and it’s what we do and AI doesn’t.
And we have this responsibility, because it’s about giving shape to the reality that will come after our children's one.
I will leave you with a quote from Winston Churchill, who was a quite cynical man, but he saw clearly in the future. He once said:
"We shape our buildings and then, afterwards, our buildings shape us". [She translates into Italian]
This is what architecture tells us.
This sentence is true for architecture, even more so for the digital, and even more so for AI.
We design AI, and we need to know that this technology has then a direct and very strong impact on us as people, as society, as environment.
We need then to be sure to design AI in a way that it allows us to take the shape we want, a shape where empathy is still a fundamental value, an aspect to be cherished, protected, and distributed.
I leave you with this. Thank you.
(Applause)
Imagine you are in a village that is only accessible by a steep and winding road.
Imagine being in this village which has a single bell tower.
It is isolated, there are 750 inhabitants.
Where you can smell the meadows, hear the rustle of the trees and you can live a peaceful and undisturbed life.
My family is from there. We live in the same house since 1400, like many other families from Esino.
This is where the story takes place.
One day, my cousin Lolanda Pensa, also from the village, an active Wikipedia contributor, contacted me to say: Pietro, let's organize the World Wikipedia Convention in Esino.
I was silent for moment - Sorry, what are you talking about?
It's the convention they hold every year in different cities around the world where all Wikipedians meet and talk Where did they meet so far?
In London, Hong Kong, Washington And now ... we're going to have it ... in Esino?
(Laughter) Sure!
A crazy idea: I'm up for it, I'm on the team.
Tell me what I have to do.
Of course, I have to tell you some facts.
I work as a freelancer, I do projects, I work in construction.
I'm a builder.
And so I do what I can do, and I start working.
The concept was to plan low-cost projects because in our application we wanted to show that it could be held anywhere, a big conference, at a very low cost.
So I had to used my engineering skills to find the best solutions to restructure our existing assets.
And so I did.
I drew up plans for a dozen projects and sent them to the municipal administration.
The municipal administration approved them and filed them away in the archive.
They put them in the archive because what was missing, in 2013, when I delivered them, was the will to do anything.
This will was missing, and that turned into complete political apathy no will to have new ideas or improve their village.
So much so that in 2014 something very serious happened: nobody in the village wanted to work in the administration.
The village had become ungoverned.
"Paese commissariato" is a phrase you hear on TV when they are talking about the mafia, when there are criminals on the loose.
No, if was just that people didn't want to show up.
Basically a lifeless village.
In the meantime, however, the candidacy went ahead.
Lolanda was working hard, because basically we had to put together a plan that would get the congress delegates from the other side of the world to Esino Lario by trains, by bus, on planes.
My wife would be at the airport, collecting them, getting them all together, and loading them onto a bus which would take them to Esino where they would be accommodated.
This plan had to be presented in America. Slowly the selections took place and after Italy, Europe, we finally ended up in America.
In America, at the Wikipedia Foundation, they asked, "But does Esino really exist?"
And they came to the village.
They came to find out if we actually existed.
They noticed we were there, real flesh and bone people, and we were working on the project.
But then there was a problem.
We had a prefectural commissioner who could only handle ordinary stuff.
The projects I had presented were all obviously out of the ordinary investment projects.
They were not expensive, but someone had to finance them.
At that point, what came to mind?
We made a list of 30, 31 items.

Our civic list.
Stella Alpina, obviously, because we are in the mountains 1,000 meters above Lake Como at the foot of the Grigna, 2,400 meters.
I seriously suggest you visit them both.
We make this list, I chose the short straw and suddenly found myself mayor.
(Laughter) You may laugh.
(Applause) But I had never even taken part in a city council, in my whole life because it would cause me a rash.
Imagine me being mayor.
Well, the fact is, the situation was quite worrying.
Nando Della Chiesa wrote an article on good news from Italy and compared me to a modern day Asterix who, in his Gallic village, took on endless challenges.
Yes, but if you know Asterix, you'll know he had a magic potion.
I had no magic potion.
But I was not alone.
I was not alone because people began to turn up of their own volition.
And all the associations - in Esino there are 15 voluntary associations.
It seems impossible, in a village of 750 residents!
15 voluntary associations is lot.
And the desire to do this thing grew.
We put our heads together and finished all the projects we had to finish, and even more.
Because local businesses had got on board they drew up a lot of small contracts to assign everyone, in turn, with a task to do.
So much so, that every business tried to out do the others and above all because: First, we were working for our village; and second. we had to finish on time.
We had so much to do.
And just a year to do it.
And a year later - actually less, as we finished a month early, we had finished everything.
Everything was ready and they were coming.
All the delegates started arriving in the village.
It wasn't easy to accommodate them because in a village of 750 inhabitants, there were not enough beds for all of them.
The locals had opened up their homes.
In the plan, actually, someone who was, let's say, from New Zealand already knew that they were staying in Carlo Nasazzi's house or Giuseppe Barindelli's.
And this is what happened: he arrived by bus, Carlo Nasazzi came, picked up the suitcases
and took them to his house, happy ever after.
(Applause) Thank you.
The week unfolded in an extraordinary way.
There was a new crazy atmosphere in the village.
(Laughs) I mean, an international atmosphere that you could breath in everywhere.
One day, I was behind two old women who thought they were alone.
I heard them chatting and I heard them say, in the local dialect: "Let's speak in dialect, so they think we are foreigners too."
(Laughter) So, everyone just wanted to take part.
It was really wonderful, extraordinary.
In the end, the numbers were really impressive.
We had 1,300 delegates with friends, relatives, family plus journalists, plus the former villagers who said: "Let's go and see what's going on in Esino".
So, it was a real success, more we could ever have imagined.
All the TV news came, the newspapers.
In short, it was an extraordinary event.
When it was all over, nobody wanted to leave.
We had to just get rid of some of them because we couldn't cope with them any longer.
Finally, the Congress finished.
And we were left to wonder, what will the legacy of Wikimania be?
What did we get out of it?
Yes, it left us with infrastructure because we actually built a lot of things.
A cinema, which was reduced to rubble for the garbage truck, we had a boarded up museum that opened and is still open today. Many, many other things.
Everything was put in order.
After the event, we just kept going, because now we had momentum.
We set up wood-fired district heating, LED lights.
All those things that a village needs to make it a little more ecologically friendly.
And they work for us.
Then, there are the, let's call them material things from our transformation and therefore the legacy of this event, of this extraordinary happening.
Something that lasted a week and then finished.
What should remain is a change in people's attitude.
Finally, I also realised that the magic potion, mentioned in Nando's article, I actually did have it.
It was right in my hand.
It was participation.
The participation of everyone in the village.
They got involved in this event and they continued to trust in it and wanted to do it because they had become aware that they could achieve something extraordinary that could not be measured, as they had mentioned.
And it has led them to grow and go from a feeling of political euthanasia to a desire to do things and to take part and being active participants in their own future.
And so, in my little village I can tell you what worked and what can be replicated as a method to be applied to get things done, even on a very limited budget, or, hopefully, in politics, if someone will actually consider it.
The imagination, brilliance and the visionary idea of my cousin Iolanda who said: "Let's hold a world conference in Esino Lario".
Crazy. But Fine.
It's an idea, but then it took a lot of will power the will power to work on it every day and laser focus on the goal until you got the result you wanted. Participation, because the will of a single visionary is not enough and anyone who has to follow it, even if the idea is crazy, to really achieve something.
We needed to involve more and more people.
The planning was very important.
Because you have to produce real and achievable projects.
Our projects all had limited budgets and could be carried out with the limited business and human resources we had.
And then the magic potion: participation.
Being able to make sure that everyone could take part , and benefit from what they are doing.
But it's a personal benefit, not a financial one.
Because those who rented the rooms, didn't really rent them.
There was a grant of 20 euros per room for cleaning and things like that.
They haven't even gone to collect it, as it was a pleasure to host people in their home.
People took English classes throughout the year even the old women who spoke dialect.
Lots of funny thing happened, but that'll be the topic for another time.
And they welcomed them.
So these things, including the magic potion and participation, they can be replicated, they are possible.
And what has changed in Esino?
Nothing. It's still a village ...
the most beautiful in the world, obviously, because it's mine, in the middle of a valley, that is accessible only by a narrow road.
At the foot of the Grigna, above Lake Como.
The village hasn't has changed, but the people have changed.
People have changed.
Go to Esino and see there is a will to get things done, to do it again. A desire to get involved, which had disappeared.
That is the best thing.
And what can I tell you?
I'm an engineer, so I have to give you some numbers.
The numbers for this change, for this trust, this desire to get things done.
In 2017, births in the village tripled.
It wasn't Wikipedians.
(Laughter) (Applause) In 2018 - everyone thought there would be no more births in 2018.
Wrong.
In 2018, we recorded double the birth rate of the previous 10 years.
Need I say more?
Thank you.
(Applause)
I have to "break the ice".
And what can a teacher do, in order to break the ice, but start an oral test?
Are you afraid?
No, OK, this time I won't go ahead.
But I really want to break the ice doing a mini-survey in order to know you, and letting you know me.
Who does love Mathematics, please raise his/her hand.
I am almost at home.
Who was proficient in Mathematics, back then?
Eh, a little bit less.
Who does think, proficiency in math is something one is born with?
All right, I will keep this in mind.
Now I know something about you, I introduce myself.
I think that Mathematics is the foundation of a fair, just and democratic society.
For that reason, I took this beatiful sentence of Nobel Prize Malala, and I rephrased this way: "A male or female child, a teacher, a book, a pen - and Mathematics! - can change the world".
Who does agree with me to add Mathematics?
Oh, they decrease.
Oh yes, they decrease.
I often see this thing.
Why?
Because each of you would have thought: "Oh, what's math got to do with justice?
I had a stomachache, the night before the math test.
I did not sleep at night to prepare myself".
Well, I instead chose to study Mathematics and to become a math teacher because it was fundamental for me to provide with critical tools my students, both boys and girls, in order to change this world, to change it for the better.
And mathematics is essential to that.
What I want to do with you, today, is to let you understand it and bring you in my journey, in order to show you the link among mathematics, justice and fairness.
But I need you to come with me, trying to go beyond the horizon and to understand that we need a different vision of math and then a different model of mathematics.
I will do it, at least I will try to do it, following a typical math argument.
What mathematics does, first of all?
It analyzes the situation, and what is the situation analysis?
Let us go see what mathematics is.
I do not want to say it with my own words: it would be too easy, and you might object, it ain't so.
I do it using some statements written by my former students, leaving the high school.
Then, can we see what?
Chiara, now attending the the last year of medicine, wrote me that: "Thanks for showing me mathematics not only as a set of formulas, but as a way to to face life making it simpler, through reasoning and fantasy".
The Fifth I class, is one my colleagues called "a desperate class". And they were wrong, because people who write this things, as you can see, do not lack hope, indeed they have a lot of it. Watch out:
"Thanks for giving us the freedom and for teaching us to think and to live".
The last one is Bianca.
Bianca is at the the last year of Physics, and she wrote me this: "Thanks for giving me eyes to look for new lands".
When I read these sentences, I said, really, with my teaching hours of math, my integrals, my stuff, did I inadvertently do that?
Well, then they showed me a way to go ahead, further and reconsider this teaching approach.
But why mathematics?
Mathematics in my students' words, freedom and a liberating force.
What did they write?
it's a powerful tool which enables us to be what we want to be, beyond stereotypes and prejudices.
Look at, they wrote this: math is life, it is a way to simplify our life.
But, why it is that?
Who ever told us that?
Daniel Kahneman tell us that.
Daniel Kahneman is a psychologist, Nobel prize in 2002 for Economics.
He studied decision theory.
If you think about it, every moment we decide.
A statistical research affirms that in a typical day, we decide about 35.000 times.
Are we sure that those decisions are ours only, or are somewhat biased?
Today, you decided to come here.
If you had not come here, you would likely not be who you will be when you leave.
Therefore this is a fundamental point.
But how are we taking all these decisions?
Most part of decisions, Daniel Kahneman says, are taken on the basis of what he calls "System one".
Which is a fast system, it acts quickly; but it is a stereotyped one, mostly based on emotions and memories, what our "belly" tell us.
But this decision - are we sure that the strategies we take and the relevant decisions we take with this "system one" are really ours, unfettered by stereotypes?
Let me show you a very simple stereotype: if you meet a man - with a woman it would be much truer, with a gender conplication I do not want to put in - and you ask this man, What is jour job?
And he answers, I am a thinker.
What do you think he does?
Most of the people say, A philosopher; A writer - my student says, Nothing; but let's put that aside.
If instead he specifies, I am a mathematician working at Geneva CERN.
And nobody ever guesses
the true answer.
Because there's the stereotype that mathematicians, scientists and so on are simply technicians, and they do not do an intellectual job.
Then Daniel Kahneman tells us that if we are to take thoughtful decisions, we must activate what he calls "System Two".
Which is a system, as he says, educated and educable, rational, logical, but slow.
Therefore, if we want our decisions to be nobody's ones but ours, we must activate and train this second system.
And make it able to check whether System One's solutions are indeed the right ones.
Well, this System Two, in my opinion, is just another name for mathematical thinking.
That is, before a problem: analise data; tell the key ones from secondary ones; set up a startegy; test it; reset it if it proves wrong; and go straight to the target.
This is the mathematical thinking, and this give us the freedom that my students told about, therefore it has to become fast.
All right, but you still may say me: "What's fairness got to do with it?"
So let's go see what fairness is.
Let us consider Ulpianus: Ulpianus was one of the greatest Roman jurists, perhaps the greatest one, and he defines justice as: "The constant, perennial will to give everybody what they're entitled to".
Sen, Indian philosopher and economist who deals with civil rights, what does he says to us?
"The concept of inequality does not consist only in income inequality, but mostly in an inequality of opportunities, of possibility, of choice, of individual freedom.
It is crucial, for each individual, to have the freedom to decide how to shape himself".
So, if a State must give to each and every one of us the freedom to become what they mean to be, and the same opportunities, it means that a mathematical method has to bring the mathematical competence to each and every one of us.
So the teaching method of mathematics has to be based, first of all, on that - I apologize for mathematical jargon - on that is a fundamental axiom: "No one girl, no one boy left behind, in mathematics and life".
Because if only one person is left out I miss one person and therefore the State turns out to be an unfair and iniquitous State.
But then you will say me:
"How can we do? Any second chance?"
We must go - remember what we told at the beginning - beyond the horizon, think something even if it is not yet here.
We must change the teacher profile, and the way we teach Math.
So what do we do?
Do a different thing, which is the second axiom: "Mathematics is for everyone and all".
Therefore there are not - that's why I asked to raise your hands - there are no gifted or denied persons; only untrained persons exist, or ones that are biased by stereotypes.
Who does tell us so? Carol Dweck:
Carol Dweck is a Stanford-based cognitive psychologist, a worldwide reference of cognitive and social psychology.
She tell us that there are only family and school conditions which somehow affect talent development.
So what should you do?
The teacher, she still tells us - and she gives advice to parents and teachers, which is this one: give always challenging cases to your sons, daughters or students, let them delve into that.
And reward the commitment, not the performance, because only in challenging cases this approach will develop.
Because if mathematics is a way of thinking, a way of facing life, I must train math thinking as if I [were] in a gym; then I have to put my muscles at work, I have to develop the capability to guess, imagine, design, to infer and check, then measure and quantify phenomena and real facts.
This is the important thing.
So challenging cases, why?
Because, no matter what, mistakes are not a limit, but an opportunity.
An opportunity for reflection and growth.
So I showed you that mathematics goes hand in hand with justice and fairness.
But then a last challenge remains that I'd like to take with you, which is this: Italy, and many countries, score terribly in math's functional illiteracy; so math, if our State is a fair and just one, has to enter in all the houses.
Not only in boys and girls, but in all of us, in all adults.
This is our last challenge, let mathematics spread everywhere.
Maybe somebody is thinking now, this is an utopia, it will never happen, and a mathematician as I am I say that it is just about finding the right strategy,
because, in the words of the great Adriano Olivetti, "The word utopia is a shortcut to dismiss what you're not willing, able or brave enough to do.
A dream is always a dream, until you do not start from somewhere.
Then it becomes a purpose, something extremely greater.
Today, Lorella's dream can only be achieved with your help, also.
Let's then dream toghether.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I was telling Jessie the story of Siddhartha.
That's a beautiful story, a beautiful myth.
It is one way of telling the truth, and children seem to love it.
Lama Norbu, I have great respect for your culture and your religion.
And I know about the invasion of Tibet, and the tragedies that happened. But I don't believe in reincarnation, and neither does my wife.
Why should you?
In Tibet, we deem the mind and the body as the content and the container.
Now, the cup is no longer a cup.
But what is the tea?
Still tea.
Exactly!
In the cup, on the table, or on the floor, it shifts from one container to another, but it's still tea.
So is mind after death:
it also shifts from one body to another, but it is still mind.
Even in the cloth, it is still the same tea.
Not for me, thank you.
So, when speaking about the future and thinking about our future, focusing on the fact that the one certainty we have is that one day - we don’t know when or how, but one day we will leave our bodies behind helps us to see a number of things more clearly.
Just to start, it enables us to rearrange our priorities, and give a new order in the process, to give the right value to what is really important for us.
Secondly, it allows us to understand exactly what makes us unique.
<i>Unique</i> does not mean <i>irreplaceable</i>; it means having a clear understanding of how we want to build our little bit of the world.
And this bit of the world also includes the things we want to pass on to our children and to the people we love, the things that we want to leave for those who come after us.
The future is a state of mind.
One we should have a clear idea about, understand how to get the most out of our resources.
States of mind must be kept safe and nurtured.
States of mind are those emotions that link us to events during our lives.
Each of you is perfectly aware that we might react differently from others to the same event, and it is this ability to interpret an event and make it a part of us, giving it a precise meaning, that changes our quality of life.
The quality of life is made up of sorrow and joy,
and the most important things are totally invisible. Sometimes, therefore, the ability to take a look at ourselves from a different point of view helps us enormously in shaping this bit of the world exactly as we would like to.
Yesterday, someone asked me how I managed, over the years, to do what I do during those meetings, those paths I take as an advisor, along with my customers.
This led me to realise that the first time I considered working on change I was very young,
I was thirteen years old.
It was the end of the seventies and my parents took me to Moscow, Russia, to attend a Russian language course.
One morning, I was told that me and other schoolmates, would be taken to visit the Red Army Museum.
Now, Russia of the 1970s was a very different one from today's. As you entered the country, your documents were taken from you and you were given a small piece of paper on which they had written your name and a number. This number was to identify you within your group of classmates.
Foreigners had no contact with the Russian population we were kept from locals, we had to be clearly identifiable and we could just follow our guide.
That morning, I don’t exactly know why, but at the age of 13, being even shorter than I am now, I managed to slip into a nearby group of Russians:
there were separate queues according to nationality and those for the Germans, British, Italians and French were very close to the Russian queue. So I was able to join that queue and visit the Red Army Museum separately from my classmates from different countries.
And when I came out, I found my classmates waiting for me, since my visit had lasted 45 minutes more than theirs. They were all totally bored, as they had just spent a whole hour listening to a description of all the uniforms worn in the war between 1917 and 1922, and they had found the visit extremely boring.
I was instead very surprised because I had seen in those rooms a very interesting window of Russian history, during the 1939 - 1945 war, in which I was told how important the Russian contribution had been in this period, how they had destroyed Germany, and how heartless and cruel Germany was towards them.
I watched a lot of documentaries that my classmates never saw. But we shared our thoughts about that, when I came out.
This experience taught me three things. First, when exploring a particular topic, you've got to read at least five or six books from different cultures, because one view of the same subject is totally partial.
They taught us that period of history, in school; but from a very different standpoint.
Secondly, I learnt that there are teachers and that sometimes what you are taught by <i>teachers</i> is different from what you can learn from a <i>master</i>, as education is narrow sometimes. Therefore you need to be able to tell the master from the teacher, as masters usually give you a more encompassing view of events.
The third thing I learnt is that every ideology is self-reinforcing and therefore we need to keep our eyes open within the ideology in which we grow up, because we must understand that everything ideological is partial, manipulative and doesn’t allow us to get in touch with our deepest self.
Why am I telling you this?
Because if you really want to embark on a process of change and begin to understand how to build your future, you need to take a little look back at the past and, above all, get rid of the shackles preventing you from letting go of old resentments or regrets.
Resentments and regrets don’t allow you to move on.
So, when thinking on our past, we need to think, how can we include all the things we underwent.
It’s interesting that, in recent years, neuroscientific research has come up with a series of breakthroughs to help us understand why sometimes it's hard to leave the past behind.
One of these is that sorrow or discomfort activates parts of our brain we don’t normally use.
This means that only when we are able to make room for sorrow or discomfort we can successfully form new behaviours and find a new way forward.
This is something we don’t do spontaneously. Usually, we leave those parts of our brain switched off,
but turning them on can be of great help.
The second thing is that, in order to go forward, we have to know where we're at now.
If we want to get to Rome, we need to know whether we are starting from Naples or Milan;
otherwise, maybe we’ll end up in Rome, but only after wasting a lot more energy, a lot more efforts than it's necessary.
So it's very important to know where you're starting from.
To know this, we need to stand still for a while.
Still.
It's something we don’t do often, these days.
We are used to rush, to solve problems,
to take a whole series of things for granted.
Staying still isn’t something that comes naturally to us,
but it's extremely important.
The other thing we need to build a healthy state of mind, one that will enable us to look at the future, is knowing that we won't get anywhere by ourselves and that the most important things that we do can only be achieved together with other people, given that we are all interconnected.
So this collective view of life is an implicitly ethical one.
I know that if I do wrong to the people in this part of the audience, there'll soon be a payback.
If I’m lucky, I'll pay that price in person; otherwise, it'll be paid by my children. So we need to take great care when taking decisions that can affect others.
Achieving a balance between giving and taking is another key element in ensuring sustainable relationships.
There are no adult relationships when one person only gives and doesn’t receive, or the other way around.
There might be relationships where there is a big imbalance, but they don’t last very long.
And if they last, they don’t do any good to those involved.
So, we should be aware that it is very important to give, but it is also crucial to be open to what others want to give us. This is a powerful element, allowing us to be successful. in creating our world, our network and also in building our future.
I would like to end with a few words on the importance of reconciliation, as this is one of the things that makes our future greater than our past,
and ensures that our future, if it is truly positive, will be much richer than what we have previously experienced. This involves the ability to include and reconcile ourselves with everything we somehow tried to cast off our lives.
The key issue here is to accept differences,
without judging others: nothing is more relaxing.
If you can allow yourself not to have opinions or judgements, you'll be more relaxed when making decisions about your life.
Above all, I wanted to spend one last minute on the importance of reconciliation, even when it is hard to look in the other person's eyes.
Finally, I wish you all a happy and fulfilling future.
(Applause)
I’m here to share a formula to become successful people.
I want to tell you a story.
It's the story of a boy, whose school scores were all between five and six.
He loved the sea but not the beach, he loved cars and helping other people.
And this is the story of his awakening.
it’s both about how he made his dream come true and how he now helps people making their own ones come true.
Teachers used to say: “He's gifted, but not committed”.
I’m sure many of you have heard something like this, once in your life.
He went to high school.
Then college time came, and he had to make a choice; what to do in life?
He wasn’t very motivated; he couldn’t come up with an idea. Then he decided to start working. He started working
as a door to door salesman.
He wasn’t selling vacuums or encyclopaedias, but it was pretty hard working.
Luckily, this company gives him the chance to get in touch with personal growth.
He finally started studying something that he liked, and some good results start coming.
It was finally going pretty well,
and he decided to start his own business.
He started a company, but it didn't fly.
He started a second one, he was earning nothing. He almost ran out of money.
As you can imagine,
he was at the crossroad: "Ok, maybe we’ve dared too much,
either we lower our standards, and settle for a more normal life, or let's start looking for a solution."
He picked this second one.
Why?
Because he finally understood he had studied an important thing.
That all of his good results have always come from his ideas.
So he could see good opportunities, but he couldn't exploit them.
He then needed a “software upgrade”, perhaps an "idea upgrade".
Why?
Because starting from new ideas, through his actions and decisions, he can get new results.
So he thought, “I need to work on my ideas.
I can see opportunities, but I can't manage them.
I still need an upgrade."
He invested all of his money on coaching.
Yes, the same guy who didn’t like school became a coach as he grew up.
He finally came up with something that came in very handy later in life.
First, everything in our physical world used to be in somebody’s mind first.
TEDx Lucca, in the beginning, was in the two organizers' mind.
Every brick in this beautiful church, was first in a designer’s mind.
And then, If you want to make something in your life, in the world of things, you first have to make it in a mental universe.
So he started to elaborate and plan what he wanted to happen.
He had to define in detail what his goal is.
At the end, every man and every person has something divine inside.
And it comes out here.
Because we can make our thoughts real. And now you may ask yourselves:
"Can we achieve every goal?"
The answer is yes, but every goal has its price.
And you need to pay the full price in advance.
And this is the oddity.
Because yes, we can achieve everything - but depending on what your goal is, you will have to pay a price.
This is not a monetary price, this is not in euro, rather, it's an emotional price.
It depends on the emphasys you will put on it.
The Universe, at first, will challenge you
with one or more hurdles.
If you overcome them, then the doors to your goal will open.
So we need to plan everything in advance: we need to focus on wher do we want to go; Then we must practice, pay that emotional price and make our climb.
In fact, it's a bit like the climber metaphor.
Anticipate everything.
So we put much effort at the beginning.
Then we have to make our climb, which will be as difficult as the top to climb. And once there, you can celebrate.
But this is also the nice part, so to say.
Because if someone took the climber on top of the mountain by a helicopter, it would probably not be the same.
So the boy started thinking about his future.
He found himself at a crossroad: taking the responsibility of what you want to do in life, and take the hardest path, the one with a mountain to climb; or taking the easy way, where most people are?
Because that's the foundmental choice.
Psychologists call this state “locus of control”.
Where do you think the control of your life lies?
If you think it’s outside, then the only feeling you can generate, unfortunately, is anger.
Conversely, if you think you create what you generate around you, then you can take your responsibilities. And that's the hardest part; but you can blame no one, you only need to roll your sleeves up.
So he decided to take the hardest path, take the responsability and start implementing all the possible actions and corrections to create a goal.
People are very good to tell what they don't whant, rather than what they want: but a problem emerges now.
It’s like being in front of a cab driver
saying to him: “I don’t want to go to neither Pisa, Florence or Milan”.
Now she still cannot go anywhere.
Only if you gave her a precise information, she could take you to your destination.
We must do the same with our lives, by creating the material universe we want around us.
None of us is too young, too old, too nice, too lucky or unlucky.
It’s all up to us.
We must take control of our so-called internal monologue.
Do you mind that voice, that quite all of us have, that keeps telling us what we can or what we can’t do?
This may be the most important part. If we had a positive monologue, if we spoke to us positively, then we woud have a positive life. ,
Otherwise, if we had a negative, weak one, then we would live a negative life.
The fun fact is that we have the control of this voice and we can use it towards us.
There is another very important thing: there are genes that our parents pass down to us, and there are "memes", so to say: parts of information, assumptions we borrow from the people around us.
We don’t usually need these memes as they came from the past.
So if we failed once, we’ll probably continue to fail.
Memes could also come from people with different goals.
At the beginning I spoke about success.
Every person has his own point of view about success.
If you ask suggestions to someone with a different idea of success, you could generate a different, lesser world.
The boy from my story has also learned, he was completely responsible and he can always pick up what meaning to assign to everything we experience.
We can give a meaning to every result we achieve.
If I have an accident, and I don't got hurt, am I lucky or unlucky?
That's what I mean: every meaning we give leads to an emotion.
According to this emotion, we can take a decision or not, and this decision will either be effective or not.
And then it could take us to other actions.
This fact could lead to a result, a circle, that could either boost or harm you.
And this is under our control too.
We don’t even know the amount of things we have under our control.
We are totally free to choose the future we want for us.
There is one more thing: RAS.
The Reticular Activating System,
What is Ras?
It's how we simplify the world around us.
We human being are very similar to cavemen .
And we need to simplify the world around us.
So what's going to happen?
It happens that our attention and consciousness let us see what we think is there.
Ever happened to you?
Whoever lost your keys, raise your hand!
Almost everyone.
Then you finally saw them.
Somebody showed them to you.
That’s RAS.
You thought they were not there, but they actually were.
It's the same with opportunities, with both good and bad things in life.
We will only be able to notice something if we think it’s there.
This is under our control too.
That said, we can now better understand how this informations will be useful later on, because things start getting better then. RAS works even better with opportunities. During a lunch with a friend a very important opportunity came to us. The opportunity was, building a car from scratch.
I’m going to tell you how this boy moved from this image to showcasing his car at the Bologna Motor Show.
We haven’t sold dozens or even thousands of these cars.
So, you'll probably see none of these cars in the streets.
But that’s not the point.
The point is how we can go from our ideal world to the real world.
In this story, at the very beginning, we had to raise money.
So we used all the possible ways and channels to attract some investors.
We started build the car after we found some of them.
Things seemed to be good to me.
after finishing the chassis, the first tests begin, as I told you before. The universe wanted to see how driven they are.
And you may have understood, by now, that guy was me. I was smiling in camera, but I actually felt pretty sad. The chassis wasn't ok and we had ran out of money.
Then again I had to choose once again. I could have said: “ok, sorry, it was a joke, I’ve dared too much, let’s go back to normal things”.
As we saw before, I choose to go on.
Why?
Because I knew what I should have done in order to pass the first test.
So I knew what to do.
My idea at that point was to keep on with the project, in order to sell the idea much better .
So I was ready to invest everything I had.
You need a lot of energy.
What keeps people from their dreams is, they stop after one or two attempts, because of fears and doubts.
That stops everything.
In this case I could fortunately handle it so I kept on working, invest my free time and money on the project, doing something I had never done before.
So I started sanding the body to slowly give the car a shape.
Then another problem emerged.
How to build a handmade car with a perfectly symmetrical shape?
It’s not trivial; we had to find a solution.
We found a fantastic, analog 3D printer that no one else had.
We used this machinery to build two perfectly equal sides. //
I measured one side to understand whether to add or remove material on the other side.
We could talk much more about it, but long story short, there always is a solution if you only think they are there.
Then our first prototype, the first shape, is made and other investors in the automotive field helped us.
We build the interiors.
Everything was still going pretty well, but another challenge was in store for us.
Because right after building the cockpit, we ran out of money again.
It was a really bad deal.
We were unmotivated, as you can imagine. “Damn it, I have been working for three years on that project, I put everything on it, I've almost finished.
What now?”
Then I had to be confident.
The universe is full of opportunities, we mustn't tell ourselves lies. I sand all my contacts this email: “Will you join me in this madness, and unveil the car at the Motor Show?”.
I sent this message to everyone then a very important person answered me.
We get a seed fund, and we manage to unveil it at Bologna Motor Show.
What then?
Because achieving your goals does make you happy,
