My boss was closer to Gandhi than Hitler some days, but other days... Here's another thing which is happening on the Internet: you browse the Internet when you suddenly notice that weird things happen on the page you are viewing.
At the exact same time, my friend and I.
He was seeking info on how to restore a "trullo" in Puglia, while I was looking for a website where I could lease a new car.
Strangely enough, the website prompted him to read a news about Tsipras, besides what he had looked up, namely information on real estate. That website prompted me
to check car tools.
So, what's the reason behind it?
Without focusing on technology, it's named "cognitive tagging" and, we like it or not, when you browse the Internet today different types of tagging are occurring.
Machines begin to understand the meaning of what you look up, the meaning of words.
If we speak natural language, we can start to get the hang of it.
Machines are now taking another step forward, that is
the capability to acquire sense and sensitivity, this is important.
First off, the word "sense" has lots of meanings.
Anatomic-wise, if we consult a good encyclopedia, we have the five senses, or you can find a philosophical meaning: the sense of life.
Then a linguistic meaning: the sense of words.
Well, machines are starting to acquire senses through sensors, since we are surrounded by sensors.
What happens inside the machine?
Machines can interpret data on your behalf, they give data a philosophical meaning, not a proper philosophical one, though, but they give meaning to data they get from the sensors.
The machine which gives data a linguistic meaning is the one we came across earlier: contact tagging and so on and so forth.
We can do it.
We can do it when dealing with vineyards, medicine, or when it comes to traffic management.
Such machines are getting more sensitive.
So, what is left of man?
Human beings have the upper hand over machines for an important aspect, which is the common sense.
The good sense, as it was called once.
Look at the picture at the bottom right, it's a well known picture in scientific literature: it's called "Anne and the babysitter".
You see, there's a lady
taking a kid by the hand. Anyone of you would say that Anne is the kid, and the babysitter is the older one, right?
Any different opinion?
This happens because of our good sense, but a machine couldn’t process
this information. The younger one could be a young kid who looks after a chubby 15-year-old girl. And who said that babysitters need to be older than who they look after?
In theory, it could happen, but not really in practice.
Another example which confirms we have the upper hand over machines: machines use statistical models, they use statistic considerations, Gaussian averages.
There's another example by Nicholas Taleb, who wrote a nice book, "The black swan": if you were a farmer and a pig had statistical skills.
Well, the pig gets 3 kg of food from a farmer, so, on average roughly 3 kg.
Overall, the farmer is a good person.
Well, the farmer kills the pig at Christmas, right?
Statistically it is correct.
Well, the good sense is black or white, it's either there or it is not.
So, if the farmer is a wretched he will always be so, not statistically.
I made two stupid examples to give you a better idea of why human beings have the upper hand over machines as for the good sense.
This is ad advert of IBM computers in 1950s.
That's what we said to sell them, "If you purchase one of those black machines down below, then you don't need 150 engineers or slide rules."
But we all know what happened: the company employed the 150 extra engineers to put them around the black machines.
However, over the last few centuries, what happened is that men went from humanism to competences.
Industrial revolution, so they learned a job, a specific competence.
Then, in 1940s, the 1950s, after the Second World War, even more competences, so much more specific: turning the side rule.
Machines began to do things which were quite meaningless right from the outset: wages-salaries.
So machines started with simple capabilities.
But then they improved, they released software able to perform a variety of things, including ERP management, warehouse management.
Today machines are acquiring sense.
What's happening to human beings meanwhile?
In theory, men have the upper hand over machines thanks to their good sense, as we said before.
Well, but in theory. There's no difference between theory and practice, in theory.
A philosopher said: In theory there is no difference between theory and practice. In practice there is.
In practice, us human beings are oftentimes losing the good sense, sometimes we even copy machines, which is crazy!
I noticed this problem.
If our strong point is the good sense, and we start to copy machines, which do not have the good sense, there's a problem.
What shall we do then?
We need to do a very simple thing: be brave to dare.
Machines don't dare because they are not aware: being brave is not even a problem for machines.
Being brave to dare means doing things wisely.
It's not that difficult, we should be able to do that, we just need our brain to be led by our heart.
Thanks for listening.
