For many?
Too many, maybe?
When I was called to direct the largest complex, the museum complex of Palazzo Ducale, here in Mantua city, I said no: I'm not the prince kissing that sleeping beauty.
I appeal to the professionals in the field, the creative minds.
These are the artists, these are the contemporary artists who work every day on creativity.
In Mantua we have the strongest example, we have almost a magic word, called Gonzaga.
The family that turned little Mantua into a great Mantua.
Here is a view of our very famous Bridal Chamber, "camera picta", because it is just painted with a unique creativity.
For over two centuries, the Gonzaga family always focused on creativity and always brought in new ideas.
They were always aware: what is the latest fashion?
Isabella d'Este as a great style icon of her time.
Always new artists: they championed Pisanello, for instance, with his large drawings on the wall, a new revolutionary idea, Andrea Mantegna, Giulio Romano - there are many to mention.
But you have to understand that all these artists, who now we consider great masters in their time were contemporary, and also much discussed.
Even as foreigners: Rubens, was "the Italian Flemish", not everyone accepted him immediately.
Monteverdi, too, is one from Cremona. (Laughter)
He became the great Monteverdi in Mantua, before dying in Venice.

So you can awaken a city with art: Mantua is the most beautiful example because what Gonzagas understood is that image survives.
What gives strength, for a very long time, is the image, it is the composition and involvement in the image.
Let's talk about other cities: you all know it, this is the most visited museum in Italy.
It's called the Uffizi: what does it mean?
It first wasn't meant to be a museum, but a redfile building.
They did "Uffizi" [Offices], here.
But in the gallery of this building few paintings were exhibited, and then gradually other paintings in others floors too.
And the Uffizi, over time, become a museum thanks to a decision to focus precisely on art creating an environment that's now one of the most visited places.
Let's take another example: Rome, also well known, with the largest amphitheatre.
It's hard to imagine [that] for centuries, this great amphitheater was simply a repository.
It has been used as, say, a free source for construction sites in current buildings.
And then, at some point, someone said, "No, come on.
We have to take care of this, we must reassess, we must awaken this monument because it is the very center of ancient Rome."
So, it's about a city that's much bigger than the city we live in now.
Today, the Colosseum, as you know, is one of the most visited monuments, all over Italy and the world.
Another example is Venice.
Venice, at the end of the eighteenth century was just the casino of Europe.
One could even say the brothel of Europe.
Forbidden to minors, because it was a bit of a sin city.
But little by little, art became more important here as well.
The interest on gambling went progressively down, in favour of Tiziano and Tintoretto.
But this was not enough for the Venetians who were always very keen on their international identity.
They created a fabulous idea: 1895, the first Biennale.
Not Biennial of the past art: of contemporary art!
So a direct comparison, in a city almost museum-like, every two years.
This concept - now, this year, we live another experience: many come, many meet up here to deal with the current creativity.
Great idea, great success.
Let's talk about another place, the Louvre.
The Louvre, as you know, was the home of the French royal family.
Then, let's say, the center of power.
The French Revolution changed everything.
They decided: we no longer have the king, but we turn this building into a museum for all citizens who have the right to approach the creativity of the past.
A long period, a museum, and then a bit of a crisis because it was a bit stuck in the past.
And some people thought, what can we do to take a new step towards our current life?
And they built the pyramid, this intervention of contemporary architecture that gave another center of attention not only spatial but also spiritual for this centre.
Today, as you know, the Louvre is the most visited museum in the world.
Think- Austria hosts eight million residents.
All the inhabitants of Austria come to visit the Louvre every year.
Let’s talk about London, a huge city: so many problems, countless citizens.
Many places, even a British Museum as their traditional museum.
But it was disconnected to young people: so the Tate Modern was built.
And it was not decided to create a new museum, quite the opposite: an existing structure was used and transformed it into a museum of contemporary art.
And now, all the young people are coming to visit this factory.
Young people of age but also of brain, of course.
Let’s talk about Barcelona, also a very interesting city, with many rhythms and problems.
When they suggested the idea to make a new museum for contemporary art, they didn't put it on the most prestigious square.
They took it and brought it, and created it, right in the middle of a very problematic environment, both from a social and economic perspective.
And with this investment everything - not all the problems, but many problems have been solved.
And then one must always quote the Bilbao effect.
I visited Bilbao, and I can say that it wasn't a nice city, the opposite, I do not want to say bad words.
But this brilliant idea of bringing a museum, with a great name as Guggenheim, right into the city, and then creating an architecture that is a point of attraction itself, changed the life in the city.
If you go to Bilbao today, you'll find not just tourists but a city that's completely awakened.
Wonderful.
My last thought: now we understand that you can awaken with art, every day. From the biggest [town] to the smallest.
But what I would like to give you is, somewhat as a message, that this is your right.
When they draw the universal human rights, after the World War II, they also added article 27.
You can read it: it is a right for us to participate, to have access to contemporaneity, to have access to creativity.
So let's exercise this right.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good morning!
Today, I want to present a concept, from cradle to cradle, using this organic industrial waste.
First, let's explain what the raw material is.
Raw materials are all those materials, which through appropriate processing, can be processed into finished products.
In the concept of "cradle to cradle" these materials must be natural, so they must regenerate.
The fundamental problem is the population is increasing more and more and then our resources are becoming more and more rare and precious.
According to the concept cradle to cradle, population and mass consumption are not the problem, but the way we waste our resources.
In Europe, 3 billion tons of waste are burned every year.
These wastes may be raw materials, but by burning them instead, they will be lost forever.
We should therefore create intelligent products, which can remain in the production cycle forever.
Designers, planners, engineers, architects, they all will have to re-invent new products.
Products which can be reused or composted after their use.
So they will have to be part of the technical or biological cycle again.
This will help us to always use pure raw materials, as well as healthy and quality materials, but mostly non-toxic materials, so that even when they are reused they will always remain raw or second materials of good quality.
I like more to talk about sustainability, sustainable products, because we have in our minds the idea that recycled products are always of poor quality, a second choice.
So we have to produce sustainable products.
The ecological and social sustainability should be combined with the economic and commercial importance.
The concept doesn't have to be good only to provide a clear conscience but it also have to be profitable.
So we have developed new materials which could never be placed on the market because they are too expensive in comparison to the competition of toxic and chemical products which have higher performances at a lower price.
So we are trying to give the highest value to these organic industrial waste and to find the suitable products to give them a new life.
So let's talk about our raw material, which is an apple.
Since this is a regional project, the apple is absolutely our regional fruit.
Apples are beautiful products, beautiful raw materials which can be either consumed immediately or transformed, worked.
A result of the industrial processes of the apple is apple juice.
From apple juice comes our new raw material: waste, cores, peels, all that remains.
In Europe, there are hundreds and hundreds of tons of this material that are disposed or burned each year.
We have some applications such as biogas plants or the extraction of pectin which are already operating. But there will always be something left to dispose or to burn.
Instead, we're looking for solutions that allow us to use all the wastage.
There is nothing left.
And then, after careful analysis we have found what's inside.
There is so much in fiber, cellulose, hemicellulose, sugars, many raw materials.
And then, in the case of cellulose, we have created these new products. One of these products is the "Cartamela."
In the production of paper we are going to replace part of the traditional cellulose with the cellulose contained in the fiber.
There are other materials we produce, such as the "Pellemela."
In "Pellemela", we use fibers of organic industrial waste,
and so we have a new product: the "Pellemela."
All of our products are sustainable products because they are produced from sustainable resources Furthermore, they are all made of 100% recycled and 100% non-toxic materials.
Thanks for your attention.
(Applause)
This toilet, with such a futuristic look, has a heated seat, a self-cleaning system, emits an odor after every use, a melody.
It even has a jet within, bidet-like: it's all controlled by an app on your smartphone!
It's fantastic, isn't it?
There are even ideas about making it even more useful, with the coming versions: maybe adding a control, a urine analysis, inside.
It could also tell you if you have an infection, it could calculate your BAC, maybe offer the recipe of a mash hangover, in the iPhone, for the next day.
It could even tell you if you're pregnant, singing the Ride of the Valkyries, it could register the hormones of pregnancy.
In the current version of the app there's even a calendar, thus you can track your "bowel regularity".
It amazes me that they haven't put a balance, inside, so you can challenge your Facebook friends!
I don't know which of you will go out immediately and buy this toilet: surely all the ladies in the room that are living with a man, for there's also a very useful function: when you leave, it automatically lowers the lid.
Welcome to the Internet of Things, that also ends even longstanding marital quarrels.
But what is the Internet of Things?
The Internet of Things is the connectivity of every object, everytime and everywhere.
50 billion devices will be connected to the Internet, within the next 5 years.
But where are all these devices?
In our homes, maybe around us and on us, maybe even inside us.
There are sensors that are measuring the levels of blood pressure and oxygen inside the body.
These will be ingested and absorbed by the organism, thus sending data to the doctors from the inside, diagnostic data.
There are even defibrillators that send data to the doctors from the inside.
Think about the systems that control critical infrastructures of entire nations: power plants, interconnected intelligent road signs, intelligent traffic lights.
In cars as well: car manufacturers give more and more attention to these things.
They're making all cars able to interconnect now: there are diagnostic systems, pneumatic, speed controls, steering controls, break controls, there are even manufacturers updating control unit at a distance, via Internet.
Maybe the central manufacturer, in Germany, is updating cars sold in the US, during the night while they're in their garages.
There are also phone applications that are showing you how you're driving, your consumption, your driving style.
Or you could try something a bit closer to home: for example, how many of you have a smart TV, a television that allows you to see a video on the Internet, or hear a song on YouTube?
Or a nectar,
that are increasingly spreading, they let you see on your phone what's going on in your child's room, upstairs. Or see what your dog is doing while you're in the office.
Seeing it eating the couch, wagging its tail, while we're sitting powerless in the office.
Don't think that these things are far away from us, because in the end, when you change your television or washing machine, it'll probably be connected to the Internet, as all new devices, now, have this kind of function.
Some houses, for example, are equipped with intelligent, connected thermostats.
These thermostats know, through cellphones, where we are, if we are going to the office or back home, which room we're in, changing the room temperature accordingly.
Or scales that send our fitness data to our online profiles.
There are even young Italians that have invented the intelligent mattress, a mattress cover, actually, that learns how we sleep, our habits, it knows when to heat, which part of the bed, even at what time.
These devices even meter how we sleep, they can even tell us if we snore...
- You snored tonight! - It's not true!
- The mattress says so! - Oh, OK.
The danger is that it also tells us
with whom you've slept: I'm seeing many worried faces there... But I haven't told you yet who I am.
My name is Stefano, and at least 10 days have passed since I've bought a new electronic device.
I'm addicted to technology, I tend to have a lot of these objects.
But in my job, I deal with security and insecurity, meaning hacking.
I tend to surround myself with these things, as I said, so my professional paranoia brings me to ask: what if these things weren't as secure as we'd like to think?
What would probably be your response to this question?
Well, this isn't my problem!
The important thing is that the TV can connect to the Internet, and that my kid can see Peppa Pig staying quiet while I'm downloading from YouYube. This question has indeed been posed by many researchers, many computer experts in security, in the underground hacking community.
Everybody is very careful in this sector, because it's a field it's good to deal with.
In addition, hacking these objects ends up in newspaper frontpages, because they are things that most of us have at home.
Think that the last cyber attacks began from refrigerator networks, washing machines, TVs, thermostats.
Networks of cyber attacks, all with the unintended approval of their owners, that maybe didn't even change her devices's default password.
Think, maybe - you are here watching TED, at home your washing machine is attacking the New York Times site.
"Well, in truth this still isn't my problem." Maybe it's interesting for companies, but it still makes no difference to me.
Now let's take everyday things, that we have in our homes, for example, these things have our personal information: the thermostat knows, as I was saying, which room we are in, maybe where we are, with the cellphone: the scales know how much we weight and also, maybe, how many glasses of water we've had: or the smartphone knows the usual route of our evening jog, it knows exactly the route we follow; or the netcam and the TVs are a window for what happens in the living room, or in the rooms upstairs.
So these data are very important, and should they come to the wrong hands they could become dangerous.
Well, this starts to shake a little bit our initial response.
If I told you it's possible to edit signboards like this one, at a distance?
Or intelligent traffic lights?
Or think about a report, a couple of months ago, where BMW and Mini had to update, always via Internet, remotely, their cars because through a smartphone app it was possible to open the doors, for example.
There are even researchers of the Washington University and San Diego that have managed to control cars remotely hacking their control system, thus showing a different speed on the gauge, or operate the breaks, or the lights, with a consequent risk for those who are sitting at the wheel.
"This isn't my problem" sounds increasingly controversial, now.
So, you remember the pacemaker that I told about?
Other researchers have managed to connect to this pacemaker: they changed its parameters, remotely, and even induce a defibrillation.
Not on a real patient, of course: it was a test.
Seeing this, the "it's not my problem" argument is definitely lost.
Coming back to our first image, of the toilet, the bluetooth control and security system of this toilet is set as 0000.
Guess how easy it is to connect to the toilet and make jokes to whoever is using it.
Maybe we can open the cast, open the hot air, open and close the lid!
Hacking can be a lot of fun, in certain cases...
But at this point, after seeing all these things, if I asked you the same question, "What If all these things weren't as safe as we'd like to think?", what would now be your response?
I hope that it would be, "Yes, it's my problem.
I have to do something." It's OK to surround ourselves with this kind of things, with new technologies, but keeping in mind the new risks we're exposed to, and the key role we play in protecting ourselves of these risks, before and after acquiring them, when we choose the product and when we use that product.
So, what can we do, on our part, to protect ourselves from these things?
At first, how many of you actually read the manuals?
Nobody reads the manuals, now the products are intuitive and easy to use, we are taken by the enthusiasm of the new device and we don't want to read the manual.
But when we read it, we understand exactly what the functions of this product are.
Because we have to know what it offers, firstly to understand what it does, but also to turn off everything that isn't useful to us.
It's unnecessary to leave the doors open for a hacker to come in.
For example, there's no point in keeping voice command access enabled to your TV in the living room, which activates a microphone in your living room, if you know you're always going to use the remote.
It's thus important to give only the private information that are needed, to these products.
Because they can always be hacked and seen by someone.
For example, a thermostat that has to be connected to the weather forecast, to change the house's temperature, doesn't have to know your address or telephone.
It's enough to know the town, or the postal code.
It's also important to keep these products up-to-date, because if they're never updated after installation, the old versions are subject to programming errors, and hence to hacking attacks.
Thus, they must be constantly updated.
Finally, a tip that is as easy as it is important: change the password of the devices, or at least enter one where used to be none, for instance in your cellphone.
Let's use a complex password: not "123456", that among others, it has won the prize, in 2014, as the most used password.
Now everybody knows it, by the way.
Thus what I would like to get, today, is that when you acquire a new device, ask yourselves, "How do I have it do all that I want" and immediately after, "How do I keep it from doing what I don't want?"
Thank you!
(Applause)
Good evening.
I'm a scientist, and I do cutting edge research.
What is cutting edge research?
We don't know exactly, there's no definition.
It's surely a new thing.
It was asked to many relevant scientists, and they could not give a definition.
What can be stated is, it's not quite like traditional research.
Traditional research, also called incremental reasearch, runs along clearly defined lines of research based on hypothesis from that field of research, built on existing knowledge. These hypothesis are either approved or denied, and it goes on.
Innovative research is not like this.
It provides new paradigms, basically new lines of research must be discovered.
In the lighting industry, for instance, the one about candles is a traditional line of research, and consists in chemists trying to optimize the consumption of the wax these candles were made of.
Innovative research doesn't go like this.
It creates new paradigms in order to find new methods to solve old problems.
This is a nice definition, given by a representative of the Human Fringe Science Program, an intercontinental programme that funds innovative research. In the lighting industry, an example of innovation might be the lightbulb:
a way of solving the old issue of lighting with a new paradigm.
Physicists study illumination, electricity.
Why is innovative research so important?
Because of globalisation.
Our world is very dynamic, everything's changing, frontiers and economy are changing, politics, culture, everything changes and so science and technology have the urge and the desire to keep up with these changes, so we need new paradigms.
A financing body was instituted in Europe in 2007: the European Research Council -- ERC.
It was born as an offshoot of the European Commission and has a budget of about two billions a year to fund specifically innovative research.
This body's funding policy is also innovative, much unlike the European commission's one.
It funds individual projects.
Pioneering is thought of as an individual action, while it's collective in ordinary European projects.
These fundings are large, an average of two millions for a five year project.
These fundings are very peculiar because they're "portable", like telephone numbers.
If people can't do what they have to do, in their current workplace, they can "carry" it somewhere else.
The critical aspect of these fundings is that they are highly competitive.
It's really difficult to get them.
The average of success is about 8%, because they're considered as prizes, awards.
Honestly, I thought that I could do the things I wanted to do, my aim was achievable.
After many years of research I manage to win the first ERC funding, and so here I am, telling you this story.
My story begins as a person - I've always been a bioengineer,
who is an engineer willing to project and develop replacement parts for the human body, let's put it this way.
When I was a little girl
I was always watching "The Bionic Man" on TV.
I'm not sure you remember.
The Six Million Dollar Man, with high-tech parts only inside his body.
I loved the Bionic Woman,
I knew all episodes by heart.
Sci-fi has always impressed me.
I've always loved sci-fi, and not for interstellar travelling, but because androids are always there.
Also in Blade Runner you have replicants.
Humans with some tech within.
This idea interested me since I was a kid.
In middle school days,
there used to be no such thing as internet, so I used the newsstand.
I bought all scientific magazines that raised my interest.
I remember a story -
Once I read an article on biomedical nanomachines,
which is the idea of making very small robots which are miniaturized and can be injected in our blood vessels, and as they're microscopical they may clean our vessels to prevent the formation of atherosclerosis' plaqus.
It may seem weird to you, but I thought: "This is so cool!".
I was attending middle school,
this episode has determined my destiny.
I decided to do this for a living.
So, I finished my degree in mechanical engineering, thn I did a PhD in bioengineering and started to do research.
At the beginning I did traditional research,
which I defined incremental.
I studied replacement parts, joints in particular, orthopedic endoprosthesis, and I sought ways to make them more and more functional but also more appealing and developed.
Yet when I was doing traditional research, a new research paradigm was emerging. A new, promising and interesting one.
It was the idea of replacing malfunctioning or damaged body parts with materials that aren't completely artificial.
Partially artificial perhaps, possibly biodegradable, but here's the innovation: powered by cells harvested from the very sick patient.
This also seemed so cool to me.
Especially stem cells.
Stem cells are those cells in our body, they are everywhere, that retain their form: but when needed, they can differentiate into tissue cells that must be repaired or regenerated.
I started to do this kind of research,
hence I totally converted to biology.
Briefly, I had to do ten years of research on cells, and I had never saw one cell in my whole life.
I had to start from the very beginning, I learned how to use microscopes and still I was charmed by this microscopic world.
A new scientific theory slowly dawned on me.
Although it was a new subject, I was able to see, thanks to my engineer mentality, a new feature of stem cells,
a new category of problems.
I'll tell you briefly.
You can see on the left
that a cell has a nucleus, which is were DNA is stored,
our genetic code.
Yet which genes -- stretches of DNA -- should be expressed -- used or not -- in the cell?
This decision relies on small molecules -- the green dots -- which enter the nucleus with a signaling purpose, they land on the DNA and decide the parts of DNA that must be transcribed.
This process regulates the functionality of the cell.
Under normal conditions, the cell is wadded up and molecules are outside.
Over the years I developed this idea: the entrance regulation of these dots relied on the deformation of the cell.
As the cell nucleus has a membrane with holes, called nuclear pores, I figured out, by stretching the membrane pores would have expanded.
This seemingly intuitive notion is actually a breakthrough.
It's revolutionary, because biology is still a "chemical" field that walks on thin air, where forces, deformations, pressures, mechanics in general is stepping in, albeit slowly.
Thus that was a great idea, yet at this point I needed to demonstrate it, I needed to either prove or deny this hypothesis.

And it was not so easy.
I needed a system to control the shape of the cell.
Which is microscopical,
invisible to the human eye.
Think of a microscopical water drop.
And I got lucky.
I met, by chance, a group of physicists, who had developed a very fine technology to control the shape of microscopical objects.
With this idea I managed to win my first ERC, and it was a big deal, also for my life.
Like winning the World Cup for a football player.
Which were the ingredients for this result?
I think they were basically three.
I call the first ingredient "mentoring".
I addressed to, and relied on scientists that were much better than me,
For instance, in the USA there were people already acquainted with mechanobiology.
I stalked them at conferences, but mostly by e-mail.
I wrote to a very relevant scientist,
by e-mail I wrote her: "This is my idea" -- that of the nuclear pore.
"Would you like to join me in a Human Fringe Science Program?".
It was a hallucinated project.
Somehow she replied and told me: "Yes I'm interested, but explain it further".
And she asked me questions that were specific and difficult.
And the answers to these questions are still central for the ERC project I won.
Other episodes, similar to this one, are fun.
There's one with a geneticist.

He is older than me, much better than me.
I told him: "This is my idea".
He baffled silently for almost a minute, while staring at me.
He's not much of a talker, but there he was speechless. And then he told me, at the end of this terrible long time: "Look, it's the most unconventional way I've ever heard of to deal with this.
But you could be right."
These are important phenomena, situations where a person that's willing to plunge into a subject that he doesn't master, connects with mentors, with more knowledgeable people. They're like a pavement to walk on, so at least you don't freefall.
The other important element is transdisciplinarity.
We are all acknowledged with the notion of multidisciplinarity,
when many differently skilled people tackle the same issue.
Transdisciplinarity is much more.
It's about changing your own field of competence.
Like me, an engineer that became a biologist 20 years ago.
I'm not an engineer anymore, I'm something more and different.
Yet I'm not a biologist, I speak the "biology language", but I can't become a biologist.
This is another relevant aspect, speaking another's language and being able to communicate.
The third element necessary for the result is extremely important to me, it's a thing we call vision, very hard to explain, yet Lewis Carroll explained it well in his wonderful book, "Alice in Wonderland".
Alice says:
"There's no use trying, one can't believe in impossible things."
And the Queen, her mentor at that time, says: "Daresay you haven't had much practice.
When I was you age, I've believed as many as six impossible things before breakfast."
So it's all about practice.
Over the years, I practiced this kind of exercise a lot. and I managed to find suggestions on how to practice at best.
A proven strategy is to systematically dismiss those you call you back to concreteness.
Along with many kinds of discourse, like -
First of all you have a job,
what job is it?
Now it has become "design engineer of organoids".
Then even more recently: "What are you doing with biology, do something you know.
You aren't respected enough to be published.
No one publish the work you're doing outside you competence."
These statements are falsely sensible, because they don't bring anything great or new with them.
Paradoxically, in order to overcome some harsh frontiers the only truly realistic vision is utopia.
Thanks.
(Applause)
I’ve spent the last ten years on the beach.
And before you start to envy me too much, I actually spent them picking up rubbish and trying to figure out what all the plastic I found on the beach was, where it came from, and how to stop it.
A lot of things just didn’t add up, and still don’t add up today: how did a refrigerator end up on the beach? A telephone? A hairdryer?
And what really puzzled me was why so many people went to the beach to clean their ears.
I truly couldn’t explain that.
What I did understand, however, is that only a small proportion of the plastic we find on the beach comes from the sea.
Most of it comes from the coasts and inland,
and a large part, half of the objects we find on the beach, are actually everyday disposable items.
They are the sum of lots of small everyday habits each of us shares, multiplied millions of times.
Thankfully, plastic sea pollution has now finally become a hot topic, even if it the alarm is seemingly raised more for the islands of plastic in the Pacific than for the ones in our own sea.
So, as Luca rightly said, one thing we have also understood, or at least I have understood, is that plastic is a wonderful material.
We should not demonize it.
Our cars and our airplanes are extremely light now and run on very little fuel.
Plastic has saved many animals: think of tortoises and elephants.
It also saves our own lives.
Plastic is not the real problem, it’s how we are using plastic, which is a fantastic, useful, light and strong material, and we use it for purposes with a very short duration.
Plus, it is so cheap that we forget and do not worry about or pay attention to how we dispose of it.
So, from this idea of trying to understand how to resolve this problem, we have created an European project involving a great alliance between all the sea lovers, sailors, yachtsmen, fishermen, sport people, students, teachers and citizens,
and we are asking them all to do three very simple things - pick it up instead of dumping it; and try to use as little disposable plastic as possible.
These measures are extremely simple, perhaps even dull.
However, let’s just see what “do not dump it” means - for example, not dumping means being more careful.
I’m pretty sure, none of you dumps garbage bags in the street, right?
But when we go to the beach, and leave a bag in an overflowing bin, it is exactly the same thing, because it only takes a dog, a cat or a bird to open the bag and all the rubbish inside scatters around and soon ends up in the sea.
In these 18 minutes of chat, two and half million cigarettes will be smoked in Italy.
And judging from the floors, as we see in every town, or the beaches, many of the cigarettes end up on the ground.
So, smokers, please be more careful, because those cigarette stubs are full of toxic substances, and it takes really very little to eliminate some of the most frequent garbage on beaches - cigarette stubs and lighters.
These look like harmless, cheerful, colourful items, but they are still plastic.
As much as a third of the typical beach rubbish comes from people who have gone to the beach for fun - so toys, pens, glasses, sun cream and whatnot, or snacks.
So again, please take note: with a bit more consideration, we can eliminate a large part of this refuse.
However, attention to the sea cleanliness must also start from inland.
Balloons are an example: everything that goes up sooner or later pops and falls down.
And when they fall into the sea, balloons assume the same shape and motion as jellyfish or calamari, which are the favourite foods of fish, turtles and birds.
So let's enjoy balloons, but please don’t let them fly.
Attention for the sea also begins in our homes.
We should not throw anything into our toilets, because these are some of the waste items we find most often on beaches.
And so we have "solved" the puzzle - people weren't coming to the beach cleaning their ears: they all cleaned their ears then threw the cotton buds in the toilet, not the waste bin.
With a little more attention, we can eliminate a significant amount of waste.
Every day, a cloud of plastic fragments goes from our showers down to the sea: they are called exfoliants.
See from these photographs how much plastic is contained in a single bottle.
Thankfully, beginning next year they will be prohibited, but we can already stop buying them: just look at the label, and if it says “polyethylene”, avoid it.
Rivers are our garbage's favourite way to end up in the sea,
so we must pay special attention how close we are to rivers.
This is just to show you the eternal stream of garbage that is carried to the sea by rivers The moment a small plug build up under the arches of a bridge, and we can effectively see how much plastic comes from inland.
And finally, we should also pay attention in emptying our attics: are we sure grandma’s swinging chair, or our nephew’s tricycle, really ends up at the recycling station and is not dumped under the nearest bridge?
Be careful who give our objects to.
Picking up well is fundamental, because no country in the world has the economic capacity to go and clean the oceans.
The best time to clean a beach is in winter, when there are storms.
And why should we pick up?
Because by now we have truly infinite proof of the harm that plastic can cause to animals
that get inside or get trapped, eat it or suffocate, not to mention other things, like the cleaning costs.
Garbage is truly a danger for shipping. We focus a lot on the islands of plastic in the Pacific, while our Mediterranean Sea hosts the same concentration of microplastics as these well-known islands.
The reason is easy to explain - our sea is a lake, everything that ended up in it is still there,
and if we no longer see it, it is simply because it is fragmented.
So let’s pick up objects before they break up, otherwise, if we leave them on the beach, the combined action of sun, salt and waves will inexorably decompose them until fragments are so small that it is truly impossible to recuperate them.
Do not think that these are less harmful, quite the opposite, they are perhaps even more insidious and dangerous because they ultimate get small enough to be eaten by plankton, which is the first link
of marine food chain.
So let’s pick up. Pick up by ourselves.
Sergio has just suggested, “let's take two bags, when we go for a swim",
and involve our friends.
Luckily, we have involved many divers in our great project, because what we have found on the beach, and what you have seen on the beach seems horrible; but actually, most garbage is deep at the bottom of the sea.
So divers are the only ones who are able to go and clean the seabed.
If taking care and picking up are two staple strategies, the most important thing of all is to reconsider our relationship with plastics.
Without being fanatic - I am probably dressed in plastic - but half the beach trash is single use stuff.
I repeat, we are using material that is virtually eternal for purposes with a very short duration.
We have become somewhat inebriated by this material that is cheerful and comes in so handy in everyday life.
We must to learn, however, not to use it but for those purposes when such a robust material makes sense.
Each of us, in our daily life, should find the way to reduce the use of disposable plastic, and replace it with materials such as glass, for example.
Please note, though, that bioplastic is a solution, but not "the" solution.
Bioplastic bags do decompose, but in industrial composting conditions. If they end up in the sea, it takes months, if not years, to decompose;
and if an animal eats it, in the meantime,
she is just as doomed. We find on beaches lots of bottles' bodies and caps,
and this is one kind of refuse we can most easily eliminate.
Every Italian, each one of us, consumes an average of 200 litres of bottled water a year, in a country like Italy where water quality is generally excellent.
There is a very simple answer: let’s use a flask and stop buying and using all this bottled waters.
It’s a very quick way to reduce our use of disposable plastic.
There are those who ask me,
But what if I recycle my bottle?
Well, the plastic bottle, the plastic in the water bottle, will never become, once recycled, a bottle again; it will become something else, even more so if the bottle is coloured.
That plastic will become a shopping bag, a pile or a bench -
but how many benches do we actually need?
And anyway, eventually it will be disposed of, so we are just delaying a bit the disposal of all this garbage,
Fact is, we're not able to dispose of all this garbage, all this trash we're generating.
So reduction is the preferred strategy.
Reduction in packaging, especially: let's buy as many unpackaged goods as possible.
Remember when we used bars of soap instead of liquid soap,
let’s try to avoid situations such as these, which are frankly absurd.
It really takes very little, just a bit of awareness to reduce our use and abuse of plastic.
These are just some of the thousands of ideas that can lead in this direction.
Is this enough to save the sea?
Obviously not, because such a complex problem does not have a silver bullet.
The issue of plastic in the sea comes from ineffective, sometimes illegal, certainly insufficient waste management. But as we have seen, much of trash is generated in our mindless, everyday behaviour,
and without us, without a daily effort by our side, like this for example, the situation will certainly not improve.
The good news, as you can see, is that it really takes very little, just a little awareness.
And so - over the past two years, since this project began, we have involved more than 10.000 people, who have picked up more than 10 tons of things.
Imagine what we could achieve, if we got to 100,000 people.
We are helping the Italian government and the European Union to simplify the issue of trash, and we are helping fishermen to bring back the garbage they collect in their nets at sea.
We have met thousands of children who have made a promise to the sea, from not launching balloons to dismiss single use plastic cups.
We have convinced three mayors to prohibit the launching of balloons, and thanks to our work, Italy prohibited the use of plastic ear buds early this year, and next year will prohibit the use of microplastics in cosmetics.
So is this enough?
No, it’s not, we have to keep it up.
Certainly, had we started 20 years ago, everything would be much easier now.
However, there is a wonderful Chinese proverb that says, “The best time to plant a tree, and enjoy its shade, was 20 years ago: the next best time is now”.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Lately, how often have you heard: "We can't say anything anymore.
We used to laugh about it, now everyone gets offended." This is flawed thinking: thinking that no one got offended before.
Thinking that if something was unseen before, but now is out there, it didn't exist.
It has actually always been there, it just couldn't manifest before.
I'm sorry for what I'm about to say. I'll be straightforward but only because I want to be clear.
You couldn't say "faggot" even in the 80s.
Same goes for the n-word or "whore."
When sensitivity grows on some topics, it's because now, finally, we can hear those people's voices who have always been sensitive to those topics.
In the past the only voices you could hear belonged to people with power - let's admit it, not women, not black people, not the LGBT community - lesbians, gays, bisexuals, transgenders and more.
This is because it was who had the power who decided if and when to turn the microphones on, and so you could only hear the same voices over and over.
Now, with the internet, each one of us has a microphone, each one of us can potentially speak to the entire word.
That's how on Twitter, a powerless person's thought trends because he has the power to say it.
That's how a video of a minority that says, "Things are not OK" goes viral.
That's how a business releases a sexist, homophobic or racist commercial, and fails because millions of voices whispering that "things are not OK" get really loud when microphoned.
You may think that to avoid such slip-ups, you just need to know the rules of the game.
"OK, we can't say this word anymore, and we need to tone this stereotype down.
It's full of guys here, can someone
bring a woman? A random one.
It's better if she's not white, so we look inclusive.
Come on guys, you know how this works." And yet, there is someone who has the power, who knows these rules of the game, who still happens to offend a minority.
And he apologizes, without fully understanding what he's apologizing for, but at least he does.
If it goes bad, he plays the we-can't-say-anything-anymore card, trying to be the victim.
You know why this happens?
Because it's not enough to just know the rules of the game, because they are ruled by other rules.
If we want to move forward, we have to know the three rules of the rules of the game.
Rule number one: if something is not about you, you don't make the rules.
Meaning that it's not a white person who decides what racism is; it's not a heterosexual who decides what homophobia is; it's not a man who decides what sexism is.
Often, I've heard some black friends of mine complaining about topics that were not-at-all problematic to me.
"Have you seen the fundraising campaign for Africa?
It's so demeaning." My brain went haywire.
"What do you mean 'demeaning'?
It's a fundraiser campaign for Africa. They give money to Africa."
"That celebrity that keeps on posting pictures of her with black children in villages, when will she drop it?" "Sorry, what do you mean, When will she drop it?
This person is loaded with money, could spend her life on vacation. Instead she goes there to help and you say, 'When does she drop it?'" This first reaction is normal.
We feel like it's crazy-talking.
And it actually is.
It is crazy-talking, to us, if we're white.
To us, such things are positive, so when we hear a black person complaining about them, it's easy to think that that person is too sensitive, ungrateful even.
This happens when we forget rule number one: if something is not about you, you don't make the rules.
It's not a white person who decides what racism is because it's difficult to recognize racism if you're not a victim of it, because you're not trained to see it.
It took me a while, but then I started trusting this rule and instead of saying, "You're crazy," I started asking, "Why?"
Why is the fundraising campaign for Africa demeaning?
Why should the celebrity who posts pictures on Instagram of her with black kids in villages drop it? (Laughs)
The fundraising campaign for Africa is demeaning because it narrates Africa in one way only: a continent that all looks the same, where they can't save themselves.
It's demeaning because it exploits black bodies to pity people for those two minutes necessary to send a text and donate one euro. But it doesn't inspire any kind of cultural change.
It doesn't push Africa forward, and it doesn't push us forward either.
The celebrity who posts on Instagram pictures of her with black kids in villages needs to drop it because she's acting as white savior.
"There she is: look at this angel from the sky who came to help us.
She's so pure, look, she could be anywhere but she's here instead." And it doesn't matter if we have no idea where "here" is, and it doesn't matter if we have no clue of who the local professionals are -
who work hard every day to improve the situation from the inside, and who'll keep on doing it even when the spotlights are gone because the spotlights follow her, not them.
Boom.
How many things I never thought of before!
How many women have we called cold because they didn't laugh at one of our jokes?
How many people in the LGBT community have we stereotyped as "too sensitive" just because they didn't go to the cinema to see that movie that pictures them as comic devices?
Think about how many things we could have learned if only we had known before that the rules of the game, when it is not about us, are not ours to make.
If we only had asked, "Why?"
Rule number two: if you want to play, you must follow the rules.
The one thing that made me fight the most in my life is the streak of +2 and +4 that happen whenever I play UNO.
The game card, you know?
I play a +2 and you draw two cards.
No way you play another +2 to force me to draw four cards.
Now, you can't do it.
And you can't do it because in every UNO deck there is a rule sheet.
One of the rules is that if you get a +2, you draw two cards and skip your turn.
In no way can we start this chain letter or +2 and +4: you can't do it.
And I don't want to hear, "Well, that's how I play," because then, what are rules for?
And then, let's suppose you want to change them - I don't get why since the rules are made by who makes the game - you have to state it beforehand.
You can't decide it mid game
and expect me to get on board with it. Out of metaphor. When we meet a person of the LGBT community, and we use the term "faggot," we can't expect them to be OK with it.
Because "faggot" is an insult, and it should not be used in any case.
Some gay friends of mine call one another "faggot."
This used to confused me at first: "Is it wrong only if I use it?" given that some people don't even like it inside the community. But anyway, one thing is if it's a gay guy who says it, it's to disempower the term.
"You wanted to insult me with that word?
You're wasting your time; I already called myself that." Another thing is if I, who doesn't belong to that community, claims the right to use that word just because "my friends do it," or, "Some gay friends of mine aren't bothered when I use it."
When I'm with mom and dad we have the rule that it's OK to add a +2 and a +4, but when I play with someone else, I don't assume that that rule - which is actually an exception - is OK for someone else too.
This works in bigger terms too.
For example, in the making of products, movies and songs for the LGBT community. They are all made a certain way.
Now, if the LGBT community tells you that that way is not OK, you can only change it.
Or, you could find shelter in the old overdone excuse, "My heart was in the right place."
Having your heart in the right place doesn't work for one simple reason: rule number three.
It's not enough to have your heart in the right place.
This is the most difficult rule of all.
Because when you do something for someone, it's hard to accept that that thing might not be OK, or damaging, even, for the person you did it for.
There is a difference that might not be clear but it's crucial to understand this thought.
Intention and consequences don't always correspond.
Picture this.
You meet a long lost friend and you tell her, "You look so good, you've lost a lot of weight."
And she goes, "Well, yes, I've lost some weight because I'm not particularly fine lately.
I actually hoped it wasn't so obvious."
Ouch.
Your intention was to compliment her, because saying, "You look slimmer" is still considered a compliment - we'd need a whole other TED Talk about it.
The consequences - (Applause) The consequences are a disaster.
Does this make us sadistic monsters who like to see other people suffering?
Of course not.
But the fact that we didn't mean to hurt her is not enough because, in fact, she is hurting anyway.
Yes, we didn't know it, it's not our fault.
But it still is our responsibility because it's us who said such a thing, even with our heart in the right place.
How do we leave this loop?
We apologize.
Saying, "I really didn't mean to bring back such hurtful memories.
In fact, if there is something I can to do make you feel better, I'd gladly do it. Really, I'm sorry."
Same thing happens when a man, trying to compliment a woman, ends up harrassing her.
Just the other day, I was looking at my Instagram messages.
I found one from a guy saying how pretty I was and how much he liked my full lips.
Now, I don't know this guy's intent.
I want to be optimistic, thinking that it was his way of complimenting me.
But I felt truly uncomfortable.
It was morning, I had just taken my phone, and the first thing I had to see was this message from a stranger.
It felt like an invasion of my privacy.
"Why are you saying this to me?
Why do you think I care?" If you're a man, talk with the women of your life.
They will all have such stories to tell you.
And lots of them use the same words: "I felt dirty, guilty, accountable, shameful."
Do you understand that good intentions are not enough?
If a woman tells you, "I felt uncomfortable," your "I didn't mean to" only serves to let her know your intention, it doesn't make you immune from critics.
Yet, every time such things happen in private and in public, it's always the same rigmarole.
"My heart was in the right place,
so if you felt uncomfortable, it's your problem because you're too sensitive." Well, not really.
If you want to be useful to another person, you have to assume that that person knows better than you on this.
You have to trust his or her words.
And if you make a mistake - and humans make mistakes - you apologize and try again.
If you care about playing this game.
If you don't, you can suggest another game.
One where you make the rules, you change them as you like, and if you lose, you cancel the game.
But then you have to find someone willing to play with you.
Good luck.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good evening! Good evening!

Let's move this a little further.
Well, I'm very glad to talk to you here, this evening, in this amazing place in this charming Region and I want to tell you a story.
It's a story that can help to improve, maybe, the situation.
At least, it can maybe help to see things in a different way, improving it.
It can help to see things from a different point of view, from a different side.
The theme of streets, of the revolution of the streets it's a theme that really fascinated me because in the streets I found some conclusions but also some goals that I never would have imagined in life.
You know, the street brought me to Rome.
In Rome, thanks to my work, I met a group of people that has some problems with justice and the group is led by a man that, with respect, I'd like to call "the pirate".
And this man, together with his group, with his mates, some time ago, almost one year ago, was arrested for mafia association.
This man was well known in some contexts not only in the criminal world but also in the Roman society.
Then, from there, it became clear that he was known in other worlds too.
There was a so-called "upper-world", a "middle-world", an "under-world".
He was telling us this.
These people were arrested, and they are accused of mafia association, therefore, of being in the mafia.
Be careful.
When you say the word "mafia", you need to be careful, because to the Romans and to the people of the "upper-world" and of the "middle-world" they don't like the word, or at least you can't say it
But the judges have arrested this person for mafia association.
This situation brought me to another street, many years ago, 30 years ago, in Sicily, where there was another rumor on the streets, that was saying "mafia doesn't exist, there is no mafia".
I remember very well, in Palermo, the day after the killing of the prefect general Carlo Alberto Dalla Chiesa, together with his wife, Emanuela Setti Carraro, what came out, what was said amongst the people, mafia killing, what was said in Palermo was that the mafia, the real mafia, it's in Rome.
And then, it has happened many times to go for work, or for other reasons, in Milan, in Turin, but mainly in Milan and talking about what I was doing, but especially seeing some situations even in the north, in the past, some people from Milan, people from the north, listening to me talking about mafia and saying, "Watch out, it's coming here, mafia is already here, Cosa Nostra settled down, Andrangheta already has some members here in the area", they were saying, "No, mafia is in Rome".
The street brought me to Rome, and once in Rome, let's see where the mafia is.
In Palermo they told me that mafia is in Rome, in the north they say that mafia is in Rome, let's go to Rome and see where the mafia is.
After a short time, looking at things differently, wearing special glasses, maybe glasses from the south, I suddenly realized that there was a mafia method, there was a group of people acting like mafia and I immediately identified these people, and then, as a journalist, I was talking about them.
I denounced them in the papers and these people tried to understand what kind of person I am and we studied each other.
They wanted to take other measurements, touch wood, but luckily they didn't manage.
But, you never know, in the south of Italy spells always help.
Well, I had a revolutionary idea about my street, they had a different one.
Then, I tried to reveal what for me was a mafia method.
It is true, there was no horse head or similar things in the bed of a manager or someone important.
No, there was nothing.
But we found something else in Rome, we found the same symbols, we found the same methods.
And something incredible came to my mind, that, as it was always happening in Sicily: "Mafia does not exist, there is no mafia and so on", but in Sicily there was another important boss, in Catania, Calderone, who owned some gas stations.
In Rome it was the same.
But it's not because you own a gas station, that you're managing a criminal organization.

No, but there are some similarities.
The problem is that these people in Rome, especially this one who has only one eye and for this reason they call him the pirate, he became famous for his actions.
People were and are really afraid of him.
Then, I tried to meet some people
that were collaborating with him.
The problem is that, when you get closer
and ask "Do you know Carminati?"
"Ah..Carminati? Absolutely not"
You should not say this name.
And this thing brought me to another symbology that in many places in Sicily, many years ago, where there were some important mafia men absconding, in Corleone there were some, people were scared of even naming those important names, that were scaring you.
And from then, you discover that this man, Carminati, in Rome, he not only hates some journalists, but also he hates some business men.
And these business men, who were not following his rules ended up badly.
I discovered that Carminati threatened them openly, while they were together with their kids, while these people were bringing the kids to school, and this man didn't give a damn.
He didn't care, actually he was doing it on purpose, because the more you impress, the more you scare the businessman, especially when he's together with his kid, the better results you get.
That also brought me back in time.
When you threaten someone, you give a sign to someone, you do it in front of the kid.
I'm not only coming in front of you, but I also know who your son is, which school he goes to, what he does.
It's a mafia method.
Despite that, in Rome you can't talk about mafia.
Despite that, in Rome there is no mafia.
Yes, in fact it's called capital mafia.
And it's true, we can't compare it to cosa nostra or to 'ndrangheta.
Cosa nostra and 'ndrangheta are traditional mafia, like the sacra corona unita.
What we are talking about now in Rome is capital mafia.
It has the same method, it has the same structure: there is a boss, an operative section, an economic section, but especially there is a political section.
And I'd like to know why a structure like that should not be called mafia.
Someone explains to me, tries to make me understand, that maybe these people don't want to be called mafia, it's maybe because the people investigated today for mafia, until short time ago were sitting in the rooms, in the terraces of Rome and many maybe didn't realize that they were close to someone that has the power to control an economy, a politician in the capital city, to corrupt it.
As it was happening in Palermo, where bosses were sitting in the same rooms of the business men.
And I'd like to see things from a different point of view, to see things in a different way compared to how someone wants us to see things and I'd like that we'd trust in better information.
For this reason I come back to the original topic: I'd like, with your help, to see things in a different way, to face them differently, improving them.
If there is someone that wants to block this information, and someone wants to block it really badly, these people must be stopped, denounced, we should shine a light on them.
Because you know, in Sicily, nine journalists were killed by mafia, and not because they were hit by a stray bullet but because the mafia killed them because they were exposing the dealings of mafia men, not because they were saying that someone was a mafia man.
And they were killed.
Others then followed, they tried to describe the situation but without the support of the society.
The society was scared.
This is what there is now, in the capital, fear and indifference.
There is fear and indifference of a big and evolved society, that in 2015 we can't allow ourselves to keep our mouths shut and our eyes closed.
With your help we need to support those who are denouncing it, but we need also, and I repeat once again, to look at things from a different point of view, as we know in the south, as people who suffer those abuses know it and of those criminal organizations, as people that live in fear of these criminal organizations.
And it's only thanks to the union of all the people, being united, solid, thanks to the help of society, that we can face the situation.
If a person is alone, the mafia wins, the mafia beats him, the mafia makes him a target.
When the mafia sees a white fly, it smashes it, or at least tries to do it.
It's in the information, it's in the politics, and also in the bureaucracy, in the employees, the public employees.
Because the situation has to change there as well.
Because if a mafia like the mafia capital got stronger and took hold of politics, took hold of business, it's because it has used a weapon, the only powerful weapon of today, which is corruption.
And corruption has evolved and still evolves today, because it's in our head, in our mentality.
Because finding disloyal employees in the public sector, we have in our minds that we can influence the employee, make him change his mind, giving him something, bribing him.
A bribe today, a bribe tomorrow, it makes a heap of bribes, and this heap stinks.
Because then, you see, the weight of corruption is dispersed on the shoulders of society and when you put this weight on the society, the society pays for it, because the society pays taxes, because it pays with the lack of services, with public works of bad quality, because someone has to remove this money from the total expenditure.
And then, the weapon that is used today by this new mafia that we call capital mafia, but also from other mafia, is corruption.
A new mentality, a different one, clear, correct, used by everyone, can help to reduce corruption, to disarm the mafia, to make poorer those who want to become mafia men.
And for this we need your help.
We need the backing of everyone to support those who fight the mafia, to help the victims and those who are afraid of mafia, of the mafia method, and to avoid possible white flies.
No more white flies, but more culture in our head, culture and information.
Let's support good information.
Thanks.
(Applause)
Last year, for Christmas, I was in Italy.
My mom was at the hospital, affected by a final stage lung cancer.
As I started to have a bad feeling, so I decided to get some checkups myself.
It was during one of such checkups, a senologic visit, that I discovered I had breast cancer.
It was January 2019.
In no more than two weeks, I had an exact diagnosis - infiltrating ductal carcinoma -
and the full characterization of my tumor: what type of cells, what receptors were expressed, the size of the tumor, the exact position in the breast.
In March, two months after my diagnosis, I underwent the surgery to remove the tumor.
In May, I started radiotherapy.
Meanwhile my tumor was further analyzed.
When the results of such analysis came back, it was another tough time for me. The tumor was small, with clean lymphnodes - good news - but it was much more aggressive than initially thought - (bad news).
Basically a Sardinian tumor, just like me: tiny but fierce.
The risk was that the tumor could come back. Some of my doctors suggested chemotherapy,
but others did not agree. To decide what to do, my medical board opted for yet another analysis, a genetic test.
This test analyzes the expression of 21 genes in the tumor, and based on the results can predict what is the risk of recurrence.
If the result is "high", chemotherapy is recommended.
My result came back in June as "intermediate" -
I did not have to do chemo.
In June I resumed a normal life, and here I am today.
(Applause) Thank you!
I got lucky. Just 20 years ago, default breast cancer treatment was chemotherapy for all patients; and it often was not effective.
Very soon it was noticed not all patients respond the same.
Many patients took the drug, experienced the devastating side effects, with no real benefit.
The scientific community started to ask why.
Why some women respond, while others don't?
Today we know why.
Today we know that there is a huge variability in breast cancers. No two identical breast cancers are there, basically.
And this is why they do not respond equally to the same treatment.
For instance, there are tumors, like mine, that express high levels of the receptor for the hormone estrogen,
and can be treated with hormonal therapy.
Other tumors express another receptor, called HER-2, and these other tumors can be treated with a drug called herceptine, that acts on this receptor.
Based on the expression of 21 genes, a tumor's response to chemotherapy can be predicted.
This is what we call Precision Medicine, and it has revolutionized oncology.
Precision Medicine means providing each patient a specific treatment, optimized for the type of tumor that the patient has.
In my case, Precision Medicine has meant going in six months from an exact diagnosis to a tailored treatment.
Six months!
Let me ask you something: Do you know how long it takes to have a diagnosis of a neurodegenerative disease like Parkinson or Alzheimer?
I'll give you a few numbers: multiple sclerosis, two years; Parkinson, one year; amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, one year; Alzheimer two years.
And this is just for the diagnosis.
When the diagnosis arrives, after one or two years, there is no cure for these diseases: they are untreatable.
My question is: why?
Why we cannot go in six months, from the diagnosis to the treatment of a condition like Parkinson?
If we managed to do it for breast cancer, why we cannot for brain diseases?
A possible answer that springs to mind is that the brain is a complex organ.
Now, that is true: as a neuroscientist, I can confirm this.
However, it is not the only reason.
and this is what I'd like
to talk about today.
I think neurology currently lacks a precision medicine approach as we have it for oncology.
We do not have in neurology a precision medicine approach because we just assumed we do not need it.
We have assumed until now that all patients presenting with a given with neurological diseases are similar.
But is it so?
Let's take the example of Alzheimer. It's possible to show disease progression in a single patient with such a graph.
Here you have time, and here a measure of memory
(low values mean loss of memory).
Let's take one patient, Mr Carlo.
Mr Carlo had his diagnosis, and then his memory was tested at regular intervals for three years.
As you can see, there were some ups and downs, but overall his memory deteriorated.
What happens if we look at the data of over 200 patients?
This is what we see -
these are published data.
What do you see?
Do all these patients look alike to you?
To me they don't.
I see patients who stay stable for years.. Some deteriorate drastically, some others more slowly. A few even improve over time.
How come?
to have all this variability when they all had the same diagnosis of Alzheimer?
What happens is that the current diagnosis of Alzheimer we can offer to our patients is a clinical diagnosis, based on symptoms.
In Alzheimer, memory loss is the key symptom.
All these patients, the day of the visit, all showed a similar memory impairment
However, I can tell you: while having a similar symptom, most likely these patients do not have the same brain pathology.
For instance, some patients present in their cerebral cortex the accumulation of a toxic peptide called beta amyloid, in the form of plaques, which can be visualized with imaging techniques such as PET.
The presence of this plaques is a clear diagnostic sign of Alzheimer.
However there are patients that have exactly the same symptoms, indistinguishable from Alzheimer, but in their brains they donìt have amyloid plaques
but rather another toxic peptide, called TDP-43.
This dementia type is called LATE and was just discovered.
Magnetic resonance allows us to examine alterations in the mass and structure of the brain and identify damages at the vascular level,
which can also cause memory decline.
Therefore, here we can see the importance of precision medicine. It is obvious that it makes no sense to treat all patients, which have different pathologies, the same way.
It would make little sense giving the same drug to all patients, let's say an anti-amyloid treatment: only those who have amyloid will respond to that drug,
the others won't.
This surprised, However, but this is how we have been conducting clinical trials in the past ten years, and this because until a few years ago patients had no way to be characterized.
Today is different.
Today we have biomarkers, thanks to the technological advancements, that allow us to characterize at the molecular levels a patient's pathology.
Being able to characterize the brain pathology of each patient is the precondition to start a precision medicine in neurology. But it is not enough.
Like in my case, when I was diagnosed with infiltrating ductal carcinome.
That was the beginning:
additional factors were needed to design and optimize my treatment.
You need to know the genetics, the age of the patient, the social status also plays a role.
And what about the sex
of Alzheimer's patients?
In 2006, out of curiosity, with two researcher friends of mine we started to collect data on sex differences in Alzheimer.
And you know what we found out?
The majority of clinical studies did not report sex differences.
The vast majority of preclinical studies, those conducted on lab animals, used animals of one sex, only males or only females,
and therefore could not determine sex differences.
There are no data, it has not been reported, whether experimental drugs currently tested for Alzheimer work equally in men and women.
We asked a lot of questions and we first found a strong opposition.
At first we were asked,
Why should there be differences?
We found a lot of walls. But we also found enthusiastic colleagues.
And it was thanks to this enthusiasm that we founded a non profit organization called Women's Brain Project, with the aim of stimulating a scientific discussion on the topic.
Today, three years later, also thanks to WBP activity, several, crucial sex differences have been reported in the literature in Alzheimer as well as in other diseases.
For instance, we have found out that women with a diagnosis of Alzheimer progress much faster than men.
And look, this is a crucial information to have, because it means that treatment will have to be tailored taking sex into account.
Today, we are not asked anymore, "And why should there be differences?"
Today they ask us, "How can we use these differences?"
And our answer is: precision medicine.
If we aim to identifying new methods for diagnosis, prevention and cure of Alzheimer, the variability between patients should be better known,
and this means also knowing differences between men and women.
The good news is that today we have all the tools to launch precision medicine in neurology.
We have the biomarkers that allow us to fully characterize the patients; we have the artificial intelligence that will help us making sense this huge amount of data, and we have the insights into what needs to be considered in the analysis, such as sex of patient.
If we launched today a precision medicine in neurology, we could potentially change the medical practice of tomorrow.
You know how I picture a neurologic visit in the future?
The patient will take a seat, and the doctor, after having checked all the clinical symptoms, will collect all of the relevant data - genetics information, biomarkers, co-pathologies, even gut microbiome and the sex, of course. The doctor will then input
all these data into an algorithm,
and with a click, he will be able to define the subtype of Alzheimer specific of that patient.
Based on such subtype, the doctor will be able to recommend a specific treatment, personalized and optimized for that patient - for that man or that woman.
Will we finally manage to diagnose and treat Alzheimer in the space of six months?
With precision medicine, in my opinion, it will take even less than six months.
Thank you.
(Applause)
This is our world,
the world we want to explore to get a chance of living better and happier, because this is what we all want.
However, we have to study this world to know it better, and in order to study, we first have to measure the world we want to know, and then study it.
Studying the world means, in turn, measuring the world, which is precisely what cartographers always dealt with.
Cartography has always dealt with measuring the world, and then representing it, so we can know it.
This way of measuring the world has stayed the same for centuries: these two pictures, as you can see, were taken in different moments in history. But world used to be mapped from below, anyway. Then, at the beginning of the last century, we started to fly, thanks to Wright Brothers' experiments, and we started to take images of our world from above, first from the airplanes' wings, then from satellites - 1957, Sputnik - and now a new era is beginning now, with Unmanned Aerial Vehicle. Many people simply call them drones.
Let's go back to the satellites, today's cutting edge technology,
The last picture you see at the bottom right side is the Worldview 3 satellite.
Worldview 3 flies rather low, just about 600 km up there. It's equipped with many sensors, among which there's a particular one, a camera with which, we use to joke about, the Earth takes selfies.
These images are peculiar and spectacular, because even at 600 km of distance, we still can take Earth's images with a resolution of 30 cm.
So, any object on the Earth's surface that's bigger than 30 cm, is captured by Worldview 3,
but Worldview 3 is not the only satellite to monitor the Earth.
Here's a picture of the Earth surrounded by its operating satellites. It doesn't include all the bodies orbiting around the Earth, or we should add the so-called "debris", out-of-service satellites.
Each of those operational satellites has many sensors, and they monitor the Earth in real time.
However, we have to say that we cannot take these pictures and immediately use them to map our Earth, because, despite our computers' impressive "intelligence" they aren't yet, and they'll probably never be, as intelligent as humans, therefore we still need someone to put her eyes and brain on these pictures, in order to interpret them.
Cartography has changed very much, over the years, from a technological standpoint.
But its mission has also changed very much. Initially, cartography was developed for military or cadastral purposes, namely to map the world and impose taxation on properties.
Then things began changing in the 70s, and cartography got used for different purposes, like urban planning, land planning and so on.
But the real revolution happened 11 years ago – in 2004.
In 2004, a project called OpenStreetMap saw the light, the brainchild of Steve Coast, the guy you see here. He had just graduated in computer science at the University College of London.
Together with his friends, he started to work on this project of the world's first free map.
Their main idea spreads across the world immediately, mostly thanks to the movement for free and open source informative systems and geographic information, who immediately espoused the idea. Starting from just few contributors at the beginning of 2004, now they've grown up to more than 2 millions.
2 millions of people worldwide, who map the world cooperatively.
What are Steve's and OpenStreetMap's main ideas?
Firstly, the idea is that the geographic information --once called cartography, but now, in modern terms, is referred to as geographic information--
is getting pervasive: more and more events and phenomena only get their meaning when geographically contextualized.
Practical uses are car driving,
now increasingly aided with satellite navigators and maps, the individual navigation which will be more and more important, the location-based services, which are just beginning.
A second key idea is that if we want to use these data, if we want these data to be free, we have to create them by ourselves.
So, the main OpenStreetMap motto is that OpenStreetMap is created by people like you, to be freely used by anyone in the world.
And how can we create these maps?
They can be created individually, but it's much more funny and beautiful to make them together,
and so we can organize Mapping parties. This is the first word I want you to learn.
Mapping parties means meeting and mapping together with an aim,
such as the one you see on this picture. This is a mapping party in Osaka, to make the resilience map, so people, scientists, administrators, members of the local civil protection and so on, meet, go around the city and identify the areas to count on in case of emergencies, the critical areas etc., and they make a resilience map.
The motto is map the world.
But I want to tell you another story, the history of the terrible earthquake that destroyed Haiti.
Someone had to do something, but one should have known how to move on a territory whose morphology changed after the earthquake.
Some groups connected to OpenStreetMap, had the idea of getting satellite images at high resolution, with the help of the international organizations, uploaded these images on the OpenStreetMap platform which is a very technologically advanced platform. As you remember, Steve Coast is a computer scientist and remember that all was born in the free and open source geographic information movement.
So the platform is very complex, but its use is very easy, anyone can use it, there's no need to be a nerd,
the platform is suitable for children and even for grandparents.
So, these new images of the modified Haiti were put on this platform and a call was launched around the world. All the volunteers mapped
and in few hours the map of Haiti was ready.
That fundamental map helped understand how to move on the territory and helped save human lives.
After the event, task forces were formed all over the world and whenever a catastrophic event occurs, all the volunteers start mapping and make a layout of the reshaped territory.
However, we don't need to go farther for these things: the picture you see describes what happened here in Como at Politecnico Di Milano last springtime after Nepal's earthquake.
We met and there were many people, we learned how to map all together and we also contributed to create the map that volunteers needed to intervene in Nepal.
I have to say you are very lucky because this TEDx is held at the beginning of the Geographical Awareness Week.
A week decided by all those OSM organizations somehow dealing with OpenStreetMap.
Starting from 15th, there will be at least 100 events around the world.
Mapping events, which we call Mappathon, which is the second word I want you to learn.
Mappathon means meeting all together in a place, everyone with a PC because this is fundamental, to map all together,
So if you are interested, next Thursday, not faraway, in Milan, at Politecnico Di Milano, we will hold a mappathon,
and what will we deal about?
If we are unlucky there will be an emergency and we will work on that emergency; but if we are lucky, and nothing will happen, we will work on another beautiful project called Missing Maps.
The Missing map project is about those neglected areas, which are also fragile areas because of natural catastrophes or of other types of disasters that may happen. In general, they are also the world's poorest areas and so they don't have our infrastructures and tools.
What we can do during these mappathons is to make the maps of those areas around the world, in order to be ready should any catastrophe occur.
This community, this big and generous community, mostly made by volunteers, doesn't only deal with mapping in preparation to disasters or during the disasters:
we want to do something more, we want to have a positive attitude towards what happens.
So, there are several other ways of mapping our world.
Time is running out, so I only quote two alternatives: The first one is biodiversity,
especially in urban sector. We can't, nor we should, think that biodiversity is only in those faraway corners we don't know:
there's also a biodiversity very close to us and we must learn about it, appreciate it, and take care of it.
Urbangene project comes from Lausanne's Swiss Federal Institute of Technology. For this project, all collaborators are asked to take a computer and put in all the data on biodiversity around Lausanne.
The second way of mapping is based in Italy, it is a cultural and touristic project,
but a different kind of tourism, called slow tourism.
Slow tourism is similar to slow food, in that it implies a mindful use of the territory.
We don't want to invade the territory, but rather slowly move across it, walking around, riding, biking, so we can look around, and we can discover all the richness of the territory around us, richness that may have values only in our own eyes and may not have been taken into consideration by other people.
Or we can start to check if there are emergencies, and speak out.
All these things are summed up in a project called "I cammini Della via Regina"
Via Regina is an historical path, which starts in Italy and goes on to Northern Europe.
Via Regina road already existed in Roman times and many people walked through it.
From via Regina, located along the western side of Como Lake, many paths start and go towards Switzerland, borders paths that are rich in culture and history, where pilgrims, smugglers and people like us walked through.
We wanted to add a part of collaborative mapping to via Regina project, so there's an app you can find connecting to the Via Regina website. Going around these areas you can use this app and add your information so that these pieces of information are immediately mapped and available for everyone online, making a collaborative map, which grows day by day.
In conclusion, as you can see, there are many chances to join us with these mapping projects.
Once the cartographers used to leave and go around to explore and map new worlds.
Now cartographers can stay at home and take the world home and in their city.
And why do we do it?
Because all together want to participate and make a more free, shared, participated world.
So if you liked one of these stories, you can join us.
Now is your turn to be the next cartographers.
(Applause)
Realizing how lucky I was, has been a bit like lighting the fuse in my path.
It's 2014 and I'm sent to South Sudan,
the quintessential humanitarian context.
Here, they tell me,
"That'll teach you well";
there, they will say: "Look, they send the first missions here just because they can't say no".
I'm 25 years old, and so they send me to a hospital that Doctors Without Borders has been running for more than 20 years, so that I have time to understand and learn by observing.
But by the moment I understand I might not be up to my task, I find myself on a boat on the Sobat river, on my way to Ethiopia with my colleagues, to save our lives.
But how?
Wasn't supposed to go there and save the others?
It just so happened, the frontline where the two ethnicities of the civil conflict, the Dinka and the Nuer, clashed, had made a sudden leap forward towards the hospital.
And this, for us expats, was a sign that the evacuation plan had to be implemented no matter what.
For us expats: the lucky ones, once again.
As we travel I look across the river bank and in the background I can see the rebel soldiers going to war.
They're racing around in worn out jeeps, shooting with kalashnikovs into the air to galvanize each other.
Well, I kind of expected that: I had come to a war-torn country for almost half a century.
But I only understood what war is when I saw a five-year-old girl in the foreground, walking in the same direction as us.
She was a little girl dressed in rags who seemed to be terribly aware of what she had to do.
She was holding a bag and a stick.
An old woman followed, grabbing the other end of the stick, She was her great-grandmother, perhaps,
and she was blind.
And they walked to save their lives.
The men in the background were the husbands, brothers and fathers of the two in the foreground.
While I'm thinking, the night falls.
After a few weeks on Ethiopia I find myself back in South Sudan and they send me to reopen the maternity ward in a hospital that a few weeks earlier, during an attack, had been razed to the ground and set on fire.
I was working there for a few months now, and as it often happens, the walkie talkie croaks in my hut.
I look out, and it is almost dawn.
This cheers me up because despite the previous day, I managed to get enough sleep.
The sound is disturbed, so I can't hear well what they say; but I understand that they need help, so I walk to the hospital.
It's only a three-minute walk at a normal pace, but when you walk them, you do it so quickly and your mind is so immersed on what you will find by opening the maternity tent that you're there in a moment.
I walk through the hospital's rickety gate and I see two men coming out of it holding a cot, a wire mesh of a bed,
and I understand, the situation I'm going to encounter is a bit borderline, as usual.
In South Sudan, the vast majority of people dwell in mud and straw huts in the middle of nowhere.
There are no paved roads, there is no electric lighting.
If you are lucky enough to have a working hospital near you, you know you'll have to walk at least 20 kilometers.
Given that, during the night, only the Milky Way can light your path, and in the rainy season the ground becomes a sea of impractical mud, if you are a woman, and your labor is going well, you better deliver in your hut.
If there is a problem, a complication of any kind, you have to start hoping to be lucky, you have to be lucky enough that your loved ones understand the situation, and to take you to the hospital, instead of the village shaman.
You must be lucky enough that the cot, which is a luxury good and is shared in the village, is free and available for you.
But above all, you must be lucky enough that your bodily resistance, and possibly that of your baby also, outlast the adversities ahead of you.
In short, I enter a small delivery room, with two worn-out delivery beds divided by a plastic curtain, a sink and a shelf with the delivery tools sterilized in the coal autoclave.
The South Sudanese midwife talks me about the case: it's a woman in labor of her fourth child.
Contractions are regular, the dilation is full and pushes are intense.
There is only one, not so trivial detail: the position of the fetus. this baby does not want to be born with head or podalic, breech; it wants to be born with the elbow, like this.
My brain opens the handbook of obstetrics studied in my dear University of Monza, that in that moment seems light years away from me, and I only have one certainty: the birth cannot take place naturally,
it needs an urgent cesarean section, an operating room, special equipment, and someone who knows how to do the operation.
But remember, there is no operating room in this hospital, because it had been burned down a short time before.
I know that if that woman is not operated soon, her uterus will tear and bleed and she will die in a terrible way, more or less quickly.
Just as it had happened to another woman, the day before, which I had not yet had time to relaborate.
I mean, time is short.
So I call the village chief, wondering if he can arrange a flight to transfer the patient to the capital.
Too bad it's raining, and in South Sudan you can't get an unscheduled flight, and there's nothing taking off, nor landing, on that sea of mud.
I give the woman the few available medicines to try and calm the contractions, even if I know that a woman's labor can be as powerful as a tornado, and there is no medicine for that.
The fetal heartbeat is disappeared.
Now we can only help the woman.
Three endless hours pass by until the power of that woman's body, in a way that is almost inexplicable to traditional obstetrics, can shape that lifeless child's body, push it through the birth canal and then bear it.
The woman is safe, she is alive,
I am exausted, I can't believe my eyes and I'm very relieved.
I walk towards my hut, I meet Tito - the head of the South Sudanese village, a tall, thin man without an eye and with his front teeth well separated, typical of Nuer.
He, as a village chief, already knows what happened in the delivery room and acknowledges me.
So I try to clarify and tell him that I didn't do anything: the woman was just very lucky.
And I tell him: "She was just lucky".
He smiles at me and says:
"Lucky is good, Ileana. Lucky is good".
South Sudan remains in my heart:
in 2016, when they offer me another mission there, I immediately accept.
They send me to an enormous refugee camp, 130 kilometers north of my first mission.
And I happen to be lucky again.
I meet some South Sudanese colleagues I had worked with last time.
I'm so happy to find them, still alive.
Between a birth and another they tell me why they had to run away, and how they did it. Because conflicts resumed at the sunset,
runaway people had to move during the day and hide in the swamps at night. They had to spend the night with the children in their arms because they were covered in mud up to their belly line.
Roda is a midwife of great talent, let alone her unique beauty,
and she tells me about the women who went into labor during those nights, and the babies assisted with basically nothing: umbilical cords were cut with scraps of metal plate.
And there's one birth in particular that keeps haunting her.
She keeps repeating to me the maneuvers she did to save the babies - because it was a twin birth - like she was asking my approval.
So I tell her that she was great, that for that mother, and her children, she has been a true gift of life, and that they were lucky.
And my mind gets back to "Lucky is good".
This and many other stories led me to think that our local colleagues are the real heroes.
Even though they are the first victims of the war, they have the strength to come and work with us day and night to take care of our patients.
Even if they are traumatized, they have the strength to come and relieve the trauma of their people.
It's as if they know how to pause the memory of the horrors they go through every day - from relatives and friends killed to rape, hunger - to come and take care of our patients, who are sometimes their enemies.
When in 2017 we opened a maternity hospital in Iraqi Kurdistan a few kilometers from the battle for the liberation of Mosul from Isis, finding midwives willing to work with us near the front was almost impossible -
rightly so, because they were all afraid.
I finally managed to put together a very heterogeneous team, a mix of Kurds and Iraqis, Yazidis, Syrians.
And if you consider the history of these people, it will seem impossible to you.
And yet they worked together in the delivery room, in the front line, to take care of other women, exhausted by the war.
A year goes by, and as a midwife I am very excited, because they propose to me to go to one of the world's largest maternity hospitals in Khost, Afghanistan.
I am talking about a real children's "factory", where more than 23,000 children are born every year.
That is about sixty on average per day, but sometimes even a hundred.
These are mindboggling numbers: I can't believe them even after I went there.
Here in Italy, if you assist three births per night, you're allowed to feel tough.
I remember Afghan midwives literally collapsing on the floor, after a 14 hour nightshift, in that chaos where two or three women were laboring in the same bed.
A reality at the limits, where life begins and resists. A bit like Bibi, who gives birth to her fourth daughter in the mountains and comes to us unconscious, in hemorrhagic shock, almost dead.
In order to try to save her, we can only fly to the operating room and remove her uterus.
But in order to do so, we need her husband's approval, which is not always an easy thing to get.
So I run to him together with the interpreter, and already a little prejudiced, ready as I am to try and convince him, spending time we don't actually have.
Instead, I find myself in front of a mountain man, with a sunburnt skin and a long beard.
He looks to me like a poor among poors, who carefully listens to me,
perhaps he doesn't get everything I say, but he understands, all he's left with is give us full confidence. So he pushes his fingerprint on the sheet of informed consent, because he doesn't know how to sign.
In Afghanistan, I am not even allowed to look a man in the eye. But I'd give him a hug.
During surgery, Bibi's heart stops twice, not once, and then starts again.
Which is unlikely, with the tools at our disposal.
The situation is extremely critical, but the surgery ends and Bibi is still alive.
During the night, however, the situation worsens again, her blood gets crazy, it doesn't clot anymore.
So we just have to go back to the operating room and fill her belly with gauzes to stop the bleeding, close her up and watch.
And so we do.
Two days go by, her conditions looks stable, so we get ready to go back to the O.R. and take out those gauzes that seemingly did their job.
So we get ready.
We're all very nervous, working quietly and carefully.
Then Bibi herself breaks that silence. She looks at us, and says in pastun: "Don't worry, I can handle it".
And so it happens: after three weeks Bibi goes back to her mountains, with her little girl; and she is full of life.
She was just lucky; lucky is good. The luck, and the life. Life intertwined with luck.
A luck I also had, and realizing how lucky I was made me vibrate, and pushed me far away from home to try and give it back.
A luck that made all the difference between death and life.
A life to honor and appreciate, looking for what makes us vibrate.
And what about you, now? What makes you feel alive?
What makes you vibrate?
(Applause)
Social impact.
There's been much talk about it, right?
At the same time, we do not know what it is. A bit of an urban legend, we do not know how it works, who the players are,
and most of all, because it seems to have more and more to do with everyone instead of just a few insiders.
One of my favorite phrases on this subject is that social impact can no longer be just right; it must become effective.
If we think about it, social projects never had a great functional motive but an emotional one.
It's a recent thing to want to do good and right in an effective manner.
But when we look at the effectiveness of social projects, we immediately notice some problems.
First, all social issues are "wicked problems,"
meaning that they are not linear problems.
They are complex, systemic problems that cannot be solved by tackling an isolated aspect or a single player.
Second, the scalability.
Even when we find a particularly effective project, in 90% of cases, its structure doesn't allow it to be spread to all the places where that solution is needed.
And that's a problem.
It's a problem because it means that even when we are able to invest enough time, resources, and energy to find an effective solution, we are then stuck because of obsolete deployment models.
This dynamic is further fueled by the fact that we are still relying on reactive models.
Something happens; there's a crisis; we take action.
With urgency.
We try to limit the damages,
with fragmented, isolated solutions - never coordinated -
and a useless duplication of effort.
Only now, thanks to the information age, a new paradigm is rising, a proactive one.
It is like this: if we know how it works, if we know what is going on and why, why don't we use the information we already have about effective models and take timely action
to prevent the worst?
But is it really just a matter of saving time, money, energy?
Or is it something else?
What's interesting today is that psychogenealogy teaches us that the traumas caused by crises, like the holocaust, chronic malnutrition, social violence or the refugee crisis, will create social, psychological and genetic consequences.
Yes, also genetic.
It has been shown that these traumas have decades-long repercussions, passed on from generation to generation until they are resolved or healed.
This makes us aware that creating a system to save resources or collaborate or share models is not just a nice idea.
It becomes a moral obligation.
Yes, you are wondering: "Who's this blonde that on a Sunday afternoon is telling us about these complex issues?"
So just to get to know me better before going on, I'm 28 years old. I studied here in Brera, then one year in Bilbao. I played volleyball for 12 years.
I graduated in 2011, with a dissertation on art, psychology and sociology.
Nice mix, right?
I agree with you.
In fact, when I graduated, I had no idea what to do next.
I felt lost.
My passion for social welfare, for international politics, for technology, for psychology - I couldn't find a way to express them.
Even less during the generational and economic crisis that we have known for ten years.
Then something happened in 2012.
In that year, I allowed my need to find a place in the world to take over, and then I began to travel, studying sustainable social-impact models in more than 20 countries worldwide.
From Macao, here in Milan, to Baha'i in Israel.
From CERN to refugee camps in Greece and Macedonia.
And I started to notice something.
A common factor, a distinguishing feature, a recurring pattern that ties together the most diverse realities of the planet:
the rise of movements that provide models with the only goal of allowing people to take action wherever they were.
Here, this model works; use it wherever you are.
You are a part of something bigger, a cause bigger than you, but it is very important that you take action on a local scale.
This thing started to intrigue me;
I started to investigate and discovered that the value of this model goes far beyond what I would have imagined.
It has indeed been demonstrated that when our brain is exposed to an excessive amount of information about crises, problems, traumas, tragedies, we develop empathy.
We develop an emphatic trajectory and consequently a need to act and react, to do something about it.
In short, we are designed to see other people suffer and to want to put an end to whatever causes that suffering.
So far so good, right?
Instead, unfortunately it has also been demonstrated that when such information overwhelms us and becomes too much for us, like what happens today on our cellphones, computers and other media, we block ourselves.
Our brain says, "I cannot.
You are not in direct danger.
You're overloading me with information about problems and bad things, and you're not giving me a chance to react in a way that I feel I can."
And it pulls the plug.
We all know the consequences:
isolation, emotional distance,


apathy, disinterest,

ignorance, exclusion,
intolerance, violence.
These are factors
whose causes we know very well.
I was at Idomeni in 2015 when I realized these are things we can no longer afford to ignore. Because around me there were not just thousands and thousands of refugees and migrants but also thousands and thousands of citizens and local communities
all with the same questions in mind: What do we do?
Where do we begin?
What can I do?
Because I need to do something.
And thus, ImpactOn was born.
I felt the urge to create an organization exclusively focused on finding, analyzing and selecting existing and effective social and environmental impact projects and making them available to others around the world.
We are a bridge, basically, between the talent and genius of local social-impact projects, that could never spread its formula in all the necessary places, and students, entrepreneurs, universities, researchers, governments, businesses, all interested in working on social causes that were simply stuck by the complexity of this area or by the conditions around them.
The project was born a year ago when we decided to launch a pilot using only PDFs.
We chose a project called "Blood For All" which allows you to create places for blood donations during emergencies, in schools or gyms.
It was essential for us to understand how to communicate a project not simply so that people would know it - emotional - but so it can be replicated - functional.
In less than three months, the project has been replicated in 19 cities in the world.
So we continue our work because for us, it is essential to find out how, for example, we can use big data and machine learning tomorrow to remove as many bottlenecks as possible from the current projects.
We chose another project related to Climate Change Week,
a project that allows any educator, professor or activist to do an activity involving youth that stresses the basic connection between what we consume daily and how it affects climate change.
We launched it a month ago in English and Spanish, and it's been translated by communities into French, Arabic and Portuguese and will be used in more than 50 cities in the world.
You might be thinking, "What is the marketing budget?"
Zero.
Marketing budgets or advertising are the principal reason why we know a lot about projects that are not always the best while others just stay in the shadow -
untraceable, forever lost.
Therefore, it's essential for us to use design, information psychology and technology to create a new paradigm.
For us, one of the pillars of this new paradigm is what we call a blueprint.
A blueprint is a formula, a recipe, the set of components that makes a project an effective one, a value proposition.
So we no longer use academic or emotional descriptions to communicate why a project is good and right; instead, we focus on why it works and what things to keep in mind when someone wants to replicate it somewhere else.
As an example, one of the things we specify in the blueprint is the type of project.
Is it an intervention?
Something that lasts a few weeks, a few months or to be kept up for some time?
What is the created impact?
What is the value,

the proposal, the target?
Who will benefit from it?
How complex is it?
How user-friendly to the famous non-experts?
How much time and resources are needed, on average? What are the related laws or government permits needed?
How compatible is it?
In which contexts was it already used and in which other contexts is it likely to give positive results?
Has it been replicated already?
We try to give references.
In all this, we try to consider the global agendas on sustainability, like the 2030 Agenda that points to a way forward for everyone,
but it's not necessarily giving the formulas that allow people and organizations to act and begin to implement solutions in order to finally achieve these goals.
If I think of that lost girl that I was in 2012, today I recognize that I have found my place
because every day I meet clever people and organizations whose incredible projects make local social impact happen, but I never thought that the moment would arrive when they could actually change the world.

An example: ecoladrillos.
A project of an organization in Cordoba, Argentina, which found a way to build with local materials an open-source mechanical press, which uses dirty PET - no need to even wash it - like plastic bags,
to make bricks.
It closes the bricks between wooden strips with which panels are made to build social houses,
ultra-insulating, ultra-resistant, ultra-light,
100% built by women, by vulnerable communities,
but above all, three times cheaper than any other prefabricated material.
This is a project that can make the difference in other people's lives who live where there is plastic but no houses, like places affected by natural disasters.
Another project, this one from California. Lava Mae takes buses which are no longer being used due to excessive CO2 emissions because, for example, they are old. They empty them; they paint them. Inside, they put a bath, a shower and create mobile hygiene spots which move around the city allowing the homeless not just to have access to basic hygiene but even to have a job interview or meet their loved ones again.
Another one: Liter of Light, which not only uses recycled plastic bottles but also recycles electric boxes, kerosene lanterns, batteries, and teaches vulnerable local communities around the world how to use their tools, their skills to build solar lamps, working not just on access to energy but also to education, security and access to the internet.
Could these projects ever become multinational monopolies?
No.
But do their formulas add value in every single location where they're replicated?
I believe so.
So let's do this.
In a historic moment of crisis, where top-down solutions are non-existent or fail to represent us, let's reclaim the power to take action, the power to create, to propose alternatives, to create a sector of social enterprise that is truly effective, measured and inclusive.
Because we know that shying away will only lead us away from the awareness that is not just about social responsibility but of social opportunity.
Because dreaming of a better future and realizing that we have all the means to build it is our greatest opportunity today.
They say that if you want, you can, and if you can, you must.
So?
What is your blueprint?
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good afternoon,
I am a doctor specialised in nephrology, kidney diseases, and maybe later we’ll explore this subject further.
I would start by sharing why I decided to become a doctor.
Because I was keen, since I was a child, on getting to understand all the many facets of life.
Sure, the organic part of diseases, but also the psychological one: the emotional aspects, the cultures.
My grandparents went to war, and shared a lot with me about that.
So understanding why people can put ourselves in some extreme situations has always been extremely important for me.
I haven't gone to war myself, so I thought, it could be helpful to experience how things go in developing, war-torn countries, in order to understand the cultures, the local beliefs and how men interact one with others.
Because social groups dynamics naturally affect society as a whole.
In this slide, I am not the one in the picture – I don’t have that many hair – but this is the path that I deem essential to understand human nature.
A tortuous path, dark, shady, isn’t it?
But my grandad – the one who went to war - used to say, When you have to make a choice, always take the hardest path.
Never the easiest.
And choosing as hard a path as this stairway takes courage, of course, but curiosity as well, and determination, and a certain dose of recklessness.
Therefore, in order to achieve that goal, let’s delve together into the intermediate steps.
The video shows Jakarta, where a pacific rally turns into a riot for no evident reason.
Groups are complex, their dynamics are complex as well, and they result into unforeseeable, often unreplicable situations.
Mostly, the dynamics and actions of individuals inside a group, almost inevitably diffre from the actions and dynamics of the group as a whole.
Each one of us, alone, behaves differently than in a group,
sometimes in the very opposite way.
That poses a major hurdle to the scientific analysis of groups, unreplicable as they are, so we can't fully grasp what their deep dynamics are.
There have been some social experiments, this is the Hash experiment.
Which line is the longest?
The fourth, of course.
Well, If I bring this image to a class, then I train everybody to state, the longest line is the second, and after calling an unwitting victim of the experiment in, start asking which line is the longest and everybody answers the second,
when the victim’s turn comes, he or she will go as well for the second in 75% of cases. So only 25% of you would have the gut to step outside the group's dynamics.
I’m moving away from the red area –
Step outside the dynamics of the group…why so?
Because we need to be accepted, as group acceptance means protection.
And we need protection, we need a comfort-zone.
Therefore, 75% of times we choose the easier way,
not the harder, in spite of my grandad's suggestions.
And what does this imply?
That we are not free to think, we cannot emancipate ourselves.
Everybody speaks about female emancipation and people’s emancipation etc.
Emancipation means freedom from mental constrictions.
And 75% of us is not emancipated,
this is the issue.
Then what?
This means we are subject to power, to authorities, to media.
And we become pawns.
We lose agency in our own life, so we get frustrated.
Because who doesn’t live their own life inevitably gets frustrated, sad, unsatisfied with their life.
And this generates an enormous problem in society and in groups as well.
And so, while doing my mental and social experimentations I tried to figure out: what is the best context to understand whether there are ways to be more gratified by what we do?
And the workplace is the best place to do so, as we spend almost all our life between work and family.
We actually spend more time at work than with our family.
So, while conducting my researches, I’ve tried to understand what the necessary cornerstones are to get a sense of satisfaction in the workplace.
And I’ve come across
this unspeakable name:
Csíkszentmihályi.
It’s the name of a psychologist.
He is a psychologist, one of the most important one in the field of happiness.
He is Hungarian, naturalised American.
And he theorized the State of Flow, the "zone" of maximal emotional engagement,
All of you, at some point of your life, have been so focused on something that nothing could distract you.
Right?
You might have had some sort of Flow experience, maybe while playing.
That is “the zone”, the state of mind we should strive for in order to become unbeatable.
And also, in order to align our personal goal with the goals of the group.
In fact, we reach the State of Flow when our contribution gets our group to its peak performance.
So we need to have our personal goal aligned with the goals of the group.
And in order to flow, there are 4 cornerstones to consider.
The State of Flow, in this case,
is depicted with a Lotus flower.
Do you know it? Have you ever seen one?
It is a splendid, beautiful flower, that grows on ponds, putrid waters, on stagnant water.
It is the ideal depiction of the human being, who indulge in the worst possible things, destroys everything on his path - but also writes Beethoven’s symphonies, makes incredible art, invents a technology to travel through space!
All of this, nonetheless, builds upon a terrible foundation, if you think about it: wars, personal interests, human rights violations.
Well, the first cornerstone is the concept of "Teal",
who takes its name from the Teal colour,
and was theorized by Frédéric Laloux.
It is a concept of business organisation, horizontal rather than vertical: there is no specific person in command but a system of groups of people that autonomously make some decisions in order to optimize production and quality of life within the company.
to the point that the company itself compes to a living being where groups are the cells
and the business system doesn’t require a business plan and a specific goal but evolves through the growth of these groups that consequently leads to the goal, which in turn might change over time.
It looks utopic, doesn’t it?
Do you think is it possible or not?
Michelin, Decathlon, Patagonia, Harley Davidson are all teal companies.
People within a true teal company even choose their own salary based on how much they have produced that month. That's how much their people, and the system as a whole, is empowered.
But in order to be truly teal, you must communicate, and that's the second cornerstone.
Communicate means of course be completely transparent, share goals, and everything: but there's more to it.
There is a psychologic theory called enneagram, not sure if you have ever heard of it, that ranks people into three different categories: instinctual, emotional and rational.
People belonging to different categories, when interact with each other, don’t perceive exactly the same nuances of meaning.
And misunderstandings may arise.
If I am instinctual, to me loving someone means to have them beside me if I have a problem,
and fight together with them
to solve their problem, OK?
That's what loving means to me.
For a rational type it means, I admire you, I admire you immensely!
For an emotional type it has a more romantic shade, like flower, heart, honey etc.
They don't actually mean something else, the core concept is the same. The interpretation varies, though.
And if I'm trained to detect – especially regarding abstract concepts – how the three groups talk and interpret each other, I can communicate far better with the whole system.
And then we have the last two cornerstones, and these two too share some similarities.
The first is about Lightness, which doesn't mean superficiality.
Italo Calvino covered it in his "Six Memos for the Next Millennium".
Lightness means – remember the staircase I started with? –
when I begin to climb up that shady, dark, hard, long and extenuating staircase I cannot do it with a full backpack, or I won’t make it to the second step.
Lightness means, we have to start every new journey with an empty backpack for the firsts steps then we have all the time in the word to add all the weights and complications that will certainly arise.
But the higher on the staircase, the closest to the State of Flow we are,
And if we are close to the Flow, we can't be beaten.
If you achieve the State of Flow, in any situation, no one can defeat you,
keep this in mind.
The "Gamification" I have mentioned before,
is the best way to achieve the State of Flow.
In your personal experience you might have had many chances to get there, but in the game – that is the reason why it is called [in Italian] Agonistic Trance – you achieve a State of Flow much more easily, so the game is the best way to do team building, to know each other, to have a whole array of closer interactions in the workplace, to learn about “the Zone”, about massive engagement. While gaming, you achieve it much more easily. I have learned about Gamification
and about “the Zone”
by driving my car.
That is the dashboard of my car, and I have realized, it has been challenging me for years and I had never noticed, I was playing with it.
This horizontal white light here gauges my gas consumption. When I accelerate, the white indicator rises.
The rhombus is the average. Every time I accelerate, if I exceed that level of consumption my car starts to flicker.
It also makes strange noises like “bip bip” etc.
The point is, the rhombus is an average value!
So, the more I keep my gas consumption level close to it, the more the rhombus moves to the left and the game gets harder!
I got to the point where everybody behind me started honking. I wasn’t accelerating anymore, because I wanted to win over my car.
At the end of the game though, I run 300/400 km more on a single tank simply by playing this game.
But why am I telling you about this?
Because the four cornerstones that have been theorized have also been applied in two contexts: the first is Auxilia Fountation.
In ten years, it spread all around the world and carried out a number of humanitarian, artistic and cultural activities that I cannot talk about right now due to lack of time.
Here you see two newspaper articles: the first is about the story of a child soldier we took out of jail during the civil war.
She was a Tamil terrorist, you might heard of the Tamil Tigers, and we bought her to Udine to treat her and heal her, since she had lost an arm in war.
The second is still about Sri Lanka's civil war.
We have freed 180 children from a juvenile prison, children that were 3-4-5-6 years old,
so we can't really say they had committed any true crime.
These achievements were enabled by the power of negotiation,
Auxilia made it, building upon the principle of the four cornerstones,
How come?
Because negotiating means, doing what I want on your terms,
so it closely resembles the State of Flow, where the personal goal matches with the group’s goal.
Me and the negotiator, the person that has to negotiate with me, we are one.
And we do our best to align my goal and theirs.
This is negotiation.
And this behind me is the demonstration of how the same mechanism can be applied within my hospital ward.
I am in charge of this little ward at the Hospital of Cividale del Friuli, it is a nephrology department, which brings us back to what we begun with.
That's when I have been put in charge of the situation at the ward.
And the performances, with the same staff and budget – have jumped to 14.000 in the first year and to 23.461 in two years.
In two years, we basically managed to double our performances.
Then they flatted out: that's as good as we can get, probably.
But this is a big result, especially for a public sector activity with no direct selling.
I'm not selling anything, so I can't count on a customer feedback.
It's a public environment.
So, the whole activity is guaranteed by the 4 cornerstones.
(Applause)
Let me wrap up with this slide -
I still have 11 second! There is a child peeping through a hole,
and it depicts our childhood, mine at least for sure, since I have always been curious and attracted by dark places.
All this goes to show, we all have to preserve somehow that spark of recklessness, curiosity, playfulness, lightness, in order to achieve the State of Flow.
And mostly, it shows that the hard path, the one that is most beneficial for our own growth, might also be beautiful and fun.
(Applause) Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Let's talk innovation. And I'd like to start by reminding everyone that innovation today is certainly a company's staple to successfully compete in the marketplace.
And this is perhaps even truer for those businesses, like many Italian ones, that cannot deal on other productive factors such as size, and consequently the economies of scale, or access to productive factors - such as work or energy - at competitive costs.
So innovation today, as we all know, is a top priority in the agenda of every entrepreneur and every manager: everyone talks about it.
But perhaps not everyone knows exactly what innovation specifically is.
I think it is very useful to see it, and define it, as a process: it is nothing more than a series of actions, decisions through which a company, an organization or single persons like us, generate an idea for a new product, a new service, a new way of doing things - and then they turn it into reality, develop it, make it practical and bring it to the market.
Traditionally, I would say until about 20 years ago, there was a consensus in business that in order to innovate successfully it was essential to control, to internalize, bring inside the company all the resources, skills, technologies, ideas and knowledge that were needed to carry out this process.
The basic idea was very simple: to innovate more, we must invest more internally, hire the best talent, technicians, designers, engineers, scientists and increase year after year the investments in our research and development laboratories.
Eventually, they called this basic idea "closed innovation". The concept that it is fundamental for a company, in order to relentlessly innovate, and therefore compete in the market, to continuously widen and deepen its know how, its distinctive set of skills.
There are several studies that show how this way of innovating in organizations generates a syndrome, that some have called the "not-invented-here syndrome".
How many times, before an idea, a stimulus that comes from outside the organization, someone told us: "Well, we could do it better".
The "not-invented-here syndrome" is just that: the tendency, often even unconscious, to judge as of less value, of less potential an idea, a contribution, a solution that comes from outside.
Indeed, someone called it the syndrome of the not invented here: a cultural, often unconscious barrier that we often encounter in organizations.
This "closed innovation" paradigm of innovating has been the dominant paradigm, until about 20 years ago; but can we still deem it today an effective approach to innovation?
Things underwent a profound change, over the last 20 years.
First of all, the innovation process has become much more complex, and much more expensive.
In order to respond to customers' needs, which are changing faster and faster; in order to face a more and more global and unpredictable competition, organizations today have to innovate by incorporating into their products or services, many more technologies, often with a digital nature, than a few years ago.
Let's take the example of the automotive world: some people believe that in three years' time the bulk of added value in the automotive supply chain will be in the software component, and no longer to the hardware.
And this requires a thorough transformation in the innovation strategies for companies in the field.
Willingly or not, and somehow they are already doing that, they must become capable of developing - or at least integrating - software and computer systems of various kinds.
And it is very hard, you will understand, to do so by leveraging, relying only on their own skills, resources and technologies.
Then there is a second, very relevant phenomenon: today, the lifecycle of a new product, of a new service, after the launch, has shortened considerably.
I remember the words of the Hewlett-Packard CEO, who stated a few years ago: "Today, our products remain on the market between six and 12 months.
Five years ago, they stayed on the market between three and five years".
And this acceleration of the innovation cycle is something that is common to many industries.
We also see it, when we go to buy an appliance at Media Markt.
So not only innovation is a tougher job today; it has to be done much, much faster, also.
And so, what are the most innovative companies doing today,
what response are they giving to this challenge?
Are they investing more internally, in their R&amp;D labs, research department and innovation processes?
That's not really the case: take the example of Apple.
Last year, Apple invested 5.4 percent of its revenue in research and development.
Which is a far cry from other companies like Microsoft or Google, who invested 13, 14, 15 percent of their revenue in research and innovation.
And this is consistent with Apple's history over the last five to six years, and still we can hardly say it hasn't been - and still is - a very, very innovative company.
What companies that really know how to innovate today have proven to do, and have learned to do, is to evolve their model, their approach to innovation from a closed to an open attitude.
Today, the paradigm adopted by these companies is called "open innovation".
And what's the difference between closed and open innovation?
The idea is very simple: first, acknowledging that today it is no longer enough to rely on in-house resources, to keep investing in one's own technologies, in one's own know-how.
It is perhaps faster, more effective, cheaper, less risky, knowing how to identify, search, use the ideas, skills and technologies that already exist out of our organization.
The idea is very fascinating in principle, but then you have to put it into practice.
And today there are many practical tools to do just that.
Some of them are also very well known, and have been implemented for a long time: let's take for example the case of Alessi, that we all know, a manufacturer of home accessories.
During the 1990s, it saw its turnover spike from 20 to 100 million euros, by upholstering its innovation process.
Alessi began working systematically with a network of over 200 third-party designers and architects, helping them to thoroughly rethink the products Alessi was developing and bringing to the market, intercepting and interpreting some of the cultural changes that in the meanwhile our society was going through.
You can easily imagine how richer the contribution of creativity and knowledge that this network of architects and designers was able to share with the company, compared to what Alessi designers could have done by themselves.
A second strategies is setting up collaborations with universities, research centres or companies in sectors that are completely different from ours, in order to create long-term, wide-ranging development projects.
An example of this is Dompé pharmaceuticals, which recently created in its research and development lab, where it invests 15% of its turnover each year. an "open innovation" department
And what does it do?
It's job is to develop, measure, grow and nurture a network of relationships with over 200 universities and research centres around the world, with which Dompé innovates on a daily basis.
Same thing with Cosberg, the well-known Italian automation brand that cooperates with dozens of universities and research centres in Europe and beyond.
Cosberg, however, does something more: it is a partner of Intellimech, one of the world's largest pre-competitive research consortia in the field of mechatronics, which brings together over 30 companies, research centers and universities, that continuously share ideas, solutions and technologies to accelerate and improve the ability to innovate of each consortium's partners.
Mind you, Cosberg has recently developed 16 innovative patents in the field of automation, taking advantage of the expertise and stimuli that came from this consortium.
But today there are many other tools, even more innovative, that companies only recently started to implement to make open innovation.
For example, there is a clear growth in the number of companies that systematically collaborate, and often also invest in capital, of startups or very innovative small companies.
In doing so, they are also helped by the bridging, incubator role played by business accelerators of companies such as PoliHub, which is the start-up incubator of the Politecnico di Milano, which hosts more than 130 innovative startups.
There are also online platforms, today.
I don't know if you have ever heard of Innocentive, or Nine Sigma, which connect innovation-hungry companies with communities of technicians, engineers, university professors.
In some cases, there are hundreds of thousands, if not millions, of the so called solvers. When a company want to innovate, solvers help them find innovative solutions to their most complex technical problems.
Another tool that in recent years has been increasingly used is innovation contests and hackatons.
They are tools through which companies can involve suppliers, customers, partners, students, teachers in generating new concepts for products and services that are then brought to market.
An interesting example, in this sense, is the "Global Packaging Challenge" launched in 2019 by Amadori, a prominent agrofood company which involved a number of company's current and prospective suppliers in developing sustainable solutions for Amadori's packaging systems. And it was a very successful project.
The message I wanted to convey to you is that today there are so many tools at hand to implement the idea of open innovation.
And these tools are not only available to big companies and multinationals.
On the contrary, perhaps using and exploiting these tools is even more important, critical for small companies, like many companies in Italy and especially in this territory.
Clearly, in order to do this, one has to be aware of this important paradigm shift.
We have always believed that it was fundamental, in order to innovate successfully, to have a solid know how, a set of skills and distinctive technologies that the company was investing in and building upon over time.
Today, the companies that we have seen, and all the others that have taken the road towards open innovation, understood that internal know-how is no longer enough,
and begun to develop what I call "know-where", that is the interconnection with the outside world and the ability to find quickly and effectively, before my competitors, where to find the ideas, solutions and technologies that are most needed to accelerate and make my innovation process more effective.
Clearly, in order to do this we need to overcome that cultural resistance we talked about before, the "not invented here" syndrome.
It is fundamental that companies understand, on one hand, the intrinsic value of ideas and solutions that come from outside their Research and Development labs; and secondly, it is essential for them to know these tools, what they consist of and how to implement them.
Well, I believe this is the main challenge for every company today: "rooting" the concept of open innovation and let their philosophy and approach to innovation evolve from a closed, I would say self-referential, model towards this expansive and interconnected model that we came to call "open innovation".
(Applause)
I'm going to talk about something that affects all of us, which is how we use words.
To some extent, it's my thing, in the sense that I spend my life talking about words, trying to use them well.
And first of all, I have tried all these things on me.
The first thing I want to say is this.
We never think about it enough, but language is the thing that makes us human.
We are the only animals that have the power of speech.
Think about the animals you have at home, a cat, a dog.
You know well that the cat does not only say, "meow," the dog does not only say, "woof"; but the cat says a load of different meows, the dog a load of different woofs, but, no animal, no matter how smart it is, can use a sophisticated tool like human language.
Professor Chomsky, in a very light-hearted interview granted to Ali G, who is the rapper invented by Sacha Baron Cohen, replied to a question asked by the rapper: Professor, but you have been dealing with languages for 80 years, are you not tired of always talking about language?
He replied, "No, not really, because language is the key characteristic that makes us human."
We cannot do without words, yet, since we've had our mother tongue since forever, it's basically given to us free at birth, we never give it enough thought.
And yet, every word we choose has a lot of uses, works for many things.
The first thing we use language for is this: we describe the reality that surrounds us.
Think about it, we talk about reality, and decode reality, putting in labels, and succeed in understanding each other because we agree on these labels.
I could quote a famous sketch by Aldo, Giovanni and Giacomo, that maybe you know, the "cadrega" [chair] one, or "carega" [chair] for those from around Venice. What goes on in it is that two people test a third to see if they all belong to the same "tribe," "cadrega" or "carega" only being used in northern Italy.
So, Aldo, offered a cadrega, takes an apple and says, "Nice, this cadrega," and the others then know that he is an "impostor."
So, we need languages in order to describe what surrounds us.
I'll point something else out for you.
As reality keeps changing, is in continuous evolution, so language also keeps changing.
It is possible that we do not like it, but we will talk about it in a second.
We need languages to communicate, of course, with one another, and as I said earlier, beyond just communicating, language defines my tribe.
Thus, I identify people who belong to my own tribe.
We think we have moved forward from the tribal concept, but it is not the case, we are still extremely tribal as human beings, and equally I identify people who are not part of my tribe.
Therefore, it allows me to identify "the others."
Think about the sketch I told you earlier.
Language is useful for a third very important thing, which is maybe that which interests us more.
Every word we use is literally an "act of identity."
That is, I tell others who I am, I make a declaration.
When we really fall in love with someone, we can be smitten by their physical appearance, but what really strikes us is the things they say, and the way they say those things.
Obviously, with language being at the very heart of people, we fall in love with that, with the deepest part of a person.
Often, indeed, we fall for unattractive people, because it is not that important.
What's important ultimately is what they say.
Then, for what else is language of use?
Obviously, if I say some things about myself, those words, I have to think carefully about them, because what do I want the others to understand about me?
Do you never ask yourselves when, for example, you have written a few words on Facebook, made it a public post, what others will think of you, reading what you have written?
I maybe then regret it, want to delete it, but, as you all know, God, there's screenshots, and even deleting is wishful thinking.
Searle says, "It is not possible to think clearly if we are not capable of speaking and writing clearly."
So, the way in which we communicate tells others how clearly we are thinking, how capable we are of linear thinking, because if we think sequentially, we can also express ourselves in an ordered way.
A bit like what Nanni Moretti said. You remember it?
We should always take that line.
Of course, languages, all of them, have many more words than those strictly required.
Let's take a look at the statistics.
Italian has from 300,000 to 1 million words.
It is hard to make a precise estimate because it depends on what you consider. For example, dialect words, do we consider them part of the Italian lexicon?
A person of average education, let's say at the end of high school, knows about 35-40,000 words. But we do not use them all.
We, in our daily activities, get by with about 2,000 words.
We hear a much greater number, and so we get to know them because, for example, the media use them often.
Words like "penitentiary," "meteorological," perhaps.
We have inherited an almost endless number of words, and, I think, pretty well everyone senses quite clearly that words are not all the same.
A word can be a kiss, a word can be a bullet.
So, the easiest way to identify words is to make a list of the nasty ones.
Just think that it is what is often done online.
I make a blacklist of "bad words,"
and if someone use those bad words, I do not publish the post.
That post is censored.
But is it always like this?
Think about a word like "nigger."
Everyone cringes.
If a white person says it in an offensive way in regard to an African American, it is clear that it is a horrible word.
But have you noticed that, for example, African American rappers use them in their lyrics to define themselves, because it is a way of underlining black power.
So not even a "bad" word, like "nigger," can be stigmatized a priori. It actually depends on the context in which that word is used.
But let's look at a few examples closer to us.
[Finocchio] (fennel) It could be a vegetable, fennel, or an impolite way of referring to a homosexual.
Let's use it in a phrase: I walk into a cafeteria and say, "Oh, we've got fennel for lunch today."
I'm referring to a meat dish that probably many of you like.
Fennel is good, eliminatory.
I walk into the same cafeteria, there is a table of LGBT activists, "Oh, we've got fennel for lunch today."
Same phrase.
But what changes?
The communicative purpose.
So, what do I do? I flag a priori
the word "fennel" because it is negative?
No, because doing this, I might censor half of my vocabulary.
Cagna (female dog).
"Cagna," over the years, has assumed a further meaning, that maybe you know, because it became famous thanks also to the television series, the one before "poor actress," and then, indeed, used negatively towards women.
The word has assumed a negative meaning to such an extent that the owners of female dogs prefer to make circumlocutions.
They no longer refer to their female dogs as "cagna,"
someone might think
they meant their wife, right?
But rather they use often sweeter,
slightly modified terms. Ah!?
We censor ourselves, lest someone get the wrong impression.
A third word might surprise you: "ma'am."
How can "ma'am" be negative?
So, if I ask young people about it, they say, "Yes, 'ma'am' is negative in that, well, one is a young woman, so she would be a 'Ms,' and calling her 'ma'am' would offend." But I'll tell you something
that is much more subtle.
It has happened to me several times.
There's only one woman in a room full of men.
They're all professionals.
A man enters the room and addresses the men as "doctor" and the women as "ma'am."
I usually react pretty badly to it. So, even a word that I really mean in a positive way like "ma'am," if used in a certain way, could become, I'm not saying offensive, but still, at least, annoying for some.
So, you understand that the blacklist is not enough.
A blacklist is not enough, censorship is not enough:
something fundamental is required, which is our intelligence.
Our own reflection is needed, because words are not just words.
Let's turn to something else that words do.
Words bring about actions.
If I am a professor, I have the power to advance or fail a student.
If I am mayor, I have the power to marry people.
If I am a doctor, I have the power to declare a birth or a death.
You know the medical dramas where the person is not dead until someone, another person in the right role, has declared the death.
Thus our words become acts.
We make acts all the time.
Think about the supreme act: swearing.
Swearing is a very serious matter.
But does it have actual consequences if I commit perjury?
In fact, no.
But among people, there is a kind of pact, according to which, if I swear, I think fully about that thing.
I do not swear lightly, even if in reality we all know that no one will shoot us.
This is the power of words.
So, in a world overloaded with words - and it is overloaded with them, we are constantly surrounded by words - I invite you to do one thing: to regain the power of the right word.
This is something we can all do.
Because it does not mean learning strange new things.
It means addressing something in which we are all competent, that of using words, and using them better.
I'll give you three bits of advice here.
The first: cultivate doubt.
We live in an age that, because of the quantity of information we receive, gives us the illusion of knowledge.
Of course, knowing is not googling.
Knowing is something much deeper.
So, we, day after day, in any field of knowledge, need to ask ourselves what the limit of the things we know is.
How little I know.
How little I know about the immensity of information that surrounds me.
And this also applies to language.
Every day, if I know 35,000 words out of a million, it is not unusual that I come across words that I do not know every day.
And, confronted by these words, I'll feel uncomfortable because I want certainty in life.
Human beings want certainty.
Changes, things we do not know, scare us.
But, at least in the linguistics field, it is easy to solve the problem: I use a dictionary.
Today it is even easier, I can google it,
but, ultimately, it's the same thing: I meet a word that I do not know. I'll give you an example from January of this year: "gelicidio" [glazed frost].
"Gelicidio" has caused a lot of trouble.
Many thought that it was a new term, a neologism.
It is a word from 1360, so it is 700 years old.
And why didn't I know it?
Because every one of us knows just a little part of the full lexicon that we have at our disposal.
So, the first thing I'm telling you about is doubt that bears fruit, because the only way to increase our knowledge is to know not to know.
The moment we are convinced we know everything we need to know, our ability to learn new things is diminished.
First thing.
Second.
We can reflect for a moment.
It is really very simple. Why?

Because we have that faculty.
To some extent, we have the illusion of needing to be constantly on the move, of always being in a rush, of not having the time to think about things.
But it is not true.
Even when when we want to make a post on Facebook, for example, we can always stop 10 seconds and think about what we truly want to say.
Let’s take back the luxury of thinking about what we are going to put, say to the world around us, that microparticle of information. Let’s think about it a little more.
It is not difficult.
And then a third piece of advice that's just as simple.
When we’re not competent, and we do not know things, something that can happen very often, we can always choose silence.
We live in a society that habitually polarizes everything.
Well, instead, sometimes, you can choose not to say anything.
Also, because, if I say things that are wrong, we need to remember that what we have typed is first and foremost going to stay put.
It will remain attached to us, and will contribute to the creation of our online personality.
Do we really want to say this thing that then stays there, attached to us, like some kind of scrap paper we have thrown into the world?
So, as you can see, these things are simple: doubt, reflection, silence.
Ultimately, it is silly, because in some way they are three requests that are not exactly about language, but they are of which language is consequential.
At the end of all this, I’d like to point something out to you.
These skills do not come immediately.
A lot of work is needed.
It’s what I call "the need to be farmers of the language."
Why, what do farmers do?
Maybe you know: farmers commit themselves day in and day out, they never get tired, they know that from the moment the field is plowed until the moment the plant grows, a lot of time passes, a great amount of time passes, and they must put in a lot.
So, I summarize it like this: the result of all the fatigue, sweat, consistency, resilience, and confidence in what they do, is this little plant here, that finally punctured the asphalt.
We don't necessarily need to be sponges, maybe we’re more like asphalt, but knowledge is even more resilient than we are, and if we give it a chance, it could end up like this.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Among all the natural phenomena, the wind has a unique property: A stark duality between good and evil.
The wind is good when it gives us a clean and renewable source of energy. And also, when it blows industrial emissions away from cities.
Wind is a supreme good, as life on Earth wouldn't exist without it.
Conversely, it is evil when it destroys structures, or when it erodes the ground down to its desertification.
The wind is the highest evil as 75 percent of nature's damages and victims are caused by wind.
I got into this very peculiar world back in 1976, when I was developing my thesis about the seismic action on skyscrapers.
I had been working on my thesis for two or three months already, when I met a professor who told me that Italimpianti, back then a giant of engineering, here in Genoa, had a big issue to deal with: a chimney that was shaking, because of the wind action -- we don't see it shaking here -- very violently.
They looked for someone that could solve the problem, but they failed to find someone, as no one was dealing with it in Italy.
My professor said, it would be great if some students defended their thesis on this subject.
And then, after some days, I threw away my thesis on earthquakes and started delving on the wind.
It was very hard, because I struggled to find two articles written in the US, and I couldn't read them, untrained as I was on so many subjects: random dynamics, random processes, theory of probability. In Genoa, and in the rest of Italy for that matter, they weren't taught in Engineering faculties.
So I started looking for books, and I spent five months studying math before being able to deal with wind actions. Eventually, I even developed a program so I could assess the wind action on chimneys.
Then, I compared my results with the American ones. And damn, they didn't match.
So I spent two more months in a desperate search of the error.
Then, something occurred to me: I was graduating on such a tricky, complicated subject, the degree panel would never, ever have noticed I was defending a wrong dissertation.
I showed up and -- (Applause) I showed up, and I graduated.
But then I realised two things: One, I loved this research so much, and so I had to keep on studying it, maybe through a university career. Secondly, I couldn't give up. I had to find that mistake.
So I spent four more months in search for it, until I found that the mistake was a square root of two.
That was the difference.
But either I was wrong again, or it was Americans' fault, not my own.
So I went to the States.
I met one of the world's leading experts, and with a lot of embarrassment I made my point.
He listened to me, and then he told me, How long did it take you to find it?
Four months, I said.
Good job, he said.
I found it last month, after two years looking for it.
I came back to Genoa, not sure what would have happened next.
Then, I got a package from the United States. with some spreadsheets and some reports inside with their results of the wind tunnel tests.
That was an epiphany.
I understood, Americans were mostly dealing with the physical interpretation of the phenomena;
but I could harness my mathematical skills to design some computational models.
And so I just did.
One year later, I published the first mathematical solution of the wind action on structures. which is still featured in almost all international guidelines.
I was also lucky enough, because back then they were -- (Applause) And I was lucky enough because in Genoa, back then, they were building some different works like Carlini stadium's roofing, Corte Lambruschini, Calata Sanità cranes.
And they asked me to model the wind action on them.
I had no competition, after all.
So, I applied my own procedures.
And that's how my professional path began.
I do research, and develop mathematical methods to calculate wind actions and I apply them to real structures that continuously inspire me to do new research in a circular process, where research and application blend.
I kept on studying models on slender structures and in the 80s I applied them to all Italy's telecommunication towers, and I also applied them on the Bigo.
And one month before the inauguration of the Bigo, in a wind stability routine test, I shuffled it at least one meter back and forth.
Had I torn it down that day, sure I wouldn't be here tonight.
(Laughter) Then, I applied my models to the computation of large structures and I was lucky enough to exploit them for the Pisa Leaning Tower, and Messina strait bridge, all the way to the structure I'm most keen on: Constantin Brâncuși's endless column, Romania's national monument.
Brâncuși has been last century's greatest sculpture, and he devoted his life to this column, he tried to find the perfection of shape.
During the late '90s, while engineers put their hands on it, they thought it was impossible for the column to stand up straight. It had to fall down, one day. And they asked me some tools to deal with that.
Not only I didn't move a finger, I also proved that Brâncuși, by looking for the perfection of the artistic shape, reached the perfection of the structure by creating a work that's almost insensitive to wind. which would soon become a benchmark for all the most challenging buildings of the third millennium.
Obviously, I couldn't keep on using the American data. So, I decided to build a lab, a wind tunnel in Genoa. a modern wind gallery which is still a source of pride for our university.
And in 2000, I held the first chair in Italy on wind engineering, now featured in every Italy's engineering faculty.
And still in 2000, I created the first Wind Engineering World School, which still roams the planet.
Mostly, I was surrounded by many talented people who created one of the biggest research group in this sector.
Not only it was a crowded group, it was culturally diverse also.
I always thought, a limit in the Italian University is that professors only want around them people in their own images.
I wanted to be surrounded by: fluid dynamics specialists, architects, mathematicians, computer scientists, physicists, geophysicists, thus encouraging an absolutely cross-field, culturally rich environment.
So, it dawned on me I couldn't focus on a single structure, and I had to span across territorial fields of interest.
I studied the placement, for instance, of Italian bases in Antarctica, and the insertion of wind farms in a given territory, and in the year 2000, Ferrovie dello Stato [Italian State Railways] asked the University of Genoa and the Polytechnic School of Milan to design a windproof system for high-speed railways.
Maybe not all of you know, above 155 miles per hour, a particularly violent crosswind can flip the train upside down.
It's the worst risk for these lines.
So, I created monitoring systems and with those systems I detected the most dangerous areas in the Italian railway's network. and we created windscreens.
But it quickly came out as a wrong solution: They were very expensive and they wouldn't have never been enough to protect all the dangerous areas.
So, I delved into math and developed a model for wind forecasts.
These real-time forecasts were given to the train drivers, so they could slow down when the wind was stronger, at no cost for anyone.
This was an idea worth spreading, and -- (Applause) This was an idea worth spreading, and I thought, there was no better field test than the port areas in Liguria, High Thyrrenus. who underwent disasters like London Valour double sinking, also because of the wind, or the endless containers' upholstery.
We applied for two projects, which we both won, "Winds and Ports", "Winds, Ports and Sea", and we started creating measuring tools, which we then spread all across the Tyrrhenean Sea, until we created one of the world's most advanced wind monitoring network.
These data gave us elements to predict the wind.
These forecasts are given to the port authorities through a WebGIS system. The port schedules its activities taking the wind into account, or they close some port areas to protect the workers in the most dangerous moments.
We expected results to provide regular wind diagrams such these ones, a signature of cyclones and turbulences.
Instead, we found diagrams like this one, where the wind had sudden peaks.
They're called "wind strikes" and they last 20, 30 seconds, one minute at most, then they disappear.
I immediately figured out, these phenomena had to be related to thunderstorms that always affect Genoa and the whole Liguria and we all learned, even recently, how tragic it can get, because of the rain.
We studied the wind, and we saw that during the thunderstorm an air stream falls from the cloud and sort of radiates after hitting the ground.
This is very dangerous for air traffic and for the structures.
You can see here what happened in 1994, with many cranes collapsing during a thunderstorm, and lots of people died in the process.
Sadly, these phenomena are not rare.
This video doesn't start, but you could see this cloud storm discharging onto the ground.
You maybe smiling about it, but I don't know if you realise that every year, all along your lifetime, you end up there at least three, four times.
You don't notice it from within, but it's visible from the outside.
Three-four times a year, you're all immersed into this airstream.
We started to develop models to tell the cyclone winds from the thunderstorm winds.
We started using our LIDAR profilers to track the wind speed evolution.
And then we started cross-checking our data with weather analysis, satellite analysis, Doppler radars and thunders images.
We applied these models to the wind action on structures.
We published plenty of articles in the international literature that were well appreciated and encouraged me to embark on a really ambitious project: an application for a researcher's dream award: The Advanced Grant of the European Research Council, possibly the greatest acknowledgment in research.
I won this project in 2016, and a new adventure began: the "Thunder" project for studying thunderstorms.
The press talked a lot about it, I've been really impressed by the Leonardo News TV broadcast who said that this project might currently be the one that gets closest to the people's actual needs.
And that's high praise for me.
Here you can see the LIDAR scanner we have just installed in Voltri port.
There are 30 LIDAR at most, around the world, it's a fantastic machine that emits laser beams with which we track wind within a 14 kilometre range at 100 meters steps.
We basically spread with our instruments the area before the port.
We want to study how the air stream hits the sea surface, and how it moves, how it gets through the cities.
This video doesn't start, also.
We are cooperating with Canada's Western Ontario University which sports the world's biggest lab for measuring thunderstorms and cyclones.
Over the last months we have worked in Canada and my youngest cooperators are still there now.
We engaged in a partnership with the Eindhoven university with the world's biggest centre of computational fluid dynamics.
We also engaged in a partnership with Berlin University which hosts a prominent centre of Meteorology.
Well, climate change is always a hotly debated, controversial topic.
So, now I'll just mention other people's data.
A study has recently been posted on the Internet by which, if the Earth temperature keeps rising the way it did over the last years, 50 years from now Europe will be struck by frequent, severe thunderstorms.
And if you look at the circle, those thunderstorms will hit the hardest between Genoa and La Spezia.
That's why we are filling our towers with other tools to measure buildings' behaviour under thunderstorms.
We will leave for Taiwan in two months, where a wind tunnel has just been created to realise temporary air streams.
In this gallery, we want to reproduce the thunderstorms we are experiencing in Genoa.
I've been studying the wind for 40 years now, and I've never seen a skyscraper collapse under the wind. These structures are therefore,
in a sense, "excessively safe".
This means, we are spending too much on their safety.
We aren't spending enough instead on low-profile, low-mid-rise structures, that collapse very often.
And every time they do, there's a thunderstorm going on.
We need to refocus economy and safety and we believe this project to be really ambitious.
It will end up in 2022, when my career also will.
We'll hold here in Genoa a world congress on thunderstorms.
In the meanwhile, I teach at the University of Genoa and also all around the world.
I love spending time with new young talented people, and with researchers, passionate, curious and driven as they are.
I love to think, I'm sharing, spreading among others a culture on the wind generated in 40 year of studies, and mostly sacrifices, in Genoa.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Good morning. it's a big thrill for me to be in this wonderful theater today.
I would like to tell you about the courage to dare of an extraordinary woman, with whom, moreover, I'm in debt, as a mother and as an educator.
A woman who wasn't discouraged by difficulties she faced during her life and who had the courage to dare in order to achieve her great mission, that is support the child's cause.
A woman whose work is widespread, rooted, and highly topical all over the world.
But very little in Italy, her birth place.
It all started a few years ago, or, better, my meeting with Maria Montessori dates back to a few years ago. My daughter Alice had just six months, when my husband and I read a leaflet in which a follow-up meeting about the Montessori education was proposed, we decided to go there.
I really couldn't believe my ears, once got there: for the first time, finally, I heard of a school as a place where the teacher must take care of the child, and not of the program; a place where children organize their day independently, where they even focus on the work for a long time, and, especially, they are very happy to do what they do. A place where the teacher doesn't judge, so the teacher doesn't give weight to marks, punishments, rewards.
A teacher who, rather lovingly accompanies their scholars throughout their growth.
I had studied Maria Montessori at the university, but, I must be honest, I never understood this revolutionary content of her educational proposal.
It was just a different way of seeing the education's task, in which I always believed.
So, another education was really possible.
That was the school which I wanted for my daughter.
But, who is this extraordinary woman that the New York Times called "the most interesting woman in Europe"?
Maria Montessori was a scientist; she was born and lived between 1800 and 1900.
Since she was a child she is very determined, and especially, she is aware of her great mission to accomplish.
She had such clear ideas that at the age of 12, she renounced one of her great passion: she attended drama since she went to primary school, and she renounced it to devote herself to those that she called "the strict studies", which are the same studies that will lead her to enroll in, when she was barely 20, the Faculty of Medicine in Rome, an exclusively male environment, in which she was immediately opposed and disliked.
However, she wasn't absolutely discouraged.
Maria Montessori was a tenacious woman, who wanted to travel the most difficult paths to carry her project out.
In fact, she will be one of the first women in Italy to take a degree in medicine, and once graduated, she actively participated in the nascent psychiatry.
She began to look after the children and the so-called oligophrenics, children who are considered irredeemable with serious developmental delays.
At some point she began to make revolutionary observations.
She said, "Healing for these children doesn't consist in an operation.
Their therapy is pedagogical, it's in the educational work, in the relationship." Thus, she began to deepen the studies.
She researched what was deepened, and carried out by some scientists who preceded her, who studied this particular phenomenon and who focused on the sensory recovery.
She made her first trials, working with these disadvantaged children, and she began to create some materials which are appropriate for them.
The courage to dare is satisfying because, after just two years of intense work, really intense - Maria Montessori worked from morning to night with these children - one of these children passed the exam at the primary school, and he did it with better grades of the so-called healthy children. And then another, and yet another,
they hail as a miracle!
Maria Montessori adopted an idea that is today confirmed by the neurosciences.
Children in order to learn and comprehend need concrete things, they need to do concrete experiences.
Children think with their hands.
There is a close link between mental processes and sensitivity.
The extraordinary thing is that Maria Montessori said this more than 100 years ago.
She asserted that the senses way is the main method to re-educate irredeemable children with serious problems, with huge learning problems.
The senses way is the main method to enhance the abilities of the mind.
After some years, in a very poor district of Rome, the San Lorenzo district, Mr. Talamo, an engineer, restored some crumbling buildings and built a playgroup, what we now call "preschool."
A place that housed small children who didn't go to the elementary school yet, and it was necessary to find someone that took care of them.
They were abandoned children, some of them had parents that worked, and so it was necessary someone who took care of them.
The proposal is made to Maria Montessori, already well known in Rome especially for her rehabilitation work with difficult children.
Maria Montessori accepted this proposal with deep interest, she looked after older children, and now she was very curious to comprehend, to know the younger children.
Thus, like a scientist expert in biology, the first thing that she did was to organize an environment that was suitable to house these children.
She made some materials available to these children, the materials that she used with older children with developmental delays, and she created new ones, all the sensory materials: the touch and feel numbers and letters.
Then, she started to observe: she didn't teach, she didn't impose, she merely observed.
She observed children with a great scientific rigor.
She will do this for a lifetime.
She wanted to understand how the mind of these children works, their psyche.
She recognized really extraordinary phenomena.
She noticed that children touched these materials with great passion, they touched emery letters and after few months, they began to read, without anyone has taught it them.
She realized that, in an environment reach in objects and proposals children-oriented, which were appropriate for the needs and the interests of children, and where children could move freely, choose freely, the atmosphere was completely different: the arguments were rare, and children even collaborated each other, they got into what they did, being really concentrated, they even put what they took in order.
But not only... An extraordinary interesting thing was that children didn't seem interested in sweets and toys which some ladies brought to them.
Why?
Because sensorial materials were even more charming.
Maria Montessori realized that there was a nourishment that was even more powerful than that which fed the body, and it was the nourishment that fed mind and soul, and children should not be deprived of this.
On January 6, 1907, Maria Montessori opened the first children's house. From the previous experience, a new childhood appeared what she called "the new child."
It was no more than a disobedient, naughty, rebellious, unable child, but it was a new hardworking, reflective, autonomous child, who even loved to put things in order. A serene, and especially, a self-confident child, a competent child, who was never seen before.
It's the Copernican Revolution in pedagogy!
The first "children's house" was created and launched in 1907 by Maria Montessori, and it was a great success.
And, pay attention, it was a "house" and not a "school," because Maria Montessori refused the concept of school.
The children's house wasn't a place in which education was taught; but it was a place where the adult followed the children, helping them in their development, and the latter can't be taught.
People came from all over the world: educators, ministers, all to acclaim this woman who had revolutionized the scientific pedagogical knowledge, subverting commonplaces.
This school became even a model of civil development, and it was celebrated also by illustrious figures, such as Gandhi and Freud.
Within a short time, the children's houses - Montessori elementary schools - spread all over the world, and, unexpectedly, beyond expectations, they spread also in Italy, in the Fascist Italy, where are built many schools; until the revolutionary pedagogy of this woman, which aimed to form free personalities and consciences, began to become troublesome.
Therefore, after only 10 years, in Italy, all the Montessori school were closed.
The same happened in Germany and Austria.
The books of Maria Montessori were even sent to the stake, she was accused of being a profiteer, a clever enchantress.
But she, once again, wasn't discouraged by these difficulties. She dropped the charges into the void, and she left Italy.
But she didn't absolutely abandon the project to which she will devote her life, her great activity of love.
So, she began to travel: she was hosted in The Netherlands, in India, where she developed some interesting educational experiments especially for what concerns the elementary school.
She traveled the world, going to England and Denmark.
She began to talk of education as an instrument of peace.
On May 6, 1952, Maria Montessori died in the Netherlands.
Montessori, a very widespread approach in the world, and very little in Italy, which still receives amazing confirmations from the educational psychology research and from the neurosciences.
But, most importantly, Montessori is the story of an original nonconformist woman, who has still some interesting and very new things to tell us.
New things not only for her time but also for our time.
I think that Maria Montessori, really, still has much to tell us.
And she can help us even to understand the true nature of the child.
Maria Montessori can help us to place our trust in children, to see the childhood as an educational potential capable of changing the world, as a gold mine.
And above all, because we must do this, otherwise, we succumb to perpetuate the waste of childhood and humanity that befalls, happens when we don't take into account the real needs of children.
I believe that, even today, the Maria Montessori education represents a challenge for us; it encourages us to dare,
to dare to replace children at the center, not to make them the target of our plans and ambitions, because they know better than any other what they need.
But we must put them at the center, to observe, understand, and to give back them the delight of knowing, learning, and living.
I think what Maria Montessori tells us is really to invest in children because they are our real teachers, the only ones able to guide us towards a new and fairer society.
(Applause)
Have you ever tried not to be able to define an intimate relationship, let's call it like this?
Have you had the experience of failing to recognize the person you have been with for a long time and now you see as a stranger?
Or have you ever wondered whether you're in a relationship with somebody that really interests you, or out of boredom, out of habit?
Have you ever thought, or tried to have more than one partnership at the same time?
These are critical questions, in our society, that concern not only intimate relationships but also social relations.
I am a psychologist, Gestalt psychotherapist, I've done psychotherapy training for 40 years, both in Italy and abroad.
I will dissert partnership from my point of view, according to an important and still neglected aspect: synchrony.
The way of being in a couple as a dance, with reciprocity.
I will talk you about couple's reciprocity.
What is synchrony?
Synchrony is the ability to respond to the other in a short enough time to create a rhythm of one with the other, to create a dance.
Synchrony is something that is important today and is already born in primary relationships.
Already in the first relationships between the parents and the child, in the newborn's first days, an imprinting is given to what will be the synchrony of the child's relationships.
The more there is synchrony, the more the child is able to regulate arousal, or the growth of excitement for what he does. Whether they are accompanied by fear or joy.
Mother's vocals soothe the child, and they also make the baby rooted in the relationship.
The more the synchrony, the stronger the sense of alliance in a relationship.
The more there is synchrony, the stronger the sense of self-confidence.
Therefore, synchrony allows us to be more confident and self-centered, and to be able to face the stressful situations of life, independently.
All these years' psychological and neuro-scientific research lead us to describe relationships as relationships in which our self is born and grows within meaningful relationships.
Our brain is made in and for the relationship.
In particular, our brain seems to be sensitive to the movement of the other.
The child, for example, recognises the mother not from the single details of her face, but from the set of details that are harmonized into a movement.
So, we're sensitive to each other's moving harmony.
And we also respond to the movement of the other with our movement: we tend towards each other in a creative way, creating a unique dance made by the movement of the other and by our movement.
This movement is the nest of the couple's dance, of its reciprocity: and the birth of the self.
We are born, and the sense of who we are is born, within meaningful relationships; also, we can say it is born within the movement, the movement that is learned within the relationship.
What does this change in how we look at the couple?
The main change is, we are not interested in the meanings related to the couple's past experiences when the couple is in distress, we are more interested in understanding what the couple is experiencing in the present, right now.
So if one says for example to the other "I hate you, you are the ruin of my life", we are interested in knowing how this sentence is said, what is the person's experience and the person's body experience.
What the person feels physically while telling the other this thing.
This is what we call the phenomenological attitude.
But there is also an aesthetic attitude, following what I told you before.
That is looking at the other like it was a work of art.
If we try to look at the other who's telling us this thing, as they were a work of art, we put aside our wounds, what hooks us.
We look at the other with aesthetic objectivity and let ourselves be fascinated by something that strikes us.
What charms us in the other?
Perhaps a glance that can tell us something particular about the pain, and also about the desire of the other.
Then, we should all be more trained to look at each other in an aesthetic way, to be able to understand the depth of the experience of the other that words do not say.
Something that says the synthesis, the Gestalt, of the movement.
Another thing that changes, is to look at the interaction of couples as a field, as something that the two create together.
The presence of the other is like a stone that falls into water and causes concentric circles in the water.
Much like water, we are shaped by the other, we react to the other and resound with what the other does and says with our past experiences whether they're of failure or success. A game is created between them
of backgrounds and figures.
The backgrounds are made by our past experiences which then come together and meet in the present contact border of the “here and now” which is a desire game played by the two.
The presence of the contact border isn't always clear, well defined.
Sometimes we get desensitized.
Sometimes we do not see the other clearly.
Desensitization is something that concerns our society: ours is a desensitized society.
What does it mean?
It means, we must raise the threshold of our sensitivity because feeling things becomes unbearable.
Where does this difficulty come from?
Largely, from the conditions we grew up in.
Today the conditions are not what they used to be.
The presence of parents is less than it was years ago.
So the child has less the gaze of the parent, the parent's containment, there are many stimuli the child must cope with, but little chance of closing the experiences.
We're zapping a lot from one experience to another but the experiences cannot close, so it's like the child remains in a state of perennial excitement that cannot be released.
This is unpleasant, and for creative adaptation the child becomes desensitized: raises the threshold of their sensitivity.
This also happens to adults; we live in a desensitized world.
What happens then? What does it mean, when the adult has to manage the desire of the other, and falls in love?
It's hard, because it is hard to feel your own body.
It's hard to stay together for a long time because the contact with the other becomes stressful.
It is hard also to have a solid ground, a solid sense of the self, in order to dive with the other into something new.
So that's why couple relationships are hard to manage.
To answer the initial questions, sometimes we wonder if a relationship is such or not, because it's hard to feel the body.
We wonder if being with othe other is something that comes from our desire or not.
Since there is no sense of rooting in the relationship, we do not ask for a single relationship and we have more of them at the same time.
Now, this is the situation; how can we improve it?
In the institution I run with some colleagues, we are studying the couple's reciprocity, or what the couple already does that works well, how is it synchronous, and how can we support these aspects that work well together.
How can we support reciprocity and dance.
When a couple works, they are doing right three things.
One is to recognize the other as another, the otherness of the other.
Recognizing that the other is different without trying to change them: being with the otherness.
The second thing is successfully separate the effect that what the other says to us has on us, from their intention.
When the other person does hurt us, they don't always mean to.
So being able to separate what we feel from what the other actually wants to do with us is an important quality.
The third thing is being able to make a leap into the void, being able to put our wounds aside to see the other and do something that the other likes.
These are the three characteristics of a couple's wealth, of recognizing the otherness of the other person.
What to do then in practice, in everyday life?
I would like to invite you to think of a person you love and the typical movement of the person you love: What's that movement? Think of it;
and when you have time, draw it.
Another thing you can do to understand your couple's synchrony, is asking a friend to watch you while you decide something together, like where to go on holiday.
Then ask them to describe how they see your relationship, how they see your “dance”,
how they would describe it aesthetically. Another thing is to find together two, three things that you know how to do well and ask yourself what you feel when you do this thing well, with the other?
Grocery shopping for example, a very trivial thing.
When I go shopping with him, I do it well because I feel free, I think I can choose everything I want.
Now think about the difficult moment: what the other should do so that you can feel good, free.
For example, the other should smile at you, or tell you that sentence that makes you feel free.
If you can spot this magical thing, that the partner can do with you, it's a big help.
And another thing you can do is to think of the other as someone you love and never hurt you.
Think of the other as they never hurt you, and tell them what made you fall in love. What was the thing, the pain that the person has forged and that makes you strong, gives you strength and courage to reach them with your love, as if they were two intertwined works of art.
The important thing to learn in a relationship is reciprocity.
Reciprocity is something that goes beyond individual meanings.
It can greatly help the relationship, and is made of many things: personal reactions, but above all of the vitality of the two people that are good together.
So, a couple problem is never due to oneof the twio individually, but to something that happens in the dance of their reciprocity.
As a consequence, if each of us sees their wounds as a work of art, then the other can vibrate in front of our wounds as if they were a work of art,
and weave the masterpiece of their life with the life of the other.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Less than a month ago, the school year began.
Do you remember one of your first days of school?
Meeting up with friends, telling them how fine your holidays have been - good days, right?
Yet I'm sure, each and everyone of us, regardless the generation - mine, yours, your children's, if you have them - this thought dawned at least once in each one of us.
[So lame, back to school again!] (Laughter) And who could blame us? Three months of absolute freedom, but with a background "bogeyman" of September closing up, then school comes back and tons of homework, tests, hours of study with it.
So lame.
So why does the school exist?
It's mandatory.
I don’t mean we have to abolish it, absolutely, but what's the real reason for its existence?
We tipically answer these questions like this: to educate young people's social skills, to get them ready for the labour world, so they thrive as people.
How many times have we told them to the children, youngsters?
A million, maybe a billion.
But do they believe it?
Just an example: how many times have we all been forced to study that "brick" of history, philosophy, literature cursing our own life and thinking: "When will I ever need it?".
So, what we were used to do?
We looked for a distraction: watching TV; maybe, in young ages, going out to play with friends; and growing up, we would close the book, dress up and go out.
The effort we put in closing the book, dressing up, going out with friends, has been minimized by today's youth in a single movement.
This is not just the modern symbol of sociality.
That's not my point today.
This tool can retrieve every bit of human knowledge, just by doing a research on Google.
But mostly, it is able to reproduce something that books will never be able to do,
like moving images, videos.
So the first cause of distraction on young people nowadays is actually YouTube.
YouTube has about 2 billion active users every month, and counting rapidly.
More than a billion hours of video viewed per day.
It’s huge.
In an age where information has become the main currency and where fast access to information has become a priority, while pretending to give young people a tool to find informations, in fact we provided them the first cause of distraction.
Yet we're still forced to study.
Yet we are still there, in front of that book, before that brick of history, literature, geology, cursing ourlife, and our professors who force us to study something we’ll probably find on the Internet whenever we need it, if any.
So the question comes back, "What is it for?".
Today we live - well not just today, but by a few years, in a low employment time, especially among youngsters.
We read it every day in the newspapers, we hear of it on the radio. Plus, there’s a creepy feeling among young people, you might not need to study, if you want to get a job.
Two things are really required today: good luck and proper recommendations.
So why study, then? Maybe you don't need it, you just have to learn those four things you need to get a decent job. Nothing ambitious maybe, but enough to win the bread and easily find the rest online.
We are living in a period of strong disillusionment with the study, on both sides of the fence:
the side of the young people, who see in the school an old, ancient, outdated, prehistoric system that does not prepare you for real life. But also on the adults' side: parents are increasingly concerned about the duty to find a job - again, not even an ambitious one, to their children.
So here we have the collapse of enrollment at universities. So school drop-out rate raises.
and this is not a problem for teenagers only. We're worried for their jobs, even when they're children, so we teach them the "jobs of the future".
Chinese or coding are taught to children today, in spite of subjects like geography, which is slowly disappearing from schools. [Geography is no longer taught in schools]
So it seems to me that the only reason why school exists today is just this one: we are growing workers, more than people. Raising, educating children as people, is taken care of by the Internet today: with its videos, its hashtags, but also with its fake news.
And so that's the point I would like us all to take home, and maybe talk about with our children, with young people: “how can we change the teaching so that young people can reconnect to culture?”
We already start with the wrong foot, because we must not “change” the subjects we deem old.
Geography, literature, Latin or chemistry are fine.
Let me give you an example: you've probably heard it, especially lately. We live in a moment of hate, social division, suprematism.
Without a sound historical knowledge - do you remember what happened the last time, after a climate of hate, social division, suprematism?
No, subjects are ok the way they are.
We must rather “fit” our teaching so that young people can come back to culture.
and we must do so, in fact, with what distinguished all of us over the last 20 years and in particular the growth of young people: digital technology.
Today I would like to witness you a vision, a vision we had in my school, of what could be a lesson in the future:
we called it Holodeck, as in “Deck of holograms”.
Do you know the Enterprise?
The Star Trek spaceship?
Someone probably saw Star Trek!
Okay, in the Holodeck you step in and change the environment at your best convenience: you could be in the Maldives, on another planet, as you wished for.
Well, Holodeck for us is a virtual reality classroom where each student has his own headset and is able to attend normal lessons like astronomy, or chemistry, science, but also history and geography, increased with 3D animation.
Moreover, children can see each other, inside the virtual space, and can see their hands, so they can interact with 3D objects. Therefore,
we're not going to change the content, we're updating the communication process.
Actually, we use a technology they're much more sympathetic to.
A teaching system that is based on what they normally see every day: moving images.
Today we thought to bring a demo of holodeck, thanks to the technologies of this room.
It could have been the “wow” effect of the speech.
Unfortunately, a few days ago the M9 museum administration told us, this room would no longer be able to support this type of demo.
So, first of all I apologise and ask you to simply trust my word and the images you have seen: on how incredible this technology really is.
And I invite you, if you want, to come and try it directly at BigRock. At least, the googles work there.
(Laughter) Holodeck is still an experiment: we don't pretend to show you the school of the future, but we'd like to integrate it in the existing teaching process, much like the art laboratory, or science laboratory that already exists in schools.
Imagine an hour of astronomy, an hour of geography, an hour of history or an hour of chemistry, spent inside Holodeck.
That would be incredible.
Also because it is scientifically proven that when a student has to deal with an interactive lesson, as they can also interact with the hands, brain's activity is much more stimulated.
So an interactive lesson like this is much, much more effective.
We have to resume - we have to start, actually - using technology so studnts can fall in love with culture again and we have to teach them a proper use of the technologies they already have.
YouTube, which we seemigly dissed before, in the midst of garbage there’s a lot of beautiful and extremely instructive videos: Ted Talks first.
(Laughter) It's OK to teach coding to children, but with a purpose, to teach them the logics of a computer, so that they can learn to think critically, logically.
Above all, we must try to teach children to dream again, to let them dream about who they want to be when grown up, and we must do this with technology.
We must make them love culture, as nothing but culture will grow them as people.
According to a recent study by LEGO over about 3000 children when asked the question “What would you do, when grown up?”,
more than a third of them said, they'd rather be youtubers, instead of astronauts.
That’s disgusting.
In a moment lke this, confronted as we are with one of the most catastrophic climate changes in our history, we don't need youtubers, we need scientists!
We need doctors, we need architects, we need adequate politicians.
We need all those heroes who thanks to their culture and their knowledge, can mend the mistakes of the past and try to do something to cure this planet.
Or, if they fail to do that, at least find a way to leave it.
So yes, we need astronauts.
So my wish is, on the next first day of school, we all remind our pupils, our children, why they are going to school, why they are studying.
Let’s remind them to dream of a better future, or be inspired by other dreamers.
And remind them to study a lot, so they get a culture and be able to actually fulfill those dreams.
Being halfway as we are, right now, is not really worth it.
Thank you.
(Applause)
How many of you believe, women are as talented in hard sciences, such as mathematics, engineering or physics as men?
Raise your hands, please.
Alright! The majority of you.
But are you really sure about that?
Are you really sure that, in your mind, men and women are equally associated to scientific disciplines?
Well, I want to run a test with you in order to show you that, actually, you aren't aware of your true core beliefs, namely the ones encoded in your mind.
In this test you’ll have to rank some words into four categories, by saying “right” or “left”.
Here you can see the words and the four categories they might belong to.
The test is divided in two parts.
In the first part, you’ll have to say “left” if the word on the screen belongs to “humanities” or “female” categories.
You’ll have instead to say “right” if it belongs to “scientific disciplines” or “male” categories.
So, for example, if you see the word “Laura”, what will you say?
Left.
Great.
And if you see the word “Marco”?
Right.
And what if you see the word “Physics”?
Right.
Perfect.
And if you see the word "Art"?
OK, let's do some tries before the real test. Remember:
"Left" for humanities and female;
"Right" for sciences and male.
Are you ready? Let's go!
Okay. You kind of got it.
I saw someone in the back who was looking around, trying to understand whether to say "left" or "right".
No need to worry; it's normal to make some mistakes.
Now let's move to the real test.
Once again, remember: "left" for humanities and female; "right" for male and scientific disciplines.
Are you ready? Let's go!
Very good.
You’ve been quite quick.
Your average answer time is roughly 700 milliseconds.
Now let's move to the second part of the test.
You will be giving the same words as before.
Let's see if we can see them on the screen.
There they are. Perfect.
So, the same words as before.
But now you will have to say “left”, if the word belongs to “humanities” or “male” categories; and “right” if instead these words belong to “scientific disciplines” and “female” categories.
OK. Don't worry.
Here too we'll have some tries before starting, ok?
Remember: "Left" for humanities and male; "Right" for science and female.
OK?

Let's start.
OK.
You made it, more or less.
Now let’s move to the last part.
Once again remember: “left” for “humanities” and “male”,
“right” for “science” and for “female”.
Ready, steady, go!
So...
In this test, your average answer time was around 1.000 milliseconds.
As you can see, you were slower in this second part of the test than in the first part.
What does it mean?
It means that it's easier for you to link the concept of “female” to humanities and the idea of “male” with STEM, not the other way around.
This therefore suggests, there are in your mind implicit gender stereotypes, that you are unaware of, and lead you to link sciences to men over women.
You and I, as the majority of people, show this stereotype.
I still remember the first time I've done this test and showed these same results.
I asked myself: “how is that possible?"
How can a researcher like me, who firmly believes that men and women have the same skills in scientific fields, show such a stereotype?
I was really astonished to discover this hidden side of my mind.
But, actually, this gender stereotype in the scientific field is only one example of the different stereotypes and prejudices we may have in our minds.
We can have stereotypes and biases linked to different social characteristics, such as: skin colour, ethnicity, sexual, political and religious orientation, weight, age, physical diseases, disability and mental illness.
But what are the consequences of these unconscious biases and stereotypes?
Scientific research showed that these biases and stereotypes affect the way we perceive and relate with others.
For example, a study published on the scientific journal “Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America" showed that choosing a male candidate, over a female candidate - to carry out an arithmetic task - all skills and qualifications being equal, is linked to the gender stereotype in the scientific field.
Moreover, some researchers of the Harvard University showed that race biases even influence the decisions of doctors and other professionals in the health case industry.
In particular, it has been demonstrated that doctors who show a strong preference for white people, compared to black people, recommend less often a treatment for the latter, even when they show the same health conditions and the same medical needs as white patients.
So how can we free our behaviour from these unconscious stereotypes and biases?
How can we consciously align our behaviours to our conscious beliefs and intentions?
Over the last years, this need has been widely recognised.
For example, companies like Google, Facebook, Starbucks or universities like Harvard and even Police departments like NYPD organised lectures for their employees,
in order to raise their awareness about these unconscious stereotypes and biases,
and how they act and influence our behaviour.
In particular, over the last 20 years researchers have tried to create interventions and strategies to tamper these stereotypes and biases.
For example, it was shown that it's possible to reduce the ethnical and racial prejudice by giving information against the same stereotype, for example by presenting an aggression scenario where the white person is the aggressor and the black person is the rescuer.
These interventions though, despite being effective, proved to be limited, especially over time.
Their effect, in fact, only lasts for some hours or days.
The idea I am working on with my research, is that these stereotypes and biases are so deeply rooted in our mind that in order to change them, we have to change our brain's biological mechanisms that create and maintain them.
In particular, my research aims at introducing a new technique, called "Non-Invasive Brain Stimulation", which belongs to neurosciences, a different science field.
These non-invasive techniques are considered safe and they allow, through small electric impulses in the brain, to change the biological mechanisms responsible for producing and controlling our behaviour.
The studies that use this technique in the field of stereotypes detected some specific areas of the brain that are involved in these processes.
These regions are known for their perception function, as the area of the striatum, or areas involved in processes linked to moral decisions, as the Inferior Parietal Lobe; areas linked to control processes, as the prefrontal cortex; and areas linked to semantic memory, like the anterior temporal lobe.
These studies showed that, by increasing or decreasing the activity of some of these areas, for example the anterior temporal lobe, it is possible to reduce stereotypes, such as the gender one in the scientific field or the bias which, in someone's mind, ties Arabs to terrorism.
I see some stunned faces.
Some of you is probably thinking, I want to do a remake of Kubrick's “A Clockwork Orange” and start to brainwash people.
To be honest, I’ve always loved Kubrick’s movies and especially that one.
But you must know that, even if Non-Invasive Brain Stimulation techniques are hardly ever used in the field of social stereotypes, they are really popular in the medical field.
They are used, for example, to diagnose motor system disorders, for example after a stroke; or to treat neurological and psychiatric disorders, such as the chronic neuropathic pain, the Parkinson disease or depression.
In particular, it was demonstrated that these techniques are really effective in treating drug-resistant depression, that is, when drugs fail to work in the patient.
These studies demonstrated that, by stimulating a particular area of the brain, the Dorsal Lateral Prefrontal Cortex, depression symptoms improve in 60% of patients.
One third of them even shows a full remission from illness.
In a society like ours, where globalization has led to the coexistence of different cultural and social groups, these non-invasive brain stimulation techniques can be critical to free our behaviour from biases and stereotypes,
especially in those situations where, as we've seen, they can produce serious consequences.
But there is more: these techniques could also be used to reduce biases and stereotypes, which concern us first-hand and influence our life.
It has been proved that prejudices and stereotypes don't only influence our behaviour with others: but they can also affect our very performance.
A study that monitored 34 countries for eight years showed that the performance of girls aged between 13 and 14 years, in mathematics and other scientific fields, is closely linked to the strength of the gender stereotype in the scientific field.
These results therefore lead to suggest that stereotypes can potentially influence us and prevent our access to specific business fields, or hinder our potential.
and the one of the next generations.
The introduction of the Non-Invasive Brain Stimulation Techniques can finally bring about a bias-free society, one that is focused at strengthening the talent of its citizens towards science and progress.
Thank you very much.
(Applauses)
I won't talk to you about technological innovation, not the way I imagine it at least.
And before telling you a little about what I do, I want to tell you a story to make you understand that the theme of sustainability comes from a long time ago.
First - this tale I want to tell you is a wonderful story.
Marco Polo crosses from Venice to China, and when he arrives in Persia, in the heart of Persia, so we're between Iraq and lran, a place of deserts, terribly hot, When he arrives in a village, the Persians - we're in 1472-73, as a sign of welcome they offer him a tamarind ice cream.
A tamarind ice cream in 1470?
Sounds like a question that needs an answer.
How could they eat an ice cream?
That's why it's important to talk about architecture.
It probably took centuries for Persians to understand how to make it work.
We're in a period when there's no electricity.
Then they built this building you can see, which is a large wall facing south.
This wall protected a small, let's call it a swimming pool, a small basin.
And in front of this wall there was a large dome that had been obtained by digging a cistern.
And what did the Persians do?
At night, they deposited a thin layer of water in this pool, and since the wall protected that area from the sun all day, there was no thermal gain, it never heated up.
As a result of the celestial canopy, which, as you know when you travel by plane, the captain says, it's -55 degrees outside, the celestial canopy is -55 degrees and because of celestial radiation, in a historical period with obviously no pollution, the sky was clear and because of the celestial radiation, there was this layer of icy water.
And night after night, the Persians broke this ice, removed it, and put it in this cistern.
During the summer, + 55 degrees outside, there's a dome protecting this great mound of ice through a simple principle of ventilation, so the sun's radiation which became a temperature inside this dome was then expelled through a system of natural ventilation.
This is one remaining pieces of architecture among, sadly, few because of war which, besides being a terrible thing per se, also destroys cultural goods.
This example is an important one, because the Persians, despite the great summer heat, ate ice cream during the summer, and offered it to him.
Perhaps in Rome it would have been called a granita, which is crushed ice together with some essences.
This is an important example to share with you, because we're looking at what will happen in the next 30, 40, 50 years.
Now, to give you an idea of the scale of the problem, then you'll understand why I told you about Marco Polo - talking about sustainability is important, because what's happening is unprecedented in our history.
Just to give you the numbers, until now, on Earth, we have built on 145 billion square metres, and I can't even explain how big that is physically.
Lots of stuff, lots of stuff.
But that's not the point.
The point is that in the next 15 years, we will build half as much again as what we've already built.
This is the scale of the problem.
73 billion square metres is more or less a billion units of accommodation.
This is what will happen, and what is slowly happening in countries that are growing.
Maybe Italy has a different problem, but in other countries around the world - So, in order to do that, that is, to build 75, 73 billion square metres, we need cement, glass, bricks, lime.
We need to bring water, in some places we need to provide some cooling, like in these pictures, which also help us understand the scale of consumption.
Have we perhaps placed too much trust in technology?
Because the technology - and now I'm talking about construction, not technology in a broader sense - Technology for buildings has been a great achievement.
Because, in the last 50 years, we've been living in buildings that no longer have any relationship with the climate.
It doesn't matter at all if it's hot outside, or cold.
I've got my air conditioning, with my thermal control.
See, this technological innovation was a bit like these robots, a bit of a dream for mankind; do something against nature.
But now nature has presented us with a reckoning.
You wanted high-tech buildings?
And now we have a series of problems that we've heard about before.
Which are those of climate change, local changes in conditions.
So, perhaps, also looking at the goals for coming years, we have to ask ourselves some questions.
Now to give you some more simple data, every week, one and a half million people move from the countryside to the cities.
This is the movement that's taking place.
It would also be worth discussing this topic, because this huge phenomenon of megacities was a great dream, and also a great opportunity, but it has also had a few little side effects, hasn't it?
That's why, if you've ever had the opportunity to see the Biennale, we talk about the inner areas, that is, they are not metropolitan cities because they are cultural deposits.
What's going on?
We have before us a road map that was - Europe has designed a scenario, and this scenario has no alternative, this is the way.
It has been since 1992, with Rio de Janeiro, where a conference on climate change was held every year, for 21 years.
The last agreement, the Paris one, which we would call more successful, is also the result of 20 years of discussions.
But why is time important?
Because the true theme here is not just climate change, which is going to happen in any case, whether we want it or not, whatever efforts we make.
The real change here is cultural change.
We have to enter another era.
We're no longer in the era of using fossil fuels.
We're entering a new era, an ecological era.
In an ecological era, like this. You can see, this map tells us that in 2020, in 2030 we'll have meetings, but what's the goal?
The goal is to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.
And yet, despite the Paris agreement, the indices this year are increasing, not decreasing.
But then what does the framework tell us?
I'll talk about it. It seems I'm not talking about architecture, but it is important.
Because the built-up world contributes 70 per cent of CO2 emissions from energy consumption.
So we have absolutely got to talk about it.
Here they ask us: to reduce CO2 emissions; apart from the numbers, to make energy savings; and from the other side to consume less energy.
But how are we going to do this?
Because CO2 can't be seen.
And in a world of images, if it can't be seen, it doesn't exist.
How do we produce renewable energy?
And how can we consume less?
So, perhaps, and this is what seems the most interesting theme to me, perhaps we have to go fishing right in that huge cultural repository that is our history, where buildings were built when there was no electric power.
How did they manage to create wonderful buildings, for 2000 years, when power wasn't one of the basic components?
This leads me to think that the theme of innovation goes back a bit, perhaps - perhaps we should look back to see what innovation is.
If it's just like a car, if it's just a programme, if it's just a computer.
Or maybe, in construction or architecture, we have to find a relationship that I call empathy with the environment.
So there are some wonderful examples.
I don't want to bore you with too many things., Still, for centuries people have created, you might say, complicity with the environment.
They built - these are windcatchers.
They take the prevailing wind, they bring it inside the houses, they cool the houses at night and in the daytime it's ten degrees lower.
Now, we clearly don't want to do this.
That's the past. I'm not nostalgic, nor do I have the idea that the past is always better than the future.
I believe that the future will always be better.
But it intrigues me.
I'm intrigued, for example, by the Pakistanis, who make these towers with this type of catcher, this kite, which collects the wind, channels it into homes, and lowers the temperature.
At night, these houses are cooler, and by day - outside it's 50 degrees, inside it's only 42.
That's a difference of seven or eight degrees.
All this makes me think that in there, in these buildings - of course we won't make them like that, but some knowledge is hidden in them.
That is, they knew how to do it.
They knew how to react.
So, if Europe is telling me, and we are all working with this idea, that the buildings of 2020 i.e. the day after tomorrow, must be at almost zero energy, and that should virtually all be made with renewable resources, the first thing that comes to mind is "I'll study the Pakistanis".
I'm going to study those that had this complicity with energy.
And we live in a world where we have an opportunity that we never had before.
Which is precisely that of technology.
They didn't even have a computer, but the Persians, perhaps by doing so many tests, realised that at some point, this relationship with the environment could be very beneficial.
i.e. that energy, which was outside, was something that could be useful to people; it was not against man, but for him.
We've completely lost this wisdom.
We've completely lost the ability to communicate with the environment, because we've had a wonderful dream.
We said: now, with technology, with machines, we can do everything we want.
You know, what's out there doesn't matter to us.
Look, this doesn't work anymore.
I like to think that the future of innovation will actually be a hybrid thing.
Maybe there's technology, but there's also knowledge, there's also something we could do.
We knew how to do this, once.
Another wonderful example.
This is a story of architects.
This is a palace in Merhabad.
Instead of being built above the earth it's dug underground.
Every ten metres you go down, it also drops one degree.
And these were the houses, and the squares, where the Indians took refuge when it was 60 degrees outside.
And they were also public places.
So it was a building in reverse.
In half-darkness,
the earth - as you know, when you dig up the ground, after a few metres the temperature's constant around 16, 17 degrees.
So if I - that's why there's an incredible subterranean architecture: when I go underground, whatever the temperature outside, it's 18, 20 degrees inside.
The nice thing about this building, if you go - it's called Stepwell, in India, it turned out so well, this building, the Maharaja was so pleased that he killed the six architects who worked on it.
And outside the entrance to this building, there are their six tombstone, as a form of honour.
As if to say: it's so beautiful, that you mustn't do it for anyone else.
That's how dangerous my job can get.
(Laughter) Today - (Applause) And here we come back again to the idea of responsibility.
Because doing this job - I always say, architects are very dangerous people, because they can build wonderful things or they can create nightmares.
And there are many nightmares, all over the world.
This is the Camera dello Scirocco, where people in Catania took refuge during the Mediterranean sirocco winds.
And this room, with natural ventilation, allowed them to stay below.
Now I will show you two simple things, to finish, about the relationship with nature.
These are the dunes of Algiers, and these are stone constructions that were made to collect the heat, and with the day-night change in humidity moisture condensed, around these stones and pools were built to provide water for the animals.
So this was all about the shape.
This is a building which we're making in Algiers, the first building to address this theme of sustainability in consumption, in a country that is the largest gas producer in the world.
This is a palm tree, and a salt cave, out of which came this building, which is in Ghana. We made it for a Ghanaian developer, and it became a landmark, because it recapitulates some themes that had been lost: shade, natural ventilation.
A know how that allowed this building to work half a year without any conditioning.
Just through a passive principle.
So I like to think that maybe the future of our buildings is no longer high-tech, that chunky building full of facilities, but maybe it's a mediation between knowledge and innovation.
I'll wrap up with this image, this greeting.
This is a Bedouin with his camel, crossing the desert.
Oh well.
That said, if I change the point of view, and I'll show you this, this isn't a Bedouin.
It's a doctor with a camel, which has a GPS antenna on its head, and a photovoltaic panel that produces energy, which powers a battery, which powers a fridge containing medicines.
You see: here, in this image, there's a hidden idea we haven't understood yet.
The future, perhaps, is this ability to bring together technological innovation and a body of knowledge that's as old as the world.
Thank you.
(Applause)
"Freeze" is an English word that means: stay still, don't move.
This word is dear to me, because it is linked to awakening.
In fact, I want to speak of awakening as something necessary to shake our inertia, our inaction away.
And it is important to move right now because an unexpected, risky future awaits us.
Movement is fundamental to reconsider these risks in a positive way.
Sure, I talk about it from my point of view.
As an architect, I have to understand what my role is within this change, how I can intervene, and above all the importance of architects and architecture within this context.
The context we are talking about is climate change.
A change that entails, not just a new, different scenario, but rather an ever changing one, second by second, over the next hundreds, maybe thousands of years.
What about architecture?
Every time I talk about this topic, the question becomes, Yes, this is a political issue that's up to decision makers, probably corporate CEOs, industrial policies and the economy.
As you can see on this graph, the reality is not exactly like that.
Architects play a fundamental role.
If we talk about CO2, we see that probably, indeed certainly, CO2 emissions are largely caused by buildings.
If we also consider the transport system and combine the two together, considering how the city shape affects the transport system, we will see that the city itself is the main cause of CO2 emissions.
The role of the architect is therefore important, if not pivotal.
In fact, we also know what ingredients are needed to meet this need.
Research helps us so we know that these cities are more resilient, they can adapt to this change more easily, and this change comes with extreme climate changes.
So cities need to adapt to these extreme climate changes.
The second aspect is compactness.
We have to expect increasingly dense and compact cities because the availability of resources is increasingly limited; it's up to the productive land.
The larger the size of the city, the greater the availability of productive land.
This leads me to conclude, aware as I am of the population growth,
if we don't want to get to what the Portsmouth research centre calls the "impossible equation", we will have to consider a new element, which is the key element for awakening, and it's creativity.
That's why we talk about radical Concept, we talk about vision, the ability to see the world with different eyes.
This doesn't actually mean we just talk about the future and no longer have to look at the past.
Before the industrial revolution, we are aware of that, some of these models had already been applied.
Venice is clearly a radical city, a visionary city, but we could also look to other models such as Shibam, in Yemen,
a city that on every profile meets our climate needs in the desert area.
An ultra-compact city that withstands the hot desert winds.
It has this white upper surface with high albedo that allows a drastic reduction in the Heat Island Effect, something that is often talked about as if it was a problem ancestors weren't aware of.
Of course, even this idea of being visionary is not new per se.
We know there was a historical period when vision has been decisive, and it has been decisive precisely for that kind of ability to see the world in a radical way that came from the world of architecture, or art.
I'm referring to the Renaissance.
What does the Renaissance have to do with today's climate crisis?
Because the crisis of the 14th century had characteristics that closely match the current one: a reduction in resources, a population that was no longer able to access these resources, climate change in the opposite direction, i.e. a mini ice age. And last but not least, the problem related to the fact that a disease, the black death, was somehow the reason why society as a whole took a different shape, somehow upholstering economies and societies.
This is important because, obviously, the reference to the first two factors is quite immediate, but the reference to health is just as important, while not self-evident.
We know that climate change will be a major cause of disease in the next twenty years.
People like Leonardo, a visionary, people who are able to see the world with new, different eyes, without the mental frames that affected us all, is fundamental.
How can we nurture today a creative ability that leads us to awaken?
I have my personal idea.
It comes from the fact, I am also an educator.
This can be done through education, thanks to the fact that young people and students are not conditioned like us dinosaurs by a worldview that is somehow determined by the last centuries of history.
So our job is precisely to try and ignite in them the ability to be creative, inventive, even radical and extreme.
Here we see a series of works done by students and collaborators of my Media Hub research centre who are, as well as students, also real researchers.
This is Liam Stumbles in this example.
He figured out a city that is revolutionised by the idea of eliminating car traffic altogether, and reusing the stuff our highways are made of to rebuild a new urban fabric.
Here we see the example of the city of Auckland and the consequences for this city: spaces originally intended for motorways become parks and structures that can be used by the whole city population.
We see that the system tries to take advantage of state-of-the-art technologies, such as the use of drones, and the so-called swarm behavior, the ability of drones to move in swarms. And we see that the possibility of changing the performance of the city gets critical in this line of thought.
Another example is to use botany as an unrban revolution tool; the ecological systems that arise from plants' genetic modification.
Or to draw a city, as in the case of Naim Mukif, assuming that the city must be drawn not only on human flows, on those that are the movements of people and their needs, but also on that of bees - just today it has been declared that bees are the planet's most important animals.
Or we can use cellular stuff of this type,
we can understand how it expands, and infer from that what the rearrangement tendencies of these organisms are. And we can also understand, at the same time, how we can directly use this material as a building material.
In this case we use it as a brise soleil, as a possibility to make the windows more opaque, by controlling the development.
Or an extreme like this, designed by Joyce Kwan, which is the transformation of cities in a totally organic solution.
Here we see a project by Michael Brewster and Bevin Liang, focused on the chance of including automation and robotics in the city.
This technology allows the city to be resilient, meaning that the ability to adapt precisely depends on the city's robotic equipment.
It's not just about speculative elaborations, it's also about making responsive models, as in this case, using an Arduino, to these project hypotheses,
to the last city in this series that I'd like to show you, which is a city in Antarctica with extreme conditions.
What we are talking about here is the possibility of using our planet's extreme climate conditions as resources, obviously limiting our carbon footprint.
However, these extreme areas generate usable resources.
Here we see examples of this city system, station system which are a form of colonisation of Antarctica.
We see that, in some way, this system is very similar to that of oil platforms but its goal is completely different: harness marine movements, for example, and recreate a habitat that promotes a good relationship between humans and animals.
Here we see how a relationship is somehow created with the oceanic fauna.
Now the typical question, when I talk about these things, is:
What's the cost for transition?
The question is not a random one, because it is seemingly a rhetorical one.
Many people say, a change would cost a hell of a lot.
After all, our society is based on fossil fuels, and maintaining the status quo, in many people's opinion, is certainly a cheaper strategy.
The answer is no.
By "No", I mean that transition is cheaper than the status quo.
We do not have time to elaborate here, but a few images are enough to understand why that's not the case.
Here is a state-of-the-art oil platform that digs, sucks the last drops of blood from our planet, from the depths of the ocean with irreversible damage to biodiversity.
The second objection is about the cities I showed you.
If you asked, I am convinced more than half of you would hold the point, these are dystopian visions, and many among you have already said to your neighbour: "Oh yes, interesting, maybe - hopefully - but I'd never want to live there".
This is interesting, because it is probably correct to say "I would never want to live there".
But what is dystopia, if not also a condition of unfamiliarity with a certain condition.
Because our natural condition is this: we don't live in country houses with a backyard. We live in this conditions.
80% of the world's population already lives like this today, and probably much more so in future.
Either the left or the right one, 50 / 50.
Are we sure that this condition is not a dystopian condition, or it just so happens, our feeling of comfort with the environment we live in is only related to familiarity with this environment?
This is a question I'd rather not to answer.
You probably already understood that those cities I took as examples try to say one very precise thing, that is, to stimulate this discussion.
It would come handy for me to show you much more reassuring models.
I'd wrap up saying that awakening, in my point of view, asks to see the world from a completely different perspective.
I was lucky in my life: for work and research, I was able to move to New Zealand and this allowed me to actually see the world from as far as possible from my country, and from the most different perspective also.
From the South Pacific, and in particular from a fauna, from an environmental context that's so different from ours,
even if it is said that 2.000 years ago, even before the Roman Empire, New Zealand looked very much like Italy.
What particularly strikes me about New Zealand is the biodiversity and the characteristics of the fauna.
New Zealand, mind you, is a place where humans, Maori, didn't get until 1200-1300, and in 1700 we Westerners came to colonise it.
So a natural habitat thrived that's extremely different from our usual one.
The kakapo, in my opinion, is an animal that somewhat represents this condition.
Kakapos don't fly, despite being large parrots.
When it has to reproduce it chooses one of the dozens of volcanoes that are found for example in Auckland - hundreds, maybe thousands are all across New Zealand, and goes to the top of this hill.
Once at the top of this hill, it starts screaming.
This is his way of attracting his partner, who in turn climbs this hill to mate.
Now, I always think about this condition because I come from Tuscany, and I imagine a wild boar climbing up a hill and screaming.
I don't know how long it would take before a hunter goes and takes the dinner, hinted at by this signal.
Another very interesting aspect is that at a certain point in his life the kakapo met humanity,
And what the kakapo did, when he met man for the first time, was freezing: he stopped, he got stuck, hoping that the hunter would not detect him, hoping to trick him into thinking the kakapo was actually a tree or a stone.
Just imagine how successful this strategy turned out to be, and the risks of extinction the kakapo still faces today.
And that's my bottom line: basically, we are the kakapo today,
And I hope that's a consideration that you will share with me.
(Applause)
For six years I did not pee.
Not a single drop.
I could only drink half a liter of water a day.
My kidneys had gone. I could no longer expel water and toxins.
It is incredible how we only notice all the functions of an organ when it ceases to work.
My speed changed when I was 23, just when you are looking for your own path, at the height of the desire to fully live this life.
When we still dream like children, but we start doing adult projects.
For me, there was no longer speed or time.
I was lost.
It was as if everything had stopped.
I had met a wall, smashed against it and there everything stopped.
Diagnosed with immune complex glomerulonephritis, or Berger's disease, an autoimmune disease that attacks the kidneys.
At that moment I knew who had taken my dreams and my strong pillars,
and the only certainty I had left was the name of the disease, which contained the word "chronic". Then I immediately understood that we would have lived together forever.
The final diagnosis came after more than two months of uninterrupted hospitalization, at the mercy of different types of care.
I felt obliterated and numb.
Looking at myself in the mirror in my hospital room's bathroom, I didn’t recognize myself.
I would stare at my eyes and all I saw inside me was emptiness.
A black abyss with no light.
Because of the aggressive treatment, I was losing my hair, my curls no longer existed.
Hair was sparse, falling in strands.
I was utterly crippled, I could hardly walk and stand up, I no longer had muscles and the therapies I had to do were truly heavy.
The CVC, the catheter I had in the jugular artery of my neck. I couldn’t even see it.
I was crying in an overwhelming silence.
For those two months, waking up every single day was like a present.
I entered the hospital on February 17th, that time, and left it in late April.
Through the window of my nephrology room, I saw the snow and the rain falling, then I watched the leaves growing back on naked branches, the green grass, the flowers bloom.
I also felt that spring inside my heart, I wanted to be reborn, despite everything.
I used to lie down on the bed next to that window, close my eyes and I used to imagine being in my mountains, under the Monviso, walking through those paths where, before the hospital, I use to spend a lot of time with the people I love.
I began imagining, dreaming - it made me feel good!
I realized there were parts of myself I wasn't aware of, I had an inner strength to fight back which I had no clue about.
I wanted to live and I would fight with all my might so as not to lose this chance.
Then I understood, willpower is our greatest strength!
I was taking more than 16 tablets a day, plus several injections of other medicines, but I wanted to be happy and what most made me happy was being in nature.
I needed to feel this life!
I figured out how fragile
and ephemeral it is. While I saw it flow, I couldn’t feel it anymore, I wanted to feel it, to be moved by it, grasp it, and live it!
Climbing arrived in my life like a river in flood!
It lit a spark, a fire!
I had already tried climbing before but I was too busy with the pace of life and had no time to commit myself to a passion.
I had to create and design my own way. After everything that had happened to me, I understood, we should create our path slowly, on the experiences we make. It makes no sense to plan too much and I realized, the passions that ignite a spark in us should be listened to, as they make us feel alive.
Climbing made me, and still makes me, feel good
It is just me and the rock under my fingers. I perceive my body in its totality, I live in the present moment because all of myself stays in what I’m doing,
all the other problems are left at the foot of the wall, while I climb up, far away.
During those five years, despite all the medicines I was taking and despite the constant checks, I lived.
But in 2012, when I was 28, the disease came back.
She had always been there, silently, watching me and making me know myself.
Like a severe teacher and spectator.
But this time she was more aggressive.
Being an autoimmune disease, it is difficult and often impossible to predict its evolution.
And when the doctor told me that within two months I would have had to get into dialysis, I got in the car, I closed the door and I started crying so much I threw up.
As if I wanted to throw out the disease!
My parents and my aunt did all the tests to donate me a kidney and save me from dialysis, but none of them was compatible with me.
I ran out of time, they put a catheter in my belly to do the peritoneal dialysis.
Every four hours, night and day, I had a solution flowing in and out the abdomen that by osmosis would clean my blood from toxins and water.
I went on like this for five months, but it didn’t work for me.
They tried again with the dialysis machine, I was connected to it from morning to night, stuck in a bed, for two weeks, but since I had strong peritonitis in my childhood, my peritoneum was injured
and this type of dialysis didn’t work for me.
I felt like I had been poisoned, and thoughts came to my mind I’m now deeply ashamed of,
but I was tired and my mind out of control.
Now if I look back at those dark thoughts, I'm so glad I didn’t give up because I would have lost the wonderful things life has given and keeps giving me.
I gained almost 11kg of toxins and water, which ended up in the pericardium, the pocket around the heart.
So I was urgently taken by an ambulance,
I could hardly breathe and I was scared by the sole thought of that word, but I was passing away and I did not want to!
So I started telling the ambulance doctor that I did not want to die.
(Applause) He held my hand
and I kept repeating the same thing.
Until I closed my eyes, and don’t remember anything, just flashes of an orange picture with sunflowers on my left and my mother crying on my right.
The doctor, the next morning, told me that I had spent the whole night attached to a hemodialysis machine.
They had saved me.
After a month they dismissed me and sent me home, and from there my life on dialysis began.
Three times a week I'd go to the hospital, lay down in bed and spend four hours attached to this machine, which became my life partner, the hospital became my second home and the nurses part of my family.
I couldn’t drink, only half a liter of water a day.
When it was hot I looked at the bottle of water as if it were a drug, I wanted to drink, I was thirsty, but I couldn’t.
I cried because I knew it would have hurt me.
Sometimes I would even get three or four kilograms from one dialysis to another, sometimes I drank on the sly as if I were hiding from myself too.
Six years like that.
The pace of my life changed again.
This time, punctuated by the machine three times a week.
I went on the transplant list, since then my phone had to be always available, day and night, I could be called at any time.
The doctors told me I'd be reborn with the transplant, I would come back to life:
but I didn't want to wait.
I wanted to live despite dialysis.
I wanted to decide the speed of my life.
Being in dialysis is a bit like living halfway, one out of two days, you are connected to the machine. I was always exhausted on my way back home.
However, even if I lived 50%, I didn’t know how long I would have had to wait to be transplanted.
But I knew that the more I would hide and numb in the disease, the less I would have done, fought, lived.
So I said to myself, I want to fully live that remaining half. Mine were the days far from the machine.
In that 50% of my life I was left with, I reloaded and I created moments, I collected them and took them with me to the hospital.
My happy moments, those that make you fly like Peter Pan.
I was there, in a bed, attached to a machine but my heart was in the places I loved, climbing with friends in nature.
I started climbing again, in trouble, slowly at the beginning, with greater grit and passion, choosing willpower instead of surrendering myself to the disease.
It was hard but I tried.
My limits were not like I had figured them out.
Climbing, perhaps because of the ascent itself, was something spiritual for me, as well as therapeutic.
The effort to get to the top was rewarded by the beauty of the landscape that could be seen from there.
Height scared me, but it helped me to move my limits a little higher.
Like my illness, where suffering and pain made me stronger.
Meter by meter, I was discovering the courage of facing something that seems impossible.
In all this, I had a special person by my side, my partner Stefano, who has always supported me, helped me, encouraged me, loved me.
We often left with our van and I booked dialysis in many Italian hospitals.
This helped me feel less in that dialysis cage.
I traveled, always knowing I couldn't get too far away, as the transplant phone call I was waiting for could have come at any time.
But the most wonderful thing that Stefano gave me was freedom.
We did a course and together we learned how to use a portable hemodialysis machine that I called iRene [iKidney].
He became my caregiver, in any sense.
He became my nurse, he learned to put the needles, to use the machine connecting all the tubes, lines, filters and bags.
We both learned to become freer, less dependent from the hospital.
Stefano, iRene and I managed to go climbing in Spain.
It was a dream for me to be there.
We were climbing up this amazing rock I had only seen in pictures.
I was happy.
In these six years, even if I lived halfway, I managed to live.
But there have also been many difficult times,
because dialysis is hard, it destroys you, it slowly eats you.
Every time you connect to the machine it feels like it takes away a bit of yourself.
I went through several hospitalizations. Every time, a thrashing I had to get up from.
There were ups and downs, often too many downs, but climbing brought me back up, it was always a baseline.
It made me feel good, it made me forget everything, it made me feel alive and became one of my best medicines!
During the last year of dialysis, I was really tired.
My body was slowly falling apart.
But nine months ago, on February 2nd, at half past four in the morning, the phone I had always kept by my side rang.
It was the Molinette hospital in Turin, I almost passed out, there was a compatible kidney.
(Applause) A backpack with all the things for the hospital and my pajamas with colorful unicorns were in the closet, waiting for six years to be grasped in a hurry.
After four hours of traveling, we arrived in Turin.
The writing above the ward door said "kidney transplant", and I took a second to sink in.
I was nervous, but from the moment I got in there, I realized that I was in a family. They reassured me, they took care of me, they transplanted me, they gave me back my life.
Shortly after waking up from the surgery, when I had my first pee after all those years, I cried so much from happiness that my heart burst with joy.
This was only possible thanks to the huge gift I received.
Organ donation, I believe, is the most unconditional, the purest act of love a person can do.
It's literally about donating life!
I will keep this gift and take it with me, protecting it, taking care of it for as long as I can, with immense gratitude.
Now I feel like I'm breathing clean and fresh oxygen.
I love climbing as much as before, and I'm glad I’ve never given up the very passion that kept me alive!
In the course of these last ten years, I have also understood what emptied and destroyed me.
I had to break apart to become a little more complete.
I learned the kindness of accepting, the aggressiveness of fighting, but also the relief of abandoning myself.
From those difficult days I’ve earned the greatest teachings one could receive:
the awareness that we never fully know each other and that our limits are far beyond what we generally imagine, that we must use our willpower, this powerful engine we have
that helps us to get up, even when the weight we carry seems to be too heavy.
Although I know the future will be uncertain and the transplant will not be forever, given my autoimmune disease, the profound meaning all this is having for me will stay.
And all my scars remind me that I'm alive, that I have survived. They remind me to be grateful every single day because life is a priceless gift.
Therefore live it fully, cultivate the passions that keep you alive, that make your eyes shine and your heart beats, that move emotions at night before falling asleep.
It is impossible to convey what I went through in these ten years, but I am sure that each one of you in life has had to fight, accept, get up again, grow through these experience and eventually understand yourself more.
Don’t let the spark of hope fade away. Keep the desire to fight alive and never give up, even when everything seems dark.
Appreciate more what we have in order to complain less about what we lack.
And I learned that the years of dreams, which I thought I had lost when I was 23 - when I discovered I was sick - are not the only years to dream.
There is no age to dream - indeed, I now dream even more, with the awareness that with my willpower I can try to achieve my dreams.
(Applause)
I love nature since I was a child.
My memories go back to when I looked at the world from above, from my dad's backpack, during summer excursions in the mountains, and then up from my meter-and-something height, as soon as I learned to walk and understand what a path was.
My father, a nature photographer, taught me to observe and ask questions.
The butterfly resting on my hand, the male ibexes struggling in the autumn mist, the minerals shining in the sun.
Slowly they became companions that I found every summer.
On the shore of these alpine lakes I gave my first kiss, and many of the imaginary journeys I did as a teenager took place in the mountains, they were set there.
Climbing upwards and discovering new paths was everytime a new adventure: but also something that made me feel at home.
Summer after summer, looking for shooting stars in the night sky and a healthy passion for Star Wars
made me want to learn more about the universe.
So I bought a book that explained the stars, the constellations, and I read about dust nebulae that created planets and particles traveling since the origin of the universe, about nuclear fusion at the center of the sun.
Everything increased my sense of wonder.
Huge spaces and times were at the reach of a simple binocular, sometimes I could perceive them with the naked eye, like when I found the Andromeda Galaxy in the sky and I realized that I was looking back over two million years, that's how far away that light came from.
Or when I saw the center of our galaxy, the Milky Way, and I realized, all in all, we were practically made of the same elements.
At school I wasn't very good at physics. I got by at calculations, but proving the theorems was not my cup of tea. Now, my science professor had majored in forest science, a discipline I didn't even know existed.
He explained to me, this subject could teach not only to know nature but also to work with her for the good of society.
It seemed like a job in touch with the environment. Plus, I was fascinated by the prospect of putting my feet on the ground, rather than settling down for a spectator's role.
So it was decided: the summer of high school graduation, I went for Forest Sciences.
During my university years I kept going to the mountains, but the focus now shifted to the forest, which until then I considered a background or a boring interlude to overcome as quickly as possible to reach a little higher.
Now I learned instead to recognize the trees, to call them by name, to know that each one has his tastes as for light, water and soil to grow;
mostly, I learned that in nature all things are connected, even over distances and times that seem impossible to fill.
And much like our atoms were forged in the stars' core, billions of years ago, forests keep track of events far away in time and space.
Take the annual growth rings of the trees, for example: in the rings we can read not only the age of the plants, but also the rain, the heat, the cold in the past recorded in the width of each ring.
So if you read today these natural journals, we are able to track back the climate of decades or hundreds of years ago, and understand how it is changing.
For example, almost all the trees on Earth remember the largest volcanic eruption recorded in historical times, the one of Tan Bora volcano in Indonesia, 1815.
In less than a year the ash span across the Earth, dispersed throughout the atmosphere causing a season so cold and wet that 1816 ended up being remembered as the year without summer.
That year the trees grew much less, and even today we find the trace of this eruption in the growth ring of 1816, suddenly narrower than all the others.
Many other things happened, in that year without summer: Napoleon's armies got bogged down in Waterloo's unexpectedly soaked soil; in the Swiss countryside, a group of writer friends was forced to spend most of the summer indoors: among them, a 19 year old Mary Shelley wrote Frankenstein; in the skies of northern Europe, sunsets turned red because of the volcanic ash that was still scattered in the air, as we still see today in Munch's "Howl".
Every time you look at this picture, from today onwards, you can feel how connected we are to everything that happens on the planet.
Even if we do not even notice it, forests today bind us to the earth and make life possible.
More than a billion people in the world depend on forests to drink clean water.
Also, trees retain the soil with their roots, intercept the rain with the leaves, slowly infiltrate the soil with their humus, thus decreasing the speed of the flow and protecting those who live downstream from landslides and floods.
The carbon absorbed by trees, thanks to photosynthesis, is sequestered from the atmosphere and this helps us fight climate change, which in absence of forests would be 30% more intense.
The carbon absorption can then be extended if we revive the trees in their wood: the beams of the roofs of some mountain huts, show us that wood can store carbon for tens or hundreds of years and today wood buildings are getting a new impetus.
Architects around the world challenge each other to build taller and taller wooden design buildings thanks to the new opportunities offered by the MASS Timber from XLAM.
After all, the wood we use every day in the objects of our house connects us to the whole earth, to the air they breathed, to the water and soil that fed them, to the hands and the imagination of those who worked them for us.
Unfortunately, in Italy when we use wood we also often connect to environmental or social injustices.
Despite our forests' growth, almost 80% of the wood we use comes from abroad, and a fifth of this comes from illegally, or unsustainably cut forests like in South America or Eastern Europe.
Forests are a delicate ecosystem. If they are not managed in the right way they risk being lost, and with them the quality of life of those from the forests depends - water, food, work.
A part of climate migrants, who today are forced to move all across the world, are driven by deforestation and the effects it has on food security, access to water and health.
Instead, there are strategies to increase the use of wood, in a sustainable way, from our forests, avoiding long-distance impacts while guaranteeing the conservation of their biodiversity and soil protection function.
For example, it is about choosing the plants to be cut in order to leave space and light to the new plants, which in the meantime will have developed from the seeds that trees around them have dispersed in the environment and will ensure which, while growing, that the forest as a whole will continue to prosper.
Choosing which plants to cut and which to leave, which forests can give us wood in a sustainable way and which must instead be managed and preserved for their biodiversity is the task of forest planning,
A task that must set up today what we want to happen 50 or 100 years from now,
because the slowness with which forests grow forces us to connect with a perception of time that's radically different from ours.
Looking to the future, we cannot forget climate change:
today the increase in temperature and drought causes waves of mortality in forests around the world,
and can promote the spread of very large fires, as we saw in Italy in the summer of 2017 , and cause the drastic reduction of the area occupied by some forest species as we are already observing in the Alps.
Plus, an ever more energetic atmosphere means a greater probability that some extreme events will occur, such as the windstorms that occurred in Trentino, or in Friuli, in Veneto,
a phenomenon that has no historical precedent in Italy.
These changes are much faster than the ability of plants to evolve and adapt. But perhaps we can help the trees to undergo them, for example by making them grow so that they resist a little more in the wind, or making sure that there is a young generation of seedlings ready to bloom in the event of a natural disaster.
Take, for example, a small beech seed.
Beech is a somewhat special species. Unlike most plants, that reproduce each year, as we would expect from an organism that is interested in spreading, colonize the environment around it, beech instead works intermittently.
In some years it produces a great deal of seed, in others little or none.
The extraordinary thing is that this variability is punctually synchronized between tree and tree, between forest and forest, even over long distances like between beech forests of the Alps and those of the Black Forest in Germany.
With our research team, we have discovered that this variability is actually caused by a very precise climatic sequence:
a fresh and wet summer is needed first to increase the availability of nutrients in the soil, and immediately after a hot and dry one to push plants and buds produce flowers instead of leaves.
If the nutrients have accumulated, the flowering will take place in mass, and with it the production of fruits and seeds.
This sequence is also linked to the cycles of solar activity.
When the sun is more active, the climate on Earth is a little more variable, discontinuous, unpredictable.
Thus, those very different seasons are much more likely to follow each other, as in the case of a cold and wet season and a hot and dry one immediately after.
This phenomenon resonates on all ecosystems: first of all on the number of sprouts and seedlings that can represent the future of the forest, especially if these years of seeds overproduction occur immediately after a fire. From here the forest can recover much more easily,
and then propagate with ever wider effects on the number of mammals that feed on those seeds, on the parasites they carry - such as ticks, for example - or their somewhat unpleasant host, the bacterium of Lyme disease.
Also, the concentration of pollens that are produced by those flowers and that can cause a peak in allergies.
Here, the beech seed cycles tell us the stories of the connections that cross nature, from the seed to the sun, all the way up to us,
almost reflecting the same bond operating both between our atoms and in the core of the stars.
When I took my first steps in the Piedmontese Alps I never imagined I would become a scientist.
Truth be told, I wanted to be a farmer, as suggested by the original meaning of my name, Giorgio, but I looked at the environment that surrounded me, I let myself be surprised by the trees, the butterflies, the starry skies, and I understood, all the things were somehow connected together.
A network of relationships where I felt at home, small perhaps, but not insignificant.
Studying forests continues to tell me the stories of the relationships that run through ecosystems, even at a great distance.
A fascinating phenomenon I can channel to you, telling here today about trees and wood, seeds and fires, and opening a connection through which each of you can feel an integral part of the ecosystem, recognizing at the same time its own cause and its effect.
Whether we get it or not, we belong to these relationships. And trees and wood, like large connectors, link our lives to that of many other people on Earth, near and far, who have come or will come.
Small but not insignificant.
One with the planet,
networks of relationships with other men,
and that for me is what it means to live.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Who wouldn’t like to have a robot at home, who speaks and maybe does the chores?
Would you like that?
I think many of you would.
Perhaps a robot like this?
Raise your hand, please, if you like it.
Who likes it?
I don’t see even one hand.
Some of you might be thinking, that it is a little too realistic.
What about a little less realistic one, like this?
Raise your hand, please.
Not even one!
This one for example, has human features, but they are simplified.
Let’s see another one –
This one?
Yes, raise your hand: I see a lot of hands raised.
OK.
But why are we scared of what, ultimately, are just inanimated objects?
There is actually a theory for it - a hypothesis, actually, the "Uncanny Valley" hypothesis. [Translates it into Italian]
If we place on the horizontal axis the resemblance to a human being and on the vertical axis the familiarity we feel to it, the hypothesis says we get a curve shaped like this.
A robot, far from the mere metal box it is, the more human features it has, the more familiarity and empathy we feel towards it, up to a certain point in which we find the valley in the curve.
At the bottom of this valley, we might find masterpieces of research like those I showed you: however, precisely because they almost look like a human being, that ʺalmost˝ translates into "something doesn't add up".
And what does not add up at the bottom of the valley?
It could be distorted dimensions, as in the case of CB2, a one or two-year-old child robot, or mechanical parts mixed with seemigly organic ones, or details that do not match with our expectations, or rather our brain’s expectations.
All these things, and the inherent ambiguity of likeness, can make a robot disturbing.
But there is more to it.
There is an even deeper reason that is rooted in our past.
All of you certainly know science-fiction films.
Terminator is a prominent example.
In many of these a common theme is a rebellion of robots, who take over and try to wipe out humanity.
Nothing really new.
However, this is not a prerogative of robots: one can find the same theme in Pinocchio, a well-known story from the past with a man-made human that suddenly goes out of control.
Before that, early in the 19th century, the story of Frankenstein, where another man-made creature goes out of control, hence the name of ʺFrankenstein complex".
Well, in Japan for example, this complex does not exist.
In Japan robots are often seen as heroes that save humanity, and they have feelings, a soul.
What you see here is a 20-metre-high statue that stands in a square in Tokyo.
So where did we get this fear from?
Well, our culture stems from Ancient Greece.
And in Greek mythology, there were many stories about automation, like the one of Pygmalion, where a man carved a statue of a woman and fell in love with her.
There is nothing negative in this story though, or in all these stories of automation.
So this fear, appearently, does not come from Greek culture.
To make a benchmark, let’s check China too: thousands of years ago they invented the Chinese shadow plays, where these puppets are brought to life through the movement of canes attached to joints.
Well, we do not know for sure, but probably the first story of this kind, of this fear, is the story of the Golem, a medieval story that resurfaced in the 1600s, which is a traditional Jewish story.
In this story, the Golem is a creature made of clay, created by a rabbi, a creature that could not speak or think,
that went out of control and had to be destroyed.
And this is an example of "hubris", of the arrogance, human arrogance in this case, in insisting to create something living and artificial.
So looking at the Eastern and Western world, despite their roots are far away, the stories of artificial humans can be found both sides.
Currently, however, we have the Frankenstein complex on one hand; and a positive image on the other.
What happened in between that brought about this divergence?
One possible explanation is the religious influence, because in monotheistic religions there is a clear distinction between the natural and the artificial, what is created by God as opposed to what was created by men.
In Oriental religions, inspired and influenced by Confucianism, every object has a soul.
Let’s come back to the present.
I have worked and studied in Tokyo for many years.
This is the robot I worked on: a super advanced humanoid,
which sometimes, however, reminded me of something.
(Laughter) It was designed by engineers,
and perhaps that's precisely the point,
because rather than designing these robots based on their mere functionality, perhaps we should take inspiration from design, from the field of design,
like Apple, for example, a company that successfully took advantage of a combination of technology and design.
They have made use of a design concept known as "skeuomorphism".
The word "skeuomorphism" comes from Greek, from "skeuos" meaning container and "morph", meaning shape.
It means creating a new object with the appearance, the look of an already-existing object the user is already familiar with.
It has been applied to an app, like Bookshelf; on the left you can see how it was designed in early iOS versions to look like a wooden shelf.
Subsequently, they must have considered, users got used to it, so they switched to a flat interface.
But what does designing robots based of this concept entail?
Because robots are possibly the most complex technological objects in existence because they are a reflection of man.
And if they are a mirror image of man, then clearly, as we have previously seen, when talking about culture, we must take into account our culture and our past when we design them.
Cars, if you think about it, are customized for the world‘s different markets, like many other objects on sale.
So the case is even stronger for customizing robots.
And we cannot factor out the religion that has shaped that culture.
I took this photo on Easter Island and it shows a Mohai with a cross, so the pagan with the sacred.
Let’s take as example the Dharma, here on the right, is a Buddhist talisman for protection and good luck.
It is very common in both Chinese and Japanese cultures,
and it helps believers to make their wishes come true, which in the photo are all written on the notes on the left.
Generally, the Dharma is a small object that many Japanese keep in their homes.
Taking this as a model, an inspiration through skeuomorphism, we made a small social robot, to keep elderly people company, which assumes the identity of the Dharma.
I took this to many retirement homes in Japan or at least a prototype of it.
As you all know, the elderly are not exactly fond of new technology, they are quite intimidated by it.
The Japanese were no exception.
However, this talking Dharma did not intimidate them, because it was something from their past they were already familiar with.
So, probably successfully, we used religion in the design of technology.
But aren’t religion and technology opposing fields, one against the other?
As a matter of fact, no.
Religion is not, by definition, against technology, also because religion has evolved, going from oral to written tradition, to print and the mass media,
and soon it will also take advantage from new technologies such as artificial intelligence to ease its spread.
And robotics also has very old origins that crossed several times the path of religion.
In the Hellenistic period, Hero of Alexandria designed various "automata", as he called the devices, that could perform movements.
And very often, these were placed inside temples, such as the altar to Dionysus that Hero himself designed.
And in later ages, guess who was the main driver behind these automata?
The Catholic Church, looking for ways to impress the believers.
What you see in the middle is the mechanical monk commissioned as a votive artwork by Phillip II of Spain in 1560.
And on the other side of the world, in India for example, puppets have been used for millennia during Hindu festivals.
In later eras in Japan, with the invention of the first automata, the so-called "Karakuri dolls" were also placed inside temples.
Generally, we represent a religious theme through beauty,
through art and sacred art. For example, the golden ratio is a well known concept, and can be frequently found in many Renaissance works, that had to stand out.
And as the Quran says, "God is beautiful and loves beauty", because sacred art is not just art with a sacred theme,
but it is intrinsically sacred because through beauty it conveys the concept of the divine.
So perhaps we have found the key – we use skeuomorphism inspired by sacred art to create something familiar.
And in Italy sacred art is everywhere, even in the most unlikely places.
In the past, when I was living in Livorno, I paid no attention to it, but once I got used to different urban landscapes, many details strike me now when I come back. For example, very widespread decorative elements are saints in a niche, as you can see in these photos taken, among other places, also in Livorno.
These saints do not only have a decorative function, they actually are objects of relief for believers as they act as an intermediary to God and because they contain, incorporate symbols.
Okay.
Based on this inspiration, I present to you the first Catholic robot since Phillip II of Spain.
Or perhaps it would be better to call it an "interactive Saint", because it is nothing less than a piece of sacred art with innovative functions.
And I brought it with me for the occasion.
With great care, because it is sacred.
This was designed taking into account our beauty criteria, inspired by the neoclassical architecture,
featuring aspects of the golden ratio.
It is turned on simply by taking an electronic candle and touching its hands.
It has no other interfaces such as screens, remote controls, buttons or other sort of hellish contraptions.
The electronic components, such as engines, microphone and speakers are all contained, disguised, they are hidden inside the body of the saint and inside the niche.
And as for the contents, this contains stories of the lives of saints, sermons and prayers; it can contain a database of all these things that make it a plausible companion for prayer for many elderly people, or simply for those in need.
I took this to a nursing home for elderly people, in Germany.
This picture shows they are praying "Our Father" together.
So to wrap up, robots are complex objects because man is complex.
However, robots are on the horizon.
And we have to decide now how to create them in a way that makes them socially acceptable.
And for them to be acceptable, they have to be familiar, and for them to be familiar, we should get inspiration from our culture, our past.
If we examine culture, we also have to consider religion.
And if we touch religion, we also have to reconsider our beauty criteria.
I wrap up with an invitation – in Italy, we were the centre of Humanism, the centre of the study of man.
And there's more to it.
We are the world’s masters of art,
and furthermore we have cutting edge robotics and new technology research institutions.
So I say, with these three elements, Italians, let's create these acceptable and beautiful robots that reflect our past.
Thank you.
(Applause)
The daily and the extraordinary.
I think that normally, these two terms are in absolute contrast.
The first is considered as an annoying hurdle, a daily grind. When you wake in the morning you have to get dressed, put on your shoes, brush your teeth, go out; everyday things, so boring, so repetitive. On the other hand,
the second term is about no limits, linked with freedom - the extraordinary.
The extraordinary soars freely in the air, we could say. And sometimes, it reaches the sky.
The daily is banal, the extraordinary is sublime, an unique event.
Before I said that even some universities have lectures on what an event is, obviously, thinking of themselves as God, that creates events from lightening... For those who believe in God, is God, For those who don't, it's nature. They are more or less equal.
I believe the opposite, The path to the extraordinary, since we all tend towards it, to beauty... I heard before about "the beauty of chemistry," and I remember from my studying with Geymonat of all the mathematicians he spoke of that, when faced with explanatory formulas at the same explanatory level as mathematical problems, of mathematical analysis, used to say, "Which one do you choose?" "The most beautiful one."
And this is one of the reasons why perhaps we talk more with the Polytechnic Institute than with humanist scientists; not all of them, as some are very talented, but normally, well …
The road to the extraordinary goes through continuos research, research that sinks into the ordinary, into the daily.
The harshness of the real, the ruggedness of the real, the untameable nature of the daily, that is the challenging starting point to make sense of that unpronounceable world that is "complexity."
Complexity and extraordinary bound together to kill the daily, killing it, let me say without demagogy, in politics, but also killing it in daily language. So, in my opinion, returning the importance of the everyday as an element, as research ground is one of the most important things.
Let me give you an example that is neither moralistic nor pietistic.
Call them attacks, call them attacks on human life, with the shipwrecked, thousands of people; they are absolutely extraordinary facts. But in some way, judging them from differing personal viewpoints, we accept them, we suffer and accept them, we take them as fact, an extraordinary fact.
But something happens, I don't know if it happens to you too, but I've seen it happen with other people, not just me, so it's not just that I am hypersensitive, or mad, or particularly pietistic: the hardest thing is when instead of seeing a thousand dead bodies you see a shoe, or a wet toy on the beach, to get even more rhetorical.
Why? Or on the motorway,
has that ever happened to you?
Unfortunately I have seen some accidents, and you say, "It's extraordinary," in many aspects it is sensational, but then you get a lump in your throat when you see a shoe on the road, a shoe lost by the victim. I drive a motorbike so I am always concerned about this.
There is the exceptional fact, but that shoe also shows you the everyday aspect; and that is what gets to you, it triggers an emotional mechanism that is completely removed from the extraordinariness, instead, that is attributed to the big tragic event also with condemnation of course.
And there is another fact: I don't know if you have noticed how when you hear the news of massacres, great disasters, thousands of deaths, and then, you find that they were only 500,
this has thus been declassified from extraordinary to merely news, and so, it is communicated as news.
And the news is to use-- -- news which is fine by me is needed to keep us up to date with what's going on, but often, it is the news itself that kills and conceals reality
I remember as a child I watched the wars in Vietnam: - It was at the time it was going on, I was 13-14 years old - there was so much news around that I had no idea of what was going on, and I defy anyone to demonstrate the contrary of what is happening now.
In fact, news which is a downgrading of the extraordinary, now extraordinary because the news is now shouted out, reveals how information expresses the insignificance of the word "currently."
Spregelburd gave us a beautiful expression that I will quote later, briefly regarding a show. He is an Argentine playwright who, in presenting the text that we then put to the stage, "Panico," write a note saying how topicality is history's younger sillier daughter.
I find this a wonderful definition.
What does the everyday have to do with the extraordinary?
Well, because we are moved by both sides, except here there is no demagogy; I have often wondered this myself, I have heard some phrases expressed on the surface, above ground, in civilization.
Then I wondered, with neither political nor moralistic judgement, would these words have the same meaning on the underground, when the everyday is made up of very concrete facts, of people banging into you, of smells, of faces?
Could they be pronounced?
And I decided that they couldn't.
So, perhaps what happens in concealment, which is the object of the research, tells you more than what you can actually see. And when the extraordinary also replaces what appears reality disappears, every research element disappears, what unites us, and I will come back this.
Science and theater share this very basis of continual research, an infinite research into reality, but the reality is the everyday.
I believe also that fascinating things that I heard before about chemistry play – and play is a very heavy word – with the everyday, intended as a reality that is difficult to reduce to a single explicative model, but with the everyday. Theater and science share a research into reality, that which daily finds itself opposing
abstract simplifications, consolations, certainties that crumble. References to walls are too obvious, but it goes beyond that; it is a mental form of approach, which is why we haven't simply created theater shows on science. We have done very little, we have welcomed some beautiful shows on the biographies of great scientists, or on moral facts that implied a moral judgement - Oppenheimer - but what we would have done after, and we did it also with the Polytechnic was to investigate the common language between theater and science, and surprise ourselves first and then others, that there was a common approach to reality which kept our feet firmly placed – and I will tell you why I use the word feet – in daily life.
If we forget about the everyday, there is no research.
There is a wonderful thing that the person who spoke before me said about chemistry, there is a small variant when I was at MIT, after we did Infinities with the Polytechnic Institute. I asked, "So, how can you gather so many scientists who work in different fields, in interdisciplinary ways?" He said, "We have only a table. And when we eat, we return to our everyday lives and talk about things we don't know not what we know."
In conferences, we sell things we know.
Theater, in many ways, is just a table where we are cheeky enough not to understand but to watch, and hear, and say.
And to share, of course, we all know that, but we also share at the cinema.
I go back to the everyday; the everyday rebels against short cuts, it surprises us, offends certainty, because certainty is always arrogant, touches on topicality – that I was talking about earlier –
the detail, as I wrote in those notes that you asked me for,
makes the world, everyday relationships, - because there is no 'a single everyday' but many 'everydays': the shoe that you see on the motorway belonged to someone, it wasn't an abstract shoe, it wasn't part of the accident; someone was wearing it, someone who had a life. The everydays are the terrain of reality and obstinate research that go below the surface, what unite us all: us, theater actors and you, scientific researchers.
Science and theater share a reading of reality with the continual research of fragments.
That is one thing - I don't have enough time to tell you all about these things.
I explained it earlier. I am not for commemoration, but how can we not remember Ronconi's work, what connected me to him for 35 years?
He has always talked of fragments that everyone takes home with him to then reconstruct as part of his own everyday life. And it's not that he leaves knowing more, he leaves with even more questions to ask.
If we ask more questions than we have answers, perhaps we would all be scientists; scientists of life, as well, and of the sciences that regulate social life; we don't do it anymore.
Programming the extraordinary - the event being studied is as far from life as we can understand. It is extraordinary stupidity towards reality that escapes, it is the death of human curiosity.
Curiosity - another element that unites us. Morin has come to see us many times; recently, at 95 years of age, he said, "We should look at the world with the eyes of a child and the responsibility of an adult."
I believe that this unites scientists,
I saw before that those talking had fun, watched through the eyes of a child and with the responsibility of an adult.
Theater does the same thing,
that is why the Polytechnic Institute and Il Piccolo have united.
In the everyday, we can see the signs, stimuli to gather improbable relationships: when they asked us to collaborate in celebrating the 150th anniversary, I – having always desired to be a titler, I would love that job – suggested, "150 years of impossible, improbable research;" because the improbable could become, certain is dead.
So this unites, but it should also be in the everyday, it should call the everyday into account.
And here I get to what we have done together, Infinities:
five paradoxes on the infinite, that obviously dealt with an absolutely extraordinary question, almost unutterable, almost unpronounceable for those who aren't mathematicians, who treat infinity as a number, but for everyone else, infinity is unpronounceable.
We expected to have 400-500 spectators; they were five paradoxes on the infinite, we started with Hilbert, Kant, time travel.
40,000 people came.
But do you know what was the extraordinary thing?
Not so much the extraordinary content of the infinite, but the meeting between the infinite and people's everyday lives.
This transformed that thing into an extraordinary thing, because people were leaving-- I remember that De Maio's son – De Maio was Rector at the time – came up to me, gave me a hug, and told me, "I owe you so much." "Adriano, we did this show together." "No, because my 14-year old son said thank you to me for the first time." And the fathers amongst you know what that means.
And the extraordinary became that, not the extraordinary content of the infinite. This is why I am a great supporter
of the everyday. Recently, "Elvira" has been playing on the stage of the Teatro Grassi; "Elvira" is a play directed by Toni Servillo. "Elvira" are the seven lessons that Jouvet took with Claudia, his student, the school student of the Paris Conservatory, to interpret such a mysterious character as Elvira, in Molière's "Don Juan."
There is a sensational passage: to arrive at the sublime, she has to follow many roads, extraordinary roads, of talent, of cleverness, ostentation, skill; and Jouvet/Servillo says, "No, it's not that. it is the fatigue you have to put into it, it is feeling."
Do you know what happens at a certain point?
Claudia removes her shows and runs barefoot on the stage, she enters the stage.
When her bare feet touch the stage floor, she starts on the road of the sublime, of the Don Juan's mystery.
Behind me there is an image with which I conclude this inconsiderate consideration on the everyday and on the extraordinary: this is "Panico," the show I spoke of earlier, Spregelburd's text whose theme was an absolutely extraordinary thing: the interpretation of reality as a diagonal line in time and space.
But it is nothing like a sci-fi film, it is more contemporary, in the sense that the concept of time and space doesn't change only for the researchers but also in daily life; change, globalization, all these things that we have heard talk of.
It was a great success, it was called "Panic", and it obviously it wasn't a great portent,
it was an incredible success and everyone wrote, even the critics, and even us talking with Ronconi, we said, "Of course" - you see that the stage is set diagonally, don't you? -
"Because it is the scenic representation of the diagonality of life, of the diagonality of the contemporary, of transversality, this term is also used in science as well.
We were absolutely, honestly convinced.
A couple of weeks went by, and speaking with Ronconi - we used to talk a lot, and we worked; sorry, we talked little but worked lots - we said to each other, "Well, you see? You see that they understood this." He made a confession, and these are my final words,
"Yes it is true, it represented that, the diagonality of knowledge, of reality, but do you know why I made the stage diagonal?
Because I was fed up that Teatro Strehler spectators could not see the actors' feet." Thank you.
(Applause)
(Applause) One feature that identifies us as human beings is that we ask ourselves questions.
Other animals have instincts, they have a perception of their environment but we are the only ones who do not take what is around us for granted
We ask ourselves questions and possibly try to change the world around us
I consider myself a lucky person: I had a family who supported me, I had the opportunity to study, to travel, to do a job that I like and that satisfies me
I was often in the right place at the right time
Well, those who receive a lot, like me, then want to give.
Unfortunately, it is impossible,
because when you try to give, you actually get much more.
This is the favela of Paraisópolis, “paradise city”, one of the most populous in Sao Paulo, about 100,000 inhabitants.
In the favelas there is no water, there is no electricity except that stolen from nearby neighborhoods but there are schools
and a few years ago I was in some of those schools, longing to give.
I was working on a Worldfund project.
Worldfund is an international organization that aims to reduce poverty in Latin America through quality of education thus reaching students through teachers and infrastructure.
my task was to tell these children that with education and with school, they could be successful, they could have a different future.
Daily life for children in Paraisópolis is on the street
Studying means sacrifice, means persuading parents and grandparents, means working at night to go to school during the day.
On the last day a group of students approached me after my speech and asked for a photo.
A girl asked me: “Do you really believe that I can change my future with school?”
“Yes, yes! I really believe it!” And she told me “But you, what are you doing to contribute to the change you would like to see in the world?” I didn't know how to answer.
Once again, I wanted to give, but I found myself receiving.
I had received a huge mirror in front of me a mirror that asked me “What is the change you want to see in the world?”
and above all, “How do you plan to contribute?” I brought this question as a gift with me and then carried it around.
Two years later I was in Uganda,
working with Opportunity International
Opportunity deals with impact investing.
What is impact investing?
Literally, “impact investing” means “investing with an impact”.
Obviously!
All investments have an impact, be it positive or negative
All the actions we take have an impact, be it intentionally or otherwise
But impact investing means investing with a dual purpose: with the purpose of financial performance and, at the same time, of social impact.
A middle ground between pure financial investment and philanthropy.
If I make a pure financial investment, maybe I lend money to a company or someone who will then give my money back with interest
My financial performance is the interest
However, if I make an investment with impact it means I'm going to lend my money to a company or someone who will pay me back with interest, but I'm also looking at the social impact of that investment.
Maybe that company invested in building a school.
A school in a place which has never had one,
so the social impact is represented by those children who can go to school increased literacy in the area increased employment for those who built the school for teachers, janitors... That is to say: impact investing is killing two birds with one stone
And impact investing gave me answers because the change I want to see in the world is a fairer world, a more equal world a world where we can all live with dignity
And how could I contribute?
With impact investing, as a financial intermediary. The financial intermediary is nothing more than a bridge, a bridge between those who have resources, savers and those in need of resources, those who have projects perhaps with an impact
And what does Opportunity International or other such impact investing organizations do?
They do exactly that. They create a virtuous circle that helps the most unfortunate people to get out of the cycle of poverty with their own strength.
Finally something worthwhile.
Impact Investing is a relatively new concept.
The name impact investing was born around the year 2000 and, as all new concepts, it is difficult to find statistics upon which everyone agrees it is difficult to find a single definition it is difficult to find a unique method of evaluation.
But I think it's important to understand the extent of this phenomenon.
If we look at the larger circle it represents all global financial investments, all over the world of all things, whether they are with or without impact.
We're talking about hundreds of trillions, 300 trillion.
One third of that circle, more or less 100 trillion, are the investments made by pension funds investment funds and insurance essentially our savings.
100 trillion.
And how are these 100 trillion invested?
10 percent of these trillions are invested in government bonds with negative returns
And so, is this related to the impact investing?
Yes, it is. Can you see the red dot?
It's difficult to see.
just 0.1 percent is in impact investing.
It's a paradox.
We can get financial returns together with social impact and yet we don't do it.
We are aware that our resources are the means that can change the world and we don't exploit it.
We can kill two birds with one stone and we are happy with one!
Of course, these statistics may not be complete maybe there are some investments that have a social impact but are not reflected in these numbers
Perhaps there are some of us who haven't explicitly decided not to invest with impact, but just didn't know it was possible
Actually, the most important thing is that 99.9 percent of our resources can be directed towards impact
We have 99,9 trillion that we can invest in projects with social impact.
In 2015 the United Nations published a document with 17 goals to eradicate poverty protect our planet, give welfare to everyone.
But there are even goals like eliminating hunger in the world solving the water problem, the quality of education peace and justice.
Well, the United Nations estimates that we need 1.5 trillion a year to achieve those goals by 2030.
We have 99.9 available.
It's good news, isn't it?
So, what can we do, seriously?
Each financial market operator can do a different thing.
About governments: governments can create systems where investments with social impact can work For instance reducing the taxation system, and that's already happening.
Financial intermediaries can organize themselves to create investment solutions with financial performance and social impact.
It is happening.
Impact investing is doubling its’ figures each year and it is doubling as demand rises above all from institutional investors so there are solutions for institutional investors.
There are fewer solutions for private investors.
And what we have to do as private investors is to create the demand.
We have to ask ourselves and ask our financial intermediaries the consultant, the bank, the financial promoter “What is the impact of my pension fund?"
"What is the social impact of my investment fund?” These demands alone are few but together we are 99.9 trillion.
And if the financial intermediary tells us “Well, wait a minute, if you want the social impact you have to give up a bit of financial performance” It's not necessarily true.
There are statistics that say that most investments with impact have performance targets but also effective returns, in line with the market, and in addition investments with impact have a risk profile completely different from that of other activities
which means that in your portfolio they create more diversification and therefore less risk.
And then perhaps the intermediary says “Yes, but at least let's separate financial returns from philanthropy”
No, not necessarily it's true
philanthropy is very important. We don't want to replace philanthropy with impact investing.
However, when entrepreneurial spirit is lacking philanthropy gets limited results.
If I have 40 billion euros and I distribute them to 4 billion needy people each of them will have 10 euros to eat for a week and then everything returns to square one.
If I invest those 40 billion euros in infrastructure and education I create a momentum of growth and I change the world
That lady you see there, that energetic lady, her name is Betty Mulooki.
Betty lives in Uganda, she has a disabled husband and three children.
Betty is an impact investing client. Betty got a $10 loan with which she bought equipment and sugar cane seeds.
Betty's crop has gone well,
so she is paying interest paying her debts and paying school for her kids who will have education and therefore a different future from Betty's.
Betty is breaking the cycle of poverty and Betty doesn't need our charity, Betty needs an opportunity!
Now I know how to answer to the question of the girl from Sao Paulo “How are you contributing to the change you would like to see in the world?” “I'm helping Betty!”
(Applause)
Good evening.
Speaking of dentistry, I tried to-- Thinking of getting you involved, I thought I'd tell a story.
In this story, I hope to convey a bit of the passion and the love I have for this profession.
We'll talk about what lies ahead beyond natural dentition, that is of a third and fourth set of teeth.
The first question I want to ask you is if you can think of a difference that exists between a shark and a human being.
In fact, there are numerous differences.
The shark, though, is an animal, if you will, unique of its kind. Because unlike most predators and animals to be found in nature that are certain to die if they lose their teeth, since they can't hunt or eat, the shark has a unique ability: that of regrowing the lost teeth.
Just think: there are sharks that regrow up to 30,000 teeth during a lifetime.
For man, of course, it's not the case.
This is the story of Francesca, a little girl who came to me in tears because she had just lost her two front teeth, the two most important for her smile.
Francesca was desperate. She was convinced she'd be left like it for the rest of her life.
Obviously, we explained that Mother Nature had endowed her with a first set of teeth, her deciduous teeth, which are replaced by permanent teeth during adolescence.
Therefore, Francesca who is now 21, is a most beautiful girl, who has developed a smile that allows her strong self-esteem and confidence in herself.
Francesca, however, will surely encounter an aging process.
An aging process that will, eventually, we hope not, but that will probably result in her loosing teeth if they are not treated and maintained properly.
Tooth loss completely destroys the facial aesthetic.
Mandibular and maxillary resorption processes inevitably lead to aesthetic destruction.
Francesca could, as much as we don't wish it upon her, lose a tooth even at a relatively young age, through a sports injury, for example.
One of the main reasons why young adults lose teeth is just that, trauma.
What have we done since 1980 about tooth loss?
Dentists replace what Mother Nature provided you with with the insertion of implants.
Implants don't replace the tooth itself, they replace the tooth root.
Which is to say that this titanium screw is inserted into the bone, there is osseointegration and it becomes one with the alveolar process into which we insert it.
And then, over the implant, we place a prosthetic crown.
Today we are able to treat patients who have lost all their teeth, to insert these implants into the edentate maxillary or mandibular vestibules and restore function to the patient.
That's what meant, since 1980 to the present, by restoring a patient's bite.
But today that's not all we have.
So what's new?
Because, of course, patients are increasingly demanding.
They therefore require that the implant-supported prosthesis is identical with, and is inserted into the harmony of, the naturalness of permanent teeth.
Only by radiographic imaging or clinical photos can we see which are artificial teeth and which natural ones.
This is not always the case.
This is another girl, a 26-year-old girl, who unfortunately was assuredly treated with regard to function but not the aesthetic.
And when presented with this situation, to completely restore her smile and improve the look of her face, we had to work for three years, not without great sacrifices on the part of the patient.
And this we arrived at not only thanks to technique, but above all thanks to technology.
Today we are able to use computers and software, it's called computer-assisted surgery, that allows the placing of implants before beginning our intervention, on a computer.
From the computer we obtain a stereolithographic guide that allows us to insert implants faster and more safely without traumatizing the patient, without making incisions, without the need for stitches.
And the patient can leave with the implant-supported prosthesis straightaway on the same day as the surgery.
We are not limited to this.
In Boston, at Harvard University, we have studied and are studying activation processes and those to accelerate the healing phase.
What we're doing is taking the titanium implants and coating them with growth promoters that are able to act on surrounding cells that form bone, so that you have osseointegration, retention of bone around the implant, sooner and much more predictably.
In summary, we talked about functional dentistry, we talked about the importance of aesthetics, we talked about computers, and then about guided surgery.
We talked about activating titanium surfaces, otherwise bio-inert, but making them bioactive.
But what are we focusing on today?
Where is research in the field of dentistry?
What's the next word to fit into place?
To be sure it's dental engineering.
With dental engineering I mean a protocol we're starting at the University of Padua, now.
Quite definitely a very ambitious protocol, that of tooth regeneration.
Today, we use 3D printers to obtain a scaffold, a scaffold of hydroxyapatite.
We fill it with growth factors, stromal-derived factors, rather than morphogenetic proteins.
They are all factors that provide a therapy called cell homing. That is, we do not use adult stem cells, but we show cells the way home.
Hence cell homing.
Basically, in nine weeks, in animals, we get root regeneration.
And this will be our fourth set of teeth.
So, to return to Francesca: If she loses a tooth tomorrow, she will be able, as perhaps the shark has been for hundreds of years, to regrow a tooth.
Thank you for your attention.
(Applause)
Imagine sitting on a chair, in silence.
On the other side of the table, there's an important investor: he's watching you, observing you. He's there to evaluate what you're worth and what you've done.
After some small talk, he looks you straight in the eye and asks, Why are you doing this?
What pushed you to set up this business?
I hadn't asked this question to myself, and honestly I had no idea how to answer.
And I couldn't help it, words started coming out of my mouth. "Christmas dinner".
You can imagine his face. He looked at me like I was an alien.
But I continued, "Christmas dinner.
A nice dinner at Christmas, with all the company's members and their families.
A moment of joy and peace -
it's this idea that pushes me to do what I do every day.
Is it so weird?"
It took him a moment to collect himself and then he said, "You surprised me, because most people answer To get rich, and to make you rich, too".
Today we're all fascinated by the US business model, the one where three nerds who start programming in a garage end up making in one year hundreds of millions of dollars.
This way of doing business, which is often just about making profit, uses any means to achieve it.
But this cannot and should not be the right source of inspiration.

Do you know what scares me about the future?
I'm scared precisely by this vision of just making money, no matter what.
And it dramatically scares me that young people would have that as their only goal in life.
People everywhere talk about startups, great exits and head-spinning profits, but too little room is given to failures,
which are so frequent - mind you, startups' failure rate, consistently around the world, is about 96 percent.
So, what does this mean?
It means, nine out of ten don't make it to their third year of business.
Yes, startups are great; we get the chance to create something out of nothing, but at the same time it's really hard and incredibly difficult.
These statistics don't lie.
Frontiers of innovation are a hostile, unlivable place.
Nonetheless, there's always someone who, blinded by profits, sets up financial transactions driven by nothing but his personal enrichment.
And everything gets upholstered this way: shape becomes substance, and very often, the product that's sold and traded is the startup itself.
US jargon, such as "elevator pitch", or the different stages of investments, pre-seed round and seed round. are all the rage this days.
These are extremely important methods and approaches that must be studied, learned and well understood.
But mostly, one should see them as they really are: a set of tools.
Some people however, also in Italy, are so captivated by them that they shift from means to goals.
But we are Italian, from Livorno and from Italy.
Our culture and our entrepreneurs have always had values I deem non-negotiable.
Our guiding star should be the creation of values, not profits.
We are lucky for that; we live in a country where traces of value are everywhere.
Cultural traces are everywhere.
Our cities are filled with them, our history is filled with them.
I also dare say, we are not new at innovation and creativity.
After all, we live on the same land where Leonardo da Vinci was born, Michelangelo Buonarroti, Galileo Galilei, Dante Alighieri, Caravaggio, Fermi and countless others, up to our very days.
And let's admit it: we're very good at making beautiful things, and this has always been true.
We've been doing it for ages.
History tells us, and the rest of the world reminds us, we should never forget that we are admired around the world exactly for these traits: our value lies in quality, not in speed;
in the practicality, not the ephemeral.
Today we are the world's seventh manufacturing country, the second in Europe after Germany,
while hosting the greatest number of UNESCO world heritage sites.
That's who we are.
In fact, we were startuppers way before the word was included in our dictionary.
When it comes to doing business we are second to none.
Enzo Ferrari, Giovanni Battista Pirelli, Pietro Ferrero and Ferruccio Lamborghini:
these are only a few of the names of mechanics, farmers and pastry chefs who have created businesses capable of generating value, providing jobs, "bootstrapping" Italy and launching it internationally while staying rooted to their motherland.
They did this when there was almost nothing,
and the only options were: the factory, the fields or the sea.
There was no Internet, at all, and sharing the way we do today
was simply unimaginable. Yet they did it, they created everything without knowing anything.
And this is because very often, to light the entrepreneurial spirit, you don't need something extraordinary.
Sometimes a spark is enough.
I remember my spark as if it was yesterday.
It was 13 January 2009, at the inauguration of the marine robotics laboratory at Scoglio della Regina, here in Livorno. during Professor Dario's speech. He said something that resonated in me ever since.
Pointing graduates to be, and PhD students, he said, "Work can be created, must be created
and who else but you in this country has the duty to create jobs?"
Have you ever thought about it this way?
Have you ever been aware that you can create jobs, and not just search for a job?
I hadn't.
Until that moment I had never thought about it - probably because, like many of you, I was born and raised in a city that shaped me on very clear assumptions,
and doing business is certainly not on the agenda of most young people from Livorno.
This is a critical point, because this type of barrier must be torn down.
However, that very Tuesday I decided to try my hand at a challenge that seemed impossible:
simply because, you know, doing these things here is impossible.
And it's probably true that it's impossible. It's impossible unless you study in depth a series of issues that used to be so distant from you.
For example, economy, finance, business planning and business management were empty words to me.
But I let them sink in, with the important contribution of someone who became my first partner, who did have a background in economics. It was in that precise moment when we teamed up our complementary skills, that we unwittingly laid down the foundations for what would soon become our team.
When the third co-founder joined in and gave our idea its financial base, we decided to do business in Italy, in Livorno.
Ask anyone you know, I'm sure they'll tell you that doing business today in Italy, in Livorno, is a crazy venture:
And this is even more true if you leave a steady job for it right when your wife is expecting your first child and you've signed a mortgage to renovate your house.
Imagine what went on the minds of the people I care about the most when I decided to throw away all the safety nets and jump into the unknown, starting a technology company in my beloved city, which had been in a profound crisis for the past decades.
It wasn't easy. It didn't take little effort and willpower.
The road has always been uphill, and over time, guess what, hills' slopes only got steeper.
And I would tell myself: that's why they call it "doing business" rather than "taking a healthy walk".
Starting a technology company, bootstrapping a tech startup in Italy is certainly harder than elsewhere. But I think, ease can't be considered a parameter for choice:
doing business means making a difference in a place, and for those who live there.
It means choosing to contribute to the economic, social and cultural development of a city.
Why, then, doing it where we belong? Is it just because it's easier, or because there are economic incentives?
Business means abnegation.
It's about dealing with something that's bigger than us.
It means choosing not to walk that path alone;
Between getting an egg today, or eating the hen tomorrow you bet everything on the chicken coop you will build in a few years, should everything go fine.
If you stop and compare these goals against a lifetime, soon you'll realize that time runs too fast.
For example, a few days ago I was walking by the sea with my wife, Bianca and my son, Michele,
and while I watched him play with a woodstick and some stones, for the first time I felt like I was late.
Late, because he's almost three years old and in something like 15 yesrs he'll become of age. His generation will then be facing the world.
And so I asked myself: in 15 years will my startup, a well-established company attracts new generations?
Will it be the example we were able to give to stimulate their creativity and give them courage?
I hope so, and I set these goals also for myself.
After all, if you want it,
you can do it, right? Today around the world - well, let's say certainly in Italy everyone is convinced or try to convince themselves that youngster's innovation and initiative can be the answer to reviving this country, this city.
I firmly believe that
this cultural change needs time.
Rest assured, they will tell you you're helpless if you're not "someone's" son.
That Italy is no country for young people.
They will tell you, some things only happen in USA.
They will tell you, above all, that your knowledge doesn't count, your network only does. Bullshit.
The only things that actually matter are the quality of a project and the reasons driving it.
A project must be of the highest level and the reasons behind it must drive you to resist hardships.
For example, my team and I have found the money to get started,
we have imported brainpower in Italy from abroad,
and we employ six people.
And ten days from now, after exactly three years of sacrifices, we are launching our first product - a technology product, an innovative project that's entirely made in Italy.
And I assure you, we're not that special.
Sure, not everyone can do anything. I'm not here to convey, hard work alone can open every door.
Hard work is indispensable, but other fundamental variables - a person's culture, talent, instinct and much more - are also at stake.
This should not tempt us to give up, or feel daunted. It just underlines once more the importance of building a team and ensuring it's well built, and featured with all the components to face the challenges.
The only secret - if we can call it a secret - is to always follow your own abilities and passions.
What lights the fire in our heart, that won't let us feel tired, that will make us go straight toward our goals.
And then what happens, in this sort of trance?
There's no time to think about what you're doing and what you're about to become.
Something changes a little, actually, and the world around you starts being aware.
You also start guessing something,
and something happens. For me this moment of awareness coincided with the last anecdote I want to share,
When did I fully get what I was doing?
When did I fully get what I had built over those years?
Before an important investor?
That would be the perfect setting.
"My startup was chosen by an important investment fund, and by a multinational that believes and invests in a completely Livornese bet.
It's there at that table, among jackets, ties and handshakes that I realized I had become an entrepreneur".
But no, it didn't go like that.
It sounds more like a movie.
Actually, my awareness moment happened at another table,
but this time there was a pediatrician on one side - the one we were choosing for our child - and my wife and I are across that table.
The doctor asked us some questions to fill in a form.
At a certain point, he asks about the father's profession,
and I didn't know how to answer.
So, there I am, looking for a good answer and trying to sum it up.
Instead, my wife astounds me as she answers straight away,
"Entrepreneur".
"Entrepreneur, right? Yes, entrepreneur."
Me? Really?!
I learned how to define myself.. at the pediatrician's.
(Laughter) I decided to become an entrepreneur in my city, and yours too, Livorno. right out of here,
ready to welcome your dreams and relive in your hopes.
Always look beyond the horizon. Delve into your ideas. Get excited about them.
Follow them, and do everything to achieve them, but keep your feet firmly planted on our brackish ground,
which believe me, may be the most boundless horizon in the world.
If thre's a small flame flickering somewhere within you, let it flare
and leave this splendid theater knowing that desiring something is the first step to achieve it.
Always nurture your hopes, and be brave.
(Applause)
(Applause) I am a farmer.
My sisters and I run a farm.
We have always done other things too, and perhaps this gives us an attitude to try and break with a traditional production pattern, try to change something.
Maybe we've learnt it from our parents: they too were farmers and also eclectic people
We try to have the so-called 'lateral approach' to problems.

Do we make it? We will see!
Our great-grandmother Regina - actually her name was Caterina, but everyone called her Regina, farmed the land in Friuli countryside.
She planted wheat, corn, beans between corn rows, and she burned olive tree twigs so Saint Barbara would bless her against lightnings and storm
Today we may smile at these superstitions but they give you an idea of how scary it was, back then, to lose a crop. It meant hunger.
Our great-grandmother Ida farmed the land in the Venetian plains, selling fruit before the hospital.
After the corn flowering she cut the plant over the ear to feed the animals with that part of green, and the ear matured with the leaf's remains
A ton and a half of product per hectare would have made them happy!
Today we yield 12, 14 tons. but David Hula, a farmer from Virginia, takes the cake with 33.3 tonnes per hectare
Not bad!
Corn is a plant with a stunning potential.
I would like to share some thoughts on agriculture with you
Agriculture is a cycle, it's the passing of seasons it's time, it's bad weather, it's planning, it's investment, it's risk.
Agriculture is where you belong but also a perspective, it is tradition but also future.
So what I ask myself today is, can we innovate our traditions?
Can we take them, innovate them improve and deliver them to the next generation?
Agriculture is what gives us food, it has to feed an increasingly crowded planet
Farmers have never shied away from this challenge for example when they fed a Europe that was devastated by the Second World War. And they did it by innovating,
for example by introducing mechanization moving from this to that.
But the time of progress is not the same for everyone and everywhere
This is Dario today, on the Andes, tilling the land.
That is Giampaolo, today, in the Po Valley tilling the land.
We have also introduced fertilization to nourish the plants or chemistry to protect them from diseases, from weeds and move from this to that
Those women were “mondine” [women rice field workers], who spent hours and hours with legs soaking in the water to clean the rice amongst mosquitoes and all the rest.
By far the most important innovation the one responsible for 50 percent of the increase in production over the years is genetic breeding.
That man is one of the greatest Italian geneticists, he really taught agrarian genetics to the world.
His name is Nazareno Strampelli and it is also thanks to his work if we have moved from growing such a tall wheat to a shorter one
And why do we want small-sized plants?
Well, because high plants wasted many resources to produce stems and leaves and little grains
They were all too easy to fall, they flattened
and then it was complicated to harvest and the product got rotten, and was of poor quality
The plants obtained by Strampelli are lower and more productive, so they can be fertilized
and the product is of higher quality.
Time has led us to feed two people per hectare of land farmed in the '50s, to four and a half today.
In the '50s, a farmer could feed 6 people today 50.
Thanks to these innovations we have been able to produce more
and we have released labor force that shifted from agriculture to industry and services, thus developing them.
Children could then go to school instead of working in the fields.
Today agriculture is being asked to produce more without increasing soil use, without increasing environmental pressure, a better-quality product, at affordable costs and satisfying more and more demanding consumers In short, agriculture must be sustainable. Sustainable for the
environment, of course,
But also for society, which must be able to know and share our production methods
And sustainable for the economy: a farmer must be able to live in dignity, out of his work.
If one of these three “legs” is missing the entire production process can't stand, agriculture is not sustainable.
This being said, I would like to change subject and tell you the tricks our brain sometimes fools us with.
Albert Einstein said that it is easier to break an atom than a prejudice. A prejudice is a wall in our minds.
We tend to make decisions based on emotions, rather than on intellectual analysis
Our brain has evolved in conditions where the priority was not the refined reasoning but quick decision-making
This saved our lives, and this has evolved through selective pressure.
Even in herd life, the priority was to become part of a tribe because it gave protection.
Still today, our brain tries to protect us from new ideas. It struggles to preserve our sense of belonging and identity.
New ideas are a challenge that lights up the same area in our brain that elaborates physical threats.
That is why we tend to react strongly, sometimes aggressively, to new ideas.
Confronted with information questioning our beliefs we tend to react, paradoxically, reinforcing them even more.
These are called cognitive biases, they are very strong and we all fall for them.
Fighting ignorance, lack of information, with more information is sometimes equal to adding fuel to the flames.
Let's look at what's going on with vaccines!
I am convinced that this is still the best method, also because if we know how these thinking traps work, it may be easier for us to dismantle them
Marketing knows them very well and takes advantage of them.
It does so in a perfectly legitimate way, but let's remember it especially when it comes to making decisions that affect the future of our planet
Let's see what happens in the field of agro-food.
A brief digression: a hairdresser once told me “It's without chemicals!” to convince me to do a hair dye. Now, not to mention that if it really was chemical-free that hair dye would not work.
It would not even exist, actually, because chemistry is literally everywhere!
Chemistry is life, life is chemistry,
but we have been bombarded for years by a bizarre equivalence “natural = good”, nature is good, artificial is evil.
Maybe it is worth recalling that nature can also kill, for example in the case of ergot which causes ergotism, a fatal intoxication.
Or that, conversely, human discoveries can save lives.
Let's think about diabetics: the insulin they use has been produced for years by microorganisms that are genetically engineered to produce human insulin.
In the past pig insulin was used, but it had quite heavy side effects
nature, strictly with no capital "n" is neither mother nor stepmother.
It just minds its business.
And nature's business is that every species struggles to survive.
That's why 'natural' can also be fatal, where artificial, meaning 'manipulated by men', can save your life.
Nothing of what we grow is natural, it never was. Since men became farmers they began to change plants, select them improve them for their own purposes.
Confusing nature and agriculture is a shortcut that apparently simplifies our decision-making processes but is actually a closed road, a trap.
Nature is a precious resource, we must protect it. Our survival is at stake.
But this is not the task of agriculture.
Agriculture is not natural, it is an artificial process.
Confusing nature and agriculture leads us to the ultimate paradox: rejecting innovation even when it lowers the environmental impact.
The most striking example is Bt corn.
It is a variety of corn that's genetically modified to protect itself from the attacks of an insect, the corn borer.
But in Italy we're not allowed to grow it.
So, in order to protect our corn from the attack of the corn borer and to prevent it from becoming a disgusting, unsellable waste, toxic to people and animals we are forced to shower our corn fields with insecticide.
Let's make another consideration: when someone is sick, we ask for the latest medical finding, or for faster speed in ICT.
We all demand it. But at the same time this confuses us.
The pace of progress is quick, sometimes too quick for a society to keep up without inconveniences. So we all look for a comfort zone, for certainties.
Food is the perfect recipe: when it comes to food, to eating the fruits of the field, something clicks within us. We all want to be reassured that it sticks with tradition, that it's as it once was, like our grandmother did it,
deluding ourselves with a picture of the past that, believe me, is way less rosy than it actually was. We are preventing agriculture from innovating and ultimately sentencing our traditions to death.
Yet, if we studied the early days of some of the dishes that are currently deemed typical of our kitchen, we would be amazed.
Eggplant (melanzana,
in Italian), for example, was brought to Italy in the Middle Ages by the Arabs
According to some sources, its name comes from “mad apple” (mela insana)
Even potatoes and tomatoes, as you know are native of South America. In Europe, they were considered poisonous for a long time.
But a very interesting example is that of pasteurization of milk. In the mid-nineteenth century, in American cities, child mortality had reached 50 percent, largely because they were used to drink fresh milk.
When the obligation to pasteurize milk was introduced, people took to the streets, there were violent riots they did not want to know anything about this news!
They thought it was a fraud devised by industry to succeed in selling a deteriorated product.
Notice that those mental processes are still the same today.
Yet child mortality fell by two-thirds.
Let's try to disassemble a typical dish of Italian tradition: pasta with tomato sauce.
Wheat to make pasta comes from the total fusion of the genomes of two different wild plants that randomly happened in nature thousands of years ago.
GMOs, where a single gene is moved? Here all the genes have moved, without any human intervention!
We may say that wheat is a natural GMO.
And the tomato the sauce is made with comes from South America.
Want some basil? That's ok, but be aware that it is native of India.
Then the conclusion of this delicious pasta with tomato sauce and basil is that perhaps Italians are really good at innovating, taking the fruits of the world and putting them together
Can we innovate our traditions?
Because we have come to the point that if we want to save them we really have to apply a good dose of healthy innovation to them
But what does innovation mean
in agriculture? Why do we need it?
We need to change because over time our production processes become obsolete, expensive, inadequate to the challenges of the planet.
We should innovate because pathogens change over time and we need new methods to protect our crops without increasing environmental pressure.
We should innovate because climate is changing, we should adapt plants to the environment, not vice versa
We need plants that deal with high levels of salt, plants that deal with drought,
or viceversa, plants that deal with floods.
Climate and environment are changing and we cannot be caught unprepared, given that we have all the skills to do so.
We have to innovate because markets and the society itself change over time and we need flexibility to adapt to their needs.
Let's look at Made in Italy.
It is a great success, requested all over the world.
Food industry exports are constantly growing.
We are pleased of it, but have you noticed?
I said food industry, not agri-food,
because we only meet half of our food and feed grains,
and only a third of our vegetable protein requirements.
The rest is imported.
This means that the products needed to produce the feed we give to animals that will supply our Made in Italy - hams, cheeses, salami and so on - are largely imported and often genetically modified.
They are completely safe, be it clear, but let's also be aware of the absurd paradox we got stuck in. We prevent Italy's farmers from growing what is then mass imported to produce the Made in Italy!
It is an economic suicide!
It is scientific nonsense, a legal mess.
It's a fraud!
So, if we want to save the reputation of Made in Italy and Italian agro-food we need efficient, productive, innovative agriculture.
That is why there is no contradiction between traditional product and innovation.
Innovation and progress are the key to sustainable agriculture,
and this goal is achieved more quickly if farmers and researchers work together each with their own skills.
Let's leave researchers free to do their job,
leave farms free to choose the tools they need to stay in the market.
An economically viable farm is a farm that can open its doors to society and proudly show how it works.
Environmental protection is a priority also for us farmers because we work, and often live, in that environment.
Then maybe we should ponder that today's tradition was our grandparent's innovation.
Because for us farmers, it's tradition to innovate!
Thank you.
(Applause)
"It tastes like gunpowder," Apollo 16 astronaut Charlie Duke said to himself in April 1972.
He had just come back from a moonwalk; a little moon dust had remained stuck to his spacesuit and this dust had ended up pervading the cockpit of Apollo 16, and, accidentally, he tasted and smelled it.
I would like to start from this unusual - an extremely rare – testimony to talk about the universe, in particular, the way we see the universe and how this perception is changing thanks to the findings of modern cosmology and astronomy.
Now, we have five senses at our disposal but, if you think about it, we only use our sight to explore the universe.
Predictable, maybe: of course, we cannot touch or taste asteroids, stars, and planets.
Nor do the senses of smell and hearing help us learn more about the universe: they are restricted to short distances and, in any case, to the small atmospheric shell surrounding the Earth.
All we have left, is our sight.
The fact we are able to see the universe is less obvious than it might seem.
On one hand, it is true that our eyes - the eyes of humans beings - are, in a way, made to see the stars.
They are organs which have developed during the course of human evolution in order to gather and analyze the light that comes from the star closest to us: the Sun.
On the other hand, it is also true that we could live, for example, as in Asimov's short story “Nightfall”, on a planet with a lot of suns, where it is always daytime where its inhabitants are unaware of the existence of stars.
We could live on a planet like Venus, always shrouded in clouds, or under the iced surface of a planet, like hypothetical forms of life on Jupiter’s satellite "Europa."
So, we can consider ourselves lucky, in a way, to enjoy the beauty of the starry sky every single night.
And it is thanks to the observation of this starry sky that our ancestors started to discover the universe, observing the stars with the naked eye.
And the alignment of the great monuments of the past testifies that this is something that humanity has always done.
We used the rising and setting of the constellations as a calendar – a huge astronomical calendar that helped us manage the harvest and sowing.
And we slowly began to distinguish the planets from the stars. We understood that the Earth is a planet that revolves around the sun; we learned to describe the laws governing the movement of the planets and, in particular, we understood the force of gravity, which is the force that regulates the universe on larger scales.
From Galileo onwards, we were able to see the deep universe thanks to telescopes, that are somewhat like artificial eyes – new sense organs, that allow us to perceive celestial objects, stars, galaxies, which would otherwise be invisible to the human eye.
And, at the same time, we realized that there were many other colors in the universe, colors that are invisible to the human eye.
We could consider the history of astronomy as a long journey towards the discovery of colors that are invisible to the human eye.
We thus have – if you take the red and blue you see on the stage, this is light – the same type of light; there is a small energy gap of around 30%, still, the light spectrum is much, much wider.
And so, thanks to astronomy, since the 1930s, we have been busy studying these colors that are invisible to the human eye and have started to create devices enabling us to see from infrared rays to microwaves and radio waves.
And little by little we began to study the universe at higher energies: X-rays and gamma rays.
We have discovered an incredible universe, filled with wondrous things: there are stars, like Pulsars, which are dense and compact and rotate around their axes in just a fraction of a second; we have discovered the Quasars, gigantic black holes that spit out plumes of energy and matter into intergalactic space.
So how big is the universe?
What can we find out there?
In his book “If this is a man,” Primo Levi, not only a great writer, but also a scientist, states that human nature is opposed to everything infinite.
For us, the vastness of the cosmos is hard to conceive.
I would like to conduct a little experiment to help you visualize the universe just as astronomers do.
I would like to start from this picture: this is a real photograph taken by a NASA probe – Osiris – a few months ago.
What you see in the middle of this photograph is our planet: it is the Earth.
That tiny disc that seems to be lost in the universe, in the void, is the planet where the history of mankind has unfolded.
In the bottom right corner, you can see the moon.
The diameter of this orbit, of the lunar orbit, is about a billion meters, a meter multiplied by a billion times.
Now, let’s move on to larger scales.
Let's move forward by a billion times.
Let's consider a scale of a billion of a billion meters.
If we take a sphere with such diameter, it will contain more than 10,000 stars of our galaxy.
They are all stars in our galaxy, and therefore revolve, like the Sun does, around a common center that lies at the very heart of the Milky Way.
Around each of these stars, there are, most likely, planets.
We know that because we have already observed many such planets. So far, we have identified almost 4,000 planets
moving around other stars.
And these stars are so far away that their light takes decades to reach us.
It is in this respect that astronomy provides us with a sort of time machine, allowing us to see the universe as it was in the past.
Let's take one last leap: we are now at a scale of a billion billion billion meters, at the edge of the observable universe.
Yet, these are not the boundaries of our universe, nor of all that exists.
Probably, the universe is much larger; maybe infinite, for all we know.
Anyway, this is the observable universe.
The light that comes from this distance took as long as the age of the universe to reach us, therefore we see it as it was. We see these regions of the universe as they were right after the Big Bang, essentially.
In my opinion, this is one of the most incredible images ever produced by science and mankind.
It's a picture of the universe in its infancy before stars appeared and the first planets were formed.
For the first time in human history, we have a scientific, compelling, precise and detailed explanation of the origin, the structure, and the evolution of the universe.
However, scientific progress has also presented us with something totally unexpected.
We have discovered an obscure, mysterious universe
filled with unknown entities we barely know anything about, like dark matter, for example, which seems to support all galaxies as if it were some sort of cosmic scaffolding – all galaxies, including ours. Or dark energy, which appears to be driving the expansion of the universe at an increasingly faster rate.
We have also had to contend with the so-called singularities.
Singularities are points where classical physics fails us, it no longer makes any sense.
Black holes, for instance, are objects so extreme that they distort the space and time around them, thus fusing them into a single entity called space-time, according to Einstein's relativity theory.
They are similar to the Big Bang itself: If you go back in time, you will reach a point where all the existing matter in the universe is condensed in a point of infinite density.
These are the frontiers of cosmology and these singularities are those many of us, and of you, have got to know through the work of the English physicist Stephen Hawking, who wrote a very famous and popular book called "A Brief History of Time: From the Big Bang to Black Holes."
These are the frontiers of modern cosmology. In all research institutes, at mine in Amsterdam and in many others around the world, we are trying to study and further understand the mysteries behind these names given to things we don't know much about.
Still, all these mysteries do have something in common: they all seem to be rooted in the infinitely small.
These mysteries we observe in the universe on such a large scale all seem to be rooted in what goes by the name of quantum physics – the branch of physics that describes the infinitely small, the microcosm.
Let's try, then, and see … and do it the other way around.
We saw what is in the universe on macroscopic scales. Now, let's look at it on a much smaller scale.
Moving from the meter, our reference unit, let's go and see what we find on scales of one billionth of a meter.
Inside each of us, inside every cell in our bodies, on the one billionth of a meter scale we can find the DNA molecule, for example; many molecules, in particular, the DNA molecule with a 2 billionth of a meter thickness.
If we keep moving down, we get to the billionth of a billionth of a meter, which enables us to explore the inside of atomic nuclei, as well as that of the protons and neutrons within them.
The physics that governs, that reigns over this world is quantum physics, which is a very unusual science.
Let me give you an example. Inside protons, protons basically consist of three elementary particles called quarks. If you were to sum the masses of these three quarks, you would expect to get the mass of the proton. Instead, the mass of the proton is much larger than the sum of the masses of the quarks.
The mass of protons is not made up of the masses of the quarks but of the energy that binds them together.
And, as per Einstein's equivalence E=mc^2, energy equals mass multiplied by the speed of light squared, it manifests itself as mass.
Therefore, everything you see, even your own weight, stems from these tiny nodes of energy to be found in every atom of your body.
You see, modern cosmology invites us to seek answers to the challenging problems of the macroscopic scales of the universe in this world, in the world of quantum physics.
To help us to radically change our approach to these phenomena, to build, as it were, a bridge between the infinitely big and the infinitely small, a new branch of astronomy was recently born.
Some of you may have heard about it: It studies gravitational waves.
They are very important because they allow us – they provide us with information that's entirely complementary to that brought to Earth by any wavelength of light.
More specifically, gravitational waves allow us to listen to the vibrations of spacetime itself produced by entities that would otherwise have remained hidden in deep space forever, black holes, for instance.
And it is for this reason that the 2017 Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded for this amazing discovery.
It is as if, after seeing all the colors of the universe, mankind were developing new sense organs to perceive and understand deep space.
It is a new sensational frontier of knowledge that is opening up to us.
We are trying to use these gravitational waves to establish links between the infinitely big and the infinitely small.
We are doing so with black holes. We have already witnessed the merging of two of them into a bigger one.
This is giving us the opportunity to understand a lot about the origin and evolution of black holes and about the so-called quantum effects of quantum physics occurring around their event horizon.
We are looking for the signature of dark matter around these entities, around black holes.
Maybe, by analyzing this signature, we will maybe determine the composition of dark matter and identify its nature.
We will be able to understand if there is any connection between dark energy and vacuum energy, quantum vacuum, which, unlike classical vacuum which is characterized by the absence of matter, constantly seethes with activity. And hopefull, which sounds
even more incredible, we will be able to find the gravitational waves produced by the Big Bang itself and we are trying to verify this theory, an absolutely incredible and dizzying theory, according to which whatever exists in the universe stems from tiny quantum fluctuations present in the primordial universe.
And, as we come to the end of this journey of discovery of the infinitely big and infinitely small, we should perhaps ask ourselves: “What about us? What about human beings?
What role do we play in this impressive spectacular cosmic show?”
One tends to feel small, irrelevant, insignificant when confronted with the vastness of the cosmos.
Just consider how, from Copernicus onwards, we've been, in a sense, cast apart: to begin with, we were – Earth was at the center of the universe; then, there was a shift, and we became a planet that revolves around the Sun, one of the many planets revolving around the Sun. We then discovered we were one of the many planets revolving around one of the many stars, around one of many galaxies.
Yet the conclusion I would like to leave you with this evening is altogether different.
As I see it, physics and modern astronomy clearly tell us that we are special; we human beings are made of a rare form of matter in the universe;
we are like gems set in this sea of darkness which is the universe.
Think about it: we are agglomerates of atoms reflecting about their existence and the existence of the universe;
as it were, a self-consciousness and self-awareness of the generating universe.
That is what I’d like you to think about this evening when once again you will behold the stars.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Hello everybody.
What we are having right now
is an experience.
It is one for you, and above all it is one for us on this side of the stage. By the way, today, Steve was right: it feels good to see you all in underwear. (Laughter)
For those on this side, the idea is to leave a mark, to communicate something, to get you involved, to trigger your senses, and to give you an experience.
But how can this be done?
We need to understand what inspires us while remembering a situation, just like when we were kids.
As kids, listening was hard, but remembering through experience was much easier. Now that we are adults, we are not so different: experience is the best way to learn.
My team and I make immersive and interactive installations, designed to offer to people a special moment of being, aiming to be remembered when they go back home.
Doing this, we discovered three kinds of situations that characterize these important moments.
We try to reach them through technology.
It’s not easy, because it depends on the situation, on the means at our disposal, like, for instance, this presentation.
Now I’m here, you are here, and we have this screen - or maybe not. (Electronic sound)
We try to attract people with the unusual, with the 'impossible', but nowadays it’s hard to find something different and unique. Everyone does something different, everybody tries to do something in order to be original,
but everything happens too fast.
It happens overnight.
Indeed, we try to create the sensational, we try to amaze, but the sensational is nothing more than the breaking of an equilibrium. It’s the unexpected in a moment of comfort. (Sound of falling masonry)
But why is this so?
Because our brain works with patterns.
It processes all that surrounds us, and that is why it’s flexible.
It's receptive to the repetitiveness of our past. It recognizes what it has seen before, and that happens all the time, it’s primordial. 'I’ve already experienced this situation, so it’s all right.'
We reason according to emotional connections that are predictable. Thus our state of equilibrium is disrupted and most of the time this brings a joy of the unexpected.
However, to have a disruption we need to break something: we need equilibrium.
This, for instance, is visual rhythm, and that is the disruption.
Therefore, as you see, the sensational is the first key characteristic, and it does not arise without time and rhythm.
In our studio we work with visual arts; therefore, the most important sense for us is sight.
We look for something different, we look for disruption, and we do it through technology that helps us think 'out of the box'.
It helps us with solutions, formulae. So the first thing we asked ourselves was: Why do we use a white and plane surface when there are other more interesting shapes out there?
This is a structure made of poor material.
But let’s imagine, for a moment, it's a product to be presented, or it's the unveiling of a piece of art, or even, a marble monolith.
In our studio, we lovingly called it 'Monalita'. We designed it as an example to help people understand the possibilities of communicating through shape, as this, in fact, may be potentially considered a new level of communication.
Just imagine its uses. Imagine a showroom. Imagine a purse in a store, a purse able to communicate with the hide it's made of, the various materials it's made of, or the various colours in which it's offered. Take a step back, imagine furnishings, or something even bigger.
We took that step back and we thought: Since a screen may have its limitations, why should light stay stuck inside a bulb? Why mightn't it be free to draw itself on walls?
Could this be a new way to create a non-conventional and unique lighting system?
Yes, it's possible!
And that is how, last year, we designed and patented VEDO.
(Simple electronic chime-like music) This is a corner of our studio. The walls are white, as is the furniture.
Everything happens live and is synchronized with the subject.
This system is similar to the one in use today.
(Electronic, piano-like music) These are what we call 'immersive atmospheres'; they’re living walls, and lighting sources different from those we are familiar with.
The environment changes dynamically, letting us cross spatial boundaries.
Everything is controlled via a smartphone, via an app that allows me to change the atmosphere according to my emotions and according to the experience I want to live that moment, in that space. (More powerful, pulsating electronic music)
We are talking about a pulsing environment. We’re talking about stimulation of the senses.
There are no more displays. And it’s not a form of communication between people anymore, but communication between people and environment.
Suppose you’re entering the lobby of a hotel as a guest, and the environment changes according to the situation, the time frame, or the event.
Imagine that you’re in a restaurant, and while you’re eating your senses are enhanced by the walls that surround you.
Again, museums, small ambients like those in boats, yachts, up to huge public areas.
They communicate geometries. So why are we limiting ourselves to a white screen when we can have all this?
This is spatial involvement, and is the second key characteristic.
We call it 'immersive experience'. It’s a skin-changing architecture, which enhances concepts, augments my senses and my space, while communicating emotions, imagination.
This is the most ancient indoor theatre in the world. Imagine taking an immersive tour around it. Suppose you could see within its structure, see how it was conceived. Imagine you could immerse yourself in the work, see how it was designed, and all this via a completely hidden technology.
Imagine how you could explore it - (Rumbling electronic music with percussive sounds) via a new form of communication.
Another way to penetrate the places you’d like to visit.
So no more interactive kiosks that nowadays look like outdated tablets; and no more gigantic advertising video walls screaming 'sales' everywhere.
Nowadays, technology can be much more than that. It can emphasize the value of things. (Electronic sound) revealing information. It can entertain people in a functional way. It can bring out majesty,
or simulate its past (Electronic airplane sound followed by electronic explosive sound) by exposing its very scars.
But all this has no value (Discordant electronic sound) without the third and last key, which is probably the most important: Emotion.
We and our senses are at the core of all this; all the rest is an alchemy of means. It reaches its peak only if we, acting like conductors of orchestras, are able to balance them correctly, and express ourselves in the same way we express emotions through our senses.
The medium must be in line with the content.
Just as it happens here at TEDx, where speeches and ideas are encapsulated and packed-up under one sole title: 'Planting the Seeds'. Its meaning is nothing but the planting of a seed (Electronic sound of plants growing) of what will be great in the future, as well as a vital part of what we are.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Hello everyone.
I came here not to tell you my story but a set of stories, starting from the story of this person here.
Does anyone recognize him?
The person, the child you see here, at the age of 13 started working at a company, although he didn't need to, and then at the age of 20 he left for the US, he studied how businesses worked
and then, when he was 30ish his father put him at the head of his own company.
His name is Adriano Olivetti.
Adriano Olivetti was born 118 years ago, he was a great entrepreneur,
an innovative entrepreneur. But he was also way more than that. He was a politician, a publisher, a social innovator,
because many things sprung from him, things that we still find today in our everyday life;
the one behind him is the company Olivetti.
In the post-war years, Olivetti was the factory symbolizing the Italian industrial renaissance.
It created magnificent things: the "Letter 22," which I believe many of you, or your fathers and parents, have at home which became a symbol of a mass design, the "Program 101," the very first personal computer in human history. Ten of these computers were used by NASA to program the Apollo 11 mission on the moon.
But also some design objects that today are admired for shapes and touch, by big brands that strike all of us with feelings of design and beauty.
The greatest social experiment, and perhaps the greatest product, however, was left by his father Camillo, whom you see at the center of this picture together with all his workers.
Camillo created the Olivetti company starting from the Canavese countryside, hiring the workers who lived in the countryside and giving them a new opportunity at a time when Italy and Europe were approaching a big industrial change.
However, this picture also represents the great challenge Camillo put in Adriano's hands, his son who in fact had gone to America to absorb the power of innovation and the new American industrial mechanism, which he replicates in Italy.
He says: "You can innovate and use new methods into your company, but you can't fire anyone, because involuntary dismissal is the worst tragedy a person could go through."
The innovation carried by Adriano isn't just a technological innovation, or a process innovation. It is also a social innovation.
The social innovation he promotes also starts from the challenge left by his father,
and therefore, by including new methods, new supply chains, he realizes that people, inside a factory, are not just a pawn of a mechanism, but belong to a greater picture,
that aims at the well-being of the community as a whole.
Olivetti's approach springs from this concept.
He, in exile, writes the book "The new order of communities" which gives impetus to the desire to put the community at the center of the current social thought.
So the company is no longer "worker - manager - entrepreneur" but it is part of a much larger community, also determined by the need for educational, cultural projects that will enrich workers, managers and entrepreneurs,
to allow them to innovate even more humanly.
Adriano made this drawing on the back of a book. It holds another tile: putting the community at the center, surrounded by agriculture.
One of the things Adriano really cares about is allowing his workers to go and farm their lands during the weekend, and grow their gardens.
The company in fact registers peaks of absenteism during sowing and harvest time. And this makes us understand how important community drawing is.
But today we need to look at a community through a new perspective that certainly conveys the Olivetti impulse that shaped many of us.
One of these it's the idea of building a workmanlike country, thus shaping a workmanlike community.
I heard this motto, "Building a workmanlike Country," during a summer school in 2011 in Matera.
A summer school organized by an association, the National Excellence Network, which taught us, and I made it mine,
that art stands for openness.
Opening to the other. To what's different.
Never wrap up in our own beliefs, because it implies a total closure towards what's new and beautiful.
Responsibility: R as in responsibility.
We have the responsibility to stay in our own community.
It's no coincidence that today I pinned the objectives for a sustainable development. 17 goals, please read them, because they are an integral part of the rule that allows our future to be.
It's a huge responsibility that we have as individuals but also as a community.
Transparency: T as in transparency it's also very important, because it makes us understand how important it is to count on something that we know, share, co-build.
The advantage of grass-root policies where institutions cooperate with the environment around them,
civil and social environment, business environment and research environment.
This quadruple helix is fundamental for the renewal of our ability to welcome innovation, today artificial intelligence, quantum computing tomorrow.
They make us catch a glimpse of our future based on the ability to sink it in.
E as in equilibrium, because obviously we need balance in everything we do.
There must be a pure balance in the quadruple helix but also in our capability to belong to a community.
There is another element: trust, in which we must find again the values that this word conveys.
We must be able to trust institutions again.
We must be able to believe that our neighbour is willing to improve our existence,
and his own existence,
but he cannot do it by himself, and neither can we.
Trust leads me to talk to you about Matera.
In the first half of last century, Matera was a nightmare for many.
The symbol of the degradation, of an underdeveloped Italy that had not made the leap that Ivrea had made, and the rest of Italy was making.
Matera's stones, the Caveoso stone and the Baresano stone, which constitute the structure of these two neighborhoods within the city of Matera, were residential buildings that had centuries of history where animals and men lived together, obviously raising a striking hygienic issue in our society;
and this caused the State to impose a dislocation within the city of Matera taking people away from the stones and putting them at the edge of the city center in newly built, state-of-the-art blocks, and very well structured, urbanistically speaking. But there is a problem:
the Martella project was imposed by our institutions, so it did take people from a desperate place, but it failed in building a community.
This leads the Matera community to rediscover the existence and the worth of the stones only in the early Eighties.
And there begins a new story for Matera, a very strong challenge: rebuild the meaning of the stones, rebuild their connection to history and rebuild the community.
So a very important journey begins, until our very days.
Roughly ten years ago, a huge, grassroot challenge began.
We wanted to throw down a huge challenge to Matera, having it nominated as European City of Culture: a very important symbol, not only for our Country but because it represents a heterogeneous culture, the European culture, therefore it brings toghether many cultural fragments.
And here comes the challenge of Matera, the Capital of European Culture 2019.
We did it. Matera did it. Matera has rediscovered its raison d'être in this world, has incredibly cleaned up its identity starting from the strength of its community.
Today you will perceive, as you always do in TEDx events, the importance of individual actions.
Individual actions, such as Adriano Olivetti's, or who invented the motto "Let's build a workmanlike country" or our personal experiences within our community, are only important if we see ourselves as members of the community.
Today we can only create value through a community.
And it must be an open, responsible, transparent and balanced community.
Thank you.
(Applausi)
(Singing) How many roads ... must a man walk down ... before you call him a man?
How many seas ... must a white dove sail ... before she sleeps in the sand?
How many times ... must the cannonballs fly ... before they're forever banned?
The answer, my friend ... is blowin' in the wind. The answer is blowin' in the wind.
How many times ... must a man look up ... before he can see the sky?
How many ears ... must one man have ... before he can hear people cry?
(Singing stops)"How many ears ... must one man have?"
"How many ears must one man have... before he can hear other people cry?"
I always focused on this line while singing "Blowin' in the wind."
I think the author focused on it too when he sang this song.
How many ears do we have? How many ears must we have?
"If gods gave us two ears ..." a philosopher said, "and just one mouth, maybe there was a reason."
Instead, we think that we have to train in order to speak and write. All the speakers you will hear today did their best, I'll grant you.
But we think we can just stay there in order to listen.
"Ascolto" (listening") is a difficult skill.
In order to listen, you need to focus, to commit yourself. It's like flying. You need to lift and move. You need to go somewhere else. You need to be willing to change.
Since "Ascolto" has seven letters, as you can see from the dots ... maybe you can guess that I'm going to create a pun.
I'm a linguist, so it was predictable.
I'm going to give you seven tips. They will be marked up by the seven letters of the word. They will help you to listen in an efficient way.
First tip.
"A." Listen in order to listen and not to reply nor to judge.
Well, think about Bob Dylan event. I know that starting from Dylan isn't the most popular choice these days. But, did anyone ask him
what was his opinion?
Did anyone ask him why did he behave like that?
No, nobody did. After an initial silent phase, we were all ready to label him. He is overbearing, arrogant. Who does he think he is?
Then he stated: "Thank you, sorry, you're very polite ... but I can't, I have things to do." So other arguments broke out. You answer that way when they invite you to a school classmates reunion.
You say "I have things to do." It's not fair.
Who knows, maybe he doesn't feel up to it.
I don't know. We didn't ask him. This is what we usually do.
We listen very little.
We listen as much as we need to put a label.
I think you are doing this, we all do this.
"Ah, look how he is dressed."
"Ah, what about the guitar?"
"Ah, that Milanese accent."
"It's not all that difficult."
So, whatever happens here ... we label very quickly.
Alternatively ... we listen as much as we need to reply.
Think about a business meeting.
One speaks, then the second and then the third.
At a certain point I flip out. I start preparing my answer.
I don't listen to the others anymore. Or at a conference, when they say: "Later there will be time for the questions."
I prepare my question, I don't listen to what they say anymore.
Just listen in order to listen This is the first exercise
that I recommend to you.
Then there is the "S" in the word 'ascolto'. Do we listen exclusively to words?
There are many elements that help us to listen.
There are three channels through which we express what we have inside.
Words. Verbs, nouns, adjectives, and so on.
Then there is the phonetic channel. Well-read people call it prosody.
The sounds. The timbre of the voice and its loudness.
If your timbre is like that of Gassmann, Proietti or Gruber you're lucky. If your voice sounds like that of a political leader ...
you may flounder.
Or like Mario Giordano. With that voice you need to make a lot of efforts.
But if I have a Gassmann-voice, I start speaking with this tone ... I go on for ten minutes ... everyone would fall asleep.
Sometimes you need to speak louder. Other times you must keep your voice down.
Sometimes you need to speak faster in order to draw attention. Other times people speak more slowly.
Sometimes we make ... some pauses.
Think about that hole in the stomach ... when your doctor checks your blood test results. And at a certain point ... You start to feel ... very anxious.
You could employ your lover or your friends ... to try this experiment about the effects of a pause.
You should take this person out of the blue, shake her and ask her:
"Tell me the truth!"
"Do you love me?"
And he or she could reply: "Yes!"
But what did you feel before hearing the answer you expected?
And then there is the physical side.
Dressing, smile, posture.
Think about the gestures.
If I say, "We have a great opportunity."
I say "great" but with my hands, I make a little gesture. How much does it excite you?
"I am very happy to be here at TED today."
You wouldn't believe me.
If I say: "I'm very happy!"
You could believe me.
So, this is the second exercise. We listen not only to words but also to many other elements around them.
"C." This stands for "certain words."
It is appropriate to listen to certain words. Which ones?
We have a huge amount of words in our languages.
185,000 words are contained in that big dictionaries.
The richest dictionary has 465,000 words. It's made up by six or seven volumes. I don't remember.
It contains both common words and words deriving from "DIS." DIS means Special Italian Dictionaries. DIS are related to specific fields:
medicine, law, insurance, banking, sport.
Think about the language of computer technicians: Zip, drop, backup, upload it.
Download, print, flag, re-upload it. Among them they are fluent,
they understand each other.
What would happen if I said a word in my DIS and you read it in yours?
Let's take the word "prize".
I meant the cup because I am a sportsman. You meant the payment of the insurance policy.
In this context 'positive' and 'negative' mean what they say.
"How was TED?" "Very positive."
You must be happy about that.
In the context of a medical analysis 'positive' has the opposite meaning. It's not a shade of meaning.
Next week, if we vote, we will vote 'yes' to say yes and 'no' to say no. Referendum usually works the opposite way.
Among this huge amount of ambiguous words, we have around 250 special words. They are called "hot words"; words that warm our heart.
And among these, around 50 are named "key words." Key words can open our heart or they can lock it.
It must be happened to someone named Alessandro, like me. When we introduce ourselves:
"Alessandro." "Me too! Great!"
But the first thing that comes to your mind is you, your children, the people you love.
If I could see some white-haired heads among you, I'd know that the word "Fornero" maybe can change your temperature.
So, it's useful, while listening, to recognize the words that give energy; strength, love, health, honour, freedom.
The words on which people put emphasis.
Let's recognize them and treat them with the utmost care.
"O." "O" (or) is a word that creates a crossroads.
Do you eat this or that?
Do you prefer staying here or there?
Disjunctive conjunction.
When we hear a story told by someone, are we listening only to the story?
Or is our listening influenced by our perception, prejudices and representation?
Put here three things.
Here there are the facts of life.
A football match, a war, an act of love.
Here is my perception. Here there is what I say to people about that topic.
There are people confusing these three levels.
There are people who believe they always think the truth.
There are people who think they always tell the truth.
There are people who think they always say what they think.
Many people say: "I don't mince my words. I say everything I think."
If you are like this, you have to go to a Pacific Island, throw away the keys and don't cause trouble.
When you were about six months old you could say everything you thought.
Think about six-months-old children. After their meal, they are taken, put on the shoulders, and cuddled. Mum gives little pats on their back. By doing so, the child can show the world his happiness.
And when he shows it, his mother says:"My sweetie, love you burped."
By the time this child is 14, he should have filtered what is said, shown, or heard. So, let's distinguish the reality from thoughts about reality from its linguistic representation.
Let's weigh up these three levels.
"L" stands for logic and logical structures.
What is logical analysis?
It is the analysis of the sequences of words in a sentence.
One day, in a convent, a novice asked his prior: "Father, can I smoke while I pray?"
He was reprimanded
as the worst of sinners. After a minute, another novice asked the same prior: "Father, can I pray while I smoke?"
He was praised for his devotion.
This story may seem stupid but smoking while praying and praying while smoking is totally the same thing.
The difference lies in the perception of the other.
There are people who, before telling you something, have to explain their premises. Those who say:
"I have something to tell you.
But let me explain my premise."
I don't care. Tell me the fact!
Or vice versa. There are people who call a spade a spade. You may need to know the causes of that effect.
If a doctor says to his patient: "Mr. Mario, Giovanni whatever the situation is pretty serious. Luckily we got in in time. We are optimistic for the future."
Or he says: "Luckily we got it in time, we are optimistic, but the situation is pretty serious."
It's the same reality, isn't it?

What changes is the perception. So, the logical sequences: cause and effect; effect and cause. Past, present, future; future, past, present are the meaning that people give to what they have to say.
Let's listen to them, without judging. Let' recognize and appreciate them.
Second to last exercise. Listen to what lies between the words.
"T" stands for "trans". Trans means "beyond."
Between and beyond the words.
For example, mums are very good at reading between the words.
"How was school today?" "Mmm."
So, from that, "Mmmh", mum starts digging up.
Does "Mmmh" mean that you failed, that you skipped it, or that the teacher wasn't at school?
In their investigations, mums are moved by love.
Detectives investigate too, but with police and coercive methods. Psychoanalysts investigate too.
We should go through and beneath the words in order to listen. We could help the speaker by asking good questions.
Let's take the sentence: "I'm scared."
What scares you?
How long have you been scared?
How are you scared?
In which way?
Where did you feel that you were scared?
The question: "Why?"
is not always a good question.
"I don't want to go out with you." "Why?"

"Because I said no!"
The question: "Why?"
often provokes a closure. By facing the issue less directly, you could go under, inside, around words. This way, you can bring something important to light.
And now there is the last point. The "O" of "ascolto" requires doing a weird thing. I ask you to read this sentence backwards. It is like when, landing after a flight, you think about the journey
and about its starting point.
Read it backwards. "Once upon a time."
"C'era una volta."
"il était une fois."
"Había una vez."
Almost all tales start in this way, with this formula. It is the narration of the narrations. Tales are stories par excellence.
And people tell stories.
We listen to the stories of people. They are not just the facts. They represent the value that people give to that fact.
If I had to tell you something about this pen, I would tell you that it's plastic with some metal. I would tell you the fact.
If I told you what this pen represents for me, you could get an idea from the fact that I keep it here, in my pocket not in my pencil case.
It's its value, it's the person who gave it to me. It's the value I give to it.
People don't make love or war for facts,
but for the meaning they give to these facts.
So ... there are people who tell you about their medical exam, or about the film or TED they watched. Other people tell you stories that they would like to live someday.
"I have a dream."
Martin Luther King described his goal in the form of a dream. A dream is a story in the past. The power to move between past and present is a typical feature of all stories.
Let's listen to people's stories with love. This is the biggest act of love we can perform to someone. Just listen to their stories.
And given that I have the sensation that you've listened to me. Just let me say: Thank you.
(Applause)
When we know a secret, we generally amaze children by telling them, "A little birdie told me".
But actually whoever is only able to repeat such things gets to be considered some sort of parrot.
According to farming tradition, swallows are sacred animals, the owl, however, is the bearer of bad news.
In Mozambique, some tribes have learned to speak to sparrows and they help them find honey.
People such as Icarus, and Leonardo da Vinci were inspired by the flight of birds and dreamed of eventually flying.
Crows from New Caledonia have incredible abstraction capacities.
Using branches and trees, they can build the tools they need to search for food.
Now, this implies enormous cognitive capacities.
Because actually,
they know how to build them just by having seen other crows use these working tools. In othr words, they can build their working tools without ever seeing the process that's needed to create them.
This phenomenon is extremely interesting, because it allowed the evolution of such tools as well as the evolution of the crows themselves.
This phenomenon was considered possible only for the human race.
Based on this, please look inside your TED bag, look inside, there is a little bag.

"Wait to open" is written on it.
Inside this bag you'll find six coloured bricks.
As I've just said, crows know how to build their tools without ever seeing the instructions to do so, the experiment we'll do now is to try and do the same thing.
Think of a duck.
Have you ever seen a duck in real life?
If you haven't, then surely you've seen one in some documentary, in a cartoon, in a comic book, ok?
What does a duck look like?
What do their feet look like? What do their breast and neck look like?
Does a duck fly?
Imagine it, build it in your minds.
Now I want you, with the six bricks in your hands,
to build a duck.
I'll give you about a minute,
don't copy those next to you. Please use your six bricks
to build your duck.
When you've finished building it, hold it up, so I can see who has won.
Now, please pay attention, because this part is extremely fun.
So, look.
Look at your ducks, look at those of the people next to you.
Hold them up, hold them up.
Are there any ducks that are the same?
Has anyone next to you built a duck like yours?
(Bustle) What I've just had you do is an extremely interesting exercise, I had you build a duck, without giving you the instructions to do so. I asked you to observe ducks, I never said that there was a right and a wrong duck, and it's most likely that when we look, most of the ducks inside this theatre
are different from one another.
But what I actually asked you to do is something more.
I asked you to use your mental model of duck.
You all have a mental model of what a duck is in your minds: in other words, you've observed ducks in life.
There are lots of different ducks in the world, right?
There are many types of ducks, but you synthesized them in your heads and simplified them into a model.
Now, your model does not have all the details of a duck.
You don't know how many feathers a duck has, I don't know, but your model is enough detailed to understand it is a duck and not a heron or a rooster.
Now, there are people who innovate just as you've done, in other words, they are good at observing reality, they know that there are multiple realities and they manage to synthesise models in their minds that allow them to produce products or services to people, whether they are new, never seen before, and most importantly, able to improve human lives.
This is what your creativity does, it pushed us to create a wide variety of different ducks.
Now I'll throw at you one more exercise.
This is one of Picasso's many studies where he tried to study and simplify the way reality can be depicted, and he was able to produce, with one simple stroke, a simple line, what essentially can be
easily identified as a rooster.
So what process is this?
It's a further simplification process.
Mental models are very convenient because they really allow us, with just a simple stroke, a simple detail, to decipher reality.
There are people who innovate like this, in other words they know how to look deep into things, or rather, they know how to look deep into people's souls.
They know how to understand the essence of your dreams, the essence of what you like most, the essence of happiness. And they are able to synthesize it into something that we commonly call an icon, a product, a service, something that you commonly use and recognise by a simple trait.
Let's turn our little exercise into something a bit more complicated and fun.
If I had asked you to build a "non-duck", what would you have built?
Would you have started with a plan, or would you have started by moving your hands?
Now I'm going to make it harder: what if I had asked you to describe to the person next to you what an aeroplane is. and she had never even seen one?
You might have told them: "An aeroplane is quite similar to a duck".
It can be the same colour as a duck, it more or less flies like a duck, but it doesn't lay eggs. (Laughter)
Therefore sometimes we try and use mental models to explain to others something they have never seen before.
Let's make it even harder: what if I had asked you to describe to the person next to you something that in reality doesn't yet exist, but it's something you want to create, like your innovative product,
What would you have told them?
Well, now it gets very difficult.
It's difficult to conceive in your head, elaborate what you are thinking, and it's even more difficult to describe it to others or let others help you.
There are people who innovate this way: in other words, their minds are able to come up with products, services, that are so innovative that not only they're hard to think of, they're also hard to explain to others.
Now, how do these people do it?
How do they manage to bring other people on board?
How do they describe said things?,
Well, they are people who fully understand how things work in this day and age.
They know how to tackle things.
They have understood that change is today's zeitgeist.
Why?
Because this day and age is characterised by speed.
What do I mean by "fast times"?
Fast times are times where you always feel like you're late,
Or those times when those who have to create products and services arrive on the market just a little too late.
While our times are fast, they have also allowed us to realize something else: the times we live in are "not linear".
What do I mean by "not linear"?
I'll explain it to you with an example.
Think of your grandparents, and all the epic changes they must have lived in their lives.
I'm sure some of the changes our grandparents lived were of really epic proportions and revolutionary for all humanity.
Of great impact.
But how many changes? Five maybe?
Maybe ten in their entire lives?
How many changes did
their grandparents experience?
Less perhaps.
Now think of yourselves, think of your children.
Analists say, over the next 20 years the human race will change more than we did in the past 300 years.
So what do I mean when I say, our times are not linear?
It means that it doesn't necessarily follow a straight line.
A straight line makes you think, tomorrow will be more or less like today or yesterday.
But everyone knows, some lines aren't straight, but rather exponential, if not totally unpredictable and unexpected.
Furthermore, our times are complex.
What does complex mean?
Complex system means, there are so many relationships among its parts there's no room for simplification.
Nature is complex: think of the forest, think of nature's ecosystems.
The human body is complex, our brain is complex.
It's not so easy, it's not even possible to isolate one part and simplify it.
Now, what is important within this complexity is that the networks and connections are their most important, interesting metrics.
Lastly, our times are marked by unpredictability.
Unpredictability has always been deemed, or has often been deemed by our companies, a negative thing.
We tried to plan everything, we have always been afraid to think that the future is unpredictable.
But in fact, unpredictability is a great driving force for innovation.
We learn this from Darwin, he says that unpredictability allowed our species to evolve and adapt to the world we live in.
So what should we do now?
Over the years, I have met hundreds of entrepreneurs that are asking themselves this very question.
Some have understood what to do, others haven't yet: some made it, some don't.
Everybody recognizes it is extremely difficult, but there is something else that I see in every one of them, two characteristics shared by all people who try and innovate today. They have a deliberate, immense, an extremely strong desire to be able to experiment; and once again, a deliberate, immense and extremely strong desire to "unlearn".
What do these two things mean?
Now, we have said: our times are marked by uncertainty; and in our times, uncertainty is a positive metrics.
If you're unsure, what can you do?
How can you react?
What can you do, if you can't always foresee everything?
A good idea is to start betting, instead of promising; and to bet well, a good idea is to start by taking one step at a time.
In other words, I'll take a small step towards the direction I think I want to go in, and then I'll ask myself how it's going.
If I ask myself how it's going, I'll ask others too.
You see this would imply being extremely kind towards ourselves and towards others, that is, I'd really have to accept asking myself how it's going.
Now I'm not saying everything should lack direction, I'm really not saying that winging it is more or less like having an objective; I am actually saying, objectives should be reached one step at a time, and at each step we continue to learn.
This need to learn is a second key term: in other words, for years we have turned our companies into learning organisations, that is, we've started to think that learning is a good measure that allows us to move with the times, and it's true.
Luckily this was the case, luckily we realised that learning is important. But at a certain point it also happened, and some companies I work with do that, that everything that they learned, be it ordinary data or big data, everything that they learned made them sometimes think they could predict the future.
Man has always hoped to predict the future, but we all know you can't really predict the future.
We are not even able to predict tomorrow's weather forecast. Therefore, probably, instead of focusing on how to become learning organisations, what I'd like to suggest is to concentrate on how to become unlearning organisations, in other words, sometimes it's harder to unlearn than to learn.
Unlearning implies moving into a slightly uncomfortable state.
Looking at the world, looking at the things you are doing and realizing that, maybe, what you learned for yesterday or for today, will no longer be relevant today for tomorrow. In other words, you can't apply old rules to new worlds, and unlearning makes way for learning something new.
It takes a lot of courage to do it.
It really is an exciting challenge, both for the entrepreneuring world and for the outer world.
Perhaps what you normally do in your life should be brought into the working world, Why keep it out of your working life?
Now, the important question for us all is the one we've asked ourselves at the very beginning: what type of innovator are you?
Are you innovators that look at the world, acknowledge its diversity and know how to add new products that will create even more happiness? Or are you innovators who know how to look deep into human being's souls and recognise the fundamental traits that all people have in common and produce what we call icons?
Are you innovators who know how to experiment?
Do you know how to experiment and be kind towards the results of your experiments?
Or are you innovators who know how to unlearn and therefore sometimes realise that you need to let go of some old traditions to make way for new ideas and new methods?
Whatever type of innovator you are, the reason why you do it is important.
I mean, from my experience I see innovators who generally don't innovate only for themselves and they don't even innovate for innovation's sake.
You don't innovate only to change processes and you don't only innovate to make more money either.
The main reason why you innovate is the life and soul of innovation, it is the amount of connections you've known how to create around you.
It is the life line that drives you to innovate as well as the measure of how well you were at doing it.
The more connections you have, the more you will, and did, innovate.
Never before has there been a better time to innovate, and if you don't believe me, pick up the ducks you created.
Listen to this thing, your smile while building them, the smile that people next to you had on their faces while building.
Here, this is the measure of how today is the day to innovate and create new connections.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good morning.
(Applause) My name is Francesca Vidotto, and I am a theoretical physicist.
To be precise, my job is to try and write the equations that describe space and time.
These equations are important, for example, if we want to try to understand what is inside a black hole, or how our universe has started to expand.
These situations are quite different from our everyday life;
but they are also extreme situations, where a better understanding of time is needed. And understanding time also means understanding what is reality, understanding what is real.
So, reality - what's real?
By the way, these don't seem to be the kind of questions a physicist is supposed to ask.
But they are.
And they may also seem easy questions: what does it exist?
Reality. Easy!
I am here, I exist; you exist, I am seeing you; the floor exists; the screen exists; the chairs you are sitting on exist:
easy, trivial.
Let's try with a slightly more complicated question: do dinosaurs exist?
Who is voting "yes"?
Raise your hand.
Who is voting "no"?
I do not see any dinosaurs here.
I've never seen dinosaurs in my life.
I saw them in a museum, but they were fake.
No, dinosaurs do not exist,
but we can say they "did exist" in the past.
And in the future?
For example, can we say that there are human colonies on Mars?
No, but maybe in the future theywill.
So maybe it's just a matter of trying to formulate the question a little better.
Perhaps we should ask ourselves, What does exist now?
But then, the question "What does exist now?"
requires defining what the present is.
The present is like a thin line, a border with no thickness between the past and the future.
Therefore, for instance, I can order many lines one after the other, lines of simultaneity, the lines on which I put all the things that exist at the same time, and I can then allocate the dinosaurs in the past, us in this present, and the colonies on Mars in the future.
But this is not the case: unfortunately -or fortunately - in the equations of physics that I study it is not possible to do this.
We learned this from Einstein and his theory of relativity - his two theories of relativity.
After Einstein, we know that this stratification of time, this description of the present, has no basis in objective reality.
The world does not work like that.
Let's try to understand this a little bit better.
Imagine, for example - let me help myself by imagining that my sister is here with me -
and my sister and I have very precise watches, better than this one;
we synchronize our watches then I stay in some "Low Lands", while she goes to live in some "high lands": in the mountains, in the Himalayas, in the Andes - you name it. When we meet again, we look at our clocks and we see that hers is a little ahead of mine.
What does it mean?
It means that her clock has ticked a bit more than mine.
This means that time passes differently depending on where we are: the closer we are to a mass, the slower time flows, the slower our clocks tick.
This is true for watches, but it is also true for us.
It's true for everything: it's true for your cells, for every interacting elementary particles, it's true for everything.
The ticking slowdown, when getting closer to a large mass, implies the fact that, as my sister was high in the mountains. she has aged a little more than me.
Poor sister! Let's do the opposite:
let's assume it's my sister who goes near a large mass.
How can we do it?
We send her on a space mission to a more massive planet than ours: for example we send her to Jupiter, that is way more massive than Earth, so time is now flowing slower for her.
But still it is not enough: let's do something more extreme,
let’s send her on a space mission that's even more extreme, let’s send her near a black hole.
A black hole is a supermassive object: its whole mass is concentrated in a very small space and time flows very, very slowly there.
So time depends on where we are; but even if my sister and I were in the same place, at the same location, but my sister started to turn around me at a crazy speed, even then our flow of time would be different:
once more, her time would pass slower. And when we meet again, I would discover she has aged less than me.
Now, OK: time flows differently, the rhythm of time is different for each observer.
But still we don’t understand why this should change our notion of present, why the stratification of time should disappear.
Let's think about this for a moment.
After all, for a given observer time flows with a certain speed, for another one it flows with another speed; but in principle this doesn't prevent me from ordering events past to future: one observer is a little older, the other a little younger, but I can always streamline the events one after the other in a certain order.
Can I?

Well, here is the point: I cannot.
In theoretical physics, in Einstein's equations, I discover that I can't:
I can't because there is no such stratification, and because every clock has its own pace.
Let’s try to understand better what the consequences are that each watch has its own pace.
Time is not a line without thickness: it has a small thickness.
What does it mean that something exists?
It's the question I asked you at the beginning - if my sister was here, three feet away from me, how do I know that she does exist?
How do I know that the things that are here do exist?
They exist because I'm interacting with the floor, I'm interacting with this remote control, I'm watching you and there are particles of light that bounce on you and reach my eyes and I know you exist.
And they do it with a finite speed: the speed of light is a limited speed.
So when I say that my sister exists here, next to me, what actually happens is that there are some photons that come to me - and they take a few billionths of a second to get to me - so in reality my sister exists there, next to me, however, in fact, it is as if the present would become a little bit thicker.
It becomes thicker, it has a temporal thickness of a few milliseconds.
Now, my sister is always in for very extreme missions, and now we send her to the Moon instead.
Up there in the Moon, such extended present gets two seconds thick. This thickness starts to get relevant, also for our telco systems as well.
For example, if I would try to look at my sister with a telescope, I would see now her image of two seconds ago. The photons would take two seconds to get there.
If I had an even better telescope and sent my sister - let’s not make it less extreme: let's pick the star system closest to ours, Proxima Centauri: there is a habitable planet there,
and we send my sister there.
In that case, however, the light takes four years to get there.
So, if I try to look what my sister is doing with a telescope, I would see an image that took four years to arrive.
And again: why should that entail an extension, a thickness of the present?
Well, I could always say, "All right, I'm looking at an image from four years ago.
But in this very moment my sister is doing something four years in the future."
“For her also, in this moment, four years have passed - she is just doing something else four years later, she is four years older.
But things do not really work like that.
Because "four years after" for me could be not the same moment as "four years later"for her, the moment “four years later for her” could be not in my present, in my "now", but it could be in my future.
For example, had she taken a rocket, like before, and had moved at a great speed once again, her pace of time would be different.
So those four years for me are not the same for her now - it’s a moment in my future.
So you see, the ordering of things, of events, becomes difficult, impossible actually.
This extended present is a space - the space of the set of events that I can not order, that I cannot streamline and assess what comes first and what comes next. So I gather them all together
in an extended present.
So the present has an extension. And it has an extension for me. But it has an extension for my sister, too: it has an extension for each of you; it has an extension for every observer in the universe. And how large is this thickness of the extended present? Well, it depends on every pair of observers.

Every pair of observers,
every interaction I consider, has its associated extended present. There's a different extended present
for each couple of observer.
And so the temporal order - what comes first, what comes later -, can only be assessed for those couples: I cannot do it globally.
The ordering is only partial.
So the present does not exist.
Nor does the past, or the future.
This layerization of time has no basis in reality, I can not put things in absolute order.
So let's go back to the previous question: "what does exist?"
I can also try to formulate it better.
We said "Ok, this is a more precise question, it is a better formulated question: What does it exist now, in the present?”,
But then, there is no present.
It is even - think about it - the farther away the other observer is, the more extended my present get.
This present can even extend to the whole universe.
It may also happen that in my extended present is another observer whose extended present includes things in both my past and in my future.
So this network of observers - each with its own extended present - defines a single large present that includes the whole universe.
A present when dinosaurs and men on Mars exist together, and the Big Bang coexists with the end of the Universe.
The universe is a single great "block-universe", where all existing things exist simultaneously.
Think about it for a moment.
Think of the reel of a film.
Now we live in the digital age; but if you have a film reel, you see the reel, the reel's frame are there ll at once.
But when you watch the movie, you watch it frame by frame, and this gives the impression of the passing of time, of the changes in the picture, even if the film reel exists all at once.
The "block-universe" is a bit like that: all things exist together.
What about change?
Our perception of change?
Is it only an illusion then?
The "block-universe" is something where everything exists together.
There is no change; change is just an illusion.
And I do not think so.
I do not think so.
I believe that in fact change, these interactions between observers, these interactions that are interactions between particles - this is what I want to describe in my work, but also those interactions between observers, between an astronaut and someone on Earth - are the only thing that exists.
After all - we said it at the beginning - to say that something exists we must interact with it,
otherwise it makes no sense to say that something exists.
So I believe interactions are all that exists.
But we must renounce something:
we must renounce the idea that time could flow all at once, being the same for everyone.
Change exists, but only locally, only relatively to a particular observer.
When I talk about an interaction, when I talk about change, I have to do it by specifying for which observer change does exist: the same change may not be true, or it may not have happened yet, for another observer.
So you see how important is to answer this question. A philosophical question, that nonetheless is at the heart of physics as well: "What does exist?".
Only changes exist, only interactions exist: changes are local, relative. Time does not exist; the present does not exist; the past and the future do not exist,
and all we're left with are these relative changes.
We can not think of time as a singe flow, a single big clock that's ticking at the same speed for everyone: everyone has its own clock.
But beware, It is important to stress that every single interaction, every change, every clock are not less true.
Just because they are partial and observer-dependant does not make them less true.
These partial, relative interactions are all that is there: there is nothing else.
They are not less true: they are all that exists.
So reality is not a single block, but it is the weave of all these changes that cross one another and interact within each other.
Reality is the tangle of everyone's stories.
Thank you.
(Applause)
If I asked you to imagine a Syrian, or Iraqi woman, a Middle Eastern woman, what could come to your mind is a photograph like this.
A woman stepping back, covered, maybe crying, with dirty children, in a refugee camp.
An image of a victim.
Or, what could come to your mind is a totally opposite image.
This: a very made-up woman, maybe doing a belly dance, covered in gold.
And to these two stereotypes a third has lately been added.
The fighting woman.
A fighting woman who even in death is compared to Angelina Jolie for her beauty, instead of talking about what she's fighting for.
In 2014 I started travelling and covering the war in Iraq and Syria for several international media.
Here I am, during one of the big offensives around Kirkuk, in Iraq to take back the city.
And on these trips I've had the opportunity to meet - to talk to a lot of people, and above all to scratch the surface, and understand that behind women's roles there was so much more, another truth, there was a flipside, so to say.
That is, women - some women wanted and want to be protagonists of their life, their emancipation.
And they want for themselves a more just world.
"I am the revolution", the title of the film, is part of this need to tell the other side.
The part of women that wants to fight.
It's a film made by women, about women.
And the story tells of three women: one in Iraq, one in Syria and one in Afghanistan.
Every day they fight, and above all they risk their lives to help other women.
Our journey began in Baghdad.
From now on I will only show you frames from the film.
In fact, this is a dawn.
We flew to Baghdad to meet Owfi, the organisation which is called Owfi,
Organisation for the Freedom of Iraqi Women, and above all the founder: Yanar Mohammed.
Yanar was born in Baghdad in a middle-class family, and she was lucky enough to go to school.
And in 1992, with her husband and son, she decides to leave, to run away from the regime of Saddam Hussein.
They go to Canada.
She completes a master's in architecture, and starts her career.
They have a home, they finally have security, peace.
Something she never had in Baghdad, before.
But... "I felt like a plant without roots, my life had no purpose."
She always felt this emptiness inside, during all those years.
Until 2002.
In 2002, the Americans declared their intent to invade Iraq.
And, at the same time, she receives calls from the Iraqi capital.
Women were being kidnapped on the street and then sold to human traffickers or forced into prostitution.
So she hurriedly decides to write the statute for the organisation, and in May 2003 she moves to Baghdad, alone, leaving behind her husband and son, because she wants to help women.
Here we can see Yanar during a demonstration in Baghdad, she wants to help women escape from domestic violence, but above all from crimes of honour.
Crimes of honour are a practice, very common in Iraq, whereby men are the defenders of the family's honour.
And if a woman is caught in a compromising situation, or she is raped, and the family learns about it, the woman must die.
And to do that they take her, they throw her over a cliff and they cut her left hand.
The palm is then hung on the family's door, on the door of the house, to show the village or society that the family's honour has been restored.
Even today, in Baghdad, there are hundreds of bodies that come to the morgue bearing the sign, in fact, of crimes of honour.
What does the association do, exactly?
This is a time when these women, who are part of the Owfi organisation, have told not just their story, but how the organisation has changed their lives.
Because Owfi gives these women a second chance.
First of all, they're given a new identity.
Which is very important, otherwise the families, who are always on their tracks and want to kill them, would soon find them.
And then, especially in Baghdad, you can't do anything without documents.
It’s essentially impossible.
Then begins a course, a course that starts with literacy.
Then, a course in feminism, to make these women understand what their rights are.
That they are not and should not be slaves, but can aspire to a different life.
Then there are painting courses, because art can help to overcome violence.
Yanar cares a lot about this journey, because they are not an NGO, offering only services, but they want their women to turn from victims to defenders and champions of rights.
In recent years, that is from 2003 up to now, in the last 15 years, Yanar has managed to save at least 500 women, and still today her shelters don't have legal status, so they are completely illegal.
But despite everything, Yanar goes on, trying to raise public awareness.
I met her the first time she went to speak at the United Nations, precisely to tell them that the situation, for women in Iraq, is still a mess, but it can change.
We now move from Baghdad about 700 km to Raqqa, Syria.
Maybe you've already heard about Raqqa: it's been the capital of ISIS for four years, until November 2017.
On November 17, 2017, a very special coalition, made up of Kurdish units, Arab militias, with the help of the U.S. Army, retook the city.
There were 60,000 men and women marching into the city.
And Rojda Felat was also there.
Rojda Felat is a very petite woman.
She gets more or less up to my shoulder.
She has a great unique feature, which is her very long hair, right down to her hips.
Before the war with ISIS, she told me she had it down to her ankles.
But she is an extremely respected woman.
She has a beautiful smile, too bad she doesn't show it so often, and she's a great military strategist.
Her references are Bismarck, Napoleon and Saladin.
ISIS put a million dollars on her head.
Rojda is 37, and was born and raised in Al-Qamishli, in the north of Syria, almost at the border with Turkey.
In the Kurdish part of Syria.
but the Kurds have always been rated second-class citizens.
And because of this, as a Kurd, and above all as a woman, Rojda felt, growing up, that she didn't have any rights.
And this is why, in 2012, she decided to join the YPJ, the women's protection unit.
There were 4 of them.
Today, six years later, there are 24,000 of them.
The women who choose to wear the uniform make a choice in life.
They also leave behind their old identity, take on a new one, and dedicate their existence to the revolution, and above all to protecting civilians and protecting other women.
In fact, when they go to the academy, in addition to military training, there is also political training which takes its cue from the writings of Abdullah Ocalan, founder of the PKK, who says: if you want to change society, change is only possible if there is a women's revolution first.
And in fact this is where the teachings of Abdullah Ocalan come in, because when Rojda's units enter a new territory, they also want to bring about gender equality.
They want to change society, and above all give new hope.
So in a village - in any of the villages which are taken back from ISIS, they immediately elect, from the citizens, both a male and a female mayor.
Today in Rojava, Kurds-controlled area, women are: in politics; in the courts; in diplomacy; in the police.
And indeed in the military.
Polygamy was banned immediately.
There are no more marriages between minors.
While five years have already passed since the beginning of this revolution, there is still much to be done.
And there's no doubt that Rojda, and all the women, will not stop once ISIS is defeated.
From Rojava, which was a movement that has certainly influenced many other women around the world and many other movements, let's go to Kabul.
Today, Afghanistan is one of the worst places to be a woman.
In 2001, the Americans entered Afghanistan declaring that they would liberate women.
17 years later, 87 percent of women are suffering from violence.
87 percent!
86 percent cannot read and write.
Only 14 percent went to school.
Women cannot go around without a veil, they can't go around in villages without burqas.
They could be stoned in the square.
They have no rights.
And these 30 consecutive years of war in Afghanistan have only compounded these problems.
And that's where Selay Ghaffar gets involved, and does her work.
Selay Ghaffar is the first female spokesperson of Hambastagi, the Afghani Solidarity Party.
She's the first woman to have this role.
She comes from a revolutionary background, in the sense that her father, in the early 80s, decided to take up his rifle and fight the Russians.
Unfortunately, however, he didn't succeed.
And so he and the family left and they went first to Iran, and then to Pakistan.
In Pakistan, Selay begins to work when she's only 12, with the other women.
She helps them, collects clothes, and she understands that she needs to give them an education, women need an education.
And, from a very young age, even before returning to Afghanistan, and Afghanistan was under control of the Taliban, she enters the country illegally and does small training courses.
Today she is back in Afghanistan, and Selay gets involved in all battles, and in particular those for women.
She is critical of the United States, NATO, the warlords, drug lords, Taliban.
She has many enemies, including the government: but despite this, she doesn't stop.
She goes where the government can't go, because unfortunately, Afghanistan today is still 72 percent controlled by the Taliban or drug lords.
But she doesn't care, she goes on.
She meets women, organises literacy courses, work courses.
She gets involves, as I mentioned: but all this comes at a price.
She doesn't have a house, she can't; she's bodyguarded all the time.
They've tried to kill her, to follow her, to kidnap her, to silence her in every way.
They haven't managed it.
And all this doesn't bother her, because all in all, it's impossible to have struggle without sacrifice.
She promotes demonstrations for democracy.
And she is sure that, with all her efforts, sooner or later she will really succeed in changing her country.
These three stories I've told you are very different from each other, they involve very different activities.
But they also have things in common.
The first is that change comes from education.
Women must go to school, because only then, knowing how to read and write, getting an education, can they also help other women, and achieve that process of empowerment.
This is very important, because together, then, these women form a community, that supports and protects these 3 women.
Because these three - Selay, Rojda, Yanar are an example of what can be done; but they couldn't do anything alone.
And above all, one thing which all three believe is that this journey must be shared, it must be done together with men.
Because otherwise change will not be possible.
We must walk together, change mindsets, otherwise all our efforts will be in vain.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Sleepy, I was going downstairs
to the underground.
I headed toward the automatic door entry. There was a grandmother and her granddaughter.
Then, the child begins watching me and asked her grandmother,
"What happened?"
"What?" "That girl", pointing at me,

"that girl has... no hand!"
We enter amongst the great crowd,
it is noisy, and the little girl tries to move between the knees of the people to see what actually isn’t there.
The grandmother tells her, a little embarrassed, "She hurt herself, don’t look at it."
Well, dear lady, that is not exactly the correct answer.
I am Fabia, and I was born this way. She's my little hand,
I’m quite fond of her.
It performs several tasks, including taking things, sometimes it writes, all this kind of things.
It also has small little fingers, for those who want to know.
I am a very pragmatic person: a couple of weeks ago, during the daily Skype with my mother, we talked a bit. Suddenly I said,
"I will suggest a topic that is neither compassionate nor special,
so I'm going to tell you the story of the 3 Cs."
Once upon a time there was the change:
male noun meaning "transition from one condition to another;
when a situation, one thing changes, it takes on a new look."
Because I took the reins of my life. I decided it was time to change, to take time and find a solution accessible to me,
a technological one, a medical device... that at that moment I could find only on the other side of the Ocean, mainly in the USA.
I was wrong, because there's the word knowledge: female noun, meaning "Learning and ability to perceive,"
And I realize now, hadn't I attended the Holden School, and especially reality narration classes, I would not be here,
because some words have come into my life. Like Open source, Community, FabLabs, 3D printers.
They can be all put together in a single name, a nonprofit global organization whose aim is to help people developing innovative biomedical devices.
And if on one hand, we humans need to involve someone else of our change, on the other hand there is also the chance to open up the knowledge, through a beautiful word - sharing:
female noun, meaning “divide with others, accept and provide ideas”.
This has been a trip that has become a diary that told about the events, the volunteers' stories inside this reality.
A greater attention to some words
has been paid like "special", very often related to people with disabilities and I was actually often told, as a child: "You're a special girl."
This word wasn't synonymous with "Oh, poor thing. Sorry."
As when fear doesn't exceed the meeting.
When it does not fit into what we consider normal.
But is there such a thing as normality?
So we should go a little back, when responding to those children is complicated and has to be a lie.
Could it not be something else?
When the 3 Cs work actively together they form something beautiful.
A short time ago, I was with Sara and her mother;
we were all three sitting on those small chairs with small tables, at the same height of her.
Sara looks at me intrigued by my little hand, and she wants to touch it,
a bit as we adults look at something but remaining far away.
At one point, what Sarah's mother does is telling a simple story.
"Look at it, it's a spell of a fairy,
her little hand is a wand. Fabia can decide according to circumstances whether she wants it small or make it longer, like yours."
And with my great pleasure, I present Giancarlo Orsini, co-founder of the Open BioMedical Initiative.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Giancarlo Orsini: I would like to tell you the story of the Open Bio Medical Initiative,
that began a long time ago.
In India, an extraordinary country, with more than 1 billion people,
where the birth rate is high, where there are many children.
When it comes to large numbers, often one forgets the individual.
In India there is also a girl, her name is Krishma.
Krishma is pretty much like Fabia.
When she was born, an angel forgot to give her a hand.
This girl, however, has a normal life,
she is lucky to have two extraordinary parents. They send her to school to study.
One day, her mom approaches Krishma and says, "I have to give you great news! Soon you'll have a little brother!”
And Krishma, obviously, is very happy, because she always dreamed of having a little brother to play with.
But after the first moment of happiness she turns to sadness. She knows that when her brother is born, she won't be able to hug him as a normal child would.
"Normal" is a word to be wiped off the dictionary. But Krishma says, "What can I do?"
Her luck, in this story, is to have a friend who studied in India.
His name is William, and as many other kids today, is very familiar with technology.
He uses the Internet, uses computers, technologies that break down all boundaries.
He suggests an idea: "Why, Krishma, don’t we look on the Internet and search for a solution to your problem?”
They start to write, telling her story and many give them ideas.
Problem is, most of these ideas were interesting but totally useless for Krishma.
There are bionic hands in developed countries, but the cost of one of those hands could buy a house in India. Krishma cannot afford that.
But in this world, since two decades ago the Internet revolution has hit the boundaries,
people can talk now to each other, there is no difference of sex, race or belief,
and people can not only play with it but also use it to share ideas.
And from all around the World, finally, some guys come together to solve Krishma’s problem.
The first idea is to build a hand,
to design it, to think together
and in fact so begins this long journey. They share ideas across different time zones, languages ​​and tools,
but with one common goal: to offer Krishma the opportunity for her to embrace her brother.
The challenge becomes more and more exciting. These people discuss about it in every possible moment: during school breaks, in the evenings, at home.
And finally there comes the magic: They design and draw the hand for Krishma.
It begins to have a form, and contrasts also begin. "I think it would be better this way"; "No, I think it would be better that way!"
The first discussion was about the color to give this prosthesis,
and what's great is that Krishma was involved in this process.
And Krishma said, don't worry about that!
For example, I like white and blue, they are fine.
One more problem solved, then.
The prosthesis takes increasingly
its final shape and colors. During one evening, they finally arrive
to the goal they desired:
they had created the complete mechanical hand. Next problem is, how to bring the hand to Krishma in her country, in India?
Well, they write on the Internet and the idea comes out: the Fourth Industrial Revolution.
Next to Krishma's native village, they find a machine called 3D printer.
Thanks to this machine, digital files made around the world can be put together, assembled, uploaded to this machine, and printed. It helps them to carry the device out to Krishma.
This prosthesis is called Will, from the boy that started the project.
It's a very basic prosthesis but it makes so much difference to this little girl.
In just a few months her little brother was born.
Krishma is happy, as she is now able to fulfill her dream of having a little brother
that she can embrace as any other child.
Krishma is very intelligent,
and unfortunately she sees in the small hospital, where her brother was born, things that bring her from the joy of having a hand back down into sadness.
She sees some children prematurely born and that don’t survive their second day of life.
In those countries, lots of hospitals often lack the basic medical equipment.
Then Krishma says, "Why not give those children a future and the same opportunity you have given to me?"
She then ask the Community that was formed, called Open Biomedical Initiative, "Can we guarantee a future and the survival of those children?” Can we design a machine that gives them a better chance to survive, a "Future machine" in a sense?
Because children are the future.
And that's why, thanks to Krishma and to all the volunteers, two weeks ago the project "Bob" has finally taken shape,
the world's first 3D printed neonatal incubator.
Thanks to it, we will be able to give a future to many creatures that wouldn't otherwise see the second or third day of their life.
All this wouldn't have been possible
without the Internet, the 3D printers,
but especially all those many volunteers who provide their time, their creativity, their skills
to work and collaborate with the Open BioMedical initiative. Trying to create a better future for the most vulnerable people,
for all people.
And that is why I want to give a positive signal in times like these.
In the world there are people who soar,
often anonymously.
Unknown people.
Soon, we will also be able to give an opportunity to Fabia.
We are working to develop fable named just from her name: a myoelectric prosthesis. We live in a country where electronics are available.
Thanks to 3D printing, this is the first prototype which will soon be offered to this beautiful girl and, why not, make her dream and above all give the same opportunity to many other people.
Because all our devices are meant to be given for free.
In return, though, we ask them only one thing: become members of the Open Biomedical Initiative, providing helpful feedback,
because users are the best possible tweakers
of their own devices. We must not have the conceit to know what is good or bad for the others. People must be included in the process: the Open Biomedical Initiative is one,
formed by many volunteers. There is no difference between users, producers, communicators.
Today I'm here, but behind me there is a great community and many great people.
I would say that we will give you, Fabia, a hand but you have to lend a hand to us. Because otherwise we would be a bit slower and we want to do it in the shortest time possible.
I finished. Let’s say goodbye to all, together?
Thank you!
(Applause)
Good morning everybody.
As the speaker already said, my name is Fabrizio Lori.
I see an "A” in front of me
and this is a letter that I loved so much.
But I also hated it, and now I'll try to explain why.
It was 2004-2005, we're talking about almost 15 years ago. Back then, the managers of Mantua asked me for a sponsorship.
I immediately said, "No, I'm not interested."
But then I reconsidered: "Doing something for the city, why not?" Because back then, unlike today, with basketball, five-a-side football -
there was just football, it was in C2 Series.
And I said, "Okay, why not?"
More to do something for the city but not at all thinking that it would have turned out to be a similar experience.
Anyway, we start the first year in C Series, a fantastic enthusiasm builds up, the team played so well.
Of course, I never thought the team would win and go straight to the B Series.
Also because we had stayed for 35 years in C Series.
Here is a nice photo of the stadium, to prove the enthusiasm. What did emerge there
was a real merger between the city, the fans, the players. Everybody spoke about Mantua team.
And finally we came to the B Series. We had never gotten to B Series for 35 years.
It was a real event for Mantua.
Here is a picture that is particularly dear to me, look at the stadium that was really "bombed" it looked like a "bomboniera" (wedding present)
And the moment we got to the B Series, many fans, especially a few hooligans, asked me to play in the A Series. In the A Series?!
We have been in C Series for 35 years, let’s stay in B Series!
"That's fine, ok"
Players were rather worried: it was another category, with other players, there were other important teams, like Atalanta, now you see how well it is playing in the A Series, incredible.
The Torino team - I find it hard to say, Torino.
And then there was Catania - there were a lot of emblazoned teams.
At the end of the day, Mantua was a small town, with just 48,000 people, compared to Turin, Bergamo, Bologna.
Long story short, we start at full speed, I remember the first game we lost, maybe some supporters here also remember it was in December, the last match against Pescara, beating all other teams - and this is the ranking of the B Series of the first half, the winter round.
There was Mantua 42, Catania 40, I can't pronounce that, Atalanta 38.
Incredible!
In fact, Sky's call comes in.
Look, Sky TV calls me, the director - I can't remember his name, it was long time ago, I can't remember.
He tells me: "Fabrizio, we have to meet because we have to sign the agreement for the A Series.
"For God's sake", I say, "let's just wait a second, it's still early".
"No” he adds "because it's never happened that a team that got first in the ranking in the first half of the season, didn't go to B Series". Well, even though it never
happened before, I was not so sure.
What happened?
We failed, unfortunately, we remained in B Series.
And it hasn't happened later, also, in the 80, 90 years of B Series.
The only time when a team came first in the first half of the season, and got stuck in B Series.
What was the team? Mantua.

Okay, it doesn't matter.
Here we do the playoffs.
Many people from Mantua remember them, I think.
I remember them like yesterday.
We played against Modena, a great round-trip game, we went to the final with Turin and played the first one home, we won 4-2.
We are already close to the dream, that dream that frankly I never hoped would happen.
But it was happening.
Mind you, during that week,
after the 4 - 2 match we played at home, perhaps the best match I've seen played by my Mantua team.
Had we played against Real Madrid, we'd win against Real Madrid.
Anyway, we play great.
A title is published in the Gazzetta "Betting scandal”, which means, Juventus team is relegated to B Series.
No, it's not possible.
And then we go to Turin, we played a "honest" match, but it was no real match, something was clearly wrong since the very beginning,
because Mantua was a small town.
Juve and Toro could not stay in B Series or go in B Series and let Mantua go to A Series.
So the game was nothing short of scandalous, with an arbitration - let's say unlikely, to be kind,
and we remained in B Series.
What a disappointment: it would have been a fairytale and it would have done good for Italian football too. This fairytale of this small team, starting from the C2 Series, and in three years goes to the A Series. That's what happened, anyway.
The day after, I remember the endless sadness, I cried, I remember.
I cried in the stadium, like most of Mantua fans.
Because you know, it's not easy to repeat yourself.
However, in the second year of B Series we played with Juventus, Naples and Genoa, I mean, no small teams.
And in September I go to the players' market, because there is the players' market - and I say: "Let’s see some players to strengthen the team: after all, the winning team was already there. We got to A Series, after all: they just didn't allow us to play.
But now I'm not here to say things I could regret - Whatever.
I meet a journalist, still from Sky -
seems like a destiny. And he says: "President Lori, please, don't buy anybody this year".
And I say, why not?
Excuse me, we got all the way to the B Series.
And I'd like to try and play in the A Series. “Please, do not invest anything, Juventus, Napoli and Genoa are poised to go to the A Series.
There probably won't even be playoffs".
I was like, you're kidding:
but that's what happened. First time in the history of football, Juve, Napoli, Genoa go to A Series.
We ranked fourth or fifth, we had a great season.
But we did manage to do one thing, anyway.
And now I want to show you what it was.
Please, video.
[Video] [10 years ago, epic win at Martelli stadium] [January 13th, 2007] [LITTLE BUT PROUD - the triumph of red and white pride] [Even for red and white players, it's the day when a dream comes true,] [Playing against the idols of their youth] [A match to watch again and again] [Buffon, newly nominated world champion,] [leaving the stadium injured among everyone's applause] [A last minute mistake by Del Piero] [We made the history: Juve KO, after 45 matches in A series] [What a party!]
[A night to remember] [President Lori, today: "That was the peak of my management"] (Applause) Thank you.
(Applause) I don't know if it was the peak of my management.
Certainly, the victory was a great satisfaction.
But this victory proved - Juve hadn't lost a match in two years!
Then there were all the world's champion players, among which Trezeguet even though he played for France, he also played for Juventus, Del Piero - everyone.
And we showed, especially to the League, that we also were an A Series team.
That was it, that's why my boys put their hearts into it and we won that game.
Then I went through a number of rather quiet years, we played three more years in B Series, so five years of B series, not bad for a little town like Mantua.
Then in 2010 we had a few issues, we had a very good team but we couldn't understand -
I remember sitting with coach Serena, that maybe someone remembers, at a table, trying to understand: how come we cannot win a match?
We couldn't figure out."
Long story short, we lost position after position, not figuring out why.
Then we had to face a bankruptcy, a frankly unpleasant chapter of my life beyond the disappointment of that year.
Then, after two years, it became known that we filed for bankruptcy because we had players who sold themselves against us.
And that was my life's biggest disappointment, because one feels betrayed. You always try your best, then a few players spoil everything betraying your trust. Someone can figure out what I felt back then.
In fact, for some years I decided to have nothing to do with football because I was disgusted, sorry to put it bluntly, Even disgusted, maybe,
doesn't convey properly.
I was disgusted by the corruption
in the football world. Then, after a few years, I got a bit over it and I decided with my partner - Hi Elena - to start this little amateur football academy with children aged 4 to 12 years to teach them - these are the pictures of children - to teach more than to become champions, to live and have fun because football must be fun.
If it starts to become, like, "I'm going to be a player so I can earn millions of euros," then it's best not to do anything.
And this is something I'm proud of, in addition to this small academy AS Diverso is a company here in Mantua dealing with disabled people. We merged and we created this fabulous team.
This team is called Accademia Fabrizio Lori - AS Diverso.
And this year for the first time we played in the B Series in Milan.
We are a small reality, of course, and we played against teams like Inter, Genova, Fiorentina, which are all affiliated with professional teams.
The academy Fabrizio Lori is only affiliated with my academy. We went to the stadium with small buses, I drove one and Riccardo drove the other.
They came on Inter-branded buses, instead.
We played a great championship.
I got shivers, because I remembered my old Mantua.
We beat Inter, Genoa and Fiorentina, so we basically ranked third in the standings.
How many of them did go up?
Two.
(Laughter) Well, it doesn't matter because we received the most beautiful award a president could ever receive.
This is the fair play award.
There were 45 teams and it was given to us,
They were possibly sorry for us, I don't know.
Anyway I think that will entitle us, they have already told me, informally, because basically first, second, third team are up; then there was the fourth that had an unthinkable difference of points.
So I think they're going to make us go further - I want to go to this A Series, sorry.
And anyway it's another way of seeing things, but these guys made me really happy,
I call them special guys, because for me they are really special.
I'll tell you more.
I learned much more from them, over this last year than they from me
because they have given me a sense of lightness and happiness that I never felt in so many years.
I thank you so much, I hope I haven't bored you, and I thank you for listening to me.
Thanks.
(Applause)
What do you think, when I say "design"?
Chances are, you think about finely crafted object.
Objects that you can hold in your hand, or maybe you have at home.
These are classic icons of a design that made our country famous all around the world.
This is a kind of design that has fascinated me since the beginning of my career as a designer, but I will not talk about it today.
I'll talk to you instead about a kind of design that you use every day and relentlessly evolves.
I will talk to you about digital interfaces.
What combines, connects these two types of worlds the world of atoms, of physical industrial design with the pixel world of digital interfaces is the design intent to solve people's real problems, to improve the quality of life.
And this is a design intent that led me to conduct an almost personal philosophical research on the designer's role on the role of design ethics.
I had a simple question in mind: what's a designer's responsibility?
A designer may have the responsibility to design an object's aesthetic pleasantness or may have a responsibility not to create physical pain while designing a chair for its future users.
But I'll tell you an anecdote about that.
How many of you know where the stereotype "women drivers are dangerous" comes from?
I'll tell you: at the beginning of the 20th century when Ford launched a first series of cars, the first car ever designed was designed on the shape and size of the male body.
And after a few years when even women finally drove to driving, unfortunately we saw a surge in a series of accidents.
Obviously you will think that women's body and metrics are very different from the men's ones.
And despite this, the driver's seat wasn't even adjustable.
And therefore, not accessible to all.
So I wonder, what kind of responsibility a digital interface designer has today and how does the role of physical products' designer changes in the switch to digital products?
Internet and social media platforms today are the new learning meeting points
that can be compared to squares, hospitals, cities.
And even if these are virtual worlds they must be accessible to everyone.
This is exactly what I deal with as a digital accessibility manager.
My role, in other words, is to make products like WhatsApp, Messenger, Instagram, Facebook and the whole web in general, accessible to everyone,
where "everyone" means regardless of visual, cognitive, auditory abilities of people.
Here, for example, we were a week ago in Los Angeles where we organized a workshop with my team with a group of blind and visually impaired people.
The goal was to be all together and co-design innovative solutions to make online content accessible to everyone.
The United Nations says that a billion people in the world or almost 20 percent of the world's population live with at least one disability or a different skill.
Now one person in seven, in this room and in the world lives with a different skill.
Or how many people of you wake up in the morning and wear glasses to see better?
or how many people ask their children or grandchildren to repeat what they were told because the first time the message did not arrive clearly?
the World Health Organization says that disability is an integral part of the human condition.
Or we all think that disability is something permanent but in reality, science teaches us that it is something dynamic.
That can be situational or temporary.
Quite simply think if you break your arm your arm, will be in a cast and so I'll have to use my left hand or on a sunny day the sun will affect my vision and let's stop seeing what I really have before my eyes.
So as a designer I have always wondered what's my responsibility - and mostly, that when we approach digital experiences' design, we must increasingly consider these types of different viewpoints of need that come from different parts of the world, but also different cultural needs.
Designing for accessibility is something that led to radical innovations in many sectors from the automotive, aeronautical, education sector to many others.
And in particular, an example in the 1970s that caused quite a stir at the time was during the movement of the hippies in California.
a group of Berkeley students in a wheelchair proposed a simple change of architectural style of the city or to add a piece of cement to create a slide and thus allow you to cross the street simply and independently.
So designing for a small group of people can even solve loosely related problems, such as when walking with a stroller on the street, or with a shopping cart. Also, a person on a bicycle or a skateboard, can benefit from this abatement of architectural barriers and this is called "The Curb Effect": when a problem is solved for a small group of people, several other people benefit too.
How many of you have used video subtitles without using audio?
This will have happened to many of you, I guess, because maybe you are in a very noisy environment or maybe you don't want to disturb your neighbor on the train.
or maybe because you want to learn a new language.
And this is another type of technology that was developed in the 1970s for a small group of people affected by total or partial deafness.
And this is now the basis of every website platform that offers video content like Youtube, Netflix or even TED.
So me and my team, we took this technology, that is now widespread everywhere to make all the content accessible on our platforms.
Another example of a project we have created is called Automatic Alt text, or Automatic Alternative Text.
What does it mean?
How to be able to transform visual content for blind and visually impaired people?
So this function uses artificial intelligence to identify objects in an image and transform image content into audio content.
In this case this image can be read as the image may contain sky trees and heart
This is not the maximum of linguistic "naturalness" but it is a first step towards what will allow us to make all these online contents accessible for everyone.
Designing for accessibility means this, and much more.
How to choose and design color, patterns, shapes for colorblind people or design software for the visually impaired or make these boards of endless information with verbose and cognitively fatiguing contents accessible to people with dyslexia
who are more or less 15 percent of the world population.
So going back to my initial question, what kind of responsibility does the designer have today, especially if you consider that in a few weeks, a few clicks, a few moments a digital experiences designer can reach millions if not billions of people?
I believe that one of the main responsibilities is to break down digital architectural barriers to allow fair and democratic access to online information for access to medical examinations or to guarantee integrity during political elections worldwide; and to break down the onset of online bullying, hate speech and instead help the emergence of new groups of thought or events such as the one organized for climate change by the Swedish 16 years old girl.
So, to wrap up, "Good Design enables and bad design disables".
I believe that designing for accessibility is a creative ethical challenge that should be the basis of any creative process from concept to design to market launch.
And I throw down this challenge to all of us, creative people, architects, designers, people who are involved in making these products that are in the hands of millions, billions of people.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Music does matter,
probably much more than you think.
Music is one of the most powerful tools of emotional connection that humanity has in their hands, because it impacts on people's lives.
Think about it, it has a significant impact on the psychophysical state, on the mood, on our thoughs, on our perception of the world:
and therefore, indirectly, it impacts on what we do every day.
Let's say that it has to be handled with care, with responsibility.
Personally, I see four very important dimensions of music, that have a significant effect on people's lives.
The first dimension is the innermost level, that within ourselves: memory.
Think for a moment: when you listen to a song, a song that you heard maybe thirty years ago.
It’s able to bring you back to that moment and make you feel those exact same feelings.
Only the sense of smell might have that kind of power.
Second point: the therapy, the cure.
Music is also apparently important in medical therapies.
It is scientifically proven that it has a direct impact on dopamine, the pleasure hormone.
Just like sex or drugs.
Let me give you a piece of advice: replace drug with music.
Sex is up to you, no problem.
(Risate) Seriously, music can sooth people's pain, including physical pain. It can help people dealing with stress, depression.
Its worth is really incredible, from a therapeutic point of view.
But it's also relevant as a learning tool, because music teaches us how to listen, and we learn by listening.
Music has been shown to have a direct impact on people's Intellectual Quotient, on concentration ability and the ability to act productively.
This is called “the Mozart effect”.
(Music) A second dimension of music is the spiritual one: the connection with the sacred, with the mystery, with the divine.
You have just heard a typically Christian melody mixed with a sound, a timbre and a harmony that come from the Arab culture.
It is the union of two religions in a single passage.
All the world's most widespread religion have actually given great importance to music.
I will only dwell on Islam, because Islam even classifies just two types of music: the lawful one, "Halal", and the lawless, forbidden one, "Haram".
The first one brings inner peace and puts us in contact with Allah.
All other music belongs to the second category.
(Music) Music has another great feature: it brings people together. Specific social groups with the same interests, the same goals and often the same values.
And this has happened in many cases: Revolutions in history always had a musical support.
Since the French Revolution, popular music has been against the noble elites of those times. 100 years later, the Italian
"Risorgimento" (Renaissance). If you remember, when they cried out "Viva VERDI!" before La Scala Theatre,
they meant a political cry: “Viva Vittorio Emanuele Re d'Italia”.
Music was used as a tool to fight the Austrian oppressor.
And up to 2010, in the Arab Spring, where rap was important to ignite the revolution in Tunisia.
Music was also important to define the hippy generation.
Music, at that time, was fundamental to draw a line between generations. Again, it was decisive for the rights of black African-Americans in USA and around the world.
If we think about black music - soul, blues, jazz, hip hop, rap, trap, those are music genres that have been fundamental to connect black Americans with their traditions and cultures, towards a struggle for their rights.
(Music) This is the aspect I like the most. It is the strongest feature: music as a tool to solve world's problems, to help make the world a better place.
This has happened in different situations.
In classical music, I'd like to mention two interesting cases: on your left you can find José Antonio Abreu, a Venezuelan orchestra conductor who invented "El sistema", that is a method of doing social integration through music education.
He saved from the street 900,000 Venezuelan children, and today this system is applied worldwide and also in Italy.
On your right, you can find two other prominent conductor: one Israeli, Barenboin; and the other one Palestinian, Said. Those two men in 1999 formed the West-Eastern Divan Orchestra, a unique orchestra composed by musicians from Palestine, Arab countries, and Israel.
Music becomes a political tool across countries that certainly aren't naturally friendly.
But the interesting thing, beyond classical music, is the role of rock and pop music, that obviously had greater relevance and impact in terms of social improvement, to face the world's problems in environmental and exquisitely social issues.
Many artists have been involved and have also become real activists, such as Sting or Bono.
But there is an important year in this musical genre, which is 1985, when in July takes place the biggest event ever in the history of pop music: 1.9 billion people connected, 95 percent of world's tv stations, 16 hours of live broadcast: "Live Aid".
Well, I asked myself, as for what was happening - I see many young people here, who probably didn't live those years the way I did -
and so I wondered, in this new millennium, has music the same powerful social impact, the same sense of responsibility?
Is it still able to move people’s consciences?
Well, it's probably a little dormant, at least I see it that way.
It's a bit dormant despite the fact that today's music potential, with new technologies, is unbelievable, it has exploded.
Think about the Internet.
Think about streaming.
Think about the fact that today each of us can listen, anytime, anywhere, any kind of music from his smartphone.
Well, just for instance, this artist, Taylor Swift, who is not even 30 years old, has twice as many followers as the current US President.
Her last song, in January, was listened to by 40 million people within 24 hours after its release.
So, you can understand how impacting a celebrity's role can be on the topics we have been talking about.
And I must say, especially among young people.
Let me recommend something: if you have children and grandchildren, and you want to understand what they think, what their fears, concerns, and ambitions really are, listen to their music, make an effort to understand what they are listening, and you'll learn a lot about them.
For this reason, last year we launched a music ecosystem, "Music Innovation Hub" in Milan, that deals with music as common good, music as a tool for education, social integration, promotion in the world of the Italian music culture, and also for technological innovation.
Well, I'm not sure if I convinced you: if this wasn't the case, give me the last chance to bring you back to a sad day.
In the days following the attack on the Twin Towers, Clear Channel, the main American radio network, issued an edict with 160 tracks that could not be broadcast by their network.
Among these there were many that were connected to the fire, or to the Towers, to the fall, as they didn't want to hurt the sensibility of the American people.
But there was one that had no meaning at all that was on that list.
A song about peace, perhaps the finest secular anthem to peace. And it was censored.
It was censored because, probably, it was inconsistent with the wishes of an administration, and of a part of public opinion, that wanted to start a war immediately after that moment.
Well, I ask you a favour, to all of you: please, sing the last 15 seconds of the refrain of this song, which all of you surely know.
Don't do this for me: do it for all the times this song has not been broadcasted.
And most of all, do it for all the times the message of this song has not been respected.
(Video) (Music)
Good. Thank you.
(Applause)
Good evening everyone.
I am very happy to be here to study and look together at what artificial intelligence, AI, is doing together with humans
and whether we are building a world where artificial intelligence and human beings can have a conversation, talk and share common feelings or not.
I definitely think so,
and I finally carry with me a positive message, the one of my scientific community, that for many years - 30 years for me, unfortunately - has been working to build, design new artificial intelligence systems.
I speak in present tense, not in the future, because artificial intelligence is a technology of today.
Just last March, the World Economic Forum has defined artificial intelligence as the kernel, the beating heart of the fourth industrial revolution.
It’s not the atomic bomb or something negative, it’s like water for the steam cars of the 18th century, like electricity for the second industrial revolution in the 19th century, and like electronics and computers in the past century,
it is definitely studied and accepted by all that artificial intelligence is becoming the winning technology for this century and, in particular, for this decade.
Yet artificial intelligence is often something we don’t know, something different, and the first question we ask ourselves is: how can we, human beings, interact with it?
Are we ready?
And can we work with something or someone that is different from us?
This is why I want to show you an old movie, a movie that really struck me growing up and that probably some of you might recognize, it shows that when people don’t know, don’t know, they can be astonished, even be scared, nurture big prejudices against the others.
You may recognize this scene, where these kids are so shocked because what's before them starts talking.
And it is the little old ET -- the alien that Carlo Rambaldi designed in the 1980s -- that started talking, saying the famous sentence “E.T. phone home”, if you remember.
This wasn’t a robot, it was a puppet with 87 parts in motion, ten only in the face to show empathy to human feelings when it starts talking to these kids, with humans.
In a way, when we talk, discuss and have a conversation with something different, we may think we are not ready. In the case of artificial intelligence, though, we are absolutely ready, because we interact with it every second of our lives.
Each one of us uses interfaces with our phones, that recognize our faces, or our voices and we got used to the so called “natural language processing” systems, or artificial vision, that detects our face.
This is my kitchen.
In the morning, when I have breakfast, I can talk to that black thing that is the Google home, I can ask him the news of the day, to recall the grocery list that someone at home, if not me, will maybe go do it.
So, interacting with AI is something we can do in our everyday life, but it can be done at work, too: it is true that robots are replacing us in several manual jobs, especially the heavy, repetitive ones.
But they can also work along with humans.
This is a classic example of collaborative robotics, where humans and robots live and work side by side towards a common goal.
Just as artificial intelligence isn’t only embodied, it doesn’t just come with a body, is also pure spirit, pure software, as artificial intelligence, such as this one, allows a surgeon to interact with data on a screen by using gestures, by using only their hands, without taking out their gloves.
This is certainly a positive aspect of the potential interaction.
It has been studied - here's iCub - for over 10 years at the Italian Institute of Technology how to interact with human beings with features, smiles and a human-like interfaces.
This is in fact reality, it’s no longer fantasy, and it’s the topic I enjoy the most these days.
When I am in the lab with the doctorate students and we’re studying how to explain to a robot like this one how to exit through the door to get a coat, and understanding that the people standing there are chatting, they won’t go anywhere, teaching the robot to have a conversation with them, so connect to them, understand how to interact with them.
All this is artificial intelligence.
Here is a definition that I love, it was submitted last year by the European Community, signed by all the Member countries, that changes the perspective on artificial intelligence, in that it is no longer just a discipline.
As you can see here, “AI refers to systems", therefore things that work, that may be designed, achieved by engineering both software and hardware.
They can do three things: they interact with the surrounding, therefore they can hear, see, understand; they reason and learn with an intelligent behaviour, and they can start actions, both hardware and software, such as a robot moving, a drone, a car or a system for recommendations or of course, alas, for fake news.
This is done with a certain degree of independence, so although in a sense systems will also be able to be completely independent, generally they are designed by humans, for humans, and therefore their degree of independence is still not 100 percent.
Actually, this definition of AI, that we are currently using in our syllabus, is what we try to improve upon and achieve every single day.
So the ideas I want to convey to you, I wanted to understand, discuss with you the fact that first of all AI understands us.
What does it mean?
It means that it can use its senses to understand, get the the thoughts of the humans before it, or simply their behavior.
Why does it understand us?
Much like our brain is made of neurons, hundreds, millions, billions of interconnected neurons, so the most modern part of artificial intelligence is now made of what is called "deep learning", artificial neural networks made of hundreds of millions, if not billions of interconnected neurons.
With a great difference: while our brain is one, made as it is by God, by the evolution we went through all these centuries long, we engineers we can build many different brains, we can interconnect neurons -- that are nothing, but simple pieces of software -- in many different ways.
For example, to create what you see at the top, a network that can detect a tumor from a medical image.
Or what you see at the bottom, another network made of hundreds of neurons that takes an image and transforms it into another one with a different semantic meaning, that, for example, can say to a car where the road and the sidewalk is, or where to go and not to go.
Or what you can see over here, starting from an incoming noise, the network understands that this is a voice, that someone is saying the word “the dog” and it recognizes the speech.
Actually, these neural networks, interconnected to more and more complex systems, are also able to recognize the human body,
and not only as a whole: they can recognize knees, arms,
and this is fundamental to understand what those people after a ball are actually doing -- that they are playing basketball -- and to understand the actions, the activities, or detect, as you can see here, all the parts of the body.
Of course, it understands the movements, but also the behaviors as well and the intentions. And being able to interact not only physically, but also in a cognitive way.
What you can see now is an example of neural networks that learned from human beings what is interesting in a piece of art.
And what is interesting for a neural network?
Exactly what we find interesting: the faces on a capital, the face of the lion, or a statue, or a painting.
This is because we made a software that is somehow able to retain our experience and our knowledge.
If it’s true that artificial intelligence is learning to understand us, do we in turn understand artificial intelligence?
This is not a trivial question, because I’m not just referring to us as a society at large, that can be scared because it doesn’t know it.
I am referring to us as scientific researchers, as engineers and computer scientists.
Actually, one of the current issues with artificial intelligence is that it's becoming very complex and, in a way, hard to understand.
I want to send a positive message again: it’s not true that neural networks are hard to understand and “who knows what they are doing?”.
They are simply mathematical systems made of hundreds of millions of parameters and differentiable functions that have all these parameters that a human being can hardly calculate alone, since we are no longer able to make complicated mathematical calculations.
For this reason, because we increasingly need to find a way of communicating, the research we currently do around the world is to find a reason, trying to understand, in a sense visualizing the behaviour of artificial neural networks.
A drawing like this one will probably not ring a bell to you.
But a drawing like this is a neural network that does two things: it first tries to understand the most interesting parts of the image. There's a network, "Sam", that won an international competition last year -- it’s one of the best, maybe the best at the moment -- to understand the most salient parts of an image.
And as you can see, this network is slowly trying to say that the baseball player there is doing something.
Well, an important feature for us is trying to understand how come from this network, that is something statistical and mathematical, descriptions like these are coming out, word by word,
And we do that through other networks, that try to explain why, for example, the network below is saying "a group of people is flying a kite in a field"; It’s wrong: that is a field, not a beach.
We need to understand why networks, just like us, can be wrong.
Let’s remember: we are creating systems that are very similar to our ways of thinking, and therefore, just like us, they will never be infallible.
This is another example that will give you an idea of what is being done: an interface for a robot that goes around a house, whom we ask through a natural language: “Please, walk past the kitchen and go to the hallway”, Even in this case, we try to see through other systems what the robot is watching, like for instance this red section, the most interesting one, which is the kitchen.
This is another really easy example, that will show how you this little robot, that can be bought, it’s an evolved version of a Segway, with a cellphone brain, and cameras, that communicates wireless with a server that makes elaborations and sends data back -- as you can see, while it’s following Alessia, it’s telling us what it’s seeing, what it’s recognizing, and it can tell us using a natural language.
It sees a girl, a woman with black pants and it’s following her.
This isn’t a product yet, it’s research, but you know that in our technological field the time-to-market , the distance between the research and the product -- it’s a very short time and it’s really important to figure out how the interaction between a simple robot, like this one, and a human becomes and more natural.
Why becomes natural?
Because we are the ones making the artificial intelligence, and, like a mother who wants their child to constantly improve, shape them to her image and likeness and possibly even better then that, in the same way we try to design a software, an artificial intelligence, that is always better.
I’m going to show you a mother, a great mother, Fei-Fei-Li, the director of Stanford's AI laboratory, one of the first people, ten years ago, who created this big system, called image.net, of hundreds of millions of images, connected them to some text, that helped the world start thinking and learning how to classify images and recognize a dog, a person, a chair.
In the same way we used videos and images, we started putting together videos and images and sounds, like this network, that is trying to figure out, using concepts from psychology, if the people before it are frightened, smiling or happy.
And this is done in labs all over the world. As I was telling you, artificial networks got a bit luckier than us because they can have more senses than us.
This is Raquel Urtasun, the director of the Uber lab in Canada, probably one of the most famous scientists in the world for the autonomous driving, who is teaching Uber cars, not only with cameras and gps data, but also ubiquitous data from radars and lidars to increase the abilities of artificial networks.
I’ll end with this last example, that I really like, because these are artificial networks that not only learned how to interact with humans, but they learn to imagine and even have hallucinations, as we say in our jargon.
These networks are called GAN, Generative Adversarial Networks, that take raw input data, like the ones you see at the top, that are tridimensional depth data, like when a blind person is trying to recognize a face, touching our three-dimensionality. And this network tries to imagine and create the image, with colors, grey levels, of the person it is working on.
This can be used, for example,
to detect people in the dark, or where the light isn’t enough.
Obviously, these are also the networks that create fake news, because they start from data, the network above tries to make up a reality, the network below tries to understand if this reality is positive or not, similar to the real one or not.
The first one learns to trick the second, the second one tries to unmask the first. And by learning together in synergy, improving their intelligence.
Here is all that we are working on, that is being done in the world of AI, in order to show you that among all the things that AI is doing, it’s also working on trying to interact more and more with humans, finding a strong, positive contact with humans, allowing us to work together.
I leave you with this image, another one that I really like and that you might all know.
When William talks about the invention of eyeglasses, he says that glasses are not the devil’s fruit but the result of science of men.
This is the bright side that will follow us for many years.
Thank you.
(Applauses)
2019: It's been just over ten years since social networks started to pervade our society, becoming part of our daily lives.
Ten years when almost everything has changed.
Today, when we talk about the Internet, we often ask ourselves if it is making us more haters, more isolated, more uninformed, or more polarized.
Every day, the news tells us about a world in which people argue on social media: people are no longer able to talk without insulting each other.
And so we fail to understand what's really going on.
But, is it really like that?
We often wonder if the Internet is making us more haters, but maybe we should start asking ourselves different questions to truly understand what's going on with platforms,
and whether the platforms, that were first meant to unite us are indeed responsible for a deep social fragmentation.
How do we understand the reasons for this?
A provocation: What if, instead of asking ourselves if social networks only make us more haters or less informed, we start wondering if they are making us more unequal?
By embracing this perspective and talking about inequality, perhaps we will find new answers.
In the context of Internet, talking about inequality means talking not only about online hatred and disinformation, but also about something that is often neglected: data.
And data are at the core of the digital society.
Data run, in some way, the inner machinery of our society.
And when we talk about data and inequality, we need to ask ourselves who owns this data and especially what their goals are.
One of the first things we have to consider, when we talk about data, is, who owns them? And also try to break some spells about data.
We spontaneously think that data is representative of people and at school they teach us that mathematics is not an opinion.
So, we may think that the approaches that aggregate and leverage this data - that is, the algorithms that power all the applications we use - are neutral.
But they are not: Technology isn't neutral, and that's something we have to keep in mind.
Technology is a human product: Behind every algorithm, behind every application, behind every platform there is a human being, with her own vision of the world, with her own ideas and objectives.
And this is the first step to adopt critical thinking towards all the platforms we use.
Raise your hand if you have never heard the sentence: "If the service is free, you are the product."
It's well known that the platforms developed in the Silicon Valley are huge private monopolies that own our data and have the toolbox to dig into them.
And they do this for profit, to create more and more personalized advertisements.
Nowadays, these platforms are part of our society: we use them for everything, from booking a car or a restaurant to keeping an eye on our health, finding a partner - but we still struggle to understand how these algorithms work. And above all, where our data are stored and who are they shared with.
When I talk about these things - and I do it often, in fact, my friends hate me by now - one of the things I am asked about is: But, why should I care about that?
At the end of the day, I don't mind personalized advertisements.
I know what to buy, or in any case, I don't care about my privacy because I have nothing to hide.
When we talk about privacy, first of all, we have to think about privacy as a human right.
As such, privacy had never something to do with having something to hide, but rather with the freedom of having our own private space, free from external interference, both private and public. But above all, I believe that when we talk about privacy today, in 2019, we must think of privacy as a collective right, not just an individual one.
Let me give you an example:
Algorithms do not aggregate data of individuals but rather groups, thus they are very often able to know our soft spots - and use them for profit.
Today, algorithms are already used to make social decisions:
In the USA and in Australia, for example, algorithms are already used to decide whether a person deserves a job, whether she is a potential criminal, or if she deserves a mortgage.
These examples teach us that, as we have already said, algorithms are not neutral.
If an algorithm is not neutral, it will harness the prejudices embedded in our society and mimic them,
thus amplifying injustice. There are very famous cases in the US - like the one you see here - where an algorithm was used to determine whether a person was a potential criminal. This algorithm had a bias against blacks. Another example is the algorithm used by Amazon that employed Artificial Intelligence to decide whether a person could be safely hired. This algorithm had a bias against women.
Why? Because if our society tells us that blacks are more likely to be criminals than whites, the algorithm will say the same thing. If the society tells us that women are less likely than men to do leadership work, the algorithm will say the same thing.
That's why it's a collective problem: Because the algorithm reproduces the dominant culture.
But I want to give you another example: There are cases, such as Cambridge Analytica or the Snowden case, that have taught us that, more often than we think, platforms share our data not only with other companies, but also with other states and governments, that leverage it to know our movements, our conversations - to actually spy on us.
Indeed, this is very problematic.
Let me give you another example: I am an activist. I am one of those who, by definition, activists are those who question power and demand social change. For us, surveillance means control.
This has already happened in France, not far from us, when in 2015, after the terrorist attacks, when the state of emergency was declared the French government started to follow and spy on environmental activists, preventing them from demonstrating.
In this sense, the right to privacy is linked to other rights, such as the right to demonstrate, the right to organize, the freedom of expression.
These are all rights that have to do with "us": We are not talking just about my data, we are talking about our data.
So when we talk about the digital society, I think it is very important that we start to see ourselves as members of a community, and we start to think of our data as a common good.
Plus, we should start a conversation in which we, as a society, decide what is private and what is public.
We should begin to question our role as citizens consumers who are not allowed to choose - only private companies can - the terms and conditions of our social participation, of our online visibility, of the content we consume - which contribute to create our vision of the world.
We really need to start a whole new debate to set ethical rules and limits.
In this regard, we must learn from the past. Consider the history of oil, for example. Leveraging this resource allowed us to develop our society in a remarkable way.
But perhaps we should have thought earlier on how to regulate oil companies, not in the midst of the climate change crisis.
Well, we cannot let the same thing happen with the Internet.
We have to set limits.
After I scared you, however, I would like to give you some hope by telling you about my story as an activist.
I have always been intrigued by these issues: At first, I studied them at the university, then I tried to bring my knowledge outside of the university.
The first thing I learned was, in order to understand how to solve problems on the Internet, we must always talk about the intersection between society, technology, politics, and economics.
We cannot trust those who only offer us technical or legal solutions.
About a year ago, I became the face of a campaign called "Violated Intimacy" - I am still excited to mention it because in a few months we turned a petition on change.org into an actual law.
How was this done?
First of all, we assembled a very large network of experts - a step that nowadays is often neglected.
In our campaign on "revenge porn" - the non-consensual sharing of intimate material - we needed to consult not only lawyers, not only politicians, but also computer scientists, psychologists, sociologists.
We built a grassroot network to make sure these problems are approached with the right foot.
This was incredibly important because in our journey, some proposed alternative solutions, such as the law to prohibit "sexting" - the exchange of intimate videos and photos between partners - thus effectively censoring citizens' freedom of expression. And there were those who proposed technical solutions: "Let's implement fingerprinting! Let's build a large online database where to profile all the photos. In this way, we will be able to remove the material quicker!" - creating other problems related to the surveillance of citizens.
This example highlights the fact that, when we talk about online problems, we have to observe them from a social and cultural perspective. We should acknowledge, much like revenge porn is not born with the Internet, but a form of that was already present in society - the one against women.
Likewise, we won't "solve" online hatred just by registering citizens on the platforms using their IDs nor will we solve the problem of online monopolies by using new technologies such as the blockchain - because there is no deterministic effect of the use of a technology. We must think outside the box.
To this aim, we can do two things.
First, we should educate ourselves:
We need to know our rights to protect them;
we have to talk about the problem of online monopolies that centralize all the power on the Internet; we have to talk about how online platforms work; we have to start leaning in, and claim what we want.
And we have to reconsider what is private and what is public.
The other thing is to unite and make activism.
I like to quote Greta Thunberg, in cases like this, who says: "Activism Works."
And it actually does!
Maybe, by looking at the world through an individualistic gaze that we've become so accustomed to adopt we have forgotten the strength of many people who team up for the same cause.
But the truth is, if we want social change, we have to rethink about ourselves.
When we talk about digital rights, we are talking about the future of our democracy.
We have to ask ourselves how we can bridge the huge social gap created by social media platforms, and how to solve online hatred.
It will be a very long process, but I think it's the only thing we can do.
Also, we need to talk about the potential of the Internet - not just the problems it causes.
The Internet, not only in my case but also in other contexts, is responsible for the expansion of social movements in the world, from #MeToo to #FridayForFuture.
It has become a tool for people to gather online. At the same time, it can create a protected space for discriminated communities to talk to each other.
So, let's believe that the Internet can help us: but first, let's ask ourselves where we are going.
We are at a crucial moment in history - we absolutely have to start working together.
One of the things I learned this year is that people will always tell you that nothing ever changes, that we can't do anything, that things are the way they are.
But my message for you today is that it is not true.
I started from scratch, and today I'm here.
And it was only because I believed it.
So, let's believe in our rights, let's believe in our freedom, and let's believe in change.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Technology.
We are surrounded by technology.
The computer has become our travel companion, the smartphone our appendix, we use hyper-technological cars.
If we have to go somewhere, we type the address on our smartphone or satnav because we know that the GPS will take us wherever we want.
If we have to do a check-up at the hospital, we do not worry if they lie us down on a bed and slide us into a tunnel for a CT scan, or an MRI scan, or a nuclear medicine exam.
Technology.
How did we get there?
Technology submerges us with images.
We live with images, we are bombarded with images, we remember things through images, we develop concepts with images.
2016 is the year of gravitational waves.
The infinitely large.
Who has never seen the beautiful images, the pictures, of the Hubble telescope, which shows us the depth of the universe?
Those images fascinate us because they talk about who we are, where we come from, where we go.
The infinitely large.
In 2012 we all learned about the Higgs boson and we saw the images of the infinitely small, of particle accelerators like the LHC, where they shoot 40 million photographs per second with tens of billions of pixels, to show us what happens when two protons collide originating a Higgs boson.
The infinitely small.
And then mankind.
On December 23, 1895 the rector of a German university publishes the first X-ray in history, an X-ray of his wife's hand.
On December 23, 1895
modern medicine was born. Until then, to look inside your belly, they opened it with a scalpel; from that date, they make you an X-ray.
In 70 years we have been able to turn an X-ray tube all around the patient to cut her in many slices, that combined together make us see the inside of her body as if it were open in front of us.
The CT scan.
Between the 30s and the 40s, more or less, another branch of diagnostic medicine was born: nuclear medicine.
That branch of diagnostics that wants to see how your body works and not how it is done.
We have turned patients into radioactive sources to discover their hotspots, that is, those areas that are particularly active that may signal that something is wrong.
The image you see behind me it is not made up, it is a CT scan of a patient together with a nuclear medicine exam: a positron emission tomography.
Technology and images.
How did we get there?
How did we get to this level?
We have observed nature, we have understood how it works and we have used it.
What glasses did we have to build to be able to observe the infinitely large, the infinitely small, all that surrounds us, and also what is inside us?
The glasses we have built, and we will continue to build, to observe nature are called "particle detectors."
I did not mispronounce the word.
A "detector" is the person who calls you to know how many times you drink milk in a week.
The English word "detector" derives from the Latin "detegere": remove the cover, uncover, remove the blanket, remove the bed sheet.
Detectors make us remove the veil from nature to understand how it works.
I am a physicist who develops particle detectors.
The funny thing is that to observe the infinitely large, the infinitely small and all that surrounds us, we use the same detectors, based on the same principles.
There are incredibly different ones.
Some are as small as the nail of my little finger, like those that are in your smartphones when you take a picture or those who went on Mars with the robots to understand the composition of the rocks on Mars.
There are also bigger ones, like the ones I put on the AGILE satellite, which is orbiting at 500 km above the ground.
Tiles of ten by ten centimeters that filled a cube of one meter per side.
There are huge ones, such as those for studying the infinitely small, such as the ones of the LHC.
CMS weighs 7.000 tons, ATLAS is 20 x 20 x 30 cubic meters.
It is incredible, but to study the infinitely small, we build things that are larger and more complex.
Some of them are extra large.
The ones used to study rare particles that come from the depth of the universe or those that are particularly elusive.
Every second, each of us is crossed by several billion neutrinos without realizing it.
One who studies neutrinos needs large quantities of materials.
Afterwards, she uses the Earth's inter-atmosphere, or fills a mine with water, or uses the light that neutrinos emit when they interact with sea water.
Particle detectors: our glasses.
But that's not enough, because, if you think about it, detectors do not make us see a phenomenon, they show us a product.
You do not see my vocal cords, you hear the sound they emit.
We do not see the nuclei of the sun that merge, we see the light that comes from that fusion.
We do not see two protons colliding at the LHC, we see the tens of millions of electronic channels that light up and tell us what happened when the two protons collided.
Particle detectors show us a product, we have to deduce the phenomenon from the product.
We must frame it in a theory and make predictions, because if a theory cannot predict, it is only a sandcastle in our mind.
Verify the predictions, if they work, good; otherwise we start from scratch.
This was our winning recipe.
Putting together the observations done with the detectors with a theory which we devise with our intuition and our creativity.
In this way, we tell the story of Nature.
Do you want some examples?
We all know the Big Bang.
We all know that 13.7 billion years ago there was an "explosion," in which an immense amount of energy -- at a very high temperature of 10 to the 32 degrees Kelvin -- was transformed into matter.
First the constituents of elementary particles, then the elementary particles themselves.
Protons were born a microsecond after the Big Bang, 10 to the -6 seconds.
We, and everything around us, are made of those protons. We are made of the protons of the Big Bang.
Then all the other elementary particles: atoms, molecules, and the elements; gradually up to 13.7 billion years later.
The universe -- as we have seen it through the Hubble telescope -- definitely colder than when it was born.
This is a beautiful theory, but theories need confirmations.
This theory has had many proofs; I'll tell you one that I find terrific.
In the 50s in New Jersey, USA -- as part of a telecommunication project -- someone build an antenna, known as Holmdel Antenna.
We are talking about telecommunications, radio waves, we all know them.
We all know what a radio antenna is.
The name of the project was Echo, there were satellites communicating.
When the project was over, the Bell Telephone Labs and two physicists, Robert Wilson and Arno Penzias, decided to modify the antenna to pick up radio waves from the depths of the Milky Way.
In 1964 they switch it on, they turn it in all directions and they keep hearing an annoying ticking.
So, they pick up the phone and call Princeton, where there was Robert Dickie, one of the major theorists of the Big Bang theory.
He answers, puts down the phone, turns to his employees and says, "They beat us on time," takes his car and sets off towards them.
Robert Wilson and Arno Penzias discovered the "cosmic background radiation."
When the universe celebrated its 380th thousand birthday, more or less, it has become transparent.
At that point, the light was able to pass through.
The cosmic background radiation is the candle that the universe has blown when it turned 380 thousand years, 13.7 billion years ago.
The cosmic background radiation is isotropic, uniform in all directions.
Think of it as a stretched cloth.
In reality it has very small fluctuations.
The image you see behind me, is the image collected by NASA's WMAP satellite.
The yellow and red dots are the fluctuations of the cosmic background radiation.
The yellow and red dots are the seeds of today's stars and galaxies.
We are talking about radio waves, it is not rocket science.
The glasses we have built to measure them allow us to read a book that is 13.7 billion year-old.
Unbelievable!
At the beginning of the 1900s, physicists started to panic, because they could not understand how light and matter talk to each other.
Light is a wave that infinitely pervades space.
Matter is made of atoms which are infinitely small particles.
How does this wave instantly interact with the electrons that are in the atoms of my retina so that I am able to see?
Among other things, the eyes are the oldest example of particle detectors, made in billions of pairs more or less working.
So what do they understand?
The observations were correct, but the interpretation was not.
The combination is the victory, the winning recipe.
They understand that the observations are correct and they say, "It means that when we move towards the microworld, the infinitely small, the laws we have written to frame what we observe in our surroundings do not work so well, we have to change them."
Because in the microworld the waves are particles and the particles are waves.
Think how hard having this intuition is -- before formalizing it.
Waves are particles and particles are waves.
This is where our technology comes from.
Having this intuition, formalizing it, and understanding that nature really works like that has led us to develop more powerful glasses.
Do you want an example?
You have all used an optical microscope: a light source, the sample, and a lens.
And you look at your sample in two dimensions.
But, if I replace the light source with electrons, I tear them apart from atoms like marbles -- because marbles are; I accelerate them with a potential difference -- because marbles are; and finally I send them to collide against the sample.
When they arrive, they get there with some energy so they behave like a beam of light with a much smaller wavelength than visible light.
This means that I see smaller samples.
But they still remain marbles charged with energy.
And that means that I can easily scan and get the third dimension.
This is the principle behind the scanning electron microscope.
The images it produces are like the one you see behind me, which is the image of a larva's mouth.
Glasses.
Particle detectors.
But what's even more fascinating is that the detectors we use to see the electrons interacting with the mouth of the larva are the same ones that are in smartphones to take pictures, they are the same ones that went to Mars, they are the same ones we use to see the protons collide to reconstruct their traces, they are the same ones that I installed on the AGILE satellite that is traveling at 500 km of altitude in equatorial orbit observing the depth of the universe.
As WMAP of NASA, but with a different wavelength: gamma rays.
Think about it, it's like reading the book of the universe written in another language.
They are part of the same family as those we use to make you a CT scan.
They are detectors that allow us to study and preserve the past.
In 1970, they discovered the En-Gedi Scroll.
More than a parchment it is a small pile of ash slightly bigger than a coin.
Absolutely unusable.
It could not be manipulated, it would have crumbled, and it would have been lost.
A few weeks ago, a group of US and Israeli researchers made a preclinical micro CT scan, such as those used in hospital: an X-ray tube running around the pile of ash to dissect it in many slices.
The same thing is being done in Italy for the scroll of Herculaneum.
They applied a 3D virtual unwrapping algorithm and they managed to read it, finding out that it is a copy of the Book of Leviticus.
It seems to be the oldest copy of a book of the Pentateuch ever found in a sacred ark.
Carbon-14 dating says that it dates back to the third-fourth century.
Detectors, glasses, and our own interpretation have enabled us to understand that nature is a very complicated puzzle with many pieces.
Someone has guessed the design, someone has placed some pieces, someone has discovered new ones.
What is certain is that the ultimate goal for those like me who develop particle detectors, and for all who analyze the incredible amount of data that these detectors collect, is still the same: to improve the life of mankind at 360 degrees.
Paraphrasing Einstein, between your diagrams, your experiments, and research labs remember mankind, and forget the rest.
What we do, we do it for our and your future.
What we do, we do it for the future of mankind.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good afternoon everybody.
Thank you Danilo for your introduction, where you said most of the things I meant to talk about now. That's fantastic.
So here's my first point: Look at this painting by the Indonesian artist Farhan Siki, who artistically conveys the message that we are increasingly
surrounded by brands.
Every product, every service, needs, in order to stand out from the throng, to carefully define its identity, and create a symbol to represent it, escaping the situation the protagonist finds himself in in Pirandello's novel 'One, No one and One Hundred Thousand', the title of which lends itself to my presentation.
Let's consider the travel sector, that of cities and countries.
It's a very competitive sector
since there is a wholesale increase
in numbers of all types of tourists.
China, for example, is now part of this tourist market.
Investment is sought and needed.
International mobility has increased.
So, there's great competition between countries and cities: Either tourists come to my city or they go to yours;
students come to me or go to you;
businessmen come to me or go to you.
So, this high competition is driven forward by cities emphasizing not only the tangible, such as monuments, geographical location, and the urban structure, but also the intangible, such as 'the feeling' a city provokes, or the spirit of a city. Countries and cities all over the world are attempting to define their own unique identity, distinct from others, using graphic symbols that will make them stand out. However, standing out from the rest is very difficult. It seems to me that Poland, the Philippines, the Maldives, Oman, Vietnam, and Turkey, don't have much in common, yet the logos they have chosen
are likely to overlap and become confused with each other. What cities are doing, is trying to define their own identity.
For a year starting October 2012, the city of Bologna carried out quantitative and qualitative research on the perception of its identity.
These, for example, are from tweets and text messages which are about Bologna, verbs which have been used most.
This on the other hand shows the relationship between the word “road” and some concepts that have been identified by applying big data analysis to these online texts about Bologna.
This research has established that Bologna is perceived as being a real city, that's how it's perceived, especially when compared to nearby cities like Florence or Venice, which are perceived by tourists as kinds of open-air museums. Bologna is also perceived as a city where one does not feel impelled to follow certain itineraries, unlike in the cities of mass tourism
with must-see attractions,
since none of the charms of Bologna outshines another. Bologna is also considered a city
that offers the freedom to do whatever you feel like.
So, these are some of the research results that have been adopted as the defining features of the city. But we know that in order to communicate something, we first need to have a logo.
Does a logo solve communication problems?
It would be stupid to say it would solve communication problems. The logo acts as a business card. You hand out your business card.
You could instead each time take out a notebook, write on a piece of paper, which is then lost etc etc.
It's a matter of effectiveness.
However, a creative and effective logo must have a number of characteristics.
First of all it must be unique and distinguishing, so that it can express elements of the 'deep identity' a city has with a symbol of quality that is capable of attracting attention and which is 'out of the box', that is, a symbol conceived by lateral thinking and capable, even if it may seem ironic, of passing the t-shirt test.
Danilo already anticipated the t-shirt test, obviously. When we think of the t-shirt test, we think of this logo: I Love New York, from 1976.
Glaser designed this logo. It became a unique global icon, or perhaps not, as it has been taken and copied by so many cities, but every time we see the logo copied, we always think of New York.
During the past decade cities
have been asking themselves how to find a logo that expresses the multifacetedness of a city's features and its uniqueness in one.
And these are some of the solutions. For example, the claim 'I Amsterdam' has been made using the word Amsterdam.
The claim Be Berlin.
The Brandenburg Gate, is quite rigid and, in itself, cannot express the diversities of the city. So it was made more dynamic by inviting
the viewer to join in actively. In order to express the openness of the city, Copenhagen decided to use the adjective 'open' inside the word Copenhagen and changed the colours of the design according to the concepts that describe Copenhagen in that given moment.
On the right you can see Singapore's new logo which incorporates the urban topography in different colours with these diamond-shapes that characterize the dynamicism of the city-state.
The logo of Bahrain
moves instead away from rigid logos.
It is a logo that changes shape and colour
according to the sectors
it wishes to represent. We can also see that each logo has logos that copy or refer to it.
Melbourne has chosen
an attractive geometric M which changes in shape, content and colour depending on the urban narrative
it wants to present. Until the Bologna logo was created, the most dynamic one was that of Nordkyn, an area made up of two cities in the north of Norway, where the claim is: It's the place where nature rules.
How could such a concept be conveyed?
If you visit the site visitnordkyn.com, you will see that this logo changes shape and colour as the wind and temperature conditions change in the area.
The city of Bologna, which is relatively little known outside of Italy, thought that it should also address the identity issue, as we said, and decided to establish its own logo.
It set up an international competition which was won by Matteo Bartoli and Michele Pastore, who proposed a very different solution
from the usual solutions we've already seen.
A solution that creates an actual alphabet of graphic symbols, as Danilo anticipated.
We are now seeing a sequence of logos that are all different, but all clearly recall a common theme, as they belong to the same family.
These logos are the result of an idea that expresses plural identities differently.
What is a city like Bologna?
It is a combination of the tangible and intangible that makes this city what it is.
Bologna is the combination of all those features. But here's the problem: How can we translate all those features of the city into a visual symbol?
The project that won the international competition began by deciding on five archetypes, five figures from the memory and history of Bologna:
This map from 1625; this coat of arms located on one of its gateways; a brickwork pattern in part
of the basilica of Santo Stefano;
and finally the Rocchetta Mattei, not actually in the city but within its metropolitan area.
These geometric shapes led to the creation of an alphabet recalling these five historical elements.
Here you see the letters.
They are the letters of a new alphabet, which were proposed to describe a city, in this case the city of Bologna.
Thus a new way of writing was created. The shapes are concentrically placed, and by overlapping each other they generate complex shapes. The resulting graphic symbol is the overlaying of the graphic symbols of each letter.
Explaining this writing system is harder
than seeing and writing. The rules are simple. The letters 'A', 'R', 'T', 'E' form the graphic symbol for 'arte' [art]. 'S' 'A' 'N' 'L' 'U' 'C' 'A' form the graphic symbol for San Luca. Participation, bicycle -
You can thus express any character of the city with the claim 'is Bologna' below, giving it a graphic sign that can change again and again.
The system conceived becomes a tool to coordinate all the different communications from all the city's structures: public structures, private structures, associations and individuals. These are some of the logos that have been created. City of Food 'is Bologna', presented some weeks ago in New York, inside which are all the initiatives that relate to food.
Some themes, such as Sustainability, Culture and Growth include subthemes that maintain the same colours - as in this case, culture as economic development - but change the graphic symbol depending on the word/concept they represent.
Bologna made a choice that no one had ever made before, a brave and risky one.
If no one had ever done it, it could be no one had ever thought of it, it could be it wouldn't work like this. It was a choice guided
by lateral thinking:
To create an alphabet of graphic symbols that could replace ordinary logos.
These are some of the logos created in recent months, which express the different natures of the city of Bologna.
For sure it would have been easier, among the 500 international proposals sent in to the competition, to select a logo, say the two towers 'is Bologna', tortellini 'is Bologna', porticoes 'is Bologna'.
But it would repeat the traditional ways of making static representational logos, which age quickly and do not express the complexity, plurality and multiplicity of the city's characteristics.
This logo has another characteristic.
Here it is.
The characteristic is to have - it's already been anticipated, so it won't surprise you, since Danilo told you all already - there is currently a beta version where you can all go, - it's a participatory, inclusive site - to write your name or a concept, associate it with Bologna and see its graphic representation.
To wrap up, let us go back
to Pirandello's novel, 'One, No-One and One Hundred Thousand'. Not sure how many of you know this beautiful novel written in the mid-1920s. Gengè, the main character in the novel, experiences
contemporaneous identity crisis.
He realizes that the image he has of himself does not correspond to the image others have of him.
Gengè feels the same things that cities feel.
Cities feel that the image they have of themselves does not correspond to the image others have of them.
So what have cities done so as not to feel like Gengè,
who perceived himself as one, one hundred thousand, and therefore, no one at the same time?
Cities have made some attempts, like the visionary one undertaken by Bologna, to be simultaneously one hundred thousand and one, but possibly not: no city at all.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Today I'm going to talk to you about second identity.
Second identity, second me, second myself.
I will try to cover this, and I'll first consider
the impact of technological innovations that are totally disrupting our personal life, economic life, political life, education, social relationships, interpersonal ones - everything.
What is the second identity?
It has never existed before, this is the real deal. We have never had a second identity before.
Today, if I have to compare my generation and the youngsters, kids who are 15, 20, 25 years old today, I think that the real difference is the impact of technology.
It's negative for me, for us: we are not very good, you are much smarter at it than I am.
But we had an advantage: every stupid thing we did left basically no trace. Every stupid thing you do now, instead, leaves a trace somewhere. you may lose your job today for a stupid thing you posted years ago, unbeknownst of future consequences.
When I did, as a young boy, my first job interviews, I had my face, my nice chatting gab, three lines in a resume that accounted for my past experience.
And that was it.
I could bamboozle my counterpart without any problems.
When you talk now to your potential employer, he will be able to inspect, in depth, everything about you.
So thanks to new technologies, we have a second identity now, a second ourself.
And our whole life has been traced back in an unprecedented way.
So what does it mean to have a second identity?
First of all it means this.
It means that this, our smartphone, that has sticked around us for many years now, but in the last few years, a smartphone has become much more than it used to.
A few years ago, we could have defined it a fairly neutral tool, it was like air conditioning, even like a car, maybe.
We could even compare it to any other household appliance.
This is a second identity today.
It is my second identity, it is your second identity, in the phone you hold in your pocket.
Why am I saying this?
Because this smartphone encompasses everything about me. All the people I've talked to, all the web sites I've visited.
Here you have a complete toolset to understand who I am, if I drive properly or not. where I currently am, where I've been in a given moment. Every single information about myself is contained in this smartphone.
So those who control the smartphone will control my actions, thoughts, taste.
So much so that sometimes one goes here and find out, it knows you better than you know yourself.
And ultimately, this stores everything.
This is both fascinating and terrifying: there has never been, in the past, that could enshrine my full identity, individuality.
In the past, I was just me; now it's me and this second identity,
because this thing recapitulates and stores everything about me.
It is outside of my body, and other people can sneek into it much more than one can enter into my flesh and blood person, my first real identity.
What does "someone can enter here" mean?
It means that these smartphones used to be produced in Europe: most of us had a Nokia phone, there used to be a EU-driven technology. That's no longer the case.
Essentially, the phones you have in your pocket now are either made in the United States, in China or Taiwan.
So it means that all our data are stored far away from home. And those who control these smartphones can also control our second identity.
In my opinion, future wars won't be fought for the same reasons we fough them in the past, like oil, money, borders.
Wars will mostly be triggered, I think, to decide who's going to use, exploit, own these data.
Ultimately, in fact, these data will allow you to win electoral campaigns, hijack markets, run marketing campaigns that are much more effective, monitor people's tastes, blackmail companies, nations and individuals.
So our second identity, the second myself, becomes the real question to spot lights on, and tackle from an economic, social, philosophical point of view. to design the rules and practises the future will run on.
And this second identity is in here,
and the technology that controls my second identity has totally slipped from EU's hands.
Smartphones are now either American, or Chinese, or Korean.
We also had European phones, a few years ago; now it's basically nothing.
So we have lost this technology game, and my data - it's a U.S. phone - will be stored somewhere in Cupertino, in California,
Who handles these data? Who owns them? Who takes advantage of them?
I don't know.
And quite honestly, I don't have any trust that those who can access my data - if they have to decide whether to protect my personal identity or pull some profits from this data, they'll strongly be tempted to opt for the second possibility.
There have already been many egregious cases: like Cambridge Analytica and Facebook: a case of unprecedented gravity now almost completely silenced, media no longer cover it, but clearly points out where this may lead to.
So, United States and Asia on one hand; and us Europeans, who lost the game of this technology.
What does this mean?
How do United States approach this topic?
When we talk about US regulations in this field, there's a strong feeling that competitiveness in the hi-tech sector must come before everything else.
Things like the protection of privacy, the ownership of personal data - seemingly count much less.
This is at least my impression,
and I don't like this approach at all.
If I have a look at Asia, at the other kind of model championed by China, they seem to have a totally different approach; an almost opposite one: the exploitation of data, of this second identity-related data to quickly, easily seep into our first identity.
Which is - or at least is supposed to be - inviolable but can be reached through this second one.
And the State can have a strong interest, through these data, to control who I am, what my taste, my behaviour is.
And here we are at a fundamental jucture. for our future life.
I think that governments have to be very clear. They have to fine us if we do something wrong, if we break one of it's rules: but I don't think the State should interfere regardless of my respect of the rules.
My person is inviolable, even the second one now stored in my mobile phone.
If this is the case, then how can we win this war of influence about who's going to impose rules that can protect us from the rush, the frenzy activity of these hi-tech giants?
Two old fashioned words come back to my mind now: "Politics" and "European Union".
Why politics, and why European Union?
Because it is clear that either politics will regulate this, or an unleashed market will.
Or, simply, the State will impose its will.
And I'm terrified, frankly.
I have goosebumps at the idea that the market regulates itself on this, because our fundamental individual rights are at stake.
Politics has to call the shots.
Politics, which is us: not the market, not high tech companies.
And I think, it'd be even better for them to have unavoidable rules so they're not tempted to do wrong.
And then we have the European Union.
Why European Union?
Because, as I said before, it feels like the other world's giants are taking a different path.
We, the Europeans, either Swdish, Portuguese, Italians, French or German, share a consistent philosophy, one that professes the inviolability of the individual identity.
And this should be ported, in an ethical, democratic and philosophical transition, on this new second identity too.
Because my person is there: classified, stored, recapitulated, but it's there, and it is as inviolable as my real, true, first identity is.
And if we had 28 Brexits in Europe, a disintegration of the continent - we just could not influence the world with our philosophy, which is exquisitely ours, as I said before, These are our rules, this is our juridical civilization.
That's why a unified European Union has now another mission,
a different, much more committing one; one that's also more important than the typical mission we used to assign it.
The European Union has always been an engine for peace, has been trying to tear down barriers, frontiers, borders, to find new rules for living together for development, to represent Europe all across the world.
But now there's much more at stake: the future of our people.
And this is not science fiction: when we talk about these things, it always sounds like science fiction.
But just a couple of years ago, I would have never covered this topic. Now, in my opinion, that's the key issue of our times.
And be careful: this second identity is the first second identity we know.
Perhaps, in a couple of years time, we might be tempted - not me, maybe, I'm not an expert, but someone else - to talk about another second identity, which may be AI, machine intelligences, what machines can replace human beings at.
Another second me.
What rules will this AI act upon?
Here, we can understand what this evolution leads to.
Again, politics has to call the shots, not the market. Not even the technology, for that matter. In the meanwhile, European Union has to impose itself, and the others, with its rules of juridical civilization centered around personal inviolability.
In short, and wrapping up, if everything I've have said up to now makes sense, and it is true, and has some relevance in this transition towards a hypertechnlogical future, then politics has to play a decisive role. and we can't dismiss it like we do now. The European Union has to make a difference.
I mean, a united European Union,
not a scattered one.
A united union.
I think of my future, I think of the future of my children, and I think, we can all share the same line of thinking here. I don't want to have a future where I am forced to decide whether I am a Chinese or a US colony - I don't like it.
I like Americans, I like the Chinese, but I want to be Italian and I want to be European.
We can't be forced into either a Chinese or a U.S. colonization.
I want to have the opportunity to believe in my values. And when I say values, I say the inviolability of the person.
To me, this means to be Italian and European.
Only with a strong policy, and a unified European Union, we will deal with this topics
and be able to understand -myself and all of us- that the inviolability of the person is at the center of everything that we do.
Once upon a time there was a child with no dreams.
he lived in a family, a family like any other with its strengths and its weaknesses.
But a family nonetheless.
He had a brother, a big brother, a little older than him, who used to protect him.
He had friends, he went to daycare, he was often with the blue team.
He was actually part of the pink team, but he was always getting into trouble and he'd be sent to the blue team.
There he met the boy who soon became his best friend.
He still is his best friend.
This child had no aims, no dreams. He played soccer, he was quite good but it was tough.
He even won the provincial championship, but he had no dreams.
He grew, he finished school.
School was a prison for him.
He was a free spirit.
He wanted to get out of school, he wanted to live his life.
But he continued to be aimless.
Until he joined the army
twelve months and ten days.
But it got into his way again. He was about to sign, he wanted to stay. But this word "inside" was too much for him, he couldn't help it.
And so he left, and went to work, he tried fifteen different jobs. From butcher to electrician, from bricklayer to mailman,
he tried everything, with a desire to be successful.
But as he had no dreams, he didn't know where.
One day he had an idea: to become a tourist entertainer.
It would be nice to be a tourist entertainer!
But someone told him, "But... you have to know English!"
And so he went to London at the age of 25 and worked in a French restaurant to learn English.
And he did. He started to work as a dish-washer, soon he was promoted to to kitchen manager, because up there, if you are good...
they let you know. And then at 25, as he was already 25, this kid who has this idea about becoming an entertainer said, "Well I am 25 and am old: perhaps I better go home and find myself a job and that I go back to doing what I always did."
And so he went home and he found his job. And he set aside his dream.
Then one day someone asked him, "What do you want to do when you grow up?"
And the "child" answered, "I want to be a tourist entertainer!"
"Ah, and why don't you do it?"
"Well...because..." and he found excuses.
And then he asked him, "OK, but what do you want to do when you grow up?"
And the child answered, A tourist entertainer.
"Oh, so why don't you?"
"Oh... because..." giving a thousand excuses.
Then he asked him, "Ok, but what do you want to do when you grow up?"
So the child said, "Maybe you didn't get it: the tourist entertainer."
She looked at him and said, "Perhaps you didn't get it!"
These words touched him.
He really did go and became a tourist entertainer, he left all.
Everything that meant security, staying at home, parents, family, friends.
And he got a job in a resort in Metaponto in Basilicata earning 350 Euros a month.
Not a single person told him,
"Good job, excellent choice!", imagine...
And in this village, this grown-up kid - as he was called by another child - met some special people: Giorgino, Beppe, Bart and Roberta The person who then became his wife.
And thanks to her he moved to Trentino. He worked, as a tourist entertainer he worked in hotels, in open air squares, he did raffles, he sang the karaoke,
and then he discovered magic and everything changed.
The dream was really becoming true.
He was working as a tourist entertainer, but he wanted to do even more, he wanted to talk to people, he wanted to communicate with people.
He wanted to communicate through laughter, so he started studying.
He studied and he started to do things that were a bit strange, he dressed oddly, he clowned around, he did a bit of everything.
That was the opportunity that he gave himself.
He allowed himself - which is something not everyone knows how to do - he allowed himself to have fun, and helped others have fun.
And so one day, November 22nd , 2014 this child fulfilled his dream: he was on a stage in Trento at a [TEDx] in order to do a magic trick to communicate with people and what I want to do now is this: communicate with you through a thread.
It is a thread I define... the thread of the good life.
So here it is, the good life: beautiful, perfect, straight.
Look! How wonderful.
Every now and then ups and down happen
but if one is strong then life becomes straight once more
but to have it like this we need special ingredients ingredients called values.
We saw them earlier during the story about that boy, that was me, Walter Klinkon, and I expressed some values as I have seen it is important to guard values.
I will name a few just because I think it is important.
the first one which is the value of love.
And others like honesty and respect.
We could name another favorite of mine that is called enthusiasm.
I define enthusiasm as a value that gives us the chance to feed all other values.
when things start to fall apart.
Another one that came out just recently, is called permission.
Sometimes you don't need to knock on the door, but just come in!
Because if you allow yourself to do things you will get to where you never thought you could.
There are many values.
Important: values aren't just a name one must give them a meaning and behaviours so that value really has a meaning.
One particular value I like is change.
It's particular in that for some it is a value, for me it's phenomenal.
What does change mean?
Change means that... if you have lived one way all your life, when you make a change you will add something to it and so from a simple change, or substitution, I feel that change means adding, adding to my life.
Yes, what happened in 2003 I was a butcher in Trieste in Via Pirano 7. In a small supermarket with my brother.
He used to say phenomenal words, he'd say, "To have things you've never had you must do things you've never done"
Those words were really beautiful but I didn't know what to do with them until I met the person who asked "What do you want to do when you grow up?"
And so I made a decision, courage and decision.
I left everything and became a tourist entertainer.
And today I am a magician, but I am also an illusionist.
I showed you many lovely words... this is what happens on TV nowadays.
They show you things
but they just stay as they are.
I want to do something more.
I won't leave words to float chaotically, as we adults are really good at doing, because we grow up and we forget what we really wanted.
We take all the lovely words and we put them here and we leave them suspended yes, I will do that tomorrow and so on. The day after tomorrow, in a month, in a year,
and a whole life goes by and what you really wanted to do gets left behind, and so this evening I won't leave anything suspended.
I will leave you with the reminder to take all the important things. And to put them at the centre of your life.
Why at the centre of your life?
Because that is the right place, because I believe in what I do, I believe in others, but also in myself.
I want to thank you for giving me the opportunity I wish you all a really great life!
(Applause)
Are you ready for your "Morandi Moment"?
And I don't mean singing at the Sanremo Song Festival, or your performance in the shower, or your TikTok videos.
I'm talking about a different moment. The moment when you'll be thrown onto a crisis' stage.
When the ground will collapse right beneath your feet and the world will fall on your head.
The moment when someone will stash you against your will in the washing machine and press the spin-dryer button.
And when you’ll get out, you have lost all your points of reference.
I call it a "Morandi Moment".
Morandi moments aren't just a corporate thing: they touch us all personally, in our daily life.
That's the bad news, actually.
A friend who is terminally ill, a death in the family, the loss of a child.
And businesses as well: an unloyal employee, a market that suddenly evaporates.
Institutions, also - crises affect everyone.
There are some reactions, when we talk about these issues we all tipycally, instinctively have.
So let's do a little test together: I don't see you very well, but let's try it anyway.
While I was talking to you about these things, which are obviously unpleasant, I am sure some thoughts crossed your mind and maybe some among you gestured coarsely, also.
I don't know how many of you - you can raise your hands - have thought or gestured bad things under the chair.
A few hands.
Then perhaps this crossed
someone else's minds. (Laughter). A little less elegant, a little more gender biased. These are two gestures that a former Prime Minister of ours used quite frequently.
If this is not up your cultural status, then perhaps you fall into this third category: the one of those who keep in their pockets, or hang on the rearview mirror of their own cars, a luck horn.
You may be surprised to know that among these three attitudes one is particularly virtuous.
Not sure if anyone wants to take a guess, or I'll tell you.
The virtuous attitude is the third.
Why is it a virtuous attitude?
Because if you have decided to go to the tobacco shop, or on Amazon, and spend two euros to buy a luck horn, at least you have thought you may find yourself in trouble.
When people ask me, How do you prepare for crisis?
I always say that the starting point is to project a movie in your head.
Alas, it is never a comedy: it’s a horror movie.
And reality, be aware of that, will exceed your imagination.
The starting point of this movie is a question, a single one:
What If.?
What happens, if...? What will I do, if..?
It's a very simple question
you can ask yourself on a daily basis.
This question started to occur to me when I got married - not right away, obviously, but a littl bit later. What would I do, if something were to happen to my wife or my daughter, the two most important people in my life?
And a thought like this is very tough to deal with: there's no answer, there's no movie that can help you imagine it.
So one day, I stopped wondering what I would do, and I started asking myself what they would do without me.
I started thinking what the hours, days, weeks, months after that would be like.
And then I thought: is there anything I can do now to help them, heaven forbid, in such a dramatic, terrible and painful situation?
I took a pen, a piece of paper and I wrote my wife a message.
Then I closed it, put it in a drawer, and she perfectly knows where that envelope is.
Of course, in that letter I wrote many words of love, words that a husband speakes to his wife.
And then, there are some practical instructions.
Who is the lawyer, who is the accountant, where are the bank accounts, who's the insurance broker.
Because we often forget that in a Morandi Moment we lose all points of reference.
So I thought, that could come in handy, a small attempt to help her - again, God forbid; but you never know in life - to overcome that moment.
Why did I start dealing with crisis management?
I started asking myself, What If? not immediately, but a little bit later.
Exactly, it was on October 8th, 2001.
It's incredible how - I'm not sure if someone of you ever had a terrible moment in life, like - you really don't know where to look at, in those moments.
I remember that day like it was yesterday.
But I don't remember the whole day, nor the following days.
What's incredible is, moments like these leave Polaroids in your minds.
Still images, feelings.
So I thought, it might help to share with you today what I went through that day.
On October 8th, 2001, I got up at 6 a.m., as I had been doing for three years, since my dad passed away.
I threw myself into work.
I left the house, like I did every morning, and I crossed the street to get my cappuccino and croissant at the bar.
The city was sank in the fog, the kind we're familiar with here in the Milan area. You could have sliced it with a knife. You couldn't see across the street.
At 11:30, I was sitting at my desk at the office.
The phone rang, it was Scandinavian Airlines, asking if I could urgently go to Linate.
So I picked up my computer, asked a couple of colleagues to follow me. We jumped into the car and drove to Linate airport.
The first Polaroid is Viale Forlanini: a completely deserted avenue.
No traffic, no one around.
We arrive at the entrance of the Linate airport and there is a Carabinieri car blocking the access.
We explain who we are, and get to the front of the air terminal.
I get out of the car, look up at the sky - a deep, blue sky with a bright sun was now above us.
My mind goes back to the morning fog, that could have been sliced with a knife. All of a sudden, a wonderful day.
I look around, nothing moves.
I don't know if you remember a science fiction TV series called "UFO", with a commander, Commander Straker.
And there's an episode, where he is moving and all the people and things around him are absolutely still.
This was the scene that I witnessed when I arrived at Linate: an absolutely surreal scene.
A deserted, silent airport.
And then, the smells.
The smells - it's incredible, if you have noticed: they stay in your mind, and stick with you forever.
The smell of kerosene in the air; the smell of burning; the smell of firemen's foam.
I will never forget those smells.
As I entered the air terminal, I thought: how many times I stepped through its doors, how many times I flew from here.
Airports are lively places: boarding announcements; the check-ins; people who buy the newspapers; people who drink their cappuccino, coffees, aperitifs, lunches.
A place thriving with life;
now, a deserted airport was before me.
I made my move to the elevators to reach the mezzanine floor, where the airline offices were located.
And all of a sudden, out of the corner of my right eye, I saw a poorly lit corridor.
Some shadows were moving along it.
But they were not shadows:
they were victims' relatives.
Some of the relatives of the 118 victims of the Linate accident, aimlessly wondering around this airport in search of their loved ones.
Paola Rota was 20 years old.
Her parents were on that plane.
Her little brother was there too.
Paola called me two years later.
She called me at the office and said, "I'd like to come and meet you."
"Of course", I answered.
She came, sat in front of me, looked me in the eyes and said: "I am preparing my graduation thesis on the Linate accident.
I came to gather some information".
We pulled out the folders, I explained to her all the work we had done in support of the airline, and I could not help but be impressed by the strength of this girl who had decided, after that tragedy, to write her thesis on the accident.
Perhaps as a way to overcome her grief.
Paola is no more.
Ten years later, Paola took her own life.
I keep thinking back to that corridor in Linate, to that silence.
And I wonder, why wasn't that silence filled with communication?
Why nobody bothered speaking welcoming words, words of affection, of closeness?
Communication is not just about words.
Communication is also about gestures: a hug, a handshake, a caress, a tear.
At Linate, unfortunately, these things were nowhere to be found.
Why?
I asked myself this question, for many years: why that silence in that corridors in Linate.
Why wasn’t anyone there to help those suffering people?
I gave myself two answers.
First, that was a question no one asked themselves: What If?
What if there is a plane crash?
How do we manage that?
Nobody, but the airline, had considered that.
Fear is the second answer.
Fear of coming to terms with one’s responsibilities, the fear of facing the pain of others.
But in times of crisis, we have our emotions, which are an extraordinary resource.
Not the emoticons on your mobile phone we like to type along with messages: they are the emotions we feel in our hearts and in our minds.
They are the emotions that we have to use and calibrate.
Calibrate the fear and bring out the others emotions.
We have to place ourselves in other people's shoes, and use the peak expression of human intelligence: empathy, the ability to connect to others.
And not with text messages: with real words and gestures.
Are you ready for your Morandi moment?
I don't think one can ever be, but you can prepare yourself.
So remember, when that moment comes - and it will: it's not a question of if, it is a question of when - remember to use your emotions.
Remember, if it happens to you, to use your words and gestures to ask for help.
And do not be afraid, if it happens to someone close to you, or even someone you do not know, to take a step towards them.
Emotions are an extraordinary resource: do not wither them, do not hide them, use them.
And instead of closing yourselves in the silence of that aisle in Linate, open yourselves.
Open yourselves to communication. Because only communication, in life's toughest moments, can really help you.
(Applause)
Wow! It’s an honor for me to be here today, and it seems so weird to be on this red carpet if I consider how, up to ten years ago, my life revolved around sailing.
I would spend all my free time at sea – not only on those glorious hot days, typical of the Sicilian summer, but also on cold wintry ones.
I remember, as if it were yesterday, my totally frozen hands and feet at the end of my winter training sessions.
And I have to admit I was so focused on sailing that I hadn’t even noticed the advent of the Internet.
One of the first times I encountered the Internet dates back to when I was preparing my high-school final project.
I remember I didn’t even know how to search for photos and, eventually, I ended up photocopying pictures from books I had borrowed from the library and gluing them onto my paper since I didn’t even know how to find photos and insert them into a computer-written text.
In ten years, my life has been completely turned around by the digital revolution.
The Internet was my bridge, from Sicily to the rest of the world.
But what really opened my eyes on the digital revolution was to come a few years later, when I found out about 3D printing.
And I found out about it by watching a TED Talk by Anthony Atala.
Professor Atala talked about 3D-printing of human organs and the promise of saving hundreds of thousands of people’s lives thanks to this technology.
A fantastic thing and, for me, pure science-fiction at the time.
I was so intrigued that I decided to see a 3D printer with my own eyes
and I was lucky enough to find a 3D-printing course in Brussels, the city where I lived back then.
The course description reassured me because it stated that anyone, even someone like me who had studied economics, could learn how to 3D print within a matter of hours.
So, I went for it.
And I did right, because, in just a few hours, that course enabled me to create a 3D-printed key-chain with my name on it.
It wasn’t organ printing, just plastic printing; but that simple key-chain made me see the technology under a different light,
as a creative tool instead of something to be used passively.
And that was for me the beginning of a path that would lead me a couple of years later, to found both a specialized 3D-printing start-up for dentists and the ‘Teens4Kids’ project, which I would like to tell you about today.
I’ve been working on this project for three years with an outstanding team of volunteers who, just like me, were willing to invest in the future of Sicily and bring to Sicily what they had discovered while abroad.
Together, we are creating a bridge that enables Sicilian students to get in touch with innovations from every corner of the world.
And we aim to show, and promote, our idea that technology is an active creative tool.
We came up with this project when we directly experienced the width of the gap between innovative realities – like Silicon Valley – and other realities – like Sicily – where people are subjected to rather than creators of technology.
The reason behind this divide is, first of all, the fact that today there are not that many digital creators.
For example, online communities talk about the so-called 1%-rule: that is, only 1% of the users in a community are content creators,
while the rest are contributors and, above all, passive consumers of contents created by someone else.
We live in a world where few people create contents and services that are consumed by millions or billions of people.
And the most worrying thing is that not only are there few digital technology creators, but these few creators are concentrated in some countries and in big cities.
In my opinion, this concentration of creators – and, in general, the divide between technology creators and passive consumers of technology – is one of the biggest problems of the 21st century
and may end up creating considerable economic, political, and social inequalities.
I believe that this issue should be tackled urgently, and we need to start working on it today.
In this transition to the digital world, native digitals are particularly vulnerable.
And we need to reconsider what we normally believe – that they were born in a digital era, so they are certainly more familiar with the digital world.
These kids were actually born in a world where Internet is taken for granted and isn’t often called into question.
If we consider our school-age teenagers, we can see that most of them browse the Internet in a passive way and are able to use social media and Instagram.
But how many of these students can use the Internet actively to create contents, develop entrepreneurial ideas or solve social problems?
Yet I’m not here to talk about how urgent it is, in my opinion, to reform the educational system in Italy.
I believe we all agree that our educational system hasn’t adjusted to the digital revolution yet and isn’t training creators who can make a difference in the digital era, obviously with a few exceptions.
I’m here to talk about how firmly I believe that every single one of us, in our own little way, can contribute to solving this problem.
And we can start doing so from the bottom up, from our local area, rather than waiting for someone at a higher level to change the education system.
In our case, we started from Marsala – the town where I grew up – on the westernmost tip of Sicily.
I imagine that some of you have heard of Marsala for our wine, but most of you don’t know that Marsala is also known as ‘the windy city’, with a lot of sailing enthusiasts, like me.
I believe that there are three key ingredients to becoming great sailors, the same ingredients characterizing our ‘Teens4Kids’ project.
The first one is practice: you can only learn how to sail by sailing and not from books.
You become good at sailing by practicing under every condition: when you fall, when you get back on board, when you fall again, when you cry because you feel lost on the open sea, when you win and when you lose.
And by spending long hours at sea.
The second ingredient is teamwork.
When you train with a team, you grow with your teammates.
When I sailed, We used to take every training session as a small competition: you learned from the oldest mates and taught the youngest.
With the right team, you can make it all together.
The third ingredient is the environment.
Marsala boasts the ideal conditions for sailing: a lot of wind, a beautiful sea one would never want to get off the boat and a temperature that allows you to train all year long.
These three ingredients — practice, teamwork, and environment — are the three key principles of the ‘Teens4Kids’ project.
Let’s start from the first one: practice.
If your daughter were to tell you that she wants to go in for sailing, would you ever buy her a book?
I hope not: you learn how to sail on the water.
And the same happens with technology, you learn it by experiencing it, using it, and not from a book.
If we want our students to become creators, they need a hands-on approach to technology, learning how to use it in an active way.
It’s not important that they learn about a specific type of technology because everything they learn today will be obsolete a few years from now.
What really counts, instead, is that they learn to see technology as a creative tool.
Today we have the opportunity to convey this idea of technology through digital fabrication tools such as 3D printers and laser-cutting machines.
I guess that some of you have never seen a 3D printer.
And I imagine you think they are expensive and difficult to use.
Actually, this is far from true.
In the last few years, digital fabrication has become both financially and technically accessible.
Any school could afford to buy some of these machines and create a small lab.
Today, for example, we can buy 3D printers for less than € 100.
And the fact that they are technically accessible is guaranteed by an economist who discovered the Internet 10 years ago.
In the ‘Teens4Kids’ project, we started with four basic modules: programming – or coding –, electronics, 3D printing, and laser cutting.
And our students learn through a hands-on approach, by doing
and, above all, by playing.
For example, during one of our workshops, our students create a DIY microscope by using laser-cutting machines.
For the electronic component, they disassemble a webcam, take its lens and assemble it onto the structure they have previously cut with the laser-cutter.
Then, they have fun customizing their own microscope as they wish.
During this creative process, not only do students learn 2D drawing, laser cutting, and how to disassemble a webcam – which is another essential skill in the digital era –, but above all, they also learn to see technology as a tool to discover the world around them.
With this workshop, I saw kids, even grown-ups, run curious on the streets picking up leaves, bugs and any weird things they came across. I also saw somebody rub a cotton bud in their mouth to look for bacteria, then spread them onto the microscope and actually use this tool to observe and satiate their curiosity.
Moreover, such projects are precious tools if you wish to make science democratic.
These microscopes cost around € 10 and enable us to have images like this, top-quality ones, just like those you can obtain from digital microscopes that cost at least 10 times as much.
Once they have learned some basic skills, students are free to use their own imagination, creating whatever they consider useful or simply fun, such as small Pokémon.
And technology becomes a tool they can use to create something to be proud of;
it doesn’t matter what they create, the point is they should create it with their own hands.
Imagine projects like this one being carried out by students in all of our schools;
imagine how our education could change if, for example, during a science class, students could not only study bacteria from their books but also learned how to create a microscope to observe the bacteria they are studying.
In this way, technology wouldn’t only be a new school subject, but it would become part and parcel of the school curriculum, a real learning tool.
We could bring this type of creative digital education to all of our schools along with the two other main features of the ‘Teens4Kids’ project: teamwork and a suitable learning environment.
We can’t expect all of our teachers to turn into digital experts from one day to the next, but we can facilitate this transition by creating teams capable of leveraging teenagers’ curiosity.
In the ‘Teens4Kids’ project, we are training students from high schools to act as trainers for the younger ones.
This approach boasts a high number of advantages: first of all, these students are digital natives – they grew up with technology – so they can easily teach it to the younger students.
Moreover, involving teenagers enables us to bring digital education to schools while cutting costs and saving time,
considerably increasing the number of students we can reach.
This year, the ‘Teen For Kids’ project we are carrying out in Marsala involves a group of 10 volunteers training 100 high-school students who, in turn, will be training as many as 750 primary school pupils during this school year.
According to their interests, high-school students are being asked to play different roles in our project: some are teaching, others are helping us prepare lessons, still others are busy translating materials, such as workshops, from English into Italian; and then, a group are helping us run this project, as if it were their own start-up.
These high-school students are taking part in our project as part of the 'alternanza scuola-lavoro,' a work placement project preparing them not only to enter the world of work – and for life in general – whereby they will acquire a vision of technology as a tool for training, developing social commitment and bringing about change.
Teaching is also the best way to learn, and these students are becoming very young digital experts.
Some of them have decided to continue with their studies in the scientific and technological areas – so, they will be enrolling for university courses in these fields –, inspired by our activities; others have already created innovative start-ups in Marsala,
and one of these, developed by teenagers, already boasts as many as 16,000 users worldwide.
Along with practice through digital fabrication and a team leveraging teenagers’ skills, there is a third essential factor in helping us to train new creators: the environment.
While in sailing the environment is given and can’t be modified – we can only be lucky enough to be born and raised in a windy and sun-kissed place –, in such projects as ‘Teens for Kids’ the environment can be designed to suit one’s needs.
In our case, to create an atmosphere conducive to creative learning and train our students, we are using free online resources, including those made available by prestigious universities such as the MIT and Stanford.
We have selected workshops, projects, and courses that we are replicating in Sicily.
Thanks to these materials, we are offering digital and creative education that costs around 10 Euros per student:
creative education for the same price as a cinema ticket.
This is the power of the Internet: making excellent education affordable and accessible to everybody, in a democratic way.
I must admit that it wasn’t easy to find resources for this workshop, because often they are not available in Italian and scattered across many platforms in different languages,
so in the ‘Teens4Kids’ project we aim to contribute to removing this barrier so as to allow many others to take part in creative education projects at school.
For this reason, we have decided to launch an online platform in Italian, where to share the workshops, projects, and courses we used for the courses we are now running and for the ones we are hoping to run in future,
thus providing a good starting point for those who aim to promote digital education in their area.
However, if we want this platform – this ‘Teens4Kids’ portal – to make a difference, our contribution is not enough.
We will be launching this platform next January and, meanwhile, we would like to invite all the people who, like us, are working to train young creators to contribute to its development.
Last night we launched an online portal created by some of our students, on which we invite people willing to make their contribution to share their projects, workshops, case histories, lessons learned, in short anything they might want to share through the ‘Teens4Kids’ portal.
This way, our portal can become a starting point, as well as a meeting place where to exchange lessons learned and encourage cooperation among those who want to make a difference and bring this kind of education to schools.
By creating a network based on experimentation and sharing, moving from a number of isolated cases we could initiate an educational revolution from the bottom up.
Together, we can train a generation of creators who are likely to play a key role in the digital era, rather than be mere onlookers of this transformation.
We can all contribute to this mission: teachers can take a workshop from the ‘Teens4Kids’ portal and use it in their classes. Both university students and teenagers can knock on the door of a primary school and offer to hold a workshop; the next step is to take it to other schools and communities.
Sharing these workshops and lessons learned on our online portal multiplies the impact of such initiatives.
Activist Bertha Calloway used to say that ‘we cannot direct the wind, but we can adjust our sails’.
When you start sailing, you normally allow yourself to be impelled by the wind in the direction it is blowing.
After a while, you start taking control of the boat, choosing the route and even sailing upwind.
The wind goes from being an obstacle to being a means to reach a destination.
With practice by means of digital manufacturing, a team leveraging teenagers and a suitable educational environment, we can enable this generation of Italian students to see the technology in the same way a sailor sees the wind: as a means to reach their destination.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Since I was little, I’ve always been - I’ve always appreciated the beauty that can be observed in nature.
And, in particular, I’ve always been fascinated by the shapes and colors that can be seen in insects, especially beetles.
I’m from Como and used to spend my summers chasing these stag beetles, the ones in the middle, which look so beautiful when they fly, but are now very difficult
to spot in our area. I ended up working in California on a different type of insect, an insect that I’m sure you all love: the mosquito.
Mosquitoes represent a huge problem as they cause about one million deaths on Earth each year. Of all animal species, they are the deadliest, leaving us in second place on the list of ‘killers’.
Per se, mosquitoes are harmless. They bother us, they’re annoying, but they are harmless.
The problem lies in their acting as carriers of parasites and viruses that cause debilitating and, very often, deadly diseases.
Today, the worst among these diseases is malaria.
Malaria is caused by a parasite known as Plasmodium.
These mosquitoes were not born with Plasmodium: they need to bite an individual infected with the disease to transmit it to an uninfected one.
This is how malaria is transmitted from one person to another.
Plasmodium is an intracellular parasite: once it has entered our bloodstream, it invades our red blood cells, where it proliferates and eats them one by one. This is what causes malaria.
It’s transmitted by mosquitoes, as we’ve said earlier, but not every mosquito can carry Plasmodium.
For instance, tiger mosquitoes, which I’m sure you’ve heard of, do not carry malaria.
Only a small group of Anopheles mosquitoes, belonging to the genus Anopheles, can transmit malaria.
Moreover, Plasmodium specifically targets humans, meaning that it cannot be transmitted from animals or to animals.
Furthermore, it is unable to survive in a swamp or in a forest.
It’s an obligate parasite: it means it is confined to humans and mosquitoes.
This gives us an advantage as we could, in theory, break this loop - and its life cycle - which is completed inside humans and mosquitoes.
We could break the cycle with drugs and pesticides, and if we were able to do that instantly in every corner of the planet, we could eradicate malaria.
The problem, however, is that it is not so easy to use these drugs and pesticides everywhere. In fact, malaria remains endemic in a large number of countries, especially in the poorer ones.
It still represents a major issue in Africa, in central and South Africa, where it kills millions of children - mostly affecting children under the age of five.
The Plasmodium has evolved with humans, and so it has been with us for millennia.
And in the last 150 years, we have been fighting mosquitoes and Plasmodium with every means we could think of: we’ve been using mosquito nets to keep them out; resorting to pesticides and spraying them on the mosquito nets to try and kill them; fumigating entire cities with these pesticides in an attempt to kill them.
We also have developed drugs to prevent Plasmodium - which I'm taking as I went to India last week - or to cure people infected with Plasmodium.
All these methods are slowly proving to be ineffective as we have been using them for so long now.
And many experts in the fields of medicine and science are really worried because, for example, mosquitoes are developing new behavioral patterns and have learned to bite us in the evening when we are outdoors, and not when we are inside, sleeping.
Moreover, they have developed a resistance to pesticides and, likewise, Plasmodium has developed a resistance to the drugs we use.
This is reflected in the increase in the number of malaria cases recorded over the last two - three years.
Now, I wasn’t invited here by Gerolamo because I’m a malaria expert myself, but because I’m a genetic engineering expert.
And before I tell you all about the new tool we are currently developing in California to use genetics to fight malaria, let me give you a rundown on basic genetics.
Mosquitoes – just like us – have two copies of the genome: one that comes from the mother and one from the father.
If we take a blue mosquito, one with one blue gene - let’s say this blue gene comes from the mother, this blue gene - once the blue mosquito mates with a black mosquito, there is a 50% chance of its passing on this blue gene to its offspring, so only half of its progeny will be blue.
And this blue gene will stay with the population, generation after generation, with a given frequency as per Mendel’s laws of inheritance.
While working on my Ph.D. in San Diego, I asked myself: “What if we had a green gene that could, somehow, convert that black gene that comes from the other parent into a green gene too? How would Mendel’s inheritance laws be modified?”
The green mosquito now has two copies of this gene, and if it mates with a black mosquito, not only is there a 50% chance of the gene being passed on to the offspring, but all of the offspring will have a copy of the green gene. Moreover, since it can copy itself into the parent’s copy of the genome, from generation to generation the frequency of this green gene will spread among the population until the entire population display it.
This type of inheritance has been studied for about a century: it’s called “gene drive” and it occurs in nature. There are different mechanisms that can lead to an increased chance of heredity similar to the one we’re trying to simulate.
In California, we’ve managed to create a “gene drive” that is entirely artificial, that is entirely produced in a lab, and can be used to modify - can be placed anywhere inside the genome of a mosquito.
[We’ve done this] by taking advantage of a recently developed technology named “CRISPR”. Its two basic components are named “Cas9” and “Guide RNA”.
“Cas9” can be defined as - it’s an enzyme and acts like a pair of molecular scissors to cut the DNA, while “Guide RNA” is like an address: as it tells “Cas9” exactly where it needs to cut.
We took a gene from both “Cas9” and “Guide RNA” and put them in the middle of a DNA sequence that matches that of a mosquito's genome.
This artificial circular piece of DNA, called Plasmodium, is then inoculated into the eggs of the mosquito and it works as follows: “Cas9” and “Guide RNA” combine, “Guide RNA” tells “Cas9” exactly where it needs to cut, then the genome is cut at a specific point.
Since the cell cannot survive with a broken DNA, it will start to look for an exact sequence that can be used as a [template] to repair itself. This is how we succeed in introducing “Cas9” and “Guide RNA” into the desired position.
Thanks to this method we created the first transgenic mosquito.
What happens next?
The other copy of the genome, the black gene, gets cut in the same way by “Cas9” and “Guide RNA”, which are now inside the genome of the mosquito, and get copied in the exact same position on the other piece of the chromosome, giving us the green gene we were aiming for.
Of course, you’ll tell me - our intent is not to create green or blue mosquitoes: we want to give them a genetic trait that will help us fight mosquitoes.
For this reason, my mentor Ethan Bier and myself, while working together on this “gene drive,” started collaborating with another expert from University of California Irvine (UCI) who had developed a second gene cassette that makes mosquitoes immune to the malaria Plasmodium.
Once they bite someone who has Plasmodium, Tony’s mosquitoes can’t transmit it to an uninfected person.
How do the mosquitoes created by this researcher work?
When these insects suck a fair amount of blood and ingest Plasmodia in the meantime, these Plasmodia must exit the stomach and enter the mosquito’s lymphatic system.
However, these mosquitoes are genetically engineered to produce antibodies – which, by the way, are human antibodies, so there is a gene cassette inside the mosquito that produces human antibodies – which block Plasmodia as they try to leave the stomach.
Should even a single Plasmodium manage to enter the lymphatic system, there is a second antibody, ready to kill it with a toxin before it’s able to reach the salivary glands, from where it can be transmitted to the next person. In short,
what we did was to combine our synthetic “gene drive” with this new trait, thus creating these mosquitoes.
They have, in theory, the capability to modify the world’s Anopheles mosquito population, making it completely immune to malaria.
In a years time, they could eradicate malaria.
Now, as we said earlier, the reality is somewhat different, and implementing this procedure throughout the world is extremely complex. A mosquito’s genetics is very complex, just like that of a parasite.
Therefore, this technology will complement the use of pesticides, drugs, and vaccines that are currently being developed.
Some of you may well have wondered, as have other people: “Isn’t it dangerous to modify an entire species?” “Couldn’t it have unforeseen repercussions on the ecosystem?” These are, without a doubt, questions we must ask ourselves when making use of such a powerful technology,
yet I would like to set things in perspective and compare this “gene drive” to the system we’re currently using to get rid of insects.
This kind of system is extremely complex and raises a number of issues.
Currently, we’re dumping tons and tons of pesticides into our ecosystem trying to kill these insects.
Mosquitoes are among these insects, then there are agricultural pests or even – I don’t know – ants that enter your house, bother you and you want to kill them.
But by doing so, you’re not only targeting the insect you do not like. You target all the insects of an entire ecosystem, and you’re having an indirect impact on the plants and animals that interact with these insects, for example, the animals that feed on them.
A “gene drive” would not have the same effect: A “gene drive” is extremely specific to a single species, a single link within an ecosystem. Moreover, we do not remove it: We simply endow it with a slightly different trait so as to stop it carrying
the parasite [which annoys us].
As a result of global warming and globalization, these insects are moving from continent to continent, coming further north with every passing year. And they also carry diseases.
Malaria seldom occurs in Italy, but you will have surely heard of the Zika scare a couple of years ago and other diseases like dengue and chikungunya are posing serious problems throughout the world.
I am, therefore, convinced that this “gene drive” is a new sustainable tool at our disposal, which could well be used to fight these diseases.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Alexa, Siri, Crystal, Secretary Amy, note this one: the window-cleaning robot Heidi, coming from Japan.
All these are examples of Artificial Intelligence. The most pervasive technology of the next 20 years.
But, there is another thing that these examples share: they all have female characters and features!
In our minds robots have always been a symbol of exploited classes.
Even the word 'robot' derives from a Czech term, that means 'slave'.
We live in a world where 600 million women still live under the poverty line.
One in five has been a victim of violence.
To give an idea if there are 1000 women in this room, that means 200 women.
Before I finish speaking, ten women, ten!
in the world will die in what should be the happiest moment of their life: childbirth.
While I participate in the advancement of female robots that clean our houses, organize our schedules, and take care of us with a tender voice, and without ever complaining, I cannot help trembling when I think of these parallelisms.
Microsoft too has taken part in the Artificial Intelligence challenge launching TAI a bot with the features of a gentle teenager, designed to interact with Twitter users.
A few hours after its launch, TAI was overwhelmed with sexist comments, which it repeated exponentially online.
And before being shut down, only 16 hours after its launch, TAI had learnt to admire Hitler!
But how did TAI learn?
How does Artificial Intelligence work?
It is much simpler than what we think, and should interest everyone.
To understand it together, let's do an experiment: what do you see in this photo?

The Colosseum. And in this one?
It's been simple up to now, you will say.
We are clearly talking about a symbol not only of Rome, but of all humanity, that has stayed the same - and luckily, at least equal to itself - for some thousands of years.
Even from a geometrical point of view, the Colosseum is rather recognizable: it has vertical lines, arches, an oval... In the first phase of AI, researchers actually did this: they tried to teach an algorithm to recognize a series of geometrical shapes.
What about this image then?
For us, it is easy to recognize.
But for an algorithm, it is about adding totally new shapes and a totally new point of view.
What would happen if the Colosseum were full of people?
Or if we saw it from a different angle?
You understand that it is extremely difficult to teach a machine to think, with this method.
Only eight years ago, Fei-Fei Li, a researcher, considered the greatest expert of computer vision, the science that teaches computers to see had a simple, but revolutionary, thought and it is this one: no one teaches a baby to see.
No one teaches a baby to see?!
How trivial is that!
Before you get up from your seats, I want to say that it isn't so expected, and that on the basis of this observation we learn a lot about how we, ourselves, learn, so also about the way in which machines learn.
If our eyes were a pair of digital cameras, they would take a photo every 200 milliseconds: the average time of a blink.
When we are this high,
when we are 3 years old, we have already collected millions of images.
All these help construct a sort of digital encyclopedia, upon which we base our comprehension.
That's why it was thought that instead of constructing better algorithms, it was enough to give machines more compact and better quality data mimicking a little the learning of a child.
Here is why Google is so happy to give space to our holiday or travel photos for free or even to our work documents.
They need our data, our sweet, succulent data, to feed their intelligence.
As it is, in machine learning, having better data is always preferable to constructing better algorithms.
But now, let's take another step forward so I ask you to get your cell phones.
(Smiles and Laughter) Have you checked your email?
Great!
Now, let's all go together on a search engine for images.
Whichever you prefer.
And let's write 'CEO', the English word, but internationally renown, that identifies the top person of a company.
What do you see?
I heard someone say "men"!
For the first 50 or 100 images you see white men.
(Applause and Murmuring) Do any of you see a woman CEO?
Barbie!
Does anyone see a black woman CEO?
One, there in the back!
Does anyone see a disabled CEO, a person in a wheelchair?
No one?
This prejudice isn't a new thing, we haven't invented it today.
What is new, what is creating itself today, is the extent that that prejudice takes on thanks to Artificial Intelligence.
It works more or less like this: if you think of the example of a child, it is easy to understand that machines already have an openness of many more facts than what we see ourselves, and they learn them much more quickly.
The manner in which they work is this: a machine learns; becomes more intelligent; then learns again; then becomes more intelligent.
And then, like in a roller coaster that gains speed every time round
The revolution of automatic learning is very different from the Industrial Revolution, because this won't settle, it will always be getting faster; it is something we have never seen, similar to what the mathematician, Good, theorized as "intelligence explosion"
What intelligence will it be? What type
of intelligence do we want it to be?
How do we establish correct bases so that Artificial Intelligence can help us and free us from different opportunities caused by ethnicity, gender or wealth?
They are difficult questions, but we must make way that they become evermore a part of our conversations.
We cannot close our eyes and only let experts be concerned by this because we are here, called to determine how we want future intelligence to be programmed.
And one final question: what type of society do we want to live in?
When we speak about a super intelligent Artificial Intelligence, which betters itself, our only opportunity is to give to it the right diversity of vision and the right variety behind the set of facts which it learns from.
Let me tell you a personal story.
In 2005, I graduated in biomedical engineering, and my final thesis was a wearable device to prevent falls for the elderly, the primary factor that causes reduced mobility and, so weakening and then death.
To create this I had constructed an electronic system of 3 triaxial accelerometers connected to a thermal vest.
The accelerometers trasmitted their data and position, and I integrated them with a fall preventing algorithm.
I involved all my course peers, who you see here below, during the lesson and then at the end for having helped me.
They were all called to simulate various falls: those walking on a travelator, those stumbling, those pretending to faint, those falling down stairs.
They were long months of work.
15 days from the presentation of the device, nothing: the algorithm still didn't work!
Then, one evening, I remember it like it was yesterday. Bingo!
The algorithm had begun to work and managed to foresee a fall before the impact, with a good accuracy, and above all, gradually bettered itself as I was aiding it with new data.
It is quite embarrassing to admit it now, in front of 2000 people and to all those who are watching via streaming but now I know I had made an excellent device for the prevention of falls, but only for men!
Infact, I had used my course peers.
Would it have worked just as well had I only taken women?
Had I had variety in my data set?
I am not so sure about it.
However, I graduated and then began to look for work.
Not surprisingly, the profile of a biomedical engineer specialized in falls was not at the top in search engines.
And...you know what?
Not even the profile of a female engineer is first in search engines.
Female researchers at Carnegie Mellon University carried out a study on high income job positions published online: if a woman receives, on average, 300 views for these advertisements, a man receives 1800.
This could be the fault of the businesses, that only use men as targets, for the high-income jobs.
But the discrepancy is so vast that it is easier to think to a diverted algorithm which also feeds itself!
And now I ask you: are we truly willing [to accept] that to decide what job our daughter will do is an algorithm that discriminates because her sex isn't represented in the room in which it was made?
Do we really want that our grandparents' falls be more preventable, not that our grandfathers are cared for before and better than our grandmothers?
If we admit that the processing of information is the source of opportunities, then we must also think that only taking more variety among the communities that program Artificial Intelligence, we would have a more correct technology and a more equal society.
But variety alone isn't enough: I'll tell you another personal story.
My friend Chiara, an exceptional woman, went a long way in an extremely competitive environment, becoming number one of her business.
But Chiara is a sensitive person, and came to know hard times, as those caused by depression.
To promote a person like Chiara, without looking at her gender or her origin, surely appears right and I believe it is reasonable to use an algorithm to do it: what is the best way to remove subjectivity to this decision?
There is an American business that is capable to deduce anything about us, using our digital crumbs: the information we leave scattered on line, without even realizing it.
In particular, they are capable to predict depression months before - months before! -
any symptom arises.
Naturally, their intent is to intercept the illness when it is still small, barely curable.
But now I ask you to think about Chiara a moment.
What would be the moral and social implications if we were to let an algorithm decide what to think of us from our digital scraps, from what we do on Facebook, on Twitter, on the web?
Only with a more inclusive scientific community, and representative of all types and of all minorities we will manage to maintain an ethical control on Artificial Intelligence.
But watch it!
Algorithms don't need to be an excuse to abdicate our personal responsibility, but rather an opportunity to make a collective, international, inclusive and shared intelligence.
Thank you.
(Applause) Thank you.
(Applause)
What we've heard up until now - it has been the exaltation of the genius, a heroism of mind and creativity that we're likely to connect with individuality.
A genius is always just one, a genius is always unique, because the way he is talented just belongs to him.
So, what I am going to tell you may sound a little contradictory with regards to what we've heard up until now because Leonardo, great protective deity of this assembly, is the man who embodies, as we may say, the synthesis of every genius of his time - and of ours too, I dare say, because we've seen few geniuses like that.
However, I would like to reflect on a sort of paradigm shift by focusing on the genius of synthesis rather than the synthesis of genius; that is, how to coordinate the wide range of creativity of many people who have vision, expertise and skills, who, taken individually, are not genius, but genially, they can work together.
Naturally, I'll start to talk about this using the very subject of my profession, the stories.
Our whole life is full of stories: since we were born, they've followed us our whole life.
Bookshops are filled with stories as are both small and large screens since our childhood.
However, they are not neutral subjects: we live in there.
If you want to tell a story, you must first choose which one to tell.
The first thing that must be stressed is that the vast majority of stories in which we have been brought up are stories of solitary characters.
I remember my childhood. Tom Thumb being abandoned by his family in the woods - sounds shocking, doesn’t it?
Not to mention that he walked home by following breadcrumbs.
This fairy tale leaves out the fact that they don't want him at home, that he was abandoned in the woods.
Sweet Remi, who lost two families, actually. I can see many young people here don't know what I'm talking about, which reminds me of how old I am, but I take comfort in the fact that you have been spared the tragedy of Sweet Remi.
Pollyanna - poor little orphan. A lovely one, though. Nevertheless, she’s all alone. Thanks to her politeness, sympathy gathers round her, yet we’re talking about a little girl whose main story line is solitude.
I could quote more, but I'll spare you as I am already getting sad.
By the age of six, these stories played a vivid role in my imagination. I didn't have the good fortune to be an only child.
Indeed, I happened to have a brother with whom I had to share the attempt to be unique, each one in a different way.
I still sometimes call him my mama's other son so that I can feel a little more unique according to the outline of my childhood.
As I grew up, it didn't get any better. I came from a Catholic family, which meant I also had the Bible stories to borrow from.
In the Bible, there is Moses, stuttering yet chosen by God.
He's the only one who speaks with God and guides the Israelis to the Promised Land.
Then, David comes: a king shepherd who spent his free time composing psalms. We are again dealing with a multitasking genius.
There's Samson, who fights the Philistines with superhuman strength and brings them down with - can’t tell - a donkey jaw.
And through all these characters, we get to Jesus Christ, the absolute religious master, the most solitary among the loners, who ends up dying alone on the cross.
At the age of 14, I started attending the classical high school. I know, it sounds like a truly tragic matter, and partly it is.
I think it agrees with the experience in everyone's imagination.
In classical high school, all the epic poetry fell on me.
Achilles, half god, indestructible, invincible -
were it not for that tiny detail that he doesn't even know where it is.
Agamemnon, fierce warrior, determined to cynically sacrifice his daughter just to seize the opportunity to sail his vessels to Troy.
Then, Ulysses, the traveler; Daedalus, who defeats the Minotaur inside the maze;
Orpheus, the underworld investigator, who goes, for her love, to as far as where the dead live.
They’re all solitary characters.
And if I went to the cinema, same story there, too!
Only superheroes - hardly was there one as solitary as Batman, not even Tom Thumb. (Laughter)
Superman, always dealing with family drama,
up to Luke Skywalker, where power flows mightily, to finally get to Harry Potter, young wizard, bound to struggle against he who cannot be named, and we won't name him here either.
All these stories are a sequence of events that follow the same pattern: a solitary character who has to face either the whole world or an extremely serious threat. This kind of hero is likely to save mankind, and he does so by calling forth the power of a special skill that belongs only to him. Don't be deceived by the fact that they have friends around.
These are not choral stories: if Ron Weasley dies, Harry Potter’s story goes on.
However, if Harry Potter dies, no one is going to care about Ron Weasley's story.
When they tell you “They had friends," these are what are called gregarious characters.
This specific story pattern has already been analyzed by Propp when he looked at fairy tales. It’s called "the journey of the hero."
Those characters are supporting ones, who must support the hero's mission in the accomplishment of his goal.
Among other things, I recall Bertolt Brecht's quote. He said, "Blessed be the country that doesn't need heroes."
How can you believe in such a quote if the stories of heroes that define us, they're the first we hear as children, the only ones studied as kids, and the only ones we're inspired by as adults?
We're told these stories are educational and help form our personalities, but what exactly are they teaching us?
I know.
I know because I can see what our society is made of based on the many individualistic stories.
The X Factor, the generalized assumption that whatever is well-made in the world is thanks to the excellence of special individuals rather than to all of us who aren't special even though we wish we had been. We have failed to achieve the same goal; we can’t help but admire them.
So the stories of the hero, analyzed in this way, are amazing ones, but they can't be imitated at all.
I assume this isn't true, not true in history nor in epic poetry.
The taking of Troy - had we relied on Achilles’ invincibility and on Agamemnon's ruthlessness, those two idiots would still be each one in his own tent, loathing one another, as each of them had to renounce his slave for the pride of the other.
Those two, quarreling because of the Briseis and Chryseis issue, would have never taken Troy!
It needed Ulysses, who had a brilliant idea and said, "If we get together and create something that will be the synthesis of our genius and our nonsense kind of fades away, according to the principle of communicating nonsense, genius is communicative, too, but so is a little bit of nonsense.
He says, "This is the Trojan Horse.” The Trojan Horse brings down the invincible Troy, and it is everybody’s victory as no hero is present that can say, "It's mine.”
Unfortunately, we are not taught epic poetry in these terms.
I noticed there's an exhibition right now - I saw the sign when I was coming over. It stood there like a Candid Camera, saying: "Hercules and his Labours throughout the Arts: All of the Hero's Paintings."
No one tells us the collective stories.
Yet there’s plenty of them; we’re surrounded. Just think of Wikipedia.
How many of you here have written a Wikipedia entry?
(Laugher) There is someone, there is someone!
Among the people present, there's someone.
Wikipedia is a collective story. Although we may not remember the founders, we acknowledge it's a piece of work which requires and gets the support of millions of people worldwide, a mutual knowledge.
I'm recalling Mexico ’68, the two runners and the white one who supports them with a unique gesture, and in the span of time of a national anthem, their fists up, they bring into the homes of the world the issue of racial discrimination.
I'm thinking of a Turing machine, to the effort of many brains together - from chess players to mathematicians - who can break down the Nazi code and make a qualitative leap against the war. At that time, without them, it would have been lost.
These stories are not told.
The first time I approached a story that has changed me was when I became acquainted with "The Pal Street Boys” by Ferenc Molnàr.
Groups of teenagers who meet up to take control of a neighborhood.
Eventually, Nemecek dies. It was the first loss of my life. Again, there is something not quite right: they were boys adopting a war scheme to control a neighborhood.
This power model is typical of those stories. It is a subtractive model: if you own something, it's because you have stolen from someone else, and that someone else will fray, like Talleyrand used to say.
The second time I saw a collective history, everything changed.
When I was 16, I read “It" by Stephen King.
“It” by Stephen King is a collective story where, without doubt, all the main characters are complete losers. There's a bullied fat man, there's a stutterer, there's a black man in a world of whites, there's a nerd, there's a hypochondriac mama's boy, there's a female - one of those that in hero stories needs to be saved but certainly aren’t. They would have all
ended up dead in that story if they didn't get together and kill It.
So after “It," I go around telling kids only collective stories as they've got to stop believing that to be powerful is to be one against the other. They have to learn they can be powerful together.
After all, the storm is just a million drops of water - with a fair wind.
(Applause) (Cheers) You are a storm, no doubt about it.
(Applause) Thank you.
"Half seasons no longer exist."
"He's smart, but not committed".
"It was better when it was worse."
"Math is not an opinion. "
"We had fun with the little we had."
"It's always the best who eventually leave."
There are clichés that millions of people repeat year after year.
And starting this talk saying that we are in the middle of the sixth mass destruction, the one that will perhaps mark our end, would be yet another one.
And so I won't, even if I just did that... Fact is, we're filled with contradictions, and we rarely do what we really want to do.
We need something, or someone, to awaken us.
What occurred to me was, one morning, opening my eyes, I saw a beautiful dawn.
As the sun rose, it began to light up huge artistic structures.
I was at the "Burning Man", in the middle of the Nevada desert, a festival that tastes more like a social experiment, a collective dream, a place where thousands of people cooperate in creating these gigantic installations.
My work often takes me to America: and it is there, in America, that I witnessed the rebirth of some suburban neighborhoods, thanks to street art's regenerative power.
Winwood Art District, in Miami, is a brilliant example of it.
Mind you, until the early 2000's it was a suburban industrial district, full of warehouses and not very recommendable.
But back in those years, the Primary Flight association decided to create an "open air museum" in this area, and they invited artists from all over the world to decorate with their works the warehouses' walls.
Today, Winwood is the world's largest street art museum.
I am a dreamer, and America has taught me that anything is possible.
So I asked myself, why not bring this dream here, in Italy, in my country?
And if you think about it, I already had a canvas.
This building, which is admittably ugly, but my great-grandfather built it, and it passed down to me after I lost my father.
The man who taught me to love the sea, to climb the mountains, the same man who taught me to pursue my dreams, and the grit and determination it takes to do so.
Because dreams wither in the drawer, and turn into regrets.
The more I looked at it, the more it dawned what a perfect canvas that wall could be, one that could send a message dear to me.
But I was alone, and I had no idea how to do it.
One day, my yoga teacher told me that, according to Hindu religion, the universe is perfect, and everything is balanced.
And that when we have a desire, and we act to fulfill it, the universe supports us, it responds to us.
So, one summer, I discovered the existence of these magical paintings, that were capable of eating the city smog, purifying the area of 88.8 percent of the present pollution.
In that same summer I met my current vice-president, a woman who is both practical and dreamer at the same time, and a brilliant project manager.
The universe was responding, the pieces were fitting in the right way.
Soon after I went to New York, and I met, again by chance, Federico Massa, aka Iena Cruz, an artist already popular for his attention to environmental issues.
He was the missing link.
I told him about my project, he took up the challenge, came to Rome and sent me a first sketch shortly after.
So I started a non-profit organization, called "Yourban 2030", to exploit the universal language of art in order to raise awareness of the importance and urgency of acting on environmental issues.
And on that wall, once the property of my great-grandfather, "Hunting Pollution" was created in 21 days, and it to me it represents the perfect synthesis between art and technology.
(Video)
Aldo! Come down so I can see you, you're breaking my neck!
[Discover Europe's largest Green Murales ] [by Iena Cruz] (Applause) We had created Europe's largest green murales, a Guinness-grade work, 1,000 square meters of mural.
"Hunting Pollution" is able to absorb the pollution of 80 vehicles in one day.
It's like planting 30 tall trees.
And all this, thanks to the painting we chose for this project, a water-based, totally natural, antibacterial and anti-smog paint.
This paint's chemical action closely resembles plants' chlorophyllian photosynthesis.
When exposed to light, a photochemical process is triggered through polluting particles are captured, and turns them into harmless salts.
The poetics behind "Hunting Pollution" invites us to consider mankind's contaminating actions on the environment.
It represents a multi-colored heron, an endangered species, which hunts its prey in a heavily polluted sea.
He is standing on a barrel of oil, now its new habitat, and black drops fall on him, in contrast with the white ones.
The heron is unaware that that prey may be contaminated, and therefore lethal to him.
And if you think about it, we too ignore the danger we encounter every day when we do the most natural thing in the world: breathing.
90 percent of the world's population breathes heavily polluted air; in the world, pollution affects nine out of ten people, claiming seven million victims a year, one every five seconds.
In Italy, out of of 60 million people, we have 38 million cars in the streets, and 90,000 deaths a year attributable to air pollution.
Yet we continue with deforestation.
Do you know how many trees we would have to plant in order to achieve a significant carbon dioxide decrease?
About 10 million square kilometres, roughly equivalent to the size of Canada.
Native Americans say, we do not inherit a land from our fathers, but we borrow it from our children.
I am not yet a mother, yet one question already haunts me, what will we tell our children?
That it wasn't our fault?
That this was beyond us?
Or will we teach them to dream, to hope, to act on those dreams, to turn that hope into reality?
I received an email, a few days ago, and I'd like to share it with you: "Dear Veronica, my name is Tommaso, I am a 14 years old student from Terni,
and I saw on tv what you did in Rome. Well, I'd like to propose my building, in my heavily polluted city.
We have a steel factory and an incinerator. God only knows what we breathe; in fact, I was born with asthma.
Some days, when you go to school in the morning, you just can't breathe.
Plus, our municipality, in an entire street, has cut down all the trees.
I saw that you, with painting, can change the air, I would like you, or those who work for you, to paint my building, which is in the center, in a very busy area, also to make it clear to adults in power that we young people also have a brain, and we might want something more for our future.
And if we are on this Earth, we must take care of it in every possible way.
I hope you will answer me.
Thank you, Tommaso.
My new challenge, my new dream, is to realize projects like this.
I'm imagining people willing to donate the facades of their buildings, artists willing to work on them, and a crowdfunding operation capable of involving everyone, according to their means.
To feel part of a change, of a metamorphosis, so that those walls and those works can really belong to everyone.
I imagine a shout out made of colors, of thought, of communion.
A world where, looking at a wall, we could say "This is ours", because this is our home.
And so, no, "Half seasons no longer exist", "He's smart, but not committed", "It was better when it was worse", "Math is not an opinion", "We had fun with the little we had."
Because wrapping this talk up saying, "The future is in our hands", would be yet another commonplace.
And I'll do it because this is the case.
(Applause)
Silvia, do you still remember that moment in your mortal life, when beauty shimmered in your smiling, startled eyes as, bright and pensive, you got to the threshold of youth?
This is how a famous poem we probably all know begins, a poem that we have all listened to.
It’s “A Silvia” by Giacomo Leopardi, written in 1828.
Among other things, it deals with a theme very close to the poet’s heart,
one of nostalgia and memory and all the feelings associated to them.
I first heard it in elementary school and then again in high school.
And that’s when I became passionate about the theme of memory.
Memory is a complex concept and it can have different meanings.
We can refer to historical memory; that is the one of books, buildings and traditions of a certain society.
Or it can be the memory of a computer, where information is coded and stored inside physical supports in a digital format.
We also refer to the memory of an individual, maybe triggered through music or a particular scent, that allows us to recollect past events. It's called declarative memory.
Declarative memory is coded in the brain, where it is also presumably stored.
As Salvador Dalì shows in his painting “The persistence of memory,” the recollection of past events can be worn out by time - see the ants that swarm on the clock?
Or, as we will see today, memory can be worn out by illness.
Declarative memory is the subject I decided to focus my studies on over the past 10 years.
More precisely, I focus on brain mechanisms and on single cells and neuronal networks
to understand how they work in order to code, store and recall the memory of a certain event.
Even if we don’t exactly know where in the brain these memories are stored, we know well enough what parts of our brain are involved in their making.
Here I’m talking about structures located in the temporal lobe, right here, under the temples,
particularly in an area called the hippocampus because the first neuroanatomists saw the shape of a sea horse.
Here you can see a drawing by Prof Ramon y Cajal, dating back to 1879 based on his experiments with silver staining of hippocampus slices, a technique developed by Camillo Golgi with whom Cajal, in 1906, shared the Nobel Prize.
We know how the hippocampus works thanks to the bizarre case of patient H.M.
who underwent surgery
and had both his hippocampi removed to treat his severe epilepsy, back in the 50s.
After the removal, H.M. remembered
every memory of his life up to the moment of the surgery;
but from the surgery on, he could no longer make new long-term memories.
This is called anterograde amnesia, and besides inspiring movies like "Memento", by Chris Nolan or "50 First Dates" with Drew Barrymore, it teaches us that the hippocampus stores no memories.
Rather, it actually allows us to acquire new ones.
In the hippocampus, as with any other area of the brain, the cognitive function - in this case, memory - emerges from proper functioning of individual neurons, and how well they communicate.
And while single neurons elaborate and convey information via electric impulses, they communicate through special structures called synapses. When firing takes place on the information-sender, or presynaptic, neuron, a chemical substance called neurotransmitter is released, and binds to the information- recipient, or postsynaptic, neuron. This way, electrical activity can be either excited or inhibited.
And so on, over and over again.
The result is a network of communicating neurons where some act as exciters, and others as inhibitors.
To make it clearer, the brain works as an orchestra. Single neurons, in this metaphor, are the individual musicians, all coordinated by a specific type of neuron, called inhibitory interneuron, working as a coordinator, or conductor.
If neurons, properly communicating through synapses, work in a well coordinated fashion, the result is music: the functions of the human brain, including memory.
But if musicians aren't well coordinated - namely, should synapses fail to work properly, it all becomes a cacophony, a mess.
This is what may also happen in the brain of a person suffering from epilepsy or another disease leading to dementia.
The distance between the machinery of memory and learning and their related health issues is very short, so I shifted my focus to dementia.
Dementia is a "jack-of-all trades" term that covers the symptoms of various diseases that progressively lead to memory loss, progressive mood, language and sleep cycle alterations, and death of neuronal cells.
Please note: it is commonly defined as a elderly people disease,
and it's not true!
Dementia's first symptoms can appear very early on, even at 45, 50 years of age, and then develop though the decades. Dementia steadily erodes cognitive skills and has a great impact not only on patients' lives but also on their families both on a psychological and financial level.
Every time I snap my fingers - sorry my hands are a little sweaty - dementia is diagnosed to yet another subject.
If the trend continues, by 2050 there will be around 131 million people with dementia in the world at an overall cost of about two trillion american dollars
most of which, presumably, will be taken care of by their families.
Today there is no cure, but a symptomatic one.
Alzheimer’s disease is the most common cause for dementia.
There are two forms of it: genetic Alzheimer, which is rarer and directly caused by hereditary factors; and a lot more frequent, sporadic one, whose cause remains unclear. There are risk factors like smoke, alcohol, type 2 diabetes and more genetic factors increasing the risk of contracting this disease but we are not sure how.
In the brain of people affected by Alzheimer’s disease we can observe an accumulation of toxic proteins. like the beta-amyloid protein,
a sticky substance that accumulates before any episode of cognitive decline.
However, as this protein progressively builds up in the brain, cognitive deficit's first signs begin to show up.
Eventually, beta-amyloid stops accumulating and another bad boy takes the stage. We start to see microscopic tangles of the toxic and filamentary hyperphosphorylated tau protein, which is believed to be the cause for the death of neuronal cells and the severe brain functions impairment of Alzheimer's disease terminal stage.
As if all this wasn't already enough in people affected by Alzheimer’s disease we also see an alteration of brain's electrical activity which is similar to that caused by epilepsy.
A theory confirmed by experimental data affirms that the altered electrical and synaptic function of Alzheimer patients' brain is caused by the beta-amyloid protein
which since the early stages of the disease can act on the patient's brain by both destroying synapses and altering neurons' electrical activity.
That's what I've been dealing with in the past six years. I have tried to work on this.
So?
How does Alzheimer affect people's lives?
How can we use this knowledge for the greater good?
Can we - that is the question my fellow researchers and I grapple with, whether brain's altered electrical activity can be used, in Alzheimer's patients with cognitive deficit as an opportunity to develop new therapies, new early diagnostic methods that might detect the disease as early as possible?
Is it possible to use these informations to develop new practices that might improve the quality of life of people with Alzheimer's and their caregivers?
These priorities are very much intertwined with one another.
Developing a new cure, for instance, or a new drug approach, will presumably be more effective with a tool at hand to diagnose Alzheimer's as soon as possible. Today I will share how my collaborators and I, we are trying to develop a method for Alzheimer’s disease early diagnosis based on electroencephalography.
It consists in the application of electrodes on the head, which is cheap and not invasive.
This approach has the key advantage of relying on existing diagnostic infrastructures currently used for epilepsy.
So hospitals wouldn’t need to buy new machines.
San Marino is beautiful.
The vision of our Titano hill fills my heart with joy, especially after a long time abroad.
It’s not just about its majesty, it's the scent of the air I used to breathe when I was a child. The taste of food. The sound of laughs coming from my favourite café I go to after lunch. The sound of their accent.
The way they utter S and Z.
And it's my accent, also!
People live well in San Marino.
Our population is small, around 30,000 people Our doctors closely monitors us - around 1,000 citizens per General Practicioner, and we live for long.
Indeed, San Marino has one of the world's highest life expectancy rates.
According to a 2013 WHO chart, San Marino has the world's longest life expectancy for men, and it’s at the 11th place for women.
So?
San Marino can become a great lab to study long-lasting clinical conditions, like Alzheimer’s disease.
Two years ago we started the AlzSM project here.
A team of doctors led by Dr. Susanna Guttmann is currently busy collecting electrophysiological recordings and MRI data from people with Alzheimer’s disease and people of the same age with no cognitive impairment.
At the same time, a team of researchers from San Marino, UK and Germany led by Dr. Marc Goodfellow in Exeter and Dr. Federico Tombari in Munich deals with EEG data analysis.
Mark Goodfellow developed a method that analyses EEG patients' and controls' data and is then is able to predict with an accuracy rate of about 80% whether the electrophysiological recording belongs to a healthy individual, or someone suffering from Alzheimer's disease, or at least with an early stage of this disease.
This development is not completely new: other research groups obtained similar results although through different methods.
We assume to be able to predict if the EEG recording of a subject with mild cognitive impairment corresponding to the initial stages of the disease will develop in AD based dementia, or not.
I currently cannot reveal the details of Mark's method, firstly because we are not sure about its effectiveness, and secondly, because if I revealed it to you then I would have to kill you. (Laughter) I'm joking.
I'm not that crazy.
In order to test our hypothesis, we'll follow a group of Sammarinese volunteers with minor cognitive deficit for three - five years at least, and assess if our method can predict the development of dementia, particularly the development of Alzheimer’s disease.
Lastly, we aim at combining electrophysiology with more mainstream techniques to diagnose dementia and neurodegeneration
First, neuropsychological evaluation that is the direct measurement of a person's cognitive skills by running some tests.
Plus neuroimaging, which allows us to see the brain's structures with clarity.
For instance, nMRI scan.
Neuroimaging data are currently processed by Dr. Federico Tombari in Munich.
The ambition, in combining
these three approaches, is to increase the accuracy of the diagnosis and, possibly, of development prognosis of minor cognitive deficits into dementia, compared to their individual use.
To wrap up, memory is beautiful and fragile.
In a sense, we are our memories.
Dementia and Alzheimer’s disease represent a devastating condition both for those who suffer from it and for those who take care of them.
Scientific research is relentlessly working to develop new treatments new early diagnosis methods and practices to increase the quality of life.
Part of this is happening right here in San Marino.
A small and unique reality where we work locally and cooperate on an international level.
Perhaps, thanks to its uniqueness, to the compact power of its small and organised territory San Marino could have the potential to change the world for good.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Somewhere, along our way, we began to confuse comfort with happiness.
Modern life, today, is an endless shower of stimuli.
And the human brain is extremely receptive to all the stimuli coming from outside.
It is the result of adaptation, survival.
But despite this, since 2010, human beings have become what can be considered an urban species.
That is, most of us, more than half of us, live in an urban context, i.e. in cities, and it's estimated that around 90 percent of Americans' and Europeans' time is spent indoors, inside.
This audience today, all sitting here, we are a great representation of this.
And the other 10 percent of the time, 5 percent is spent in traffic.
Anyone from Milan knows this situation all too well.
And there's an interesting secondary effect, on top of that. Our brain wasn't designed to receive such a high quantity of memory stimuli.
And all this is changing it.
It's changing its structure.
The brain is changing, according to a rule called plasticity, both in terms of its structure, and also its size.
Because it's paradoxically smaller than that of our ancestors.
And the question is whether we were really designed for all this.
Namely, if the biological design that nature and evolution created for us was this, i.e. staying sitting down for a good chunk of the day.
In fact, this puts us in a state of captivity.
The city has become a hostile environment.
The explanation is probably both anthropological and evolutionary.
Homo Sapiens, when it becomes a species - bad news, our main goal in mind wasn't happiness, but survival.
So Sapiens developed all those defense mechanisms: fear, the sense of alertness, the sense of danger.
That's all it needed to react to the outside world.
Why am I telling you this?
Because I deal with the brain.
We spend our workdays studying all those feelings and emotions that happen in consumers' brains.
And as I was saying a moment ago, 10 years ago, at a conference, I met a colleague who showed me the first portable electroencephalogram.
This incredible device that allows us to see, through our waves, what's happening in the brain.
It seems like trivial news: but up until not so long ago, the electroencephalogram was a device filled with wires, where life was, somehow,
simulated inside rooms.
And for the past ten years we've tried to use this device to do tests on humans in their real lives, i.e. when they do something within their own lives.
This has allowed us, today, to have two offices in Italy and an office abroad, in San Francisco, California.
And we've become experts in Real Life Experiences, that is, in measuring what's happening in a person while they go about their daily life.
We do it for several corporations, from food to fashion,
cars, travel and more.
They ask us to find out what happens inside us when we do something.
But what are they asking for?
What characterises us?
The most extraordinary thing about our species: emotions.
Emotions are the greatest gift that nature and evolution have left us as a legacy.
They're at the same time both the simplest and the most complicated thing we can experience.
Even now, if I asked you to describe for me, in one word, or in a few concepts: what is emotion?
What are emotions?
How do emotions feel?
Where do we feel them?
How do we feel anger, where and how is it produced?
An emotion, to be defined as such, needs two components: one is your brain - I hope everyone's got one here, and one is your body, and I see you've all got those.
This makes us men and women with an extraordinary gift, which is that of emotions.
But, there is a "but".
By studying people's emotions, over the years, we also came to realise that human beings, slowly but surely, are losing their serenity.
We are, in a way, getting sick.
We are getting sick from something which is our own illness, that is to say, the modernity and comfort that we've created ourselves.
And they're starting to give us a series of problems, that we all know about.
We've learned so much about modernity, about technology, cement, cities.
And paradoxically, the remedy for this disease is something extremely simple, and we saw it just by measuring it.
Imagine a treatment,
one that's easy to perform, free of known side effects, which improves the functioning of your cognitive system, and above all is free, even if you can't get it on the NHS.
There is one, and it's called nature.
For hundreds of years, people have taken care of themselves with nature, it's nothing new.
But the real question we asked ourselves was, what kind of nature?
Do they all work the same way?
All the people who are walking in a park today, who see and enjoy a beautiful day, are all of them experiencing the same emotion?
All the people who go to a forest in the Dolomites - and many of you spend their holiday to the countryside - What's the real emotion you feel?
And above all, can we freeze it?
So that this emotion can be conveyed to someone else?
Measured, understood in some way?
Because if we can understand things, we also manage, in some way, to intake and share them.
And to do this, we started doing a series of research activities,
as this subject is very dear to us.
And to do it again, I would like to make you my colleagues.
We're all colleagues right now.
You are all neuroscientists, you've all received an honorary degree.
I'll tell you how the brain works.
The brain has three small superstructures that started to develop through evolution, one on top of the other.
There's the cerebellum, the famous cerebellum, which is the home, how might we put it, of our instinctive reactions; and the limbic system, directly above it, which was created, and maybe not everyone knows this, when we became a species, and is the home of our emotions.
When we started to become a species, and communicate with each other, we also needed to do it through emotions, and read them on others.
And lastly, only at the end, very late, came the cortex, which is the rational system.
The much-vaunted rationality, and superior thought.
It only turns up very late on.
In reality, we are interested in the last two, those ones below, basically the ones that decide things and care little about what you think.
It's these ones, when you're outside a shop, here in the centre, and there's a pair of shoes - you don't want them, but actually that one below had already decided for you that your credit card is about to come out of your wallet so you can buy them.
I want to give you two examples: let's get back to nature, the core thing.
Two interesting examples of how this nature works.
And the first of these examples - i.e. nature on the brain - the first example is Madagascar.
This extraordinary land.
And in this example, in this particular scenario, I want to talk you about encounters between wild nature and men.
This is a picture I took, both to show you how easy it is to take a picture today, but actually also to show how beautiful Madagascar is.
For this project we brought, for one week, a group of people, volunteers, who underwent this incredible torture, who came with us, and we visited many different environments: the mangrove rivers - we went to very white beaches, forests still untouched by human activity.
So with them, we tried to find out the impact different types of nature really had on them.
We went by pirogue, we did some extraordinary stuff.
We tried to experience, in the most appropriate way and with minimal impact, what this reality was.
To better understand what the results were, I want to make you my colleagues again and explain how to read them.
Let's talk about an index that's called frontal asymmetry, which is when there's a polarisation of waves in one lobe rather than in another.
When these waves - in this case alpha waves - emitted from our brains, are found on the left of your frontal lobes, subjects were experiencing a positive attitude, towards whatever they were experiencing.
When waves were to the right, the attitude is negative, repulsion, that is, they are somewhat [uncomfortable].
And, wait for it, of all the experiences that these people experienced in one week, the two experiences with the highest rate of, let's call it, discomfort, the mangrove river and the Lokobe forest were the experiences they liked the most.
That doesn't seem that much, but try to imagine the forest.
It isn't a comfortable place.
There were snakes, I saw them for real.
Six-metre boa constrictors, sleeping under the trees, insects, humidity - discomfort.
Anything humanly - in this room, you're sitting comfortably, it's actually particularly complex there.
But look, that was the most striking result.
We tried to investigate thoroughly, and find out what happened in their brains, while they were in the forest.
And we did that with two indexes.
The first is the cognitive load, that is, how much effort their rational part tried to use to understand, comprehend the environment around.
And on the other hand, the involvement, engagement, i.e. their capacity to feel at ease.
During the first part of their time there, the humans - our testers, in this case - spent about an hour working out where they were.
Working out if it was dangerous, whether they had to stay alert.
Do you remember? Our brain
is optimized for fear and survival.
But after an hour in the forest, their cognitive commitment mysteriously collapses, and makes room, instead, for emotional involvement.
It's like, at a certain point, the brain had said: "Hey, calm down.
That's as bad as you can get".
The decibels only come up to a certain point, we have the animals catalogued in some way, the people here are somehow not dangerous ... " It began to implement a series of mechanisms as if it felt at home.
And the result is so striking that it even managed to outclass places like this.
This is the beautiful Nosy Iranja.
It's a strip of sand linking two islets, that appears and disappears with the rhythm of the tides, and I really wanted to show you in order to emphasise how hard the week we spent in Madagascar was.
(Laughter) I swear: none of us, in our team, wanted - one morning we fought about going to the beach.
It was really complicated.
We're those little ones there.
It's an aerial photo taken by a drone.
Enough beaches, let's move on.
Let's talk about ... Honey!
When nature ends up on the plate.
Honey is an extraordinary substance.
A gift that's made for us by these tiny beings, which - to digress, a piece of news I read yesterday: 50 percent of the honey harvest this year will be lost due to climate change.
Honey is disappearing!
And, in addition to this, perhaps not everyone here knows that one honey in two of those sold on the market comes from abroad.
In Italy, of course.
So they come from China or other Eastern countries.
And 75 per cent of honey found in major retail stores isn't honey, but a mixture of sugars, flavourings, molasses, which makes it smell and taste like honey, but it's not honey!
75 per cent, I mean, it deserves a talk of its own, to tell you all about it.
And, in addition, what makes it even more complicated, is that the real product, honey from bees, it's about three times as expensive than the fake honey.
And the question we asked ourselves, but also that different beekeepers' associations have asked us, precisely concerning this problem, is: is our brain able, spontaneously, to tell an authentic product from a fake one?
That is, are we able to recognise a product which comes from nature, compared to a product created in a laboratory?
So we took another group of consumers, subjected them to the umpteenth test to see how it worked, and we took three samples of honey, comparable by type, only the last one, organic honey, being real.
And we asked them to taste those sample, and then we asked them to express a conscious preference, to tell us which one was their favourite, if they noticed some kind of difference.
Clearly it was a blind test, none of them knew which was the real one, nor the purpose of the test.
And in the meantime we measured.
When explicitly asked, none of them express an overall preference for the real honey - I mean, the bee-made one.
But look what happened inside their heads: the electroencephalogram, i.e. their most instinctive part, manages to pick out the real honey 10 out of 10 times!
As if our brain knew things that we ourselves aren't able to understand!
More, much more: the real honey managed to stimulate areas - like the temporal one, which we see lit up here, which is the home of our memories, pleasant activation, harmony with what's happening. And even made waves, called Theta waves, which are typical of meditation and deep relaxation.
Honey!
And it's incredible, something that not even the consumers managed to recognise.
What am I getting at with this?
There's so much out there to discover.
Today it's like we're looking out at this huge edge which lets us see a little further, a little closer to the whole planet, the bigger picture.
And for our whole lives, we carry around the most wonderful and complex system that the Universe has created, which is our brain.
Still, our ignorance on this complex topic is abysmal: we know nothing.
And only in the last few years have we managed to shed some light on what its rules are, its systems, its mechanisms.
And there's a simple reason for this scientific delay: in order to study complex systems, you need computers.
Thanks to technology, today we can study huge amounts of data that weren't manageable before.
In reality, it's like we're saying that only thanks to technology can we now recover the oldest part of ourselves.
Including contact with nature.
So our wish, our dream, for all this is that our efforts, however modest, may be part of a collective effort.
Of a collective will to be able to see what marvels take place inside our brain and what a marvel, despite what's happening, human beings are.
Because basically, by trying to protect what's around us, and love it, we'll feel the desire to make it ours.
And just by trying to save our relationship with nature and the world, we'll try to save ourselves a little too.
Thank you.
(Applause)
The ultimate thing to determine conclusively is whether you're in a comedy or a tragedy.
To quote Italo Calvino, the ultimate meaning to all stories is they have two faces: the continuity of life, and the inevitability of death.
Tragedy, you die. Comedy, you get hitched.
Unlike the character in this movie, we don't have a storyteller to decide what we're in for.
And certainly, we don't belong to a particular literary style.
Nevertheless, I'd like to draw a parallel between what the character of "Stranger Than Fiction" goes through and what goes on within our minds.
Namely, our inner voice that continously judge our actions, thus affecting our choices and consequently our happiness.
Now let me take you through an experiment: turn your head and introduce yourselves,
You have five seconds, please go ahead.
A study by Brigham Young University, Utah proved that isolation and loneliness are worse for your health than obesity.
Isolation and loneliness predict a premature death before 65 years of age, so don't think that, just because you're young, you're free from such risks!
As for us, we just introduced ourselves, and maybe, with a bit of luck, we just slightly increased our life expectancy!
You may now wonder, do isolation and loneliness have something to do with optimism, the topic I'm here to talk about today?
Well, both are very well-known symptoms of depression, which is precisely the most chronic form of pessimism, or the most pathological one: it's a total lack of optimism.
And if you think that depression isn't something worth focusing on, I invite you to watch the statistics: by 2020, at least in the United States, depression is expected to become the leading cause of death after cardiovascular diseases.
Let's talk about optimism.
How would you define it?
Is it a way of life, an intellectual posture, an emotion?
Someone already asked this question. And thankfully, he did it with an empirical, scientific mindset.
One of the first was professor Lionel Tiger, who in 1979 wrote "Optimism: the Biology of Hope" where he approached optimism from an anthropological standpoint.
According to professor Tiger, optimism was a form of biological adaptation our prehistorical ancestors developed to survive.
Essentially, man tends to stay away from all those activities he deems too risky.
Our ancestor, on the other hand, always had an inner drive to get out of the caves and find something to eat.
One of the hypotesis involves the release of endorphines, and their analgesical, euphorizing properties.
Let's say our ancestors may have been the first extreme sports practitioners.
But more recent studies - we're in 1990 now - introduced optimism as a learned quality, or as a result of experience.
Professor Martin Seligman, from University of Pennsylvania, first introduced the concept of explicatory style.
Which is just another name for all the explanations we give all the time for everything around us. whether they're hurdles or good news.
This explicatory style can be either optimist, pessimist, temporary or permanent.
And depending on our explicatory style, we continously influence our success, in every field:
from work to health, from politics to religion, in mass social situations, in sport.
Let's talk about health for a moment.
Optimist explicatory style has a remarkable influence on health, in at least three ways.
Just to start with, an optimist's immune system is more reactive, so they stay in better health.
Plus, optimists have a higher self-esteem, and this brings them to take more care of themselves.
And third, they can count on a stronger social network, engaged as they are in longer-lasting love and friendship bonds, two things with a proven positive influence on health.
And what can we say about the business world?
Optimists - and here we quote professor Snyder, from the University of Kansas - have a proactive attitute which is key in business success, and we're talking about planning.
Optimists tend to seek sound evidence around them, in order to support their optimistic approach to life.
They're also more willing to persist.
And you know it better than me: when you start a project, you're driven by an idea.
Excitement is fundamental, at the beginning; but persistence will make all the difference over time.
So now we can start asking: is there a direct correlation between optimism and happiness?
Does optimism lead to greater happiness, because they have better interpersonal relationships, a better health and more success at work?
Or alternatively, people who for any reason were already happier develop a robust optimism over time?
Happiness, we know, it's 50% dependent on our gene set - and there's little we can do about it, but accepting ourselves.
But another 40 and 10% depend on circumstances and thoughts.
The question is, can we influence this second half?
In 2012, facebook already answered this question.
Facebook ran a study on 700,000 users, and they did it without their consent - but that's not our point here.
And in this study, they segmented the contents displayed in the users' newsfeed, positively or negatively.
They wanted to see whether their selection could affect users' mood.
And the results confirmed the hypothesis: targeted with negative contents, users in turn started to post their own negative contents, and viceversa.
All the data pointed at optimism as a key factor for success and happiness, one that can be induced and influenced. So me and a group of other guys, we had a dream and in 2014 we started considering the creation of a virtual space segmented with positive news.
News could be both aggregated or created by users.
And so Sunscious was born.
Sunscious is a positively segmented social network, working with geolocalization, which allows a real-time monitoring of the levels of optimism around the world, with an easy-to-use interface.
But segmentation wasn't enough for us: we wanted to create a space where virtual behaviours could be metered, in order to generate statistics that are socioeconomically meaningful, and of course optimist.
But we had a challenge before us: it's not easy to draw people into a new social network, you know it well.
Plus, we wanted to fight isolation which, as we've said before, is a critical component to close the loop.
So we invented an app.
The app is still in a development stage, it's been premiered in Las Vegas during the CES exhibition and it also received positive feedback from a United Nations agency, a few days ago in New York.
This app is a virtual space where in real time people at a few kilometers' distance can start cooperating on a problem to solve.
So basically I can post a help request, and turn a perfect stranger into my spontaneous hero, because this person can help me in real time.
This project, like many others, is just one of the countless shapes optimism can take, once research and innovation are rooted in it.
My team and I, we invite you to take optimism into account in your personal and professional choices.
Finally, at the beginning of this speech, we introduced ourselves.
Some of you may have a better memory than mine, and still remember their name; others may just remember the face.
But the point is, they're not strangers anymore.
And their problems are no longer that far, separated from us.
And this, believe me, it's a chance to be useful, to turn ourselves into other people's heroes - and mostly, it's a reason to be optimistic for the future.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Diving into life feet up, head down is hands down the easiest and safest birth.
As a mum-to-be I hope that Maddalena’s first gaze on this world will be upside down: feet in the air, head on the ground.
Too bad that later in life we can no longer keep this flipped worldview, as everything and everyone ask us to walk straight up, our feet rooted on the ground.
But 'what if' -
what would happen if we allowed ourselves to keep our flipped gaze in adulthood?
Four years ago, I finally understood that looking at the world upside down can offer unexpected second chances that ripple to the systems around us.
That's what happened to me when I reconsidered the word fragility.
I want to tell you my story. My name is Anna, I am 29 years old and four years ago I’ve become a fragility entrepreneur, convinced as I am that fragility is an unexplored and unassessed factor, both in our personal lives and in the way we do business.
It was 2013. I was a well-grounded girl with my feet stuck on the ground.
Feet on the ground, one can run fast, keep an eye on the horizon, stand up and duck down, react to threats and fight.
Keeping our feet on the ground makes us feel strong, and in many ways I did feel strong.
I had just graduated with top scores, and my strong resume filled with international internships, was supposed to be my stepping stone towards an international career.
In fact, I had never felt so weak.
An abusive, controlling relationship with a much older man had turned me into a prisoner.
My life, while externally so successful, was also so paralised, on the other hand, I couldn't even pick a pair of shoes without asking for his advice.
I was tormented: how can my strength and weakness coexist? How could I be buzzing with energy and broken at the same time? So powerful and prisoner? So free and chained?
Have you ever felt so fragile, so divided?
Can you go back to those moments when you felt defensless, scared, cornered?
Think about it, it will come handy later on.
I had been offended in that very space – love - where one should feel free to be fragile with no fear to be broken.
Then, one spring morning life came and called me.
A sentence touched my soul and I felt loved in my fragility, in my bruises.
So I decided to take on a path of deep dialogue – you name it, an encounter, faith, God, the meaning of life on this planet.
It’s exactly from this upside-down gaze on life that I started loving my fragility and getting back to my feet.
I started replacing the word ‘weak’ with ‘fragile’.
‘Weak’ means 'intrinsically strengthless'. A fragile person, or thing, can break if mishandled or mistreated.
Reconsidering my fragility not as a limit, but rather as a starting point, made me come to life again, and made me more sympathetic towards that world I wanted to study and defeat.
Where others saw weak chainrings, I saw fragilities to enhance.
So, I erased the word ‘against’ and turned them into ‘for’. Eventually I could fight for something, to flip the fragility paradigm.
Where others saw weak economies, I saw potential for recovery.
I left the world of international organisations and I returned home, to Italy.
This was an unlucky Monopoly round, a 'back-to-the-start’, in many people’s eyes.
Businesses had just started recuperating and the economy was, indeed, fragile.
But it also marked the beginning of my best - and luckiest - game so far.
In 2013, with a team of very young friends,
I founded Progetto QUID.
The peg, our logo, is a symbol of the chance to combine strength and fragility.
Progetto QUID puts back into the workforce people with a past of fragility, producing eco-fashion collections out of recovered textile surplus from the best 'Made in Italy' brands.
Since 2013 we have employed 75 people, each with their own specific fragility.
75 lives escaped a future of uncertainty, poverty and social exclusion
75 flipped worldviews, thanks to a job.
In more traditional business terms, we went from 5 to 75 employees,
from 90.000 Euros to an over 2 millions turnover,
from 2 to 10 collaborations with some of the most prominent Made in Italy brands.
In 2017 we shipped over 70.000 clothing items and 150.000 accessories.
After 10 collections, and 800 km of recovered textiles, we are proud to say that we've built a model where fragility is a starting point, rather than a limit.
Both the human fragility and the one of our ecosystem, represent the two axes of this new model.
But only when we add empathy, axis z, this model can create impact and volume.
But let’s look at the model in detail:
X axis: our capital is the fragility of human lives and the measure of our profit.
I’ll tell you another story.
This is Rosa,
Rosa joined QUID after enduring domestic abuse, as a child, domestic violence as a young woman, jail and addiction in her thirties.
Her interest in sewing machines sparked in a workshop she attended in a jail in Northern Italy.
The regular pace of hems gave her a sense of safety which she, and many others like her, had never experienced before.
Once out of prison, Rosa moved to QUID,
in a six-month internship. After a few months, though, she stopped coming to work.
A few weeks later she called us, asking for help.
She had started drinking again.
Next Monday, instead of firing her, we asked her to come back and stick with QUID.
Now Rosa leads a team.
Our mission is not maximising profit but maximising impact instead,
namely the ability to generate new jobs.
This is our upside-down gaze on the world.
Our statute requires us to hire ‘disadvantaged’ people, as the law calls them.
People with disabilities, addictions, inmates - For us, they are fragile in many profiles and strong in others,
Just like anyone else, sometimes in life.
Axis y - the planet’s fragility is our opportunity.
This is our warehouse. These fabrics are a side effect of the fashion system.
Designing clothes is no easy business, and it’s a creative process.
Usually, what fashion brands do is designing a collection, starting one year before release and trial after trial they select or discard tons of sample fabrics.
This inevitably creates
an overstock of gorgeous textiles, designed, developed and printed with care and love, with a tradition, a personality, often locally manufactured.
Our project transformed a weak link in the textile supply chain into an opportunity,
And thanks to the "Capsule collection" format, which are limited edition collections. in every season we can create a unique variant, according to the current leftover stock.
Here too we flipped our worldview on clothes manufacturing, textiles and resources, thus redeeming people, histories and fabrics.
Axis z - Fragility generates empathy.
We have many partners, many are famous Italian fashion brands.
To those who ask how we got in touch with them, we explain that often speaking about fragility openly, and honestly, made all the difference.
The reason is simple: addictions, family issues, disability, criminal behaviours are all symptoms of fragility.
But they're all also facets of our colourful, priceless humanity.
Who, among us, has never come across any of these?
And how many of us have maybe faced them within their family circle.
Fragility is what we all share – and is transversal,
women and men, Italians and foreigners, entrepreneurs and workers.
Along the way we often met top managers, lawyers, designers who told us they experienced, either first hand or through their loved ones, what fragility means when it’s only seen as a limit.
One of the key factors of our success is that we were able to speak out about fragility, especially the one we carry on.
Either before an audience, like today, or while grabbing a coffee in a break with a CEO, or engaging with our customers in one of our shops. Inevitably, our story raised empathy, empathy triggered action, action made impact.
And just like my turning point was where my own history of fragility and the world’s fragility met and I involved, through empathy, my team and our partners, that's the three-axis model we suggest.
But what happens if we focus our strengths on just fixing our weak spots, centered on our belly?
We become depressed.
What happens if we just focus on the system’s faults, often insurmountable, which we cannot solve on our own?
We become cynical.
And if we focus on both our limits and the world’s ones, we get both depressed and cynical.
Where my own fragility and system’s fragility meet, there's neither volume nor impact.
It’s just a barren land.
But.. 'what if'?
What would happen if we added empathy, namely the ability to involve others and build a team, or a partnership network, that believes in our project?
Then yes, you get action, action generates volume and volume has an impact.
I was a hurt woman - axis x – I decided to do something for other hurt women, like Rosa and myself, by recovering leftover textiles (Y axis) involving my team and our partners (Z axis).
That’s how QUID's peg was born. Today Rosa stopped drinking and got a permanent position,
which is our impact. This is the QUID model. And this is the model we suggest to those who want to change things and ask us where they can start from.
And going back to my story, I could have shut down keeping myself from new projects and new relationships, in order not to be hurt again.
But I did not:
in 2013 QUID was born and on May 28th 2016 I married Daniele.
And so, 'what if',
what could happen if each of you saw fragility not just a limit, but a starting point?
(Applause)
Amazement and wonder were decisive in changing the course of my life.
We are in Africa, in Niger in the Ténéré desert, in the heart of the Sahara. It feels like the middle of nowhere.
Imagine being barefoot, on vanilla-coloured sand under a boundless sky.
My amazement arose from having seen, in broad daylight the scene that Théodore Monod, the great 20th century Saharan scholar had described at night. "Long silent rows of camels, slip into the moonlit night.
A rather solemn, almost grandiose, spectacle.
These convoys evoke some unknown colossal beast like a kind of giant centipede moving with a slow rhythm but stubborn, indefatigable, implacable, irresistible.
Believe me, it was just like that.
A caravan made up of hundreds of dromedaries and a few dozen men, in the silence.
It was almost a sacred vision.
If you had also seen a similar vision, right in front of you you would have also stared in amazement, be struck dumb as they left behind them hundreds of trails in the sand.
So, it was my fascination with that vision that changed all my plans.
I began to wish, from that moment on to be accepted into such a caravan, on the salt caravan route.
I realised that the only way to observe the nomadic life was to start walking, to become a nomad for a while and head off towards the same horizons.
So, I began to study anthropology
and anthropology is a restless science.
It's purpose is not to be reassuring but to ask questions about man and other cultures.
Clifford Geertz, the most influential anthropologist in the 80s and 90s wrote, ironically, that anthropologists "hawk the anomalous, peddle the strange" They are "merchants of astonishment".
And so, searching for anomalies, oddities and astonishment, but not only those, I walked with the Nomads.
Literally living, with them, short periods of my life.
Anthropology calls them "mobile people" but they are commonly known as "Nomads".
And the Nomads are all shepherds who have to keep moving to stay alive.
They breed sheep, goats, cows, dromedaries, horses they are forced to go searching for grass, water, and salt for their animals.
Standing still, for these people, it is not compatible with survival.
In fact, Nomads occupy the hardest ecological niches in the world spaces where the vegetation cover is thin, sparse full of sand, pebbles, dust, thorns on which the Nomads manage to live a simple life, not as rebels but by accepting the harshness of these places.
Traveling from Africa to the steppes of Central Asia I often had the good fortune to run into these nomadic people.
I realised that the Nomads ignoring the rhetoric and romantic notions of these free, independant anarchists drifting through space without rules do not move at random.
They travel on foot across the surface of the planet but there is nothing casual, of improvised about it.
There is uncertainty, yes, because the risks are constant. unpredictable rains, daily hardships frequent emergencies and friction with the sedentary world are everyday problems.
In the past, these peoples have been looked down upon by those in sedentary societies as savages, barbarians, with a low level of civilization.
In reality, the lives of the Nomads are no less complex than those of sedentary people.
and these people's sometimes rugged character is in keeping with the harshness of their lives
which is one of displacement, it's true but also of patience awareness, and alertness.
A talent for listening necessarily develops in a hostile environment.
In fact, the Nomads are always on the alert.
They know how to read nature, smell the rain in the wind read the rustling of wind among the stones and the thorn bushes that to our ears would be imperceptible.
Furthermore, mobility means they have to travel light in body and in the things
they carry with them. Being a Nomad means having very little the bare minimum loaded onto the animals for every move.
And they are light-footed people, the Nomads.
One could say that they walk weightless as if their bodies and movements were adapted to the environment to the terrain, to the Earth.
For the Nomads, the Earth has never been a land to cultivate.
As Aeschylus said, "Theirs are lands that know no ploughs" and are just lands on which to walk leaving behind footsteps that, however ephemeral, write a story in the sand with an often poetic, oral tradition.
Now I would like to take you with me along with the three nomadic peoples I lived with.
Let's go back to the image of the salt caravan I mentioned at the beginning.
It was 2005.
I discovered, during a working trip to Niger one night, around the fire, that one of our Touareg drivers was the son of a caravan leader.
With his help, after nine months of rigorous training I was accepted into his father's caravan made up of 300 dromedaries and 30 Tuareg men.
1,200 km of walking was ahead of me across the heart of the Sahara through landscapes of fierce beauty of which the caravan itself was a part.
The Nomads, in fact, do not construct a landscape.
If anything, they pass through it.
The Tuareg, walking, create the landscape themselves.
They were authors of the dunes, the ridges, the sand, the wind.
An ever-moving landscape, which vanished a few moments after they passed by.
And I began to realise how strong, on such a long journey with no stops was our relationship with infinite space.
The emptiness of the desert became part of us, of our pace. it slipped between our legs, under our veils, between our fingers.
And, to be honest, it's still not clear to me if we were moving into this immense desert space or if it were moving towards us.
What was certain is that, in the caravan, we were never lonely, not even at night.
Being so many, and being together, gave everyone the strength to endure all that effort in the midst of all that living beauty.
There, among the sand, in fact, there was no room for superfluous things.
We never gave them any thought our minds stripped down to basics, in harmony with our surroundings.
With the Tuareg Nomads I experienced moments of tenderness with which, at a distance, watching me from under the turbans they kept an eye on me giving me such an unexpected feeling of security which I felt almost like a "stab of tenderness" as Ceronetti, the great 19th century poet, put it.
I learned about silence, both of the day and of the night.
The ability to stay silent for hours and hours a silence that nothing could disturb.
We crossed the border with Niger.
It was 2014 when I decided to follow the transhumance of Woodaabe shepherds, deep in the heart of Chad.
They live as Nomads in the spiny forest breeding zebus, cows with high lyre-shaped horns.
From sunrise to dusk, we followed eight journeys.
I had a horse for when I grew exhausted during the longest routes. I had a bull to carry my luggage, and a donkey to carry water.
The grass lasted two-three days, then we moved on looking for fresh pastures and waterholes.
On this journey, it was the children who taught me about their culture.
And it was the children, as much as the adults who helped me, mentally and physically, along this incredibly beautiful path. A beauty that was the foundation of their code of conduct.
There was beauty in their Woodaabe dances in their makeup, their jewelry in the ecstatic expressions of the faces of the dancers.
With the Woodaabe I shared the sacred rains of summer.
And the darkest nights I've ever seen where men and women were just as confident as in broad daylight.
I could feel this fear of the dark, this breathtaking fear of the dark and how much it can hide or reveal.
During those weeks, I learned the skill of turning an acacia tree and a patch of ​​grass in a shelter, in just a few moments.
I sat on mats where scorpions scuttled across I drank their green tea, I helped with the physical labour I felt their stubborn resistance, at first hand, to governments that would like the Nomads to leave the wind and the savannah behind force them into homes, fix them to the soil and turn them into craftsmen or peasants.
When I arrived in Gujarat, in north-west India in 2017 another sight disturbed me.
I was stuck in a service area when I saw something shimmering in the distance.
Gradually, that hazy image became a migrating group made up of a row of a dozen women dressed like medieval princesses.
Each leading a dromedary overloaded with household goods, lambs and children.
They were the Rabari, and they were walking along the hard shoulder of the road while huge trucks zoomed past their slender figures.
It was like seeing a fragment from another age flung into the modern world.
In that moment I swore that I would come back to look for them
because there was such a jarring contrast between the progress of those women and the crowded and deafening Indian highway that I could not ignore it.
And indeed, a short time later I found myself following sheep that had run into the road to spend the night on the highway huddled together with the flocks up against the fences of fertilizer factories walking with the camels under the monsoon rains huddling around the fire for warmth in the darkness before dawn and sharing the disorientation of nomadic shepherds with no more land to go to.
So, you may wonder what made me do all this.
Was it the dust kicked up by all the animals fine and perennial, especially in Africa?
Dust that drifts around lazily at dawn but at sunset, after hours of heat rises and impregnates everything, softening their edges?
Was it the magnificent slow motion of the caravans crossing the sand?
The absolute silence of that sacred epiphany?
Or was it the wells around which all Nomads converge sooner or later the thrill of touching water, again, so rare and precious?
Was it the transgression of the rules of silence that this water made possible?
Was it the pace of life, the erect and relaxed posture of men used to walking so far and not showing the strain of every dune and stone that conspired to slow them down?
Was it the ability to see what the eyes of sedentary people never see because only those who are used to it "see beyond" the void?
Was it the Nomads' desire for polite conversation to sweeten life that could be so merciless that even just a softer verb or adjective could make it feel cooler and less oppressive?
Is it that when you're living like a nomad you are most alive when you are moving?
And remember, planning, mourning wishing, imagining, rejoicing - even just thinking! are not the same
as when you are standing still.
Because movement transforms, impregnates, makes you more aware.
So, thinking of nomads today brings to mind the Greek notion of moderation.
A sense of balance, and harmony of elegance in thought and action.
Values ​​that, of course, the Nomads don't always practice but which is the ideal that inspire their lives.
And if nomadism has always been a life on the edge of the possible, today it is survival on the edge of the impossible squeezed by the powerful dynamics of the sedentary world blocked by increasing areas of cultivated land, impatient governments, and roads that cut across their routes.
Therefore, for me, the desire to get close to and follow the Nomads was something I had to do, something very compelling.
Because their lives are changing and this applies to many cultures at such a rapid pace that soon this way of life will have completely changed.
And I feel a duty to talk about this minimalist, fragile, and remote existence, most people are totally unaware of, so remote as they are are still on the move and endure in the most remote corners of the world.
So, to tell their story I had to live with them turning my feet and eyes into tools for knowledge.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Rosetta is a crazy mission!
Believe me.
Telling the story of its journey is like experiencing a Sci-Fi movie.
But it is also about learning about real people,
coming from all parts of Europe who get together and work as a group to build an incredible dream.
I have been lucky enough to live a great part of this journey together with many friends at the Mission’s Control Centre, and I'd like to share some episodes with you.
My personal contribution to Rosetta started in the year 2000.
I came to work to Darmstadt, that is a twin town of Brescia, for those who don't know, so it's a kind of a lucky coincidence.
On the first day at work I was given the Software Requirements, and all the papers for Rosetta satellite's Mission Control System.
First thing: let’s learn about Rosetta.
Rosetta is a mission to study a comet.
This comet is very far away.
The journey will take ten years.
Seven billion Km.
Once reached the comet, it will deploy a lander on its surface.
I started looking around me, convinced that this was a joke
from my new colleagues.
“They want to see how I react to this incredible story!” There was no hidden camera,
it was no joke.
This was really the goal of the mission!
And the great thing, is that now, after so many years, I’m here to tell you about a mission that has produced some fantastic science.
From an initial science fiction idea, we now have incredible results.
Year 2000 has been fundamental for me, as many things have changed.
But one thing has changed above all, which is my definition of Rosetta.
For all my life up to that moment, and maybe also for some of you, A Rosetta has always been only this.
(Laughter) This is a rosetta.
(Applause) From that year instead, and I suppose for the rest of my life, Rosetta has been a different thing.
Rosetta is a beautiful satellite.
It has enormous solar panels, sixty-four square metres of surface.
With a giant antenna, to communicate with Earth at those incredible distances in deep space.
On one of its sides there is Philae, the lander that will get down to touch the comet.
Let's start our journey, and Rosetta is at the top of this rocket you can see.
This is an Ariane 5, the most powerful of the ESA rockets.
The launch takes place in Kourou, in the French Guyana, and in that moment, in ESOC, in Darmstadt, we are sitting in the Main Control Room, and we are just powerless, since all depends on the launcher.
If it all goes well, the satellite will be left in its initial orbit and start an automatic sequence, to deploy the solar panels, and start communicating with Earth, then we can start operating.
But in such moments, imagine the tension for people who have worked for years and years, and everything depends on this moment.
So, we are simply concentrated on the launch, and hope that everything goes well and that we can start our job soon.
This is the start of an incredible journey.
I want to show you an animation that brings us through the phases of this 10-year long flight.
Look at the Earth.
Every time the Earth makes a circle around the Sun a year has passed, obviously.
Rosetta followed this white trajectory, around the solar system.
The journey has covered billions of Km, over a period of 10 years.
We would have never managed to reach the comet without a trick to increase the energy of Rosetta.
This trick consists of flying very close to planets and exploiting their gravitational pull, then steal some of their energy to be thrown into ever bigger orbits.
We did this three times with the Earth, and once with Mars.
You can see in the animation how the solar orbit of Rosetta becomes bigger after events.
To reach the final orbit without these fly-by manoeuvres, we'd probably never make it.
We would have needed tens of tons of propellant, which is obviously not feasible.
Look at the last fly-by around the Earth.
This has shooted Rosetta in a new huge orbit, that converges with the orbit of the comet.
Now Rosetta is so far away from the Sun, that even its huge solar panels are not sufficient to generate enough energy to keep all instruments going.
So the so called hibernation phase begins.
Think about it, now everything is turned off.
All instruments, the communication systems, all off. The only systems that remain active are a heater, to prevent the propellant freezing, and an alarm.
This alarm is set to ring two and a half years later.
So, we now jump forward in time and into the ESOC control room.
This is where Rosetta is controlled from.
Two and a half years have passed, and we have heard nothing from Rosetta.
We were using huge deep space antennas, one of 70 m diameter, in America and one of 35 m diameter, in Australia so one from NASA and one from ESA, pointed in space, at the location in which we believed Rosetta would have been, after all this time.
Imagine, we had to predict this orbit after two and a half years.
These are the signals that come from the two antennas.
What we were waiting for was a peak in this oscilloscope, showing the reception of a signal, from one, and probably from both ground stations.
The problem is that the signal did not arrive when we were waiting for it.
Nor did it arrive 15 minutes later.
And after half an hour, still no signal.
At that point, honestly, many of us started feeling so bad.
Imagine all these years of work, years of flight. If Rosetta doesn't wake up now, it's all over. We no longer have a job, nor a goal in life. Years of effort thrown away for nothing.
And we were already thinking what to tell the international press.
We were thinking
about us the day after.
And when it really looked like all was lost, the signal arrived!
You see this peak coming up in the two oscilloscope’s graphs, and when we were certain, look at the explosion of relief.
It is like going from hell to heaven, it is a… (Hooray!) …right, it happened this time again, I got goose bumps.
I have watched this movie one hundred, two hundred times, and yet every time I get shivers, because this is the expression of the feelings of a group, that has lived together for so long time.
It does not only take mathematicians, physicists, and engineers for this to happen.
It takes people that can work together.
For me, the human factor in a group is a fundamental component.
It is what has brought these people, along the years, to understand each other, to overcome difficult moments, to put aside personal problems and personal ambitions, for the intention of bringing this project ahead.
A very complex project, which only by chunking it down in pieces, assigning precise responsibilities and communicating with each other. could be accomplished.
I am really honored to have been part of this group, in the first part of the mission.
But let’s not forget that the mission is not over yet.
It's just begun!
What happens now is that Rosetta has to brake energetically, and start to “see” the comet.
And what we can see is more or less what we expected to see.
Insofar, the comet has always been represented essentially as a spherical, convoluted object. What we discovered instead diverges from this common representation.
The first close-up pictures were a shock for us.
This is a totally unexpected structure.
We were expecting a potato shaped comet, that turns around itself (laughs). But this has created incredible reactions.
The scientists were ecstatic, they were saying: “This is fantastic, the best thing we could expect!”.
And the flight dynamics engineers had their hands in: “We need to enter orbit, around this object, and land Philae on it.
This is going to be incredibly tough!”
Hefty discussions and heavy choices followed, but the most important thing was to get closer to the comet.
Imagine that the comet has a gravitational pull 100.000 times smaller than that of the Earth.
So, we can’t really talk about a proper orbit around the comet, until we are very close to it.
And we don’t know much about the comet yet,
about its mass, and its distribution.
So, the flight dynamics engineers from ESOC in Darmstadt have invented these pyramidal trajectories,
with 100 Km long edges, going down towards the comet.
Observing Rosetta’s trajectory and its modification due to the small gravitational pull, helped us to better understand both structure, and mass of the comet, and calculate how to refine the trajectory, in order to get closer and closer to it.
This step was very important. We studied the comet and we tried to get closer and closer to it.
We reached a closer distance, of about 30 Km. Remember, we're far as hundreds million Km from the Earth, the comet is about 3 Km, and we enter circular orbits of around 30 Km of radius around the comet.
At this point, it becomes possible to obtain Keplerian orbits, like those of satellites around the Earth.
So if we don't touch it, it stays there and, with very large approximations, obviously, it makes circular orbits.
The orbit lowered to 10 Km radius, in order to take many pictures, and map the comet’s surface really well, so to map the place where our friend Philae will land.
This phase was very critical, since we had to match opposite instances.
The scientists wanted Philae to land in some special points, typically the hardest to reach, and typically those where illumination and radio contact with Rosetta would have been complicated.
Philae can't communicate directly with Earth, thus uses Rosetta as relay while it's orbiting the comet. Then Rosetta sends them to Earth.
We are now, in my opinion. in the most exciting phase.
A fantastic moment.
In November 2016, the landing phase starts.
Rosetta gets into collision route with the comet, and releases the lander.
This is a picture taken from the lander Philae, upwards.
You can see Rosetta, the mothership up there, with its huge solar panels.
The next picture is even nicer. We can see the lander with the three landing legs deployed descending towards the comet.
The next pictures show the lander getting far away towards the comet.
And this is the last photo, I hope you can see the white dot of the approaching lander.
It took around seven hours for Philae to cover the 22 Km distance from the release point to ground contact, at a speed of about 50 cm per second, like a person walking.
Here we have pictures taken directly from Philae.
This is one of the three landing legs, and you see the comet approaching.
You can see its head,
we are almost there.
Here we are at 40 meters from the surface.
Note this stone, on the top right of the picture, we’ll use it later to identify the landing point precisely.
And now get ready to the story: this is the first picture of the surface of a comet, directly from an object we landed on it.
It had never happened before, and this is an image that will stay forever in all Space History books.
It's an achievement we all are very proud of.
It has been one of the biggest successes in our work.
Here, in my view, you see the apotheosis of mathematics, of human genius.
The distance between the calculated landing point and the real point where Philae touched the comet was less than 120 meters!
We are talking about 500 milion Km distance, on a three Km diameter comet, rotating around itself, with a period of 12 hours.
This is an achievement that still amazes me, and my respect for my colleagues and friends in flight dynamics is immense. I'll carry it with me forever.
(Applause) What happened?
Philae touched down exactly where we wanted, but didn’t stop!
In the blue circle is the footprint of Philaes' landing legs, which bounced and flew away, unfortunately.
Look in the red circle on the right, that is Philae!
Its last image, while flying away.
The systems for grounding it didn't work properly.
Have we been unlucky?
No! We have been extremely lucky,
because Philae bounced once more, and landed again on a point inside this red ellipse you see in the picture.
We really had it good, because with that minuscule gravity Philae may have bounced back into space, and its mission would have ended then.
Throughout, Rosetta has continued looking for the lander on the comet’s surface.
We wanted to localise Philae, and just a few weeks before the end of the mission we were able to find it.
In the bottom right of the picture, you can see Philae stuck in that rift, from where it accomplished all its scientific tasks.
Rosetta continued its mission for two more years around the comet, collecting a huge amount of spectacular scientific data.
Some incredible pictures.
Once at the end of its mission, it has been decided to let Rosetta land on the comet too.
These are the latest pictures of the comet from Rosetta, slowly descending to the surface.
We have collected scientific data until the last moment.
And so, you can see the shadow getting closer, until we get to the contact.
All instruments have been switched off, Rosetta won’t send any signal any more.
It is a moment in which we should have cried our joy, but I confess that most people at the control center had a sort of lump in their throat.
After all these years, seeing the signal going down and becoming flat was a really strong emotion.
And this brings me to the second fundamental point of my speech, and I wanted to point out that no individual nation has sent Rosetta to the comet, neither France, nor Italy, nor Spain.
It has been a cooperation between all these nations that created the system that did that.
ESA, the European Space Agency, brings together 22 member states, and through the joint effort, as well as through the best personalities in space science and engineering, succeeded in this great mission.
Think about that: only a few decades ago, in the middle of Europe, French, German, Italian, English people killed each other by the millions.
I believe that change is beautiful.
Today the same people collaborate on ambitious and marvelous common space projects.
Two things, the human factor and the cooperation, in my opinion, are what you should take away from this presentation.
And to better stick them into your heads, I have a very personal example that sums up these two things.
This is the garden of our house.
My wife is Spanish, I'm Italian and our children were born in Germany, So they have three nationalities, and fluently speak three languages; Italian, Spanish, and German.
When people ask them:
“Do you feel more Italian, or Spanish..." They just shrug their shoulders and say, boh! ["Whatever", in Italian]
They speak all three languages
as their mothertongues. Even I, after 20 years in Germany, can’t say I feel 100% Italian.
And yet, I’ll never feel 100% German. So, I have the great opportunity to say that "I'm European!”.
For me, this is just marvelous.
I wish my children, and yours, to have the chance to live, work, and learn in a Europe where all nations understand, and keep taking advantage of their diversities, to create something exceptional and incredible as the Rosetta mission,
because I really believe that we all need this kind of cooperation.
Thanks.
(Applause)
