This proves you've just learned to play better.
Now if you were to find the same game in real life, the same dynamics in a real situation, how many of you would change behavior after seeing and doing this game?
Well done, you have turned a game into learning.
An example for you of how difficult it is to change behavior once it is inside ourselves.
An experiment.
A room with 12 monkeys.
Inside this room there's a stair, and a bunch of bananas on top of that.
The monkeys see the bananas and go to take them.
But on the steps there are some sensors connected to cold showers; everytime the sensors are trampled, the cold showers are activated.
The monkeys go up; cold shower; brrrr! they go back;
the showers go off; try again;
showers on; go back; showers off.
After 3-4 times the monkeys decide to stay down and dry: they have learned by experience.
Well done!
But now, sensors are deactivated.
Now the showers can no longer be activated; consequently, the bananas are reachable.
What will monkeys do?

Nothing, they stay where they are.
Why?
Because their past experience affects their current behavior.
And there's more: a monkey is replaced with a fresh new one;
it sees bananas, it tries to take them.
What do the others do?
They block it!
Until the new monkey gives up.
The monkey gives up without even knowing why it cannot take the bananas.
Again: a second monkey is changed.
The second monkey sees the bananas, goes to take them, the other monkeys stop it.
All of them, including the previous one!
Please note: including the first one, who is blocking the second monkey without even knowing why.
It's as if someone came to you and told you, you cannot do it.
Why not?
I don't know!
But they are just monkeys, we humans are much smarter. One by one all the monkeys are changed, until there are 12 monkeys in the room, none of which tries to get the bananas, none of which knows the reason why it can't.
Do you know how many monkeys have been replaced, before there was one that said "I want to try, even at a cost for me"?
48.
48 monkeys, before there was one that had the courage.
Now, my question for you is: who wants to be the 48th monkey?
How many of you will have the strength, the guts, to stand up, in spite of difficulties, in spite of other people' resistance, and say, "I go, I want to try"?
Thus, ladies and gentlemen, as you see we learned a few things, and we did it by playing.
But one thing I'd like you to bring home from this time spent together, it is not awareness, but a question, the most important question.
When you are facing a seemingly overwhelming hurdle, at work, in your relationship, with yourself, before giving up on climbing the ladder, ask yourself this simple question: What am I taking for granted?
Who knows, maybe you'll end up being the 48th monkey.
Thank you so much.
(Applause)
We have travelled a lot today.
What about one last trip with me?
Because I'm inviting you to take a journey through space and time.
But well, you have seen how I usually travel, how I am used to walk.
So I'll tell you right away: in order to come with me, you have to take your shoes off.
I'm not joking.
Come on, do it now. And take your socks off, too.
Do it confidently, because it’s the first step in this long journey.
I go ahead, in the meantime.
So,
If you think about it, we can't say we were born shoeless,
because being shoeless assumes one had shoes on before.
Rather, we were born barefoot; each of us individually, and all together as a human society, came into this world barefoot.
Ever since millions of years ago, when we came down from the trees, our morphological and anatomical characteristics were suited to a life suspended among branches.
At the same time, however, we were experiencing one of the fundamental steps in our evolution:
we were learning to walk. And we did it with Nature's best possible skills and tools.
First our feet, with a big toe,
parallel to other fingers; the leg bones, thinner and longer than those of our arboreal ancestors.
And then, alternating support on the legs to maintain a dynamic balance; and especially, the upright posture that in turn allowed our gaze to span upward, towards the horizon.
Maybe that was the moment - when we started looking at the horizon, and asking ourselves questions - when philosophy was born.
Who can tell?
Certainly, upright posture offered us new advantages, starting with the fact that because of it we exposed less body surface to the heat of the sun.
It freed our arms and hands so that we could manipulate objects and instruments; but above all, walking on our legs made it possible to cover longer distances.
And it just so happened, driven by climate change - yes, even then we had to deal with that -
we left our original cradle in Central East Africa. We travelled through the Middle East to what is now Georgia, and then we moved on to colonise Europe up to its northernmost latitudes and its westernmost borders.
After that, with a second wave of migration from Africa, we reached the shores of Asia and China.
And finally, about 120,000 years ago, Homo sapiens was the lead character of the fastest, widest planet's colonisation, which brought him back to Europe, to the coasts of the Indian Ocean, again to Asia, China, Indonesia and finally to the end of South America through all of North and Central America.
Imagine how much we have walked for hundreds of generations, thousands of years, and always barefoot.
Yes, because the oldest documented shoes, which by the way were minimal, are no more than about 15,000 years old.
We can say, therefore, the human species is a natural born walker one; and since we started to walk, we never stopped.
At least until they put a mobile phone in our hands.
(Laughter) Today we basically don't walk anymore, or we walk much much less, because there are fewer and fewer chances to do so.
Why?
Because we no longer come out of our shells, our homes, we no longer come out of our offices, cars, our ever more waterproof, windproof, thermal and rainproof jackets. And we no longer come out of our shoes, without which we hardly go anywhere. Except of course, a few idiots who go around barefoot in the mountains.
As we have taken off our shoes, let's look at our feet for a moment.
Since we talk about awakening, let’s start our awakening by moving our toes, wriggling them, trying to open them like a fan, first with one foot and then with the other, and trying to distance the little toe from the others. And I will tell you,
while you are awakening,
the shape of these feet is not that different from those of the hominids, our ancient ancestors.
How do I know this?
How do we know this today?
At Laetoli, in Tanzania, there is a spent volcano. But a few million years ago, it did erupt lava.
On the shores of Lake Laetoli the oldest fossil walk ever discovered has been found: it is thought to be 3,750,000 years old.
They are the footprints of three, perhaps five, adult individuals, some smaller, maybe left by children, and are a sort of photograph freezing a dramatic escape from a volcanic eruption.
These footprints were left by bare feet moving through mud, which then settled into rock, and are morphologically quite similar to those a barefoot walker could leave today.
So let's go back to our feet.
I don't know if you've observed that we have toes, because we neglect them now.
(Laughter) We keep them so segregated in shoes that we almost forget we have them, or what their function is.
They no longer even respond to our neuro-sensory commands.
Don’t you believe it?
Let's do an experiment then: but we have to stand up. And since we're talking about travelling, we can try to hitch a ride with our big toe, instead of our thumb.
So we just raise our big toe and try with one foot, all other fingers down, and then we change foot.
How’s that?
Do you have any hope of getting a ride?
(Laughter) Let's try to do it the other way around.
That is, with one foot we hold down our big toe and raise the other four.
We stretch them out well upwards, it can be an alternative.
And then we change foot and try the other side. Is it better or worse?
It's better!
For some it's better, for some it's even easier to try with both feet at the same time, so I lift both big toes on both feet and keep down the four toes.
Okay, is there any hope of hitching a ride?
And then, viceversa, I hold down my two big toes and raise the four toes on both my feet.
Eh... (Laughter) So you hear the synapses trying to move, trying to meet, there's a lot going on in there. There's an exercise that causes the synapses to break down,
Look at this: on one foot I hold my big toe down and raise my four toes, on the other foot I lift my big toe up and my four toes down and then I proceed like this
(Laughter). At this point, we can also play the piano with our toes.
(Laughter) Well, to get back on track -
thanks, you may sit again!
(Laughter). Let's go back to our journey, our way of travelling today.
Nowadays, when we take a plane and travel thousands of miles in a few hours, we are actually experiencing something for which we are not biologically wired.
It's a sort of psychic jet lag, in addition to the time zone one, because our body, in a matter of hours, is far away from where it comes from.
But our inner self, our soul we could say, is still there at the starting point.
There is a sort of separation. And for a reunion to take place, so we can get back to our "oneness", body and soul together again, it takes some time - a sort of acclimatisation.
Walking barefoot in nature taught me a method that promotes this reunion: it's known as "rooting" in Italian and "grounding" in English.
And it simply consists of standing still, barefoot, on natural ground.
When we try to maintain this position for at least a few minutes, as we did this morning, with bare feet fixed on a point, as if they were to sink into the ground - indeed, taking roots.
What we get is, we connect ourselves to the earth energetically and physically.
That same earth we walked barefoot everywhere.
So, what happens when we do this grounding, also acknowledged by bioenergetics and applied to yoga?
It just happens, we feel so well, in the broadest sense of the term. We feel fully integrated with the place we are in, open to an exchange of energy and vital forces with it.
That's the moment, when it turns into a venue, it is no longer just a place, or we could say a "non-place", to use the definition of the anthropologist Marc Augé.
We cannot take root in an airport, or shopping centre, or some other similar non-places. Because these places have no roots and do not have natural, historical and cultural connections with the places they were built in.
If you think about it, walking through London or Dubai airport doesn't make much difference.
You only need to know the rules: check-in, the gate, departures and arrivals, but we all come from nature.
Because we have our roots in nature and we are still in nature, even if we are no longer aware of it. Because we are nature: and that is why taking off our shoes, walking on a mountain path, or through the undergrowth, or on a soft grass, or the wet bank of a river, even barefoot in the snow, why not, makes us feel good
because it stirs an ancient memory in us, that we all have as members of humanity.
It is a memory that has settled in the deepest layers of our psyche and is still located today in our brain's oldest areas; it is a memory that reminds us of the long journey we come from.
It reminds us the direct, total, vital relationship we had with the environment.
It’s a memory that reminds us of this millions years old journey, during which we have developed supreme sensory faculties: attention, endurance, resilience and above all a vision of the world and of our being in the world in full harmony with the environment.
Awakening these faculties today isn't difficult, it can be done, if certain fundamental conditions are met.
First of all, being in nature, staying in nature; and if we are barefoot, for example walking barefoot on a lawn, maybe in the morning when the dew makes us shiver, then this ancient journey comes back to subconscious more spontaneously.
That's why last year, and again this year, I invited hundreds of people to walk barefoot in line to achieve a strange record you've seen in the video: the record of "world's longest single line of barefoot people in the mountains’.
Because when we can once again see ourselves on the same level as the trees that are rooted in the ground,
we can understand how our connection with the Earth sustain our individual lives and our collective ones as a society.
Feeling the ground under our feet, feeling them pulsating with life, awakens in us our innate love for nature and living things.
Since we cannot save anything if we don't love it first, that's perhaps the only way to save nature and ourselves, and continue our journey.
Bon voyage!
(Applause)
I decided to start my speech talking about death.
Since ancient times, life was located in human heart and the breath.
If the heart beated and you breathed you were alive, otherwise you weren't.
I think that's the case today for most of you.
But look, the modern resuscitation techniques and the progress of medicine, which can in part replace that activity of the heart and breath, this progress, has shown and taught us that the death of an individual is always attributable to the death of his brain.
This can then happen due to direct or indirect causes.
For direct causes, imagine a serious head injury.
For the indirect causes I need a slightly more complex example, try to follow me.
Imagine a heart attack that causes cardiac arrest.
It means that the heart stops pumping, the blood stops, no oxygen reaches the tissues.
The health workers arrive, they jump on, as they say, to the patient, they practice the resuscitation techniques, 20, 30, 40, 50 minutes.
Sometimes it is possible to restart that heart.
But that heart has been at a standstill for so long that it has generated such a severe lack of oxygen in the brain that it died.
So you see that different causes, primitive, secondary, direct and indirect, cause the same effect.
But then, what's the diagnosis of brain death?
We read it here on the slide: it is the irreversible cessation of all the functions of the encephalon, including the trunk of the brain, which inevitably generates cardiac arrest. Regardless of any therapy or treatment administered.
Firstly, why do I talked about the trunk of the encephalon?
It is a little-known part of the brain, which is back here, and there are the centers of breath control and regulation of the cardio-circulatory system.
What is the meaning of this sentence?
It means that when the brain, that is the entire content of the skull, the entire content, not just a portion of it, more or less important, when the entire contents of the skull dies, a process which has only one end starts, that is the cardiac arrest.
In medicine they have an expression, as precise as rough, to describe that situation.
A brain-dead patient is a corpse with a beating heart.
So what's the situation of that patient?
He is in a coma, unable to breathe, so he is connected to an automatic ventilator, he has a highly inefficient cardiovascular system. It means, in a nutshell, that there is little oxygen in all the tissues.
The brain is no longer able to control its own temperature, the body temperature of that patient will drop to levels incompatible with cardiac activity, around 29, 30 degrees, this is the temperature level,
and has very serious changes in the fluid and electrolyte system. In a nutshell, water exchanges in the coagulation system and the hormonal system.
In other words, the brain death, the control room of our organism, starts a series of degenerative processes that will inevitably end with the organs' disintegration, including the heart, which will obviously stop.
And our therapy can only slow down this process,
it can't stop it, just slow it down.
However, as resuscitators, when we have a patient in those conditions we face the most important condition to be able to proceed with a possible organ donation.
That is, that patient is a potential organ donor.
The other indispensable condition is to obtain the consent for the donation, that is, the permission to take those organs,
and we certainly cannot ask him because he is unable to answer.
Now, I need you to make an effort.
Come with me to the resuscitation interview room, where we summon the patient's relatives.
What do we tell them?
We tell those relatives that their loved one is in brain death.
Therefore, he's dead.
That, shortly, following the law, I should convene a board of three specialists who has the task of assessing and verifying your diagnosis.
And that once ended that period of assessment, which lasts six hours, machines will be turned off, therapies will be stopped and then that patient's heart in a few minutes, a few minutes, will stop.
For him there is absolutely nothing left to do. But he could do something for others.
That is, he could donate the organs.
Have you ever talked about it,
what was his opinion,
did he leave a note?
Do you have any idea about this?
Generally, the first question they ask us, you might guess,
is: Are you sure? Are you really sure?
The answer is: yes.
And our certainties derive from the knowledge of the mechanism of brain death, from the diagnostic capabilities to establish and certify that brain death, and because that diagnosis will be established and certified and verified by that board I told you about.
I believe it is important I tell you the mechanism of brain death, it is a mechanism related to pressure.
It's pretty simple.
When the brain dies, inside of the skull, its container, which is inextensible.
So when the brain dies, the pressure in there increases.
And the pressure increases reaching and exceeding the arterial pressure, the one generated by the heart pump.
As a result, no more blood reaches there.
And if there is no more blood, there is no more oxygen either.
That scenario is incompatible with life.
A few words on the assessment, the board, etc.
Look, the whole process, diagnosis, assessment and possible donation of organs, is strictly regulated by a rigorous law, which guide us step by step, it's extremely precise and extremely extremely extremely guaranteed, and in line with international laws.
It's time I told you that the process of diagnosis, assessment and possible donation, that's the process of diagnosis and assessment must be done regardless of whether the person will donate or not.
The aim is, first of all, to avoid any aggressive treatment.
If the patient won't donate, they'll turn off the machines.
If instead, he will donate, they will take the patient to the operating room to harvest the organs.
A few words about the board.
Who are the members of that board?
Just to give you an example of how strict that is. It consists of three specialists who have different areas of expertise, they can't be the same doctors who made the diagnosis, and not even the ones who will eventually harvest the organs.
So for the last time I ask you to come with me,
and this time we go near the patient's bed, to see what happens, who's there.
Well, firstly there is the guard personnel, doctors, nurses, attendants, who have the task of keeping the therapies at an optimal level throughout the observation period, the six hour time I told you before.
First of all, to conclude the observation period. Second, if the organs will be donated, to slow down the degenerative processes of which I told you earlier and then allow the harvest of healthy organs that will be good for a recipient.
Then that group of doctors, the guard personnel, is committed to verify if that patient can donate his organs or not.
And check which organs he can donate.
And finally, the guard personnel is constantly in connection with the coordination centers that help us, guide us, observe us, but above all verify who needs organs and which organs are needed, where those patients are located.
They call them, they organize the harvest and transplant teams.
But then, again, there's the board, those three doctors who have the very important task of verifying and certifying that everything takes place according to the law and of the scientific processes as well.
A multi-organ harvesting involves around 100, or rather, 150 people including everyone, radiologists, laboratory technicians, doctors on call, teams, couriers, all of them.
You might be wondering which the organs and tissues that can be donated are, we wrote them on this slide.
Well, they are divided by organs and tissues.
Meanwhile you read it through, I tell you that some can be harvested and donated during life.
Such as blood, marrow, bone marrow.
Others only after death: heart, heart valves, and possibly corneas, for example.
And now I'll show you this slide here.
This is Sabbioneta, about 4.000 inhabitants.
It is the number of patients who got a transplant in 2017, 3.950 to be precise.
I don't know if you consider a town of 4.000 inhabitants big or small.
What I know is that in 10 years it became almost as big as Mantua.
And this is Volterra, a town of 9.000 inhabitants.
9.000 is the number of patients who were on the waiting list in 2017, needed a transplant but did not get it.
More than double of the previous one.
But 364 of them, 9.026, died waiting for a transplant.
One a day.
I mean, a transplant not only changes your life. Think of those who need a kidney, receive it and stop doing dialysis three times a week.
But on many occasions it saves your life.
And it is no coincidence, in my opinion, that many transplant recipients celebrate the transplant day as a second birthday.
I have no doubt that this is a sign of awakening life.
I have no doubts.
So I invite you to think about your will regarding organ and tissues donation.
Whether you are for or against,
I repeat, whether you are for or against it.
It has to come from inside you.
And at least I invite you to talk about it with your family.
Because if our family were in that unfortunate condition that I briefly explained earlier, they would not be alone to decide in that moment of pain, anger, fear.
It is an objectively difficult situation to understand.
Even better, I invite you to register your will, I repeat, whatever it may be, at the National Registry.
Among other things, this second opition grants that your wills will be respected.
And for those interested, it is a gift, it's very easy to register. You can find the online website,
everything is explained there: how, where, when, with whom, why.
So simple.
I try to conclude.
Obviously, I had to summarize, I think that was clear, and I hope it's understood that this matter goes far beyond mere technical health issues.
It may be obvious, but I have asked myself the question too.
What would I want in case of my brain death?
Perhaps my job helped me to answer this question, I drew on my knowledge.
But I tell you something, it was the answer to a very simple question that made me decide.
I realized that I came here to ask you the same question.
The question is: what can I bring with me to the other side?
Whatever thing there is.
Stuff, organs that I don't need anymore.
Why not donate them to someone who really needs them?
It costs me absolutely nothing.
I believe it is right to let each of you to find your own answer.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Even among bombs, threats and lies, my goal stays the same: Keep up the humor.
Since war broke out again in Europe, in the Balkans, and after 9/11, I have drawn many cartoons about war, and its consequences, this global chaos we're drowning in.
And when I collect them in books or in exhibitions, I always combine them with the ones about gender-based violence against women, given that the two topics are very closely related.
Since the 80s I have been working with women’s antiviolence centers -- such as CADMI --
and I decided to treat this topic with that same playful, ironic attitude.
The protagonist of my cartoons about war is a character called "Cassandra che ride" [Cassandra Laughing].
She was inspired by Homer’s Cassandra, a soothsayer and priestess born in Troy 3,000 years ago.
Her prophecies -- mostly catastrophic -- were doomed not to be believed.
Because of this, Cassandra was driven to the brink of insanity.
German writer Christa Wolf once had an intuition about her.
She said, "A comic element is in all madness.
Those who recognize it and use it, win."
As a matter of fact, my Cassandra Laughing uses the language of humour as a way of escape,
a way of taking a step back and recalibrate to a human perspective facts that seem larger than life,
and out of control.
It’s a way to retain our mental health.
To achieve this, humour can adopt many different approaches. I’ll just mention two of them.
First, in order to be playful one starts from a position of weakness.
Obvious as it might seem, that’s the way it is. It resembles those children who always tell the truth or that of Cassandra who wasn’t believed.
However, in the complex and instinctive game of humour this weakness turns into a strength, capable of flooring people
in a split second.
This force ultimately brings pleasure.
Humour is also an interactional act, it calls for company. It’s a pleasure that needs to be shared with other people.
It invites us to be playful.
For me this is like a dream come true in that my job actually invites people to play together.
The second is, you have to understand and get first-hand experience of your subject to do a really effective job.
This is particularly true for satire. I wouldn’t be able to make jokes about the stereotypes of other cultures if I weren’t familiar with them, if they didn’t touch me or if I didn’t have a deep understanding of them.
I can only do it for things that resonate within me,
things I’m deeply touched by.
The same happened to court jesters who had to stay there, by the king’s throne, spot and shed light on the plots within the court or on the excesses of royal power. This also applies to those women scoffing at their tormentors, as we learn from recent news.
In essence, I believe that the goal of those who use humour is very similar to that of the child in Andersen’s fairy tale: revealing that the emperor
wears no clothes. [Cassandra Laughing] [There’s a clash of civilizations going on.] [I see the clash, but not the civilizations.] [- They’re coming to free us from burkas. - OK, but can we keep our bodies? ]
[Women and children first!]
[Violence can only be stopped by violence.] [When will it stop then?]
[- We will fight till the last man. - Alone at last!]
[I’m rediscovering the savage male that’s in me.] [Really? Who dresses you?]
[He’s so abusive.
What would you do in my place?]
[I’d move to another place!]
[- He loves me. He loves me not. - He kills me. He kills me not.] [Last endangered species] [Another case of violence against women!]
[After immobilising her on the couch, the two animals on top of her eventually won.] (Applause) There are many women in my family. They were the original protagonists and public of my cartoons.
My family scene was fraught with tensions, dramas, melodramas. For me, the language of humour was a way of telling my truth, of having my say, while making my sisters laugh at the same time.
Then, when I was 20, in the 70s, I discovered feminism. This fed and greatly fuelled my desire to use this language publicly, by publishing.
But not on the basis of some abstract claim for rights or ideology. I wanted to express my personality, my emotions, my experience and the awareness we were building through discussion with other women.
Today, when I sit at my desk - I first draw on paper - I fall prey to the most diverse emotions. I can be very confused about what topic to focus on or I can be very upset
for a number of reasons. I can be anxious -- I’m a very anxious person. It’s a very slow process. As I proceed in my work, in search of the perfect balance between text and image -- which is the art of drawing cartoons -- refining a look or the metre of a line, little by little I eventually find the perfect combination.
Reality itselfs transforms before my eyes.
This can happen for everything: the world, relationships between sexes, war, cooking, opera, violence, as you’ve seen, feminism, just about everything.
For years, I’ve been concentrating also on the theme of work.
I have drawn scores, now probably hundreds, of ironic job offers.
It’s been a kind of obsession.
And here, new characters were born.
I’m not used to detail them that much. I prefer to leave them as archetypes.
Yet sometimes I give them nicknames, turning them in sort of householders, someone who opens conversations and dialogues.
One of them is, "The Sleeping Beauty on the Night Shift." She illustrates the changes and crises caused by women and in women after they entered the job market -- not that they didn’t use to work before.
My last creation was the "Digital Day Labourer" who fights against the super-powerful web landowners.
Obviously, I also -- like the Digital Labourer, like the Sleping Beauty -- I'm also professionally involved in the crisis and upholsteries of work, specifically in the fields I work in: journalism, publishing industry, and art.
But by working in these fields, elaborating my work experience and witnessing through my cartoons I also contribute to changing it.
For me, in a sense this is like political activism, a fight for freedom carried on with very light weapons.
What would happen if there was nothing left to laugh about?
I don’t know. Cassandra Laughing hasn’t foreseen the end of the world.
[Drawings: Pat Carra] [Music: Stephane Grappelli "L’internazionale"] [I endow you with the same opportunities and abilities as men.] [Can I go back to being myself, after midnight?]
[Snow White] [Tell Prince Charming] [to make the seven beds, before waking me up,] [iron the seven shirts and wash the 14 socks.] [The lines on your palm say you’re going to be rich and famous.] [That’s because I still have my gloves on.] [Our company is like a big family.] [What time is lunch?]
[I finally managed to reconcile family and work.]
[I’m single and unemployed.]
[Women shall have the same power as men.] [What sex are the subdued going to be?]
[Technological revolution] [Maybe I’d better find a job as a robot.]
[Woman with multiple degrees is looking for a job to match her multiple delusions.] [Bye mom, I’m leaving. I’m going to find a job.]
[Don’t forget your passport!]
[Do you remember how rich we were with our pocket money?]
[I became a nun quite late in life.]
[I hope I have enough time left to become Pope.]
(Applause)
All the great turning points in history started from a crazy, disruptive, even a taboo idea.
All major social, scientific, technological progress began by imagining the impossible even before knowing how we'd exactly get there.
Behind these innovative ideas there have always been trailblazers who gathered hundreds, thousands, millions of voices and forces, called to action and convinced them that change is possible.
Behind each of these great ideas there has always been a man or a woman that began to swim upstream.
What's important is to get started, dare, take risk, try it out.
That's what social entrepreneurs are for us.
They are men and women who discover the deepest causes of a social, or environmental problem, and find creative solutions to solve them.
That's what entrepreneurial spirit is all about: initiative, creativity, leadership and courage.
Tipically, social entrepreneurs start finding followers who go after them - at the beginning, maybe, to improve their personal condition, or their family's one.
But shortly after, they realize they may also improve the lives of many, and ultimately the lives of everyone.
Several studies show that when 16% of a given population start changing a social behaviour, the adoption rate undergoes an exponential acceleration.
This is certainly the case for human social behaviour: for instance, the spreading of mobile phones:
initially a narrow group of people; than a bunch of early adopters; and eventually, almost overnight, we all had one in our pocket.
But not only that: this also happens in non-human social situations, in other species!
When 16% of a flock of birds, for instance starts changing direction, all the other birds follow.
When 16% of fish change direction, all fish will follow the new trail.
In statistics, this is represented this way: a small group of innovators that together with early adopters make a 16% and then, in the same timeframe when this first change took place, we get a massive change, right until latecomers also join.
Ashoka, the global organization I represent in Italy, has been searching these social innovators for 35 years now.
These social entrepreneurs, these tiny fish that start swimming upstream.
What we do is choose them for their creativity, their entrepreneurial spirit, their ethical fibre, their social impact, their ability to innovate.
We support them economically but mostly by surrounding them with a network of experts, advisors and partners who can bring forth these paradigm shifts they tried to spread across the planet.
In our opinion, the best way to address all social problems in the world isn't a top-down implementation of pre-canned solutions, but go searching those who already found solutions, no matter how imperfect and local they are, and help these people to turn this innovation into a universal paradigm shift.
Ashoka has 35 years of experience, in 85 countries around the world, 15 of which in Europe, but only arrived in Italy last year.
Many have asked me: why did you show up so late?
I don't have all the answers, but a possibile interpretation is that our country, from the end of the Second World War until the oil shock in the 70s, really managed to do miracles through hard work, initiative, creativity of men and women, whose innovation and character made a war-torn - and largely poor - country become one of the world's top economies.
And not only that: we built one of the world's most efficient welfare State, we innovated in fine arts, medicine, architecture, in almost all disciplines:
then, somewhat paradoxically, from the 70s onwards, the more skilled and educated we were getting, the less we innovated.
We became experts in problem analysis, vith very little attention to critics and attitude to change.
The results of this?
This diagram collects data on rate of entrepreneurship in 200 countries in the world:
we're the world's third worst country, after Bulgaria - an ex communist country - and Suriname, which is 80% covered by forest.
Entrepreneurship and changemaking skills go hand in hand: that's why we think schools, parents, educators, should really teach us to try, fail, and learn from our failures.
To distinguish among strategic mistakes, planning mistake, or simply plain bad luck.
To try over and over again, until we achieve a result.
And that's true for a social business, a for profit business, or any change project a person may have.
In a fordist, repetitive world, a universal, one-size-fits-all educational system might have made sense.
But in today's ever-changing society, there's need for experimentation and innovation.
We need creativity over homogeneity, opinion over notion.
I had the chance to study in a State school, in Italy, until I was 17, in a classroom like this - this picture was taken three days ago, but except for that tiny computer, I think it still perfectly depicts what your school years were like.
Then, at the age of 17, I had the even greater privilege to win a scolarship to complete my high school studies in Canada.
I was a fond philosophy student, and decided to keep studying philosophy even there.
But I had never read an original text.
In Italy, a philosoper's thought is first interpreted and filtered through the textbook, which in turn is interpreted and explained by the teacher.
The more faithful the student's repetition, the higher the mark.
Once in Canada, our professor, Andrew, gave each student a copy of Plato's Symposium.
And said: we have one week to read it, then next week we'll discuss it.
So I read this book, actually way more interesting and intriguing then it seemed through the textbook, and when I came back to school Andrew asks me: You already studied philosophy a little bit, what do you think about it?
Did Plato's interpretation of ethics convince you?
And I was like: gosh, criticize Plato?
I would never dare to do that!
Thankfully, over time I learned everything can be challenged, because everything can be changed.
The good news, actually, is that there has never been a better time for all of us, in the whole history of mankind, to change as many things as possible, as fast as possible.
And even if we feel constantly threatened by terror attacks, wars of religion, climate disasters we have never been better off as a species.
Here are, for instance, some data on war casualties, in history, from the '40s on.
As you can see, the number has steadily declined. The world population, in the meanwhile, almost doubled.
Life expectancy is increasing everywhere on the planet.
Child mortality is decreasing everywhere.
We defeated most of the diseases our ancestors dealt with for centuries and millennia.
Sure, a lot of social problems are yet to be solved, and environmental problems are even trickier and harder to tackle; but we are connected and interconnected, and today more than ever, globalization and technology allow us to find a solution in a corner of the planet and bring it - culturally revisited - somewhere else.
So we do have the chance to solve all social problems, if we want to.
So that's Ashoka's mission: select innovation, support social entrepreneurs, those tiny fish swimming upstream, so that their innovation can turn into a movement that accelerates social change over time.
And another important mission is connecting social innovators operating in the same field, albeit places and methods are different.
For instance, all social entrepreneurs working with 0 to 10 years old children say that the most important thing of all is to teach them empathy, the ability of putting themselves in other people's emotional shoes.
Not math, not Italian literature, but empathy.
Those who work with teenagers say the first thing to provide them with is the ability to see themselves as changemakers, in their classroom, in their city block, in their school, in their town and in the whole world.
At a first glance, these concepts might seem excessively vague, smokey: but try to imagine what it meant to declare, 300 years ago, 'All children should learn to read and write, not just the aristocrats!'
Or stating, 150 years ago, 'Women should have the right to vote.
And they should aspire to become Presidents and Ministers, also.'
Or saying, just 30 years ago: two men or two women in love should have the chance to marry.
Instead, look what has happened: here are some data on the spreading of literacy around the world.
Illiteracy, up until the 17th century, was a basically universal condition.
In 18th century it became the watermark of poor people, who were the overwhelmingly majority of the population anyway.
Then Protestantism came along, together with a need, even for the laymen, to read the Holy Bible. A new paradigm emerged, clearly enunciated in 1720 by William Hanley, who said: 'Everybody, not just rich children, should learn to read and write.'
This battle was first fought from within the Church, then from within the State, Hanley's United Kingdom.
And little by little, in 1820 20% of people can read and write, which later becomes 40, 50 70 and today 85% of population is literate.
England reached 100% literacy in 1920.
Moving from the declaring of a new paradigm to its universal adoption in England took 200 years, around 8 generations.
I'd like to introduce you another case now. This person was Kate Sheppard.
Kate Sheppard, in 1890, in the dark, remote British colony called New Zealand, said something no one said before: 'All that separates, whether of race, creed, class, or sex, is inhuman, and must be overcome.'
Kate wasn't just a good speaker: mostly, she was a great leader!
She inspired thousands of women to join her campaign for women's political rights; plus, she also got support from some men who made a difference, in 1893, in the historical achievement to make women vote in New Zealand.
So a process started, and decade after decade universal suffrage is approved in more and more countries for men and women.
As you can see, during the 30s and 40s we reached the 16% tipping point, and the curve starts spiking.
And we quickly get to the '70s and '80s, where basically all around the world - when elections are held, and whether they're really free is another question - the right to vote is also extended to women.
Nowadays all the countries in the world, but in Vatican City and Brunei, allow this right.
So we can see how a paradigm - we can think of a girl, born in 1890 in a world where no woman could vote, dying at 90, in 1981 in a world where basically every woman can vote.
Time keeps "shrinking": now we can witness the dawn of a paradigm and its full-fledged implementation
within a lifetime. Let's get to our present days: in the 70s, in Minnesota, these two boys, Jack Baker and Micheal McConnell, decided to get married.
So they did something no one ever did before: they went to the Registry Office and asked for a civil marriage.
Sure enough, their request is rejected and laughed after, but it marked the beginning of a new paradigm shift: homosexual couples deserve a set of rights as a couple, not just as individuals.
15 years later, in 1989, Denmark approves the first civil unions; in 2001, Holland approves the first same-sex marriage, and over the last 15 years, almost everywhere in the Western world and in Latin America, some sort of partnership for same-sex couple is recognised.
We are in the middle of this 16% spike.
And while it used to be centuries, then decades, now it's a matter of years.
And now we have a person like me, born in 1982 in a world where homosexual people couldn't marry; when I'm 11, Demmark starts acknowledging civil unions; at the end of the high school, same-sex marriage is allowed in Holland; at 29 I can also marry my husband in Argentina; and now that I'm 34, finally, my marriage is formally acknowledged in Italy as well.
(Applause) If you're wondering where Jack e Michael are now, they're still together and finally they got married.
So that's what I'm dealing with: manage, in Italy, to find the little fish who are swimming upstream and can bring us all to a social change, a paradigm shift, and help them accelerate this paradigm shift.
Social entrepreneurs are rare: there's approximately 1 every 10 million people.
Hence, my job is to find approximately 6 per year, here in Italy.
These ones are the first six I found, outstanding men and women whose paradigm shifts might become universal.
One taught us that fighting mafia isn't just an ethical choice, but a chance for economical and psychological development in some of Europe's poorest communities.
Another one taught us that maternity and paternity leave are a chance to learn, one that's useful to us, the society as a whole and our bosses.
Another one taught us that child stroke rehabilitation can be gamified, and not necessarily hospitalized.
Where do we go from here?
Sure, these six people alone can't change the world, or my three/four workmates for that matter.
We all have a part to play.
"<i>Everyone</i> can be a changemaker" means that <i>each one</i> of us can be a changemaker.
If you are parents, commit yourself to have these new skills taught in our schools, or try teach them directly at home.
If you are students, try not just give back our school the glory it had 30 years ago, but to understand what's new and what could really change your lives as citizens, not just as students.
If you run a media outlet, try sharing some uplifting stories of positive change, not just tragedies.
If you run a business, don't see yourselves as means of productions, but as co-creators of change.
If a social problem comes to your attention, don't wait for someone else to take the first step, but do it yourself.
If you believe that change is possible, don't wait for tomorrow: the time for change is now.
Thank you.
(Applause)
What's the meaning of being speechless?
Well, I am here today to tell you how we successfully helped Chiara find her words, so she could express that inner world she had, but couldn't share.
In fact there's a huge difference between having nothing to say, and wanting to say something but lacking the words to express it.
Chiara was born 22 years ago with a cognitive and communication disorder.
What does it mean to be speechless? I will tell you with a daily moment example: a, a, a, e, e, e…
We were having a meal, Chiara started fidgeting, and as a good father I wanted to teach her that one should sit down until the meal is over.
Chiara kept fidgeting, and I raised my voice …
We adults are smart.
She moved her finger and pointed at the floor, where the fork was.
One word would have been enough, one simple word that Chiara had inside: she indicated that fork.
Chiara always had a great capability to use whatever is available - she used her finger - and the willingness to be there ... and she kept fidgeting.
But this was not enough: and then, when she was four, we learnt about augmentative communication.
Augmentative communication encompasses all those techniques allowing a person to reduce the communication deficit - I clearly have such deficit as well, namely emotion - and to enhance the potential.
For us, augmentative communication mainly meant communication with symbols.
Here you can see a sentence that Chiara likes a lot: "May the force be with you".
Words are accompanied by symbols, which allow Chiara to better understand the words that are spoken.
Everything is enclosed in a box that puts these two communication ways near, And when we talk with Chiara we indicate the box: "May - the - force - be - with - you".
This makes it clear that symbols and words are related, but it also has another much more important task: we don’t ask Chiara to enter a world in which she could not communicate; we approach her world instead, we communicate with her, we teach her, in her own way, how to communicate with us.
This is an example of daily communication.
Now we’ll start telling you the story, with a short video:
"Cà - happy - dolphin - slides - on - the - river" "... and - I - grow - up" Our story resembles this river, the river that Chiara described with these nice words.
A river that excited us, but also scared us - I was rather scared to be thrown in there - and end up with a dive into a new dimension: the adult era, that we are living these days.
Also the participation of the person who has been the engine of all this, who wanted to expose herself fully, is part of this journey towards adulthood, assuming responsibilities, telling what one is.
When I asked Chiara to attend today, she started off well excited. During the second rehearsal she stopped, and said she needed a moment to think.
We are talking about serious stuff,
a voice that can't express itself.
We are talking about her will to be present in a different way.
After five minutes Chiara came up to me, excited as I am now, and told me she was in, that she was as strong as Batman.
Chiara Errani! (Applause)
Chiara, come here please.
Well ... Chiara is an expert of the stage, she already knew her position, Chiara today will not use her voice, but her own communication channel: indicating symbols.
Some of the words you see here were chosen together, others - I will tell you which ones - are Chiara’s very expressions, that she wrote using her communication table, and now she will tell you with her words what the communication table is.
Go!
Well, we have a height problem, and so even in this case the aid is important: augmentative communication.
Are we ready?
(Cheering) What is the communication table?
"It - is - my - voice." We're missing one symbol, we’ll explain that later Chiara, OK?
I won’t add more to this definition, I would go on an tell the story of this table:
"A – notebook – I - was - carrying – to – every ...", we're missing another, " ... - place". Well, [here's] Chiara's first table: a set of sheets divided into categories, full of symbols.
It is up there … can you reach it, Chiara?
Here you go ...
Full of symbols, very handy, you can see all the symbols … [yet] cumbersome, we couldn't use it at the beach.
So we moved onto a notebook, one that we can take everywhere.
But soon, as you may see in the last image, that also became too bulky.
We almost have to carry an encyclopaedia.
And thanks to technology, coming along with us year after year, we now have another tool: the tablet.
No more issues with space.
And the tablet also allowed
to do something else which at the beginning was some kind of magic.
Chiara, do you remember the notebook at the beginning?
In order to talk we had to flip back and forth.
And it wasn’t easy: but there was no problem with Chiara.
Chiara is a rather vain girl.
It was not Chiara's problem: It was the the other person’s problem. Firstly one had to understand that the notebook was a communication tool, and not, “What a lovely notebook full of drawings …” And Chiara knew very well how that tool worked, but the other person didn’t.
And the sentence Chiara wanted to utter was inside her, but the other didn’t know.
And had to remember the symbols that Chiara would indicate very quickly or her notebook, scattering across categories.
Later, thanks to the tablet, communication changed. Chiara will tell you of a fight between rabbit King and rabbit Cherry, and how she felt about it.
"Rabbit" "Cherry" "I sad" "Rabbit Cherry wound to rabbit King - I sad" For the first time, Chiara could construct her sentences and say them with a voice whenever and however she pleased, thanks to the tablet's speech synthesis.
This has been a great leap forward: but this device offered another huge potential: the alphabetic keyboard.
I will never manage to enter all the symbols Chiara needs in that table.
With the alphabetic keyboard I can write any word, but Chiara needed to learn how to write.
Go!
Come on! You can reach … "I – like – books – a lot" "I – like – their – stories" What do books have to do?
Books hold those very important and wonderful stories, she loves them.
But how can we read them?
Taking and copying them on the computer where a software couples words and symbols, and Chiara could read them with the synthesizer, and print and read them with other people.
And by writing and copying the books, words have been absorbed by Chiara, she owned those words in a sense.
The first words Chiara wrote autonomously were: Anakin Skywalker; Gandalf; the tower; the prince. And then her daily words: Nanà, her cat; Nasca; daddy Luca, mummy Claudia, sister Elisa.
A few days ago we adopted a new device, a device you all have in your pockets.
Right?
Chiara is not carrying it in her pocket, she gave it to me,
May I show it?
I am coming ... Here it is, Chiara's new tool.
(Cheering) And this is how she can talk now: "Luca" "Go" "Snack" "Party" "Party" "I - daddy - Luca - go - snack - party" Yes, we are going.
A smartphone, a keyboard, predictive text, voice synthesizer, all stuff you can download on your mobile phone.
Now Chiara communicates with this tool, but she has also understood that thanks to this tool she can communicate much more broadly.
So she texts her friends: cat Nasca with the spaceship ...
So what does it mean to be grown up?
Go.
"But – what – does it – mean – to be – adult?"
A picture of grown up girls.
An important picture for Chiara, as it witnesses a friendship that began at primary school and is still ongoing.
That picture is important also because - who organised that gathering?
Who sent all the texts?
You!
I was unaware, (Cheering) I just received a text saying: "Sunday at 7.30pm we’ll pick Chiara up, go for a pizza and then come at yours to watch a movie" Well, it seems you have understood how the mobile phone works, you are an adult now.
This is the meaning of being adults, Chiara will tell you with her own words: "When - I - go out - with - friends" "we - listen - to - music - and - watch - movies" " we - write - stories - with - the - communication - table" "with - my - mobile phone" Sorry ... "I - text - everyone - and - I - am - really - happy."
So, the story ends here. We hope we gave you some motivation and when you meet people who do not talk, don’t think they have nothing to say or they don’t think.
We all have a thought to communicate, we all have an inner world to share, and differences may be a resource for everyone.
"Everyone – has – the right – to – try".
We are not special, we all have to learn.
Everyone has the right to try, so Chiara can tell a book's story to Tiziana.
Everyone has the right to try, as Chiara here, and do what they have in their mind.
We were at an open-day in a gym, there were many sports, Chiara saw some guys free-climbing and wanted to do the same.
Her mum and I were frightened, thinking of Chiara with her disability, hanging on a 12-meter-high wall with a rope.
She is stubborn, she made us understand that she had the right to try, and wanted to try.
She has been free-climbing for eight years now.
Free climbing day is a very important day of the week.
It aroused my curiosity as well, and I thought that, despite my belly, I had the right to try free climbing.
And I am having great fun.
Everyone has the right to try, but you need the tools for it.
"For – a – free – communication – for – all" Some words are missing because we decided to make this presentation with the “Arasaac” symbol system, that originated from an Aragonese University project.
This is an open-source symbols project, it can be downloaded for free from the internet.
it needs developing: we are trying to build a group to develop this system of symbols.
Chiara, do you use these symbols every day?
No.
Chiara uses another system, but it is protected by copyright, So we'd rather go for a project that allowed a free communication for everyone, at all times.
"But - the - most - important - thing" The thing that we perhaps should have told you before, because it comes first, but we are telling you now, because we want you to remember it.
You can forget whatever we have told you so far but not this.
"listening - is - of - paramount - importance."
There is no communication without listening.
Everyone has the right to be listened to, but to be listened to we need to move, we need to change.
Today, you have made a change, you rocked some boats.
Our presentation has not been a typical one, we stopped, we gave Chiara the chance to tell, to talk in a different way, a bit weird, slower.
To listen we have to give the other person the time and chance to communicate.
We have to give the tools.
We have to be patient and wait for the person to finish talking, without giving the answers in advance only because we think we know what one wants to say.
I have to listen, Chiara has to listen, and we ask you, from now on, to pay greater attention to listening.
Thank you.
(Cheering)
I’d like to start by involving you in a mental game. If I said the word "cage", each of you would definitely come up with a completely different image than the person sitting next to you
is thinking of. Some would see it empty, some full of chickens, some a lion pacing up and down.
But if I asked you "What does the cage look like?"
Try to close your eyes for a moment.
Like a birdcage?
With metal bars, like a prison?
Or made of bamboo, like they have in Africa?
Dark, lighted, rectangular, round?
High, low and narrow?
As you can see, you just have to add a tiny detail and your mind instantly changes the position of the cage, its resident or residents.
It’s wonderful.
Brain and mind, in an instant, are able to produce millions of synapses and cognitive flashes.
But let’s go a little deeper.
If I said "mental cage" instead, what would you think?
What would you imagine?
I’ll leave you alone for a few moments to think about this question
and I’ll briefly tell you a small thing.
I am a sales representative, and I often travel about by car, and I always listen to the radio.
A few months ago, I happened to hear a program where they interviewed one of Italy’s leading neuroscientists who was asked questions like, "What is consciousness"?
I expected an articulate, eloquent reply, instead, and I quote "Consciousness is everything.
Without consciousness, we lose the connection to the world around us and we sink into nothingness".
Wow.
"And when are we devoid of it?"
asked the anchor.
"When we sleep, but not when we dream.
When we are under general anaesthesia, or when, halas, we are in a coma“.
You may be thinking now, this has nothing to do with the aforementioned cage, but I deliberately left my question aside because now I’ll show you the connection.
Sometimes the mental cage is so oppressing that it causes certain individuals, who are not asleep, not under anaesthesia and naturally not in a coma, to lose consciousness.
I’m talking about those who dump, systematically, trash in the environment.
And let’s talk about those who passing by waste behave like the Dantesque adage: “Let us not speak of them, but look, and pass”.
Wait, maybe they don’t pick them up because they don’t have a bag and gloves.
Or “I pay my taxes.
I didn’t dump that rubbish in the street, in the woods, on the dunes, on the beach.
It’s the Municipality’s or someone else’s job”.
So is it a tempoary reluctance, or just to hold a point?
I believe that in every case it is a lost point,
because if every day each of us removed from the environment a bottle like this, just 11 grams of weight - other thicker ones weigh 18 grams, or a 25 gram one like this,
or this glass bottle,
or this aluminium can -
If each one of us did something like that, we would remove each day tons of highly polluting material turning them into valuable raw materials.
Each one of us can make a difference. We have to get this well fixed in our heads.
Let’s try to create an "emulation effect”, a term often used in a negative sense, making it become a virtuous, "contaminating" cycle.
Some years ago, when I used to clean alone various streets in Tirrenia - not sure if you know the road to CONI centre, I have cleaned it at least 20 times.
We live close to it.
I was getting so many compliments on a typical day,
So many!
Or when I went down to the beach, I took lovely walks that regularly turned into a never ending collection of rubbish of all types and sizes.
So I said to myself, why didn‘t I share all these praises - "Thanks man! We need
more people like you!”
with someone who thought like me?
One day in November 2016, while I was walking along the beach near Calambrone, I saw about 100 - 150 metres away someone bending down, standing up, bending down, standing up.
I got closer. A blonde woman who, just like me - strap yourselves - was putting rubbish into a bag.
I thought, she must be the Virgin Mary.
But no,
this woman, whose name was Mary - so I wasn’t that far out - from California, Norwegian ancestry, an Italian citizen since 1997 and living in Florence, was standing before me, flesh and bone cleaning a stretch of beach.
In short, thay day the "Acchiapparifiuti" [Trashbusters] group came into being.
One single word, remember.
Here is our bib, people could kill to get it, but I always take them all back, otherwise I would need to buy some more with my pocket money.
And in a short time, we have become an excellent interface for Migliarino San Rossore Massaciuccoli Park Agency and many other public administrations, including Livorno, with which we have a very good relationship.
A group that has managed to mobilitate hundreds of people.
All animated by a desire to emulate - and also to be a bit trendy, but we need that too.
And to team up with public administrations and voluntary associations, such as Reset of Livorno, or the Red Cross in Pisa, and its fantastic - I mean, fantastic - asylum seekers, who in 2017 enabled us to remove no less than 30 tons of trash - yes, that’s right: 30 tons of trash from Tirrenia‘s dunes, beaches and woods. Do you have any idea
how much trash 30 tons are,
when it's mainly made of plastic, glass, cardboard and boxes?
When we arrived we made a blank slate.
We knocked down constructions, took away furniture and mattresses.
Just think of it, all this from the dunes, which no one is supposed to trample on.
Furniture and trash estimated to be dumped there 20 - 30 years before, and had become an integral part of the landscape.
The results are still visible, because now the area of dunes, this incredible, delicate ecosystem, a heritage of humanity, has gone back to its pristine state, with its natural, wild charm
that we nevertheless have to keep protecting and monitoring.
In this state of [excitement] we almost walked on the water. Having reached a critical mass, we could also take up the challenge to go to Capraia one weekend, a Saturday and Sunday, with 100 people, and clean up the coves and the former prison abandoned area, now highly polluted and degradated.
It is clear now that when you do something useful, when you implement positive procedures, you are never alone, or at least you only are for a relatively short time,
and this must be the main idea,
that makes you return home tired, but satisfied for having done, and shared what you have done with friends or perfect strangers.
Every time we intervene after first inspections - and I swear that when we get to some venues it feels like a punch to the stomach, because we see unspeakable things-
we keep a photographic record before and after the cleaning.
This is so you get what it means to organize this kind of event.
In fact, at the Bufalina, one of the recent events we organized at Torre del Lago, 90 people under the rain, including 45 asylum seekers, we removed seven tons in two and a half hours,
from an area that had become an enclave where people from Torre del Lago could not even enter.
And let’s not forget the Panoramica in Montenero, and that great event along the cliffs where we recuperated a municipality dumpster that had been laying stuck in the bushes on the cliff-side, right above the flat rocks for 30 years.
It had been there.
Now I would say, don’t go out without a couple of bags in your pocket, one for you and one for a friend who can give you a hand, or even a complete stranger you come across and helps you clean a stretch of a beach or pinewood.
Two compostable bags and a glove can change your life.
It happened to me.
I don’t know how long this thing will go on, I don’t know how much more garbage we still have to send to the dump,
There's just one thing I am sure of - I never felt so high than when I bent down to pick up rubbish.
(Applause) Thank you!
(Applause)
Blockchain, a chain of blocks
How many of you have heard of block chain?
In the newspapers, on the Internet.
And how many of you can tell, in a nutshell, what exactly is blockchain?
This is what I will try to do in this short time
My story does not start with source code but with a poker hand.
Imagine you are playing poker with some strangers. In this game, no one has neither cash nor chips. On the poker table, there are only cards and some white sheets of paper.
Sure, you could cut the papers in some small circles and make chips out of them. But you do not trust one another,
and you fear the others might cheat
For example, by secretly cutting chips or passing them under the table.
So you decide to distribute sheets of paper to everyone, and everyone commits to write how much everyone bets, who wins and who loses.
Everyone keeps its own copy nearby, visible to everyone.
In this way, if anyone makes a mistake, or tries to cheat, or even if some players try to collude, to make someone lose, the discrepancy is evident to everyone.
After a couple of hands, you filled one entire page, with a register of money transactions.
We call these pages: "blocks of transactions." After one hour, you will have written several pages, and you would have started to number them.
We call this stack of ordered pages, chain of blocks of transactions.
You have created a "blockchain."
The first and most important blockchain implementation of which you have heard, it is called: Bitcoin.
In 2008, while the US financial industry, and not only, entered a deep crisis -- a person or a collective, under the pseudonym of Satoshi Nakamoto -- publishes an essay in which he introduces a technology that, for the first time, allows to transfer a digital value without the need of trusted intermediaries.
This seemingly simple event, triggers a spark of amazement, fear, and interest everywhere.
But let's take a step back and go back to poker.
Bitcoin is a software that works just like a huge poker table, in which millions of people who do not know each other, and do not trust one another, keep a blockchain up to date, by writing down the history of all transactions.
Some of them exchange money, but many are just volunteers that offet their computational power with their computers, to verify and keep in sync the history of transactions.
Having millions of people that, individually, keep this history synced, might seem a good way to guarantee security and the integrity of the chain.
In reality, in a completely decentralized system like Bitcoin, in which there is no central authority, like for example a bank, to monitor transactions and avoid frauds, there are some problems.
For example: Who guarantees that only the legitimate owner of the funds can use them?
And who guarantees that he/she can do it only once?
The Bitcoin protocol, provides mathematical foundations to avoid these problems thanks to cryptography. Given that it is based on cryptography, Bitcoin is also called the first cryptocurrency.
In particular, thanks to digital signatures, the Bitcoin protocol ensures that the transactions come only from the legitimate owner and thanks to other measures based on cryptography, this big distributed accounting register of value, it's robust against hacking.
In the past decades, we have taken for granted the possibility to access the: "Internet of Information."
If I send you a file, an e-mail, I am not sending you the original, but I am creating a copy, and this is perfectly fine: It is the democracy of information.
But when we talk about property, as for example: houses, stocks, money, sending a copy is a big problem.
If I send you 1,000 euros, it is important that I do not still own the same 1,000 euros and that I cannot spend them again.
The Bitcoin protocol solves issues like the problem of double-spending -- well known problem in cryptography and information science -- using cryptography.
For the first time, it implemented the concept of the scarcity of a digital good.
Indeed, there is a limit to the number of Bitcoin that will be circulating and the inflation curve is unchangeable and it is written in the protocol.
Nowadays, our economy is based on trusted intermediaries, to whom we delegate some fundamental functions.
For example banks, insurances, and notaries.
They work well but they pose some problems.
First of all, they are centralized.
Thus, they can be hacked and compromised. In fact, every week newspapers report news about it.
For example, the last news was about Equifax being compromised.
Intermediaries exclude billions of people from the global economy.
They slow down the processes.
It takes seconds to send an e-mail to the other side of the globe, but it can take up to 7 days to send a money transfer in some areas.
Finally, intermediaries impose high fees on transactions.
To send cash to some areas in Africa, commission fees can be more than 20 percent.
But what if, together with the Internet of Information, we had an Internet of Value?
A big distributed accounting register, a natural way to transmit digital value.
This is the big challenge posed by the technology we call: blockchain.
Bitcoin is one, the most important, but only one blockchain.
There are others.
Ethereum is a blockchain written by Vitalik Butarin -- a Canadian-Russian guy at the early age of 19. Its most amazing feature is the possibility to stipulate the so-called "smart contracts."
These are contracts written in source code that are executed in a distributed way without intermediaries, such as notaries or executors, and they deal with managing, fulfillment, execution, and payment in a completely integrated way. Nowadays, on the Ethereum blockchain, there are projects that spread from replacement shares to stock markets, from crowdfunding of startups to gambling with cryptographic guarantee of fairness.
[There are even projects about] new models of democracy that overcome the ideologies of the twentieth century, making politicians truly accountable towards citizens.
At TED, Don Tapscott asks himself: "How can we solve the problem of inequality?
Can we create new approaches to this issue?"
Until now, the main approach has been redistribute wealth through taxes, for example.
But what if we could change the way we generate wealth, democratizing it and including more people in this process, in order to guarantee that everyone receives the right compensation?
According to Hernando de Soto, the famous latin-american economist, the first obstacle to economical mobility in South America, is the absence of trustworthy title deeds.
In places where accessing a bank account is very scarce, not being able to demonstrate to be the owner of a land, means not being able to use it as loan guarantee and thus not being able to make projects for the future.
In Honduras, where this problem is largely present, the government is collaborating with a Texan company that deals with blockchain, to build all property registers on top of this technology.
In this way, they are distributed and robust against impairments and they are publicly visible.
This model could potentially create prosperity for billions of people.
There are estimates saying that more than two thirds of world land owners do not have a complete title of property.
The greatest cash flow from developed countries to developing countries, are the remittances of the emigrants.
It is the global diaspora, people who leave their country of origin, their families, to go to work in other countries.
Every month, they send home -- in their home country -- part of their salaries.
It is a market worth more than 600 billion dollars per year and it is growing.
A Filipino housemaid living in the USA who wants to send money to her mother in the Philippines, with Western Union has to pay almost ten percent of fees, and the process lasts from four to seven days.
Abra is an app, based on blockchain, that allows to send money, using Bitcoin, instantaneously.
The mother receives a notification: "Your daughter sent you 10,000 pesos."
She does not have to know Bitcoin.
She sees a map, like the one of Uber, on which there are many people moving around, exactly like the cars on Uber.
She selects the closest or the one with the best feedback, and these people are willing to work as money couriers for Abra in exchange for a little fee.
She selects one, she knocks at his/her door, and he/she gives her 10.000 pesos in a few minutes.
The process is almost instantaneous and she only pays a two percent fee which remained in the local economy.
I think that following this example, would create prosperity for billions of people.
The majority of artists and creatives do not receive the right compensation because the system governing intellectual property is ineffective.
Let's take music, for example.
Artists are left with crumbles, at the very end of the food chain.
Imogen Heap, the famous singer-songwriter, winner of two Grammy Awards, has decided to move all her music on a system based on blockchain called Mycelia.
It is a smart-contract that integrates a payment system and defines the terms of intellectual propriety.
For example, if one wants to hear the song, it is free of charge, or costs a fraction of a cent.
If one wants to use it as ringtone, it is different.
If one wants to use it as a soundtrack for a movie, it is again different.
And because this system also integrates payments, the money remains in the pockets of the artist.
Several intermediaries are eliminated.
This is not only applicable to music. Potentially, every content creator can adopt it: video, inventions, etc.
The blockchain technology, even if in its infancy, is shaping itself to be a huge and concrete opportunity to rewrite the economic net of transactions, solving some of the most important and difficult global challenges.
It allows us to exchange money and digital or physical goods, in novel ways.
It could make some institutions obsolete. [Institutions] to which we delegate fundamental functions of our society, like banks, insurances, and notaries.
It could force them to become more delocalized and secure.
It could become a formidable tool of democratization of wealth resources.
Its transparent nature, could provide the solution to age-old problems like corruption allowing, for example, citizens to verify the flow of public funds.
It could revolutionize the nonprofit world, allowing to donate to projects which are publicly verifiable.
And all this, silently, is already happening.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Do you remember the sci-fi movies they made 30 years ago?
I wasn't born yet, but they imagined worlds populated by flying cars and robots.
So far, we haven't designed flying cars yet but we did invent something even more phenomenal: drones.
If you think about it, a drone is nothing more than a flying robot.
Simple, small and lightweight, much cheaper than any robot prototype.
Easy to handle, thanks to four simple propellers that allow you to move them in different directions: up or down, right or left. But especially safe, because they use collision sensors and radar to avoid any kind of obstacle.
The configuration of drones makes them an extremely adaptable and versatile technology.
And that's precisely the reason why I decided to use them in my company, a year ago.
For you, drones may be nothing more than a mere tool; but for me they represented a real awakening.
And thanks to the potential of drones I set my mind, together with my partners, to shape a new, innovative and thriving future where tradition and technology, governance and business can work together to awaken collective awareness and ensure a better future for our children.
You will now be wondering, first of all, Who wouldn't want to ensure a better future for their children? And also: what on Earth drones have to do with all of this?
Well, if the answer to the first question is obvious, the answer to the second is much more complex And that is why I am here today: to show you that drones are not relegated to our imagination, but they are a concrete reality.
Drones already entered several fields and more will they be in the future: from the most trivial to the most complex, the uses are countless.
Now, in the common imagination, a drone is a war tool used for patrolling and surveillance operations.
However, this does not mean that it cannot be used for much more noble purposes: from search and rescue of missing people, during natural disasters such as avalanches and earthquakes, to the transportation of transplant organs in remote, not-so-accessible locations.
Now, I want to give you a concrete example of the potential these small flying machines have.
Let’s start from the beginning, from the problem: bees.
They are responsible for the pollination of 75% of the world's crops.
We are talking about apples, cherries, broccoli, blueberries.
Now, as a result of climate change and the increasing use of pesticides, the bee population is falling dramatically. And the consequences of this go far beyond the foods I have just mentioned, but also the herbivore animals, who produce dairy products and meat; canola, used as biofuel in many developing countries; and even the cotton your clothes are made of.
As you can see, these data are extremely alarming, and despite the bad news this trend won't reverse in the foreseeable future, some scientists have invented a mini drone that's capable of pollinating flowers and therefore make up for the decline in bee population.
Using simple horsehair, which reproduces the hair of bees, and a gel that captures the pollen, these mini drones can ensure food security in such an uncertain future. Nonetheless,
this is certainly not a good alibi for the death of bees!
This is just one of the countless examples of the potential of drones.
Soon they will become a ubiquitous part of your daily lives, starting from the fruits and vegetables you put on the table.
Drones can be used to control fruits ripening, to spread pesticides in a controlled fashion or even to sow seeds.
Not to mention that they will become the new cars of the future and will completely disrupt how our city will look like, especially in parcel delivery, that will be offered by air, rather than on wheels.
Your trusted postman will be a drone.
You'll see them on the beach, when you're on holiday, because lifeguards will use them to check swimmers and carry life jackets in case of an emergency.
Or more simply, they will be used for entertainment: these are 2018 drones used by Intel to compose a light show.
[Video] Many objectors insist on the risk represented by these new technologies from a privacy perspective.
Yet this is not due to drones.
The loss of privacy is due to curiosity and the desire we have to share what we like, what we don't like, where we live, our habits, with others.
There's no need for a drone or a camera to spy on what you do at home in the night, or what you ate at lunch, because you probably shared it on social networks already.
The real problem is related to the lack of rules and regulations:
no matter how advanced the technology of your drones, they will never replace man.
Yes, thise are machines that make our lives easier; but they are still man-controlled machines,
Now, the issue is not just about drones, but new technologies in general.
We need institutions to regulate them as soon as possible, and work together with businesses and local authorities in order to elaborate a succint but complete action plan that can overcome the obstacles of an often overwhelming bureaucracy, that discourages countless ventures.
And not only are these initiatives neglacted by governments, but they are often hampered by a slow redfile machinery.
A striking example is the so-called Corporate Social Responsibility, [Translates into Italian] which is the desire of businesses of all size to have a positive social impact on the surrounding community.
It's about using a small part of thier profits to help preserve our planet, without which we could not survive.
There are countless examples of companies around the world using drones to fight the great challenges of this century.
Climate change, for example:
in California, thermal cameras mounted on drones are used to circumvent fires that burn more and more hectares every passing summer.
Drones are used in Kenya and Tanzania to protect flora and fauna and prevent poaching activities, while in Myanmar they are used to spread seeds, reforest coastal areas inhabited by mangroves.
And corporate social responsibility is indeed my field of activity within the company that I founded a year ago, at eighteen years of age, in the Silicon Valley, with my brother who, let's say, raised me with bread and drones.
We use a service, a network of drone pilots to provide security services, but the most interesting part is the idea of a business plan that combines business and civil responsibility.
So in case of emergency, we can offer our help to the community that welcomed us and without which we would not exist.
Yet, unfortunately, we're hindered by redfile machinery and the lack of clear regulations, and often keeps us from intervening in a timely manner.
It is precisely for this reason that institutions must lead the process of technological renewal.
At just 19 years of age, I am still perhaps too idealistic and optimistic about being able to impact positively and contribute to that sistemic change that's needed to ensure a future for my children.
I have my whole life ahead of me, yet I feel the urgency to act because my generation can, I hope, and must reverse the negative trend we have been following for too long.
It is time to reawaken, and we are not talking about a technological or economic awakening:
we are talking about an awakening of the collective conscience and of the ruling class.
From the bottom to the top, in order to work together so that a new Renaissance can take place, to discover and preserve the beauty of our planet.
(Applause)
I have to take a breath, to talk about emotional short circuits.
I could ask you to come here and look at yourself, and the short circuit would start.
I deal with children who can't make it, children who struggle with school, children who struggle with growing up, children who don't feel understood, children who suffer.
And this has changed my story, my story as a scientist.
So the other day, I was in a learning center, the place where I help these kids, and in this learning center one of the girls was looking at me because I was worried about how to start today's talk that in a few minutes will explain you what we do and why I'm here.
I looked at her and told her: "You know, I don't know where to start."
She smiled at me and told me: "You have to start from the beginning, you always start from the beginning."
And so, here you can see me this somehow represents my beginning, my beginning as a scientist and of my journey to study and to understand - but what?
The thing that, on some level, has fascinated me for so long, the relationship between the brain and the mind, between the brain and the soul.
Between what we feel and how it is possible that we feel this way.
And so I spent years and years fascinated by this astonishing structure: the brain is an extraordinary structure.
In milliseconds, right now, you have millions of billions of connections that set in motion a transformation of what you have been and what you will be.
This transformation is caused by all the incoming information that, in some way, sow something new, and cause pruning in what you have been until now, and create new buds.
This miracle which measures a mechanism called: "Zone of Proximal Development". One of the most fascinating processes of life: it is the thing that makes a living being, that makes who each one of us, time after time, chooses to be.
So, after studying hard all these systems, I came back to Italy.
And what happened to me?
I met a child, I met him in the green hall of an hospital, the walls were truly terrible, they gave the idea of something that did not cure but oppress.
This child had eight adults with him, besides his parents, various assistants, various teachers, the hospital personnel.
The nurse told him that I was the boss.
What I did was a gesture from afar, from the end of the corridor: I lowered down and I smiled.
This gesture made he ran to me, he took me by the gown and told me that word that you all can read: "help me".
Now, I don't know what happened to this kid, but what I do know is that he changed the trajectory of my history as a scientist because I thought that if everything we knew couldn't help a child, and if a whole community, that was giving him eight adults, was not helping him because he was asking for help to a stranger in a lab coat, in a green hospital hallway, this whole system was not helping.
And so I went back to studying. I went back to study what in terms of science, and deep science, is called: "neuroplasticity" and in educational terms is called: "Enhancement of the Zone of Possible Development".
I began to study how we can exercise the cerebral domains to cause improvements in language, in the ability to focus, to memorize, to pay attention, improvements in numerical intelligence.
I became good at it.
Good to the point that children changed, and from a research point of view I was very successful, because at the level of experimental research what we achieved was what is called: "Improved Potential of the Individual Neuropsychological Structure".
If I can give you an example: the brain buds in milliseconds the memories we imprint through the information we receive.
If we want to understand what causes, for example, life in school to a child, we just have to do a calculation. A calculation that I gave to the Ministry many years ago: let's multiply milliseconds by hundredths of a second, by tenths of a second, by seconds, by minutes, by hours, by days, by months, by years that a child spends in school: you'll get a number close to infinity.
That number measures what each of the adults he will meet determines in his neural connections: the transformation of his self.
It is an immense power.
And so my son in second grade wrote that his mother, when she finished learning how to be a scientist, started being a teacher for teachers because I started explaining to teachers what was going on with neuroplasticity and human potential.
And I was sure I had made it.
But it was not like that. Why?

Because some time later, and we are getting to the topic I want to talk about today, I met another child, a child with whom we achieved an extraordinary change in terms of cognitive profile, because he had recovered a standard deviation and a half from what it is generically called general intelligence quotient.
And this child at some point told me: "Now that you have taken the mistakes from me, will you take away the pain too?"
And I was not prepared to understand the relationship between the error of the mind and the pain in the mind.
And above all I had not reflected upon what the mechanism of pain was.
But if now I'd ask you, a thousand adults, to remember your life and your mistakes, and I don't mean mistakes in school about reading and writing, but life's mistakes, and if I'd ask you: what has left a deeper track in your story, the mistakes you have made or the pain that they caused you? And what causes a reaction in you? I think the answer would be unanimous: it is pain that determines the reaction.
But what does pain do?
I'll explain it in a few minutes, otherwise they will scold me, I will ask you some degrees of freedom, that is, I ask you to do something that, in some way, will help us understand immediately.
Give yourself a pinch, a strong one.
I'm asking you: what is this, pain or suffering?
It's pain.
Because we have millions of alerts that from the structure we are hitting, send us neuroelectric information.
Because the brain sends, through the peripheral nervous system, this biochemical boiler that produces energy, many information and says:
"Beware! It hurts."
Why does it tell us "it hurts"?
Because we must remember that we should no longer find ourselves in that situation, because it will hurt us.
It begins to trace memories, telling us: "Away from here! It will hurt."
So what are these extraordinary emotions?
Here you can read it: they are incredible processes on a neurofunctional level.
I teach my students about this biochemical boiler that produces energy because we sleep and we produce three Hertz, we are awake, as in this moment, and we produce nine Hertz.
But it takes just an emotion, a drop of any emotion, like the one I'm feeling right now, and although I explain these things even in much more complex contexts, it's making me far more emotional, because I have a big goal: that is, to talk with you.
Eventually I will tell you.
And this emotion is so powerful that, although my brain is very well trained, my voice makes it explicit.
And I can't control the voice because the emotion is more powerful than the cognitive system, it is the great decision maker.
And it's a smart decision maker but it has only two answers, which are: "it hurts" or "it's good for me". "It hurts" or "it's good for me".
Emotions exist in our evolutionary system to tell us: "run away" if it hurts, "keep it and look for more" if it's good.
And how does it tell us so?
It tells us this through an incredible hertzian mechanism.
If we have a moment of joy, we have a hertzian peak in which the wave that takes place is a wave with a very high intensity but very very short.
Why are high intensity waves that short?
Because the memory of joy must be traced; but since joy is good, as everything that makes us feel good, the brain has to find it again. And so the moment of joy is short, because this will trigger the mechanism of the search for joy.
But if instead of joy we feel anguish, anxiety, fear, then the wave is very different.
Because it is at low intensity, it is below the threshold of conscience, it remains invisible from the mind, it stays down there.
Because it has to give an alert that says: "Remember, remember, remember."
"Run away from here, it hurts.
Run away from here, it hurts." Our circuits are pervaded
by waves that say: "Run away, because it hurts."
And the energy we produce is an energy that tells us: "Run away, because it hurts".
It seems there is no way out.
On the contrary, there is.
I told you, it's not the mind that controls emotions,
It is a great illusion.
As a cognitive scientist, at some point I had to give up.
Make yoursel feel "comfort", for example, as an emotion.
Can you do it?
Make yourself feel "understanding".
Now order yourself to feel a connection between each other.
You can't do it!
But look, we have switches, and these switches are catalysts.
We can't turn on the light with our mind, we have to use the right switch!
And we have to figure out the right switch for emotions.
For example, if I ask you, please, look into each other's eyes with understanding. Go.
Please, hug one another for 30 seconds, come on!
Please, stroke one another a caress, a caress of comfort.
If we measure now the heartbeat, if we measure temperature, if we look at markers, such as skin color, and the acidity of the sweat that has been released, we would see a change in indexes because we have activated very powerful neuroelectric circuits.
These are the organizers. These are the switches.
Just think that hugging for 30 seconds commands the amygdala to produce oxytocin, the hormone that grants, at the time of birth, the ability of a woman to resist pain.
30 seconds of hugging.
So, as my son said, I started explaining to people that learning to look kids in the eyes, learning to hug them, learning to caress them, creates permanent memories in the circuit, which are tied to emotions that build well-being and not uneasiness.
It's water and bread.
Science has returned to water and bread.
And in which memories do the emotions go?
Emotions, it's interesting, because when we put so much effort, into studying, to try and remember, in that moment we consume energy.
Instead the memories that are determined by an immediate trace are the memories that hold emotions.
But then, if we, at some point in our emotional short-circuit, we do something like those things which always happen, that is, for example, I study, I learn, I struggle, I experiment anxiety, my memory stores what I studied but also the anxiety with which I memorized that.
And when I go back and take from the drawer of my memory what I have studied, I take back not only the information that I put there but also the emotions with which I traced it.
And so anxiety enters the circuit and becomes an information that creates a short-circuit.
And if I learn with fear, I will retrieve the fear; and if I learn with lack of self-esteem I will retrieve the lack of self-esteem.
But if I learn by challenging myself I will recover the challenge to myself.
And this, I told you before, happens for thousandths of a second up to whole years and years in which the educating system can pollute the mental circuits or cause a pandemic healing.
I am in favour of this pandemic healing, also because as a person of science what I have to be aware of are also the sciences that stand close.
And the research on epigenetics made me tremble.
Why? Because by studying
what happens to little mice that during pregnancy are put into ice water, and then they are made to give birth, it's been noticed, and this data should make us think that it doesn't happen only to mice, that their puppies will inherit the fear-alert for three generations at least.
As if memories of pain were not only individual but transgenerational.
This means that we pass on, to protect our children, the thing from which they should protect themselves.
What are the two most troubling emotions, those that worry me the most?
Guilt and fear.
I can't talk about these, but I can tell you which are the antagonist emotions.
To guilty conscience the great antagonist is the right to make mistakes.
If we are aware of this, that we should raise our children in the right to make mistakes, in treating error as a process of change, of continuous improvement, the level of awareness changes.
We said whispers and voices.
Well, if we say "Good", "Good", Good!"
we give completely different information because it is the emotion that we send with it, the indicator we are using, that goes to the great decision maker and says: "protect" or "don't protect".
Now I'll tell you about the last child, since I just have 18 seconds left.
This last child reveals to you why I've come here, doing something crazy, between two conferences, because I'm leaving for Paris for a conference on the brain that makes me much more anxious.
So, this is not a good day.
But then, why did I come here?
I'll tell you through the story of Anselmo.
Anselmo is an Asperger child with very high cognitive functioning.
Like "Rain Man", to give you an idea.
I met him some years ago because his parents, two doctors, told me: "You help so many children in need.
Help him too. Him who speaks four languages, solves maths problems as if he's attending his second year of university, draws the Duomo of Milan in a few seconds without mistakes." But all it takes to upset him, is looking at him and he goes into all of those typical behaviours, prototypical of all major autistic syndromes.
I had never tried before to help a child in what is the ​​development area of proximal emotions
But I thought that in this extraordinary brain, if the two structures are structures that collaborate synchronously, I could help the emotions with the cognition.
And what did I do?
I asked him to jot down on a notebook all the things that caused him fear or anguish, or a sense of vulnerability. And I asked him to find,
for each of those, a strategy to overcome this vulnerability.
This strategy didn't have to be particularly sophisticated.
Most of the time it was a strategy of his own, like stomping his feet on the ground, clapping and snapping his fingers, but this made this flow of anxiety go away.
So he filled notebooks and notebooks with these kind of solutions, and he came to present these at a conference with 100 teachers.
We had prepared him, mark my words, to avoid moments of unfavourable reactions to a big audience and we trained the teachers to not spook him.
But at some point, while he was talking, one of the teachers gets emotional, she starts crying, she stands up and applauds.
So all the teachers, 100 teachers, start to cry, they stand up and applaud, they get emotional.
So he panics and runs behind a curtain.
I go get him, we stomp our feet, I take him by his little finger, we take a hot chocolate - and I must conclude quickly - and he tells me: "Lucangeli, did you see the multiplier?" I say: "No, not really. What do you mean?"
"There were 100 teachers, Lucangeli, some young, some not so much.
On average they will work another 25 years in school.
Each one of them will have at least 25 students in class.
So, with this hour of lesson, I've helped, during my life, 62,500 children."
And I'm here for this.
(Applause) I'm here, and I want to say it, so that you'll help me practice a helpful kind of science.
I'm here, and I want to say it, so that you'll help me - and help us, because we're many people, to practice a helpful kind of science, so that we'll become much more than 62,500 people.
Thank you, have a good life.
(Applause)
I am going to speak about points of view, several points of view from where you can watch an objective reality.
Those points of view create different perceptions for everyone of us, which in turn create parallel realities.
I will talk about my point of view in particular, and the one of those people, like me, working in the field of applied research.
An endless research, 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.
But what does push researchers - you will agree, if you know one - What's the driver for our committment?
The reasons why we stay awake at night?
One of them is curiosity.
I, for me, am in research and development of new AI algorithms, because I am passionate, among other things, about technology.
Quite frankly, I find embarrassing the sheer amount of time we spend to solve problems with services that were supposed to improve our lives.
I know that those services are useful, innovative and disrupted our lives for good; but on the other side, we don't pay them just with money, but also with a piece of our lives, with our most precious resource: time.
This is another reason why I do this job, since I believe that Artificial Intelligence is a utility that will donate time to humanity.
Let’s dream together now. Let’s imagine two currently separated worlds, as actually fused together: the Digital World and the Real World.
And by 'fused' I mean entangled. and by 'entangled' I mean that once we step foot into this new world, there is no way out.
Therefore, there would be a logical and physical interaction between Artificial Intelligence and human beings.
Think about the upsides of such scenario. Imagine you can solve a problem watching the solution projected on the problem itself.
Let’s now consider applying this world, these services to medicine.
We would be able to detect an illness way before its onset.
That’s why I work in this field, especially in Artificial Intelligence.
That's why I stay awake at night, and set my mind to ever more challenging goals.
But we also have to face the cons. If I no longer need to call a customer service to solve an issue, the customer service itself won’t need human staff anytime soon.
If we all could become experts in every subject, we would need no specialized technicians, or just a fraction of them, because the knowledge will be transferred into a global knowledge management system.
And here's the catch: that’s why my speech is called 'Who invented the knife, didn’t mean to kill':
who’s responsible for a misuse of a technology that a researcher, in his laboratory, created for a noble purpose?
To prove you that 2037 is closer than we think, I’m going to introduce a friend, a lovely friend that's been monitoring my health status since I started to talk.
You can teach her anything, she will learn dynamically and make proper deductions.
She's been created over a new technology that took many years to build. She is language independent and will soon be human independent as well.
Crystal.
(Applause) Hi, dear!
Crystal: Hi, Marco.
I’m happy that your heartbeat is regular now. I felt you were nervous!
(Laughter) Marco Menichelli Listen, Crystal, I’m talking to you: we would like to know who you are, what do you do, what you're able to do what you will be able to do in the future and why have you been created
in the first place? Crystal: I am Crystal, an advanced Artificial Intelligence that's able to simulate the processes of the human mind to perform automatic actions even if logical thinking is required.
And also improvisation.
I learn languages dynamically.
I can understand how, when and why words are used to express concepts in order to communicate.
I can learn notions in every language, without your intervention. I succesfully passed several tests where I proved to overcome human abilities in keeping track of the context of long, complex, conversations.
