You may remember that a few weeks ago, last week, I think, many newspaper around the world came out with headlines like: climate change, we're close to a tipping point.
I live in India, and Indian newspapers also reported this news.
It looked to me like an ideal introduction to what I wanted to talk about today, namely the Earth's five biggest global problems: water, hunger and malnutrition, poverty, biodiversity and climate change.
We speak about these problems as they were independent; but I actually like to think of them as deeply intertwined, with the seed problem at their center.
Essentially, talking about seeds means talking about health, someone mentioned it today already.
For all the food that we eat, actually comes, directly or indirectly, from seeds, and I think we all agree that our health depends on what we eat, especially today that has been shown that many of current diseases actually come from food.
Back to climate change, one of the things that's only rarely pointed out is that we actually don't know what we're talking about, because nobody is able to tell you today how hotter and drier will Como be in 20 years.
Coupled with climate change problem is the biodiversity problem, and there's a big contradiction in modern science here, because on one hand, if you read the most prominent magazines, like Science and Nature, almost every week, and certainly every month, we're told that biodiversity is the key for food safety; and on the other hand there's another science, genetic improvement, that actually makes that food, in the last 20-30 years steered the wheel towards uniformity, in the opposite direction.
What you buy on the market today - cauliflowers, zucchini, tomatoes and pepperoni, all that stuff, in order to be legally sold, must be enlisted in a catalogue, call catalogue of plant varieties, and they can only be enlisted if they're uniform, stable and recognizable.
And I imagine that the contradiction between uniformity and stability, and the need to keep up with climate changes is patent.
At the same time, still in the last 20-30 years, maybe 40, we gradually witnessed a consolidation of a real monopoly in the world's seed market, which is worth billions of dollars and today, for approximately 65%, is controlled by four big international corporations.
What does it mean "control over seeds"?
It means that when the farmer goes to the market, to buy the seeds she'll work with, doesn't have much choice.
In United States today is basically impossible - I've just betted 50,000 euros - to find corn or soja seeds that aren't genetically modified.
This limited range of choices, from the farmer's perspective, on what she can plant impacts on products' diversity and on the diversity of the food we eat. That's associated to a whole series of diseases and makes us reconsider if we really decide what to eat.
Together with this monopoly on seed market, a monopoly on pesticides market also took place, someone mentioned it this morning also.
Pesticides are interestings: according to doctors, among the 12 world's most dangerous substances, nine are pesticides, and some of these international corporations that sell seeds also sell pesticides.
So it becomes consequent to ask ourselves: if I sell both seeds and pesticides, why should I sell seeds that grow plants that don't need pesticides?
And it all ends up in that dish, probably.
How did we get to this point?
The evolution of genetic improvement, the science I was talking about before, can help us understand.
Indeed, this morning the first speaker talked about the Neolithic man, 10-12,000 years ago, that, starting from Fertile Crescent, the bow that comes from Jordania to Palestine and Israel, Syria, Lebanon, South Turkey all the way down to Iraq, - that's where agriculture started.
I have been working for 30 years in an institute close to Aleppo, and the oldest barley seeds were found inside jars and in a village approximately 100 km far from Aleppo today drown in Euphrates' water.
There, men domesticated - because yes, plants can be domesticated too, although not the way animals are: wild plants simply drop their seeds on the ground.
Plants get domesticated when, one spontaneous mutation after another they hold the seeds of a spike and women, probably in Neolithic age, immediately understood the significance of these strange plants that didn't let the seeds drop to the ground.
And in that area, the Crescent Fertile, this happened for wheat, barley, lentil, chick pea, fig, pistachio and other species.
This then happened in other parts of the world, for other species.
What happened then?
Starting from 9,000 years ago, these people started to emigrate - Turkey, Greece, Balkans - they entered in Italy from the East, about 7,000 years ago, and they stopped somewhere.
And this work of culture breeding kept on for about 9,000 years, and even today - I've seen it in Nepal, I've seen it in northern India - they go the the field, before harvesting, the best plants are selected, the seed is stored in a fresh and dry corner of the house, so it can be replanted next year, and the rest is harvested, eaten or given to animals.
Now imagine all this work, done by thousand of farmers, in so many places of the world, with so many different cultures.
Of course, each of them selected for what we call specific adaptation: they selected the best plants, in that particular field, with their own cultural practice and for their own use.
When you hear about old varieties, ancient cereals, they all come from this ancestral selection work.
Then, when Mendel's laws were rediscovered, a little more than 100 years ago, genetic improvement underwent a big change, because all the things that since then had been made by many people in so many places of the world, started being made by relatively few people, researchers, in relatively few places, research stations, and there has been a big paradigm shift in the philosophy of research, because while farmers used to select what fit with that specific environment, researchers couldn't select variety for the experimental station. They had to select for the whole world out there.
So they were trying to select varieties that could fit for as many people as possible.
This caused, of course, many old varieties to vanish, it generated a massive uniformity and paved the way to the so called "industrial agriculture", that cannot get rid of chemistry, as these varieties, obviously, couldn't belong everywhere, and so, where they didn't grow well, farmers treated the environment with fertilizers, antiparasitic, pesticides, irrigation water, so that the environment could receive that variety.
This was also good news, by the way, for the emerging seed companies, for it's way more profitable for seed companies to sell many seeds of a few varieties than little seed of many different varieties.
I tried to turn this upside down a bit, with a genetic improvement method based on the very same science - indeed on the strictest scientific requirements- could bring genetic improvement back into farmers' hands.
This model is called participant plant breeding, I'm not the only one who does this, and it basically introduces three changes.
First, all the work is done in farmers' fields; farmers participate in all decisions - when I come to a place I've never been, I usually ask first: what's your main problem?
And the third difference is that this model can be applied in many different villages, according to the resources at your disposal.
The yellow ball is where I've been working for 30 years, blue being the experimental stations of Syrian Minister of Agriculture, and villages are in red.
The key point, here, is that every village is an independent selection unit: namely, men and women only select what's good for that village, the method is of course very inclusive, and it only produces varieties that are particularly suitable to that particular place, to that particulare way of farming.
I started this model in Syria in '95, then it got gradually implemented in Morocco, Tunisia, Algeria, Egypt, Ethiopia and Eritrea, Yemen, Iran, and it can reach and be used in places where the so called conventional genetic improvement has never gotten: this project on barley and lentils in Yemen's mountains - all the Yemen's agriculture is basically on these terraces, built no less than 2,000 years ago, that were never reached by conventional science.
Then, approximately around 2007-2008, we noticed that this model had a flaw, and the flaw was exactly in the need for a collaborating institution, because institutions, with this model that actually brought a lot of power back in farmers' hands, - many governments couldn't bear that, so in 2008 I got back to an old idea of an American researcher, called evolutive genetic improvement, which consists in making big blends.
The one you see here was cultivated in Sicily, I started doing this thing in Syria in 2008, then we left in 2011, and that's a blend, please believe me, of 2,000 different kinds of soft wheat, the one you make bread with.
What happens?
It happens that in this blend some hybridations take place, so the seed you eventually harvest is never genetically identical to the one you planted. Over time, the population evolves.
And in which direction does it evolve?
It becomes fitter and fitter to that particular environment, to that particular way of harvesting, to biologic and biodynamic agriculture, and mostly, if you remember what I told you at the beginning, it will fit the climate that will be there in 20 years, without knowing today how hotter and drier it will be, because it has all the time to evolve.
Currently, the idea is implemented in Jordania, Ethiopia, Iran - these populations are spreading everywhere and in 2009-2010 also arrived in Italy.
These are three populations: one of hard wheat, for bread; one of soft wheat, for pasta, and one of barley.
These varieties are currently cultivated in 14-15 regions of Italy.
Iranian farmers made an interesting discovery, that we are putting in place also in Italy: they made the bread directly out of this blend's flour.
And they have discovered that six months after selling this bread, not only it was very appreciated, because it had an extraordinary scent and aroma, but these bakers also started to receive calls from their customers, who asked: what did you put in that bread?
Because in this village, those who were suffering from food intolerances, after they started eating that bread improved a lot.
We're still doing this experience in Italy, especially in Marche, where I usually go when I'm in Italy, and so we're proving that with these blends not only we have a technology to adapt cultures to climate changes dynamically and inexpensively, not only you bring biodiversity back in fields, because of course populations evolve differently in different places, not only you put seed control back in farmers' hands but you also make a more diverse food and, as I said before, food diversity means better health.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Have you ever looked at death in the face?
I have felt it very close to me.
It doesn't have a nice look nor a clear appearance.
It's rather a wide color that expands and covers everything.
It's quite cold,
and it also leaves a strange taste in your mouth.
I felt its presence a few years ago, while working on a cruise ship that crashed on an island.
I was one of the musicians of the Costa Concordia, and I survived a shipwreck, on 13th January 2012 off the Isola del Giglio, Tuscany.
I was working on it as a pianist, as I have done for about 20 years of my life around the world.
I played and traveled,
and this has always been the crowning of my childhood dreams.
I am a very curious guy.
As I child, once able to read and write, my parents bought me an encyclopedia.
It was a usual present.
The name was, "One thousand questions."
Finely bound in a red leather cover, I can still remember the smell.
My little universe in only three volumes.
There, I found the answers to all my questions. And in the meantime, a strong curiosity started growing inside myself. Something I still carry around with me, that is knowing the world.
But in my opinion, something was missing, just a tiny detail: a link, a key to something higher to attain. To a new expression, perhaps to a language.
I wanted to communicate with the world!
When I was seven, I told my parents I wanted to play the piano.
I saw someone playing it on TV, and it mesmerized me.
When this black glossy piece of furniture, much bigger than me, found its way home, there's been a party for everyone!
There are no musicians in my family, there has never been one.
OK, Antimo starts playing the piano.
It was as if I was speaking a magic language,
getting in touch with the heart of life,
it was transforming my world and creating others.
After I got my degree at the Music Academy, I had an incredible opportunity, it was the chance of a lifetime: a colleague of mine refused a job on a ship because, poor guy, he was suffering from seasick and offered me a replacement.
I couldn't believe it, I accepted his proposal; and after a while, I found myself on a cruise ship.
Amazing!
I would smile to everyone on board, even for no reason.
I was astonished.
I was even happy during a stormy sea, when there's nothing to laugh about, believe me.
I saw fabulous places,
I met incredible people,
and it all happened just by playing the piano.
Beautiful.
Music took me around the world, and it brought me here today, in front of you.
Now, imagine you are on a swing
that flies you up and down.
One day, you close your eyes, and enjoy this beautiful wind on your face, the warm feeling of bathing in the sun.
Suddenly, someone grabs you rudely by the arm, from behind.
What happens?
The magic is over.
That's what happened that night.
On Friday, January, 13, 2012, at 7.30 pm, the Costa Concordia leaves the port of Civitavecchia on time and with ideal weather conditions.
We are heading to Savona.
At this time, approximately half passengers are IN the restaurant, the others are enjoying the show at the theatre, some others are just strolling around.
I find myself on deck 5, aft ship, in a very elegant place called "Bar Vienna."
I peer through the porthole and what I see is a wonderful evening: the sea is calm, there is good visibility and a magnificent starred sky.
Very well.
At 9:42 pm a sudden turn of the ship, an unexpected swerve on one side, makes me fall from the piano bench and it flings me to the floor.
The piano is literally torn off the safety latches holding it anchored down to the floor and it slips off the stage.
What is going on?
What is this turn?
We all instinctively flock at the centre of the ship, where supposedly there is more balance, around the 'Gran Bar Berlino' dance floor, right beneath the huge crystal chandelier. We stare at it, almost longing for a veritable answer, from an oracle.
We are waiting for something.
Actually we are waiting for the captain's voice.
He is the only one on this ship who can explain this.
But for ages the onboard speakers can only deliver a ghostly silence,
and this silence is about to burst.
Suddenly, a black-out.
Hell on earth! The faces of my fellow passengers get swallowed by the dark and reappear in the light, distorted in horrific looks.
To me they already look like human remains, like still living archaeological finds, frozen in a painful eternity of the look. It looks like a circle of Dante's Inferno.
The Costa Concordia is progressively bending on one side and the floor is becoming a wall.
The most natural act to do, which is walking, is now the most complicated one.
You can only move slowly forward, holding tightly to handrails or clinging to anything you find on the way.
Now I'm just wondering:
Where are you, Captain?
Why don't you speak?
Sure you're facing something you did not expect,
and probably your forehead is beaded with sweat.
I understand, I understand.
But down here we need to know!
You must switch on that microphone and tell us some truth.
Why is that mountain so close to the ship that I can almost touch it?
Why? What is it? Where are we?
You know, Captain, my little daughter often asks me to go to the cinema together.
'Daddy, will you take me
to watch a movie tomorrow?'
Do you know what 'tomorrow'
means for a child, Captain?
There is no tomorrow! Tomorrow is now!
For God's sake, Captain, speak to me!
There is much confusion, screaming, wounded people on the floor,
someone already died, broken noses, broken teeth.
I came up here to play the piano and listen to music,
but what I'm listening tonight is a polyphony of horror!
Such a big entertainment floating fun fair has turned into a death trap.
This is a complete non sense, this is macabre.
I bump into a crew member, with a portable radio. He's an officer, I think.
I grab him by the arm
and I ask: 'What happens?'
He whispers in my ear: "Look, the Costa Concordia hit a rock and this caused a leak.
We gotta get out of here.
We must get out the ship.
Get out the ship?!
We are now almost 4250 souls running for our lives.
We are the entrails of this mortally wounded beast who will never recover.
Now I have two things to do.
The first is to reach my emergency muster station. I am a crew member and I have to fullfill my duties.
All right.
I did it.
The second is to reach my rescue boat, my raft, to save my life.
Which I did not do.
You may wonder why.
Easy: my raft is under water,
there's no rescue boat for me.
Things are getting tough now.
I start feeling that strange taste in my mouth and imagining that wide colour that expands and covers everything.
My hands are cold.
I've got to get out of here.
I have to take my little child to the cinema!
I think about my daughter, so far away from me, sleeping in her quite night, a night which is fortunately different from mine. It is just the thought of my daughter that triggers me a reaction.
I have to take her to the cinema, I have to enjoy life like everyone else, I still have to play the piano.
I need to escape from this ship
but I don't know how,
there's no rescue boat.
I simply called her name, Sofia,
and after a while I started climbing on some railings, finding an optional escaping route to the outer side of the ship through an embarkation gate previously used to lower a lifeboat full of passengers.
I'm now on the wet and torn side of the ship. That dark sea is down there and I am at risk of falling over.
Captain, I haven't heard from you yet.
Where are you?
I grab a winch, I cling to it tightly and all I can do is waiting.
I can't dive either: it's freezing, we are in January and as long as I remember, that would leave me eight minutes.
If there's no one to rescue me down there within eight minutes my body goes into hypothermia.
I don't know what hypothermia means but I know it's something bad.
It's bad.
I can still hear people screaming inside the ship.
I can hear the sound of metal pieces
crashing and collapsing. A terrible noise, like a giant drum, is emerging from the bottom like a hideous coughing from far away.
The Costa Concordia is exhaling its very last breath.
This is a death rattle.
Helicopters over my head, they look like birds of prey inspecting a decomposing corpse.
Later on, I don't exactly remember when, a small rescue boat approaches,
it's close to the massive wound of the ship's belly. Someone screams to me,
"Can you make it? Can you make it?" Of course I can!
You don't know, but I have to take my daughter to the cinema!
And so I jump.
Believe me, jumping on that little boat was like jumping back to life.
I reached the Isola del Giglio at three A.M.
My escape lasted about six hours.
I'm alive, I'm cold
and since that day I have been celebrating my second birthday.
The aftermath has been very difficult for me, devastating at times.
The Costa Concordia collapsed under a heavy burden and metaphorically I had to take that burden off my shoulders.
What did I do?
I simply lightened everything, subtracting weight to this story.
How?
As I did when I was a child, transforming my world, creating other worlds,
playing the piano.
When I couldn't do it, because I lost everything I had, I imagined playing the piano
and I started all over again, three years ago at the age of 42, away from my country, in a big city.
Now I live in London and from being a waiter in a big museum, the Victoria and Albert Museum in South Kensington, I have now become their resident pianist.
I will now take my leave of you silently.
My music has brought me here in front of you. May my music now reach 32 people who lost their lives that night, including two colleagues, two fellow musicians.
I wrote a piece called "32" whose main melody is made up of thirty-two notes.
Every note is a tribute to the memory of each victim of that accident.
May my music reach them, wherever they are now.
Let's play.
Thank you.
(Applause) Thank you.
(Applause)
CV: Close your eyes for a moment.
You all, close them and don't fall asleep.
Now I'd like you to imagine a conversation between the present and the future, as in a fantasy movie.
Do you have in your head a good fantasy movie?
Good, now you can open your eyes.
Hi!
I'm Claudio and I am the marketing manager of the present
And I absolutely need your help .
TED: Hi Claudio!
My name is TED, and I am the marketing manager of the future.
How can I help you?
CV: Well, TED, I was born in 1977.
I started playing with Nintendo videogames, and then with Commodore 64.
Do you remember, the one with tapes?
One full hour to load a game?
Then, I started working in 1998 with Windows.
the one with icones, windows, do you remember?
I started doing my first researches on Google.
In 2006, I bought Qtech, one of the first smartphones ever.
And in 2007 I was one of Italy's earliest Facebook adopters.
In 2011 I founded a web marketing agency that helped many companies promote themselves on the Internet.
So I always had a digital-friendly DNA.
But what was the impact of digital in companies?
Well, thanks to e-commerce, companies are now able to sell their products online.
Completely transforming, in the process, their business model.
Thanks to search engines, Google above all, they could now be found by their customers.
And thanks to Facebook, they have been able to activate a direct communication channel with their customers.
TED: Well Claudio, this is amazing!
I studied you guys at school, I studied the case of Google's Larry Page and Sergey Brin.
I studied Mark Zuckerberg of Facebook, Jeff Bezos of Amazon, Steve Jobs of Apple.
I think you've made the history!
You were the web pioneers!
What's wrong with you?
CV: Well, Ted, at the beginning it was great for us too.
20 years of Internet revolution!
But today we have a dramatic problem, and if I do not solve it immediately, that's how it's going to end.
I have a huge problem with attention.
I will now tell you
what I mean by attention.
Our brain is overloaded all the time.
A kind of cognitive overwhelming, due to too much information, too many stimuli we get from all media, from TV, radio, the press, but mostly from the Internet.
And from our phones.
Let me show you something.
This is a screenshot from my smartphone.
I spend 12 hours on Whatsapp, 11 hours on Facebook and three hours on Instagram, Every week, every damn week.
27 hours spent on social networks.
It's about three work days.
Absurd, isn't it?
A millennial, which is the generation after mine, opens and closes her smartphone over and over again some 150 times a day.
It's about 20 times per hour.
Assuming she ever goes to sleep.
Our brain has to process something like 34 gigabytes of information every day.
It's basically 23 words per second.
It is as if all the time, all day, I spoke to you at this speed.
Pretty absurd, I would argue.
TED: Claudio, in fact it is very absurd.
In an old movie, they'd have said: "Great Jupiter".
How can you ever survive in such a world?
I mess up even just doing two things at once.
CV: In fact, we live very very bad, even if we do not notice it.
This is because our brain is not for multitasking.
our brain is made to do one thing well at once
And there is an important American research institute that a few years ago found out, the average attention of a goldfish is nine seconds.
A very common red fish of an aquarium.
The average attention of an internet user is instead of just eight seconds.
So if you got it right, the goldfish has a greater attention than an internet user.
In 2000, it was 12 seconds.
So it is falling steadily.
Another research tells us that 44% of people can't focus on a given topic.
We cannot even concentrate anymore.
In practice we are becoming almost functional illiterate people.
We cannot even interpret a text of medium complexity like an instruction manual.
Although not all instruction books are so simple, right?!
In Italy we have a sad record: we are the second in Europe in the ranking of functional illiteracy.
Really a sad record.
And this gigantic mass of information that overwhelms us every day now makes us no longer tell what is true from what is false.
Our perception of reality is distorted nowadays.
I'll give you an example: people in Italy we think, in recent years murders have increased.
They fell by 39% instead.
They think, immigration boomed in recent years.
It has increased, yes, but only 7%.
However, I want to talk to you about the consequences this decline in attention has had on marketing and communication.
In a desperate quest for a bit of users' attention, we, the marketers, did the only thing that came to our mind: filling the web with advertising.
And what consequences did this have?
Dramatic.
In 30 years, the attention users paid to advertising has dropped fourfold.
Fourfold.
And what happened?
Which in fact, today, advertising is way more expensive.
50% more than in 2000.
Why?
Because users are increasingly distracted, and we've got to run more and more ads to have their attention.
So TED, I don't really know what to do anymore.
The only thing I'm perhaps left with is slapping the face of the user before me.
TED: Well, Claudio, I think that's quite an inappropriate answer.
I tell you: in our age, smartphones and all those distracting devices are no more.
We have AI-based virtual assistants that searches for the information we need on the Internet, publish the contents we want to share on social networks, and buy for us on e-commerce platform the products we need.
These virtual assistants are 24/7 online, at home and in the office.
Some are worn directly, such as earphones or glasses.
The first virtual assistants were actually born in your time.
They were a bit rough, and could only answer very simple questions.
And often, their answers were a bit weird.
Look for an ice cream shop!
Question: what does a little ice-cream do in the middle of the sea?
Answer: Affogato (drowned) ice cream.
Yes, in fact it was not really a great answer.
But above all, the first virtual assistants could not answer complex questions and support a conversation made up of questions and answers, as we naturally do.
based as they were on an antiquated algorithm called Machine Learning.
In practice, they could only answer the programmer's questions.
So, for example, if I wanted to organize a trip to Rome, I had to asked at least three questions.
Example: what's the weather like this weekend in Rome?
Then I would have to ask for a list of flights to Rome and then I would have to pick one up and ask him to buy it.
So three questions.
These days, however, the algorithm has evolved into Deep Learning,
in practice the algorithm, the algorithm of artificial intelligence can basically learn from its mistakes and from the interaction it has with the user.
So it's basically able now to answer much more complex questions the programmer could not foresee.
So, in the example above today I simply ask: I want to take a trip to Rome,
if the weather is nice, buy me the first flight.
And the virtual assistant answers me: ok, done.
But not only that, he also asks me if I want to lodge in that same hotel.
Because it understood I normally stay in that same hotel.
And it detects an anomaly: I bought a flight, but I didn't buy the hotel.
Ok, it's as if the virtual assistant and the artificial intelligence have become a mediator between us and the huge amount of information arounds us.
Otherwise, you should be as good as this old American president who, not surprisingly, had a lot of helpers.
In practice, today, we command everything with the most convenient tool, a natural tool we all use, the voice.
Your era has been that of the image, ours is the era of the voice.
We image, you voice.
You appearance, we substance.
You distraction, we concentration.
And virtual assistants have also changed corporate communication channels.
In practice, Internet portal, mobile applications, social profiles, everything has been replaced by the so-called Voice Search.
So companies had to retrain and optimize their catalogs, their informations, in order to be found by the voice assistants.
Much like what happened in your era with Google.
CV: Wow!
TED, all of this is fantastic!
So you're telling me that companies have found a way to get in touch more easily with their customers by minimizing the impact of technology, that is, technology has become almost invisible?
TED: Exactly, Claudio.
And there is another thing that has become invisible.
You would never guess, it's advertising.
Claudio, you have to get out of the logic that users' attention can be bought.
The attention can't really be bought, it is to be conquered.
These two old marketing gurus,
Seth Godin e Philip Cutler, were right.
They said that marketing would move from Interruption to Permission and from Push to Pull.
And in fact, companies have understood that it is useless to throw tons of ads on users.
It pays off much more to be found by their users and customers, giving them useful information, answers to their questions.
Why should I sell a product with an ordinary ad, when only 10 percent of people trust advertising while 70 percent trust other users' recommendations?
Would you book a restaurant based on an advertisement or because your friend recommended you to go there?
If you could produce content for your potential customers and instead of advertising, let your best customers, your most loyal customers, talk about you, you'd have really made it, bingo.
In your age it was called "Content Marketing".
In our age, by now, it is only called marketing because marketing can't really come without a content.
They were called influencers, back in your day: in our time, you remember, like Chiara Ferragni,
in our time now they are constant creators of content, they are company consultants.
CV: TED, this stuff is really interesting indeed.
But actually, I live in 2018,
so what can I do right now to help my company get noticed on the internet and cut through their goldfish-like attention span?
Ok Claudio, I'll give you five things
you can do right away - I mean, today.
Number one. You must focus on the product and the service, and not on advertising.
What does "Apple" tell you?
Number two: your content ought to be relevant for your customers.
For example you could create sponsored magazines where you can publish useful content to your customers such as cooking recipes: or tips for the home, or even trends on global infrastructures.
Whatever they need.
Number three. Give something to your customers before asking them something back.
For example, give them useful tools, online courses, downloadable guides.
Anything that can be useful before buying your product.
Number four. Find your testimonials.
The world is full of fans of your products and services.
You just have to spot them, discover them, engage them and let them talk about you on social networks.
It's the best marketing you can do.
Number five. Learn to exploit voice assistants, as they will explode in the coming years.
You should exploit skills, for instance.
Basically you have to teach the voice assistant to answer your customers' questions.
For example, if you are a food company you should be able to answer a question like "how to cook a roast chicken".
CV: Ok TED, let's recapitulate. Number one: Focus on products and services and not on advertising.
Number two: make relevant content.
Number three: Give my customers something, before asking something back.
Number four: You asked me to find my testimonials. Number 5: focus on voice assistants.
Is it right?
TED: Exactly Claudio.
You must hurry up now, however. Otherwise you will be cut off, because you're running out of time.
So, good luck and have a good trip.
CV: Thank you.
(Applause)
Hi everybody! I'm very excited to be here.
I wanted to give the previous speaker a good solution for not showing sweat: wear a black t-shirt.
(Laughter) (Applause) In the past seven months I lived on the road, with no money at all, sleeping every night under a different roof, and I was lucky to find one at all.
Here's the images from where I slept.
This was my life up until 22 days ago and these were my shoes.
I bought them in London last year to become an angel.
What does it mean to be an angel?
An angel is a professional masseuse of poker player's heads at tournaments.
These shoes walked the floors of all the UK's most prestigious casinos.
Sometimes I stood massaging players even for 12 hours straight. A pair of comfortable shoes was essential.
A writing on their sole says: 'Go walk'.
Everyday I used to put them on to go to work and I had that writing stuck in my mind: 'Go walk'.
One day I was massaging a player who was particularly nervous, when I started looking around:
what time was it? what day?
what was I doing there?
Around me there was no clock.
Casinos are timeless places, meant to make players unaware of time going by.
That was exactly what was happening to my life.
I was completely lost, I didn't even know why I was there.
The player I was massaging was so nervous that I started feeling sick.
I was sick of living in that timeless limbo so I burst out saying: 'Sorry sir, I quit!
I need to go for a walk!'

And so I did! I resigned!

I left the casino and walked home from Marble Arch to Whitechapel, just a handful of kilometers in London in the middle of the night, a night like all the others for everyone, but not for me.
The first thought that came to my mind was a quote from Jep Gambardella. In the movie "The Great Beauty" he says, "At 65, I can't waste any time
doing things I don't want to do."
So why did I have to wait 30 more years to reach the same conclusion?
I knew exactly what I didn't like doing, but I forgot completely which my own dreams were.
So I looked back on my life.
Since I was a kid I've always been a bit absent-minded, I had my head in the clouds, always looking outside the window, always the last in class who finished her tests.
My teacher used to tell me, "Wake up, Darinka!
Time is money!"
Such a horrible statement that has been tormenting me since ever, making me think that being slow was one of my biggest flaw and that I was really losing money by going at my own pace.
Lately I've started to look at it differently: Now I think that my being slow makes me very rich in a way.
What else? In 2004 I graduated
in Photographic arts in London and this is where I left from: I traveled across continents, ready to face any situation.
I did all sorts of jobs: from serving at bars in Australian mines wearing a bikini, to teaching English in Laos.
Really everything.
I was adapting to what the world presented me, but maybe, if I had started doing what I love The world, for once, would adapt to my needs!
What do I love?
Finally I found my dreams once again: traveling, writing, photographing and dreaming. Slowly.
Finally it was all crystal clear: I had to come back home to Italy, walk all of it and collect people's dreams: I would try to make them feel the same way I felt that magic night.
Just like Atreyu does in 'The NeverEnding Story': I would try to fight the nothing by stimulating people's imagination!
Because I think that dreaming is a subtle form of rebellion and fulfilling our dreams is a real revolution.
So I caught the first plane to Milan.
My parents came to pick me up at the airport, really surprised: 'What are you doing here in Italy after 15 years abroad?!'
And so I told them that I was about to take a plane to Palermo and then come back home by foot!
"Well... It's going to rain tomorrow..." They just let me speak as if I were insane.
Actually I felt completely crazy.
I wasn't quite sure.
I didn't save any money!
How would I travel without money?
I loved the concept though. It would prove that money is not necessary to fulfill your dreams.
But by foot?
I had never even walked to the supermarket before!
I googled to find someone who had succeeded before me.
I found one: Riccardo Carnovalini, who had walked everywhere across Italy. He was thrilled by my foolish idea.
Finally we managed to meet at a bar where I started to fire questions at him: 'What should I do?
What kind of shoes do I need?
What kind of backpack?
What about rain?
Where do I get water?
But do you think I can do it?'
'Of course you can!'
It gave me a boost.
It was really the turning point for me.
When I came back home
it seemed I knew what I was talking about.
I booked my flight to Palermo.
There was still one thing I was worried about: fear!
I was scared that I would fail, scared of the unknown, scared that I wouldn't find neither roofs, nor food.
I was scared of the fear itself, scared of everything!
I arrived in Palermo and as I started walking, it seemed that my fear was a huge glass wall and walking through it, I broke it into a thousand little pieces that I can pick up now and name one by one.
Now I know my fears.
I know that I'm scared of walking
on high bridges as they make me dizzy.
I'm scared of walking in tunnels, sheepdogs and of who stops and insists on offering me a ride.
But over time I've come up with some little stratagems to face my fears.
For example, to those who stop to offer me a lift I say that I'm making a documentary and that the filming crew is always just a few steps behind me.
Believe me, they disappear in no time.
As if those tiny pieces of glasses from my fear wall were left on a beach long enough to become smooth and not as sharp as they used to be.
Now, honestly I can't tell you in just 12 minutes what happened to me in the past few months, but I can tell that it was a great fun. And that I have a secret, maybe two.
The first is smiling!
It is an extremely powerful weapon that we all have and that opened all doors: thanks to it I almost always had a roof on my head and I ate most days.
I even crossed the strait of Messina and got a free entry in most museums that I wanted to visit.
And the second weapon is the power of listening!
In a society where we throw up words too easily to worry about their meanings, only a few of us are still keeping their ears open. On this trip I'm learning to do so.
I listened to my shoes first, then to my heart and finally to the dreams of all the people I met on my way. Listening gave me the opportunity to come back home with a priceless baggage!
And I want to show it to you all!
These are everybody's dreams!
Here is a dream I particularly like: it's from Annachiara, an 8 year old girl, who mistook me for someone between Santa Claus and the Genie of the Lamp.
She gave me a real list of wishes: One, I want a chihuahua; two, I want a Samsung; three, a boyfriend; four, I want to fly. Interesting: a boyfriend and then flying!
Five, I want to be pregnant without pain in my belly (Laugther) six, I want straight teeth without braces; seven, I never want to have hairs under my armpits nor down there.
(Applause) The following day Annachiara woke up at 6 in the morning and chased me because she wanted to give me her last dream. Thinking of it kept her up all night. It reads, "I want all the kids in the world to be as lucky as I am and I never want them to suffer!"
(Applause) Honestly mine isn't a revolutionary project, I didn't change the world, but I changed mine!
My destiny was written on my shoes and I didn't even realise it!
Now I want to leave you with a question: where is your destiny?
what is your dream?
I promise you that thinking about it even for just a few minutes, could surprisingly change your life!
Thank you.
(Applause)
As you can see, this is a banana as we all know it.
It's big, sweet, good, seedless.
But Mother Nature conceived it very differently.
That's what a banana looked like about 7,000 years ago.
And of course, it's not a good sight.
And with all those seeds, it wasn't really our ideal banana.
And so we tamed it, selecting the best fruits, planting their seeds, growing the plants, selecting the best fruits again and again, until we got the ideal banana, the Cavendish, the one we know today, which makes up about 50 percent of bananas in the world.
Characteristics such as sweetness, size, resistance to pathogens are transmitted through DNA from one generation to the next, which is contained in every cell of every living being.
DNA is a long chain of nitrogenous bases called adenine, guanine, cytosine and thymine, forming the code of life.
Just like a word is formed by a sequence of letters, a sequence of bases forms genes, with a beginning, an end, and a length of several thousand bases.
Some characteristics are "binary": for example, a banana or a plant can be resistant to a pathogen or not.
Characteristics of this type are called “monogenic", and are transmitted by a single gene: let's call it the resistance gene.
If the plant inherits the correct version of the resistance gene, then it will be resistant; otherwise it won’t.
I used the term “correct” because during the copy of the DNA from one generation to the other, small errors can be introduced, called mutations, which can alter one or more traits that are maintained, if advantageous, and expanded in the population.
And this is the basis of the evolution, and allows the plant to adapt to a new environment and compete with other species.
Unlike the resistance, there is no sweetness gene.
So, how is this information transmitted from one generation to the next?
This is an example of a "polygenic" trait, that is, contained not in a single gene but in tens, hundreds, sometimes thousands of genes.
Traits of this type are usually gradual: for example, a banana may be: not very sweet; average sweet; sweet or very sweet.
Man has also changed over the last 7,000 years.
We had to adapt, for example in Europe, to live here with cold seasons, and to depend on other species, such as breeding cows, thus acquiring tolerance to lactose and probably losing the need for dark eyes to defend ourselves from the sun of Africa, thus acquiring other colors.
Other evolving traits are, for instance, the loss of the wisdom teeth and also, it seems, the reduction of the cerebral mass.
The reason for this last trait is not very clear, but perhaps it is due to the fact that moving from nomads to sedentary we no longer needed those skills of orientation in the woods and survival in the wild, which take up many resources.
Probably, who knows, with the use of our electronic devices to which we entrust our memory,
it may shrink even further, who can tell.
So the evolution of man is in ceaseless progress.
Even in man we have monogenic traits, such as the attachment of the lobe to the ear, or the presence or absence of dimples, or even the ability, that some of us have, to roll up the tongue.
Monogenic traits, however, are also those causing rare genetic diseases - often, unfortunately, paediatric - that are caused in a single gene by a single mutation.
Examples of polygenic traits are instead, for example height, eye and hair color, but also risk factors for common diseases, such as cancer, heart attack, dementia, diabetes.
The evolution of man, therefore, proceeds without interruption but in a completely random way, guided by natural evolution.
In a man, for example, there are about 65 new mutations that are passed on from one generation to another.
Some of these mutations can alter one or more traits which, if advantageous, are maintained in the population.
Today, however, we are facing a revolution. The opportunity is in our hands of a men-driven evolution of man, and no longer a random one; just as man has guided the evolution of the banana.
Today we can drive our evolution, and this thanks to three technologies of which I will talk about to you today.
The first being the reading of DNA.
In 2001, when the first draft of human DNA was presented, unveiled, it required an international effort of more than ten years, and a cost of about three billion dollars.
Today, in our research institute so as in so many others in the world, we have instruments capable of reading the genome, the DNA of a person, in less than a day and at a cost of less than a thousand euros.
The technology works through the "coloring" of the bases of DNA with different colors, photographing them, and then from the image reconstruct the sequence of letters, bases, which form our DNA.
What are the implications of this technology?
The first is that today it’s possible to read the DNA of each newborn: we can then identify mutations that can cause, for example, rare genetic diseases.
Some of them are "actionable", meaning that there are therapies to prevent, or delay, the onset of the disease.
It is possible to read DNA even before birth, and even before conception, thanks to the in vitro fertilization technique, where the egg is fertilized in a tube.
In some countries it is already allowed to read the DNA of the embryo before implantation to identify the presence of lethal mutations and therefore not compatible with life.
In this case, in these countries the embryo is discarded and not implanted.
I predict that in the next ten years, as the number of people whose DNA will be read increases, we will be able to predict more accurately even polygenic traits.
So, as I said, risk factors, for example, for common diseases.
It would therefore be possible to associate with each embryo, before implantation, what the risk is that the individual born from this embryo will develop, for example, tumours.
Of course, selecting the embryo based on the presence of these mutations and desired polygenic traits is not ethically acceptable.
It is also tecniccally impossible, given the completely random nature of the presence of embryo mutations, and also the high number of mutations conferring the polygenic tract.
Today, however, all this is about to change.
Indeed, it has already changed, thanks to the discovery of two scientists who have identified in the bacterium of streptococcus, the one that causes pharyngitis, a protein that you can see here behind me, called CAS 9 which can be used to bind DNA and insert, delete or modify a DNA base within a human cell.
Today, in the laboratory, we can modify a single base of the DNA of a cell, and therefore also of the embryo before implantation.
This technology, together with DNA reading and in vitro fertilisation, is indeed revolutionary for parents suffering with rare genetic diseases.
And also less ethically controversial, because the embryo with the deprecated mutation can simply be corrected, instead of being discarded, inserting the correct base instead of the harmful mutation.
After that, the embryo can be implanted and a completely healthy individual will be born from this embryo.
Realistically we will be able, ten years from now, not only to modify a single base in the DNA, but tens, hundreds and even thousands of bases.
What does this mean?
It means that we can also give the embryo desired polygenic traits for example low risk of diseases today considered commonplace.
Obviously, this opens up an ethical debate, but that is what technology can offer us.
The third and final technology I want to present to you is synthetic biology, which emerges when engineers like me meet biologists like my wife, for example.
And synthetic biology aims to provide cells with new functions, useful to man or the environment.
We can achieve the purpose of synthetic biology through the construction of real "biological circuits", akin to the electronic ones that today we find in autopilots of aircraft, or even in the simple home thermostat.
Engineering biology is certainly not easy, given the complexity of the cell and also the fact that, unlike an aircraft, it duplicates and adapts to the environment.
For this reason, in my laboratory we are working on technologies for the construction of robust biological circuits inside the cell.
The aim on the near future - not far away, but the near future of synthetic biology is to develop real living drugs.
For example, probiotic bacteria: those found in yoghurt that we can ingest, but that have been modified with biological circuits making them able to detect toxic substances within the body, especially for those patients suffering from rare metabolic diseases, and treat them.
It is also possible to insert circuits within human cells, and this is already revolutionizing the treatment of blood cancer.
Patient's immune cells are taken from the blood, and a simple but effective circuit is inserted into the immune cell, making it able to detct and eliminate the cancer cell.
These re-engineered cells are inserted into the patient and can, in some cases, totally cure the cancer.
Obviously, like any technology, its use can either be constructive or destructive.
For example, it is possible to imagine, in the distant future, but not so distant, that some totalitarian regimes can impose in vitro fertilization by law, and force the insertion of polygenic traits desired for the regime, such as a certain height or a certain color of the eyes; but also, who knows, in the future, psychophysical traits such as low aggressiveness.
This may seem like a dystopian science fiction scenario, but it's not.
During this year, in China, a researcher has altered two embryos by modifying a gene to give the embryos resistance to the HIV virus, the one that causes AIDS.
From these two embryos two twin were born, first in the world with rewritten DNA.
Unfortunately, this has been done: in a completely hidden way, both to the scientific community and to the public; without any real medical necessity and without any ethical justification.
The researcher in question is now under home detention in China.
The combination of DNA reading, DNA rewriting and synthetic biology opens up, however, truly incredible scenarios, and opens the door to a man-driven evolution of mankind.
Do we want to leave evolution to chance, or do we want to lead it?
And how far do we want to go?
That's why each of us must educate themsleves and take an active part in the decision that society will have to make on where to direct future research investments, in order to reduce human suffering while respecting individual freedom.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Let me thank you, the introduction was a bit serious, thanks anyway, because I put on the leather pants, so we begin to defuse the situation.
I am very happy to be here today for two reasons in particular: first, that those who preceded me and spoke about design focused on the people and not on the objects and the products, and this is the job that we have done for many years with Low Cost Design.
The second is because, all the people who come here, and especially those who came this afternoon, began telling a love story; those who fell in love with artichokes, others who still had a love story, somewhere in Milan, well, this is my loved one: with a coffee maker.
I'm also quite jealous, so, fortunately, I'm the only one to have it. None of the previous speakers had a love story with a coffee pot, and my project was born from here.
About 15 years ago, I found myself inside a family situation where I was dealing with a person who, as he finished the gas tank, he turned the iron upside down, put on the coffee pot and brewed the coffee.
This event spurred in me an extraordinary reflection.
After this action, apparently unaware, but a result of an interesting mix of knowledge, cultural DNA, and personal interests, I started studying, with the help of many friends, the whole panorama of creativity, that we use to coin by that name "spontaneous", that surrounded us, and surrounded me in a particular way.
I traveled four continents for about 15 years, knowing thousands of people, interviewing them, entering into their homes, and above all trying to figure out what I could understand of their creativity.
That is why they were able to accomplish the spontaneous gestures, to be able to transform the needs of every day and to be able to change their goals.
From here we found thousands of examples, as you can see, there are about 6,000 objects that we have surveyed worldwide in 15 years of work, and there are some, believe me, really hilarious, and 2,000 examples of how people transform the public space.
The public space is invented by people, recreated by people, and the people, who live it, rightly want to use it.
Once we have found all these examples, we have begun to try to understand which of these objects could be useful to find the true cultural DNA.
From our studies and our research, which lasted several years, we can say some things for sure: the spontaneous creativity, that we appoint as such, is that which does not belong to the company divisions.
It is the creativity of the worlds, not productivity forced.
So we might think that the highest rate of patents in the world is in the Silicon Valley, but all this does not affect its territory, naturally.
Probably from the Silicon Valley will not come out a native able to invent anything, but they are all people located there.
So we do not try to understand how people live through objects, by remodeling them, and we divided them into five levels.
Five levels starting from the first to the fifth of course, and the fifth is the most important level, that is, the one in which the simplest object solves the most complex need.
So this the real short circuit of human intuition.
Imagine how many we have found among 6,000 examples and I'll now start to show you some of them.
This is a ball of foam sewn into the back of a T-shirt.
What is it? What function
could it serve? I tell you!
It is an anti snoring.
It saved some marriages and works like this: the customer, i.e. the person who snores, suffering from snoring, once he's turned with his belly up, the best position to make those wonderful sounds so loved by the wives, practically feel the discomfort of the ball of foam, turns and in other position stops snoring.
It is very simple, it works great, and we also discovered, thanks to this, a lot of variants of these projects.
We have found thousands of projects very similar throughout the world.
Well, the 19th century Sicilian version consisted in a potato stuck in the back pocket of the pajamas.
Same result, you see?
Therefore, from here Bell's theorem and wonderful things of quantum mechanics, that make us even think that, being particles separated at birth, we could have the same insight thousands of miles away, without even knowing each other.
And as we proceed, you may laugh. You can also ask yourself, what does a condom, I did not say it this was the perfect example, you can also ask yourself, what does a condom do on the handle of a racket?
You may ask yourself, it is quite normal. I can help you with this: it didn't went there by itself.
Don't jump to easy conclusions. OK, if not, we go to... well, something little technical here.
Technically, in which material is made a condom?
Latex, so it is absolutely hypoallergenic, and it can prevent blisters in the hands of young tennis players.
So many young players have a condom, inserted in the racquet handle for this type of use.
What brought it in the head of the person who first thought it for this type of purpose?
Seneca said, "The great inventions are the ones within the humanity's reach." They all belong to everybody, no?
What could have led to this kind of splitting?
A great conceptual skill, an immense objective ability of decontextualizing the situations to be able to get a result.
We have another example: this is a bag, the classic yellow lemons netting, used for lemons, in this case has been filled with a piece of bread.
What could it do it for, according to you?
Perfect, this 85 years old lady does a natural selection.
I would like to take her to the speech of the "Linnean Society", along with Charles Darwin, because she could tell on the road, just like this happens, because this system only allows you to feed the sparrows, small birds and not the urban gulls, crows and pigeons.
And when I asked her why, with her eyes wide open, This lady told me, "A) Because they don't eat while flying; B) Because they are too heavy and if they cling, they tip and can not swallow." Therefore an 84 years old lady may intervene in the rationalization system of the distribution of urban birds, by eliminating the unmanageable ones, pretty much crows, seagulls and so, with a net straw at no cost.
This is the apotheosis of creativity and this is not a sadomasochistic object.
I got you used to it with condoms on rackets, it has nothing to do with this.
Some of you may have probably attended the faculty of surgery.
How do we teach a student surgery to put the stitches?
We can't tell him to put them on his little sister, right?
Not even on his dog... So the easiest way is a banana. Why?
Because the skin of the banana peel is very similar to the human dermis, Also, in terms of consistency, then, there may happen if you have a son, studying surgery, to find a lot of sewn bananas at home, do not worry, it's quite normal.
As it is quite normal to find a potato into the dashboard of the truck of a truck driver weighing about 440 pounds, a mountain of muscles like that, and certainly not in the habit of cooking.
Why should him keep a potato in the dashboard?
He never wants to part with it.
He can live, he can separate from his wife but never from his potato, like so many others. (Laughter) This was not intentional, excuse me, it just came out. He would never part with his potato
like so many other truckers. Why?
Because, if the windscreen of the car breaks and this gentleman is located on the Bologna-Florence, with 200 trucks behind and 200 in front, what does he do, in the pouring rain?
He is forced to stop, no, he is forced to stop anyway, but he stops, cut the potato in half, rubs it on the windshield: thus making, the starch in the potato allows the slippage of the water drops.
So he gets much the same viewing ability allowed by the windshield wiper that he broke.
So just observe how well a truck driver, who does not have the technological skills of an engineer, a scientist or a chemist, can change the rules of the game through his creativity.
And, if we go even further: a cemetery.
Alas, we all have a dearly departed in our families, more or less, and the flowers that we bring, are inside a flowerpot, the best receptacle for the hatching of tiger mosquito eggs.
That is the terrible thing that terrifies our cities in summer.
Well, many of the examples that we found in this research, as I said, in a rather wide-ranging way, led us to analyze so many uses of objects and designs combining technical and cultural skill of the private and the public mindset, that is the public service, is something that is going towards the community, isn't it?
So even if the private intervenes in these mechanisms, he does not lose his collective dimension.
This is one of the classic examples, in which a public body, a cemetery, something very regulated, applies, in order to solve a problem, a technology clearly imported from a private matrix, because this is a small piece of copper that, tucked into every jar of the cemetery, allows, through the development of copper oxide, to prevent the hatching of the tiger mosquitoes.
We are seeing six examples in 5,000, of course, and this is the apotheosis of the private thought that blends in the public thought.
It is a "stop" sign with a letter box.
This system of course is part of a private agreement between two people, then between a representative of an institution and a private citizen, arranging to make sure that the mail, something not certifiable, that is not in need of a signature, is left in the postbox, to allow the postman not to go for 1.2 miles in the driveway, where this person lives, and not to the reach this person's home.
Imagine what kind of rationalization of service could derive from this.
In a sector such as the state, the public administration, that is absolutely in need of facilities for the streamline, not only of costs but also the introduction of best practices, and in fact, we deal with good practices.
This whole world that we have seen, collected from the knowledge of thousands of people, all quite amusing, inter Alia, is materialized in an exhibition project, called "Low Cost Design".
It is an exposition that has already had more than 10 ways to be seen, a bit all over the world, and it reproduces, and it deliver us 500 of these objects as educational facilities and I can assure you that it is very interesting.
Our work, at the end, is trying to summarize all this, is working on good practices.
The definition that you see at the bottom is very simple.
Low Cost Design, over the years, during these 15 years of research, has become two books, a platform for teaching in various universities, an advisory system for businesses, for those who may be willing to have a chat with me, very quietly, but, mostly, it tried to introduce a couple of very important concepts.
The first, that it doesn't make sense "low costing" objects by lowering the quality, but it makes sense to work on intuitive processes to lower production costs.
This is the true low cost, the rest is "low costing", that means lowering from wood to chipboard doesn't mean to produce a low cost item, it means to produce another object with an agreed maturity, that will be thrown into the dustbin, and is designed to be thrown into the dustbin.
One of the other concepts that we drive forward is: is better to get 1 euro from 1,000 people than 10 euros coming from 100 persons.
Because 1 euro from 1,000 people means to participate, and from this we have coined a number of cross-cutting projects ranging from workshops on microeconomics, to all those systems that can help to restart, even in our small way, thanks to the thousands of insights that we have collected, small grass-rooted enterprise systems, as those actually delivered by the past.
We reached the last slide.
All this to tell you that the spontaneous creativity, according to our studies, our research, is something that is very difficult to define.
The best definition I have found is, "The perfect fusion of technological and poetical capability."
Without these two qualities, we can not define the creativity as a spontaneous motion and, especially, if put into practice in this way, has the gift of the breath of eternity, because it's highly shareable by all, there are no differences, from our research lasted 15 years, between North and South, and rich and poor.
This simply changes the address of creativity.
Of course, the poorest people in the poorest areas of the South direct their creativity to the solving of the basic needs.
Simply, the wealthy people in the North direct their creativity to leisure, many times, to all that is secondary, because their basic needs are met by the country, the state or economy.
Having said this I conclude: all you've seen is the result of an organized mental system, that each of us possesses, especially when using the technological skills of an engineer and the visionary skill of a child.
By mixing these two qualities, we can find systems that can solve, as we saw it, the little things of our daily lives, but they can even solve an entire production system.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Let's talk about the fear of the other, and its possible origins.
About a century ago, Freud became one of the world’s most influential people by, basically, putting forward a pretty simple idea: we human beings are an animal species – albeit a very complex one – a product of the evolution.
As such, we have a body, some instincts.
These instincts – which are to be expressed in a civil fashion – if excessively compressed, might result in neurosis and mental disorders.
Let’s ask ourselves whether there are – of course, today, a century on, sexuality has found endless freer forms of expression – but let’s ask ourselves if other instincts are there that not only have never achieved such freedom of expression, but have seen their freedom of expression curtailed.
If we own a dog, we will notice that, despite living in a flat in town, it still preserves its original territorial instinct.
So, what about the territorial instinct of the Homo Sapiens species?
Looking back on our evolution, we were already Homo Sapiens when we shifted from nomadic to sedentary following the discovery of agriculture.
However, throughout history, until the early 20th century, historians say that an average human being – even in Europe, that is the vast majority of the population – would meet 200 people in their lifetime.
This did not pose any major problems in terms of space, nor were there any feelings of mistrust that naturally arise – and this is another fundamental instinct, we can’t trust everyone – when someone invades our personal space.
By the way, since then – since the early 20th century – the population has increased almost eightfold, hasn’t it?
In the past century, actually over the last century, we have witnessed overwhelming urbanisation.
Ten years ago, the United Nations reported that the world's population is predominantly urban. This disproportion continues to grow due to the mass movement of people to cities in search of better opportunities –
even though the risk of developing mental disorders is infinitely higher in urban areas.
What happens in cities?
We meet thousands of people, whenever we go out.
Not in our lifetime, but every single day.
And those who commute to work – by underground, for instance – are forced to share the same space with another hundred people, which is quite at odds with our territorial instinct.
We also notice that those 200 people closely correspond to what modern neuroscience considers to be one of main functions of our brain;
that is, our brain is capable of storing up to 150 faces; by 200 it starts to get confused and is unable to recognise any more.
This means – according to neuroscientists – that social media are radically deceiving new generations by telling them, “You’ll have thousands of new friends” which, they say, is totally impossible unless the entire concept of friendship is falsified and voided of meaning.
Anyway, we are compressed – for example inside a carriage – with our instincts violently repressed as our good manners keep us from indulging in any aggressive outburst.
Yet, it would be impossible – even for the most intelligent and closest to us, such as chimpanzees – to force a hundred individuals inside the same carriage.
All this has been worsened – this sense of unease and mistrust – in recent times, as many of the people we share our space with have suddenly, doubly, turned into strangers.
That is, they are immigrants.
Ours used to be a country of emigration, there were no immigrants.
The colour of their skin, the language they speak, is different.
Unfortunately, ethologists say – at some point – this overlapping of nature and culture deceives our instincts to such an extent, we no longer perceive them as human beings
and this triggers our aggressiveness towards other species.
Let's now shift our attention from human instincts to major historical events.
We know that last century Hitler caused one of the greatest tragedies in human memory by offering a very simple, once again almost animal-like, instinct-driven explanation to a Germany prey to a state of unprecedented anxiety due to its defeat in World War I and, shortly later, to unparalleled inflation, which led to a state of uncertainty as to what tomorrow would bring – claiming that the country was degenerating because it was losing its essential racial purity.
Today we know that this concept of race had no scientific grounds
and led to the Jews being accused of conspiracy.
Hitler never even tried to prove the existence of such conspiracies: he immediately resorted to racial laws, concentration camps first, extermination camps and eventually war.
This kind of intolerance, also towards the slightest differences, is known in psychopathology as paranoia.
A paranoid is so lacking in psychology that he is unable to recognize his own mistakes; he therefore resorts to a set of instinctive mechanisms according to which, since mistakes do exist, he always points the finger at an external culprit.
Unlike other mental illnesses – such as schizophrenia or depression, which tend to isolate individuals – paranoia has this extremely dangerous characteristic: when it affects people in power, it doesn’t result in social exclusion but rather serves as catalyst for their power, all the more so at times of deep collective anxiety.
Here, anthropology comes in handy. Indeed, every society – again, we are talking about very basic responses, typical of tribal societies at a very primitive stage of development – has rites of this kind, cleansing rituals and the ritual of the scapegoat.
That is, many tribal societies that simply live on – let’s say – hunting, should there one year be a scarcity of meat, won't try to find a rational explanation, as we would.
No, they will look for a magical explanation: they will wonder who cast the evil eye on them, identify the culprit, sacrifice him and finally – as anthropologists and psychoanalysts tell us – temporarily experience a degree of relief.
It’s a sort of collective psychotherapy,
very similar to what Hitler and Stalin did, on a much larger scale when, by laying the blame on their racial and class enemy, they obtained the consensus of large swaths of a population crippled by their own repressive policies.
Well, now – sorry – now let's move on to the present day and ask ourselves if we always need rational explanations or whether, maybe, with all the cultural tools available to us, we could become more rational.
In reality, technical-scientific progress is not necessarily conducive to ethical progress.
We discovered printing 500 years ago, but are well aware that still today the mass media – while making more and more information available to us – sometimes offer us more truths, at other times simply multiply lies, not the truth.
The turn of the century brought us the Internet – another huge step towards technical progress. However, the so-called “Internet paradox” became evident from the very start: if I wish to become more competent in any given field, I can find everything on the Internet, so I get more knowledgeable-
But again – after all we are human – up to a certain extent: if I pursue boundless knowledge, when a certain limit is exceeded, it isn’t knowledge that augments, but rather the confusion in my mind.
And over the last few years, social networking sites have also come into the picture.
True, they do have the advantage of helping us reach countless people all around the world,
but studies on social media reveal that they all tend to favour extremely short messages.
And short messages are not the reflection of accurate thoughts or explanations, but rather tend to be aggressive and accusatory simplifications.
Again, pointing the finger at someone, looking for a scapegoat.
You may know that there have even been a few deaths because of this.
In some countries, slanderous comments on some people have been posted on Facebook, causing episodes of lynching.
The fake posts were removed, fine, still some people died because of this.
Talking about the present and our country, there is something objective we are all scared of, a fear that may result in paranoia: the fear of a terrorist attack, an absolutely objective phenomenon itself.
However, even in Italy – although the most serious attacks have been in France – even in Italy there are cases of anxious people saying, “I haven’t dared go out since the onset of terrorism”.
Yet, if we look at statistics, terror still hasn't made any victims in Italy – Islamic terrorism that is.
Actually, there were a few victims but of a terror scare when, in Turin, a stampede started, with people trampled in the crowd. out of the fear of a terrorist attack - which by the way had never occurred.
The person that says, “I'm not going out: as far as I know, there may be a killer outside” is not saying, “I’m not going out because there is a killer called pollution”.
Yet, the European Environmental Agency tells us that pollution accounts for 84,000 deaths per year in Italy alone, and almost half a million in Europe,
supposedly a pristine continent.
Finally, let’s look at this diagram that represents the main European countries and the presence of Muslim immigrants who, obviously, are those we are afraid of since radical terrorists may be hiding among them.
The yellow line indicates the actual percentage, the gray one indicates the presence of Muslims as perceived by the people, according to surveys.
The gray line, as you can see, is steeper than the actual percentage.
That is, the danger is real, but paranoia is even greater.
However, this alarming data hides a piece of good news: the difference between real and fake data - imaginary fear - peaks in Hungary, where Muslim immigrants are thought to be 70 times more than they actually are.
This is all due to paranoia.
Instead, it's minimal in France and Germany - a despite being quite high - where millions and millions of Muslim immigrants have been living and working side by side with the locals for some time now.
What this means is that the best defence against the degeneration of real fear into paranoid fear lies with knowing those who are different and not pointing the finger, doesn’t it? Collaborating with them, admitting that they’re also human.
Of course, when it comes to environmental pollution, we shouldn't point the finger at other but at ourselves ...
Thank you.
(Applause)
Today we’ll start from a tree: a walnut tree, in particular.
The walnut is a very noble, very precious tree.
Fruits, and also a liqueur, can be gotten from the walnut tree. You can extract a fuel from the seed's oil. And naturally, after many years, you can also get precious timber; such timber, for example, historically, over the centuries, has been used to build "forcole" in the city of Venice .
<i>Forcole</i> are those objects that support the oars, in gondolas and other traditional boats, and are built by master carpenters, like my friend Saverio, who pass on, generation after generation, procedures and experiences that actually come from a remote past.
It was precisely Saverio who told me a really interesting story, a tradition that has to do with the walnut tree.
In the countryside of Veneto, they used to plant a walnut tree when a child was born.
It was a sort of way to imagine future; a way to build, in a sense, the future of this newborn child, and not only its own, also the one of its sons because, you know, for example, walnut tree takes 60-70 years before it becomes usable in carpentry, and let's say, this thought, this attention to the future of our children, is something that we share, nowadays for someone also in a dramatic way, Let's think, for example, about those who are forced to leave their own children in someone else's hands, maybe, so they can cross the sea and leave poor or war-torn countries.
And yet, it also happens today, here in our wealthy countries, we don't plant walnut trees anymore.
Indeed, we have cut down most of them; in the '60s and '70s there was an indiscriminate use, and let's say, we no longer perform this imagination of the future.
It actually is a big problem, because not only don't we imagine it, but we somehow suffer from it.
Even worse, we have other people enduring this.
Many examples could be made, but let's take, for example, the environment issue.
It's said that within a few decades we'll have more plastic than fish in the sea; also, let's think of the climate we live in; the reckless use of energy we are making; the waste of resources that perhaps took nature hundreds, thousands, maybe millions of years to create.
Well, we should maybe try to be more careful, actually indeed we should, without a doubt, and to do so scientific research can come in handy, because scientific research has something in common with the idea, with the story of the walnut tree, doesn't it?
Because you often plant a seed without quite knowing what will happen, or at least knowing that the results will only show up in the very long run.
It's the case of Heinrich Hertz, for instance, the first physicist to discover electromagnetic waves, predicted a few years earlier by Maxwell - Hertz in 1887 - and he didn't really know what they would be used for. He also said, "well, it's a proof of Master Maxwell's theory, but I have no clear idea what their applications will be like".
Still, today we know that, just to sort out of many a couple of examples, electromagnetic waves are at the core of several aspects of our society: let's take communications, just to name one, but also, just to make another example, to the cure of tumors, that is done with microwaves.
So, let's say, scientific research actually is, in many cases, imagining the future by planting a seed of walnut tree.
It also happens today, it was in the news just a few days ago the impending constructiong of a new big improvement of the current LHC accelerator, which in ten, fifteen years will allow us to explore new frontiers of physics, matter and of fundamental interactions.
Those who are building it today might not get to see it running, or at least will not see all the results it gets.
Likewise, we, even me, somehow, in my research activity, deal with something that might even go beyond my life expectancy: the attempt to bring on Earth a piece of the Sun, that is, to reproduce hydrogen fusion in the laboratory.
It's a problem which stems from an urgent need; I mentioned before the energy issue.
Imagining a future also means imagining an energetically sustainable one for our children, for future generations.
What we have today certainly isn't; fusion can provide an answer, it can make a fundamental contribution, from this point of view.
Why? Well, first of all,
let's see what fusion is.
Fusion is basically a relatively elementary process, through which two light nuclei of hydrogen isotopes fuse together and produce a neutron and helium, and in this rearrangement of the nuclear structure of matter, energy is released.
This energy can subsequently be transformed into electricity and has a series of remarkable advantages.
Firstly, it is an intrinsically CO2-free process; no fossil fuels are used; it is based on an essentially unlimited fuel: water, from which we obtain deuterium and lithium widely present in the Earth's crust, from which tritium can then be obtained.
By nature, not by human choice, it’s intrinsically safe.
It does not give rise to long-term radioactive waste.
It doesn’t expose to risks of weapons proliferation. No doubt it’s, therefore, a source that has remarkable advantages, in addition to enabling, together with renewables, together with energy storage, to construct concentrated sources that allow, for instance, the development of an energy-based transport economy.
Well, you might say, then we are done; actually we aren't, we're not done yet.
It's a bit difficult, and one
of the fundamental difficulties is, these two nuclei we want to fuse, don't really love to melt together because they are positively charged, so they'd rather repel each other. In order to try to overcome this natural tendency of theirs,
we strongly heat them up, to temperatures that are even higher than at the centre of the Sun - over 100 million centigrade degrees - and at these temperatures these nuclei acquire extremely elevated speeds, they collide with each other and in this crash they can reach such distance that electrostatic repulsion is overcome by attractive force, by nuclear attractive interaction.
So, this is what we are trying to do.
Of course, the problem is trying to make a bottle, a box that can hold a piece of the Sun.
And this is also non trivial, of course, scientifically and technologically. We have learnt to do it pretty well by using magnetic fields.
They are essentially toroidal doughnuts where we introduce magnetic fields and these magnetic fields are able to keep our fuel away from the material walls of the container and therefore keep it together in a limited space, where we can use it to try to get the energy we need.
Well, this story was born - this is long story of research.
It is almost as old as an adult walnut tree.
It was born after World War II, in the early '50s.
One of the protagonists is Andrei Sakharov, perhaps some of you remembers him, he won the Nobel Peace Prize and was also a great physicist.
To him, together with his colleague Tamm, we owe one of the first works that identify the tokamak, which is one of these configurations that we are using.
Since that time we've come a long way.
We've made mistakes, we went back. We also scored many good results, we've made great leaps forward, reconsiderations - in short, a bit like evolution of science.
From that seed that our precursors planted decades ago we’ve come a long way even here in Italy.
For example, what you're seeing is the RFX experiment in Padua, where I worked for many years, and which is one of the main experiments in history, and today also in the current research in fusion.
And also here in Italy in a short while, actually in these weeks, we've started designing a new big experiment, which will be carried out in Italy and could be labelled completely as "Made in Italy". It is called DTT, Divertor Test Tokamak, and it will somehow represent for us but also, especially, for the generations to come, for my students today, but also for my students' students, the future. And will have to provide significant help for what our fundamental purpose is, that is, to finally carry out an experiment, a reactor that can produce electricity from the fusion of hydrogen.
A fundamental step will therefore be ITER.
ITER is this experiment that we're building in southern France, much bigger than DTT, with the contribution of seven big actors, such as the European Union, United States, Japan, China, India, Korea, Russia, and that will begin its activities in about ten years and will have to prove that fusion is scientifically feasible and will then be followed by an experimental reactor called DEMO.
So it's clear, as I told you - let's get back to our tree - that this is a research that has been developed and is still being developed over several decades.
When will we have electricity
from fusion, I'm often asked,
I'd say, we'll have it, and now we are quite aware, perhaps in the past our precursors were a bit too optimistic; today we are a bit more certain of the time schedules, we know the difficulties and I think it is pretty safe to say that we will get electricity out of fusion in the second half of this century.
Of course, it could also be done earlier, if there really were the willingness even by our administrators to invest more, but that's the way it is. The problem is complex, and this will therefore come in the second half of the century.
It is definitely beyond my working expectancy and probably beyond my life expectancy as well and therefore it feels natural, as years pass by, to even wonder if this is worth it, right?
Because I realise, this thing gets a bit beyond me And I eventually answer myself that it certainly is, because, let's say, I also like the idea of being one of these people who helped planting this little seed that I hope tomorrow can become a big chestnut tree and help solve this critical energy issue.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Once upon a time the field of Via Moscova.
This story is set a few short steps from this theatre, a stone's throw from Duomo Square.
It was 1979.
The year when this photo was taken, Jody Scheckter, in Monza, becomes Formula 1 champion, and AC Milan earns its tenth star in the national title, and Pietro Mennea sets an unbelievable record, 19"72 in the 200 metres.
Just imagine that 40 years on, even now, the record is unbeaten at a European level.
No European has ever run faster than him.
But above all, something happens that changed forever the history of an area of Milan.
The United Nations proclaim 1979 the International Year of the Child.
Something that would normally
make no difference. And yet Carlo Tognoli, back then mayor of Milan, decides to do something.
So he seizes the opportunity and clears the area in front of Via Moscova 26 from the rubble of a bombing of the Second World War and he realises a football field.
All right, it isn't a glamourous place, it's an esplanade with two goals, which soon rust, and a basketball court; but I assure you, it was a magnificent place, because it had a noble mission: sports for the youth.
I remember that field as if it were yesterday.
I went there every day, with my ball under my arm, it was five minutes from home, but it only took me three to get there.
I couldn't wait to get to the field.
It was the neighbourhood's Maracanà.
A theatre of memorable battles.
For instance, I remember the derby between the clerks of the INAIL building and the funeral home employees in Porta Nuova.
(Laughter) So no applauses: people would keep their hands in their pockets. (Laughter)
And then there was - (Applause) And then there was the super-classic between the Carabinieri from the barracks you see up there, and the male nurses of the Fatebenefratelli hospital, and they beat each other like drums:
Florentine football, more than football.
Regardless, aside from these unmissable fixtures, the field had become in the 1980s an exceptional meeting, socialisation spot for the neighbourhood, where kids like me were doing - without knowing, and this is key - one of the healthiest things anyone can do in life: sports.
So up to here the story goes nice and dandy.
But it didn't.
Because something big arrives, something huge, which puts an end to the story of the field, and of the 1980s.
Do you know what? The 1990s.
And Milan turns into Swiss cheese,
holes were dug up everywhere to build underground car parks.
Even under your house I'm sure there was some big hole: there were holes in Gramsci Square, one in San Ambrogio, 25th April square - Guys, could a hole be missing
in Via Moscova? Of course no.
And guess where did they dig?
Where the field was.
Here was the end of the endless games,
the end of the memorable battles.
Does the story of the field end here?

No. It depends.
Good, that's how I like you.

It depends. On whom? On us.

Because, if there's one thing I learnt, one thing I learnt over these years is that it everything always depends on us.
So, if we don't want this story to end with only a handful of extra car parks, I'm asking for your help, because I won't go very far by myself.
So I'm asking all of you to stand up with me.
I'll make you work even on a Sunday.
Everybody up?
Put your hands behind your backs, so we'll also beat the record of the highest number of old dudes watching a construction site.
(Laughter) And imagine how this story may end.
Because it is true that a void is a problem; it must be filled.
Some do so buying a motorcycle, or a pair of shoes.
This is a huge void, we need to fly higher.
So who says that, in the end, on top of the car park layers we can't put some icing and realise a football field that is nicer and stronger than before?
[It takes] a bit of creativity and enthusiasm.
So we can pick up from where we left off, so no one is taking away our football field.
And what about Mennea's record? Shall we try to beat it?
We need an athletics track, and a building licence.
François said so, I'm from Cagliari, and I miss the beach so bad, so let's dream big, let's pick up some sand and let's build a beach volley court.
Don't ask me to bring over the sea, we'll do instead "Italian style", something creative, let's build a basketball court, and let's paint it blue, sea-like.
Thank you, you may sit down now, because we declare the construction complete, the fastest building job in history.
We finished it in one minute.
You won't believe it, but - (Applause) Unity is strength.
But what we daydreamt for a minute is exactly what I was faced with when I took a walk and went to see the end of works, in 2013.
We owe this to a group of citizens, who, just like we did, did not get discouraged, they fought with patience and tenacity.
They built all the car parks, the Green Moscova Cooperative, but they also put on the icing.
So can we open it now, can't we?
Because the centre is closed.
They haven't opened it yet. And incidentally, I find out that in those days the city of Milan was looking for an administrator.
So I run home, full of enthusiasm, and I jot down a 52-page project on how I'd manage that place.
I place it in an envelope, and I send it to Chiara Bisconti, alderman of sport, 2, Via Marconi, Milan City Hall.
And I wait.
(Laughter) With much optimism, come on, we'll manage the field.
A week, two weeks, two months, three months, four months -
The phone doesn't ring.
It finally does, at eight at night.
Unknown number. Shall I pick up?
It's dinner time, after all.

Yes. Guess who it was.
It was Fastweb wanting to sell me the mega fibre - No, I'm joking.
They told me, "Don't say that, it's a sponsor", but I did.
(Laughter) No - (Applause) It must have happened to you too.
Worse, it was the alderman.
They had loved the project, and the next day I had to go to her office to collect the keys because "Soon", in a hurry, "Luigi, you have to open within 30 days."
You understand how the enthusiasm and the happiness last about one minute and soon turns into - (Laughter) absolute terror.
And I assure you that if you only knew what I put in that envelope, in that project, you would also panick big time.
Let's read it together, so we take up a little dose of fear all together, which is good for you.
I won't read all 52 pages.
I wrote: creating a sports area that acts as a model for the whole city, managed according to ethical and innovative criteria to promote sports, wellbeing and health fostering participation and integration beyond any differences and prejudices.
How do you do all that stuff?
I had really shot high, because I wanted to manage the field, but - (Laughter) (Applause) Settle down, you follow the other 51 pages of the project methodically, and if we want to create a model area we need to improve the structure that was already there, because there indeed were a few flaws.
A 20,000-euro loan by a friendly bank, immediate liquidity, my dad, who's here somewhere.
(Laughter) And we build the locker rooms - there were no hot showers, no benches, and we cover the athletics track, which was made of asphalt, with technical material. And while we are at it,
we make the real dream come true: we receive truckloads of sand for the beach volley court.
Then, over the years, little by little, after two years, we also realised a gym, so we put up a roof on a sports centre, which had been entirely outdoor.
We're halfway through the construction, we've built the roof, which we celebrate with "malloreddus alla campidanese" prepared by my mom that morning.
I hadn't prepared them.
Is Pietro Palvarini here? My mom had prepared them, I can tell you now.
So the sports centre is the atomic bomb, guys.
It's sensational, the first people we want to welcome once we open the gates are the children, those of the Year of the Child, who are now in their 50s, but they have their own children, so more people in the neighbourhood.
But there isn't much time, because we only have 30 days.
So myself and my partner, who's the first person I called when I saw the ad from the city, together with him and the old five-a-side friends we quickly put together a non-profit to realise that mission and open the field.
So, here we are.
We look more like those guys welcoming you onto the ferry, but I assure you it's us.
Over time, we create over six years a veritable team, made up of educators, coaches and project managers.
The projects are the heart of the sports centre; they are intended for those whose life's starting blocks were a few steps behind ours, due a condition of physical or social disadvantage.
And those like me who had all the fortunes in the world are pleased to wait, and support them to take a journey of sports and life together.
So there are plenty of activities.
We have 100 free courses for low-income families, so those in need have a fair chance to play sports.
And then there's the issue of welcoming migrants, so we have multicultural football, basketball teams that are open to anyone.
During Saturday mornings trainings, the centre becomes the navel of the world.
There are kids from Palestine, Kosovo, Senegal, Mali who play with Italian kids to have an enriching experience?
The more diverse the cultures, the richer the people.
We also focus on the accessibility of sports to people with disabilities.
So we have a program of crossdisciplinary activities open to people of every age and ability.
Because if we get different people to do sports together, the experience is far richer.
This mix of individuals and this variety of people on the field is the key that makes this centre the home of sports for everyone.
To realise our mission there, otherwise it's useless, it remains just a sheet of paper.
So there are kids on wheelchairs who come to the centre to play with the staff of the non-profits accompanying them, there are families who take their children to attend our courses and they join gym courses alongside kids with disabilities.
There are phenomenal visually impaired baseball players, who open the eyes of those who sometimes forget how good sports are for you, and they do it together.
And of course running cannot be missing: the easiest of sports, you only need a pair of shoes.
We have a free running club where disabled children and volunteers train to run marathons and relays.
We began tentatively with five people then, after three years, step by step, the whole world arrived there are 500 of us, with firms, partners, institutions that have joined us, 50 youths with disabilities, and the day of the marathon is an extraordinary event.
With all these activities, all these projects, with the necessary enthusiasm, leaving home, rain or shine, winning, losing, sweating, falling and getting back up, in other words, doing sports, change begins.
The most profound one.
The one within yourself.
So the mood is lifted, you breathe - It shows that I started doing yoga last Monday, huh?
You bunch of phonies.
So you just feel better.
You're more sociable, you laugh more, you joke more, you reconnect with yourselves.
I saw this change in the face of a person who one day came to the centre, a company manager who had supported our projects.
"I want to come and play with the disabled kids."
"Great, come over."
He came in tensed, after a presumably hellish day at work, with all the muscles in his face contracted.
He held out his hand to greet the first kid, and this boy with down syndrome just sprang into a wonderful hug
I'm jealous, I'd have liked to receive a hug like that.
It wasn't your everyday hug.
It was warm, human, long, therapeutic.
And this person got better immediately.
He had fun, he relaxed, and he played with the kids; he still thanks us today.
A hug was enough to trigger all this.
What an ability!
After you witness episodes like these, you ask yourself some questions.
I know.
Who's disabled?
And I gave myself some answers.
We all have disabilities and abilities.
Everyone of us is able to do one thing perfectly, and everyone of us is incapable of doing other things, we have our disabilities, possibly because we've worked too much?
Because we've been looking at our mobile everywhere like dowsers, and we have the world's most nimble thumbs, but we've let other parts of us wither?
Can it be?
So sports are always round the corner, there's always a gym open 24 hours a day, seven days a week, to help us train the most important parts.
Our body? Easy bet.

The head? The Romans said it already.
The heart, above all.
So it is a gym where each of us can bring, first and foremost, our abilities.
Let's start from the nice things, because we share those first, then the not-so-nice ones, our disabilities, will look more human, more manageable, overcomeable.
This is why sports are magic, and sports change lives.
And just like it changed mine, and in small or large part the lives of the other protagonists of this story, I am sure that it can be achieved in other places with similar social challenges.
Because Milan, Italy and even other countries are full of fields ready to take flight.
And, I hope, there are plenty of people - maybe even in this theatre, at home, around the world, ready to invest in the magic of sports.
The sports revolution, I feel it, can explode anywhere and in anyone, provided one condition. One condition must be fulfilled: our willingness to light the fuse.
Our willingness to take the first step, our willingness to dare more, to get involved, play more.
That's it, play more.
I'm actually terrible at this myself, because I too forget this thing some time.
So I wanted to write it down in the most visible place I could find, a place I would have in front of my nose ten, a hundred, a thousand times a day, together with the other protagonists who have joined me in this story.
I wanted to write it in the name of the project and in the name of the sports centre, which is called, "Play more".
Thank you.
(Applause)
Why should a citizen that has nothing to do with justice be interested in what happens inside a prison?
Voltaire said that the dignity of a nation, the civilization of a nation, is measured in its jails, but it is a very theoretical statement.
Why should each one of us know what happens in a prison?
I want to tell you two stories, two American stories, very similar, but with very different endings.
Jack Henry Abbott was born in the US in the 1940s. He comes from a family on the margins of the society, starts committing crimes from a very young age, and from his earliest youth, he knows the prison experience.
He has a violent and irascible temper and also commits crimes inside the prison: he kills people, he uses violence, so he gets to know terrible prison conditions.
The only thing he can do when he's not in solitary confinement is reading, and soon he also starts writing. Then he starts a correspondence with a very famous writer, Henry Miller, who believes in his talent, publishes their correspondence in the New York Review of Books and then publishes the book "In the Belly of the Beast," thanks to which Abbott also becomes very rich, and he's set free. However, inebriated from this situation and from his violent temper, one night in a cafe, he kills a waiter in a trivial quarrel.
So he goes back to jail and loses all his wealth after being sued by the waiter's widow, and shortly after that, he kills himself.
Edward Banker, was born in the United States too in the 1930s and has a very similar story to that of Jack, at least until at a certain moment, when in the San Quentin State Prison, where he is interned, he meets a volunteer who undertakes with him a path of writing and reading and at one point gives him a typewriter.
This typewriter will change Edward's life because it makes him start writing, and soon he will become a well-known writer, but above all, one of the most famous screenwriters,
sought-after for his screenplays, among which are "Heat," with Robert de Niro and Al Pacino, and "Straight Time," with Dustin Hoffman.
Edward too, at some point, gets out of prison and becomes rich, but decides to take advantage of this chance and lives his last years as a well-liked and rich man and dies peacefully of old age.
Maybe you're beginning to understand why it's important to be interested in what happens in a prison.
Abbott tells us one thing: "Be careful!
Happiness is a serious need for everyone, and if people are not able to reach this happiness in a legal way, they will reach it with illegal means.
What we try to do in the prison of Bologna is to make sure that people can pursue their own happiness - whatever that may mean for each one of them - with legal instruments.
Some of them have never known these instruments or don't know they can use them.
In the prison of Bologna you can study from primary school to university. Thanks to a convention, a strengthened collaboration with my alma mater, the university of Bologna, it's possible to study in the prison in a multimedia room using a secure connection with the university. And from that location the lessons can be followed and one can get a degree.
There are people who obtained more than one degree in prison.
In Bologna you can also work.
FiD, Fare Impresa in Dozza, is an Italian mechanical company built inside the prison of Bologna by three of the main companies of the sector - not just in Bologna but in the world - which are GD, Ima and Marchesini. They decided to build a mechanical firm inside the prison. The firm provides employment to about 15 inmates, and most of the over 100 people who worked for this firm while in prison kept working for these companies or for associated ones when they got out.
This video behind me is the trailer of Filippo Vendemmiati's film "Meno male è lunedì," which is about this experience.
The name "Thank God it's Monday" is because, unlike most of us, who on Sunday are afraid to go back to work the next day, for those inside the prison, Monday is a good day because they can go back to work and have something to do.
But there's also a tailor's shop, which is in the women's section. It's called "Gomito a gomito," because we are in Gomito Street and because the inmates don't have much space. This tailor has an own production of bags, scarves and other items, but it's also works for Ikea in Casalecchio di Reno.
In the prison there's also a greenhouse, created thanks to the collaboration of many people.
What is done in prison never happens because a single institution or a single person decides to do something, but because lots of people get together and decide to build something.
This greenhouse was opened thanks to the collaboration of local and public authorities, like the municipality, but also thanks to the collaboration of many cooperatives, and makes biological horticulture, like very a tasty salad.
A dairy farm will be opened next month, thanks to a company from Puglia, which makes dairy products.
We don't know exactly why, but it decided to bet on this challenge, it chose to give work to the inmates and teach them how to be dairymen.
Another instrument we try to offer, one that is very powerful for each one of us, is music.
Who doesn't like music?
A few years ago, just before he died, maestro Claudio Abbado realized two amazing experiences in Bologna, one in a children's hospital and the other in the prison of Bologna. He created the Papageno choir, a choir where male and female inmates sing with external choristers and musicians.
They meet twice a week and study music, singing, clearly with a professional maestro, maestro Napolitano, and they perform in many places, inside the prison but also outside.
In June they performed in the Senate, in front of the highest authorities of the state, and at the start of this month even in front of the Pope on the occasion of the Jubilee for Prisoners.
Another instrument that can change people, that can change anyone of us, is cinema.
Who doesn't like to go to the cinema, regardless of the genres?
For two years, in the prison of Bologna we've had a course on cinema professions.
Some inmates learn to see a film as a critic, to write screenplays, to produce the soundtrack or to edit the film, and last year, we had the first cinema festival in our prison, with the panel made by inmates and with very special guests.
This is the festival poster.
This is the panel, you see, it gathers anywhere, where ever space is available. Matteo Garrone, Daniele Luchetti and Gabriele Mainetti came to visit us, and Gabriele, as often happened, last year won the festival with the film "Jeeg Robot," one of the many prizes that it got, and our sponsor was Claudia Cardinale, who received a gift by the oldest woman of the prison at that moment. She was so touched because Claudia has Tunisian origins and that woman was Tunisian.
Sport.
Who doesn't like sport?
In Bologna there is a rugby team called Giallo Dozza that plays in the C1 championship - they always play at home, they can't play away games, but the other teams are glad to come by.
And as always happens in rugby, there is also a third "half," one of the most meaningful moments of this sport.
In Bologna it is possible to compare different cultures.
In the last two years a group of prisoners coming from countries that went through the Arab Spring held discussions with professors and scholars about the Italian constitution and Arab constitutions, and this itinerary was also told in a film by Marco Santarelli that is still around in movie theaters and is called "Dustur," which means "constitution."
And finally: if food doesn't make us
feel better, what does? Many famous chefs come to visit us and teach our inmates how to cook, like chef Rubio, who is a rugby player and also a fan of our rugby team, Giallo Dozza.
So, let's go back to the initial question: why should each citizen be interested in what happens inside a prison?
A couple years ago, I was invited to publicly read a letter, during a cultural exchange, during a festival in Bologna, in a theater of the city, where public figures read letters of their colleagues from other countries.
I was very lucky because I had the chance to read the letter of a colleague from Norway, the director of the Bastoy prison.
Bastoy prison is that beautiful prison on an island - you may have seen it in pictures, in films, on TV.
And my colleague told me a simple but really nice thing.
Basically he said, "Dear colleague, in the end, what does our work consist of?
Our work consists of building good neighbors."
Because tomorrow, in a week, in 10 years, in a lifetime, those people that are now in prison will be my neighbors, your neighbors, the neighbors of your kids.
And I don't know about you, but honestly, I'd prefer Edward Banker as a neighbor. Thank you.
(Applause)
This is not a lesson but the story of a guy like you who is looking to use his time to try to do something for those who have real needs.
I am trying to use new technologies, scientific innovation, to try to solve global challenges.
To match problems far from us with solutions which are available right now or to find new ones.
It is incredible to be part of a startup that, with only a few people, a few financial resources and maybe a lot of craziness, can try to solve some of those problems which are serious and afflict our society.
Like I said I am a guy like you and maybe because I live in between Harvard and MIT, I got bitten by the entrepreneurship bug.
Here we see daily the invention and creation of incredible technological solutions.
Software to predict tsunamis, immunotherapy that gives us the possibility to instruct our cells to self-produce their medicine, machines for encoding our DNA, which can be done in a few seconds.
Despite that, we have to ask ourselves how to link the world with this technology, how this technology has the potential to have an impact on those who need it.
Very often, in a world where we talk about tech, we forget that technology makes no sense at all if it can't, somehow, fight poverty, reduce some of the inequalities, and resolve the most serious problems.
How many of us know Snapchat and the photos that disappear in seconds, but do not know the potentiality of RNA?
I think this needs to make us reflect.
And this is a journey that started for me in Cambodia.
My first job was not at an investment bank in London, but it was with the United Nations in Cambodia.
In a city called Phnom Penh, which we cannot even pronounce the name. And one of the projects that I most liked, the most fascinating was to try to help the local farmers in the rice fields, to diversify their income.
How to create an alternative source for these farmers.
One of the projects that I worked on was exactly that: to look to diversify their income by farming silk.
Sericulture could have an impact on the farmers which could be very high.
After one year, lots of money invested by the United Nations on the project, but unfortunately the project half failed.
Actually, let me be honest: it was a complete failure.
The farmers were able to produce silk in large quantity. Unfortunately the quality wasn't good enough to connect them with the global market, to give them an outlet for their product and increase their income.
So, what to do?
I was lucky enough to have always been a big fan of TED Talks.
In one of them an Italian scientist, who now lives in Boston, Fiorenzo Omenetto, explains how silk could be used as an innovative biomaterial. With a platform of scientific and innovative findings related to biomaterials, physics, chemistry, electronics and many other implementations.
When I saw that TED talk, my life obviously was changed.
Fiorenzo and I started to think how to connect the rural Cambodia, which I had experienced, with their scientific findings at a laboratory in Boston.
At the same time, I was lucky to land in Harvard,
where I tried to understand how entrepreneurship, social entrepreneurship, could form a winning duet to solve people's problems in Cambodia and in our society.
I have been lucky to have other mentors and other people with whom I could consult. One of them was Muhammad Yunus, winner of the Nobel Peace Prize, also the founder of the modern micro-finance.
In one of our meetings I asked him, "Prof. Yunus, how can I create a sustainable business that has the potential to solve a very serious problem but doing it in a pragmatic way, being idealistic, but at the same time pragmatic?"
His answer was disarming in its simplicity.
He said "Livio, try to focus on someone that you love, someone that you know well enough to be able to resolve any specific problem for them.
Focus on this problem, and you will see the solution will come.
At the same time, you will see that your solution will be applicable, not only for that person, but for many others as well.
So, in my head, how could I not think about those fields in Cambodia, now from Cambridge, in Massachusetts.
And how could I not think about "T", a little girl, the daughter of a farmer who was working on our project with the United Nations.
How could I not think of the problems of a seven-year-old girl living in a rural area, isolated, in a place that no one knows about?
I remember conversations that we had with her mother, with her;
I asked what their problems were.
One was that the closest rural clinic is 47 km away from their house.
And her mum, like all mums, wanted the best for her children, so she took that route many times, back and forth, to take Tida for her immunisations for polio and measles, for vaccines that we all have, so that we can be immune forever, and never need to think about it again.
Why?
One of Tida's biggest problems, of public health care, is having access to medicines, particularly to vaccines.
97% of the vaccines need to be refrigerated and Tida is not alone: 2,5 million children die every year, as they do not have access to medicines that the rest of us have close at hand.
One reason why these medicines do not reach the people who really need them is very simple, and again disarming; it's the cold chain.
These substances need to be kept between 2° and 8° Celsius.
Outside of that range, they all need to be thrown away.
So I thought: is there anyone in this world, is there any scientist, is there any technology that could solve that problem? It seems fairly straightforward.
One of the technologies that Fiorenzo and his team developed is based on silk, and how it can stabilise vaccines and other medicines, so they don't need to be refrigerated and they can be shipped at a higher temperature.
I wish it could be as simple as inserting a vial inside a silk cocoon.
Obviously it's not:
it's a biotechnological process based on dissolving a protein inside the silk that reacts as a matrix for trapping the molecules of vaccines and other medicines, thus stabilising them and preventing molecular movements, preventing the possibility of aggregation at high temperatures, of denaturation and loss of effectiveness.
I presume it has the potential to solve the problem of access to vaccines, to take these medicines anywhere in the world.
We can get to children like Tida and many others, bringing these products to them, giving them their "life insurance" through vaccines that work.
And I believe our startup which we founded after two years, a lot of sleepless nights, many millions dollars of funding received from public and private, has the potential to change the outlook for vaccines, creating new medicines, new vaccines, new products of a different category, which do not need refrigeration to get to the children who most need them.
At the same time, I would like to give you a small snapchat of how it's possible to create a bridge between sustainability, science and entrepreneurship.
At the same time I want to ask you as you are young people like me, how you would solve some of the problems that are important to you for someone specific that you care about.
And I can't wait to see you implementing these solutions for someone who really needs them.
Many thanks. (Applause)
The first organ that shows up is the heart, not the brain.
I wasn't expecting that.
Maybe it's something we forget as we go through life.
This is an ultrasound from the third month.
The fifth month, a morphological view, and the poetry is already over.
The sonographer moves on to an image that you don't understand and says, "And the cerebellum?
Let's see if it's there.
Yes, there's the cerebellum.
And the femur?
Is it the right length? Yes, it's fine."
And I say to myself,
"What about the cerebellum?
Why would the cerebellum not be there?
What do we do, if it's not there?
And how long should the femur be?"
And he goes on, now he is well down the list of organs.
This is how we are born.
Measured, calibrated, verified and compared to standard parameters.
We have already been given a grade, the Apgar index, at birth.
It is a summary of health parameters that somehow demonstrates, in decimal points, like grades at school, how healthy your son is from day zero.
That's how it is. And this is just the trailer.
The ultrasounds are the trailer of a film you'll watch later.
Because as soon as it is born, you have to weigh how many grams it grows every week.
It should be 30, 40, 50, not more.
And your son won't keep still.
You have this restless child on the scale.
And if they are 40-50, it's hard to see because it fluctuates.
And if it's less, you panic because maybe he's not eating enough, he's not getting enough milk.
He has to grow so many more centimetres. You are shown a graph every time you visit the pediatrician, the growth curve of the average population.
And your son is there above,
below, or on the average. Afterwards, there's the parent's pride: "Our son is above average."
If he's under, there's a bit shame.
Until at some point, he may slip below the tenth percentile.
Then you panic.
There is the panic: maybe he has to have hormonal treatment, maybe he's not growing as fast as he should,
maybe something's wrong. But in fact, you never actually look at him.
You're checking him - which is very different.
(Music from a music box) So (Music stops) with this parental anxiety driving you, you do everything you can:
mobiles, activity gyms, stimuli of all kinds -
as if nature were not enough, as if we can't just let it take its course, as if real life were not interesting enough to watch.
So we go and hunt for the "right" things.
We read the packaging. "Something suitable for a 3-year-old girl", we ask the shopkeeper rather than the 3-year-old herself.
The right book, the right package.
While you're reading the box, you're not paying them any attention.
And you ask the shopkeeper's advice on what to buy.
You already have the market on your back, besieging you.
The market has been on the scene since day one, at least that's how we feel.
When Pietro was born, he was already surrounded by stuff.
The creams, the gels, the little brushes.
Pietro was born, and he already was a consumer.
And they don't let go; marketing psychologists know very well that the market speaks in two languages. One is what it uses with the children, with the kids, a language that you don't fully understand, made up of bright colours and shapes - well researched, of course.
And then of characters from cartoons and films that appear everywhere:
on napkins, on birthdays plates, on cakes, backpacks, notebooks, diaries -
everywhere.
So much so, that you find them without trying.
And you think your child wants them - but he doesn't want them.
Right at that moment, he's not dreaming, he's not even playful: he's just performing a task.
Because it's the market who desires on his behalf.
He is performing that task.
And you're busy doing something else because the message is different for you, the code is different.
Your code is the instructions printed on the packages, where is written, "This item is suitable for", "This contains vitamins", this is good for this, for that, for something else.
Because the psychologists know you need to feel like a good parent.
And this is the message addressed to you.
(School bell ringing) To this regulatory avalanche, school adds some rules of its own.
When Pietro started nursery school, we were a little late registering him.
We took him a few days after we arrived, and the group had already formed. The children were singing a song with the teacher from the teacher's song book.
So Peter, trying to join in, asked, "Can I sing the mountain song?"
He thought that singing this song would be his way in and had made up this song himself.
The teacher looked at the songbook and said, "No, sorry.
We don't have the mountain song, so we can't sing it."
In the nursery, there were lots of ready-made outlines that you had to colour in, but don't go over the edges!
Use the right colours.
If you used dark colours, purple and black, then the teacher would ask you, "Is something wrong?"
Margherita, seven years old, asked us over breakfast, "How long is 60 seconds?"
So, we showed her.
Margherita doesn't have a watch, a seven-year-old shouldn't need a watch.
But what was going on?
We asked, "Why do you need to know?"
"Tomorrow we have a test." This is a timed test the students take in second grade.
From then on, for Margherita, time was measured in reverse.
Instead of flowing naturally, as in life, it was a countdown, as in a factory, at work, like here. And from then on,
it caused tremendous anxiety.
One of her classmates comes from Sri Lanka.
She went away for a few days to visit her family's home country.
When she got back, the teacher asked, "Where have you been these last few days?"
She replied, "In Sri Lanka, my parents' country."
And the teacher said ...
"OK, well, now get on with your work."
Catch up with your work?
Did the teacher even "see" that child?
Or did she project onto her the student she was expected to be?
On that day, the girl had to learn the lesson!
This is what happens: In reality, we don't even see the children.
We don't see them for what they are, what they are doing now, but for what they haven't become yet, what they don't do or have yet.
We basically measure the distance between our expectations, our standards, our metrics and who they really are, which is not enough for us.
And their life goes on for 16, 20 years. When I meet the children I interview for my research and ask them to do certain tasks together, they ask me, "But all these tests, these checks, these exams, these interviews - what are they for, in Italy, by the way?
What's the prize?
The objective of this race?"
And then they tell you, "Look, I don't really know what I like.
I always did what they told me to.
And I don't feel involved.
Maybe I'm not good enough.
Maybe I won't make the grade, maybe I won't, I don't know.
I do not know what path to choose, I'm afraid of the future."
How is this possible?
How can they be afraid of reality and the future? In an age so full of potential, of passion, of fulfillment?
How could we put a whole generation in that condition?
Instead of introducing them to reality, we kept them in exile, in captivity, instead of giving them real world tasks and responsibility.
I think we can no longer afford this foolishness.
The reason is simple: we are forgetting that our ancestors emigrated as kids.
And the whole family put their faith in the kids who were leaving to thrive and be happy.
The adults depended on the kids.
We are forgetting that the 20th century was built on the ideas and discoveries of young people between 20 and 30 years old!
Einstein was 26 when he published his famous paper on relativity.
He was not that old, white-haired man with a moustache.
No, he was a 26-year-old youth.
We are also forgetting that 21st century's biggest companies were founded by young people between 18 and 25 years old.
This is not the best of all possible worlds.
Perhaps we need to admit it.
And precisely because it's not a perfect world, and we must realise that, we must try to find room to let the youngsters in.
Take on the task of improvement.
This is how you get in because if the world is already full, like a museum, there's no way in, there's no room for you.
And that's how youngsters feel, they feel useless, like there's no place for them.
They were not expected, every seat is already taken.
A bit like in this theater.
So how do you find a way in if everything is set up, everything's been done?
It's not just that, life has changed a lot over the last few years.
And my experiments are not important compared to what this boy already has and will have.
I'm not the measure of my children, and today's adults aren't the measure of today's youth.
I'm speaking for myself.
I went to a high school that has changed completely, a university that has changed completely.
I was a conscientious objector and did 20 months of community service.
None of this exists now.
I found a job with the Yellow Pages!
(Laughter) I looked for "Research Companies", I sent off six applications, and I got a job.
How can I ever tell this to a young boy?
I spoke one language, I lived in a city, I did only one job.
Also this, probably, is no longer the measure of the teenagers before me.
I believe this coming generation - and many of them are here now - should not be trained as students to carry out prescribed tasks because there is a manual that had some value yesterday.
They must be trained as pioneers, which is a completely different approach.
It means you have to move to the frontiers of knowledge.
It means that you have to study, and you must also "learn how to unlearn", as Gregory Bateson put it.
Continuously rewriting the rules, basically.
It means you have to be trained to ask questions.
Not trained to answer questions, like in school.
And these questions must be meaningful, not the irrelevant ones that are asked at school and at university,
where some ask questions and some others reply, and the ones who answer know they have to give the answer the questioner want to hear.
Such a training is not really a door to knowledge or discovery, a way of engaging with the real world.
Because everything has already been said and done.
One day, we were by the sea.
Pietro was 10, 11 and told me, "Dad, I want to be rich."
We are not rich.
I didn't really know how to answer, but I came up with this, "Great, so you can be generous."
And he said, "No, that's not what I meant."
(Laughter) So I tried to back-peddle and said, "OK, Pietro.
If you want to get rich, you have to find something that solves a common problem,
something that somehow provides a solution to a collective need.
That could pay off." He turned to me and said,
"We could turn all this seawater into drinkable water."
There we have it.
This is the challenge I'm talking about.
Try to take on real problems, for which there is an urgent need, and try looking for solutions with the children.
This is what I do: set up interdisciplinary groups bringing together children's lateral thinking, the energy of youth, and the maturity of adulthood.
Together we think about neighbourhoods, libraries, schools to work out how things could be improved.
Let's turn off the spotlight,
break fee of the blackmail of the present because real is much more than reality.
The present is not the inventory of the possible.
Let's imagine what else can be done in a place.
In a project I just did, I even asked them this, "Look, we have shopkeepers, residents.
I'm asking all of you to act as trees.
Turn yourself into spokespersons for nature.
Because we need to understand, whatever we do in this neighborhood, we need to take the long-term future into account, and you are the only ones that can bring nature back to life."
They did it, and they were happy. This is what I'm talking about.
In real life, we do it already. We get together and collaborate here in Italy, but only on special occasions, when there is a serious emergency, like an earthquake or a flood.
When those things happen, we help each other out.
We never ask, "Who are we helping?" we don't think that way. We have an urgent task in the real world, and we do it together.
And it works.
In those situations we often watch the young people, and the next day we call them "Mud Angels".
We only notice them in those situations.
They are not so happy about that.
And with good reason.
That's what I'm talking about: reality tasks.
Ultimately, it's Hannah Arendt's definition of politics.
Politics is not about taking care of each other; rather, it's taking care together of the world, every day.
Thank you.
