In the beginning it was the Word.
Words change our world,
the reality we live in.
They do have this power.
Let me give you an example: Six percent of children under the age of 18 are diagnosed today with ADHD: a narrow box where to pigeonhole non-compliant boxes.
There is no doubt, were I born now,
I would surely qualify for Stella Maris, the local center for this type of disorders.
My mom used to solve everything with a two-sided strategy: first she would give me a slap; then she would say I was "silver lined"
And you can already feel how liberating this expression is.
The way we call things, change them.
Words give or take away something, they are never neutral.
"Disorder", is a diminishing expression; "silver-coated" empowers.
In the beginning it was the Word.
Mine was "silver".
Actually, this silver has given me so much.
It gave me the opportunity to engage in so many things, to think about ten thousand things and make a million of them. And last but not least, raising two couples of twins born two years apart.
Two couples of twins born two years apart.
(Applause) The way we call things, changes them.
Someone called it a catastrophe.
For me, it was the gift that changed my life and taught me a couple of things, let me share them with you.
Up to that point, my story was as ordinary as many others: university, friends, parties; then love, the engagement, the marriage.
And then I got pregnant, like in the best traditions, and "everyone lived happily ever after".
But no.
Here the thing begins to twist.
The first knot is connected to the twins and the first information I get from the operators - ostetrician, gynecologist and the pediatrician - is that the twins are almost never born naturally. And above all, twins cannot be breastfeed.
Or better, you may breastfeed, but no one has ever seen it done.
The world defined for me by someone else.
Another box, another limit not to trespass.
This Word, however, was at odds with the wisdom of my 90-year-old grandmother Gilda.
She always mumbled something about women who in her days, with a handful of beans, could take care of and nurse a host of kids while we, steak-fed modern women struggle to raise a single child.
The words of my grandmother Gilda, in my head, were creating my reality they were helping me to see beyond the box, beyond the horizon.
In fact I go on, I'm a hyperactive person - even better, I'm "silver lined".
So I work as hard as I can, and I feel fine and energetic.
And I proudly call my pregnancy "a groovydance".
But the second know comes ahead.
One of the babies had a malformation. And he must be operated right after birth.
The issue is serious: and the words, the verb, say that 50 percent of those who were born with this malformation can't make it. Third knot: what used to feel like a beautiful thing becomes a further obstacle instead.
The twins are born in advance, so they're smaller; and this puts another risk factor on the operation.
This new story overwhelms me and my groovydance becomes a dramatic one.
The expectation becomes oppressive and unbearable.
I will stay at home, doing nothing.
But you know, I can't stay idle for long, and therefore I start reading, training, learning.
Knowledge becomes my new anchor, my way of finding other words, of creating a new world for me.
I won't bother you with the details of the operation, or the month I spent at Meyer; but this is what Cosimo looks like today.
So - (Applause) Stop it, I'm abouy to cry.
So, everything went great and he increased the percentage of solved cases.
But what happened to me while we were in the hospital is the story that I want to tell you.
The greatest transformation in my world, the phase of life where I learned the importance of not trying to stop the wind with my hands.
Right after birth, someone asked me whether I would breastfeed.
I was very confused and didn't know it.
He pointed out, however, hadn't I done that, given the very delicate situation, I would have had to make up for it with ten among supplements and medicines.
But double weighs, bottles, formula gave me an idea of ​​heaviness, of fatigue
like rowing instead of having the wind in your back.
But one day, finally at home, I sat at home with my babies on my comfortable sofa.
I brought food and drinks, music, remote controls, tablets, and everything I thought I'd need over the next year.
I curled up like a doghouse and attached my babies to my breast.
My husband said, it smelled like a fox's den.
But I told everyone that I had finally figured out what a mother is for: breastfeed and nurture her puppies.
And that from that day one, anyone would find me stranded there.
The feeling of lightness I felt was explosive and frightening.
Being pushed by the wind without resisting.
I made so many mistakes, unfortunately.
Silly things, avoidable with a minimum of experience.
One step forward and two steps back, like now.
But nobody could help me.
I fought against ignorance, sloppiness, clichés, fears and anxiety.
Even those who supported me did not know what to tell me.
Because yes, twins can be breastfed. But no one had ever seen it.
They called me "freaking crazy", "nostalgic", "breast obsessed".
Someone even called me "Taliban".
Yet none of those words offended me.
My milk story was a beautiful, perfectly successful one.
Even more so with the second pair of twins where I could finally apply everything I learned with the first.
At that point, however, I felt a new emotion.
I felt it was essential, even urgent, to transfer what I had learned with trial and errors to help as many mothers as possible because nobody else was doing it.
I felt a little like, the world is on fire, and no one can extinguish it but me.
There is no time to lose.
A hyperactive as I am, I started studying, became a lactation consultant
and worked in districts, hospitals.
But unfortunately, there's no money for new courses, local health agencies cannot invest and everything becomes a bit harder.
And so we had to give up.
Some time ago Umberto Eco received unanimous praise because he said that Facebook gave all the idiots the opportunity to speak to a wide audience.
Now I am no one to contradict the great and beloved Umberto Eco: but a tool is just a tool, it's all about what you make of it.
A knife can either kill a person or peel an apple.
To a silver-lined person like me, Facebook gave the chance to really look beyond the horizon of what's possible.
To achieve the dream of changing the world.
I created a facebook group called "Allattamento materno gemellare" [Twins Breastfeeding] And today 1,700 twin mothers from all over Italy, and also from abroad, breastfeed two or more twins also thanks to my support and the wonderful moderators of the group - this is the group. Twin breastfeeding.
I breastfeed two or more twins, as I said, thanks to my support and that of the wonderful moderators of the group that help every day and every hour, respond to doubts, fears, and anxieties and despair of these mothers who feel so lonely,
thus creating a network of solidarity, support and love,
Anything can become an opportunity.
Even the ones that starts with many knots.
Always give yourself the opportunity to believe, to grow, to help.
In the beginning was the Word: my word, today, is evolution.
Thanks.
