Today I won't speak about the anti-specialist.
The anti-specialist is me.

I am-- I could define myself as an artist.
Although I use different languages in my daily work, I decided not to major in any of those.
It's weird for me to be here today, this event is planned by students from some university. University, for me, is a place where dreams get wrapped, they get sold as products on the market.
In other words, university is a place we decide to attend if we want to major in a subject, a subject we choose, right?
Today I'd like to talk to you about this choice we all make.
Let's focus on this topic, I decided to share some stories with you.
The first one is nothing new, it's about something we all know, everyone experienced it at some point.
It starts more or less like this: we were young, we were all newcomers on this planet.
We had just landed, and we were
all excited to discover its lights,
its shadows, its surfaces.
"Mum, look! Look at this! It's an ant!"
"Yes, it's nothing new."
Then, one day we got asked, "What do you want to be when you grow up?"
Well, all of us found out two things that day: the first is that we all get old; the second is that we all have to choose what to do in life.
If only we had been ready to reply this way back then, "Sorry mum, I'm counting the ants!"
But kids love such questions, don't they?
It's just another way to make them go nuts.
We also come up with nice things like the following.
I'm about to mention two kids I personally know.
"Frido, what do you want to become?"
"A pilot!"
Sorry, it's a little slow.
"And you, Rocco?"
"An elephant." Luckily enough, our kids can't even imagine how hard the professional world is.
When we ask a kid, "What do you want to be when you grow up?"
we are not really asking what they want to be.
We are asking, "What job would you like to do?"
"What's your dream job?"
If I asked my kid, "Samuel, what's a job?"
If he could, I'm sure he would reply this way, "A job is something which takes me away from you."
Ok, let's move on because the story carries on.
Basically we grow up, we find out that we age too fast, faster than we woud like to.
We get to a point in our life when we go from being kids to teenagers.
This stage of our life is called puberty: it's a time when the great majority of us lapse into the worst rudeness.
Maybe you are more polite than us Romans, I am from Alta Tuscia, exactly from a small town of 5,000 inhabitants.
When I was in secondary school, we would throw things from one side to the other of the classroom, we would upturn desks, our teachers,
I swear I was there.
You start talking about sex, drugs, you start smoking, you have no respect for anyone in the most shameful way ever.
We start to explore.
Well, the only idea we acually have at that age is the concept of "cool" and "not cool": this is cool, this is not cool.
Well, what happens in this period with our kids, teenagers, it's hard to define them, is that it's extremely difficult to deal with them.
So it comes natural for an adult to have some sort of "innatural feeling", as I define it, something which is not fair to say to our kids.
It sounds more or less like this, "You are a lazybones!
Your life means absolutely nothing!
Stop dreaming now!"
Stop dreaming?!
Our dreams grow as we grow.
We don't simply grow up over time even our feelings are involved in this process of growth.
What happens?
It happens that our dreams grow at some point. they get so big that we lose control of them, they keep growing and we get overwhelmed by them. We feel useless because when we look around everyone looks so determined.
I have to catch the train in 3 minutes.
If I miss it, then I'll have to wait for five more minutes.
And us there, weighed down by our dreams.
Why are our dreams so heavy?
That's because we know that the reality around us is difficult to accept and to cope with.
Nobody will ever help us out to pursue our dreams.
That's why we often make quick decisions like, "I have to catch the train, I'm sorry. Bye!"
Let's move on, the story goes on.
We are close to finish our high school.
I reckon, most of you here can rember how it feels, can't you?
We felt stunning, ugly, happy, and depressed
at the same time, back then. Most of us at that age were attracted to the opposite sex, or to sex itself, which is basically the same thing. One day we got asked, "What do you want to do now?"

"Hey, wake up! I asked you
what you want to do now!"
"You have grown up."
"Me? Am I grown up yet?"
It seems like we grew old
without being aware of when it happened.
We need to have high expectations at some point,
we need to make a decision.
"What to do with my life? What to do?"
"I want to study engineering."
"Good!"
I noticed the true answer to that question is not really taken into account.
I mean, when we get asked, "What do you want to be when you grow up?
What job would you like?",
we get a list of possible answers we can choose from.
The list is endless, it gives us the illusion that we have a lot to choose from.
In fact we have even more to opt for.
Most of the time what is not on the list isn't--how to say,
"Me? No, I have no clue..."
For example I can list a number of wrong answers.
One of those being, "I want to be a musician when I grow up."
Or rather, "I don't know, I have so many interests.
I'm fond of astronomy, I love literature, I love theatre as well."
Oh, it's too much!

But there's another answer which in my opinion is the one everyone dreads, that is, "Me? I don't know!" What? Don't you know?
But did you notice how much "I don't know" scares us?
In the society we live in, we can't afford not to know something.
And our kids know that very well.
Our kids grow up with the constant fear of not knowing enough.
"Dread" is a little bit excessive, though I can remember I made the best excuses when I was a child just not to say I didn't know the answer.
There's another example: when we attend primary school, or maybe secondary school, and we get asked, "What's the capital of Turkey?"
I know it but it's escaping my mind.
The most ridiculous lies just not to admit you don't know it.
And I was one of those girls who want to know everything, it was an obsession.
I mean, don't try to make me believe I was just convinced to know everything.
No, I actually knew everything!
I knew everything, and I was always ready to answer any question.
"Any plans for the future, Beatrice?"
I want to go to New York
and be an illustrator. Wow!
Well, that was my answer at the age of 8, 12, and 16 years, there was clearly something fishy about it. Well, same answer even when I turned 19, and I was so obsessed that at the age of 20 I got a scholarship of thousands of dollars to be able to study in one of the most famous illustration universities ever.
My dream was about to come true, wasn't it?
So I took the plane, and two days after I left for New York, I fell in love.
What a disaster!
After two months I was pregnant.
The funniest thing is that I was really happy!
Happier than I had never been before, and I didn't even know how to define my excitement.
I turned down the scholarship, I dropped out of school, I left a city I didn't really like.
I felt like as if I jumped down from a moving train, and I felt like as I found out that the landing is not so bad.
But after spending two and half years of my life there, hard times because no one has ever said that life is easy, as well as no one has ever said the easiest way is the best one.
Well, after two years and a half, I was sent a letter by a person I absolutely admire, an illustrator, she wrote with heart in hand, "Beatrice, if you don't study it will take you a long time to become a great artist."
I wanted want to reflect on this concept of time.
If it took 200 years of effort to figure out what art is, it'd still be worth it.
But if after 200 years, I still couldn't get the meaning of art, despite putting considerable effort and energy in what I do, it would be worth it the same.
Because it's my life after all, it's my time, your life and your time.
So, I would like to talk about time and how much the world we live in needs our time, I want to show you how much the world pushes us to give up our time.
