There's a very long train which puffs golden steams, moving at the speed of light.
You, who are about to get on that train, if you are sceptical, please wait.
You, guy who got on the train and you feel it's not your place,
please get off, now!

How? Jump down!
Jump down from the roof and you'll find out it doesn't hurt so much.
When you open your eyes you'll notice you can still walk, get up and you'll see your bruises will heal your pains will go away.
You jumped down, got off the train and finally understood your place in the world is here.
Simply here.
There are other people around you
because lots of people jumped down earlier than you.
Welcome.
Would you believe me if I told you this once was a carrot?
Today, I want to talk to you about how to transform a carrot in a bioplastic.
But why the carrot?
Because in 2016, in Europe, 55 million of tons of vegetable waste were produced.
These wastes are not used, they are somewhat thrown away, then either composted or incinerated.
But if I take this carrot and instead of looking at it as something to eat, I look at it from another perspective, this becomes a material
one that has been perfectly engineered by nature for its intended function: growing, having a structure, operate until it's needed, and then being reintegrated in the environment, at the end of its lifecycle.
In order to do so, nature takes advantage of a lot of macromolecules: of cellulose, pectin, lignin, hemicellulose and so on.
All these serve a special purpose: support and strengthen the plant's different parts.
But if I look at it from another perspective and try to reverse engineer nature's machinery, and eventually find a way to produce this thing with this macromolecules, wouldn’t it be a remarkable progress?
I could then do all the things that must not last long, and I would find, in the process, an alternative to plastic.
This is the underlying idea me and my colleagues of the Smart Materials lab, at Italian Institute of Technology, have been worked on for some years.
We developed a process to convert vegetable waste in biodegradable bioplastic.
In the spirit of circular economy, we'd rather process food industry's leftovers.
Therefore, in order to get our raw materials, we call food processing companies and ask them to give their businesses' leftovers.
In general, I have to say, their feedback is even too enthusiastic.
We typically receive huge bags of vegetable waste, while all we need for our tests is a few grams at a time.
We've spent the first three, four years adding and mixing vegetable waste with other biodegradable materials, in order to give vegetables new properties, add features they wouldn't naturally have.
But what I want to talk to you about is the last process, one we've started developing a couple of years ago, which is focused on producing materials and bioplastic that are 100 percent vegetable-based.
We saw that using water, acidulated water, and controlling the temperature and the process time, we can dismantle nature's architecture to combine all those macromolecules, namely pectin and cellulose.
A plant's outer layer cells are very well organised, and for a reason: give the material the strength it needs.
As for me, after I dismantled them, I put them back up together.
But this time I reassemble them in a completely random fashion,
Why?
Because, in the plastic we use every day, molecules are actually cluttered.
So we managed to have a plant-based material, whose cells are as cluttered as plastic.
This gets me the best of both worlds, because plant molecules provide me with a completely biodegradable bioplastic, while my molecules' rearrangement gives a plant-based material a plastic-like strength and resistance.
If you think about it -- we're in bioplastic manufacturing since many years now, and one day we set our mind to actually use these applications.
And so we did something that, I have to say, every scientist and engineer is frightened about: we left the lab, and talked with real people.
(Laughter) We went to Bolzaneto veggie market and tried to adapt our technologies to that particular market, to that market's leftovers, in order to produce bioplastic for it.
The idea was to prove that our transformation technologies trigger a new system of circular economy, where market's waste are turned into useful stuff for the market itself.
Roughly one year ago these days, we were in Berlin showcasing the results of our research: artichoke biodegradable pellets, wires for the 3D printer, tomato peel-based fruits baskets.
We proved "on the field", in a sense, that plant-based packages are a fact, ones that don't pollute at the end of their life cycle, because they can be disposed of, they can be biodegraded.
They're made of plants.
This is very important for us, because the packaging sector accounts for 40 percent of all the plastic produced worldwide.
Forty percent of plastics becomes packaging.
But while a packaging's life cycle is a few weeks, a few months, the plastic it's made of stays for one thousand years.
Plus, these packaging, and this kind of plastic waste, are the most likely to be spread in the environment, because their collection, use and management is particularly difficult.
Just consider how much plastic you've stumbled upon today.
How much plastic you can find in Righi's beach. How much plastic you find when you go to the sea, when you go to the beach.
And how much plastic is found in the stomach of animals, how much plastic they eat.
And we eat them, shortly after.
Think about how much plastic is in fishes’ stomach.
What if that plastic wasn't ordinary plastic, but our plant-based one?
How much happier would the fishes be?
I'm particularly keen on these technologies because they allow me to think about a new paradigm to produce all those things that must not last long.
I won't use plastic anymore, fossil-based, non-biodegradable as it is, because I can use natural, plant-based materials that don't pollute when disposed of.
And I can consider designing materials which are set to vanish when they're over. Which, if you remember, was our initial goal: reverse engineer nature.
By the way, we set our mind to only work with waste, with the goal of optimizing our processes for vegetable waste, because we didn’t want to compete with the food chain.
Our waste are actually a renewable resource and are generated all along the food supply chain.
Therefore, unlike many corn-based bioplastics currently on the market, corn that is specifically farmed to produce bioplastic, we use something that is usually thrown away now.
This gives us the potential of improving sustainability in two, not one, supply chains.
To the packaging, throwaways' supply chain, I can provide new materials that are biodegradable and made with renewable resources.
In the food supply chain, I can instead repurpose all those piles of leftovers, currently thrown away.
And you may think now, if those technologies are actually so cool, why don't we see them everywhere?
Me and my workmates, last year, we've been working to develop these technologies proving that they are useful, that materials, bioplastics can be made, and objects can also be made of.
In order to take the next step, we now need companies that manufacture these bioplastics, designers who tinker and exploit these materials design these new objects and took advantage of this material's programmable biodegradability.
And then, we are scientists, we are engineers, and we're homesick for the lab, for our natural environment.
We want to design new ways to address other potential food chain leftovers' sources.
You might remember, if you have seen "Exploring the human body", animals don't use cellulose as a building block, but proteins.
Will I successfully reverse engineer proteins?
That's what we want to do, it's our next goal. We will soon start a new project focused on repurposing proteins, cheratine proteins in particular, which are found in animals' feathers, which in turn are a waste of the textile industry.
"Circular economy", "Blue economy" are beautiful expressions, but they are not empty, just for mouthwatering.
They express the potential of a new way of life for us as a society.
We need new technologies, and I've just shown you a couple.
We need to cooperate with supportive companies to exploit our new materials, our new technologies. with the ultimate goal of eliminating plastic altogether.
Because, in the words of the poet, “Nothing comes from diamonds, but bioplastic may come from waste”.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Most of the few things I know today, I learned in a prison.
Not for being a prisoner, but because I worked in a small prison-island for over 25 years.
There, I met both human and non-human prisoners, which will be our guide through this short journey to peace and nonviolence.
But to get there, I'd like to start the journey from afar, from another prison.
Greece, 1970's.
George Mangakis, a law professor, imprisoned for political and ideological reasons during the colonel's regime,
writes a letter from his prison cell called “Erasmo and the mosquitos” which then, with other pieces of writing, went into a book called “Writers from prison”, published by Feltrinelli publishers in 1998.
I will read you a meaningful excerpt. He wrote: “I would like to write about another friendship which began two autumns ago.
I see it as significant.
It shows the bond that can grow between two unhappy beings.
I was in an isolation cell for 4 months,
in all that time I didn't see a single soul,
only uniforms: guards and interrogators.
One day I saw three mosquitos in my cell,
they were fighting for survival, resisting the beginning of the cold season.
During the day they slept on the wall and at night they buzzed around me.
Initially they exasperated me.
Luckily for them, however, it didn't take me long to understand that I too was fighting to survive the very cold season.
A drop of blood would saved them,
I couldn't deny them that.
At sunset, I uncovered my arm and waited.
After some days, they got used to me and were not longer afraid.
They came close to me confidently and naturally.
For this trust, I owe them.
Thanks to them, the world was no longer limited to an interrogation room.
Then, one day, I was transferred to another prison.
I never saw my mosquitos again.
This is how we are deprived of our friends in the arbitrary world of prisons,
but we continue to think about them often."
You may be wondering, why are we starting with this excerpt?
I think it's important as a starting point for the short journey we're sharing.
It touches the concept of community between living beings, the need to share our presence with another being, even if it's well outside our own species.
Prison matters to me, and it matters to us all.
Chains and prisons are part of our individual and collective lives.
For these reasons it should interest us all.
My starting point, the stage and setting, the place that put me to the test, is the small island of Gorgona, part of 7 beautiful islands of the Tuscan archipelago, a national park in the middle of a great transboundary marine area: the Pelagos Cetacean Sanctuary.
If we consider the etymology of the word “Gorgona”, “Gorgo” means difficulty.
Gorgona really challenged me, but at the same time has been great food for thought.
This island, a prison since 1869, has a particular characteristic. Every prisoner works, and many of them work the land and work with animals.
I met imprisoned humans and also imprisoned animals, like all the non human animals that live to produce things for humans.
Both sentient beings, both with the same spirit for life, both with the same desires.
Except that humans eventually leave the island, as prisoners usually serve the last part of their sentence on Gorgona, while the animals stay behind. The animals usually leave the island in the form of pieces of meat, or transformations of their secretions, like, cheese.
In this journey I have met many friends, and I'm in debt to many human and non human beings.
I'd like to mention Valentina, who is a very close friend, and who is here also to represent the whole world of animal and human guests on the island.
We call her the Zen-Cow as we have noticed that she has some profound meditative and contemplative qualities, developed while ruminating upon life, and most of all, a capacity to live in the “here and now”.
So we always called her a Zen Cow.
14 years old. 7 births.
Another person to whom myself and many others are deeply in debt, is the German doctor Samnuel Hahnemann, founder of homoeopathic medicine, who has given me many theoretical and practical tools to survive and work in this place.
He provided me with knowledge, unknown to me as a western doctor. This is the search for real causes which are always invisible to the eye, that vital energy and strength within every living being.
Essentially, the idea of unique, global health.
We are all interlinked and interdependant from an ecosystemic viewpoint, responsible for life.
All this lead to very practical action, for example freeing all the animals,
because this action is the first one to guarantee them a good health. They are called animals precisely because they move around.
We have managed to achieve a good level of freedom for the non-human animals in the prison.
As you can see here, there are cows grazing, looking out to the sea.
There was a problem, however: all this effort, quality medicine, looking after the animals' wellbeing and the fundamental needs of every species under my care, in the end nearly always lead to them dying in an objectively violent way within the walls of a small, standard-approved slaughterhouse.
Obviously all this raised questions for me: what am I doing in all this?
What attitude should I have towards this?
What does my final judge say about this, my conscience?
How can I reconcile my ethical views about life with my medical practice to face the mysterious journey that we call life?
The fundamental questions, including: love thy neighbour.
Who is my neighbour?
I asked myself, and other people too, Where is the boundary for the definition of “neighbour”?
Is thy neighbour only a member of my species?
Does it include non human animals?
What about vegetables?
Obviously, I consider that my neighbour is everything that lives.
The diversity of non-human animals shouldn't be defined in terms of superiority and hierarchy, but in terms of opportunity.
Diversity broadens my and our mental, spiritual, cultural and ethical horizons.
And most of all, how can I change my view on non human otherness?
I'd like to mention a phrase from a philosopher called Adorno, who says, “Auschwitz began when someone looks at a slaughterhouse, thinking: they're just animals.”
I no longer want to think “they're just animals”, because they're not just animals.
They're animals like we are, they desire the very same things that we desire.
Yet, we have an ample legislative corpus, national and international laws: the ethics of my profession, the famous article 13
of the treaty of Lisbon: the Universal Declaration of Animal Rights. We have important texts like “Animal Liberation” by Peter Singer.
We even have a book about the “animal issue” within the Biolaw Treaty.
There is a lot of interest in some fundamental ethical steps:
to acknowledge the sentience of non human animals, acknowledge their wellbeing, protect their lives and their needs.
The Cambridge declaration of consciousness, 2012, even recognises the consciousness of animals considered inferior.
Yet we continue to treat our so called “pets”, by curing their illnesses and protecting them, while “livestock animals” are cured and exterminated.
It is my duty - as I'm also here on behalf of those who don't have a voice - to remind us all that we send at least 60 billion non-human “livestock”animals to their deaths in slaughterhouses,
in addition to animals for other uses.
It's a daily and annual extermination.
We can't ignore the impact of livestock farms on our planet, we have also heard about climate change during this TEDx conference.
We mustn't forget this: they have a fundamental role in climate change.
But since it's still a taboo, we hardly hear about it.
A documentary film that is currently shooting in Italy, Cowspiracy, deals exactly with this problem: the taboo of livestock farms.
We inflict every possible violence upon animals.
Every possible, thinkable violent act has already been committed.
And again, it is my duty to remind you and show you just some of the atrocities that we inflict everyday on non-human animals.
(Video) 8 Cruelties you'd rather not have known about [A glimpse in animal exploitation industry] [1. Forced feeding for fois gras] [2. Castration without anesthetic] [3. Grinding alive] [4. Skinning alive for furs] [5. Punched to death] [6. Cruel experiments] [7. Over crowded during transportation] [8. Dumped alive] [These are normal procedures] [inside industries that use animals.]
Marco Verdone: We are showing this video, like other videos and investigations because if we don't see, we don't understand, therefore we can't change.
Obviously I have asked myself, what do I do?
What am I doing here?
What is my role, my duty, what's my presence?
I consulted my wise friend Valentina, and one of the first things I did, the most powerful and immediate thing you can do even from this very night, and is a non action.
I made a pact with my friends, my patients: I said "I will never eat you again", because you aren't just a food ingredient for me anymore.
You aren't objects.
You are living beings, sentient beings, just like me.
(Applause) Thank you.
I witnessed, I have spoken, like I am doing now, I wrote, I have tried to bring their intangible presence by giving voice to who doesn't have one.
We have put, for instance, a “Charter of Rights for the Animals of Gorgona” in a book called “Ogni Specie di Libertà” (All Species of Freedom).
A book of many voices, including a piece written by an ex prisoner of the Island, and one written by the Director of the prison island himself.
In the first article we are reminded of a simple concept.
All animals are not things, nor machines.
But are, as us humans, sentient beings that feel desire and fear much like we do.
Recently, we created an original document which we called “The Decree of Mercy”.
In other words, we managed to grant some animals, starting from Valentina, then extended to other animals too, the status of “refugee” and therefore a protected entity, a being will never be killed for food or any other reason, and for whom we will acknowledge the full duration of their natural life.
Exactly what we would wish for ourselves and our loved ones.
Most of all, we considered them pioneers of the rehabilitation process.
Here we have Valentina and other companions on the journey.
All these actions have prompted a movement, and a couple of important associations including LAV and Essere Animali promoted a public petition that gathered thousands of signatures to request all the animals on the island to be protected, not just the few you have seen.
Another recent thing: some important people in Justice, Communication and Culture, including Luigi Lombardi Vallauri, Stefano Rodotà, De Luca and Tamaro, people who you will have probably heard of, have pledged their support for an appeal to permanently safeguard the life of each animal on the Island, sent to the highest authorities of the Government.
What is our dream?
Our dream, as the article 27 of our beautiful constitution reminds us, is that if the penal system aims to rehabilitate the convicted, then this rehabilitation has to take place under principles of non violence, of peace.
Therefore, we dream of a prison without slaughterhouses.
But we also dream of a world without prisons, or at least without prisons as we know them.
We dream of a world without cages and slaughterhouses.
We dream of a place, like this one, the Ippoasi Peace Farm, a sanctuary and shelter for former livestock animals, with whom we can establish relationships based on empathy, knowledge, acknowledgment of non-human subjectivity, the knowledge of the capabilities and diversity of non-humans which must be respected, protected and, if possible, even internalized, to amplify and broaden our vision of the world, which needs a dramatic upgrade for thousands of reasons.
Obviously, we would like to start from the latest generation, human and non-human cubs.
I would like to conclude this short journey with two women:
one is Margherita Hack who wrote: "I believe that killing any living creature, is somewhat like killing ourselves, and I don't see any difference between the suffering of an animal and that of a human being."
I see it exactly like her.
We started from a prison with bars and I would like to conclude with another kind of bar: an empty cage.
I would like to conclude with a last thought, of a Polish poet, recipient of a Nobel Prize in 1996, Wislawa Szymborska, who wrote: "Among the bushes he found a cage,
He took and kept it So that it stays empty."
I dream of a world with empty cages, a world with no suffering, no exploitation, no speciesism, and, allow me to say, especially today, a world without terrorism.
Peace!
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good morning everyone,
my name is Sebastiano Scròfina. I deal with new alternative currencies, an unusual, weird topic.
The path that leads me here actually started long ago, as a little kid. I was inquiring adults about everything, and I asked, "What is money, and why do you struggle chasing it?"
As an Italian, when I found out I said, "Let's just print more of them and we solve the problem."
That was my first encounter with money.
But what is money, really? I did not get any really satisfying answer then, nor did I get one in the following decades. Not even university economics textbooks carry satisfying answers on what money really is,
they just explain what money does, the functions of money. But money defines so much of our lives,
we live for money, at least we need it, some people die for money; but still, we can't define what it is.
Before trying to answer, I'd like to start with a handy example to show what we can achieve with these new systems of alternative currency.
Is there any banker here?
Oh no, he ruined my script.
I wanted to show you how we can pay back the hardships imposed upon us by bankers like him.
With these simple JavaScript code, we can do the equivalent of filling out forms, wasting time and money to open a bank account.
With this code, using Bitcore, a library based on Bitcoin - a new currency you all have heard of - we can open an account that allows us to get money, store it, and send it anywhere in the world.
This is an alternative to opening a bank account.
Of course, it's an innovative, peculiar one: but just to give you a taste of what one can achieve with alternative currencies.
They're not payments systems like Paypal or credit cards, but brand new currencies that differ from central banks' money.
This graph shows the evolution of these systems in the recent decades.
Today we only hear about Bitcoin: but let's put this phenomenon into context. It started few decades ago:
we show from the 80s to the 2000s alternative currencies worldwide, experiments with alternative currencies have grown until a certain point at an exponential rate.
This shows that there is a trend, there are cracks in the traditional currency system.
Cracks that are being filled by people around the world who try to solve problems.
How do they do it?
In this map I classified some of those alternative exchange systems.
On the y axis we have the geographical scope of the system,
some are very local currencies, that serve local communities, others are accepted globally, like "fiat" money, as it's called here, that is what we usually call "money": Euros, dollars - central banks' money.
But on the x axis we have the fiduciary nature of the system.
Again: what is money?
Is it a commodity? Or just a credit?
For millennia we've been used to think that money is a tangible good.
Or a digital one, bitcoin and criptocurrencies, the family of bitcon-like money, are basically digital goods.
Bitcoin is sometimes called "digital gold": it's digital, but it's scarce,
you can't duplicate it.
Other systems, on the other hand, are based on the different idea that money is credit,
like the system called "mutual trade".
Now, TED is about ideas worth sharing, so let me share three ideas, found during years of study and work in this field, that carry a big disruptive potential I want to share with you.
The first one is the "mutual trade" we just talked about.
In this paper, US economist James Todder analyzes the macroeconomic consequences of reciprocal trade systems. Indeed, a totally esoteric title, but with a true and fascinating story behind.
Starting in the 1930s, a group of Swiss entrepreneurs faced the economic crisis, lack of liquidity and recession, something akin to what we live today.
Following theories of an anarchic Austro-Argentinian economist called Silvio Gesell, they decided to create a "virtual" currency network so that entrepreneurs could trade without money.
The idea was: when you have untapped productive potential and consumption needs, why must you suffer a crisis?
They created a kind of a mutual trade network: how did it work?
The problem with barter is also called by the economists the "double coincidence of wants" problem. It is hard to find a counterpart to swap with,
so with "triangular barter" you get from a company, and then you give to a different company, thus clearing your position.
So this kind of triangular barter happens via a clearing house which manages the so-called "mutual credit".
Does this have consequences?
There are huge ones.
First, this system gives people credit without the need for savings.
This is really big, and actually this credit usually comes at 0% interest rate.
These aren't just fancy theories: more than a million businesses, worldwide, already do this kind of trade with a virtual currency as unit of account - so this is very practical.
Maybe the most interesting consequence is that credits, in these systems, tend to be spent as fast as possible: you don't want to accumulate this kind of virtual currency with such a limited scope of acceptance.
But this, paradoxically, helps the economy by helping those in debt to come back into balance. That is very fascinating, it's linked with current issues like Greece.
These systems tend to come back to equilibrium.
What about the macroeconomic consequences?
In this graph we see, in the last 50 years, correlation - which is not causation - between a macro-economical parameter, like unemployment in Switzerland, in blue, and the growth of the Swiss reciprocal trade network, called "WIR", in purple.
This study actually analyzes
much more data but the idea is simple: during economic crises, when currency flows slow down, but not the productive potential, these networks support local economies and sometimes help SMEs to survive.
Another idea worth sharing was elaborated by Canadian engineer Ryan Fugger in 2004. in this paper with an esoteric title, "Money as IOUs in social trust networks: proposal for a decentralized currency network protocol"
It sounds very complex,
but the idea is simple: Fugger asks - and there is a common thread with the previous idea - "how can we have an economy without relying on the banking system, basically rendering banks obsolete?"
Truth is, most of the money in our bank account is just fiduciary, it is a promise, a bank IOU.
It is bank money, created through fractional reserve, nothing else than a promise.
So Fugger asks: why do we need a middleman to exchange promises? isn't it cheaper to exchange promises via those social trust networks that already bind us?
You can see this here: in the left we see Alice, who wants to pay Charlie via the traditional system.
She pays the bank, who intermediates and therefore charges a price.
In Fugger's idea, Alice pays Charlie, who still receives a promise: but not through a bank, instead from Bob, who's a trusted friend of Charlie
and Bob gets paid with Alice's promise because he trusts her.
Fugger's point is, since the current banking system is already based on trust,
why not make it even more fiduciary, with a more capillary network? We need to develop a way to rout this economic information we call "money".
The third idea worth sharing is Bitcoin.
Starting from a question that's similar to Fugger's one - how do we transact without relying on the banking system - in October 2008, this white paper is released by "Satoshi Nakamoto". The author addresses this, and also other issues.
Another one is: How do we develop a payment system that's independent of political authority to freeze accounts, revert transactions, gather the identity of who's transacting.
And other more ambitious goals like: how can we get rid of a central bank?
how can we go back to commodity money, free from central banks' power to inflate the monetary base?
These are the founding ideas that have been implemented by Satoshi Nakamoto, who explains in the white paper, and then implements Bitcoin and the block chain, its underlying technology.
It is basically a ledger, where you can record information, mainly information on this currency.
A currency with a capped amount, 21 million units maximum. Transfers of the currency are recorded on this ledger, shared across a network of machines around the globe.
First of all it is transparent: you can see how this currency flows. Secondly, it is resilient: it is extremely hard to manipulate the ledger.
For the first time we have a digital currency that's not subject to the whim of any single authority.
In truth you can do much more than simply using the currency: you could use the block chain to do more.
In this example we see how, with a simple HTTP call, we can do something that today takes a notary.
Here, for instance, we're issuing, and tracking on the ledger, 500 shares: these could be equity of a business, recorded on the ledger which cannot be altered so anyone can check the validity of those shares and transcribe them on this ledger, - we can basically do, almost for free, what used to take a notary.
Well, I tried to give you hints of what you can achieve with this weird things called alternative currencies.
A common thread links these ideas: they weren't created by economists;
they want to make the financial and the banking system obsolete;
but most importantly, behind these efforts there's a strong ideal drive concerning the possible economic world.
These ideals may differ a lot among them, but nevertheless they're strong.
This is why I fell in love with them and then made me turn this love into a job: the idea that by creating currencies we can implement different ideas of the world, philosophies.
In J.M.Keynes words, behind practical efforts we often found the spirit of a philosopher.
Our economic future is up to us, it's not been written yet.
How is it going to look like?
The one Keynes wished for? Or Hayek? A mix of both visions, or something we can't imagine yet?
Currency is a language, one that alters our perception and renders real the worldview of its designers. It is a way to imagine new possible worlds,
Designing currencies, in a sense, creates worlds, realized by those who use them: a language no one speaks is worthless.
So what I find exciting is that our future is a blank book, yet to be written: we'll write it together with the awareness with which we'll choose which of the possible monetary worlds we want to create and nurture.
Thank you.
(Applause)
My name is Paolo Sammicheli, and my next car will travel at 40 Km per liter.
I am here to tell you the story of Wikispeed and of a modern hero, with a heroic name: Joe Justice.
In 2008, Progressive Insurance offered a ten million dollars prize for an XPrize competition, challenging the attendees to build a car that could travel 100 miles per gallon:
for us Europeans, this means 100 Km with 2,8 liters of petrol.
In 2008 there already were some 100Mpg cars.
But they were more like this bobsled:
they could just host the driver, and sure they didn't meet legal road safety requirements.
One of the things the XPrize commission asked us to do was to meet these road safety requirements to drive in United States.
Joe Justice decided to join, and at first he was basically alone, but thanks to social media, he started sharing via web his experiences, his mistakes, and the lessons he was learning.
And pretty soon, a community of 44 people, from four different countries, virtually knocked on his door to help.
And so Wikispeed was born.
Wikispeed, in just three months, with no corporate or university support, built the first prototype and joined the competition, achieving a whopping tenth place, beating more than 100 other cars from all around the world sponsored by universities and companies, in what the New York Times called "incredible".
How did they do that?
Joe Justice, just like me, is a software developer,
and he took the best practices of software engineering to enable him to create products and innovate quickly.
Flexible methods with weird names like Scrum, Kanban, eXtreme Programming.
With the $10.000 consolation prize, the team could afford their car's road legalization tests.
In 2011, Wikispeed received an invitation to show the car in the world's largest auto show.
In Detroit, Michigan, in a sense "the belly of the monster".
The world's most important car brands would also attend.
Great excitement in the team, but also panic!
The Wikispeed team wanted something nicer, for a car show, than the prototype they used for the challenge.
But creating a custom shell, would take three months and $36,000.
So Joe came back to school, and took training in composite materials.
Once back with the team, they started making models of the new Wikispeed body. Small at the beginning, and a full size model later on.
And in only three days, at a cost of just $800, they built a carbon fiber body for the exhibition.
It was so beautiful. Thankfully, because they were placed on the main floor, between Ford and Chevrolet.
Everybody talked about them that day: The New York Times Online; The National Geographic; Wired Magazine; even Rai 3 with "Report", in Italy.
Today Wikispeed has 1000 members from 40 countries, and they build cars.
If you go on the Wikispeed website it's just like any open source software project: you can download the plans for free and build it yourself; or you can order the parts, and mount it yourself; or you can ask them to build one for you.
And they are doing other interesting projects like micro-houses that cost less than 100 Usd with a bed, a bathroom, and a lockable front door to help involuntary homelessness with recycled material.
But the most stunning thing that Joe Justice and the Wikispeed team did is showing that you can apply some specific software methodologies to build tangible products, debunking a common belief that this wasn't possible.
They organized the team with a method called Scrum, that in software creates cohesive and very productive teams. All members work in pairs, taking the idea from eXtreme Programming, so that knowledge is passed from a team member to another, thus also increasing quality and safety.
And the car is designed with what we call in software "object-oriented programming", destructuring the car in pre-defined modules, with stable connecting interfaces so you can change or improve single parts of the car without redesigning the entire project.
Still today, the Wikispeed chassis is the lightest chassis on sale in the United States with a five-star rate equivalency in a crash test.
They achieved it because security tests are designed and created even before the components to test.
We took this idea from software also: it's called "Test-driven development".
But above all, they proved that even in building physical products a team's mood is a productivity multiplier.
Every industry can benefit from these ideas.
And today, Joe works as an advisor, helping companies all around the world to find new ways to create better products.
And here's where I met him last summer, Colorado, north of Denver, at the Scrum for Hardware Gathering, which was the first international meeting of these crazy software guys driven by the idea of contaminating the industry with ideas coming from software.
We've been discussing for two days about those software practices that are best portable to hardware, and the less portable ones.
Eventually we wrote a document, a manifesto, with principles and values coming from our own experience.
Saturday morning we went to a garage, at Hubert Smith's home, the guy with the glasses behind me.
We had a Wikispeed build party - namely, we built a Wikispeed car from scratch.
Upon my arrival to the United States, I said to myself, "I'll probably be the only Italian, maybe even the only European."
I was wrong. Upon my arrival, seven out of 30 participants, plus me, were Italians, all working on a company based in Ivrea, founded decades ago by former Olivetti employees.
During the day, we had a lot of fun building the car. It was also quite tiring. At the end of the day Joe was super happy. We mounted the four suspensions on the chassis; attached the wheels, the brakes, the steering mechanism; fixed the body, which got damaged during the shipping; and integrated all the parts to ensure a perfect coupling.
According to Joe, and could we have stayed four more days in the US, he told us, we could have turned our Wikispeed car on!
At the end of the day, exhausted as we were, we all drank a beer together.
I went close to our host and I said, "Hubert, your next car is going to be built by a bunch of nerd programmers, and most of them come from Italy.
I suggest you buy an all-inclusive insurance!"
(Laughter) That day, a community of people was born, with the idea to contaminate industries of all kind with ideas coming from software.
They wrote a document, a manifesto with principles and values around this idea.
In English, firstly, then translated into Italian.
A website was then made with experiences from companies around the world who started using these ideas.
In English, firstly translated into Italian.
I felt something similar during my first travel to the US, years ago.
I went to California, to Silicon Valley, to attend a conference at Google HQ.
On Saturday, with other colleagues, we went to visit some museums.
And in San Jose, the administrative capital of Silicon Valley, at the Museum of Science and Technology, we found an exhibition about Leonardo da Vinci, a 16th century engineering.
Once in, just around the corner, I found the history of Siena's engineers and our old aqueduct.
On my way out, our district's flags hovered around the ticket office.
I basically flew across the ocean, going to the world's most technological place, to find out that the world's most technological place was my own town, but 500 years ago.
Now it looks like a new Industrial Revolution is coming, they call it the Fourth Industrial Revolution.
They say that it will no longer be the bigger fish eating the smaller, but the faster fish eating the slower.
That's exactly what happened in the software industry in the last 15 years. In a few years, a bunch of young guys in a garage beat the giants of the stock market.
So if all the things that gave glory to software in the last 15 years - the sharing, the open source, the team work and the audacity - if all these things are about to seep into all kind of industries, well, I'm sure it's going to be awesome!
Thank you!
(Applause)
This is the time of women.
Did you notice that?
This is the time of women, we live in women’s time.
Women are the protagonist of this time: in the Western world, these days,
women are everywhere.
Women are protagonists in society, in their work places, in politics, in public affairs, in arts, in culture, in science.
Women are protagonists.
All subjects and projects are now discussed with womens' perspectives in mind, the gender impact, the gender perspective, the effect from the female viewpoint.
Women are then protagonists of this time.
But women aren't just protagonists of this time: They are also victims of this time. Or better, victims of these “times”.
The times of women are unlike mens ones
Women have given up to their time to donate it to others.
The idea of relativity of time, taken from theoretical physics perfectly materializes in womens lives.
Womens' and mens' times are different.
Women are divided between so many different times: family time; procreation time; the time for raising and taking care of children; the time for work.
Women are asked really a lot: to stay in shape, to address other peoples' times.
If the real rich and happy people are those who have time, for themselves and for others, then women are short of time for themselves.
They have little time for themselves, busy as they are to share it with others. Women are asked time.
If we are to describe the life of a working woman, maybe mother of a small child, what would we think of her life?
What comes to your mind?
We are always in a rush.
Our life is a continuous race, a rush against time, an endless fitting in.
We are victims of a failed reconciling of times, victims of the hard, if not impossible, reconciling of times.
We are victims of multitasking. We are expected to be perfect mothers, a pillar for the family, the fulcrum of the traditional family, and also not so traditional one.
But we're also asked to be fantastic workers, competitive with the male colleagues, to break the crystal barrier and get to the top.
Women are asked so much.
But they are not given nearly as much.
Women in the modern, western world find themselves in a difficult situation.
We are in a difficult balance, often forced into dramatic choices:
give up a part of us, to the idea of procreation, or just postponing it,
which can sometimes mean, to give it up eventually anyway.
And sometimes, conversely, to sacrifice work, career.
We walk on a thin line. And we live a personal deep distress.
Women are forced into a dramatic choice, that men just don’t know.
We are always subject to a constant pressure, that we typically experience individually.
But here is the mistake: it is not an individual problem!
It is a collective problem, actually. because the individual problem becomes a collective one the moment it has a collective impact.
Women are in fact at the core of the labour market.
Increasing employment in Italy means increasing female employment.
Female employment is lower than male. And Italy's female employment is among the lowest in Europe.
Female rate of employment is lower than male one.
The rate of female inactivity is extremely high.
Which means, women have lost their hopes.
What the rate of inactivity signals is a lack of confidence of women in the labour market.
It signals that women are discouraged: either they were excluded from the job market, or never entered, and have now given up looking for it.
A rising rate of inactivity is indeed a profoundly sad signal.
Increasing Italian rate of employment means therefore increasing female employment, to focus on women and revive their work.
On the other hand, let's have a look at our country.
This dramatic choices we're individually forced to make brought along a negligible birth rate.
Italy's birth-rate is among the lowest in the world, way below the rate of substitution.
We are a growthless country: no jobs and no children.
Too few children, too little work.
There is a short circuit. What can we do?
And now you see that women's individual drama has a collective projection, one that does matter for our country.
It is not that personal. It's a collective matter, where State authorities are involved.
It is something politicians have to deal with.
Politics should focus back on women.
As an expert in labour law, I will tell you: there are indeed many protections, in the labor market.
Labour market offers many protections for female workers.
But what women
do benefit from protections?
Essentially, only employed women. Only subordinate work is protected.
But let’s look around: how is the labor market today?
It is changing. It is not just subordinate work.
But the labor law keeps neglecting a big chunk of female workers: self-employed, freelancers, employed with vouchers.
The famous gig-economy almost torn to shreds the employment relationship.
Which morphed into many, many different kinds of work, and most of them are not protected.
Women are then divided between a legally protected minority and other much less so.
But even those who have many protections, truth be told, often cannot make their life choices easily,
Why?
Because an excess of protections
inevitably backfires. Often, they turn into a boomerang.
So it may happen that an employer, instead of a highly-protected woman, with all her welfare encumbrances, would rather hire a man and get rid of them.
So we have to do something, change our point of view, our perspective.
We must start from an observation, women in this country represent a relevant part of the welfare.
They have replaced public welfare in a number of situations.
Women take care of both children and elderly people, they get where the state fails to get.
Women are a relevant slice of welfare.
So if politician and the state have to invest on the welfare, and if women a part of the welfare, then the state has to invest on women.
Investing of women translates into pouring money on them, betting on women, particularly on younger women, younger generations, to change the country and move it forward.
But how to do this?
Let me throw in an idea, as TED is the right place to do it. It can be a concrete scheme, or just a provocation.
But it is feasible.
People speak about minimum wage: I would provide a minimum maternity income
to the women who give up part of their job to take care of their children.
Only for a limited time - maybe until kids are 13 -
they can give up some working hours and procreate, breeding their children.
A minimum maternity income that would allow women, regardless of their contract: employee, self-employed, voucher, freelancers, you name it -
a dignity income to relieve women from this dramatic choice, and allow them to take a break, while keeping a foot in the job market, so they can raise their children.
And raise their children in a way that is not restricted to the early years of their life, because Italian women have a different culture than, say, northern European or American women.
Italian mother wants to be an Italian mother: she wants to be there, in her sons' life.
She wants to be present, a point of reference, a protagonist.
So we need to allow women
to satisfy this need, to make this choice.
A minimum maternity income, which asks them to stay in the market.
Either with a proper training, which is an investment for themselves, and for the future, to return back to work after the break: or with a number of working activities,
We have to take what's good in the gig economy, sharing economy, collaborative economy,
Their low-ranking jobs, their humiliating flexibility, can be turned into a force for good, if conceived as conciliation tools
that allow women, shall they decide to take care of their children for their first 13 years of life, to keep a foot in the labor market and not get compelled out of it, and then being easily reintroduced in the cycle of production.
This can be some food for thought.
Next to this, I wish to add one more thing:
an additional figurative contribution that makes up for the fiscal shortfalls of women who sacrifice themselves for that stage of life.
We also have to consider retirement.
If an additional figurative contribution is there for wearing jobs, I would provide it for women too.
The maternal usury stems from the double, triple workload it entails, and should possibly be a function of the number of children.
That would actually be a sound investment, a useful tool. A minimum maternity wage, plus a figurative additional contribution.
Sure, state authorities would have to pour money, but it'd also allow women to take a leap.
And the whole country would take a leap, as we figured out that women are the key to unlock the system.
Women can increase employment.
Women can increase welfare sustainability. Women can grow our country’s population.
Of course, such measures have to undergo a test first.
Where?
Public administrations, for instance, but not only.
Companies should also get involved, at least those who take on this social responsibility, to give women job opportunities.
Jobs that may be even very flexible, with incentives to the companies that decide to support women, so they stay in touch with the labour market.
This might be a worthy idea
to take the leap, to limit female unemployment, to increase women employment, to defeat female inactivity, And to include those women who decide, for a period of their life, to devote to their family and kids, but without giving completely up to their aspirations of independence, career progress, emancipation.
And also, this could reboost our desire for motherhood.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Capoeira is a martial art
invented in Brazil by African slaves
who, after many days of forced labour, went back to their Senzala - the slave quarters that were really prisons.
And within these confines capoeira was born as a way to relax the body and the mind
and to take something positive, somehow, from such a difficult situation and dream of freedom.
So, we can really say that capoeira was born out of a struggle for freedom.
I started my journey witrh capoeira 12 years ago, at the "Sul Da Bahia" academy in Milan.
My teacher was Mestrando Pedro, a student of Mestre Railson.
What fascinated me so much about capoeira?
It is an art form.
It is a profound art form.
Yes, there is fighting, and competition; but there also music, dance, a sense of unity. There is singing, there is culture.
And therefore, in its harmony I discovered something important that brought me a sense of peace.
Burkina Faso, likewise, has been an important country in my life. I've been going there for more than 20 years.
It's a harsh country, like most countries in sub-Saharan Africa.
There have been some uneasy situations but they are well-known, I'm not here today to talk about them.
A group of wonderful friends, founded in 2009 Kolon Kandya Italia Onlus association that dedicates itself to two objectives: social entrepreneurship projects and social inclusion projects.
Why?
Because, thanks to the things we experienced as we were able to travel and spend time with these people we realised there were two main foundations on which we could build projects.
Social entrepreneurship, because companies and jobs don't just provide an income and personal dignity.
They provide a comparison.
And social inclusion, because we need to work with young people:
young people are the future, not only in Burkina Faso.
And it was in this context that the capoeira project began in 2012 with the support and good-will of Mester Railson and Mestrando Pedro, who supervised the technical aspects.
The association, of course, enthusiastically embraced this project.
Why?
Because capoeira became the lever, the instrument of a much wider educational project.
Precisely because of its versatility, capoeira enabled us to work with the kids, because it appeals to them.
There's the fighting, the acrobatics, the discipline.
But it's also great fun.
I like to say, sometimes, that capoeira is just education with a happy face.
There's music, there's history, there's culture.
Many things that kids find appealing and you can build trust on.
Without trust, you cannot make shared plans especially with young people.
Today, 2016, after six years, this project is well established with absolutely everyone working together.
It's a school: today we have 53 capoeira students, between 6 and 22 years old who attend capoeira courses every week.
And we can say that the centre, in the city of Bobo-Dioulasso the second largest city in Burkina Faso, where we have our headquarters this centre has become a bit of a focal point in the neighbourhood for young people and their families.
It's very satisfying
to know every face, every child, everyone by name.
Squaring the circle, in this project is definitely festival time, or batizado, in the jargon of capoeira.
That is when, every year, Mestre Railson and Mestrando Pedro come to Burkina Faso and an event takes place, a beautiful, multicultural happening.
Guests come from all over the world and the kids play an important part as they have a hand in the organisation of the event It's when Mestre Railson, and Mestrando Pedro supervise the capoeira exam for the kids, to test their level - because it's not just a game.
Capoeira is discipline, a martial art, let's not forget that.
So, it's an emotional time for everyone, especially the children.
At this stage, certainly, Kolon Kandya plays a vital role because it provides the economic support for the batizado festival, and much more.
All the children who pass the exam can rely on the support of the school whose educational role is just as important in completing their schooling.
Not just here, but everywhere.
It is the foundation to get responsible, knowledgeable men and women in the future.
And I have to say, this is the most pertinent part.
I'll close with an anecdote.
One evening, after a long, heavy, and enjoyable capoeira training session, the kids asked me: "So, why do you teach capoeira?"
And I couldn't just tell them it was because it's an important part of their education.
I tried to think of a way to express my most intimate feelings, from the heart to explain what capoeira meant to me and why it is something that I believe is a really valid and important discipline for the kids.
And I began like this: "Think about the slaves.
Try for a moment to put yourself in their shoes.
It's the worst situation, a human being can be in.
You have no freedom.
But instead of giving in to anger, to their worst instincts, they created capoeira.
Something that allowed them, through sport, through discipline to dream of a better future, one they could freely live in.
So, I hope for you kids, that this thing, this capoeira will be your rock.
Because you will often feel down and you will experience some hard times.
And I hope that the discipline of capoeira will help you find a way through those hard times"
And now, let's see some of the magic of capoeira.
Thank you.
(Applause)
It certainly happened to some of you to try to follow a diet.
The bold among you, raise your hand.
(Laughter) All right.
Then maybe a part of you did not see it through, because, at some point, you can't take it anymore, because the food it's not so good, let's face it.
And then it's always the same dull dishes.
The bold ones still raise their hands.
Here is the good news: It's not your fault.
I'm here to convince you.
These diets are "perfect" from a nutritional point of view, but they are completely wrong from the gastronomic point of view.
What does this mean?
It means that humans are more than organisms to be fed.
We do not have only a stomach and a gut that digest and assimilate food.
When they set a plate in front of us, we look at it with both eyes, maybe drawn by colours and shapes, we have a nose to smell, we have a mouth to taste flavours, perceives the textures, and even two ears that hear the crunchiness when we chew.
All five senses are involved in eating.
When we eat, these senses transmit sensations to our brain, generate emotions, arouse memories.
All this happens in us while we eat and it's beyond us, because we must eat.
Everyday, many times a day, all the days of our life, we must undergo this experience.
And this experience can be positive or negative, can be a source of joy, can be boring, it can also be disgusting, but it is a problem that we must solve.
Because it can even lead to major depressions.
Now, I won’t ask you to raise your hand, but this happens to me: if I eat badly, I feel poorly and cannot work properly.
Instead, if I eat well I can prove a theorem.
It happened to me. It happens.
Well, what have humans done historically
to solve this problem?
We invented cooking.
Because no animal cooks, but us humans.
If we cook, it is because we are forced to face this problem of sensoriality, because we can live very well with no television, no electricity, even without reading or without speaking, in extreme cases. But we die without food.
Cooking was born, and cooking is so important that even today in many hospitals, we are studying the so-called "meal therapy". Because eating well, from a sensory point of view, favours and speeds up healing.
For instance, you've all seen those typical, sad hospital trays, brown or pale white, dirty trays with the cooked apple on top, the yellow soup and, if lucky, a white cheese.
Now imagine a nice coloured tray, with colorful, fragrant foods on top, in fanciful shapes.
You will want to eat them and enjoy the experience.
You are happier and heal sooner.
I then expect science
to help me solve this problem.
It is not so simple, actually.
Science is very good at telling us what is good for our body. But the same approach won't work for gastronomy: if science has to tell us what is good to eat, we have already lost our game, because the concept of "good to eat" is not an objective one, nor a scientific concept.
“Good to eat” varies from culture to culture, country by country, person by person - and also, throughout your day, morning to evening.
You like to eat different things.
So, we must change our approach.
We do not want humans for science, we want science for humans, a science that meets humans.
Thus, humans must tell science what they like, and then help humans design the food they like.
But how can science do this?
It all comes down to innovation.
Since it early days, cooking calls for constant innovation.
If you take Apicio's cookbook, the first in ancient Rome, you’ll notice that they ate cow udders, dormice, used lead as a sweetener, and seasoned everything with a rotten fish sauce.
In the Renaissance, they spiced everything, while tomatoes and potatoes only hit our tables at the end of the 18th century.
So cooking changes all the time.
Cooking must keep up with changes in the world and society.
The problem is that these changes, in the last few years, have become so fast that cooking must evolve even faster.
So back to innovation.
But why is innovation needed?
There are several reasons.
Now, watch and have a laugh with this 1950s poster.
There is also an Italian version; but this is wonderful because it is international.
Butter is good for you and good for your health.
Nutritional advice: butter is slippery, eat as much as you can so you can lubricate veins and arteries.
It was as recently as the 50s, okay?
Let's say that now, if we saw a child eating those big sticks of butter, we would remove his/her parents’ parental authority on the spot.
(Laughter) Now, maybe we want to reduce the saturated fat content, make a shortcrust pastry with no butter, but maybe with oil; it's very difficult, I will tell you.
Therefore, our nutritional knowledge changes, and it does so consistently.
Medicine evolves.
But it's not just this, changes also involve the ingredients available.
I have already mentioned the example of ancient Romans.
I can mention my grandparents, who lived peacefully without knowing, for example, what a kiwi was.
Now, however, you use it to decorate your cakes with greenery.
And then there's more, because tastes change, Along with the body, we also feed the mind.
The great anthropologist Lévi-Strauss said that food, rather than good to eat, had to be good to think.
Tastes change, and this is something we’ve always known.
Take a look at this page from a French calendar of 1760. This is before the French Revolution and before Parmentier introduced potato consumption.
That's where we are.
There is a nursery rhyme that tells about the cook's job and says: "Every year, a nouvelle cuisine", “nouvelle cuisine” was used even then, “Because tastes change every year, and a new ragu every day.
So become a chemist, Justine", which is the cook's name.
In 1760, everything was already clear.
It was already clear that science had to contribute to innovation.
At that time, Chemistry was the dominant science.
But what's so difficult, you may ask, about innovating?
Basically, I take three ingredients, throw them together, and I've innovated.
No, it's not that easy.
It's not even a question of chemistry.
Chemistry is a science that governs flavours and aromas, and partly also colours.
Adjusting flavours and aromas is not hard to do. Not sweet enough? Add sugar or a sweetener. Not acid enough? Add vinegar.
Not to mention salt, of course.
The real deal, the hardest thing to innovate in the kitchen are structures, textures.
Just try to make a pastry replacing butter with oil.
Even just using fructose, which seems a healthier sugar, in place of the ordinary sugar, the pastry gets soggy, it never dries properly, and never gets crunchy. Meringues get sticky,
if you put fructose in it.
The problem of structures it's a very hard one to crack, and, not surprisingly, it's modern cooking great revolution, contemporary rather, starts precisely by dealing with the problem of new structures.
The revolution begins with this historic dish: 1994, Ferran Adrià, poised to become the greatest cook of his time, invented this soup of textured vegetables.
He deconstructed minestrone.
What is the idea?
Minestrone is healthy, it contains good vegetables, but it lacks an elegant consistency, you would never find it on the menu of a three-star haute cuisine restaurant.
By changing its consistency though, the whole thing becomes more pleasant.
Just imagine: floury potatoes, fibrous beans, deconstructed basil.
He added basil jelly: but above all, he added one major innovation: that violet froth which you see on the right it is a beet foam he produced with the siphon, which is the instrument at the centre.
The siphon was invented by Austrians to whip cream faster.
He's in a sense the first hacker of modern kitchen, as he used the siphon to do something completely different from its intended function.
He prepares a foam that tastes like pure beetroot.
Mousses already existed, but eggs and cream were added later, so they tasted like beetroot, but also eggs and cream.
He attains a pure taste.
He keeps on exploring, but realises cooks are at lost with no backing from science.
In 1997, therefore, he published a book which is a milestone in the history of cooking and it's called "The secrets of El Bulli", where he writes this prophetic statement: “If we manage to start a collaboration between cooks and scientists, borders open up and the possibilities are unlimited for the new culinary art ".
It's 1997.
Slowly but surely, in the early 2000s the first collaborations take place.
In fact, the development of science had arrived just at the right time, because, in those years, a new discipline was born that the English call "soft matter", and the French "Matière molle", all of Europe calls "soft matter". In Italy, we call it "materia soffice” (soft matter) because it disgusts us a little calling it “materia molle” and my university course is called “Fisica della materia soffice”, but in fact it is “materia molle”.
Just two words to tell you what it is: at school, they teach you that matter has three states, solid, liquid and gaseous.
The solid is rigid, non-deformable, with its own shape and volume.
The liquid has a given volume, but fills the shape of the vessel.
The gas expands freely instead.
Well, this taxonomy is useless in the kitchen.
Besides salt and sugar, there's no real use for it, because a jam is neither solid nor liquid, let alone polenta and whipped cream.
But, look, it's not just a question of cooking.
Materials of our everyday life do not fall under this classification either.
Look at the clothes you are wearing, are they armor? Are they diving suits?
(Laughter)
They are jackets, they must fit with your body’s shape.
So, they are not solid, but are hardly liquids.
They are soft matter.
There you go: this is soft matter.
Soft matter becomes the battle instrument, the creative tool of this new kitchen and we now get back to Adrià who made it to the cover, in 2003, of New York Times Magazine
with one of his creations
based on these studies.
It's an air. But what is air?
It is a thin, beer-like, cappuccino-like foam, which plays a very interesting gastronomic role, by conveying into our mouths aromas and flavours without filling it with matter, as the gas evaporates soon after.
Airs let me get you eat tobacco on ice cream.
If you get to the kitchen, take lemon juice, some soy lecithin, whip it with the blender, and serve it on a fish, on a sole.
Instead of killing the sole’s taste with lemon drops that corrode your mouth, give a slight hint of lemon to your fish and don't even add calories.
What's better than this?
Let’s move on.
Folks, Adrià is a true genius.
Melon caviar.
This is a first example of the innovations brought about by this great revolution.
Using a new substance, already used by the food industry, which is called sodium alginate, he prepares these balls that are soft, chewy on the outside and stuff them with a very tasty, concentrated lemon juice inside.
Melon caviar.
With another ingredient that is called xanthan gum, Andoni Luis Aduriz, another great Spanish chef, makes this cake which is called Vanity.
Vanity is made up of chocolate bubbles that vanish when you eat them.
But sponges are a recursive theme in the kitchen of Aduriz.
Look at this beautiful sponge, made by imitating a natural form.
There are new ingredients.
Now you buy agar agar at the supermarket; 20 years ago, I bet you didn’t even know what it was.
Then, there are new tools: this is liquid nitrogen.
Let's make an ice cream in ten seconds by uncorking a bottle of Moscato and creaming it instantly.
During the lunch break, you will taste a creation prepared with liquid nitrogen, namely whipped cream popcorn with coffee and vanilla.
Let’s push on.
Iconic dish of contemporary cuisine: on the right "Marimontagna", that is, Joan Roca's "seas and mountains", a raw oyster served on woodland distillate.
But how you distil woodland?
With this strange contraption which you see on the left.
It's called rotovapor, and it is a low-pressure distiller which boils water at 40 degrees.
Distil without cooking, preserving the raw aromas of everything you have, even forest moss.
The same technology is behind
this depression pot.
We remove the air. Why, you may ask?
Because that’s how water boils at low temperatures and can fry at low temperature, that feel extremely crunchy but never burn.
They won't brown, and they're less harmful than normal fries.
But there's more to new kitchen than just new tools.
Take a look at this creation by our own Carlo Cracco.
These are pure yolk spaghetti. He made them with his "marinated egg" technique, which is typical of Italian molecular cuisine.
It's a kitchen that leans on traditional ingredients, absolutely traditional tools, but used in a new and creative way.
Carlo took an egg yolk, set it on a bed of sugar and salt, and covered it with sugar and salt. After a few hours, the yolk thickens, dries up, and becomes workable, and we have spaghetti with only yolk.
With similar techniques years ago, we invented frying in glucose: we fry in melted sugar, which is grease-free, and offers a whole series of other advantages.
I’d like to give you another dish for homework: the egg curd.
Take a pan, and break two, three, four eggs inside - your choice.
Then, instead of cooking them, pour in ethyl alcohol for liqueurs.
Stir, it coagulates instantly like when making cheese.
It looks almost cooked, the albumen becomes white.
Then squeeze it, before you get drunk,
remove excess alcohol. You can also wash it with a little cold water - indeed, we better wash it.
Squeeze everything and you have a ricotta, egg curd.
You can eat it in endless ways. The last experiment I did it is seasoned with a little honey and trust If I tell you it's fantastic, but indulge yourself.
It is a neutral raw material, you can do whatever you want.
Well, all the things I told you, have finally taken shape now.
As you can see, gastronomy is a science.
So, all of us in the field in recent years, we told each other at some point: "It is good that these works don't get lost.
It's fair to point out, we should create a new discipline, which is called scientific gastronomy".
We met in Barcelona in March, at the University, in an extremely official setting, to produce this Barcelona Manifesto, which is now travelling around the world. This document states exactly what I just told you in this fifteen minutes: that humans need to eat well for their psycophysical well-being.
That cooking is a cultural trademark of the human species.
That gastronomy must be a holistic discipline, you can't reduce everything to physics, chemistry or biology.
There are psychological, emotional, philosophical, and cultural aspects.
This is the Barcelona Manifesto.
But the most beautiful thing is that this Barcelona Manifesto, perfectly realizes a kind of dream, a prophecy, left to us by the first gastronomist of the modern age, a gentleman called Brillat-Savarin, in the early 19th century. He wrote an treatise entitled “The physiology of taste".
The treatise starts with a series of aphorisms and there is one in particular which was truly prophetic.
He said: “The discovery of a new dish is more beneficial to human happiness than the discovery of a new star".
Thanks.
(Applause)
The blockchain is decentralised.
But everyone knows about it, it's written on all newspapers.
It's a database organized into blocks that are readable by everyone, everyone can write small data on some pages of some block,
and no one can modify the content of the blocks, nor the order of the blocks.
The potential of this technology to the humankind is arguably unlimited.
Who doesn't appreciate the immutability of a database?
Who doesn't like transparency?
Who doesn't like the ability to let strangers trust each other?
So why do we talk about it?
Because there are some caveats, and they are basically three fundamental questions.
It's a shared register, in a first approximation; but how do we define it?
And after defining it, how do we implement it?
And, in the end, what is it for?
Let's begin with the first question.

What is a decentralised blockchain?
First we must define and understand it.
Have you ever seen a zoologist sorting animals by the number of their limbs?
Sure, we have bipeds, tripods, quadrupeds -
but they don't,
not even according to color.

Because these features are not essential.
Because animals, unlike minerals, are alive; so ranking them by reproductive strategy, by how they give birth to newborn, is more meaningful than their fur color.
Very well - still, what is a decentralised blockchain?
Often administrators and others say: "You can measure it by the user amount".
More users, more decentralization.
Were I an emperor with millions of subjects maybe I wouldn't be so decentralised?

Maybe the number of nodes?

No, and why not?
For the very same reason:
these are not essential features.
So what is essential?
What's essential in a blockchain is choosing the next block.
Because blocks track all the transactions that took place.
If they're not on the blockchain, they never took place. And if I could choose the next block and say: "These transactions will be written in the world and these others won't, because I don't want to",
I would wield a greater power than Louis XIV, the quintessential absolute monarch.
So, let's focus on this fact: the participation in the generation of blocks makes a blockchain decentralised.
Whatever you might have heard, from now on I advise you to follow this path.
Now that we have learned
what a decentralised blockchain is, how do we build it?
It's not so easy, because a blockchain is a strictly technological product, and so without technology you don't get much far.
At least in blockchain.
And why is it difficult?
Because up until a few months ago, there was a famous trilemma: if you wanted to use blockchain, you can choose only two out of three properties. Decentralisation, security and scalability.
If it was true it would be a disaster, because I'm "Blockchain by design", you are a client, and I tell you: "Welcome to 'Blockchain by design',
you want a blockchain?"
- "Sure!" - "I'll give you an unsafe one!"
- "No, well I'd need a safe one."
"Alright then, I have an unscalable one."
"I don't need it to play with relatives and friends.
I want a scalable one."
And in the end what do we lose?

Decentralisation, exactly.
Luckily - it used to be like that, but this triple dilemma is false.
The truth is, the vast majority of blockchain technologies don't guarantee decentralisation.
There are many technologies you've heard about. We'll discuss briefly: Proof of Work, Delegated Proof of Stake and so on.
None deals with decentralisation,
not even a little bit.
Let's see why.
Proof of work: this was
the first great idea.
Hat-tip to Mr. or Mrs. Nagamoto, or the Nagamotos, not sure how many they are, who invented this so called Proof of Work, which means: I don't choose a Proof of Work, and you neither.
Let's do this:
we generate a math puzzle that's extremely complex.
The first that solves it comes out with the solutions and is entitled to append the block to the blockchain.
It's still a democratic system.

Unfortunately, it's also extremely slow. In order to be sure that there aren't two solutions in a range of few seconds; the puzzle must be so difficult, that even if millions of people try to solve it, only once in a while one must succeed,
or we wouldn't know if the blockchain goes one way or another.
There would be a fork.
It is costly, also. I don't know if you've seen,
try to google "Blockchain miner" and you will find a picture of thousands and thousands of highly specialized computers, whose single purpose is to improve the chance that you will pick the next block.
The system is so expensive - and what happens, when things get so expensive?
That only few people can afford it.
For example the Bitcoin chain, perhaps the most popular one, is controlled by no more than three mining pools.
Now tell me if a chain controlled by three subjects is centralised or decentralised.
I think it's quite centralised.
Moreover, these three computers combined are enormously polluting.
They consume - At the beginning they consumed more electricity than Lichtenstein, then Massachusetts, Switzerland -
it's as bad as you can get.
The next technology is very easy:
delegated Proof of Stake;

almost trivial, I'd say.

Let's choose these 21 people.
Aren't they honest?
Yes, they do seem so!
And I think they will remain honest, so we allow them to choose the next block on behalf of us all.
Well, let's say the Proof of Work
started from an idea -
there was this puzzle everyone can solve, and it eventuallly turned centralised. But was it centralised?
It is centralised and will stay so forever, because it's not the right way of doing things.
I don't want to lose time over other features:
let's just say, all of these approaches started with a false logic.
Which is: An economy is safe overall if the majority of a portion of this economy is honest.
Didn't you get it?
Of course, because it's meaningless.
Let's think of a whole economy,
You see that small portion?
If those are honest, everything works, otherwise we are doomed.
And what is this portion?
In the Proof of work it's those three miners.
In the Delegated Proof of Stake there are 22 members and no one knows how they got elected.
And this is no good.
What do we need then?
But we don't have to panic.
Once the problem is framed, I'm confident in human intellect and generally in technology.
Technology states, we should simply,
radically change our approach.
This instead has, I think,
the features we want: it's called Pure Proof of Stake.
What does it mean?

Here we have Bitcoins, and the well-known tokens.
Each Token has the same ability to generate a block.
Let's say, all these Tokens, all these coins, where are they?
They're in the wallet, ready to be spent, invested in the chances offered by the blockchain.
If the majority of coins, across the whole system, is in safe hands, then the system is safe.
Let's focus on the third feature:
what does it mean?
It means, there can't be a small group of people who say: "If I feel like, I disrupt the blockchain".
For this to take place, the majority of coin hareholders must unite, coordinate and shoot themselves in the foot so they can disrupt the very economy they hold the majority of.
Maybe they'll think twice, before doing so.
And how does the creation of blocks take place?
Very easy.
Each block is generated by a committee.
Who are the members of the committee?
1,000 coins are randomly selected: these randomly selected coins will belong to someone, and they are the members of the committee.
That committee will select a block.
Next time? Another block,
another committee!
1,000 coins are selected again, the owners start a committee, that generates the block.
What should I do if I were chosen?
What if one of my coins was selected
and I join the committee?
I'd have to send a message on the net, easy to recalculate.
Here you can see how blocks are generated.
Now you may say, thank you Silvio for this birdview summay of the pure Proof of Stake.
But who selects these committees?
I don't, otherwise it would be
very centralised.
No one choose them, they're auto-selected.

What? They select themselves?
This is perhaps the worst idea I've ever heard, because if were dishonest and could pick up myself, of course I'd do it, in order to join the committee and choose the block.
I'd also auto-select myself for the next block, and what then?
I keep selecting myself, so I keep choosing the blocks.
But it's not that easy.
What does auto-select means?
It means you have a fine cryptographic fruit machine.
There's this popular lever.
You drop the money, pull the lever, and either you win or lose.
As this fruit machine is cryptographic, I cannot cheat.
I can pull the lever only once, then win or lose.
If I win, I get a ticket
that proves my winning.
and I say: "Here's my winning ticket!"
Being a proof, everyone can check it.
And here's my opinion on the block.
It roughly goes on like this.
Let me explain why this is decentralised,
because each time there's a different lottery.
You pull and then it's lose or win,
each coin comes with its jackpot.
As if it had a small hand pulling the lever.
If you own more coins, maybe you'll have more chances.
1,000 coins all around the world, it is decentralised.
Maybe it's as decentralised as you can get.
Why is it scalable?
How much time does it take to pull this lever?
One microsecond.
And once pulled? You do remember?

We transmit the winning ticket and your opinion on the block.
It's pretty easy to do.
And now we will see why it's very safe.
Let's pretend I'm an evil and powerful figure.
I can corrupt 1,000 people all over the world in the blink of an eye.
And of course, I want to bribe
the members of the committee.
Yet there's a problem: Who should I bribe?
Because you may be pulling the lever,
But what do I know?
Should I bribe you, him, or this lady in Shanghai?
I don't have a clue.
But as the autoselected, those with a winning ticket say: "Here's my ticket and my opinion on the block", I get to know them and I can bribe them all instantly.
But what do I get?
They already said what they had to say.
Their ticket, their opinion on the block, are spreading virally throughout the network and I cannot stop it. much like USA government cannot prevent a Wikileaks message to spread virally.
So the system is safe, because first I don't know who to bribe, and then it's too late to bribe.
Besides the blockchain itself, this promotes a global democratic progress by keeping the world decentralised.
And as you can see in the trilemma, by doing things unconventionally what was deemed impossible can actually be achieved.
Luckily, there are not many impossible things in life:
usually you can solve what is considered impossible.
Still, why do we want a decentralised blockchain?
Because decentralisation is great source of safety: the more people join in this system, the harder it is to corrupt it.
If it's all centralised, I just throw a bomb at it and blockchain is no more.
If instead it's widespread enough that everyone in the world with a coin can generate the block, the system is much harder to tamper with.
Another reason is, it provides much more than the network safety. Let's remember, blockchain is a communication game.
And this communication protocol takes place on the Internet.
So not only one can attack the protocol, they can also use old fashioned strategies like cutting the wires and reprogramming the router - and all system beforehand, by reprogramming the computer and cutting the wires, could disrupt the system, even if everyone's honest.
So, that's no good.
This is an additional protection, just what we want.
And as I said before, true decentralisation guarantees disintermediation with no frauds or costs.
Hence it guarantees - We all want to interoperate, interact with each other.
If we could do it safely, even with people we don't know, I think it would be a great gift for the humankind.
This gift comes from technology.
I'm a technologist, let me tell you; but I think, technology is very human.
Since we came down from tree and built our first tool, we live better. And we likely became more human than before.
No disrespect for humanism, it's something that adds on.
And as the world becomes more complex, the chance of achieving many of ours ambitions will depend on the quality of the technology we managed to build.
One thing, though.
Just like everything else, it takes a lot of passion and willpower.
But do we really have something better to do than trying to fulfill our ambitions?
I don't think so. So let's deserve our technology and enjoy it as much as possible.
(Applause)
Claudio: Good morning, Robert! After you.
Robert Peroni: Thank you. Good morning!
C: Robert has been a successful extreme mountaineer for years.
In his life passion and courage have always pushed him to do everything he did.
He was prompted by these two values. These values ​​are those that belong to the right hemisphere of our brain, right?
The so-called "emotional intelligence".
Therefore today we decided to share this talk, this chat with Robert and entrusted it to Luciano Ziarelli, who is the vocal coach of TEDxTrento, and besides he was almost my mentor. For a couple of months, he followed me, he offered me lots of coffee at his house, and so on. We invite him on stage: Luciano Ziarelli.
(Applause) During his second professional life has dedicated himself to this work, am I right Luciano?
But this is another story; have a nice trip.
Luciano Ziarelli: Thanks.
Robert, everyone here knows about at least an abstract of your life, and to say 'it is adventurous' is to say nothing.
One of a kind, that's for sure.
The first question that comes to my mind is: what prompted you to turn your life into what it is now and that you will tell us a little today? What is the motivation?
RP: It's simple: the will to live and be curious.
The sedentary lifestyle isn't right for me.
I believe that one has to go away to be able to come back.
Many people never go away and so they can never come back.
If we have two stones and we wish to arrange them, we have first to open them, look carefully at them, and then arrange them.
Otherwise life is gray for me.
LZ: Well, this must be true.
Talking of your life, before becoming an extreme explorer, an extreme mountaineer known all over the world, you also did some unique things.
You walked across the deserts, in Afghanistan, the Sahara and so on. So, I wonder now, then you came to Greenland.
But is there a common factor bringing these two worlds of the infinite together, after all?
As a desert or the plateau of Greenland, that you have gone through.
What is the common factor, if exists, for you?
RP: It's hard to explain, you should go there.
LZ: It's a bit cold for us, however...
RP: No, it's simple.
However, if we go up to 8000 metres, we reach the top in ten minutes, we have a 360 degrees view, an incredible, fantastic view. On the plateau of Greenland, which is two million square kilometres,
there is this view 24 hours a day.
The air is so clear that we can see more than 200 kilometres far, and this means something.
Firstly everyone who goes up there once, gets sick, goes mad.
No, I'm not mad, I go a little mad. However, you are completely free up there. Nothing can stop us there, it is like staying on a cloud, and since the eye doesn't see those forms to occlude the sight any more, as here the mountains, for example, where I was born and that I love, it doesn't have that fixed point any more, the eye is free to wander and so we have a huge expanse, and that means, right away, that in our brains and under our skin, we have a sense of freedom.
It is something different.
There's no limit: it's something without limit.
LZ: Of course, it is a place without limit where, however, also the sense of time is different, but not only the sense of time.
You said 'you have light for 24 hours', well, maybe not for 24 at all, however there are less or more 22 hours of light or dark, it depends.
How can you get used to a life like that coming from South Tyrol?
How can you live in the dark or in too much light for so many hours and without a time limit or a visible boundary?
How can you do this?
RP: It is not for everyone, I think.
LZ: How did you do this for 30 years because for one week we could succeed too.
RP: Yes, it takes a little time, but we are there right at that point; you don't have to over-think.
You have to do it, just do it.
You have to go in, and don't pull down the curtains, there is only the sun for 24 hours a day.
We shouldn't pull down the curtains: you have to leave them open.
Our biorhythm is recharged again and it becomes normal, we were like that.
Now we turn on so strong lights at night that it becomes day. During the day we close everything, we get inside somewhere, everything is closed: we are completely out of normality both day and night.
However, the normality is just there.
We realize after a week or two that someone can't go on, because he over-thinks, 'now it's 8:00 am so I have to get up, it is noon so I have to have lunch...' LZ: Talking of eating, from the Mediterranean or South Tyrolean diet to the Inuit diet: what do they eat?
What do you eat?
How can you get used to this diet?
RP: We are at the sore point of this situation. (Laughter) You have to know one thing: now the seal hunting has been banned in Greenland and in the rest of the world, we have huge problems. We lived on seal food for thousands of years, the seal was the same as potatoes, meat and salad for us, it was everything, and now it's gone.
We ate only the seal and it was also nice because in our restaurant, "The Red House", we said, 'we eat at 7 pm'. And then all of our guys were not ready at 7pm, of course, the cook was not ready, then I came in and said, 'Look guys, it's 7 o'clock!
You have to go outside to bring menu', and they looked at me and said, 'It's really strange: every day they are hungry at 7 o'clock'. It is a strange thing, that means that we are a bit canned, I mean we don't have that freedom anymore.
You eat when you are hungry.
That would be right.
LZ: That would be right, but do you always eat seal?
RP: No, we decide the menu... LZ: So, tell us something about "The Red House"!
You should know that Robert has launched, in recent years, a small business enterprise: he has built a small hotel,
that offers hospitality to those coming from Europe and the world who want to experience the thrill of Greenland in a more or less short visit, a week, 10 days or whatever it should be.
How did you come up with this idea?
How does it work?
Who do you host in this red house?
RP: Right, I have always said to the guys, 'You have to build a house so friends could come from Italy, Germany, from all over the world and if you have friendships they make you earn money, you may work and so on'. They have always said, 'We can't do it, we have nothing, do it on your own!'
I said, 'No, I don't want to do it. I don't like walls, I am a bird, I have to fly, I have to go out and I have to be active'.
But then I did that and now we have a small hotel and we get together, and the meeting point, between this company and the other one, is that we are completely the opposite in lots of things.
But the greatest thing is that everyone is right.
If we put on a round table and we talk about something, job, commitments, then I share mine, I am the boss, everyone agrees. Then someone else gets up and says the opposite thing, and they all say, 'He's right'. So I say, 'How is it possible we are both right?" And then a third person gets up and says something else completely different!
Then they say, 'I'm going away'. There is no consensus, but everyone is right, this is the society.
We have respect for everyone: everyone does his own business, everybody has got their reasons for himself, you are right.
LZ: Robert, unfortunately I have to inform you that's the way it is in Italy too. It seems that everyone is right.
It is a long time that you are away now,
but that's the way it is. We will go back to Inuit as the story that Robert can tell us is too interesting.
However, you have to know that this is the only man in the world who made something that no one has ever done before and that no one will probably do again: he went through the entire plateau of Greenland along with two people as mad as him.
Do you want to tell something about this? Where did this idea spring from?
What prompted you to experience something in which, frankly, the chance to survive would be equal to what?
RP: It was not always easy, no.
I went through Greenland a couple of times on the classic pathway which is about 550 kilometers, and then I thought that one special thing to do was to go beyond that limit.
Then I thought about going through the northern Greenland for 1,400 km and everyone told me that it was not possible, in no way.
You may not pull so much weight. Me as you are not so muscular.
I found two friends and then we left without radio, no telephone connection, there was no GPS, we used the old sextant relying on the technical horizon as many years ago, and we tried to cross this white expanse.
We had some problems, of course, but it is always like that: if you want to do something special "to get into the future",
and we are just at that point, just there, we can always take a small step forward, but it is not that the question.
An invention is something more: it is really different.
If we get in a dark room, for example, we enter the room, look around and if we keep the door open with one foot,
and everyone who is outside, says, 'Oh my God, that guy is getting into the future!'.
What is the future? Nobody knows.
It is an invention, it is something inside, here.
If one really goes into the room, closes the door and then invents what is possible to do, just will do it.
He has to withdraw into himself.
Then he will succeed in doing something,
as good as he gets.
This would be the future, but who can go into it?
This expedition was like that.
LZ: Well, listen, talking of this expedition, lots of extreme explorers have reported that in the toughest moments of adventure, some of them have sensed a strange virtual presence alongside them, and most of them found it, how can I say, comforting,
and in moments of despair they felt encouraged.
To you, during these 1400 km of nothing, did something like that happen or not?
RP: Right. I felt some presences, it was a hard thing for me.
I went on over 1000 km, my friends were back in my wake, but occasionally I felt some presences.
So I realized that somebody was skiing next to me.
I heard "ssh, ssh", skis was going forward, and I knew that I was alone, I was completely alone, my friends were far behind.
Suddently I had those fears, an incredible phobia, not just a fear, and at that moment I crouched on the other side, because I believed that someone would have beaten me with a bat right here.
I think this is a phenomenon that often happens in these situations that are so serious and so hard.
I was afraid of not being able to survive, not for my life but for my friends.
It was a sense of guilt for me.
I also felt other presences. This is extraordinary, even beautiful, it could also be positive, as you said today, but it was negative for me.
LZ: Listen, I read your wonderful books: at the end of your last book, you wrote that the Inuit use to say, 'The soul does not die, it passes'. So, Robert, in a hundred years, where would you like your soul passed, where, or to whom, and why?
RP: Poor is anyone who will take my soul!
(Laugher) (Applause) The Inuit, Ivi, as we call them, men of this incredible land, live in the present,
do not live for tomorrow.
They do not know the word "future", for example. They believe that mankind has so many little souls: the legs are souls, fingers are souls, the face is a soul, and they have a main soul, that would be their character.
This would be their life.
And they also say, 'That person does not have the shadow!
Can't you see that there is no shadow?' You answer, 'What do you mean?
I see the shadow'. 'No, that is not a real shadow'. And you ask, 'Why?' 'Because that guy does not have a soul'. When one dies, and it is very easy to die, this soul goes to the next born, and that guy will grow in all good and bad qualities of that soul.
That is a fleeting soul.
LZ: Dear Robert, I think that, not only me but also everyone else, would listen to you for half a day, but TED format states that the maximum time allowed is 18 minutes.
So, it was an honour to have you with us, an infinite pleasure, thank you.
RP: Thanks.
(Applause)
I'm as nervous as if it was my first day of school because for those who teach, each day is as the first one because you need to captivate the people in front of you. And why do I have the right to take 10 minutes of your time this afternoon?
Simply because I hope I can tell you something meaningful.
Talking about school, everyone has something to say about it because you may be a teacher or have a wife who is a teacher, or have a child or a grandchild who goes to school, and, of course, everyone went to school.
School is taken for granted, but school is a place where beautiful things can happen as well as so boring, So boring. To convince one to change paths and perhaps leave school.
What can make the school hours interesting, captivating? What can help make the most of a student's talents so that each child can make his own way.
How can these hours can be meaningful in broadening their horizons?
I would like to begin with an episode that impressed me:
a long time ago, a girl committed suicide at Termini train station. She left this note for her parents: "Forgive me. You gave me everything: the necessary, the superfluous, but not the essential."
After that day, every time I've looked at a teenage face and at my children's faces growing up, I've always kept in my mind that note.
What is essential in order to enjoy the gift of life?
But what does such a deep question have to do with school?
I will tell you another episode:
about 15 years ago, one of my students, Tullia, who's now a doctor, an orthopedic, a very cheerful girl, she was 15, one of those girls who always raises her hand to ask questions she urgently asked me for an appointment. She came to talk to me, she sat down in front of me, and with her piercing, sharp eyes, she asked me: "Miss, I calculated that each year I spend 1,000 hours at school. That's 30 hours per week, 33 weeks per year, it's, more or less, 1,000 hours. with an average of two hours studying in the afternoons. So, each year, I dedicate 1,500 hours of my life time to school. Do the math: 3 years of preschool, 5 years of elementary school, 3 years of middle school,
5 years of high school. That's thousands and thousands of hours of my life. If they had been meaningless, I would have lost many hours of my life attending school.
Why do I have to study?"
I had to take her question seriously, I answered her: "Dear Tullia, school comes from 'scholè, ' that in Greek means 'free time'.
You probably don't believe it now, but once, only few children had the possibility to not work and dedicate their time studying, learning, knowing new things.
But, as all the gifts, you need to be able to value them: you have to understand what meaning a given gift has; you need to unwrap it in order to understand what's inside. That is the meaning of those thousands of hours that our students take each year.
In Italy there are 7.5 million students that every morning cross the doorway of schools, 800,000 teachers spend their time with those students That are billions of life hours that should be full of that discovery - this is the heart of school - discovery of one's self and reality, of what is truthful, good, and beautiful that are hidden inside reality.
So many worlds intersect in school. The aim of the school is not to study books;
throughout the disciplines, Physics, Mathematics, Philosophy, Science, Italian, we get in touch with reality throughout that assets of work, knowledge, and experience others gave us as gift so that we can re-invent it we, teachers, with our students.
This should be the spirit of each hour of lesson. This is the reason why at the beginning of this year, I decided to take my students on board of the Voyager.
I'd have loved to do it for real, but it had been impossible.
Too expensive.
So I simply took them to our gym, and showed them that extraordinary video clip where the astronauts see far away on the horizons a little dot, the Earth rising And they, instead of starting to measure, started saying: “What a beautiful thing!”
This question: “What are you doing Moon up in the sky?”, it's the same
as in the "Night-time chant of a wandering Asian sheep-herder" by Leopardi.
This is what I meant to give my students this year again: you go to school to open your mind to the world and its reality, to discover yourself.
And through this relationship between students and teachers, past and present, we can build the future.
Ducati's laboratory "Physics in motion" I contributed to open seven years ago, takes advantage of the attraction for motorbikes to explain the rules of physics. It was born from this passion, entrepreneurs' passion, engineers', technicians', and teachers' passion who know that there is nothing more absurd than an answer to a question that is not asked.
Every day, we must bet on the presence of students and their huge desire for what is the truthful, good, beautiful, their fulfillment, happiness; this desire of understanding, even though it is concealed. We must study thousands different ways in order to achieve this goal.
Last Monday, October 13, we launched a forth year of vocational high school in Ducati &amp; Lamborghini for students who had dropped out of school.
44 students that dropped out of school decided to come back to school taking a course that combined studying while working.
Do you know what they wrote on a post-it we hand them the first day?
A post-it where they had to write what brought them to this project. They wrote: 'Curiosity,' 'passion,' 'desire to make innovations,' 'awe,' 'collaboration,' 'responsibility.' And they were students who had dropped out of school, who had no job neither were studying. The famous NEET, those students who seem impossible to reach at them.
Actually, it is possible to reach anyone because it exists this great catalyst which is the desire, and this is what we have to instill. This is the big revolution that should happen in school; it can happen without any laws. Because from tomorrow morning I can go to school with this desire to discover what's good, true, and beautiful that are inside of the reality together with my students and my colleagues. And this is easy, as easy as opening the window and looking outside realizing there is a sky up there. (Applause) Elena Ugolini; Do I need to bow? Host: Elena Ugolini! Wonderful! EU: Thank you. Bye. (Applause)
I’ve listened to, I’ve watched hundreds of TEDTalks in my life, but I would have never thought I would give one.
I am not exactly where I thought I’d be in life.
Raise your hand if the situation you’re in - personal, familiar, physical or professional, economical - is the one you planned for, years ago.
I wonder if who we are and where we are, are the result of planning, of a method, of careful control over actions and their consequences. Or if it’s the result of reactions to random events that have happened to us.
Are we masters of our own destiny? We are masters of our own destiny.
All of us have heard this mantra over and over, in several contexts.
Thinking that life is not a series of random facts but it’s something we can control, does reassure us.
We are masters of our own future.
We choose our future with our will, our stubborness, our skills.
But do we?
Because sometimes it seems to me that life is like trying to run on a wire.
You know, like those trendy endless-running games, where the unexpected's always around the corner.
An unexpected work event, an unexpected health complication, an unexpected logistic accident.
Even small events, or positive ones.
I’m not necessarily speaking about big tragedies.
The unexpected comes and spoils our plans.
My expectations are let down.
The first question that I ask myself, the first doubt, is: I must have done something wrong.
I could've anticipated it.
And then, the anxiety of replanning kicks in.
“What do I do now?”
The necessity to feel back in control.
Now, this sequence of planning, inconveniences, replanning is a huge psychological effort for me, day in and day out.
And it stresses me out.
I feel a weight here, on my chest, that keeps me from breathing freely.
I feel trapped in a cage.
And so, I question myself again.
How come I can’t enjoy that daily routine I built during the years?
How come, I can’t live my days more lightly?
So, I start looking for distractions and I try to plan some me-time.
Two hours a day that are just for me, when I start daydreaming.
Like those urban legends where a friend leaves with his family to open a beach bar in some tropical country. Where everything is cheap, it’s always sunny and kids play barefoot in the sand.
Come on.
But, if planning stresses me out and stress makes me miserable and in need for distraction, is planning wrong n general?
Is it wrong to think about consequences to our actions?
Is it wrong to consider who we want to be in the different roles of our life?
Men, women, parents, children, friends, workmates.
Is it wrong to push harder to achieve our dreams?
Apparently not.
It’s too much of a simplification. It’s a simplification because today the world is messy, scattered, and guided from technologies that sometimes seem to go beyond our limits of thought and action.
It’s a fascinating technology, yet unsettling at times. We use it to connect, to observe, to reflect upon ourselves; we constantly use it to choose.
And we choose what to listen to, what to read, watch, how to travel.
We constantly choose, and we feel in control over such choices.
Then we take a look around and all we see is a regulated world. But it’s full of insecurities, obsessions, discord.
That’s because we live in a complex world.
And it’s hard to find your way around a complex world.
And complex doesn’t equal complicated.
Complex in its original meaning
of “composed of interconnected parts”.
It’s a concept of multitude and singleness.
It’s a world that is simultaneously global and local.
It’s a fast-paced, accelerated world: everything is a system.
It’s like the world evolved, and entered the complex era, but our mind didn't. And we are left behind.
So what?
Up until now I just shared some thoughts, some questions, some trivialities, maybe a bit of anxiety.
Probably, if we stop to think about the world, we could find ourselves paralyzed.
Really, I get those kids who don’t want to get out of bed in the morning, those who don’t want to leave their houses.
But the problem is, I have two daughters.
What should I tell them?
I just needed an inspiration, one simple idea.
I looked for it and I think I found it in the world of sports.
I experience it every day, and it tells amazing stories.
And I strongly believe that sports can teach values and unique emotions.
Have you ever wondered why great sports stories are so engaging for us?
Why do we love them so much?
Two things: they are authentic and unpredictable.
Try and think about an athlete during his race, during his match, in his moment.
Focus on his face.
Look at the tension, the rivalry.
It’s authentic, it’s tangible.
He knows he’s not given a second chance or a do-over.
And he knows that the outcome is unpredictable. You can have all sorts of statistics, analysis, surveys, assessments, but you can never know the outcome.
Isn’t this exactly what we are talking about?
Isn’t this what life looks like when we face a complex world?
It’s real, unpredictable life.
So, I said to myself, “Let’s look at how they do it.
How can the protagonists of these extraordinary stories who I look up to so much, face the inevitable unpredictability of their challenges?
Media pressure, fans’ expectations. The paralyzing moment before the start. They should have it too.
What do they do?
Do they improvise, simplify?
Do they let fate decide?
Or do they have control over the situation
and they know exactly what the result will be?”
None of the above.
Better, both.
They train at their best, they practice on everything they have control over like nutrition, physical shape, sleep, technique, technology.
And then they give their best, spare no energy.
Sometimes they win, sometimes they lose.
Maybe it’s the opponent’s skills, maybe an unpredicted event, or a mistake.
But can they be frustrated, can they be stressed, knowing that they were fully prepared and gave their best during their performance? Well, I think
sport gives us the tools to face this complexity.
Or at least, it inspires us.
It inspires us to train every day to the best of our potential and to learn something new each day with curiosity.
And then being able to face every single event in our life, each moment, with conscious lightness.
To be disciplined, but not hard on ourselves.
To feel in charge, but not guilty.
To be brave in facing new challenges, to leave our comfort zone, with no fear of failing.
There’s no such thing as failure.
Failure is just an unsuccessful attempt.
Defeats are real, delusions are real, and even deep wounds are real, the ones that scar you and leave a mark.
But we have to be aware that defeats are what push us to be better, to grow, to be stronger before a new challenge.
This idea, this approach is something I liked from the very beginning.
It lifted that weight off my chest.
And I’m trying to use this idea in my everyday life, in the workplace, today on this stage: successfully or not, it’s just the beginning.
Then I tried to bring this idea in a project that would go beyond myself, and that would leave a mark.
Together with some partners and friends, we started an adventure that we called “Akto”,
an Esperanto word for “act, play”.
“Akto” has a double purpose: to offer young adults from developing countries an accessible high-quality professional education.
And then to offer them actual work opportunities in the sport field.
We believe that education is one of the most important challenges for a sustainable future of our planet.
We strongly believe in sport's values and we want to do our part.
“Akto” was launched this past October in Cabo Verde and this year gets to Brazil and Portugal. It will affect thousands of young adults and it will offer hundreds of work opportunities.
Will we make it?
I’d like to say that I’m sure of it, but the future is unpredictable.
Meanwhile, all we can do is get our heads down into the game.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Thank you.
Last time we ended our talk with a "thank you" and this time we'll start with a "thank you". Two years ago, we were on this stage and today we are back
and for us it's truly exciting.
In the past two years, a lot of things have happened.
Two years during which the same process of thinking back to that time when we were preparing the TED talk, which has been a sort of therapy for us, a family therapy, because in that moment we opened up to an audience and we told our story.
That date, June 2013, has been a crucial moment for us, on one hand, we were living this experience in the intimacy of our family, on the other hand, we were sharing it, we were giving something back and opening ourselves to other people.
Today we're here again because we wanted to tell you what happened to us since then, since that moment, since June 2013.
We've learned so many things, and thanks to them we've carried out certain actions.
Today we're going to talk about it.
The first thing we've learned is the value of shearing, for us.
What it meant to us, after having experienced this trauma within our family, sharing it with other people.
And when we opened ourselves to other people, we realized that it wasn't just our problem anymore, but it had become a collective cause.
People from the audience, or members of our family, people that didn't know our story, came to us saying: "The same happened to us and we reacted this way".
So, we started this process.
However, we soon came to realize that those parents who had already experienced a similar trauma before us hadn't been able to gather enough information and become more and more experienced.
Every time a similar diagnosis was given to a family, it would have wakened that family. Families that, like us, would look on the internet for a diagnosis that didn't bring results.
So, that was our first response, we realised that the first concrete action was to fill the existing gap of knowledge on this matter and create an organization.
Today that organisation exists, and it's called fightthestroke.org . Its mission is to be on the side of the children who survived a stroke and of their families.
This organisation is based on these five principles, I will only talk about two of them, that are extremely important.
First of all, we start to work together to raise awareness. It's important that people are aware of the issue of stroke in infancy, because today, as I said, when parents face this kind of diagnosis they research on the internet, and what kind of answers do they get?
Conduct a healthy life, don't smoke and avoid fat foods.
But these aren't risk factors for childhood stroke.
Hence, it's important to inform people that stroke in children is an issue and a frequent one, because it happens to two or three children out of 1.000 newborns.
So it's a problem that needs to be known.
Another aspect that we want to highlight, on which we are working in our organisation, it's the importance of early diagnosis.
In these past years we discovered that the age between birth and two years, it's crucial for these children.
In those early years brain plasticity is at its highest level and with an early diagnosis, thus a prompt intervention, it's possible to save those children.
The second thing we've learned - and we did it from our experience, by being "annoying" patients - is to never be satisfied with one diagnosis. We learned it in the moment
when our doctor told us that our boy's disease was called "cerebral palsy".
Nothing more static, nothing more still, nothing more hopeless.
But we actually realised that there was something to do: ask more questions, ask for a second opinion.
With our being annoying patients we passed this attitude to others, it became contagious.
We influenced other parents ... To do what?
To share their experience!
Our message was: today you cry, today you despair, dwell on your pain. But tomorrow, start working right away, fight, and do so with a smile.
All that led to this book. We wrote a book, "Fight and smile", because TED Talk's six minutes weren't enough anymore.
We had gathered so much knowledge, we had met hundreds of experts on this matter, around the world, it was time for us to share this knowledge.
This book also served another purpose: the book's sales financed medical research.
We are continuing our research project, that involves, as we mentioned earlier, mirror neurons. Why?
Because it is fundamental for us to continue working with an Italian specialist, Prof. Giacomo Rizzolatti, because this research can be the turning point in the life and the future of these young ictus survivors.
(Applause) As Francesca said, it was a journey, a never ending journey in which we've learned many things.
The last aspect she was talking about it's the relationship with the doctors.
This picture was taken a year ago, more or less in this same period.
It wasn't a happy period because Mario started having epileptic seizure, not just one, but a series of seizures and in that period I remember that we told him that the ambulances are the best way of moving around Milan, because it had become our usual form of transport.
During that time, although we were feeling down, we had actually started to view things from a different angle.
Francesca mentioned our relationship with doctors, we had so many questions and nothing could stop us.
When Mario's seizures started a doctor once told us: "How could you expect me to tell you in only ten minutes what I've learned in ten years?"
We couldn't accept those types of answers, anymore.
We didn't want to be quiet anymore, waiting for somebody to give us some answers.
We had to find a solution to this problem, putting the pieces together.
So, as it often happens, we went to the library, looked at different books, and discovered an incredible natural phenomenon that consists in putting together small things, creating something new and beautiful.
I'm talking about the flocks.
It's very common, you can see them in squares, birds do that and so do fish in the sea.
So, last September we created a flock of thinking heads.
We gathered together more than 500 people, for three days in Milan, discussing together the relationship between technology and medicine.
Why did we think it was so wonderful?
Because we gathered together young people, students, doctors, families and for the first time, they were all at the same level.
It was hard at first, I can assure you. You cannot imagine the doctors' expressions: they felt trapped because there was no stage!
Nobody cared about their background, it wasn't important how many articles they had written, because if they had done their job the issue wouldn't be there any more.
And for a moment, we managed to even them out.
Why did we do that, gathered all those people together?
Because we wanted to find new solutions.
Those that were available weren't working.
I hope it won't happen to you, I wish that it never happen to you, but if you have to run to the E.R. you realise how crazy it is, from the child perspective.
How crazy it is, in 2015, seeing a child crying just because he has to do some medical tests.
How everything is absurd.
There must have been a solution, a better option.
And this "Call For Brain", this sort of call-up that we organised on September helped us develop some solutions.
It also taught us to another thing: sometimes certain obstacles seem insuperable, the issue seems far too great. However, sometimes, as on that day, when doctors, patients, families, try to put themselves in each other shoes, changing their perspective, and looking at the world through different eyes, through the eyes of a three-year-old child.
We think that something terrific happened, that gave us what?
It gave us the strength and the will to develop, as Francesca was saying, a new method of rehabilitation, that starts from the present method, what you see on the left: That is Mario, some years ago, that's the typical rehabilitation, you do it few times a week, in a specialized center, only with specialists, parents have a very marginal role, it's very mechanical and mainly based on performance.
After "Call for Brain", we developed a prototype, because it's impossible to predict the solution, or someone would have already done it. The idea of prototype is to learn, fail, try again, keep trying. A prototype in which we've put together some ideas, developing a technology that allow to do rehabilitation from home.
It might seem insignificant, but think about it ... We can assure you, that the child's response in his environment it's completely different to when he's doing it in a place where he doesn't belong.
It needs to be something that comes from the child because a child of that age wants to play, and bubbles, in their simplicity, are the perfect game: Why complicate your life with a complicated game?
That was our prototype, and from that one and thanks to that, we started developing our last step: the idea of rehabilitation from home, using technology, and based on the concept of mirror neurons.
Two kids from different parts of the world, with similar problems and about the same age, can really benefit from the mirror neurons mechanism, because they can learn from each other. While it's more difficult to arrange a meeting in person, this has become possible thanks to technology.
This is the last point on which we're working.
We've never really stopped.
And on this matter I'm going to give the floor to Francesca.
The last lesson: learning to open up, to create a better environment.
That's what happened to us, but not accidentally.
We don't believe that you need to be on stage to be a hero and that being on stage would make you better parents.
We believe that there are opportunities that can be taken.
Why a picture of Heidi?
Because if you remember her story, and the Miyazaki's adaptation, that was one of the main messages of that cartoon, right?
Clara travels from Frankfurt to the Alps, where she gets up from her wheelchair and she starts walking.
Why did that happen?
We're not here today to talk about miracles, our story is not a miracle or a fairy-tale as it wasn't for Clara either.
We believe that environment can have a positive effect by giving a sense of calm and that a positive environment can ease the rehabilitation.
Clara got better on the Alps, and she got up from her wheelchair because she started to do her exercises. Not because of the sun or the nice landscape.
That's why this is the most important thing that we've learned, and what we would like to do with you today is to get you involved, to become a better mirror with us.
(Applause)
Roberto: Come, come, come! Francesca: There they are!
There they are! Can you see?
See? They're all there, and they can see themselves too!
Thank you, thank you so much.
Roberto: Do you know what the best part is? Why we absolutely love to show you to Mario?
Because from here - can you show them again, please?
We're all the same. Francesca: All at the same level. Roberto: All equal and it's wonderful!
(Applause) Roberto: Shall we go?
Francesca: Thank you, again!
(Applause)
Federico Sacchi: May 15th, 1983. A day that for most of you probably means nothing; but I promise you, it's imprinted in the memory of millions of Roma FC supporters.
It’s when the team wins its second Italian Championship: a victory dreamt for 41 years and conquered with four vantage points over Juventus FC.
A scudetto marked by a three to one victory - at the expenses of my beloved Torino FC - in front of the 60,000 fans standing at the Olympic Stadium.
That night, the new Italian champions are guests at La Domenica Sportiva: a historical national broadcast that was at the time, a must-watch show for fans.
Mid-show, a guest is announced and on the screen appears a 40-something man fully white dressed, with a shoulder strap guitar.
By the looks of it, he’s probably having technical problems with the guitar.
But when suddenly the background music starts, he goes with it and starts singing.
(Music) Andrea Anania: There’s a handsome man in Italy, who lives in Rome, who lives in Rome.
A funny football player, who plays with his heart and soul,
who calls his ball “my love”.
Honey, the goal. Hello, hello, hello, hello. This man, this prince, this man, this magician, this marvel, this artist, this good one, this human. His name is… FS: The song gets brutally interrupted here, but it’s clear who the protagonist is: Paulo Roberto Falcao,
the Brazilian ace, called by his fans “The Eight King of Rome”, one of the main players responsible for winning the Italian Championship.
The guy dressed as a house painter, who won over Roma FC players and made them laugh, is called Jorge Ben,
and is the artist that more than anyone contributed with his songs to the creation of Brazilian football mythology.
"Falcao" is an instant hit, but it cannot hold the candle with masterpieces from the past.
Yet, it shows three features that make Ben’s songs unique.
First: a catchy melody, that sticks in your mind for weeks.
Second: simple lyrics, but only on the surface.
The funny football player who calls the ball “my love”, makes us smile, but if I told you that until adolescence Falcao did sleep with that ball, that line gets a whole new meaning.
The third is irony.
Here’s how the song goes on. AA: (Sings in Portuguese) He's called Falcão, Falcao, Falcone, Falsao, Falcon, Farson, Falcone, Farsao, Falcon, Farcon.
FS: These are just some of the ways in which his name was mangled.
Ben knew it, because during interviews he was asked more questions about Falcao than about his music.
A classic grotesque Italian situation, that the artist exploits to compose a catchy refrain.
One of those things that only great composers do. And Jorge Ben is great, for sure.
I can surely state that we are in the presence of one of the main pop artist of the twentieth century,
but I see your faces.
(Laughter) Have I overdone myself?
Let’s do this: raise your hand if you had never heard of Jorge Ben before.
Well,
I can bet my bottom dollar that each of you, at least once in your life, have heard six of his songs.
Too bad you did it in the worst possible way, because of the crime perpetrated by a band called Two Man Sound.
These three beautiful examples of Belgian manhood, became millionaires at the end of the ‘70s by adapting Brazilians hits into dance music.
Their masterpiece is the seven-minute “Disco Samba”, a song also known as “Conga line”, (Laughter) the diabolical song that on New Year’s Eve turns us into toddlers and makes us dance in a single line, frantically.
“Disco Samba” is a medley of big Brazilian hits in which Jorge’s repertoire gets literally sacked.
AA: (Sings) Pe pee, pee pe, pee pe, Pe pee, pee pe, pee pe. Zazueira, Zazueira. Fio Maravlha, we only want you. Ay Ay Caramba.
I live in a tropical country. Blessed by God and naturally enchanting. FS: These are the first five songs spoiled in “Disco Samba”.
You never paid attention to the sixth one, probably, because it’s at the end of the medley, at which point, on New Year’s Eve, we are all too drunk to pay attention to something.
It’s actually the one that everyone knows.
AA: (Sings in Portuguese) Oh lariá laiô obá obá obá. Come on, get out of my way 'cause I want to pass.
Because the samba is vivacious, and what I want is to do the samba.
FS: Are you familiar with the expression “To start off with a bang?”
Well, “Mas que nada” is the first song written by Jorge Ben at 21.
He releases it in 1963 in “Samba Esquema Novo”, first of nine albums with which he revolutionizes not only samba, but all popular Brazilian music, coding a sound that critics defined as Samba rock, Samba Soul or Samba funky.
Forget it, and trust me: this is music from another planet.
The grooviest acoustic guitar in history, a unique voice, for both timbre and flexibility, amazing musicians all at the service of marvelous songs.
It’s a journey that climaxes in 1972 with the release of “Ben”, the album that features Jorge’s first song about football.
A song inspired by an episode connected to his favorite team, Flamengo FC, the legendary Carioca club where he played as a kid, before an injury nipped in the bud his soon-to-be career.
We are at Maracanà Stadium, in Rio de Janeiro. It’s January 15th, 1972, Flamengo - Benfica.
It’s the second half of the game, the score is a tie zero. Fans start chanting a name: Fio.
João Batista de Sales, known as Fio, is a midfielder with a bizarre technique and incredibly protruding teeth.
Two features that made him Torcida’s favorite.
As usual, Fio is benched, because he rarely gets to play.
But that day, fans want no excuses: they are still chanting his name.
(Background guitar) 44,000 voices plus one: Jorge Ben’s, who was witnessing the events, when an exasperated Zagalo decided to put Fio in.
AA: (Sings in Portuguese) And again he arrived with inspiration With a lot of love, with emotion, with an explosion and...goal!
Shaking the crowd at minute 33 of the second half, after making a celestial play ending in a goal.
He did a one-two, dribbled two defenders, touched and dribbled the goalkeeper. He just didn't enter in the goal with the ball Because he was humble and goal!
It was a classy goal where he showed his maliciousness and his race.
It was an angel's goal, truly a brilliant goal to which the thankful crowd was enchanted: Fio Maravilha, we want only you.
Fio Maravilha, score one more
so we can see it. FS: In Fio Maravilha, Jorge elevates to an epic event an ordinary sports chronicle.
An action that symbolizes the harmony of Brazilian football, made of fantasy, improvisation, and respect for the opponent.
He gets his inspiration for the lyrics from Brazilian football commentators, and builds music as a climax that only peaks in the end, with the jingle we all know.
The result has an epic power, a power that turns a player into a legend, an action that would be otherwise forgotten.
In the album that contains “Fio Maravilha”, Jorge pays tribute to another hero of his: Saint George from Cappadocia.
Nothing weird, if we think that in Brazil, football players are as venerated as saints.
But Jorge has another bizarre passion: alchemy.
In 1974, this passion leads him to release “A Tabla de Esmeralda”,
album in which he sings about hermetic philosophers, Saints, alchemists, alien gods, and celestial agriculture.
I know you won’t believe me, but the album is an absolute masterpiece.
To prove that I’m not crazy, in Rolling Stone Brasil's rankings of the greatest Brazilian albums, “A Tabla de Esmeralda” ranks sixth.
In 1975, Jorge releases another masterpiece: “Solta o Pavao”, the album that contains Ben’s second football song.
This time, the protagonist of the song is not a specific football player, but a position: (Guitar strums) the “zagueiro” (defender).
AA: (Sings in Portuguese) Goosebumps, defender, he’s a good defender he’s the guardian angel of the defense.
But to be a good defender he can’t get too sentimental.
He has to be slim and graceful, be cold-blooded, self-confident and loyal.
The defender has to be a rascal. In case of emergency, with the ball on the pitch, think fast and concretely, or you can go back to playing in the forest, because the Championship is at stake.
Defender. FS: “Zagueiro” is not a simple celebration of a less praised position, but a handbook for those who aspire to become a football legend, but just aren't cut for the point man role.
I’ve always thought that Ben wrote it for all the kids to teach them the values of a great defender: loyalty, sacrifices, and responsibility,
to create an honest example to inspire tomorrow’s champions.
In 1976, Jorge releases “Africa Brasil”, yet another masterpiece, that leaves in a vinyl's grooves the essence of Afro-Brazilianity.
Because Jorge was one of the very firsts to bring African elements in Brazilian music, and address certain topics.
With “Africa Brasil” he goes further and writes three songs about three legendary characters from the African-Brazilian community.
The first one is “Zumbi”, the slaves’ liberator, the last leader of Quilombo de Palmares, a sort of Brazilian Spartacus.
The second is “Xica De Silva”, the legendary slave who became queen by marrying a diamond merchant.
The third is “Umbabarauma”.
AA: (Sings in Portuguese) Umbabarauma, goal scorer.
Play ball play ball player.
Jump, jump, fall, get up, go up and get down, run, kick, find a hole, thrill and give thanks
See how the whole city empties out on this beautiful afternoon only to watch you play.
Umbabarauma goal scorer. This is the story of Umbabarauma an African point man whose mind is set up,
Umbabarauma.
FS: Football fans among you will be probably digging in their memory to find out who this damn Umbabarauma is.
Forget about it.
There’s no football player with this name.
Umbabarauma represents all black football players and Brazilians’ passion for football itself.
Because Umbabarauma is both the supreme point man and African warrior at the same time.
Umbabarauma is African and Brazilian; and thanks to the power of Ben’s music, Umbabarauma now belongs to Brazil’s collective imagination. It’s the glorification of Jorge Ben as an author.
He generated, with a song, a mythological figure.
(Guitar strums) It’s the glorification of an Ethiopian immigrant’s son raised in one of Rio de Janeiro's most miserable suburbs
who had a dream since he was a kid: to become a football player.
Dream torn apart from an injury right when he joined his favorite football team.
It’s the glorification of a kid that throws in the towel, embraces the music and works hard, for years, until he becomes a music legend,
just as idolized as the football players he was admiring so much.
Because Brazilian football, with that injury, might have lost a future star, but gained its biggest poet-songwriter: Jorge Duilio Lima Menezes, also known as Jorge Ben. (Guitar strums)
(Applause and cheers) Thank you. (Applause)
This is the pangolin of Eric Joisel. It is an origami, it is folded by only one esagonal sheet of paper, without any cut.
Origami's rules are easy: (Applause) you take a sheet of paper and fold it, you don't use any scissors or glue.
Origami is an art - and it takes a bit of courage to say it - because almost all think that origami is simply... a child's play.
I will try to change your mind.
Today origami is this: every model has its author, this is a horse made by Roman Diaz... who is a veterinary capable to capture the essence of animals.
Instead Robert Lang is one of the origami's theorists. Among other things, he formulated a theory... about how to design complex origami, of which I will just tell you something.
This is a model of mine, a three-dimensional pram.
Satoshi Kamiya is known for creating very complex models like this wasp, while Giang Dinh made simplicity his magic bullet: he works with wet cardboard, he sprinkles it and he can obtain these plastic figures.
Eric Joisel is an other author, the pangolin's author too, and he is a master in creating also human figures and using the volume of the paper to create effects like, for example, Harlequin's legs.
How have we reached this point?
Let's start from the simpliest thing: the square base.
It is a paper square. First you fold the medians, then the diagonals, close it and you obtain this figure: it is a square base and it has 4 flaps.
No surprises, it comes from a square: 4 sides, 4 angles, 4 flaps.
Folding a model by John Montroll, at a certain point, you get your hands on this stuff, which seems a square base, but has 5 flaps instead of 4. So you ask yourself, "But how, I started from a square: where does the fifth flap come from?" The trick to understand this, to understand how it works, is to reopen the sheet.
So you reopen the sheet and you will see that the author... didn't do nothing but drawing a pentagon inside the square... and hiding all the exceeding paper.
So the message here is, "If you want to understand how it works, reopen the model." This is one of the scorpions made by Robert Lang... it has a lot of tips.
To understand how this runs, it has to know how an umbrella works.
So imagine: a closed umbrella is the folded end, an open umbrella is the paper which is needed to obtain that end.
A bigger umbrella will give a longer end.
This is the same model, the same scorpion but reopened.
It has been folded and then reopened, marking the position of its different ends.
Every point is a circle, an umbrella: that big umbrella, the blue one, is for the tail, the red ones are the paws, the green ones are the claws.
Now we go back to our presentation.
This is the figure you have just seen, and this map of the folds... is called technically ‘crease pattern’. This is the Lang original version, where you can see all the work folds.
Therefore the problem of projecting a complex origami turns into... the mathematic problem of having the circles on the plane opportunely.
It's not only circles, you can see that there are also some figures, they are lightly out of sorts but they would show some streams... which divide the points and can give a topology to the model.
Let's take a step back in time: there is as such geometry of origami, made of axioms and theorems.
It is a more powerful geometry than the ’ruler and compass‘ one, meaning that all the workable constructions with ruler and compass... are also workable with origami, but origami can do other ones, such dividing an angle into three equal parts.
A small example of origami geometry: we start from this square, we make these folds... and what we obtain is this figure here.
They are only three folds, but you can glimpse a big triangle, which I state it is equilateral.
It is equilateral because its sides... are three of the square's sides, so all they are equals, ok?
And if it is an equilateral triangle these are exactly 60 degrees, therefore I can find very easily 60 degrees from a square.
An other example concerns... the division of a sheet in equal parts.
As you can see, the points on the diagonal are six... and so they divide that segment into five equal parts.
On the other diagonal the division is in thirds, therefore, through very few folds, which are that ones highlighted here, I can obtain a division in fifths and thirds.
Why do I need this for, maybe we will discover it later.
In origami, one of the best moves is the twist.
The twist is built on a central polygon going around and some parallel folds... which spread out two by two from the polygon. It is so called because when you make this fold, the central polygon actually makes a rotation.
What is twist used for?
The twist it is for creating some roses like this.
This one is built on a pentagonal twist, it is by Naomiki Sato.
Or it is for creating tessellations, like these here.
Especially look that in the middle, which soon you will see in a new light.
Tessellations can also be very complex, like this made by Alessandro Beber: this has some twists with 12, 3, 4 and 6 sides.
Twists are also used in technology: here the question is that of a solar panel... which has to be locked up in a rocket... or a shuttle, to be thrown and, when it is in space, it has to be opened with minimum effort and damage.
This is the series of moves to be done.
It is this.
This is how it is thrown, then, once it is high, it opens.
(Applause) This is a twist... and this is a tessellation of a twist, it is the former beehive.
So, as you can see, each hexagonal stuff... can be opened.
Therefore these are related to each other.
I go mad for motion origami, which are these I'm showing you.
This is a fractal: it means that I have a row made of 12 external petals, a bit smaller one, which have the same shape but different dimension.
And this is a single paper sheet.
(Applause) The surprising thing is not only its opening, but its closing, because paper remembers.
(Applause) I want to amaze you again with a pair of motion models: this is called flexicube, it is made from a single paper strip... which composes 8 little hinged cubes, in order to have this continuous movement.
Instead this is its father: the double star flexicube, a model by Dave Brill.
Although it is a flexicube also, it is the only model not made of... a single paper sheet, there are 64 fitted modules.
It can go round also, but at a certain point I can stop, I can open the trunk and pull out the first star.
But it is a double star felxicube, because there are two stars.
(Applause) Here I let you imagine how much geometry is inside here, but this is half cube and this an other half cube: they are pulled together.
But also this is half cube: this is half cube, the star, and if I turn it, it will become a box for stars, that is a box which exactly has the space to contain a star inside.
(Applause) Why would anyone make origami?
There are a lot of reasons.
Origami instigates manual skills, fantasy and creativity, makes children like Maths.
The reason why I fold origami... is because it is nice: it is magic, the magic of transformation really.
The ‘Center for diffusion of origami’ is a no-profit association... which gathers all Italian origamists and also organise meetings.
The meetings about origami are hotbeds of ideas... where everyone brings his own models, shows them, explains them and makes them fold by others.
In this way there is a fusion of ideas... that has also brought to meetings about origami and teaching.
Now we are organizing... the third meeting for next April. It will be a great chance, for teachers and instructors... who wants to use origami... for their job, to get in touch with different opportunities.
I wanted to finish with Zsebe's bear, showing how it folds, but I don't know if I still have time...
Yes? Do I still have time?
Claudio Ruatti: Take a minute!
RG: I will take a minute then, here it is!
I have already folded something because otherwise I could not do it.
Here one can already infer a little structure: this will become a bear, with head and tail.
From the head one can obtain the ears in some way, then the great thing is the way you shape the head.
Now here we need to zoom in really to this part.
So, first you fold up, now I have exactly a rhombus.
I fold up its face, then I squash from the inside... and I close the top part, which will become the bear's hump.
It is taking the shape of a bear, you can see its face, but now the basic move: it is an upstream fold here, followed by a downstream fold placed right down, which make the bear's face pop up.
Sorry, it has come off a bit like that.
