Thank you.
Do ecosystems have an acoustic intelligence?
To try to understand this, I am conducting a long term research project, which studies the most relevant element that in a dense forest reaches our senses: Sound.
I've always had a sort of obsession to collect the sounds of nature, ever since I was very young, in the countryside and woods of Urbino, the town where I was born, where I would literally spend entire nights recording, waiting to capture the perfect soundscape.
The idea was to give the chance to others to experience the same marvel of listening to the acoustic environment.
Today I am a composer, a sound designer, I teach electroacoustic music, and I have been studying the world of natural sounds for about 15 years, ever since I had this experience, in February 2002, in the Amazon rainforest, in a central area, that confirmed most of the intuitions I had had up to that moment.
During the recording of a dusk chorus, that gradual moment of transition between day and night, that is usually very active in natural habitats, I understood that the order and balance that I was perceiving was the tangible result of all the ecosystem's processes, moment by moment.
I recorded the sounds using high-sensitivity microphones, and then for three consecutive hours, I listened to the coordinated and interdependent voices of the different species that were playing a specific part, presumably "written" by natural evolution, or rather, based on the natural selection of 65 million years of evolution.
The hundreds of voices of this polyphony were primarily activated by light, temperature, humidity, almost in an automatic way, but every individual emitted acoustic signals, sound signals with a precise, specific vital function: nothing was left to chance.
And these sounds fit into acoustic niches of frequency and of time over the course of 24-hour cycles.
I could perceive that this clock, however, was a very fragile mechanism, much more fragile where the system had a higher biodiversity and in the habitat where the canoe was entering.
In the whole planet, today, that miracle of bioacoustics synchrony is becoming extinct.
(Environmental sounds)
The recording we've just heard in the background of the video was done in Amazonia in 2002.
If we went in the same exact place in the same season today, at the same time on the same day and with the same technology, the recording would probably be already poorer and less organized.
Let me tell you a few data on extinction.
The present extinction rate, recently calculated, is 100 to 1,000 times greater than the normal extinction rate, the natural one.
IUCN - International Union for Conservation of Nature, the global authority on the status of the natural world publishes an alarming data: 1/8 of the birds, 1/4 of the mammals and almost half of the amphibians which belong to the known and monitored species have joined the Red List of Threatened Species.
The predictions in the 1990s, which estimated that 30,000 species per year, or four species per hour, would become extinct, proved to be correct.
We are in the middle of a silent catastrophe, not addressed by the media.
Life is collapsing globally in what is defined as the sixth mass extinction, provoked by the conscious actions taken on the biosphere by one species, the Homo sapiens.
Deforestation, pollution, and invasive species are the main drivers of the extinction crisis. In addition, today's climate changes are modifying the natural habitats, impoverishing them at a more severe and faster pace.
The situation is so serious that there is no time to demonstrate that things are changing, even through my recordings. I'm often asked: "Are you comparing the records between past and present?"
I don't feel the need to, but I feel compelled to record fragments of this intangible sonic heritage as soon as possible, and in as many places as possible,
The project "Fragments of Extinction," conceived as early as in 1998, consists in recording the entire circadian cycles, 24 hours of continuous recording in specific habitats, prioritizing those with the highest biodiversity. The selected habitats
are around the Equator, between five degrees south and five degrees north.
We know that at the Equator the difference between seasons is minimal and the duration of day and night has been the same for millions of years.
Because of this, rhythms of soundscape are extremely regular and very beautiful.
Within this area, priority is given to "primary forests" or, in other words, forests that are in their original state, intact.
In these primary forests, the priority is given to those forests that are undisturbed to this day, where there is no human activity,
or untouched places.
In these remote ecosystems, we can still trace the acoustic signature of evolution.
However, for the benefit of the future generations, we have to hurry in order to capture this primordial polyphonies, because they will be deteriorating fast.
The project "Fragments of Extinction" has always been at the crossroads between different research disciplines and approaches. Technological: through 3D-sound recording, utilizing the most advanced technologies available. Scientific: through the ecological and ecoacoustic analysis of the data. Artistic: through the creation and realization of installations for museums, immersive systems to listen to tridimensional audio documentaries.
Environmental: this approach obviously allows me to communicate what is happening through an interdisciplinary project.
So obviously, this project is faced with several difficulties: to carry all the equipment you are seeing, for example. In the last trip we did 24-hour recordings with 38 active channels, meaning recording 38 tracks simultaneously using 38 microphones.
Just to give you an idea of the amount of data recorded, we brought home a terabyte and a half in three weeks.
We managed to make all these technologies and batteries function in places where electric power lines are hardly ever available,
mounting and installing them in these challenging locations, as we often had to travel to a high altitude for several reasons.
Lastly, the difficulties in everyday life, like humidity, the biggest enemy of technology, which often reaches 95%, with thresholds close to 100%.
In this picture from 2008, we were in the Central African Republic.
We had to utilize a smaller, automated system because we couldn't stay in the forest during the night, as there was a risk of attack by elephants. They are being hunted, as we know, and so today they attack humans.
Let's hear a small fragment of two species of monkeys and one of elephants: the low-frequency spectrum.
(Environmental sounds) I want to tell you one last episode.
Here we are in Amazonia, this picture is from February 2016. After several hours of canoeing and walking, we reached a very special and undisturbed area of the primary forest. I decided to stay alone that night, to take care of the equipment and to impact this particular habitat as little as possible during the 24-hour recording we had to do.
Well, when the night came, it was close to complete darkness on the forest floor. I was scared. I was really scared.
And after hours of wrestling with my thoughts and fighting an impelling need to leave this environment, almost completely unknown to science and where I was feeling a stranger, I collected myself and calmed down through the act of listening. I listened like I never listened before; I heard the flow of the events; I was listening to the essense of what kept together this extraordinary system that was alive around me.
It was exactly the act of listening that allowed me to come back to my senses and made my fear go away.
Let's listen to an unaltered excerpt of this recording.
(Environmental sounds) Over the years, I changed from a composer who created music to one who looks for it in nature, who preserves it and, eventually, brings it to the public.
It wasn't easy to give up the creativity that is part of what a musician does.
But I created, during these years, immersive spaces designed for people to experience in spherical periphony and with a definition never tried before, these primordial soundscapes, as if they were in a time machine, or in an unusual concert hall, or a temple, if you prefer.
And if we now extend this vision to our grandchildren's generation or their kids' generation, the recordings we are talking about will be the only way they will know how extraordinary and complex the biophony of the natural world was.
I want to leave you with a question, a question I often ask myself: these places, these spaces, these eco-acoustic theatres, could they really become a way to reconnect with the natural world and help with the paradigm shift that humanity will have to make necessarily, in the next decades?
Thank you.
(Applause)
'From Fish to Philosopher'.
This is an excellent book by Homer Smith, published in Boston in the early 1950s.
Homer Smith was a great physiologist.
Most of what we think we discover in the laboratory can be found in Smith’s books, one better than the other.
In this one, he states: 'We are not what we eat, but what our kidneys are able to preserve.'
What are our kidneys for?
To produce urine, you'll say.
True.
But the kidney does many other things too.
Kidneys allow us to live here, in spite of the environment.
It seems normal to us to live on Earth.
But the Earth is actually hostile to life, at least it was, and in some ways it still is.
Our body is two-thirds water.
Our cells are immersed in a fluid that has, and must have, a very particular composition.
That's exactly the kidneys' main function: to precisely maintain the correct composition of the fluid surrounding our cells.
Just a little bit more sodium, Na+, in this fluid, or a little bit less, is enough to damage your brain.
What about potassium?
Too much potassium can stop your heart.
Don't worry.
The kidney takes care of this, and you don’t even realize it.
The kidney gives us the freedom to go too far sometimes.
The kidney knows extremely well how to eliminate what is in excess.
We can consume whatever in excess, salt and water for example, and the kidneys eliminate what's not needed.
And it does it on a minute-by-minute basis, with a precision no apothecary scale could hope to match.
So, our kidneys take care of getting rid of the excess.
However, if our kidneys suddenly stop working, we will die within a few days.
That's because the waste our bodies produce everyday is toxic.
But the kidney is also able to adapt well to damage.
In fact, you can lose up to 80 - 85% of your renal function and still be relatively well.
And then?
There's dialysis.
But dialysis doesn’t replace all the kidney's functions,
like its role in stimulating red blood cells production by producing erythropoietin,
or its role in protecting our bones through vitamin D.
Without our kidneys, we would all suffer from rickets.
How many people live on dialysis?
About two million people all around the world.
This costs us more than a trillion dollars.
A huge amount of money.
People on dialysis are to be found mostly in the United States, Europe, Japan and Australia.
90% of patients on dialysis live in those countries.
In the rest of the world, in developing countries, however, there is very little or no dialysis.
But five billion people live there.
In a way, dialysis is just for rich countries.
Being in a position to change this has been a great challenge all over the world and for all of us.
This issue has been the focus of many years of research.
But how do we end up on dialysis?
I'll tell you a story.
Mario is 50 years old, and he's always been healthy.
When he was young, during his medical check-up for military service, traces of blood were found in his urine, but nobody paid too much attention to it.
He goes on feeling in good health for years until one day he starts to urinate a lot, at night too, and he has severe headaches.
He has enough of it.
He goes to the drugstore to get painkillers. The pharmacist takes his blood pressure:
It's 200 over 110.
'How is that possible?' he asks, 'I’ve always had normal blood pressure.'
So, the pharmacist tells him to go to the hospital.
Once in the emergency room, he has some tests done.
Doctors say that he’s suffering from acute kidney failure.
Two weeks later, Mario is on dialysis,
four hours a day, three times a week, every week of every month, every month of the year.
And this will be the case until he gets a kidney transplant.
This is an ordinary story of high blood pressure and diabetes, which in time can damage your kidneys.
So we wondered how many people might have a similar story?
An estimated 10% of the world's population have some form of kidney damage, and it will progress if it’s not stopped in time.
So it's very important to think about this.
Usually these are people who have proteins in their urine, especially albumin.
Our studies have shown that the presence of albumin in urine, even in small quantities, will sooner or later damage the kidneys, and then the heart too.
Thankfully, kidneys can now be protected.
Some wonderful news that I want to share with you,
and it's about a change.
We used to think that dialysis was the only way, but now the possibility of protecting our kidneys exists.
Which is?
With drugs that lower blood pressure and belong to the angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors category.
Difficult words.
These drugs are used to treat hypertension, but they also halt the progression of kidney disease
and, in the long run, also protect against heart attacks and failures.
This discovery was made here, in Bergamo, thanks to a great collaborative effort between researchers and clinicians of the Mario Negri Institute and our hospital.
This treatment is now used worldwide,
even in the United States, which is usually ahead of us.
How did we do it?
(Applause) How did we discover drugs that protect our kidneys?
With the help of animals.
Without rats and mice there's no advance, and medicine hits the buffers.
We should all be aware of this, each and everyone of us should know this.
Thanks to these small rodents, we have understood the relationship that exists between the loss of normal kidney function, the unhealthy passage of albumin into urine, and the presence of inflammation, which indicates first stage kidney disease.
The jump from mice to men didn't take long.
We found out that those drugs that were effective in mice and rats are very effective in humans.
If a patient is not treated - the red line - his kidney function deteriorates over time, diminishes, until dialysis or a transplant is needed.
But with the drugs we studied and discovered, renal function is maintained.
There's no renal function loss, at least for the first three years,
and, something we had not expected, renal function actually improves after three years.
Why so?
Because stem cells are involved.
With these drugs we are able to maintain the store of stem cells each of us already has in our kidneys.
Stem cells are everywhere, we have them in all our organs.
We have found them in many parts.
Here in Bergamo, we've seen them in the kidney.
We've studied and found them in animals and in humans.
We have isolated them, as the jargon would have it.
We have characterized them.
We have seen what they produce.
We have seen what there potential is.
With the drugs that protect kidneys, we've been able to maintain the integrity of these stem cells.
This means that these drugs can stop the progression of kidney disease, and that, day by day, the kidney successfully repairs itself
thus improving kidney function.
To be sure, in order to treat kidney disease and to stop its progression, it has to be detected early enough.
If it's not, it can be too late.
How we do detect it?
By a simple urine test.
We only have to check for proteins, especially albumin, in the urine.
If they are present, it's a sign.
It needs to be followed up.
This almost never happens, we don't think about urine.
There is little awareness of kidney disease.
The kidney has been neglected, even in common everyday language.
We talk about having 'the heart of a lion' or being 'lily-livered', but there are no expressions about the kidneys.
In the Bible, there are many references to both blood and the heart, but the kidneys are only mentioned once:
To judge people, God looks at their kidneys.
This is because kidneys were considered the seat of temperament, emotions, and virtues.
And then, nobody mentions kidneys in literature until Shakespeare
in the late 16th century. In the third act of 'The Merry Wives of Windsor', Falstaff mentions kidneys when he tells Master Brook, a gentleman in disguise, about his misadventures.
Falstaff, having hidden in a basket of filthy laundry which is thrown into the Thames, asks the gentlemen what he thinks
of such an event for 'a man of my kidney',
meaning for a man like him, with his temperament and disposition, and also, to a certain extent, with his build.
If it weren't for our kidneys, we would still be living in ponds and would be fishes or frogs.
It is the kidneys that have allowed us to evolve and have allowed certain animals to leave the oceans and rivers and begin to live on dry land.
Without kidneys, here on Earth, there would be no philosophers.
Thank you for your attention.
(Applause)
