Would it be a tiring day according to you?
Yes, but not because the universe decided so.
It's because our brain works "as if".
You put yourself in a "tiring day" mood, different from the one you were in at school when they said, "there'll be a sub in tomorrow". You got into a happy mood, you even liked the subjects you used to hate just because there was a sub.
An example that made it clear to me: try to think about a fork. Do you have a fork in your head?
Not stuck in your head.
A fork and an empty plate. Do you have them in your mind?
Try to visualize the fork scraping a plate, a fork scraping the blackboard at school, it's a negative sensation, right?
And yet, there's no fork, no plate and no blackboard.
But we reacted "as if".
Every word creates a suggestion in our minds and knowing it, we use language in a smarter way.
Rather than saying a tiring day, what else could I say?
"Differently easy day" sounds better.
Instead of "ugly", "not beautiful".
Which one do you prefer? "Not beautiful"
or "ugly". "Not beautiful".
The word "rich", in neurosemantics, is one of those that makes us happiest.
Rich in demands is better than demanding, right?
Rich, lucrative, abundant.
Stimulating for her, numbing for him.
(Laughter) So, using language this way, you changed your mood already.
Otherwise, you act like my aunt who, even in good weather, says, "It's a really nice day but I have to go to work anyway".
If you say I have to do that, you get into a worse mood.
Now that she knows about the fork scraping the plate, she looks at me when it's good weather and she says: "I hope it doesn't spoil!"
(Applause) Let's do this fulfillment that is based on the three steps method, -awareness, physical and mental attitude.
Clap your hands aligning the fingertips.
Oriental disciplines say that it boosts energy levels.
Believe it or not, clapping your hands that way, smiling energetically, creates energy, doesn't it?
At least my hands became red.
We will do it for 40 minutes, ok?
No, I'm joking. Only twice.
Clap your hands saying these positive words which are good for the harvest, the grape harvest, world peace, peace of mind, that are: "very good, very good"
and lift your enthusiasm.
Ready? Go!
"Very good, very good, yeah!"
Thanks everybody!
(Applause) (Music)
Who's the real Francesco?
Who is the real society? Who am I?
Who are we? Who are you?
This is the question
that resonates inside me again and again.
I come from a land, from a valley, from Valtellina. From there I looked at nature,
we all always have nature around us.
Nature can inspire us.
I started trying to ask myself, what is a real apple tree.
What it is, what it does, an apple tree.
What does it produce.
And the interesting thing I realized was, an apple tree makes apples, from a human point of view.
However, I also saw that if the apple tree considers its true essence as apple tree, an apple tree's business is also absorbing carbon dioxide and then release oxygen.
So, what is the real apple tree?
Is it only something which gives apples or is it something which, also, does that absorbing operation to release oxygen?
So who am I really?
Am I someone who works according to social patterns or am I someone who also does something more?
Then I began to think about which are the social patterns in this.
And I discovered something really interesting for me.
I began to notice that the world is full of broken rules.
Of broken social rules, of broken cooperation rules.
Thus I began to think that the broken rules had to be broken.
So I started to repeat to myself the mantra of breaking the broken rules.
And therefore the apple tree not only makes apples, but produces also oxygen.
As a matter of fact, nobody taught the apple tree to produce oxygen, it learnt it itself.
So the apple tree really knows what the real apple tree is.
As for me, I'm still thinking about who the real Francesco is.
Then in life, each one of us has to deal with what happens to him.
We work a job over another and another.
And somehow I found myself understanding and searching - leading, founding, running companies.
And somehow I always found myself in charge of something,
I started asking myself: What is the real meaning
of what we do? Where can be found?
Who are we?
And looking at the mountains I began to think, I saw myself as one who picks up the heart, at a point, which is the oxygen produced.
Looking at the mountains of my dearest valley, I started to say: Looking at the top, it's a bit this mountain's place where I want to go.
It's my goal, in a sense.
If you ask a climber what he wants to do in life, he wants to reach the top of the mountain and walk the path down.
At this point, I asked myself, about the organization: Man, what is the organization?
What is the goal of our organization?
Who are we, really?
Because we are an organization that produces excellent apples.
Well, we do not make apples, but we produce excellent services, excellent products, the market recognizes us as very good.
But what is our real purpose?
Who are we really?
And what is the organization really?
And from there we began to answer our questions all together, and so we began to understand that to give us meaning, to give meaning to the organization, to the company in which we all work,
or as I prefer to think, to the company in which we all serve.
We are servants of our true being. Because we don't think about -
want to think about what the future is.
Many people tell me, Yeah we should leave a better future.
We must leave a better future?
I ask you.
I don't think so.
I think that we have to leave a better present because, if you think about it, all of us are here today.
I am standing on this red dot, you are sitting and we are in a condition where someone will sit again tomorrow or stand up again on this stage.
It is as if at this moment I was already living someone else's future.
But then I have to think about its future and then head to that direction, or rather I have to think that this red dot and your chair must somehow be preserved, we must take care of it?
And therefore it must be left, because that is already a loaned future that you have from someone else.
But if I start thinking that I borrowed from someone else, I begin to understand that like any loan I get - like, I go to a bank to borrow money - I have to give it back.
So this someone else who will come from the future will give me, I will have to deliver his future back because I borrowed it.
A bank generally asks me for interest income and therefore I will also have to give back interest income.
This is the meaning of what I have to do, so I don't have to build a better future, I have to improve my very present.
It is only by doing that, that I return the future of those who will come after me here, who will come after you there and who will come after us,
it is really systemic.
It is always a bigger matter of those who will come after us to find the world.
Think, there are great artists. Think of Michelangelo's David,
there are artworks that can withstand hundreds of years and therefore manage to take, have borrowed the whole future and are delivering it.
The magnificence of art.
Now think about how the system changes if a mindset like this starts to seep in a company, that there is a loaned future.
Think of your relationship with a customer.
Do you just deliver apples, to customers, or also deliver the impact you generate in growing those apples?
Customers choose you on that basis as well.
Of course you have to know how to make good apples in the current market, so you can't set a price ten times as much on apples.
But think about the company, the company is serving, the company together with the customers which are serving an evolutionary purpose.
So what this organization is doing?
This organization knows why it works,
the organization becomes a platform where people can develop, evolve, find themselves.
This is the meaning of an organization, so does it makes sense to have a hierarchy within the organization? Does it make sense to have someone who tells you what to do?
No, you already know.
Because you know you want to turn CO2 into oxygen, by producing apples.
You know this. So you know you have to go towards that direction because you'll build an impact on a future that has been loaned to you.
So a company can be self-organized, a company can be a living organism which continues to discover and learn day by day.
This is the meaning of what is happening.
This is the meaning of how, if I get to understand who I really am, who we really are, I can do.
And there, of course, there is a company's board of directors.
And what kind of leadership should have the board?
Think, we are climbing down from that mountain of mine, and from my dear valley I go down.
A CEO is at the service of the company's evolutionary purpose.
It's not the boss.
The boss is the purpose, it is what we all want to do.
And everything else serves this purpose.
It is called servant leadership.
And so, even the capital.
Now, in Italy we are lucky because we have the benefit companies that are the tool that allows us to realize that.
We have a B-Corp certification etc.
So the real point is that companies become places, platforms where people can wake up, where people can find themselves, where people make sense of their work beyond their mere salary.
We can afford bread, so the salary is sensible.
It is normal for an apple tree to produce apples.
Let's not question it.
But the important fact is that we must understand that we have an impact on society because we loaned a future from someone.
So the goals are the same of a traditional organization, and everyone play them all.
I act as HR, human resources, I act as CFO, I am a little jack-of-all-trades.
It is obvious, if we are all serving a purpose, I can share with you all the information to get there.
Salaries can be public, the monthly statement can be public,
the budget can be available to everyone, to all those who want to change the world through the company.
Think if the human resources is within each of us who hire people for a company: that serves a purpose - but makes apples, remember it.
If I am in that situation, what do I want?
I want people - If I had to hire someone to work with me, I want that person to exploit the company as the place where she can thrive.
So the first thing I would say to this person is: I know you know how to handle apples, I know you know how to prune, but I also want to know if you really want to come here to develop yourself.
And it is here, a little thought because when one thinks in an entrepreneurial way, one begins to think, one asks himself about what scaling is,
a company must be able to scale.
Also the impact that the company in an entrepreneurial way, it must be able to grow and become exponential.
There's all this Exponential Organizations movement that tells us: we have to do things.
And the important part I want to tell you is: we must find a way and we must continue to break rules that are already broken.
Consider this: you have a negotiation
with a purchasing department. You go there and they asks you, as a first thing, a rebate,
They tell you: anyway, I want to have with you a long-term relationship.
It's like if I meet a beautiful girl, I look and say: "I want to marry you, but you smell".
(Laughter) And so the relationship is strange because I want to marry you but, er, there is a problem to fix.
But if it's a long-term relationship, I mean, we're already finding a solution.
This is what happens normally, I go to the purchasing dept. and they always tell me: look, we wanted - we understand your supply, your emails etcetera - but anyway, the money you ask me, that they were 100, they will be 80 because for us it is 80.
From there I started thinking: oh man, this thing is hard here, it's like I really want to establish a long-term relationship but I can't.
So the part that I started to think about, seeing myself as part of a system, because we are all part of a system.
And if we are part of the system we must begin to think that, in a mutual way, we must help the other party to do - what? To trust the system, and from my side too,
get the other party trust me.
So understanding that the other party is right.
So its quantification is right.
And so the point is: there are two weights, two dimensions, two sizes of the t-shirt for the same body.
Who doesn't have two shirts of different sizes but that fit for the same body?
Each of us has them.
Why? Because there are two weights,
two sizes, two weights for the same body.
So the important thing, understanding this, is, our customer is always right.
So his assessment is a personal assessment, his personal perception of value.
It's really interesting this topic, if I start thinking about it and say: the discount itself was a broken rule,
because it is normal to ask for a discount, but nobody knows why
if you ask them. It's broken!
But the important aspect is that that discount is a thing on which I can negotiate.
It is a point on which I, together with the client, can delve into, because, remember, I want to expand the impact, the common good I want to produce compared to my purpose, compared to the top of my mountain.
So what can I do?
I can transform this concept of difference in price and make it as if it were a bet.
Like going to casino and make a bet thinking of winning everything, and then thinking of taking this bet, this difference of money, and say: we want to bet them for our evolutionary purpose, because we want to expand the "who we are".
If you think about it, I started at 13 years old to wonder who I really was.
Then, in the organization where I lived, with my colleagues, I always asked: who are we really?
And at this point, with customers and suppliers and all the stakeholders, I asked: Who are we really?
Who are we - even more, what is the meaning of our work?
Why do we have to work together?
Why do I have to supply you apples?
The interesting thing is that, thinking in these terms, even very large corporations understand and say: "Oh yes, we are doing something important"
So we bet this money, as if it were a bet, and declare an interdependence between the two parties.
So we declare that these two parties are interdependent on each other to build, or return, a future to someone who will come.
For what I told you.
And so the important point is to be able to establish through an agreeable language - and we are in 2019 we have it, we have the ESG, we have the SDGs, the goals, the sub-goals, the targets, we have the world of Benefit Corporations.
We legally have a legal structure like Srl, Sb, Spa, Sp, which allows me to say that I can follow an evolutionary purpose,
let's define ourselves for how we want impact in the world and let's start.
An example?
We could entrust us with a client, maybe an insurance company or a bank, and say: "look, I see that you produce a lot of paper for your office works. Try reduce it.
If you reduce it, I will bet this money with you on you being able to consume less paper"
Reducing paper, mostly, today means cutting less trees, it means impacting less.
Well, I don't see trees clearly here, but if I think of this future I have to give back, maybe one day I will see them too, the trees I have sawn.
And think how interesting it gets if we add the legal ingredient and I say, dear customer or dear supplier, I can do the same thing with a supplier, with a supplier I can create a - I can go to ask to make his employees happier.
You can measure happiness, it is called psychometry.
So I can do weekly surveys to measure happiness.
I can add these clauses, these discounts, called conditional rebates.
Conditional rebates are a broken rule, and I can go, apply these conditioned rebates which says: dear customer, dear supplier, if that doesn't apply, if you don't do actions that make your people happier, where you cut, use less paper because I send you the file via PDF, I have to ask you back that money because we lost the bet.
And what will I do with it?
In an absolutely clear way, because we are all serving a purpose, I will use them to fill up, to give back what we are depriving as a future of those who will come.
We call this interdependence agreement in which all together we recognize through a contract that we are parts of the same system.
With this, what I want to tell you is that with our actions, if we continue to live by the rules and we don't try to question the rules sometimes, I don't say to violate, but I simply say to rewrite them, to digest them - we have a very great, the stomach is a great digestive system and we can really be great digesters.
So if we don't begin to see these rules, if we don't begin to understand what the impact is - and to scale the impact we produce, we won't do something effective for the future.
And the last message I want to leave you is that each of us, through his actions, produces impact and draws in his life.
The impact can be positive, neutral or neutralizing and negative for me.
The choice is up to us.
But every time, the moment I walk one step forward, I produce that impact.
Only by finding ourselves, only by finding the way in which we act, we can paint the world we want.
We are all painting.
Namasté.
(Applause)
There's a book, written by the journalist and writer Caitlin Moran, that hit the shelves a few years ago, called "How To Be A Women". [Translates into Italian]
Simeone de Beauvoir already said that in the 1940s: You aren't born a woman.
You become a woman.
Being a woman is not a pre-existing condition, it's not something you just happen to be.
It is a process actually, with a series of mostly mandatory steps.
Woman, long before a "what", is a "how".
Among all the difficult things that you can do as a woman, the thing I'm doing right now is one of the most difficult, ever: speaking for like 10, 12 minutes without being interrupted - tipically by a man - it's a sort of exceptional event.
(Laughter) (Applause) And when you point this out, a man usually interrupts you saying that that's not true.
I was born in 1972.
Back then, pregnancy was a crapshoot. My parents found out I was a girl the very moment I was born, not a minute earlier.
So they didn't have four, five months to project their fantasies on how I would be.
I was a very slim, bald and a baby girl!
And now I am a cisgender woman.
If you perceive a gender like a hill to climb - it's not accurate, but bear with me - I am already on the hillside I belong to.
I'm already here, I'm "cis".
Anyway, you are born a girl - someone feels it more than others.
And you spend most of your life trying to learn how to become a woman.
It's about addition and subtraction.
Mostly subtraction actually, because in order to become a Woman - with a capital W, like in Carmen Consoli's song, you need to learn how to "be less": less noisy, less messy, less exuberant, less mindless.
And if you aim to be considered a "Lady" this is brought to the extreme.
A real lady is elegant, and elegance is minimalism, sobriety, sacrifice.
Being a woman means, mostly, learning how to take as less space as possible.
So being skinny has nothing to do with beauty, as fatphobics say.
Forget about it.
People don't give a damn about other people's health.
The point is that women's body is conceived as something mostly meant to be looked at,
and if the watcher's eye isn't satisfied, then that body needs to be concealed.
So we moved from the witch hunt of the past, with a sort of an epic taste, to a present where we say, "You don't really want to see flabby arms on a sleeveless dress."
Skinnier, prettier and well groomed are the only three additions women are allowed to.
Every girl's brain is hammered with the same concept: nobody cares what you think.
A girl's thoughts are always negligible, irrelevant.
Nobody really cares what girls really think, want or wish for.
Conversely, when girls stand up and take the stage, they're attacked!
Think about Greta Thunberg, think about Malala.
And Malala, you may recall, survived a shot in the head.
Now, make no mistake: we address a lot of concerns to girls!
We worry about how they dress, how they speak, what they eat, whom they date.
We're keen on telling them they should be careful!
That they shouldn't go out at night, and they should carefully choose whom they go out with.
So a very high wall is built around women, of small and big limitations to their personal freedom: because "It's a nasty world out there!"
The one thing we're not worried about is to make sure the boys don't represent that very girls' nasty world.
(Applause) Somehow, from behind this wall, we should learn how to be leaders.
And if we fail and surrender our leadership to men, then we're told it's our fault, that we do not lean in, that we are not brave enough, that we don't want to stand out, that we'd rather stay behind the scenes.
And this is where feminism steps in.
Actually, feminism is much more than that: there's no facet of life where feminism couldn't step in and rewrite, upholster, rebuild everything.
Feminism, for those who missed the last episodes, was born at the end of the 18th century with the pamphlet of Mary Wollstonecraft, called "A Vindication of the Rights of Woman". Back then, women meant nothing: they didn't own anything, they could not study, they could not work, they could not vote.
Wollstonecraft is among the first women to claim for the right of women to be human, and to exist also outside of their homes.
From there on, the feminist theory has evolved, and came up with highly sophisticated work.
It shifted focus!
We are still defending rights - different rights, these days - but it's not dealing exclusively with protecting our right to exist in a world where we don't write the rules. Feminism now deals with rewriting the rules, making them more balanced and liberating for all.
Feminism doesn't create an anarchic world; it creates a world where the struggle for power is replaced by the sharing of knowledge and of resources.
Cooperation over competition; solidarity over dominance.
Who wouldn't be happy with that?
Well, a lot of people, as far as I can see.
(Laughs) Being woman leaders, leading something, leading parties, political movements or professional teams is a complex issue,
and it's even more so if you are surrounded by the walls they built for you.
You can't be a leader behind a wall.
You're not born as a leader. You become a leader.
Leadership is made of responsibility, competence, ability to take risks, firmness, courage, endurance.
A feminist leader is not necessarily defined by her ability to stand out, but for her ability to cooperate and help others become leaders.
Feminism doesn't take up space. It's a multiplication of spaces, of voices, of perspectives.
And the more you climb the prestige ladder, according to statistics, the stronger the male dominance is.
All the aforementioned features of a good feminist leader are usually swept away before leadership as we know it, which is usually competitive, pyramidal, assertive:
only one man in command, like Captain Ahab on the Pequod's deck.
And we all know what happened to Achab and the whole Pequod, don't we?
The leadership as we know is fragile.
It's based on the cult of personality, more than on creating and sharing ideas.
It's a leadership that men contend, share and pass on in exclusive circles where women are only admitted if they're able to replicate exactly the same dynamics of subjugation and dominion.
(Applause) This happens everywhere at work.
The higher on the ladder, the more money involved, the harder it is for women to make room in a society that favors aggressiveness and assertivity even when there are no adequate skills.
Sure, women can be terrible too. The saying goes, "Women are women's worst enemy", because at first you're tought that only bad people can make a career in life, then they punish you, because "A woman shouldn't behave like this".
A woman with capital W is kind, understanding, thoughtful, welcoming.
What is called firmness and character in a man, in a woman becomes... "Being a bitch."
(Applause) Actually, (Applause) Bitch with capital B.
(Applause) Being a woman, and being a leader, means being able to overcome a great fear: being able to speak out loud, instead of whispering in yet another man's ears.
You know, "behind a great man
there's a great woman." Now I'd like to share an anecdote with you.
For a couple of years now I've been talking about feminism and gender related topics in public venues - and sometimes, it gets very public. It happened that together with my feminist discussion group we launched #quellavoltache [#thattimewhen] that was basically a starting point for discussing sexual harassment in Italy, starting on the web.
I committed to talk about it on newspapers, TV, radio - wherever needed.
And this is a daunting challenge. One must be very careful not to flinch the focus away. If you speak feminism in public, people would expect you to jump as a circus seal when they throw fishes at you, screaming: "Where are all the feminists?"
Women should assumingly take a subordinate role. Either they say what you want, or they're not worthy their credentials.
And in order to do so, I had to overcome some of my fears.
The first one was saying silly things in public. The second one was to come up as a feminist without the support of other feminists.
And then the third fear was, people might think I was right, which would give me credit
and put me in a pivotal position, with all the responsibility
that comes with it. So I was in a school meeting, talking about feminism as usual.
At the end of the meeting, one of the teachers came up to me and he said, "The way you speak is so man-like."
(Laughter). I'm not sure what sort of face I put up; I never know, when someone praises me using men as a benchmark. But since I'm a woman, they educated me to be kind, I didn't really want to chew and spit that little man on the spot.
I also understood, he was genuinely trying to be nice to me.
So basically the thing is, you spend most of your life trying to become a women: but in reality, what really counts when you're out there, is trying to be as man-like as possible.
(Applause) Now, I'm 46 and it took me at least 30 years trying to become a woman who sets her own conditions.
But there's one thing that I did learn to do. I am no longer afraid of my voice.
(Applause) Thank you!
Hello everyone! I guess I have
to say something pleasant.
This morning, on my way to Bologna from Rome with a group of friends, I had the chance to read a newspaper. The first bit of good news was on page 19.
Mass media constantly bombards us with negative news, everyday, all day long.
This builds a kind of blanket through which it's hard to see what's already going on.
Tesla Motors has invented a simply better car.
100% electric, with no compromise in quality and performance: 500km range, 0-100 km/h in slightly more than 4 seconds.
They're building a network of recharging stations, called Superchargers, all over the world where Tesla owners can recharge for free.
In 10 years, Tesla's market capitalization has overtaken that of Fiat Chrysler.
On this wall of their factory in California, they display all their flags and patents.
They number 136.
We usually see on walls like this a demonstration of the innovation and R&amp;D capability of a company.
Well, a few weeks ago, founder and CEO of Tesla Motors, the genius Elon Musk, wrote a letter to Tesla shareholders, saying: Folks, if I want to be the change I seek, I must open-source all these patents.
My competitors should be able to access Tesla technology, if I want humanity itself to choose its means of transport, of sustainable travel.
It was Friday night.
Monday morning, this was the result: up 10% on the markets, up 30% since then.
If you think about it... it’s as if a totem pole of the current economic paradigm - research, patent and defend your patent - had fallen for Tesla.
And it fell to some extent thanks to the markets, which themselves constructed this totem pole, and probably defend it.
Several days ago, the Rockefeller Foundation - one of the largest private foundations in the world - announced that within 5 years it will dump all investments related to fossil energy, coal, oil.
They decided to join a campaign that will divest a total of $50 billion within the next five years.
Rockefeller, the dynasty most closely linked to oil in the world, decides to move on beyond that same past which is the root of its success.
This is fantastic news.
Indeed, I ask you to applaud the fact that $50bn will be invested in another direction!
(Applause) A few days ago I went to Rome's 'Maker Faire': young people, small start-ups and businesses 'that do', that create.
It is part of those emerging philosophies of innovation, such as do-it-yourself, frugal innovation, or, last one, jugaad.
For these philosophies, innovation is an art.
The art of overcoming structural constraints under difficult circumstances, improvising effective solutions with limited use of resources.
The other good news is that it is particularly true in those contexts where there is flexibility, creativity and limited resources.
Three conditions Italy may well recognize.
Indeed, Rome's Maker Faire in early October was an amazing success: 90,000 attendees, which is something
when you think the New York record is only 37,000.
Moreover, there were hundreds of start-up companies and young makers innovating in robotics, drones, 3D printers and more. One thing I've discovered is that Italy has the second largest number of manufacturers of 3D printers. But beyond that, there were companies
that develop new ideas with 3D printing.
There's another thing I'd like to share.
These are my daughters, one of them wearing a brace for a broken arm.
I went to the local pharmacy to buy a brace for her; € 50 or so, and I could choose between three sizes - A group of guys connected with the Politecnico Di Milano make a perfect-fitting bespoke brace using 3D-printing, which only costs a few Euro cents.
I was, wow, just a scan of your arm and you have a personal brace.
'Even better,' they replied, 'we have printed in 3D an object of a material, which, when soaked in hot water, becomes more malleable and can be put on your arm to fit exactly.'
No need to scan, which might be a problem.
Just a few cents.
And this is an example of what you can do with a 3D printer that an Italian startup has created.
Stumbling on this, I must confess that despite being an engineer - in my past life - I remain passionate for the things that can change the way people think.
This is something that really struck me.
A very recent research from Accenture shows how today people think that the business community is as responsible for improving their lives as governments are.
The same percentage for governments and businesses:
Some companies have already got it, and that's great. These company are called For-Benefit Corporations,
and are able and willing to respond to this type of call.
They're often referred to as just B-Corps.
According to Robert Shiller, Nobel Prize winner for Economics 2013, B-Corps are businesses that have overcome the dichotomy between non-profit and for-profit in a progressively explosive mix, and have better results than all others.
He's the only economist in the world who predicted the crisis of 2008,
so he probably knows
what he’s talking about! B-Corps are, to explain them quickly, businesses that all have multiple aims: In addition to maximizing profit, their mission includes the objective of making a positive difference to people and environment.
The first point therefore: not only profit. Certainly for profit, but more besides.
Second, accountability: It’s not only about shareholders anymore.
For-profit companies are only mandated to maximize ROI for investors.
B-Corps replace shareholder with stakeholder.
They take into account all their stakeholders: employees, communities and suppliers.
Third, transparency: It is not considered mere duty anymore, but real value.
I will give you a few examples of B-Corps now.
This first one really struck me.
In developed countries, we never face such issues -
Nearly 1 billion people worldwide have no access to spectacles.
15% of the Earth's population struggles to work, take care of their children, cook, do all the things we do on a regular basis, with problems with their sight.
A pair of glasses is estimated to increase productivity by 35% and monthly income by 20%.
There's one company, founded in 2010 in New York, that is trying, besides making spectacles and sunglasses, to address this issue.
For every pair of glasses sold, another is given to someone in need of them in a developing country.
In 4 years, more than 1 million glasses have been donated, in addition to another million sold.
Moreover, the number
for the last year was 500,000, proof of fast, exponential success.
This is a real company, and it is a for-benefit corporation. The economic impact of these 1 million eyeglasses has been calculated at over 200 million dollars! Let's now come back to Italy,
with another example.
An Italian company recently
celebrated its 100th anniversary.
Only a few companies last 100 years,
and they represent only 0.036% of the active companies in Italy.
But the goal of this one is even more interesting, They plan to exist for another 100 years.
What must I do today to survive for another 100 years?
One of the key points in this company’s strategy has been to work together with their suppliers.
It is based on the rule that a company only thrives if its suppliers also thrive.
Thus, in working with them, if the suppliers evolve the company too will benefit from this evolution.
It is an Italian olive oil company,
and you won't find their brand on shopping malls because their business model is based on direct sales. They deliver oil and other products directly to your home.
How did they go on with their suppliers?
They got them together, dozens of companies, hundreds of people, often small farmers, and they involved them in participative models, unthinkable for a farmer: Sit around the table, imagine and write and define what you want to be, in order to define a new quality standard.
This company already has an above average quality level in terms of health properties and taste experience, but nonetheless posed themselves questions such as these: What can we say about the organizational methods of my suppliers?
What can we say about the energy they use and we use?
How much and of what kind?
What can we say about water or waste output?
Together with their suppliers, they're defining a new quality standard for which other olive oil producers will have to strive in the future.
This is an Italian for-benefit company.
Perhaps you’ve seen this advertisement.
Few companies in the world can say, 'Don't buy my product.'
It seems almost a paradox, given that a company aims to make a profit.
In this case, for this California-based company, this is not a paradox, since at the same time as they make longer lasting products, they have one of the most efficient repair services in the world, and also one of the most efficient end-of-life recovery systems in the world,
in order to give products a second life.
And it is a for-benefit company.
Let's return to Italy, bouncing back from the United States. First a question: Who's seen the film 'Gravity'?
'Gravity' is about a specific and possible problem: space junk.
Two satellites might collide and their debris could trigger a sort of avalanche, hitting other satellites, leading to the complete destruction of all communication systems we use habitually: GPS, navigation systems - we couldn't watch 'The X Factor' anymore!
What's more, since we had astronaut Luca Parmitano on the stage, it would create a blanket of waste around the Earth which would make space exploration impossible for many generations.
This slide shows you the number of satellites currently in orbit around the Earth.
I discovered there were 3,600, but only 1,000 of them are working.
70% of them wander around
the Earth uncontrolled.
When we think of the problem of garbage in our streets, well, humanity is doing the same thing in space.
70% of these objects wander uncontrolled.
There is an Italian company that decided to take sustainability principles out into space.
They asked, "What can I do to address this problem,
to reduce the systematic increase in the concentration of these objects and the risk of the situation of concern?
Well, an Italian company, acclaimed worldwide,
produce smart engines that are installed on satellites to allow a safe and controlled return of satellites which might otherwise cause damage.
Current satellites don't have such system.
This is an Italian company, and it is a for-benefit company.
How do you emerge as a for-benefit corporation?
I'm not talking about companies not yet started.
Yes, a company may start up with this ethic, they already exist.
In United States they have created a legal status.
A new company can choose to be a for-profit, a non-profit, or choose a third way - for-benefit.
It's been the case for a few years.
27 American states already have the law; 19 states are working on it.
In Canada a federal law is going to introduce the for-benefit concept.
Brussels is also talking about it.
Right now my partner Eric is working at the Leopolda in Florence talking to members of the government.
So something is happening in Europe too.
While waiting for it to become a legal form in countries beyond North America, certification is available.
The B Lab is a non-profit organization that promotes this concept and supports its acknowledgment into the civil codes, also helps companies to understand if they’re moving in a direction which is, as we say, future proof.
Any manager or entrepreneur
can go online to B Lab's website.
There there is a tough questionnaire to assess the impact of one's organization on the matters of concern.
This is a typical final report, quite brief, that covers the assessed areas: governance, workers, community, suppliers and environment.
A minimum of 80 points must be scored to be classified as a for-benefit. With results below this score, improvements must be worked for.
Many companies that we have met in the last few months did not get 80 points.
Some of them are working to improve, while others have discovered that some choices made in the past have left them at a point of no return.
But the good news is that there are
many for-benefit companies. There might never be a billion, but there are already many.
More than 1,100 have been certified around the world in the last few months, while there were only 600 a year ago.
As this concept spreads, more companies will attempt the assessment.
At this moment in Italy we have six for-benefit companies. Around 100 other companies are undergoing the certification process.
These are the first Italian companies certified as for-benefit.
Earlier, we talked about several for-benefit corporations. Let me share their names with you now.
The first is Warby Parker - look at their websites to learn more; the second is Fratelli Carli in Imperia; the third is Patagonia, here you can see the founder; the fourth is D-Orbit.
I'll conclude with my company, Nativa.
We provide Future Proof Design, helping companies to become future-proof.
And I’d like to tell you a little anecdote from our story.
Inspired by these concepts while founding Nativa, we wanted to put them into the statute.
The Chamber of Commerce of Milan rejected it four times.
I don't know if there are any entrepreneurs here, and something similar has happened to them.
It's not my first company and it's usually a mere formality.
Why so, then? Because we were introducing some innovations that the officer at the Chamber of Commerce couldn't understand.
But eventually, after our umpteenth visit to their offices, we understood what the real obstacle was:
The first line of our company objects included the word 'well-being'.
The officer in charge had never seen that word before, apparently. Yes, 'well-being': we wanted Nativa's business to be driven by that final goal.
I conclude, therefore, that all the experiences of this for-benefit probably revolve around that very word. For-benefits seek profit but at the same time want to impact favorably with the environment and people.
And ultimately, this could lead to maximizing well-being.
Thanks.
(Applause)
I deal with geopolitics.
I will not bore you with treaties on what geopolitics is.
Fortunately, or unfortunately for us, it is a much-discussed topic today: everybody deals with it, talks about it,
yet it remains a rather esoteric discipline.
We need to point out, before I go on with my presentation, that by "geopolitics" we mean is the study of conflicts in a bordered geographical space.
Put in even simpler terms, geopolitics is not the study of international relations as such.
But it helps to identify the constraints forcing international players to act in specific ways.
It's a dismal discipline par excellence, as you can imagine, as it focuses on duties.
Today, with this definition in mind, I will talk to you about globalization.
We will try to understand what globalization is, another big issue that is more or less popular.
Some love it - not so many; some others hate it - way more.
We will try to understand what it is, what its strategic substrate is, and then which phase we are in, and how long it could last. And above all, whether it is a new phenomenon or not.
On a geopolitical level, what you see behind me - I am consistent with what I have just told you: geopolitics is based on territory, space.
Everything we discuss must be associated with a map.
These are maps from "Limes", the geopolitical magazine I work for.
This map in particular features a pompous-sounding term: "American Empire".
In geopolitics, the very word "Empire" does not come with a judgement. It exists as such,
often against the will of its founders. Americans probably didn't even mean to build an empire.
Yet they have got one, they built one. And as it is always the case, they will keep it and die as an empire.
Who knows when: as late as possible, we wish them.
But what this map shows us - what we are most interested in - is globalization.
This is what globalization is about.
If we asked - it is quite common in Italy - to people who are not familiar with strategic issues, to comment on a specific strategic issue such as globalization - economists, in particular, who are all the rage in Italy - they will tell you that globalization is that situation, that condition where trade takes place in a single global market.
If we "dress" this assumption with an ideological explanation, we could add that they do so because they get on well with each other or because there is a human predisposition to trade.
The problem with such statement is the lack of the cause-effect principle.
How does globalisation come about?
Why does it exist? How long will it last?
What you see on this map
is the division of the planisphere into U.S. Navy fleets.
Or how the Pentagon, long story short, looks at the planisphere and how it divides it by the competence of its fleets.
Because - in a nutshell - the globalization we are experiencing is nothing more than the control over seas and oceans by the United States of America.
That is it!
This is the core of globalization.
Why this?
Because today, so in 2019, not 100 years ago, 90 percent of the goods shipped across the planet travel by sea.
And clearly, those who control the seas where 90 percent of the goods travel, so basically all of them, can create a single global market.
The Americans did this at the end of the Cold War, when they ended up being, following the implosion - or suicide, you choose - of the Soviet Union the only superpower on Earth. They found themselves controlling all the seas, and thought it fit to create a single global market.
Let’s take a step back.
Another prejudice on globalization, if we analyze it in a non-strategic, non-geopolitical way, is that it is an unprecedented phenomenon.
We are human beings: social networks actually attest we think we live extraordinary lives.
Otherwise, why take a snapshot of our dish of pasta, or share who we fell in love with, as if it was something special?
Likewise, we are convinced that globalization too is exceptional: it must be like this, who has seen it before?
Actually, at least a couple of globalizations took place before the current one:
a technical one, which was the first one, the "Pax Romana", the original globalization per excellence.
Back then, the Mediterranean sea was by far the most important sea on the planet; so controlling it was enough to create a globalization.
When Romans defeated Carthaginians, at the end of the Third Punic War, and gained control of the whole Mediterranean Sea, they created an effective globalization, in a technical sense.
That is, they became rulers of the maritime trading routes; and through them they created and managed a single market.
After Romans, there came the British.
The first instance of globalization in strictly geographical terms, is the British one: "Pax Britannica" in Latin.
Not by chance, globalizations are defined using Latin words: "Pax Romana", "Pax Britannica", "Pax Americana".
This indicates continuity, exactly the opposite of the innovation we think is taking place.
Back then, the British, by supervising all the seas and oceans with their exceptional navy, created the history's first globalization.
We're in late 19th century, the so called Britain's Victorian Age.
These blue lines you can see on the map of the current globalization are nothing but the main maritime routes of the planet, because the goods of course, do not scatter across the world randomly.
Cutting costs is a pressing need: the less we sail, the less we spend and the earlier we arrive. As a result, there are sea routes that are mandatory and marked, which are those you see in blue.
These are the sea routes controlled by the superpower, all of them.
In a world that - this also makes us human - seems very chaotic. A world which seems extremely dangerous to us - and it’s hard to understand why.
Two or three generations ago, our grand, great-grandparents would gladly accept - those alive today would still accept - to experience this condition, even if it looks very frightening.
It is placid, instead, especially at our latitudes.
But something that just doesn’t change - in case we were under the impression that today's world is drastically changing - is the US control over the seas and the oceans.
This has been unchallenged for several decades now, due to their command over those blue sea routes.
In particular, look at the circles, namely the straits, the isthmuses of the world: what does it mean for a superpower to controls all the seas and the oceans, technically speaking?
No one can realistically have control over the seas and oceans square kilometre by square kilometre: it is impossible.
It means instead exercising control over those binding, inescapable passages like the straits, isthmuses of the planet, where ships are forced to go through: the bottlenecks.
Suez, Gibraltar, Bab-el-Mandeb, Malacca, Panama.
We can name countless: Hormuz... All those straits you're forced to go through.
And given the power they wield, United States can prevent the passage of any fleet on the planet, be it commercial or military; each and anyone of them.
US unfettered maritime power is still unassailable today, because the United States, as in any other globalization of history, like Romans and British, are a "thalassocracy".
In other words, they are a mostly maritime power.
They gaze at the world from the sea.
Not only because - in a very violent way, like every great power - they founded their own island by exterminating or defeating nearby populations.
In fact, they currently live on a North American island, they can only be reached from the sea, so their perception has always been a maritime one; they also judge others by their ability to sail the open sea.
Why is it so essential, in any absolute domain that then leads to globalization, an absolute control of the sea?
Because it's the quintessential strategic advantage.
First of all, it is easy to understand, maritime infrastructures exist as such: they don’t have to be built, they cost nothing.
The sea is already there: you "just" need to learn how to sail it,
and it’s not that easy. On average, you may die of fear, especially at the beginning, but the infrastructure already exists.
In strict military terms, controlling the seas buys you the ability to neutralize any coastal enemy.
Preventing them from leaving home.
Forcing them to leave the sea out of terror.
Being terrified of what may happen at sea is common to all great civilizations and all major powers, and not only in the early stages. But often also dictates their fate.
The British, as we said, created the globalization immediately prior to the present one, which came to an end with the First World War.
Terraneous as they were, they deliberately became a maritime power.
They do so in 16th century, at the cost of inhuman efforts, because the transition from land to sea power is in itself inhumane, almost cruel.
Back then, the British - or better the English - who built their maritime power, took to the seas out of fear of being attacked, once again, by enemies from the European continent.
The attacks against England from the European continent have been a constant feature in English history, to the point of distorting grammar itself; the Norman invasion of England transformed English grammar.
In some cases, it has even led the English people to place the adjective after the noun: in English, "General Secretary" became "Secretary General" because the Normans arrived by sea and imposed French usage on English grammar.
When grammar is turned upside down, cruelty peaks.
So those who control maritime routes can both create a global market, as the Americans did, and dominate the planet because they wield the tool that proved to be the most efficient - and evil - one can count on today, for all our talk of trifles such as financial hubs, telecommunications hubs and the like.
These are the critical issues, for the existence of our planet: they're sound, and they travel on water.
What we see here is an attempt of counterglobalization, the current attempt at anti-globalization.
So let's delve into it: how long will this globalization last?
Where do we stand?
A small premise: globalization has nothing to do with free trade.
Not only because free trade does not exist, and never did; it is pure ideology, all the countries of the planet impose duties on something or against someone.
Every single one of them.
The system's pivots apply them the least - the US, in the case at hand, just like Romans or British did in the past. In order to make others dependant, these countries massively import, so they cause others to depend on them; and then, physically, they hand their own currency over to them.
Someone is bound to object: this someone, as of now, is China.
Chine is neither a maritime power nor a thalassocracy but it is trying to defy the United States by promoting a counterglobalization known as the "Silk Road".
China is well aware of how things stand, and since it exports a good share of its GDP and does it for 90 percent of its goods on routes controlled by the Americans, you can well understand, it is under Americans' thumb.
One may well be thinking, China has launched an offensive against US - especially in Italy, where strategy isn't our strongest subject -
yet sea inexorably tells us another story and provides us with a map that lets us understand what power relationships are really like.
Chinese aren't threatening Americans in the Gulf of Mexico; Americans are preventing Chinese to get to Taiwan, actually.
That's the difference between them. By building the corridors of the "Silk Road", China tries to deals with US globalization: and as you can see, its strategy is essentially land-based.
China plans to build land-based structures from its coasts to Germany through countries that are not exactly at the forefront in terms of economic and civil development. in central Asia. As it can’t control the seas, it is trying to go via land.
These are power balances.
Then there is also a maritime side of Chinese "Silk Road" strategy, born of utilitarian considerations: they don't openly challenge the American maritime power, because it can't be defeated today, and one can measure through that how long will globalisation last. Well, I’m nearing the end.
Even when it envisages a maritime dimension in its counterglobalization drive, China does so by considering to build ports in the Indian Ocean so as to reach Europe, more specifically Mediterranean shores, where it already bought the port of Piraeus in Athens, and is seriously considering buying the port of Trieste, in Italy. Not being in a position to freely sail
the sea, they "step across it".
Like when you are on the beach, the sand is hot and you step from an umbrella's shade to the next one.
China tries to do something similar by building ports they can step on, without sailing the open seas and incurring American retaliation,
which may be meted out even so.
So at which point is globalization?
Globalization is not dying out because Trump imposes duties on others.
Globalization is not free trade: it is the control of shipping routes by a single power. It will only come to an end when Americans lose the ability, or the strength, to control all seas and oceans, as I have explained to you previously.
Since we had already spoken of China - which remains a land-based country, although it is rapidly building up its fleet - when China manages to impede American the control of at least a coastal sea.
When China "balkanizes" a part of globalization.
At that time, globalization as we know it nowadays will end, will turn into something else. It may become a 75 percent, or a half globalization; or the United States will simply implode, thus letting the world return to the pre-globalization phase, the one where seas and functions were shared out among different paxes.
What we need to remember is that when dealing with globalization, we should avoid being sidetracked by ideological issues on free trade and the like, and take instead a look at what is happening on the seas. Seas will enable us assess where we stand in terms of globalization and planetary domination.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Thank you
(Applause)
Thank you Good morning everybody
Today I have the task to talk about time in a logic of sustainability and so I'm talking about cork
I'm talking about something we all know but probably not enough, to make a comparison on what the concept of sustainable development means.
Cork has been existing long before man just think that cork is a heritage of the Mediterranean and is an oak of the durmast family that developed this bark to be protected from fire because in this area of the world, millions of years ago, there was fire.
And this bark made of air is so powerful that prevents the tree from burning
So, it is a thousand-year old concept of time a concept of time facing the speed of today.
Just think that cork is a heritage of the Mediterranean because we find it especially in the Western basin of the Mediterranean and in the Iberian Peninsula, including my country that is the leading producer of cork.
I want to oppose it to the time of speed, to the time of “everything is fast” because if you do not do things you miss an opportunity that will never come back.
But there is a world, a parallel world, what I call a “slow” world, where clearly nature has its own times
Consider that this heritage is one of the 35 biodiversity sanctuaries in the world
It takes 25 years for a cork tree to become adult and every nine years it is decorticated, as a sheep is sheared.
A cork stopper succeeds in becoming a cork stopper after 45 years therefore this tree vegetates among us for 250 years
It is an extremely sustainable tree because it helps adjust the hydrogeological cycle it prevents social and environmental desertification employing more than 100,000 people it is renewable and above all we do not need to throw the trees down to produce cork stoppers
It also absorbs 112 grams of CO2, it is a unique matter.
Just think that there are 800 million cells in a cubic centimeter
of a cork stopper The cork stopper, which is the main element to close a bottle of wine gives the wine itself elegance, prestige, and a classic value.
We are talking about something that deals with time because time is an extremely important value for us
It is an extremely important value because being sustainable is an extremely important value, sustainability does not have to be understood just as environmental sustainability is a broader concept.
According to the United Nations declaration of 1987 being sustainable means having the ability to meet one's needs without compromising the needs of future generations
Just think that, as human beings, we must leave our children at least the world we inherited from our parents.
Therefore, sustainable development is not only environment.
Sustainable development is also economic development economic sustainability and social sustainability what I want to talk about today
So, what's the challenge?
The challenge is how to sell “slow” in times of “fast.”
This is the challenge I face every day with all my managers, with all my collaborators how to sell “slow” in times of “fast.”
Therefore, culture becomes fundamental because it will be able to spread the values of sustainability
only through the spread of this culture I will succeed in instilling the value of what I propose in the head of people dealing with us.
So, who is our first target?
Clearly you, the consumers.
My first goal is to inform consumers.
Today customers are changing today the great trend of consumption that is, the most interesting segment for those who sell, is the millennials
Millennials are those people who have become adult between two millennia so I think most of you
This being said, how does a millennial think?
What is the focus he/she is looking for when he/she has to buy something?
The millennial looks especially for just causes.
Every product should incorporate this just cause in some way,
so we have decided to communicate this just cause in the cork stoppers, to be able to give these cork stoppers the intrinsic value from the viewpoint of sustainability so someone who uncorks a bottle understands that it has a value and it is important to defend that value.
This aspect becomes fundamental to distinguish us from other products that on the contrary, have an artificial origin.
And then, the media: we have been managing relationships with the media for ten years and probably we are also a kind of “case study” in how to deal with them because media are a fundamental means for us so I'm talking about television, about radio, about print media about bloggers, about internet, social network, all media become fundamental to spread this message.
When I make a journalist, or a blogger, see this matter with their own eyes they fall so in love that they too have the pleasure of defending a just cause and they start to see this product with completely different eyes
Then this turns into communication what gets to each of you because this is our task – to create culture.
And what is the result?
The result is clearly that a journalist or a customer who understands this thing becomes our ambassador therefore a person who will spread this culture and more culture means more value
However, this alone is not enough because this is the axis of environmental sustainability but as I said before, environmental sustainability is not only environment
Sustainability is also economic and social sustainability.
When we talk about economics, when we talk about results, companies do not exist by chance companies exist because there are people. People become fundamental that is, your team is critical to spread this value to try to promote this concept
So, our role is being talented managers
That is, the concept of “one man show”, “I order you and you obey me” no longer works
Actually, I think it never worked
What works today is being able to handle talent because people have enormous abilities
We must stimulate this ability we must be able, through what I call “delegation of responsibility”, to make these people understand that they are not a cat, they are a lion. We all have a lion inside us we just have to wake him up.
Then we are the generation of change things do not change by decree things change because we want them to change. That's why a manager must have the ability to let people out of their comfort zone
they have to exit from their sleeping area and, on the contrary, to constantly meet new challenges.
So, how do we do this?
How can we constantly make a person meet new challenges?
How can we train a collaborator?
I start from this, from the potential development analysis
Through a diagnosis I see the potentials how the ground of that person is built I go beyond an interview, a job interview, because that does not tell you anything it tells you especially how this person is built what is his/her psycho-aptitude profile.
And the result turns into a sort of histogram which allows you to understand exactly how this fertile ground is built.
This fertile soil has three macro-areas: the area of feeling, the area of energy and the area of relationship.
From the correlation between these areas I can locate how I can actually sow the right fruits to make something important grow in this fertile ground both for the person and my company.
It's a question of understanding what “muscles” should be strengthened what muscles are already perfectly enhanced and fit to the challenges, because only with the right team we can challenge the rules of market
Training becomes fundamental because once you've identified the people you need, you have to train them, and above all not only the collaborators of your company but also those who are on the market those who have to spread your value
Then what we are trying and I am trying to do is to develop the concept of being self-motivated.
Why? Because when a person
wakes up in the morning he/she must know what he/she should do, or where he/she should go he/she must know what is the value that he/she should pass to the market
Then I've been using this modern business model canvas for some time a very simple way of teaching how to do a business plan how to define what my journey is in some way.
But we can't look at the journey of this week, we have to look at a time space of at least three years because in three years I can clearly change something. We change every day but just a little bit.
The other fundamental thing is to develop the sense of belonging of people, therefore their motivation motivation to be part of the group motivation to dress the company's t-shirt and this becomes fundamental because in this way you'll have warriors fighting with you and you will realize that you can win only if you are together
This component of economic sustainability allows all your collaborators to be aligned with your project
And lastly, the social sustainability component
I am convinced that being sustainable also means being able to get involved for others to help the neighborhood in some way help the territory where you are integrated.
We've been doing it for years because a company is fed by a territory and the territory gives you the employees, it gives you the suppliers but beyond this beyond paying salaries, beyond paying bills you also have to leave something to this territory because it really deserves your support.
So, in 2011, I created the social face of my company it's called ethical project
You may say "What is this ethical
project? What is this thing?"
We have decided to collect used cork stoppers
Each of you definitely drink wine (particularly because we are in Veneto, Italy) and when you uncork a bottle you look at it you don't know where to put it
So we have thought “But why don't we recover and grind this cork stopper and convert it into an insulation material?”
You know that cork is the greatest natural insulator existing on Earth. Remember that it protects the tree from fire
So, we started this project, We collected more than 350 million cork stoppers from 2011 to today, in 6 years
They are not many, considering that around 800 million cork stoppers are thrown into the rubbish every year but we were able to intercept one small part of them and we have created a great movement because everyone wondered “But why can't we recover these cork stoppers?” Today we have more than 5000 collection points in Central and Northern Italy and even a few in Southern Italy.
We have more than a thousand volunteers working with us and help us recover cork stoppers
We recover cork stoppers, transport them with authorized means we grind them and then resell them to green building companies.
And what is the result we achieved?
First of all, we managed to donate more than 200 thousand Euros to charity and this is already an important milestone because we are helping many of the 40 non-profit organizations working with us but especially we managed to close the circle
A circle started in the forest with an extremely sustainable movement and respectful of the environment passing through the way we approach the market to the end of life of our products.
So, what would I do with an extra hour, or 40 minutes, per day?
I would try to make my passage through the planet Earth as sustainable as possible. Probably in doing so I would not need to go and look for water on Mars. Thank you.
(Applause)
What would happen, if you woke up tomorrow morning in a world without music?
You would’ve lost something beautiful, something that you like. But would it be simply a case of giving up something that you like, like eating pizza on Saturday evening? Or would you encounter a much more profound change?
Music is everywhere, it’s found in all cultures, in every corner of the world, because it’s what allows us to connect with each other.
It’s a relational glue, just think of what normally happens at concerts, and it’s the soundtrack to our lives and events.
Music seems like something we’ve always had inside us.
The strange object that you see behind me is an archaeological find, more precisely it’s a bone.
It’s the bone of a cave bear dating back to about 55,000 years ago.
What does it have to do with music?
Some scholars have focused on this bone, precisely on these holes; and have tried to reconstruct it, formulating a hypothesis that, today is much more appealing.
In fact, it could be the oldest musical instrument in history, subsequently nicknamed the "Neanderthal Flute".
It often happens, what we stumbled upon in the first steps of human evolution can also be found in the first steps of our personal evolution, as single individuals, in the evolution of our lives.
Neuroscience studies show us that we are natural born musical. Our brain as newborns, in the first hours of life, manage to specifically respond to music, musical structure, melody and rhythm, to music’s different emotional nuances.
Indeed, the presence of music in our lives seems to deal precisely with our brain and, in particular, with changes that occur in the oldest circuits at the evolutionary level, the deepest ones, even anatomically speaking.
A substance plays a key role here which is crucial for the regulation of our behaviour, every day.
This substance is dopamine.
Dopamine is a neurotransmitter that we usually release right in the oldest, deepest areas of our brain in response to stimuli such as food or sex.
They are stimuli that attract us, give us pleasure, that motivate us, reward us and are also somehow related to our survival.
However, what we have recently discovered is that dopamine is also released in response to music.
So let's see how many of you, at least once in life, listening to a song, a piece of music, have experienced this feeling here.
I’d say at least 90%.
These are chills, goosebumps they’re our body’s physiological or psychophysiological responses that we can link to intense pleasure.
Part of my research precisely focuses on this kind of phenomena, approaching them, however, in a rather particular way.
In fact, with colleagues based in Barcelona and Montreal, we directly activated and deactivated the mechanisms in the brain that we thought could account for these phenomena, and in particular the release of dopamine.
A pharmacological study allowed us to increase and decrease - temporarily, so don't worry - the release of dopamine in people's brains. We did this while our attendes were listening to music.
It could be their favourite music or pop-rock music we had chosen, music that is normally heard on the radio.
What we discovered is that when dopamine increased compared to when it decreased, their pleasure responses also increased.
Namely, the participants told us they'd rather hear a certain song and they also had more associated physiological responses: shivers, goosebumps.
Additionally, when the dopamine increased, what we call motivational answers did also increase.
In this case, the participants were willing to pay more to obtain the song they were listening to.
Namely, they were willing to give money to have that song, that music in their life.
So the pleasure and motivation linked to the release of dopamine are key to the understaning of the role of music in our life, or at least why it’s in our life.
However, the fundamental question remains: all of this cozy pleasure, so intense that it affects our lives positively, is an end in itself?
Namely, what is the true role of music in our lives?
To try to answer this, I suggest to keep exploring brain activations together, and I ask you, as much as you can, to try to imagine being here alone. Put on your headphones, your earphones, and start listening to one of your favourite songs.
What will happen is that your brain starts to switch on, creating a veritable cascade of activations concerning areas that are activated and regulate our emotions, our behaviour, as well as the areas that are involved in perception, movement, language, and memory.
Music creates a veritable neural symphony in our brain.
It activates and modulates it entirely.
In doing so, it’s able to modulate the anatomy and its functionality.
So now we can take a fundamental step forward.
Given that most of these neural substrates, most of these areas activated by music are actually areas that we activate every day to perform many other activities - hearing, reading, talking, walking -
then we can start thinking about using music to stimulate these other regions and then these other daily functions.
That's what have done today many psychology and neuroscience studies.
For example, studies on music's ability to stimulate areas involved in movement, and how this can be used in cases of movement deficit, such as Parkinson's disease.
Or studies focused on the close relationship between music and language: music is a veritable universal language, and we can use this relationship to improve, for example, dyslexic children’s reading skills.
Much research has been done and, as often happens in scientific research, there is still much to do and support.
Today I would like to talk to you about a portion of this research.
It’s something that particularly interests me but actually profoundly affects us all. It’s the relationship that exists between music and our memories.
But first, let that neural symphony activate in your brain thanks to the notes that Andrea will play.
[Music and memory] (Music) Music has a very strong - (Applause) Obviously this presentation wouldn’t have the same value without all of this.
I know it's an added value.
Music has a very strong evocative power. A song has literally the power to let us travel in time, because thanks to a song we can rediscover feelings, experiences, people, emotions we associate with that song.
Perhaps this is what happened to you now with this version of "Bohemian Rhapsody".
Judging by the applause, I’d say so.
Because it’s undoubtedly familiar to you.
Others recognised the song.
Fans, immediately, from the first notes, and others took a little longer.
Along with the recognition, some remembered the associated lyrics and others went beyond this and were able to associate this song with memories from their own lives.
We brought music's evocative power into the laboratory, aiming to understand what its base mechanisms were and, therefore, better understand what was behind it.
We ran several studies: on young people, elderly people, where we asked them to retain information, with or without music, while monitoring in the meanwhile their brain activities.
What we discovered is that music can really help us remember information better and in doing so, while it helps our memory processes, it modulates our brains.
Modulating those areas that we know are important to store and retrieve information, as well as areas that are involved in the expression of our emotions and, therefore, our pleasure.
Here we encounter our pleasure responses again.
Here they aren’t an end in themselves: they become relevant and fundamental. Because we found out, the better we are to activate our pleasure, reward, and motivation responses in reaction to music, the more chills we have, the more these responses can activate regions that play an important role in forming our memories. And consequently, the benefits of music on our memory will be greater.
Obviously, this has very important implications, especially if we consider cases of memory deficits, especially if we consider our society, which is experiencing an increase in ageing, both normal and pathological.
During my research I had the greatest opportunity to see Alzheimer patients who were completely extinguished by the illness, being able to recognise a song from their past and emerge from that apathy, even just for a second.
Music has the power, through these people’s emotions, to bring back some personal memories, that is, memories of their lives that seemed lost until a moment before, precisely because of the illness.
And in some cases, music also manages to facilitate the learning of new information.
This type of research lets us understand a little more about how we operate, how our brain works, our most complex, perhaps most fascinating organ.
I believe, these studies also teach us something.
In this case, they teach us that our emotional responses, emotive, pleasure, those we deem more instinctive, archaic, shallow, irrational, we can actually take them and use them to modulate, improve, and stimulate cognitive functions that instead we deem high and complex, such as learning and memory.
All neuroscience and music studies move in the same direction, stressing the importance of music in our lives.
In our lives means in our society.
Not only in our homes, in our headphones: but also in education, when we take our first steps in society; and in a clinical setting, when instead we deals with hardships on our journey.
Music is a powerful instrument, but this power is non-invasive, cheap and not only it can, it must be accessible to all.
So let's fill our lives with music, giving our brains the chance to profoundly change, transform itself, throughout our entire existence.
Let's give our brains the chance to change, which is fundamental for our cognitive functioning.
Let's listen to music, let’s make music, let's not miss out on even a second of that pleasure, of those shivers that it can give us.
Let’s release as much dopamine as we can.
But let's carefully choose the music we listen to today, because it could be the very music that will reactivate us tomorrow.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Claudio Ruatti: Guys, we're here together to introduce this man who became a friend right away, we already have something in common.
We like the water, we like the wind, the air, the good wine, the mountains. That's all!
Matteo Miceli --
A simple sailor, one who can't be stronger than the sea, but who can only get along with it.
Let's watch the video.
(Video) Journalist, a new adventure for the sailor Matteo Miceli.
The Roman yachtsman is going to sail right across the world with his Eco 40.
Narrator: The Atlantic Crossing World champion Matteo Miceli, who was born in Ostia, but is a citizen of the sea, is ready for a new adventure with his Eco 40, a boat which can reach 28 knots, built in six years by the sailor, with the greatest environmental regard.
Matteo Miceli: This adventure will take me right around the world from Rome to Rome, without layovers or any assistance, by myself.
It will take around 5-6 months.
I will leave from Rome, come out of Gibraltar, and head toward the South Pole.
I will circumnavigate it, through the Furious Fifties and Roaring Forties, and I'll come back to Rome, sailing across the Atlantic.
Cape Horn is behind my back, I passed it.
The sea's strength is unbelievable.
The distinctive trait of this passage is that I wind my clock back of 24 hours, so I live this day again.
Can you recognize me?
I'm not hanging upside down anymore, I passed by never-land, and I'm now going back home.
(Video ends) (Applause) Matteo Miceli: Great video, well done.
Claudio Ruatti: Thanks to the volunteers!
The merit is theirs. Dear Matteo: I'm a little excited, because you are someone who would be called here "a heart with legs" because you are very spontaneous.
Let''s start from the Shackleton story.
It is a cult, as a metaphor, for doing business, for team building, to never give up, to solve problems, and so on.
You could be a new model, because of your project, a 12-meter boat, named Eco 40, six years of technical arrangements, and quite a crazy idea: to circumnavigate the globe alone only you and your boat, in an eco-friendly way.
MM: It has been a real challenge, which lasted long, without any resources, right from the beginning.
The only resource was that of the team, of those who foresaw this project,
and it worked, with great efforts. Sailing around the world with no layovers, in complete independence; no water, no food, no energy, except that produced by the boat.
It therefore, has been a great challenge, a special adventure.
CR: Where did this idea come from?
Who inspired you?
Have you seen a film? Read a book?

MM: Shackleton has surely been one of my first inspirations; the resilience, will, strength to face the sea with great respect; he is undoubtedly a legend of mine, a reference point back in those days.
But above all, the will grow step by step.
I've accomplished so many other challenges.
We went through lots of experiences, many shipwrecks too, and that makes you dare.
CR: Often the sea is a striking life metaphor, isn't it?
You go up, you go down, you sink, and then find a beach.
MM: Sure.

CR: Yet, you decided to become a true lone wolf, to leave alone; what was the drive behind doing that?
MM: It wasn't actually a need, rather a development.
After I've tried in many voyages, with the crew, building a team, competing in regattas, - that really makes you deal directly with the energy, the nature, so it is like challenging oneself.
It was a great trial, which made me understand that without a reliable team you go nowhere.
CR: So you had real supporters. I guess not only technical but also psychological and moral ones.
I imagine your mother telling you, "Hey, where are you going?" MM: One day, we were doing a presentation, and my dad raised his hand, as he was about to ask something, instead he said, "No, no, four bypasses." As to say that he was not worried at all.
CR: Then, it comes the time to realize the great amount of work; after years of efforts, it's time to go.
What did you feel at that moment?
MM: You feel much, much... You feel pretty everything, really!
At the departure day you feel like a million bucks, you have a lot of worries, but there's also a lot of people
who supports you.
There were 200 boats, over 3.000 people, who came in spite of little advertising, just by word-of-mouth.
It was a great excitement to slip moorings, and this is a metaphor, too.
CR: Speaking about slipping, has the idea of giving in ever come to mind during the venture?
MM: A ton of problems have happened, and the team on the land was much more thoughtful than me.
They said, "Now the autopilot
is not working. Stop! Stop!"
Then, there was a trouble
with the bowsprit, a pole which holds a sail to the bow, while I was crossing the Atlantic.
Then the ship's wheels, a chicken of mine died; the fair haired. CR: Maybe you didn't know, but this man brought two hens with him and attempted to farm a vegetable garden during his...
MM: 146. CR: 146 days of expedition.
MM: A true experience of self-sufficiency, the energy was produced only by solar panels, windmills, and water turbines; it really was zero impact.
The energy also supplied a freezer for the fish I caught, it produced clean water from a desalination machine, while the hens and the garden compensated a little my diet.
CR: I imagine you were also talking with them, as it happened in "Cast Away" with the ball, "Tonight I'm inviting chickens, I'll have a dinner with them."
MM: This in an animal which really becomes like a pet; most of you had dogs and cats.
Chickens are exceptional.
They even make an egg every day. (Laughter) Something not all animals do.
This story makes me smile, but ancient sailors navigated with a little garden - a stern's part is still called to this day that way - and with animals, because there were no freeze-dries, there were no materials or other means to store so much food. CR: Talking of food, what sideboard did you have?
An enormous pantry?
MM: Essentially, I brought with me only food, water I produced during the sailing, and unfortunately, due to my scarce fishing in the Atlantic, and subsequent scarce supplies, every time I caught a fish and put it into the freezer, it lasted 10 days.
During my southern sailing - which lasted two months - from the Cape of Good Hope to Cape Horn, there were great winds, and big waves,
waves 15-meter high, maybe even higher than the theater, and fishing becomes difficult. (Laughter) CR: I think even floating is a difficult thing!
MM: Yes, but the problem wasn't that I couldn't hold the fishing rod, you see, the sea temperature was three degrees Celsius. There were icebergs and many threats, it is not easy, so I relied upon some freeze-dried food, which is now used among alpinists and other adventurers with much ease.
So, that was the emergency. CR: Like K-rations.
MM: Exactly, K-rations. CR: For those who still remember of the military service.
But in all that, I suppose, there was some fear, maybe. MM: Of not making it?
CR: Yes, of really not being able to make it, and thinking, "Ow, I guess that..." MM: Well, there was, in the end, you saw the video, I foundered. The ship didn't last. In the south, I hit a large cable left by another ship, and the keel started to move, it barely worked, until it got lost and an immediate overturning took place. At that point,
a series of procedures began, which have been studied by the team in detail, precisely not to improvise a shipwreck.
Even for that there is great preparation.
Everything worked out well, because, even today, communication is extremely important in case of a shipwreck.
Everything went just fine.
CR: Can we say that you have been a bit a victim of the garbage in the high seas?
How did you find open waters?
Are they polluted?
Let's talk about climate, now that we are facing this topic.
MM: I must say that I departed delighted, looking at the sea, a joy of cleanliness.
Then in the Atlantic, I had to climb on the top of the mast, and there I saw a lot of floating waste, which you're not able to see at the sea level, if it's not passing by.
One day, in the middle of the Indian Ocean - we are talking about the true South, where icebergs pass by and no ship navigates - at sunrise, I found a boat totally covered in tar.
This means that happens even in our seas, which is the largest share of our planet, and sadly we are eating from it.
CR: You've been a witness of this aspect, too.
If I'm not mistaken, you told me you were a mobile buoy.
MM: Yes, my university did a great job; I was the reference point for the Space Agency - and I want to thank them again - for every photo they took.
They were monitoring the icebergs during my sailing, and I was a reference point for the satellite photographs, which are not the pictures you see on Google Earth; those were X-ray photos, or "radar" photos, you could say.
I was a landmark to them, and it was a well-done job, I was given specific instrumentation so that I could be an oceanographic buoy during all my sailing, and this data is really valuable for the research.
CR: In essence, you did a kind of sea georeference. MM: Exactly, which seldom it is done by letting some buoys out in the sea.
CR: In the video we see a very ironic side of you which made everyone smile here.
We have seen you playing a double role, that of an ogre and that of a calm Matteo.
You must know that he is very active, also from a social standpoint, isn't it?
MM: I like to do activities and sailing is really a wonderful means, it the best for all less fortunate children, and for many people that are not lucky.
It is an instrument we use exclusively for pleasure.
There is no desire of teaching, no desire of saying, "Here is how you set it, fasten the mainsail...", as we say.
There is only the will to hand over this paramount pleasure given by the nature, through the sailing itself.
CR: In the video you went live and talk to people in an hospital, have I got it right?
MM: Yes, at "Bambin Gesù" Hospital, clown-therapy sessions are held, but recently, for example, we take the kids to sail, together with their families, when they finish a very heavy chemotherapy cycle.
And that is really fulfilling.
CR: To be outdoor.
MM: It gives back some pleasure to the family, which unfortunately, is fixed with the kid's real problems.
CR: So you take the whole family to spend together some days.
I would say that this alone deserves to be applauded.
(Applause) CR: Let's get back to the shipwreck, because that is not an easy thing, not even from the technical standpoint of a rescue. You were lucky to be on the route of other ships or similar.
MM: Yes, I would say that amid the shipwreck I was really lucky, because if I had sailed downward, to the south of the Pole, in the middle of ice cold, everything would had been different.
There is no commercial ships sailing around there.
It happened slightly above the Equator, very close to commercial routes, so a satellite radio reception system instantly kicked in.
We started to communicate with Rome's operational center, then we communicated with a satellite, then the radio, the VHF, in order to talk with the ship that was passing by, and that came to rescue me.
CR: What did you think when you understood that it was over?
MM: Poor chicken!
(Laughter) CR: It is floating... MM: There is not much to laugh about, because... CR: There is an emotional bond. MM: Surely, yes!
It has been my first worry.
The ship rolls over, and everything becomes dark, so you immediately rescue it and place it in a dry place, then you go looking for a container for it, you come back, and is not there anymore, so in the end it is sad. CR: You didn't leave only the chicken though,
you also abandoned almost your entire equipment. MM: In fact, all the work done by the university, all these data sheets; we recovered them all, together with the most part of clothing that were lent to me; most of the technical apparel wasn't mine, so I said, "Gosh, Al least let's take this home."
CR: I was thinking about swimsuits and sun creams, but no... MM: When they come to rescue you, you have to abandon the ship, for sure; you even have to move away from it, because the boat does so much backwash, that you have to distance yourself, stay on a rubber boat and be rescued from there.
It was certain I had to abandon the boat, but I had the time to place a tracker on it, a localizer.
So we even managed to go there and rescue it.
Bring it back home.
After so much struggle, we got there after six days of sailing - on a 1970s wooden fishing boat - and that really was an adventure, even more than going around the world's.
CR: I see you are explaining them, "Look, I'll do the work, take a rest."
This has put back together your challenge's mosaic; to recover Eco 40, bring it back home; is it still there?
MM: Yes, it is still there, and we hope to put it back in order, because the project is still that of clean energy, self-sufficiency, and involving the youth is what makes me most enthusiastic about in these last months on the mainland.
CR: And your excitement hasn't stopped yet, I know that year 2016 will be filled with many good news. MM: Yes, we have a lot of ideas, and I was pondering over them already while I was sailing.
I have a strong desire of doing much more not only sailing.
There is a northwest passage in the middle of the ice.
It's true that a path opens due to the melting, a passage forms
from Alaska to Canada, in August, and we want to sail through it.
Not an easy thing though an exciting and funny task.
CR: I must say it Matteo.
If this is not having the courage to dare, I don't know how to describe Matteo Miceli's challenge otherwise. (Applause) MM: Thanks! CR: Thank you, Matteo!
MM: It has been a pleasure. CR: Thank you!
MM: Thanks.
Empathy basically corresponds to our need for communication and sharing.
We live in a time of economic crises, of natural disasters, of personal and social relationships that are increasingly torn apart, of violence committed as a result of hatred and fanaticism.
And so, the call for empathy serves us to help others, to take care, or at least not to be indifferent to the misery and deprivation of vast populations of our planet.
However, to help others it is necessary not to consider them only passive victims.
Sometimes we think that they only need material help.
Instead, it is a question of knowing that they are men, women who have a history, a project for life, desires.
And right here we must ask ourselves: why, in order to love others, must we see them suffer?
I already talked indifference: often, even if we see someone else suffering, we flinch away.
Now, empathy is precisely the ability to connect us directly. Even just by looking a person in the face, seeing for instance that she has a slightly grimace of lips, or a little sadness in her eyes: well, empathy puts us in direct contact with people, starting from gestures.
Often you don't need words either, to understand that another person has some kind of problem: including of course, for example, when she is angry with us or needs something else.
This is an ability - and we need to know this, and repeat it to ourselves very often - that we all have.
In fact, today we know a lot about empathy at a scientific level, we know its history in the evolution of the species: it has been fundamental for cooperation and association, and also for the care of the young and the weak people.
We also know a lot about the brain circuits associated with empathy.
But thinking that empathy is an instinct, or that it triggers a burst of neurons, is not enough.
Because if this were the case, we could not explain why the fear of the other is a fundamental aspect, not only for our society but also for our life.
Fear, sometimes, even fear of falling in love
because we are afraid of being betrayed, abandoned.
Moreover, we think about how hard it is today to communicate between the elderly and young people, between men and women - this is a sadly unfortunate story too, especially in recent times; between rich and poor, between North and South; between people who live in the city and people who live in the countryside.
The world in which we live, therefore, makes empathy what I call a wakeup call.
That is, a warning, which can also be strong, violent, to invest our energies in the risk, in the trouble we often run into, of discovering the other.
Empathy must become a wakeup call for us, because in this way, I believe, it will become a fundamental turning point for the future of this world, which is going through deep crises.
And I am not only thinking about economy and finance, of course; I am also thinking about the environment.
There is just this turning point when each of us contributes with a piece of us, in meeting the others, facing the experience of relationships - only in this way, perhaps, will we stop a catastrophic drift.
Discovering the other is the quintessence of empathy.
It may seem quite simple: instead, if we think about digital society, the media, the web, but also about this globalized world, discovering the other is not that simple. I mean, these very digital devices, but also airplanes, which allow us to quickly move to another part of the planet, put us in contact with people who live very far from us, and we totally ignore their language, their culture we totally ignore their beliefs or faith.
Precisely because these new forms of sociality, in which we are all immersed, bring us into contact with other unnamed people, often groups and populations, victims, for example, of natural disasters; or victims of these great migrations.
On the other hand, they're others we share the same world with.
They are parts, actors in our own scene.
Precisely for this reason, we often simply rely on immediate sympathy, or even on immediate repulsions or aversions.
I give examples that everybody knows: why do we get excited about the photo of a child - for example that famous photo, which has gone viral, of the child who is pulled out of the rubble in Aleppo, right? And then, we Italians, who get along very well with an immigrant who works in a market, or in a bar: and yet in the square of Milan's central station we get irritated because we say: but look at all these alleged refugees, who do nothing all day long.
This is a real contradiction: but it shows us, above all, the fatigue, hardships of dealing with the difference of the other, whether he is a young person, an immigrant, an old man, a foreigner.
The first lesson of empathy, from this point of view, lies precisely in listening, without letting ourselves down saying: well, I am selfish and therefore I am not interested.
No: we must listen to these spontaneous responses, these immediate aversions of ours.
We are also, as they trivially say, "we have our own problems".
But listening to them and taking them seriously means accepting that our attractions, our sympathies and our aversions can also generate something different.
And therefore, they can also lead us to recognize the value of those who inhabit the world with us, who have emotions, have thoughts, have desires like us.
Science is working very hard on this, and for example - this is in my opinion an interesting result - it tells us that there are no naturally empathic people.
One commonplace is that women are more empathic than men.
Experiments in neuroscience have quite dismantled this kind of cliché.
Which also means, there are no cold, calculating, selfish men, either genetically or naturally anti-empathic.
And what does all this mean?
It means that the skill of empathy is totally embedded in the brain; but we must also choose to cultivate this skill.
Considering, as well, that it's very delicate and very fragile.
Other studies, just as interesting as those I have just mentioned, tell us that it is easier, for instance, to be empathetic with our family members, with those who belong to our group, to our environment, rather than with those who speak another language, with those we don't know very much. Likewise, you find it harder
to be empathetic when you are stressed, or when you are depressed.
I'm quoting this kind of knowledge just to say how realistic you have to be, also about ourselves.
That means, being empathetic doesn't just mean being good, as in not being bad.
We all live, according to the circumstances of life, some phases of life, whether we are younger or adult, whether we have other kinds of problems, we live moments when empathy is dampened; it can also be a little limited, a little scarce; but all this does not mean that the empathic experience cannot be renewed or resumed.
What does that mean?
Life, after all, is a great school of empathy.
It is true that we get in contact with others in an extremely exciting way, as far as love and friendship are concerned, and we are involved with our emotions but also with our sensitivity, with our feelings.
And yet, let's remember that all it takes is a conversation with a friend, coming out of a cinema.
It is enough to open the window and look outside, for example, to see an old man who is a bit shabby and struggles in walking. That's enough to raise our emotions and our feelings; so this possibility of opening ourselves to the stories of others, to the existence of others, although just for a few seconds, is a possibility within everyone's reach.
In this sense, I was saying, life is a school of empathy.
Traveling teaches us to empathize. Learning foreign languages, in order to understand who speaks a language different from ours; to converse, the pleasure - even boredom, sometimes - of conversation is very important, precisely, to live and develop this formidable human skill that is full of potential.
In a globalized world, after all, we have to learn every day to live together with others: men and women, parents and children, students and teachers and between workmates.
Now this re-learning, which actually clashes with the speed, with our daily problems, is, however, crucial for making the world more human.
And, above all, for appreciating the humanity of those who live the very same earth. we live in.
With this, I would like to spread the message that empathy - I repeat: a skill we all have - is creative, and generous.
Above all, it leads us to look ahead.
And this is way more important in a world that today is afraid of the future.
[Think about] Greta saying: you adults are stealing our future.
These are huge words, for us adults.
Empathy helps adults, too, and not just young people, to look ahead.
To risk, to accept fatigue, even the conflicts of interest, of values and ways of life that we surely find, when we start relationships with others.
Because in this way, after all, we enrich our lives and our experiences.
I will now finish by asking you something.
Just one gesture: turn to your neighbour, and look at him straight in the face.
Then, take a deep breath; and, as if you feel freed from a burden, smile.
(Applause)
I'm this instrument.
Something is still missing, to do it, but we get to that in a moment.
There are musicologists who say that the first time, in prehistoric times, when a man shot the first arrow and pulled a bow, he invented the harp, which is the musical Bow.
Well, this instrument, of course, throws sounds, it doesn't throw arrows.
Sounds which can move us.
In history we have Orpheus, who calms down animals, David, who calms down the human soul.
And they are art exceptions.
But till today, this instrument basically maintains its primitive soul.
But what would happen if we, instead, would like to change its image?
A very standardized image, why do we all have an image of the harp as it's so linked to the human history?
Eh, peculiar things happen.
First, we move the mind, we change our image, and this is always interesting.
Secondly, instead, is working on the sound. and then moving a bit further the sound that you have in mind or you have always heard from the instrument.
And that's what I'm here to do, today, showing you what would happen if we link ancient and modern and what are the differences.
It's nice to work on the sound, because it's the matter of which we are made, I believe.
The vibration is the universe, everything.
That's why the musician has a big responsibility in handling this thing.
And the first piece I'll play, in fact, is about this.
It's named, "Temple of sound."
It's a piece by Roberto Cacciapaglia, he works a lot with this magical meaning, the power of the sound.
I'll try to do the same, with my vision of this piece.
And therefore I invite you to enter in the temple of sound.
(Applause) (Applause) Thank you.
Always for "What if ..." there is another piece.
It's a "What if ..." a bit different from the previous one, in fact here we talk about a very famous piece written in '900: “Adagio for strings”, by Samuel Barber.
It's a piece which I believe is included in all "Best of" classical music.
But a few years ago a Dutch DJ, one of the most famous in the world, his name is DJ Tiësto, created a pop version.
That's how a classical piece for strings has become one, has been voted as one of the most beautiful dance pieces of all times.
I've decided to create a version for the harp.
I believe that it's a very dance instrument, as you can see.
(Applause)
'Scotland forever.'
This was the battle cry of the Scottish soldiers as they galloped towards the French lines during the Battle of Waterloo in 1815. It is also the title of this painting that was painted by Lady Butler, roughly 70 years later, in 1881.
Lady Butler, as a woman, never participated in a battle, so she had to study, she had to gather information, she had to do research, because she had to depict the uniforms of the soldiers that corresponded to that era. She had to study the anatomy of animals, of horses so that she could depict them with that very peculiar perspective, from the front, which was very original.
Thanks to my work, I had the possibility to pay tribute to this image, probably the most particular and renowned image of this event, an image that touched me a lot since I was a boy.
As I said, I am a paleo-illustrator, an illustrator that focuses on paleontology, meaning that they try through the study of fossils to depict the prehistoric world.
Like every artist, the paleontology illustrator has her head among the clouds, Jurassic clouds, Devonian in my case.
I like nature, I'm fascinated by it, it stimulates me by giving me input on how to create the illustrations you will see.
However, inevitably, every time I observe something in nature, I tend to transform it into something prehistoric.
When I walk on a nice mown lawn and I find myself in front of an oak tree, straight away, I tend to transform it into a type of giant tree with a particular shape, with an odd appearance - alien.
The lawn, obviously, becomes a marshy land rich with fallen trees, and lush vegetation.
The dogs that are running in front of me can easily be small carnivores that are following each other, that are competing for the prey. Above my head I see the long necks of sauropods nibbling plants ten metres above the ground.
And behind the bush that has just moved, surely there is some sort of large animal, such an aggressive carnivore that can jump out at any time.
In my head I keep trying to resolve the problem involving the illustration that I have been trying to create in these days.
I have studied very deeply the main subject, of which I obvoiusly do not remember the name. I always confuse it with another who also has an absolutely unspeakable name.
I studied its anatomy very well however I don't like the posture that I gave it and to represent better the image it would be better to frame it from above to have a precise and complete vision of the landscape. I will have to move the allosaurus on one side to make the image more balanced and to have a more comprehensive view of the scene.
The fact is that I have to deal with paleontology.
Paleontologists and geologists are my travel companions, they are the ones that supervise me they give me all the information and indications allowing me to create an illustration that considers the tiniest anatomic details.
At the same time though, they are also those who tend to harness me. They channel me in a narrow artistic corridor to mitigate the risk that the momentum, and my desire to make the frame exciting, results in an illustration that would be anatomically impossible.
It is a sort of "CSI: Paleontology."
I have everything that I need to do my research, I have all the clues, I have the sketches, the drawings made by those before me, the indications that have been given to me from paleontologists and geologists, who have clear in their heads how the prehistorical world was, because they study it.
Their problem is to make it visible. My job is to take their knowledge and create an instant photo of the distant past.
It is a work in continuous evolution.
Suppose there is an animal you have studied with a lot of attention, the Edmontosaurus, of which I had curated every minimum detail - even the little marks present on its skin. The day before the delivery of your work, researchers publish a new study that highlights an anatomical detail that is truly insignificant: The animal has a fleshy crest right in the middle of its forehead. It is my responsibility to try and update all that animal's illustrations at the speed of light.
The same thing can happen, for example, for an animal such as the Tyrannosaurus.
The Tyrannosaurus are among the most well known prehistoric animals in the field of paleontology.
For some time, paleontologists are convinced that the Tyrannosaurus were covered in a sort of "primitive plumage" - called proto-feathers - similar to a soft down, that in my head obviously appeared straight away like a sort of gigantic chick... a "werechick." (Laughter) Therefore I had to transform that old illustration of which I was particularly proud into something that was anatomically fixed - being an illustration of a few years back - and also covered in this plumage.
And what happens next?
When you find yourself in front of this type of problems technology obviously becomes the fundamental means to solve them. This is the reason why the paleontologists use a laser scanner to have three-dimensional images of the finds, or they perform CT scans to observe the interior of a fossil without the need to break it or cut it.
Illustrators use Adobe Photoshop with the typical overlapping layers, while the old maquette - those models made in modelling clay - are substituted with sculptures created using new digital three-dimensional modelling tools, therefore 3D modelling.
It is an extremely complex task, thorough, and requires patience and dedication. It is not rare that after working a lot on the texture of the animal, I go to sleep, close my eyes and continue to see scales, little scales, more scales, spots, claws, serrations and all these types of things.
However, once the goal has been reached - that is, after reaching the final model, scenarios open before you where initiatives are inspired that aren't exactly scientific - (Laughter) but providing great satisfaction and fun to those who spent a long time working on these subjects.
The paleontologists have extraordinary resources at hand. Absolutely incredible material like the small 'Ciro,' that is the Scipionyx samniticus, that small dinosaur that was found near Pietraroja and it is a baby.
We know because the proportions of the body tell us, the head is very big - in fact, the eyes are very big compared to the head.
There is even an open fontanelle, just like a human baby. We know what it ate, because the fossilisation is so incredible that one can see even the inside of organs, it is a unique case in the world.
And inside these organs have been found small feet that correspond to those of a small lizard.
We know where it lived: In an environment similar to modern day Bahamas.
The sediments tell us.
We don't know how it died - this remains a mystery, but it is through the artistic representation that we can continue to dream, to imagine something that at the end will remain unknown.
Thanks.
(Applause)
Girl 1: Good evening everyone.
We are a group of students from the Agricultural Technical Institute of San Michele all'Adige, and we study both agriculture and the environment, given that one cannot exist without the other.
Our project "Be the Change You Want to See" has aimed to study climatic changes and how these influence agriculture and are influenced by agriculture.
Man can change with his actions the quantity of greenhouse gases present in the atmosphere.
These gases work just like a blanket around our planet, blocking the sun rays inside the atmosphere, which then warms up.
This phenomenon is called the greenhouse effect, and within certain limits it is necessary for life to exist on Earth, because otherwise our planet would be too cold to live on.
When the level of these greenhouse gases becomes excessive, the climate of the Earth changes, and this can cause disastrous phenomena.
Do we want to or can we live in a world like this?
Girl 2: President Obama said, "The choices we make today will influence the amount of greenhouse gases that we will emit into the atmosphere tomorrow.
I refuse to condemn the world to this disaster that we can't come back from".
Our planet is warming up.
In the last few centuries we've indeed had an increase in temperature which will likely continue.
This phenomenon has started specifically in the 70s, and since 1981, we've been experiencing the warmest years of the century.
Little changes in the average temperature of the planet translate into climatic disasters.
Some examples are tsunamis, tornados, droughts.
The oceans are warming and are becoming more and more acidic and with the increase in temperature, polar ice-caps are melting and consequently, the sea level is rising: in the last 10 years, it's risen over 17 cm.
One day we may no longer be able to see Venice because it might be submerged.
One day we may no longer be able to feed everyone because plants need time to adapt to the new temperatures and don't always make it.
What will happen to the world under these circumstances?
Man 1: Climate change has a devastating impact on agriculture.
This is due to the increase in intensity and frequency of atmospheric phenomena.
This is proven by what's happened the last few weeks in Northern Italy with all of the flooding.
Climate change also entails a serious risk for the livestock, both directly and indirectly: directly because the increase in temperatures causes heat stress for the animals.
This leads to lesser milk production and therefore lesser fertility.
Indirectly, because drought burns and depletes pastures and this means a lesser availability of feed for the animals.
Clearly it's difficult to dispute these issues and we must spend a lot of money in order to fix them.
Wouldn't it be better to remedy the problems by preventing them?
Girl 3: William Arthur Ward said, "If you can imagine it, you can achieve it." We had decided that the time had come to do something.
The first step to resolve a problem is becoming aware of it and sharing it, so, after documenting it and interviewing various experts on the phenomenon of climate change, we prepared some articles for La Stampa, a website with some social network pages.
After five months, we were ready to export our model and expand it with a trip to a country particularly sensitive to the topic of climate change: the United States.
In particular, our destination was the creative Northern California.
At this point, we made a video to document our trip and above all the reason behind it.
We left for San Francisco: San Francisco is a very sensitive city to environmental topics and the people that live there have always been ready for the change and ready to improve it.
Indeed the use of public transportation and bicycles is seen there like as a real and proper lifestyle.
Moreover, from Stanford to Silicon Valley, to the visits to the big hi-tech companies we had a life lesson that we'll never forget.
We breathed an air of intellectual, physical, and creative freedom like never before.
The great teaching of these visits that they left us is that error is a symptom of moving to perfection.
So why feel wrong if you're wrong?
Man 2: Mahatma Gandhi once said, "Be the change that you want to see." Once we visited the famous Stanford University, we also visited Cal Poly, and the Hopkins Marine Station, where we spoke with researchers about the subject of climate change.
Then we went on our "road trip" into Sonoma Valley which we had only heard about in enology books.
There the farms have their own vines defoliated by goats and they use solar energy for their transformation processes.
In Death Valley at 118 degrees we were able to see the potential of wind energy, but also the devastating effects of desertification.
We saw many things and every time we think about it our hearts cry to us for how beautiful this trip was.
Being climate ambassadors has made us aware of our role on planet Earth: it's as if we grew up ten years in just two weeks. (Applause)
Barbara: I think that every teacher should spend a period of their own life doing academic research.
During my doctorate years, indeed, I discovered that the thing that I liked most in the world, what I wanted to do when I grew up, which gave me more emotion, was to pass on the beauty and the craftiness of nature to who would have worked with nature everyday.
Therefore I decided to start my career as a science teacher in English, the language of the sciences.
Which is the acronym that you see in the little book: CLIL, and I decided to do it using some guys that would have surely worked closely with nature and they are guys from an agricultural technical institute because there's effectively no environment that can't give agriculture.
I think that absolutely everyone, everyone in the world, should have one person that gives motivation or a model.
I researched my model and I searched for it amongst my teachers at the United World Boarding School which is an international school that I myself had the chance to attend.
A school that is born with the idea that wars happen because one doesn't know the reciprocal culture.
They were wonderful years, those spent at the United World Boarding School.
in which I came to understand that content was exclusively the means, and not the end.
They were years in which I learned, because my teachers had told me, that everything can change only and exclusively thanks to my hands.
Years in which I felt like the main actor of my life, not the director.
So I firmly decided to make it also happen with my students.
I wanted them to feel curiosity, the desire to learn and for them to become infected of it.
I wanted them to feel knowledge as a means to start their personal experience: exploration to research, action, and for them to be capable of communicating it to others.
I pretty much decided to therefore, start this personal challenge and I started to write a project for climate change.
A project that would search to go beyond simple emotions that are provoked by theory, for how devastating it is.
A project that could be a type of life gym for my guys.
A project in which they themselves would have found the actions.
You've heard from their voices that we did a bunch of things.
On the day before we left for California, I knew that I had close to me a group of young women and men that were ready and determined to change the world.
That they knew how much they could do and that they knew their power. And I felt in my heart that I had done my work as a teacher, to have lit the fire inside them because at the end this is what a teacher should do.
During our trip we hit so many destinations, some even a bit unconventional: from the big cities to the research centers, from the universities to the companies that have changed the world, from the natural parks to the manufacturing businesses, from the oceans to the deserts, everything to stimulate their autonomy, their creativity, their curiosity to see the world and a little bit also to contribute to it.
Their creativity was also put to the test with a project idea that they had.
It was fun, because when we were in America they looked at the Italian bars with the typical eyes of an immigrant. those eyes that Italians often have when they leave their Country.
When they look for their beautiful country inside the Italian bars, where they think they are hearing Italian people as if they were the quintessence of their country.
So we entered these bars and were thinking they'd hear their beautiful language, however it was not so.
And here they realized that in America, like in the rest of the world, having an Italian bar doesn't at all mean having and using Italian products or at least having Italian people;
but why not change?
So we thought about establishing a brand, a brand for bars abroad that utilize Italian products or have first or second generation Italians inside.
It's not about nationalism, it's about an idea like that, perhaps, to push "Made in Italy" a bit.
An idea to give a hand to this Italian economy which is troubled a bit, we think.
Whatever is at the end of this idea anyway, has also been able to stimulate the autonomy of the guys.
So it could be a thing that they will decide to do in the future, finally they've arrived in their final year of high school, now the future is in their hands.
And they could decide to go it perhaps by establishing a seal or quality brand that does use Italian products or internally has Italian expertise.
A project that has stimulated their autonomy, that has tried to make them see how the world really is.
A world with some of the best eyes, where youth must be inspired, active, and protagonist in their lives.
"Be the change you want to see" is the name of our project, it's a phrase of Gandhi, we really wanted to dedicate it to him.
Because we think that social and personal transformation go hand and hand, because when logic brings you from one to two, motivation can carry you anywhere.
(Applause.)
Do you remember the waste emergency in Naples in 2008?
Well, go back in time to that moment: maybe you even remember how you spent that May Day weekend.
I don't know you, but I was in Naples.
It was a beautiful city, with wonderful monuments and a fantastic blue sky.
And piles of garbage everywhere!
Garbage was everywhere, next to museums, monuments, even in Capri!
It was an unbelievable view: this one.
(Music) (Video) [Saint Gennaro Sanctuary] [Nisida] [Playground] In 2008, I was looking for an entrepreneurial idea that would gratify me in the future professionally
Back then, I supported companies in terms of innovation projects and structural changes. But helping others reaching their goals made me feel like a wingman, more than a protagonist of my professional life.
I don’t know about you, but I believe in those subtle signals that are indeed you destiny, not "coincidences".
That striking contrast between beauty, culture and waste sparked a loud and clear idea: I was going to realize high quality, Made in Italy products using only regenerated materials!
Yes, but only someone crazy can think to combine two traditionally distant sectors like fashion, design and waste!
Anyway my surname - Pazzi [crazy, in Italian] helped me to believe in my idea and stick with it even if waste was only seen as a problem, as a cost, and not as a business opportunity.
At the time, Italy used to pay other nations, especially in Northern Europe, to dispose of our waste.
For us it was a cost, for other nations it was a double profit. They were receiving money to dispose of our waste and thanks to it they generated enough energy to sell to other countries!
In Northern Europe there was a new kind of economy that had a circular shape.
So it is an economic revolution to manage and transform waste.
This kind of economy didn't exist in Italy.
But what is circular economy?
First of all, circular economy means changing mentality.
Until today, economy operated using a linear model, where each product can meet only one need and after fulfilling it,
it becomes waste this then turns into a waste disposal problem for the environment. A problem our planet can no longer bear.
Conversely, the circular approach treats waste as a resource and not as a problem. In the circular economy, after its first use, any object can become something else, can change its purpose, extending its product life-cycle and not be a waste anymore. Reusing – recycling – reducing is the paradigm
on which circular economy is based.
For example these products, which are made by transformation of interior panels of refrigerators and from coffee machine cups.
This is the new circular economy paradigma, so different from linear economy especially in its results.
Just some numbers: in the world we are more than seven billion
three billion live in urban areas producing 1.5 billion tons of solid waste every year.
The production of waste will rise to 2.2 billion tons in 2025.
I don't want to alarm you, but here are the decomposition rates of waste over years.
You must consider that there are 5 large islands of garbage in the oceans.
The biggest one is in the North Pacific Ocean
and it is estimated to be almost as big as the United States.
No country acknowledges this waste as their own, and so they don't commit to clean up these islands.
So we started a project: turning waste into gold. And our mantra is: let's turn waste into beauty, as functionality, eco-compatibility and beauty can coexist.
We believe that even our market, consumption-based society, can bring about something good, which is absolutely compatible with a function, an aestethics that's tied with sustainability.
We have created an ecosystem filled with skillfull people, where productive and creative skills merge.
We made these people meet and offered them a fertile environment to develop their projects.
It’s amazing to think that a lot of superstar designers have immediately joined my project and took quite a daunting challenge: can you see these materials, from our library of materials? None of them knew anything about them
especially their processability.
So we cooperated with the Politecnico di Torino University and Matrec, a portal for regenerated materials, in order to create a materiotheque. A library of recycled materials, with their data sheets and samples.
So we started, 10 years ago, a permanent research, that we still do today, because it's an endless challenge for us, realizing these products out of waste regeneration.
The processing phase is of course critical, as it's the most technology and innovation intensive.
What we most care about is giving those materials the same dignity of raw prime ones, whilst making beautiful and economically sustainable objects.
This is an important challenge because there are a whole series of filters which often lead to a dead end.
For instance, we turn 100 cans into a plate, or bend the leather like an origami, to get a fine paper basket.
We love pulling "beauty poems", from waste, we like to say: ones that are tied to waste and sustainability.
Ones where beauty meets sustainability. And luxury is also compatible with these new sustainability concerns.
I got to know and admire a number of people, who had the strength and charisma to spark a bold conversation on these issues, by leveraging their popularity, their public exposure.
Livia, Colin Firth's wife, is certainly among them. She has been standing up for over ten years for respecting humans and the environment.
She always exposes to the public opinion unethical behaviors in fashion, especially fast fashion business.
Plus, she dares movies and fashion stars with her "Green Carpet Challenge" project, to wear sustainable clothes during mundane events, such as the Oscar Night.
Ellen McArthur grew up in the countryside, far from the sea, and she had a dream: crossing the oceans.
And she did it, better than anyone else! In 2005 she set the world record of solitary circumnavigation. A remarkable accomplishment that gave her international popularity.
In 2010 she took advantage of such popularity and took on a new challenge: she established a Foundation to start a sinergy between business and education sectors. in order to accelerate the transition from a linear to a circular economy.
A new business model which only brings about advantages, in materials processing, sustainable energy and thus generates a positive impact on economy.
According to the European Commission, 580,000 new jobs are expected to be created should these policies be implemented. With an estimated saving, for European companies, of 72 billion euros.
A whole lot of money, thanks to a more responsible consumption, and an efficient use of resources.
As you can imagine, that changed a few things or two for me.
I'm not as good as Ellen to ride alone. But my journey, up to then, was also a solitary one.
Finally, a new challenge came along. Now we could have a network of companies and create a web portal, where companies that are interested in switching to circular economy develop new products, such as this wallet created with the suits of MotoGP riders.
It's a committing deal, of course, for circular economy is very demanding for a company.
But it's very rewarding, also.
Circular economy asks companies to implement a sustainable business model. So they have to re-design their products with the goal in mind of a greater efficiency in terms of costs, energies and other elements that involve all of the business areas: production, logistics, marketing and sales.
Sure, that's a challenging task.
On the other hand, circular economy is largely rewarding, in terms of competitiveness, reputation and indeed, turnover.
That's because a thorough redesign of products brings about a re-design of the whole company, and generate a competitive advantage over other companies.
And it's also true, for instance, that the moment you redesign your productive cycle huge efficiency gains can be achieved using sustainable energy. The energy bill gets typically cheaper, thanks to both energy savings and renewable sources.
Last but not least, not having to spend any more money to dispose of your waste but you are able to give it new resources and therefore new value.
That's another critical point to raise. Material research isn't enough: re-design is also very important for us, let me insist on that. This bracelet is realized with 270 buttons and it's in turn recyclable, because it’s made of just one material, so I can throw it in a plastic bin, one day, and it'll be reused in a different way.
This is only an example: each company can choose whether to pick up this opportunity or not.
The critical point is that every company will have to deal with this change, because this change is already ongoing, is irreversible and keeps accelerating.
A company can choose to either grab the opportunity now and turn it into a critical factor of its success, or they'll have to deal with circular economy anyway, and fit in the market's needs, just to survive.
Every company plays its game: but this will happen, it will be fast, and we all have to deal with it now.
This of course marks a phase change in my journey, as it's no longer a solitary one.
I went to Naples and I had a dream: turn waste into beauty.
My dream now is to involve other companies in my journey. Together we'd gain speed and traction in promoting an economic paradigm shift that not only protects environment but is also a good business opportunity! Protecting the planet would then have a stronger, common case.
Are you ready to embark on this journey with me?
(Applause)
Good morning!
I am very excited; thanks Claudio!
But after an introduction like this, it makes me look immediately annoying; maybe we can say that, being a few decades older than him, I have done something more, nothing special.
We are, therefore, talking now about the four 'I' for creativity and diversity.
Let's start from the first three.
The first is Innovation, that can only be generated by the intuition.
And intuition, luckily, sometimes generates the Invention.
It is important to understand, we have to ask ourself, how can make these three I interact.
It is important to understand the connections that make them animate, one next to the other because it is essential to facilitate their creation.
And in this context, we wondered, what is the role of diversity in unleashing creativity.
'Intuition', as almost all the Italian words, you know, has a Latin origin.
Intuition means 'looking inside' and then try to understand, from inside, what we are talking about.
But intuition is not just that thing that seems a bit cold and comes from the brain, and it is not even just poetry and imagination.
It is a way in which our mind works.
And our mind does not work like this: it does not work as a computer, luckily!
We are not yet replaceable by a computer.
Our mind works, as previously mentioned by Claudio, by tags, by keywords.
If I say 'yellow', some of you think about the sun, some other thinks about saffron, somebody else thinks of a sunflower, and somebody else thinks of the t-shirt he wanted to buy yesterday. I do not. Our mind is working by links.
Luckily; and these links can be somehow connected partly with our synapses, partly with our creativity, partly with our intuition and then they generate creativity.
There are tools for those involved in this activity, there is the brainstorming, which is a way to generate, in a group, new ideas, new thoughts, new proposals, and there are some simple rules, as I marked: we have to gain our focus on the subject we want to manage; we must not interrupt anybody in order to let the creativity and the ideas flow; we should neither judge, nor interpret, we have to let the creativity go forward.
The other word, 'Invention', of course with a Latin origin, comes from 'invenire', that is, finding, search and find.
What is it?
Thanks to the invention we do have creativity, or what is now called 'serendipity', in other words we can find something, casually or by chance.
I mentioned Marconi, but think about Christopher Columbus.
Christopher Columbus discovered America by serendipity, by chance: his idea was to go to India, as you know. And this is an element, but this is not enough, we'll get to the point.
Innovation, another easy word to understand, the word 'nova' makes us understand 'to find', to look for something new.
And we believe that innovation must be without excuses.
We do not need brakes for innovation, because it is with new ideas that we can grow.
Innovation should be simple, sustainable, safe, aware, competitive and inclusive.
Intuition, as Einstein said much better than me, "The mind only works if it is open like a parachute." And we get to the parachute, speaking of intuitions.
The first idea of the parachute was by Leonardo da Vinci. When? In 1485.
It took 400 years before somebody was able to make the first parachute and he did it it exactly in the same way.
And from there, from this intuition, we've got the innovation, innovation that now is a useful thing.
Another innovation: Newton's apple. The apple fell and he understood and found how to define a new law, the law of gravity.
There is another circumstance which is essential, it is the possibility to go ahead outside the schemes, because if we are locked in the usual patterns, the creativity is compressed.
And now, alas, we are coming to the fourth 'I'.
We want to have the hope to be able to change: but our school is like that.
Our school is terrible.
If you think of a six years old child, stuck him in a school desk - stuck is the right word - throughout all the morning, and sometimes all day long.
How can we hope and think that he or she suddenly may become a creative person?
We turned off all his/her creativity during the first years of his/her life.
We can do better.
Compulsory education, Ivan Illich said, switches off the urge to learn.
Mark Twain was even funnier when he said, "Luckily, I managed to avoid that the school might interfere with my education."
It is comical, but it gives us a good idea of what I mean.
So, this is much better.
Surely, you've seen this movie. Unfortunately the actor died recently. These are the things that drives our interest: that school can be changed and that we can try to change it!
We are in a time of 'spending review', Claudio said it before.
But we can try to change without spending anything.
Our young people have a smartphone, it's in their pockets,
At home they have a computer.
Our teachers are not so ready to conform to this, but our youngsters will 'grow up', in particular there will be, sooner or later, a generational change and people in their twenties will be the teachers of tomorrow and they will have to change things dramatically, to move those school desks and to make changes in their classrooms, even physically, it makes no sense to keep the classroom as it is now, because now I can learn what a child, at the same time, is writing on the Internet in Australia or Iceland, it makes no difference.
Ivan will talk about that later so I will not speak about this, but it has been a beautiful experience, a CoderDojo at the Chambers of Deputies: the countenance of this child says everything, but Ivan will tell you more about that.
I count on that, I count on that,
I think it will be the right solution to improve our country, because children are ready, they are many, beautiful, full of energy, determination, we just do not have to put a stone over their head, anything but the gallantry's glass ceilings. Claudio was talking about.
By the way, please note, that here in the first row there is a busy place just to remember women that are not here because of femicide.
(Applause)
Remember that, "If I just see, I'll forget, if I can read, I will remember, if I do something, I will learn." And this is the point: we have to give our children the chance to do things
not just teach them.
We have to put together notions, which are important, information, of course, because we do not want non-educated children.
We are glad to give them notions.
But we have to use teaching methodologies allowing these children to let their creativity flow in order to enhance their diversity, because diversity is in each one of us like in those apples from Trentino.
Diversity is in all of us: we are, fortunately, strongly different, by gender, by history, by culture, by skills, by passion, and love.
There are people who can do the high jump, there are people who can do the long jump.
Each person brings its own value and the school should have the goal of enhancing the value of each of those diversities.
We are talking about ecosystems and this is another picture from Trentino, fantastic! This is a beautiful region.
The ecosystem was defined by Darwin in a different way.
Each environment has its own ecosystem.
The ecosystem is based exactly on the value of diversity.
It is on the diversity that we have to prompt in order to ensure we start from the intuition, invention, innovation, and instruction.
Should we stop here? Maybe not.
I believe that we need
a fifth 'I', "inclusion", but that's another story, we'll talk about it in another thread.
Thank you!
(Applause)
Design -
we all appreciate great design.
There are extremely iconic designs, like the Valentine typewriter; there are chairs that apparently pretty much every designer considers designing at some point in their lives; then there's the design that comes from printing, hybrid, or mixed design.
Then there's also - as a recent example - the fusion of hardware and software.
The issue here is: yes, these are examples of great design,
but why do we lack the ability to create good design, not necessarily excellent, but consistently good?
Well, I'd like to start with the premise that, on a fundamental level, design is cross-disciplinary.
Design involves and includes multiple disciplines - definitely the visual, but also the psychological like understanding how it'll be used. It also includes technological and business-related aspects because we have to understand how to deliver it to users.
This brings us to one of the issues I want to address today, which is OK, we can have lots of skills, but then, we run into the ego.
Designers clash with their egos for a fairly simple reason: we are immersed in our work, and we project much of ourselves into it.
So, what ends up happening is that we identify with the design, the idea, the concept, with whatever has been created.
This obviously leads to an intermingling of a person's selfhood and the work performed.
Let's take the term "ego". While there are obviously many different and specific psychological definitions, there are also common definitions, the ones most frequently used that usually have negative connotations:
a sense of self-importance, of our self-esteem.
And while this can be good, the downsides cause many problems.
It is precisely because the ego resides within each of us that it is the greatest enemy of good design which is innately cross-disciplinary, connecting, and rooted in necessity.
So the ego seriously impacts on people, because it leads us to self-denial.
A person with a big ego cannot accept criticism; they close up, defend themselves,
and sometimes even become enraged.
There is an emotional disconnection that doesn't help understand others.
And obviously, one loses the capacity for growth, unable to accept advice and suggestions.
Then, there is the impact on the design itself, because this inability diminishes the creativity of the result, of the solution;
the quality worsens.
There is no chance to experiment.
And obviously, this lack of exploration affects the final design.
Finally, the team is also involved.
This is a very interesting aspect because very often this is underestimated.
but the impact of it on the team is huge and this is especially evident in the long run: if a team member is continually demeaned, they won't feel they belong.
If one always decides for all, then, "Why am I even here?"
Collaboration is destroyed: individuals and their individual talents are not empowered.
And obviously, this lowers team morale; it damages the group and in the long term, society itself.
So we must confront the ego.
There are various ways to do this, but I will start with a quote by a great Italian designer, Bruno Murari who said in a lecture that we are only people, so each of us has their own style, their own way of doing things, their own special talent; however, when I put myself into a design, when I design, I put all of that aside to create a completely different thing for that product I am currently working on.
At a personal level, the first thing to fix this problem is to separate the ego, this defensive mechanism, from our identity;
understand we are not our ego.
In a sense, we have to start detecting this voice inside us that says "I."
If we agree this voice is there, then this is the first step.
And it's a step worth-taking especially for us.
This comes from research on mindfulness, mediation, and neuroscience.
And one of the suggested models tells us that we look for focus, for the ability to be in the moment, keep concentrated, and identify the essence of our work, of our design:
yet, it is impossible to sustain.
So the focus slowly deteriorates, and becomes part of a vicious cycle of thoughts that in turn trigger other thoughts and all start to pile up in our heads.
The ego is one of these vicious cycles.
The interesting thing is that there are a ton of strategies on, "How to maintain focus?", "How to maintain and create the ideal conditions for staying focused?"
However, research - especially in mindfulness - shows that while focus is definitely an important aspect, it is equally important to train our ability to recognize when we are in a vicious cycle so we can escape it.
And this is a skill that can be learned.
Clearly, initially, we can only maintain focus for 1% of the time, then it improves.
It's a talent and a skill that we can develop.
This helps us break out of the vicious cycle of the ego, our defense system.
Actually, the simplest thing to do, if we realize what's about to happen, is to go out for a walk.
Research shows that the best way is to have a walk in the park.
In terms of design, there are various really interesting ways to confront the ego. Interestingly, much of designing - even if not overtly stated - seeks to minimize the role of the ego in the process.
One example is Pixar.
In Ed Catmull's words, everyone involved in a Pixar film's production gets together each morning, to share the work they did the day before.
It could be incomplete, partial: they discuss everything.
This is a way of shifting from individual work to collective work.
It's a way of confronting our ego, and understanding that day after day, project after project, this can be confronted.
Also extremely important is the ability to criticize the design not the person.
It's very easy to say, "Ah, what you've done is really ugly," or, "We have to fix the problem you have in your design,"
when it'd be a lot easier to say, "We'll solve this problem together, we'll solve the problem we are faced with!"
The separation is helpful because it allows us to tell the team that they have done a good job, and together, we'll solve the remaining problems.
Together, we'll improve what's already been done.
It's a way of avoiding the ego.
At the team level, though, it can be very difficult to know when the ego is expressing itself as confidence and self-assurance or as arrogance.
The difference can be seen, for example, when someone presents a design before a group of vice-presidents, for example. It's one thing to enter the room, present the design confidently, knowing the research has been done, the ideas have emerged, yet staying open to advice to cooperative improvements.
It is another to enter the same room not being sure this is your best design.
Sometimes, it's easy to mix-up these two approaches, especially at the beginning.
To detect the difference, it's enough to just notice how willing the person is to listen to others.
The ability to listen is absolutely critical; not just because design - as is so often pointed out - is user-centric, but also because it's important to draw upon all the input we can get, wherever it comes from.
This allows us to work on our own listening skills, but also to recognize the two types of ego we are dealing with.
And then, one fundamental thing: the ability to give credit, the ability to acknowledge that others also contributed to the project.
It has happened to me all too often that - at the contractual level - I'm asked not to mention I worked on a corporate project.
This is utterly destructive.
One could ask: but why give credit?
Doesn't giving credit feed the ego?
Well, if it's done badly, yes.
However, the ego also gets activated because it's hungry for recognition.
If we find ourselves in a team, a group of people, a work group where we know that our good job will be acknowledged, the ego has no reasons to activate itself.
Credit can also be given with small gestures, with a simple, "Thank you," obviously, accordingly to each person's contribution.
But shifting away from this everyday wisdom for our daily work life, our own little world, there is another issue I keep thinking about: why do we keep rewarding it?
Why do we keep celebrating individuals, managers, and great designers when they are exhibiting all the negative ego traits?
We have what can essentially be called biases,
i.e. set ways of perceiving the world that come naturally to us: we find it natural to think so, even if the interpretation is quite negative.
For example, one is the survival bias, i.e. we only study people who have been successful and not the multitudes who haven't.
We study successful designs and designers and not those who have failed.
And when we look at this more closely, we realize that very often, a design, a solution, a company has not attained success thanks to the ego: but despite ego.
Another really important bias is what I call smartass bias, with an English term.
I can't find a good translation of the word in Italian, but back in the 1980s research was done, and when two feedbacks were given, two identical pieces of advice, where one was posed negatively and the other kindly, the negative one was always perceived as the more intelligent.
But, if we see the world this way, we should realize we probably need to make a fix.
It's clearly preferable to work with a kinder person, one that's able to work in a non-confrontational way.
So, how can we overcome this bias?
Well, we overcome biases by noticing them.
The last bias is what we call the solitary genius.
This idea, incredibly popular in the media that holds a single person is the creator of that design, a single person carried out the great design, and a single person has led this society forward.
Ironically, combining these three biases leads us to celebrate individuals who, in reality, have always worked together, in groups.
For example, let's take Steve Jobs; here's celebrated for his design ability, for his organizational and business ability, for his genius. However, it could be that his most important skill was always having, throughout his career, the best talent he could find by his side.
And we don't have to go that far away: Italy had Adriano Olivetti, the great entrepreneur and publisher who did so many great things for Italy; an exceptional visionary.
Looking closer, though, Olivetti also had stellar designers around him.
For example, Gae created the "store" concept, the Olivetti stores, that are said to have inspired even the Apple ones.
And some people get it: if we look at Lady Gaga, her creative team has a brand;
the people who work with her are celebrated with a brand, with a special identity, the "Hous of Gaga."
She understands that it's difficult for the world to acknowledge all the people involved who are so critical for her success, and so, she created a brand to help them get recognition.
Finally, the point is: we must confront our ego.
Let's do it in ourselves and help others do the same, and confront it in the work world, because, at its core, design is a very humble discipline.
Successful design is by definition, humble.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Hello.
Hi everyone.
It's always difficult to work before going to eat, however.
