I'm really dumb.
You feel all excited when they tell you so, you feel like you're riding a horse running wildly towards a wonderful future made of new sneakers, new washing machines, phones with seven billion pixels.
I'm afraid, this kind change makes us always stay the same, Because, come on, let's be honest.
It's not easy to change for real.
I mean, apart from the shoes and the phone, changing is complicated, because in the end what is it you want to change?
The things we don't like, that really bother us, the things that make you feel bad.
Maybe we want to change because deep down we hope - I don't know, for a miracle to happen, for a U-turn, we hope that changing direction brings us some happiness.
Because, in theory, we change to improve, don't we?
I, for me, it took me 35 years to quit smoking.
Since my first day as a smoker, basically.
My friend Lucio, has been changing diet every week for 20 years now, trying to lose 15 kilos.
Michael Jackson spent almost 50 years trying to become white.
Death didn't satisfy him.
Now, now I've set my mind to do ten push-ups a day and do three flights of stairs, 60 steps when I get home.
It's not easy, though.
After eleven steps, I run out of breath as if I had run the New York marathon. And I say to myself, "I'll do the rest tomorrow". And I take the lift.
But even the easiest things, like not letting others finish talking and yelling at them, "You've understood nothing!"
Do you think, that is easy to change?
Or throw the paper and the bottle out of the window.
Do you think it's easy to stop that?
It's really hard to change for real.
Plus, not all changes are equal: but first and foremost, we change for what?
Perhaps the changes we want most are those that take place within us, those that transform you, but we, modern people, iI do feel, we are amateurs of change.
There are people who didn't wait government incentives to truly change themselves,
Like that good-for-nothing young lout that didn't miss a happy hour and a party in Assisi.
The billionaire daddy, the Loro Piana of the time we could say - then the young lout changes radically.
Drops all the dinner jackets, the shoes and daddy's money and from that day wears a jute sack.
Foolish.
Crazy, we would say.
After a few months, 5,000 heirs of his same age are ready to follow him dressed in rags like him.
What did they see, that we fail to see?
And that other moron, yes, rich moron, never worked an hour in his life, a passion for duels and the sword, lady-killer.
Even Infanta of Spain was rumored to be one of his lovers.
It's not written about, but it's known.
One day he goes off to war, saying "I've had enough, I'm bored, I'm going to war", and a cannonball makes a hole in his leg.
Forced to bed for months, he spends convalescence reading stories of horses and duels.
The last book he comes across is a story of saints.
From that day he changes radically, and founds an order which, after six centuries, will bring a certain Francis to the throne, to the throne of Peter.
What had he read in that book that we can't understand?
And that murderer who became a murderer for not letting a passerby past on the sidwalk, and who, after taking the life of his enemy, changed radically, becoming a friar and one of the protagonists
of The Betrothed. And then, that unnamed person who first spread terror and death, and then transforms himself and changes.
"Ah, but this isn't true," you say.
It's just literature.
True, literature.
Good intentions, nothing more.
And so I wrap up reminding you of that person who was riding, like us probably.
Intoxicated, happy, convinced of his truth, chasing, imprisoning and killing those who didn't think like him.
One day, maybe chasing after the last person to be executed, he tragically falls from a horse.
He goes blind - sorry, visually impaired - for three days, and then he changes radically.
He put himself in the shoes of those he persecuted.
But what happened to him on the horse?

A mind alteration? A stroke?
A drop in blood pressure?
Had he drunk too much?
A hallucination?
A crisis of depersonalisation?
A mystical-religious delirium?
What would he have seen or felt while riding the horse that we can neither see nor feel?
Compared to him, I am an amateur of change.
I could never do what they did.
First because I can't ride a horse.
Then because I've never had the rich father who sold fabrics.
And then war has always frightened me, always made me shit in the pants, so, even if I wanted to, no bullet has ever hit me, not even by mistake - I don't know, a hunter - to have to stay in bed and read certain books. No.

However, I say this in front of everyone, motivated by an irrepressible yearning for change, today I commit myself publicly to reach at least the twelfth step.
