Good morning everyone.
I was talking to Walter before, behind the stage while we were preparing, and was wondering if there is any illusion to hide the emotion and sweat, in particular, so that, if at the end of this presentation will look like a raccoon during the mating season love me anyway, after all.
This morning Flavia Marzano used an interesting word: brainstorming.
A word that sometimes is a bit abused. What I'll try to do in the next ten minutes will be brainstorming with you telling you what my life has been.
These 30 years of life and where they took me.
I was born in a small town near the banks of the Po river, more precisely, on an island: the Ariano island, a thin strip of land that is dug right by the Po river.
And I had a very happy childhood.
I owe so much, especially, to my grandparents.,
I grew up with them in their house, and if there is a particular object that I remember precisely in my grandmother bedroom, is an old trunk.
A trunk that was my best childhood friend.
When I had finished my homework I holed up in that room, and I hided everything in the trunk.
Things that I found around: toys, stones, anything, buttons, because my grandmother and my aunt were dressmakers.
And depending on what I wanted to do during the day, depending on the game I wanted to invent, I opened the trunk and took out objects that I needed to tell, to create stories.
I also spoke very often by myself, and my father came home and said, "But... it's all good with this guy?" To tell, to narrate. The power of words.
Words can exude life, passion.
A dear teacher on mine always said, "Remember, Andrea, the power of speech, because speech can move mountains, the enthusiasm of words can bring people together and can once again build worlds!" First key word of my brainstorming is therefore storytelling: one thing that I always carried with me for the first 18 years of my life.
I wanted to be a writer, I wanted to deal with theater among other things, I wanted to write for the theater.
But then you know things happen; hormones, falling in love when you're a teen.
There is another good Italian keyword, that is 'necking', we use especially during adolescence.
And it was precisely because of a 'neck' that I ended up in Milan.
In fact, if I have to think of me and Milan the keyword that comes to mind is: idiot. Why did I decide to go to Milan, do you know why?
Because I fell in love with a girl with blond hair and blue eyes.
I was very far-sighted, because after I came to Milan, among other things pulling the train emergency brake that did not stop at my station, since being a good country guy I had never taken a train.
It had to stop at my station, it did not stop: I pulled the emergency brake.
Never do it!
You do not know what the consequences may be.
And then I was very far-sighted because this girl after three days I arrived in Milan disappeared from my life, I never saw her again.
I stayed there to study marketing.
It sounded good, so much so that I came home, and I said, "What do you study, Stefania?" "Law." "And you?" "Preservation of cultural heritage." "And you, Andrea, what do you do?" "Marketing and Communication." I felt cool, eh?
12 years ago it was an important term, wasn't it?
Marketing, there is a bit of confusion around this term.
Here, beside me there is always the prototype of my Venetian entrepreneur, Toni Sugaman, who says, "The publicist is in charge of marketing." In fact, I always tell my students - because, even though it does not seem, I earn my living teaching, I'm a professor - that the answer is always in the name of things, and 'marketing' means 'market'.
The first thing a person who deals with marketing must know is precisely to analyze the market and understand our time.
A few years later, fortunately, I changed.
I changed because I felt once again incomplete.
There was storytelling, there was marketing, but I still wanted to create, build worlds, and I moved to Padua, where I met among others an extraordinary person who since ten years shares his life with me, who supports and bears with me.
And I ended up studying at a design school where I remained, I have been there for eight years now, and I have the honor to teach there.
And there is a particular episode that I remember as a student, and that I always tell in my classes, that concerns the person you see behind me, a designer, Yves Behar, the founder of Fuse Project.
It is one of the most renowned design companies in the world.
Yves Behar, right on aTED stage recounts an incident that involves a bit his life as a young designer.
It was the early '90s; in Silicon Valley, there was great excitement, and he thought that as a designer, if he had to design something, he had to design a laptop.
And he tells this story: he ends up in one of these American meetings, right?
you know, with these managers who are all a bit doped, they talk about empowerment, success, horizons, innovation...
He, perhaps a bit silly, raises his hand and says, "Sorry, concerning innovation what do you need this [NUM LOCK] for?
Are we sure that people want it?
I have accidentally pressed it the first time when I was 14, I was convinced that the computer
was possessed. It did no longer work.
What the hell serves the num lock?" Bear said these things to these managers, who looked at him and answered him, "You are a designer, think about making a beautiful object!" and in fact he resigned.
Because in his life Behar did not want to be a designer, to be a fashion designer, to play around with colors, with materials.
And from this lesson I learned another important keyword, which is 'service'.
The designer is at the service of mankind, and if there are any designers in here, design is sometimes the triumph of individualism.
Fantastic designers who design for other fantastic designers: the triumph of the ego at times.
Instead, I think that design is a very unselfish craft, one that has to be unselfish.
One of the things I love most about my job is to be with kids: to provide vocational guidance, to explain them what it means to be a designer.
I always try to say that a good designer is not only in his head or in his hands, in his ability to draw, it's not that.
Rather, it is especially in his gut and in his eyes.
Because we feel things in our gut.
When we are in love, when we are angry, what is it that makes us sick?
Our gut.
When we see for the first time the person we will love forever, what hurts us?
Our gut.
Life happens in our gut, and in our eyes there is time.
There's our time.
Another keyword, a German one, we are in Trentino Alto Adige "Zeitgeist".
A word that belongs to the 19th century philosophy the spirit of time, the feeling of time.
When I think of this word, I think of her: Coco Chanel.
It's not that Coco Chanel created her clothes just to make shapes, just to play around with materials.
Her clothes have been able to reflect the spirit of her time.
This woman invented the uniform of the modern woman.
She understood that women did no longer want to be angels of the hearth but "Women", in capital letters.
When we talk about creativity,
did she considered herself creative?
She didn't? She was creative.
People say that one who does something creative who does something unusual is innovative.
I want to turn this idea on its head a bit, because this woman did not do something innovative.
This woman has just stared at her time.
She looked straight at it, listened to it with her gut, felt empathy for her time and sometimes, what we call creative, it is simply common sense.
There is a beautiful book by Rifkin, by the way, about empathy, "The empathic consciousness".
And perhaps, design, today, really has to feel, to be great again, in empathy with people.
Maria Montessori, sometimes I think we forgot her in Italy, she did not create a school for children: she created the school of children.
It looks like a play on words: one preposition changes, actually it distorts the world of education.
But why?
Why she did not put herself at its core, she did not put a product or service, but she put people at the core of it and design today must be this, in my opinion.
She lived among children.
Design today is car, furniture, pop, it is fashion, trendy, it is visual; it is a lot of big words.
It ends up on newspapers covers, on glossy magazines.
But I wish that design tomorrow stops being a project for people, and turns into a project of people; and design can do so much for this world.
Because in design there is the power to create, the power to tell something more than a chair or an object.
And I hope the design enters schools.
I hope that design gets to hospitals, and social assistance.
I started working with social cooperatives, and you have no idea of how much this tool can also change people's lives.
And I would say one thing, it should be present in schools, design, how we teach Italian, Maths, Religion.
Design should be taught to our children from a young age.
Because design has to become something habitual.
And when I think of habitual I think of this girl, Emily Pilloton.
Because these things that I'm telling you are like a virus that began to explode and in ten years, but hopefully much earlier, I hope to see me here with all of you and that there are thousands of designers who have begun to do what she does or what Daniele Pario Perra will tell you about this afternoon. And that is to put
people at the core of it to bring back design in the midst of people.
I called this speech (English) "born to stay together", born to be together, the design is born to be with us.
Think that this girl uses design: two tires, soil, and chalk to invent a new way to teach math to children.
Storytelling, sense of time, empathy, the everyday, service, these are my keywords for design.
We approach Christmas, and this is the best wish: I want to wish design, so that it leaves behind shops and showrooms and comes back again in the midst of people, to teach us to be adults/children again brave and disenchanted.
Thank you.
(Applause)
We wake up, and the world has changed.
Let's wake up, the world has changed.
Let's wake up and change the world.
They are three sentences that use exactly the same words with very different meanings and tones.
The words are: awakening, of course, here; the world and its change.
I want to talk you about change.
We are not used to change.
Are we suitable for change?
We are unfit to change; as living organisms, we tend towards balance.
Anything that changes our state drives us to waste energy in order to get our balance back.
As economic operators, our destiny is to manipulate the production factors in order to obtain an advantage.
Any change in production factors or in the working context is a problem, it forces us to work, to convince people, to bring us back into balance and perhaps we will regain an advantage.
And then we are Europeans, Italians, Mantuans, we live in a corner of the world with the best lifestyle.
If late Steve Jobs were here and looked at us, he would not say: "Stay hungry".
I definitely don't think so, neither do you, I suppose.
(Risate) So that is not the spring that is leading us to change.
What change are we talking about?
Let's talk - there are two authors: Friedman is an American journalist, one who goes around the world interviewing influent people.
And Teller manages the world's largest research budget, he holds Google's research budget.
They agree that there is a very high level of change that they call "high-altitude change", featured by three parallel lines of speech.
The first line is natural, it's called climate change, we already heard the rain a little ago.
The second is the social change: the globalisation of people.
After the globalisation of goods, we are going through the phase of accelerated globalisation of people.
There are currently 350 million people moving from the place where they were born or grew up.
We do not realize that much, perhaps because 92 percent of them move within the same continent.
And then the third line of change is the technological change, Moore's law.
It has been for 18-20 years that we double every two years our data processing capacity.
Our ability and speed to process data is hugely higher than we can use.
These however, as we said before, are high altitude changes.
But close to us, on the skin, where we feel the drops of water, where we feel cold, there is another kind of change.
The first factor of this change, we may say, is speed,
Speed is very important, because we are realizing that if we go faster than it's needed, we smash against a wall.
We make mistakes more easily.
We can't take it anymore, to go faster than what we're doing now.
It is very likely that the response to speed is continuity.
Continuity is a factor that causes us to reorganize ourselves completely, changing our mental and organizational sets, both daily and long-term ones.
Let's get this straight: when you are in a hotel and you turn on the hot water tap, water gets instantly hot.
It takes a little longer at my house, with the boiler nine meters away.
Why?
Because in a hotel, or in a similar public place, there is a water circuit that keeps hot water in constant motion, so the hot water flows a few inches from the tap; and the moment you open it, it responds.
Here, try to imagine your organization, your life as if we were constantly keeping this system moving; those who have to innovate, for example, know it well.
If you start doing research when the market asks you for an answer, a product or a service, you can't do it.
Nobody gives you enough time to do what you need to do.
We are running out of time.
Either you're already in place, or you asked yourself questions when nobody asked you questions, or if the initiative comes from marketing, this means that someone among your customers has already seen something done by someone else, and it's too late.
The second important characteristic is authorship, because we have grown up in a world where we give great value to individual genius, the man of providence.
Solutions only come because somebody, at some point, thinks about it and is gifted to solve it.
When we know scientifically that collective intelligence is enormously more powerful than any single genius.
The third element is linked to the scarcity of resources.
We were born and raised, I mostly speak for my generation, remember the previous clip, thinking that everything will be available to us forever.
Instead, we find ourselves working in a circuit, in a system where all the outputs, everything we discard must be put back as input into another system.
Each output must become an input.
Circular Economy, call it as you like, System Design.
This, too, is a major change in the framework we work in.
And then, we grew up in a situation where knowledge is secured - as we said - in silos.
If I work in a sector, I better not invade the sector next door, right?
Mind you, they are called corporate divisions.
As clear as you can get, we also gave them the right name
so that you know, one doesn't speak to the other and if one ever does, they don't speak the same language.
Here, we discover that in recent times the most interesting things come from knowledge crossing: neuroscience and biotechnology are always situations where one meets and exchanges or looks at phenomena from multiple perspectives.
And then the product, the product!
We are - let me do it, the Made in Italy. The culture of the product.
We know how to evaluate with touch, smell, taste, flavor, sight, even at a distance, the quality of things.
We have, compared to the global average, a very high product culture.
Too bad this is a time when process quality rules.
The quality of the process became very important, we noticed.
There is a Channel 52 that some of you watch it, I am convinced, and so do I, that's focused on how to produce the jam, how tyres are made.
Things like this; it's an American broadcasting translated into 27 languages, 850 million people around the world watch it for at least four hours a week.
What will they make of their whole tyre-making culture?
It's a passion, though, isn't it?
Two years ago, I watched an episode of "Report" TV program on how geese are processed for duvet and unhappy geese which did not voluntarily donate.
The next day, at New York Stock Exchange, this company lost a capitalization of 180 million dollars.
I spent the first part of my life with my mom telling me, buy it, but make sure there's a duvet.
And the second half of my life with my wife saying: "You're crazy, you're a monster!
You bought something with a duvet in it!"
I'm no longer sure what to buy.
(Laughter) And then material issues, material things, and we grew up in the material.
We are in a world where we either deal with material things, because we have a body, or we deal with digital things.
Yet we find ourselves living and working in a world that finally understood that every material thing must have a digital twin. Otherwise, it doesn't exist.
But every digital thing must have a material twin.
The material twin is fundamental. Amazon also knows that: the world's single economic player that could not have appear on the scene, is now trying to open brick-and-mortar stores around the world.
It's essential, we have a body; if you're in the digital world without appearing to our senses, you do not exist.
And then again privacy, privacy:
they're spying on us, be careful.
No one else minded our business,
we thought, except maybe some friends.
No, our things enters the public and global domain, maybe everyone knows them but us.
(Laughter) So imagine in a world where information is the main asset, our information are spread out and whoever is willing can use it.
This change is a bold one, it has to do with our ability to deal with it and with our ability to handle something we call anticipation.
They are the future sciences, there are many scholars from many different fields who several decades ago started studying the future as a scientific phenomenon.
And they can give us some directions.
We are going very fast.
We may have already heard about their first piece of information: The future cannot be predicted.
They say, you can build it; interesting.
The second information they give us
is that the future - we know portions of it.
Imagine a cake; we own some slices,
and one of these slices are invariants.
If man has been on Earth - cognitively speaking, so to say - for 250,000 years and has behaved in certain ways for 250,000 years, this means that probably tomorrow, I do not know what should happen to change our behavior.
Therefore, through the study of constants - anthropology deals with them - we have portions of certainty.
And quantitative data on everything: demographics, climate - everything.
Since 1,500 AC, the parish archives of the Christian world collect newborn names, their birth and death date, when they were married, when they were baptized and so on.
Imagine the amount of data we have and that allows us to make curves and have an ambition of perspective.
Then we also have qualitative data, like trends:
fashion lives on trend, we know what color will be a must next year.
You know this year is the coral, right?
We already know for next year.
So imagine how much data we have, both qualitative and quantitative.
And then we have the "latents", which is a bit of a difficult word for us.
We called it in our world, the world of fashion design, we called it the weak signals.
Weak signals are something that is already in the present but anticipates the future.
Imagine that time is this thing here.
This is the past and this is the future.
We are in the present, we cut it here.
What is the present?
The present is the permanence of some elements of the past. You see my fingers shrinking and the beginning of some factors that are already part of the future.
And so, by studying and removing the elements of the past, we have a portion of the future
and if we analyze it compared to its current dynamics, the spread, how it grows, we already have elements that allow us to study the future.
And then, future sciences people tell us, there is no singular future; there are futures.
There are many futures and they compete to come into being.
So we have to work on scenarios, to figure out how to adapt to the various possible futures.
For example, we are also told the future is a collective matter.
Collective!
The more people we can convince that the future desirable for us will also be desirable for them, the more likely it is for that future to come true.
A child who wants to go to the stadium knows this; he begins to stalk his father, then his uncle, then his mother, his sister, his sister-in-law's cousin... the more people he gets to involve, the more likely it is he will be taken to the stadium.
And so the future, let us remember, is a collective fact.
And then, future begins in the present, so the action we take today is an important part of what will happen tomorrow.
We also saw it with toilet paper [in the TED video].
And then the future includes attitudes.
We have attitudes towards the future: there are four of them.
The first attitude is an Italy's world excellence.
It's called an urgent attitude.
A disaster has happened before, something.
Come on, everybody, let's patch it up.
The patch.
I think we are the ones who invented that.
If we had a patent on the patch, we'd be so rich, wouldn't we?
Then there is a second one, called "emerging attitude".
Some elements are emerging; if we keep them under control, they anticipate something that is already happening, and so we begin to take action.
The third behavior is called preactive attitude.
Nothing is happening now, but something will,
and so I begin to forecast it - they're called scenarios - and study how to best behave should some of these scenarios come true.
And I can do it both to counteract them and to make them happen, if they are favorable.
The fourth attitude is called proactive attitude.
And it is precisely the action of putting energy into investing so that favorable scenarios comes true and the bad ones do not.
And then another last element on which futurology and future sciences, anticipation, lead us to reflect, and which is very important to be able to work on tomorrow, the timeframe we mostly care about. Not tomorrow literally, the 26th, but tomorrow in the world of marketing: next year, next collection, next season, the product I'll bring to the exhibition, the holidays I have to organize.
So, to understand if my tomorrow is right, I have to study the day after tomorrow
because it is the day after tomorrow that gives me the direction.
If you meet somebody telling you: "Am I going, right?" you have at least to ask him: "It depends where you are headed," right?
And so the prophetic words of two great Italians, Totò and Peppino, who said: "Volevam savuar, noio volevan savuar, [We wanted to know] to go where we have to go, where do we have to go?" The prophecy is already there.
I mean, if we don't know where we are going, how do we choose the right way to get there?
Thank you for your attention.
(Applausi)
Being called a "changemaker" is so amazing.
It's a word that was used with reverence – almost reverential fear – for decades, so now I really want to contribute to dispelling this idea.
Actually, you don't need to be a Nobel Prize winner; you don't need to be a genius or a hero:
everyone, in their own small or big way, can be an activator – actor – of change.
There are countless examples: I think of street children in Haiti who renovated the road safety system; of one single German female researcher who managed to connect refugee scientists with local ones; of a North American teenage girl who wrote hundreds of letters and succeeded in convincing the education authorities to change the textbooks that featured stereotypical images of women.
So now, the bridge I would like to help create this morning – the change I want to face – concerns the third sector, the so-called non-profit.
In particular, the way we look at third sector organizations and how we relate to them.
With "third sector" we refer to a galaxy of associations, NGOs, volunteer organizations, social enterprises, cooperatives, foundations, and charities.
340,000 organizations, 820,000 employees, 6.5 million volunteers here in Italy. A sector that has grown by 60% in the last 15 years and today accounts for 8% of the Italian industrial system.
But why are we interested in the third sector?
It is not, it's not just for the numbers.
We are interested in the third sector because it deals with the causes we have at heart; because it cares about humanity, the planet and the most vulnerable people we love.
We care about the third sector because it makes the difference for a better world because it has the ability to imagine the impossible and rally hundreds, thousands, millions of people around a common cause.
Seeing mentally challenged people first and foremost as people; referring to disabled children as simply children; taking care of the elderly as “resource people” or of a sick child as a child and not just as a patient. Offering shelter to women victims of domestic violence, fighting racism, promoting interculturalism among new citizens and helping people understand that we live in an interconnected world, one in which, for example, the extinction of sea turtles in Policoro can wreak havoc on the biodiversity of the entire planet.
Ok, the current climate crisis concerns only some people and not others but, at the end of the day, we’re all on the same boat.
Not only do third sector organizations bridge the vacuum, or the abyss, left by Italy’s obsolete and inefficient welfare system; not only do third sector organizations substitute for others; third sector organizations can – this day an age I’d better say they must – make the difference: change the world, transform society, trigger processes that can generate value, not only in terms of the end result they produce, but all along the way – a long and winding road – that they themselves have paved.
So, this morning I would like to take the opportunity to bust two myths, two fake myths – two ideological walls – two fake myths that, as a matter of fact, have prevented the third sector of our country from changing the world, keeping it under the thumb and leaving it in a situation of total subjection and dependence.
Two fake myths reducing structural costs to the bone and working only on a project cycle basis.
As to the former: the ruling mantra is "Third sector organizations must cost us little money.
All the funds are to be allocated to projects."
So, no funds, no money for communication, fundraising, management, as well as for technological, digital, and administrative development.
It is a path, an isomorphism process that creates a hunger cycle.
Donors do not want to finance structural costs, third sector organizations adjust accordingly and start to cut structural costs, donors finance fewer and fewer structural costs.
In our country, this dogma is undisputed in all the procedures and practices of public and private donors, but also among public opinion.
Even when we donate, when we give our tax donation, when we contribute money for a cause, when we sign a petition or campaign, when we join crowdfunding, we want to finance projects, not organizations.
So, this ideology has given life to numerous project hubs among third sector organizations, with all too often inadequate organizations, structures, and staff.
A lot of young people in our country – maybe our best brains and hearts – wish to change the world, wish to be the protagonists of this change; they approach the third sector and then they realize that not only for a lifetime, every year, they would earn 35-40% less than in other for-profit sectors, but that they would also end up working in underdeveloped and underequipped organizations, which are asked to do more and more with less.
So, in my opinion, the question is: “Why is it that in every sector, companies – generally speaking, the organizations that invest in people, that invest in skills, that invest in financial development, in communication – are those that are most likely to be successful?”.
Yet, this is not true for the third sector. And even entrepreneurs that know very well how important it is to invest in the organization of their company when it comes to philanthropic activities, only want to finance projects.
In Italy, this ideology is deeply rooted in different traditions: the culture of Catholic volunteering, the subconscious of a country in which – for hundreds of years – social services were carried out for free by the Church and, particularly, by unpaid women – both religious and lay.
A series of “window-dressing” declarations in the fight against the culture of craftiness so as to prevent smart, or pseudo-smart, people from managing to finance friends of friends. The general lack of social faith in our country and, on the part of donors, the fear of taking responsibility for their choices.
Third sector organizations are desperately in need of support, flexible funds, of funds for organizations, funds for missions, for organizations to be strong, capable, innovative, and creative.
Funds for missions, and not only on a project basis.
So, let's move on to the second point:
projects can be a very good thing, but they are an end in themselves, tied in a centripetal bowline knot, twisted on themselves, unable to transform society, incapable of building a systematic transformation and sparking social change.
Working on a project basis means working to attain specific aims, reinventing a team with consultants, collaborators, and project-dedicated employees each time; it means working for extremely short periods of time and time frames – six months, a year, two years – periods that are ridiculously short when really investing in social change.
I think of Amazon, which operated at a loss the first six years and still had a patient capital: that patient – flexible – capital that the third sector lacks.
To deal with major issues – increasing inequality, racism, climate change, cultural impoverishment, today’s big issues, all intersectional issues - we need to invest in interdisciplinary missions, in missions, and give organizations the flexibility to invest in communication, to invest in fundraising, to invest in digital technologies, in the organization itself.
The organizations and people are in place, what we really need to change
is the approach we use. On the side of donors, how to commit money, to fund, support - go beyond the logic of the provider that gives funds to the beneficiary and the beneficiary that receives them.
Instead, we need to build a long-term financial partnership –
and not for six months, a year, two years – but really for 5-7 years, at least, and be able to finance on a mission basis.
On the part of third sector organizations, it is also necessary to stop adapting and therefore be able to actually say what the required investments and costs are; to avoid the isomorphism that forced them to think: "I exist because I make projects and I make projects to exist" while trying to secure tenders.
There are already many pilot initiatives – very few, unfortunately, in our country, for the time being – new financing options different from tenders, which are still prevalent in our country.
In my opinion, today we do have the opportunity to build a new bridge – a new bridge between donors, investors, financers and third sector organizations – which is actually a bridge that concerns us all
and in which each one of us, all of us together, can truly make the difference.
Young people approaching the third sector for the first time and not so young people who, instead, want to give what they have acquired in terms of skills and competences: all together we can make the difference because this is a real cultural change
and so the bridge that is to be built is a change, first and foremost, in the way of thinking and in terms of culture.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Armin Holzer: I'm Armin Holzer,
I'm 28 and I come from Sesto Pusteria, from South Tyrol and, living in the Dolomites, I've had the fortune to discover a passion for the mountains.
From a young age and also thanks to my parents who took me to the mountains, I learnt many different sporting disciplines and one of these is the slackline.
Living in the mountains I discovered the mountains but also air space.
This is a slackline which as you see links from left to right, and I put it in the high mountains and walk from one peak to the other.
Walking on this line makes me feel free, it connects me with nature, and walking above here and now could seem like an individual sport, it seems like I'm here alone, but, behind all this, there is always a great team of friends.
And this simple red band doesn't only serve as a bridge between mountains but also between cultures and mentalities, and different personalities.
That's why I present to you my friend Alessandro D'Emilia, with whom I've done so many things together.
(Applause)
Alessandro d'Emilia: Hello!
Hello everyone,
My name is Alessandro and I've also had the fortune, like Armin, to live in the mountains since childhood: in Misurina in the heart of the Dolomites.
This life in the mountains causes you to see the same panorama every day through different eyes.
The first time that I met Armin was about 5 years ago: we were quite close to a mountain which everyone knows, the Tre Cime, below which there is a refuge.
We met there.
I saw him with a backpack almost double the size of him and I asked him what he had been doing.
He replied, "I went highlining". I said straight away, "What? Highline?" I had never heard this word before.
Careful, watch what you're doing!

AH: I'm in front of all these people.
Not like the mountains, I'm a bit tense!
AdE: From that day
we got to know each other and we went to explore the peaks of those mountains together, but not only with climbing shoes or with skis, but carrying this strap with us which had united us,
helped us to get to know each other. The first one of these crossings was on the Cima Grande in Lavaredo: We left early on that day, we arrived at the peak, we set everything up and managed to walk across in the same day.
But I can say that that slackline taught us <i>that</i>-don't leave dangling items at the end of a line even in every day life, it's worth living to the fullest, in every moment that we find ourselves.
From that moment I began to sleep on the peaks of the mountains, because when you're climbing or skiing, you try to go up and come down quickly.
This slackline however made us relax on the peaks of the mountains, to stroll, to enjoy the view from an exclusive and unique viewpoint.
That morning on the Tre Cime we woke up to a sunrise that we'll remember for the rest of our lives.
We woke up and were above a sea of clouds, as often happens in the mountains.
It was an experience that has remained imprinted in our heart and made us look to other horizons.
AH: Exactly.
Speaking of dreams,
That morning, I woke up a few hours before you. I was there upside down, looking outside and I fell straight back to sleep.
I thought I was dreaming: in the end it turned out to be real, and it was beautiful.
To quote Walter Bonatti, "He who climbs higher, sees further",
and for us it was a bit...

AdE: Inspirational.
AH: Yeah, kind of an inpsiration
to try new things.
At some point Massimo Braconi, a great friend of Belluno, invited me to participate in an expedition to China, to climb the Muztagh Ata with skis, which is one of the highest mountains of the Pamir, in China, and after giving it some thought,
after a few days of calculations -
because it costs money to get there.
AdE: We were young,
and weren't working much.
AH: True! But I accepted.
I told him, "But why only take skis?
Let's take the slackline too, let's try to do something new".
I was keen to try and walk suspended in thin air, which no-one had ever tried before.
And so I proposed this project to Ale, and he also said yes straight away.
Arriving in China we soon realised that walking in thin air isn't like walking at the sea, let's say.
Everything is different, internally, your mind changes.
AdE: Yes, even the way you react to what happens.
AH: Everything.
Luckily, when we arrived there at the base camp, which is about 4,000 meter high, we found a lake near the camp which allowed us to train ourselves at that altitude.
Since it was the first time in thin air for us, it gave us a bit of security.
We didn't know how the body would react, how we would acclimatise, we were worried.
You said, "I have to drink more, there's a risk of being unwell, mountain sickness".
This slackline helped us to acclimatise better, because it was something we knew well, it gave us a lot of faith and tranquillity.
While we were climbing the mountain - to climb these high mountains you have to go up and down to acclimatise yourself - at some point we found a perfect spot about 5,000 m high to set up a highline and we dedicated ourselves to realising this project, because we were very curious to see what happens when you walk suspended at 5,000 m.
At the beginning of the crossing, you're afraid, everything is strange.
I was sitting on this strap, I turned around and I said, "Let's hope the anchors hold".
Even though you know everything's secure, everything's double checked.
But even then, I have to say, without Ale's help, it would never have been possible to do something like this.
I got to one side, I crossed to the other and I arrived like I was in a utopia.
It seemed like I was awake in a dream, and it was quite a strong sensation, and afterwards, when he went, perhaps he'll explain, how he went, how he had
this mantra in his head.
AdE: Of course, Armin's initial point that Slackline can seem like an individual sport, but in reality there's a team and a friendship that's based on trust, because sometimes there's not even time to speak.
You leave and you decide: one goes from one side, the other from the other side; you put tension on the strap, to assure each other that everything's fine.
Because of training, you have the certainty that you can trust your partner, your friend.
But at times not even that external motivation is enough, but we have to find it in ourselves.
Each one of us does it their own way, according to what they like to do, how they feel in the moment.
In that moment, I remember perfectly that out of nowhere a phrase came into my head and I couldn't get rid of it for the entire crossing.
This phrase was: the passion in the heart frees the mind the mind frees the passion in the heart
At a certain point, understanding this, I couldn't stop saying it because I instantly found a connection between what we feel, between what we desire truly and sincerely, every desire, which also requires awareness and the will to believe it to the end, otherwise you'll never achieve it. AH: To do it in the moment...
AdE: Do it in the moment and the place.
It's not enough just to have an idea, you also need the process that will enable you to achieve it, that's the thing that enriches you the most.
This day, we could say, ended with this incredible backdrop above the icy peaks of the Muztagh Ata.

AH: We were taking a break, relaxed.
AdE: At one point
a small eagle took flight
and was circling above us.
AH: It was enormous, not small!
(Laughter) AdE: You understand at once that you're in the right place at the right time and this gives you the strength to go on, to believe in what you're doing and to always think of new projects, of new dreams to achieve with other people and with your friends.
Unfortunately, like all good things, you have to awake from your dreams and return home.
And in that moment, when we returned home in Misurina, the landscape which seemed to us to already be fully explored, which we felt was our home, we looked at it again, and we had other experiences which we had already experienced with new emotions in our heart and other horizons to be able to discover.
And on the Monte Piana, a mountain close to the Tre Cime, we went up one afternoon with our skis and sealskins to do some trekking.
We realised that this mountain was a magnificent spot.
It was a plateau...
AH: ... with canyons, where you can set up
hundreds of different lines
ADE: And it was in our own backyard.
Returning from this experience we found new possibilities to be able to train ourselves and to be able to enjoy what we had to the fullest, right in our backyard, without having to travel thousands of kilometers to have the motivation to believe in.
And this took us right away to another thought: that this place was so beautiful that we couldn't keep it for ourselves and to do slacklining, as nice as we are, there were only ever two of us.
We began to invite friends, they too became enthusiastic, by this point four or five years later. AH: 5th year. AdE: yeah, this will be the 5th year - it grew and became a kind of community which comes together each year for a meeting of highliners and athletes from around the world: more than 25 different nationalities, this year there were more than 350 of us.
On this very mountain, that most people know for its tragic history because along the Tre Cime the Dolomites boundary line passed through, 100 years ago during the First World War.
So for us it was already a very important place that made us reflect when we were there suspended and walking on this mountain.
The fact that we could share it again, to have the fortune to experience this place in the company of other young people from around the world, was a real achievement of an even bigger dream, that wasn't only a record or something personal, but the possibility to share those emotions which came from a tiny crib and then evolved over time.
AH: Also to start the winter
not with the roar of bombs, but with our song, and be up there.
AdE: With silence,
rather than the racket of bombs.
But this boundary line was also very important for us, because it was our backyard, and also in these times in which the borders are unfortunately places of conflict and hot-spots, this was a strong message for us: to be able to dedicate ourselves not only to what we like doing, but also to live and see how these sporting activities can help to improve, to grow even in everyday life and to believe in what you're doing.
What do you say? Do we still have time?
Have we talked enough?

AH: We've prepared a little video.
AdE: We still have time
to show you some pictures: this passion for photography that we have evolved at the same time with the will to tell a story and share the different experiences, and so, our greetings and we thank you for your attention that you've given us until now and we leave you with this video which in two minutes will attempt to gather and transmit to you also, in this beautiful theatre, the atmosphere which we had on the Monte Piana a few months ago.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
"And this is design food: design-based foods are nicer" "Why are this design?" "Because we used design cakes - this is a design cupcake.
And these are design tortellini, which are so good. Design cupcakes are so good, with a little salt, lots of olive oil. And this was made with little salt, and plenty of fish. (Applause) This was a little girl, two and half years old, to whom I asked this question: "Why are these design tortellini, why is this design food?
She was playing in the kitchen, just like all kids do,
and she replied, because it’s good and it’s delicious!.
How many of you, if I speak food and design, think of something beautiful, esthetically interesting, coloured or decorated?
Raise your hands. Almost everyone.
If I were to ask how many of you think that it also means good, then perhaps there would be fewer people in agreement.
No worries, 90% of people think that design is only connected to food aesthetically.
This is what I am asked every time I do a presentation on food and design,
such as "come with the beautiful examples of cake design, a beautiful dish, something colourful, a lovely restaurant".
Today I would like to talk with you about something different, and show you that the design of the world of food can also have other applications, something that perhaps is not that well known.
Think of the great economic damage in Marche, Central Italy, after the earthquake, because the stables were destroyed. The animals were dying of hunger and cold.
A young designer, a student of design, met a farmer and said, "Why don’t we use these drones to bring feed to the animals in need?
This also gives us the chance to monitor the situation, and hopefully find a solution to the problems".
In Syria, 95% of some cities were completely destroyed, buildings were torn down to dust.
Now we hope, after eight years of conflict, that these people can return home. Yet, their places will have no buildings, their cities will need reconstruction.
And rebuilding is the easiest part:
the idea will be to feed all the people in these cities.
So let’s imagine that from the rubble we can create new bricks, from which we can produce and cultivate edible food.
In these growing cities, where the population continues to rise, how will we feed the city?
There are technologies, such as hydroponics and aquaponics that can be used for these emerging scenarios, and we can use water, air for our crops.
Understanding the flows of immigration in the Mediterranean is no easy business.
Yet the only consistently sure thing is, people in the Mediterranean Sea are tied by their ingredients. We have always shared the same ingredients.
Let’s imagine an instrument that binds us together in a dialogue, as with the same instrument we can cook all the kitchens of the Mediterranean.
Belonging and integration, instead of confrontation and attack.
Packaging helps us in many ways, such as preservation, transportation and an improved ability not to waste.
However, it can also be true that packaging itself is a waste, because not all packaging is biodegradable and is absolutely not natural.
So let’s imagine a different kind of packaging, that comes from food itself,
a packaging that will expire when the food inside will.
Think of a packaging made from olive oil, rice or algae.
Eurisko researchers tell us that 51% of people are interested in this model, or have already used a take away service.
So this is a big trend. But what if takeaway food was also handmade, home-made food, as it used to be, following traditions? So we could rebuild good neighborhood relationships because we can buy from people next door,
and this food could become a means of communication and reunion.
70% of marine birds and 80% of turtles eat plastic.
I have no data for fish, but they also do and we eat them afterwards.
An artisanal beer company met a designer and they thought, "why don’t we make biodegradable plastic beer rings, so they can dissolve, made with the leftovers of the beer, of the fermentation process, like wheat and barley, to create something that will dissolve in the ocean, and in turn become food for animals?".
A food system that creates another one,
because food can change and become something else.
Our leftovers can become stuff to feed 3D printers to create new objects. For example,
what if farmers could produce containers from their leftovers and waste. These farmers could find in design, in turn, network-based solutions. A network that could allow them to solve many problems of the current market.
As you can see, I’ve used many labels: "design for food", "urban food system design","social food design". These are some of the categories we use, and it is also hard, often, to draw a line to assess where social design ends, or systemic design ends.
Let's just say, all of this is design: design applied throughout the food supply chain.
What is design?
Design is nothing more than the skills and knowledge we need to solve complex problems.
Design, in the English language means "planning",
not "beauty", like we often think.
There is no doubt, design has changed over time because societies, and their needs, have also changed. But it is always design.
At the beginning there was the industrial expansion, we were always in search of new products, objects and therefore, there was an intensive use of industrial design, in what became popular as "industrial design".
As we evolve, scenarios change, design is applied in different contexts: the attention starts with a focus on the user, then on the interaction between users and objects, and then, with the digital increasingly present, to a very high experiential level. So we focus on the experience.
Imagine that a designer must raise your interest for a piece of plastic, like your iPhone. It is simply a piece of plastic, but how many experiences you make with it?
Possibilities for a designer become countless,
yet there is also a constant risk to create unnecessary, useless things.
So we re-focus on the mankind, humans, their values.
Design compels us to think, what are the real values for men?
In food, this is fundamental, because the values are determined by the relationship we have with food.
Our relationship with food comes from cultural mediations that we create all across the food chain.
The food chain is long - production, distribution, marketing, consumption and post-consumption. We mentioned the waste, the education.
It is fundamental to work and project on top of human values.
When people say that food design, or design applied to food, is a new trend, it is not so true, as I mentioned before: during industrial booming, design approached food producing, creating and manufacturing new products and objects.
I think that you will recognize this:
It's the wheel of licorice, a patented design since 1934.
[Design does need food] Back then, and with the economic boom, and industrial expansion, we can almost say, design mostly needed food. The more food there was, the bigger the need to design.
Which also partly explains why design sold its soul to enemies, with marketing and advertising. The more they designed, the more not-so-healthy products came on the market.
However, we should also acknowledge design for what happened. In fact, design managed to create identities, something we have brought over time; to create a simpler, more mobile, efficient, effective life and also to give us icons, that have become the icons of the design culture.
Sure, there are also interesting, splendid, cute projects that are not really useful in daily life.
For example, these are office objects to conveniently eat a snack at the office.
If it is true that food design is not new, or the application of design in the agri-business is not new, it also became true, much more recently, that the interest of design turned to the agribusiness world.
What does it mean?
It is more recent, in fact: it dawned at the onset, or slightly after food studies.
Food studies are academic studies on food.
Food Studies didn’t start in Italy:
they started in the US
at the end of the 90s. People started asking, Where does my food come from?
Is it healthy, sustainable?
What will be the food of the future?
All of these questions required responses, and academics must respond, and find the answers, analyze the system and find the solutions. Because in 2018, we still live in a world where people are dying from too much food and others for lack of food.
We know how to create sustainable systems and we have fancy technology that can make this all possible, but we don’t do it!
And with all that waste, we could feed and give resources three to four times over to all the people in need. I am not only talking about Africa.
So there is a real interest from the food world to use design because it can offer solutions.
[Food needs Design] This is what design is asked to provide: solutions to solve the problems.
Fact is, food design is nothing new, but the interest has changed. And I experienced it firsthand.
I even have a personal anectode that I shouldn’t share, but I will anyway. In 2012, I had just finished my PhD studies on the use of new technologies in the agri-food sector to see how new technologies could change our way of eating.
And how design could help the process.
I went to a conference in New York where I presented the results of my research.
There were only ten people, maximum, in the audience and left before the end of my speech,
while muttering, to the best of my understanding, "Let's hope the world this Italian lady refers to will never happen…"
One even came to say, "I had so much fun".
So that was the end of the presentation.
I have to say, I left the venue frustrated. I thought, maybe I should forget this, no one will ever care about this design thing.
So for three years, I never talked design at food studies conferences.
Inspired by the 2015 Expo, in Milan,
food systems operators gained back confidence in what they were doing.
I was invited again to speak at a conference in the US about "Creativity and Food".
I decided to join, and I did something I was not supposed to: I changed the date on the first slide then used my previous material of the 2012 presentation.
There were 50 more people, all of whom stayed till the end,
with applause, questions - a big success.
I shared this with some - not everyone, not to upset them. But anyway, I told them, "This is actually not my speech! You think I’m anticipating you the food of the future: it's not, this is the past.
I presented it in 2012, and it was already the past back then.
This is just the proof that your interest in design has changed. You are more interested in what I’m presenting today because you are now finding new solutions in it.
You have changed your point of view, I'm not saying anything new.
For me this was very important.
In fact, since 2014, many conferences were held on food, design and their applications.
We have spread the message to professors, teachers and even ordinary people, because these events, these conferences, were open to the general public and people were really interested in the subject of food design.
But - you know what?
We have not yet affected those who make food, the true actors of the agri-food chain.
Our contamination is still weak:
we haven't spread this message to farmers, beekeepers, breeders,
restaurant owners, food producers, food companies, food industry, managers, those involved in tourism, mayors, agronomists, food engineers, politicians -
sorry, I could not find the politicians' playmobile, so this is what's left.
The thing that attracts everyone about design is the concept of creativity.
Creativity is a simple concept. This idea of creativity came out with a popular term now:
“design thinking”.
Design thinking means: “I want to learn how to think like a designer”.
What do I mean?
I like a lot this scene of Big Hero 6, a fantastic animated movie:
if you haven’t seen it, I recommend it.
The older brother tells his little brother, who is a genius, but is stuck in his thought process, "Look at things from a different angle, and you will get an idea". Look at the scenarios with a different perspective.
This is how to think like a designer. And although it is true that everyone, more or less, can be creative, it is also true we're not all designers.
Ezio Manzini, a great Italian design theorist, said: "Everybody knows how to sing, some can play music, but not everybody is a director".
So, who is a designer?
Designers are people who channel their intuitions.
The difference between intuitions and design is that the designer uses intuition to help guide the design.
A designer is this creative person who helps other people become creative, the customers they design for, and the people who work for him.
A designer is the person who can become a real change-maker, and help others to become change-makers.
So let's imagine a designer who, starting from simple rituals, as the designer doesn’t invent, but finds actual rituals, and exalts them through new products. From a simple ritual, like smelling food, an idea like this may come out. An application that tells you this apple at the market is better because it has a good smell.
What would change?
The market itself, as we wouldn’t buy apples simply for their beauty or low price, but because they smell good.
This would bring people to revive a skill that, sadly, we are all losing: "How food does smell like?"
This would change the economy, of the food market, the experiential concept, and the aesthetics of food.
Design is always systemic, it is always transdisciplinary.
What do I mean?
It brings together disciplines and competences,
creates an environment where they contaminate each other.
Design uses empathy and cognitive flexibility to help people speak to each other.
And we need design, we really need design,
especially since the UN stated that by 2030, these 17 goals must be reached and food is a transversal element.
If we do not begin to work together in a systemic manner, they will be hard to reach.
Design is a tool that we can use for this purpose. And believe me, in future jobs, there will be much more design.
In the food sector, in particular.
This is Zoe, my daughter, now five years old, from the same video that I showed you earlier, when she was two and a half.
Her favorite toy is a camera that I got for her,
thinking it was a regular press-and-shoot system. Instead, inside there is an app that allows the subject of the photo to become a character in a story. She can then add music and words, and create stories with it.
It came with no instructions' manual and I am certainly not the person who taught her how to use it.
She has used this object since she has been using for three years without getting tired of it, enhancing her creativity.
My daughter is a so-called "digital native", as she was born into a world where technologies are easy for her, because it is part of the culture she was kind of born with.
It comes natural to her to acquire tech knowledge, behaviours.
My wish is for he to become a sustainable native and live in a world of sustainable natives.
What do I mean?
To be born and grow in a world where all the services, systems and products we have allow us to easily live a sustainable, healthy life. I imagine a people-friendly city, places for food education, a house that tells me about food and its own life cycle that I have to respect, industrial products that take the environment into account and respect it.
John Tackara, a theorist of design, said: if we succeed, one day, to make biodiversity and sustainability as sexy and as interesting as the digital world is today for us all, we will be on the right path.
So maybe here is the answer: "Why does food need design?'
Design can create and recreate new relationships between us and our food.
Thanks.
(Applause)
Hello guys, hello everybody!
And thanks to Flavia for the excellent talk that already well introduced my own talk.
So, I’ll be very brief, I just start from the prehistory, in the computer meaning of the term.
But don’t be scared, Claudio, I finish in the time slot I have.
Let’s begin with when I was young, at that time it was rare to find somebody with knowledge about computer science. Thus like me, many others have started learning something alone, in their little bedroom.
I still remember very well the first experience: I was about ten years old, my father bought me a computer. A computer that still I didn’t know what to do with.
So I’ve taken the PC, I went in my room with a book and started to copy for two days, without interrupting me. I started writing and writing something, a.k.a. "coding",
and after two days, magically I’ve seen an object appearing on the screen. That went flying, and flying, it was fantastic! And flying, but didn’t stop.
I had to unplug the computer to regain back control of the computer.
That was a fantastic experience,
where I’ve learnt two things: One that the computer is stupid (with all due respect),
you have to explain everything to it, step by step, one thing at a time.
Second, that I was not so much more smart
then everybody, I have just to put a bit of my self, reasoning a bit,
logically organize the thoughts,
and that’s it.
Ant this I simply liked. I decided that our life together would have been long. Because there were and there are many things to do yet.
Indeed, as also Flavia told previously, there are a lot of people passionate of computer science, kids, adults, teenagers, and people that simply have to use them every day, whether we like it or not.
Smartphone, tablet, laptop, computers of every kind, it’s part of our life now.
It is not pure and merely prerogative of the experts.
It is part of every profession, occupation, even a craftsman needs a computer beside.
There may be something that can be frightening in here,
it can scare that our children are prisoners of the technology, passive of this technology, one might wonder it.
But answer is simple, it’s no,
it’s part of the life evolution,
it's part of the technology evolution.
Every time there is a new technology we need just to understand something new,
and the only way of doing that is to teach our kids and to ourselves to use the technology, make it part of the every day life being active about it, understanding how it works.
Easy to do, isn't it?
I don’t know, but I tried. I have my experience of this.
I’ve taken my child, the elder one, I’ve been waiting he was big enough, that was able to read and write, having a minimum of mathematical skills, having a minimum of logical reasoning. So I’ve taken the computer and put it in front of me, I’ve taken my son and put beside me.
And I’ve started slamming on the keyboard and from time to time looking at my son with the air of saying, "Anyway he doesn’t understand." In any case I was explaining
something about what I was doing.
How it ended up? Very well.
After an hour I finished to implement my function simulating the coin tossing with its graphical Gaussian representation of the probability to get head or tail.
Just great, I enjoyed as usual. My son?

Ah, yes, very good for him too,
was sleeping, like a little angel on the corner of the table.
Well, I said to myself, too bad, my son doesn’t like computers.
It doesn’t matter, there're many things
to do it’s not a big deal. However at the end I thought also that maybe I’m using the wrong approach,
may be he needs something more pedagogic, something closer to the children.
So I’ve started looking
for something else. First place I have been, was the school, but it was light years away from something computer oriented, something that would teach to my son to be technology-ready, to be a friend of the technology, an active part of it.
Looking ahead,
it was 2012, not long ago, we were living in Ireland, I came across to a movement, - currently a no profit foundation called CoderDojo - that was doing computer programming courses for young people for free.
Super; I haven’t hesitated a second, I’ve taken all my children plus some friends of them, and we started participating.
At the beginning there was something strange, a method a bit let’s say, anarchic, no guru at the blackboard dictating notions and telling, "Now push the bottom left button or the upper right one."
No, it was not like this,
it was a bit different, and I’m going to explain how it works using the rules written by Barbara Di CoderDojo Milano.
So, CoderDojo is based on a couple of rules, seven to be exact, the seven golden rules.
The first one is "learn by doing",
already a first difference from my approach and what I've seen at CoderDojo,
is the child in front of the computer, it is him that starts slamming the keyboard, what ever he’s doing, doesn’t matter, because he’s competent.
We have to start from the kid's
competences, and let him grow.
OK? They know what they need to do, somehow.
Of course at this point we don't have to stay in front, we have to stay besides them, and we follow them.
The teachers, so called mentors, don’t stay in front of them but beside and turn around to visit all of them, trying also to encourage them: the older children, who already came several times, explain what they know and how to proceed.
This is simple because in the learning curve the most important thing is when you start teaching.
OK? First you listen,
after you start doing and when you teach you learn better.
This is important.
Furthermore, we like mistakes,
children has the right to be wrong, as in every matter, like artisans for example, is the practice the most important thing,
and the technology in nothing more,
we have just to practice: stay there, coding, doing.
And if you make something wrong is not like the carpenter, a little trick, with computer you push CTRL-Z
and you get back and you can redo, easy.
After that, "Don't say no!", positive thinking.
It is useless to say to a kid, "You are not able to do that!" but rather saying, "Look for me you could do better!", or also, "Help me please that we do that together!" It’s a strange approach I know, very different from our usual habits.
And especially we have to encourage because every body has his own competences and whatever step they do, just take it as it is and be positive and encourage him to continue on that way.
And the most important thing of all, enjoy!
Learn with fun.
Going further, use a more adapted tool for starting.
Indeed even here in my experience I took my usual programming text editor, the one I normally use and it is tough to start.
Rather, there are tools like this one, Scratch, that has been built by the MIT from Boston, which is a graphic tool, but nothing is taken off from how developing applications, the coding way of thinking.
It’s like a LEGO and every piece has is own reason to be there. And children learn to compose them building the computer program naturally.
And the fact they have immediately a graphic feedback, it allows them to understand what they have done. If they have been done wrong, right or what is missing.
This allows iteratively to build their program.
What happened before coming to CoderDojo Italia?
A year ago we left Ireland to come back to Italy.
There were already few dojos in Italy
but not in the city where I moved, here in Trento.
So I had the desire that my children could continue to enjoy coding with CoderDojo. So I started asking around and immediately, really very easily I’ve found a lot of enthusiasm and in a few days we have created CoderDojo Trento.
With a lot of passion and help, here in Trentino, of young people and adults. In few months we have created five centers, dojos, that more or less alternatively every week provide a session.
Here, only in Trento and vicinity.
This is a contribute to a movement that in Italy is growing very well and fast, because it's important.
As Flavia already has shown before, a month ago, the 22nd of October we have had the opportunity to go to the Parliament and the Ministry, and we have done a normal very stunning session in the "Queen’s room" in the presence of some politicians.
Another very important thing I learned is that although this is a global movement that is rapidly expanding through the world,
it is focused on the local ecosystem, the local ecosystem,
like a real network. And everything is built over the competences we can find in our territory.
Furthermore as Einstein says us, diversity is important and it's made by the territory.
Einstein says, "Everybody is a genius, but if you judge a fish on its ability to climb a tree, it will live its all life believing it is stupid."
This is important to understand, if we want our children today learn to use the technology in a more active way, as Carmelo from CoderDojo Bologna says, "CoderDojo transforms children from passive technology users to super heroes."
I add that we have to provide them the super powers. These super powers come from the society where we live, the local ecosystem,
so the only way to prepare our children to enter the working world with the right competences.
And the right competences nowadays are held only by the people that use the technology for real every day, for work for living for passion.
We cannot wait for the school on this matter.
We have to use all the structures and doing our best to create more and more spaces, like Hackerspace, Impact Hub, FabLab, Science Fairs, but also the family itself, research centers, and local businesses, that have to learn to share and collaborate to diffuse the knowledge.
So I conclude saying that of course it is important to learn to learn with fun, because this is what I've learned: nothing good grows up without the grain of the enthusiasm of children who learn with fun, so I wish our children will promise us a forest of creativity!
We wait for you in the next CoderDojo in every town of Italy, whatever age you are!
Thank you.
(Applause)
Radio - (Music) We all probably know what a radio is, it's a medium we've always had with us.
Inside our cars, at home, in the bathroom, everywhere.
But we probably only associate the radio with music.
The radio is that medium that is 100 years old today and is under attack today.
An attack that may be legit, but maybe, if we look globally for a moment, we’ll find the DNA of something that explains why this medium stuck around so long
We associate the radio with volume, with changing stations, with content.
Radio is not just music.
Radio cannot be compared to Spotify or YouTube or TikTok.
People tell me all the time: "Radio is dying".
Well, I think there are some viewpoints that we are obliged to take into consideration.
The radio is in real-time, it broadcasts local content, the radio broadcasts emotions.
The radio is not a Spotify playlist or a podcast.
The radio is not on-demand.
Today's radio, I mean
Yesterday's analog radio, instead, could be turn it off, gave us company, it gave us content, it was throwed us a party, made the party mood,
it was a brand that gave us an identity
But today’s radio is a one-way radio.
It's one of those few media where we can’t hit like, where we can't hit share.
Radio doesn't track you today, it doesn't listen to you.
Lately, Alexa has come out, a lot of devices came out besides the phones you have in your hands, and you often hear that it listens to you.
The radio doesn't listen to you.
That's the difference.
Now, I'd like to tell you why I'm so attached to the radio: I was born into it because of my father.
I would like to tell you about a humanitarian mission I coordinated a few years ago in Kenya, about 70 kilometres from Nairobi, in a... small village of about 2,500 people who was sending messages to the Radio Maria community in Italy through a missionary.
Unfortunately this village, just to give you some context, their houses are made of mud, there is no light, a fridge -
They have nothing to store food.
No culture, they speak a local dialect, they only understand each other; and we brought a transmitter, an antenna, to launch the first content.
It was a party for them, even if it was religious content.
It was a party because they felt someone close to them, helping them.
This is the radio that saves lives.
And when I say that radio is a medium that shouldn't probably be influenced by digital, it's because it's on a different mission.
Coup on Ivory Coast.
First thing that happens
when there is a change, a movement, a political change, a state attack, the first thing they do is they bomb the radio tower. Because in doing so they cut off communications, then the web if phone lines are there, and the television.
The first thing to rebuild?
They put together a field radio because it's cheap, it's fast, everyone knows how to use it.
This is the radio.
Radio changes a Country’s paradigms.
That's the difference with radio: it gets where others fail to, because it doesn't just have a commercial purpose.
And it doesn't need likes, it doesn't need competence, and above all, it doesn't take your data.
The different thing is that radio gives you a voice, it gives you something extra
because there's no voice there, they have no social: Instagram and Facebook are not everywhere.
If we only consider Milan, Rome, New York, London - everything seems easy.
But you have to travel the world from Papua New Guinea to Vietnam: that's where you have to see the medium, and where a medium changes a Country.
With this.
This is a simple radio.
We've made a few million of them.
We've distributed these with World Family, this project, we reach over 70 countries, more than 50 languages, local dialects - that's the hard part:
The hard part is getting in touch with the community.
We helped refugees from South Sudan to pass through a camp that was as big as Copparo.
We used this.
Not Instagram or some stories.
But perhaps we don't need it anymore, because today we have to give out our data. I heard it before:
if we don't know where we are, if we don't use tags, if we don't say what we're doing, we are [lost] - that's how it is.
But people are talking about detoxing, detoxification, removal, taking back one's identity.
Radio - People ask me to magine what radio will look like in the future. It's changing.
I see a new radio for tomorrow, a radio with your music connected to your Spotify, your horoscope, with your favorite podcast, your favorite program, like Cruciani show or lo zoo di 105. or your favorite podcast.
But it's not yours.
It belongs to others, and you compose it.
But here's the problem: they ask you for something in return.
Your identity, your preferences, your emotions, when you turn up the volume, probably some keywords that they will use to sell you something.
Technology supports us, 5G is coming.
Now, this is not a technological problem, it is not a content problem, it’s knowing whether we want to give our identity up.
Everything we have, we are giving it away for free.
Radio still leaves you free.
Here, in Italy, we are the founders of the radio.
About 94 years ago this gentleman (Audio) "from 1895, at the beginning of my first experiments, I had the strong intuition, I would almost say the clear and sure vision, that radio-telegraphic transmissions would be possible through the greatest distances".
He, Mr. Marconi, in Bologna, turned the radio on about a century ago.
Help me not to turn it off.
(Applause)
You never forget the first videogame you play.
For me, born in the 70s, and I guess for many of my peers, the first video game was Pong.
But I have to say that my first real start was when, for my 10th birthday, I got a Commodore 64.
I began writing my first programs, my first games, and realised what I wanted to do when I grew up.
So, I devoted myself to programming and video game development.
Since then, I've been immersed in this fascinating world, and all its developments. I must admit, there have been incredible, unimaginable changes.
In 40 years we have gone from extremely simple concepts, like Pong - just a bar and a square ball - to the extremely realistic and varied world of today where the player is able to do practically anything.
I like to say that 40 years in video game development have been equivalent to 4,000 years of history in the development of human civilization.
And video games today, in fact, are no longer just simple entertainment that we got used to in the beginning, in the 70s and early 80s.
Now they are something much more complex.
And we should try to understand the impact they have in society today. In this brief discussion we'll try to analyse them from various perspectives.
We'll start with the economic impact then move on to the cultural and artistic impact then on to the technological aspect and finally the social aspects, which affect us all.
Let's start with the economic aspects which are, perhaps, a little more striking.
To put the world of video games in context let's investigate it in parallel with other modern entertainment.
The record industry is worth around $15 billion a year.
Cinema is bigger, worth around $40 billion.
The video game industry, though, is by far the biggest of all these, and this year's estimates are almost $140 billion.
That's a really huge amount.
Obviously the North American market and the Asian markets are the biggest.
In America, video games provide work for around 220,000 people in Britain around 50,000.
But even in Italy there is a lot of activity, and things are moving in some very interesting directions.
In Italy, the turnover is about 1.5 billion euros a year but is still growing.
There are also 80 studios developing some very interesting and innovative products.
So, even here, this market, this industry is in a phase of very rapid development.
Turning to the cultural and artistic aspects, I'd say that it is obvious that to develop a video game today, you need some remarkable artistic skills.
This is a photo I took at an exhibition in Singapore.
It's a painting, an oil painting on canvas created by the Ubisoft Singapore artists.
And it was created as pre-production, concept art for one of the Assassin's Creed games.
So we can see that this is, really artistic work of a very high standard, of excellent workmanship for a video game.
But the interesting thing is that video games can themselves become an artistic vehicle for individual expression for their creators.
And this is also something that has not happened just recently.
In the early 80s a first attempt was made to create a sort of interactive drama.
The game was called Deus Ex Machina produced by Automata 284, an English company.
It was basically an interactive drama. In the box the game came in there were two drawers.
Obviously, at that time you had to load video games on cassettes and had to wait five - ten minutes before you could start playing.
Actually often longer.
Anyway, in this pack in addition to the cassette containing the actual game there was also an audio cassette to be played during the game on your stereo system, so one can follow the narrative and understand exactly what the story's main character is going through.
Some of the leading British actors of the day were involved in the project.
The game was also really simple, actually.
But it was based, very loosely, on Shakespeare's "Seven ages of man".
In practice, we moved from DNA through childhood, youth, maturity, old age and, finally, the death of the player.
Very simple mini-games, which, of course, make us smile today but Deus Ex Machina was the first to push video games up to another level.
A higher level, let's say, of artistic expression.
Today this is one of the most interesting aspects in the modern video games scene.
Maybe not so much for so-called AAA productions, which obviously make billions; but for the smaller productions from independent developers.
And I would like to show it to such a game, This War of Mine.
Maybe somebody here has played it.
It is a very interesting product because it basically shows how today's developers try to present their stories and also raise awareness on a range of issues.
The game "This War of Mine" is inspired by the tragic Balkan war of the 1990s. It's unusual as it doesn't tell the story from the soldiers' point of view, and it definitely doesn't glorify military action; but, rather, it reveals the cruelty of the war and how it was experienced by the civilian population.
Let's watch a short trailer.
For soldiers, war is all about victory.
For us, it was about getting food.
I lived through hell with these people.
We were a group. We would do anything to stay alive.
I will never forget those days.
The things you see in war stay with you forever.
So, it would be true to say that today's video games are, in their own right, one of the most interesting forms of individual artistic expression that we can place almost at the same level as literature or cinema.
Now we come to the third level, technological impact. This is a marriage, if you like, between video games and technology.
There are some interesting aspects that have evolved, and in a sense been turned on their head in a totally unexpected way.
Video games are created as a technological product inspired by technological innovation.
The very first low-cost microprocessors such as the Mos 6502, the Zilog Z80, were core products that allowed the first video game systems to be released for Atari 2600, Commodore 64, to the ZX Spectrum.
All the young people of my generation remember these machines with great pleasure and a lot of affection.
But then something happened which was really inspirational, and video games became the driving force of technology.
A reversal of perspective took place. This was very interesting, dare I say, even fascinating.
And this change happened with the Commodore Amiga's relaunch in the mid-80s, in 1985.
The Commodore Amiga had originally been conceived as a video game console. But because of the recession in North America in 1983 the Amiga Corporation was forced to completely repurpose the project and redesigned it as a home computer.
And it was light years ahead of any other machine at the time.
The first IBM, DOS-based PCs were created just to run spreadsheets and simple office utilities.
The Amiga, on the other hand, was an all-round, multimedia machine.
Something that had never been seen before.
And basically, from then on the efforts put in making video games more appealing started to influence, inspire technology in many other sectors.
This is something that today is even more obvious. For example, let's think about driverless cars.
Much of the hardware now being used in autonomous driving systems to crunch the data and running the AI video systems have actually evolved from the GPUs produced by Nvidia and AMD that were originally designed for the 3D graphics' real-time manipulation, something that requires a large number of mathematical operations.
Also in other fields, such as virtual reality.
The virtual reality systems we see everywhere today began to be used on a large scale thanks to video games.
But it is a field that allows us to have many other perspectives and many other applications.
By the way, right now I'm setting up a new research program with my colleagues in Singapore to try to alleviate some of the symptoms of Alzheimer's based on the technology of virtual reality based video games.
So, there is a whole range of really incredible opportunities that are opening up thanks to technology originally designed for games.
Finally, let's get to the social impact, which is, perhaps, a little more controversial.
And there are some myths that must be debunked here.
First of all, that video games are something that isolate people.
The opposite is true: video games bring people together.
There are millions of adults and kids who play together, meet up and follow each other online at all times of day or night.
So, really, video games are something that brings people together, instead of dividing them.
Another misconception is that only teenagers play these games.
That is absolutely not the case.
Today, the average gamer has an average age of 35, and almost half of them are adults.
They are no longer children or teenagers.
Another surprising fact is that, in the total population of gamers half of them are actually female, so we're not just talking about men.
Women with an average age of 44.
Obviously, this is an indication of the great flexibility and great variety that we have in video games today.
The games that a 50 year old female enjoys are clearly not the same games a child of 15 would look for.
When you look at the more playful games, like Candy Crush or Hidden Object and games like these, which involve searching for hidden objects these are very popular with a specific type of gamer.
In addition, video games are becoming popular in many other areas of our lives thanks to a phenomenon known as gamification.
Gamification can be defined as taking some of the typical aspects of video games such as points, rankings, levels, and so on and applying them to completely different fields.
And we see this in some companies that are using it to motivate their employees.
But if you have, for example a fitness app on your mobile, this works in exactly the same way. It keeps you motivated, by constantly reminding you what you did yesterday how much should you run today, how much your friend did so you can run more than them, and so on.
And it goes on: it awards you with prizes, badges trying to motivate you in ways that closely recall video games - the field these ideas come from - one after the other.
This is a very interesting aspect, that obviously affects us all.
But there is, of course, a downside. I can't just mention the positive side.
Since the late 80s, video games have always been thought of as a violent hobby, not suitable for young children.
This is actually something that the industry took note of, of course and set up a lot of rating authorities in Europe, North America, Asia that are responsible for indexing and rating video games according to a whole series of criteria to tell whether they're appropriate or not for a certain type of user.
So, just like in the cinema there are games suitable for children and games that are not suitable.
The problem is that these ratings, in fact, are often ignored.
There was some recent research, in Britain that showed that practically half of the families interviewed allow their children to play games they theoretically weren't supposed to.
So the ratings are there, but you have to know about them be aware of them and apply them.
I think, unfortunately that here in Italy the situation is pretty much the same. It's a situation we need to improve.
There is another problem that is often mentioned and that can have serious consequences.
According to Build, even, Germany was banned from the recent world championships because the players were playing video games well into the night.
This may be true.
Obviously, video game addiction is a serious matter.
It has also recently been classified as a recognised disorder by the World Health Organization.
In fact, any type of hobby or activity can become an obsession.
We must be careful and do everything in moderation.
In my opinion, a more serious problem we must take into consideration is linked to video game addiction. There are now games, mainly the Free To Play games, but not just those which try to entice players to spend on the so-called loot box.
These are, let's say, surprise packages which contain rewards actually we don't know exactly what we'll find in there and the prizes are distributed in a pseudo-random way. Some of them are very rare and the player obviously tries to get hold of. So they keep spending, waiting for that particular prize to appear - and of course, they'll hardly ever appear.
This is a truly perverse mechanism that can be addictive in the same way in an way that is absolutely analogous to gambling.
There are countries where this type of game has already been banned in Japan and in Belgium.
But this is an area where I really hope that the industry itself will understand they have to apply a degree of self-censorship and moderation without waiting for legislation.
And certainly, such games should never be played by minors.
So, in conclusion, video games and games in general are not just games anymore.
As the historian Johan Huizinga said, in his book "Homo Ludens" published in 1938, in real life, there is always something primary in play that transcends daily life's immediate needs and gives the activity meaning.
All games have a meaning.
The aim of a game is to help us learn something new to practice, to rehearse, even to distract us in certain cases.
But the idea is that the game, actually, goes beyond just the game itself and it is then that the videogame becomes a incredible tool.
Because it's extremely flexible it can be used in any discipline for any purpose.
So games, actually, video games do not end with Game Over, but keep going. They become a steady
influence in our lives.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Faced with this image, you won't be indifferent, I know, because this is an image of war and evokes many feelings.
It's not just the war that destroys, that creates disasters, that creates wounds.
It is a war that elicits very, very strong feelings.
Why did I choose this picture to introduce my theme?
Because I would like you to focus precisely on these feelings, which are not only devastation, but dismay also, desperation, astonishnent and fear.
This is felt by a lot of women in an underground war that's alongside us every day, one I discovered almost by chance.
It was 1990, I approached some lawyers who contested what then was a US decision to attack Iraq, to attack oil countries, and we were lawyers for peace.
And there we contested that arrogant domination approach they wanted to impose.
I don't know if any of you remember, people were talking of a possible Third World War, the Gulf War.
Back then, a woman approached me at an assembly where we talked about all these things, and she said to me, "Why don't you join our association?
We are establishing a group of women that take care of other women who are suffering from violence."
I was immediately intrigued.
I went, I accepted this invitation and I really discovered an underground war, a war that women were talking us about, but compared to today it was still unknown and unspoken.
But who were these women?
They were many types of women.
There was a lot of prejudice, it was thought they only were desperate women, without resources, abilities, education.
Actually, this was not the case. I even found some of my colleagues who told me about episodes of violence that always sound unbelievable.
Housewives, managers, freelancers, lower class, middle class and upper class people, people with good economic status.
It is typically thought, if someone has a good status, is less likely to be influenced.
It's not true.
We immediately said, this violence was transversal.
And so why was it still existing?
An aphorism comes to help me: "Almost anything is easier to get into
than to get out of."
It's really true: in fact, we listened to all those stories and then we said: "How do we help these women, so that they can escape violence,
joining them and supporting them?"
So we started studying what could be done in a careful, competent and also scientific way.
But everything always came to us from the stories of the women.
The "cycle of violence" was studied in the late 70s in the US, by Lenore Walker, who was a therapist. After listening to so many women, she elaborated it and brought to everyone's attention. how these stories can develop.
So, what is the cycle of violence?
She tells us how you enter this tunnel, and how difficult it is to leave it.
There is the first phase, called "Tension Building", which is about provocation, where there are many particular moments where - By the way, every story is different.
There is the woman who tells us about a not-so-tasty pasta, or something like that, taken with the dish and thrown into the waste bin.
Or the pasta thrown over the woman's head.
I'm quoting stories I personally listened to.
And one wonders, why should a woman stick with such a situation?
This is the first question a woman is generally asked.
Well, I'd like to bring you the voice of a woman I assisted and who escaped violence, and I'd like her to tell you, because she explained very clearly, right in front of a judge, what drove her.
Woman: "Because I loved him, and I thought he could change".
MU: Because I loved him, and I thought he could change.
This is just as typical.
One says, "Well, that's weird: how come these women
put themselves in this situation?"
Yet, we must come to terms also with our emotions.
And the other thing?
You go through a phase where the tension builds, and small or bold provocations are thrown, to one where the tension erupts.
It erupts with an aggression - but make no mistake, it's not just a physical assault.
There may also be verbal aggression, maybe also scaring people through the breaking of objects, through the slamming of doors, through a whole series of acts that lead to the person saying, "Here I decide what to do and how it is done."
And so, this is the phase where, after the initial confusion, you arrive at dismay, because the very person who loves you is doing that to you, it is a person that you maybe entrusted with many confidences, they're a piece of your life.
And so, how to react to dismay?
Let me bring you again the voice of this woman.
"I always nurtured a hope that it was also my fault, and that this would not have happened, had I behaved differently."
I repeat, she says: "I nurtured a hope that it was also my fault".
Gosh.
A sentence that should make you shudder.
When I heard it and read it, in the criminal trial, I thought I misread it.
In reality this is how it is.
She hopes it's like this to be able to escape it.
The problem is that you don't escape, because - guess what happens?
There is the so-called "Honeymoon" phase, which is perhaps, in the end, the most dangerous one, even more dangerous than aggression, because it makes a woman think that regret and loving care, which the violent subject now shows, to save a situation he's perhaps losing control of, maybe will make him change. Maybe something will happen so there will be no more, that situation won't be repeated.
What Leonor Walker tells us instead is, this is a recurring phase of a cycle, it's a tunnel and when we intercept women we would like them in that stage, between the honeymoon and the abuses, to decide, because that is the right time to escape, it’s the moment when dismay turns into fear, the feelings of a war re-emerge, and something can be changed.
Instead, the cycle starts back again if you don't escape and if you believe in that regret.
I'd like you to hear her voice, for the last time.
Woman: "What was beyond me is how he, after our quarrels, where he harshly insulted me, 'Fuck you, bitch', horrible words of all kinds, after he calmed down, he wanted to have sex with me. And I couldn't say no, otherwise everything would start all over again."
She had already taken into account the impossibility to say no, otherwise it would have started all over again.
A paralyzing fear, for a woman in this situation and allows violent people to get by.
In all these phases, when it comes to abuse, when the cycle starts again, the abuse that comes next time, I always say so the all the women I meet, will be increasingly severe, so running away as soon as possible is absolutely important.
And what are the numbers for Italy about gender violence?
You may even have heard them already.
One woman out of three, 16 to 70, has suffered violence, and they're seven million women, says our National Institute of Statistics. four and half milion of whom experienced sexual harassment and for more than a million underwent even more serious forms of violence, like rapes or attempted rapes.
This is the situation of the numbers.
These numbers are robust, repeat themselves, and we have brought to light also with the work of women's help centres.
But with our work, we also highlighted the costs.
Because we must also ask ourselves this question: what happens, when a woman is stuck in a violent relationship?
Many things happen, also in the outer world, like going to the E.R., requiring a therapist, taking medications, calling the police, maybe she will sue him. Suing seems to be a silver bullet,
but it is not necessarily the case. Very often it is not, unfortunately.
There are courts constantly at work on cases like this.
There are all the legal costs, the costs of social services and women's crisis centres that play their part.
And all these costs are multiplied by the productivity factor, because a woman under violence will no longer be the same person, socially speaking, she can't be as productive as someone that is, more or less, living their life peacefully.
What is the result of this operation?
It has been estimated, 26 billion euros.
And we believe, this is also underestimated; I'm talking about Italy, not about the world, of course.
And in 2017 in Italy, about 50,000 women went to a women's crisis centre.
You understand, numbers don't match.
They should all be reconsidered, and shortly I will also tell you how, in order to involve all of us in what we can do, even if we don't work in a women's crisis centre.
Why?
Because we would also like to see you, women especially, in our centre, maybe to help us.
Thre are many ways you can help women's crisis centres, but I'm not here today to ask for this kind of help.
It's not a personal help, nor a financial aid, from the point of view of being there and of supporting us, because that's what one generally does when it comes to violence.
What I would like to ask you it is to help us all cut this number of women who are forced to turn to women's crisis centres.
How, you might ask?
Well, that's a situation we must evaluate all together, because today we're talking about changes and we have to think that, unfortunately, no big changes on the matter were seen recently. That's because even the top manager, even a skilled person with financial and cultural resources can be involved in this situation, because it is a matter of intimate relationships.
It is said, violence is a relationship, is determined by a relationship of domination between genders.
If this is true, let's think about it, because there's prevention, which is important for us.
What does prevention consist of?
We can all do prevention, starting with education, with the education of our children.
I always say, there are stereotypes that we are still unable to shake off.
Boys are told, "Don't cry like a sissy", Boys go for football, rugby, boys practise sports, let's say, with a certain strength, virulence; girls are told instead, don't be a tomboy. Behave yourself. Be polite. Be nice. Be nice, mostly.
When we do all this -
Many women, many girls go to dance school, the dance for little girls is now the whole world.
So when we take these steps, we risk having built a structure that leads that man or that woman, that future man, that future woman to be able to enter the violence mechanism. Because it is a mechanism where one dominates and the other one is victim.
So, be careful, surely here, in this room, there are people who have either suffered violence or have acted violently.
And, if statistics can be trusted, as I believe, we should start asking ourselves questions about how we behave, and this is the first uncomfortable thing I'm going to tell you. Because if we continue behaving like this, we will also be accomplices of certain forms of violence.
If we ignore, for example, what happens between a female friend or colleague and the male colleague, who may behave incorrectly - or worse, aggressively, because, anyway, "you can say many things to a woman", she is not going to react much like a man would.
So, let's lay the groundwork for this.
What I'm asking you is to change, to change our way of thinking, knowing that women are inside the cycle of violence also because sometimes we turn our head away or because we have "designed" a manner for women to be within those types of situations and relationships.
Hence, we should change our minds for changing our actions and maybe everyone will be a little more free and happy.
Thank you.
(Applause)
François de Brabant: So, Aurelia, my first question for you is, perhaps we should explain why you agreed to come here and talk before the public from TEDxMilano stage today.
Aurelia Rivarola: Yes, at my age, and if you consider it's my first stage, it wasn't that easy for me to accept.
But I thought, this venue could really offer a moment to spread something I, and many colleagues, are in.
A moment when something that has been whispered so far can finally be shouted, maybe, which is the right to communication.
We all say, communication is a fundamental human right.
But what do we actually do, when a person cannot speak and often cannot also count on the other communication paths that we all use, like gestures, mimicry, body movements, the language of the body?
Well, they live a life -- people in this condition live a very isolated life, a life in which they're often considered not even capable of thinking.
And so I thought, this could be an opportunity to spread something which is known as a name but is not known at the community level, so when there's a person who doesn't speak, we often think, she cannot even think. FdB: We are so glad that you chose this audience, I think not accidentally, filled as it is of curious, open and innovative people, to address this delicate and actually very little known topic.
But who's AAC for, which you'll later explain to us?
AR: The Augmentative Communication, we'll talk about it later, is for those children, or adults, that for congenital causes -- say, people who suffered from asphyxia at birth and now have an infantile cerebral palsy; they are the people we see in a wheelchair which often can't even move their hands and have no autonomy; people with significant cognitive delay, often due to genetic causes, genetic diseases that cause intellectual delay and, for many of the people who are affected, inability to speak.
They are children with autism, with developmental dyspraxia.
And then there are some acquired causes: a stroke, for instance; a head injury, people who cannot express themselves anymore.
Evolutionary neurological causes: they are all forms of degenerative disease.
Now we talk a lot about ALS patients: all ALS patients, later in life, cannot speak. And already in the onset
they can hardly speak.
And then there are temporary causes.
It just so happens, in maxilofacial surgery departments, ICUs, some people cannot speak, maybe even temporarily.
And for these people, in addition to the trauma, you can easily understand what happens, it takes them longer to recuperate and follow treatment less persistently.
We've been looking for so many years to spread awareness on this -- the importance of communication, even temporarily.
And our ICU colleagues told us, some time ago: if they don't speak, we take better care of the body.
As if body was the only thing to cure.
All these people are good candidates to the Alternative Augmentative Communication.
FdB: You've really broadened -- It seemed like a niche problem, but it can happen to me also.
I mean, your description opens us to a world of possibilities so it's even more important to know what it actually is.
We have mentioned, and shortly covered, the acronym: but what is AAC?
AR: Let me get back to something you just said.
It can happen to everyone, hopefully not, however it is important to think that helping these people with communication is in our interest too.
We want to communicate with everyone, even with those who cannot speak.
Not just as recipients of the intervention, but also as people who want to know, stay with everyone and they cannot be with these people.
FdB: Now, that's one more reason to delve into AAC.
AR: AAC stands for Alternative Augmentative Communication.
And it's all that can help a person who cannot speak to communicate.
So it's a set of tools, techniques, support.
But above all, it is the ability of communicative partners to interact with these people and ease their communication.
FdB: When was it born?
AR: The Augmentative Communication -- the first seeds were planted in the '60s - '70s in North America and Northern Europe and -- FdB: Sorry to interrupt, but I guess it all started on historically precise premises.
AR: Yes.
Lots of Vietnam veterans, who suffered head injuries, could not talk anymore, so the speech therapists had to reelicit the language.
It was mandatory: we felt so guilt for them.
So these people tried to recuperate the speech, but they could not, and neither could their speech therapists.
But changing the toolset, they could at least communicate and be protagonists in their lives.
And they did it secretly,
in the early days, until these tools, these tables we use with aphasics, sometimes, tables with an alphabet that the subject indicated with hands or eyes, could allow a communication.
Little by little, we figured out that sometimes language couldn't come back and many people who came from the field of education, but also from the field of technology, because they immediately tried with technological tools, met to found, in Michigan, a company called Isaac, which stands for "International Society Augmentative Alternative Communication" that tries to spread worldwide the Augmentative Communication.
FdB: I brought a moment ago a beautiful description, which I read, of what AAC is about: "Every time they ask me what AAC is, I always give the same answer: it is the voice of my son".
This is from Michela, Stefano's mother, which gracefully recapitulates, I think -- AR: Parent's definitions are always straightforward and beautiful.
FdB: Here we see now the tools you mentioned.
AR: Communication tools range from simple to technological tools.
The aspects -- there are very simple tools like simple symbols, symbols that represent things and can be indicated with the eyes, with the hands by children who cannot speak, even in the absence of the object they mean.
These symbols -- in the first image you saw a mother proposing a question to the child and the child can answer pointing at "yes" or "no".
Symbols are often assembled in communication tables that are totally personal, as well as symbolic systems.
Around the world, there are many symbolic systems: they are assembled into tables that are portable if a child walks, or put on the table if they don't.
And the child can indicate it with their hands, eyes or coding techniques.
There are many technological tools: here you see some tools which are called VOCA, Voice Output Communication Aids.
Some are very simple: a message is recorded, the subject can press the button and the message begins.
And really, many times even a simple message gives a child, or an adult, a sense of existence.
I remember a little girl that at the age of 2.5 rushed to the hospital because she couldn't breathe anymore, so she was tracheostomized.
She was already talking, when she was hospitalized.
And she enjoyed it very much, one of these two-message buttons, as she could tell the nurses "go away" when they came.
So she was experiencing the sense of being the cause of something in the environment, because they are people who typically can't decide.
There are, of course, some simple technology tools and other more sophisticated technology.
Stephen Hawking, everyone knows him, he's an ALS patient who selects messages, words and so on through ocular guide.
Generally, you don't choose a single instrument, but a communication system, as we call it, depending on communication partners and what your message is.
With a communication system that was simple for the patient, yet very complex for the partner, a child with a remarkable stubbornness because she wanted to know, she asked her mother what happened to her at birth, why she was in a wheelchair, so they really provide a sense of full existence.
FdB: This last image reminds me of what you told me: Leonardo is talking, is communicating and stays -- AR: Leonardo is a child with a serious syndrome, a mental delay.
However, after many years of treatment, here you see him in the garden at a party, he opens his table and says what he wants, so it can be autonomous. The contents are personal:
techonology doesn't change the character.
But it provides a sense of existing.
FdB: In the first image, when there were so many people, you might have noticed a very famous person, the physicist Stephen Hawking, who uses -- he also gave a TEDTalk, however, he uses a voice -- AR: A registered voice, yes -- FdB: Not his own one.
You told me that -- AR: Yes, we just held, this Friday, a conference on AAC and ALS.
And an American colleague came, from the Children's Hospital in Boston.
He is a specialist in Augmentative Communication who has now found the way, he's been doing it for so many years, to register, for these patients, lots of messages. And he manages to have them taped
by people who still have their voice.
Many ask, "How do you do this?" and he says, "I tell them,
these messages can be useful".
And then he talks about the software solutions that work with ocular indication, where sentences come out that were spoken by the patient, with their own voice.
He showed us a lady who calls her dog; the dog hears a familiar voice, and comes back to her.
Then there are all the "legacy messages", as I like to call them, the last messages said to their children and loved ones.
FdB: It's interesting what you mentioned, that technology and innovation also help a lot this way.
If one considers that your tables are basically tables with icons, much like the interface languages with the objects we use today.
Now, however, the question is: are the tools enough?
AR: Right, there's a lot of emphasis on the tools.
But without a project, without people that are willing to relate, to give time, to make room, to listen, instead of invading with their own voice, these tools are useless.
What really matters, actually, is the work with their communication partners.
Otherwise there are -- I'm literally quoting people who use Augmentative Communication, but they say: first of all, they must believe in us; and then they have to give us time, because time is very important.
A patient with ALS said, recently, that when he's composing the message, everyone starts talking, because they don't tolerate silence.
And since they don't stand silence, they start to talk.
So not only does he fail to finish his message, but he can't follow the conversation.
The work with communication partners is essential and unique.
FdB: You're here also because -- as it affects the TED world, the reason why TEDTalks are there is -- there is also an action you have thought about.
AR: Yes. It wasn't our idea -- FdB: No, however -- AR: They thought it in Canada, an action -- by the way, this October is the month for raising awareness of the Alternative Augmentative Communication, so it perfectly fits in.
And then they are doing so many actions, including this logo that should be exposed in shops, in theaters, in public venues, where inside, there are people who are willing to listen to those who do not speak, using any communication tools, without involving the guide as a middleman.
And this is very important for the dignity and the sense of existence of the disabled person.
(Applause)
At the end of the nineteenth century, a peasant from the British countryside sees a child stumbling and almost falling into a ditch on the land he was working on.
Despite the cold weather, the peasant decides to jump in.
Despite the cold weather, the rain and the effort, he saves this child he’s never seen before.
The next day, the child’s father visits the peasant.
He's a rich man, and tells him: “You have saved my child. I know you also have a child;
and as a sign of gratitude, I will pay you his studies up to university”.
Back then, it was a great deal.
The son of the rich man graduates and discovers penicillin.
His name is Fleming, and he saves the world from a terrible disease.
The son of the peasant also caught tuberculosis, but Fleming’s penicillin saved his life.
This child grows up and his name is Winston Churchill and he saves Europe from Nazism.
So, with a mere jump in a ditch during wintertime, the peasant saved the world twice.
The meaning is: we have no idea how much power each of our actions has,
we have no idea what their outcome will be.
The most important thing is that everything that we do follows an act, a moment, a thought that we call idea:
therefore, the idea comes first.
I’d like to start with three very important quotes: I'll read them, as you might not know them,
The first is by Gandhi: "The difference between artichokes and lotus flowers is, the first doesn’t believe in the next life”.
I’ll read the second: “To choose an apple we need a keen eye, but to bite in it we need courage”.
I’ll read the third one: Lao Tsu, some call him Sun Tzu, is a wise man who lived 3,000 years ago, who tells the Chinese emperor: “Rule your big empire as softly as you'd squeeze a little fish”
I don’t know if you know them, but these are important quotes, right?
Ghandi, Steve Jobs and Lao Tzu.
Well, they are bullshit. I wrote them, sitting on the toilet bowl.
Now, why did I read them to you and why did you believe in them?
Because we believe in brand power. What we buy isn’t important, the brand on top of it is.
During the break I talked with ten of you - now I see that you are asleep, but let’s pretend you’re not - who told me, “Interesting”, “Why are you here?”
“Because it’s TED!”.
They didn’t name any of the speakers, because you are here for the brand.
Now, the ability of the brand is to sell a plastic bag - Vuitton - and charge you more than leather for it.
This is the power of the brand. And we need brands.
Why?
Because we need social passports: the watch, the car, the things we buy define us. And we use those products, that become brands.
The difference between product and brand?
The difference between them is, Adam and Eve were two bodies made of dirt; a brand is after God gave them life. A brand is a product that speaks:
an icon is a product that lives on forever - Nike is an icon, Apple is an icon.
Now, the today's theme is contamination.
The amazing thing is, all across the centuries “contaminatio” had a negative nuance.
Contaminated water is spoiled.
Up until 10 years ago, contamination was something bad.
Now, suddenly, Sugar and Pavarotti sing together
and the fusion cuisine is contamination, right?
And music: French bands with English names sing and play Argentinian tango.
We are in the time of platypus, do you know what that is?
It’s the proof of God's sense of humor:
(Laughter) It's a jack-of-all-trades animal: it’s a mammal but it lays eggs; it has webbed feet but also nails; it has a beak but it bites, it secretes a venom like snakes - it’s wonderful!
We are in the time of multitasking, it’s the proof that there was contamination way before us, so we have discovered this wonderful word.
But what do we think about contamination?
I’d like to show you a fragment, a few seconds that I found on Wall-E, to tell you what I believe we truly think about contamination.
Can we show the video… (Video) Oh Lord, 100% contaminant. Well, so we entered in a fully-fledged contamination era.
Why does a creative talent need to know that contamination is something positive?
Well, if we wonder why we are able to eat a fruit during this season it’s because some bee, some butterfly, especially the wind have pollinated them.
So let’s say… creative pollination, [translates into Italian] just to release the negative veil that is conveyed by the word contaminatio.
We live in a time of creative pollination where all of us do nothing but pollinate each other.
What really happened in advertisement in the past few years is there isn’t a transmitter and a receiver entity, but the world has changed and we are all influencers.
I’m not saying we are all bloggers - yet, but soon we will be.
We don’t listen anymore, but now that, as Michele Serra says, “ignorance has been legitimized”, we can say everything about anything, which is amazing, finally!
I would like to show you what we really think commercials are, with another animation, not Wall-E.
(Video) “Believe in me, only in me”, says Kaa to little Mowgli.
So for years, we thought there were hidden persuaders and we were no longer operating subjects, but operated objects. What’s changed is, we are the ones who decide what to do. And it’s a really important cultural change, as through socials we are telling others what to do - clearly, we are still primitive, at the beginning, just think that every day on Instagram 50 billion photos are spit up, in this little dirt ball we call Earth.
Almost all of them are about happy hours.
We have fairly uniformed, let's say.
So why do we need to link together advertisement and contamination?
Because usually, a creative act requires a duality.
Sure, one can enjoy their teenage alone in their bathroom, but usually love is better made in two,
Tom &amp; Jerry are two, but also Laurel &amp; Hardy, Garinei and Giovannini, but composers also need someone to play the music, and the other way around.
This is why our body is made of a duality, an emotional and a rational part.
Each of us is male and female, each of us is emotional and rational.
In fact, we are not really used to contamination, we haven’t invented sweet and sour, which comes from Asia, Orientals are ahead of us in this,
but we'll get there little by little.
Our culture separates the first from the second course, north from south, black from white and so on.
But eventually, by designing the maps of the world, blurred borders will eventually replace the stark ones we're used to, because our dirt ball is smaller and smaller, as we are more and more people.
The sense of this is: since we are little, we are told to be creative.
But why?
Why do we need to be creative?
Because ideas bring money, but not the other way around.
And we also think, some people are creative and some aren’t.
This is bullshit, it’s not true: not only are we all creative, but we all have to be.
Mind you, despite having two eyes, one nose, two ears, mouth, everything symmetric, each of us has different features.
We are all made of 20.000 billion cells, all of them with their own intelligence, but each of us is unique, as nature never repeats herself.
Before coming to TED, I said ok, let’s take a look at snowflakes.
No, sir, there isn’t a snowflake with the same composition as the other ones.
If we are unique elements - and this is wonderful - we must think, act and move in a unique, special way, because every time we have an idea we solve a problem.
Now, you can say: “But I am not that good”.
All right, you meet Eve - you know that Eve is this latest generation analogue robot - and you are Wall-E.
What do you do if she can fly and you can’t?
I’ll show you what you can do to fly when you actually can’t.
(Video) Isn’t this pure poetry?
With the fire extinguisher, it manages to fly!
Ideas can overcome anything, you see.
The importance of being creative lays precisely in this, in random ideas.
If we have a flower shop, or a bakery or anything else, any kind of business, any physical action is preceded by a mental action.
So, when they invite you to TED, they say “you have to say something big”.
I have two: the first is that all of us have something big to say or to do, something no one has done before.
The second is: what came first, the chicken or the egg?
The egg, brothers, because the egg is an idea.
What came first, the seed or the baobab?
The seed, because the seed is an idea.
Your eye is made of three parts: the idea, that becomes a condensed dream, or energy, that becomes matter.
And we are much more than a body.
I asked a chemical company how much does a body cost, more or less - yes, even yours, it’s the same.
Less than two euros, as three quarters are water, then copper, calcium, phosphorous… The chemical stuff we're made of is dirt cheap.
Do you understand, we are worth so much more as thinking beings?
Ok, I’ll give you a hint - for depressed people, this works way better.
In our brain we have an amount of energy enough to power Chicago for an entire night.
But we don’t use it, because we think “Oh, this is too much for me”.
So we start from the small things,
to see things creatively means to elaborate a creative system in order to think.
In my life, I have been a radio DJ, I have written novels and essays, I have worked as an architect, I have worked as an advertiser, I am a creative, I do commercials, I have been a director.
Every time, you do something that have you reconsider yourself.
There is a beautiful quote by Longanesi, that says “My fantasy is stuck, I need a little sorrow”.
We need to live different lives during our life,
and every time we think we are afraid, it’s as if we were driving with the handbrake on, we can’t go anywhere.
Fear is the exact opposite of courage, and courage is creativity’s twin brother.
You can’t think of anything new, if you’re not convinced of being unique.
Therefore, we have to start from little things.
At a Japanese school for kids, they made a multiple-choice test with the question: “When the snow melts,
what does it become?”
- Audience: water!
- LM: Water, well done. 
You passed. 
Someone said “stream”, “waterfall”.
A kid answered, “spring”.
He didn't pass, obviously.
(Laughter) Well, we have to be like that kid.
We are something unique, and it’s a wonderful thing to be here, and talk about creativity, not abstractly, but practically,
only because each one of us was born with an absolute potential.
We just haven’t developed it yet because we don’t believe in it.
But an Indian spiritual guide said, that we should talk using words that nobody used before, live emotions in a way that nobody has lived and love in a unique way the way nobody did before.
So we can’t write “I miss you” anymore or “what are you up to?”
Have a great evening! Thank you. 
(Applause) All right, all right, there is an encore.
We have two, three minutes left, and since you behaved I'll tell you a fairy tale, I know you love them.
You might think that creativity is a distant thing and you think it’s - there’s a nice story that you might like, that comes from India, called “The perfect woman”.
Now, we think that we want the best situation to be creative, right?
Right temperature, right table, right type of paper, and we always blame the canvas, or the noise, or the radio.
And there was a guy telling another guy “Look, I want to find the perfect woman” and the other replied “There isn’t one in the whole world”,
But the former replied, “I am sure somewhere there is a good, pretty, nice, smart and sweet woman -" and the friend said, “You’ll waste your entire life and never find her”. But he did insist, “I’m sure I’ll find her.
I’ll travel all the cities, all the nations. And I will find the perfect woman, because I know she’s there”.
So he left, and several years went by.
One day, the guy who stayed in the village, one day, old and with a long beard, sees his friend coming back, runs to meet him and asks him “So, did you find the perfect woman?”.
And the guy replies “Yes, eventually I found her.
But she wanted a perfect man”.
(Laughter) (Applause)
During an institutional trip with the Mayor and other politicians of my town, I had a terrible attack of gastritis.
My stomach hurt so bad that I decided to take a plane and return to Rome, to the hospital.
I was hospitalized for a week, and it actually was a very fun and relaxing week because they put me in a room with five other patients, aged 65 on average, who spent days and nights telling me lots of stories and adventures.
Imagine, the five combined were over 300 years old, so imagine the number of stories to tell.
I was listening them, intrigued by the fact that despite being ordinary people, each of them told me about a different world, more or less generous, interesting or exciting.
Here they are.
(Laughter) What really struck me was that all of them, be it the age or the number of accumulated disappointments, those gentlemen had stopped believing in the power of change.
"Nothing will change," they said, "and if it does, it's for the worse."
And from that moment, the goal of my hospital stay changed drastically.
I didn't have to heal anymore, I was maybe already cured.
I had to convince them, before going home, that something, somehow, can always be done.
I then spoke about those people who do not settle, those who don't remain indifferent, and choose to turn ideas, dreams, hopes in real life projects. And I talked about myself, who join and engaged, in my teenage years, to those movements that grow and die like mushrooms because they fail to build real participatory processes, the movements of pure protest. And once I got this, I chose to take a different path, I made a small choice, which changed my life.
I turned my desire to engage myself, participate and be the change in a concrete, literally "readable" project.
I founded, with a schoolmate, a great publishing project, and a movement around this that on a project basis gives room to the ideas of almost 400 boys and girls, turning them into reality.
Scomodo [Uncomfortable], the newspaper name, is a monthly magazine, intentionally printed on paper, covering news and culture in more than 100 pages, every month.
Covers sport important signatures: this is Altan, and these are some of the others.
And you might not believe it, but boys read it, and a lot too.
The goal is to re-educate a generation to get interested, involved and feel an active part of the society they live in.
After this, Scomodo, which is a very complex project, can be summarized by two main viewpoints: one is participation, the desire to take action.
Articles are discussed together: we collectively decide what to talk about and how, and the initiatives - from lectures to concerts, that fund the magazine - are designed to make attendees the protagonists of an experience and no longer just a user.
And the urge, the other hand, to build the cultural and social alternatives we miss and do it with the newspaper.
When we think it's not enough to say that we can have access to all the information we want but we must teach ourselves to do it.
And we do it by filling with content, ideas and people, empty, abandoned city spaces.
This is Flaminio Stadium.
It is a 25,000 seats stadium in central Rome, 25 times today's venue - it is huge.
700 of us occupied it at four in the afternoon to suggest a possible different future for so big and beautiful place.
It was talked about a lot.
In the first months of the project, while we sought to put ideas in order, no one had told us what it could have become.
No one had ever prepared us to the amount of emotions, hardships and problems we would have to learn to deal with.
And do you know why I'm here?
Because we never asked for it.
We wake up each morning and choose to get involved, without too many questions on the growth of a project that no one feels like her own, and we all feel like ours.
Scomodo stems from this very idea: a simple, powerful idea that became shortly after a plan to achieve it.
We held the first meeting, then printed the first number and people began to speak about us.
Newspapers wrote about us. In high schools and universities, students had stickers with our covers on their helmets.
They read and spread the magazine.
In the first year, we grow from 53 to 440 members. We're Italy's most participated cultural reality.
We end up distributing 7500 free copies, each month, in Rome, at 200 points of sale and in 13 other Italian cities.
Scomodo is Europe's most printed student magazine.
Now with a little more structure and organization, we work on 13 projects, some with important partners: Internazionale, Treccani, Green Peace.
(Laughter) (Applause) Thank you. (Applause)
And we work on the project launch, and on starting chapters in four other Italian cities.
After three years of meeting in parks, squares, bars, we're now even building a space, and it will be a space for Rome, a place of everybody for everybody, with a one-of-its-kind process of shared building, that already involves nearly 800 people, in building a new place that will address almost all the desires of those, like us, who live our town, which is Rome, if it wasn't clear.
(Laughter) Behind all this is a bunch of freaks, nerds, losers, klutzes - 28 out of 30 have vertigo, I swear.
We're ordinary people, we could be our children, we could be the ones seated next to you, or you at our age.
I say this to point out, we never felt like we are something more than others, if not the hope of contributing to turn this country into the one we meant to live in.
Often, while running behind this hope, we feel a bit like Peter Pan's lost children, dreaming an insland that's not there, brighter and shinier, who acknowledges the future investing in the present.
But indeed, the island is not there, it's away from reality.
So please allow me, dear TEDx attendees, to say that if we feel lost children, perhaps it's not entirely our fault.
Our generation comes from decades of cultural, economical, political divestments, away from education, culture and, I dare say, also democracy.
As a result, a generation came out of objectively depressed kids.
Look around you: we're apathetic, indifferent, ignorant,
Bookstores close, we don't read, we don't care; theaters close, newspaper close, we're not breathing air anymore.
And no one cares.
Well, instead of getting angry and join the choir of No, we tried, and later found out, a toolbox to grow up in civil society, among adults, the very adults we used to be so mad at before.
Scomodo exists also thanks to our parents' support, of subscribers' support, of all the people who never stopped believing in us.
And knowing that when things go south, when everything seems to be over, someone's there, ready to say, "No worries, you will make it.
Indeed, we will make it"
It's not just important: it's everything, it's fantastic.
Without a community behind it, a project is nothing, is not really real.
I say all this with a goal and a dream: the goal is to convey to you the importance and the potential for hope, as hope motivates our choices in real life and changes, even huge ones, came out of a combination of choices,
And the world does need those changes, the world doesn't want to stay the same, the world needs to transform, the world needs innovation, the world needs us to believe in him.
The world needs to improve, and we need the world to improve. And my little dream is for you to join, once out of that door, and contribute to do this.
Do it for yourselves, do it for us, do it for any one you want to, but do it, because we need it.
And because, after all, my name is Tommasino and I'm not actually good at anything special. But I took a train, came here and I'm so emotional.
And I only did that to say, maybe you can what you wish for.
Thank you.
(Applause) Thank you.
(Applause) Thank you, thank you.
(Applause)
We chose to complete "Open to", the event's theme, with "Action".
Action means -- I had to look in the dictionary, to be really sure-- "Being open to new ideas, to new perspectives, to re-evaluate everyday life, to look at things in a different way."
And it is also a call to do something, not to stand still.
What I'd like to talk about, in these 17 minutes at my disposal, is something different, that is literally out of this world, seemingly at a long distance but actually very close to us.
International Space Station, a home lab spinning around the Earth at 28,000 kilometres an hour.
We go up there to find things that are not here on Earth; and we can search and discover, paradoxically, things there that then help us in our everyday life here.
And it's an isolated, confined place; it's not easy to get there, you have to take a rocket.
They're not just around the corner.
It's like having under the chair a mini atomic bomb that shoots you up when it's turned on.
It doesn't take long to get to the 400 kilometers of altitude of the International Space Station.
Let's say, it's like Milan-Rome in eight and a half minutes, more or less.
Then you get closer to the space station; and finally - if all goes well, and it usually does - you usually get into this club of extraterrestrials. Because I do believe in ETs, it was us!
These extraterrestrials who basically live and work in this lab house.
And that's where troubles begin. Because you've been training, year after year, even ten years; then you come up, and you're totally lost.
They say, partly joking: when you get in orbit, your IQ is divided by three.
Because basically your brain is busy figuring out what's going on.
You've trained yourself, you know exactly what's around you, but you haven't taken into account you don't feel the force of gravity anymore.
So when you get there, and try to keep your feet on the floor, the wall called "deck", you discover that it's not there.
It says "deck", but it's not the floor.
Which one's right? Is it him? Is it me?
The others down there? I don't know.
And in a sense, that's what happens
here on Earth also.
We've heard it before:
ife is a constant change.
When we learn to do something, hone it to perfection and settle with it, we're on the right path to self-destruction.
Because if life changes, things change, the world around you does and you don't, you will lose your job and won't move on.
So you have a choice here: either you become an extraterrestrial, and try to figure out what that thing is, or you stand there complaining for those things you'll never find.
It's useless for you to cry that there is no gravity: "I want gravity back! I'm trying to eat,
and everything's flying away!
I can't find anything, seek them on the ground in vain!"
This attitude is pointless.
But this is what we normally do in life: we don't try to change. We have this inertia to change which is actually trying to understand how to get results in this new situation.
It's not easy, when you don't know what to do, to look ahead, explore and do things; but that's what you have to do.
So when you understand that you don't have to keep your feet on the ground, [and you have to] grab the handles to browse the space - you let it all go, can't find anything and do like Scott, who got there three months before me, and already had become a nice, peaceful, quiet ET. he looked like a vampire, up there, when I stared at him.
[I said] "I want to sleep there, because you sleep better there."
Then I realized, it was exactly the same thing.
So shaking this inertia is something that we should do. Changing the perspective:
instead of focusing on what's missing, look at what's there but that we'll never find out if we don't work on it.
The ISS is a workplace, actually.
Business time is 7:30 AM to 7:30 PM.
You do a whole series of activities, experiments - Houston decides, not you.
But in the evening - whatever "evening" means there: when you're out of service, let's put it that way - we can go to the window, look downstairs and you can see some incredible things that we try to shoot: it's one of the nice things we can do.
And you see something like this.
This is Italy,
seen from the south, and I shooted it in 6K. You can find it in this video by European Space Agency on YouTube, if you want to.
And you see Italy as you normally don't.
This is a real time passage: you can see the full one in the video, I'll have to stop it in a bit.
But you can see the Alps up here, sheltering us from the northern wind.
We have the Mediterranean Sea below and around us that moderates the atmosphere.
We should be much colder: we're at 45 degrees, quite high.
I mean, our country is really, incredibly kissed by nature.
And then, when you look at it more carefully, you see Italy again. But you see it like this,
and you start thinking: where are we?
In Val Padana.
And in Val Padana, more often than not, the situation is not so rosy.
In fact, every now and then the traffic stops in Milan.
But there's more to it: Milan is a relatively small town.
And this allows you to look at the Earth from the Station, to see things at a distance, with an all-encompassing view.
I use to say, looking at the Earth down here is like pretending to see a portrait with your nose pushed against the canvas.
You can see some details, but not the larger picture.
That's what tells you - look at -
Where are we here?
Bellagio, you detect Lake Como, we are more or less under this little cloud here.
That's fine.
But we don't realize how nature shifts from our intuition of it.
This is a series of rivers in Argentina, south of Buenos Aires; and interestingly, every time you pass over them, they are different.
Rivers try to take the path of least resistance:
this is the Orinoco, South America. Look at this photo, where one can basically see how many times over the years this river has changed its course.
We think of rivers as fixed, stationary structures; but the opposite is the case: they move.
If we were to look at ourselves with our terrestrial eyes, ten years seem so many, a thousand kilometers seem so many; if we looked at them with an extraterrestrial sight, we would see that rivers are living, ever changing things.
This is the Grand Canyon that has even dug valleys two kilometers deep.
He took everything that was in there and took it somewhere else.
This is the Mississippi, which in 2011 decided to overflow and flooded a number of cities.
We say, the Mississippi did overflow: how come?
But that's what rivers do when they get stuck somewhere: they take another route.
And where do we build the cities?
Near the rivers, because it's much simpler: we need water.
But we don't realize that rivers then go out.
Yet another general view. Then the Caribbean: when you look at them up close, you see the sands pulled, you see these little islands, you see these shows from up there.
They might seem random, but they're not that random.
This is Australia.
And then you look at something that we started to build by dredging all the sands and coral reefs -- current buildings aren't enough, cities are full,
so we build something outside
and we don't realize we're totally changing nature around here.
This is Venice.
Venice sometimes gets flooded.
I don't know if it was a good idea to build a city there.
But we should also understand - Venice is beautiful, I was there less than a week ago;
but the situation is a bit problematic.
This is Vesuvius seen from space and it's interesting how many buildings you can see around there; if you take a picture at night, you can see exactly where Vesuvius is.
A million people dwell around it.
If that one bursts, as it did a while ago, we have some problem, we'll have to be careful.
Italy is super bright, looks like we have free power.
Look at the rest of Europe: it's almost dark.
Then you get to Milan: and here we are, mindlessly burning energy.
Here's Milan - by the way, it's seen from above.
Italy is very, very beautiful.
As I say, it's an incredible country, seen from up there.
But we have to manage our resources carefully.
I wrap up this talk with a video, a video you can find on Youtube.
If you do a research for "The Sound of Silence - NASA", you will find this video published by NASA.
We made it with NASA when we were in orbit.
I was taking a lot of pictures of passages, I took some 500,000 of them;
the Russian was doing the details. Then we put this thing together.
So I wanted to see it with you because it shows you how the Earth looks like from up there,
how different it is, and how we should look at it in a different way to be able to act, to do something, to be careful about what we do.
(Video) This is dawn as seen from space, it takes about seven seconds to go from night to day.
Then, seven seconds later the world lights up and you see everything white and blue, clouds and oceans.
Then every once in a while some land - you see something, but it's really hard to even understand where you are or what it is.
There's a more complete vision, something strange.
This is the shadow of the Moon in a Sun eclipse that we photographed from space.
This is Italy.
Look at Africa, right around the corner; we're really attached to Africa.
This is London as seen from space.
This is Rome: Termini Station, the Colosseum.
At night, cities light up.
This is Mecca in Saudi Arabia.
The glaciers of Patagonia.
An Aurora Australis.
The Moon with all the planets aligned, in this lunar dawn.
At night, cities light up.
This is the North of the United States.
We are everywhere like a Christmas tree.
A storm up there.
This is Washington DC, in the United States.
This is Moscow, 18 million people in this ring.
An atoll in the Pacific.
A dark Greece and sunburnt Africa.
Islands in Egypt.
A lake in Australia.
A view of the Sahara desert.
A meteorite impact in the Sahara.
A volcano in Kamčatka.
A hurricane came against Houston, when we were in orbit,
the eye of a typhoon came against Japan.
The Caribbean: an emerald spot above the earth.
Here I wanted to take Italy, this Spain, Africa, the Mediterranean Sea.
Here comes Italy, here comes Italy, here we are - no way, a huge cloud.
But it took Apulia.
In Africa, there are no clouds above the deserts;
the Nile, the Sinai, Israel, Saudi Arabia, the Red Sea.
The aurora borealis.
A river in Madagascar, red for deforestation.
A road in a desert in Iran.
The deserts of Namibia.
New York.
Paris.
And still an overview, where you can't see our mini nations, where you see something, but you don't see much.
At the end there are considerations to be made:
let's finish this video, and then we'll wrap up with a couple of considerations.
(Applause) I was telling you that when you are far away, you no longer see details,
but you see a greater picture, which is extremely important.
One looks and sees some land, something; but in fact what happens is that we are down here, in our houses, in our gardens and in our cities and we do not realize that we are down here and we have a wall above us, which is this border.
When the sun goes away, it stays visible for a few minutes: it's the atmosphere.
That is our wall, that shields our mini boundaries, our mini gardens from the emptiness of the universe.
If there wasn't this wall, we would not be.
So we have to be careful with what we do: our little gardens are fine, but that wall also is because we wouldn't be here wthout it.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I start with a question: can food communicate with our DNA?
And if it communicates with our DNA, can it affect health?
Let's think of a first image, the bees.
We have three bees: the worker bee, the drone, and the queen bee.
All three have the same DNA, the same genome; but the queen was fed by larvae with a different food: royal jelly.
And as we can see, the queen's dimensions are different.
But not just the size: her function is different.
It is the only one, inside the hive, that can mate and give rise to a new colony.
So the food communicated something to the DNA, because they all have the same DNA.
And we need to go, in order to understand that, into the small core of our cells.
Two microns, enclosing three billion bases, which is our two meters long, extremely packaged genome.
Think that in the late 90s it was thought that the study of the human genome would have provided great answers both in understanding diseases but also in understanding the DNA.
It soon became clear, we were only at the beginning, because only 2-3 percent of DNA encodes for a protein, and therefore a function.
Everything else - think, 98 percent - is a DNA that at the time was considered junk DNA, sorry for the English term,
junk that served as a backup to ensure that mutations did not fall on genes.
In fact this DNA turned out to be the beginning of a new adventure: this DNA was functional to regulate the expression of genes.
How's that?
There are chemical markers on DNA, flags that are affixed to DNA during development.
These flags, which can be directed either on the DNA or on the proteins that wrap the DNA, or even on some RNA, which are the mold of anti-sense DNA, are affixed during development so that the cells can perform their function.
Mind you, we have the same DNA in our cells, in each of our cells.
But each cell operates differently. Why?
Because it has the first parts of DNA which are different, thanks to these flags, these markers that say to a gene: you have to be expressed in this cell, you have to make sure that this cell has this function, and that another cell has another one.
To better understand, let's imagine that our DNA is an orchestral score, and that genes are the instruments.
If you play all together, there would be no harmony, there would be no symphony.
It wouldn't be the music, there'd just be noise.
Actually, every gene knows perfectly well, thanks to these flags, these markers, when to start working, when to play, to shut up, whether to play loud or slow.
This harmony takes place every second, every instant within our cells.
And there is a study, an extremely interesting study, done on mice - in biology we always start from an experimental model, and often on mice.
Two mothers with the same genetics were given food to eat.
Mother was fed with low-nutrient food; another mother was fed a nutrient-rich food.
And especially rich in vitamins, specific nutrients.
The result of this was, the pups of these two mothers had a phenotype, that is, an evidence of what they are different in - even the color of the coat changed, because the food has communicated something different during the development of these these pups, to the genes.
So let's think about food at present.
We have on this small planet, as Edgar Morin says, people dying of hunger; people who eat in excess; we waste food.
We have people who get sick because they eat too much, too bad.
And we also have a paradoxical situation, called "nutritional desert": I leave home, and in ten minutes I can not find any food rich in vitamins.
I can only recover the so-called - I don't like the term, as food must always be respected - food that has only calories but no essential nutrients.
And the most interesting thing is that the environment, food, lifestyle and even emotions can edit DNA.
They can talk with the DNA.
And this writing can be transmitted - especially during the first 1000 days of the child's life, from conception to the third year of age - this writing can also be transferred from one generation to another.
There is evidence, historical evidence of this intergenerational transfer of how much the environment, and food, influenced the health of subsequent generations.
This is a historical study, which took place in Holland.
A population of 45 million Dutch people was studied, who during World War II was confined to a very restricted area of the Netherlands.
It was winter: on one side there were the German troops, on the other side the frozen canals.
This population lived for about nine months eating very little, they were almost starving: they even came to eat the bulbs of the tulips.
And it was calculated, they ate about 500 kcal per day.
We then studied the babies born from them pregnant mothers.
These children maintained their mothers' programming: their mothers lived without food, and when these children were born they were programmed to resist the absence of food.
When the war was over, they had access to food and these children were more exposed to health vulnerablity: more exposed to diabetes, obesity, overweight, cardiovascular disease and even cancer.
And this vulnerability, this increase in risk also reappeared in the second generation and now we are studying the third generation.
We must think of the small scale, so that the small can suggest actions to be taken on the large scale, to improve our society, and also the health expectancy of our society, and rethink the origin of diseases.
And that is the best way to promote health: bring the science of the small scale, these wonderful mechanisms that occur in our cells, in the great choices.
Invest especially in the social classes that are less aware of all this.
They have access to poor food, because it is cheap, and maybe they have a low income, and they may also have a low level of education that cannot allow them to access these types of messages.
Above all, investing in mothers, [Right to health / Health and Equity] because the right to health, also enshrined in the Constitution, is also a right of intergenerational justice.
We must care about our health, because it is a precious good, but we must also think, we can pass it on to the next generations.
Then we need to wake up a little bit, and this is my awakening.
There is an origin that gives a greater vulnerability to disease: the first thousand days of the child's life are important.
And there is perhaps - this is a question I often ask myself - a silent evolution that leads a society to have a different health perspective because their access to food is different.
And will this evolution continue?
Fortunately, it can be reversible if conditions change.
And if the mom's food, our food, changes.
So, if it is true that it is important, for the health of the unborn and of the following generations, the environment and the food that mother eats, then we must take care of the food and everything that surrounds the mothers, their environment, around what are the new lives.
So we have to consider every eating moment.
And curate this moment.
It can be the hospital: we can't forget, treat food like yet another hotel service.
Food must convey values: it must also be deemed important, and promote health.
Many parents, in schools, are more concerned if the children do not eat, but never worry if the child eats badly.
Always the worry: my son doesn't eat.
But let's worry about what they eat, let's worry if what he eats is important for his health.
Then there are companies: at any time, but also among friends, a health marketing that can also span across different social worlds.
We think in every moment that we eat how much we can communicate through food.
It must always be good, but also functional to health.
Because food is an interconnection between our past, our present but also our future.
It connects us with the Earth, because food is work: it is produced and transformed, we must respect it.
With human beings, with the environment.
A great English agronomist said that the health of man, animals, land, water and air is unique and indivisible.
Consider also: we feed on molecules, which are carbohydrates, where the carbon is in a high energy chemical bond.
But where does this high energy bond come from?
From the plants!
And plants, on the other hand, use solar energy to transform a carbon molecule, with a low-energy CO2 bond, into high-energy oxygen.
For the transitive property, we can say that we eat thanks to the energy of the Sun.
So we have to respect this kind of environment around us
which allow us to be part of our history.
Let's think of the scents that evoke emotions, the scents of our grandmother's kitchen.
Let's think of Proust and the Madeleines.
Let's think about how much food binds tradition, history: it is part of our past, but it is also part of our future,
And it is also part of what we can transfer to future generations.
Sometimes, however, you also eat standard foods, with standard scents and standard flavors, and I think this is disqualifying for taste, our organ of sense.
We have five sense organs, and the sense organ of taste is a wonderful organ: we have five tastes through taste, but through the vomer-nasal organ and the sense of smell millions of possibilities to appreciate flavors and aromas.
Sometimes the food is industrial, very processed. A food that endlessly repeats itself.
And we must invest in the culture of food. Which is a different culture from prevention, but they can really have links in common.
Also, nourishing and loving are a mother's first acts.
A feeding food is a loving food.
And then, of course, our traditions.
Our traditions account for a distinction between a daily diet, with frugal but still tasty food, and a holiday that we have to expect and wait so we can share a richer food with our beloved relatives.
Then what is epigenetics?
From "epi", Greek, "above" DNA.
All these modifications on top of the DNA.
It is a bridge that links the environment - alas, even pollution - emotions, food, that encompasses all this, to our DNA.
We thought of DNA as an immutable code: tall, short, blue eyes, black eyes.
In fact, we understood, through these latest studies - there are not so recent, because they dates back to 15 years ago - that we can have a dialogue with our DNA, and we can also transfer our history to the following generations.
And in fact I think, a fairer, more equal health perspective for all.
So that new generations will have even less vulnerability to certain diseases.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I've been stolen, I've been robbed.
Not now, before I came here.
Half of the attendees today don't know that, probably. All the women here have been robbed.
Robbed of their identity, their history, their strength, their courage, their wealth.
We are not in history books, yet we have done it; we are not in literature books, we are not in art, in science. And yet we have done them all.
We have even been stolen of the quintessential female prerogative.
It is called - some philosophies call it - the theft of motherhood.
In the Greek myth, women come from the head of Zeus. Athena is born from the head of Zeus,
a woman is born from a man.
In religion, in the Bible, Eve is born from an Adam’s rib.
We come from a man!
Even our absolute power, the generative one, is denied to us.
Consider the symbolic significance of being raised like this.
That's what we study in history, that's what our education tells us.
So I've been robbed of my own identity.
Our most important thing - think of our second name: in Italy but also elsewhere in the world. Women and men both introduce themselves as daughters and sons of a father.
We just have a paternal surname.
What about our mother, who gave us birth?
What about her story, her blood, her intelligence, her laughter, her strength - where is she?
Socially speaking, we don't exist, we are only a father's daughters or sons.
This is called identity theft.
Imagine if you, men, had your identity taken away,
the part that is passed on: I took my mother's surname, my name is Paola Di Nicola Travaglini.
The maternal surname always comes after,
and I found out I actually took a squared male surname, because I took my mother's grandfather surname.
Plus, the feminine is taken away from the language.
Languages hinder, “dismantle” the feminine voice.
Institutional functions, prominent positions of power, are matched with the male
Italian article “Il”. The alderman, the mayor,
the president, the judge.
It took me 20 years to realize I was signing myself in the male form, nobody asked me to.
And yet I was a woman, I had given birth
to two wonderful children.
I had a husband, I had a social life, where I undoubtedly was a woman, but I was signing myself as a man. I was “Il Giudice” [The judge],
And as I realized 20 years later,
this is only true for positions of power.
The florist, the cashier, the nurse are not asked to sign themselves as men.
Instead, they say that institutions are “neutral”, that's why you have to use the masculine.
It's a trick, it's an alibi, this is not the case.
Not so long ago, in judiciary power - not only in Italy, but in the rest of the world - women couldn’t enter a courtroom and judge someone else.
In Italy the first competition open to women was in 1965, the other day.
And mind you, the symbolic representation of justice has always been a woman.
A powerful, important, strong woman, with a balance - “Dike”, in Greek.
But women in the courtrooms just could not enter,
they could not witness, their words didn’t count,
so they could not judge or assist anyone.
Shortly, the institutions are not blindly neutral at all.
The institution sees very well and prevented me from entering.
So what's the point here?
The point is the prejudice, the stereotype.
Because we have been forbidden to enter the roles of power with the stereotype that we are fragile, that we are inadequate, care-oriented, irrational, passionate, deceptive.
Well, it is from the Upper Palaeolithic on that these stereotypes are around,
and stay unchallenged, here in Italy and around the world, for centuries and centuries.
That is the huge mountain before us we have to pull down now.
So how can we think, this all does not enter in our daily life, and in the halls of justice?
Today I sign “La” [Female article] judge, proudly.
“La” judge because all the women before me passed me this extraordinary wealth.
Without them, today I would not wear a toga.
These stereotypes, whereby women are dedicated to care and men are dedicated to power, enter the courtrooms, fill them, and potentially affect judges’ deliberations.
A stereotype is hard to see because we are all, at the same time, victims and protagonists of it.
The stereotype is unconscious, it just passes on.
One does not have to talk, it just spreads over.
Women who denounce violence are not believed,
because violence against women have belonged for millennia to the hierarchical structure - mind you, since more then 40,000 years ago.
And we must eradicate it, trusting the voice of women who denounce it;
but we are unable to do so, often to the point of unfairly saving the offender, because we justify violence against women.
And violence is not just slaps and punches.
It is also not becoming a primary hospital, being ridiculed for being women, it is the inappropriate joke, the downsizing to a joke of what should have never been treated as a joke.
Some examples, then.
In 2017 in New Delhi, the High Court declared an alleged sexual violence against a young girl not to have taken place because she had not shouted.
The same thing happened in Spain in 2016, when a young girl who had been raped by five men she did not know was considered “inert” because she failed to react.
And then we come to Canada where a judge in a courtroom asked: "Madam, why didn't you close your legs during that violence?".
Perhaps these gentlemen, and these ladies, do not know what the thanatosis is, that form of blockade that cause 70% of women undergoing a violence not to react.
So why do we need to say that women must resist violence?
Because this way, we will never have the evidence that will allow us to say that a violence took actually place.
So our job now is to deconstruct these stereotypes and prejudices, with conscience and knowledge.
A short time ago I had a case that media outlets stained with horrible stereotypes and prejudices against the victims. It was a case of child prostitution.
it was a 14-year-old girl who had had sex with men, men like you, men who were clients of 20-30 years older who were queuing up to get a seat, an hour, half an hour with those girls.
The papers called them "baby call girls", "malicious teenagers".
Like they were the ones who brought those men into those rooms.
Truth be told, that’s the narrative that justifies violence.
That justifies paedophilia: that's the proper label for a man who goes with a 14-year-old girl.
But we flinch away from the men, from the clients - they're in the shadows during trials, they can't be seen in the newspapers.
The same is true for violent crimes.
We only talk about women, who are the victims.
Good, and all this is to justify it.
And so when I had to decide how to compensate for that horrendous crime that had destroyed or stolen the adolescence of that little girl, who had stolen the beauty of her relationship with the male gender, I had to decide, faced with the request for 20,000 euros, whether it was a proper mean to give her back the dignity that those sexual encounters took her away.
I deliberated, as a judge, in the name of the Italian people, on behalf of which I represent justice- and gives me a sense of responsibility and enormous anxiety, as you can imagine - well, I decided that no amount of money could buy back what that little girl had lost,
but books on the history of women, on their thinking, on their intelligence, on their ability and on their wealth.
From Virginia Woolf to Emily Dickinson to Anne Frank, and her diary, to Hannah Arendt, Luce Irigaray, Melania Mazzucco and many others.
And so through this, I felt that that very young girl should understand and know without any maternalism or paternalism, that she had not been violated.
Because she belonged that extraordinary story of women, of their scientific, literary production, of those extraordinary battles that do not show up in our books, they are not there. Nobody wants us to know them.
But she came from that extraordinary, millenary production of women's history and thought.
That is why I believe that we, the judges, we have an extraordinary responsibility, because not only we speak in the name of the Italian people, who are made of both men and women, but also because ours is a public word.
And if we perpetuate stereotypes and prejudices, we inadvertently turn stereotypes and prejudices into a universal word.
And I don’t think this issue can be confined in the courts of justice: violence against women and our absence from history, literature, science and art books is certainly not a merely judicial matter.
And there’s a bloody revolution in store for us, one that calls to each one of us: men and women.
No one is counted out.
And it is a bloody revolution because it’s about our daily life, our behaviour, our words.
If they are right or if they are wrong, if they reproduce those lively and stubborn stereotypes towards men - depicted as courageous, ambitious;
and reproduce the female subjection: fragile, caring, non-existent.
Well, this is a very difficult revolution; I think it is the first time in the history of humanity that we are facing something like this.
We do not need blood, we need words, we need a concrete behaviour that affects the daily lives of men and women in our homes, so that men can give up the position they have taken on and women can reclaim their right to happiness and freedom.
And now, each and every one of us must do so.
(Applause)
For about ten years, we have started this practice in the Dolomites, awarded in 2009, as you know, "World Heritage Site" by UNESCO.
So in 2009, we established the value and status of this asset for its outstanding universal value, for the aesthetic and landscape quality.
Well, that's what we work on: my job is redefiningthe concept of heritage and landscape.
The Dolomites are "heritage", we have defined their value with this word, but we live in the Dolomites.
Behind me you can see the images of the sites we have been working on for ten years now.
They are very important sites in the history and in the making of the identity of territories and landscapes.
Landscapes, in a sense, did not exist before man: man must make them, contribute to their co-generation.
These factories you see are former factories, production plants or former schools, former alpine colonies.
All sites introduced by this decommissioning particle "ex", because they are no more. They have been trailblazers of their territory.
People worked in them, income, economy, sociality, culture were generated in them; and then they stopped.
Now you see them in a third phase: the first phase was their making, by those who conceived them within the landscape around them and along with the flow of history.
The second phase is the dismissive one, the end of their productive and propulsive life within the landscape, for the territory.
The third phase is this one, because for ten years, as I told you, we have been facing these landscapes' critical, unsolved issues. I mean, these large sites who once were of great value and then have lost it.
They have lost their original identity.
The third phase is the awakening one.
We need to understand if we are able to think of another functional use, if they can be useful again to the territory, or if they no longer exist because they are mere vestiges.
We knew all about them in the past, much less so in the present; we lost the ability to act on them, so we can no longer imagine their future.
So what are they?
Graveyards to contemplate with nostalgia,
or a living heritage, common goods with a regenerable potential?
Well, this was our bet: two years after the award, we started to investigate these large collection of sites.
The Dolomites are certainly a heritage, but they are also, in many profiles, a heritage of stereotypes, of clichés, of rather trivial ideas, through which one can see a mountain that either no longer exists - that of our grandfathers in their knickerbockers, or anyway a mountain that struggles a lot to look ahead, to look at new horizons, because it is already given.
But we are saying that landscapes must either be generated or regenerated: they are not given forever, sometimes they are temporary.
In the first factories, in the first images that you have seen, that we faced in the first two years, this has happened: after working for the territory, they were laying down, and then someone has tried to move them: public or even private bodies came along, and what did they do?
A restoration.
And what came next?
They stood as still as they were before the restoration.
What does this mean?
That the economic resources arrived, but the real resources did not arrive!
So this heritage was "betrayed" because of a lack of resources that were being put
in reawakening the good.
And what was missing? An idea.
Because if you just restore a good, with no idea of what to do with it, you "betray" it, and do it a bad - and expensive - service.
Well, with a cultural project inspired by a functional logic - because the test is also to verify whether the culture is a didactic, exornative apparatus,
or it can be a real activator and a concrete driver, a part of the codesign of landscapes and territories, we have addressed these sites.
They were over, yes, but not "spent" in our opinion:
there certainly was a great potential there.
What is this potential?
Consider that, as you are seeing, they are extraordinary for their aesthetic values, for their context, then they are certainly still, and maybe the most important thing is to pick the right ones: not all sites are likely to be regenerated and useful, they must have a logistical "consistency", to use this practical word, and then there must be a functional idea in the attitude, in the attempt to regenerate them.
That means, some sites are better not to address them, you have to "ponder" their restoration carefully; others, if they really have much potential but are worthless in their current state, it is appropriate and necessary to address them.
It is a matter of responsibility - and sustainability, as we'll see.
Well, the sites we deal with are just a few, not all of them: it is not industrial archaeology, is a restricted selection but a wide one.
Since 2011, we have worked on about 20 sites: sites with a certainly great potential, but nothing in reality.
So there is a large measure to fill.
Because if we all say that a site is worth 100, here we are in Borca di Cadore, a former ENI village that Mr. Enrico Mattei made in the fifties, with architects Edoardo Gellner and with Carlo Scarpa in one part, if we say it's very worthy but not anything in reality, we are doomed to fail in awakening the potential of this site.
And whom is its potential for?
For the legit owner?
No, all the sites have a legitimate owner, public or private, but as you are seeing, now you are seeing the Monte Ricco fort in Pieve di Cadore, then you will see the Vajont. The sites we choose are so strong, formidable and exceptional, that either they recapitulate themes that are larger than themselves, Or you really have to be blind not to see their potential.
So their value, as a heritage asset, belongs to us all, not just the body who owns their walls.
And that's what we work on: with a project of responsibility and reuse we strive to resume the cultural heritage value, for the benefit of us all!
What can these sites become again?
It depends.
In the first cases, the factories you saw in the first images, they have returned to being factories.
What have we done?
We approached them.
They had failed.
We built a series of relationships.
We have almost 500 partners.
Who are these partners?
They are territorial and extra-territorial partners, public and private, companies, businesses, scientific or research partners.
Basically, there is a great moment of activation of territorial and extra-territorial networks and we try to show again the appeal and potential of the sites.
It's a bit like having a large basket of fantastic fruit in the kitchen, but the light is off.
Nobody eats spoiled fruit, but turning the light on is enough.
It's not easy to "enlighten" a site that's been abandoned for years or decades where, let's say, the particular society in that particular context has lost hope.
Everyone would lose it: first it worked, then it didn't work anymore, then we restored it and it still hasn't gone.
Is it a damn resource sink?
No, it's an asset to be networked again.
But how can you create a network, if you don't turn on the nets?
We used to talk about dopamine, remember this morning?
There is an essay from 1974, I guess, by neuropsychiatrist Oliver Sacks,
called "Awakenings", "Risvegli".
It's about disease, lethargy and cure.
I am a curator.
Dolomiti Contemporanee is a project of landscape, assets curation.
An injection is needed of cultural dopamine, a neurotransmission is needed.
Confidence needs to be instilled in the territories that have lost it, to rekindle themselves.
These sites belong to the land around them, but then they are so important, you see the Vajont, that they far exceed their geographical location within the territory.
Fact is, they are excluded from the flow of history.
Do you know what happened in Vajont on October 9, 1963?
Not only 2000 people died, all the men of the Earth died a little.
So that place is there and it is also not there.
It belongs to the whole humanity.
It is an emblematic place, it is at the same time a particular place placed in a point of space-time, and a universal place.
So turning the school back on, the old Vajont's elementary school and making it a center for contemporary culture of the landscape and the mountain, means facing a terribly decommissioned site - Because Vajont is in a sense the land of the living dead: there is a dam there that attracts thousands upon thousands of people every year, who tend to see a gravestone, a wall of tears.
To face this landscape, without acknowledging the fact that it can coincide with the landscape of tragedy, is a betrayal:
landscapes are for those living people who must build them.
There is a definition of Edoardo Gellner, who built the former Eni Village, namely Enrico Mattei's social welfare program, and many other things. Gellner says that "the landscape is the sum of natural environment and human action.
Good action, good landscape; bad action, bad landscape, no place for man".
So the Vajont can't be a forgotten, backward-looking landscape, hostage to a tragedy that once was and is no longer there, or paralyzed in memory, which can also act as a pathogen,
because it does not allow, if you don't elaborate upon it, to conceive another landscape, projected in the future.
In Vajont they're in need of a projective situation, so if you come and see what the new space of Casso is, it is a cultural device that opposes the predatory hegemony of a tragedy that cannot be the identity of those living today.
There is no forgetting, there is no absence of memory. There is a vision that wants to return these sites back to the men, to those who made and then lost them.
Well, all this gets done the way I'm telling you: in each site you make a residence, artists come, even architects, designers, landscape specialists, economists of culture, scientists, philosophers.
Marc Augé himself came, we brought him to the Vajont, as well as in Borca di Cadore to reflect on these places, not no-places!
But are we able or not to effectively project them in the present
We are, I think, at a time when we can't charm an audience, but to convince the territory that stuff belongs to them, that it can be useful again.
In Vajont you have to live: the former Eni village of Borca di Cadore can be used for many things, not for itself.
They are not enough for themselves, it does not matter how beautiful or important they were.
They are not enough.
A bold man action is needed,
Action is deliberate: no one has forced us to do it.
I work with about 30 young people from the territory who want to do something for the territory, and then we deal with the sites
through the strategies of the networks we activate the territory, of which we are the reactor, let's say.
Let's have the territory regain confidence in a relaunching venture.
That's why we have 500 partners, both indigenous and coming from afar. There are many international, national, many local, large and small,
All of them are essential, because we have no money.
Do you remember before?
Money had come without ideas.
It wasn't heritage, it wasn't resource.
Then we arrive without the money but with a wealth of ideas, and the sites start again.
The first sites are already restarting; commercial, productive activities have also joined, because they rediscovered the intelligent logistics of those sites, they were not dead at all.
Some sites count more than others because they "weigh" more, physically, historically, aesthetically speaking and so on, as the former village Eni, where we will return shortly.
More structural platforms are activated there, much like in Vajont.
You don't just go and start a business in Vajont, We had to live in Vajont for two years and get understood by the residents: we could have been the last fools, or jackals, but we weren't.
For what I said before we reason in a logic of public, not private functionality.
There is no selfishness by the curators or artists' side: everyone contributes and bring a piece of the relaunch program,
and this does not betray the art, that is always itself.
Except, the artist is intelligent, sensitive, ingenious, one may say enzymatic, as it triggers processes, he is not satisfied with definitions or judgments, so the site is not dead for him: they help regenerate it.
Sites become workplaces of artistic and cultural production where networks are triggered, also because we bring a lot of diverse people together.
There are scientists of the forest.
Do you know what Vaia storm is?
October 2018, 14,000,000 trees
torn down by the wind. If you come to these sites, particularly in Casso and at Monte Ricco fort, you will find group exhibitions of contemporary art with 30 artists working on themes of Vaia, together with climate scientists, forest scientist, biologists, chemists.
So art does not make a little somersault, it is not a mere exornative apparatus; it is part of the mechanism of the regeneration strategy.
Sites then restart and we also put them in the available platforms.
Back to Borca, you know that for some time now we won the contest for 2026 Olympics, Milan-Cortina.
It is said, they have to be sustainable.
And they can only be sustainable if you don't build new architecture, and responsibly detect what's already available.
Borca Eni village is 100,000 square meters wide; that were built very well, as Mattei and Gellner were behind it.
There were no budget limits but above all ingenuity limits.
That is a space where serious considerations should be spent on.
Shall we take it back?
It's done so well, it's not a ruin.
It's near Cortina, it's not an antagonistic logic, either Borca or Cortina.
The Olympics, after all, are not the 2026 ski races, they are a chance for development in the territory, here to there.
And so it is still necessary to take this assets, and rediscover, revive it.
And also, if we speak about sustainability and low land consumption, see what's there before doing something new.
It's that easy.
Mr Mattei said one thing, "The ingenuity shared by scientists and artists is the ability to see things where others fail to".
You have to be blind not to see the potential of these sites, I think.
So it's not enough to see them, and actuallty Mattei didn't just see, he did a lot.
If these sites are really worth a lot in potential as we say, they must be seen and then they must be done.
We have to build the landscapes of the present, that's the contemporary gaze: as I told you, it's not nostalgic, it's not retroverted.
It tries to project forward its own potential, aware of its past, but forward.
That's something one must consider, we must understand if we are actually able to awaken this heritage or it's enough for us to say that the Dolomites are World Heritage Sites, without entering the territory to see what we can really do with the tools at hand - culture, sensitivity and intelligence, to restart these sites for the benefit of us all.
(Applause)
In 1707, off the Isles of Scilly, not far from Cornwall, the British Navy experienced one of its greatest tragedies: four warships crashed into the rocks, and 2,000 sailors lost their lives.
At the origin of the disaster, a problem the whole Europe was struggling with: no one know how to quickly, and accurately measure the longitude in the open sea.
The British government decided to attack this problem head-on and with a parliamentary act, allocated a tremendous reward - today's equivalent of about three million euros - in favour of anyone who can find a solution.
They also designated a selected committee, to evaluate the numerous proposals that the prize attracted, headed by no less then [Isaac Newton].
Newton, on that matter, had a very precise position.
He was convinced that only an astronomer, relying on the scientific method, could solve the problem.
Despite a fierce competition among the scientific cream of the time, the most impredictable contender eventually won the prize.
He was not an astronomer, nor a mathematician; he was not an Oxford or Cambridge academic. In fact, he had never studied in any academy.
He was a humble self-taught carpenter from the Lincolnshire, called John Harrison.
For ten years, together with Professor Gino Cattani of NYU, I studied the process through which novelties enter and take root within socio-economic contexts.
It is in the course of this activity that I have come across a surprising category of individuals: innovators as extraordinary as unlikely, often ostracized if not ridiculed.
with unusual socialization paths, who operate at the margins - if not entirely outside - of the system they try to innovate.
In other words, outsiders.
To find them out, I have statistically analysed tens of thousands of artists' careers.
I have tracked back the life of John Harrison,
the life and the achievements of, according by "The Times", "The Most Influential Woman Of The 20th Century."
I analyzed the innovation trajectory of perhaps our generation's most iconic entrepreneur.
With the same curiosity, I have studied the professional evolution of our time's most venerated filmmaker.
And I have discovered that the world of outsiders is full of surprises worth spreading.
Let's go back to Harrison.
Why was Newton wrong?
Because his total adherence to the scientific paradigm led him to see very clearly what Harrison did not have. But he dismissed what he had: an incredible passion for clocks, which he had begun to build since the age of five developing in his carpentry an exceptional understanding of materials, oscillating mechanisms, counterweights.
Armed with this experience, entirely different from the dominant expectations, Harrison faced and cracked the longitude problem by creating a marine chronometer: the first clock in the world capable of withstanding the extreme conditions of transoceanic voyages and accurately measure the time at a given terrestrial reference point.
In summary, a mechanical solution, not the astronomical one that Newton was expecting.
The pattern is always the same.
Outsiders innovate by bringing experiences that are new to the context they enter in, but familiar to the context they come from.
Take the case of Coco Chanel, the illegitimate daughter of a laundress and a street peddler. Abandoned and raised by nuns in an orphanage, she found in this humble context an inspiration for some of her most iconic aesthetic ideas.
For example, her proverbial predilection for black and white is unanimously attributed to her protracted exposure to the colours of the orphanage uniforms and the nuns' tunics.
The unmistakable logo is a decoration of the windows of the Romanesque abbey of Aubazine, home to the orphanage, whose austerity also inspired her rigorous and functional style.
The rockstar of film directors was raised by a sixteen-year-old single mother of Cherokee origin.
He dropped out of school when he was 15 and enrolled in a drama school, which he abandoned after two years.
He entered the film industry without any canonical training but after spending five years as a clerk in a video store where he literally absorbed thousands of hours of movies often ignored by the mainstream: blaxploitation, Z-series horrors, wuxia pan.
Works stemmed from this concoction that became cult movies among cinéphiles worldwide.
The status of an adopted child of a proletarian family, the modest education, a young age in 1973 made Steve Jobs anything but an insider of the personal computer industry.
But it was by wandering at the margins of this industry that he came across a trappist monk, Robert Palladino, whose calligraphic teachings inspired him to develop the unmistakable style of the Mac, as it's well known.
What does all this mean?
It means that we must train to bring new eyes into our worlds. And to do this, we must train ourselves to look at the fringe, if not entirely outside of the world we want to innovate.
Cognitive science tells us that this training consists of feeding the brain with experiences that are far away from our areas of specialization.
A research team from Michigan University compared the Nobel Prize winners with a control group of scientists comparable to the Nobel winners, but who never won the Nobel. They wondered: what distinguishes those who change the game so much, they win the Nobel Prize?
Look at these results: Nobel Prize winners, on average, compared to the control group, are twice as likely to cultivate a music-related hobby; seven times more likely to have an hobby associated with the visual arts; 12 times more likely to be interested in poetry; 22 time more likely to cultivate a passion for the performing arts: theatre, drama, magic.
Not only must we train ourselves to overcome our familiar cognitive boundaries, but also our social ones.
A few years ago, I mapped the network of collaborations of 12,000 Hollywood artists to try and understand if creative success arises at the center or at its margins of the system.
These are the results.
As you can see, individuals at the very fringes don't have the highest creative success, the "Renegades From The Human Race" - in the words of Dan Valentine, one of the world’s greatest venture capitalists when he first met Steve Jobs in 1976.
But not from those at the network's center either, the "network kings".
Statistically speaking, the optimal chance of success in a border zone between the center and the periphery: that is, in correspondence to those individuals who belong to the system and yet don't lose touch with its borders.
The best metaphor I could come up with, to convey this idea, is "One arm inside, one outside".
We should firmly hold on to our core context, with one arm; but project ourselves with the other, towards its borders:
Exploring non-customary social settings; reading poems if we have always read history books; learning a musical instrument, if we have never done so; traveling in the south of the world if we sticked with the north.
OK, thank you, all clear, you may say now. Well, not exactly.
Thinking like an outsider can have advantages, but walking to the outsider’s side is rather complicated.
To explain you why, I have to introduce you one of the 20th century's most famous social psychology tests, better known as The Asch Experiment.
In the early 1950s, Professor Asch invited eight people in his laboratory to participate in a test.
In reality, the guinea pig was only one and the other seven were figurants, previously instructed by Asch.
The exercise was straightforward.
Asch showed them a figure like this one asking them all to identify the line on the right as high as the one on the left.
Easy.
The first participant looked at the figure and answered correctly, two;
and so did the second, and the third, and the fourth.
And everyone else, up to our eighth “guinea pig”, who also answered correctly, two.
The second round was similar, with no surprises.
But from the third round on, the staging began.
Asch showed this figure.
The first participant looked intensely, and answered, one.
The second, without hesitation, did the same and answers one.
And so did the third, the fourth, the fifth, all the way to our unaware guinea pig who started to look around quite puzzled.
With the corner of the eye, he looked at the other seven participants in the room and started to think, it should be three.
But in fact... it could also be one.
Yes, it must be one.
And so he replied, one.
Asch repeats the experiment hundreds of times and the result is the following: even when the answer is self-evident, 75 percent of the guinea pigs answer incorrectly, and stick with the groups' error, if they belong a group where the other participants are told to give wrong answers.
Why?
Fifty years later, Gregory Berns from Emory University replicated the experiment, used magnetic resonance imaging to monitor brain activity during the experiment.
Turned out that guinea pigs, when they give wrong answers to fit with the group, express hyperactivity in the parietal lobe, the brain area responsible for the perception of reality.
Berns runs several experiments and concludes that social pressure can induce an unconscious distortion of reality, leading individuals - but also whole groups of people! - to see and therefore believe in something that makes no sense.
Sometimes, as Asch himself had presaged 50 years earlier, with tragic, devastating consequences.
But there's more to it.
The MRI also shows,
the few nonconformists who withstand the social pressure exhibit a hyperactivity of the amygdala, the emergency system of the brain.
There is one stimulus that triggers amygdala the most: fear.
Fear of swim upstream alone.
Let's recap: bringing a new point of view into an established social context is healthy, and we can train ourselves.
But it is a cognitively, and especially emotionally expensive thing to do.
Thankfully, not only Asch revealed the problem, but also showed a way out.
Conformism is as powerful as it is easy to unlock: just one ally, a single dissident, is enough to flip those percentages and break the siege.
What's the ally's profile then?
How can we find them?
Why do they help the outsider?
Honestly, I don't know.
I do know, however, that often the ally is a person, or a group of people, who has for whatever reason a particular affinity for the kind of novelty that the outsider advances. And possibly, also the credibility the outsider usually lacks.
In the case of Harrison, these allies were James Short first and King George III at a later stage.
James Short was an eminent member of the Royal Society who dedicated his life to building precision instruments, although optical ones, and that's why he sympathized with John Harrison.
King George III - luckily, before becoming completely mad - took Harrison's cause to heart because he had a passion for watches, he avidly collected them.
For Chanel, the ally was Misia Sert, the patron of the arts, and the group of artists who gravitated around her and “adopted” Chanel because they shared with her the new modernist impetus, with its emphasis on geometry, functionality, simplicity.
For Steve Jobs it was Mike Markkula, the first person to believe in his project after dozens of no.
Markkula, we know it, was wealthy and passionate about technology; but he also was just over 30 when he first met Steve Jobs.
Look at this chart.
It shows the relationship between age and dopamine density in the body.
Dopamine is a neurotransmitter that declines with age but is particularly active between 20 and 40 years old.
A study from Vanderbilt University suggest that dopamine has a fundamental role in fueling our predisposition to novelty, risk, discontinuity.
Not only is Markkula a credible ally, he also has the right age to embrace Jobs' challenge.
Second message for those who want to take the outsider’s path: "Keep the amygdala in check, find your Marrkula!"
(Laughter) (Applause) If we don't find our Markkula, there is another way, which is also the message I leave you with.
Outsiders can also often exploit what I call "fracture points".
Fracture points are temporally defined events that create stress in the system, catalysing attention where there was no such attention before.
A particular class of fracture points I have been dealing with are those Max Planck sarcastically refered to in one of his famous aphorisms.
Namely, the disappearance, literally the death of the system's gatekeepers who, by dying, make room for new.
In cooperation with Simone Santoni of the City University of London, we applied this provocative intuition to the music industry, asking ourselves, What happens in the aftermath of a superstar's sudden death?
Does this open a window for peripheral artists to access the system?
Does novelty really have a higher chance of entering the system?
To answer these questions, we have assembled one of the world’s largest databases of musicians, songs, music awards, and sudden superstar death events.
What we found did surprise us.
Look at this chart, that shows who is bestowed a grammy award - the top acknowledgment in the music industry - with or without a "shock" caused by the sudden death of a superstar.
Genre by genre, after the shock of a system the chance for peripheral artists to be acknowledged increases dramatically, by 40%.
Plus, the albums who won right after a shock are on average more original in their acoustic composition.
The death of a star is a "fracture point" that makes room for novelties.
OK, so?
So last year, I talked about this study to a class of execs,
and at the end of the presentation one of them stood up.
He was a rather stocky R&amp;D manager.
He stood up and stared at me.
Like this.
And he said: "Thanks, Simone.
I have always known it, but now I can state for sure. We have to suppress the boss!"
(Risate) (Applause) I understand, the boss is not seated next to you right now.
(Laughter) No, the example is extreme, but the intuition is very simple. Fracture points are events that produce stress in the system increasing its tolerance for novelty, much like in 1707, the Scilly’s wreck, and the subsequent prize, created an entry port to the improbable Harrison.
Thus, third message for you tonight: fracture points matter.
What did Newton fail to see?
What none of us will ever see if we do not acknowledge that new perspectives can only come from new places, far away from our daily routine.
That swimming upstream is scary, but a single ally is sometimes enough to break the siege.
That systems under stress are more tolerant of diversity.
In other words: One arm inside, one outside; Keep the amygdala in check, find your Markkula; Watch out for fracture points. But first and foremost, don't fracture the head of your boss!
(Applause)
Do you know who this is?
It is a platypus.
An animal that lives in Australia.
A strange animal. And it is probably for this reason, that it has always attracted my attention.
It is saying hi!
(Laughter) Look at it!
Its beak is like a duck’s.
It has webbed feet exactly like a duck.
But it doesn't have two, it has four.
Its fur is like a minks', or possibly more like an otters', And it has the tail of a beaver.
But the extraordinary thing is that it is a mammal, but it lays eggs.
It lays eggs, but like any mammal it nurses its young.
What animal is it?
It is semiaquatic, therefore, it lives both in the water and on land.
It is the only living representative from its family and its genus.
It has no relatives. It is a bit lonely.
Poor thing, described this way he sounds like a nerd, a bit of a loser.
Instead if we go and read the major texts on our education in Nature, for example the Manual of the Young Marmots the platypus is an animal with some very unique qualities: It has a sense of electrolocation, which very few animals have. Which means it locates its prey by sensing electric currents.
It is an Olympic swimmer and it swims without the sense of sight. But, rather, it uses its other senses.
It can swim for hours in freezing cold waters. And its body temperature never changes.
Therefore, I think of it like Keanu Reeves’s stuntman in the Matrix, a real cool dude!
However, it hasn't often been described in very exciting ways.
According to the Australian legend, it came to being as a crossbreed between a duck and a mouse.
The mouse kidnapped the duck and took advantage of it, and the platypus was born.
Not a great start.
The first British settlers, who found one, captured it, killed it - naturally - skinned it, brought its skin back to Britain, and they examined and studied it.
The British scientists said, that such an animal simply could not exist.
That undoubtedly it must have been a fake, made up by some Asian taxidermist.
In philosophy there is a metaphor: anything that complicates classification,
and as such cannot be classified, and as a consequence, is incomprehensible. and, consequently, it doesn't exist.
It is commonly listed among vulnerable species. Which is the list, that precedes the red list of critically endangered species.
However, to this day it still hasn’t ended up on that list.
I have always felt like a platypus.
Not so much in the way I look, (Laughter) all my bits are from the same species, or so it seems. But because I have always felt different to the world around me.
I lived in Selva del Montello, a tiny village in the province of Treviso, but in my mind I was in Seattle, in the States.
Because that is where my great-aunt Oliva came from, who, after a lifetime living in Seattle, returned to Italy around the time I was born.
Aunt Oliva was my baby sitter and she spoke to me in English.
In a village, Selva del Montello, where, still to this day, the official language is a dialect.
If you speak in Italian you are already considered quite strange.
As a young girl, I would say I wanted to be a carpenter.
(Laughter) "A girl?"
"Don't you dream of becoming a nurse just like your mom?"
"No, a carpenter!"
I’ve always been a real chatterbox, in a family of taciturn people.
In my home, you could spend hours without uttering a single word.
I was different.
I felt one way and realised that I was perceived in another.
After all, I felt fine, but I realised that I was perceived as being weird.
And all around me I heard a chorus of blunt opinions and phrases.
A sociable kid in a geographic area populated by unsociable bears.
"Chatterbox!" "Big mouth!"
"A child with a creative predisposition"
And more: " I want to go to art school."
"You cannot live off art!"
I went to university and what did I choose to study?
Oriental languages.
"Japanese?"
And then I decided to send out my CV before finishing my degree. And an American multinational company hired me straight away.
"Who knows when they will ever hire you?" "Straight away!"
"Without a degree you won’t be hired, and you will never find a good job."
After spending 10 years working for that American multinational company, I decided it was time for a change.
I realized that I was no longer happy with who I was and I made the decision to resign. Without a plan B.
So I quit my permanent contract, with no plan B.
"Are you crazy?"
"Don't you realize there is a job crisis out there?"
"You're mad!"
During that sabbatical period, sabbatical sounds cool! (Laughter) I went back to being that young girl carpenter.
I opened a small business. A workshop for buying and selling second-hand furniture.
Not antiques, old used stuff.
I would buy the furniture, paint it,
restore it and resell it.
Guess what?
"What do you think you’re doing?"
Well partly they were right: The Everest of bureaucracy in Italy does not help a small workshop.
I set aside "Owl on the Sofa", that’s what it was called. And I re-plan my life, I went back to doing what I had done for ten years. And where I had left my heart. But I went back as a freelancer.
A free-lancer.
Well, What do you think?
The support?
"Who’ll take you back, after being a carpenter in the midst of it all?"
"You’ll never make it!"
I will forever remember that sentence, spoken by a firm consultant who I had asked for advice.
"What do you think if...?"
"You will never make it!"
I’ve been a freelancer now for eight years, happily!
(Applause) Here I am!
During this path, I would stop and look inside myself and I noticed something different.
I felt I was a different species.
I was a Platyrianna.
(Laughter) I have always managed to achieve what I had in mind.
I have always reached the places I’ve aimed to get to.
And I can assure you I have seen so much magic.
Where did I start?
I have always dreamed a lot.
I say about myself I could be a dream donor.
And I have always found the tools to make these dreams come true.
We know what it’s like: when we set out, we grab a dream and bring it to the ground, we try to make it come true. We meet many obstacles on the way, we all know this.
The first obstacle is those around us.
But at a certain point, after a long time, I understood that my biggest obstacle was not conditioning, being judged, or what other people thought.
And it wasn’t even my inner voice conditioned by those things.
I had another problem, and it was very big.
I had this absolute need to have two very different souls coexist inside me.
The creative, artistic one and the very rational one.
Two beautiful souls, very powerful. With the same right to exist.
If one soul pulled me one way the other pulled me the other way. I tried to gratify one and I tried to gratify the other.
I went forward with my hand-break on.
I was potentially explosive, but I was held back.
Those internal contradictions, those tensions, are felt by everyone.
And everyone in someway feels, that they are somehow different.
But this difference is trivial.
The point is not the diversity itself, but how we actually feel about that diversity.
I suffered a lot and yet I did a lot.
Do I make my dreams come true?
I do!
Do I plan?
I do!
If I fall, I get up again, and adjust my aim.
I do it!
I cover my ears, because I think the platypus covers its ears to avoid becoming extinct.
I do it!
Achieve results?
I do!
And while I am achieving those results, I am already doing something else.
Because I’ve realized that it’s not the result which makes me happy but it is the path I take to get there.
It is the doing which makes me happy and that is where I wish to stay.
That is why, I firmly believe that doing is the activator of happiness.
I always do. Even when I find myself surrounded in darkness.
In that famous tunnel where you can see that tiny light at the end. A light which becomes bigger until you are flattened. Because that tiny light was the headlights of a huge lorry.
I stand up again. A bit flattened.
And then I tell myself this phrase: "Marianna look at your feet!"
"This is the moment to look at your feet and to go forward."
This is the phrase I have repeated to myself most in my life.
And it is a phrase which has ended up in Platyrianna’s manual.
Here it is!
I’ll get it now and I want to read you some things.
Platyrianna Manual is a collection of effective quotes, to motivate and stimulate, of rules, instructions, tricks and stratagems. Orders I give to myself.
It is my “To Do” guide.
A bit like the Young Marmot's manual.
Inside, on the first page, there is a phrase I wrote to myself, which is Platyrianna’s mantra: “I choose who I am in my life and I don’t let others choose for me.”
Said by my parents, in dialect, sounds like this: "Assa star, tanto co' ea pensada l'ha anca fatta." Translated from the dialect of Selva del Montello: "Forget it, she hasn’t even finished thinking about it and she is already doing it."
It is divided in many long chapters.
And I have the pleasure to share with you.
Some parts that have been very enlightening for me.
From the chapter "I trust".
"I trust myself and my intuitions."
"I trust my friends, allies, my cheerleaders.
With their pom poms. Those who jump, dance, and always cheer for me. Because they love me."
From the chapter "Respect": "Respect my values and for this one I monitor myself constantly."
"Respect the physiological time of things and people."
I am a very impatient person. I want everything, now.
That's not how things work.
From the chapter "I allow myself":
"I allow myself moments of discomfort, As I know I will get over them,
"I allow myself to see the fun in everything I do."
"I should enjoy myself!"
The chapter "I protect myself from all Academics"
In everything you do, there will always be someone with an important title, more important than yours, who can tell you how you should be doing something.
And they are probably right.
Let's just do it that way anyway!
And, eventually, we'll shift our aim.
"Everyday I will protect myself from other people's delusions."
We're in the era of "everything all at once", but not for me.
I don't understand: if it doesn't apply to me, it won't apply to others either."
From the chapter "Not":
"I will not try to be ready, because I won't ever be.
"I won't fall into the trap of being a victim of perfectionism."
I won't go on, if, after having listened to my heart and analizing with my head, my gut feeling says: "no!"
Because my gut feeling is always right.
From the chapter "I Accept":
"I will accept errors." Because I know that they are integral to evolving.
"I will accept crisis." Because it is in moments of crisis that I find out who I really am, get to know myself better, and manage to adjust the goal I have set for myself.
With this system of "having something in mind and doing it" and with my manual at hand I recently wrote a book.
With a well-known co-author. Without him, who would have given my book the time of day? (Laughter) It wasn't easy: the first hurdle was the chorus of negative voices, which in reality was actually just my inner voice, said: "You are not a writer!"
Which is true.
But I still wanted to do it, because I knew deep down that it could help someone.
Then there was the real voice of the editor: "We do not publish books like this."
Oh well, remember what my parents said?
"Forget it!"
So the book was published.
It was a terrible process. Full of falls, never-ending tunnels and total darkness.
Oh well, it was published. The first objective achievement was that,
and to give the editor a response, after only three weeks it was reprinted.
But for me the real achievement was something else.
I started to receive messages
from strangers. Not from my friends, who love me and were lavishing me with compliments: Like: "We are so proud of you!"
But from strangers.
"Sorry to disturb you, I wanted to thank you for..." "Thank you for writing about this topic."
"Thank you for having written about this and about that."
Those thank yous were an injection of total trust and extreme happiness.
But, above all, they made me realize, that doing this didn't just make me happy,
but it also made other people happy because of me.
And that is a marvellous thing!
I will conclude by saying: The platypus may be strange.
But let's feel good by being the platypus.
Let's take care of ourselves, and accept every part of ourselves.
Know who we are and love ourselves. And really try to be the person we want to be in this life. Us!
Forget about everyone else!
(Applause) Thank you.
