Now, can we try to open it? "Haha".
Audience: Haha.
Very well. Despite the laughter itself is a funny thing, you can sit again in the most comfortable and suitable position for you, despite the laugher brings opening, in social life too, but in this case, simply biomechanically, it moves, open areas that are normally protective and that close our voice.
This is just a hint of what I do.
But our body speaks, it tells us that our body, our existence, our life has been shaped by fear,
namely the fear of error, and since it's telling us so, let's try to react.
As the fear acts, if it makes you unable to act , the countertrend is the action, so act!
Small actions: breathe deeply, every day; laugh loudly, every day;
make everything you want to do, respecting the other person, of course, but really give yourself the chance to do something whenever you think you won't do it because you are afraid that it may be wrong, experiment, test it!
We don't try, we don't experiment anymore, we no longer take this opportunity.
Ever since we were kids, we have been experimenting,
until we stopped doing it. OK?
Because kids can learn and so they can be wrong, the adult can not.
Children learn, adults do.
We only do what we can already do.
But that's enough!
We can go a little bit further, do we really want to be creative?
So our creative power lies in what we can potentially do and not in the limit of what we can already do.
Tear down the limits of fear!
Let's step out of our limits!
Do whichever thing you want to do.
Make mistakes!
You have the right to do it.
When we are children, falling is one step of learning. Okay.

It is said that a human being, to be able to move, to advance and take a step, needs to get away from their center, to lose their balance, to get lost.
Now if falling is one step of learning, then it's our choice. We can either stay still
or make mistakes. Thank You.
(Applause).
We are the stories we remember.
So, we must start with a story.
The stories that pass through time and space are the stories that define us.
This story begins at sea.
It is a rough sea, and there is a boat fighting against it.
The sailors row with difficulty, they know that the sea means death because, in the Odyssey, the sea is death.
It's crossing the unknown, the definition of the world and of oneself.
Tied to the mast of this ship is the hero we all know, Ulysses.
At one point, all of a sudden, this rough sea becomes flat, calm.
And fear captures any sailor who knows that, if there is not even a bit of wind, reaching a port becomes difficult.
And in this almost mystical silence a whisper is heard:
voices of women - incredibly sweet.
They are the voices of women who have a very strange body.
Their body is not what we imagined with the help of cartoons.
It is a bird’s body, but their voice is very sweet.
They promise to tell the sailors of that boat what is happening on Earth.
And in particular what happened during the Trojan war.
Now - you all know that I am talking about the sirens of Ulysses - what kind of temptation is this?
One who is returning - at least he is trying, he’s been trying for 20 years - to return from the Trojan war; And you, as a promise, as a temptation, tell him: "I will tell you what you have already experienced".
Clearly it is a very strange temptation.
Why does this hero of ours, who has tied himself to the mast of the ship, wants to hear exactly this song about the story from which he is returning?
To know if that story has entered history.
In the Greek world, entering the history of epic means finally having an identity.
Every culture is structured around this great challenge: how can we keep our name from falling into nothingness?
Birth is an occurrence that we have not chosen, and our greatest fear is that we will return to the void from which we came.
Ulysses wants to know if his name has finally entered History; and this is worth a temptation like that of the sirens.
But when the ship passes by the island of the sirens, around the rocks only bones of men can be seen.
All of us here have this same problem.
In the morning we get up and look for a reason, a performance through which we can finally be someone.
And this generates in us that fear of living - that "fatigue of living", as a philosopher calls it.
Why? Because we have transformed
our self into a performance.
Our lives resemble onions: we have many layers with which we try to define our identity, but we don’t know where the deep core has ended up.
"I come from this city, I have this job", and we try to build on the periphery of our ego an identity, so that our being does not fall into nothingness.
Because, I repeat, we have only one problem: how can we keep things from falling into nothingness?
And this scares us.
This tires us.
And every culture finds its solution.
When I meet the parents of my students - the rare times when they both come to the meetings, because it seems that nowadays education is entrusted only to mothers: Dear dads, come and talk to us teachers, your children will be proud of the fact that you have taken an hour of leave for them - I say to them: "Ah, now I understand".
“What it is that you understand?"
"The reason why your child is like that".
(Laughter) Because your children do not resemble each of you individually.
They resemble the relationship between the two of you.
Because the only way for a man to be born and to be born again is to be generated.
And only when there is a relationship, there is a principle that gives birth to that nucleus of being that allows us that permanence in existence, that does not depend on things that continually escape us.
And finally we can breathe a sigh of relief.
This is why roll calls in the morning are important.
I believe that the greatest revolution in contemporary school, especially in the Italian one, depends on how we call the roll.
Concerning animals, nature provides for the fact that they pull out armor, claws, fangs to be able to survive in the environment in which the pup immediately has to make do.
And why instead man cub takes so much time to make do?
Because the man cub is counter-evolutionary.
He maintains his frailty, because frailty forces other men to care.
This is what differentiates us from animals.
Our claws, our armor, our fangs are here and here.
A relationship means giving the other what the other needs.
When you drink a glass of water you quench your thirst, you have come into contact with a relationship: the simplest thing: H20.
Two atoms of hydrogen and one of oxygen is a relationship; each gives the other what the other needs.
And it’s only a relationship that generates and regenerates a person.
In an era in which we delude ourselves that we can gain time, and struggle to gain time - an absurd expression, as if we were not part of time. We don’t have time at our disposal, time has us at its disposal.
The only way to gain time is to grow, and the only way to grow is to always be reborn as a new being.
So I had fun redefining the ages of life, in “The art of being frail", starting from this very element.
It is now quite a widespread idea that the stages of life are disposed on a straight line: there is a beginning and then, following an almost inertial principle, we will eventually get to the credits.
So we find ourselves struggling to conquer that permanence, to know if we have entered History with the capital H.
And if there was another solution?
If we could really be happy without having to prove it to anyone?
Coulnd’t it be that a living being lives in the measure in which it is generated and regenerated?
So, generating these children of yours doesn’t happen once and for all.
It happens every day during which someone decides to put that person back into the world.
Only in this way do we gain time and let others gain time, because we don’t stop growing and making them grow.
Life does not resemble a straight line, it resembles a spiral; whenever we are close to the center that generates this nucleus of novelty that each of us is - because to be born is to be unpublished to the world, none of us here has the same fingerprints, none of us has the same iris,
in such a way that now our technological objects recognize us so.
And we love Siri, we love Cortana; We look at that screen on average 200 times a day.
Imagine yourself staring at your son, your daughter, 200 times a day.
Just like that, all of a sudden, 200 times.
They will ask you,"Are you nuts?"
(Laughter) No!
It's because you're important,
and there's never been anything like you on Earth.
It is exactly what we seek from those objects: the fact that they know that we are unique.
But isn’t there another way to know it?
Yes, but it’s a tiring one.
Life is a spiral: the closer we are to this center as it moves forward, the more our life lives and expands; the more we move away, the more our life disintegrates.
And what is the way in which the stages of life are realized?
I had fun, through the words of a poet, in redefining them, because they have nothing to do with a straight line.
What is it with adolescence that we must keep for a lifetime?
What is it about maturity that we must maintain throughout our life?
So I redefined life this way: adolescence as an art of hope; maturity as an art of dying; the third age, or reparation, as the art of being frail; and the last as dying or as the art of being born again.
What happens during adolescence? Why it is called the art of hope?
Because for the first time the unpublished script that each of these youngsters came here to tell, emerges.
And there is no longer the easy solution dictated by pleasure or obligation that is typical of a child - everything they want, they cry and they get it.
The unpublished finally comes to light and it is the reason why a boy, a girl, feels for the first time that freedom is in his or her hands.
He or she is ready to break any walls, as long as that novelty is realized.
And this is what makes young people the main characters of their own lives.
Protagonist is a beautiful, ancient word, which indicates the one who fights in the front row, the one who fights first.
But nobody fights without a reason to fight.
And adolescence is the moment when that ardour that we can call rapture, vocation, as you wish to define it, finally comes into our existence.
Through the relationship with the world we understand our purpose.
I remember when my literature teacher called me and told me: "Alessandro, this is my favourite book of poetry,
you can borrow it. Bring it back in two weeks".
That day I became a teacher,
because that teacher told me his secret. He saw in me something I myself couldn't see the mirror. This is what that roll call is.
It is the fact that, in my eyes, when I call that name, they must see that I am already lovingly aware of the story that will come to life.
I do not know it, but I know that the core of my belief - and "professor" comes from to profess - are the first and last names of those students.
That is why they are more important than the lesson itself.
And then the second phase begins: full of this ardour, of this vocational impulse, we think that the world is waiting for us.
Then maturity is the art of dying, because, one after the other, all the things we think we have to bring into the world will be dismantled
and nobody will be waiting for us.
But since we have freed ourselves from the pleasure principle of the child, we know and learn that that fire feeds on the obstacles it encounters.
Imagine Michelangelo in front of a block of marble
daring to say: "This Carrara marble is such a pain. It is so tough".
(Laughter) And yet, it is precisely that hardness, that resistance, that gives us masterpieces such as those by Michelangelo.
We must understand that this frees a teenager from the claim, the narcissistic delirium, typical of those who remain children forever - that there is nothing to explore in the world, and that the world only serves as an affirmation of the self.
Only those who have found in the world a reason to die will explore it for real.
So maturity is the art of dying without losing the ardour of adolescence.
And so we enter the third age, the repair or art of being frail, that depends on one thing only: the quality of the relationships we have with the world and with others.
To say, "I love you" to somebody has now become too simple.
Sometimes, instead of ,"I love you", see if you can write, "I don't want you to die, and I will work hard so that this will not happen".
Let me say it again: "I don't want you to die, and I will do all I can so that this won't happen".
To love a person is to be the keepers of their destiny, like that teacher who gave me the book that day,
because that morning he wasn’t thinking of yet another boring school day to face and of annoying pupils to put up with.
From his library he took the book that could be save his student.
because a relationship thrives when you give the other what the other needs.
You take care of the world only when you have something to bring into the world.
If today I am a happy teacher at the age of 40, is because when I was 17 a man lent me his favorite book of poetry.
It's like I'm explaining water, the simplest of things,
but water is a relationship: H2O.
Giving the other what the other needs.
So we can allow ourselves to enter the final phase of our life which isn’t, after all, the final one:
dying, or the art of being born again, means exactly that, when a person discovers what is the unpublished book they came to write on this earth,
the struggle they fight every day to accomplish the task, in others before than in themselves, is what confirms to them they did something beautiful in the world. That other people may or may not know about it, it doesn't matter.
So, each time I call the roll, what I should do is get down on my knees in front of those 20, 25 faces who are the very world that was entrusted to me.
The first time Ulysses appears in the Odyssey, he is crying,
because real heroes shed tears more than anyone else.
And why is he crying?
Because he longs for Ithaca, his wife, his son, his father.
Calypso, the immortal and beautiful goddess, is surprised, and asks him,
"Why are you crying?
Why are you fragile, why are you mortal?"
And Ulysses replies, "Because my home lies beyond this sea".
And she begs him, "Stay with me!
I will make you immortal
and I am much more beautiful than Penelope.”
Charming, Calypso.
These goddesses who don't know death, and don't know what loving means.
And Ulysses replies, "It's true, you're more beautiful than Penelope.
But she is my wife. She is the one I’ve chosen."
And for this reason, Ulysses chooses mortality, chooses frailty, chooses a small, and quite inhospitable island.
Because, there, his son Telemachus awaits, and his father, Laerte, his wife, Penelope. And for this we will
remember him forever and ever,
because what defines our life is what we are able to love.
What defines our life is what we are able to love.
So I would like to close with the verses of a poet, who died of cancer at 50 years of age, and asked for these words on his tombstone, the verses he had written in his last poem, "Did you receive what you wished
from life, in spite of it all?
Yes.
And what did you wish for?
I wished I could say I was loved, I wished to feel loved on Earth.
What we are able to love is what will never be ripped from us.
What we are able to love is our heritage, the rest is waste.
What we are able to love is our history".
(Applause)
The topic of this TEDx event is "heterotopia",
although I don’t know how many speakers will explicitly talk about heterotopias today.
Thus, for the sake of clarity, I’ll start with a definition of heterotopia,
a personal definition, one that sounds clear and sensible to me.
A heterotopia is a subspace of the generic space we live in,
a subspace even in a broader sense: for example, a subspace of the Web.
A subspace where the rules of the generic space -- prohibitions, expectations, and so on -- are overturned, modified, simplified or mirrored, depending on the type of heterotopia.
Now, let’s make an example of heterotopia: carnival.
Someone may call it a heterochronia -- although I think that, after Einstein time and space are one.
The carnival is clearly a heterotopia, and has a feature heterotopias typically share: it’s a "discharge" moment, a space where contradictions, instead of exploding in a full-blown revolution, find their place in a contained, socially accepted revolution, with well-defined temporal boundaries.
This way, society prevents revolutions and outbursts -- and this is bad.
Porn is also a heterotopia: when you visit a porn website, you are warned. It’s a place where some rules are suspended, or reversed --
on a porn website, for example, you can see a girl in postures, attitudes, and behaviors you can hardly find on common websites;
they're actually forbidden
everywhere else. Carnival also looks like an upside-down world. A child, for example, is forbidden to dress up as Batman and go to school.
Porn is an upside-down world as well, isn’t it?
It embodies the same subversion of usual relations that we can find in heterotopias.
Alas, much like carnival, porn is also an outlet, and that’s bad.
That’s bad because it keeps contradictions, arising from how sex is treated in the common space, from exploding.
Therefore, when porn -- like carnival -- stays confined, delimited, it becomes a negative phenomenon.
I believe that we should have the freedom to dress like superheroes -- I mean, social freedom -- in any context, even when going to work, even if we hold a position of power.
And yet, porn tends to trespass, to invade the common space.
Prominent examples are nudes
on ordinary gossip magazines, or sex scenes in art movies.
Such trespassing of porn into common space is strongly opposed,
for instance in social network’s rules.
I’ve been told so often: “Ok, you work in porn. Just don’t talk about it, shut up!”.
I can’t even talk about porn per se.
Among the opposers of porn's trespassing into common space, there are feminists, catholics, muslims, but also intellectuals, atheists too, progressives and conservatives, all united against porn invasion of common space.
In my opinion, porn's trespassing into public domain is a sign of progress, for a very simple reason: it rubs the nose into people's irrationality.
An example: why shouldn’t a child watch an intercourse scene projected on a giant screen in Piccadilly Circus in London, or in Piazza del Popolo in Rome?
What's wrong with that?
The problem lays not only in the influence religions still have on people but mostly in current schizophrenias between practice and reason --
for example, when a couple of atheist parents decides to baptize their child for the sake of tradition, or just not to disappoint granny.
Or still, in the USA, a parent that decides to circumcise his own son -- so we are talking about a genital mutilation here -- just because it’s always been like that.
Someone will bring out usual objections like, “Everybody is free to do as they want or as they believe."
But we are not.
Because it’s actually legal to instill religious irrationalities
into children's minds, it’s legal to refuse to transfuse a child, maybe in the name of Geova, and cause him the loss of a limb --
it already happened.
But it’s illegal to show a 13-year-old a PG-14 rated film, just because it shows plenty of all-nude scenes.
So, where is freedom here?
The truth is we take freedom away from rational people, to give it to irrational ones.
Another example: abortion is legal in Italy, yet most of the women who want to have an abortion struggle to do it in a public health facility because most gynecologists are conscientious objectors, in such facilities.
The truth is, freedom is never neutral to rationality -- there is always a core of shared truths.
So we must ask ourselves: which freedom do we want?
Do we want the freedom invoked by religious leaders, popes, or do we want rational freedom?
According to that relativist freedom, they can have their medieval parades, with people self-fustigating in the streets, while I cannot freely walk naked.
There is no symmetry in this freedom, in this acephalous relativism;
there is only the desire to protect irrationality, not to face our own contradictions.
Today, two ways of thinking seem to face each other:
a strong, religious one; and a weak, secular one.
I believe instead, that a strong, rationalist approach should prevail, one that allows to show a movie with nude scenes to a 13-year-old, but forbids to instill him with religious irrationalities.
(Applause)
Do you know how they say that people complement each other?
I think it's true.
You know, my husband is a fantastic person.
But he has that particular habit of doing things at the last minute.
He's always on the last minute, whether he's packing his bags for the holidays, or he has to bring me somewhere, and he knows I have to be on time.
And that drives me crazy. Because, of course, I'm a planner, I'm the opposite. I have everything, long time before, organized in my online agenda.
And I hate, I hate to be late.
Sometimes I think that the problem is that I'm an engineer, and engineers are usually very organized people.
But of course: we have to deal with things that have to work for a long time.
We cannot do things on the last minute.
But how can engineers design something, and be sure that this will work for such a long time?
It's not just a matter of organizing your online agenda, right?
Let's talk for example, about the transport system.
You design a bridge today, and this bridge will be built, let's say, in the next three years.
And it has to work for 100 years.
Sounds incredible, but this is the normality for every engineer.
So how can engineers design things, and be sure that they will work for 50 or 100 years?
Do we even have an idea of the requirement of the traffic system in the future?
Because if I design a road today, I have to decide today that this road will be a set amount of meters wide.
And I decided, based on the actual requirements of the transport system.
But how are we sure that this will work for the requirements of the traffic system of the future?
Well, the story demonstrates that it has not always been like this.
Right? So how can engineers design things and be sure that they will work for such a long life?
Do we even have an idea of how the traffic system will be in the future?
Electric cars?
Maybe no one will even own a car anymore.
Maybe we will move in fast pedestrian trains, hidden in tunnels.
And these could be not so far in the future.
So how is it possible to design something today and be sure that this will work for the future?
Well, Norway has signed up for this particular challenge.
This is the E39.
It is a European road, running for 1,100 kilometres along the west coast of Norway.
And today, to drive from Kristiansand, in the south of Norway, until Trondheim, in mid Norway, where the E39 ends, it takes 21 hours.
The long drive is caused by seven ferry stretches along the road, that are even nowadays the only way to cross some of these fjords.
Let's take a moment, and think about this.
There are areas, along the E39, where you need three hours to reach the nearest hospital.
Three hours!
Well, if you have an emergency, you rely on helicopters - if the weather is good enough.
So the people living along the E39 must be the healthiest people in Norway, or the luckiest ones.
I'm joking, but one third
of the national population lives there; and more than 50 percent of the national export is produced in that area.
So this means that there is a huge potential;
but this potential requires people, and people require services.
They need to reach jobs, hospitals, schools.
They need an efficient and reliable transport system.
Without a transport system, we are like isolated islands, and no one wants to live in an isolated place.
That's why people are moving, abandoning rural areas and moving to the cities.
So that's why we need an efficient and reliable transport system along the E39 in Norway.
Now, what about the ferries?
You know, in Norway, when people think about ferries, they often think about the sveler, the eat-on-board.
What are the sveler?
Well, they are, they are - OK, they are a sort of pancakes, sorry I said it.
Well, I can share some inside information with you: in Norway, people think that the best sveler you can eat are the ones you eat on board at the fjord ferries.
So that's why people will never abandon the ferries.
Okay, I'm joking. But the ferries are a natural way for the truckers to have a break.
They are a national touristic attraction. They are part of the Norwegian culture.
Ferries will remain, of course:
maybe they will be electric ferries, for example.
But for sure, the traffic of the future cannot rely on them.
Not only being on time for the time you need to travel; but also for the huge impact that the boats have on the environment.
Let's talk about environment:
more bridges, more fixed links and less boats could be a way to reduce the impact on the marine fauna?
And these areas are areas of thousand of marine species that are so precious, from the environmental point of view.
They're also the area of the aquaculture in Norway, and this activity is considered the second oil for Norway.
So just try to imagine which kind of impact an increase in the noise in the fjord areas could have on the environment: huge.
So let's build some bridges!
Well, this is not the easiest thing, I would say.
Some of these fjords are really wide: they could be up to six kilometres.
And they can be also really long, and more than one kilometer deep.
So the traditional technology for the bridges is not feasible there.
So what can we do?
In 2013, the Norwegian government has inserted the crossings along the E39 in a document that is called "National Transport Plan".
And since then, a complex process has started to develop new technologies to be able to cross these fjords.
This could be one of the solution: floating bridges.
It's an existing technology: we have floating bridges all over the world. And they could fit also for our case.
An alternative could be another well known-of bridge: a suspension bridge.
But instead of relying on traditional towers, they rely on these tension-like platforms, these floating towers that are used in the offshore field.
I know what you're thinking: how can this structure be environmentally friendly?
For example, if you think about migratory path of birds,
because this could be a problem for some of these fjords.
So we do have an alternative: a submerged, floating tube bridge.
It is a submerged bridge, but floating.
This submergence will help us counterbalance the vertical loads of the structure, taking advantage of the Archimedes' lifting force that every submerged body has.
And this submergence is also helping us to reduce the main seaload on the structure - like waves, for example. And this is a huge difference in respect to the oversea bridges
that are fully exposed to the environmental actions, like winds and waves, and currents.
And this structure is a way to create a hidden bridge.
Well, if you choose the solution with floating pontoons, you can create them as floating island, green islands to minimize the impact on the landscape.
But we have to say, there is no solution that can fit for every case.
The important thing is to evaluate the best solution for each specific case.
But there is one thing that you can do for every application of this structure.
You can find a way to design the road in order to produce less CO2.
You can find a way to design structures that are more resistant to the environmental loads.
You can find new materials, lighter materials or more resistant materials. Materials that help you design safer structures, or more resilient structures.
You can use the loads of the nature around you to create green energy.
You can do so many things! But how is all this possible?
This is the only way to design for the future: it is called research.
More than 50 PhDs have been affiliated to the E39 project in Norway, over the last years.
Design for the future is possible, if you invest in people and knowledge.
These knowledge will help you today to design the best design you can for your future.
It also create a good practice, because also the consultancy will invest in research.
It creates jobs for the people hired in the companies;
it creates a huge international society that is investing in young professionals coming from all over the world.
It creates growth on so many levels.
After the publication we have done for the submerged floating tube bridge, many countries have started to think about this solution again.
Norway is inspiring.
Norway is sharing its experience.
Oh, I forgot. This fantastic lady, she is a colleague and she is the project manager of the fjord crossing project: the E39 project in Norway.
Let's see if Norway can inspire other nations also on some other topics.
Just an example: gender equality?
At this point you will wonder, who am I?
I am the biggest fan of the submerged floating tube bridge.
I'm a chief engineer, and I work for the Norwegian public administration, and I'm responsible for the studies on this fantastic structure.
I moved to Norway six years ago, and I've seen the group of people working on these projects growing.
I met people coming from all over the world,
and I had the chance to study some of the most exciting topics for an engineer.
I also had the opportunity to feel as a part of a huge international community. That from Norway to South Korea is working towards the same goal.
We have seen some of our projects becoming reality, and we had to face, and we will have to face opponents, for our projects.
We discuss, we get angry, and we fight.
We fight for what we believe in.
It is a huge journey,
and I feel so lucky I had a chance to be part of it.
I really hope that, one day, we will see a completed coastal highway road E39 in Norway.
Even if politicians have been farsighted, their priorities can always change in the future.
Everything can change in life, right?
But one thing is sure, one thing will remain: the knowledge that Norway has generated.
Because these people, wherever will they be, they will definitely be the people who could plan, could design our future.
So do you want to design for the future?
Let's start organizing our agenda,
because you have to invest in people. You have to invest in knowledge.
And you have to start doing it today.
(Applause)
Let's try to imagine to see, just this high speed train pass; we heard the sound just for few seconds. When did it leave?
Where was it headed?
Who are the people, who are the travelers, what is the story that led them to embark on this journey?
This is the train of human knowledge, or rather, the train of the scientific research.
When did it leave? We do not know.
Probably, at the very moment when man appeared on Earth.
When, intrigued, he began to try to understand how things are made, how they work.
Where was he headed? We do not know.
We can't define the spatial coordinates nor the temporal coordinates.
And who are the travelers?
They are all researchers of all kinds. Whenever there is a new researcher, the train stops for a moment, just long enough to bring him up and to let him offer his original contribution to this trip in the short time of their lives.
And once he gets on the train,
what will he find out there? He finds all sorts of the people who came there before him, researchers who have taken this journey, then he finds books, treatises, notes, and he can look at them, he can browse them, and at the same time, he meets people that are sharing this journey with him, with whom he can talk and exchange opinions.
I jumped on this train too, it was during my second year of university, when, by chance, I attended the presentation addressed to the future students of the University of Genova.
I walked in, I sat down, and I heard a professor who talked about this. I did not know it, but, at that moment, the train stopped for me and I got in.
This is a nerve cell, a neuron set on an electronic device and the activity of this neuron, somehow, is able to modulate the operation of the electronic device.
A research group at the Max Planck Institute in Germany, was able to create a neuroelectronic interface where biology meets electronics.
I had never heard of such a thing, and I was immediately impressed, and I said, "Here, I want to study this."
I have indeed changed the course of study to follow what that teacher I did not know, Massimo Grattarola. I later discovered he was a pioneer in the field of bioelectronics and neuroengineering.
I followed him up to the doctorate, when, unfortunately, a disease took away his precious human and scientific guidance which I still miss very much today.
From that day, maintaining the amazement that I had going into that room, my journey continued, this train began to travel, and an interesting adventure began for me.
I finished my studies, I did a doctorate, I made the Post-Doctorate in Chicago, Then, I came across this field of research I did not know: brain-machine interfaces, where electronics and robotics meet neuroscience and biology, considering possible clinical applications.
Brain-machine interfaces.
What an idea! To attack the brain of a machine, or a computer, it's a weird idea, yet interesting.
Already in 1927, you could see this: in the film "Metropolis", Lang makes us see a robot assuming human features after being connected to a woman's brain. Or, more recently, the famous Dr. Octopus is able to control his robotic tentacles with sensors placed directly in the brain. What does an electrical engineer have to do with the brain?
Let's start with a number: 10 to the power of 11.
A hundred billion is the number of cells that we have in our brain.
It's a little difficult to conceive, but let's try: just think that the world population is about 7 billion, then to reach 100 billion, you have to put together 14 times the world's population.
But even more difficult is to think that each nerve cell may have 1,000 to 10,000 connections with other cells coming to a number of 10 to the power of 14 connections, an amazing thing.
We can imagine every nerve cell as an electronic computer, that takes the information, processes it, and then transfers it to other computers.
In what way?
In this way.
What you hear is the flow of information happening in our brain, is the passage of messages between a neuron and another, this sound is obtained by recording the electrical activity of a neuron and transforming it into an audio signal.
Think that neurons use to communicate electrical impulses, and the analogy with electrical engineering is incredible, because they are not just electrical impulses we can record and measure.
But we can also interpret these pulses as if they were a digital signal. Do you remember the bit, 0/1?
Messages can be represented as a sequence of 0 and 1, just like our electronic computer.
But here's where the problems start.
When we want to grasp an object, messages leave from our cortex, then, passing through the spinal cord, they come to our muscles, which, in an absolutely coordinated way, are able to grasp the object.
There is a problem here: we do not know the meaning of this message. We don't know the meaning of the words, of the phrases, of the alphabet.
What would happen if we were somehow able to decode this neural code?
It's here where the brain-machine interfaces are born: From an idea as simple as difficult to achieve.
What would happen if the connection between the cortex and the muscle gets corrupted, due to an accident or illness?
Would it be possible to directly capture this message from the brain to control a wheelchair or a robotic arm?
Here, these are brain-machine interfaces. And here, if we would be able, in some way, to decode this code we could do this.
In 1974, the research of the Brain-Computer interfaces began.
And this gentleman used this type of computer.
As you can imagine that, since that time, many steps forward have been made, and both the technology and our knowledge in neuroscience has improved.
But what is a brain-machine interface?
The main point are the sensors, which can be put both in the brain, both on the surface, both externally.
Then there is a computer, which takes signals, processes them and turns them into motor commands to an external device: a robotic arm or something.
Think about completely paralyzed people, whose greatest desire is to be able to communicate with their loved ones.
There are systems based on brain-computer interfaces in which the person only has to fix a letter on a screen, and the system, by taking the cerebral signal right from a headset positioned on the head, can automatically figure out which letter he has selected, and people can communicate, up to 17 characters per minute.
Or this: you can take the neuronal signal, and, since we have the ability to modulate, thinking to the movements, I think I move my right hand, I think I move the left hand.
This group of Lausanne has succeeded in transforming this signal in a motor command for a wheelchair and make it move both right and left.
Or,many research centers were based on the signal recorded directly into the cortex.
And this is the state of the art, this is the work of Brown University in which they took three patients, including this completely paralyzed lady, 15 years now, 58 years old, who lost the use of the word, and by recording the activity directly from the motor cortex is able to control a robotic arm and somehow grab a bottle and take it to her mouth, thinking at the movement.
These are the brain-machine interfaces.
As you can imagine the road is still long.
As you see this movement is very slow and difficult, we are very far from clinical applications, but the road is drawn, the train left and there are more and more researchers around the world who are willing to give their time and sacrifice their lives to this type of research and to respond to the desire to understand how things are done and to help other people.
Even in my small way, I do a search like this: we tried to develop a system in which we try to take the behavior of the spinal cord and the function of the spinal cord and get it done, instead, right from the cortex, in regions that, among other things, have nothing to do with the movement.
It's something a bit strange, but thanks to young researchers, and to the research funded by the European Community, we are moving forward in this direction.
As you can imagine, this thing excites me very much, even in the midst of many difficulties, specially related to a complicated theme. I'm not going to get off this train.
Thank you.
(Applause)
There are 22 million cocaine users around the world,
9 million of them in the Americas and 5 million in Europe.
What makes cocaine consumption, which seems to be spreading relentlessly, so appealing to people all around the world?
The answer is quite simple. Because it produces a great pleasure.
A pleasure that closely resembles that of the first love, a pleasure we all remember and long to experience again, just the way we felt it for the first time.
But were it just for this reason, cocaine wouldn't be that big a deal.
The problem, as you can see on this slide, comes from the fact that cocaine, aside of producing pleasure, damages functionally - namely, decreases the efficiency of a very important part of the brain, called prefrontal cortex.
This cortex is called prefrontal because it's behind the forehead and accounts for about a third of the brain. It's responsible for a number of key functions in all mammals. Good functioning of this part of the brain is critical for any mammal's survival, men and women included.
The moment cocaine decreases this functioning, our consciousness, our ability to make decisions, our ability to control impulses, is progressively and inexorably reduced.
And this is the reason why, indeed, cocaine addiction is considered today, by world’s cutting-edge neuroscientists, a full-fledged brain disease, a mental disorder.
How did society deal insofar with cocaine consumption?
First of all, none of what has been done has passed any test of effectiveness.
Past strategies could be grouped into three major families: drugs, to cope with the desire and the intensity of pleasure; psychotherapy, which tries to shed a light on past conflicts, or any underlying problem that may have lead to cocaine use; and rehabilitation facilities, in order to fix behavior patterns that have been disrupted by cocaine.
But none of these three, either separately or together, ever really worked.
In the fourth canto of Divine Comedy's Inferno, the philosopher Democritus, "che 'l mondo a caso pone" believes that the world and the universe came out of the encounter between Chance and Need. Dante sent him to "Inferno", the Hell
and made a great judicial mistake, as he did not deserve it at all.
Since the beginning of time, the world and the whole universe develop and expand precisely because Chance and Need meet.
And the story I am about to tell you comes from a piece of newspaper.
It was a crumbled piece of "Il Resto del Carlino", of Friday, April 5, 2013, The father of a patient - a 32-year-old cocaine-addicted physician, who had already been treated for a couple of years, and still continued to practice as a physician, thus putting the health of his patients at risk - actually gave it to me.
What was written in it?
"The light ray that will defeat cocaine," the headline said. It described an experiment made in the United States by a guy, I had never heard of, called Antonello Bonci, an Italian who went to the US 15 years before to lead the National Institute on Drug Abuse (NIDA), which was, and still is today, the most important center worldwide for drug addiction research.
What did this article say?
In the interview, the neuroscientist explained that a number of laboratory rats that were made addicted to cocaine -- just imagine, cocaine-addicted rats would press their lever up to 2000 times per hour, for a small dose of cocaine. However, after undergoing a new surgical technique called optogenetics, lost interest in cocaine.
How could this happen?
You know -- or I tell you now -- that in the rat's brain there is a pleasure system, which is hundreds of times smaller than its human equivalent but works in more or less the same way. It is called the limbic system.
This part of the brain, which can be called "the brain's guts", generates all of our passions; love, hatred, sex drive, hunger, thirst and so on.
What does optogenetics do?
Optogenetics is a revolutionary scientific method discovered in Stanford by a psychiatrist named Karl Deisseroth, who inserted in a particular way small channels into the rats' brain cells and made these cells sensitive to light.
All that was needed, was to put an optic fiber into the rat's brain, the size of a hair, turn on the light and the limbic cells -- because this system is genetically determined, it can activate with extreme precision any given part of the brain, and we chose the limbic system -- it input -- once the sensitized part was illuminated, the rats stopped looking for cocaine.
Clearly, a ray of light from the American laboratories must have also hit my prefrontal cortex because from that moment on the sky was my limit. I undertook -- and so many people asked me how I did, it but I really don’t know -- the endeavor of translating the rat method to humans, trying to obtain on men, similar results to those obtained with rats. However, we couldn't use optogenetics, an invasive and unauthorized technique.
I figured out I could do it through magnetic stimulations and impulses.
You see that there is a coil with an internal electrical current, a coil that emits magnetic stimulations which can reach the limbic system, just like rat's brains were reached out by the optogenetic system.
We had all reason to think we would get results in humans similar to those obtained in rats by Bonci with his experiment.
And here is the rTMS room.
In almost no time, out of the blue, I had to find a way to get this medical device, and here it is.
It's made of a comfortable, dentist-like armchair
the patient can lie down comfortably on.
While he is lying down, a so-called neuronavigator identifies with great precision an area, the size of a 2-euro coin, under the left temple.
This area is treated with 12 minutes of magnetic waves that produce a similar effect to what was observed in rats.
We called the first patient treated with rTMS Beatrice.
A 38-year-old woman who had been using cocaine non-stop since the age of 14.
She sat in this chair, and after 12 minutes of treatment got up, opened her eyes, smiled at us and said, "Something extraordinary has happened.
I no longer crave for cocaine.
I no longer think about cocaine.
If I think about cocaine, I don't want to get it anymore.
That never happened to me."
Imagine how we felt when we heard something like this!
We had definitely broken through a barrier: we ported the results of rats directly to humans, as it has been recognized internationally. But we couldn't take it for granted.
We immediately started to work.
We wrote a first paper based on a small pilot sample of 16 patients treated with rTMS, compared with 16 control patients under conventional drugs.
And we noticed even there that those treated with rTMS showed a much better outcome than the other control patients.
And since then, more and more patients began to respond much like Beatrice did.
They were tens, in the beginning, and then became hundreds. Now we have almost exceeded 500 patients.
But I'd lie if I told you that everything went smoothly.
As someone said before me, those who exceed the limits pay a pledge.
We have also paid our pledge.
The first one being, about a fifth of the treated patients didn't manage to stop using cocaine.
With all our technological innovations, we had no way of helping them quit.
That's why we invited several psychoanalysts to help us study the situation
and look after the patients. They took care of these patients and many of them, thanks to the combined treatment of TMS, that's applied to all patients plus this stubborn 20%, who received a focused additional help with psychotherapy and psychoanalysis, finally responded and most of them also stopped using cocaine.
This news spread worldwide and we were contacted by many high-profile magazines such as Science and National Geographic.
They wanted to know us, and we worked with them for months. It was a very nice experience to be interviewed and have our patients interviewed.
In September of last year, these two newspapers published our work. The National Geographic decided to give us the cover, and distributed our article in 23 languages ​​around the world.
From that moment on, our fame - with great satisfaction but also great fear from our side, started to increase.
Why fear?
(Applause) Thank you.
Why fear?
Because although we were sure that our method works fine, science can't be based on observations alone.
Scientific data are also needed.
We had worked for years collecting data very precisely, delivering them to the US, where independent experts studied them, evaluated them, elaborated on them. And a few weeks ago, we received their results.
Our data proved that on a sample of about 200 patients, which is a very large sample, followed up for 24 months, two years, which is a very long period, the overwhelming majority of patients had completely stopped using cocaine.
We were then acutely aware of having found a fourth way of treatment.
A fourth way that has all the rights to pretend that it could be the right way to defeat cocaine once and for all.
It is a fourth way that doesn't replace the previous three, but can be used together to further increase the efficacy of rTMS.
From that moment on, our team of three, four people at most grew up to dozens of people: doctors, psychiatrists, neurologists, psychoanalysts, psychologists, laboratory technicians, statisticians, administrators, etc.,
And all these team members, including also our American collaborators, decided to combine their efforts to have this treatment method spread worldwide as much as possible, in order to replicate our results.
But we have explained, and we are explaining, to all those who ask us to be able to work like us, that humans aren't only made of brains.
Humans are made of brain and mind, body and soul. And we must consider people in all their complexity, if we want them to find the strength to completely give up cocaine.
And not only the patients, their families are also considered,
and the environment where cocaine found its way to spread.
This is poised to bring about the necessary cultural change that will allow many people to save their lives and many other people to definitely improve theirs.
Thank you.
(Applause) Alessandro Cecchi Paone:
Congrats, professor. Truly fascinating.
It's one of the perks of attending TED, to be among the first to know that such an important discovery, as you said, was made for both individual and collective health and wellbeing.
I guess the audience will agree with me that, should this knowledge spread, you'll soon need a bodyguard service too.
LG: Check.
ACP: Oh, it happened already?
LG: I already have them.
ACP: I'd volunteer, otherwise.
I do it all the time, as a bodyguard.
Thank you, see you soon.
LG: Anyway, some give others pain,
some give them joy.
I think I'm among those who gave others more joy than pain.
ACP: And you only tell me now?
I run away with you, immediately!
Go find another presenter!
Thank you, professor.
(Applause)
I am Belgian, Flemish, so I will speak to you in..
In Italian, right.
20 years ago, I entered in that green building you see here. It is the Museum of Jurassic Technology. the Museum of Jurassic Technology. And,as you all know, Jurassic was a prehistoric era when dinosaurs populated the Earth.
However, the museum doesn't actually deal with dinosaurs, nor with technology.
It shows, for instance: A sculpture of a pope in a needle's eye to be looked at through a microscope, a bat stuck into a block of lead, and therefore invisible, the horn of an English woman,
It is difficult to explain this and I will not even try.
But when you step out of this museum, from the darkness of the museum into the dazzling lights of Los Angeles., you know the world ought to be more complicated than what you simply see around you: roads, cars, traffic lights.
The museum has put you in a state of wonder, which is often a state of doubt as well.
I found out later, someone wrote a book about this museum, and called it a rightful heir of 16th century's cabinets of wonders.
In fact: it was all hard to explain all the wonders in the 16th century.
All the news, the news with the discovery of Americas had greatly expanded people’s imagination
It had created a big confusion that governed Europe, for over a century.
Everywhere cabinets of wonder, called wunderkammern, were created to collect this wealth of novelties. They showed anything, real and even fake things.
While many of these collections nurtured a baroque taste for the extraordinary and the boundless, others became instruments for research.
In Italy, and that’s why I really like talking about this in Italian, in Italy, between Leonardo and Galileo, Ulisse Aldrovandi in Bologna made a collection with a clear goal in mind.
He wanted to "classify and recompose knowledge", with a microcosmic reconstruction of the macrocosm.
At a certain moment he proudly writes, “Today, in my microcosm one can see 18.000 different things, examples of species created by God”.
For a long time, reconnecting everything to a creator was the only way to create coherence in this chaos of wonders.
But little by little, wonder had to give way to doubts about how the world was described in the Bible and in the classical texts.
Aldrovandi saw the need for a change, and for a reconnection of knowledge.
His first task consisted in dividing his 18.000 specimens in Naturalia and Artificialia, things made by Nature and by Man.
This might seem a no-brainer,
but back then it was a radically new approach to reality. An approach that led to modern thinking, where division between Nature and Man became the division between science on one side and art, politics, religion on the other. I am a scientist for the world, I am a climatologist
I thought to see today’s climate change in the context of all changes on Earth, a story that's 4,5 billion years long.
Look at the past 600 million years, when life migrated from ocean to land. The fossil records tell us about several mass extinctions caused by natural cataclysms:
meteoritic impacts, volcanic eruptions, and the climate changes that came from them.
These mass extinctions, these fossils layers divide the prehistory in geological eras. For instance, Jurassic went from 200 to 150 million years ago.
The first humans appeared 7 million years ago. Homo Sapiens is hardly visible here.
I therefore reset the clock and start 200.000 years ago, when Homo Sapiens came along.
There were the human revolutions, the cognitive, agricultural, industrial.
And here we are, in the last 200 years, the modern and enlightened ones, in which we find … music, Haydn!
And so many things, I can only read them.
We are 1 billion, just to start.
There is the noise of steam machines. Machines that allow to make even bigger machines.
Colonialism, the brutalization of Man and the first grabbings.
Social revolt. Bombs and Fertilizers. The First World War and the global boom of the population.
We are 2 billion.
The Thirties, New York competing with London and Berlin. Weapons of mass destruction, and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. Oil, raw material for plastics, fuel for cars. Sprawling cities.
We get to three billion.
Demand, supply and mass production. The glorious sixties and the explosion of creativity. The throw-away society. The first heart transplant. More plastics, plastics everywhere. Pesticides and GMO. Freons and the ozone hole. Space travel. The PC: Pacman and Super Mario. CNN and MTV. Chernobyl, East, West, North, South, with China in between, Africa forgotten. Internet, financial markets. Search for sustainability. Open mining. Biodiversity: burning forests. Global networks, global warming, global terrorism. 20 billion chickens. Automatic slaughterhouses. Head transplants. Virtual life. We are more than 7 billion and it's the inequality between this women and this women
that generates conflict after conflict,
emergency after emergency, disaster after disaster, war after [Explosion] Excuse me, maybe I.. (Applause) Maybe I exaggerated a bit. Anyway human action on this planet now has a power that can compare to that of the prehistoric cataclysms.
But now, we are causing climate change.
And even without using atomic bombs, we are driving species to extinction at the same rate as the natural mass extinctions.
For these reasons, it makes sense to call our times the Anthropocene:
the era of the anthropos, the mankind.
An era that could be remembered, in millions of years by a layer of fossils of creatures, mixed with cement, radioactivity and plastic.
You know, maybe I didn’t exaggerate that much; everything I showed can be seen on Internet, on YouTube.
Several philosophers, scholars focused on our particular era, have called YouTube a Wunderkammer of our era.
In fact, YouTube shows everything: and therefore shows the chaos we all live in:
a true pasticcio (mess) of the 16th Century.
Maybe we still live in the same mess and as somebody wrote, “we have never been modern”.
There are differences between the 16th century and today. Whereas then novelties came out of nothing, from unknown continents, today novelties are designed by a scientific-technological complex for mass consumption.
And whereas, back then, novelties were real wonders, now novelties make no wonder anymore, replaced as they are one after the other, and we use to believe that everything is possible.
Lack of wonder, by the way, means lack of doubt and of a critical attitude towards how we deal with our planet.
We remain stuck in the model of consumerism, disconnected from any kind of value, but money. A model that flattens human culture. And could destroy our civilization, if not another mass extinction.
Look, I cannot change the world alone, but I do believe that the world can be changed. I have therefore created a cabinet of wonders.
Do you remember the Museum of Jurassic Technology?
Well, I have founded the Museum of Anthropocene Technology, which is about the Anthropocene and somehow about technology.
It shows, for instance, a work that sheds light on a light bulb. A photograph printed with the very air pollution that the photograph is showing. A pair of glasses.
It is again difficult to make sense of all of this. But now I will try.
Take those glasses: they are a simple object, just like these.
Made of plastic and glass
Here, something is written, which I cannot read because, well, I need some glasses
(Laughter) I know it is written: "Made in China", and this makes explode this object in something much more complex.
Yes, it might be designed in Italy, but I can see Chinese workers manufacturing them, polluting the air and the waters of their city.
I see the wars in the Middle-East, for petroleum, which is needed to make plastic. Then I see the ships carrying the oil to China, and those that bring glasses to Italy.
And why is it made in China?
Because labour is cheaper.
So are we earning too much, or are they earning too little? All that makes this object
a thing, a marvel, and I cannot simply throw it away.
Quite the opposite, I want to put on a pedestal.
Long story short, the MAT wants to be an instrument to look, with the aim to ignite again wonder and doubt. To show that things are more complex than consumerism makes us believe.
The MAT wants to recompose our chaos, going beyond the divisions of Modernity: nature vs society, science vs art, facts vs values.
Doing so, we hope to find new ways to see and live the world,
ways that can help to solve our big collective problems.
A final thought. Obviously, one single museum risks having little impact,
especially if it is in Laveno Mombello, near the lake
But what if a network of museums would be there?
A capillary network like the one of the churches.
Saint Peter's in Rome was not enough to preach the church’s doctrine,
there needed to be cathedrals, churches and chapels everywhere.
And just like TED isn't enough to spread ideas,
and we need all those TEDx’s.
So, beyond the MAT, some sort of MATx is already being considered.
I was talking about this with my friend.
(Applause) Thank you.
I was talking about this with my friend Fabio, some months ago.
He said: you need to talk to Francis.
Francis who?
Pope Francis!
Pope Francis, really!?
You mean using churches as museums?
Indeed.
I believe it is beautiful, for everyone, to enter in a church:
from the dazzling light of the city into this dark and silent space, where the frenzy stops.
But besides being able to salute God and his Saints, if you feel for it, or to reflect about life after death, why not be able to stand in front of a work, a marvel, that makes you reflect about life today?
Why not dedicate an altar to our “common house”, as Pope Francis himself calls the inseparable bond between Nature and Man?
Thanks.
(Applause)
It's 2000, and legendary Silicon Valley programmer, namely Bill Joy, writes a fairly eloquent editorial for Wired: "Why The Future Doesn't Need Us".
Joy's argument is very simple: If we continue to improve our ability to build machines that can perform complex tasks, we are very close to inventing the last machine: a machine that will make us all superfluous and obsolete.
20 years later, it’s 2019, the press and the entire Silicon Valley, where I come from, are trying every day to convince us that we are very close to a world where Joy's prophecy is almost fulfilled.
The so-called fourth industrial revolution, the one empowered by AI, Artificial Intelligence, thinking machines.
I am Jacopo, I'm the founder of a start-up called Tooso, together with two friends - clearly much more serious than I am.
I live in San Francisco, and I work in Artificial Intelligence.
I accepted the invitation to this talk, mostly, to explain to my parents what I do across the ocean all day long.
(Laughter) AI, San Francisco, Silicon Valley: On paper, I am the right person to come and convince you of Californian "gospel"; but if you expect a typical Silicon Valley talk you’re going to be fairly disappointed today.
The truth is, I don't blame you for buying this rhetoric of thinking machines.
In recent years, Artificial Intelligence has achieved extraordinary results, succeeding in doing things that were previously considered typically human, such as recognizing kittens on YouTube - which is an activity all of you are very fond of, I know - and, on a more serious level, beating the Go world champion.
Go is a very complicated Chinese version of chess.
And the world champion of Go, as you can see in the picture, was not particularly happy with the outcome of the game.
Today, most of Artificial Intelligence revolves around the concept of what we call "neural networks," which are usually represented by these vague schemes of neurons and connections, hence the name "neural network," vaguely inspired by the human brain.
What happens is that you give an input to this network, which processes it - let’s say the image of a cat - and it tells you what is contained in these images: for instance, a cat.
The truth is that to understand why Silicon Valley is wrong you don't really need to know what a neural network is or all the formulas explaining how it works.
In particular, I would like you to forget everything you know about neural networks: think of neural networks as a magic box for today.
It's a box that initially is completely empty, and you start filling it with images of dogs and cats, one after the other.
Every time you put an image in this magic box, you say: "This is a dog" or "This is a cat."
The box is magical because after you do that 100 thousand times, or ten million times, the box will be able by itself to tell a dog from a cat.
But what they don't tell you on the glossy covers of Wired or Time Magazine is how much it costs to learn what a cat is on YouTube.
Learning what a cat is on YouTube took 16 thousand processors and the equivalent of 10 millions kittens, which are really a lot of kittens.
It took five million games at Go to beat a human being, which is the equivalent of 500 years spent playing.
For Google, the cost of electricity alone to program that computer is estimated to be 25 million US dollars.
So next time you complain about your electric bill, think about Google's.
It's a pretty intuitive idea, actually, if we think about what happens in our everyday lives: If I go to Munich tomorrow, and after two days I come back and perfectly speak German, you'll all be stunned by my capability to learn: You'll think I'm a genius.
If I lived in Germany for 40 years, got married to a German woman, worked for a German company, had children, [and] I come back and speak German - besides being very useful, nobody would think that I am a genius.
What happens when Silicon Valley tries to convince you that Bill Joy's world is near and upon us - is that the shift between the human-grade concept of intelligence and learning and the one we use for machines is profoundly different.
And in this shift lies the biggest trick.
Let's do an experiment - unfortunately, I have nothing to make you sing along.
I don't know how many of you ever saw an "Aardvark" in their life.
Those who have seen an aardvark, raise your hand.
None? Wow, someone!
I wasn't prepared for anybody
to actually say "yes".
It’s not really important what an Aardvark is - it is a sub-Saharan mammal.
The nice thing is, when I mentioned "Aardvark," you all looked at this portion of the image without me suggesting anything.
Why did you do that?
Because you recognized some ears, some eyes; in short, you recognized all the typical characteristics that make him stand out from the background.
Not only: after a single example you are able to identify it in new photos - even if it's portrayed from a different angle or under a different light. Moreover, you can distinguish an aardvark from similar animals, such as an anteater.
With just one example!
When a neural network, a magic box, sees an aardvark for the first time, it doesn't know where to look.
Each portion of the image can potentially contain an aardvark.
That's the deep reason why you need to show it ten million of them.
Because if you show ten million different images of aardvarks from different angles, with or without light, the AI can finally distinguish the mammal from the background.
If we move from images to language, which is my field of study, things get even worse.
This is a classic task of lexical analogy, very simple.
The dog barks and the cat, obviously, meows.
I can take my laptop that’s over there, run state-of-the-art Artificial Intelligence algorithms and in about three hours, reading the equivalent of 10,000 times War and Peace, the system can answer to this question.
But this is not the only way to get it done.
There is a physical system, very expensive - especially in America, where I live - and very complex, that can solve this problem in a much easier way.
The amazing thing is that some of you might have this physical system at home: Some of you might even have more than one.
We commonly call this system a "child"
For your information, it's a scaled version of a human being.
A child can solve this task in about three seconds, once you point to a cat and say "mew."
A child is so good at learning that between 12 and 18 months old she can learn 75 new words a month.
A child is so good at learning, that when you drop your car keys and curse, the child will learn it, whether you like it or not.
(Laughter)
Three hours, 10,000 War and Peace - and boy, I was told that War and Peace is really long; three seconds.
This is the real gap between the Artificial Intelligence we have today and real intelligence.
The distance between the world we live in and Billie Joy's world is the distance between three hours and three seconds.
The good news is that we can use children to build better machines, trying to steal some of the secrets of these incredible system.
This is a standard experiment, in which we tested a six month old child with a little game: there is a rail with a little train, as you see the train runs along the rail; the yellow screen is lifted up and you see a cube behind the rail.
So far, nothing weird: a little train that simply runs along.
As good scientists, at this point we trick the six month old child and we do exactly the same thing.
But when the screen is lifted up, after the train has passed, we put the cube in the middle of the rail.
Obviously, this is just to trick the child, because it’s physically impossible: In our world, solid bodies do not interpenetrate.
But she knows nothing about physics: she is a six month old child.
Even if you wanted to explain it to her, she could not understand it.
So it’s interesting to understand: what goes on inside the mind of a child?
When a child sees the possible world, the ordinary world, that child is happy, like all children are, I guess, looking at a train running along rails.
When a child sees an impossible world, which physically cannot exist, here is what happens.
(Laughter) The child stares for several seconds at the experimental setup, somehow wondering what is not working properly in reality.
But there's something even more interesting: When you take the train that works in an impossible way and you give it to the child, she will do this.
(Laughter) The child will test the sturdiness of the train.
In fact, if you change experiment and you take a toy car instead, and you trick the child - it's really easy to trick six month olds, apparently - you take a toy car and pretend that it goes up, instead of falling, as gravity predicts. When you give the car back to the child,
do you know what she will do?
She will drop it from her high chair, to test that it actually obeys to the laws of gravity.
These are two fundamental differences between how we build Artificial Intelligence and children.
The first is that to learn quickly we already need to know something: In the case of a child, she already has an intuitive notion of physics in her brain, and uses it to learn.
The second is, a child won't stand still, like a neural network, waiting for you to provide millions of images of cats.
A child is like a little scientist: she uses the world around her to run some experiments, understand how reality works.
The good news is, we can already use these principles to build systems that work in reality, and we have already begun to do so, in our own way.
Let's take a very simple case you are all familiar with: purchasing clothes online.
Go to an e-commerce platform, write a word, say "sheath dress," and press return.
What happens at the other end is that there is an AI that tries to understand the meaning of this word.
How do we program an Artificial Intelligence to solve this problem?
Well, we have to do it as children do.
The first thing is, provide the Artificial Intelligence with preliminary knowledge that explain our reference domain.
It's not physics anymore, it is a more mundane domain: clothes. But it is useful for our Artificial Intelligence to know, for example, that clothes are organized into dresses, skirts, jeans; and that clothes are made of a certain color, a certain fabric, and have a certain style.
The second ingredient is the experiment: How do we get a search engine to run experiments, an AI that can only observe your browser?
Well, using all of you as guinea pigs. Every time you end up on a website which has one of these new Artificial Intelligences which learn as children do, every time you click on a product when the Artificial Intelligence offers you the results of the search engine, you are the metaphorical equivalent of the table the child was beating the train on.
Artificial Intelligence is using you, your feedback, and your Intelligence, to learn by using the environment very efficiently.
By using an Artificial Intelligence that works with these principles - and not like most AI work nowadays - you can learn that "sheath dress" and "dress" are actually synonyms, with only ten examples.
Ten examples are not three seconds: however, in the continuum between three hours and three seconds, ten examples are the first step down the right path.
Why should this be interesting for everyone, and not just for people like me who are clearly looking for an excuse to stay on the other side of the world, far away from their parents?
For two reasons.
The first is practical. There are so many areas in your life, for example areas covered by privacy, or areas covered by very rare events, in which even if you wanted you could never accumulate ten million examples.
We would like to use machines, and all the things that machines can do better than us, in these areas.
So learning with ten examples is a great way to use them more widely.
The second reason, perhaps more important, is connected to society.
A world where the benefits of Artificial Intelligence are only reaped by companies that can collect millions, billions, hundreds of billions of data is a world in which few companies determines what progress is.
It is a world in which our vision of the future is largely determined by those who can afford it.
That's why programming machines that work a little more like children somehow democratizes access to all the new technologies and to all the good things we can do together with the machines.
Artificial Intelligence has made huge steps ahead in the last ten years, we can’t deny it.
But the only way to produce "real" Artificial Intelligence is by taking inspiration from the real intelligence that we have at hand: the one of human beings, and in particular the one of children.
So at the end of the day, with all due respect for Bill Joy, the future has actually never needed us more.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Well, I want to start with a question: how many of you think they use their cell phone too much?
Well, there are a lot of us, aren't we?
We're not alone, we're in good company: if we look at the latest data available they tell us that every Italian stays on average six hours online every day.
And this is true for big, small babies, so probably the number of hours we spend online is much higher.
But the question I ask you is: does all this use of our mobile phone really make us feel better?
Spending our days watching social networks, trying to stay connected?
I would say no.
No, we're not particularly happy,
even though technology has become part of our daily lives, it doesn't actually make us feel better.
That's why what I'm trying to do in my research centre at the Catholic University, is to try to figure out how to use technology to make us feel better.
The challenge is to use technology to make us better, to awake us, to make us change.
But the starting point is: what does it mean to change?
Change is a theme that we have heard so many times today: but what does it really mean?
And knowing what change means is in fact not difficult:
it means being different, it means transform ourselves.
The question is how to do it: how do you change?
That is trickier.
I am a psychologist and in fact psychology has tried to answer this question in many different ways.
In reality, however, when you analyze how people change, I noticed a thing: actually, we can change in two different ways.
Two ways that could be defined as as "top-down" and "bottom-up" I’ll try to explain:
in a bottom-up change, the starting point is our intuitions, feelings, emotions.
We try to modify what we sense, what we feel, and then, through this change, we also change at a higher level: we change our behaviours, our visions of the world.
Or we can do the opposite: we try to work on concepts, on how we think, interpret reality, and through this change in interpretation, changes the way we experience emotions and consequently our behaviour.
But why are there two ways of changing so radically different?
Actually, Nobel Prize winner, Prof. Kahneman, gave us the answer. He won the Nobel Prize because he realized that within our minds are two different ways of thinking.
We have two ways that he called “intuition” and “reasoning”.
What's the difference between them?
Intuition is rapid, immediate, generates impressions.
Reasoning is slow instead, it cannot work in parallel: one must commit to it, we have to spend resources to be able to reason.
And what is the problem for change?
The problem for change is that a change is not effective but when it manages to touch the intuitive dimension and the rational dimension at the same time.
If it fails to do so, then problems arise. For example, we can know very well how to change: rational dimension.
But when we try, something blocks us, something that is inside us.
Or the opposite may be the case: we can feel within us that there is something wrong, but then we don’t know what to do.
What is the most effective form of change?
The most effective form of change is what is called experiential change.
The basic idea is actually very simple: we are all familiar with this form of change by the name of "trial and error".
We know the mechanism:
we start, have to do something, we use intuition; we try, we nosedive into something, And then, what if we fail to reach the goal?
Well, we stop, we reflect, we use reasoning to understand what went wrong.
At this point we try again, we do a different strategy and so on...
And this mechanism actually works.
So why don't we use it every day?
Because there are some problems.
The first and main one is that in fact, in our daily life, errors come at a price.
We cannot afford to make mistakes.
We are at work, we want to try and try again until our boss is happy, but what is the risk that she fires us in the process?
So the problem is that very often we have to try and try again, through experience; but the context does not help us.
Then there's another problem: every time we make a mistake our self-esteem goes down.
So what's the problem?
That we can't continue to make mistakes. At a certain point we say "why waste time, trying again?
It's so pointless -" and then we stop.
What's interesting is, technology can help us today.
Also because technology has changed:
when we think of technology, we usually think of an object, of something physical: our smartphone, our laptop, our computer.
But the lesson of Steve Jobs is that in fact technology is successful when it turns into an experience.
When I was a student and I had to write my thesis, I used a computer with an MS-DOS operating system.
For many of you, probably, this word does not mean anything.
It was a very, let's say, rigid, command-based operating system: so if I had to copy my file from a floppy disk to another one, I had to use the language: copy A: B:.
And if I forgot the two points, I didn't copy anything, and the thesis was lost.
It happened to me.
(Laughter) Today it's an experience: I take the mouse or I take the finger because then Steve Jobs had the intuition to understand that the technology could be used even before we know the language.
If someone has a one-year, two-year-old child, he knows very well, even before talking they can use the technology effectively.
So technology is turning into an experience.
And in particular, the technology that is closest to the experience is virtual reality.
Virtual reality is a simulative technology,
I think many of you have seen it sometimes: I wear a helmet and I suddenly feel in a different world than the actual one.
What happens?
Inside the helmet I have sensors, and all these sensors are able to detect my movements, pass this information to the computer inside the helmet which recalculates my point of view, giving me the impression that the world around me has changed.
What is the real strength of virtual reality?
That within virtual reality I feel present:
it's not like watching a movie, where I'm outside; here I'm inside.
In fact, for the last ten years or so, virtual reality has been used effectively to treat a whole series of problems.
At the beginning of this year I published this article that tried to collect the main effective applications of virtual reality in a series of complex areas.
And what emerges from the systematic analysis of the literature is that in fact the virtual reality is effective to treat anxiety disorders, to reduce and combat phobias, our fears; but also to address complex problems as we will see, such as mental disorders, anorexia...
Or work to reduce acute or chronic pain.
But why is virtual reality so effective?
Well, we have understood part of the answer: because it is an experience.
Knowledge is one thing: I know what I have to do.
Another thing is to do it.
And in virtual reality I can try and try again until I can really do what I have to do.
It is no coincidence that today virtual reality is routinely used to treat problems, within the training process.
For example: do you want to learn how to fly a plane?
You will have to use a flight simulator, which is a virtual reality system.
Do you want to become a surgeon?
Here again, virtual reality is the key.
But the second strength of virtual reality is another:
virtual reality uses the same mechanisms that are inside our brain.
Let me be clearer. Cognitive sciences have told us that our mind is basically a simulative system.
When I was young, and I thought about perception, I could only read in the textbooks that there was something that touched me, so my brain felt it, and here we had the perception.
Cognitive sciences actually told us this is absolutely not the case:
our minds, even before we perceive something, tries to predict in advance what will happen to us.
And this mechanism works to reduce the cognitive effort of our brain and applies far beyond perceptions. What are our habits, basically?
They are simulations:
simulations that allow us to stay within the comfort zone.
In psychology it is said that change begins from the ability to get out of the comfort zone.
And what's this comfort zone for neuroscience?
It is our brain's ability to simulate:
in the comfort zone, we know what to expect; and then we can get out.
What's the problem?
That every now and then this simulation system is wrong.
So how do we correct it?
Let me give you a series of examples: since virtual technology is a simulative technology,
we can use virtual reality simulations to try to correct the simulations of our brain.
An example we have worked on for a long time, at the Italian Auxological Institute, is the case of anorexia.
Anorexia is an obvious example of a brain's failed simulations:
the anorexic thinks she is in a fat body, but actually her body is thin;
it is a simulation of her brain that does not allow her to see what she really is.
And what did we do to try to fight this problem?
We forced the anorexic, using virtual reality, to enter a body that was different from hers.
The brain, at that point, was no longer able to make predictions, it no longer knew how to handle that different a body, and somehow forced itself to review and reorganize its image of itself.
Keep in mind that this mechanism does not only work for the pathology:
I'll tell you this other example that is very interesting and that, in this case, is not made by my laboratory but by a group of colleagues.
These colleagues said: a lot of people are not cut out, or feel not cut out for mathematics.
I can't do mathematics,
and very often this feeling of being denied for mathematics becomes a simulation that blocks us before a mathematical task.
But what if I let you enter the body of the world's most iconic scientist, Albert Einstein?
You see what happens in the slide.
In fact, while entering Albert Einstein's body, the person who felt denied in mathematics improved his performance.
Why does this happen?
Because in fact, through virtual reality, we could breakthorugh the limit the mind put on itself; it came out of the comfort zone.
It came out of it's depressing simulation. Very often, limits are self-imposed.
Keep in mind, this technique is highly effective and with our friends at Become Hub we had thought of a virtual experience for you to wake you.
Sadly, due to a series of last minute technical problems, I can't let you live the experience in virtual reality, thought I would have loved it.
But if any of you have the availability of a helmet, there's a link in the slide to download the experience.
What we had thought was just a real journey, an experience that tried to combine the stimulation on the intuitive level to a consideration on the rational one.
I can only show you, unfortunately; but, in my opinion, it will wake you up!
Thank you.
(Applause) (Music) Observe what appears before you: a wooden pier, the sea, a boat.
A subtle ray of light that illuminates the water, like a face, a road to travel.
While you find within you the resources to get on the boat, untie the sail and start sailing in the middle of the sea.
You find yourself in the middle of the sea, and you discover, your fears lie below the deck, under your feet. They're disturbing, they're terrible. You understand, as long as you remain offshore, without a goal, without a target, you won't have to face them, look at them, hear them. And when you turn the rudder,
the sea becomes agitated, the sky becomes dark, stormy and fears emerge from the depths, to threaten, frighten you.
But while you continue to sail in the stormy sea, with the rudder in your hands, you realize that the boat can not sink and that fears have never really hurt you.
They can threaten you, but once the rudder is in your hands, you have control of the boat which you keep headed to the shore.
Fears try to frighten you, waves shake you, but that's all they can do. Let fears get close to you, while the wind blows against the sails, and slowly come out of the storm.
Keep your hands on the rudder, heading towards the shore, while you notice the soothing sky, the calm sea, the sun, the wind, the fish, the birds, the other boats... a world to explore and appreciate.
So, I'd like to tell you a story.
We are in the summer, in a locality, in a village, in a region of China.
In this region of China, a man and a child with wooden cages in their hands move prudently through the countryside.
We see them climbing up and down the trees with some small boxes, and grabbing something from the branches of the trees, inserting it into the boxes and closing them.
They go up on another tree and do the same procedure again.
A group of Italian tourists, not by chance a group of Italian tourists, are curious.
The tourist guide says that he is a teacher and a pupil of his who are bringing cicadas to school to introduce children to cicadas.
Among the tourists there is a teacher; and of course the teacher, when I heard the speech of the school, already knew what they would do.
What could a teacher do with a cicada at school?
He can at most study it from the point of view of science; can also dissect it, can also work explaining, and I would say not only explaining but understanding, the form of the cicada.
"No" - says the Chinese tourist guide - "they take it to school to make the children hear the song of the cicadas"
As you can see, this is another point of view.
Teachers usually bring objects to school, dissect them at an abstract level or at a practical level,
but, especially in the Western world, they hardly bring an element and make the cicadas sing.
That's a difference.
But what does it mean that it is a difference?
It's a difference because we are stories of difference.
But be careful, because not only we, you or I are a difference;
the difference lies in the relationship that I establish.
It is not in the cicada, but in the relationship that I establish with the cicada.
That's the difference.
And so let's try to understand what it means in our lives to be a difference but not as "oneself"
but as a "relationship with"
As a "relationship with", what is important, said a great anthropologist, psychoanalyst and psychiatrist, it is that if we in our existence break this structure that connects the relationship, the structure of our relationships with the things and with others, we run the risk of losing the value and quality of otherness, whether they are things or relationships.
If we lose value, we lose meaning, we do not lose the thing.
The other can be an enemy: and this therefore means that I have a relationship with an enemy.
But the other isn't an enemy per se, so it is in the relationship that I feel that he is an enemy:
this is the difference.
And it's important to understand it: then we're a difference.
What can help me convey me this?
We are a difference in feeling, rather than in understanding.
"In feeling more than in understanding", what does it mean?
It means that understanding the other almost takes on a position of function: I explain it, I analyze it, I evaluate it, I dissect it.
This is understanding.
Even if there's this under-standing If I understand it, I categorize it.
Feeling - no, let me wrap up with understanding.
Be careful, because understanding - I mean, reason, rationality's job is precisely to create a structure.
And this structure becomes structuring itself and therefore it is a continuous running behind to structure things, until they become minimalist, until they become almost necessary, functional to a minimum design of our intelligence, of our knowledge.
Feeling instead is something else.
Because it is born inside, it is born in the intersections that we have within us and in these intersections we function by relationship. The relationship that concerns us is the one that allows us to be in the world because we are in relationship, we are difference, we are relationship.
And we are in the world only for the relationship.
Now I would like to do with you, if you agree, a little exercise.
OK?
Then try to feel your breath.
Listen to your breath.
Let your look go inside, look inside.
Feel it.
Feel the flow of breath within you.
Feel it along your body, hands and feet.
Feel that going beyond you.
Feel it well inside.
Feel the wave, the shape, the noise, the sound, the light and dark, the shapes.
Try to go beyond, beyond, beyond, beyond the breath.
What we hear are shapes, flows, circularities.
We do not feel finite, determined, linear.
The internal breath does not lead us to the structure, but leads us to the circularity of meaning.
The circularity of meaning is not yet comprehensible, it is still a feeling.
But it leads us to see the elicitation of other things.
This circularity also leads us to something that is even deeper within us, that we hardly ever treat, that is resonance.
Resonance is what sounds inside us when we do something or when we think, when we dream, when we imagine.
It's something within that is not expressed in something particular.
Remember that our first resonance is the sound, because in the belly of the mother the first thing we pick up is the sound.
The sound of the mother's steps and of the mother's rhythm, the sound of the water inside, of the amniotic fluid: this is what we hear and this resonates.
And this resonance when the baby is born remains inside, doesn't remain outside, because outside it uses other channels.
So when we are in relation to one thing, to the other, we must feel the resonance, because in the resonance we understand why we have that relationship.
What kind of relationship do we have with otherness?
Any otherness.
That relationship leads us to understand if we are able to make intersections, if we are able to make contaminations, if we are able to build something with otherness or if instead we are firm in our rational perception, in our structure, in our linearity of Ego, of identity.
I am me. No, I am relationship!
And to do this operation you have to do a whole job which is to understand the meaning of these flows, these forms that I then recognize in real concrete acts:
When I meet a man, I am a woman and I feel a difference; the same goes when I meet a migrant, because I am a native;
when I meet a young person because I am an old person, not a young one.
When I meet the "I" and the "You".
Here I feel a meaning in this relationship, which tells me what behavior I can have, what behavior I have had, and what behavior I can have in the future.
I have to share this sense of me with the difference, because Bateson said, "In order to enter a relationship we must lose some of ourselves".
You don't relate to anything, not even walking, not even video, the Internet if you don't lose a bit of yourself.
And having said that, it is clear we can only understand this if we open our minds to complexity.
Complexity is not complicated, it is not finiteness, it is not even the plural, it is not even the multilateral.
Complexity is a continuous movement, those forms that we had within us, that actually tells us how we can move, and what our chances are.
You will shortly see what our chances are.
Keep in mind, the access to complexity has not been given to us.
Yet complexity has always existed, but we haven't been given access to.
It was not given to us due to a form of culture that prevented us from having this freedom of relationship.
Because those forms of cultures led us to accept the domination of someone over the many.
They told us that the mind is different from the body and that it is hierarchically superior to the body, and they told us that we can have enemies.
Today they tell us, migrants are our enemies, women are the enemies of men and the other way around.
They tell us all these things, that old people exploit young people.
They tell us this today: it is always that deterministic culture, which structures us in thought, not that which opens up to everyone and also allows us a choice and also allows us to build choices.
Because I don't make a choice if I don't build it, because I make the choice in the relationship, I don't choose randomly: "I am here and I choose him or I choose her", no.
A relationship must be established, to make the choice.
Even our language: "Decide, choose, organize, do" Sure, but in a relationship.
When I know, when I sit down, when I open myself.
Like that tourist, that teacher who understood that you can't just dissect the cicada at school.
Then if we analyze this thing from a sustainability perspective, bring a cicada to school in the cage that cries desperately because it is in the cage it is not a question of good heart, it is simply a question of points of view.
I like that teacher, and I'd like to become like that teacher, and I hope I will.
Maybe at my age it's a bit hard,
but I think I am. And some of you also, in my opinion, can either know that you are or you can place yourself on this relationship.
This essence of the relationship. Until it becomes - what?
This culture has used us, it accustomed us to structure ourselves, to categorise us in a certain way, all the way up to hatred, conflict.
Hatred is worse than conflict.
I hate you.
I might know nothing about you, but I hate you anyway.
I've only heard of you being here.
You arrived by sea,
you are a woman - I hate you.
The children, the elderly, all the professionals.
This is the culture of hatred, it is the culture of structuring, of limits, of limitness and of radicalism.
But keep in mind, this leads you to do one thing: we have to re-educate ourselves through the relationship.
We have to re-educate to relationship, to differences and we have to find another grammar of life, another experiential grammar, another grammar of knowledge, another conceptual grammar.
Why is this important?
I'll tell you why it's important.
Teachers know - but parents also know this, all people in my opinion know this, I don't even have to say it: re-educating means opening minds.
But do not open minds as in 19th century, through the dream, the imagination restricted to those limited fields, to that local area where we grew up.

Whether it was agriculture or industry, they are always local fields.
In complexity, re-education and the imagination behind it that have always existed -
Here, too, we have been prevented, or limited, forced, oriented to do so.
This imaginary today has more possibilities, because in complexity there are more possibilities: and also more uncertainties, more confusion.
But if I am in the relationship, I understand and know it, I see the resilience and opportunities that are inside and outside, inside and outside of me in the other, in things, organizations and institutions.
So I have to think that this imagination must not be a dream, an expectation, a probability that today few have, but a possibility of different lives.
A possibility of different lives.
Young people know, if they don't find something here, they move; if they don't do it with that job they can orient themselves in another job.
We must aim at the possibility of different lives and this possibility is given to us by imagination, because today imagination is concrete, it is a social practice.
It is a practice that involves us in groups.
Through a difference I can think of something else.
I can't think of an open imagination inside my only limited place. I can only think of it if I go out, if I meet, if I establish relationships.
And if these relationships establish the difference and not the identification with the other.
Let's leave the identifications.
Let's get out of identifications.
We owe it.
To whom do we owe it?
We owe it to our human nature.
Nature-culture.
It is not either nature or culture; it is nature-culture of humans and also of non-humans.
Be careful, because we must also think about non-humans, including things.
We owe it to our children, to the youth. and we owe it to ourself.
We owe it to our own and others' diversity, and to all diversities, because young people, we, diversities, will build the future.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Listening has been a staple element of my professional growth.
I worked for 40 years at Monza hospital, where I followed more than 3,000 children with leukaemia and lymphoma.
85% of that 3,000 fully recovered,
and this is an exciting figure, today; not only here in Monza, but in the national and international circuit.
So the goal of real recovery exists in the pediatric field.
But I also brought 600 of them to death. And this is perhaps the saddest part, but I was also greatly enriched by those 600 children who didn't make it.
So I asked myself - someone once asked me: what are the limits of medicine today?
The limits of medicine are [the inability] to heal everyone: if I could heal everyone, maybe I would even change my job.
But the non-limit, for a doctor - and this is what I always set my mind to - is being able to make everyone feel good, even those who are poised not to make it.
So I deliver true healing wherever possible; but the quality of life must be for everyone.
This principle has led me for 40 years.
And I have learned a lot, from children and families, as I actually do believe in the principle of communicating vessels: I try to give a lot, but to be able to continue on this path I also need to receive a lot.
And the search for this gifts, that children and families gave me, has allowed me to keep it up.
So I wanted to share with you two fundamental concepts.
What does it mean to stay aside of a child and a family on the path of care?
What does it mean "knowing how to listen?"
I had a teacher for this, Elizabeth Kubler-Ross, with whom I shared a period of my professional life and actually gave me concepts I then applied in everyday life.
Respect, both for children - even if they are very young - and for the elderly.
These are two tipycally neglected categories: children don't get it, and the elderly are at the end their life.
No, they need respect and caring skills also.
The fourth is the concept of a therapeutic alliance, which you may have heard.
But the therapeutic alliance is not an easy concept.
It means ponying up one's energies, energies we often have but don't even know we have, and come out in times of need and trouble.
What does that mean, what did Kubler-Ross teach me?
I took this excerpt because I think it's very meaningful.
She says, people are like stained glass.
They sparkle and shine when the sun is up; but when it goes down, they only reveal their beauty if there's light inside.
What does that mean?
We have to believe in what we're doing.
We have to be there, the patient wants us to be there.
So, these two dimensions, believing and being there, bring us close to our patient.
And in my case, as a pediatrician, to parents, who need that presence.
I have taken the concept of accompanying from some families, who, without knowing each other, echoed the same concept.
This is a family who lost their son, they write to me - this letter was a huge reward for me - it says: "The meeting with Dr. Jankovic, who accompanied us for a short but intense period of our life, was for us the discovery even pain may not be considered a private matter. But a common starting point on which to build, through memory, a new reality.
The great ability of sharing" - therefore "accompanying" means sharing, it means putting something together even if it is difficult, even if it is not as simple or as linear as we would like it to be- "that we experienced, has helped us to understand how useful it is not to get lost in our own pain but turn it into a source of energy, aimed at sustaining those in the same need,
and it is through this understanding that we express our gratitude and joy to have met you".
They lost their son, and acknowledge you.
It is obvious that as a doctor, from a professional point of view, I need data, statistics, percentages, significance; but as a human being, even single examples like this are enough.
And listening also requires, it was mentioned earlier, a non-verbal capacity
that children - the sick in general but children in particular - give you.
This little girl, Clementina, gave me this poem a few weeks before death.
And I highlighted in yellow the things that struck me, because she says:
"I would like a cat. A nice holiday.
I would like to grow my hair. Sometimes I'd like to disappear".
Then she says, "I wish I always felt good".
So the children care about their health, too.
It's not true she doesn't understand: she cares, she wants to join in her struggle for recovery.
"I wish there was magic, and being magic": she had understood very well the traditional treatments we were doing, unfortunately, were failing with her.
"Then I wish everyone in the world were happy, for everyone's wishes to come true,
go deep sea fishing,
to travel the world.
I would like to see the stars, enter the Sun" - she was already projected into the afterlife.
So she simply asked to stay close to her, because she knew what was in store for her.
And Veronica, 13 years old, and I took this with me as a will and as a commitment, even when I go to talk to volunteers, to people - that is, we must try deliver what we are asked.
She says: It's important - "What matters in life is not knowing how to appreciate it.
What counts is knowing how to live the moment when it ends."
And this is for all of us.
Us as health workers, and those who want to be close to the patient.
So we get back to that concept of normality of life, which is not easy but the sick person wants it.
Compassion is no use, merci is no use: it annoys, almost. But being present at any time, that is important.
And normality calls for an effort by our side.
I consider sick people, even sick children, like warriors, strong warriors who are forced by conditions to elicit their energies.
But who is the real hero, in the care of a child or a sick person?
Caregivers are the real hero.
But not only us as doctors, or as medical staff; but also the people close to them, primarily their parents.
A short time ago, I was struck by a seven-year-old boy with leukaemia.
The treatment lasts for two years, so at nine his treatment were over, and is considered out of therapy.
The concept of healing takes a little more time, a couple years of observation, but he was out of therapy.
Ten years later, he writes a letter of gratitude to his mother: "Thank you, Mother, for what you gave me during the treatment".
Why?
Because mom was working.
She'd go to the hospital with her laptop, yell at him, nudge him - that is, she gave him that normality he thought he had no access to, as he was ill, so his case was more serious.
And this thank you after ten years - a meditated, thoughtful one - has a great value.
A four-year-old boy pulls on my scrub and says: "Dr. Jankovic, why has my mother gotten so ugly"?
And that makes you think.
And in fact my suggestion, even with the parents, is: go home, go to the hairdresser, show them that life flows regularly.
And this, I dare say, is a heroic act.
I mean, doing something beyond tradition.
And a 16 years old boy I brought to death, only one 19 year old told me explicitly: But Dr. Jankovic, am I dying?
That's a tough question.
I don't say an unforeseen one, but one you don't expect, or you're not sure what to answer to: but I was there.
I was there with him, I was talking, we were talking
and I said: you're in a hard time, dealing with death; but you're here without pain, we're talking about your next holidays - we were both well aware he wouldn't make it, but we're talking about this so it doesn't seem that impending.
And he, like all children, who are big provocateurs, said: "Actually, you're right".
He gave me a poem, before leaving us, by Marta Mederos, entitled "Die slowly he who becomes the slave of habit" which made me think a lot.
In fact, it's true: we are slaves of habit, we are afraid not to play by the rules.
We are afraid to do something that goes against the law, rather than ethics or anything else.
And so Tonucci, a psychologist who visited our Centre and also a cartoonist, made these cartoons to say how important the attitude is towards the sick, and therefore the child.
"Now let's not let Paolo notice."
And the child, wonderful wicked being - if the child wants to put you in trouble, he will, he puts you in the condition of being in trouble - he doesn't say: "How did it go?",
but: "It didn't go well, did it?"
There's already a negative frame, and that puts the parents in crisis.
But what Tonucci was able to grasp well is the fact that this gives the sick person the task, also, of supporting us healthy, us adults: "I must try to cheer them up, otherwise they suffer too much."
The children of a certain age, with whom I shared a healing path, told me: "better not to say everything, my parents could suffer".
So for me it's important, when we talk about listening - listening is the basis of communication, communication is really the fruit of correlation and sharing that this path requires.
So yes I was - I'm not saying heroic, but I changed the script, I talk directly with the children about their illness.
Children from the age of four, five years and up.
And what did I do original?
Always with the support of psychologists, so I didn't do things my own way, but I spent a period in the US for this very reason,
I talked to children alone, without the "catalytic" presence of their parents: they are obviously informed, and I have their consent in advance.
So you speak directly, and they are happy with that because they are treated like human beings, to whom I am not talking about percentages or stuff like that.
I don't say directly, "Hair will fall out": these are details.
The interview has to be dynamic, I have to guess what they want to know; but it's got to be a scientifically valid interview.
I use the word leukaemia, I use the word tumor: but children, most of the time, don't even know what it is.
So it's an adult fear.
And by talking to the patient and the siblings I open up that communication within the family that also puts the parents in a position to accept those difficult questions, or those sometimes intriguing questions that they ask.
Because parents can say: "Talk to Dr. Jankovic, talk to Dr. Spinello and the staff, so they can explain you exactly what is happening."
At that moment, sharing and communicating with the child becomes a "winning" moment, I dare say.
Why winning?
Because even the sick people, even the youngsters, must be listened to.
And this also applies to the adult: "Sharing" means "making him aware".
One of the mistakes we make, as doctors, is scaring people.
Pediatricians also do that: we scare parents, we scare the child. "If you don't do this..." but that's not the way.
This is ephemeral, it won't get you very far; instead, understanding what is happening, and sharing a path that will take its time - when I talk to children, I always make a metaphor with a thriving garden.
In a flowered garden, weeds grow without anyone wanting them.
In a marrow , the garden in this metaphore, good cells generally grow - this is true for tumours and leukaemia - but sometimes other lazy, wicked cells grow, that bother the healthy ones.
And so this metaphor actually gets them a little bit closer into what's going on.
And even when the disease can reoccur, that's fine.
Because in a beautiful, well kept garden - and this justifies the sustained gardener's attention, which is the care that we give for a certain period.
Is it right to say, two years?
I don't say two years, it doesn't matter: but I answer precise questions that the child can ask.
The child can tell me: Jankovic, I see so many bald heads.
Will I lose my hair, too?
Then I say yes.
But if I say it before he expects my yes, and I am "invasive" towards him, I discourage him.
I've seen little disheartened girls without knowing what was being said about the disease: their mind was on that.
That's where qualitative discourse, in my opinion, makes all the difference.
And we must have the courage, also, to start that alliance I have told you about, our inner forces can really be directed towards a positive goal.
And the desirable goal is, in my case, when 85% of children heal, but 100% improve their quality of life.
Anger, fury, depression, all unfortunately deplete our energy.
The help we can give is instead to direct these energies towards a common goal that the other realities can then take on too.
Other prominent players are the institutions, volunteers, people who actually want to provide an all-encompassing care of the patient.
And the last thing I wanted to share is this: listening means, also, seeing what they tell us and what they teach us.
There was this kid who wasn't responding well.
I go to see him, I tell him, You look a little sad today.
Do you want me to bring you a football player, a sportsman, a singer?
Maybe bringing important people... And he candidly admits, I'd like Mrs. Antonia.
The janitor of his room!
I said: why Mrs. Antonia?
You know, when she comes here she always tells me stories, fairy tales, we keep each other company.
Mrs. Antonia!
It wasn't what I wanted for his good, as a doctor; but that's what he was asking, at a time when Mrs. Antonia - who was ill, wasn't there - was giving to him.
And Mrs. Antonia, in fact, brought him to death.
And another child, who always brought me drawings of trains, was an incredible six-year-old boy.
When things started to go awry, he brought me drawings of trains with a black cross instead of a level crossing.
Which tells you something. I said: Claudio, have you ever been on a train?
No.
Then I arranged to go on the train with him.
At first, I thought Pendolino, but it was too fast.
Then I turned back to the Monza-Lecco which is a little bit more manageable, and we had an incredible day with the great solidarity of those who actually contributed to this: the stationmaster, the railwayman, people who gave us their solidarity.
And this is another form, a way of being close and making sure that it is not a gift but a quality of life for the child poised not to make it.
And this, by the way, has greatly helped relieve the parents' grief, because they phoned me for several years not the day their child died, but the day we went together by train on the Monza-Lecco.
So the ability to listen remains indeed fundamental.
And Marcello, seven years old, left me this poem that fully recapitulates what I shared with you insofar.
It's called "Dolcezza" (Tenderness).
It says: "Much like fire in the burning night, I saw your eyes.
They cried.
They loved.
They smiled, your eyes.
They're dear, they're pure, they're as sincere as tricks.
I love them, I feel them imprinted in my life.
They are your eyes."
And he ends: "I find them tired, today, but still tender, believe me Mom."
Marcello, seven years old.
Thanks.
(Applause)
When my children's friends ask me, what's your job?
I reply: "I make little eyes for robots".
To be precise, my job is inventing the algorithms which are the part of a robot's brain in control of the eyes.
Sight is very important for us: we explore the world with vision, with our ability to perceive it.
Consider that 30 percent of our neurons are dedicated to vision.
Only 4 percent to touch.
Also consider that there is a part of our brain which is expressly dedicated to face recognition.
And that the first face a newborn baby learns, one of the very first things that a newborn learns, it to recognise its mother's face.
Because it depends on the mother for protection, for food.
And therefore, our ability to perceive is essential to understand the world, and that's why we have to try to give robots these same perceptual abilities, if we want them to really become a part of our life, and help us.
But seeing is more than perceiving.
Let's do a demo.
I need your help.
Raise your right hand if you think this is a cup.
Whoever thinks it's a cup, keep your right hand up.
And now, instead, whoever thinks this is a pen holder raise your left hand.
You can all keep your hands up.
You have all surrendered.
(Laughter) What did I do?
What happened there?
When I showed you this object, you recognised it as a cup because over, and over, and over again you drank coffee, tea, or whatever your favourite hot liquid is from an object like this.
Hot liquid, because I'm holding this object by the handle, because cups have handles.
Because we want to use them, they are containers we want to use to put some hot liquid inside.
So the hand must stay away, I don't want to burn myself.
But it's also a container.
And sometimes, I'm sure, you've seen it used, or used it, like a pen holder.
Because it's a container, I can put things in it.
I can even put water in it.
I can even put a seedling in it, why not.
So, what am I saying here?
That the perception of this object doesn't tell me everything about what the essence of this object is.
We understand what we see not just based on what we perceive, but we also understand it based on other modalities.
On what we do with this object.
And then, what happened in our brains, when I showed you this cup?
What happened is that we have models, that can be defined as "meta-modal", formed of many modalities, where the perception is what makes us click, it's what we hook on to.
But then there's all this other information which depends a lot on all the things we can do with these objects.
On all the actions we've done with these objects, and that's how we store it in our memory.
That's how you understand what you see.
And this is the type of meta-modal representation that we need to make robots understand the world, to show them how we see.
But it's still not enough.
We have to take one more step, we have to go further.
I've got a story for you.
This is a photo from my family album from an Easter many, many years ago.
I'm the girl worshipping the Easter egg, because I - I liked chocolate, I still like it - that's how it is.
But I was also a disciplined child, so I waited until the end of lunch to scoff the egg.
And it didn't end there.
Because besides being very greedy, I've also always been very vain.
I liked, and I still like, clothes.
And this paper was beautiful!
All shiny and colourful.
And so, after eating the egg, I took this paper, I wrapped it around myself and showed up this way in the living room.
I had never seen anyone use the Easter egg paper like a dress.
Nobody had ever told me that the paper from an Easter egg could be made into a dress.
But I had seen so many fabrics.
I had understood, I had seen that clothes are made of fabric.
I had seen my mum at the sea in a sarong, which looks a lot like this object, taking it and making it into a dress.
And I recognised that this beautiful coloured paper had these same properties of flexibility.
So what did I do?
I imagined that using the paper from an Easter egg you could also do what you do with a sarong.
And I did, and I wore it.
This is the point, this is the main thing.
Besides being able to understand the world with representations that go beyond how we perceive it, with meta-modal representations, we are able to imagine, we are able to go further.
When we see something, we are able to not only think about what it is, to understand and grasp what it is, but also to imagine what it could be.
This is one of the great triumphs of human intelligence, and is still today one of A.I.'s biggest limits.
I'm sure you've heard about robots doing concerts.
These robots perform perfectly, exactly, the actions and gestures they were taught by their teacher.
If this teacher is a great musician, the concert will be wonderful.
But it will be exactly nothing but that single and unique interpretation.
Compare this with what human beings are capable of.
Think of a jazz orchestra made up of musicians who meet, play together for the first time and bring their talents together and create in the moment, through improvisation, a wonderful music, that no one ever heard before.
This ability to imagine, this ability to go further.
This is one of the great challenges which artificial intelligence, one the research in this field is trying to tackle now.
It's not easy: we have to move from an analogue world to a digital world.
When I work on images, the images are made of pixels, so everything is discrete.
And what we have to do is write programmes, and write algorithms that manage to capture, at least in part, what I've told you about.
To do this we have a tool, for a couple of years now - five years, to be precise: Deep Learning.
A bundle of algorithms that try to simulate the behaviour of the human brain.
We implement functions that mimic neurons, and also mimic synapses, the connections between neurons.
And these algorithms are called large capacity algorithms, because they're algorithms that can handle millions and millions of bits of data.
In my case, images.
These images are the "food", the "fuel" if you like, that we put in these algorithms, which produce - they are data-squeezers, so to say.
We feed them with data, and they can distinguish the noise from the signal.
Which is one of the essential details.
And since there are many, many images on the web, there are lots of data, trying to build meta-modal representations, especially in cases with no concrete examples, in my research group my vandals and I told each other: "But why don't we try teaching the robots?
Teaching them to learn from the web?"
After all, when we don't know something, what do we do now?
We get out our phones, and search the web.
Why not the same for the robots?
This is an example of what we do.
Here you can see the robotic arm.
We asked this robot to clear a table after we had breakfast.
In the robot's memory there were various objects: there was a cup, a banana.
But there was no jar of honey.
And so the robot perceived that there was something on the table, but it couldn't say what it was.
And so it went to search on the web.
What did it do?
It took a photo, and with this photo it found many other similar images, many other similar perceptions.
And after a while, it started to notice that these similar perceptions it found on the web were very often associated with one phrase: jar of honey.
And that's how it learned the name of that object.
And with the name of that object it could search for other information too.
It understood that it's an object that's composed of parts, because it has a lid, and therefore, when a robot has to go and get it, it's not a good idea to pick it up by the lid, as if it's not screwed on, I'll take the lid off and the jar will stay there.
It learned there's a certain weight that can be expected, so the strength that the robotic arm must use, to pull up the jar, will be a certain type.
It learned that it's a rigid object, it's not like a milk box.
So when it presses, when the hand closes, the tactile control must be properly modulated.
Etc, etc.
And I'll show you that this information, which we find on the web in the form of text, so as a symbol, we learn it through actions, from our experience; and we are able to - click!
take it from our experience, and make it symbolic and accessible to everyone.
And so, after learning all these things, the robot discovered that this mysterious object is a jar of honey and it was able to pick it up and continue clearing the table.
We have built a meta-modal representation of a jar of honey, using the web as a starting point.
And what do we do with our imagination?
With the ability to imagine?
What we imagined is - why don't we give the robot hallucinations?
In a legal way, sure. Only legal.
This thing you see here
is a hallucinated apple.
We took from the web some pictures of apples, and we taught the robot to imagine what this apple would be like in three dimensions, because the information I find on the web is all two-dimensional images; but I need to understand the depth, the shape of the object.
And therefore, starting from this image we managed to hallucinate the form.
This is a small result, but for us it's actually a very important step.
Let me give you another example.
At the moment I see - what I can, with these lights, I can see all your faces.
I can't see the backs of your heads, but I've seen people many, many times, and so I'm assuming that the back of everyone's head is spherical.
Maybe there, where I can't see, there's an alien from Star Wars with a beautiful curly head, and so there I'm wrong.
However, if we're all human beings here, our heads are round.
We do this all the time.
When we perceive things in an incomplete manner, so as to pursue, in any case, the goal we must have, to have a model of the world, we complete it with whatever is most likely.
But this ability to complete is the ability to imagine, because usually I go with what's most probable.
But designers, the guys behind the characters for Star Wars, look for the improbable.
So, if we've found the way to have a probable apple, maybe, in the future, we'll also find the way to get a Star Wars apple.
Putting all these pieces together, we managed to get an R1 which learns about flowers from the web.
These are all objects the robot has already learned to recognise, and these bars you see there, that rise and fall, measure the confidence the robot has in what it sees.
In naming what it sees.
Now it detects the oven mitt.
And, you can see - sometimes it hesitates.
And now we show it the flower.
- [English]: What is this?
- I think this is a cellphone.
Mmm.. no.
- Learn what is a flower.
So it needs to learn what a flower is.
- OK, let me search the flower images on the web.
And so it looks for images on the web similar to what it saw.
And it finds lots of them.
It also has the name "flower", which can help it.
And what we do to help the robot contextualise this knowledge into its experience of the world, whenever it needs to use this model, is that we take all these flowers, we take off the photo, automatically, online, everything but flowers, and we put it in a scenario which is very similar to the one the robot sees around it, to help it recognise the flowers it can see around it.
- [English]: OK, now I know the flower.
Here's the flower model.
- What is this?
- I think this is a flower.
And so it has learnt.
We are still a long, long way from being able to say that we can recognise any object, but we're making small steps in this direction.
When these little steps take us all the way to the end of the journey, we will have robots which, at our children's birthday parties, will take the lights off the Christmas tree and try to dress up for the party.
You're all invited.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Silence is the most deafening noise any of us can hear.
The moment you scream like crazy, asking for help.
It's a time when you're feeling a cold blanket on you: you know you're right, you know that what happened to you was through no fault of your own, yet no one listens to you.
Or they look at you, but they don't really see you and pretend they're passing by you with a bang on your shoulder, saying that "everything will pass".
Your closest friends disappear when you're in need and through financial hardship, and you stay with your bad feeling in your heart and stomach and have to find solutions.
Because for those like me, a generation that very often was born within companies, grew up with companies, daughters of entrepreneurs, the company, the business, is like a brother who grows with you.
And at a certain point, it is entrusted to you by a father who trusts in your abilities, even if you are only 16 and then 18, with bills to pay and tight deadlines.
Your dreams end up on the drawer, your studies end, because 35 years ago having a diploma was not as important as today, and you take charge of your work, which gradually becomes your passion.
When this is no longer the case, when all this is put at risk by the actions of others, you have to make a choice.
Because it is essential for you to understand that although you formally have the sole responsibility of your business, you are also responsible for the lives the families of the people who believed in you and spend weeks, months, years next to you.
The indifference came from friends, family, institutions, the whole world.
When my company, after ten months of suffering and a carnet of checks in the hand, saw its main customer file for bankruptcy and reopen in 24 hours with another name, leaving a disaster behind him.
I denounced myself to all Italy's tax agencies, stating that if, after 42 years, my father's company could not pay the contributions was just because we hadn't been paid, so we chose to pay wages over taxes, we have not found anyone to take charge of the matter and take it to a higher level.
So what I had chosen as my passion, and took care of since then, would be destroyed.
And if the company failed, I would also fail: as a daughter, as a wife, as a mother.
Because beyond a certain threshold, your company merges with your life, and your life makes room to accomodate your company's needs.
Your son grows up with the company, like you did before him.
In front of all this, I had two choices: the simplest was to "freeze", wait for everything to collapse because nothing could make a difference, rules cannot change in Italy, and the smartest always wins.
But staying there would mean surrender to those who had hurt me and raped me in my house: in my soul, in my ethics and in my profession, which is a fundamental part of me.
So I chose the second way: fight.
Fight and denounce what had happened, something that until then had never happened in the Veneto area.
I sued my client, and began to write a diary, a diary where everything I tried to do day after day, was put down in words. So should I have failed to carry on anymore, my son could read my reasons, directly from by my pen, and not from other people's voice. Because I am sick and tired of hearing that entrepreneurs take their life because they are depressed. They are desperate! They have knocked on every imaginable door, and have not found answers anywhere.
So they pay with the last extreme gesture, taking their lives, leaving a circular to their families, where they relieve them of debts to the state if they don't touch their inheritance.
So let's stop saying that we are depressed, because after trying the impossible, a person drops the ball and leaves their dearest ones.
It's been a hard change.
Changing my kind and docile attitude has not been easy, even more so swimming upstream.
My diary has become a book, called "I don't want to fail".
The book was welcomed by the good politicians, got the attention of good politics in our region and then in Rome.
In 2015, I got to have an amendment to the 2016 Economic Stability Law that implemented a fund: a fund for delinquent victims of a third-party fraud, nicknamed "Serenella fund" in the Senate's note of 19 November 2015.
The fund is a loan that lends up to 500.000 euros for companies that received a "preferential" treatment as mine.
It is to be repaid in ten years, has a pre-amortization of 24 plus six months, and is at zero interest.
It is not a final solution: ultimately, money ends. You repay your current debt, and the next year the same thing can happen again.
And since it can only be used once, my awakening is what I wish you, because this part of my relay race has a baton. And the awakening of everyone is to grab that baton and move it another bit forward so this won't happen anymore, so that credits are secured, so that companies are secured, so that people are secured, whether they are entrepreneurs, workers and employees.
A head without hands goes nowhere, even less so the hands without head.
Let's have mutual respect: the company, our local companies are family units, where entrepreneurs go to their employees' weddings and baptisms.
It wasn't a cheap change for me.
It costed my a lot, as I have lost most of my friends and most of my family connections.
But I didn't want to be the one who bury his head in the sand anymore. So every action that we choose to do for the sake of ourselves has an impact on others.
And the point I'd like to hold to convince myself I acted in the right way, is that you can't always be compliant for the good of others.
Because our lives and our well-being are just as important as those of people who tell us, "You have to change".
We need to change when we feel like inside of us, and we have a good reason to do so.
All this has given me a regional fund, which in 2017, for a window of ten months, only for entrepreneurs in Veneto, has created a fund much like the national one.
It allowed me to change jobs after saving my company:
Today 24 employees work there, thanks to my brother's wise management.
I work with a group of professionals who also take care of the dreams of entrepreneurs and companies.
They taught me to study, read and trust in what I do and to believe we can give roots to dreams.
Donna Moderna magazine put me in a list of 100 women who did something great in the last 30 years.
Honestly, I didn't expect to get that far, just as I didn't expect to get to Brussels' following training courses where you can really breathe a different Europe while retaining my pride of being an Italian.
We have an association called "La stanza delle idee" (Room of Ideas) which takes care of entrepreneurs like me,
But after spending four years hearing situations like mine, stories like mine, which almost never have a happy ending, I couldn't bear it anymore.
So on December 31, 2018, after ten years, I decided to close it, because I was fed up with other people's sad stories.
I want to live my life, my awakening, my satisfactions, what I earned.
On January 1st, a message arrived on Messenger from Eleonora, the lady you see on this photo. She wrote: "I am Eleonora. I live in a small village in Sardinia.
In October, my husband took his life and left me with these three children.
I don't ask help for myself, I just want to know how did you create the association, so that no one in my land can suffer what I went through.
Seven days later William Beozzo, in representation of Mercatone Uno's suppliers, got there, asked if the crazy actions done four years earlier could be repeated by someone else.
And so we set the machinery back in motion, and this year in June we managed to change the amendment to the Serenella fund, which today is the staple legal tool for Mercatone Uno suppliers.
And so me saying "Enough of it!" has become a new awakening and I really think, had I to say enough again I will go through another one, so I better be quiet, happy with what I accomplished, happy with who I am and happy with the time I will still be allowed to improve.
I wish you a happy awakening, I wish you a daily awakening.
From little things, like cooking dinner for your loved ones, to taking a piece of the relay race of those around you, because life is wonderful, but surprises may be in store for us all.
And since we do not know when the bad ones come, let's enjoy the good ones
but let's prepare to be supporters and allow ourselves to be supported in moments of need.
Good awakening to all, thank you.
(Applause)
Good morning, ladies and gentlemen.
President Golinelli, I'm extremely happy to be back here in this city of knowledge and culture, for the past few days.
There is a commonality in our goals that you spoke about. But mostly today, it delivers us a challenge that's even more ambitious than the one we discussed recently when talking about Opus 2065. Kids in this room can imagine what society will be like in 50 years, and what can we do to shape the best possible future.
The stakes are higher today. Why?
I have an interesting challenge for you.
Dr. Pauri, I haven't followed
any TEDEd Club training,
so I hope you'll appreciate the naivety of my performance. I've been asked to deliver a TED talk about TED!
I taught and studied mostly rhetoric, so I'll try to take advantage of my old memories, of what is actually the oldest, most effective way of public speaking. The ability, the capacity to argue before a vast, diverse public, effectively, convincingly, persuading about our own ideas, about what we think are the winning ideas, the ideas that represent us. Ideas that are the meaning of school, and the meaning of ourselves.
I believe that's basically the essence of TED. When I was first introduced to the idea, with her gently stubborn manner by my friend and counselor, and also undersecretary to education, Elena Ugolini, I asked myself, what values can we give to our kids, through a national agreement - the first in the world, I'm so proud of it. Can we provide added values compared to what we learn in school, even in school, in the many chances you have to introduce yourselves in social contexts, in a public spaces, and convince others that your ideas have value, have a meaning?
And then build a relationship on top of that, of any kind?
But then three fundamental elements occurred to me, that might have to do with TED. But I immediately say, these are not what convinced me - plus, I insist, Elena's gentle stubbornness - to sign this protocol.
One: does TED have something to do with marketing?
Partly yes, maybe.
Because when one introduces herself, you introduce an idea, a project. A worthy one, one we deem as the most important in that moment of our life, especially young people like you, and you have to convince someone; that's the project, that's the idea.
So it also has to do with marketing.
It's not a bad word, it's not an element we absolutely have to exclude from our kid's training.
But maybe there's something more. TED is also a way of teaching.
I told you, I'm a classicist by training, I've studied classical rhetoric in depth.
I taught rhetoric, also.
It's a fantastic discipline. If you like TED, please, even if you're not classicists, let's get back to what our ancestors, from Greece to ancient Rome, and then through the Middle Ages and the Renaissance, taught this part of the world, the Western civilization, in terms of ability to argue, in terms of chance, with words, with the power of speech, to draw those before me to my cause, and enter a discussion
relationship with them.
So it's a way of teaching. And it's actually said, I agree with you TEDsters around the world, that it's also a way to disrupt teaching
because this puts kids behind the bench. So I reverse the traditional school paradigm, the main lectures.
Or, if I put teachers in this position, just like I'm doing this morning, I force them to get rid of some school orthodoxy.
So it also has to do with teaching.
And it might be a professional bias of mine, but it may also have to do with politics.
Politics in a broader sense, of course.
Not politics as belonging to some political party,
Yes, of course.
Because politics is about speaking, folks.
In its highest, most noble meaning, politics is active participation in public life, in social spaces.
So what does it mean to be politically active?
It means showing your ideas, their value, your worldview, through speaking.
So this also has to do with politics.
These three arguments would already be enough to convince a Minister of Republic to sign a national agreement, and allocate resources.
We did this first call, but another richer one is already in the pipeline, so that more and more children - more than 350 joined this year, more than 22 finalists, I wish all of them good luck - will perform after and better than me, on this stage.
So these three elements would already be enough.
But I want to make another point.
And in order to tell you, I need to explain what I found in TED, and what I hope <i>you</i> will find in TED. Generally in teaching, you learn how to express yourself. How you get in touch through word with others. I invite you to get this from two TED-like speeches which are available online. You might know one of them, but probably not the other. They aren't branded TED talks.
One is very long, but there's a shorter version. It's "The Last Lecture" by Randy Pausch, a computer scientist at Carnegie Mellon, who died eight years ago from a devastating pancreatic tumor. He held the so-called "Last Lecture," which is usually given by professors at the end of their career, to pass on to their students the legacy of their principles and methods.
He reinterpreted the format in that dramatic moment of his life.
But believe me, the lesson is not dramatic. It's brilliant, but mostly has a value I'll shortly get back to.
The other is the world famous Steve Job's "First Lesson." It's about 15 minutes long so it would even fit with the TED format.
I quoted it the other day also, because it's a very instructive lesson. If you haven't watched yet, do so.
He spoke at the opening ceremony on June 12th, 2005, to Stanford graduates.
I call it the "First Lesson": Jobs, you'll know this, never graduated.
He actually dropped out from classes because they seemed pointless to him. He didn't want his step-parents to pay his expensive college fees. Then he started taking calligraphy courses. When he designed the Macintosh, these calligraphy skills were like a treasure trove of knowledge.
So what will you find in the talks?
You'll find some similarities. I found a couple, maybe you'll find more between the last and the first lecture.
The "First Lesson" is my personal favorite. Jobs didn't become a professor later in life.
You'll find a very clear message that's targeted at you, because they were both talking to students. Pausch was also talking to his three children as it was meant to be a testament to his memory, after his death actually happened.
The messages are: "Always follow your curiosity, your instinct, your insight, and never get stopped by what seems to be an obstacle in that moment," says Steve Jobs.
And Randy Pausch says: "Don't lose your wonder, even after your childhood!"
Usually, wonder is for children when they see, for instance, a wonderful place like this, President Golinelli.
And I believe that children coming here, from nursery up to their 30s, are certainly in awe by the beauty, the originality of this place.
That's the point, don't lose that wonder.
I think some among you really got this.
When Alberto invites us to see reality differently, as a chemical combination of elements, well, that's one way to trigger a reaction of wonder.
They both were also talking about another thing that sometimes stops us in life, especially if we aren't used to dealing with it: obstacles.
Randy Pausch says: "An obstacle is there so we can measure ourselves by our ability to overcome it."
So they are a rather good thing to have.
Steve Jobs, in other words, makes a similar point about failure.
Failure can become a moment to restart with even more energy, with improved capacity to test your strength, willing to do some things that had never been done before.
When Steve jobs got fired from Apple, you may recall - Jobs himself, who invented the product that later became the style icon of a whole generation - he met his wife, or better, his life companion, rethought the whole computer industry, and the really productive season of his career began.
Talking about failure also means talking about persistence, resilience, the ability to cope with bad experiences, and move on.
Gabriella, in her photos and blog posts, when she says that hands are even better than faces and other features of our body in telling our story, does something similar. At least, that's how I read it. I like it, I totally agree.
Then the third, unescapable call, is the passion, the love one puts in what she does.
Sure, that passion might evolve into an obsession,
into attachment to the project, to the things we do.
And that's perseverance, that's resilience. Alessandra, speaking about her long career in rowing, spokes about these values, and I'm convinced that these values, and an apparent talent for athletics that nature only gives to some of us, has reached the same results.
Plus Andrei, who built in his garage, a bionic hand, right?
A little robot.
Okay, I won't delve into it, because it's a mysterious field for me.
So guys, the point of my story is that maybe what TED can offer is the ability, the practice of speaking in public, convincing others that what we're saying isn't just what we truly believe in that moment, but what we think is the next big, worthy thing.
But something fundamental about us is involved; the value of authenticity.
I don't know if you agree with me, but in this moment of history, and even in my personal life, let's come out. I feel a dramatic thirst for authenticity to express ourselves as we are,
a quality that sometimes in life is at risk.
Not just by growing, but by entering worlds that "frame" you and only allow you to say some things, but not others,
in some contexts, but not in others,
Which eventually degenerates into the so-called politically correct, which has dominated much of the Anglo-Saxon and global culture, and there would be a lot to say about it. I'm a linguist, so it's a somewhat professional obsession, but there would be a lot to say on how much authenticity could, and must be, regained through speech.
So that's why I decided to sign the protocol,
we like to say, the world's first protocol with TED. There are 17,000 past TEDx events, so you can well imagine, our contribution might be quantitatively negligible, but qualitatively, it will be an extraordinary thing because it will give our kids the chance to train the expression of their authenticity, the most extraordinary value.
Thank you so much, good luck.
(Applause)
FdB: Katia. KP: Here I am.

FdB: Big and fast changes. KP: Very fast.
FdB: Very fast.

What's happening to teenagers?
How do they live it?
What's happening?
KP: Hard to say, because teenagers today usually described in opposite ways.
We speak about adolescents by representing their demotivated and superficial traits.
We represent them as listless and fragile boys.
Or we praise them when we think about extraordinary, out of the ordinary people and we fall in love with them, but we fail to channel these specialties within the big contemporary generation.
Should we draw a bottom line from help requests to doctors, we have to think about a generation of teenagers that's substantially sadder than the previous one, more introverse, less prone to express outwards their rage and aggressiveness.
So they're less inclined to fight and shout, they rather risk to end up becoming introvert and quitting their project of growth,
drenched in a gloom that leaves them demotivated.
FdB: An example may be interesting to make us understand.
KP: I think the examples that many of us unfortunately know, not only those that work in clinics, among our relatives, cousins, friends, are stories about boys that once were special kids that were described enthusiastically at the nursery and primary schools:
"That nice little kid, fast, smart, always moving, all figured out, maybe a bit too hard to handle; but you can see their own nature, their own personality, he may be destined to be rebellious and unsubmissive. And he may be one of those who will be able to find their own amazing path.
When this child goes to primary schools, although he says: "I want to do this, to study birds,
I want to study animals; no, only dinosaurs; no, just fishes, I don't like this stuff." This signal is warmly welcomed because it means they're all figured out and they're connected with their predispositions.
Yet once he's at the secondary school, this view changes completely, and the same features that were first welcomed with: "Wow, look how much energies, how many opportunities", at the secondary schools are seen differently. It gets worried, alarmed, because: "Oh my god, he doesn't follow the rules.
We gave him instructions that he didn't follow.
He may even fall behind with his studies, just because he's not able to use the trails that someone else's has left before him.
So the view turns from skillful to alerted, worried and sometimes even disgusted. So adults' view of teenager can also be like this.
And these kids, that suddenly clash with a view they'd have never expected, as they were raised in ten welcoming years of extremely positive regards, first they try to understand what happened, who made the mistake: those who came first and maybe saw a talent, a genius that didn't exist, or maybe the blames are on those that came next and are hostile to adolescents. And so they're indolent.
But all this questioning soon loses its importance as what really matters in the mind of these kids is that things went the wrong way, thus they sure have failed on something. And they may feel ashamed for this attitude and humble façade, but also for this failure to keep promises.
Then shame and guiltiness alternate and at that point the pain is too much, there's no more future. What happens next is that these kids become more introvert, they stop to believe that there's a future project or that it actually makes any sense.
You suffer less with lower expectations, locking yourself up in your home, also for the fear of showing yourself to the other people who will note the differences between the primary schools' cheerful you and now you dramatically show how average you are. Thus, a total failure
of the growth project.
FdB: But what went wrong?
Who's responsible for that?
KP: From a certain perspective, it's everyone and nobody's fault,
which means maybe we should start all over by accepting the idea that both a positive and negative judgment is in the view of the one who observes, not the one observed.
We have a very serious problem nowadays - I mean we as adults - in modulating consistently educational models between childhood and adolescence.
We are the adults that feel betrayed, because they've seen a future - I would say assured - vanish.
Sure, you had to do things, to have a future: a bit of responsibility, a bit of effort; but then that positive future was not questioned.
Today it is, and we've convinced ourselves that the future before us is spoiled, gone.
And we're also adults that experimented firsthand how dramatic is to teach rules, stick to the rule with no awareness, "mindlessly", thus with a subordinate view, a mind that doesn't reason and that doesn't question the rule we enforced.
And this has determined a burden that we adults put on children, the youngsters, by telling them they must be brave, they must be strong they have to find out very early what their skills are, in order to be themselves and find their own path, so that every challenge they are going to face, will also be an obstacle they'll be able to overcome.
But we didn't consider adolescence, which, compared to childhood, contemplate the other, the different, and the unknown. So, for us valorizing a child means that we're teaching him the rules into the relationship: "Do it because I love you. Do it because I've invested a lot on you, and I know what's good for you." It's an internal rule, that comes from a familiar environment that we value because it belongs to us, but the adolescent, if he does his job well, is destined to go further into the unknown, the different, unexplored.
At that point we adults, as we're still used to supervise their growth, because we know the rules, we know responsibility, we get scared, we don't know how to react,
there we go back to the old model fashion, we ask them to submit to the rules on behalf of responsibility, in the name of becoming adults.
We ask them to stick to the very model we rejected, as we saw it didn't work, but we don't have an alternative one. And we are - while waiting
for better options, by acting like this, we unintentionally dismiss the possible creation of an opening toward future.
FdB: Given your experience and the number of cases you analyzed, went through and helped, what can be done?
KP: Many things, thankfully, because retreating boys are always a growing number.
Social retreatists are those that lock themselves up at home, but there are always more cases of mental retreatism, meant as a mental shutdown, not thinking at all, so as not to feel the pain.
We really need to start again, aware of the fact that we're the actors that can make a difference.
The project of giving so much value to children and then taking it back as adolescents, thinking that this way they'll be tested and their attitude strengthened and tempered, is in fact a suicidal project for contemporary adolescents, because it means assimilating them to a relationship, to a particolar view and then suddenly, without any prior warning, take that support back, taking away that safety area, the relationship with adults in general, the closest ones, but also teachers and all adults surrounding them.
This means handing them over to fear, to solitude and gloom.
Then we have another serious problem that is a consequence of the speed you mentioned during the introduction.
Changes happen so fast that institutions are stuck in a very difficult situation, because it's evident that today we have to modify our knowledge, we have to create knowledge not the way we approached it, not the way we learned it at school. That was a knowledge made of contents, straight linear passages, made of relations between who knows and who doesn't know, who has to learn and so has to receive those contents and learn to repeat them. Nowadays, knowledge is evidently procedural.
It's in motion, it's constantly evolving,
it needs exploration, it takes thoughtful minds, minds that experience knowledge.
And here we are still within a model, those books we talked about this morning that rest high on top of the shelves, which are not able in fact to create devices for mind training.
Complex competences can't be trained, without getting the kids' hands dirty, and without modifying those tools and making them flexible.
We also have another problem still about knowledge and how we figure it out.
We adults are grown - since the technological revolution of writing, we relate through names with a knowledge made of definitions, made of casuistry, of packagings and analysis, of differences telling us: "We learned this notion"
And we apply this view on everything, even when we give grades, when we give a judgment, when we do a diagnosis.
So, whether we're parents, teachers, clinicians, in fact we apply these kids a taxonomic logic.
We tell them: "You're like this,
before you were brilliant and skilled, then suddenly you become one that makes us worried, you don't follow the rules.
Maybe we can't get along with you, we don't know what you're doing.
How can we supervise this path of yours, which already has arguments, contents and manners we're not aware of?"
Here we put labels on it,
unaware of the fact that, if identity is in movement today, one becomes himself, and you need a life to become more and more yourself. we need adults to question the deep meaning of their functions.
It's not a label that can help us through,
beyond the label, we need to think about why it happened. what's the meaning, what resources can you activate, how did you activate them.
Last big topic, huge topic but we should try to talk about it between adults, before involving kids. We condemned these kids since they were children, in this passage from a formal family made of rules, of absolute values, of the affective family, made of relationships, We gave them an addiction on our sight.
We are the ones giving value, and indeed when we get scared we take it away from them, so these kids heavily rely on us and on our happiness, in some ways.
When we get scared, we shutdown, we got worry and we declare that at that point the future is all wrong, all lost.
At that point these boys have a problem at surviving. But they also feel burdened by the responsibility of having somehow disapponted us, damaged us.
And it becomes a schism mechanism, where all subject go their own way, struggling and in distress.
The most evident example
of this running apart, everyone on his own, is the fact that since the kids are very young we tell them: "Find your talent, find your gift, run fast, decide as soon as possible."
The infamous "I either study dinosaurs, or don't." The idea that your path must be focused on yourself, on your ambitions, besides of competences.
Actually, as that knowledge should be co-created, co-experienced, you have to consider the others' existance and enter a project of co-responsibility of knowledge.
So much so that today kids renounce to the project of growth and to the project of knowledge, if their knowledge is detrimental to someone else.
It becomes one of the most common risk factors in a classroom, when it's clear that competition will privilege someone and dismiss someone else.
You can notice this aspect in the individual, as well as in groups, because at this point it's not enough to state: "We must give space to kids because they aren't superficial, nor incompetent.
They're strong instead, use technologies, have their own future".
We should begin from this point:
it's their future
and ours, as we visualized it, doesn't exist anymore.
But we cannot step aside and let them deal with everything because they are kids we raised to share, to participate emotionally.
Now we have to participate to the building not only of knowledge, but also of the future project. It's like if we said: "It's not how we expected anymore, everything is different,
we don't even know what will happen,
but if we do that together we will get something back."
It's like stating we're not supervisors of the whole process, but we supervise the fact that it's worth it. And together we can still co-build a future.
FdB: Wonderful message.
(Applause)
I'm here to tell you why, in Belgium, I decided to look fafter my kidnappers' mothers..
Six years ago I was kidnapped in Syria while I was working with a Rai TV crew.
And during this kidnapping I was able to recognize the group that arrested us, a group I had already talked about in my reportages.
Its name was "Jabhat al-Nusra", which means People's Liberation Front.
It then became ISIS. During the first hours of my kidnapping I thought of this name: Jabhat al-Nusra, People's Liberation Front.
Liberation... from what?
Perhaps it was just a liberation from freedom.
At least this is what I experienced during my captivity.
I was kept in solitary confinement, covered, literally covered, by a blanket, because I didn't have a burqa.
So I was meant to be hidden there.
But I wasn't alone. I was in isolation, but not alone.
There were armed militia men watching me, day and night.
They were men, but I was also with a woman, my jailer, and I then learned to classify them by their languages and dialects.
But who were these militia men?
Some of them were French-speaking, which means they could have come from Tunisia, as well as France or Belgium.
So when in 2018 I moved to Brussels, I thought it was time to go and find out who the mothers of these "Foreign Fighters" were.
A dialogue with them did mean a lot to me; it meant, above all, reconciling myself with that tremendous experience, that was the deprivation of freedom. But it also brought me to discover, understand whether it is right or wrong to always blame the mothers as the root of all these faults.
I met two women: the first was Saliha Ben Ali, an extremely strong woman who loves Belgium, which allowed her to grow up in a free and non-patriarchal culture.
She loves Belgian's laws, its freedom of expression, its diversity, its tolerance.
All principles that she made her own and then passed on to her son.
I read her biography and it felt like going back to high school, when I read the Greek tragedies.
She's like a heroine of a Greek tragedy: who struggles against an unescapable destiny, against an evil fate: she does everything right, then everything goes awry.
Her son, Sabri, was 18 years old when, in 2013, was brainwashed by a seemingly charitable organization called "Sharia for Belgium", and decided to leave. Obviously it wasn't
a completely sudden decision.
There had been months of radicalization when Saliha used to stick around and control him. But the son's escape project did took place overnight, actually, because Sabri cleverly started to disguise his intentions. and made her think he came to his senses.
So she let go.
She had been preventing him from leaving the house, but when she saw her son coming to his senses after a beautiful holiday they spent together in Morocco, on the beach, doing things that were deemed a sin, under his new vision of Islam, she thought, my son is 18, he can make mistakes and change his mind.
After all, who is my son?
Is that playful boy who used to pull my skirt?
The one who used to protect the weakest children from bullies?
My son is back, she thought.
But one morning she woke up and found Sabri's room empty.
She immediately understood what had happened. She called the police to have him arrested.
But the police do not arrest an 18-year-old who travels alone.
He's free,
even though this new adult, until the day before, could not make his bed.
But he is an adult now.
Saliha experienced this great tragedy,
but she decided to react. She funded an association: 'Save Belgium',
a name that indicates their desire to help other families in the same condition, and to save a generation of potential new foreign fighters.
I also met another woman with a similar story called Fatiha.
She is not only the mother of foreign fighters, she's also the grandmother of innocent children
that are now in the Al-Hol camp.
This is a detention camp in a desert area of Syria close to the border with Iraq.
This camp is very well known, as widows of the Daesh, of ISIS,
tipically dwell there. Recently the United States Department of Defence urged the local Kurdish forces to close this camp, but the European countries have an obligation to take back their citizens, the "foreign fighters".
Most of them are women and children.
But what is the fault of these children?
When I spoke to Fatiha, she showed me the photos of her grandchildren aged between one and six. Some of them are blond, because her son's wife is a girl from Flanders who converted to Islam. Others, on the other hand, have darker skin because both Fatiha's daughter and daughter-in-law had to remarry, and marriages there are arranged last minute. I saw this group of children and I thought, they are innocent
and they deserve to return to their grandmother in Belgium.
During our meeting, Fatiha expressed her sorrow looking at the image of her dead son on the computer.
I thought about the nonsense of a digital mourning.
I would be lying if I told you I felt any compassion watching the photo of that dead guy, with uncombed hair, purplish lips, with a clean wound on his heart. I saw him and I felt nothing: he could have been one of my kidnappers.
But I would be lying if I told you that I felt no compassion for that crying woman.
And I would lie if I told you that I do not care about the fate of the children of that Nureddin, of the man who made the jihad and died.
Now it's been six years since my kidnapping. I became a mother,
and it changed my mind.
Even back then, though, before I experimented my biological motherhood, I had already understood that the mothers of those foreign fighters were not doing so well, mostly.
Perhaps they were living the same anguish of my mother, knowing I was in a country at war.
How did my life change?
First of all, I take every passing day with deep gratitude, as a gift.
I don't think I have any trauma, but I feel a deep need to understand what is the behavior behind the deviance. What drives a person to violate the human rights of another person?
That drives me mad.
And it doesn't come only from criminals, from the ones we call terrorists: it happens every day.
So I get angry before a violation of human rights of immigrants, of unaccompanied minors, and people who are deprived of their freedom of speech, much like for me.
So what can I do?
I can talk about it, because I am a journalist, and so I am doing here with you.
I feel the desire to help, just as I was helped to get out of that dark cave of what my life could have become had I remained in the hands of my kidnappers.
Thank you.
(Applause)
What a pleasure to have music experts here.
Wait, music experts?
Yes, because each and everyone of us is music.
If I manage to have you leave today with this awareness that we, human beings, are music, it will be a great achievement.
Actually there's a problem in language. It is often said that one must be "brought" to music, meaning one must have a particular talent.
I read it differently instead.
"Being brought" might also mean, someone has to bring you to music.
And that's why I am here today.
I have this ambition, to raise the awareness that you are music.
A few years ago, I read a book entitled: “Drawing on the Right Side of the Brain” by Betty Edwards, a very interesting book.
At some point there was a short story about the two sides the brain is split into: the left, rational side of the brain, that allows a sailor with sextant, compass and nautical charts to reach islands that are sometimes as far as 2000 km away in the middle of the Pacific Ocean to reach the other island, all with a vertical or linear thinking, the one of the left hemisphere.
Then there's a resident in one of these islands. His goal is to reach the next one, 2,000 km away. What does he need to do?
He has no sextant, no compass:
but he has intuition and takes other information, that which passes through the right side of the brain.
Exactly here we find a connection with music, and that's why I say “we are music”, because we are precisely structured to make music. He senses where the other island is,
and then he'll get some help: the water, the smell of sailing conditions, the stars, and ultimately finds his way to the other side.
The bottom line is
that an integration, not a separation must be found between these two sides that nourish our brain, an integration that fully makes us who we are.
Yes, as a musician there's a problem:
when someone asks me, "What do you do?", "I'm a musician." "No no, I mean your job!" As if being a professional musician wasn't a job.
It is actually an extraordinary human specialization, it is the one thing to which I would like to get to today.
What I'd most like to convey is, take your inner musician back. And how do we discover that we are musicians?
We have a set of life modalities, what I call the “survival kit” in order to stay in the external world that are extremely musical.
For instance, think about this family scene that might lead to a divorce: “Mario, take the rubbish out.” “Mario, take the rubbish out.” “Mario, take the rubbish out.” (Shouting) “Mario, take the rubbish out." Mario was not listening to his wife, for she made a mistake: she wasn't musical when she asked him to do so.
“You don’t listen, I always have to repeat hundreds of times!” No! Mario is simply focused on something else and the right side of his brain is completely taken.
But had she been aware of our musical nature, what would she have done?
Precisely what every composer does: she would orient the repetitions. “Mario, take the rubbish out.” (Shouting) “Mario, take the rubbish out.”
Be sure that Mario already took the garbage and is on his way out,
there's no need for a third time.
Why?
Because oriented repetitions are a representation of what we do in our existence.
When we repeat something, there are two possibilities: either we repeat to stress, or we repeat to mitigate.
Another interesting book was: Lateral Thinking, that makes you explore the inner working of a brain's right side.
Many of you may now wonder, what does this have to do with music.
But what did you expect?
Me coming here and playing you something, or describing how symphony works?
If I'm here to prove that you are music, this is a family chat about something we all share.
What does this lateral thinking say?
That you can remain in linearity and perceive things as coming along in a lockstep, within a relentlessly flowing physical time.
However, in a music piece the goal is trying to express the end right from the beginning.
How amazing this thought is.
And yet… What is the GPS if not the representation of all this?
What happens when you have to go somewhere and put the destination in?
The whole path shows up.
So what's screened into the display, for your peace of mind?
The end - your destination - at the beginning of the journey.
Another demonstration: the act of thinking.
When you speak, hopefully you already have your point in mind,
So what's left for you to do?
Just elapse that thought through time with a clear end already within you.
It is the potential held in the seed, only waiting to find its way to blossom.
And how does this affect our existence?
Narrative frameworks are mesmerizing: When we hear a story, we are absolutely captivated.
Why?
We know that it'll try and answer three fundamental questions, the ones a music piece fully embraces. That's why I say to you, take your inner musician back. For each and everyone of us has this ability.
How does a music piece work?
It begins, it will reach a climax and then it goes downhill.
This is true for all narrative structure.
That's what we try to teach children when they have to write an essay.
They're told, Try to imagine a structure, you will have to expose what happens. There is even a musical form actually called ”Sonata Forma” structured in: Exposition; then you have to develop the ideas showcased in the Exposition.
In music there are terms like: Exposition; Development; and then you wrap up: Reprise.
Wow, so musicians can greatly help us understand how we are made.
Yet which prerequisites do we have?
In the kit we received, we have the dynamics, that men transposed later on into this extraordinary mean of communication that sound is.
For instance: a “fortissimo” noise cause us great anxiety.
Why?
We are struck by a huge adrenaline rush,
because we have to get ready to either escape or stay still in order to understand how to behave in that situation.
So what is the ”fortissimo” in music?
When a piece reaches a paroxysm, this does resonate in us!
Then there is a relationship among sounds, which is critical.
What is the great problem of our times?
Thinking sound and music are the same thing.
They're not.
Sound is only a regular vibratory motion of elastic bodies.
What turns a sound into music?
Human conscience.
So think how extraordinary this thought is, for those who believe that music is dying or is already dead.
As long as there are humans, because they are music, music can't die.
But people are needed to reconnect you with this awareness.
I always say, I have a socratic attitude: instead of seeing us as empty vases waiting to be filled with information, I'd rather get back, after two millennia of history, to the expression Γνῶθι σεαυτόν, nosce te ipsum, know thyself.
It's all within you already, just take it out. And that's what Socrates and his maieutic art help us do.
However some fields operators are needed, some obstetricians, in a sense, who can help us reacquire this awareness.
Let's try an experiment together now.
Please stand up for a second, and try to represent these two sounds with gestures:
(Singing) Would you rather do this… (Singing) Seeing your gestures, it seems to me you'd rather do this: (Singing) Sit down.
July 6th, 1789.
Danton, Marat, Roberspierre:
"So guys, there’s a bit of stress."
"When do we have it?"
"On 14th."
(Laughter) "The 14th... we’re in trouble!" Then one of the three has a spark and says, "Folks, lots of people will come, but we don’t have an anthem yet!
We have to set up an audition."
Musicians undergo a lot of them; you go to an audition, show your material, usually there's a commission that says, "Ok, thank you, we will let you know." And what happened?
Several people showed up,
one played this: (Piano music) They listened and then said, "Yes, uhm.. this could be interesting. Thanks, we'll let you know."
Excuse us, we're a bit in a hurry.
Then another one came, (Piano music) "Well, this was a bit better..."
Others came...
It is tough to think of a piano in 1789, but anyway....
Finally, one sat down and played this: (Piano music) (Singing) Ta – ta, ta - ta. Oh, so there is something that really characterizes us.
We instinctively have the sense of extroversion.
Namely, something that begins from the individual and projects itself outwards, everytime we play this interval - the cornerstone interval of music, it's the only technical thing I'll share - The fifth interval, the distance between the first and the second sound.
This.
(Plays the fifth interval) (Plays "La Marseillaise") That's the essence of the melody.
A Japanese invented a method to bring music to children.
Many of your sons have probably gotten in touch with it, the Suzuki method.
One of its most characterizing melodies is: (Plays the melody) (Plays the interval) Here another important thing also happened.
That narrative structure with a proposal moment, an opening, than has to find its natural compensation.
How horrible would be a song like (Piano music) Yes ok, but this is only for expert, it's a complex theme and it won't have any effect on children.
Instead…. (Piano music) How satisfying it is for our need of a narrative structure.
Something that starts, reaches a culminating point, and gently goes downhill.
So what am I trying to leave you with today?
We are music, let's take it back.
Think about the execution time in a song, how we instinctively get it.
Why?
Because we have the rhythm within.
Our heart is constantly beating, giving us a pulse.
Our footsteps are binary: push, relax.
And when someone criticizes a concert?
During the execution, people hardly say: Sure, tonight the modulation inside the second circle of fifths wasn't fully satisfied, and even the connections between the melodies... No.
What we usually hear is, I am not an expert - wrong - but I had the feeling like they were not as one.
And then there is another extremely important fact. The metric that inside a speech has to come out... The metric that inside a speech has to come out. The metric that inside a speech has to come out. The metric that inside a speech...
We constantly make music.
Take this ability back to you, because each of us takes music around.
Let’s tap into this huge resource we all share.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Emanuela was waiting for me outside the airport.
We loaded the suitcases on an old grey Fiat Tipo and we headed to his house.
After 20 endless, dark minutes, the car stopped near a gate.
Emanuela, a hundred meters before, began honking to get the attention of someone beyond the fence.
We waited a few moments before two strange shadows usher our way to the house.
One of them had a ski mask and a huge torn black sweater. The other one was wearing a baseball hat and a grey bomber.
They both were wearing tight and patched trousers.
They were the night watchmen.
They helped me with the suitcases and they sat in front of my bedroom window.
They spoke to each other softly in an unknown language.
It was a bit like listening to a lullaby.
I fell asleep after a few minutes.
I had a quick breakfast the morning after and went through that gate again.
I ended up in the middle of a crowded street dressed mostly in rags.
I saw a swarm of children with faded aprons.
The air was unbreathably hot and wet.
Old cars sprouted black smoke from the exhaust pipes as they accelerated to overcome the wooden carts, pulled by humped oxen or pushed by skinny men.
I slipped into a flea market.
The stench of the drains alternated with the scent of spices.
Meat hanging down, wrapped in swarms of flies, dried fresh fish, season vegetables, tropical fruit, everything was exposed on wooden stalls
and then, there was everything.
Buttons, pots, blankets, bike replacement parts, eggs, Chinese radios, total chaos.
It was March 1, 2006.
I was Finally in Africa, Madagascar.
Only two days ago I was in a suit and tie in the bank office I would shortly resign.
I had decided to change my life.
I created with some friends the Mangwana association, which means “Tomorrow”, “Future”.
We had the idea to start a soccer school for street children.
So I gave all up and I left for three months.
Then I stayed for 12 years.
Madagascar, in the collective imagination, is the country of biodiversity: there are untained forests, heavenly beaches.
Madagascar is also known for a famous cartoon with cute little animals like lemurs and fossas.
I have crossed the whole country, from its plateau to the shores, and I’ve found something very different.
I saw women queued for hours at the gates of the freezones, which are textile facilities' sheds, hoping to find a job with no contract, insurance or social security of any kind. to a little more than one euro a day,
with only one guarantee: if they didn’t show up once,
they would lose the job. I saw children cracking stones on the street in the dust all day, under the sun, without water and food.
I saw some Little boys in mica mines or others ones looking for precious stones.
Hills were eroded by landslides due to massive deforestation, vast territories are becoming deserts, once virgin beaches are now crowded with tourist facilities.
I'm always been a sailor.
I had a boat moored in Viareggio on the Burlamacca canal and in those years I was also a skipper.
I went to Madagascar also because I have always been fascinated by the stories of pirates.
On the island of Nosibora, better known by its French name of Saint Marie. By following a map you can reach a cemetery where corsairs and buccaneers are buried, including the infamous William Kidd.
I was in front of the headstones in this lost corner of the world, when I realized that pirates still exist and are looting Madagascar.
They are the foreigners, multinationals, corrupt governments.
They no longer use galleons, sabres and cannons, but much more sophisticated and devastating systems that have catchy names like “Land Grabbing” and “Biodiversity Offsetting”.
Land grabbing mans, literally, the hoarding of the land.
It’s the acquisition of land by private individuals or states, usually for monoculture, in order to meet your country's food needs or produce bio fuels.
Often these acquisitions do not consider the sovereignty and rights of those who live there.
Biodiversity Offsetting is a biodiversity compensation.
Basically, a multinational company can plunder and devastate an entire ecosystem after promising to recreate a similar one in a different place.
It’s like devastating an entire area with the promise to reforest somewhere else.
Both practices are allowed by the World Bank and the European Community.
The result is that Madagascar is one of the world's five poorest countries.
90% of its forests have disappeared.
Half of the population is undernourished and has no access to drinking water.
Fundamental rights are constantly violated.
I immediately got how dramatic the situation in Madagascar was and I dropped the idea of the football school.
Together with the association, we created a water network that piped drinking water to three villages, about 1000 residents.
But I have seen many projects like that: wells, hospitals, schools and markets. A good part of them turned out to be real "cathedrals in the desert", with the loss of millions and millions of Euros.
Some projects did not always meet the real beneficiaries’ needs, because they were planned and then dropped from above.
I could came back to Italy to tell all the story, to report everything. I knew that I wouldn’t do anything good, just the ones that came before me.
I had to stay. I really wanted to stay,
but I didn’t know what to do.
Then it dawned on me, in order to create something alternative and durable, I had to learn their mothertongue and find someone who could really knew this country,
In other words, I needed a guide, and I did find one.
Randriamarofara Jean Delphin,
a fantastic man.
One of 11 brothers, raised on a peasant family, he spent most of his life abroad and he came back to his country to transmit and share both his knowledge and his experience.
I still remember the first meeting: we were sitting face to face and this white and red fabric on his shoulders, with the image of Madagascar.
He put a document on the table, it was a thick book.
And he said to me, this is the "Wanna" project. "Vanuna" means both “integrity” and “responsible man”.
I wrote it, and I’ll make it real whether you help me or not.
It was a long conversation, and he closed it with a warning: if you really want to do something for Madagascar you must be ready to die for this country.
Once I came back to Italy, I shared it with my father and he also warned me: ”Watch out Massimiliano, this is the plan of a new state - in other words, a revolution."
On March 17, 2009, a coup took place.
I was there those months,
and I directly witnessed violence, riots, reprisals, demonstrations, deaths.
The triggering reason was the agreement between their president and a South Korean corporation that provided for the granting of 1.3 millions of hectares of arable land to produce wheat and palm oil.
In practice, more than half of Madagascar arable land.
It was an example of Land Grabbing.
That agreement failed, but many others didn’t.
In other words, new governments, still bad old habits.
It's important to note that in Madagascar land is inherited from ancestors.
It passes down, generation after generation. So it’s difficult to find any official register,
and land grabbers meet no formal obstacles. Once they have signed the agreement with the government, the population will only have two possibilities: you can either leave, or stay and work in your own land as an underpaid employee or even in slavery.
Actually, you can also rebel.
But there's a high chance to die.
So what can communities do in order to protect their own lands, rights and houses ?
A solution might be microcredit.
Me and Delphin, we took paper and pen and repackaged agricultural micro-credit.
I basically found myself in Madagascar, working as a banker again.
But there is a difference: we removed all the money.
We don’t borrow money, we borrow a bag full of seeds.
We give it to Razeeta, who grows them and at the time of harvest he gets three bags of rice.
He uses the first bag to feed his family.
The second bag goes in a community bard, and then he sells the third one.
He uses the proceeds to buy the things he need the most and two more bags of seeds.
He uses one of them for a second yearly harvest, and he gives the second one back.
We give this one to Dada.
Another family, another cycle.
An unstoppable multiplying effect is going to happen.
Microcredit is supported by community fields, in order to grant access to land to people who can’t afford it.
As I told you before, community barns support microcredit.
This is a powerful financial tool.
Its strength is simplicity.
I dare you to find an investment which both ensures you equities and doubles or even triples their value in 8 months.
There aren't.
The barn is real.
In April's harvest, 100 kilos of rice are sold for 35 euro.
In December's rainy season, the value doubles, sometimes triples.
Year after year.
These actions guarantee these families better life conditions, children’s education and healthcare, forest’s protection and development
and also food sovereignty.
We still talk about food safety as a solution both to fight against world hunger
and to ensure enough food to everyone.
But at what cost? Land grabbing, maybe?
Now it’s time to talk about food sovereignty.
Razama must be free to farm his own land in harmony with nature.
He as to be able both to eat quality food and to pass his knowledge down to his descendants.
That would be food sovereignity.
Microcredit is only one of Delphin Project’s actions.
That’s a real silent, non violent revolution.
When I accepted the risks I was taking in joining the project, I thought I was sharing a utopia
but the results came and the dream became reality.
Women in Madagascar have very few rights before being grandmothers. Olga is 42 years old. She is the president of a 1500 people movement.
11 of the 20 members of the General Assembly are kids I've been working with with for 10 years.
I know them since they were babies.
Here are some of their names: Dago, Justin, Tain, Fatim, Michelle.
They have gained a consciousness about their rights that no one could ever take away from them.
The Vanuna association, together with its educational and training centre, have already finished their first autonomous sustainable year.
In the field of associations
there are lots of examples that deserve both to be supported and enhanced.
It’s important for us both to make the right choice and get good information to choose correctly our battles. Our lifestyle is more important too.
Climate changing and inequities are constantly under our eyes.
We're running out of time, we can't ignore them anymore.
All together we must lead a change that could save our planet.
And we must do it now, or it'll be too late.
I could spend hours making a list of all the actions which could either preserve the planet or defend our rights.
Unfortunately, my time is over also.
I’m telling you the most important thing.
I invite you, I urge you to start making the right questions.
You must also only pick the real answers, steering away from the convenient ones.
Are bicycles and trains less polluting than cars and airplanes?
Obviously yes.
By entering a beautiful building, filled with lights and showcases, and there's a dress I can buy for just 9.99 euro, Is it cheap because of women and children exploitation?
Yes, it is, and the answer is on the label.
You are likely to know a certain product’s supply chain.
You are likely to know where, who and when a certain cosmetic, toothpaste, phone, or dress has been made.
Everytime they offer to come to Africa and help, I suggest to stay in Italy.
The more conscious and responsible we are, the more useful we can be. Day after day.
One year ago I understood that my mission in Madagascar was accomplished, it was finished.
I didn’t need to work there anymore.
They didn’t need me anymore, they now have both their life and their autonomy.
And that's ok.
I considered doing something else,
Sometimes it happens in life.
I thought myself: “It’s now time to think both about myself and my family, I need a decent salary.
”But then I decided to go on.
So much work to be done.
I want a better future for my children, who are here.
So I decided to go on.
I thought I would replicate everything I did in Madagascar, in Senegal too.
I'll talk about this in another TEDx speech.
Last February I was in the Fianarantsoa Educational Centre’s balcony, I was watching the children playing in the backyard.
At one point Olga came to me, and she said: “You know Massimo, I’m more then 40 years old, and I’d really like to become a mother.
I want it with my body and soul.
It always will be an unfulfilled desire, thought.
You know, If I had a baby I'd give him all my love and all the attentions he needs; I would raise him the best I could.”
Then she sighed a bit and she said, as she was watching tenderly that backyard: “If I had a baby, who would raise all these other children?"
Delphin and Olga represent both integrity and strength.
I'm inspired by this kind of people.
Thanks.
(Applause)
Hello!
The topic I chose for today is a topic that I keep on chasing, without ever being able to capture. Not just in my life, but also in my researches, in my writing: happiness.
But I chose this awe-inspiring subject first of all because what makes this place so precious is not just the miracle of these magnificent ruins, but that life itself was embedded in these ruins, life that suddenly stopped.
So the whole world -not just archaeologists - discovered not just what was left of civilization, like ancient coins, found in an abandoned field, that had already run their course.

No. Here, everything stopped suddenly,
and so the true fascination was finding life.
Life: a theatre that was full a minute before, a home, still inhabited.
The power or Pompeii and Herculaneum is this: it's the exact opposite of what is simple: the opposite of death, tragedy, disaster. But what succeeds after all is life.
And so now I thought it was necessary to go back and explore. To talk about the impulse that's bringing thousands and thousands of people to pursue happiness.
Because what the media are risking here is to make serious mistakes. Reading the news can be risky. We read about the death toll, about the different political views, about the reflections on how to organize the borders, about who has the right to decide, about what we can do to stem the flows. But the only reflection that is connected to the deepest reason that drives people to risk their lives, their children's lives, is the pursuit of happiness. (Applause)
And it's so embarrassing to say it, that we don't say it. Imagine what saying it would mean.
I'm used to being a target to being criticized. So if you're trained for that...
Imagine how embarrassing it could be for someone who's aware of it who's is involved in it, who takes part in a talk show, or a meeting, a political meeting in a town hall and says "These people are just pursuing happiness..." He'd be considered sentimental, a do-gooder, naive -in the best case-.
But there's no need to be embarassed in front of reality. Try listen to the interviews: "... I'd rather die than stay in my own country..." The possibility of imagining another life. Well, no one knows that better than the Italians of the south. What it means to change your existence, to have to look for another path, elsewhere.
And so this world speaks to us, mainly to us.
And reflecting on happiness means reflecting right now, on the most dangerous topic.
I repeat: we don't talk about it.
What does happiness take?
What does unhappiness take?
Now let it be clear: I understand that happiness is a private issue.
If I were asked "Should the state worry about happiness?"
No.
"Does happiness involve the society, the people, our own individuality, the ability to relate to others?"
Yes, of course.
But there's a variation of happiness which is social, public.
Melancholy, what we call depression, that often seizes us, seizes me, doesn't only have a private meaning. But it's often the judgment that the body or a part of our body gives to the society... that is unbearable.
And so there's a subversive possibility of happiness.
In my life, I've not always been good at getting along with happiness at finding it. Actually, I've always felt embarrassed each time I recognized a road that could have made me happy. I felt guilty, I've always felt guilty. As if I didn't deserve it.
Then suddenly your life changes, as happens to so many, and you start to have another perspective a perspective that is helping me, even now, to resist to the foolishness of the Lega (Italy's Northern Political Party) to resist and to try to understand why these opinions flood on social media in such great numbers, why these ideas are uniting people under the same position. (Applause)
I was asking: what is this thing?
Which in reality - and the power of words can help here - in fact you understand the value of something only in the moment you've lost it.
I believe It's the fate shared by humans, and literature is able to help. Sometimes literature allows the miracle of valuing, giving weight to a thing - I define thing: a person, a feeling, a meeting, a moment - before you've lost it, maybe even before you come across it.
So when I lost my freedom nine years ago I began to understand its worth, its quality.
It's always like this.
I was told by those people who had been through worse things. Arrested intellectuals dissidents, people imprisoned in the Middle East, or in Africa, so compared to them I was fortunate.
They used to say: "That's the way it is!"
No one who hasn't spent at least one day in jail understands jail. No one who hasn't been ill can understands someone who is ill. You can be empathetic. In the same way, if you haven't lost your freedom you can't understand what it means.
I don't care if I'm considered naive or romantic, I want to consider these flows of people -and not many of us are doing the same- as an incredible flow of desire that's coming to us.
Desire, will to succeed, to put yourself on the line.
I see this before everything.
Then there's another logic, a very simple one the logic that the fishermen of Lampedusa have taught us: we need to split this question into two. First, we could welcome them all, we could go to Libya, we need to understand what to do, Europe has to intervene... These debates are all necessary, but are on another level, On another level from the maritime law.
The maritime law is that: no one can be left at sea. And that law is the only one we must obey when we're facing this problem.
After that, every debate is legitimate but the maritime law is the only one we should listen to in this situation.
The south is undergoing a process of desertification. These words are used by the renowned Swiss research centers Desertification.
Under different circumstances these words would have been an immense backlash, but they seem normal now.
The whole problem of the South comes from the fact that we count for less in the national political balance, because we know that the alternative, is either enduring or leaving, either trying to make it at all costs or leaving. So we emigrate, at that point it's like if the class -the ruling class - but not only, everyone in the South knows that -sooner or later- that's the only way. It's like solving the problem on our own. What's the result? It doesn't allows us to see migrants as a great resource for the South. Immeasurable. We already have cities that have become African.
Castel Volturno is the biggest
African city in Europe. With all its problems: there is an enormous presence of Nigerian mafia, but there is also a healthy community which was able to built its own identity. Africans of second and third generation are born in Castel Volturno, new Castellani. I mean, we should take pride in this. An African mayor, even if there, politically things are getting better, but I mean all this should be a main point for debate. Not simply numbers, details and what they're making us believe to be the important topic. If we're cold, cynic, if we don't say the word' happiness', then we are authoritative. On the contrary, if we describe the feeling that makes a woman
-as we heard earlier - take her one-year-old child and hold him across the Mediterranean. Well, if we talk about this, we're clearly inefficient. We're not facing reality, We want easy consensus, because we're calling for feelings. And that's right what I want to do. The is courage we should all have. (Applause) It's easy to attack a feeling, and make fun of it, it's very easy. Cruelty, distance, they appear stronger, sometimes even more fascinating, don't they?
If you try to be good, you're a 'do-gooder'
There's a great book, called 'Life and fate', by Vasilij Grossman, a massive book, read it if you have some time to dedicate to it. It's a masterpiece, a wonderful writer, who deals with the tragedy of the totalitarianism of communism and national-socialism.
There's a passage that I've never forgotten, that I often repeat. There's this character, the protagonist, who says: "I've never believed neither in justice, nor in good. Because in the name of justice, in name of your own good, the good of humanity, and for justice the worst crimes were committed." Crimes that the main character, was witnessing and experiencing
And he says "I've always believed in goodness" And that echoes in that incredible page.
That word is not predictable, it's not a subterfuge, it's not psychologically fragile, not sly...
It's powerful... and he explains further, "Goodness is a one to one relation because we can value it in the immediate, because I can feel the instant transformation of reality, of my life, everyday with that gesture."
It may seem incredible, but in a moment like this we are just talking now, but later this video, your sensations, your comments will get everywhere. Think that now more than ever words are being hold, oppressed, blocked. And it's unbelievable that it's happening now when words managed to free themselves from the chains of typography, of editing, of censorship. Right now, in these days words are back to being at the centre of persecution. A tweet in Mexico can kill someone.
A Tweet, literally. Which means people, journalists, bloggers, who tweet. Tweets that work, that start to be popular, commented. They get killed for this.
Or intellectuals who are whipped, as in Saudi Arabia, killed, as it happened in Paris. What power did they have?
What power did the blogger Felina have? When the Zeta, one of the richest groups of Mexico which fills Miami and New York with cocaine, decapitate a blogger who was on twitter with not more than a thousands of followers. What was wrong with her?
What could she do? To such a powerful group. Powerful enough to intercept her, with a hacking operation, -because she used a nickname- go to her house, behead her, take a picture of her body and post it on her account. How is that even possibile?
What could cartoonists do, those like Charlie Hebdo? Who was he compared to the power of the Islamic propaganda?
But that's exactly the point.
Malala I'm pleased to see that there's a trace of her journey to maturity. But we're talking about a girl against the greatest heroine traffickers of the world. The Taliban.
You know that 90%
of the world's heroine comes from Afghanistan. From Moscow, through Naples, to Paris, Montpellier. Everything comes from Afghanistan. Apart from a small percentage that comes from Vietnam. So the Talibans, and the guerilla, their government and their laws are riddled with heroine money Think about their moralism A fourteen-year-old girl becomes their number one target.
And then you think: it's possible then.
Yes it is.
Because a word, a position, a choice, sharing all lead to that insurgency. I use this word. To erase what doesn't work.
The insurgency, I like to think of that as happiness, I quoted that blogger and Malala on purpose because in their writings and in their choices there was happiness.
She wrote, for example, that in some places in the city, Mexico City, border cities, drug traffickers were transforming the places into sad places. They were buying kindergartens for rich people's children. They were transforming gyms into beauty salons.
She was speaking about an unhappiness that was conquering the land and she wrote to defend her happiness.
Malala wanted to go to school because it was good. She was defending her happiness. The hypothesis of a possible happiness. Just the hypothesis.
And these men are scared to death, -we're talking of two different structures here- the one religious, the other interested in profits the other interested in profits, and in spreading its own belief.
So, what I want to say is that suddenly we get to realize that happiness is a bomb. It's contagious. Disciplined, capable to get into our minds, to talk, better than anything else.
Now, I want to conclude with two images.
I found the first one at Murakami. It pictures happiness as an allegory, and unhappiness as history. It says In the end happiness is an allegory, whereas unhappiness is history.
I don't agree with that.
I believe that the goal is this: to make happiness become history, a history that can be told. The flows we're witnessing must be told like this, without the fear of saying this word out loud, without the fear of narrating personal stories, feelings, personal choices. Without the fear of compromising our own self-determination.
There's a story I like to tell about a book by Warlam Schalamow, who I consider to be my real teacher so I try to imitate him, learn from what he did.
He spent 20 years in Soviet gulags, because he wrote two literary articles against the regime. Think about the price he paid. He wrote a masterpiece called "The Kolyma Tales". I'm glad because when I was on TV, I managed to make a lot of people reading it. So some of you already know the story. But there's a story I want to tell you and these wonderful stones, that are watching me, and embarrassing me.
There's an inspection in the barrack, they were cutting wood at - 30 degrees and the inspection is simple. "Hand in your prosthesis."
The old man removes his glass eye, the other one a wooden leg from the battlefield. Someone hands in the glasses. Someone a cane. And Shalamow, a young guy, six feet tall, strong, not beaten by the cold. He stands still. Seeing him like that the soldier asks "What do you have to give?"
"Nothing"
To make a joke the soldier says "You're giving me your soul!"
Shalamow doesn't take the joke. "No, I won't give you my soul". The soldier, bothered by the answer, says "A week in the hole!" which was basically a death sentence it meant spending a week inside a well, with next to nothing to eat without cover, just with a coat, "If you don't give me your soul..." "No, I won't."
"Two weeks in the well if you don't give me your soul."
He restates, "I won't give you my soul."
"Three weeks if you don't give me
your soul."
-he survived them-
He describes in the book how his companions were staring at him, as to say "You're risking your life for nothing"
And he wrote: In the very moment someone asked for my soul I realized that I had one. Me. I was a materialist, I didn't even believe I had one I realized that it was the most precious thing that I had."
That's it.
Thank you.
Something symbolic happened some time ago, in my opinion.
A prominent thread manufacturer, operating all over the world, asked its target group, mainly women between 25 and 40 years of age, where did they learn to knit.
In 60 percent of the cases, the answer was: YouTube.
YouTube was the grandmother of our generation.
All over the world, young and old people, got back in touch with materials and tools
in a concrete way - i.e. making - or just with their fascination, through photos and videos produced and posted all over the world and spread on social networks.
We tend to imitate what we see.
This general rediscovery of hands-on practice has had several causes.
The economic and work crisis of course; but also a certain alienation, as clients and designers, due to a too standardized offer, which is a natural result of productive delocalization.
All this together with a rediscovery of a whole set of values: ecological, social and ethical values.
It wasn't just a grassroot movement.
During the same period, manufacturing has left the companies where it had been locked up in recent years and made mechanical and mute - and moving away, in the process, from the daily life of ordinary people - to return back home now with new meanings, new technologies and new products.
Today, paradoxically, we see companies open to the public like secret palaces
of the Italian Environment Fund days
(only in Lombardy and Piedmont, currently; but soon hopefully all over Italy) and raising during those days the same kind of interest and fascination.
Workshops of any kind of artisan technology and cocktail parties where people talk about sewing, silk-screen, sourdough:
making stuff has become part of our daily life again.
But the digital world is not only a mentor; it is, above all, a sales showcase, we know it well: a sales showcase that new brands and more structured companies have learned, and are still learning, to get more personal, more tailored, in fact, by restarting a whole new supply chain.
The ability to use digital tools related to communication, getting to be seen, drawing people's atgtention by posting photos that are emotional, emotive, engaging while being aware at the same time about the right words to use, the timing and secrets of indexing has become more and more important,
and in the meantime training opportunities on the subject have multiplied at all levels: from online courses to professional workshops, to specialized consultants, born precisely from this necessity.
Of course, the combination of digital communication and online sales has had some very important repercussions.
First of all, the reaching of new targets and widespread niches.
Paradoxically, I used to be able to reach all the customers in my territory or where I had shops or a commercial network in a given region.
Now, potentially, I could reach anyone interested in my product, from Toronto to Pescara.
Second factor: involvement and feedback.
The fact that I have a direct contact with my audience through social networks exposure, allows me to see the real effect that my product has on my client.
Which means: solve problems, accept requests for customization and obtain greater loyalty and personal satisfaction in return.
This is the era of involvement, of direct dialogue with the public.
So what's the problem?
Sure we have to be seen: in order to get noticed, we have to show up.
The image has become the center of our world.
A good image makes us choose, connect with the brand, confirms our first impression and strengthens our confidence about a purchase.
But we have very little time to do so. A fraction of a second, the time it takes for the index finger to flow from top to bottom in our Instagram and Facebook feeds.
Our potential customer lingers, observes quality and image, product availability, cost, and then, perhaps, decides to follow us and perhaps decides to buy.
In order to break through such a tight window, we are headed towards a type of easy communication: easy images, immediately recognizable, didactic, already familiar, almost pre-digested.
There is no time for a next level of understanding, you have to strike and do it now.
A communication with an expiry date, that is already expired when it's posted.
Soon enough, we will remember this period as full of anxiety for the beautiful photo: how many times have we been asked
for a beautiful photo?
Shots from above, with white backgrounds, the typical storytelling photos with those wrinkled craftsmen hands working on wooden tables, but now with hipster beards; a logo that "works" because it's already an avatar, the sharing anxiety.
But in this race for communication, and here I come to the point, how much of our time do we dedicate to design and improve our object, what we are putting on the market?
In comparison, very very little.
Because we are rushing to post.
The result is soulless, "stamped" products, with wonderful and refined logos and photos, but all looking the same.
Or self-referential products, with non-existent targets, but posted with perfect hashtags and at the perfect time.
Here you see "cute" products: but is cute enough?
Is it really enough?
The attention for the image is fundamental nowadays, otherwise the public will be indifferent.
But the product, its ability to speak to the public that we want to reach through interesting and significant details, the strong idea behind it, is even more important.
We cannot rush it out of the anxiety for sharing and posting a new photo.
If there is no product, there's no point for communication; and sooner or later, its real worth will come out.
I have spent the last ten years observing, writing, connecting, making the world of new craftsmanship and entrepreneurship grow.
From small self-producers to subcontractors in production districts, the path has been similar: such a strong digital frenzy that we lost sight of the goal we had at the beginning, getting our product to be known and seriously appreciated.
The prototype is such precisely because it can be improved.
We no longer take time to try and change things.
We constantly have this eagerness of sharing and it's fooling us, sorry about that, for the quality of what we are doing.
The iconic "Made in Italy" products, those that are part of our history, from which we are worldwide recognized, whether it be fashion, design, food - were borncame out from a cooperation.
A cooperation between designers, creatives, planners and a hugely skilled and tasteful workforce that improved every prototype and sample.
This method is at risk of wreaking completely havoc due to an excessive attention to the image rather than the product.
In the meantime, we create a distance with our audience again.
This kind of distance, given by a perfect and refined image that we have aimed at in this period, on the one hand reassures us because it is highly perceived, on the other hand it makes us lose sight of that direct dialogue and that small imperfection that in the last period had made us authentic again.
The difference.
We can no longer focus on what unites us, but on what distinguishes us.
Let's do it as creatives, as craftsmen, as individuals.
Let's start again to design, to prototype, to improve.
Let's channel our creativity and perfectionism, not only on communication but also on the product, because that's what remains.
You have to be your own moodboard, your own reference moodboard.
You have to be aware, not Instagramable.
There are a lot products without a soul.
Wonderful photos, apps that try to show us all the beauty of changing the color and background.
Picture after picture of bleached wood backgrounds, lately pastel colours.
It's impossible nowadays not to consider these details, because if I only have a one second window, I have to stand out.
But why give all this importance only to the picture, and not to what we have been famous all over the world for?
The contact with materials, tools, the possibility to change your mind.
Because sometimes, in order not to disturb an image that's been painstakingly studied and refined, we are afraid to change our mind, to evolve towards something else, even if it might be the best thing to do at that moment.
We can no longer be prisoners of the image.
This perfect image is actually drawing us back.
It is important, once again, to give the same importance to what we were doing before.
Shut down the Internet, shut down Instagram: get this advice from someone who's always on Instagram!
The design process should be told at the end, when we are convinced.
It's not to be shared step by step in the process.
Let me give you an example.
Consider the artists who have cooperated all these years long, from the '60s onwards, to create the best design products that made us famous all over the world, What if they had stopped at the likes on the picture of the first product, do you think they would have continued to improve the products, and would we have now the quality products that we have?
Hide your prototypes, hide your tentatives.
You must be convinced first.
Narrating new products, showing trials, our first samples certainly gives charm to your feed and engages the followers, but at the same time it traps us.
Don't share the tentatives, don't share the design process.
I know, it's the opposite of the current mantra, and it's less fascinating.
But in the meantime, we obtain a fake reassurance about products that in the end are just first sketches.
And the history of the Made in Italy is not made up of first sketches.
So be your own moodboard, be aware of whay you're doing, and don't be Instagrammable.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good morning everyone!
I'm here to tell you a story, a story that began long ago, when I was working in a field, in a "colorful" reality, where innovation and creativity were not only always present, but were necessary for the survival of the company, in a world where hierarchy did not matter that much, especially when it came to bringing new ideas or solving problems.
Everyone, but really everyone could do their part and contribute.
A world in which diversity was considered a great value, where the keywords were idea, autonomy, sharing, but above all team, where you felt, I felt part of a group that worked every day, and fought together for a goal that was almost always clear and shared.
Then, one day, everything changed.
I went to work in manufacturing companies in the production world, in the factory.
And there, unfortunately, the colours disappeared.
I found a different world, a world where, rather than feeling part of a team, you often felt, too often, like Don Quixote fighting against the windmills.
Fighting alone for a goal, often not a clear one, mostly not shared by the rest of the team.
A world where the keywords were: control, hierarchy, repetitiveness, where the team almost did not exist, where the individual contribution was always very limited, a world very close to a "tailoristic" vision of the company.
The company is a machine and the workers, the famous resources, are the gears and, therefore, as such always interchangeable and replaceable short term.
A world where the manager, the boss is there simply to plan, command and control.
A world that still considers commanding and rigidly controlling the best way to run a company and also to bring the results.
And here the question arises spontaneously.
I have asked myself for a long time, frankly, "What's wrong with that?" There are a lot of companies, most of the companies which are structured in this way
are still here!
Maybe that's okay?
Maybe this is the correct way?
There are indeed many examples in which this management works: for example, think about the army.
If we look at the army, the quintessence of the kingdom of hierarchy, control, command, discipline.
There is a person who commands, a person who orders, and all the others perform.
And does it work?
It works perfectly, right?

Or not?
It does not work.
It does not work in the army and it does not work in the company, we all know it, we all see it, in what a crisis all companies find themselves today.
By chance, I found this book in my hands.
A book about armies and battles, that has dismantled, challenged, one after the other, almost all of my beliefs about the military world, including the myth of command and control,
talking about approaches used long before Taylor, even in the nineteenth century by the Prussian army.
To my surprise, I discovered they have been adopted also today, for example, by the US Army.
This book is a continuous parallelism between the army and the company, between the uncertainty of the battle and the mutability of the market, the uncertainty of the working world.
One of the identified reasons, one of the identified key points as a possible reason for failure in the battle, rather than in company results, is the gap, the discrepancy existing between plans, actions and results.
The leaders make the plans, but then people act in a way which is not the expected one by leaders. That's not because people fail to match expectations, or do not have the skills, but because what's obvious to someone is not necessarily obvious to the other and vice versa. Therefore the results are very different from those that the leaders had wanted.
This happened in the Prussian army but strangely it happens also in our companies.
Why in companies?
Why is there this discrepancy?
Because usually the manager in the company, rather than the general in the army, tends immediately to explain what and especially how, in detail, starting making plans and expecting they magically get transformed into perfectly executed actions.
But, in this recipe of what and how, there is a missing key ingredient: why, the why of the thing.
In reality, people even before hearing the details of what and how, would need an answer to a really simple question, but a really very simple question, "But tell me what you want, what you really, really want."
Because what happens too often is that we start specifying the smallest details and elements, planning and defining very detailed plans, but a lot of things are just assumed, leading to misunderstandings, disagreements and losing sight of the main goal.
The really "what", the "really, really".
How can we answer this very simple question?
Separating clearly the "what" and "why" from the "how."
Rather than the "what" and "how", the leader, the manager, must take care of "what" and "why" and leave the "how" to the troops.
So, the general or the manager must make sure to explain the "what", but not in the smallest details, giving the overall goal, the aim of the "what", making sure that the "why" of the request is really understood.
We must conquer that hill, why?
Because that's where supplies go through.
So on one side "what" and "why", on the other side leaving full autonomy on "how" to the troops.
Because it is right to plan around a table, and to study previously the best way to win the battle, rather than to get a result in the company, but in reality, in the true reality, it's on the field, in real time, that people can make the best decisions because it's only then and there you can decide the most correct action for the changing context, the famous uncertainty of the battle.
And it is only there that you have all the information you may need that you did not have before, at the table.
That's the theory.
A theory that, among others, in practice worked very well in the Prussian army.
A theory that convinced me to change.
Wow, if the change succeeded in the army, we could succeed as well in the company.
Changing.
So, fear, terror!
Because change frightens everybody, because change is difficult, because change puts you in crisis, and takes you away from your comfort zone.
And then I, as a good mathematician, mentally insane, I took all the statistics that have really reassured and encouraged me affirming that most of the initiatives to change have failed, or are doomed to fail.
So, think!
(Laughter) I started thinking, studying, analyzing, planning, convinced that I must be alone, or almost alone to solve the problem locked in my ivory tower, in my office, to plan the future and change.
Fallen in the middle of the trap of the detailed planning, previously, a table of "how".
That "how" that I should have let in autonomy to people, to the team.
I remembered a sentence that the general von Moltke repeated often to the troops, "No plan survives in contact with the enemy. " So maybe rather than the plan, the thermonuclear mega-plan, it would have been better to try a step-by-step approach, in small steps, choosing short-term targets, limited in time, simple to understand, easy to verify; small steps, and verification, and according to the feedback, adjust and decide the next steps.
But all this could not be done leaving people only to common sense, but everything had to be supported by a sound methodology, however a methodology embracing these principles: the dynamic methodology was the perfect answer.
A methodology born in the '90s but formalized in the manifesto in 2001, born in the software, but nowadays already used in production and service companies, that among other things is based on an adaptive empirical approach, that is to say small steps, short iterations, continuous improvement, a loop of improvement, verification, adaptation, decision on the "how" in the field.
It was exactly what we needed.
So we started, first of all, looking for the context of the company without talking about dynamic methodology, or about detail implementation, which always frightens people, but concentrating, focusing on the "why" of this change, on concrete results for the company, without talking about revolution because this frightens.
The revolution always leaves casualties behind.
It's no use to think to throw everything away.
The approach in small steps is an evolution and it is much easier to be accepted.
Then we have worked on the team, because it was necessary to create a climate of trust.
You cannot think to give autonomy on how without trusting each other.
We tried to figure it out together, to be aware of the why, the what, then to figure out together the business strategy and goals, why we got certain requests, and our role, how we should have interconnected with the rest of the company.
The procedures have been reviewed and revised, where necessary, by the same people who should have used them, by the troops, and we have begun to put them into practice.
As part of the small steps, we have started formal check moments also these are simple techniques of dynamic methodology, then specific procedures in view of a continuous adaptation.
Sharing, maximum transparency.
We have put around the office, in visible places of passage, boards with post-it to make visible to all the activities and schedules, the project status, delays, problems, without hiding anything.
We have made a 360° change.
In conclusion, we are at the end of a great fairy tale? I've found again my rainbow of colors?
No, or better, not yet.
The first concrete results, however, for the company, we have seen them. we have already shared them, we have won the first battles and in all this people are definitely more aware of their role, they feel important in achieving a milestone in the company and are definitely more motivated.
More motivated people work better and the results can be seen.
Let's say that we feel a bit less often like Don Quixote lost in the fog.
We know that the road is still long, in small steps, continuous adaptations in a hostile, difficult environment.
The path is not and cannot be straight, due to all I've tried to tell you.
But I have found again a little color,
we have found it again. And the good thing in all this is that I've got confirmation that people, in any environment, in any field of work, can always surprise us for the better.
With new ideas, with different solutions, but always, most of the time, just what we needed.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I don't know you, but I believe in coincidences.
I think what happens to us in life is all but casual; let me explain.
1981: I graduate in Law, but I don't want to become a lawyer.
I enter into the commercial department of a big company: I'll deal with sales.
But after 14 years, the company decides to cut the whole sales branch. Suddenly, I find myself with a team of 20 and no product to sell.
No fear, I immediately find another chance: now I'll deal with corporate art and design items.
But in life, as well as in business, surprises are always around the corner.
And less than two years after, my partner decides to manage sales directly.
So what does he do?
He doesn't renew the contract, and my best agent follows him.
Once again, I'm left with no products, and 19 people waiting for my decisions.
Well, I can tell you, if it never occurred to you, that's enough for someone to panic.
But the way I'm made, one day is more than enough to weep and moan.
I think, that's where one's true character shows up. I'll tell you where I think my stamina comes from.
I'm the third daughter of a General of the Italian Army, and I grew up with the motto, "Do your duty and shut up!"
On top of this, I was an agonistic swimmer from 11 to 16 years old up to 20 km a day, every day, including on Sundays, because there were competitions.
Sometimes you win, sometimes you lose. But when you lose, you never, even for a moment, consider quitting. Indeed, Mondays you're there, in the swimming pool, stronger and more determined than ever.
One day I said to myself,
"Well, Susanna, it's the second time someone steals your product and you're left with no job.
And at that moment, something clicked: I have to make those products myself.
Except, almost any promotional item is made in China.
I really have to make something one-of-its-kind, unlike anything else.
I want no competition.
It has to be the opposite of the same old gadget.
- But what, exactly?
- And here's the case.
I'm in a bar. A person I barely know just gave me a notebook.
Nothing special, but I open it and I read, "No tree was cut to produce this notebook."
A light, a flash stroke me: a seemingly deep hidden memory I didn't even remember to have.
1982: I'm on a train compartment.
By my side, two university professors are talking, and I'm listening to them, "We're all sitting on a huge garbage pile. It's a huge problem for the future generations and for the environment, and we absolutely have to start thinking to do something.
Certainly, waste disposal business will be a thriving one, tomorrow."
Gosh, at that moment, these people were talking about the future.
I was 23, but they were also talking about me.
Well, 16 years later, the dots magically connected.
The notebook, the train - I found my way!
I will produce design, "Made in Italy" items, but they'll only have to be produced with recycled or reused materials.
Attached to them, there will be a story I can tell through the materials they're made of.
I will brand them "Communicating Objects."
But you know, we were in the late 90s. Aside from recycled paper and recycled leather, there wasn't much to be found on the market.
So what will I do?
Well, I try to ask my customer, "What do you usually throw away? What's your waste?"
And I'll try to make unique objects for them.
Well, over the years, we really made a lot of communicating objects.
I brought some of them here today.
I visited a customer, it's a tomato canning company. As usual, I ask, "What's your waste?" And they answer, "Dry tomato skins."
Gosh, how will I give a second life to a pile of tomato dry skins?
Well, I know Lorenzo, a designer
running an artisanal business and making beautiful wax items.
"Lorenzo, what about putting my dry tomato skin inside your wax objects?"
"Look Lorenzo, tomato dry skin is so beautiful, your objects will look even greater, you will see."
And between bee wax and dry tomato skin, flower pots are born, tabletops, and candles.
Now, instead, I'm visiting a marketing and communication manager who works for a dealer of a big car brand.
She has to prepare some gadgets, and she likes my idea to use recycled and reused materials.
She has a shoestring budget.
On the other hand, she talks about 100,000 pieces.
I ask the same question: what are your waste product?
She said, "Components of decommissioned cars."
Colored car lights immediately come to my mind: red, orange. And so I think, let's make a pen. She likes the idea.
The car light recycled material is purchased using the customer's indications.
But then, how can you make a pen?
A mold for a pen's body, back then, cost 70,000 euros.
Quick math: 100k pieces, €70k: 70 cents just for the mold,
while 18 cents buy a finished Chinese pen.
That's unfeasible, nobody would buy them.
But we were in the early 2000s. Italian manufacturers are complaining, decimated as they are by Chinese competition.
I wonder: how many underused molds can there be in our territory?
The research brings me to Gigi.
Gigi is a pen manufacturer, and he has a mold.
I go to Gigi and say, "Gigi, I give you the plastic, you put the mold, and together we'll make a pen."
And combining my plastics with Gigi's mold, pens are made out of car lights.
Together with Gigi, I re-evolution his pen.
Well guys, I love this business so much.
So let's recapitulate --
(Applause) Let's recapitulate: I can recuperate unused molds among local businesses; I can slash down the cost of materials by taking advantage of recycled and reused materials; I help local craftsmanship; and I make unique objects for my customers.
Everyone is winning!
Of course, as you all can imagine, this hasn't been an easy path to take. But what pushed me to endure was the awareness I was doing a healthy business.
Plus, I wasn't polluting the land I lived on. Indeed, I was somewhat helping to remediate it.
So despite the hardships I went through, I love this business more and more every day.
Without even noticing it, I created a circular economy.
Let me explain what it is - not because I'm an economist but because they also explained it to me.
A circular economy is a self-regenerating one.
It's a system where all production lines are conceived to make sure that someone's waste is someone else's resource.
Well, I did it for 20 years: unconsciously, at the beginning; now, with more and more awareness.
For I have up cycled tomato's dry skins, namely I reused a waste material and through a creative process I managed to create another material.
What did I do with pens, instead?
I merely re-cycled them, following the supply chain of car lights' reuse and recycle which, through a new productive process, I used to make my pens.
But what am I actually doing at the end of the day?
With my objects, I'm extending a material's life; but eventually, this doesn't save them from the landfill, or they'll end up in the recycling supply chain.
After 20 years, I want to do something more.
But what, exactly?
And here's still the case.
In 2012, I got a phone call in the office.
Cristina saw my objects at an exhibition.
She loved the idea and wanted me to design her promotional items.
Cristina is the handy woman of Vittorio.
Vittorio has a company that manufactures graphite electrodes.
Of course, objects have to be made with their own waste.
And their waste is graphite powder.
Wait, waste? Graphite powder?
It's so beautiful to me.
I assure you graphite powder looks gorgeous. I can only see it as a prime material.
And hey, we all know that pencils are made of graphite. And pencils are quintessential promotional items.
Well, Vittorio used to dispose of 12 tons a year of "inevitable" waste graphite powder.
At a considerable cost for his company, by the way.
Do you know where does graphite powder usually end up?
In a landfill, directly underground.
It used to be a hopeless waste product.
Well, we absolutely have to come up with something.
I start looking for a "Pencil Gigi."
And little did I know
no one manufactures pencils in Italy any more.
I immediately made up my mind to become Italy's only pencil manufacturer.
And of course, my pencil-- (Applause) And of course, how is my pencil going to be?
Innovative, distinguishable from any other pencil; -it's made in Italy, for goodness sake!
It has to be perfect.
As you might have guessed by now, I'm a networker by nature.
I knew Andrea some years ago, we did some works together.
Andrea is an expert in productive processes, and he knows very well the molding materials.
Then Marta, an architect and a fantastic designer.
And working with them, after almost a year of research, "Perpetua la matita" was born.
(Applause) Perpetua is a real re-evolution because for the first time, we did something that didn't exist before: we might call it self-cycling!
Self-cycling consists in self-consuming the waste product. So, Perpetua users recycle and consume, by writing, 15 grams of graphite.
Do you know why are pencils made of wood?
They usually have two glued wooden shells in order to protect the graphite mine, which stains your hands, and it's very fragile.
Well, there's no wood in Perpetua's body: it's 80% made of Vittorio's graphite, and it doesn't stain your hands.
And what about the pencils with an eraser behind?
They have a tiny metal collar, whose job is to tie
the eraser to the body.
Well, Marta didn't like that collar.
A tragedy: my pencil will not have
that ugly collar, period. OK.
And I hated the glue, by the way, so we were on the same wavelength.
Poor Andrea's tests after tests.
Eventually, we managed to fuse the eraser directly to the graphite body. Graphite and eraser
glued in a single body.
And by the way, did you ever try to write with a pencil?
The mine gets broken and no sharpener is around?
Perpetua solves this problem for you because it writes even without a sharp mine. You can sharp it later on, if you feel to.
And then, when I get angry - my coworkers know that, they're in the last rows - I can take my Perpetua, throw it on the ground and pick it back up. Perpetua was made in such a way it doesn't break when it falls down.
Thank you!
(Applause)
Good afternoon.
Today I want to talk about a dream: the dream of caring about healthy people.
Of course, when I say caring, I don't mean therapy, quite the opposite.
But I mean it in the sense of Franco Battiato’s song: I take care of, I take care of you, I am interested in something.
"I care", we would say in English.
But in fact, if we think about it, when we talk about this topic, the institutional body that deals with the subject is called "Ministry of Health",
not "Ministry of Diseases".
But despite this, when we think about our health, we immediately imagine a pharmacy, the hospital, the surgical room, the clinic.
And we think of diseases.
And we think that the diseases are all different: cardiovascular diseases, tumors, neurodegenerative diseases.
We are in the situation where Ptolemy also was when he thought that the Earth was at the center of the Universe, and everything revolved around the Earth.
Then Copernicus came along, and persuaded us all, the opposite was true, and the Sun was at the center.
And so he did what we still call a "Copernican Revolution".
So, today we find ourselves in medicine with the need, with the desire, to make a Copernican revolution.
Instead of diseases, we want to reassess medicine around the person, the man.
But when we think of man, we have such a tradition that we immediately think of suffering, sick people.
Something like this hooded man at the Holy Thursday procession that takes place in Lanciano, Abruzzo.
And we imagine that this model of the sick man can give us useful informations for a whole series of other people who under the hood, as we see, all look the same, and so the model of the sick man looks relatable.
In reality, if we catch the hooded men in a moment when they don't wear the hood, we realize that they are all different from each other, and therefore this model is not good.
I therefore want to quote here Don Milani, who famously said: "Obedience is no longer a virtue".
Let me tell you, equality in medicine is no longer a virtue.
We are not all diabetics, we are not all hypertensive, we want to see things in a different way.
Of course, if we all were alike, like these umbrellas in Rimini, it would be something much simpler: but in reality, our situation is closer to what you see now, in this image.
But before this confusion, before this complexity of the human condition, what can we do?
Fifteen years ago, with my research group, based in Molise, we thought of changing the perspective a bit.
And we made a study that from the name itself, from the very title of the project, "Moli-sani", ["Sani" means healthy in Italian] clearly underlines and indicates that we are more interested in health than in disease.
And we are more interested in understanding what's before, what's at the origin of human well-being.
We drew lots, as Brett said this morning, we drew 30 municipalities, in Molise, and we asked the mayors the list of their residents.
And in each town we took a number of people, we drew a number of people in proportion of residents. And at the end, we took 25,000 people.
It was a figure that at first really impressed me.
And we thought: now let's see how this population is.
But we immediately realized that it was not worth knowing how many cancers there were, how many cardiovascular diseases.
We thought we'd go a little deeper, to the roots.
And we figured out, all the diseases that seem so different to us, in fact, if we dig underneath, they actually behave more like trees, that have common roots or at least that intersect a lot.
So our interest has shifted from leaves and branches to roots.
Let me give you some examples of what we found in this investigation.
One of the roots underneath those "trees" we have seen, is inflammation.
When one says inflammation, we imagine a very high fever, a pain in the knees that keeps us from walking. Arthritis, arthrosis and everything you can imagine.
But this is something that has happened already.
We do not want to do a medicine of “Guards and Thieves”, where one first waits for thieves to carry out a theft,
and then the guards chase them.
We currently do the same: we first wait for a disease to develop, the onset of inflammation. And then we run after it, trying to cure it.
Not very smart.
We'd rather see something more like this image.
We are not interested in the visible tip of the iceberg: sure, everyone sees it, traditional medicine can see it.
We want to go further down, we want to study and understand what is in the hidden part of the iceberg.
And this, I assure you, is a very fascinating undertaking.
A few examples: this inflammation - which is not the pain, the fever - can be measured by parameters that we have developed in our laboratories and we call it subclinical inflammation.
All of us here have subclinical inflammation; but none of us know, not even me.
But if we check for those parameters, in a sample population like “Moli-sani”, we realize that there is a diversity.
Nobody realizes this, but we have seen that as long as this subclinical inflammation increases, the very risk of dying also increases.
So we stroll around peacefully, carrying within us a subclinical inflammation that shapes, promotes what our risk of dying will be.
It's a really fascinating thing.
One might immediately say now, give me a drug to tame this inflammation.
No way: the drug comes later.
It's a staple component of that medicine we don't want to do.
What can we do to reduce this inflammation in our lives of healthy people?
So we were able to study and understand that eating in the Mediterranean way - it is commonplace here - sticking with the Mediterranean diet reduces this inflammation, that nobody notices; but not even us, when eating the Mediterranean diet: fruit, vegetables, olive oil, a little fish and so on - nobody notices that eating in such a way reduces the inflammation.
These are all phenomena that go under the iceberg: but they make an important difference in our current health status and what may be, tomorrow, the risk of getting sick or even dying.
So we wanted to verify: if the Mediterranean diet reduces this subclinical inflammation, what does it do in reality?
And as you can see, it reduces mortality from cardiovascular diseases, but also total mortality, for all possible causes.
Therefore, an interesting finding is that if one follows this type of nutrition they reduce something that we do not see, but also reduces something that we can experience such as mortality from today's most common diseases, which are cardiovascular diseases.
However, apparently, there isn't just the Mediterranean diet: we all know it is good not to smoke, it is good to walk, to do physical activity, to control our weight.
If we put together all these good habits, and then we go to measure, in the ten years of observation, that we are running on this population of “Moli-sani”, we see that the “Moli-sani” who do not follow these lifestyles have a mortality curve, the red one at the top, much higher, much more dangerous than those in the same environment - all else being comparable, of course: the variation, here, are only lifestyles - who follow a healthy lifestyle: their mortality curve is much lower.
So there is no need to go to hospital, no need to think about pharmacies, no need to think about drugs.
We can control our health, and also predict and prevent all the things we fear, in a way that gets much closer to health than sickness.
Then, after seeing these things, we were very happy and very satisfied.
Then we went to look a little closer, and we realized that the Mediterranean diet is not the same for everyone.
It's not good for everyone indistinctly.
The more educated you are, for example, the more advantage you take of the Mediterranean diet.
You will wonder why,
and we still can't state for sure: but we know, because we have studied it, that those who have a higher level of education read labels better, their analytic abilities are sharper, and are less prone to believe the fake news that newspapers and television tell us about health every day.
Every day there are also people who have television channels that say a lot of nonsense.
But the more educated one is, the stronger the critical system to resist this type of bad informations.
Plus, we also realized that not only the doctor, but the Internal Revenue Service plays an important role in our health.
Most “Moli-sani” answered the question about family income - looking closely at whether we could be tax agents disguised by a scrub. And so we were able to study and assess that income is more important than cholesterol.
We all want to check cholesterol; but family income, personal income is extremely important also.
Why is that? Let me give you
a simple example.
The Mediterranean diet is characterized, among many different things, by extra virgin olive oil.
Then, in our questionnaires, we asked: do you consume extra virgin olive oil?
Yes, yes. OK, a plus for you.
And you, do you consume extra virgin olive oil?
Yes. OK, a plus for you.
Then, second question: how much does a bottle of oil cost to you on average?
14 euros.
How much does a bottle cost to you on average?
Four euros.
So it's true that both participants follow the Mediterranean diet. Both of them season salads with extra virgin olive oil.
But you easily get how huge the difference is:
therefore, even within the Mediterranean diet, equality is no longer a virtue.
And it is no longer enough to say the Mediterranean diet is good for us: one must precisely define origin, quality, and understand all these things in order to have a better idea of how our health is going, and can go on, or the risk of getting sick.
And the last thing we have seen, and is of great interest to us - we are in an ageing society, so we are all getting older.
Let's face it: ageing is an issue we all certainly care about very much.
We have discovered in “Moli-sani” we shouldn't care much about the age declared in our passports, and in our identity cards.
The biological age, metered by a series of parameters - that we are trying to define better, matters much more.
What is this biological age?
The biological age corresponds better to the risk of getting sick and our chances to keep ourselves in good shape.
Enough, therefore, with a medicine that settles down with checking pressure, cholesterol, blood sugar, urine examination.
We want a medicine that investigates men, that investigates people in all their facets.
Their passions, readings, where they live, how and where they travel. their socio-economic situation.
And so, like Diogenes, I also go around, among the people, looking for man, looking for the person, looking for the individual.
Truth be told, I don't care much whether they're healthier or just less sick.
I'm interested in making them happier.
And I'm interested that they've found meaning in his life and his death.
The research goes on.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good evening everyone.
I am here to share with you a bit of my life, because over the last few years it is punctuated by the flights of eagles and falcons, in flying shows, mediaeval rievocations and bird control.
The way of falconry is a steep one, it's a road filled with sacrifices, but also many satisfactions
and many, many lessons to learn.
The first one I learnt is that dealing with a bird of prey can only be possible
if you earned his trust.
If a hawk doesn’t trust me, it won't absolutely cooperate with me.
The first bird of prey I trained is a male steppe eagle, called Hurakan.
He crossed my path shortly after I had started this new path of my life.
I can’t recall the first time I called Hurakan to my fist, as I have held him on my fist all his whole first year of life long,
and I spent more time with him than with anyone else.
But I will never, ever forget his first day of free flight.
He was a chick, we might say, he was 3 or 4 months old, feathered and ready,
but he was still calling me as if I was one of his parents.
He chirped every time he saw me.
When I realised he was ready and able to take off the flight, I brought him to fly on a hill.
That day there was a strong wind, it was ruffling my hair and his feathers. He left my fist and landed on the ground,
observed for a second the landscape before him, then he opened his wings
and the wind, so to say, claimed for him. He was high above my head, keeping chirping and calling me.
He was sort of balancing in the air.
Then I nudged him to go, and he started playing with the streams.
It was a unique moment, really a huge emotion.
Then when the time came to call him back to my fist I saw this tiny silhouette in the sky getting bigger and bigger, then closer and closer.
And at the end, with a few beats of wings, he was back on my fist.
This powerful feeling was just one among many situations that made me decide that the life I want is the life of the falconeer.
Even going hunting abroad and see golden eagle, goshawks, eagle owls and falcons in their element, at their best ,
watching them doing what nature has thought them for, actually, because they are predators.
That also convinced me.
I can’t describe with words the beauty, the strength, of a peregrine falcon stoop that breaks the air before you; but I can tell you witnessing such a situation makes you want to yell for the greatness of what you've just seen.
These birds follow an instinct-based training.
Basically, I do from the ground what a parent would do up in the sky.
Falcons, in particular, sharpened their flight skill so much it can be thought of as a weapon.
In order to teach them to fly, in order to elicit all his predator, flier skills I must simulate a hunting set.
I use a lure.
A lure is nothing but a rope with a bird-shaped piece of leather tied to an extremity, or just two dried wings.
I spin the lure around in the air, and while it spins, my falcon flies free.
He is free and he learns: he learns to fly, to stoop, to swoop, to twist, to take advantage of the right currents in ascending and speeding up.
It’s a bit of a dance.
You need to take the hawk's timing.
Not to mention the adrenaline, because, trust me, they really are speedy rockets.
What about adrenaline?
This also connected me to this art,
as birds of prey are not pet-therapy animals.
They are not tender, the do not try to appease us, they are not interested into physical contact.
They allow it, but it is not a staple element of a relationship with them.
They are pure instinct, even many years after their birth in breeding centers.
So what's a falconeer do? What do I do?
I tend not to tame,
not to inhibit this wild instinct. I nurture it, I try to elicit it.
The gratest satisfaction for a hunter falconeer is see his or her hawk is not just catching preys, but rather, when he's competing with a wild animal.
This proves you actually honoured the bird you trained.
In free flight, where of course there is not a prey, the most satisfying thing is seeing the flying ability of your eagle or falcon. And then he comes back to the fist.
The comeback is a magical moment, as it can't be taken for granted at all.
Because the moment a hawk, or an eagle, leave the fist, they are free.
And their return only depends on how we behaved towards them before.
You must have built a relationship before.
About flying demonstrations, which is what I do for a living, one thing I love is showing people not just the connection between a falconeer and an hawk, but more in depth what these birds are, because very often people have no clue about their life in nature.
People often don't know the difference between a little owl and a long eared one.
And there is the chance to explain them, show them,
and I am persuaded, the best way to explain and get people appreciate and respect these creatures, into the wild also, is watching them fly. Showing them what they're able to do.
Seeing my falcon speeding to the lure dismissing everything around, laser-focused on me, is a beautiful sensation I like to share with other people, because it often happens, they keep their nose up in the sky wondering, if they see a silhoutte in the sky, What was that? Why that?
Because if you know something, you understand it. And if you understand it, you respect it.
And I believe, respecting these animal leads to respect for nature as a whole.
This is why I love my job so much.
And even if public opinion is sometimes divided about falconry, I want you to know all the birds of prey we train come from breeding centers,
every single one.
Indeed, breeders and falconeers have often cooperated with corporations to reintroduce birds species in nature that are threated of extinction.
Why?
Because falconry is an infinite source of knowledge about hawks, since it is an ancient tradition.
Consider that – sorry.
This gesture I'm doing, belongs to a tradition that's about 6,000 years old.
Six thousand!
And in 2016, Italian Falconry has been declared Immaterial Heritage of Humanity by UNESCO.
It's been a huge acknowledgment.
I, for me, also had the chance to travel a lot, during these years.
I have met falconeers from all over the world.
From all over the world I have been to Spain, I have been to Austria, I have met falconers from Kazakistan, it has always been an experience I've learnt a lot from. Travelling and discovering different methods, different training techniques from different traditions, all of them belonging, of course, to the world of falconry.
Some of these people who explained me their methods have even written books, and I will always be thankful to these people, to these falconeers.
But I haven’t just met male falconeers. I have met even a lot of women falconeers too.
So many, because falconry, in spite of other ancient forms of art that were often precluded to women, has never been a privilege of men.
There are plenty of frescos, tapestries, engravings of women with falcons.
Dames riding horses with falcons.
Today, therefore, there is a strong presence of women in this field. Women directing Falconry Centers, breeders, bird controllers, hunters.
We really are all over the world.
Come on, yes.
Now let me introduce you - Meet Hurakan.
He is wearing a hood, now, let me briefly tell you why:
this is not a common situation for him, there are strong lights, cameras, a short ceiling, so this is a situation where he might get stressed.
In falconry, the hood is a very common tool, and it stems from centuries of studies on birds of prey.
It is made this way so that seams don't annoy the hawk, it doesn’t squeeze them, it respects the width of the peak, and it doesn’t touch the eyes.
It’s a simple instrument that keeps them from getting stressed,
so it is really for their health.
With Huracan on my fist, I wrap up and say you goodbye,
and I wish you all to fly high, right like eagles do, even against the wind.
Because an eagle knows, whether it is a strong wing or a breeze, the price for getting higher is flying against the wind.
And I wish you to follow your dreams, even changing the path of your life, if needed, leaving behind what you were doing before.
Because in life, sometimes, a jump lets you find yourself in flight.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Dear friends, in Ferrandina, province of Matera, took place the story that I’m about to tell you.
It's the story of the Bibliomotocarro, a small travelling library in the shape of a little house even with red tiles and a smoking chimney. It immediately gives a charming, pleasant impression.
As soon as they meet me on the streets, everyone smiles.
A smile is a business card, in a sense.
But, besides being charming, it also gives the impression of hospitality and accessibility.
The Bibliomotocarro, with the project "To the outskirts, across the border” to promote books and carries out reading and writing activities in Basilicata’s very small communities.
So it takes the books to where they’re needed most.
Without denying any child the right to hold the book they want in their hands.
And it does so with amazing simplicity.
“The books on wheels”, this is the name of the travelling library service with free book loans, which has one special feature: it doesn’t have a loan register.
The Bibliomotocarro sows trust with the awareness, however, that this sowing of trust, day after day, kilometre after kilometre, as you heard, 20 years, 200,000 kilometres, produces a harvest of responsibility.
Over the years, the Bibliomotocarro, founded in 1999, has had the ability to innovate without losing its identity.
The vehicle stayed the same.
A humble, simple, poor, and slow vehicle.
However, "The books on wheels” on its first run turned into "White Books”.
Before I tell you what the "White Books" are, I’d like to tell you a story. I was at San Mauro Forte, a municipality in the Matera hills, and we were carrying out "The books on wheels” activity.
First-year secondary school children were choosing the book that they were going to keep for a month.
The Bibliomotocarro usually comes back after a month.
At a certain point, a little Bulgarian girl Adalina, shyly, stood behind me and asked me in a whisper: "Teacher, what if we wrote the books ourselves?”
It was such a strong expression!
"Teacher, what if we wrote the books ourselves?”
She was asking to talk about herself and to tell her own story. When her Mum told her that she would leave her country and that she would come to Italy, to Basilicata, to the province of Matera, to San Mauro Forte.
How nice, Adalina, we'll think about it.
In truth, I didn't think about it at all. I went to Matera and bought 200 booklets with 80 blank pages, with red, green, and blue covers that could look like books. Then I delivered them to all the towns in Basilicata,
giving the chance to tell stories, to tell their stories.
What are the "White Books"?
These are books composed of stories written by children from different towns.
A story begins in one place, then that story continues in another and then in yet another place.
Stories that intertwine.
A travelling writing workshop, but, above all, an extraordinary opportunity for children to tell stories and to tell their stories. There is a great need for such stories.
And then, the Literary Cinema,
to enter with your head and leave with your eyes.
Yesterday I went to the Isola district and then to the Chiesa Rossa Library and the children in these suburbs entered the little house, which is actually a travelling cinema, and saw the animated short films made by children inspired by literary texts.
I read a poem, a nursery rhyme, then I said, "Children,
let's go to the cinema!"
I opened the back doors, they entered and this cinema came to life.
"From the page to the world" is another workshop.
We start with a page of literature, and the children write other stories.
These stories then intertwine, becoming a unique story, a script.
The children become the performers of a story that they wrote themselves starting from a beautiful page of literature.
The Bibliomotocarro was born in school.
42 years as a primary school teacher. When I was a teacher, I speak of 25 years ago, every day I perceived this growing disaffection over books.
This progressive weakening of the relationship between books and the childhood.
"Children, there is a problem, something must be done!"
“Teacher, you could do something.”
And saying this, they sowed a seed inside me.
And what did this seed find?
It found a candle.
Dear friends, my parents were farmers.
And when my mother turned off the lightbulb in the house -
I said "the" lightbulb: we only had one; and I was eight or nine years old, so I'm talking about 65 years ago - I was discovering the passion, the pleasure of reading, the joy of reading.
In order to do that, I used to light a candle.
That candle never extinguished inside me.
It’s still lit, and it feeds the passions that leads me
to travel 200,000 kilometres.
“Teacher, why are you doing this?”
“Because I like it” is the answer I often give to incredulous people when they hear, I left at four am.
Who ask themselves, “Why do you do it?”
Let’s go back to school.
Because in school, dear friends - In the dear old primary school, extraordinary things happened.
What happened to me?
That, in the meantime, the children were growing up, they passed from second to to third, to fourth grade.
When I heard them say, "Teacher, there’s a problem here!
The books are moving out of reach.”
They no longer told me “You could do something,”
[Instead, they said] "Teacher, you must do something.”
Grasping a fundamental element, because this would have been great if an architect, engineer, shopkeeper or workman had done it.
But what if a teacher did it?
It would be beautiful.
They were right, as usual.
They, the children.
So I had to do something.
“You must do something.”
It was a matter of understanding what.
And then it was enough to call attention to that emerging problem Was enough to express a cry of alarm, like: “What are we doing?
Can we live without books?”
No, it wasn't enough.
The initiative had to have a possible solution within itself. And the only possible solution was to put wheels under the books.
Put them out of their comfort zone.
And there had to be three wheels those of a humbler, simpler, poorer vehicle, I repeat, a slower vehicle.
A revolutionary idea.
Books have always been placed up high, a symbol and expression of an aristocratic, elitist culture. Books written by few, and read by few.
It needed to expand, the democratic base needed to expand. We needed to search for new readers with an accessible vehicle.
In my region, the motocarro is used to carry fruit and vegetables to the street corners, at your door, and the children run after it.
Here, the same thing happens with the Bibliomotocarro and they know that what they'll find are books, not pop-corn or ice cream.
Yet they run.
It’s accessible, a vehicle that could and can allow for this expansion of the democratic base.
Was it easy? No.

I encountered obstacles.
I'll tell you a story that will seem like a fairy tale.
It has the form of a fairy tale but it’s a true story.
In the first few years the Bibliomotocarro could not exceed 30-35 km per hour
on the great roads that follow the rivers,
the Basentana, the Val d'Agri, the Sinnica, the Bradanica.
I couldn't explain why.
Sure, the Bibliomotocarro is one ton of weight, but there was something else.
And I discovered what it was.
It was the annoyance and disapproval of the books which did not like what I had done, putting them in an uncomfortable position.
"But who does he think he is?
What does this teacher think he is doing?
We were on high, noble shelves”,
said one book to another. “They have put us on modest shelves, in a lowly and humble motocarro and we’re driven around the streets.
We are The Books.”
And when I heard this I said, “We’ve done it, won the game, because I knew they’d change their minds otherwise they wouldn’t have been masterpieces of children’s literature.
In fact, after some time, I started hearing Captains Courageous saying, "At the end of the day, the teacher’s idea isn’t so bad.”
"Yes, it's true", added Peter Pan.
“We were on high and noble shelves, but they were also covered in dust.
He takes us from one town to another along thes beautiful roads of Basilicata, and, above all, we pass from the hands of one child to those of another, which is why we were written in the first place.”
So today, dear friends, the Bibliomotocarro even reaches 50, sometimes exceeds 50 km per hour because I am thrusted by the enthusiasm of the books which have understood that together we must go in search of new readers.
It may seem like a fairy tale, but it’s a true story.
Of course, I realise it has the form of a fairy tale, but that’s what really happened.
In the first few years, when the Bibliomotocarro appeared, I surprised many people who said to me “Teacher,
what have you done?
An old thing, outdated.
Still with books!
You’re stuck in ancient times
Books are old-fashioned now."
And I answered: "Look, the Bibliomotocarro is a post-modern idea.”
And while today we talk about humanising technology, I was already saying, 20 years ago, that the Bibliomotocarro was an expression of modern humanism. Where modern, however, is an adjective. That is something
that is added to the noun.
What did happen instead, dear friends, over the last 25 years?
Is this modernity?
Observing our behaviour, everyone's behaviour, young and old, men and women.
What do we do, dear friends, when we go anywhere?
The first thing we do is see if we're connected,
if we're logged on.
If there’s network, otherwise we fall into the darkest panic. It gives us a special lust to know that we can be reached from every part of the world. Then we are disconnected from each other from those who are next to us, husband and wife, mother and children. And so the adjective, modern, said: “I have to be your adjective,
you who are in my hands?
But I’m playing my own game.
I’ll become modernity.”
And, in fact, it became modernity.
But modernity without humanism, which is what we are witnessing, isn't even modernity but a sterile, futile, empty, trivial modernism.
And so, I have the pride and ambition to say that with this humble, simple vehicle, putting wheel under books, I not only promote books and reading and do it, believe me, in communities with less than a thousand inhabitants and suffer this endless phenomenon of depopulation; in many towns where there are no schools or where the school classes are multi-age.
Doing it there not only gives you a social and cultural role, but you understand that you play a role that has a profoundly ethical value.
As I said, besides this which is already important think about the "White Books”, this travelling writing workshop.
The Bibliomotocarro has the pride and ambition to support the concept of modern humanism and to do so with the modesty of these means and with the simplicity of the proposal.
Thank you.
(Applause)
(Music) (Music ends) This is what my life needs today.
Shortly, I will explain to you how I had a sort of epiphany the moment I made my decision.
Perhaps, it can be captured in one phrase: "I love not man the less, but Nature more."
Maybe these were not the exact words I had in mind the day I made my choice, however, I was fascinated as I discovered they had been written by a hand that was not my own.
They are, indeed, the words of the poet Byron.
Since then, they have been my guide.
I was still a young girl, but they legitimize, in the most technical sense, my existence on this planet and what I chose to do with my life, along with my most recent and drastic decision.
For the past four years I have spent most of my life on board one of the Sea Shepherd's vessels,
an international organization whose aim it is to protect the oceans.
The Bob Barker was my home for several months.
I am strongly convinced that it is essential that each one of us assumes responsibility to make a choice, in order to save our future.
Sure, it is much easier to ignore, to switch TV channels, to turn the page, because knowing forces us to make a choice.
And, at times, choices clash with personal pleasure, with habits.
They require hardship and sacrifice.
I made my choice, and my encounter with Sea Shepherd, in fact, completely random, was love at first sight for their direct action protecting animals and their habitats, which makes all the difference, and which I so desperately needed.
And so I left everything behind, my home, my job, my family, my private life.
When I look back to when I was living a so called 'normal life' I can't forget the looming feeling of depression, stemming from a life which I had never felt was mine.
And when I realized I wasn't really living anymore, I felt fear strangling me in a grip that, for days on end, took my breath away.
Well, before it was too late, I decided to drop everything and to honor Byron's words.
Because, if I'm not afraid of dying, I am terrified of not really living.
On board, I am the designated photographer.
I have to document what happens on the high seas, often thousands of miles from the coast, from civilization, where the law does exist, but is not enforced, so it is just as if it did not exist.
Sea Shepherd ensures that the law is enforced.
I was talking earlier about ethics, morals, ideals, but the law is something different, it is real, tangible, it regulates our lives for more than obvious reasons.
And so, the choice I made was also made in the name of the law, in order to save the creatures who live in my beloved, deep blue.
I'd like to tell you something: every year, in the Antarctic Ocean, thousands of whales gather during their northern migration.
They gather to feed on krill.
The beauty and harmony of this cycle
have continued for thousands of years, until humans decided that whales would make a good food source, at first, but then turned them into a mere way to make money.
So much so that they hunted them
to the brink of extinction. Then, in 1986, the International Whaling Commission declared whaling illegal.
However, it still allowed whaling for scientific research.
So, Japan, on the sides of its ships, writes 'Research' in huge letters, delivers to the Commission alleged research projects, completely nonsensical, and keeps killing, with impunity, thousand of whales.
And it hunts them where it is easier to do so, and less likely that anyone will go and oppose its activities: the Whale Sanctuary in the Southern Ocean.
From 1986 to 2002 Japan has killed over 25,000 whales, illegally.
Sea Shepherd doesn't sit around and watch.
The World Charter for Nature is very clear.
Every single citizen of the world holds the right and the duty to act in order to protect wildlife and natural ecosystems.
And so, ships and crews are readied in order to stop Japan's illegal, irresponsible and bloody massacre.
Killing a whale is not an easy thing.
An explosive harpoon is driven into the body of the whale, who, desperately fighting for its life, dies after a prolonged agony, to become lunch meat that, these days, hardly anyone eats anymore, in Japan, or elsewhere; or to be turned into beauty products.
Sea Shepherd puts everything on the line, its ships included,
but the more powerful weapon of the shepherds of the sea is the passion of every single one of its volunteers, who refuse to stop, while facing any danger, to put an end to illegal actions.
Let me tell you one of the many stories I have lived in first person.
I was on the monkey deck of the Bob Barker.
My task was to document the events taking place.
I still remember those moments with such strong emotions.
I felt like I had been thrown into the middle of an action movie, because, honestly, my eyes could not believe what they were witnessing.
Because the hunting season lasts as long as the Antarctic summer, three months, at least, the Japanese factory ship had to approach the tanker in order to fuel up - another illegal activity in those waters.
And we are back talking about legality.
Well, in total desperation, the Captain of the Japanese vessel, violently pushed his 8,000 ton ship towards our small Bob.
He flooded us with the salty, icy water from his water pumps to keep us from governing our ship.
For me, in spite of the wide angle of my camera, it looks like an enormous giant moving towards us without hesitation.
The Bob is dangerously listing to one side.
My heart is racing, but, strangely enough, not because I feel in personal danger, but rather for fear they can really push us out of the way, destroy our ship, and defeat us in our purpose.
I can hear myself saying, over and over, "Don't give in, Peter, don't give in."
It will only be in the next days that I will find out what was actually happening right below, on the bridge.
Peter's determination, our very young Captain.
"I will not move.
If you want to take my place, you will have to sink me."
(Video) Man: We need to stop this refueling from taking place.
(Collision noise) Japanese ship: Mayday, Mayday.
Bob Barker, we are getting rammed.
Sea Shepherd: Nisshin Maru, this is the Bob Barker,
I will not move. I will not move.
You will have to sink me right here next to the Sun Laurel.
I'm not going to move for you.
(Music) Japanese ship: You are destroying our boat.
Sea Shepherd: Steve Irwin, they are destroying us!
(Music) Steve Irwin, we've regained propulsion, I have power, I'm not backing down.
(Music) (Applause) Marianna Baldo: Thank you.
And how did it end?
Well, from up there, through our Bob's gashed steel plates, I see the gigantic black silhouette backing up, defeated.
We were actually sending them home, they were out of fuel.
We had won the battle.
That day we had, indeed, saved the whales.
That day, as well.
Earlier, I mentioned the law.
Well, after a four-year trial, at the International Court of Justice, in The Hague, we finally heard the verdict.
March 31st, 2014: "Japanese whaling is a mere commercial activity.'
The 'Research' writing on the sides of the ships is acknowledged as a lie, as a ruse.
From that very day, the Japanese government is prohibited from setting hunting quotas.
Thank God, the law prevailed.
But now let me take you to another part of the world.
Because I chose to protect the oceans, after the Antarctic season I moved to the North Atlantic.
I decided to navigate around this small archipelago, the Faeroe Islands, an area through which large dolphin groups migrate.
These beautiful animals, highly intelligent and sensitive, and with a sense of family as strong as our own, swim through these waters, tired and trustful, in search of food and a place to rest.
However, alas, what they find is death.
The islanders mercilessly slaughter them all.
And what once was a subsistence hunt, for decades now has turned into a feast, "The Feast",
a terrible, gory massacre inundated with blood.
Entire families are exterminated.
A genetic patrimony disappears, forever,
following the path of extinction.
And then we discover that the majority of their slaughtered flesh returns to the bottom of the ocean piling up in actual cemeteries.
I saw men of all ages, boys included, wade in the water up to their waists, possessed by a desire to kill, with the strongest satisfaction, those innocent and helpless creatures.
In the meantime, women enjoy the show from the shore, and even push their kids to the waters edge, so that they can participate in the slaughter.
I could not even fathom having a child who, with a carving knife in hand, proceeds to slaughter a defenceless animal. And not for hunger, but for the sole pleasure to kill.
What kind of values would my kids grow up with?
Those barbaric traditions are supported even by the Danish government. In order to obstruct us, and those who, like us, oppose all of this, Denmark even employs its battleships, its police force.
Last summer I was, in fact, in the Faeroe Islands, trying to photograph the slaughter of about a hundred pilot whales, that, unfortunately, I would not be able to save anymore.
I was arrested even before arriving on site.
Think about it, I was on a tiny Zodiac, and I only had my camera with me.
In the next few days, the prosecution, the trial and the conviction to be jailed for my sole intention to stop the slaughter with a camera that, needless to say, was confiscated.
(Applause) Thank you. Believe me, there is no way to forget the cries of those animals, their fear, their pain, the terror they are subjected to, in those last, endless moments, while they fight for their lives, the adults trying to protect the young, as the ocean turns bright red with the spilling of their blood.
It can't be forgotten. Ever.
By stirring the waters, whales and dolphins, along with the phytoplankton, contribute to the oxygenation of the air on our planet.
Protecting these animals is also a way to safeguard the oceans.
And taking care of the oceans is a way to ensure that we can continue to be able to live on this planet.
Because the one thing that all living beings have in common, indistinctly, regardless of our species, is that, to live, we must breath.
So, I think, I strongly believe, that we cannot always expect that someone else will take care of things for us, let alone those which concern everyone, indistinctly, globally, because we are 'the other' and the planet on which we live is our home.
All of us, deep in our heart, wish for things to change, for a better future.
I bet you all do, too.
But how many are truly willing to make some change in their lives?
I am trying.
And the first step of my transformation was to shift from a selfish thought like: "Me, here, now", to "All, everywhere, always."
Thank you. (Applause)
When I decided to become a doctor, and enrolled in medical school, my decision had been very strongly influenced by one man; the figure of our family doctor who made house calls whenever we were sick.
He visited me, he visited my sisters and then he stopped to chat amiably with my parents.
Different times.
Probably, indeed certainly, by other people too.
However, in my final studies I found myself dealing with patients with luekaemia, cancer patients, and then I started to take a different approach to medicine.
A different concept. And I liked the idea of being able to make a personal contribution without taking anything away from family doctors, past and present to this different approach to medicine and wanted to try to do something more.
Life is, as you know, a bit strange.
Sometimes a letter can change your life.
This is a letter I received in 1984.
Written on a typewriter, as you can see. The Italian Association for Cancer Research informed me that I had been awarded a scholarship to study abroad.
It was a scholarship for a limited period of time that I took advantage of to go and work in New York.
I went to work in an immunology centre where in 1984 they began to do the first research into immunotherapy treatment.
Then, as often happens in life, I ended up staying for six years.
And so, when I was 29, 30, I found myself at a crossroads.
Should I stay? Or go home?
I decided to come back and set myself a challenge of trying to set up a project in Italy with the best people I could find in the field of cancer immunotherapy.
I can say that this challenge, at least this one, has been met because today in Siena we have a centre in which over 50 people work, many of them active researchers in the field of cancer immunotherapy.
But what is immunotherapy?
It is essentially a paradigm change from how we used to treat cancer in the last century.
This is a very old slide, which I have been using for around 30 years, which shows, on a timeline, the strategies we used and, perhaps, would soon be available to treat cancer.
Until the end of the 1800s, the only option was surgery.
Often devastating, futile surgery because we were not able to learn from our mistakes.
Then, at the beginning of the 20th century, radiotherapy became available which certainly made a contribution, and continues to do so to the treatment of human cancers.
Then, in the 1960s, chemotherapy came along which was considered, in the 60s to be the definitive strategy in the fight against cancer.
However, already over the last 30 or 40 years, there was a small group of oncologists around the world who thought this was not good enough and believed, more importantly in the fourth sphere you see here in yellow. This was the idea that our immune system could be used to fight cancer.
It was, if you like, a group of visionaries that could be found at all the international conferences, at the end of the conference, huddled together in the most isolated rooms.
They were also looked down on a little.
But they had a rational basis on which to keep believing, very stubbornly that it was possible to use our immune system that all of us have in our body and helps us, in the day to day external environment, to fight cancer.
I used this slide many times to say: "And then there is immunotherapy, which is a promising strategy".
Well, it's not the case anymore,
because today immunotherapy is the fourth strategy we have in the fight against cancer and even to eradicate it from within using our own body.
Which, as you see, is able, over the course of our lives, in all of us, to keep in check and destroy cells that have tried to become immortal by becoming cancer cells.
This happens.
Occasionally, though, these cells become so powerful that they can overcome this control mechanism and then the clinical evidence of cancer appears.
"Overcome this control" means that our immune defences are no longer effective.
They have been knocked out.
What can we do with immunotherapy?
We have completely changed the paradigm.
We no longer use any of the three strategies that have been used over the last 100 years but we use drugs, proper medical drugs to boost the patient's immune system.
So, we no longer focus on the cancer but on the patient's immune system.
And when we succeed at doing this and when we have all the right ingredients, we manage to get clinical outcomes that are spectacular, let me tell you, and that in some types of cancer - I'm thinking now of cutaneous melanoma, which is a skin cancer for which, until a few years ago, we had absolutely nothing to offer our patients, we have now more than tripled the survival rate of these patients to ten years after the beginning of therapy.
So, we are getting extremely good results that for some cancers have been better than chemotherapy.
For example, melanoma.
But we are working on many other cancers and achieved significant results with treatments that are now available to everyone, available to all oncologists in Italy and abroad.
So we have drugs that work well for lung cancer, bladder cancer, head-neck tumours -
it's a list that grows longer, a bit like a triumphal march almost every week.
Almost every week we learn more about the effectiveness of this therapeutic strategy.
So, there has been an total paradigm change.
However, I have to admit I'm a big fan of this treatment, because I have always worked in the field of immunotherapy.
I'm excited about what's happening today.
It must be said, that the international scientific community the very one that was so sceptical until a few years ago, has come to concede that that group of visionaries had something after all.
They had the imagination to realise that really remarkable results could be achieved.
So today immunotherapy is accepted by everyone even by the sceptics, those who didn't believe in it, as an absolutely vital strategy in the clinical pathway of our patients.
And it goes without saying that many of those sceptics have become experts themselves. but this is the way things are, it's the human nature.
However, what is far more important now is to look to the future.
That is, to go even further and to succeed there where we failed for decades.
And this is definitely possible because today we have the scientific, clinical basis to understand how we can go even further and increase the percentage of patients that respond well to immunotherapeutic treatment.
Because now, even in the best of cases, around 50% of patients do not respond to this type of treatment.
But the last decades have not been passed in vain because, through our research we, the international scientific community, have succeeded to understand the underlying mechanisms that regulate this system. We originally were watching a very beautiful dance in this particular case, a "pas de deux" between the immune system - our immune system, that is inside every one of us and cancer.
This is a dance that requires many elements to work.
We have to have a profound understanding of the roots of this interaction.
And the roots of this interaction are complex because there is, on one side, the cancer with all its many facets all its characteristics, all its diversity and a wide range of mechanisms that it puts in place to evade the immune system's defences.
And, on the other side, we have our immune system which is slightly different in all of us.
It has different levels of effectiveness.
So only by reuniting these two dancers in the best possible way we'll manage to control the disease as well as possible.
But this means adding complexity on complexity.
We are learning that the cancer hides from the immune system not just by changing its characteristics, but by developing its own niche - the so-called "tumour microenvironment" - that often uses our own immune system, certain cells in our immune system, to defend itself against the immune system that we have specifically activated with our drugs.
So, there is still a lot of work to do.
A huge amount of work to do, but we have a sound basis which comes not only from what we already achieved but also from the knowledge of how our immune system works, a knowledge that keeps expanding day after day.
And, in this light, I like to remember that last Monday, these two scientists - Jim Allison and Tasuku Honjo, were awarded the Nobel Prize for their fundamental discovery.
In the media you will have read the touching stories of their sick friends with a cancer.
All nonsense.
They were not interested in cancer itself.
They were interested in understanding how our immune system works.
And they succeeded, because they managed to find out that the workings of our immune system, operating every day, at this moment, in every one of us is based on the physical interaction between the cells of the immune system, that talk to each other to optimise the control of any foreign invader through the molecule membranes.
From this fundamental discovery they came to understand that there are negative feedback loops that can turn off our immune response. They then created drugs that acted mainly on two molecules, one called CTLA-4, the other called PD-1, that were able to apply the brakes, to block these negative signals in order to enhance the immune system's response.
This, in turn, is then able in about 50% of patients, as I mentioned, to keep the cancer under control and eliminate it allowing patients to live much longer than could have previously been thought possible.
So, these two scientists have certainly contributed in a fundamental way to the success of today's immunotherapy by starting off with, then working on the fundamentals of how our immune system works.
I was speaking to a friend a few days ago, an excellent wine grower, who told me: "To make a great wine you need to know the roots and the soil these roots grow in. Otherwise, you will never be able to make a great wine".
Thank you.
(Applause)
I firmly believe in progress, the subject of today's TEDx.
Think of evolution, the subject I deal with, the evolution of living beings.
What a glorious story of progress: in three and a half billion years it has taken us from amoebas to Donald Trump.
(Laughter) If that isn’t progress, what is?
So, I'd like to discuss with you - this is progress, sure; but I would like us to reflect on the ambivalence of progress.
I firmly believe in it, but we must consider all its facets.
This gentleman is Steven Pinker, a brilliant good colleague of mine.
Read his books, they are very well written, very interesting.
He is a cognitive scientist and an evolutionist himself. He wrote important books, in particular on the evolution of language.
However, a few years ago he started a cultural, philosophical and, I dare say, civil campaign.
See the title of the book?
We must rediscover Enlightenment values.
And God only knows how important it is to reappreciate critical spirit, the importance of raising doubts.
And above all, Steven Pinker points out we should go back to data, evidences, and put them at the basis for any reflection.
The evidences he describes in his books are like these ones.
For example, take a look at infant mortality rates, over the last two millennia, how they have plummeted, what a steep decline has it been. And this is a fact, it is a sign of progress.
And again, the evolution over time of our life expectancy, from 1800 - 1850 until a few years ago.
Here too we have made an extraordinary progress.
Similar graphs could apply for access to clean water and many other basic parameters.
And, of course, this is true, there is no doubt.
And Pinker is right to emphasise this, because those like us, who we were born in a generation, and tend to take for granted our predecessors' achievements, forgetful of the terrible deaths, without anesthesia or antibiotics, in the last century, one century and a half ago. This is a fact.
But is this enough to be sure that we are on a path to progress?
If you take another look at Pinker's data, there's something else: from an evolutionary point of view, for example, population growth is also considered a sign of progress.
And from a strictly Darwinian point of view, it is: population success is a sign of progress and success.
It means that we have been better at acquiring and consuming resources, another value that is always considered to be one of the positive factors of this progress.
Of course however, you can see, given how unbalanced this growth is, there could be problems if, for example, in a few years Nigeria will host more than two thirds of Europe's population. Two thirds of EU countries combined!
So, we must be careful and understand what lies beneath these data.
Let’s look at this other piece of data: this is the wonderful Oxfam report released a few months ago on worldwide inequalities.
Of course, Pinker is right: we are evolving and we made tremendous progress in the last 150 years.
But not every one of us enjoyed this progress indistinctly.
Look at how inequalities are growing globally, look at the number of people - 42 in 2017, then 26 in 2018, who are just as wealthy as the world’s poorest 3.5 billion people together.
Or take Jeff Bezos: he alone has a capital that is 100 times more - Ethiopia's health budget is just one percent of Bezos' wealth.
With a small token from Jeff Bezos the future of Ethiopia could completely change overnight.
Do we like a world like this?

Not so much, yet it evolves.
But it does so in an unequal, unbalanced way.
We are evolutionists, and so we have to analyse, every time there is a trend, what its implications are.
So Pinker is right.
But this progress is, first and foremost, unbalanced; ambivalent; and not for everyone.
And then, I would add, it comes with side effects.
There are many: people talk a lot about climate change, of course, environmental crisis.
Let me show you some dry data, just to make you understand: according to "Nature" and "Science" - which are not magazines of radical ecological militancy, rather the two world’s leading scientific magazines - Homo Sapiens has destroyed, wiped out, depending on the groups you take into account, 35-40% of all other living beings.
One species alone killed more than a third of all other species.
It had never happened in evolution before.
This is also a bad idea from an evolutionary standpoint.
For example, if insect numbers drop, a large proportion of the world's crops depends directly or indirectly on pollinating insects.
We are doing something that goes directly against our own interests.
Or, look at the issue of plastics and microplastics. We also work on it at the Università di Padova, we are trying to quantify them.
Everyone is talking about macroscopic plastics, the famous island in the Pacific, thousands of kilometers of floating plastic.
Of course it's a horrible, terrible thing: but that plastic is visible.
What we should worry about the most, actually, and what we scientists alsp underestimated for too long, is microplastics, the very small ones, smaller than a third of a millimeter, penetrating all the seas and oceans of the world.
In Italy, the Po river from the Po Valley constantly pours thousands of tons of plastic into the Adriatic Sea.
Microplastics have even penetrated
into ocean depths. Yet, three centuries ago we gave ourselves the name of Homo sapiens. As Oscar Wilde once said, "No participle was ever given in such a hasty manner.
Yes, he was way too pessimistic, I know. Just think about microplastic, though: they end up inside all animals, in almost half of world's cetaceans and in almost a third of fish including those we eat and buy at the market.
So we throw away our rubbish, we wash synthetic fabrics, such as fleece, washing machines discharge these microplastic fibers into the water and eventually we eat our own waste.
Homo Sapiens: a homo who generates trash, dumps it into the sea and then eats it.
Mind you, the Ellen MacArthur Foundation, the leading international foundation that funds research on plastic, has made an estimate I still find it hard to believe. But take a look at their estimates, you can't argue with that.
If we keep this pace, and fish year after year as much as we are doing now, indiscriminately in many parts of the world, while dumping plastic and microplastics in exchange, by 2050, in terms of mass, the Earth’s seas will contain more plastic than fish.
More plastic than fish
in the Earth's seas!
In 2050. Homo sapiens. OK?
There is a problem, obviously,
that we have to consider.
Now, being evolutionists we consider it this way, without ever giving an ethical evaluation of the matter but rather, in the early stages, a merely technical, scientific one.
What is going on?
These are the consequences of what we humans did for so long and that has undoubtedly led, insofar, to our success.
As a great evolutionist, Richard Lewontin, once said, "We are the ones who stopped adapting to the environment, running after environmental changes". Instead, we figured out another extraordinary, powerful strategy: changing the environment so as to make it suitable to our needs.
We are the builders of our ecological niche, which has granted us a great success, a great fortune.
But now a problem has emerged, and we are becoming aware of it.
And it is yet another hazard concealed in our idea of progress -
which again, I'm not going to question. I believe in it, I am also an optimist.
But you have to be careful when it comes to that concept of progress, you need to relativise it, put it in context.
What is happening with climate change and all the examples you know well?
What is happening is, now we have to - I mean, my children and grandchildren will have to adapt to the environmental changes introduced by my generation.
You may have noticed, in international climate debates,
the buzzword used to be mitigation, right?
I mean, we were optimistic: let's mitigate, let’s slow down, let's give this global warming a break.
Now two keywords have emerged: mitigation and adaptation.
In other words, it is here to stay.
We have to adapt, we have to live with it, we have to try and live in a world that will change because of the niche that we have built.
Other species, like beavers, do it too.
This is a beautiful beaver dam.
Beavers do the exact same thing:
they build a beautiful artefact that is functional for them, for their reproduction, for their survival, changing the world in the process.
Because, for example, they create a lake, a pool of standing water that can host other species.
They change the world to make it more suitable for them.
We are like beavers who went slightly out of control.
We are beavers who got carried away a bit, let's say.
So, what is climate change for us evolutionists?
It's a great evolutionary experiment.
I know it sounds cynical, but it actually is a large scale, global, evolutionary experiment that is changing the world.
It will favor some species to the detriment of many others that are already becoming extinct due to climate change.
But we, can we really save the planet?
The planet will save itself, won't it?
What can we understand if we are far-sighted, if we are smart enough?
That we are changing this planet so rapidly, so quickly, so light-heartedly that we risk to pay the consequences, and dearly.
We risk to pay them dearly: If ecosystems are no longer so generous with us, in terms of fertility - you know about it right?
Desertification, all the problems related to climate change, and in terms of the water cycle, etc., we will have to pay for those ecosystem services. that will get rarer.
And economists know, when something is scarce, you pay for it. It may be the so-called evolutionary trap.
Is there any glimmer of hope?
There actually is: you see, this diagram is a bit abstract way to say the same thing.
We change the world, so we have to adapt to the changes we ourselves have introduced.
So, what does that mean?
When you have children - I am a father of three, roughly the age of Greta Thumberg - what am I passing on to my kids?
I am passing on my genes - well those I already have, along with my wife's, of course - I pass on ideas, culture. I pass on my inventions - so there's a cultural transmission - but I'm also passing on the environmental changes I introduced into my generation, which they’ll have to deal with.
They are climate natives and, being such, I trust hope they'll also have a different mindset and level of creativity to get out of it, as they were born right into it.
But this is only a hope.
Again: as an evolutionist, it is always good to remember that this is an old story.
We ourselves are a very young species - we are only 200,000 years old and have made the most out of them. It was an impetuous, wonderful evolution and we are only 200 millennia old. So far, there have only been 8,000 human generations, that's all.
If I go back in my evolutionary history, I only have 4,000 grandparents, then no more human history.
We are indeed a very young species, but also a very ambivalent one.
Quick example: this is the map of the great human migrations.
We are a young, African species that left Africa multiple times, the last time 60,000 years ago.
We are all descendants of a small group of Africans, about 60-70,000 people.
All of us, seven and a half billion, who left Africa that time.
When they left Africa, what did our pioneering ancestors do?
They did wonderful, beautiful things.
We have just discovered, cave paintings didn’t originate in Europe, but were made earlier. These come from Sulawesi, Indonesia.
So it means, these early pioneering migrants who founded the human species were already capable of these wonderful things. At the same time, these groups could live not only in the Tropics, at the Equator - look at this: this is a mammoth hunted in the Arctic at 22 degrees below zero in a very ancient era: 45,000 years ago. The African hunters - these were people of colour, just like today’s Masai - lived at 20 degrees below zero and hunted mammoths.
Why on Earth did they do that?
We are the only species on Earth capable of doing such things.
No other animal would live in Africa to leave and dwell at 20 below zero eating mammoths.
There is something firmly rooted in human nature here, in this ambivalence.
We are very creative and very destructive. We do what I was showing you before, wonderful rock art, but we also show great coordination and organizational skills if we are to hunt mammoths, for example.
So this is still an unanswered question.
You see, this is the tree of human evolution.
Look how many human species there have been on Earth, a great many.
We are the last branch up there, and we have been left alone because we helped the extinction of all other human species.
This can't be progress, eliminate all those who resemble us.
Still, this is us, an ambivalent species.
A few years ago, at an international congress in Lisbon, I ironically suggested, but I swear I was joking,
Excuse me, you experts of conservation have said that there are endangered species and threatened species.
Why don’t we add another category, the "self-endangered" species, that have got themselves into trouble or may risk doing so in the future.
There are two candidates: Homo Sapiens, for the reasons I told you, and the Irish elk, aka the Megaloceros - which wasn't an elk and wasn't Irish, but we don't have time for that - that contributed to its own extinction with its oversized antlers.
The only difference is that the Irish elk did not realise it was becoming extinct, while we have all the tools we need to understand we might have got caught in an evolutionary trap, and we should get out of it as soon as possible.
Now, and it is quite worrying, they are seriously considering introducing this category.
I was joking, but apparently
we should actually be concerned.
So, I absolutely agree with you: 60 percent of you are optimistic, 30 percent are uncertain and 8% are pessimistic.
Well, we must be optimistic, quoting Andrea Camilleri, but with a pinch of salt. I perfectly understand all those 30 percent of uncertain.
So I agree with the outcome of your vote. Thank you.
(Applause)
Good morning.
Well, let's get straight to it.
I'd like to invite you all to tiptoe very quietly back into 18th century England.
We all have, under our arms, a book written years ago by a talented writer from New York, Dava Sobel. The book is called 'Longitude: The True Story of a Lone Genius Who Solved the Greatest Scientific Problem of His Time'.
England, at the time, was ruler of the waves, but had not mastered the determination of longitude.
Position calculations were imperfect, ships sank, cargoes were lost, and the human toll was very, very heavy.
So the British government decided to launch a competition, a race.
The winner, who came up with the appropriate solution, would receive the equivalent of about $6 to 7 million in today's money.
Of course, the challenge was taken up by those with the required knowledge: scientists, astronomers. Among others was Nevil Maskelyne, whom you see in this image, in this slide, who was the Royal Astronomer, and therefore also Commissioner of Longitude, judge of the competition.
All appropriately educated participants naturally felt a strong sense of responsibility, feared making mistakes, feared the consequences of errors, so they looked over, studied, examined maps showing the state of knowledge as it was. They did research and analysis. But it wasn't the way to solve the problem.
They thought that by observing the stars, the movement of the planets and satellites, it was possible to find the solution.
But it wasn't the case.
However, among the competitors was a craftsman, a person of little education, a carpenter, who however had a love of the clock mechanisms of the time.
His name was John Harrison.
He was the one who solved the problem, with a marine chronometer. But it took decades to admit that Harrison had the right idea.
Astronomers, scientists said 'Bah, he's uneducated, ignorant. Could we ever give him credit?'
Facts showed that Harrison was the winner. But for years ships continued to go down.
Neil Armstrong, not many years ago, in a fine speech, I think in the presence of the President at the White House said, Without Harrison and his chronometer, we would never have got to the moon.
This episode, this anecdote, admirably told by Dava Sobel, leads me to go on to say that the great English writer and critic, William Hazlitt, was probably right in what he maintained. He lived between the 18th and 19th centuries,
and said that poorly educated people were endowed with an exuberant inventiveness, were free from prejudice and did not lack conceptual flexibility. Such qualities he attributed to Shakespeare, who, notorious for little formal education, created wonderful works. He contrasted Shakespeare to John Milton, the author of <i>Paradise Lost</i>', a person with great academic knowledge, in many ways similar to the astronomers and scientists who lost the competition with Harrison.
Well, with Dava Sobel we can now travel to Comfort Island, the Island of Comfort, that you see here on the left.
It is an island inhabited by people who are very knowledgeable.
However, it is an island where it's difficult to get to and live on in an age of knowledge.
It may seem paradoxical: why so?
Because in this age of knowledge, an infrastructure is needed which is missing on Comfort Island. This structure, to use the English jargon of today, is 'knowledgification'.
Knowledge in action, knowledge which drives innovation in companies that break the state of the art.
Knowledgefication needs a central source, and this is creative ignorance, ignorance that does not precede, but follows knowledge and overcomes it.
However, on this comfortable island there is something very precious: the Philosopher's Path.
There is such a path, it exists, in Tokyo - sorry in Kyoto, in Japan.
It [was originally] called the path of meditation: the seasons go by, people walk along it, the phenomena of nature repeat themselves and you have a sense of security, a sense of order that cushions surprises or even eliminates them. It's a bit like when the Maya of Yucatan, seeing Cortès' ships appear on the horizon, said: How strange, there's a bizarre weather condition.
They did not recognize the ships.
But on Comfort Island there's a minority, and this minority is formed of the creative ignorants.
They don't like it on Comfort Island much, they consider it a kind of penal colony. So they take a ship, the ship of creative ignorance, and set sail. The ship heads towards a land where extraordinary things happen.
The ship really exists; it was launched to sea by the Unreasonable Institute, built in Boulder, Colorado, a few years ago.
Who are the passengers on this boat?
They are saplings, or are like saplings.
A sapling says to its father: When I grow up I don't want to be a tree, I want to be a book. Preferably an e-book, so that my family does not have to die, because one must cut down trees in order to make the paper.
So they are rebels, people who do not follow the paths traced out by the knowledge maps of their parents.
However, the same saplings, while rebels, don't like passive ignorance. Who does not want to climb the pyramid that leads to knowledge?
But here there is a difference between people who are very passionate about their knowledge and those who rebel. The passionate ones descend inside the pyramid going thoroughly, in great detail, and gradually descend missing the light, losing the vision. They increasingly know more about less, until they know everything about nothing.
And the more they know, the less truly new things are done, what actually doesn't happen to creative ignorants.
And so we have, as I call it, the Syndrome of the Well. There are some striking examples.
Blackberry, on the appearance of the iPhone said, this is a toy.
And there are many examples of these syndromes, and Blackberry, now, in the end is trying to recover by taking other points of view.
Not to mention Nokia, recently, and so on.
So what is the next big thing to put in motion?
It is precisely this kind of ignorance, an ignorance that is learned, which is aware, genuine, full of purpose, of motivation, of imagination, that is creative.
But these gentlemen, these rebels, are they isolated examples?
No, they have a long history, from Socrates to St. Augustine to Nicholas of Cusa, who wrote about ignorance that is learned; to the German philosopher Fichte, who said that the conquest of 'not knowing' is an infinite journey; to the reformer of education in the United States, Dewey, who wrote about how important genuine ignorance is in changing understanding.
And today, Hans Magnus Enzensberger, a German polemicist, polemical political scientist, has written that creative ignorance comes from gestures of refusal of maps of acquired knowledge. And a colleague of ours, of mine - I'm an academic myself, Professor Stuart Firestein, famous biologist at Columbia University, gives a course called Ignorance and wrote a book called <i>Ignorance</i>. He tells his students to acquire the negative capability, to live in mysteries, in the unknown and in the unexplored.
I myself am holding, at my university, in Dublin, a dialogue, a conversation on ignorance and will probably do the same thing this winter in another school in Paris.
Of course, the passage from the house of knowledge to the house of creative ignorance is one that is very difficult, dangerous: it requires the abilities of an acrobat, a very experienced acrobat because over him, against him, blow the winds.
And what are these winds?
They are the winds of the traditional school, and above all, in countries like ours, of professional orders that fix, as in the Middle Ages, rules, standards that hinder the creative ignorant in the creation of new paths outside the maps of knowledge.
And speaking of the traditional school, I would like to remind you of an Italian intellectual of the early 20th century, Giovanni Papini.
Giovanni Papini, in the 1910's, wrote that schools should not have desks and chairs anymore, that it was necessary to replace the classes with an experiment laboratory, where students, among themselves and with the teachers, talk about their studies and their research.
Dialogue, conversation, even telling a colleague, Look, I use your idea in a field where you don't, in a different field. Thus beginning a game that is at the same time one of cooperation and competition, a co-opetition, as they call it in Asia.
But did real Papinis sort some effect?
Yes, but unfortunately not in Italy.
You will have read, some of you, in Corriere della Sera, some weeks ago, that the Rector, the President of Stanford said, No more lessons.
They are all online, all the material is free online.
The Stanford campus serves to allow discussion between students and teachers. (Applause)
Thank you.
This is what Stanford has done, but it's not enough.
While we want to reform our universities, in the most advanced nations, universities are redefined, not reformed.
It is the futurists, the technologists, the scientist entrepreneurs who are creating the university of the third millennium.
And the Singularity University, who some, especially young people, know, has also landed to some extent in Italy.
For this university, we have invented, with a colleague of mine, who is the vice-president of the Intel Corporation, this formula, which is vaguely reminiscent of the physics of Einstein. It is a metaphor.
A university that creates, is an entrepreneurial energy source (E) which is equal to a mass (M) of the motivations of the participants to move knowledge to action multiplied by the three creativities (C) of Akio Morita, founder of Sony: creativity in technology, creativity in product planning, and creativity in marketing.
The Minerva Schools have revived, are reviving, the epopee of the wandering clerics, which had its centre of gravity in Bologna, when, in 1088, the first university of the second millennium came into being.
The community of Minerva students, who come from all over the world, take the first semester in San Francisco.
They all stay together, live together - the lessons are online, even if the teachers are from San Francisco.
In the six subsequent semesters they will move on to six different cities of the world considered key cities for learning the processes of disruptive innovation.
Finally, there is the Unreasonable Institute, that of the famous ship.
This is the ship which, riding the seas, brings the saplings in those cities which are considered key ports of innovation in the world today.
One thing I would love to see also in this country.
The interesting thing with all these phenomena is that the participants, not knowing that a thing cannot be done, and that if you do, you are wrong, do it and succeed, like Dropbox and PayPal founders did.
So, to conclude my talk with you, I would like to say this: I wish those people with much knowledge not to descend too deep into the well, to use maps to do incremental innovation, to do better what they already know, that's great, but not to descend too deep into the well. Respond to consumers' demands.
To the creative ignorant who abandon the maps not to respond to consumer demands, but in order to grasp their latent needs, I wish a pleasant ignorance, but one that is creative.
To all of you, and to them, I wish a time rich of the wonders of the unexplored.
Thank you.
(Applause)
My grandmother was a beautiful woman, and back then I was a very curious kid who made her a promise when she died of cancer -
an almost impossible promise.
She said, “I’ll understand why, so you no longer die”.
I lived in Verona, where it would snow.
I’d always get emotional when it was, and one day I saw on a stamp a snowflake like I’d never seen when it had snowed before.
So I decided to try and pay attention to it.
When I was four I got a microscope from a grandfather, one for kids and I started using it whenever I could.
This passion has been with me since then, and is still a big part of my life, but one of the most important things that I’m most proud of in my career, that’s sort of related to the promise I made to my grandmother was when a colleague and I understood that aloin - using microscope - a molecule derived from the aloe plant, was important in the treatment of child neuroblastoma, and caused her to lose a child. But what was even
more interesting at that time, more than the work we published on "Cancer Research", was when the same colleague decided to have another child.
We were already immersed in a nanotech hurricane, a hurricane of passion for nanotechnology, one that all of you are also immersed, that permeates everyone, even those you’d less expect to.
Nanotechnological hurricane, nano.
I mean, I won’t ask you to pull out a hair; but if you did, and tried to see it, to observe it, you’d be able to see it. Now imagine watching something 100,000 times smaller.
That's what I mean, when I say nano scale.
100,000 times smaller than a hair.
When I say nanotechnology, I mean -
Let me express it in Richard Feynman's words, a nobel-prize winning physicist in a visionary lecture at CalTech in 1959, just after Christmas.
"I would like to describe a field where little has been done, but in which an enormous amount can be done in principle. It’s is not quite the same as the others in that it will not tell us much of fundamental physics, but might tell us many interesting things about the strange phenomena that occur in complex situations, and most importantly, would have a huge amount of technical applications.
What I want to talk about is the problem of manipulating and controlling things on a small scale".
Feynman said that on December 29th, 1959.
So, when I say nanotechnology, I mean our shared capacity and ability to control material and observe it on such a small scale.
Feynman was asked the one thing he’d want his students to remember from his lectures.
The first thing that came him to mind was something that you might remember from school: F = MA.
Force, F, is equal to mass times acceleration.
He thought about it a bit more, and something else came to mind: Einstein, E = MC squared.
Mind you, energy tied to mass, multiplied by a huge number like the speed of light, squared.
Then, the definitive answer came to him. And Feynman said: “I’d like them to always keep in mind, and I’d like you to always keep in mind, that matter is made of atoms.”
That's what exists at the nano scale.
And that’s what we’ve got to remember when we get sucked into this hurricane of passion that in a sense, recalling this morning's wonderful speech, remembers "Like a hurricane" by Neil Young in an incredible love story, that’s like a hurricane, that holds you and never lets you go.
So, we were talking atoms,
those little Lego blocks.
Six atoms.
We share six atoms.
Well, 99% anyway, then there are some other, different atoms.
Huge numbers, to be sure, and we share them all, but then you might ask yourselves, why don't your neighbor or me look like George Clooney - or the person next to you, either.
Because the really important thing is how these atoms are organized at a very, very small scale, called the nano scale.
This is also true for little boys and girls - my little niece, Irene, in this case - or for iCub, the little humanoid robot.
And, look, if you look closely at what happens to Irene, to my niece - she also shares with us those six atoms - the iCub need something more, because they’re made of plastic, steel, silicon.
It’s a bit more complicated, in a sense, to figure out.
But if we give Irene a little candy, if i give you a little piece of candy, you’d be fine all day, up until the evening.
Sure you won’t be that happy, but you’d be able to do all the things you normally would.
For an iCub, however, a candy would make no difference at all. They need something else, a quantity of power that some of you could compare to a washing machine in spin cycle.
That little candy for us, a very important piece of candy, is what’s called deoxyribose.
What a strange name.
But if I’d said DNA?
Two meters of DNA that are coiled up inside each of your 100 trillion cells, in your 76 organs, where everything is written down.
And these are a few notes with which one could make infinite combinations, a little bit like music, where just seven notes can make an infinite number of melodies, and all of this highly compacted information, if you were to put it inside an egg, you’d be able to collect a vast majority of data available today: big data.
At Harvard, they were able to encode onto DNA, one gram of DNA, 700 terabytes of data.
OK sure, that’s a huge number: but instead of thinking about numbers, think that 700 terabytes would weigh 150kg of traditional memory, against one single gram.
So, in this hurricane of nanotechnology, there’s one revolution after another.
Revolutions.
Well, we’ve already spoken about revolutions today, we went through many of them.
The Jacquard textile machine; the railways; the car; the computer. But if you think about it, the textile machine has remained the same.
Besides maglev, the railways are always something that travel on two rails.
You can change the engine in your car, the way we produce energy, but you’re still behind a steering wheel on four tires.
Maybe not though, it’ll drive itself, but that’s still the point.
Computers are lighter, store more data, better connected, but stays the same.
Incredibly, nanotechnology is being revolutionized day after day.
With a pencil, you drawn a sketch, a doodle on a sheet of paper
The pencil, a genius idea of the Bernacottis. She first suggested putting graphite inside a piece of wood.
And from that piece of wood, stuffed with graphite, well, when force is applied to a piece of paper, you detach the carbon atoms that make up the graphite.
How are they structured?
It’s one atom, carbon.
It’s structured in loose layers that break up easily.
But look, if carbon had to structure itself, the same atom, in a different way, it would be a diamond.
I can see some of you smiling, Some women might be happier to receive a diamond as a gift instead of a pencil, maybe not her husband, though.
And if we manage to get a single sheet from that graphite, a single monoatomic layer of carbon, what we get is graphene, an incredibly flexible material.
You can see in these animations what is flexible and what isn’t, conducts electricity, is transparent or opaque.
If a woman had to go to the opening night at the opera, was all dressed up, a little bit late, but that’s OK, and only at the last minute realized she had no bag to match her dress.
We're going to the premiere, something quite important.
Were the bag made of graphene, she could take a picture of the dress send the information wirelessly to the bag and voilà!
She could go to that opening night perfectly dressed.
It might be a bit frivolous.
Here’s an animation to show what we can do with graphene. With only three grams of it, we could - if we wanted, we could do it tomorrow morning - cover an entire football pitch.
But we’ve already done many things, they’re commonplace at this point: there are graphene powders and inks with which we can make helmets which are already on the market, shoes, tennis raquettes.
Many objects, starting from nothing
but carbon organized in a slightly different way than graphite and diamond.
You can make sails stronger and lighter, and your mobile phone -
we do that already. And these sails used in regattas, and your mobile phone could be like this today. And the screen protector that you use to protect it.
Now, that graphene sheet, a single-atom thick structure of carbon atoms, if you crumple it just a little, it has hills and valleys.
The valleys collect hydrogen and the hills release it.
Why not try to make batteries, and perhaps think of a way to make hydrogen-powered cars.
This is the nanotech hurricane we are surrounded by, and what does my little niece Irene think?
She's OK with that.
For Irene, nothing is impossible.
It's a bit like when Mandela said, "It always seems impossible, until it’s done.”
For Irene, everything is possible.
She knows she's now in a reality where, to be aware of what’s coming, she's got to learn, She's got to study, to understand.
She’s in a knowledge vortex, one which comes from learning the fundamentals, which means studying in school both greek and quantum mechanics.
She enters that fantastic tale, I don't know how many of you have read "The Aleph" by Borges.
It's a wonderful story, you walk into a house, go down 19 steps, bend over a bit, I would do that in spite of my back pain, actually, but you can see everything from there.
We have this in our hands, this nanotechnological hurricane.
So, what would I like to leave you with from this quick talk?
Well, two things.
Two things for when you get home.
One is a bet.
I’d like to make a bet with you and I'd like you to go back home with at least one new idea.
One.
It seems not so much - a new idea - but it is.
And the other, first for the youngsters but actually for everyone, is that this technological hurricane changes all the time, it's constantly changing.
[Think about] the chances of growth, work and jobs that we have.
A lot of new jobs are being created by this nanotechnological hurricane.
A lot.
They’re almost unimaginable.
One after another.
And I think that this is that thing that brings us all together. fond members of this technological advancement on a very, very small scale.
Thank you so much for your attention.
(Applause)
Pain is a thing: suffering is a choice.
That's it. Pain does exist: we all, sooner or later, descend in some abyss, more or less deep down.
And when you're there, you don't know how to get back up.
Anyone who says to you "be strong" is going to get screwed.
And that's right: the dark forest is personal and exclusive.
Then there's the suffering,
which is a choice, a daily choice.
At a certain point, you're so familiar with the abyss that you will look up and think,
"What will I do? Do I go up or not?"
I do remember every little thing of those moments: the excruciating pain, the terror of dying, the dreams while I was in a coma.
But I also remember a great strength, a strength that made the difference, shared with my family, friends, nurses, doctors.
The pain remains, but we can choose what to do with it.
Whether to carry it around, to work, on the beach on holiday, on the sunbed, under the umbrella, when it's raining, while having a drink or running.
I, for example, take him for a walk to the mountains, to higher and higher peaks.
I've always been a determined, athletic boy.
I've always had that look, that spark of audacity in my eyes.
I always try to go beyond that personal physical limit of mine.
Before that date, before January 21, 2015, I was known as an ordinary boy from a remote country village near Lucca, with a great passion for mountains, who did every mountain specialty: cave, trekking, alpinismo.
Mostly, in that period, I practiced climbing, where I achieved great results by climbing even the seventh grade.
I used to run, but I rarely did it: only once in a while, for training.
I used to delay a lot of things, too many.
I had many limits - not physical, but mental limits - to be able to follow my dreams fully.
I thought I had unlimited time, but in reality I was wrong.
Little did I know what the fate had in store for me.
At 29, I knew that pain called fulminating meningitis.
Collapse; ICU; coma; a five-month ordeal.
I wake up, I suffer, but I choose.
I'm no longer who I used to.
Meningitis, I call it my inconvenience, a bacterium, a meningococcus - as I always say, it's an ugly beast - which in a few hours robs you of everything.
We can talk about "fulminant meningitis with meningococcal sepsis", that's the correct term.
I remember, that morning: it was very early, I wake up and I felt a cold I had never felt before.
I'm not a cold person at all, but that cold came from within.
I was under a lot of blankets and clothes: it didn't go away.
Suddenly the fever, a temperature rising higher and higher.
And with every passing hour, I was getting weaker and weaker.
Every time I put my foot on the ground, I felt a strong pain, as if I traded on needles, a pain that gradually, but constantly increased.
Suddenly, it got to 43 degrees.
I thought: the thermometer is definitely broken.
Soon after, the darkness.
I went into a coma, only to wake up a month later in the hospital.
Unaware of everything, without knowing why.
I'm here by miracle.
When I woke up, I thought: Damn, I'm alive.
I was lucky.
Today, my body is shaped around signs [of] a disease that tried to take my life, but failed.
I only allowed it to take off seven fingers and part of my legs.
Yet, it's to these signs, to this sudden destiny, within my new life, that I actually owe my new life.
These signs today have "ploughed" through my face and cut my skin.
Accepting my body?
No problem: just a moment, the time to wake up.
My mind never understood the real change, it was just a new way of doing things.
I can be what I never thought I'd be: my own hero.
The way I perceive myself is exactly just as before.
Instead of legs, I would have had prosthetics: titanium and carbon.
Thanks to these two elements, I was able to take my first steps in my new life, between hopes, fears and moments of joy.
Thanks to these new legs, I have never lost the strength and the idea that I will do the same things I used to do.
It's true: I had to learn to walk, write and eat again.
Suddenly, I had to face a new enemy: everyday life.
I faced this challenge like all the challenges that life, and the mountain, threw at me; and I won it.
I learned to appreciate and value what I was left with, without wasting too much time thinking about what I no longer have.
I learned to be looked at differently; but it never made me uncomfortable, in any situation.
I feel great about myself, I have my dreams, my passions.
The reason people look it's because they're not used to seeing.
My body, my brain always projects an image I'm familiar with. Nothing can change it:
thinking about my footless life has never frightened me, in any situation.
For example, I remember with great joy the night when, together with my friends, much to my surprise, unbeknownst to me, they organized a party in my little town.
I never thought there could be so many people there.
While talking all together that evening about climbing tours in the mountains, I thought: yay, I'm up, I can go everywhere.
Actually, I was barely standing up that night, using crutches.
However, the idea of having a bit of normal life thrilled me like a teenager on her first date; and anyway, the company and affection of that evening really made me present. And once again, despite everything, the protagonist of an important evening.
Nobody cared what feet I was wearing, nobody noticed my wide white pants to hide the prosthesis.
On the other hand, they complimented me very much on my smile.
But after all, people look at us the way we consider ourselves.
The more we esteem ourselves, the better people will look at us.
It's hard to believe it, for those who only see the images.
What I had to accept instead was that fatigue, when the normality of daily gestures turns into pain and effort.
Training a normal body requires effort.
Training a body with two prostheses requires determination, concentration, endurance and strength.
I put all of myself, in those first workouts, I remember very well the immense effort: but little by little, those very small results came in, and satisfaction grew.
And so I set my mind to achieve increasingly difficult goals.
At the beginning it was a few steps in the paths behind the house, together with my dog Kira.
Then I went to the running track, for the first few runs; and even there it took a lot of determination to endure countless problems and pains given by these prostheses.
It dawned into my mind already in the hospital: I want to run.
I have no logical explanation: my reaction, as soon as I knew my feet were gone, was: I want to run!
It's not a purely logical explanation.
The destiny wanted to stop me, and I suddenly thought: my worst revenge is to go fast, run.
But the yearning to run grew stronger and stronger, day by day.
Until, after the first implants, I started to have some walks in the mountains.
My desire to start was unstoppable.
I had everything; the desire and determination.
But the most and the best was missing - that is to say, the running prostheses that were impossibly expensive for me.
I lunched a fundraising campaign and within a month I successfully raised double the funding goal.
We made a few prostheses: a couple of track prostheses, the ones I wear on, and a couple of racing prostheses.
So I finally had all the tools to race: from that moment on, it was only up to me.
And so I did.
I managed to join, in my still ongoing sprinting career, the Italian national team in the Fiamme Azzurre sport group.
Four Italian records: 100, 200, 400 meters and 4x100 relay.
I became the first Italian biamped man to run under 12 seconds in the 100 meters flat; I couldn't win a silver medal in 2017: two bronzes, and a silver at the Europeans.
All thanks to these thousands of people who made a small contribution to a great dream of mine.
As a child, I also had a dream: I wanted to become an athlete.
One way or the other, I made it.
I don't think I can't do it now. First, I assume that I can do everything. That is to say, I don't limit myself by saying: ah, God, I have prostheses, I can't go there; I have prostheses, it's risky.
No, there are no thoughts like this in my crazy brain.
I'm the same person: in my brain there's always the same image of myself, just as before.
Nothing, nothing can change it.
Sometimes I happened to fail miserably, like when I tried to climb Mount Prado, just a month after the very first prosthesis.
I remembered an easy path, but I didn't even get halfway there.
Or when I first came back to climbing.
In my mind all the patterns and movements were in place, but they didn't match with my new feet.
These failures weren't accounted for, but they were actually legit.
And they helped me to understand what I needed to expand and modify, and learn how to use it
so I wouldn't fail again.
My story, however, does not only smell like revenge: also of passion, the one for the mountain,
the one that this destiny, this bacterium, tried to wipe out and failed.
Everest 2020-2021: 8848 meters, the top of the world, never reached by a multiamputee like me.
A goal, a finish line, a stubborn certainty: I will get there.
The flag I will plant on the summit will not only be for me, but for all those who need to believe in something, to believe in the best things in life that can always happen.
All those who need to be inspired.
Do you think I'm conveying a message?
Yes, I do hope so.
What I most like, and motivate me, are not really the medals, the achievements.
But knowing that I've helped someone who, like me, had an inconvenience, makes me even happier and more motivated to go higher and higher.
My words are a confirmation: the pain remains, the legs aren't there and fingers aren't there either.
But suffering is a choice.
I made a choice. Successes at work, social achievements, money - a lot, sometimes a hell of a lot; relational micro-victories; unexpected triumphs; beautiful comfortable homes; even our families, so reassuring and so unknown, always make us feel winning, almost immortal.
We only realize this when we soar past our small/large personal affairs.
I, Andrea Lanfri, simply a man, have won.
I won because I'm full of dreams, even if something tried to tear my life in pieces.
But life is strange: it hides, and you only feel its presence when you realize it can slip away. And the only thing we can really do
is grab it with all our strength so that it won't let it slip away.
My story, after all, is simply life versus death.
(Applause)
Ladies and gentlemen, welcome to today's talk, which will deal with a particular musical instrument called the launeddas.
This instrument is only found in Sardinia, but it originated from, or spread to, other corners of the earth.
I will try to explain, as much as I can, how an instrument such as the launeddas has moved around in the last 3, 4 or 5,000 years.
First of all, I would like to say that the launeddas are considered a "holistic" instrument, which means that it is an instrument whose music touches the feelings, the body, the heart, everything.
This is the definition given by Saam Schlamminger, a German researcher who studies trance music.
Other researchers, including a Spanish friend, say that the launeddas are a "total instrument"
because they involve melody, harmony, rhythm, counterpoint and a series of elements that resulted in Sardinians calling the instrument "a concert"! This is because nothing is missing.
I believe that if you want to understand the launeddas, the best thing is to watch a short video featuring Michele Deiana, a young launeddas player who studied with Orlando Mascia, performing a brief piece of a "Procession" on the launeddas.
(Music) (Applause) Now you have an idea of what the launeddas are.
To be more specific, here, we have a picture of the regular instrument and, to be even clearer, I have one instrument here with me.
This is an instrument built only with natural ingredients: there are three different types of cane, with some beeswax above, and some string to tie them.
All these materials are organic. It is an "organic" instrument, as the English would say, as it is built completely of natural elements.
The weight is incredible: 60-70 grams maximum per instrument!
Now, this instrument only exists in Sardinia, where it has been living for thousands of years. It developed and expanded a lot.
To get an idea, let's see an image depicting all the possible variations
within the launeddas. This is not just one instrument, but it consists of nine families, each of them cut into several keys, as shown in this image - you can find it on our website - where you can appreciate the complexity of this instrument.
87 instruments altogether.
As we have seen in Michele's performance, each instrument has three mouthpieces,
so we have 87 instruments with about 300 mouthpieces. This is something incredible, even just from a construction point of view.
Each player is able to manufacture these instruments, or they will find an artisan who can build them.
But where does this instrument come from?
We have a picture of a "bronze statuette of a launeddas player," which is kept in the Archaeological Museum of Cagliari. As observed by Bentzon, a Danish researcher who studies the launeddas and Sardinian music,
this is the first documented evidence of the launeddas, showing that in the Nuragic period, 1000 B.C., the launeddas were already known and played to such an extent that they dedicated them a statuette.
Hence, this instrument comes from the Nuragic civilization, it has survived until these days and today, it has the same structure and, hopefully, the same music, but we do not know that.
Today, we find similar instruments in several areas in Europe. The Danish researcher Andreas Fridolin Weis Bentzon hypothesized that this three-pipe instrument existed in many parts of Europe, but during the Middle Ages, it transformed into the bagpipe.
This picture indicates where the instrument once existed, in the green areas, but completely lost its original form: Scotland, Ireland, the United Kingdom, Spain and even Sicily, where, only yesterday, some friends and I discovered the evidence of the existence of a three-pipe instrument.
In these areas, the instrument disappeared completely: we have found depictions of it, but no instrument.
The only place where it survived is Sardinia!
The Danish researcher Bentzon assumed that, during the Middle Ages, this instrument turned into the bagpipe, for reasons that might be related to the cane, in my opinion.
This slide shows...
These are two images from the manuscript
"Cantigas de Santa Maria" by Alfonso the Wise, circa 1280.
Bentzon was familiar with the first image, on the left, showing the so-called "Spanish launeddas," but not with the second, which we discovered only recently.
Both images were part of the same book. They confirm Bentzon's theory, according to which this instrument turned into the bagpipe during the Middle Ages.
As you already know, the bagpipe spread all over the world thanks to the English, the Dutch and the Scots.
Today, we find bagpipes in Canada and in America, and all Arab states have their own bagpipe orchestra.
The Sheik of Oman, who visited Cagliari, also had an orchestra of bagpipers, who wore a kilt like the Scots. Clearly, in those hot areas, wearing a kilt must be pleasant.
Bagpipes spread all the way to India and Southern Africa. They are everywhere.
But, until proven otherwise, the instrument that gave life to the bagpipes is the three-pipe instrument, that is, the launeddas.
This is very important for us, since it shows that Sardinians have the ancestor of all the world's bagpipes, and that we can show the whole world the skills needed to build the instrument, its reeds, etc.
In other places, this instrument disappeared
because the heart of the instrument is the reed, which is this thin, nearly invisible strip without which the instrument cannot make any sound.
You need a special type of wild cane to make it, which is found only in Sardinia.
Without this special cane, in other countries, like France, Spain or England people used to make their reeds from elder. However, since a reed in the mouth can get wet and blocked, they introduced the bag to be able to blow the air without a reed in their mouth.
We could make reeds for all the world's bagpipes here in Sardinia, and this is already happening, to some extent.
This picture shows
a friend, Pittanu Perra, an engineer who researched the launeddas at the Iscandula School, and trained to build them.
He is so good that, currently, he is exporting reeds and pipes everywhere.
He does not charge for it, as he has a job already, but sometimes, he gets instruments made with his reeds, in return.
So he receives instruments from Japan, Norway, from any corner of the world.
Why did I mention Pittanu, with his selection of reeds and cane pipes for any kind of instrument?
Because this Sardinian skill, the ability to build launeddas, is not taught at school, but it is transmitted orally from the maestro, who teaches how to build and play this instrument.
His specialist skills are so high-level that Pittanu Perra was mentioned at New York's Institute Of Fine Arts as the living hope of recreating the instruments of music archeology.
He already managed to rebuild the Greek instrument aulos.
Last summer, we were in Greece together and the Greeks went literally mad, they said: "Zeus or Apollo must have sent you!"
They had not heard an Aulos for 2,000 years, and Pittanu built and played one.
He also built an instrument of the Minoan civilization which also disappeared, with the only thing that survived being... a drawing in a grave.
He also built other instruments, which are currently used by Professor Billia, a music archeologist, in her conference tours in America and elsewhere.
Therefore, what I would like to say is that Sardinia has a treasure which is the envy of everyone.
Other places, other nations or states would have made this a key issue.
First of all, we have a rare live remnant of the Nuragic civilization, which is still producing music.
The launeddas should at least become a symbol of Sardinia; there are already many people who envy us for it.
For example, our Irish friends have a Celtic harp on their passports.
In the future, should we have our own passport, we could have the launeddas on the cover, so that wherever we go, people could say:
"Ah, you are from Sardinia!"
because of the unmistakeable symbol of the launeddas.
All the skills acquired by many young players and builders should be taken into consideration by governmental institutions and taught in schools. These young people make great sacrifices, including financial ones, to earn these skills.
There should be some kind of support to allow the growth and the development of this knowledge and these skills, which could in turn benefit a lot of people.
Another important thing is... the cane. This is an asset that in Sardinia does not get its deserved attention, but that is crucial for manufacturing reeds for oboes, clarinets, saxophones, etc.
A selective farming of this cane should be developed, from farming to cut and seasoning, up to the finished product.
Because we could supply reeds for all the bagpipes in the world, which are using plastic reeds!
We could supply a biological element for all plastic reed users and improve the sound of their instruments.
I would also love it if music students, in Sardinian schools, at least, could ditch those plastic recorders and use a mancosedda, a small pipe with the same notes,
maybe even one they built themselves!
All those who manage to build their own instrument at school fall in love with music, because everybody loves working with natural building blocks.
These are just some of the things I would like to see happening in Sardinia, but now I would like to close this conference with another video, where Michele Deiana shows us some ballu tundu music, another crucial element of the Sardinian repertory.
Let me thank all of you, as we listen together to Michele's sonata.
(Music) (Applause)
Good evening!
This is the bay of Khor Rori in southern Oman, a port that flourished in antiquity with the trade of incense.
The Punta Linke at 3,629 meters in Ortles Massif.
And the church of Sant'Andrea in Mantua, the co-cathedral built by Leon Battista Alberti in the late 1400s.
These are just three of the places where the mountain guides of Trentino have worked in recent years.
Already at the beginning of the 90s with a group of colleagues, we started to look around because the situation was changing very quickly.
The glaciers, due to global warming, are shrinking dramatically, ridges will become increasingly more gullies where there were the classic mountaineering routes on ice now, many of them, aren't there anymore.
This is the glacier of Careser, now we arrived at about half of the area in 30 years.
The way Bonatti and Grand Dru in the Mont Blanc has fallen to about 400 meters.
So those mountains which we are loyal, far from crowding of the ski resort and from the lights of New Year's parties, struggle to remain intact, because in the meantime we are less and less to live there and also because, perhaps, the walls of the Dolomites, the wide horizons, and ridges of ice, do not enjoy the consideration that they deserve.
There are also other difficulties one above all: we have a bureaucratic meddling and viscous that slows us down in many activities.
This is a difficulty for us still higher than those that we face on the mountains.
With these assumptions, we tried to react and that is where did the idea of forming a cooperative come from, which is based on three key concepts: that of cooperation, innovation, and contextualization, understood as the capacity to undertake initiatives even outside the context of the high mountains, that belongs to us, and though looking to capitalize those that are our peculiar characteristics.
The boost came from the exchange we had during the training for work at height where we discussed with structural engineers, skilled workers, and other people with professions very different between them.
And that's where we thought in these activities perhaps we could even find a role for us.
We have the ability to move with a certain dexterity in harsh environments, we can well resist to fatigue, cold, skip a few meals, work all day if needed and also a bit of the night.
With these assumptions, we went in Oman where the Sultan decided to regularize a wall to achieve a maxi projection of about 500 feet in length and 130 in height.
Our job was to cut the roughness of the rock that stuck for more than five feet and profiling them with explosives.
Then British engineers wanted to fill all of polyurethane foam, Thing that we refused at all so to avoid an open dump.
We proposed another solution that we'll see.
Conditions were very difficult for us, almost absurd, because we had to work with temperatures of almost 50 degrees Celsius on the wall that required a series of adjustments.
We were working 10 hours a day with a break every two hours to rest and hydrate.
Heavy and uncomfortable at first, great respect for the camels because who harms a camel pay about 8,000 euro of fine.
We engineered a yard bringing something from Italy, the rest we built there on the spot.
Where you could not work with the machines, we worked as you normally do on the walls and always under the watchful eye of the police because they were extremely attentive to what was happening.
We then undermined all the holes and in the end we made them jump.
This is the result.
With these boulders we then filled the cavities, realizing the typical stone walls of the terraces that are used on the Alps without distorting the context of the places and this was appreciated.
Here, instead, we are on Mount Vioz, nearby is the tip Linke where there is a station of Kaiserschtzen that during the First World War had not only defensive functions but also logistics functions because there was a cable car coming up that was starting from Cogolo Peio.
The Superintendence of Cultural Heritage of the Province of Trento decided to restore these places to deliver them to the memory in the form of a museum.
Our job was to restore the artifact of which there were now only the walls and especially to free the ice tunnel dug into the permafrost, which is the soil permanently frozen, with the ice functioning as cement, of incoherent materials.
We realized the site by helicopter.
Over three years, we have worked to restore this gallery we also used infrared burners where there were unidentified objects because there were 75 mm artillery shells that we've found, several of them, and was not the case of hitting them with hammers.
Also the University of Milan intervened who adapted some columns of some sections of the ice.
When the weather was particularly bad we were busy by restoring the trail that was also used for the transportation of the food.
We used many tricks to resist these artifacts to the pressures of snow and also to the heat implementing the measures used in northern countries for railways.
This is the result of the, almost finished, gallery and this is how we see now the place with preparation completed.
The emotion that one should have when entering is that the soldiers are just gone.
This is the exit on the west side which faces the Alps.
In Mantua we have been called by the diocese after the earthquake of May 2011 because it had damaged the summit of the body of the dome this lantern at the base of the windows where the columns are very narrow and were almost sheared.
The project was to bring up eight lattice structures, connect them together and make a real circle.
We rent one of the largest cranes that there are in Italy, allowing to reach 400 feet high and 230 outreach.
You see very well in this photograph.
we were working up there every day.
The work of leveling required great concentration.
You had to be careful because under the square was not closed, so if anything escaped, also a nut of a bolt that escaped could create great damage.
We have set up our lines life and often working late again with a lot of concentration we managed to finish this job just before the arrival of the snow.
A few days later came the snow so we didn't have plenty of time.
So our choices were those of trying to find activities that would give us satisfactions about implementing these projects in cooperation between us, fact that is not at all obvious to mountain guides, and to try to bring improvements in the areas where you work on.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I'll tell you a story that started 1,2 billion years ago.
More or less.
It's a love story,
With a quite unusual couple: they are two black holes. For millions and millions years, they spin around each others, slowly getting closer and closer.
For millions and millions of years.
Even among black holes, love is complicated!
At a certain moment, they were so close that they collided and merged in a single black hole.
That generated a huge gravitational wave, which traveled across space and time for countless years and kilometers, and last September it got here, on Earth, and we saw it!
Then, after months and months of analysis, statistics, and whatnot - and poor sleep also -, we announced to the whole world we have seen the first gravitational wave.
This story could finish now, but I bet you have a lot of questions now.
Like, what the hell is this guy talking about?
What are these gravitational waves?
Time for some history.
Let's come back to 100 years ago, when a man you probably heard about already, he was called Einstein, if I remind well, wrote a paper about something he called relativity. He said a lot of crazy things:
moving clocks run slower than still ones; motion shrinks objects; space and time interact among each other.
Amazing!
And he didn't stop: ten years later, he also said that each mass affects the space around it. This is really crazy.
And as I already said, one year later he introduced gravitational waves.
But what does it mean that mass warps space?
Imagine that space is like an elastic surface. If you walk on it, you just go straight, and it is done.
But if you put something on the surface, it gets distorted, and you have to walk around this warp.
So, for instance, the Sun warps the surface around, e we let the Earth run around it.
What happens?
Earth follows this warp,
like when there's a vortex in the sea, and ships follow the vortex and cannot escape.
Let's go on a bit, now.
Ok, let's take mass in movement on the surface.
If these masses move, the surface also moves,
and what are the consequences?
That not only near masses move, but also those far away.
If we drop a stone in the water, what happens?
Waves propagate far away.
So this surface that continuosly moves far away from the masses, these are gravitational waves.
Now we know what they are.
What do they do?
When a gravitational wave comes to Earth, it happens what you see in this movie.
That's scaring, isn't it?
So, why on September 14th you did not see this?
Why you do not see your friends moving like this?
When a gravitational wave arrives, distances are squeezed and stirred.
But they shake very, very little. We are speaking of distance less than a nucleus of an atom, we cannot see it with our eyes.
So, how can we detect a gravitational wave?
In the 60s, scientists developed resonant detectors, made with aluminium bars.
They tried to see if these bars were enlarging.
The idea was good... but it didn't work very well.
So, some years later, they built these so-called interferometers.
What are interferometers?
We take lasers, because lasers are always <i>cool</i>.
And what do we do?
We shoot a laser beam and let it run on two different directions.
The laser beams travel the same distance,
and when they come back to the starting point, they sum and cancel, so we don't see anything.
But if a gravitational wave passes by, the two distances traveled by the lasers beams are different, they no longer annihilate like before, and we see some light.
Looking at this light, we see the waves.
So, we essentially understand how to see these waves, but what can we do with them?
Now our friend Fido, behind me, invites you to play a game.
At my signal, substantially close your ears for some seconds.
I raise my hand,
so when I'll put it down,
you can restart to hear.
Ready? Understood? OK?
3-2-1, Go!
Good, very good.
A second game now, our friend likes to play.
Now you close your eyes, and my voice will tell you
when to open them back again.
Ready? 3-2-1 Go!
Now relax.
You are in the park, birds are chirping.
There's also a gentle breeze.
Your boyfriend, or girlfriend brought a picnic. Ready to eat?
Go ahead.
OK, you can open your eyes.
What did we do with these games?
When we close our ears, that's how we looked at the Universe so far: with light, with eyes.
This is the light visible spectrum, but you can see that there are holes, it's called absorption spectrum.
Why?
Because when light travels in our atmosphere, it meets hydrogen, helium, nitrogen, and whatever is there.
Hydrogen takes something from the light, so does helium, nitrogen another piece, and we have holes.
Let's do one example: it is like sending a mail to a friend of yours, and the postman delete some words.
What a bastard...
You can read the mail, of course, but you lost some words.
Same thing with light.
Gravitational waves envelopes are so tough, that no postman can open them.
But there is more to it.
When you used only eyes, you could just see a stupid man on the teather with a hand up.
But when you listened to me, probably someone of you really felt to be in that park.
Gravitational waves are a new messenger to describe the Universe.
We have already a lot of them: visible light, infrared light, gamma rays, and everyone gives new, different information.
So even gravitational waves are in a sense our way to hear the Universe, not just see it.
So do they give us new information.
Really?
Yes!
Take black holes, for instance.
For the first time we really heard black holes speaking.
Up to now, we cannot see, because they are black.
Nothing can exit from a black hole.
But gravitational waves from black holes arrive to us.
And we saw it.
Should we stop here?
We can, but we want more.
There's more than black holes in the Universe.
Neutron stars are also there, for instance.
Neutron stars are objects more complicated than black holes: the equation of state, the internal processes...
Then light coming from neutron stars gives a lot of information, but we don't know everything.
We can learn more from gravitational waves coming from neutron stars,
when we will see them.
Something more?
Why not? Supernovae!
Super novae are huge explosions happening in the Universe, that make light, neutrinos, gamma rays and infrared light, and gravitational waves.
They're another messenger that can describe how these explosion could happen.
And last but not least, the Big Bang.
We know very little about it, basically nothing!
From its gravitational waves, however, we could discover the first moment of Universe's life.
Those moments we don't know yet.
So what do we do now?
Let's play!
Now really we can detect gravitational waves.
We can play with them.
We can go back to their generation process.
And we can learn something more about how love works in the Universe.
(Applause)
Becoming a parent, becoming a dad, is a wonderful experience.
When you have a child, you immediately think about his future, what you can do to raise this child well.
You also mind the details, the sport your child will practice, the school he/she will attend, the job they will excel at when they grow up; and maybe the grandchildren that will make you a grandfather.
But the moment they tell you - and your son is only two years old - your son is autistic, suddenly the future doesn't look like this anymore.
The future becomes shady.
Sport gets out of the picture, you don't see it.
School will be a problem, you already know.
You know there will be no jobs, no grandchildren.
In the US, an autistic child is born every 52 children, while in Europe there are about six million people with autism, the size of a small State.
And what about Italy?
In Italy there are 600,000 people with autism.
Let me say it again, 600,000: it means one child, one person out of 100 Italians.
And despite these striking statistics, very little is done for autistic children.
Mind you, the most brilliant initiatives in Italy - and when I say brilliant, I really mean shining bright - not only in Italy, worldwide.
Maybe someone recognizes the White House, in this image.
April 2nd is World Autism Day: institutional buildings light up in blue.
Someone has decided that blue is the colour of autism, so institutional buildings light up in blue.
So beautiful. Very useful to raise awareness, yet absolutely useless to meet all the needs of people and children with autism.
On April 3rd, the blue of these beautiful buildings is gone, and schools are still left with no support teachers. Which still lack on April 5th, April 6th, May 7th - even in June, at the end of school.
And the few support teachers are often not specifically prepared.
Early diagnosis is needed.
My son was diagnosed, fortunately, at the age of two. Early diagnosis is crucial:
but then, before he starts the treatment, he gets four, five, six years old.
Early diagnosis becomes somewhat useless - it's a good thing to have, but it really has to be done early.
Therapies are fully covered by families.
The National Health System funds next to nothing on autism, And these crumbs are contended by us all.
For example, me and my family, we did psychomotricity for my son, speech therapy, then hippotherapy and all that could be done, all the things that families tipically try to do for their children.
Then, aside from these classic therapies, we started making pizza.
Somebody's laughing.
Pizza: mozzarella, tomato, basil.
The food most eaten by Italians, and also a World Heritage.
Why did we start making pizza?
Because when you have an autistic child, as it happened to me, to my family, you tend to close yourself off.
In part, you close yourself off to protect your child, as it's hard to endure all this. When your child starts to flicker around, it's hard for you to stay calm. When your child begins to jump for no reason, and you see others watching him, it is difficult for you to remain calm. When your child talks to no one in the wind, utters meaningless words and you see others staring at you, your child screams and people judge you as a bad parent, because your child is rude.
Autistic boys are big, seemingly healthy boys, you can't really tell at first sight that they are autistic.
Why did we start making pizza?
Because it gave us a reason to invite a lot of people home.
Pizza got our friends coming to visit us and my wife and I could finally stay with our friends, with adults;
but the other children came too. My son Leo's classmates came, our friends' children came; and Leo could stay in his home, in a familiar environment, and do whatever he felt like.
He could jump, he could play, he could pull out his lightsaber.
Leo uses his lightsaber as a social connection tool to make friends,
He always carries two of them around: one for the stranger, and the other for himself.
So they play and he makes friends with other people, he carries friendship from home.
Now I have it: today he gave it to me,
I told him I would talk to strangers, and he said: Dad, bring the lightsaber.
So I'm glad Leo helped me with this, too.
It's getting difficult, we were saying; but Leo started making pizza with his mom one day, kneading pizza.
It looked like a strange thing, and talking about it, even with experts on autism, they told us that it's actually strange, because many kids with autism don't like to touch dough, flour, water,
so much so that at some point, me and my wife, we said: but if a little boy like Leo is able to knead dough, pizza, maybe even older guys can do it for fun. I mean, older autistic boys: and so we invited home Alessandro, Lorenzo, Gabriele, Lollo, the "good giant" Matteo, a series of boys of working age, between 18 and 24 years old, and we talked to their parents.
What was a playful thing for us - we told these parents - could become an opportunity for the future.
That future we failed to see.
We sometimes say "autistic spectrum" because it becomes a ghost: the future is a ghost, for us.
With pizza, instead, we thought we could shape this ghost, not just the round one.
And so we set our mind to organizing a pizza making class. Still, they told us: the kids will never make it.
There are too many variables at play.
And mostly, their predictions
were actually correct. We started with a group of ten kids: and the first ones who put their hands on the dough responded just as predicted by the psychological literature.
They put their hands in the dough and... brrr, gross!
A lot of them were disgusted,
but not all of them.
Alessandro, for example,
started making pizzas in a wonderful way.
His teacher, his maestro - we call him maestro - taught him that pizzas should be slapped from the inside out, so that the oxygen goes to the edge.
And the edge gets softer, tastier.
And Alessandro does it in a wonderful way.
The same maestro, two weeks later - damn it, I say - explained to Alessandro that pizza dough is a living thing.
Because it is made with sourdough, because it grows, because it has to rest for 72 hours.
Well, Alessandro stopped making pizzas.
Now some of you smiled because they got it quickly; it took us two weeks to understand.
Living things don't get slapped!
Alessandro; autistic; literal interpretation.
You don't slap living things: Alessandro stopped making pizzas.
We had to tell him he had to cuddle them, and he started cuddling the pizza.
We're the only ones in the world making cuddled pizza.
More than ethics: respect for the dough also!
(Laughter) Alessandro learned how to make pizzas, and Matteo also learned with him.
Matteo is the good giant, a two-meter tall autistic boy.
He's huge, and speaks with a soft white voice: when he talks it's a joy, a true wonder.
Alessandro and Matteo learned how to make pizzas, and we enrolled them in the World Pizza Championship. 700 pizza makers from all over the world: Japan, Greenland, America, Africa.
700 pizza makers.
We could have enrolled them in a special section, as one usually does with disabled people.
It would have been a cheap victory: they were the only two disabled ones!
One first, and the other second.
We decided to sign them up with all the other 700.
It was a game to us, not a competition.
Well, in this real-world game, the two autistic pizza makers both ranked in the first 100.
The cuddled pizza "rocked", you might say.
But what's the important thing?
It's not getting into the top 100; it's proving that you can do it.
It's proving that kids can learn a job, even those jobs they're not supposedly able to do, because they have a bad relationship with flour, with water, and all these things with a strange tactile experience.
But running a restaurant also takes waiters: pizza makers are not enough.
What did we do?
The guys who couldn't work the dough were enrolled them as waiters.
We set up for them a "mise en place" class.
I didn't even know what the "mise en place" was, I never did catering before "Pizza Aut", the name of this restaurant.
Anyway, we taught them how to serve in a professional way, how to clear and set the table in a professional way.
While we were doing that, we noticed a thousand problems.
Remeber, these are autistic kids: many of them have a hard time, relating to others is a major challenge.
Many of them have a hard time talking to others.
So we asked a multinational company, whose name I won't mention to avoid product placement, if they could build an app for us,
a visual app made entirely in augmentative communication, all with images.
This way, through images, the kids took the orders in an incredibly correct way.
They go to the customer at the table, with the help of images they recognize pizzas and their ingredients.
And they work as waiters, just like all other waiters do.
Of course, technology helps them.
The same happened with pizzas. I told you that the kids have become excellent at making pizzas: but when they take the shovel and put them in the oven, they forget to take them out, and burn them all.
Because autism is an evil beast: they turn away, prepare more, and forget about those.
We invented - it already existed, we just tinkered with it - a tunnel oven.
There is a sort of carousel: the kids put their pizza on this wheel, the wheel brings the dough inside the oven; the oven cooks it; the same wheel takes it out, and another autistic waiter takes it and brings it to the table.
Opening your mind, opening your heart, opening your thoughts bring us creative solutions that build inclusion.
Mind you: on July 9th, just to fully work on inclusion, we went to the Senate of the Republic to make pizzas.
It was an incredible experience: here is the press conference. At a certain point, Francesco stood up and blurted things out to the journalists.
Such a pleasant experience.
(Laughter) But what happened?
Senators of the Republic understood deeply, firsthand, what autism really is.
What this spectrum is, which affects people completely different from each other.
They understood, there's a lot of things you can do.
And with them, we're working on a bill to make it easier for autistic kids to enter the job market.
A law, hopefully, not only for Italy, a law that can be ported to other jurisdictions, where companies can consider hiring people with autism not out of social responsibility, not out of their good heart, but because young autistic people are just as capable of productive work.
When children work, they become extraordinarily capable, effective, their self-esteem goes up and they do incredible things.
Mind you, we did 100 nights around Italy in 100 different restaurants.
From Veneto to Calabria, Sicily, Marche: the moment we get to Sardinia, we've been everywhere in Italy.
In 100 evenings, we baked and served 30,000 pizzas.
And someone told us it would be impossible.
Actually, you can really do a lot of things by opening up, listening to what the kids are able to do and building the proper setting so they can do it.
After our visit to the Senate, and the preliminary work to create their jobs, we thought that our kids could finally take a different role in society: no longer as inmates, patients, disabled, handicapped; but as workers instead.
Look: right now you all, in the hall, are paying my boys a disability allowance.
The moment we hire these boys, they will pay your pension.
That's the real revolution, that's the real inclusion.
(Applause)
I want to start with a question, and I want your gut reaction.
By a show of hands, let me know how you think the world is doing. Who thinks it's doing okay?
Okay.
Lower your hands.
Who thinks things are going badly?
Okay, do the math for yourself.
This graphic was created by Hans Rosling, a brilliant Swedish scientist, maybe you've already seen it.
It shows us what happened over the last 218 years.
We're in 1800, each of these dots represents a country, the Y-axis is life expectancy,
the X-axis is income.
The tiny yellow dot out in front is England in 1800.
40 years of life expectancy, 2,500 euros of average income, in today's value.
Look what happened over the last 200 years.
You see? During the 1800s,
almost nothing happens.
Income goes up a little, but life expectancy stays very low.
Look what happens at the turn of the century.
See the dip in the years of the First World War?
It recovers immediately.
See the Second World War?
Now look what happens in the post-war period. An extraordinary, exponential acceleration.
It's a real transformation of society.
The global population, every country in the world attains a high level of well-being and life expectancy, which is really extraordinary.
There is a force, an extraordinary engine of growth that made this possible: technology.
And there is business, the operating arm of technology. Through companies, entrepreneurial activities, we managed to transform the human condition in an extraordinary way.
So today, there is a whole series of indicators you see here: poverty, education, literacy, democracy, vaccination, child mortality.
All these indices are extraordinarily positive as we could have never imagined a few decades ago.
But now I need you to look into the future a little.
Then I'll ask you another question.
When we think about the future, it seems far away.
Hands up, how many of you in this room have children?
Okay, a good half.
Think about the year when they'll be your age today.
Or, if you don't have children, when children born in 2018 will be your age today.
For me, it's 2051 and 2055.
Think about what happened in the previous 200 years, and imagine, with this extraordinary acceleration, what could happen in the next 20, 30, 40 years.
Bear in mind that we are experiencing a change that is not linear but exponential.
Exponential means that for a long time nothing happens, and then a kind of explosion.
You can see, when I was born, there were fewer than four billion people on Earth.
Now there are almost eight billion.
You'll realise that we're living in an era of disruption, of the unimaginable acceleration of a whole series of aspects, a whole series of trends, both positive and negative.
And we find the same dynamic almost everywhere.
There are hundreds of parameters, indicators, activities, and their impact that are following the same trend.
So, for a long time, nothing seems to happen, then we have this explosion.
We are in the middle of this explosion.
At the same time, we live on a planet that has been the same for billions of years.
There used to be so much planet, so few people; now there are so many people, and the planet hasn't changed.
And we have adopted an economic model that has potent consequences.
A model that worked fine until now but possibly won't lead us to a desirable future.
I'll show you what's happening today in the middle of the Pacific, in the Midway atoll.
Look at these images.
This is what happens when we adopt a business model that works up to a point but is poised to change in the future.
We see what's happening to animals, to the albatross, in the Midway, and actually, the same thing is also happening to us human beings.
So, as we look at this image, let's consider breastfeeding. In some areas of the planet, the most polluted ones, there are hundreds, thousands of toxic chemicals that are now found in breast milk.
Breast milk should be treated as toxic waste.
Without exaggeration, this is true even in Italy's most polluted areas, not to mention China or other regions.
It's clear that we must completely transform our economic model.
We must transform our paradigms.
And the other thing I want to share with you is this.
See this red line?
800 metres long, Sempione Park, not far from here.
If we consider a human body's volume, each one is about 70 litres.
All humanity, the whole human race could fit within an 800 metre cube.
The volume of every human on Earth today.
And yet this microscopic dot - this pixel, compared to the dimensions of the planet, through technology and its business models manages to impact the machinery of the entire planet.
You realise that the rate of change is not linear. The future will not be a projection of what we experienced in the past.
In the future we will see the drastic acceleration of a whole series of phenomena.
This wheel represents every country on Earth, and these animated bars represent the average temperatures compared to historical zero.
Look what happens from 2000 onwards.
The more red it becomes, the more the planet is heating up.
Do you see the non-linearity of these changes?
The sense of urgency that pushes us toward change?
What actually happened from 2000 onwards is that the oceans lost their capacity to absorb heat and excess emissions.
So the Earth, instead of continuing to absorb our excesses, began to turn into an emitter.
It is no longer able to absorb our environmental impact.
So, we have to move on from an extractive model - and I'm not just referring to the mining industry
but to a business model driven by a single goal: search for value, collect it, concentrate it, extract it and concentrate it, extract it and concentrate it.
Because this extraordinary machine that is business - companies today - if we look at the default model, it is driven by a single goal: distribute dividends or maximise value to shareholders.
If that's the single goal, the consequence is very simple but quite shocking. People and the planet are not considered in this business goal.
You must realise that although it worked until now, given the conditions and the speed of change we are experiencing, it won't work in the future.
It is essential that it changes.
To give an idea of ​​how well this model works, I'll show you an equation.
It seems a bit odd: 8 = 4,000,000,000.
This model of the value concentration has been working so well that today eight humans on Earth - they could fit in this golf cart - have as much wealth as the Earth's four billion poorest people.
So, business is an extraordinary force, like earthquakes, volcanoes and climatic events.
But the time has come to transform its very essence.
The time has come to transform it into something that is regenerative, not extractive, something that recognises the interdependence of everyone involved and the whole system it belongs to.
Let me read you this sentence: "Every company must serve a social purpose.
It must produce results that are not just financial, but that benefit all its stakeholders also, shareholders, employees, customers and the communities they operate in."
The first time I saw this, I thought it had been written by, maybe, Adriano Olivetti, an almost mythological entrepreneur.
Actually, this is an excerpt from the letter that was sent at the beginning of this year by Larry Fink, the CEO of the world's largest investment fund, to all CEOs of the companies the fund invests in.
Larry Fink wrote to all the companies his fund invests in - BlackRock manages almost seven trillion dollars of assets, more than three times the GDP of Italy - he wrote, "What is your mission?
You have to have a mission, you must have a meaning, you must play a role that positively impacts the planet."
He said, "If you don't take this mission into account, you will have lower returns, you will be less profitable, you will do worse than other companies because you're not up to the collective challenges ahead of us."
Benefit Corporation came out of all this.
Benefit Corporation represents an evolution, perhaps the most advanced representation of what a sustainable business could be: the concept of using the business as a force for good.
How can we use this extraordinary potential to help solve the major challenges of our time?
Any company can start to reinterpret and reorient itself in this direction. In this way, it can turn into a regenerative company.
This means that the company measures what really matters and manages all its impact with the same thoroughness and rigour that is now applied to economic and financial measurements.
A regenerative company has a vocation written into its statute.
In the United States, it has been a legal form since 2010. These companies are recognised as existing not only for profit, but more than profit, for benefit, and have this mission
written into their social objectives.
A regenerative company competes, not to be the best in the world, but to be the best for the world.
And so, if we imagine that all companies, or more and more companies, move in this direction, we'll see business becoming an engine of lasting and shared prosperity.
Kennedy's ideal becomes reality.
And so, here is a chart inspired by Carlo Maria Cipolla, in which I tried to represent the main types of companies or organisations.
There are, let's say, the "good" ones, that have a positive impact on others but don't necessarily create value for shareholders.
Then there are the locusts, those who destroy value for others, keeping the benefits for themselves.
Cipolla would actually call them the stupid ones. Those who harm others and in the process, also themselves.
So the great majority of companies are extractive and generate value for themselves and their shareholders, but they don't necessarily generate any value for anyone else.
Finally, there are regenerative companies, the Benefit Corporations, that create just as much value for themselves as for others.
So this is a model available today, if we want it, that we hope to disseminate as much as possible.
Bob Shiller, the Nobel Prize winner in Economics in 2013, said a few years ago, "I believe Benefit Corporations will be more profitable than other types of company precisely because they are responding to the needs of the 21st century."
These are companies that use technology to do something that was unthinkable not so long ago: measuring all its impact, measuring what matters in a comprehensive, rigorous way.
So, for a dozen years, we've had a technological platform called B Impact Assessment, open and accessible to anyone in the world, which job is to establish if a company is creating or destroying value for society.
It's not just about economic value but also environmental and social value.
Today it is possible for a company to take all their data, measure their activities and, using technology, do something that just a few years ago wasn't possible: to find out where it ranks, from 0 to 200 points. At 80 points we find ourselves at the threshold - let's say, in equilibrium.
Under 80 points means we have an extractive company, which takes more from the world than it creates.
Over 80 points means that it is regenerative, creating more value than it extracts. We hope to have as many of these as possible.
Currently, it is quite difficult to pass this threshold.
This graph here shows us more than 70,000 companies in the world that have been rated using this protocol, and there are only 2,650 that have passed this threshold.
But it demonstrates one basic fact: that it can be done.
These are all companies who measured their impact, in 60 countries and across 140 different industries, that show that it is possible to run almost any business in a way that creates more social, environmental and economic value than you extract.
Do you realise how revolutionary this concept is?
So, in 2012, what did we do?
When we founded Nativa, we copied this model from the United States. We said, "If they can do it, so can we."
We contacted lawyers, notaries, legal experts.
We wrote our statutes, in which we declared our aim to make Nativa a regenerative company,
one that goes beyond the concept of profit.
So, we set up the company
and went to the Chamber of Commerce.
But our statutes were rejected.
Why were they rejected?
We had something so innovative, extraordinary.
We were not asking anything.
"No, it cannot be done. The law prevents a capital company from doing something other than producing a return for its shareholders."
So, it could not be done.
We persisted. We were rejected a second, a third, a fourth time.
The fifth time, exhausted, the Chamber of Commerce agent said, "All right, all right.
We'll register it. Nobody will care."
And then we begin to operate in limbo, a grey area. We hoped nobody would notice so we could get on with our business.
But this, let's say, didn't work out well.
So, together with Paolo Di Cesare, co-founder of Nativa, we started to push this idea ourselves. We began to contact people who would understand the opportunity. Maybe they could help us promote this innovation.
We created a working group that within 14 months basically wrote a bill.
This bill was presented before the Senate and was introduced as an amendment to the 2016 Stability Law.
And as you see here, the law was passed on December 22, 2015.
This is me with senator Del Barba and Paolo Di Cesare.
And for the first time in Italy, a new legal form was introduced where Benefit Companies were recognized as companies that "in the exercise of their economic activities, in addition to the aim of creating dividends, pursue one or more goals for the common good."
It is a rewiring of companies' DNA.
Italy thus became the world's first sovereign state to adopt this legal form.
Today, it exists in 34 states of the USA, in Italy, and Colombia, and there are 15 other countries who are introducing the same model.
Italy finds itself as the country where this model is growing the fastest.
More than 85 Italian companies did pass the 80 point threshold in a measured, verified, and certified way, and we have more than 300 companies who adopted this legal form.
Overall, in Europe, when Nativa was born, we were Patient Zero. There were no other Benefit Corporations. Today, there are more than 700 Benefit and B-Corp certificate corporations.
Today, Italy is a planetary laboratory.
The world looks at us and says, "How did they do that?"
We want this same model, the one we have in Italy, to spread to other countries.
We believe this concept of a regenerative company is also very similar to our own DNA, very close to our entrepreneurial make-up.
And so I hope that, as citizens, you start looking in depth at what you buy. Where is it from, who made it, does it have a positive impact or not?
If you are an entrepreneur, find out if your company is creating or destroying value.

Do you know? Have you ever measured it?
Today, this is how to move forward.
If you are an administrator, now dozens of cities around the world, one example is New York City, are adopting this model and spreading the idea around because, obviously, the more companies of this type are in a given territory, the better it is for the economy, for the environment, and for society.
I am positive, optimistic, despite what I forced you to see at the beginning. I believe peoples and nations will always do the right thing, but only after exhausting all the other alternatives.
I don't know if we have run out of alternatives yet.
I believe we are fast approaching this wall and much more quickly than is commonly believed. But I also believe that we have the opportunity and the tools, and that the only thing that might be missing is a little leap of faith to speed up this positive transformation.
Thank you.
(Applause)
At this point in the evening I ask all of you a little effort. Please try to associate one image in your mind with the word "museum". Especially a natural history museum, or a scientific museum,
Chances are, most of you will think of a boring image of a somewhat dusty, silent place with a soft, suggestive atmosphere and myriad of objects in glass cases displayed orthogonally. In fact, this is how it is.
This is our inheritance from the Enlightenment museum.
As Karl Popper said, this is the Enlightenment's naivety. The Enlightenment created the museum and inherited collections, the "wunderkammer", private collections gathered by rich lords from the Middle Ages to the 17th century. The Enlightenment made them public, thus creating the museum.
Nevertheless, this kind of museum has followed one paradigm for long: "Don't touch".
"Don't touch" ruled for a really long time.
Like many of you, I belong to a generation raised on the following motto: Look, don’t touch is a thing to learn.
It's a social virtue.
It's about nurturing the behavioral aspect of a virtue: don't touch.
We must wait until the begininng of the 20th century before something changes. But something happens at a certain point that flips this point of view:
In London, in 1851, an amazing event took place: the first Expo in history. The Great Universal Exhibition, as they call it.
The very word "universal", in 1851, shows what an ambitious project it was.
In fact, it was meant to celebrate the British colonial power.
It was something unbelievable.
Just to make it clear, six million people visited it in just six months. Basically one third of the entire population of Britain at the time.
13,000 exhibitors, gathered from 45 countries.
Actually, that was the seed of what, 150 years later, we'd start to call "globalization".
Never-before-seen things were shown for the first time.
There was everything in there:
the world’s finest artisanal products; textiles, ceramics, exotic animal trophies; the exhibition of Koh-i-Noor, the world's largest diamond of that time, up until the exposition of scientific tools, scientific experiments, and measuring instruments.
Yet, this is the most fascinating aspect: the exhibition of machines.
Steam engines, industrial processes - We spoke earlier about loom.
This was the presentation of the first Jacquard machine.
This is industrial revolution on stage.
This is the vertigo of modernity.
Look at this great promise of a better future. Modernity, finally, emancipates humankind from nature.
This is the ultimate dominance, we’re dominating all of this, and we all will enjoy a better life.
Even more fascinating, what I want to stress now is that this event shows the spectacular, extraordinary power of science and technology.
As we said, this event was attended by six million people.
At the beginning, entrance tickets came at a cost of £350. In just three months, price plummeted to £3 so that everyone could visit it.
In fact, everyone did - the Royal family and the working class.
And everyone was astonished by viewing this exhibition of science and techniques.
The event was such a great achievement because of the discovery, spectacularity of science and techniques. It was also a huge economic success - it was perhaps the only Expo who ever made money in history: around £18 million, based on today’s exchange rate. The decision was then made to open three museums in London: the Victoria &amp; Albert Museum - it goes without saying, Prince Albert had patronized the event- the Natural History Museum, and the Science Museum. Now, the Science Museum is exactly one of those we want to understand the most.
It took a lot of time, but in 1934, the Science Museum made a relevant step: it launched the Children's Gallery, so a space for kids, where children could touch stuff for the first time ever.
Physics experiments were displayed through "hands-on", touchable exhibits. Things then start to change, a change was triggered.
This was the beginning of an experiential museum. This model is participatory, interactive, and requires direct fruition.
Then, this gentleman changed the game.
His name is Frank Oppenheimer. He was an American particle physicist, a professor at the University of Colorado, and brother of Robert, the world-famous Oppenheimer. Robert Oppenheimer, as you know, worked with Enrico Fermi on the Manhattan Project. Essentially, we are talking about the inventors of the atomic bomb.
Now, Oppenheimer happened to be in London for an award, did some research, and was deeply impressed by the Science Museum, and the European scientific museums.
When he came back to the U.S., in San Francisco, he launched the Exploratorium and everything changes.
The Exploratorium still exists, and it's an extraordinary place.
If you have yet to visit it, please do, because it’s truly an incredible place. Indeed, it’s a game changer since it’s not, technically, a museum.
How does it define itself?
It defines itself a Public Learning Laboratory.
The Exploratorium is a place where science is not experienced but through interactive experiments.
Kids need to participate.
To invert and paraphrase the motto, we could now say: "You can’t learn, if you just look without touching."
In fact, the opposite is true: if you want to learn you need to touch.
This is the embodiment of the "Edutainment" concept, that is, content is conveyed, scientific content in this case, such as the laws of physics, is conveyed through the so-called "Gamification", a playful dynamic.
What’s exciting about Exploratorium is its goal, which is also in its name, Exploratorium.
It aims to create active explorers. In the Exploratorium's values, one can read the following: we do not want young people to come here to find answers. Rather, we want them to come and learn how to ask new questions.
Now, this is another fundamental change.
Look at this photo. It looks older than it is, but it was taken in the 1980’s.
In 1986, the Science Museum inaugurated the first Launchpad Gallery, which is a fully interactive gallery also dedicated to scientific demonstrations.
There's a funny anectode, told by the museum director at the time, an 8-year-old child who, for the first time - among the first entering that hall- exclaimed in awe, as the doors opened: "Wow, it's toy heaven!”
He had found the ultimate playground.
In fact, this started the transformation of the museum. It kickstarted a new era: the "Please, touch" era.
It's a game changer.
Museums begin to use totally new languages: scenography, show, performance, and interaction.
What I wish you understand, though, is how cultural paradigm changes.
Which is to say, the previous museum had inherited a tradition that had, how can I say, two key points:
the curator and the visitor, who used to go and contemplate.
The paradigm completely shifted. This means, reality becomes more complex, the scope of science and physics widens and clearly, if one doesn't feel as though belonging to this universe, if you don't experience it personally, you keep being an outsider.
The whole way of talking science changes.
Thereafter, the museum changes completely.
It completely changes, including the "Don't touch" part to the point of this naughty one to the right, hopping on it. This is what forces us to build structures three times more resistant than they are meant to, because we need to provide for a "touch" that, at this point, is no longer as easy to measure and forecast.
New ideas grow, and become installation mechanisms.
This is again at the Science Museum: one can slide down the staircase, and in the process, run a scientific experiment because they experience materials' friction.
Here is the CERN Museum in Geneva with its landscape made of microparticles.
This is an exhibition on DNA, in Rome, that we organized a few years ago. In order to explain chromosomes, we built a forest of interactive chromosomes. As previously mentioned, we need to make visible an invisible scale. We need to make people understand how a system works, so we take it to the extreme and it becomes an installation, an object that people can even access.
Then, there are cases like this one, that are quite fascinating.
This is - let's call it a poop museum.
(Laughter) Actually, when a child enters he receives a beautiful poop hat - Not sure how this may affect a child's self-esteem, anyway- (Laughter) He’s put into a giant toilet bowl, and flushed down through the entire sewage system.
(Laughter) It’s in Tokyo - it’s real, and an experience. I trivialized it’s name as poop museum, but that’s not its real name. It’s called,
“Toilet!? Human waste, and Earth's Future”. In fact, this is Edutainment at its finest: trying to make children understand the complexity of an urban infrastructure, such as sewage, and trying to offer environmental awareness.
Therefore, Edutainment at a very high level indeed.
Everything has really changed, so the museum also becomes a subject that needs to enter the marketing world. It must start promoting itself, communicate, and become competitive.
It’s no longer that thing, it’s something else.
This statement makes it very clear.
And it wasn't spoken by a layman. This statement comes from New York's former Digital Director of MoMA, one of the world’s most visited museums.
He says, we don’t compete with other museums; we compete with Netflix and Candy Crush.
Our topic is no longer a competition through cultural means. Rather, culture has become an add-on to an experience that competes with other entertainment experiences.
Here’s where I come in,
because this is my job. I do this job with others, around the world. I try to elaborate this passage, to find the design key of this new entity that we still call the museum, to avoid misunderstandings, Yet it’s no longer a museum, but something else.
It’s still nameless, but it’s another thing.
Now, what becomes difficult for me is that compared to previous museums, that could keep working for at least two, even three generations, I'm in this situation.
I’m the one on the left.
By the way, I actually had a disgusting sweater like that.
(Laughter) In short, as many of us in this room, I belong to that generation, which, in fact, grew up with the book as a medium of knowledge. Then we accessed other media too, but books were a staple medium.
So we became information seekers.
In order to broaden our knowledge, we needed to seek information.
In fact,
that photo could come from any of our family albums.
But when I needed to search for a photo to talk about the generation that I design for, I could find only one photo that might communicate effectively.
However, this is the generation we need to design for. It’s a multi-tasking generation of digital natives who are facing the opposite problem: they do not need to seek information, rather, they must deal with the so called information overload.
This generation needs support to manage all their information.
In short, a 2008 University of California study -
so we're talking about 10 years ago, and the situation is presumably even more pressing today. This study estimated that we are exposed, on a daily basis, to roughly 34 gigabytes of data - just through news, TV, billboards, smart phones, and so on.
This means roughly 100,000 words a day.
For example, War and Peace, which is one of the longest novels in the history of literature, has around 544,000 words.
So it gets really complicated.
It seems like the attention span has dropped to eight seconds. This makes designing for today a very complex task.
Hence, what I always tell my students is that at the end of the day, we design filters.
What we really need to learn is how to build smart filters.
I’m not talking about content filters - that’s a curator's business.
It’s not our job. Instead, we need to select all the information we receive and start figuring out how to distill it into many bits, finer than the eight second attention span so that, together, these can weave an interesting fabric.
Today, any smartphone lets us access knowledge sources on many aspects of science. So a museum cannot compete with such a thing.
The museum must entrench, must engage us, must make us understand a story’s entire picture which is becoming more and more complex. Finally, it must then offer you a deepening. In fact, I cannot steal you from Netflix or Candy Crush through a museum lecture.
You know what the most important part is?
Neither I nor anyone else in this field across the world today has a job title for it.
No one of us got a specific training for this kind of stuff.
We are all self taught.
I’m an architect, my business partner is instead a musicologist, and my former one used to be an electronic musician and a graphic designer.
We are all people coming from totally different backgrounds and learned how to do this qsubsequently.
Our teams include designers, computer developers, artists, scenographers, theatrical technicians, IT experts, science communicators, storytellers, and engineers.
We are a true example of multidisciplinarity, not because we like big, modern words, but because no one really knows how to do all this.
We are all learning to create it by hybridizing several diverse skills.
Perhaps on a positive note, we all teach at the university, so we’re teaching the younger generations how to do this job, how to build a system and a methodology.
Difficult things might happen.
Here’s an example of what we have recently done.
You may have heard of the M9 museum that just opened in Venice - or in Mestre, to be more precise - around last year.
It is a very big museum that took on a very special challenge, an extraordinary experimental project, similar to what we experience every day, when we experiment.
It’s a museum of the 20th century that does not contain a single physical object.
It is based solely on immersive and interactive experiences and talks to us the 20th century through this kind of narrative. It follows the Anglo-Saxon tradition of historical tales, of “Public History”. Instead of telling history from the point of view of the powerful, it tells it through the mechanism of self-identification. You try to put yourself in the shoes of someone who was there.
Among other things that we designed, we have been asked to tell the factory.
We needed to tell very young kids about this very important world of work that many of us still remember.
We didn’t know where to start, so at a certain point we bet on an experiment. Let’s try this: let’s ask ourselves what the assembly line and video games doi have in common.
Both share environmental stress, the point mechanism, and penalty in case of wrong-doing. So let’s try to work this out.
Let’s build a mechanism which, through gamification - also known as “serious game”, a game that is not meant to entertain but is rather serious - renders a factory's inner machinery.
This is the final installation. We also came up with this idea: that despite everything today is either digital or electronic, the interface is mechanical because we wanted to keep something specific from the 20th century.
We also referred to Ansaldo from the 1950’s and we reconstructed a kind of video game that truly reproduces every aspect of the assembly line.
We ask ourselves, every time we complete a work, if we made it right, or if we got it wrong.
It’s a drama because you never know, because it’s an experiment and there is no path to follow.
[Vanishing Mediators] And this always made me feel very close to this definition given by a philosopher that I appreciate very much. He’s alive, he’s Slovenian, and his name is Slavoj Žižek.
Some years ago, he coined the term: “Vanishing Mediators", which defines those who, like us, are at a threshold, between before and after, and we are mediators because they mediate.
We are mediating between what was before - We are trying to imagine what can come after. and "vanishing" because we will last just a very short time, because like they just told us, big leaps await us in the years ahead if compared to what has already been achieved.
I keep saying that 50 years ago a son, a grandfather, and a father used to share nearly the same lifestyle. There just was a talk about that.
I see young people that totally change their point of view every 15 years.
Žižek said, so that you understand what he meant by vanishing mediator, he used Charlie Chaplin as an example when the film industry began using sound. Charlie Chaplin, in a way, put his foot down and had an attitude that was a bit conservative since he tried to understand the traumatic effect that voice would have on that expressive form. In fact, voice was an extraneous intruder. Chaplin understood that this novelty would not benefit his own poetic at all.
This is how I feel. Those in my field, I believe they should share the same attitude, understand this mediation role and understand where we need to surrender more or less. Because at the end of the day, they outsourced to us the narrative of science, the narrative of knowledge. Therefore, we have an important role to cope with, and we must be aware of it.
We know where we come from and the next door is before us. It’s very difficult to understand the right thing to do.
We ask ourselves this for each project.
During every project, we try to be consistent, careful to carefully study every subject, and find the right way to communicate them.
We don’t know if this is the right way.
Tomorrow morning, with the next project, we’ll come to the table to think about it.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good evening.
Do you know this place?
The Como Lake.
I was born near Como, several year ago.
Through the Lariani Mountain Trail I started what I will call, "meetings with myself".
When I needed to relax, I used to go up to the mountain, and said everyon I have a meeting with myself. And I went to the Lariani Mountain Trail.
No matter the weather or the season.
This meeting with myself used to...
I was 13, 14 years old... Sometimes I skipped school to see black grouses flying away.
During this meeting I exercised my creativity, fantasy, I felt like to do everything.
A meeting with myself.
I liked it, a lot.
During this meeting, sometimes I met friends, got to know new people, and mostly, I met people who lived up in the mountain.
More than alpinists, I have always been fascinated by the old men living 10 minutes far from the village. I liked the idea to live there, struggling with the mountain
to get what you have to.
I liked this way of living.
But soon difficulties began.
I struggled to understand, before sharing with someone else.. what king of job was it...
What kind of job it is, doing what it entails
living up to the mountain.
It is not a well defined job.
So, one day, more than the equipment- the equipment was not created to shovel the snow, later it begane a tool, but a tool was first created, to achieve a result in particular conditions.
For example to hunt, to get food, to reach a town...
Tools were create to be able to live in challenging conditions.
I have always been fascinated by this way of living.
And it also was the phase of the first questions, as Larian Mountains -
This picture was taken during a critical period of my life.
I was 15, on the Bernina mountain, first time at 4000 meters.
I will get back there several times, and it is like a standing point for me, where important decisions stem from.
First arguments began, because my desire to live up there, had to face the traditional reality, and my relatives' model of life.
The shtick was, your passions don't get you a living.
First you find a job, than you practise your hobby.
Yes, but I wanted to live there.
Yes, but it's not a job.
Ok, let's make it a job.
But when you're 16, 17, your not so strong to struggle against the way of thinking of you parents, friends, society and so on... So you strive to make people understand - and I haven't succeded yet, what your point is.
After 20 years, there are still people coming up to the lodge, and they know everything, but they still think,
I made it because I'm rich.
That's what they think.
Unbelievable.
So you start to think hard.
I was about 20, 25, I gained experience, I made several choises...
Once, during a meeting with myself, I put rationality first, before everything else. My rationality affected
all my decisions, all my ideas, it was anchorage.
If something was rational, I would have done it, otherwise I wouldn't.
The question was: what is life?
I have always been taught to create my certainty first. Because future is uncertain.
Yes, but let me live until tomorrow.
"Future is uncertain", or "make sure you don't miss anything".
During one of my meetings with myself, it dawned on me, if every moment of your life is uncertain, I don't know what will happen in 10 minutes, neither in one minute. If I do all my best to avoid uncertainties, because it is better to know your future, this is not living at all. Because life itself is uncertain.
Life is a sequence of moments.
Instead of fear uncertainties, let's learn how to deal with them. Learn how to take lessons from it, not to be afraid of it.
On the contrary, I'll keep on playing safe, without creating anything worthy.
Unless you work for an insurance company. Then of course, you keep on saying, "Oh yes, it can happen"

But I want to live, I want to live.
But, what is life?
Getting a family, traveling, documenting?
One day I decided it's not this.
For me, life is living myself.
I want to use 100% of my capabilities.
I want to live completely.
I want to feel, to sweat to walk, to watch, to listen.
I want to live completely.
One day, I was 26, during a memorable meeting on the Bernina mountain.
Everyone knew me.
I took a important decision on Bernina mountain. I went from rationality - I dismissed it.
My irrationality took over, my passions for mountains.
I didn't feel like having
a certain future. I don't want to live in a way I don't like.
When I was around the mountain, I felt good, I helped people who lived there.
When I came back home, at night, I wasn't doing so bad because I'd done what I liked - I did it well, I helped my father.
It was a nice feeling.
When you feel good, everything is better.
On the Bernina mountain, "Ifs" and "buts" were replaced with "I choose to" or "I choose not to".
Uncertainties does not help you.
I learnt to get rid of uncertainties only later in life. When I got hurt, and I was alone.
You get hurt and think, "If I paid attention, If this, if that..."
But all those IFs did not heal me.
After pondering everything, you're still sick. And you still must deal with your problems.
What for?
So I stopped thinking about possibilities, to do, or not to do.
I do something, I don't do something else.
I didn't care the reason why to do something, for me was important doing what I wanted.
My passions took the control -
it's like changing an operating system, you change your way of living, you change everything.
I find a place, and go to live there.
Imagine what the others said:
"You went too far away",
"You like the mountaing, but this is too much"
One day...
This is the Grignan mountain,
I was doing, undoing and the like.. You have big problems, it's a big effort, thirty years ago was completely different. What do you eat? How do you live?
You live up the mountain because you like it - ok, but how do you live?
But I felt a subtle feeling.
A feeling of stillness and freedom.
And I said, I won't miss it.
I will hold it firmly.
All around me there was a chaos,
but inside I had a feeling of calm and freedom.
It's ok.
I started to live day by day. I had been living for two years in tent, summer and winter. I wandered from Courmayeur to Friuli to find a place with: an hour by feet to get there, breathtaking landscapes, water sources, isolated - no other lodges around - and sunny day in winters.
I found my place in Valsesia valley, near the town of Alagna, 6500 feet asl.
When I came back home, I told my brother - relationships were complicated, back then, "I have found a beautiful place to live".
He thought about a house just outside the town.
"Finally, you tourmoiled soul"
"I have found an amazing place"
(Laughter). 6500 feet high.
My rationality was torning up. No way.

You can't do it.
I had a meeting.
