Think about it for a second.
From the very moment we are born, we start growing old.
After all, what is switching from your mother’s uterus to a midwife’s arms, if not growing old?
And do you remember how fun was growing old when we were young?
My friend Andrea and I, we used to do an absurd thing.
We used to draw hair on our chest to look like we actually had them.
Someone used to shave with toothpaste and toothbrush. When we were going to the theatre, and they asked us our age, we used to answer: 18!
Even if we hardly were 15.
We watched our bigger brothers and sisters with a kind of yearning, because they had something we missed: A girlfriend for more than two days, a more powerful motorcycle, exams at the university - shortly, they had more years.
Then, something changes: Guess what happens?
It is a matter of adverbs.
The adverb "already," is increasingly replaced by the adverb “still.”
Do you remember your mother?
"He already walks," "He already talks," "He already runs." Then it suddenly flips:
"He still walks," "He still talks,"
"He still goes out with his friends."
Not to mention the taboos of our time.
"He still works."
And needless to say, "He still has sex - at his age."
As if actions like walking, living, running around, having sex had an expiration date, like yogurt. Look, we are sorry, your actions are expired. Come back next life.
My point is, we're always the same.
The person we used to say "already," and now "still," is the same one.
You see me here.
It’s always me.
I am only a few seconds older then a little while ago, when I warned you about that.
Because it happen that society at some point decides some things aren't OK. And one of those things is the fear of growing old. It is called gerontophobia.
Too bad our society didn't realize that a globally aging population - in 2050, people older than 65 will be more than two and a half billion, And we’ll have to deal with that, it will be an important, fateful phenomenon.
Actually, society might have noticed it.
Have a look at these newspaper titles, maybe you've read them already:
'Warning! The silver tsunami is coming"; or again: "Aging population, a time bomb for the Planet," just not to make a drama.
What are they telling us?
They are selling us that it is a disaster, a catastrophe, that something seismic, irreversible is happening. And how can we deal with this, if not run away and save ourselves? What else would you do,
before a tsunami wave?
I think that "saving ourselves" is one of the keywords in this story.
Because they are actually telling us, save yourselves from getting old.
That’s it, in the modern world we could be anything but one: It'll be forbidden to be old.
Now, if threats in the press haven't scared you too much, I invite you do one thing: Go in a supermarket, a scent store, or do something even easier: Open your bathroom cabinet. This is the one in my home, shot two days ago, before coming here.
We're full of anti-aging products we bought long ago, we do not even remember when. thinking this was a model we had to fit in.
But try to think to that very word "anti-aging," Consider the prefix "Anti-."
When is it typically used?
Anti-fleas for your dog's parasites.
Anti-tetanus, to eradicate a lethal disease.
Anti-tank, to destroy a tank.
The anti-aging industry seems to be on a mission: keeping you not only from getting old, but also from looking old.
And what do we do?
We believe it and we support an army that fight a battle against a clear target: ourselves.
It's a paradox: Buying anti-us things, thinking they will make us better.
In reality, if you think about it, the effects of this anti-aging mood can be really as deadly as an anti-tank mine, because being isolated from society has been compared to a death risk of smoking 15 cigarettes per day. It's higher than obesity, just to give you a benchmark.
It raises up to 65 percent the risk of clinical dementia, it boosts Alzheimer biomarkers, worsens heart diseases, depression, and the risk of suicide. Long story short, the one against the aging of population it one of the most subtle and corrosive discriminations we're dealing with these days.
Now, something to consider. If you think about the great social movement against discriminations like racism or sexism, for sure we haven’t fought them yet, but at least we have learned to detect and "frame" them.
We can’t say the same about ageism, which is literally an age-based discrimination and stereotyping against the elderly.
Now, two things for you to consider.
The first one is about the word ageism.
It has been created in 1968 by the American psychiatrist R. N. Butler.
The word has stayed in English more or less until now.
If you do a quick search for "racism" and "sexism" you find them translated from Afrikaans to Zulu, in all the languages of the world.
Not so for ageism.
"Ageismo" is Italian's terrible adaptation,
which means nothing.
I personally have campaigned for an international conversation on how to proper translate the word "ageism" in local languages. It is not a trivial issue. How can we detect and then fight a discrimination with no dedicated word to call it in Italian, in Portuguese, in Hindi?
My second consideration is about the word "prejudice."
We weren't born with prejudices.
They grow in a social context, the socio-cultural context we grow up in, then get "frozen" in shared assumptions and eventually, we use stereotypes without even noticing it.
It is as if we contaminate ourselves, and those around us, with a virus we aren't aware of.
Can you recall some of the prejudices we apply every day?
"I would have given you 20 years less”
"90 years old, and still the wit of a 50 year old" as people in their 90s couldn't be witty.
"Old people are slow";
"Old people are boring";
"Old people are useless, they are a burden for society." “They are slow at work, less performing." How many times have we thought it, or at least read it?
There is a prejudice that really drives me crazy.
When we link the word "technology" or "innovation" to the word "young."
As if something could be possibly created only if you are young.
The implicit message is terrible: If you are not young, you can’t take part in the society.
I remind you something: if you go to look for patents that were registered in the last 10 years, you’ll notice that the majority of them have been enrolled by people between 45 and 65 years old.
Certainly not young people.
Do you know when we talk to old people with an idiotic voice?
"What a beautiful girl we have here" "What are these tiny paws near these beautiful little eyes?"
Would you like this treatment, when you are 70?
And when we have a friend that doesn't understand what we say: "Hey, do you have Alzheimer?"
And when we do always the same: "Ah, you know you are old when candles cost more than the cake"
Then, we actually grow old. Do you think this stops us from acting this way?
No, it's always an us vs. them.
Imagine to be in a square a bench filled with people in their 70s looking at people in their 80s sitting on the bench in front of them.
"Look how old are they, they don't understand anything anymore." And so on, back and forth in the ladder of age.
Then there's one stereotype that's worst than the others, expressed by those doctors or nurses that only see old people as "bed-keepers" - “Ah, let’s hope he will die.” Let’s say it clearly: Ageism is one of those discriminations we still can't grasp the scale.
It affects everybody, regardless of race, religion, sexual orientation, no matter how tall, short, skinny or fat you are, your nationality or mother-tongue.
Incredibly, as of today no constitution mentions it.
This is the Article Three of the Italian Republic Constitution.
Does any of you in this room reads the word "age" in here?
Likewise, this is 1948's Declaration of Human Rights.
Do you see the word "age"?
77 percent of the American men, over 65 years old, declared they have received, at least once, an act of discrimination related to their age.
A study on over 400,000 terms, from 1810 until 2009, showed that the words "old," "elder," and "old age" have taken an increasingly negative meaning in the last 200 years.
Ageism is really everywhere.
Look at this case: This is a Tumblr platform. That's the application form
you can fill to sign up.
What was the point in trading “old” with “young”?
Just to please someone?
In fact, ageism is everywhere.
We perpetuate it all the time, and we don't even notice it.
But something might be changing.
For example, grey hair turned from a cause of discrimination to a sign of style.
I have seen a couple outside.
There are new ways to give meaning to the passage of time, even aesthetically speaking.
The magazine Allure, some months ago, has decided to ban forever from its own vocabulary, the word anti-aging.
Researchers as Liat Ayalon, I work with here in translating the word "ageism."
Becca Levy, Tracey Gendron or writers like Ashton Applewhite are dedicating their lives trying to make ageism a critical topic, much like racism and sexism, we talked about before.
They're all women.
Is it just a case?
I don't think so.
Women are mostly affected from ageist dynamics,
because of their body.
Where does the anti-aging concept come from, after all?
For their work, or on their work,
in everyday life. Let me share an example of what happens in Italy.
Hours spent by women in volunteering and assistance, mostly towards elderly people, are 50.694.000.000 every year. 50+ billion unpaid hours.
The figure is even more staggering if you compare it with the paid hours worked every year by Italian men and women. It's "only" 42 billion hours.
So Italian men and women's aggregate certified work in a year is eight billion of hours less that women's unpaid work.
So I thought,
ageism is also a discrimination that involves a part of population that has never been affected from any kind of discrimination in the human history.
Namely, average white man.
And who's the average white men
these days, you may ask?
Those who were born between
1946 and 1964: the baby boomer.
Every day, 10,000 of them enter the old age, according to demography.
So, those are the ones that now have to face this kind of discrimination that scares me a bit: After all, who did create racism and sexism, if not the average white man?
So one risk is that this social category creates "ageism in reverse," against youngsters.
Typically: “No, you are too young, you have no experience for this job.”
Which is something that happens every day.
And that's why we have to do something, all together.
We have to support those women and we have to catch the opportunity to deal with baby boomers every day. and explain to them, there's ongoing discrimination.
And they can help us accelerate out fight against it so we won't find ourselves with the same discriminations we are still fighting with today.
Today I stand here as a baby boomer, together with those women, to amplify the message.
To tell you, one can stand on this stage with all the wrinkles in the world, and there is nothing to be ashamed about.
You must all help me, make the ageism a widespread word, not only for some circles, one that only sociologists or gerontologists can understand, but a commonly used word.
I am here to tell you that diversity is the most powerful innovation weapon we have, that there's no "young vs. old" war, and there's an amazing chance to join our forces and change the world together.
In fact, if you think about it, baby boomers are those behind the internet, the GPS, the smartphone, Artificial Intelligence maybe even before the baby boomers, self-driving cars.
They're now working on the quantum computer.
Think about how many things we can, and we could, do together if we built a bridge, not a wall, between the two ends of the society.
I am here to ask you to replace the word "anti-aging" with "pro-aging."
I am here to suggest you to look at yourselves in the mirror and tell yourself, that person you see in there will change a lot, I can guarantee you.
But people don't judge you for your looks.
People judge you for the idea that power gave to your look.
That should be fought for, not wrinkles.
I am here to tell you, there's no ongoing tsunami.
I am here to tell you to spare some saving. For a travel, not for a funeral.
Do you know how many days are there between 65 and 85 years?
8,000 of them.
I am here to tell you, I'm not sure if you'll be happy, as happiness doesn't correlate with age. But I am sure you could fall in love, have fun, play, travel, live without no one fucking you around because you do it when you please, and you think it when you're 80.
I am here to tell you that we can only make it if we do it together.
I've understood one thing: The future I couldn't image is nothing but the present in progress.
And living it is the finest thing we can possibly do.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Let's start with a seemingly easy question.
Who, here, is convinced to be dreaming, right now?
Raise your hand, one, two -
not many, thankfully.
(Laughter) Another question, linked to the first - even though it doesn't seem so. Who of you would trust someone who betrays you every day?
Every day, every night actually, it fools you into believing false things. Is there someone
who would trust that person?
No, we all agree on this.
Yet this is what we do: we trust someone who deceives us every day.
This is what we all do, because our mind fools us every day, every night, when we sleep, for about an hour and a half.
That is the duration of the so-called REM phases, the sleep's dreaming phase.
Which kind of experience is dream?
We all know that, we believe what we see.
If we're having a nightmare, we are afraid, because that monster is chasing us, we believe it is true.
If we have a love affair with someone, we believe it.
And the experience we are living, we live it intensely.
Our mind deceives us in an extremely convincing way, involving all our senses.
It involves sight, involves hearing, involves touch, involves emotions.
Our brain and our mind create for us, when we dream, some sort of movie, natural virtual reality.
Some of you may have tried to use virtual reality, right?

We are immersed in it, we believe in it.
Even more so in a dream.
It is a "natural" technology, so to say, and it's even more effective.
Yet during the day, when we are awake, we trust our mind, which is the same that betrayed us in the night, the same that deceived us, that perhaps inspired us during the night, but still generating something that just is not true.
It is fake, it is illusory, it is a shadow.
This idea that our mind deceives us has deeply crossed our entire Western culture generating two approaches, two ways of thinking extremely important for all of us.
We could not be here now if we had not reconsidered the deceptive nature of our senses.
One approach is science.
With the scientific method, science wants to go beyond the limits of the individual mind, the deception of our senses,
That's why we use a meter.
If I guess by naked eye, something is five feet meter long, I know I can deceive myself, my mind is not that accurate, I need to use a tool to objectively measure that length.
This holds true for everything in science.
We set up models, but we do not trust them. We must run experiments, we must take measures so that our idea, our fantasy, is somehow confirmed by the external reality.
So the scientific method is totally aware of how deceptive our mind is.
And it doesn't count a single scientist's opinion, because she may have made a mistake, she might have made experiments, let's say, only in a certain direction. Another scientist is needed who questions, tries to falsify, what the first scientist stated.
Another discipline that strongly takes into account the deceptive nature of our senses is Western philosophy.
There's also the Eastern one, but we are more familiar with the Western one.
Think of Descartes, René Descartes, a great French philosopher, one of the fathers of modern thought.
He starts his philosophical study, his philosophical exploration, precisely from the fundamental doubt about the relatability of his senses
and tried to find a sounder base than his senses, one that a philosophical system can later be built upon. And so he starts saying "I think therefore I am", which is well known.
You see, science and philosophy, and various philosophical approaches that we do not explore now, are fully aware of our senses' deluding nature.
But we, as individuals, we rarely ask ourselves during the day: am I dreaming or not?
Instead, we believe in what our senses, all the time, make us see, hear, feel, touch.
But I'd like to stress with you now a particular method we can use to become more aware of our senses' deceptive nature.
And this happens just while we dream.
That is, while we are having a normal dream, trying to nurture the ability to wonder if that is a dream or not.
Let's assume I'm chased by a nightmare's monster; while I'm running away, I contemplate the situation and ask myself: is this a dream or not?
And I turn my face to the monster.
It is an extremely difficult change of conscience, it is not easy, but perhaps some of you will have experienced it.
Some of you may have had a dream where you felt it was a dream and maybe you guided it a little bit.
Those are called "pre-lucid dreams".
A lucid dream is an extremely intense experience, where we are fully aware of the fact that we are dreaming.
You see, as a teenager I had that experience as I clumsily practiced meditation techniques during the day
and one night I happened to have a lucid dream of this extremely intense type, which shaped a bit, let's say, the way I saw, and still see, consciousness and awareness.
With the first dream then, immediately reported on the lucid dreams's book, was the first in a series of hundreds and hundreds of lucid dreams that I had in my life and still continue to have.
And they have a great transformative power.
Initially with the first dreams, you learn a little to play.
One thing I did, for example, was to get up a few inches off the ground.
So I tried to challenge the laws of physics and move from one place to another of that extremely detailed dream reality, by sliding.
But then, why stop at that?
So in later dreams I elevated myself more, and even passed through walls.
I had the chance to fly over a city, to go from one city to another, always with extreme realism.
Meeting people generated for us by our dream world - we cannot control the people in the dream, they have a strange independence of thought; but we can decide to go to them.
We can transform our body, because we have a dream body, which is the same one of when we are awake,
but if we look at our dream hands we can transform them, with an effort of lucidity and awareness, giving them six or ten fingers, or the hands of an animal, to become a wolf.
One can become an eagle, not only to fly but also to have the body of an eagle.
It is possible to become a dog, a woman if you are a man, or the other way around.
It is possible to be in two places simultaneously, being aware of that - and why not, even three places.
Once it happened to me to be in three different places, I was flying in one, walking in another, and melted with the floor in the third one.
Not so pleasant but interesting.
And anyway, you're in control. You can wake up at any time.
So, we said about philosophy.
There are various traces of our awareness' ability to move into the world of shadows.
And the best allegory for this idea is a very famous one I am sure that many of you will have heard or studied. It is the Plato's Cave, or the Allegory of the Cave, as it is also known.
It's a story, I recapitulate here, written by Plato 2,400 years ago in his Republic, staged in a cave deep within the Earth.
There are prisoners in chains who are forced to look only at the bottom of the cave,
with no chance to move or look back.
That is their world.
Behind them, unbeknownst to them, there is a huge fire and between fire and prisoners there are people passing with objects.
So the prisoners see, at the bottom of the cave. a number of moving shadows.
That is their reality, and they therefore believe that this is the only reality.
Those shadows are all that is there.
It just so happens, one day, one of those prisoners is released from the chains.
He is able to get out of his limited world.
The first thing he does, he sees that there is a huge fire and he sees there are three-dimensional objects he had absolutely no idea of before,
And he understands that shadows, once thought to be the objects of the world, are only a projection of the actual three-dimensional reality.
Then, at a distance, he sees a light, which is the entrance to the cave
he had lived in until then, he goes there, he comes out and sees that the world is much more complex and very rich, colorful, full of light and there is a large fire, much larger than that of the cave, which is the sun, which enlightens everything. And he can interact with these objects, he can run, he can touch them.
Awakened as it is - you see, this is a metaphor for awakening - but there is another passage, which is what also happens in lucid dreaming, that is, he decides to get back to the world of shadows.
He returns down inside the cave where his inmates are still prisoners,
and he wants to tell them that the reality of the shadows is not the real reality
but there is a much more complex reality, much richer, much finer, much more true, out there.
As you can see, allegories describe mental processes, just as dreams.
indeed allegories and dreams overlap, they have a close connection.
In this allegory of Plato there is this mental process of awakening,
A hard process, also because initially it's hard to wake up, blinded as we are by light; but little by little, we wake up completely.
But then you see, there's this second passage, the return to the world of shadows, the return to sleep, the return to the illusory condition where we try to interact but from an awakened standpoint.
So you see how it is like to wake up in a dream.
Recent neuroscientific research has focused on this phenomenon.
It used to be the exclusive realm of philosophy or maybe some anecdote told here and there.
Then it became a mostly psychological field, and now even of neuroscience.
So what happens in a lucidly dreaming brain?
Well, the message is this, we have a part of the brain that is critically important, called the prefrontal cortex, the one we have behind the forehead.
And you can imagine its role as a sort of conductor of all of our mental abilities.
It directs the vision, which decides when to pay attention, what we must pay attention to in our visual range.
So the conductor - as it doesn’t play an instrument itself but regulates, let's say, the productions of all the other instruments.
Thus it is a kind of boss of our mind.
That's when we have normal, ordinary dreams, the prefrontal cortex is off, while the rest of the brain is normally active almost as if we were awake.
So it's a very active brain, but in the prefrontal cortex.
What has been discovered is, when we have a lucid dream - a very rare event, very hard to reproduce in labs, prefrontal cortex gets activated,
not just as completely as if we were awake, but in many substantial parts it's more active, consumes more oxygen.
It was also found, those who are most prone to lucid dreaming or developed this ability to be conscious in their dreams, have a thicker prefrontal cortex, we can measure it with an MRI, which signals a greater predisposition to be the conductor, to have an active conductor in their mind.
Many studies - and many are still ongoing, is an extremely recent research topic.
The prefrontal cortex is my favorite research topic, so I started dealing many years ago on how the prefrontal cortex regulates and directs our behavior.
So what can we do, in order to increase our ability to be aware while sleeping, in the dream?
Well one technique is that of asking yourself, even when awake, if we are dreaming or not.
I mean, all of a sudden, am I dreaming or not?
Asking the question, rather than giving the answer, casting some doubt on how actually real is what we see.
If it is our projection or is an objective fact.
Repeating it several times during the day makes us, say, create a kind of habit.
And it may happen, one of these times we ask that ourselves, that we are actually dreaming.
And so I'm talking to someone in my dream world, and I stop for a moment, saying, am I really talking with this person, am I really watching a TED talk or is it a dream?
And try to give youself a valid answer.
Doing this several times during the day - but let me get this straight, not to escape in a dream world!
Exactly for the opposite purpose: enhance awareness, lucidity and presence in what we are experiencing.
By doing so, we can become experienced lucid dreamers.
And which advantage do we have in our conscious world?
Think of a situation when you're hit by a negative event: for example, you're fired.
Bad, objectively bad. But how much negative projection we create around such an objectively bad event?
We began to underestimate ourselves: why have they fired me?
I didn't deserve that job, I never showed that I was good at it, or at that other thing, I have no more chances left. What will my family think about me?
All this, you see, is dream-like: it is a dream dress we put on real and concrete facts.
Training to lucid dreaming helps us to tell objective reality from our projection.
In this way we can live better. Not to escape in a distant world, but to be more present in reality.
Thus getting closer to the freed prisoner of Plato's Cave and be able to go around, out in the outside world, in light of the sun, or even return to the world of shadows and not let them to capture us again.
So I say goodbye to you with a wish for a good awakening in dreams.
(Applause)
