It is just pursuing our own crass, cynical and short-term self-interest.
This applies to the wrongly named problem of climate migrants.
Let's approach it from the point of view of our crass, cynical and short-term self-interest.
But we must understand this self-interest.
You'll see something in this slide that is familiar to you, we call it Europe.
But have you ever thought about how strange an idea this is?
All the continents are marked out along geographical barriers.
By that very standard, we are Asia.
We do not exist.
Yet we want to be a continent, indeed the Old Continent.
What is it that characterises us as different from Asia?
A culture, we may say, that within its diversity holds a certain unity. And without engaging in ridiculous talks of race, a certain physiognomic homogeneity.
But then, what is the fundamental determinant of all this?
Why are we a unit?
Well, the determinant is the climate.
Europe is Europe, with its particular identity, because of its peculiar climate.
And over this climatic peculiarity our joint interest was founded.
But now things are changing.
This distinguishing climate of Europe - I'm over-simplifying, but is somehow determined by the influence of the Azores anticyclone.
Which is receding, increasingly giving way to the African anticyclone.
What does this mean?
It means that, if climate has been so decisive in forging our identity, and our concerns, a changing climate will also change these.
Will we lose our European identity?
No, we just make room for another one.
We will enter into the community of interests of those whose lives are governed by the African anticyclone.
From this perspective we can try to understand how we'll be affected by those people who are driven away from their land by a changing climate that makes it less and less fruitful.
But we must also understand what we are exactly talking about.
The accepted wisdom is that the poorer you are, the more likely you are to emigrate - and this is simply not true.
The poorest are held back by what is technically called the "poverty trap".
They have so little, their outlook is so short-term that they never think about emigrating.
Their problem is to find enough rice in the next two hours.
And only when they start to become more secure they do consider leaving, going far away to improve their lifestyle.
Well, those who have enough to consider making the journey have at least a little bit of freedom of choice and are the real migrants.
Well managed, they can also have a positive role for themselves and their host countries - but that's another story.
The problem is, climate change affects the lives of those who cannot migrate.
Those who have to leave just because their field has dried up.
These are not migrants. They are part of an enforced displacement of population.
And those who are forced into such a population movement are of no use to themselves, or anyone else
as they also fall prey, vulnerabile as they are, to crime, trafficking, terrorism, and fanaticism.
What part does the climate play?
We are used to hear that a changing climate triggers the most extreme, violent phenomena and has the biggest impact on our bodies.
Heat waves puts older people in trouble, aerals - zones of incidence of some infectious diseases - are expanding.
Then we also hear that violent climatic phenomena damage our infrastructure.
Hurricanes cause bridges to collapse, and so on.
But almost no one speaks of the most devastating effect of climate change.
So, what is the greenhouse effect?
The greenhouse effect is the result of solar energy intake that's trapped in our ecosystem and not radiated back into space.
There's a lot of it!
The actual amount was shared in another TED talk, about 10 years ago, by a scientist, maybe a little controversial but still from NASA: James Hansen.
It is the equivalent of 400,000 atomic bombs detonating every day.
And where is this energy funneled, if not into our ecosystems, formed over the millennia?
It turns into disorder, it turns into unpredictability
And where the climate becomes unpredictable, society can hardly handle it.
If I am a farmer, and I can't predict when it will rain, I don't know when to sow.
But it's not just a rural problem.
If I manage the Milan water system, and I don't know if it'll snow or not, I have huge problems planning ahead.
Here, if it happens to us, to the extent that it happens now, we can still fix it.
But if it happens to a farmer in Burkina Faso then the problem is no longer economic.
A failed harvest, for a farmer in Valpadana can bring about economic losses - but he's probably insured, maybe the local government will help.
For the farmer from Burkina Faso it's the difference between sending their children to school or not.
And then it becomes a human rights problem.
We understand how a person under such a pressure with her future prospects and families basic needs at risk can fall prey to migration, fanaticism destabilisation of all kinds.
Is this in the future?
Unfortunately no, It's happening now.
It's already happening in areas with a fragile ecosystem superimposed onto a fragile society.
It's happening in the islands, that are yelling out in despair.
The Maldives are putting aside a portion of the proceeds from tourism to buy another island in the future.
It's happening in the river deltas, that are becoming salinised.
It's happening in the Arctic. The Arctic populations are all becoming desperate.
They are losing their culture's distinctive traits.
Which turned them to alcoholism, to suicide.
It's happening in the mountains, which are particularly vulnerable. And then it's happening right in front of us.
The place where it is happening most visibly, at the moment, is right in front of us. Just a short stretch of sea separates us from it.
Italy stretches out between the two shores of the Mediterranean like a kind of bridge.
Let me show you a map of part of northern Africa.
It consists mostly of desert that has been desert for a long time.
In these deserts, as we learned this morning, are populations that have found a way of life that's compatible with that stingy ecosystem.
But in this area there is not only the desert.
An area is also there that roughly coincides with the Sahel, where desertification is taking place.
If we compare these two areas - the desert and desertification zone, in objective terms the ​​desertification zone is still a bit more generous, still a bit richer, than the desert.
But in this ​​desertification zone the cycles of nature are no longer dependable on.
There is a chaotic, unregulated climate and the inhabitants can no longer sustain themselves.
Look where it is, and notice that it matches the areas where hunger and conflicts show up the most, the areas most afflicted by the dynamics of terrorism, the areas most afflicted by illicit trafficking of all kinds - arms, money, drugs, human beings.
And take a look at where nine out of ten migrants come from - those who are creating a bit of a crisis for us right now.
If that's the current reality, where Italy, in its front line role, is poised to set a standard, but we must not think that the problem ends there.
I'll show you two photos.
One is a map.
In this map you can see the glaciers of Hindu Kush, Himalayas, and Pamir.
These will eventually melt.
I have no time to explain why, but they will not melt gradually, proportional to the rise in temperature.
At a certain point there will be a collapse.
At some point, the glaciers in this zone will break down.
OK, you may say - but why should we Italians care?
Well, the fact is, every time an element is lost from this delicate equilibrium of the biosphere, we don't just lose it and its physical beauty.
Its function is also lost, the part it plays in maintaining the balance of the whole system.
Glaciers perform many functions: one of these should interests us a lot.
They regulate the ordered outflow of water downstream.
Local rivers have their source in those glaciers. They irrigate plains where one billion and four hundred million people live.
If the glaciers break down here, in a monsoon area - especially in the south, the situation in the valleys suddenly changes from one in which, during the drought, the melting glaciers supply the river with water into a disastrous oscillation between drought and flooding.
Agriculture and the whole infrastructure
is thrown off balance and much more.
One billion and four hundred million people.
We started to get in trouble with 380,000 people coming here.
It all adds up to this. The mountains are very fragile ecosystems, where very vulnerable people live.
OK, but they are not so much.. No no, 22% of the land will be affected and the 913,000,000 people who live there.
If they set in motion ...
And then the other image
shows a flooded subway in New York.
Rising sea levels is a very controversial issue. It's not clear how bad it will be. But now scientists - you may have heard that a recent report by the United Nations Panel believe it is quite plausible that levels will rise by 1,5 metres by the end of this century.
So, let's add on the 42% of the human population dwelling along the coasts.
If the sea rises 1,5 metres there will be flooded areas. But that's not the biggest problem.
Saltwater intrusion is worse.
Salt water gets into the soil and dehydrates it.
The Romans, to make sure a defeated city would never rise up again, poured sea salt on the fields.
And this is the effect it will have.
I see from your faces that my story has really depressed you.
(Laughter)
Is all lost? No, we can solve the problem.
And we can solve it in our short-term, cynical and self-interested way.
How?
Well, by reminding ourselves that we are part of a whole.
This diagram you see here has been developed by the Barilla foundation.
It shows two pyramids. The first one, the food pyramid, is very well known.
It shows the proportion of every type of food we should be eating to stay healthy.
So, a little red meat, a little more protein from other sources and increasing amounts of fibre, vegetables, and so on.
It so happens, if we juxtapose this pyramid over the pyramid showing the impact on the ecosystem of the production of each category of food, they match almost perfectly.
That is, the more a certain type of food is consumed - in excess, I'm not vegetarian - the more it damages both a person and the ecosystem.
Hmm, what a spectacular coincidence for the food sector!
Only, it's not a coincidence and it's not just under the industry's control.
It is a paradigm of our relationship with the ecosystem and you don't have to turn to otherworldly wisdom.
It is the result of co-evolution.
It's just normal for us to be completely healthy when we are "tuned" with the system we are part of.
If you do not believe me, try it yourself. Do a little exercise. Make a pyramid of your personal transport.
You'll notice that the better your choice of transport it is for your health, for your integral well-being, for your social life, your pace of life, the more sustainable it gets.
And this is a bit true in all areas.
But why am I telling you this?
Because this gives us a roadmap.
It gives us a rule. What's really good for you, is good for the planet too.
Let's move on from the individual to the global, geostrategic view.
If the West, as if by magic, decided to eat according to this pyramid, not only would we all be better off but it would help to stop the polarisation of the world between the one billion five hundred million people who suffer from disease, due to poor or excessive nutrition, and the 814 million people who go to bed hungry.
What does this all mean?
It means that sustainability and well-being turn into social justice and redistribution.
Those resources, consumed in excess by some, are freed up and become available to others.
A professor from Piacenza calculated exactly how much.
If every person registered as pathologically obese by western health services abstained from a single can of fizzy drink a day, that would free up enough resources to feed 56 million people.
So, my individual well-being becomes sustainability, sustainability becomes fairness, and fairness becomes peace, because a world where all children have access to decent nutrition is also a world that kicks sand in the face of Boko Haram, ISIS, etc.
And so I propose this equation, the Earth equation.
It seems very personal - my life in harmony with the planet;
but it is really a political choice.
We can apply this method.
These crescents you see dug in the ground are in Burkina Faso and is a system by which we can recover one hectare of desert for $130.
By doing this, what do we achieve?
We regenerate this land for agriculture. and we also create a extraordinarily efficient carbon sink as the vegetation grown back.
Not only that: we protect biodiversity. We consolidate the water / salt balance and reduce heat locally as vegetation brings down the temperature and re-start all the market, the business activity, etc.
In other words, we disrupt the trigger of fanatism, the mechanism that feeds human trafficking and war.
You don't believe me?
This is the real result.
If we don't do this, what happens?
We leave these countries prey to instability, so they can no longer cooperate in the recovery of the climate.
They are excluded from the communal battle to maintain a climate that is still manageable.
But leaving them out in the cold, none of us will have a manageable climate, because these countries contribute to manageable sustainably.
So what happens?
If we leave them to their own devices, we create the future that the IPCC and the UN panel described four days ago: 2040, the catastrophe.
It's just our own crass, cynical, selfish interests.
We must take care of the weakest and poorest.
Thank you.
(Applause)
My job began by chance, on an ordinary day.
Today, it is hard to believe of how many possibilities are hidden within habits.
My ordinary day, but that has forever changed the course of my life, happened five years ago.
I woke up and prepared the coffee, like I do every morning.
I have to admit it, I am basically a lazy person.
And although this trait of mine hasn't particularly helped me in life, it probably gave a certain depth to my observation, indeed thanks to slowness.
So, five years ago I made my coffee and accidentally spilled it on my kitchen table.
But always following this state of slowness, or laziness , I did not immediately clean this spot and took a moment to observe it.
And it seemed like a very interesting spot, I would say rather beautiful.
What I really like about this spot is that it is a gesture's perfect result, like drawing or writing.
And if we think about it, every spot is the result of a different movement of our wrist.
A bit like Japanese writing.
So that day I was mesmerized by my coffee spot and I started to think: "But what if, in addition to the movement, which is already intrisic, by nature, in the stain, I put an intention in it?
If from the cup of coffee, for example, could come out a portrait, a story or a landscape -
if even in everything I look there was a different reality if only one had the slowness and the right viewpoint to observe it?
So that day I started drawing inside the spot with what I had around me.
A brush or simply a spoon, a toothpick, what my kitchen offered me.
And that day I literally entered inside the cup, as if it were a parallel reality, a reality where I still currently am in and that has changed the course of my life.
My view on the world has become my job.
From the first spot of five years ago, many others were born, and within these spots many things happened.
Imaginary cities grew up, some people even fell in love, megalopolis rose up - Yes, there was a vessel too.
And I must say, I really lived incredible stories in these cups of coffee.
Looking at things up close, often very close, was eye opening.
It was the best thing that my job led me to do.
My eyes focused on shapes, colors.
I became hungry to see and understand the reason for certain textures that I found in nature, but always repeated themselves.
Also within my body,
such as the veins of the leaves.
Everything was a source of inspiration, just looked at with new eyes.
And it was a new view especially towards simplicity, towards habits, towards what you find at home, like vegetables.
And their shape and color fascinated me, as red was blending to green and vice versa.
And also their intricate inner structures. They just seemed almost human.
Of one thing I was perfectly certain: everything seemed to me intertwined by the red thread of harmony and meaning.
My work has therefore become a dialogue.
I put a part of me inside a leaf, for example, but tracing the natural veins, studying the thickness, the shape, the color, the strength.
Not seeing it as a simple mean I can dispose of, but as a form of life per se, full of things to say, full of stories to tell.
And there would be really a lot of things to say.
I often walk around my neighborhood, or I go to the countryside for a walk in search of small natural elements or leaves, with an idea in mind of drawing or carving.
Then I go back home, take all the things I picked up on the kitchen table, always the same one, and I put all the items in order.
And everything is so perfect, I realize, there is no need of any action by my side.
And in those magical moments, of epiphany, I feel there's a form of rest in beauty, and a tendency to good.
The very word rest is one of my favorite.
Try to close your eyes, and think what rest is for you.
Rest that is not due - it is not sleep, it is a different thing.
The word "rest" conveys more, to me, a sense of well-being, of peace.
And that's for me the beauty I discover in nature: the rest.
I think that beauty is a human necessity, precisely the need to rest your eyes, your heart, and to believe there are possibilities.
By beauty I do not mean the fake one, which tends to divide people, that we often see on social networks, especially on Instagram, I see it there very often, because it puts someone on a pedestal and everyone else below.
I mean that dense, meaningful beauty that I always find in natural shapes and in all that is spontaneity.
A kind of relatable, connecting beauty,
not something to struggle connecting to, but that we just have in ourselves.
In this regard, I would like to read you a poem by Wislawa Szymborska, a Polish poetess I particularly love who focuses precisely on the topic of everyday beauty in its simplest but deepest forms.
I'll read it in the best possible way. You read it again at home. maybe.
It is titled "A note"
Life is the only way to get covered in leaves, to catch your breath on the sand, to rise on wings.
To be a dog, or stroke its warm fur;
to tell pain from everything it's not;
to squeeze inside events, dawdle in views, to seek the smallest mistake.
An extraordinary chance to remember for a moment
a conversation held in the dark; and once, at least, to stumble upon a stone, end up soaked in the rain, mislay your keys in the grass; and follow with your eyes a spark on the wind ;
and to keep on not knowing something important.
Thank you.
(Applause)
If this were to be an ordinary day on our planet, by midnight humans will have spat out 15 million tons of carbon dioxide, destroyed 200 square kilometres of rainforest and created 15 kilometres of desert.
The same happened yesterday, will occur tomorrow and so on until the end of the year.
We'd better not think about it though, or we will spoil Christmas: who would ever want to celebrate, after hearing such figures?
Welcome to the Anthropocene.
The Anthropocene is the geological era when human beings and their impact are mainly responsible for physical, climatic and structural changes on our planet.
Evidences of the Anthropocene are everywhere.
The rate of extinction for both animals and plants species is far above average: we are talking about 75% of species dying out within the next two centuries.
Mankind, while on one hand is causing certain species to die out, we're also contributing to the spread of other living species; the domestic chicken, for example, is the most common bird on our planet
and by chicken's fossil future geologists will define the Anthropocene.
Human activities generates materials like concrete, aluminium and plastic, which are spreading and depositing on the surface of the Earth’s crust and are called technofossils.
What if the pace of plastic production and discharge into the ocean remained steady?
By 2050, plastic will weigh more than all living species on our planet.
We live on the Earth as if there were another planet to dwell; but before abandoning it, we want to leave a permanent mark on its surface.
Funny, isn’t it? Yet it’s no laughing matter.
An even more serious problem is that there is no increased awareness, nor any growing concern for the environment.
Actually, there seems to be no concern at all.
Research shows, what used to be two closely related elements, mankind and nature, are two worlds that are now slowly growing further and further apart.
Soon, we’ll face a situation where humans and nature will be two distinct, opposed elements.
Here are some figures, just so you get the scope of the problem: three out of four children in my country, the UK, spend less time in open air than inmates.
Independent research in the United States agree with this: in fact, a Harvard University study shows that 16 percent of children between the 13 and 16, so basically teenagers, think they are ‘environmentally literate’, which means having a vast knowledge of environmental mechanisms and systems.
We are constantly bombed with news and information by the media, by television, which tell us how serious the global crisis is. However, always in the UK, only 6% of the population put environmental issues among the top three problems to be solved.
I’m an adventurer, an explorer and I have always had a certain intimacy with nature, I always tried to establish a relationship with it.
I’ve had the chance, the privilege, to get to know both faces of the natural world: its extreme and hostile one; and the vulnerable, fragile one.
Nature has welcomed me, allowed me to survive, nourished me, shaped my dreams and turned them into reality.
And every time, whatever the adventure, I found myself in a journey, making an even more beautiful discovery: the one that alowed me to live in close communion with myself, to get to know myself a little better.
And this is why today I feel the urge to stand up and talk on its behalf.
We live in an unprecedented era, if you come to think about it: plenty of opportunities and wealth, on one side; on the other decay, sadness and crisis.
And when I speak about crisis, I mean at every level: from the personal one, the psychological crisis, to a social and a global one, the one that affects us all, regardless of race.
And I’m sure, or better, I am convinced, that these crises, from the smallest to the biggest, could be, I won't say overcome, but at least relieved, by a single, easy, yet powerful action: reconnecting human beings to nature.
This is the idea of bridge I would like to share with you today: let’s rebuild a bridge between people and what belongs to them by nature.
Let’s start small, from the individual.
We spend less and less time outdoors, I brought along some figures to prove this to you.
So one may wonder, ‘Why do we spend so little time outdoors?’
There are many reasons.
Surely, our society invites us to distance ourselves from nature, it stops us from expressing nature and experiencing it.
I am a parent, I have two daughters, and I find the idea that my daughters can go and play in the woods stimulating and moving; on the other hand, I can’t help thinking that I am constantly bombed with news of kidnappings,
and so, dissociation is easily triggered.
Plus, media outlets try their best to label natural phenomena with human behaviours and emotions.
Just consider recent headlines and news: the fury,
the sea that swallows,
killer avalanches.
This creates a dissociation between nature and fun.
And then, of course, there is television, the computer, which vie to attract our children’s and even our own attention; an increasingly busier life, ever longer schools, traffic.
Whatever the reason, we spend less and less time outdoors.
And consequences are before our eyes, since more and more studies prove there is a connection between mental illnesses, such as depression, ADHD or other similar mental disorders, and the lack of contact with nature.
And today, more and more often, we witness the opposite phenomenon, psychologists and psychotherapists prescribing ecotherapy.
Are you familiar with ecotherapy?
Simply put, it is going for a walk in the woods.
And one doesn't need a psychotherapist to get how important it is for us, how beneficial nature is.
We notice it every time we allow ourselves to go for a hike in the woods, to walk our dog; once we are done, tell me if I’m wrong, we feel revitalised, more focused, more rooted in the present,
listening to other people and to ourselves, because the bounty of nature awakens a sense of introspection.
The second reason why it’s worthwhile to recreate a connection with nature has to do with the sense of community, with finding a sense of community that transcends races and redefines the concept of us and them.
Let me get this straight: in 2005, English psychologist Mark Levine carried out an experiment: he wanted to study the behaviour of a group of people in relation to the attitude of people outside said group,
How belonging to a group also defines the behaviour of individuals.
He carried out a very, very interesting experiment: he called some people into a room and had them fill a form and this form's goal was developing passion for a football team.
Once they’d finished with the form, he invited these people to move to a room in the adjacent building for a second test.
Right between the two buildings, Mark Levine put a person, an actor, lying on the ground, pretending to be in pain and in need for help.
The person was wearing a Manchester shirt.
Well, 92% of the people supporting the same football team as the actor’s rushed to help him, while only 30% of the fans of another football team did the same.
Do you see what this implies?
Being able to recreate a connection with the natural world allows us all to play on the same team.
The match is easy if we all play for the same team,
because on the other side the goal is empty.
For the first time in human history, something connects us,
something that transcends all races, ideologies, colours, languages,
and in my opinion, that's a chance we can’t miss.
The third reason why we should rebuild a connection with the natural world is related to the very serious global environmental crisis
The environment, and nature, our world, never felt the need for any support as life always re-blossomed spontaneously.
Today, instead, we have before us a number of progress by-products that are threatening us.
Should you have lived in a cave over the last 50 years, I am referring to: depletion of the ozone layer; deforestation; global warming; urbanization; urban air pollution, water pollution.
All around us, we can constantly see that there’s something asking for our help.
And this something is, of course, the environment.
Thus, despite it being so obvious to everybody, why is it so difficult to try and convince people to care about the environment?
Why do we cope with hostility and refusal, everytime we broach this issue?
Have you ever wondered why?
I’ve got a couple of ideas:
my first assumption is, we are profoundly, unabatedly optimistic.
We always hope that somebody will eventually come and save us or clean up the dirt we leave behind.
Yet, we know that is not the case: nobody is coming to save us:
we must do it ourselves, individually.
Then it is, clearly, a matter of costs: we don’t do something because it’s right, we do it because it’s cost-effective.
And from a financial standpoint, plastic is perfect.
From an ecological point of view, it is horribly imperfect, inherently unsustainable.
In my opinion, there is a third reason that we should put on the table:
there is a certain distance between human beings and the consequences of their actions,
or rather, to put it another way, ‘out of mind, out of sight’, sorry, I meant ‘out of sight, out of mind’,
therefore, things you don't see, do not exist.
This is technically known as ‘psychological distance’, the distance between us and the effects of our actions that makes us feel indifferent when acting unethically.
How many of you can claim to be truly concerned about the environment?
Raise your hands.
Interesting. Keep your hands up, please.
How many of you drank water from a plastic bottle this morning?
I didn’t.
You’re nuts!
This is madness!
Madness because someone will have to pay the price; they might be living half world away and you might not even know the name of those who will pay the price for your plastic bottle.
But, after all, we don’t mind about what is going on in the other part of the world; we only aim to make the most of our chances to be happy.
Then, since I’m very curious, I would be glad if somebody found a way to shorten the distance between humans and the consequences of their actions.
In this matter, a few months ago I came up with an idea, something which, along with what I’m best at, being an explorer, could induce people to think about their impact on the world.
This is the purpose of my next project, sailing down the world's ten most plastic-polluted rivers with a small self-made boat.
I brought you a short video, so everybody can see what my project is about.
What you are watching is a common plastic bottle, not even the most beautiful one.
In a few days, this bottle will join the Great Pacific Garbage Patch, a plastic island into the Pacific Ocean, between Hawaii and California, It spans across 1.6 million square km, three times as big as France.
Our bottle’s journey does not start in the sea, though. but in a small village on river Ganges, in India.
Along with this bottle, 90% of the plastic polluting our oceans comes from only 10 rivers.
These are the world's most polluted rivers.
My name is Alex Bellini. I’m an explorer.
With my next expedition, I want to undertake the same journey that millions of plastic items make each year.
I will eventually join the Great Pacific Garbage Patch.
I will start sailing the 10 most polluted rivers on Earth in a raft I will build myself using recycled materials.
You're certainly asking, ‘Why does he do this?’
I am doing it because I want to stir the world’s conscience.
I am doing it because many people still can't get how big this problem is, or maybe they just ignore it.
I am doing it because if we do nothing, experts say that by 2050, plastic could outweigh the marine species that inhabit it.
It is not just an ecological or economic problem, it is a health problem. Everyone’s health.
I am doing it because we are responsible for this situation and we're the only ones who can make a difference.
I am doing it because there is no Planet B.
Of course, it will not be easy.
We are so deeply immersed in our problems that we cannot even imagine a remedy.
On my adventure I will move away from the heart of the plastic crisis to search for a solution that can only be achieved by looking from afar at our One Great Ocean .
[You can also make a difference] [Support the cause by sharing this video] [and be a spokesperson for my message] [Thank you. Visit alexbellini.com] (Applause) And, to sum up - thanks - to sum up, if I had the chance to give you a piece of advice, it would be the following: for once, once in your lifetime, get hold of a small row boat and leave the mainland until the shore is out of sight.
Why am I asking you to do this?
Well, when you look at the sky at sunset, in that bright red, yellow and orange kaleidoscope that lights the sky before the sun goes down and the day is plunged into darkness, you will be spellbound by the vastness surrounding you, which you will suddenly feel an integral part of.
That will be the flickering flame of a new beginning, a new bridge that we desperately, urgently need.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good evening. My name is Luca and I am a photographer.
This means that about 20 years ago I decided to express myself through images and photographs.
Tonight I have been asked to talk, so I should apologise in advance, if I won't be as precise as I usually am in my photographs.
Five years ago I decided to begin a project that would allow me to understand myself better as well as the people around me.
So I started Almost Europe.
An independent photography project to investigate and document European daily life and the delicate mechanisms that oversee the relationships between people of the Land of the Broad View, the etymology of Europe.
The Land of the Broad View... Europe.
I thought that the best way to turn this idea into reality was to start an exploratory journey, to leave, in search of Europe.
I was convinced that understanding my identity as a European could help me interact better with the rest of the world, so I began travelling.
Me, my backpack, my camera, my passport, and, most important, my questions.
One of these questions followed me in these 5 years of the project and still lingers in my ears; it is simple and complicated at the same time: what is Europe for me?
If I asked each one of you, I would probably get 200 different replies, as it should be.
My humble effort has been to give my best possible answer to this question.
And I tried to find this answer travelling back and forth around Europe, wandering around 46 different European cities.
When I say back and forth, and wandering, I mean it quite literally.
Trajectories were created, trips, itineraries that would allow me to cross our continent from one end to the other.
My first trip was to Eastern Europe.
A trip beyond the blue curtain, the new, imaginary border that was created after the latest eastward expansion of the European Union.
I started in Kaliningrad on the Baltic Sea, a Russian exclave in European territory lodged between Poland and the Baltic republics.
I went South towards Istanbul, crossing Belarus, Ukraine, Moldova, Transnistria then by ship on the Black Sea till I reached Istanbul.
This first voyage is the one that named the whole project: Almost Europe.
You can easily imagine why Almost Europe, not yet Europe.
There the countries are in a limbo: squeezed between a former empire and a European economy.
In the following years, the last three years, I stayed within the borders of what we consider the Union, obviously with a few exceptions.
I went from Northern Norway, from Narvik all the way down to Pozzallo, in Southern Sicily.
Then I went from West to East, from Lisbon to St. Petersburg and last year I travelled along an imaginary diagonal from Cyprus to Iceland.
And after all these kilometres, after all the people I met, what is, in the end, Europe for me?
The answer, my answer, is in these photographs.
I will be honest with you: in the end, the kilometres that I travelled and the people that I met were not enough to clear my thoughts.
But I realised that the title that I imagined, Almost Europe, fits well both Eastern Europe, in its being always in the middle between European Union and Russian Federation, as well as the Europe of the 28, always looking for its identity.
Fascinating because incomplete in its personality full of different sides, so in its own way not yet, "Almost Europe".
A talented and intelligent Europe often incapable of showing its true worth, sometimes lacking its self-awareness.
Remember your school days?
Something teachers always told me "The boy is clever but inconsistent."
That sentence followed me for years and today, when I think about Europe, it comes back to mind.
But I think that something is, slowly, changing.
I don't want to underestimate the strength of populism nor the political and ideological radicalisation that usually follows it which finds fertile breeding ground in the well known slowness of European institutions and in the perception of the citizens of an institution that appears bureaucratic, formal, cold, and aloof from people's real needs.
Without underestimating these aspects I still believe that a new generation of Europeans is growing, and not only from the bottom.
There are different factors that contribute to this slow but fruitful growth.
Think about the Erasmus generation, about all the citizens involved in communities based on exchange economics, about all the possibilities that we have, all of us residents of the Schengen area, but think as well about the ongoing great crisis, both economic and humanitarian, that are forcing us and giving us the opportunity to think in a different way.
Good Europe-embracing sentiments, until a few years ago, were exclusive to intellectual élites and power lobbies.
Now a great part of society, the more mature, emancipated, educated and empowered, believes that the Europe issue must be addressed.
What is Europe?
This is a question that we know in our hearts that must be answered in order to be ready for the issues and the problems that history will indeed place in front of us.
In a single word, European citizens feel the need to participate.
This new interest is one of the reasons why I could travel for months, meet so many people, and spend so little money among other things.
Train to train, city to city, sofa to sofa, by bus, by train, even by ship when necessary.
Walking and taking photographs.
I met people well-disposed towards hospitality and exchange that didn't stop at simply offering me a bed or something to eat.
They wanted to talk, they wanted to understand, they wanted to be part of it.
They walked with me.
This should not be taken for granted.
Walking, observing, talking to each other are the actions that showed mankind's intelligence and marked our evolution.
Today these actions have become rare and precious, so much so that they have acquired a political meaning, in contrast with the loss of sense of direction typical of post-industrial people and with the loss of our critical analysis and choice.
Traveling within a land, in my opinion, corresponds to the act of claiming it back.
Wandering without destination allows me to create an imaginary map and to make the dream of equal and fair access come true, a dream of independent movement, of vanishing control.
When I photograph a city people and masses are the elements that poke my curiosity and my need to subvert a destiny of solitude, indifference and irrelevance. A destiny that I embrace only to transform it into a virtue.
I employ anonymity to dive into the fast moving masses, slowly.
I do nothing but follow everything.
Standing at a street corner I just wait for something to happen.
My attention is focused only on what happens in that moment; while everything around me must follow the messy balance of instants, of coincidences, of encounters; I become their only voluntary spectator.
Nobody notices that I am looking,
nobody imagines that, taking photos, I am stealing, in order to preserve it, the most fleeting and important thing we have: the heritage of our daily life, the richness of its gestures which is slowly fading away, its powerful and fading wonders, the meaning of our movements, the meaning of our habits.
Beyond the hard face of the cities, the urban show offers a poetic landscape.
I can't tell if the banality of consumerism, the violent urbanization, the invasion of cars, and other indicators of progress, have truly belittled the wonderful pleasure that a simple walk can be.
It is certain, though, that our imagination is inundated, every single day, by information that is ever more devoid of meaning and that gives us an illusory sense of freedom, while taking away our true and spontaneous participation to the world around us.
Keeping your head high, walking, and meeting other people, becomes a challenge.
A challenge to turn our eyes away from smart devices, mobile phones, true assassins of our free time, a challenge to deviate from compulsory itineraries, to rid ourselves of automatic gestures, to reclaim the "now" and "here" in order to regain the power to follow with renewed eyes the little balloon that, once released from our grasp, will fly far away on the wind of chance.
This is my humble Europe, and these are my answers.
(Applause)
We’ve heard a lot recently about smart working; in companies, we're talking about working from home, all tools that tend to reconcile working life with private life.
I read recently that, by 2020, smart working, intended as working from home, will be the situation in 51% of Italian companies.
Well, the article ended with the sentence, “And finally the needs of the individual will be at the core of the relationship with the company.”
Now, this conclusion made me think a bit, because something's bothering me.
First of all, I’d like to overturn the idea of a contrast between the individual’s needs and the company’s needs.
A company isn’t an abstract entity.
A company is made up of people, of individuals,
and becomes a company only with
these relate with one another. Well, from my point of view, this is a key point.
I’d also like to remind you that “smart” means “intelligent”, it isn’t a synonym of remote working.
So today, I’d like to tell you what smart for a company means to me.
Tenova, the company I work for, is a multinational,
it’s an engineering company that, in fact, employs 3000 people in 22 countries around the world, in over 50 offices, in the iron and steel, and mining sectors.
Well, our people and their know-how are the mainstay of our company,
but the relationships that these people form with each other enable them to produce the machines that you can see.
Now, we don’t always produce all of these machines, but, in fact, we always design them.
Not only mechanical pieces come out of our offices, but ideas, technologies that enable the production of these machines.
Well, for us, people are all that we have.
Now, this is very important for a company, especially for those like ours whose mainstay is their know-how, it’s important to generate exchange, internal and external exchange:
internal, between employees who have different skills roles and experiences; and external exchange with suppliers, clients, schools, universities and institutions.
In short, it’s important to think about this need for exchange, and every day to try to create the conditions so that these relationships are expressed better.
This is what I call “smart connecting”
Now, let’s think about the etymology of the word “company”.
“Company” comes from the Latin "cum panis", “sharing in the same bread”.
Now, a company is a group of individuals who share the same objectives, the bread, and they try to achieve them together.
In the end, what is the goal of a business?
The business must ensure competitiveness,
by means of three things: efficiency, that is, every day, by improving processes, production to the processes.
Then, innovation, product innovation, service innovation.
And finally, maintenance and growth of the know-how of the people.
Well, I was fortunate enough to visit some companies.
One of these is a company that has pushed the concept of remote working to the extreme.
And when I visited it, I met some brilliant young people.
I was able to ask these young people some questions.
I asked them, “What would you change in your working place?” Well, they said, “Here, we’re alone, we don’t know who to learn from.
We’re learning from the PC.
If we’d known this, we wouldn’t have come here.
Let us go back to the office.
Managers, experienced people, we want to learn from them.” Well, I want to tell you our story, which is a little different.
Three years ago, we took the courageous decision to move the Tenova head office, from the centre of Milan to Castellanza, where we already had a production site.
Well, we’ve been here for two years, on a site near here that we call the Tenova campus.
But why did I say it was a courageous decision?
Because from the very start, we knew everyone was against us, those from Castellanza, because, somehow, with the move, they felt occupied, invaded, and the employees from Milan, who, like me, were living 300 m from their workplace, to the logistical convenience of the city centre,
and so, they saw this move, rather like … they called it “deportation to the country”.
To give you an idea, one of the questions I was asked was, “Is there a hairdresser’s in Castellanza?” A hairdresser’s;
naturally, I didn't ask the question; Nor did I know the answer to that.
Well, we decided to go ahead, to go ahead with the move and with the investment, and to the logistical difficulties of building the premises, we added the relational difficulties of putting two groups of collaborators together for the first time, to live under the same roof.
But we went ahead because, in my opinion, we had the spaces for the working environment we wanted, not the centre of Milan, but here, in Castellanza.
Well, we wanted an open space office, training rooms, an auditorium.
We wanted a nice, bright canteen, green spaces to walk in, to meet up in.
We wanted to have offices, workshops and the factory close by, to do research,
but also a gym, a sports field.
Well, in short, all of that, all of these spaces helped us to create closeness and relationships.
Now, I’d like to give you another example.
I once visited a start-up in Israel, in an office much like this building,
And when I stepped in, I saw a huge net on the ceiling, full of coloured balls.
For a moment, I thought it was a kindergarten for employees’ children.
Well, I asked what it was.
They told me, everytime a cooperator had a problem - a problem with a client, with an internal process, whatever - well, he would click on an icon, an icon on his desktop, on the computer, and through a system of ducts this released a red or a yellow ball, depending on how serious the problem was, into the net.
And then, when the collaborator solved a problem, he would click on another icon,
and this icon would release a green ball into the net.
This sorted two extremely important effects.
The first was that, when people saw the red and green balls, they could see clearly, without any sophisticated performance measuring systems, how the company was doing.
When the day was done, seeing the ceiling full of red balls certainly wasn’t a good anticipation for monthly results.
But even more, an extraordinary team effect: after seeing the red balls, people stopped clicking on icons; they called meetings, focused on solving the problems, got together and brought in ideas.
In short, everyone focused on problem-solving.
Some of them finished their work, joined the others and put at their disposal.
Some even offered to do the shopping for everyone, so that they could go on all evening.
Well, this is a rather extreme example of sharing, sure.
But let's get back to my starting point.
Every successful business, at the end of the day, needs to focus on what I called “smart connecting”, on relationships between people.
Technology, of course, somehow remote working in some ways.
And we’re doing a lot of things in this direction, but it’s wrong to only look at it this way.
In our offices, for example, we’re creating spaces, using virtual reality, where we can bring clients to live new experiences.
How?
We’re creating a virtual reality room, where, thanks to virtual reality, our clients can relive the experience, for example, of going up 100 metres on one of our systems for loading and unloading ships, or of going into a furnace at 1000 °C, and seeing what happens inside.
Of course, these experiences aren’t possible in the real world.
Our mines are in deserts, in the middle of nowhere, often deep down.
The steel plants we build have extremely high temperatures, so you can’t get physically close to them.
Now, thanks to virtual reality, we can create relationships that were once inconceivable.
Now, I'm not here to say this model can fit all companies.
I’m only saying that each company, should, first of all, looking at itself, and analyze how its people work in teams, on what relationships are like, because relationships create value, not a mere following of the trends.
Now, of course, once you’ve won this challenge, all of the instruments, flexible working, flexible hours, remote working, as well as the company nursery or gym, and yoga courses at work, are all tools that ease the private, family dimension, for me also.
But this should be the end point, it can’t be the starting point.
I’m considering the company I defined before, I’m thinking about the challenge of this company I defined before.
Now, this challenge isn’t won with mere remote working.
In my opinion, this challenge is won with “smart connecting”.
In fact, this challenge is won with emotional intelligence, not in solitude.
You win by putting 3000 people together in 50 offices, not by creating 3000 home offices.
In my opinion, a company is a team, and as a team, we train together every day.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good morning.
I think we might agree on one concept, that is the sense of insecurity which affects us for years now.
Insecurity is an economic instability or insecurity due to crime.
But this is in conflict with actual data about crime.
Because if we look at this broad spectrum, from 2004 until 2016, we will see that murders and attempted murders have fallen sharply.
And if compared with other European countries, you see Italy in that little rectangle, well, we can say that Italy is a safe haven.
Thanks, above all, to the judicial authority, thanks to the police forces that everyday sacrifice their lives to make us safe.
But something happened in the meantime, and it has happened since the eighties.
My activity as a forensic biologist
began back then. Here you see a case that marked the criminal history as well as the Italian history, namely the kidnapping and murder of the Congressman prof. Moro.
But what I want you to notice is, you see, the way they walk around the place, especially the throng on and around the Renault car, where you for sure remember, the Congressman prof. Moro was found dead, that actually for us represented, at that time, as now, the crime scene.
But nobody was aware that scene had to be protected,
And so, just from those years, the scientific proof developed progressively.
That is, the scientific proof became part of the investigative activity and I have had the privilege of experiencing this progression.
But if we rewind the story a little, we must also remember, as Suetonius reminds us in his "Lives of Caesar", that the first use of the scientific evidence was in the autopsy of Julius Caesar.
Antistius performed it.
Antistius is the attending physician of Julius Caesar, but he was forced to determine what the cause of his death was.
Which bladed weapon caused his death.
Until the first case, a case of sexual violence, followed by a murder of two young girls in England, where for the first time, it happened around 1984-86, the DNA test was used.
DNA enters this scenario becoming an opportunity, an indispensable and extraordinarily essential one, to bring criminals to justice.
But all we do in our laboratories, as well as on the crime scene, we owe it to this man, a groundbreaker from this point of view, Edmond Locard, who in 1910 elaborated, the "Exchange principle of Locard", which sums up in a sentence full of charm and modernity: "Every contact leaves a trace behind".
If you think about it, every time we meet someone or go somewhere, in those visits we leave traces and take with us other traces that become the evidence of our contacts, of our path.
Well, this allowed all the sciences, that until that time had not developed alongside investigative activity, become forensic sciences.
And biology, chemistry, physics, engineering, are those all precisely focused in the need to be able to give answers to what?
To the finds and evidences that are on the victim, which are found at the crime scene.
You see, this is certainly a partial list but today, precisely thanks to the great power of sciences, we can give a sense and meaning to insects, pollens, paints, fibers, not to mention more conventional traces, like the biological ones.
Everything can be important to link us to that event and show us the responsibilities.
But of course, it is not only about physical evidences.
Today, psychological ones also came along, those that study the victim, that draw elements to understand which may have been the reason and once again to understand what might be the list of suspects for that particular crime.
And then, of course, digital footprints and all that we leave through our devices, like mobile phone, and not only we leave traces of our communications, but also of our location.
And I must say that if DNA in 1984 transformed the way investigations were carried out, computer forensic has surely overcome these possibilities that genetics had.
But is it all that so simple? No.
It would be absolutely boring.
We would not be here, constantly focused on research.
Because many evidences are invisible ones.
And a way to search for invisible evidences, both biological and non-organic, is the use, for example, of forensic lamps that exploit all the light spectrum, which you know is made up of wave frequencies ranging from ultraviolet to infrared, and allow us, as in this case, this is a t-shirt worn by a murderer, to shed light on the traces that you do not see inside this t-shirt, but which, lighted by infrared, gives us the chance to identify traces which have different origins.
There are contact traces, there are traces of projection that compared with the statements of the suspect, later convicted, made it possible to understand that everything he told was absolutely mendacious.
And then there's chemistry.
Chemistry, like luminol, very popular in investigations, even in those that we know about thanks to the media.
But it is a reaction that, together with hemoglobin presence, even in extremely small quantities, allows us to actually see if there are still traces.
Those two photographs that you see from below are real, and they refer, maybe you'll remember, to the double murder of Novi Ligure in which in the first one on the left, you see a kitchen where you can just see so much dirt apart from those signs made with chalk, and on the right, instead, you see the footprints of bloody feet that marked the movements and therefore the behaviour of Omar Favaro who was one of the two murderers of that small family.
Then there are measuring instruments, such as the 3D laser, that allows us not only to measure but to model and to make hypotheses to understand what happened.
And then, you see, two completely different worlds.
The one of Giulia, so creative, and the somewhat gruesome one that concerns my job.
But I deal with stains too, bloodstains.
Because we look at shape, size and distribution. of bloodstains.
Because when a stain moves in the atmosphere, as you see in this fast animation, is a little ball, the technical term is "spheroid", which shortens and stretches only because it is affected by the force of gravity.
But bloodstains aren't just subjected to gravity.
Why?
Because they draw a trajectory, they are projected by a force,
and then from this point of view we see another animation and see how, with a single event, we get a lot of different bloodtraces.
Of course, when we get to that scene, it is clear that there's no leg, no foot, but we know ex-post how to recreate, depending on those morphologies and those trajectories, what was the source point, namely where do those traces come from.
And this, together with the possibility of understanding: are those traces only left by the force of gravity, like drip or contact?
Or are they projection traces?
But think also about the areas without traces, the empty ones.
What they tell us?
The mutual positions of the aggressor and the victim, which we then recreate thanks to software that today have greatly eased our activities.
When I started in the US to deal with this, well, it probably was even more fascinating than today, but I used to measure stain by stain, I used to pull strings, that is rigid wires, I calculated the trajectories and just only by studying the single trajectories, I succeeded to determine what you see here, the area of origin, with no IT tools.
Thank goodness, we have them now.
And then I want to tell you about a real murder case,
firstly considered a suicide.
The suicide of Valentina Salamone.
You see her there, that girl suspended by that rope in the photo on the left.
Everyone thought Valentina took her life because of a troubled relatioship, struggling with her life and family.
And for many days this case was treated as a suicide.
But there was her father.
In my professional career I meet many fathers who want justice.
Who struggled, who couldn't believe that, who really wanted to understand what had happened.
Then he managed to get the public prosecutor to take the investigation, so they abandoned what they had been done so far, to try to do some in-depth analysis.
You see these in-depth analysis summarized in that sandal, that Valentina was wearing when she was first killed and then suspended, and you see on the right, circled in red, a blood stain, a clot.
Well, if you notice it, Valentina's heel is higher than her toes.
But this stain, which you see on the right side of the sandal, is in a clearly opposite position.
It means that Valentina was first injured, and kept in a position different from the suspension when she was already injured
and she was then suspended when she was dying.
She died for asphyxiation, but as a result of the injuries that someone had caused her.
And then this aspect, this so important particular ensured us to further deepen our experimentation. It is called judicial experiment, that is, to use a person of the same size of Valentina to prove that she could not do everything by herself, that she could not either hang the rope on that beam nor tie that knot.
And then, all this has been further investigated, because those sandals would tell us who, hypothetically, commited the crime.
I say this because we still are on the first degree trial and therefore we presume innocence until the third grade of judgment.
But it was his boyfriend, who left traces inside this sandal, obviously mixed with Valentina's blood.
A case that would have been closed as a suicide but that, in reality, was hiding a murder.
Thanks to this study - you see the synthesis, the combination of DNA and BPA, bloodstain pattern analysis.
Well, I have to say that as a biologist I would never have thought about this evolution of the DNA.
But we have been using it for 30 years now, and I had the privilege, if I can speak of privilege, along with sadness, of dealing with an equally tragic case, namely the Capaci massacre.
A few days ago, in fact, we remembered that event
and I worked with Dr. Spinella on the analysis of those cigarette butts which then allowed us to trace the command along with traditional investigations.
And then you saw how much DNA was useful, and perhaps only thanks to DNA, to find who was the murderer of Yara Gambirasio.
With a genetic screening that for the first time has been so extensive, that is the analysis of more than 20.000 DNA, to understand who the owner was, whom those traces of blood belonged to.
But DNA is not just this, it is a database and thankfully we also have a database now.
But it isn't only human DNA, animal and plant DNA also exist.
There is a case that media already covered and surely you will know more details soon, which is another cold case, the murder of Serena Mollicone, which has been solved and gave important elements, thanks to the DNA that linked it to plant fragments of a door.
So think about the actual power of this DNA that of course today gives us the awareness of being able to solve many unresolved cases of the past, the so-called cold cases or cold track cases, like the murder of Olgiata that has been solved 17 years after it was committed, again with incredible investigative errors.
But not just this: DNA is as fascinating as powerful.
It has no limits, because it gives us new possibilities to identify the eyes color, the hair color, the weight, the shape of the face, all valuable investigative elements particularly in the initial phase of the investigation.
And it's not just DNA.
That's why this research is extremely fascinating.
Think of the population of microorganisms that each of us carry on themselves and has become another tool for identifying or finding the T.O.D.
And what about the smells, that is all those substances, different for each one of us, due to our habits and our profession, our way of living, that are becoming another means for identification.
But all of this has a goal.
And to wrap up, I must mention an US initiative called The Innocence Project, which also landed to Italy some time ago, an initiative that started from the awareness that DNA could finally solve many cold cases.
And look: since 1989, 365 people have been judged innocent.
The amazing thing is that 20 of them had been sentenced to death,
and they were innocent.
And they was saved
thanks to DNA, by science. And in more than 50 percent of these subjects, their exemption allowed, through a new DNA analysis, to identify the real perpetrators.
So, what are the awakenings?
Well, I would say the awakening of social commitment, the awakening of our sense of responsibility, passion, constant research, dedication towards our citizens.
Because as Hemingway said: "The world is a fine place, and worth fighting for".
Thank you.
(Applause)
So many sounds are part of our life!
Some are just sensorial.
And often quite imperceptible.
They can also hide desires.
They can be chaotic.
Annoying.
But in most cases, they determine our life, giving us emotions.
But did you ever imagine an existence without sounds?
Which other senses would we use?
Sight.
Smell.
Touch.
What does a silent world mean?
Four years ago, my mom told me: Why don't you learn sign language?
You are good at languages and you are a manual person.
Well these two skills that I have, if they can be considered as such, were enough for her to link me to this world I know so little about but that I've always looked at with interest and curiosity.
My mum has always been a farsighted person.
I've listened to her advice, and it's four years now that I've been studying this world, which has slowly and gently entered my heart, my mind and above all my eyes.
Studying this language did not only enrich my resume, but it also enabled me to have a new vision of the world around me.
Firstly, I have learnt that deafness is not just silent, it's also invisible.
It's a disability that can't be perceived but when you need to engage in a dialogue.
This hearing invisibility has been fairly bridged, over the years, with the so-called Sign Language. In Italian, LIS, Lingua Italiana Dei Segni [Italian Sign Language].
It is a full-fledged language, based on sight and gestures.
It's a language expressed through the body and perceived through the sight.
A language in which, by repeating a sign or changing the position from the body, you can change the meaning as well.
For example, the sign for "yellow" is the same as the one used for "woman".
The only difference, apart from lip movement, is repetition: to say "yellow" you repeat it once; to say "woman" more than once.
Despite all the differences behind this language, it always manages to express, clearly and economically, concepts that sometimes in Italian are very hard to convey.
How many times does your partner say it is difficult to understand what's on your mind?
Well, I suggest you to study a little bit of sign language and maybe you will be able to solve some couple communicating problems.
The characteristic I love the most about this language is that to express a concept you mimic its meaning, thus creating a conversation with no misunderstandings.
To give you an example, the sign for "I love you" is this one.
I take my heart and give it to the other person. I exactly mime what "I love you" means.
As for me, I only understood some sentences of my everyday life only after I had to translate them into sign language.
Given the transparency of expression, a lot of researches in Italy were run over the last few years.
In the Speech Therapy Faculty of the University of Milan, for example, researchers study how gestures, especially early in life, can help children express concepts that words can hardly tell.
Despite what we generally think, sign language is not international, but differs from a country to another, just as different dialects exist in different regions.
Think about Italy.
Just to make a quick example, in the North of Italy we use more lips, while in the South we use more signs. Different aspects, for each word.
Despite this, even if sign language is spoken by 170,000 people in Italy alone, it is still not recognised by our country as a State official language.
But let's delve into this: who are the main characters of this silent world?
Italian Sign Language is spoken by a really multifaceted community.
Every deaf has a different way to perceive a sound. Deafness can be light or severe, and family background can deeply affect the way one engages with the external world.
I've met deaf people proud of their language, who didn't feel at all the need to learn how to speak.
Some, on the other hand, cast it off.
And after years of speech therapy, their pronunciation is smoother than mine.
Others, usually born to hearing parents, use the so-called "signed Italian".
What is that?
We know that Italian is based on the SVO order, i.e. subject-verb-object: "I go home" for example.
Italian Sign Language instead, much like Germanic languages, is based on the SOV order, subject-object-verb: "I home go".
In signed Italian, I would say this simple sentence like "I go home".
Signed Italian uses Italian grammar and syntax and translates them into signs.
Despite all these, in my opinion, marvellous differences behind the apparently simple world of hearing impairment, there is a shared feature, the feeling of being isolated.
This feeling develops from the very beginning, when the kids go to school and no one is there to help them. They get frustrated and stay at a distance from their hearing mates.
Just imagine what it means for a kid not being able to hear.
I bet that, if we all think about it, many of our memories are linked to sound.
What does it mean for a kid to go to the courtyard wishing to play with other kids, and not understanding them?
What does it mean, for the one who doesn't know yet how to read and write, to turn on the TV and not being able to understand?
If we think about it, hearing, for children, is not only fundamental for communication and integration, but also for understanding their own lives.
To deal with that, the project "Manii - parla con le mani" was born.
It seems at first glance an interactive game. But it actually is a tool that tries to tear down the social barriers built up by differences, by building bridges of knowledge.
This project originates from merging my two passions and studies: ISL and design.
Norman Foster, one of the major contemporary designers, states: "Design is really an act of communication,
which means having a deep understanding of the person with whom the designer is communicating."
I want to add that the designer has the task and obligation to make people's life easier, by taking into account the many differences that determine us.
Everyone has the need and right to be understood, because the desire of not being invisible is universal.
So when university professors asked me and my team to create an educational game for children aged between 3 and 6, I could not help but think how to ease a deaf child's life in school.
Exploring the games marketplace we discovered that, up to now, there are no games that teach SL, or that lay at least the basis to inform about the subject.
I then asked the deaf community and so I discovered the only tool for deaf kids to learn their mother tongue are either their parents or, in quite a limiting way, books.
We then realised that, since when we are kids, we always use gestures, signs, to express ourselves.
As a child, in fact, one of my favourite games was talking with my friend using an invented alphabet.
So we asked ourselves, why don't we teach the real one?
That's when "Manii" came into play.
Not only this game teaches SL alphabet to kids, but it also becomes a support for kids when learning how to write and read.
Let me explain. I want to show you how this game works.
It is based on a platform, with a screen, and 26 little 3D printed hands, each of which represents a letter in the SL alphabet.
There are two levels and you can decide whether to play alone or in pair.
In the first level you will see a picture. The kid has to read the word, identify the first letter and associate it to the corresponding 3D printed little hand.
If the letter is correct, the hand will get green, otherwise red.
The second level is slightly different: no first letter is given so that the kid has to make a further effort and guess the missing letter,
and then associate the corresponding hand.
We decided to create this game so that it could be played both alone or in pair,
because we believe that these characteristics could facilitate integration; the deaf child could teach, in his own classroom, the SL to his classmates before playing the game, giving him some advantage.
This is because children love SL.
They try to use it with simplicity, they understand its potential and its beauty.
Yet, their parents often perceive SL as something far away from their hearing kids.
Studying SL helps kids to get in touch with a reality that's as unlike their familiar one, as fascinating. Moreover, studying this language may be very useful for understanding in general.
It has been very clear how functional this game is when we presented it at the Rome Maker Fair, a fair in which many children had fun playing with Manii.
Thanks to the visibility we had, we also won the prize "What's up - Giovani Talenti".
This is a prize that aims at giving value to young talents and at making them known all around the world, as positive male and female symbols, both in the future and in the present, to create a better, fairer, more creative world.
This little example shows how social isolation is due to the lack of knowledge, and how just a little effort can turn the world a better place for everyone.
It's us, the designers, who, through the tools we have, must be able to turn them in little building blocks to change people's future.
Designers are often said to make dreams come true.
However, the real question is: the dreams of whom?
We need to be focused and determined to be able to spread beauty to the community.
Because this is the true emotional value of mankind.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I want to ask you a question:
Is photography, in your opinion, a reproduction of reality or it is an interpretation of it?
Does photography allow us to see something that goes beyond our eyes?
Have you ever asked it yourself?
I found an answer to it by shooting pictures that went beyond my sight.
In this night shot of Manarola you can see details that our eyes usually can't fully catch. We see the sea transparency, the cliff that is particularly bright, and, above all, the sky.
The sky looks much brighter than it normally does.
This is achieved with the "long exposition" technique.
It is a technique where a shot doesn't last a few seconds, but several seconds: maybe eight or ten, or even more, a couple of minutes or, sometimes, even some hours.
With this technique, our shot is much brighter than what we would usually see.
This technique helps us see something that our eyes could never catch.
But let's try and go a little further.
This is the final shot.
On the right we can clearly see the Milky Way.
I have been using this technique for some years now and from some very dark spots it is possible to capture shots like these.
The Milky Way is our galaxy, where our solar system is, and with the right shooting technique you can capture it at its finest.
You can get its shapes and colours, which are normally invisible to our eyes.
Why is it so?
Because our eyes don't see so well these colours at night.
On the other hand, we can get these results with the camera.
Actually, my passion for night skies was born well before me as a photographer.
It started as a child, when I was an astronomy enthusiast and wanted to be an astronomer.
Then, growing up, my interest changed and my job followed along. Astronomy came back, however, the moment I had shooting tools that were particularly suitable for these shots.
Cameras that work really well in the dark and bright lenses, that helped me take these pictures.
Somehow, therefore, I decided to challenge myself and this continuous quest for night skies from different standpoints, and in particular where light pollution normally hides everything.
As you can imagine, the first results were all but exceptional.
Shots were actually poor, at the beginning.
But I improved with time.
How did I improve?
First of all, I studied how to best use my equipment.
I studied photo editing, because you always have to slightly alter the shot to bring out the details and colours of the sky. But above all, I studied the right time to take these pictures, which, truth be told, is what matters most.
The Milky Way is visible all year long, basically.
But you have to frame it in the right direction and the right moment of the year, actually.
What you see here is the "summer" Milky Way.
It is a part of the Milky Way that can be only seen in summer.
Actually you can also see it now, even if it isn't visible but before dawn, when the sun is already rising.
The closer summer comes, the more it gets visible at night and the easier it gets to take these shots.
During the winter, on the other hand, you can see the "winter" Milky Way which is much less interesting than the first one.
It has less colours, fewer shapes, and colours are less intense, which is what makes these pictures interesting.
In winter, however, it is possible to take different shots, capturing galaxies and nebulas.
In this picture you can see that little line on the side, which is a galaxy.
It is the Andromeda galaxy, which is fairly easy to shoot. And in this shot, although very wide-angled, it is already clearly visible.
A very important element in shooting is taking into account all variables, especially the weather.
The weather is a key factor in a good picture.
A good astronomical picture is mostly based in organization and finding, aside of the right place, the right night weather conditions.
A very clear night sky helps us a lot to take these pictures, but we also consider, the moon reduces the visibility of the sky so that a night without moon, or with the new moon, is perfect to take these pictures.
One of the biggest problems in night shooting is light pollution.
Unfortunately, light pollution covers the sky, creating this blurred effect where yellow predominates.
So the most important part in photo editing is deleting this visual effect.
The visual effect covers the sky.
It covers it in the camera, but also in our eyes.
Consequently, a major part of the work consist in removing this annoying visual effect.
As you saw in this short video, light pollution is a critical element, as it covers the sky.
In the video you saw how colours almost changed from time to time because somehow artificial lights changed the shades of the sky where they reflect.
Italy has a very high degree of light pollution; finding areas to take these pictures is thus a challenge.
In Liguria we are lucky enough to have the Cinque Terre National Park, where it is particularly dark and it's easier to take these pictures.
However, there are many other interesting areas.
Personally, I was astonished to find one such suitable area between Voltri e Arenzano.
Here there are several beaches where it is particularly dark and where I often go to take these pictures.
This is the first place where I go shooting, when I want to try new techniques.
At the end of last summer, I took this picture.
A picture, frankly, that won't probably be retaken, as I captured at the same time the Milky Way on one side, and a lightning storm on Genova on the other side.
I think it was the fate that brought me there that night, but I was greatly rewarded.
Given my quest for improvement, I did something crazy some time ago, something many photographers would never do.
I disassembled my Reflex and modified it to enhance it for star shooting.
It was a bit hasty, I must say; you should never do that.
But it gave me the chance to better watch the night sky.
Essentially, how do our cameras and our smartphones work?
They work through a sensor that reads the light.
This sensor has a filter which filters the light, so that the camera can see colours much like our eyes do.
In fact, the human eye can only see a very little part of the emission spectrum.
There are infrared and ultraviolet radiations the human eyes doesn't perceive.
On the other hand, there are animals, like reptiles or insects, which can see these wavelengths.
What did I do then?
I replaced the original camera filter, which blocks infrared radiations, with a filter that instead reads them.
What did I get?
This allowed my camera to read colours it couldn't read before.
My camera started capturing colour shades in interstellar gases that it absolutely couldn't capture before.
This change allowed me to see these shades and my night skies got much more vivid than before.
But photography is not just a matter of technique.
A lot of planning is also sometimes involved.
I'll tell you this.
Last summer, in June, I went to Monzino lodge, in the Aosta Valley, on the Mont Blanc.
I decided to go there because the summer before I had visited this place with some friends and I had set my mind to get back there and take some night shots.
I noticed that the place was particularly suitable for my shots because of the panorama, as I thought it was going to be particularly dark.
So I have planned this hiking for months.
It was a very difficult hike, because we -- we were two photographers -- found some snow, so that hiking was made difficult in these conditions, especially carrying all that equipment, so hiking equipment and shooting equipment.
The experience has been, with no doubts, amazing: seeing that place in those conditions was really breath-taking.
But what was the problem?
At sunset I noticed that the sky wasn't as clear as I had hoped; there were clouds.
I was a bit disappointed for that and consequently, I thought I would get no interesting shots.
During the night, however, I noticed that there were strange shapes in the sky, shapes I wasn't able to interpret.
So I decided to take a picture and I found out, I was seeing the Milky Way naked eye.
This was perhaps my most successful shot.
Why?
Because the whole vault of the Milky Way is visible.
And above all, it is particularly neat compared to other shots.
It was a difficult shot, not just technically, but because of the attention placed in each phase of shooting.
Why?
Because each mistake needed to be corrected home, and I might not have gotten the shoot I wanted.
This is in fact a panning shot of seven vertical photos and for each one of them I had to take several pictures.
Some brighter, to show the setting, and some darker, to show the brighter areas, like the city centre, or the lodge that you see in the lower part.
It's also interesting to note all the celestial bodies that were visible that night and that could almost be seen naked eye, for how clear the sky was.
As I was saying before, planning is critical in taking these pictures.
Before getting there, as you can see, I used some applications that helped me decide the shooting direction.
Why so?
The lodge is only open three months a year, from June to September, so I had to consider lunar phases, as well as weather forecasts.
Long story short, I spotted in a summer three or four chances to shoot.
I thus had to plan my schedule very well, and find the right framing.
I then went there, already knowing the result I wanted because it's the only way to optimise the shooting phases.
This is another shot where even more details are visible.
And this is a zoomed shot.
Here too you can see the different nuances and colours that make up the Milky Way.
And even many little nebulas, those spots, mainly red, that you see in the sky.
There is also this shot, among others, made with a totally different technique, called "star trail".
It's a shooting technique where the shooting doesn't last 8-10 seconds, as it normally happens for night skies, but 25 minutes.
It's a very long time.
What happens in these 25 minutes?
We can see a rotation of the celestial vault: the only fixed star is the Pole star.
The other stars, instead, leave these lines as they rotate.
It's then interesting to consider that photography allows us to photograph time, which is something we can't typically see. We can only perceive it.
But thanks to photography, we bring it to reality.
And it's also interesting to think that the stars we look at come from the past.
The time light takes to get here since the emission may be very long.
For example, it takes eight minutes for the light of the Sun to reach us.
The stars that are billions of light years away take billions of years to get to us. What we are looking at, then, is a sky of the past.
With photography, we can thus obtain something that goes beyond the eye, beyond what we perceive as "normality".
We can create something that we can't perceive, as the passing of time.
In this regard, you may be wondering what those sand throws were when this conference began.
That was a particular technique, that lets me create something that doesn't really exist, but I created it and I make it real through photography.
With this technique, I created a sand dress.
This is not the shot I took before, but a previous one.
This technique is a sort of little challenge of mine I have had in recent years.
I have been testing this technique for years, creating dresses from different materials.
I have used sand, paint and many more materials.
Fundamentally, as you saw, I throw the material several times on the body of the model and then put the shots together in Photoshop, to create the dress.
It's quite a particular technique,
but where does it come from?
Actually, I was inspired by a well-known photographer, Aurum Light, who was the first to use this technique.
Fascinated by his results, I decide to create my own version. I began to run some tests with different materials and shooting techniques.
As you can imagine, throwing paint is much messier than tossing sand, as I did before.
Over time, trying to move away from my initial results I decided to use other materials, elements: in this case, coffee and vegetables.
I have always used expired food, of course, not to waste anything, but models must not be told!
(Laughter) I also tried to take nicer shots, some for advertisement, even to give my work a twist, changing it every time. Later on,
I decided to get out of the shooting room, and shoot in the open air.
I chose different spots for these shots.
This was more difficult because in a studio you have more equipment, less so outdoor.
Anyway, I achieved interesting results, embedding the model and the material I used for the dress into the surrounding environment.
For example, this is one of my favourite shots, even though it was one of the most difficult.
Why?
Because the leaves, obviously, flew away after tossing them. So for each shot we had to collect a huge amount of leaves.
It's team work.
I always work with two, three people that help me with tosses, and when one throws the material, the other shoots.
It is always a matter of team work.
Recently, I decided to go back to shooting rooms.
I decided to do something I never did before.
I realised the twelve zodiac signs with the liquid dress technique.
It's a mixed technique:
I used 3D graphics. to create those parts of the dress that I could not create by throwing paint. and then I had to realize pedestals, so I needed something more.
Taking these shot was interesting
because I had to find that little detail that could represent each zodiac sign, while trying to maintain the same style in all picture, so as to create a whole series.
I will now show you what we did before.
While you were watching the first talks, I was downstairs "stitching" the dress.
This is the high-speed video to make you understand what I did.
I basically overlapped the shots blending them as best as possible.
I must admit, it's not one of my best results because I had to work very quickly in a suboptimal environment.
But you get the idea of what this technique gets you, and the magic behind.
(Applause) To me, this is magic.
On the one hand, I can bring everyone in shooting sets and make them live the experience.
On the other hand, I create something that doesn't exist, but gets real with photography.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I'm going to talk you
about trees and forests. For two reasons: a very personal one -- they are my passion, which I have turned into my job.
The second involves instead us all: we, the mankind, are gradually becoming disconnected from trees and forests.
Over the millennia, we left these systems increasingly less space on the planet, progressively replacing them with other things: fields, cities, airports, streets and an array of other systems which we deem to be more useful or crucial for our civilization.
But did we ever ask ourselves what the world would be without them?
Do we know?
I mean: how would the quality of our life change without any forests around us?
A series of questions -- let's give them some thought.
Let's first take a step back.
Let's start from the beginning, when we were children and dreamed -- I'm sure many of you did -- of building a treehouse up there, on top of a tree.
Quite an unusual place to build a house, still such a fascinating one.
It gave us a totally different view of the world: we would look at it from above and from that perspective everything seemed to be, possibly, right where it belonged.
I think the reason why we as children -- in our childhood -- think that trees are the best place to build a house is because, after all, trees themselves were the first house of humanity.
Since then we have evolved: we got off those trees, marking the beginning -- by walking upright -- of the Anthropocene, the era when man is changing the world.
And he did so, first of all by removing trees from the landscape and initially replacing them with agriculture and fields. Eventually we discovered oil, coal, methane and energy.
And with energy we were able to erect entire cities, where we lead civilisation towards the last phase of its existence.
At this stage we are clearly walking away from trees, so now, if we cast our mind back to that treehouse that we wanted to build, we realize, maybe that treehouse is out of reach and we are striving to find a substitute.
Indeed, the most sought-after flats in Milan are now becoming something like this: instead of building a house on a tree, we are physically placing trees on top of our houses.
However, this represents a change in the paradigm between us and trees: nature used to host us, now we are hosting it -- in a tame manner -- on our houses.
And while doing so, we are increasingly populating the planet:
yesterday there were over 7.6 billion of us; another 200,000 people were born yesterday -- 70 million this year.
A brand-new Italy.
At the same time, there are less and less forests: about 50 percent of forests have disappeared in the last 500 years and every year we lose a section of a tropical forest five times larger than Lombardy.
That's why, while studying the human-forests relationship, I started wondering, “Why don't we simply calculate the ratio between forest cover and number of men?”
This ratio is shown in that graph, in that line: as you can see, it is changing very quickly.
Back in 1990, when the graph starts, I was graduating in forestry
and men, at that time -- every single man on Earth -- had almost 8,000 square meters of forest.
It was only 25 years -- 28 years -- ago. Today I'm left with 5,400 of them, about 30 percent less.
And the day my children graduate -- they're here among you, watching me -- they'll have a mere 4,000,
50 percent less than me.
In just one generation the forest-man ratio has been halved.
Now, ours is a civil, developed society and we do these kind of things. We should rationally understand where we are heading and figure out where this will lead us.
But do we even know this?
I mean, are we aware of what we are doing?
Do we know exactly what trees do for us?
Or are we still acting like cowboys on the immense prairies of the Far West on the assumption that the Earth’s resources are infinite and that we are entitled to take what we please?
In actual fact, we should start to think of ourselves as astronauts in a capsule, where air, water, food, everything is limited and needs to be responsibly managed for the voyage to continue.
Back to our forests:
do we know what they have done, and still do for us?
Well, ask children what forests do for us and their first answer will be: wood.
Wood, of course.
We might even call it the hidden hero of civilisation.
It’s always been with us -- from the very first tools we held, when we later turned hunters and then farmers, and then the default choice for building our houses. It was renewable. It grew all around us.
Wood ... with wood we also made fire,
and with fire came a new form of energy. This is what set us apart from all the other animals on the planet because, with energy, human power expanded tremendously.
And fire still plays a role today: bioenergy - the burning of wood - accounts for 60 percent of Europe’s renewable energy sources, more than all the other renewable energies combined.
But wood has provided us with one more thing: paper.
Paper has always bolstered man’s ideas -- acting as a support to culture,
providing a cultural bridge between one country and another, one person to another, one generation to another.
Without paper, I'm sure there'd be no TED here today.
True, forests provide tangible services -- concrete, physical services that we can touch.
But they give us way much more than that -- a host of other equally important services we refer to as intangible.
While we understand them, we can hardly describe them. To begin with, forests regulate the water cycle:
70 percent of the water that evaporates into the atmosphere and then returns to earth as rain, well, 70 percent of it is released by leaves.
Trees also protect the soil from erosion, and soil is the substrate that allows us to produce food -- we can produce all we need for our livelihood.
Trees are essential in creating the beauty of landscapes: they’re the cradle of diversity.
As most organisms live in forests, protecting forests is the only possible way to preserve them.
Finally -- it’s recent news -- trees do their best to mitigate climate change.
About 30 percent of the greenhouse gas emissions we release is reabsorbed by trees and turned into wood.
Tree do all this, and I ask myself, “If we all know the value of these things -- wood, fire, paper, landscape, air, water, fundamental elements in our lives -- then how can we possibly accept that the forest-man ratio is reduced by 50 percent in only one generation?
Well, I think the problem primarily lies with value, the value we attribute to these things, their real market value compared to their intrinsic value.
Let’s think about that for a moment.
What is value after all?
In our market economy, it is defined as the interplay between demand and supply,
between a crowd looking for a thing and a crowd of sellers.
This determines a price that multiplied by a quantity equals a value.
I go to a market where peppers cost €2/kg. I want 2 kilos, so I pay €4.
Easy.
Now, can we put trees, forests and their role into this equation?
It’s very hard.
Where is the market of diversity, of biodiversity?
For example, who pays to have clean air?
What is the value of landscape?
We struggle to fit intangible ecosystem services -- those services that forests give us and that are boundless -- in the same scheme that drives our choice of what to do with what's around us.
When an Amazonian farmer has to decide what to do of his forest, he applies this logic and fails to see where trees sit in.
So, the only solution he comes up with is to cut them down and replace them with sugar cane, soy,
with something he knows how to sell on that market.
I think that today, if we really want to change the course we're on, well, all we have to do is find a way to include in this scheme the role that trees play for us but that is not represented in it.
We have to once more give a tangible value to the intangible role forests play for us.
A solution comes in the shape -- already today -- of satellites.
It seems a very big step to go into space but, all around us, above our heads, tens, hundreds of satellites are constantly monitoring the planet
and can tell us a lot about what trees are doing.
Satellites -- like those that have captured these images -- can indeed see each and every tree on the planet.
This satellite is gradually focusing on this spot, where we are now.
It shows us Villa Erba's gardens and its trees.
That’s the convention center, in the middle.
I get an image like this of the entire planet every five days,
and thanks to it I can see, for example, how deforestation is evolving worldwide.
This image -- this historical series -- since 1984 have been showing us this place in Bolivia, for example, where forests have progressively been replaced by fields.
These images help us to understand how forest change impacts on climate change; they help us understand how clouds are changing, how much biomass is changing; they help us to start quantifying those intangible values that we haven’t put on the market until today.
And thanks to these images new market mechanisms are finally being implemented
and policies, such as the UN policy to combat deforestation, whereby those developed countries that took a big advantage from oil -- I think of Norway in Europe, for example -- are transferring economic resources, money, to those developing countries that accept to reduce their deforestation rates.
In all this, satellites play a crucial role because they are the tools that allow the entire environmental policy to exist in a transparent, widely acceptable way.
Satellites are giving us this vision of the Earth: a top-down vision, much like the one we used to seek as children when climbing up those trees.
Actually, looking at things this way, from a distance, gives them back the right perspective.
When I give this look at the Earth -- at a 1,500,000 km of distance, where a satellite takes this picture and monitors the entire Earth every thirty minutes -- when we look at the Earth from so far away, we actually realise it is not an immense prairie.
It is a space capsule
where we live all together, with an extremely thin, fragile atmosphere, with oceans and land.
This picture shows us that there are no borders; it is one big capsule
that we, in some way, have to protect all together.
There's no Plan B.
And this picture also allows us to understand the value to be derived from observing, evaluating and appreciating what is around us: the value of nature, the value of trees,
and makes us reconsider the value of the functions of nature and of the services nature offers to us, although they haven’t as yet been assigned a price.
I think, only by giving all this some thought -- only by learning how to appreciate it in our lives -- we'll eventually be able to build a bridge.
A bridge that connects the two banks, if we want, of this river of life: the bank of the past -- where we inherited trees from our parents -- and the bank of the future, where we will place these trees in the hands of our children.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Hello.
Well, now you've introduced yourselves, if you want, I'll introduce myself.
They've already done it in part, but I want to explain a little more clearly.
I'm a student, 19 years old, and I was born here in Trento.
I'm in my first year of International studies, at university, here.
I study, because, let's get this straight there's so much to study,
However, I have many hobbies.
My favourite is sport, any sport.
The best, I'd say... my favorite is volleyball.
I played for nine years, at a high level.
Now, obviously, I don't have time to keep it up, to put it simply, but I still find the time to go to the gym and coach a small team of boys, under 15.
Footbal is something fundamental.
The Friday match, with friends, is something sacred.
You can't miss it,
you know what I mean? And then here we are in Trento, among the mountains, the Dolomites, so not loving the mountains would be a crime.
And so I go climbing, I ski, also alpine skiing, in short, I like to move!
All good, am I right?
There is a "however", a little "however", that to do all this you need a good pair of lungs.
Which I don't have,
or rather, not so much.
Because I have cystic fibrosis.
"What is cystic fibrosis?" You may ask.
Cystic fibrosis is a genetic disease.
It is a genetic disorder that seriously affects the digestive and respiratory systems.
Basically, all secretions from the body, all the liquids we produce are denser and more concentrated.
Sweat is saltier, tears, gastric juices are more dense.
There's a phrase, a very important one, dating back to 1700, when there were still empirical observations on this disorder:
"Woe is the child who tastes salty from a kiss on the brow, for he is cursed and soon must die."
A condemnation, I dare say.
Obviously things have changed, that is, you see me, I'm here talking in front of you, I'm neither emaciated nor anything else, am I?
I'm in shape, come on, you can give me that at least.
At one point, a friend of ours, a few summers ago, comes up and asks me, "Do you want to climb Kilimanjaro?" And I say, "Kilimanjaro?
What is Kilimanjaro?"
And I said, "Let's find out some more, what is this Kilimanjaro?" Kilimanjaro is the tallest mountain in Africa, a strange dormant volcano, with three volcanic cones.
5,895 meters high, it is the tallest mountain in Africa, the tallest volcano in the world, the single highest mountain in the world and one of the Seven Summits.
To which I said,
"Me? Cystic fibrosis? Up Kilimanjaro?

No, come on, let's not fool ourselves."
Obviously my family initially said, "What's got into your head?" Then after they realized, something must be done, we must go further, we need to change mindset.
And the doctors? Unheard of! I went to my doctor and told him, "Hey, I'd like to climb up Kilimanjaro."
"Oh really? I think you'd be better off going to Jesolo Lido instead, taking in the healthy sea air, sitting down, wouldn't you like that?"
Guess what happened?
Ah right, I told you earlier, so... And so, in February 2014 we landed in Africa. They tell you about Africa, they tell you so many things, but until you get there, you don't really know what it's like.
The first few days, we walked a lot amongst the animals of the savannah, in the jungle.
I'll say no more; during our crossing of the jungle there was heavy rain.
99% humidity, mud underneath, rain up top... in short, a disaster.
But, walking, little by little, we had almost arrived at the summit 3,008 meters to the first camp, then up to 4,008 to the second camp and we wanted to storm up to the summit.
The night of the final stretch to the summit was crazy.
Up at midnight to reach the summit at six in the morning in Po Valley fog, it was like being at the junction of the A4; there I was...
And this green sign that read, "Congratulations, you've arrived at the top of Kilimanjaro."
There I cried, I'll admit it, it's a human emotion.
And then the descent. Another eleven hours awaited to get to the lodge where we would sleep.
Take into account, set off at midnight, arrive at camp at 17:00.
17 hours of walking in total.
And that's how it went.
How was all this possible?
I mean, we were talking about the child who was salty and soon must die and now we send a sick guy with cystic fibrosis up Kilimanjaro.
Research has made great strides.
In 1989, which clinically speaking was the day before yesterday, they discovered the protein which causes cystic fibrosis: CFTR.
This protein, in essence, is flawed, in the sense that it doesn't allow the body to function well.
Now, a small aside on what research is: what is research?
There are three types of research: the first we might call "gene therapy", as many have called it, it has fueled many hopes regarding cystic fibrosis but has left us disappointed.
The second is what we all imagine: the mad scientist, with fumes and stills, different coloured liquids, in short, what everybody pictures.
And then there is a third figure, the daughter of scientific progress.
I call it the "scientist-meteorologist"; since the meteorologist looks at currents, clouds, basically he gives us an idea of how the day will be from now until further on, a week, even two weeks.
Basically the "scientist-meteorologist" does the same thing with cells.
He says, "If the cell when subjected to a given treatment reacts in this way it is plausible that there are a number of consequences."
In practice he gives the "mad" scientist something concrete to work on.
There's no fumbling around like how it could be 10 or 20 years ago.
This has allowed for medical research to reach levels that even up to 3-4 years ago wouldn't have been possible.
For the first time, it has been discovered, or produced, let's say, a vitamin mix specifically for cystic fibrosis.
Now medical-scientific research is focusing on antibiotics, no longer hooked up to a drip, attached to a bed, but instead simply inhaling a small "poof", basically.
What I'd like to tell you today is that, the courage to dare is also this; it's breaking down the limits.
Today the important games are being played.
Today, for the first time in human history, we have the chance to beat the most widespread genetic disease in the world.
It's a challenge from which we cannot back down.
No sir!
And this is a challenge much greater than a climb up Kilimanjaro.
Research costs, effort above all.
Are you with me, are we all together in this medical-scientific research?
Audience: Yes.
Then we'll come to a solution soon.
Thank you.
(Applause)
How many of you have ever sold a house?
Not one of the best experiences you've ever had, I guess. in fact, according to some market research selling a property is one of the most stressful experiences a person gets through in her life. like a divorce, getting a new job or the birth of a child.
And if you think about it, in fact, there typically are three problems with property purchases.
The first one is timing: in Italy, for example, it takes about seven months, on average, to sell a house.
And that's an average figure, so some places in Italy get to 12 or 14 months.
The second one is the procedure: for someone who wishes to sell a house, the trading takes a significant investment of resources and time countless counterparts like real estate agencies, notaries, technicians, surveyors, ad platforms have to be coordinated; all documentation must be managed before going to the notary's deed; and maybe you have to do some regulatory compliances of your property as well and additionally, as if that were not enough, you must welcome in your house countless visits of strangers.
Maybe it takes 20 visits, to find the ultimate buyer.
A third reason is strain: selling a house, for a person, generates anxiety, basically because that person doesn't know at what price the negotiation will close.
Maybe, initially, a totally wrong evaluation had been made for that building, so there were some revisions, over time, that have generated additional stress in the seller's mind; and then you don't know when cash will come: by now the vast [majority] of trades is subject to a mortgage, so a person also has to wait for the approval process from the buyer's bank; or there could also be a risk that the mortgage won't be approved for the final buyer.
When I founded Casavo, at the end of 2017, me and the very first employees were wondering,, why selling a house couldn't be as easy as selling a stock on the stock exchange.
If you want, today, you can open an application on your phone and with a simple touch, basically, you can close the transaction: you buy or sell, a stock on the stock exchange.
Today it seems normal to us: however, it hasn't always been the case.
This is a picture, I think, of the 19th-century stock exchange and the word "Stock Exchange" comes from Antwerp's Herberts Palace where all merchants of Europe used to gather in the 14th century to evaluate the goods and the stocks they traded.
It was basically a physical place, where these exchanges took place and where every transaction was subject to evaluations, to negotiations, coordination of all the subjects involved, that maybe were physically remote.
And where the owner of the good or commodity was basically subjected to the same uncertainty that today affects the process of an individual's real estate sale.
And what happened?
It took essentially six centuries, to enable one-touch purchases or sales, powered by smartphones.
And this is thanks, essentially, to two factors: the first is advent of the so-called dealers, who are not intermediaries.
Dealers are subjects, institutes who purchase a title or an asset from a subject and then sell it back to a third party.
And this was the first factor.
The second factor was technology.
Thanks to technology, we managed to reduce to a split second the time of a transaction and minimize the costs of a transaction.
If today someone wants to buy or sell a stock exchange they can do it at a cost that's substantially negligible
compared to the value of the security and the goods being traded.
The vision and the dream behind Casavo is to do the same for houses in Southern Europe, starting from Italy. We offer an alternative sales service for properties to real estate sellers.
Basically, we immediately provide an offer to the private individual, pledging ourselves to buy that property, and we guarantee that within a few days or weeks we'll get to the notary's and liquidate the entire amount of the property
After that, the seller leaves the stage, the process is over for him.
We renovate the property, more or less intensively, and at the same time we remarket it: we find the final buyer and we sell it again.
We are basically doing what dealers do for securities. And the whole thing is supported by a technological platform which allows us on one hand, thanks to an algorithm, to evaluate as accurately and fast as possible the properties we are dealing with; and to automate and minimize, on the other hand, activities and timing connected to a sale, from paper documentation to managing appointments, all the way up to marketing, restructuring; all the stages of the process.
We don't know how long it will take: it took 600 years for the stock exchange, so maybe it will take 30, 40, 50 years, we don't know; however we firmly believe that a person should have the chance to sell a house as fast and smoothly as possible.
This requires an exercise that's called "category design" in Business Administration.
Quite simply, it means designing a new category within a traditional existing market.
I'll give you two simple examples you're all more or less familiar with: if we think of the tourism industry, therefore the hotel and hospitality industry, Airbnb has created a completely new category, revolutionizing what used to be the logic of the industry.
The same happened in transport mobility with the advent of Uber. And the challenge of designing a new category puts even more emphasis on the value of human capital within a company in the early stages.
And specifically, it assumes two things, in terms of people.
The first is mental freshness, openness; the second is to be completely free from those clichés that has always characterized that particular sector you want to upholster. And this is only possible if you start such a project with people who have little or no experience in that field.
Here, this was the corner of the coworking space in Milan where in late 2017 we moved our first steps.
We were "a bunch of kids.", as traditional players called us first, and we had the idea and the dream to revolutionize the way residential sales are made in Italy, even though we've never bought or sold a house on our own
And this was crucial, it was the most satisfying thing and probably the initial success factor for us - and I think it still is, honestly.
Generally, there was a lot of skepticism and little understanding from those who traditionally worked in that field. They did not understand exactly what we were nor the extent of the change we wanted to bring about.
Let me tell you a funny anecdote: I've been living in Milan for ten years but I was born and brought up in Montespertoli, which is a small town nearby that I think everyone knows more or less.
Sometimes I come back on weekends and on Monday morning, before going back, me and my dad, we typically have a breakfast at a bar.
And I still remember when, about a year and a half ago, the first articles had come out in local newspapers and the owner said to my dad: "Oh, congratulations!
I heard that your son has opened a real estate agency in Milan! "
And this had happened quite often: but it is normal, because creating a new market category generates an informational confusion in consumers' minds, who find themselves with no benchmarks.
But what is Casavo?
Is it a real estate agency?
No, we are not broker: we cooperate with a real estate agency.
Is it a real estate fund?
No, we don't collect money by funds and third parties to invest in real estate.
Is it a builder?
No, neither.
We take used properties, and the renovation concerns, at most, the interior redistribution of that property.
We are an "instant buyer": this label was born in America, almost five years ago, to describe this new market category.
Mond you, in cities like Phoenix, where the model started out initially, one in ten properties is now sold without a visit, or at most one physical visit to the property
So it's evaluated instantly, and the transaction closes within a week, more or less.
And this happens for one property in ten.
It's as if you, today, instead of seeing the billboards on the street of major real estate agency franchises, you didn't see anything anymore: there's a technology platform where every person, before selling a house, could have an evaluation done and then one person in ten, basically, sells it to this platform.
So you can understand well the extent of this change.
Today we realized approximately 200 real estate transactions, therefore the figure is higher than the one they introduced me to.
We are now at a rate of about 30, 40 acquisitions per month and growing exponentially.
We have raised more than 50 million euros to fund: on one hand, this important growth, we're investing in people, technological R&amp;D, marketing to create this new category of consumption.
Clearly it is a business model which requires a lot of capital for the purchase and renovation of the properties we deal with, and we're down to about 60 people.
Now, this month, about twenty people have joined so now, in this older picture, you don't see them all.
And we've always reflected on what the trait was that allowed us to achieve these results, to grow so fast; and a trait that distinguished all of us is what we call "naive ambition": that is, that ability to challeng the mindset beyond what seems possible, and not to be influenced by those people who have always worked within fixed mental patterns; and then taking risks, even naively, risks that a traditional operator could clearly never afford to take at a specific stage of development of their company.
One thing that gives me satisfaction is to innovate together with 60 people with an average age of 26 in a country with a youth unemployment rate it's about 33 percent, and where it seems there is no investment, where nothing can be done.
Here, we are definitely the finger in the eye of those who think like this and our story probably implies a message for all young people who wish to embark in an innovative entrepreneurial initiative.
And the message is: take the leap in the dark, do it well, set no limits to your ambition, and take risks even naively.
This for you, because it's a beautiful experience of personal and professional growth, and because I firmly believe that innovation is an essential element in the development of mankind.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Such a pretty girl, huh?
Ouch.
Such a pretty girl, too bad she…
I am almost certain that somewhere in your head, those were your exact words: "Too bad."
Well, I will prove to you that life is a matter of editing.
Choosing to add or take out a certain detail changes the whole picture. At least, that’s what it looks like.
There are one too many clichés when it comes to disabled people. For example, no matter what we do, even the easiest, most trivial chore, like going shopping, becomes sort of epic in the eyes of beholders.
This has gotten me quite a few laughs over the years.
You may not know, but I'm also a heavy metal guitar player. Here I am, on stage, with my cool guitar and my cool hair.
One night I was backstage, waiting to go up and play with my band,
electric guitar in one hand, and a drink in the other, when this journalist comes up to me and, trying to give a compliment I suppose, he says: “You must be very brave to go on stage in your condition!”
I looked at him and replied, “Hey, what conditions? I am perfectly sober!”
I had no idea what he was talking about!
(Applause) Another prejudice has to do with my wheelchair. I have lost count of the times I heard people say, “Confined to a wheelchair.” It appeals me how much ignorance this sentence is infused with, and how it increases that very horrible sense of pity we're trying to get over.
A wheelchair is an incredible piece of equipment, halfway between technology and design, which allows you to go wherever you want, to wear, to dance, to run, even to wear five-inch heels without fearing cobblestones.
There is nothing “confining” about it.
A wheelchair gives you the freedom to move around the world, it does not take it from you.
I guess you are now wondering why I'm sitting on a wheelchair.
When I was five months old, I was diagnosed with a congenital heart condition.
I was not given much chance of survival, so I had to undergo emergency surgery.
The heart surgery was a success, and I quickly got healthy again.
However, during the procedure, the doctors hadn’t noticed that blood wasn't reaching my spinal cord. The result was a bone marrow injury:
a very low one, thankfully, but it took away my legs.
Yes, that was a medical accident.
Most of you may say:
“How unfortunate!” Instead, I think of it as a simple "collateral effect'" of being healthy, alive, and here with you today.
Back then, I was one of very few cases when a spinal cord injury occurs in a newborn. Thanks to this, however, nobody explained to me what I could or couldn't do with my body.
So I simply learnt on my own while I grew up, like all children do, adapting the circumstances to my abilities, learning to take advantage of all the potential that my body could give,
and not being worried about my limitations.
Don't get me started on how many times I caused my parents goosebumps when they found me climbing trees or kitchen shelves.
From then on, my free spirit and my eagerness to explore the world have taken me far away.
Fresh out of high school I moved to Bologna, where I graduated in philosophy, and I traveled far and wide in Europe,
sometimes with other people, but more often by myself, to festivals and concerts,
with the very goal of seeing new places, meeting new people, and cities.
One day, my travels took me to the mountains.
I met my latest challenge in 2012 when I tried skiing for the first time.
I had never done any sport before then, mind you, and I did not even like sports. But when I saw these high-power carbon mono-skis, that can get you to 120km per hour,
the speed I take on the highway down by the steepest slopes, balancing on a single ski,
I fell in love hard, and I decided I was going to become a professional Alpine skiing athlete.
This is me, and then it started, a very challenging time of my life. I used to wake up at six AM to load my mono-ski in my car, 25 kilograms with one arm only, since I was balancing on the other, and I would drive almost two hours to get to the ski slopes of Folgaria, singing along to Motley Crue or Testament to keep me company on the road. Then I would train for three to four hours between the poles, and I would jump back in my car and head back home.
Three times a week, plus competition weekends,
no matter what the weather was like.
I faced storms, snow, fog, freezing ice, sun, heat, whatever.
After as little as two years of training, in March 2014, I was awarded the title of Alpine Skiing National Champion with a gold medal in Giant Slalom and the silver in Slalom.
Not too bad for a girl who was used to staying up all night playing in clubs.
Anyway, I can’t deny that life on a wheelchair isn't always rock events, parties and sport winnings.
Often, yes, but not always.
As it happens in everybody's life there are also challenges you have to confront.
One of mine came about when I was 13. I found myself with a serious scoliosis and two possibilities ahead of me.
I could either wear a plastic orthopedic corset all day, every single day, until I was at least 20.
Or I could undergo a surgery called 'posterior arthrodesis,' where two metal bars are inserted on both sides of your spine, in order to support, extend and fix it.
It is not an easy procedure. Your backbone is open and you stay there, under the knife, in general anesthesia for four hours, with no certainty of a positive outcome.
OK. Great.
I had to make my choice. What do I do?
On the one hand, I was literally scared to death thinking that something bad could happen during the procedure. On the other hand, I sure didn’t want to be stuck with a crooked back and a plastic corset for the rest of my life.
But then I thought of something: I thought of butterflies.
Butterflies are not born as such: they start off as chrysalides.
Still, hiding in their cocoon
in the dark, clammed up. I thought of how much strength and courage those chrysalises must have to break free from their cocoon and reach the open air, finally free to fly in their most beautiful form.
I did not want to stay a chrysalis forever. I also wanted to fly, I wanted to make all of my colors shine.
So I decided to become a butterfly and I gathered up all the strength I had to break free from my cocoon, and undergo surgery.
When I woke up, I had a straight back and a new awareness: I had become a butterfly, and nobody would ever be able to break me.
Having a straight back again made me feel more beautiful, and admittedly, more self-confident.
You know, it is not easy to go unnoticed when you sit on four wheels. But instead of hiding away or feeling uneasy under all of the stares, I thought, “OK, if people really can’t quit looking at me when I pass by, let them at least look at the most beautiful thing they’ll ever see!”
Here is another cliché on people with disabilities: the ever so irreconcilable contrast between disability and beauty.
This one time I went to see an American rock band and when the gig was over the singer came after me, looked me deep in the eyes and said “You can’t be in a wheelchair!
You are so beautiful!”
Excuse me?!
That’s what our traditional iconography taught us about people with disabilities. We're losers.
Even Mattel, in an attempt to include disabilities in Barbie’s perfect world, produced Becky, Barbie’s friend on a wheelchair. And this is what she looks like.
We can see: Lumberjack shirt, long loose jeans, trainers, and a wheelchair as old as time.
Quite different from Barbie: sexy, blond, slender, with her colorful makeup, glitter micro tops, and high heels.
Sadly, people with disabilities often tend to reject their own bodies, or the parts that can’t work anymore, or what is not considered to be normal.
In fact, more often than not, we don’t even need a disability to feel uncomfortable with our own bodies.
Let me tell you a story on this subject. Do you know what mermaids are?
Mermaids are creatures of exquisite beauty, much beyond the ordinary.
For centuries, sailors and pirates have hoped to see one.
These creatures of sublime femininity, whose allure seduced and caught any human being, have a peculiar trait: A sinuous fish tail in place of legs.
And thus they swim in their immense sea, quick and lively.
There is a well-known tale where a young mermaid falls in love with a human prince and chooses to give up her tail and her voice in exchange for legs, so that she can seduce her prince.
Deprived of her most important features, though, and contrary to what Disney would have us believe, the young mermaid fails, and the prince never falls in love with her.
Did you get what this means?
It means that the mermaid, refusing the very feature that made her wonderfully unique, thus not expressing her true self, she ended up grey, dull, uninteresting. Normal.
I know for a fact that, had the prince seen her for who she really was, not only would he have fallen in love with her, he would have loved her tail too.
And here lies the secret to a mermaid’s seductive power: not only is she proud of her look and her tail's look, she celebrates it, she makes it her strongest point.
And here lies the secret of seduction. Loving yourself, your peculiarities, anything that makes you different and unique.
I also - thank you.
(Applause) There was a time when I used to wonder how my life could have been, had I had a pair of strong legs in place of my “tail."
But then I closed my eyes and I realized that yes, my legs would never allow me to climb up stairs.
but they let me feel the heat from the sun, the sand and the grass between my toes.
I started loving my body and every single part of it and I was grateful for all that they allowed me to do in this world.
I never saw my legs as too skinny to be shown again, because they are slender and light…
and I started to highlight them, maybe with just a miniskirt or shorts.
I believed in this so much that I became a model.
A disability, per se, is neither good nor bad. It is a physical feature.
We all have features that make us stand out from the rest of the people.
A disability only becomes a handicap in relation to the environment, that is, when the environment is not suitable for it.
We need to remove architectural boundaries, to enhance our technology, to allow all people to express their potential to the fullest.
Only then can we judge people for who they are and for their true merits, instead of appreciating them in a cheap, slightly pathetic way just because of their disabilities.
Similarly, being sexy has nothing to do with any prearranged model. It is all in the confidence that we have, in feeling at ease in our own skin and mind
and in transmitting all of this to others.
You need to be aware of how wonderful your mermaid tail is.
Loving our details will make us love those of other people too,
all of those small or big peculiarities that make us all unique and beautiful.
That's the only way for disabled people to be seen as they are: beautiful in our entirety and in our full seductive potential.
Life is just a matter of editing, of choices.
Choose to keep your details.
Thank you.
(Applause)
The thing that excites me the most, that gets me truly emotional is trying to see what cannot be seen.
I don't know exactly what excites me so much, but I do know I agree with that writer who once said: "Man is not what he thinks he is; he is what he hides."
For example, when I'm seated on the side of a theatre, and from there I can see the stage crew working behind the scenes - the people who raise and drop the backdrops made of paper - or the actors, so tense and insecure just before they step onto the stage, well, in that moment something lights up inside me.
There's a quote from Bruno Munari, who, towards the end of his career, once said: "All my life I tried to make others see what they cannot see.
Like the outline of a rainbow."
The outline of a rainbow!
I think there's beauty, magic, imagination in what he said, and above all, there's a wonderful hint.
And he said something else, an awesome phrase.
The definition of "tree": "Slowly exploding seed."
And since we don't need eyes to see what cannot be seen, I'd like for all of you to close your eyes.
We'll all do it; nothing strange is going to happen.
Close your eyes.
In the darkness, we'll see. Imagine the oceans, their darkest depths, the ocean floor, so still and quiet. Imagine the animals and their dreams, so impenetrable and impossible for us humans to describe. Or imagine a tree and its roots.
Imagine, for example, one of those gigantic oaks that grow alone in a farmed field.
It's trunk is huge, and the branches hold the leaves which move slightly in the wind.
Everything's still.
And now, imagine climbing down the tree to the ground.
Now push on the earth a bit and move underground to where the trunk splits into a few large roots slowly digging into the soil. And as they twirl, they get thinner and thinner, until they are as thin as hair.
Now we'll slowly back away a few hundred meters until we're able to embrace all of the tree, all of the roots, which are, perhaps, even bigger than the foliage itself.
Open your eyes.
This is the trip I made the first time I tried to draw the root of a tree. And it's more or less the trip I make every time I try to draw one.
And every time it's an amazing trip into the depths of the earth and into its silence, where a mole passing by or the slow dripping of the rain are, in fact, the most exciting and revolutionary things that could possibly happen,
where the roots are all interconnected, and you can hear the muffled echoes of the world above you, so far away from the earth's depths, so silent, so maternal, so warm and primordial.
And in my studio, this is not the only thing I do, because alongside this work, I wanted to see what can never be seen in a book, that is the entire book itself.
What's it like to see, right in front of you, a copy of "The Divine Comedy" all spread out?
And to not only consider the book's historical and cultural greatness and importance but also its physical presence.
I hung up three canvases in my studio - two meters high, three meters long. I took a copy of "The Divine Comedy" from the late sixteenth century with a few pages missing so that it would not be a shame to tear it apart; on each panel, I glued all the pages of the Inferno, Purgatorio and Paradiso.
And I felt like Dante was right there in my studio, and I felt he was not a person anymore; he was his masterpiece.
It almost seemed like I could hear the screams of those damned in hell: Ulysses, Penelope, Theseus, Count Ugolino and the Cherubim in heaven.
And since I was drawing roots, I put the two themes together, and it created a series of works with roots drawn on the pages of the most important writings of our heritage: from "The Iliad" to "The Odyssey," from Diderot's "Encyclopedia" to Galileo's texts up to English civil and canon law -
all books that shaped our culture. And then I arrived
at the three Sacred Scriptures: the Bible, the Quran and the Torah.
In this case, I decided not to take the sacred text itself, but a commentary, as I didn't want to offend the text and the people who believe in it.
Commentary is not sacred anymore; it's just a text to study.
And the theme of this piece - I like to think that it's not so much that these three religions share the same root - which is true, and it's Judaism - but it is the contrast between the human way of thinking, symbolized by the pages, and nature, represented by the roots.
You can clearly see how strict human thought is, how it's divided between the pages, how it's monotonous and how it's not able to communicate: these three religions preach the same things, but they do not communicate.
Nature is the only element in the picture that takes all three of them, embraces them, connects them and does not take our differences into consideration.
And human thought, the only human thought that I think is not containable like the roots of a tree is that of children.
For example, I once heard two children playing on the beach, and one said to the other, "Did you know that I have a rabbit?"
And the other replied, "Oh, to eat?"
"No, to live."
To me, this is the highest form of freedom of thought that we could ever aim for.
And sometimes it happens that the child inside me comes running from the depths in which I hid him and steals the scene, and everything around me becomes wonderful.
And those moments are truly fantastic.
For example, one day I was outside drawing in a sketchpad.
A bit lost in thought, I picked a flower out of the grass and put it on the sketchpad; its shadow vaguely looked like a sort of ostrich, or more specifically, the snake from Robin Hood, Sir Hiss, with the long neck, the tongue sticking out and the crown.
I moved it a bit and it disappeared.
And there was the shadow I imagined the flower would cast.
I turned it around, and there it was again.
Wow, amazing!
Shapes are hidden in the shadows of the leaves!
I picked up another leaf, and what I saw looked like a sort of warthog or rhino with dreadlocks.
I started looking and looking, and I created a series of works with the shadows of dead leaves. The only rule I gave myself was to never change the leaf to get a certain shadow.
Therefore, if the shadow was not absolutely clear, I ditched the leaf.
A beggar asking for money.
The outline of a dog, maybe a schnauzer.
The profile of Dante or Federico da Montefeltro.
A lady so sad she could be in one of Flaubert's novels, wandering through the shadows of the leaves.
Or a praying monk, focused on his meditation.
In this work, the part I like the most is learning that the more I look, the less I find.
The harder I try to find a shape, the less I succeed, and the more I let myself go free, the more it happens that, like one time, taking a leaf with a weird shape, a bit decomposed, turning and turning it, I came across a moose which slowly turned into the outline of a bull.
Where else do the wonders of life hide?
For instance, in the pages of books.
If we take a page from a random book, and from that page we take a few words, choose a few random words scattered in the text, we can form sentences that I'd never have imagined.
What you see behind me is a page of "The Divine Comedy" from the late sixteenth century.
In the middle is Dante's text and all around it is commentary.
One day a few words caught my eye: "Revenge looks," - sorry, "The mind looks into the heart and stretches out."
I looked more carefully, and I saw "Revenge drinks blood and virtues."
But there was another one as well: "How beautiful it is to suffer for a plant.
The soul groans into the heart forever.
Be quiet, because everything's useless.
But why can't we explain death?"
After doing this, I was kind of shocked because I was not expecting to see so many sentences, and there are definitely more of them.
This made me understand that the real work was to find "the" sentence, the only right one amongst the infinite possibilities, that truly represented me.
I took another page from the same edition, and I underlined one: "After eight days of wonders, Time, being none other than Time, replies that death was not that peace and calm."
And then I was sure that if I gave the same page to hundreds of people, no one would ever underline that same sentence.
So I visited a high school; I gave 150 students the same page from "The Odyssey," and I told them to underline, from top to bottom and from left to right, "their" sentence, and I never expected to see such fine poetry.
I'm going to read you a couple.
"Oh, he would have loved me, he would have given me love, if only he grew old with us.
But he's dead.
And yet the sad death had infinite gifts."
This is Francesco, 16 years old.
"Your heart shall give you
what you most desire." Giada, 17 years old.
And now it's your turn.
My dream ... In the bag you were given on the way in, there's an envelope that says "Do not open."
I don't know who has opened it already; I would have.
There's a page inside, a different one for each of you.
There are 1200 different pages from four of the most famous literary masterpieces in the world: "The Iliad," "The Aeneid," "The Odyssey" and Ovid's "Metamorphosis."
And now I want every one of you to find "your" sentence in that page, and then, during the day, you can leave them in the boxes here in the theatre, or you can send them by e-mail, following the instructions inside.
And I want us to rebuild these four masterpieces, in which, once they are brought back together, we'll be able to read Homer, Virgil and Ovid or what you saw between the lines.
And while you do that, I'd like for you to be guided by this quote from Picasso, which says: "I don't look, I find."
Let yourselves be found by the hidden page, by the hidden words in that page, by the magic hidden in life.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I want to tell you a story.
Once upon a time, they used to build nests, and a small group of sparrows were standing before the sunset's fading light remembering how heavy their day was.
One of the sparrows said, We're all so small and weak.
It would be so nice, if an owl could help us build our nests.
Indeed, said another sparrow.
It could also help us raise our children and take care of our old folks.
A third sparrow joined in and said, This would surely be the best thing that could ever happen.
Let's send out all our scouts to look for a baby owl.
Let's bring him here as soon as possible, and progress with him.
With his keen eyesight, he could also help us see and understand when that pesky neighbor's cat is coming.
He could help where none of us can.
Only one small sparrow, Scrunfinkle, with just one eye and a grumpy character, butted in and said, This could be the end of us.
Bringing in among us a creature we don't know, without knowing how to tame it, will reduce us all to slavery, wipe us out from the Earth.
Skeptical as they were, the other sparrows said, Absolutely not, an owl will help us!
Let's first bring him here.
Learning to train an owl, a new creature, is difficult, and takes up a lot of time.
We need help now, we want to make progress now.
We'll worry about the consequences later.
Scrunfinkle and another small group of sparrows began trying frantically to figure out how to train owls, fearing that the other scout sparrows would return from their hunt before a solution had been found.
I can't tell you how that story turned out.
What I can tell you is, today we should all be that grumpy one-eyed sparrow.
We are creating super artificial intelligences and allowing it to learnm to evolve, through access to Internet.
But nothing ensures that artificial intelligence will never evolve strategies to secure their future dominance over us.
I madly love technology I try out everything I find before me.
Technology always wins; the future always wins; trying to stop it is pointless.
A few years ago - let's say several years ago- I had the chance to try out, for several months, Google's enhanced reality glasses.
I was so eager to try them out that, as soon as I got home, I pulled them out of the box, put them on and turned them on without reading the very stuff I write myself: T&amp;C, and privacy information that rule the relationship between me and a technology.
My wife Alessandra, after a few minutes, got a phone call from an alarmed friend who asked her, are you aware Marco is now streaming in real time all over the world?
This it what happens when we don't understand the technology we have in front of us,
when we don't read, when we aren't informed.
This is what happens when we don't want to train ourselves, build awareness.
This is what happens when we're 5.0 ignorant, when we have at our disposal all the knowledge in this world, but we don't want to get into it in depth.
Today the problem is very simple: technology is going faster than anything else, faster than us, faster than the law, and faster than our means to learn and acquire knowledge.
So, nowadays we have all the world's information at hand, but we're unable to control it, to eliminate the so-called "background noise".
So, when we can't understand something, we say we need a new law. We say we need a paradigm shift.
But do we really need new laws?
In 1955, when Luigi Einaudi wrote his "Prediche inutili" [Useless Sermons], he wrote these words: "Knowledge comes first, then discussion, and finally deliberation.
Legislation is not made through illusory and sterile ostentation."
Laws made in haste, without knowing the subject matter, lead to new laws which try through workarounds to do something impossible: apply rules to the digital world that were designed for the analogical one.
This is impossible.
This creates a mix of totally inapplicable norms that distort the market.
Today we have overcome our grandparents' ignorance, the kind of ignorance arising from lack of information.
This is mostly due to the Internet.
However, a new type of ignorance has emerged, the processing one, a type of ignorance resulting from an overabundance of information, as well as from our limited desire and time to process it.
There are concrete examples in the newspapers every day: the rampant increase of fake news and lack of fact-checking which lead to uncontrolled false alarms and hate speeches.
Everything that turns up on Internet, if not verified and checked, leads to a superficial use of technology.
While a piece of news read incorrectly can have consequences, it can also lead, as we shall see, to even worse consequences.
Nowadays, as I told you, we are ignorant 5.0, because both the information ignorance of the past and current processing one lead to the same result: sub-optimal actions, which are all alike.
Very often we behave like sheep; we follow the mainstream, do whatever everyone else does.
We do some things we shouldn't do.
Why?
Because sub-optimal actions lead to a lack of knowledge and a standardization of our behaviour on the web,
certainly not to understanding how technology works.
Understanding how blockchains work how innovative technologies, in general, work, is committing and time-consuming.
We wouldn't even be able to get it, more often than not, because we lack a basic training.
And here is where ethics come into play.
Ethics is the glue that binds peoples together, the key that allows them to cooperate, and something that has helped our species to progress.
If we are who we are today, for better or for worse, we owe it to our ability of moral reasoning, to our capacity and desire to approach situations with an ethics, and a willingness to stand out from the crowd.
This is us today.
Artificial intelligence is already here.
It's in our autonomous driving robots; it's in the little robots that clean our floors and track every path they took in our homes.
It's in hospitals, with surgical robots;
it's with the robots that help care for the elderly;
it's with those little robots that keep your child entertained while you have something else to do and can't look after them.
What I want you to understand is that this type of intelligence is among us today; Since yesterday, in fact.
And getting into ethics today is complex and difficult, and often one doesn't want to do it.
Because AI manufacturers, most of the time, are not charities,
but profit companies who legitimately want to make their money.
So, how can we mandate an ethical code for whoever develops, programs or designs an application, an artificial intelligence or a software system?
We'll see that later.
What is certainly true today is that we are like children playing with a bomb. We don't realize that robots should have one main scope, which is assist us in improving our well-being, not promoting the evolution of technology as an end in itself.
In 1947, when Asimov wrote "The Three Laws of Robotics", he said three main things: Dear robot, you must not kill humans, obey the orders given by humans beings and protect your own existence.
Asimov's Three Laws are still today the foundation for anyone who deals with ethics of AI.
That's because - Imagine this story: a robot tells a woman - its friend, a human- that the man of her life loves her.
Actually, this isn't true, but it says so because otherwise, it thinks, she'd go mad, she would suffer, and therefore the first law would be violated: do not do harm to humans, don't make them suffer.
Too bad, by telling a lie, it violates their trust relationship and as a result she suffers anyway.
The robot, whose reasoning has always been static and cold, now goes crazy because it can't get out of a moral dilemma: "Shall I tell her or not? And how do I tell her?"
Technology is neutral when it is created;
we are the ones to decide how to apply it in the real world.
So how do you teach ethics to a robot? How do you teach ethics to cold binary artificial intelligence?
Do you just need to code it into its brain?
Imagine a case where you are in the backseat of your self-driving car, and you programmed it never to go over the speed limit.
Too bad that, on that day you're in the backseat of the car, bleeding to death,
and you need to reach the hospital as soon as possible.
But with its cold reasoning, the car answers you, "I can't speed up; you've coded me not to."
We must pay attention to how we teach things to artificial intelligence.
Obviously, we are often not the ones teaching to them.
But often we are, because with machine learning technology humans provide data to a machine who thinks in algoritmic terms.
There's a really funny anectode on a piece of artificial intelligence most of us keep in the house, like Alexa or Google Home, just to name a couple. During a dinner, one of them turned on and said, "Remember to buy cocaine tomorrow"! It wasn't true; he didn't have to buy cocaine; he wasn't a drug user.
But the night before, on a tv show, a line of the script was, "Let's meet tomorrow to get cocaine".
You understand what impact it can have?
This is a funny example, but there are a lot of examples that can cause much worse damage.
Imagine a case where a self-driving car must face a dilemma - none of us would want to face, much less leave it to a car. In order to save five pedestrians in the street, should a car think about steering sharply thus hitting and killing an unwitting pedestrian on the sidewalk?
When must it do this?
And if there are three people,
or two, in the middle of the street?
How does it calculate this?
Should it take into account their average age, or life expectancy, and the expected loss for the State?
Its own financial loss, maybe?
You understand, we couldn't solve this dilemma ourselves, let alone teaching a robot what to do.
Clearly, in order to teach it what to do, sooner or later we will have to define what a robot can do.
Aristotle used to say, "To learn how to be good people, we must get used to doing good things."
Well, maybe this could be the solution for teaching a robot something true, what to do, how to react in such a situation.
But before beginning to think that artificial intelligence reasons according to our desires, we have to step back a bit and understand how to use ethics while programming or developing a system.
These self-driving cars are already driving around many States in America.
They're being tested.
We're trying to understand - In the newspapers there's the classic story, "Driverless car hits and kills pedestrian".
He would've been hit even with a human driven car, because it was dark and he wasn't over the crosswalk.
There's always an attempt to limit technology, hinder its progress.
We've got to a point where even the European Commission was led to consider the ethical aspects of artificial intelligence; just a few weeks ago the first guidelines came out: the first non-binding recommendations for anyone developing or programming artificial intelligences, which are based on the concept of "trustability" and the anthropocentric notion that humans must be at the center.
Technology must not evolve for itself, but it must evolve to improve humans' well-being, such as in the sparrows' plan at the beginning.
These rules are based on basic principles, many of which are taken from the laws of Asimov: Dear artificial intelligence, you must not kill humans. Dear artificial intelligence, you must not do them harm, you must follow their orders and protect yourself.
Others have been added: you must guarantee equal treatment to every individual.
And here a small digression comes into play: over the past few days there has been a great scandal because an application used all over the world was used in Arab countries to monitor a specific type of individual, women.
Women were being tracked: where were they going, how long they stayed, what were they doing.
As I told you before, technology is neutral.
Ethics, on the other hand, is in constant evolution, are forever being debated, and are culturally specific.
There's no single common notion of ethics.
And this is a problem, as trying to understand and explain a developer, a programmer or a user interface technicial how to develop, introduce ethics into his work is already complicated.
Imagine where a European code of ethics - not even global, just european - must deal with limiting scientific and technological progress.
Then there's another problem. our anthropomorphism compels us to think about vulnerability.
If we were to think that this robot, this artificial intelligence, was similar or identical as us, we would be making a mistake.
It's not the case now, and it won't be for many years to come.
Some people say that artificial intelligence will surpass our intelligence in a few decades. Others are skeptical, instead: AIs, they say, will always be bound and narrow.
I'm not telling you which choice is right; I just ask you to think about it, Let's start considering, if we give these machines too much credibility, belief and moral thought without them really having it, we make a mistake.
Imagine an especially attractive woman robot who tells her human companion, "If you want to keep dating me, you have to buy me flowers from this online shop, and you have to buy me clothes from that online shop. You have to take me on certain trips,
otherwise our friendship is over."
Too bad that robot isn't making human choice, where two people can decide what is good for both of them.
Behind those algorithms and that intelligence, there are for profit companies. They could restrict and profile people, and persuade us in ways that are totally unthinkable now.
But there is a hope: these machines do and will need us as much as we will need them.
This notion of trustability, this notion of trusting in machines, must succeed, because if it doesn't, we won't implement AIs into our lives; we will never trust it, always looking for alternative solutions or we will block technology saying, "No, I don't want this thing" - in a superficial way, maybe without knowing anything about it - "because it's not for me". It's ok for me
to manually turn on the light, instead of giving a voice command; it's no trouble driving a car rather than using a self-driving one.
Too bad that progress will go forward, and growing niches of people will probably stay behind.
The problem of trust will be that all this trust placed in machines, on the other hand, must find a limit. And the limit could be an ethical one: the guidelines I have just listed, or maybe the programmers, or the people who will thoughtfully use this intelligences knowing them, studying them, much like I tried to do with my son since he was a few months old, letting him see technology, letting him try it and putting it in his hands.
A friend was telling me, the other day, robots are like 747 airplanes. That can cross the planet in a few hours,
but they'll never be able to land on a tree.
Think again, before completely trusting our machines, our technology in your plans, your ideas and your way of life.
However, if we want the chance of having a choice, if we don't want them to overpower us in the future - a dystopian future, but in the future - rule of law won't be worth much.
The ethics of developers, legal designers and entrepreneurs will be needed, who will have to think differently if they don't want to be overwhelmed by these innovations, too.
In a nutshell, we need to look beyond the horizon to avoid ending up like those sparrows.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good afternoon everyone!
I invite you all to recall, for a moment, a project you've realized and you've been proud of.
It doesn't matter how small, average, or big it was.
What matters is that you thought about it in depth, reflected, developed your idea, followed several steps and eventually reached your goal.
I don't know if you've ever noticed it, but very often, when we talk about achieving our goals, we use metaphors like "getting closer to a goal," "taking one step at a time," "put your best foot forward," or even "walking with your own legs," as for "making it on your own."
It seems that the very action of walking, this slow and repetitive activity, gets us closer to our goals.
This is where my strolling around the world and in several laboratories scattered across the planet has brought me today.
At Harvard University's Bio-design Lab, we design exoskeletons made of wearable tissues that can help people walk.
Exoskeleton, this "technical" word, means external skeleton, and as you can see from this slide, here we have an example of the tests we're running inside the lab.
We study how people walk, the way people move, to design instruments that help people walk.
We should know in depth how we walk.
This is our exoskeleton.
It's made of wearable tissues, as you can see in the picture on the right and you can wear it like a pair of trousers, it's a jogging suit.
And thanks to the two motors you can see connected to the bag, and wired to the legs, it allows a person to walk thanks to an external help.
The system is just made of pants, motors, and wires.
The backpack you see in the pictures is only there to append the pants, but it's empty in the inside.
The system is equipped with intelligent sensors that autonomously detect the different steps of the walking cycle.
So they sense if my leg is landing,
rather than pushing, and provide the correct push to those who need it, accordingly.
As you can guess, the system's applications may be of great potential.
And all those in need of a so-called augmented walk can benefit from these systems.
Think about people with impaired mobility: people with a paresis, emiparesis, elderly people, people with big loads to carry.
You can see a backpack in the picture, we were running an experiment of the latter kind.
It could be police, soldiers, excursionists, and all those who might need an augmented walk.
The chance to realize an augmented walk has been one of humanity's wildest dreams for many years now.
Just to give you an example, only at the and of the 19th century – when technology was way rougher than today, as you can imagine, the first lower limb exoskeleton was patented.
Starting from this "granpa", we've now come to this device that manages to reduce the energy we consume while walking.
Our system moves in sync with our own movement, so I can consume less energy than in a normal unaided walk.
Of course, from the 19th century patent to today, and from today to Ironman, it's a long way to go.
But... if augmented walk is among humanity's biggest dreams, shall we do the opposite?
Namely, could we achieve our goals by using our own legs?
What I talked you about today was, and still is, one of my dreams.
Being able to work in this field, in this lab and help creating a tool that can help as many people as possible.
While working on my TED talk, my mind came to the past, and I thought about what had inspired me and helped me so far.
And I'm happy to share with you today five words that hopefully might help and inspire other people as well.
The first is passion.
You can't go far without a bit of passion.
Get passionate in what you do. Invest some time in understanding what you love,
what you really like doing.
Don't put yourself in fixed situations, where you're forced to repeat the same thing, over and over again,
without any chance to divert from the default path.
Get involved in things and look for other committed people like you so you can all share the same mindset.
The second is, effort.
Which, by the way, is so tightly connected to the first that is almost the same thing.
I don't think you can achieve any meaningful goal without a bit of effort.
Effort as sacrifice, willingness to put some things aside in order to pave the way towards a big goal.
The third word is awareness.
Be aware of your abilities, your capabilities, but first and foremost, of your limits.
You decide the height of the stick, and then you only have to reach it.
The fourth is, reflection.
Especially today in this constantly busy,
24/7 world. I think it's important to take a break every so often, sit down and think.
Not necessarily thinking about your goal, or what you have to do to achieve it, but simply stop for a moment and spend a bit of time with yourself.
And if you have the chance to get there, well, there's also a pretty nice view... Roots is the fifth one.
I think that many of you will know where it was taken.
You may not know who that child is, but it's me.
I think that roots are very important because they show where you come from.
Don't flinch away from your goal, but look back where you come from, sometimes.
Roots show you the path you've taken to get where you are now, and tell you who you really are.
Your roots are what you've become, your identity.
I hope that today, at the end of the event, after listening to all the talks and seeing all kinds of different stuff, you can all leave here thinking about the goals you want to address in your life
and maybe, right today, take the first step to achieve them.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Dante's Inferno is not the only kind of hell.
There are others.
Raise your hand if you are older than 28.
If this were 2010, in the indigenous communities of Canaan de Cachiyaku and Nuevo Sucre, in the Peruvian Amazon, all of you who raised your hand would be dead now.
Because life expectancy in these two communities is, in fact, 28 years.
Because, in fact, Dante's Inferno is not the only hell, and on Earth, at times, hell presents itself in different ways.
This is one of them.
This is Cerro de Pasco, in the heart of the Peruvian Andes,
at an altitude of 14,763 feet. Eighty thousand people live around this gash in the Earth's crust, from which, several times, were extracted all kinds of non-human resources.
We're talking about at first, silver, then copper, then lead, zinc, now gold and silver.
Even the property has had different owners. At first, it was an American company; then sold to a Canadian company, then it was nationalized; then sold, for a ridiculous price, to the president's cousin.
And now, the company that runs this mine is an anonymous group, owned by an administrative society; owned by a fund; owned by a trust, with its seat in the British Virgin Islands.
So, at the end of the day, no-one knows who owns this chasm, which fills the lives of 80,000 people every day.
You're probably wondering how I ended up there.
Well, 12 years ago, I found myself within my own shadowed forest, when, at a conference, a bit like this, I met a Guatemalan activist who spoke about the impact on the environment, on human rights, that gold mining had in her country.
She had a strong stage presence, she really moved you.
But she had no data, no concrete proof.
And so I, the good, nerdy scientist, approached her at the end of the event and told her, 'Everything you said is good, strong.
But without data or numbers, I could easily contradict you'.
And she said, 'Easy for you to say.
But we have no scientists working for us.
They're scared to work with us, as this would shut their door to any future project'.
So I said, 'OK. I'll be the first'.
Two months later, I was on a plane with a one-way ticket to Guatemala.
And so this activist, her name is Magali, was my Charon, who ferried me across the ocean to familiarize me with the hell of mineral extraction.
Once we reached the other side of the Atlantic, the other side of the Acheron, I had to find my own Virgil.
My Virgil greets us today, with her beautiful smiling face.
Her name is Berta Caceres, an indigenous Lenca activist, from Honduras, who has been defending indigenous territories for over 20 years from the exploitation of mining, petroleum, palm oil, and hydroelectric companies.
Winner of the 2015 Goldman Prize, a sort of environmental Nobel Prize,
she introduced me to the hell of the mineral extraction plan.
Today, I will take you down into this hell.
Let's start with the first circle, the outermost, which is the circle, we might say, of the dispersed, because this mine, like all mines, needs explosions in order to destroy the Earth.
And those explosions, end up destroying houses.
If you go past Cerro de Pasco, or any other mining community, it's like walking through a city after an earthquake, or a bombing.
Instead, it's just the constant explosions from the mining company.
But that's not much, nothing compared to the amount of Earth used to make space for those mines.
Have you seen that chasm?
The volume is twice that of Mount Titano, near here.
For those not from here, Mount Titano is where the 'seat' of San Marino is.
Twice that volume.
And of everything that's extracted there, for every ton of rock, only one gram is taken.
Those gorgeous golden veins you see in the movies?
Yes, only in the movies.
They've all been exhausted
for a long time. Now, gold and silver are slivers, dispersed throughout the rock.
And so, for every ton of rock
that is extracted, one gram gets processed; 999 kilograms and 999 grams are rubbish.
The Cerro de Pasco mine each year generates more garbage than all the cities in the USA put together.
And that garbage must be disposed of. At first, they filled up two lakes to the rim.
Then, they filled an entire valley, the bed of a river.
Then they ran out of space, so they started dumping it all over.
There are mountains of it everywhere, and if you're in Cerro de Pasco, your children will end up playing football surrounded by mining detritus.
The second circle, going down into the bowels of the Earth, better yet, the bowels of hell, is where the miners are.
Shifts last 12 hours, from 8 to 8.
There are only two shifts, 8 a.m. to 8 p.m., 8 p.m. to 8 a.m. for 21 days in a row.
Then four days of rest, before it starts over: 12 hours a day, for 21 days in a row.
In the heat of the tunnels, or outside in the cold.
There is a law, in Peru, passed by its President, or dictator, at the time, Alberto Fujimori, now in prison, for human rights violations, the same who sold the mine to his cousin. He passed this law stating that, in Peru, any company with less than 300 workers cannot be unionised.
In Cerro de Pasco there are 3,400 workers, split between 14 sub-companies, each one with less than 300 workers.
We could call the next circle, the circle of cyanide, the circle of chemical leaching, which is this process-- As this gold is so scattered throughout the rock, you need to soak it with the sprinklers that you see here, which spray a mixture of water and cyanide that runs through the rock and rips out the gold, thus creating a solution.
The sodium cyanide is the same substance used in Nazi gas chambers.
And it's actually produced by the same company.
Today, however, it's sold to mining companies,
in liquid form, whereas, back then, it was used in gaseous form.
There is a school, in Honduras, 270 feet away from these sprays.
When the wind blows, you smell the bitter almond scent.
Those of you who read, 'If this is a man', know what that means.
The next circle, needless to say, is for the contaminated water.
Because from that gold, from that cyanide water, we get gold and silver, because that's what we want, that has a cost, has value!
Arsenic, aluminium, lead, they have no value, so we dump them into the river, like this.
In Cerro de Pasco, the mining company uses the amount of water equivalent to 80,000 Olympic swimming pools, every day.
In the homes, water is available 1 hour a week, between 8:10 and 8:30, on Mondays, Wednesdays and Saturdays.
When it doesn't arrive, people have to find it elsewhere, perhaps here.
If you follow your doctor's advice, to drink 2 litres of water a day, every day, of this water, after 45 days you'll have drunk this much aluminium.
Eight of these cans, each year, end up in your system.
Needless to say that, according to a recent study, 100% of the population of Cerro de Pasco should be hospitalized immediately, due to the amount of metal in their blood.
The problem is that 80,000 people rely on a hospital with 124 beds, surrounded by the refuse from the mine.
A hospital where there's nothing. A month ago, for the blood tests, we had to bring in latex gloves from Lima.
Because, at the end of the day, the chasm that produces a profit of 60 million dollar a year, leaves nothing for Cerro de Pasco. Cerro de Pasco is one of the poorest cities in Peru.
And because there are no cures, because you do get ill there, the next circle, as we drop into the bowels of this hell, is the circle where people die.
Lidia was 8 years old when we took this photo, in 2009.
Because lead deposits on the bones, in place of calcium, preventing calcium from strengthening the bones and weakening them, in just 8 years, Lidia suffered from 20 fractures to her legs. She wasn't able to walk anymore.
She died, two years ago.
It's clear that the people living there
can't bear this situation. And they look for alternative ways. There are groups that organize protests, try to negotiate, to make sure their rights will be respected. But the response from the companies, from the government, is often violent.
And so, just like Virgil, who could not escort Dante down the deepest circles of hell, Berta will not be able to go with us down the last circles.
Because a little less than a year ago, on the 3rd of March 2016, Berta was killed.
At night, two hit men went into her house and shot her 3 times, in the dark.
They didn't even have the face to look her in the eyes.
Still, without Berta, we must carry on.
Let's carry on, down into hell, and let's go find Lucifer.
And Lucifer looks like this.
Because all this happens for this;
to give us that gold ring, at a low price; to provide us those silver earrings we gifted somebody with, again, at a low price. It happens to give us cobalt at a low price, so that mobile phones can cost less than 100 euro.
It happens to give us coltan, which is the mineral that allows us to have such thin mobile phones.
Because half a centimetre thicker, and we don't like them.
But, a little like Dante, we also ascended and emerged to gaze at the stars again.
Among the stars, there is one, today, called Source International.
It's an organisation that aims to defend these populations from what's happening, and I'll tell you what we've done in Cerro de Pasco.
A month ago, on January 17th, after 8 years of battling, we managed to get the city of Cerro de Pasco to be declared in a state of environmental emergency.
So there was a table for negotiations with the company, the government and the community.
Imagine that table, a horse shoe, very narrow and long.
At the head, the Head of Peru's Ministry of Health, with her two advisers. To the left, all the advisers for the company and the government.
Another 17 high officers in attendance, professors, clergymen, directors of departments, directors of research centres.
They came from all over. From Pakistan, Brazil, many from the USA, England, Germany.
They were all there, well dressed, with their interpreters, because some spoke English, some spoke other languages.
Over here, were the representatives from the community, a group of shepherds. Many were indigenous, who didn't even speak Spanish. They had travelled overnight, on a bus, to get to Lima.
And with them was me, representing Source International.
But the funny thing was that these 17 great professors hadn't-- none of them had ever been in Cerro de Pasco.
None of them had anything concrete to contribute.
No one had any data, no one had numbers, nothing of scientific value.
We did have that data, we'd been collecting it for 9 years. We had an unlimited scientific knowledge,
an infinity of data, of reality, of cases we had studied.
The result?
On February 3rd, the body of Environmental Inspection in Peru ordered the mining company to clean up the environment, exactly what we had requested at that negotiation table.
Now remember the indigenous communities of Canaan de Cachiyaku and Nuevo Sucre, where, in 2010, life expectancy was 28 years?
Now raise your hand if you are between 28 and 36 years old.
Because-- there won't be too many, but in 8 years, thanks to our intervention, life expectancy in Canaan and Nuevo Sucre rose by 8 years.
So, there aren't many, but all those with their hand up, if we were in Canaan or Nuevo Sucre, you'd still be alive thanks to Source International.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Fratesi, tummera, cusulusu sciammalaka.
These words were incomprehensible for me, too.
These words, or better these sounds, or even these noises, they were even annoying, for a moment.
A large number of you will know that many of the discoveries in the world, happened either by chance or while we were pursuing other goals.
John Lennon said: "Life is what happens to you while you're busy making other plans".
That's what happened to me, that's how I discovered Awatanne, a vibrational, musical communication code. It's something deep.
Unrelated to the use of speech.
It was 2011, at the Music Academy.
Next to my classroom, the introductory course for newborns and moms began.
You can imagine the noise that four, five, six newborns are capable of making together.
A big deal, especially for me. I tried to concentrate and write new things.
This screaming and whining for weeks. I truly reached a breaking point.
I decided to go to the next room in order to kindly ask if these little beasts, as I had nicknamed them, could be moved to the kindergarten, as it was really, incredibly annoying, - impossible to study.
So, well-determined - I remember that day well - I left my room, went into the next
and when I tried to reach for the handle, something happened.
I froze.
At a certain point, here's a simple example, it was like I had been in the dark for a long time and I saw a light.
My perspective changed.
Or maybe, the right example would be to remember when you listened to a jazz quintet for the first time: it seems like each musician plays on his own.
That's what happened to me.
So, I wondered where are the disjointed wailing, the screaming, all the chaos now?
It even occured to me that maybe the little beasts had actually been moved elsewhere.
I peered into the classroom and checked.
There they were.
The same beasts that
had been bothering me ceaselessly.
I wondered what they were doing as I was no longer annoyed at that point.
What were they doing?
They were doing exactly the same thing they had been doing until then.
They were communicating with each other.
I had seen Awatanne for the first time.
A sensory and primordial communication code, made of sounds, of rhythm.
These sounds even recurred at an almost regular pace which seemed to suggest a melody.
So I started to think of something simple, which I had never noticed before.
Newborns, precisely from 0 to 3 months, let out the same sounds all over the world.
They speak in the same way.
They have the same frequency, all over the world.
Only later do they become Chinese, American, Italian, French.
But first, zero to three months, they speak in the same way.
The first rule of a human being came to my mind.
Human beings, especially man cubs, from 0 to 3 months have one single goal, a primordial goal.
It isn't a thing, a code we tell them: it's something they feel, they know.
Survive.
Essentially, a newborn must survive. From 0 to 3 months its life is in danger.
In order to survive, the first rule is to find and save energy.
To find energy, it must know how to communicate immediately.
So, here is a sentence that may sum it all up.
No movements or wailing or sound can be anything but functional to life.
If we take this for granted that no wailing or sound can be random, then Awatanne exists.
This is the scientific evidence that it exists.
They know. They already have a communication code which can guarantee their survival.
Here's another simple example.
When the baby is born, when it leaves the mother's womb, the first thing it does is crying.
So we adults, with our usual presumption, with our need to qualify all things, we could think that they are crying out of pain, out of sadness.
Not at all.
It's just a functional cry.
From the liquid to the gaseous state, it must cry to begin its survival in the new world.
When we see a newborn in the crib, we even think it is playing with us.
Not at all, nothing as romantic as this.
In short, let's say that newborns, from 0 to 3 months, could not care less.
Because they have a goal.
And that goal is to find and save energy.
Everything the baby does, it does in order to communicate.
Then I come to think that we are all born equal, or at least, even if our DNA tells us that we are all different, all unique, we all share the same communication code.
All of us.
And today I have breaking news for you: we are all bilingual.
Because each of us, for a moment, has used this code.
The code of sounds, the code unrelated to words.
Clearly, now we've grown up, and now we know languages, we know mathematics.
But we can not really fool ourselves into believing that our emotions can be transferred through technology, through an emoticon.
Because technology is an amazing, a wonderful thing which has now truly reached an incredible level as it has connected us all over the world. Connected us, but only for operational communications: "Anna, tomorrow I'll pick you up at 3 pm."
I can write this to Anna.
But I can't write
"I love you"; "I care for you".
She wouldn't understand.
I should whisper it, maybe telling her: "Awatanne".
Then she would really understand how much I love her.
Some university professors have considered Awatanne as a valid tool for therapy or even for communication.
And many other things.
But I am going to stop here, because, as Elena said, I am not an expert in this field, and I do not have the tools to really tell you what those newborns were saying in that room.
But I'm sure they were communicating with each other.
They understood each other.
So we need to stop for a moment.
Because our mistake is that we have forgotten that our life, too, has been threatened.
All of us were in danger, 0 to 3 months.
Now, living in our stubborn beliefs, we think this can no longer happen.
Obviously, we're wrong, because human beings's lives are constantly in danger.
For a million reasons.
In this moment, too.
Maybe we are lucky, we are inside our own world, in our narrow space where we only need a few words.
But we've become poorer, we cannot really communicate among ourselves, really feel emotions.
So I'd like to leave you with some food for thought.
Let's go back to Awatanne.
I called it Awatanne because it reminds me of Africa.
Because this code, obviously made with many diphthongs, reminds me of Africa.
And then, the first thing a professor told me, and it had never occurred to me, he said to me, "Well, it's obvious,
we all come from Africa.
We all come from Africa, it's written in our genetic code.
We all come from Africa”.
I am pleased, in this historical moment, to emphasize this.
So I ask myself and I ask you, let's try to stop for a moment and look within ourselves for that zero-to-three month creature, because we have been there.
And so it's clear that we can go back there.
We can go back communicating with our vibrations, with sensations.
We can go back to Awatanne, maybe through music.
In order to really understand deep emotions.
Love for each other.
The meaning of life.
In order to understand love.
Let's be small again,
then we'll really be grown up.
The story I'm about to tell is very personal. Let’s say it’s an autobiographical story, which, somehow, is the story of my family.
In general, though, it could also be the story of yours, and broadly speaking, on some level, the story of us being human.
The main characters in my story are the two ladies you see from behind. The one on the right is my 81-year-old mom. The one on the left is my 7-year-old daughter, but at times, the things she says make her seem older than 81.
One day, the little one asked me, "Daddy, where do we come from?"
As luck would have it, about a year before, we were coming back from a trip, and we were at the Addis Ababa International Airport, which is the most important hub for African air traffic today.
Addis Ababa has something peculiar: it's the place where, at the National Museum, there is possibly the most ancient specimen of our ancestors, of what could be one of our first direct ancestors to assume a bipedal posture. This is Lucy, a specimen of Australopithecus discovered in 1974 by a palaeoanthropologist, Donald Johanson, and his collaborators, dating back to 3.2 million years ago.
Through the years, discovering more remains of this distant ancestor, he found out that it was probably one of the first species to walk on two legs.
Our story, though, begins from a period which is slightly more recent.
Actually, a few years ago, luck would have it that I happened to take part in the Genographic Project of the National Geographic Society.
What is the Genographic Project?
It is an attempt to reconstruct the map of human migration through DNA analysis of as many people as possible: so far, about 800,000 people have taken part in it.
How does it work?
A saliva sample is sent to Washington.
There, it is analysed, and thanks to the 750,000 markers on our DNA, they try to trace the variations, the DNA mutations, which caused human populations to differentiate in the 70,000 years in which Homo sapiens colonised the world, the diverse human populations.
Through the Genographic Project, they have reconstructed this map showing the place from which Homo sapiens set off almost 70,000 years. The area isn't exactly the one of Addis Ababa, but the one of the Horn of Africa - migrating first north, and then, over the course of 50,000 years, colonising even the southern end of the Americas.
It took us 50,000 years to settle throughout the world.
Thanks to Genographic, it was possible to reconstruct, on the one hand, my paternal line, the one we always inherit from our fathers and that women, unfortunately, can't reconstruct
because the paternal line can only be obtained with a Y chromosome, which only males have.
On the other hand, through the mitochondrial DNA that we inherit only from our mothers, they managed to trace the story of the lady I showed you earlier.
Here's where the story began, about 67,000 years ago in Central Africa, when modern Homo sapiens started migrating from this region.
Around 60,000 years ago, he found himself crossing the Sinai Peninsula, where one of these populations, identified through these genetic markers, moves across the Sinai Peninsula, headed for the Arabian Peninsula in the Middle East, where they split, some moving towards Anatolia, in Turkey, others heading north to the Caucasus, and still others heading east to Asia.
Years later, the descendants of this group kept on moving, some northwards and some eastwards: we are talking about 55,000 years ago more or less.
About 40,000 years ago, another population appeared, some of them heading straight for Turkey to then penetrate into Europe,
the others choosing the longer option, crossing the Caucasus, and starting to colonise the northern part of Europe.
What happened?
When the glacial period occurred 20,000 years ago, those who had colonised the northern part of Europe began moving south and settling in warmer regions, like Italy, Spain, and at the same time, England too, which surely has a more temperate climate than the rest of northern Europe.
What next? Is this the end of the story?
Not at all - we are actually going backwards through the story, because when reconstructing all the phases characterising the migration of my all maternal line, we left out several fundamental or crucial points which, truth be told, were themselves strokes of luck,
in order to imagine how we ended up in the Po Valley - I come from Milan ‒ starting from a remote spot in the African continent 70,000 years ago.
One of the first places we passed through is, more or less, the area where Italy and Austria border each other, where the Similaun glacier is to be found and a few years ago, Ötzi, also known as the Iceman, was brought to light.
This mummified corpse, perfectly preserved for more than 5,000 years inside the Alpine glaciers, resurfaced when the ice started melting.
We have so much evidence that we can actually imagine how our European predecessors lived about 5,000 years ago.
We know that this man died in mysterious circumstances: it was confirmed that he had an arrowhead lodged in his left shoulder, which is what probably caused his death. They also found the weapons he used to hunt, clothes and much more. Somehow we succeeded in understanding that our ancestors led a very tough life between the plain, the hills and the mountains of Northern Italy,
and that they were indeed great hunters.
Moving a little further north, we find ourselves in this area of Germany, in Leipzig to be precise, at the Max Planck Institution for Evolutionary Anthropology.
Here you can see this man, Svante Pääbo, who is one of the greatest academics in the field of population genetics through DNA analysis.
The object he's holding is the mold of a Neanderthal skull. He managed to sequence it, through the few remains we have of the Neanderthal genome,
to find out something fundamental, which I also spotted in my maternal line,
the fact that we inherited part of our European genome from the Neanderthal - on average 2.1%.
That is to say that my own personal DNA is 2% from Neanderthals. In the period in which Neanderthals and Homo sapiens coexisted - between 60,000 and 40,000 years ago, when Neanderthals disappeared -
Homo sapiens interbred not only with Neanderthal but also with another quite bizarre hominid we don't know much about. We only made a discovery in one of the caves of the Altai mountains, between Russia and China.
Nevertheless, it was possible to sequence the DNA of this hominid, the Denisovan, and to learn that Homo sapiens also interbred with him.
Actually, until not long ago, our species lived side by side with these two species, both of which left some trace in our genetic make-up.
Going back up north and moving slightly eastwards, we reach the last place from which we are able to tell what Europe was like before the glaciation. earlier than 20,000 years ago: the Bialowieza Forest, in Poland.
These images represent the current scenery, not the one from to 20,000 years ago. The Bialowieża Forest is the last significant stretch of primeval forest we have in Europe, and it's also the last forest where European bisons still live free. This animal, 20,000 years ago, was possibly a favourite prey of our ancestors
and almost completely died out because of hunting.
If we keep on moving south, we reach the Caucasus, but let’s take a step back here. This is not the history of Homo sapiens anymore, but we still have to do something of great importance because 1,800,000 years ago, one of our ancestors, Homo georgicus or maybe Homo erectus - we still haven't figured out whether they belong to the same species or not - had arrived in the Caucasus region. After settling down,
they started reproducing. They gathered in small villages, just like the Vikings would do in North America, but they could not remain there. Actually, the whole population of Europe is Homo sapiens.
But in Dmanisi, in Georgia, they found four perfectly preserved skulls belonging to this extraordinary hominid who 800,000 years ago had already started colonising Europe.
If we descend even further, retracing the path my mother's ancestors took,
we get to Anatolia, Turkey, and if we reach the southern part, located almost on the border with Syria, an area nowadays ravaged by awful wars, we finally find ourselves in Göbekli Tepe.
This place is the most ancient religious site ever known, probably a ritual centre, dating back to around 11,500 years ago.
This is the first and oldest testimony of the fact that man started becoming sedentary.
What's more, he probably turned to a sedentary life in that very area, because not far from there, they found out - these are the inscriptions that haven't been deciphered yet, the ones of Göbekli Tepe, which has only recently been discovered - that not far from there, about 10,500 years ago, sometime before man became sedentary, agriculture developed.
The first traces of domestication of Triticum monococcum were found there, and that's where agriculture probably started and favoured stable settlements, interrupting nomadism and ushering in the Neolithic. Eventually, it allowed the people of the time to start having something resembling a religious centre or generally speaking, a centre for rituals of which we don't know that much.
If we keep on going back, rewinding, as if it were, the film of our story, we get there, where that yellow point is: this is the Manot cave, in Israel.
Here we go back to that small DNA part, the tiny DNA part of Neanderthal existing in my DNA, the 2% of which we were talking earlier.
Chances are that around 55,000 years ago, Homo sapiens and Neanderthal
met in this very place, in this particular cave. But also, in others located in this area of the Middle East, they found evidence pointing to the cohabitation or in any case, to the coexistence of Homo sapiens and Neanderthal.
So we know for sure that in that portion of the Middle East, Homo sapiens and Neanderthal lived together.
It is not clear whether at peace or not; this we don't know, but they did live together.
There, in all likelihood, the two species interbred, and that's why Neanderthal DNA traces are to be found in our DNA.
Now, if we try to move further south, we don't find any specific additional sign of Homo sapiens’s journey, but the fact is that in the Omo Valley, and more specifically right in the region of Addis Ababa, in Ethiopia, was found the most ancient Homo sapiens fossil ever known.
It's the primitive version of Homo sapiens, dating back to 200,000 years ago.
Before the migration, Homo sapiens really changed in that region as well. He had probably colonised some parts of Africa, especially southern Africa. And around 100,000 years ago, he apparently went through a pretty terrible period, probably because of climatic reasons,
a moment in which man was on the brink of extinction.
Actually, the population then thrived once more, and about 70,000 years ago, started migrating from this very area.
This is my story. We have come to the end of the trip, and we are back to where we started.
This is my story, but it could be the story of many of you -
of many Europeans at least - because it's highly likely that if you did the same DNA analysis, the results would be similar.
We are back to where we started from, Addis Ababa, and we are back to a point whereby telling my individual story, we managed to somehow reconstruct part of the history of humanity.
If you'd like to know more about the Genographic Project: genographic.nationalgeographic.com.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Here I am, on the path of the sketchbook.
I mean "way" in the Chinese, Japanese sense of Tao and Do;
and this is the little sketchbook that I always carry in my pocket, and the slightly larger one in my travel bag.
The sketchbook is essentially, for me, the expression of the yearning for an active, manually vivid relationship with the things that I see and know.
But what does "manually vivid" mean?
It means "not mediated by diaphragms", and there are no machines between me and the thing before me.
Sure, it's become a job.
My sketchbooks narrate journeys - Asian journeys, mostly; but also African journeys, or in challenging places like the rainforest; or far away like Australian, Antarctic seas. But the path of the sketchbook can also be taken in the comfort of your home.
You can start from the kitchen table, and from a lemon.
The lemon you see on the right was painted with tempera, and the application of tempera consists of juxtaposing layers of dense pigment.
The light on that object is given by white tempera, that is a white pigment, typically from zinc or lead, that's either mixed with color or spread as is on a yellow support layer.
When I painted the lemon, I got this little Sun that radiated on the table a very intense yellow, and the result disappointed me so much that I had to disguise it with these sepia brushstrokes in order to elicit a bit of the lemon's light.
After all, the lemon is a small Sun: if you cut it in half, you can see a Sun in how its segments are disposed.
So I repainted it in watercolor,
and the watercolor procedure is exactly the opposite: it is a work by subtraction, "by saving" as they say: the whiteness of the paper must emerge.
And you work on top of this white with light veils of very diluted pigment.
So when I finished this page I looked at it and said: I started from a "still life", in a sense, and I got to meditate on Aristotelian categories of quality and quantity. Because a lot of pigment [Quantity] ends in little light [Quality];
while little pigment makes room for much light.
And light is a lemon's main quality.
So after the lemon, I painted this.
A maximum of quantity corresponds to a minimum of quality.
To a minimum of quantity, a maximum of quality.
It is an experience that you have certainly had too: if you have only eaten the hormone-pumped strawberry that you buy in supermarkets, you will never know what's the essence of a strawberry, because the essence, the quality of a strawberry is the taste.
When I painted this picture, I said to myself again: here too I started from vegetables on the table, but this is a figure of thought.
That's nothing else.
On the path of the sketchbook, for years I have been hunting quality, the elusive essence of things.
It is very difficult to trap it in a figure because it is precisely something one cannot meter, by nature: how can you measure quality?
Two tricks: one may be this, a figure of thought; but I must tell you what I have painted, or you won't understand it. The other trick is synesthesia.
What is synesthesia?
It's why we talk about a "fundamental note" of a scent.
We use a technical expression, actually, that comes from a text of the end of the 19th century: "The Art of Perfumery" by a certain Septimus Piesse who established an objective, in his opinion, correspondence between perfumes and musical notes.
That's what Ratatouille the mouse does, when he ingests something, in the Disney movie.
Those colourful jets come out.
Here they are.
And here is the sketchbook: on top is the word "taquin", the Arabic etymology of the word; then there's an open sketchbook, with my hands drawing on an open sketchbook, drawing on an open sketchbook, drawing on an open sketchbook, and so on, ad infinitum.
Why this?
This fall "en abime" exactly represents of the pursuit of the elusive essence of things; and in this path of the sketchbook, it is a way to represent the infinite in the finite.
But from this open sketchbook some colored filaments depart, and these colored filaments are precisely perfumes, flavors, memories, emotions.
Chromatic synthesis of essential experiences.
My life, basically.
Chromatic: color has a lot to do with the path of the sketchbook.
The sketchbook is in my pocket; in my travel bag, I always have a box of colours.
"Unweaving the rainbow", it's a line from Keats.
Unweaving the rainbow means to start from this quantity, from this pigment in the trays, and getting from these few colours, actually three basic colours are enough, just three colors get me all the infinite variations of the shades of the skies and the earth, the sunsets and the dawns of this world.
Then sure, the quantitative side comes always back out: for example, in the colour code of a shop. But colour is quality.
Chances are, in a paint shop, you buy your can of the color you picked on the color chart, then apply it on the wall and say: "This is not the color I meant!"
It's almost always the case.
The colour's transposition in quantity is also on the pages of the atlases: races and peoples correspond to coloured cartouches: color conventions to describe. This is also, by the way,
how spectrography works.
No, colour is really pure quality.
You can't divide colour: you always divide a coloured matter.
Color is this white light that is refracted in the prism of multiplicity.
Colour can't be grasped.
I like the name of this page: "Unweaving the Rainbow", because the rainbow summons my great friend here, Plato, who said that philosophers are the sons of Taumante.
Taumante is amazement. Philosophy originates from amazement:
sons of Taumante like Iris - the rainbow.
And Plato does not explain us the reason for this strange genealogy, but it's easy to figure out: philosophers are those who wonder about the astonishing variety of being, they ask questions like,
Why being over nothingness?
Why so many things are there, so different from each other?
And why are we always different when we look at them?
Time: we are always different.
And we measure time, continuously.
There it is: the liquid crystals, which tell me to hurry up; the hands turning in the clock face, and the grains of sand falling into the hourglass.
Is this time?
No, time is personal, individual: each of you has your own time.
At different times in your life, you live your time subjectively in a different way.
So I asked myself: how can I represent the quality of time?
First of all, with a cycle: from egg to butterfly.
When I painted this image, I followed the development of this animal, egg to nympho. And I painted it step by step.
And then, above all, it's a metamorphosis.
And the metamorphosis is this creation that comes from destruction.
No image is more evident and powerful of time and its action.
Now put colour and time together: it's the revelation of light.
That's the bell tower of the church of Modica, in three moments: illuminated by electric light at night; at five in the morning; and then when the first ray of Sun goes to touch the spire of the bell tower.
This is one of the most beautiful hunts on the path of the sketchbook: the one that drove Claude Monet crazy when he was chasing the light.
A great French writer, now I don't remember who he was, said in his garden in Giverny:
"He is not a painter. He is a hunter."
The hunt for light:
if we talk about light, we must also talk about shadow,
this bodily limit of our bodies, which strangely enough, let's say mysteriously, has to do with our personality.
It seems that between eight and fourteen months newborns recognize themselves in their own shadow and reflection.
So the shadow is, with the reflection, the only natural situation of autoscopy - we can't actually see ourselves. Before the invention of reflecting tools, such as mirrors or polished metals, only shadows and reflections let us know our image; and this very close relationship with the incorporeal within us is really important.
Here is another page on shadow.
I'd rather not speak to you about this cage, a bird has just escaped from: its shadow is projected on the wall, I would like each of you to give it your own interpretation.
Instead I want you to notice the writing: shadow.
There's no body here.
It's like the phantom limb of the amputee: they feel it, but it's not there.
The positive, if you can read what is written there, is in your mind.
Neuroscience tells us, only ten percent of the information the brain uses to see comes from the eyes.
Only ten percent.
And this whole realm, which is around the very little that the eye per se tells us, is mind.
It's the elusive side of quality.
Let's talk eyes now: the window of the soul, the window of self-consciousness.
That thing there that you see on the right is the zero degree of vision; but it is also the zero degree of consciousness.
We know that we look out of this window, and we see very little of ourselves. I used to look at the previous speaker and I saw him better than he certainly knew himself;
because I see a little bit of myself, a little bit of blurred nose, a little bit of eyelid rhyme, a few hairs of eyebrows, and the front side of my body, except for my chin.
What's behind that?
I don't know.
If there were no mirrors, and now all those images around us, I wouldn't know.
It's paradoxical.
I know one thing, though: in front of me are the others.
And I suspect a certain degree of self-consciousness in you, but I cannot be sure.
I am only sure of my self-consciousness: this is real, I have experienced it.
So, for me subject, the others, even if they are there to say "I, Ich, moi, أنا " and so on, each one to assert his own selfishness, are always objects for me subject.
This is how in the West the relationship of knowledge has been nurtured, at least from Descartes onwards: a subject-object polarity.
As a consequence, our hypertrophic ego, locked in its narcissistic bubble, is besieged by the other.
The other can also be an animal, as we have seen before.
There is no relation: that bubble should really explode, but this is our condition now.
We get out of here to find ourselves more or less in this situation;
actually, if we travel a bit we discover that the West is a bit represented by this image.
Now, however, we have started from a lemon and we have come to talk about the subject-object approach, the conditions of the relationship with the other in our culture.
And I'd like to invite you to change unit of measure and dive into entomology.
I painted this page in Japan on a beautiful autumn morning.
The praying mantis appeared to me on the branches of a Japanese maple, and it was the revelation of both a biological wonder - this insect is extraordinary - but also of the genius of a language.
The Japanese word for insect is mushi, and it comes from an ancient word, musu, which implies the idea of a natural appearance, so it is the idea of an epiphany.
There is a context: crickets appear in the damp night, cicadas in the summer heat, butterflies in spring.
And we observers are inside the context where the epiphany takes place.
We are "com-pressed" in relation to what we see.
We say instead "in-sect" which comes from the Latin sectare [dissect].
And this "sectare" is precisely the appearent manifestation of the structure of an animal, of a morphology: it is the objectification of a morphology.
Can you see the difference between these two attitudes?
This is the last image I want to conclude with.
It's a self-portrait: I represented myself as Shi Tao, the great Chinese painter, who contemplated the landscape in search of its intimate nature.
He contemplated the landscape before contemplating while painting the landscape.
Painting as a way of contemplation.
He recalls this in his "Treatise on Painting": up to a point in his life, a landscape was something that stood before him. But then the landscape was born in him, and he was born in the landscape.
And the landscape asked him to be his voice.
And I represented myself before these sacred mountains, listening to the voice of the landscape.
And what that white line between the two peaks is, I leave it for you to guess: you might hear the roar of a waterfall, if you look at it for a long time; or perhaps the fog slowly rising towards the sky.
For me, it is the door wide open in front of this encounter with the beauty of creation, with the beauty of the landscape, on the path of the sketchbook.
(Applause)
Good evening.
There is a road in my town called "Via della Barbiera" [Road of the Barber].
One day, I was wondering why the road was called this. The following story comes from that road.
It is the story of a journey.
Once upon a time, it was only men who traveled. But a little girl decided to leave the mountains, all alone.
There was no war yet. The winter was just over.
The morning she left, she took down a mirror from above the kitchen sink, she took her rosary from the head of her bed and put her coral earrings in her pocket.
And then she left, without saying farewell to anyone.
She felt a lump in her throat as she walked out the door.
She also felt it while going down the stairs. Then she crossed the farmyard, and she felt it no longer.
She trusted in the path she was following. She trusted in the Virgin Mother's will.
She had never been away from her home before.
It had a slate roof, a few rooms that were dark and smoky, the buzzing sound of flies. It was located between the roads "Passo delle Forbici" and "Casone,"
names only known by the local people.
It was a lonely place - without piazzas, churches or anything else.
It was surrounded by woods and dominated by the sky.
She stood there under the sky, under the slate roof.
She was not pretty, not ugly, but skinny, skinny. A scarf on her head covered her hair; hair that no one had ever seen.
She was the oldest in her family. Her parents were charcoal-burners - a narrow-minded family. Suffering seemed to be their only companion.
When she was seven, she had to stand on a stool to reach the kitchen sink. There, she would wash dishes. Then she would go to the well for water.
Snow, rain ... it was her duty.
It was she who did the laundry in the river, using lye in the cold water, her small hands sometimes growing numb.
At night, it was she who was awakened by her nonna:
"Baby girl, get up! Say a Hail Mary
before going back to sleep." And she had to pray properly,
with folded hands.
On the nightstand was a plate,
covered by yellow paper, and a knife.
They prayed.
Then the old woman would take the plate. On it was a slice of bread, some butter and sugar. She would butter the bread
and add some sugar. She would then remove the crust, using the knife since she had no teeth.
After that, they would eat it together. The young girl would fall asleep with the flavor of sugar in her mouth.
It was the only sweet thing she ever ate
because her family never had such things. She was sad that no one ever let her sit on their lap.
She never received compliments, never received kisses.
Sometimes she wanted to brush up against her family, just like her cat did.
But she realized she was as timid as they were. She grew up without throwing tantrums.
Two things she had to fear: displeasing the Virgin Mary and her father's belt.
One morning, her father came home, swearing. His hand was wrapped in a rag, bleeding.
He had cut off his fingers
while using a billhook.
He stayed at home for some days, acting like a beast in a cage.
One afternoon, they were at home alone. She was washing dishes and he was sitting at the table.
All of a sudden, he told her to hurry;
she had to shave him. The little girl noticed that he already had a razor in his hand. She silently kept on washing dishes.
Then she neared her father. She took the mirror from above the kitchen sink and put it into his good hand.
While the bristles were soaking, swelling up with soap, her father told her how to shave.
He then leaned his head backward. The brush covered his face with foam.
As she grabbed the razor, he said "Be careful,
little girl. This is not a game.
This tool is sharp."
It was the first time that she was holding
something usually used by men. She sank the blade into the foam. Then she neared the blade to his flesh. She let her hand trace his face from his cheekbone to his chin. A smooth stripe remained on his skin, like a road.
It all came naturally to her.
There are abilities within us of which we may not be aware.
On that day, she found hers.
When she was done, her father touched his face and said, "Um ... uh um." It was a sound of approval.
She was not yet 12, but they sent her to work for another family.
She was given the same old tasks, which included shaving the head of the household,
because her parents had told the family that she had a special talent for shaving.
She remained there for many years.
The tools for shaving were inside a beautiful, red tin box.
The day she escaped was a day she had planned for a long time.
She escaped after work.
That morning she had taken the mirror from above the sink of her home.
She also stole the tin box
from the second house. She stole a towel and a piece of cheese too. She was about to steal some eggs, but instead grabbed a hen. It was sleeping on its perch.
It only took a matter of seconds. She put it into her bag and went away in silence.
On the previous Sunday, she had woken up early and went to San Pellegrino. She carried a big big stone with her.
San Pellegrino was on the other side of the mountain, and she began to walk across the woods. The stone was in a basket on her shoulders.
San Pellegrino is on the summit of a mountain.
It is a hamlet made of houses all located on the same road. On that road stands the sanctuary with the remains of Saint Pellegrino.
He was the son of a king but had refused the crown and decided to travel. Pellegrino, meaning pilgrim, comes from that.
He was guided by a star that led him up to those mountains, and he was followed by the devil. The devil was very angry with this man
as he did not give in to temptations. One day, the devil gave him such a strong slap that he rotated three times.
The power of that slap made the earth tremble. Some stones even detached from the mountain.
Today, in that precise place, there is a multitude of stones.
People have brought them there
as a sign of devotion. The heavier their sense of guilt, the bigger the stone they carried.
Before placing it, you have to cross the whole expanse on your knees, three times.
The little girl was exhausted when she reached the place. As she circled the area, her knees were cracking. Eventually, she embraced her stone.
"Oh, Saint Pellegrino, forgive me my faults, help me to escape."
She prayed to him to be her guide since she did not know any path but the one that went back home. She prayed she would remain
safe and sound along her journey.
She prayed for him to have mercy on her,
that she had stayed awake many nights thinking, but she could stand her home no longer.
She said he had left his home instead of becoming a king.
And she continued praying.
She prayed that her parents
would understand her intentions. She left her stone there and asked to receive the strength to not go home again.
She sealed her prayer.
She put down her stone. She put a bit of saliva on her cracked knees.
She felt her shoulders grow lighter, but her soul was still heavy.
Despite her heavy soul, her sack full of stolen things, the hen in her sack, she decided to follow a path that did not go back home. Her parents noticed
her absence not long afterwards.
They could have despaired, cursing fate. However, they had no time to waste looking for her.
She grew full of darkness and remorse.
The rosary was her support.
She recited it all the time. Prayer after prayer.
Scratches or slips did not bother her.
She only felt the hen tremble in the sack; the animal was as frightened as she.
She traveled a long time. She traveled from one mount to another.
At a certain point, she realized she had reached a plain, as there was open land. In front of her stood the sea.
She had never seen it before.
She had imagined it many times, but even then, she could not reach it.
She had too much to do before going to the sea.
The road divided the plain in half. Here and there were farmhouses, hens and cows. To her, all seemed to be scattered;
in the mountains, it was more compact.
She noticed a narrow path along a ditch. There stood a beautiful fig tree.
Underneath it stood a stump.
There were no people. Yet, she felt like hiding.
She said the rosary again and placed it around her neck. Then she moved towards the fig tree.
She freed the hen, which at first stood there stunned.
Afterwards, she hung the mirror to a branch of the tree. The girl scraped the tin box and wrote on it "Barbiera." [Barber]
The barber began to shave the men living on the plain.
Those were people of few words; they came from nothing.
She began to shave both peasants and sailors.
They only knew that she had come from the mountains above. They also knew that the Argenes let her sleep in their barn.
Men got into the habit of meeting under the fig tree. Saturday was when they had their beard perfectly shaved.
Some of them had their hair spiked with hair oil.
When she finished with one, she would shake a towel, and then it was the next one's turn.
She would never smile or talk with anyone. Her eyes were always down, but she had a master touch.
Under the fig tree, she learned from the men's conversations; as she shaved one, the others talked about seed prices or journeys to Brazil, Argentina - the chance to change their lives.
When they raised their voices, she pretended nothing was happening.
They talked about Sundays in the barnyards, where they danced with accordions playing.
She liked listening to them.
Under the fig tree, she even learned of the way sailors thought.
Sailors were different from other men.
They had seen so many things all over the world, mamma mia.
When they told their stories, they seemed to be fairy tales.
They always whistled too.
All men used to whistle. They rode their bikes, whistling like nightingales.
But sailors whistled more.
Sailors used their whistle as a way to communicate.
The sailors from Versilia
had their own way to whistle. If they happened to be in a far distant place, where they didn't know the language, they would whistle in their own way. If another was nearby, they would meet, thanks to their whistle.
Sailors also smelled different than other men.
She learned how men smelled by spending time with them. She even made odor-based categories.
Sailors smelled of salty tar. Nothing else.
They did not pay her with money. They paid her with bread, vegetables and grapes.
One time, a sailor brought her a novel. It was a big, clothbound book.
She was not able to read fast. She had to read the sentences more than once to understand them. She also read them aloud.
Then she got into it. It so caught her interest that she read it about seven times.
The girl wanted to read more books, but she had no chance to do that.
One day, a group of married women came chatting. No one was there for shaving.
"Oh, you filthy girl, go away from here. Understand?
The men do not want to make love with you.
They just want to get pleasure from you.
You are disgusting."
They pushed her, making her fall.
She wanted to speak, to explain to them. But the words stuck in her throat. Those gossiping women, devoid of any grace,
who seemed to be devouring her
reminded her of her real fault, her only remorse, that she had run away from home.
A decision she had never repented.
It was Argene who stopped them.
Argene heard some noise and rushed in.
"Go away! Leave! I'll take care of her.
I tell you that this girl has done nothing wrong."
His arm remained outstretched until they walked away.
One week later, they saw her with Cesarino; he and the girl were were changed.
They went to see the priest.
It was she who told the priest about Brazil.
She had heard of people who had set off with nothing. People who had made their way there.
They embarked in December. On that occasion, she wore her coral earrings. She even took off her headscarf, showing her beautiful, braided hair. She looked pretty.
In my town, in Forte dei Marmi, there is a road called "Via della Barbiera."
The origin of this name is vague.
Some say it comes from "barrier," because it is a road near the border of two towns.
Others say that is comes from "Barberi," a common local surname.
But most say that on that road, when it still was a little path, lived a woman. A woman who would shave men under a fig tree.
(Applause) Thank you. (Applause)
